insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('9d67fe0c-87b5-5f20-8e54-abe91f5b3a17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the term for the process of sperm production in the seminiferous tubules?', '["Spermiation","Spermiogenesis","Spermatocytogenesis","Spermatogenesis","Oogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatogenesis', 'Spermatogenesis is the complete process by which spermatogonia in the seminiferous tubules develop into mature spermatozoa. Spermiogenesis covers only the final morphological transformation of spermatids into spermatozoa, while spermiation is the release of mature spermatozoa into the tubular lumen. Oogenesis is the female counterpart.'),
('5854dedc-0e98-52d7-8f4c-db743ddee222'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the correct sequence of spermatogenesis stages?', '["Spermatogonia→Primary spermatocyte→Spermatid→Secondary spermatocyte→Spermatozoon","Spermatogonia→Primary spermatocyte→Secondary spermatocyte→Spermatid→Spermatozoon","Primary spermatocyte→Spermatogonia→Secondary spermatocyte→Spermatid→Spermatozoon","Spermatogonia→Secondary spermatocyte→Primary spermatocyte→Spermatid→Spermatozoon","Spermatogonia→Spermatid→Primary spermatocyte→Secondary spermatocyte→Spermatozoon"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatogonia→Primary spermatocyte→Secondary spermatocyte→Spermatid→Spermatozoon', 'Spermatogenesis follows a fixed order in which spermatogonia divide to form primary spermatocytes, which undergo the first meiotic division to yield secondary spermatocytes, then a second meiotic division to form spermatids that mature into spermatozoa. The other sequences reverse the spermatid and secondary spermatocyte steps or begin with the wrong cell type.'),
('12259f81-5ac1-5bdb-8aa5-0a7dbdda0816'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'At what stage of meiosis does the first meiotic arrest occur during oogenesis?', '["Prophase I","Metaphase II","Prophase II","Anaphase I","Metaphase I"]'::jsonb, 'Prophase I', 'During fetal life, oogonia enter meiosis and arrest in prophase I, remaining in that arrested state until ovulation. Meiosis resumes at ovulation but arrests again at metaphase II until fertilization, so the other stages listed are not the site of the first arrest.'),
('4db0055d-088b-52ee-9cdc-ea57dc55f02b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the enzyme that helps sperm penetrate the zona pellucida?', '["Hyaluronidase","Trypsin","Collagenase","Neuraminidase","Acrosin"]'::jsonb, 'Acrosin', 'Acrosin is a protease stored in the acrosome that digests the zona pellucida, allowing the sperm head to pass through. Hyaluronidase loosens the cumulus cells surrounding the oocyte but is not the zona-digesting enzyme, and the remaining enzymes are not part of normal sperm penetration.'),
('50698d08-d214-54d2-9e15-3137c4fe37e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Where does fertilization typically occur in the female reproductive tract?', '["Cervix","Isthmus","Fimbriae","Fundus of uterus","Ampulla of fallopian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of fallopian tube', 'Fertilization normally occurs in the ampulla of the fallopian tube, the widest portion of the tube near the ovary. The isthmus is closer to the uterus, the fimbriae only sweep up the oocyte, and the cervix and fundus of the uterus are not sites of fertilization.'),
('efca1bf1-a660-5ca3-b68e-a3612e68fa5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the structure formed after fusion of male and female pronuclei?', '["Embryoblast","Morula","Blastocyst","Zygote","Trophoblast"]'::jsonb, 'Zygote', 'Fusion of the male and female pronuclei produces the zygote, the single diploid cell that begins embryonic development. A morula is the solid ball of blastomeres formed by cleavage, the blastocyst has a blastocele, and the embryoblast and trophoblast are cell populations within the blastocyst.'),
('66366a49-62dc-5c6f-bd9d-904aa8350d5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'How many days after fertilization does implantation normally occur?', '["7-10 days","14 days","21 days","3 days","5-6 days"]'::jsonb, '7-10 days', 'The blastocyst begins to implant into the endometrium roughly 7-10 days after fertilization. Earlier stages such as the morula at 3 days and the early blastocyst at 5-6 days are still traveling through the tube, and 14 or 21 days would be far too late for implantation.'),
('c741dc6b-095c-59e5-a598-b19ec744d781'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which layer of the blastocyst directly invades the endometrium during implantation?', '["Epiblast","Embryoblast","Trophoblast","Hypoblast","Inner cell mass"]'::jsonb, 'Trophoblast', 'The trophoblast is the outer layer of the blastocyst that invades and erodes the endometrium during implantation, later differentiating into cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast. The embryoblast (inner cell mass) gives rise to the embryo proper and does not invade the uterine lining, and the epiblast and hypoblast are derivatives of the inner cell mass.'),
('c0332329-f0a6-5469-9b4d-55463737bb1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the cavity that forms within the inner cell mass?', '["Amniotic cavity","Chorionic cavity","Extraembryonic coelom","Umbilical cord","Yolk sac"]'::jsonb, 'Amniotic cavity', 'The amniotic cavity develops as a cleft within the inner cell mass, between the epiblast and the overlying cytotrophoblast. The chorionic cavity and extraembryonic coelom surround the embryo rather than lying inside the inner cell mass, and the yolk sac and umbilical cord are separate structures.'),
('1cda1cc0-2a6b-57ad-93f1-afa6f4c8c4d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which germ layer gives rise to the nervous system?', '["Mesoderm","Notochord","Neural crest","Endoderm","Ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Ectoderm', 'The nervous system develops from the ectoderm, which thickens into the neural plate and then the neural tube. Mesoderm forms connective tissue and muscle, endoderm forms the gut and its derivatives, the notochord is a mesodermal rod, and neural crest cells are a special ectodermal population.'),
('26993dac-245b-538a-972a-4f2b5196dcb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The notochord is derived from which structure?', '["Primitive node","Neural crest","Prechordal plate","Neural fold","Primitive streak"]'::jsonb, 'Primitive node', 'The notochord develops from notochordal cells that migrate through the primitive node (Hensen''s node) and form the notochordal process. The primitive streak is the migration route itself, the prechordal plate lies rostral to the node, and the neural folds and neural crest are ectodermal derivatives.'),
('4acd1a5b-1b05-5a50-9737-7de4eaba2662'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the process by which the neural plate folds to form the neural tube?', '["Gastrulation","Induction","Neurulation","Histogenesis","Organogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Neurulation', 'Neurulation is the process in which the flat neural plate elevates at its edges to form neural folds that fuse dorsally into a neural tube. Gastrulation establishes the three germ layers before neurulation, induction describes signaling between tissues, and histogenesis and organogenesis are later events.'),
('6192ad11-7ac8-5bc7-a427-a0ad18a7a481'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What congenital abnormality results from failure of the neural tube to close at the cranial end?', '["Myelomeningocele","Meningocele","Spina bifida","Anencephaly","Hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 'Anencephaly', 'Failure of the anterior (cranial) neuropore to close produces anencephaly, in which the forebrain fails to develop and the vault of the skull is absent. Spina bifida and its meningocele and myelomeningocele forms result from defective closure of the posterior neuropore, and hydrocephalus is a disturbance of CSF circulation, not a closure defect.'),
('3a7788f8-76aa-5f5d-8c42-24eab222d9b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common form of twinning?', '["Conjoined twins","Monozygotic (identical) twins","Symmetric twins","Asymmetric twins","Dizygotic (fraternal) twins"]'::jsonb, 'Dizygotic (fraternal) twins', 'Dizygotic (fraternal) twins arise from the fertilization of two separate ova and are the most common form of twinning. Monozygotic twins come from one zygote splitting and are less common, and conjoined, symmetric, and asymmetric twins are all monozygotic variants.'),
('74eb1a0d-4962-56c2-a82d-d97d8088c148'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What determines the sex of the embryo?', '["Number of X chromosomes","Presence of Y chromosome","Presence of SRY gene on Y chromosome","Müllerian inhibiting factor level","Testosterone level"]'::jsonb, 'Presence of SRY gene on Y chromosome', 'The presence of the SRY gene on the Y chromosome directs the indifferent gonad to develop into testes, thereby determining maleness. The number of X chromosomes, testosterone, and Müllerian inhibiting factor act later or are secondary to testis formation.'),
('dd139cdf-4541-5f03-9ef6-916846286b58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common chromosomal abnormality in live births?', '["Trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome)","Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)","Klinefelter syndrome (47 XXY)","Turner syndrome (45 X)","Trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 'Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)', 'Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) is the most frequently encountered chromosomal abnormality among live-born infants. Trisomy 13 and Trisomy 18 usually die early, Turner syndrome fetuses often abort, and Klinefelter syndrome is far less common than trisomy 21.'),
('cb0b6849-fc91-5f61-b3cc-2e849fe88240'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'At which week of development does the primitive streak appear?', '["Week 2","Week 5","Week 4","Week 3","Week 6"]'::jsonb, 'Week 3', 'The primitive streak appears at the beginning of the third week (around day 15) and initiates gastrulation. In weeks 2, 4, 5, and 6 the embryo is either too early for the streak or already past the stage of streak-mediated cell migration.'),
('c2433e71-c6f7-5eb6-900b-1549b1cbb207'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the fate of the primitive streak?', '["It becomes the neural tube","It degenerates and disappears","It forms the notochord","It forms the placenta","It becomes the umbilical cord"]'::jsonb, 'It degenerates and disappears', 'The primitive streak is a temporary structure that actively degenerates and disappears by the end of the fourth week. It does not form the neural tube, notochord, placenta, or umbilical cord; the notochord arises from the primitive node and the neural tube from ectoderm.'),
('b298015b-6885-554d-a2b7-06e64e44c692'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The neural crest cells give rise to all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Melanocytes","Dorsal root ganglia","Odontoblasts","Lens of eye","Adrenal medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Lens of eye', 'Neural crest cells give rise to melanocytes, dorsal root (sensory) ganglia, odontoblasts, and the adrenal medulla. The lens of the eye is derived from surface ectoderm by induction from the optic vesicle, not from neural crest.'),
('c57a956c-6fa6-589d-93ff-ff63d144dba4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which of the following is derived from the intermediate mesoderm?', '["Skin","Heart","Neural tube","Kidneys","Gut epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Kidneys', 'The intermediate mesoderm forms the kidneys, ureters, and gonads. Skin and the neural tube are ectodermal, the heart develops from splanchnic lateral mesoderm, and gut epithelium is endodermal.'),
('08146797-d078-5656-84fe-fc844529cd2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the structure that connects the developing embryo to the yolk sac?', '["Allantois","Umbilical cord","Neurenteric canal","Notochord","Vitelline duct"]'::jsonb, 'Vitelline duct', 'The vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct connects the midgut to the yolk sac and normally obliterates; its persistence can produce a Meckel diverticulum. The allantois connects to the bladder, the umbilical cord contains the vessels and ductus, the notochord is a mesodermal rod, and the neurenteric canal briefly connects the amniotic and yolk sac cavities.'),
('be2433d3-17d1-585f-b9d3-28fac04e1d66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Failure of what process leads to situs inversus?', '["Epithelial-mesenchymal transition","Anterior-posterior patterning","Dorsal-ventral axis formation","Left-right axis determination","Convergent extension"]'::jsonb, 'Left-right axis determination', 'Situs inversus results from a failure of left-right axis determination, so organs are mirrored across the midline instead of assuming their usual sided arrangement. Epithelial-mesenchymal transition, anterior-posterior patterning, dorsal-ventral axis formation, and convergent extension do not govern left-right asymmetry.'),
('b516fc69-02ad-5c06-9351-c63eb8b3e71f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which signaling molecule is critical for anterior-posterior patterning of the embryo?', '["FGF8","Sonic hedgehog (Shh)","BMP4","Wnt3a","Retinoic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Sonic hedgehog (Shh)', 'Sonic hedgehog (Shh) is a morphogen released by the notochord that patterns the neural tube and ventral midline structures. FGF8, BMP4, and Wnt3a participate in other signaling pathways, and retinoic acid primarily patterns the hindbrain and limb, making Shh the intended choice among these options.'),
('7092d580-7385-5f29-b873-1d9ae584cd70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the function of the SRY gene?', '["Testis determination","Adrenal development","Gonadal descent","Brain sex differentiation","Ovarian development"]'::jsonb, 'Testis determination', 'The SRY gene, located on the Y chromosome, encodes a transcription factor that triggers testis determination in the indifferent gonad. Ovarian development occurs in its absence, and adrenal development, gonadal descent, and brain sex differentiation are downstream or independent processes.'),
('e0a92270-e18a-53c8-9618-0fd1481d0e4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The zona pellucida is composed mainly of what?', '["Glycoproteins","Elastin","Collagen","Polysaccharides","Lipids"]'::jsonb, 'Glycoproteins', 'The zona pellucida is a thick extracellular coat of glycoproteins (ZP1, ZP2, ZP3) that surrounds the oocyte and mediates sperm binding. It contains essentially no elastin, collagen, lipids, or free polysaccharides.'),
('abe9c273-474d-58c5-9357-17cc5e45f791'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What prevents polyspermy after fertilization?', '["Capacitation","Acrosome reaction","Zona hatching","Cortical reaction","Fusion of pronuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Cortical reaction', 'The cortical reaction, in which cortical granules release enzymes that modify the zona pellucida, blocks further sperm entry after the first sperm fuses with the oocyte. Capacitation and the acrosome reaction occur before fusion, zona hatching happens later, and pronuclear fusion occurs after polyspermy is already prevented.'),
('a7a385fd-9686-55d6-909f-7292d31d241d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the term for the process of sperm acquiring the ability to fertilize an oocyte?', '["Acrosome reaction","Fusion","Cortical reaction","Capacitation","Zona reaction"]'::jsonb, 'Capacitation', 'Capacitation is the process in the female reproductive tract that makes sperm able to fertilize an oocyte. The acrosome reaction, cortical reaction, and zona reaction are later events at or after sperm-oocyte contact, and fusion refers to union of the gamete membranes.'),
('aadc66d0-02ff-5c5c-bca4-a78f408f83a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The inner cell mass of the blastocyst gives rise to what?', '["Trophoblast","Embryo proper","Placenta","Chorion","Yolk sac"]'::jsonb, 'Embryo proper', 'The inner cell mass (embryoblast) gives rise to the embryo proper and to extraembryonic tissues such as the yolk sac and allantois. The trophoblast forms the chorion and placenta, and the chorion and yolk sac are not derived from the inner cell mass alone.'),
('a71100db-3c1e-5f90-9f4f-b7eb1432827e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the extraembryonic membrane that forms the fetal part of the placenta?', '["Chorion","Amnion","Yolk sac","Umbilical cord","Allantois"]'::jsonb, 'Chorion', 'The chorion, formed from trophoblast and extraembryonic mesoderm, is the fetal membrane that gives rise to the fetal part of the placenta through its chorionic villi. The amnion surrounds the amniotic cavity, the yolk sac supplies early nutrition, and the allantois contributes to the umbilical cord vessels.'),
('f51eb968-480f-5550-9e57-ce013e06eaa1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'At what stage does the morula enter the uterine cavity?', '["8-cell stage","Blastocyst stage","16-cell (morula) stage","32-cell stage","4-cell stage"]'::jsonb, '16-cell (morula) stage', 'The morula, at about the 16-cell stage, enters the uterine cavity around the fourth day after fertilization. At 4 and 8 cells the cleavage embryo is still in the fallopian tube, the blastocyst stage appears later in the uterus, and the 32-cell stage is already a developing blastocyst.'),
('2ed41d3f-f9cc-5f9c-a8ea-856b57445824'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What fluid-filled cavity appears in the blastocyst?', '["Umbilical cavity","Blastocele","Chorionic cavity","Amniotic cavity","Yolk sac"]'::jsonb, 'Blastocele', 'The blastocele is the fluid-filled cavity that develops within the morula as it becomes a blastocyst. The amniotic and chorionic cavities are extraembryonic spaces that form later, and the yolk sac and umbilical cavity are separate structures.'),
('76c29eb2-0474-5784-9ee7-065106465055'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the cells that form the outer layer of the blastocyst?', '["Hypoblast","Epiblast","Inner cell mass","Trophoblast","Embryoblast"]'::jsonb, 'Trophoblast', 'The trophoblast forms the outer cell layer of the blastocyst and later contributes to the chorion and placenta. The epiblast, hypoblast, and inner cell mass (embryoblast) are internal and give rise to the embryo and extraembryonic membranes.'),
('9f6f9927-b186-5180-a9f9-d35ded248694'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The hypoblast gives rise to which structure?', '["Neural plate","Intraembryonic mesoderm","Notochord","Extraembryonic endoderm","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Extraembryonic endoderm', 'The hypoblast forms the extraembryonic endoderm that lines the yolk sac. The neural plate and surface ectoderm are ectodermal, the intraembryonic mesoderm arises from migrating streak cells, and the notochord forms from the primitive node.'),
('4307bd00-1d17-5709-9e14-e465cb2a6e3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The epiblast gives rise to which germ layers?', '["Only mesoderm","All three germ layers","Trophoblast","Only ectoderm","Only endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'All three germ layers', 'The epiblast is the source of all three germ layers: through gastrulation it gives rise to ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm. It does not contribute to the trophoblast, and it is not restricted to a single germ layer.'),
('2ad42117-b276-595b-975e-c72a54f98de4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the first event of gastrulation?', '["Development of somites","Formation of the neural plate","Formation of the primitive streak","Formation of the coelom","Appearance of the notochord"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of the primitive streak', 'The first event of gastrulation is the formation of the primitive streak, which appears at the start of the third week. Somites, the neural plate, the coelom, and the notochord all develop later, after the streak has formed.'),
('9ee789da-9e3b-5dad-802b-b7a239cbf2ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Cells migrating through the primitive streak form which layer?', '["Intraembryonic mesoderm","Surface ectoderm","Neural crest","Endoderm","Notochord"]'::jsonb, 'Intraembryonic mesoderm', 'Cells that migrate through the primitive streak during gastrulation form the intraembryonic mesoderm (and some endoderm), establishing the three-layered embryo. Surface ectoderm remains on the outside, the notochord forms from the primitive node, and neural crest appears later.'),
('cd69ec69-339c-5756-ab0f-73b068fd9504'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What structure induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate?', '["Amnion","Primitive streak","Notochord","Prechordal plate","Yolk sac"]'::jsonb, 'Notochord', 'The notochord induces the overlying ectoderm to thicken into the neural plate, the first step of neurulation. The primitive streak initiates gastrulation, the prechordal plate induces the forebrain, and the amnion and yolk sac do not induce neural tissue.'),
('f6b743fd-bc83-59eb-a019-6b31109b01cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The neural crest cells are derived from which region?', '["Floor plate","Notochord","Roof plate","Ventral region of neural tube","Dorsolateral region of neural folds"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsolateral region of neural folds', 'Neural crest cells originate from the dorsolateral region of the neural folds during closure of the neural tube. The floor plate and roof plate are specialized regions of the tube itself, and the notochord and ventral tube are not sources of neural crest.'),
('2a0868ff-2c3b-53ce-b05d-39f1c7786c0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the first pharyngeal arch also known as?', '["Laryngeal arch","Hyoid arch","Thyroid arch","Maxillary arch","Mandibular arch"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular arch', 'The first pharyngeal arch is the mandibular arch, which has maxillary and mandibular prominences and is innervated by the trigeminal nerve. The hyoid arch is the second arch, the maxillary arch is a prominence of the first arch, and there is no standard laryngeal or thyroid arch among the pharyngeal arches.'),
('2bd0b025-3208-5a6a-9639-873dece69425'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What does the second pharyngeal arch form?', '["Maxilla","Thyroid cartilage","Palate","Hyoid bone and stapes","Mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Hyoid bone and stapes', 'The second pharyngeal arch, through Reichert''s cartilage, forms the stapes, styloid process, stylohyoid ligament, and lesser horn and upper body of the hyoid bone. The maxilla and mandible come from the first arch, and the thyroid cartilage and palate develop from other structures.'),
('beaf5195-4fb1-5c6a-bc23-312f7d488d40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Somites differentiate into which three components?', '["Sclerotome myotome dermatome","Dermomyotome syndetome arthrotome","Epiblast hypoblast trophoblast","Endoderm mesoderm ectoderm","Neural tube neural crest notochord"]'::jsonb, 'Sclerotome myotome dermatome', 'Each somite differentiates into a sclerotome (axial skeleton), a myotome (muscle), and a dermatome (dermis). The other groupings mix unrelated structures or name tissues that do not come from a single somite.'),
('ec0134b7-216b-56d3-8990-7b89a067f516'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The sclerotome gives rise to which structures?', '["Vertebrae and ribs","Kidneys","Dermis of back","Heart","Skeletal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Vertebrae and ribs', 'The sclerotome, derived from the ventromedial part of each somite, gives rise to the vertebrae, ribs, and other axial skeletal elements. Skeletal muscle comes from the myotome, dermis of the back from the dermatome, and the kidneys and heart are not somite derivatives.'),
('4517059f-86a2-5fb0-a9e6-2846777a0797'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The myotome gives rise to which structures?', '["Tendons","Skeletal muscles","Dermis","Blood vessels","Vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal muscles', 'The myotome gives rise to the skeletal muscles of the body wall and limbs. Tendons and dermis come from other somite components, blood vessels form from mesenchyme, and vertebrae are derived from the sclerotome.'),
('727455a8-0397-5f80-a332-baad31851645'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The dermatome gives rise to which structures?', '["Liver","Heart","Kidneys","Lung","Dermis of the back"]'::jsonb, 'Dermis of the back', 'The dermatome gives rise to the dermis of the back and related dorsal connective tissue. The liver, heart, kidneys, and lung develop from other germ layers and structures, not from the somite dermatome.'),
('f7753644-f3db-5928-b91b-9b03c6f342fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the fate of the cloacal membrane?', '["It forms the perineum resulting in decreased functional activity","It forms the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It degenerates completely mediated by specific receptor activation","It ruptures forming the anus and urogenital openings","It becomes the rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It ruptures forming the anus and urogenital openings', 'The cloacal membrane ruptures in the seventh week, forming the anus and the urogenital openings. It does not become the perineum, bladder, or rectum, and it does not simply degenerate without contributing an opening.'),
('af8e4bd3-c4db-5d60-a79d-1f2ceb86e505'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the CNS?', '["Microcephaly","Neural tube defects","Anencephaly","Agenesis of corpus callosum","Hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 'Neural tube defects', 'Neural tube defects are the most common congenital anomalies of the central nervous system. Anencephaly and spina bifida are specific neural tube defects, while microcephaly, hydrocephalus, and agenesis of the corpus callosum are separate conditions and individually less common.'),
('96a80a5e-02e2-568a-9a96-a0d35d1e0d2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Folic acid deficiency during pregnancy is associated with which defect?', '["Neural tube defects","Talipes equinovarus","Hypospadias","Cleft lip","Pyloric stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Neural tube defects', 'Folic acid deficiency in early pregnancy impairs neural tube closure and is strongly associated with neural tube defects. Cleft lip, hypospadias, talipes equinovarus, and pyloric stenosis are not caused by maternal folate deficiency.'),
('615cbf3e-babf-5954-88ea-6dc37ce28f4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the embryonic origin of the adrenal medulla?', '["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Neural crest cells","Endoderm","Intermediate mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest cells', 'The adrenal medulla arises from neural crest cells that migrate into the developing cortex and differentiate into chromaffin cells. The adrenal cortex comes from mesoderm, and the ectoderm, endoderm, and intermediate mesoderm are not the origin of the medulla.'),
('3a2f5fc6-9d77-5008-a810-2da5d15e8fff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the embryonic origin of the adrenal cortex?', '["Mesoderm","Endoderm","Surface ectoderm","Neural crest","Ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoderm', 'The adrenal cortex is derived from mesoderm, namely cells of the intermediate mesoderm near the urogenital ridge. The medulla arises from neural crest, and the surface ectoderm and endoderm do not contribute to the adrenal cortex.'),
('dc5a7c02-6093-57e8-b171-5383b9763e07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What structure divides the cloaca into the urogenital sinus and anorectal canal?', '["Perineal body","Urorectal septum","Cloacal membrane","Genital tubercle","Labioscrotal folds"]'::jsonb, 'Urorectal septum', 'The urorectal septum grows down to divide the cloaca into the urogenital sinus anteriorly and the anorectal canal posteriorly. The perineal body is a later muscular structure, the cloacal membrane seals the cloaca, and the genital tubercle and labioscrotal folds form external genitalia.'),
('fb21b947-b629-548c-b73a-b6b9df039836'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the significance of the foramen ovale in fetal circulation?', '["It connects the umbilical vein to IVC","It carries oxygenated blood to the fetus","It shunts blood from right atrium to left atrium","It shunts blood from pulmonary artery to aorta","It drains deoxygenated blood from the fetus"]'::jsonb, 'It shunts blood from right atrium to left atrium', 'In fetal life the foramen ovale is a flap-like opening in the atrial septum that lets oxygenated blood from the placenta bypass the lungs by crossing from the right atrium into the left atrium. It does not involve the umbilical vein, pulmonary artery, or ductus arteriosus, and fetal lungs receive only a small fraction of cardiac output.'),
('fc214131-2d38-56b2-8bc9-7bda1db00740'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What structure connects the ductus venosus to the inferior vena cava?', '["Azygos vein","Umbilical artery","Foramen ovale","Umbilical vein","Ductus arteriosus"]'::jsonb, 'Umbilical vein', 'The umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood from the placenta and enters the liver, where a portion is shunted through the ductus venosus directly into the inferior vena cava. The umbilical artery, azygos vein, foramen ovale, and ductus arteriosus do not feed the ductus venosus.'),
('5829021d-d58a-58ca-9206-724d442b4f5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The ductus arteriosus connects which two vessels?', '["Aorta and vena cava","Pulmonary artery and pulmonary vein","Umbilical vein and umbilical artery","Pulmonary artery and aorta","Superior vena cava and inferior vena cava"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary artery and aorta', 'The ductus arteriosus is a fetal shunt between the pulmonary trunk and the descending aorta, allowing blood to bypass the non-functional lungs. After birth it closes to become the ligamentum arteriosum; the other vessel pairs are not linked by this shunt.'),
('8886ab9f-beef-5e6f-9b30-5e0564a1a146'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What fetal structure becomes the ligamentum teres hepatis after birth?', '["Ductus arteriosus","Foramen ovale","Umbilical artery","Umbilical vein","Ductus venosus"]'::jsonb, 'Umbilical vein', 'After birth the left umbilical vein obliterates and becomes the ligamentum teres hepatis, which runs in the free edge of the falciform ligament to the porta hepatis. The ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum, the ductus arteriosus the ligamentum arteriosum, and the umbilical arteries the medial umbilical ligaments.'),
('20a3ec04-0309-519c-acbb-7229ddac8eaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What fetal structure becomes the ligamentum venosum?', '["Ductus arteriosus","Foramen ovale","Ductus venosus","Umbilical vein","Umbilical artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ductus venosus', 'The ductus venosus, which shunted umbilical venous blood into the inferior vena cava, fibroses after birth to form the ligamentum venosum in the fissure on the posterior surface of the liver. The ligamentum arteriosum, ligamentum teres, and medial umbilical ligaments derive from the other fetal shunts and vessels.'),
('ac55a4d9-0b1c-5043-a4cd-7c9386b59586'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What fetal structure becomes the medial umbilical ligaments?', '["Ductus arteriosus","Ductus venosus","Allantois","Umbilical arteries","Umbilical veins"]'::jsonb, 'Umbilical arteries', 'The umbilical arteries carry deoxygenated blood to the placenta and, after birth, obliterate to form the medial umbilical ligaments on the anterior abdominal wall. The umbilical vein, ductus venosus, and ductus arteriosus leave different remnants.'),
('c24f226b-bd28-5333-8783-143ec65955cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What fetal structure becomes the ligamentum arteriosum?', '["Umbilical vein","Foramen ovale","Umbilical artery","Ductus arteriosus","Ductus venosus"]'::jsonb, 'Ductus arteriosus', 'The ductus arteriosus closes after birth and persists as the ligamentum arteriosum, a fibrous band between the pulmonary trunk and the descending aorta. The other fetal vessels give rise to different postnatal remnants.'),
('3f7b914c-5598-5830-ad6d-6b4df286ac8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the first structure to appear in the development of the heart?', '["Ventricles","Atria","Primitive heart tube","Cardiac septa","Coronary arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Primitive heart tube', 'Cardiac development begins with the fusion of paired endocardial heart tubes into a single primitive heart tube, which then loops and partitions. Septa, atria, ventricles, and coronary arteries all form later from this initial tube.'),
('a0d809a3-ee7e-5e26-8995-026e6f27eb73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The primitive heart tube develops from which germ layer?', '["Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Surface ectoderm","Neural crest","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoderm', 'The primitive heart tube forms from splanchnic mesoderm in the cardiogenic area, while the endocardium and cardiac jelly also derive from mesoderm. Ectoderm, neural crest, and endoderm contribute to other structures, not the initial heart tube.'),
('0b861e6a-5e65-5ccf-8fc0-a3b8c7ec3aa6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the first pharyngeal arch artery?', '["Maxillary artery","Hyoid artery","Mandibular artery","Stapedial artery","Carotid artery"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular artery', 'The first pharyngeal (mandibular) arch artery is the earliest of the six arch vessels and largely disappears, leaving small remnants; it is not the maxillary, hyoid, or stapedial artery, which belong to later arches. Most of its territory is taken over by other vessels.'),
('3f7d3aa5-d4a5-5b06-bd78-3491f679390c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The second pharyngeal arch artery gives rise to which structure?', '["Maxillary artery","Carotid artery","Hyoid artery","Aortic arch","Stapedial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Stapedial artery', 'The second pharyngeal arch artery persists as the stapedial artery in the embryo, which later helps form branches supplying the face. The hyoid artery belongs to the third arch, the aortic arch to the fourth, and the carotid artery develops from the third arch.'),
('646b602d-22d7-5e33-9cb3-fee09cc66729'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The third pharyngeal arch artery forms which vessel?', '["Common carotid artery and proximal internal carotid artery","Aortic arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ductus arteriosus mediated by specific receptor activation","External carotid artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common carotid artery and proximal internal carotid artery', 'The third arch artery contributes to the common carotid and the proximal part of the internal carotid artery on each side. The fourth arch forms the aortic arch on the left, and the sixth arch gives rise to the pulmonary arteries and ductus arteriosus.'),
('eaffb2a4-cc31-5bf9-8ab6-35bd49903a19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The fourth pharyngeal arch artery on the left forms which structure?', '["Subclavian artery","Ductus arteriosus","Aortic arch","Pulmonary artery","Common carotid artery"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch', 'On the left, the fourth pharyngeal arch artery becomes part of the definitive aortic arch, whereas on the right it contributes to the proximal right subclavian artery. The ductus arteriosus and pulmonary arteries derive from the sixth arch, and the common carotid from the third arch.'),
('c00104a0-7c4a-54a2-850c-a721f4d04cea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The sixth pharyngeal arch artery gives rise to which structures?', '["Aortic arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carotid arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary arteries and ductus arteriosus","Subclavian arteries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary arteries and ductus arteriosus', 'The sixth pharyngeal arch artery forms the proximal pulmonary arteries and, on the left, the ductus arteriosus. The aortic arch derives from the fourth arch, the carotid arteries from the third, and the vertebral and subclavian arteries come from dorsal intersegmental vessels.'),
('e4c38a7f-0f82-5558-b67e-1b05c7985b89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the fate of the vitelline arteries?', '["They form the celiac superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries","They form the iliac arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They form the gonadal arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","They regress completely without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They form the renal arteries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They form the celiac superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries', 'The vitelline arteries supply the yolk sac and, as the gut develops, persist as the celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric arteries. They do not regress entirely, and the iliac, gonadal, and renal arteries have other origins from the dorsal aorta and lateral branches.'),
('63a5ca4c-08ea-5132-8e77-7a4f5faabf6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The umbilical arteries carry what type of blood?', '["Oxygenated blood","Deoxygenated blood","blood","Mixed blood","Lymphatic fluid"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenated blood', 'The umbilical arteries arise from the internal iliac arteries and carry deoxygenated blood from the fetus to the placenta for gas exchange. Oxygenated blood returns through the umbilical vein, so the other choices are incorrect.'),
('61d35ac6-97cc-5d51-9673-b1cdbef4892e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The umbilical vein carries what type of blood?', '["Lymphatic fluid","blood","Deoxygenated blood","Mixed blood","Oxygenated blood"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygenated blood', 'The umbilical vein returns oxygenated, nutrient-rich blood from the placenta to the fetus, entering the liver and ductus venosus. The umbilical arteries carry deoxygenated blood away, so the reverse pairing is wrong.'),
('7d090ec9-1976-5a84-a6b9-6ef95f9169e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common cardiac congenital anomaly?', '["Coarctation of aorta","Tetralogy of Fallot","Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","Patent ductus arteriosus","Atrial septal defect (ASD)"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular septal defect (VSD)', 'Ventricular septal defect is the most common congenital cardiac malformation, often closing spontaneously when small. Tetralogy of Fallot, coarctation, PDA, and ASD are less frequent overall.'),
('f04d6bb5-cf09-5d5f-ad51-e7dc9c9bdd16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Tetralogy of Fallot consists of which four defects?', '["VSD overriding aorta pulmonary stenosis RV hypertrophy","VSD pulmonary atresia ASD RV hypertrophy","Pulmonary stenosis RV hypertrophy ASD overriding aorta","VSD ASD PDA RV hypertrophy","ASD VSD PDA aortic stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'VSD overriding aorta pulmonary stenosis RV hypertrophy', 'Tetralogy of Fallot comprises four lesions: ventricular septal defect, overriding aorta, pulmonary stenosis, and right ventricular hypertrophy. The alternatives mix in lesions such as ASD, PDA, and atresia that are not part of the tetralogy.'),
('62da4eb0-bac9-5456-ab3d-404c112db6cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What causes transposition of the great arteries?', '["Abnormal septation of the atria","Failure of neural crest migration","Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to spiral","Persistent truncus arteriosus","Failure of ductus arteriosus closure"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to spiral', 'In transposition of the great arteries the aorticopulmonary septum fails to spiral, so the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk from the left ventricle. Abnormal atrial septation, neural crest failure, and persistent truncus are separate anomalies.'),
('e1a063f6-7c7f-53ad-ac5a-bc00abc458ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Patent ductus arteriosus is common in which condition?', '["Marfan syndrome","Fetal alcohol syndrome","Prematurity","Trisomy 21","Turner syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Prematurity', 'The ductus arteriosus closes under the influence of rising oxygen tension after birth, so it frequently remains patent in premature infants. Marfan, fetal alcohol, trisomy 21, and Turner syndrome are not the leading association.'),
('cce60160-1716-57e4-b014-ff06b220199a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the coronary sinus?', '["Left horn of sinus venosus","Primitive ventricle","Primitive atrium","Common cardinal vein","Right horn of sinus venosus"]'::jsonb, 'Left horn of sinus venosus', 'The coronary sinus develops from the left horn of the sinus venosus, which persists to drain cardiac venous blood into the right atrium. The right horn contributes to the sinus venarum of the right atrium, not the coronary sinus.'),
('89f3b4d6-3a77-5243-bdd0-11aea9a27677'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The sinoatrial node develops from which structure?', '["Bulbus cordis","Interatrial septum","Atrioventricular canal","Left wall of sinus venosus","Right wall of sinus venosus"]'::jsonb, 'Right wall of sinus venosus', 'The sinoatrial node forms in tissue around the right wall of the sinus venosus near its junction with the right atrium. The atrioventricular canal gives rise to the AV node, and the bulbus cordis and septa contribute to other cardiac parts.'),
('30363a56-359e-5e16-a031-b60350021ca1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The atrioventricular node develops from which structure?', '["Interatrial septum","Sinus venosus","Interventricular septum","Bulbus cordis","Atrioventricular canal"]'::jsonb, 'Atrioventricular canal', 'The atrioventricular node and the AV bundle develop from tissue at the atrioventricular canal. The SA node arises from the sinus venosus, while the interatrial and interventricular septa are partitioning structures, not conducting tissue.'),
('145bd905-2c54-54fe-94b7-8fde9fe31d61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the membrane that separates the oral cavity from the nasal cavity?', '["Oronasal membrane","Palatine membrane","Cloacal membrane","Urorectal membrane","Buccopharyngeal membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Oronasal membrane', 'The oronasal membrane is the thin partition between the primitive nasal cavity and the stomodeum (oral cavity), and it normally breaks down to open the posterior choanae. The cloacal and urorectal membranes lie in the hind end, the buccopharyngeal membrane separates the mouth from the foregut, and a ''palatine membrane'' is not a recognized structure.'),
('d4704a1f-bdc3-55c6-bd48-110985130039'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which congenital anomaly results from failure of palatal shelves to fuse?', '["Pierre Robin sequence","Oroantral fistula","Treacher Collins syndrome","Cleft palate","Cleft lip"]'::jsonb, 'Cleft palate', 'Cleft palate occurs when the lateral palatine shelves fail to fuse with each other or with the nasal septum. Cleft lip results from failure of medial nasal and maxillary process fusion, and the other listed conditions have different mechanisms.'),
('a8700c81-4b0e-5ab4-abb7-0007c70ca6ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the thyroid gland?', '["Mesoderm","Pharyngeal pouches","Foramen cecum of tongue","Branchial arches","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen cecum of tongue', 'The thyroid gland arises from a median diverticulum of the floor of the pharynx at the foramen cecum of the tongue and descends through the thyroglossal duct. It is endodermal in origin, so mesoderm, pouches alone, branchial arches, and neural crest are not its source.'),
('ea2f56bc-005b-5db7-a493-45d18ac8923a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the remnant of the thyroglossal duct?', '["Pyramidal lobe","Thyroid cartilage","Foramen cecum","Cricoid cartilage","Hypoglossal canal"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen cecum', 'After descent of the thyroid, the thyroglossal duct normally obliterates but leaves the foramen cecum at the base of the tongue as its remnant. Persistent ductal tissue may form cysts or a pyramidal lobe, while the described cartilages and canal are unrelated.'),
('415f52b2-7dd4-5e0e-a2ca-215472e58936'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is a thyroglossal cyst?', '["Branchial cleft cyst","Parathyroid adenoma","A remnant of the thyroglossal duct","An ectopic thyroid tissue","Thyroid cancer"]'::jsonb, 'A remnant of the thyroglossal duct', 'A thyroglossal cyst forms when portions of the thyroglossal duct remain patent, appearing as a midline neck swelling that moves with tongue protrusion. It should be distinguished from branchial cleft cysts, ectopic thyroid, and neoplastic or parathyroid lesions.'),
('c6911459-427b-5c94-9032-e7c2fb1ec4cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'From which pharyngeal pouch does the palatine tonsil develop?', '["pouch","Fourth pouch","First pouch","Third pouch","Second pouch"]'::jsonb, 'Second pouch', 'The palatine tonsil develops from the epithelium of the second pharyngeal pouch, with surrounding mesenchyme forming its lymphoid framework. The first pouch gives the auditory tube, the third pouch the thymus and inferior parathyroid, and the fourth pouch the superior parathyroid.'),
('b63d897e-c932-548a-b95f-df1cfefa6cf1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The thymus develops from which pharyngeal pouch?', '["Second pouch","Fifth pouch","Third pouch","Fourth pouch","First pouch"]'::jsonb, 'Third pouch', 'The thymus develops from the ventral wing of the third pharyngeal pouch on each side, migrating into the superior mediastinum. The second pouch forms the palatine tonsil and the fourth pouch the superior parathyroid, while the fifth gives the ultimobranchial body.'),
('9d0df9f8-f38d-5532-a695-074f2483d01f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The parathyroid glands develop from which pharyngeal pouches?', '["First and fourth pouches","First and second pouches","Fourth and fifth pouches","Second and third pouches","Third and fourth pouches"]'::jsonb, 'Third and fourth pouches', 'The inferior parathyroid glands arise from the third pouch (along with the thymus) and the superior parathyroids from the fourth pouch. Pouches one, two, and five give rise to the auditory tube, tonsil, and ultimobranchial body, respectively.'),
('7b2ef8cd-7396-5d55-8d85-33b739167457'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The ultimobranchial body gives rise to which cells?', '["Parafollicular cells (C cells) of thyroid","Tonsillar epithelium","Mucous glands","Parathyroid chief cells","Thymic epithelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Parafollicular cells (C cells) of thyroid', 'The ultimobranchial body, derived from the fifth pharyngeal pouch, gives rise to the parafollicular C cells of the thyroid that secrete calcitonin. Parathyroid chief cells, thymic epithelium, and tonsillar tissue arise from other pouches.'),
('658d2a14-1b03-53ad-add1-48c539ff7061'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital neck mass?', '["Cystic hygroma","Cervical lymphadenopathy","Branchial cleft cyst","Thyroid nodule","Thyroglossal duct cyst"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroglossal duct cyst', 'Thyroglossal duct cyst is the most common congenital midline neck mass, resulting from persistence of the thyroglossal duct. Branchial cleft cysts and cystic hygromas also occur but are less frequent, and lymphadenopathy and thyroid nodules are usually acquired, not congenital.'),
('0b710e01-362c-5531-a9c5-fb36945e5ac0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which pharyngeal arch gives rise to the muscles of mastication?', '["Fourth arch","First arch","Sixth arch","Second arch","Third arch"]'::jsonb, 'First arch', 'The first (mandibular) pharyngeal arch supplies the muscles of mastication, which are innervated by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve. The second arch forms facial expression muscles, the third the stylopharyngeus, the fourth the pharyngeal muscles, and the sixth the intrinsic laryngeal muscles.'),
('215dee47-f725-5d58-a5d6-6705cc1a386b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which nerve is associated with the first pharyngeal arch?', '["Hypoglossal (XII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Trigeminal (V)","Vagus (X)","Facial (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal (V)', 'The first arch is innervated by the trigeminal nerve (V), which supplies the muscles of mastication and the maxillary and mandibular derivatives. The facial, glossopharyngeal, vagus, and hypoglossal nerves serve the later arches or tongue.'),
('be92e1de-67f8-5090-a0a4-612d8f889886'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which nerve is associated with the second pharyngeal arch?', '["Hypoglossal (XII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Vagus (X)","Facial (VII)","Trigeminal (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial (VII)', 'The second (hyoid) arch is innervated by the facial nerve (VII), which supplies the muscles of facial expression and the stapedius. The trigeminal, glossopharyngeal, vagus, and hypoglossal nerves belong to other arches or to the tongue.'),
('3ee95346-2b94-5676-a235-4ff7d848f4bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which nerve is associated with the third pharyngeal arch?', '["Trigeminal (V)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Vagus (X)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (IX)', 'The third arch is innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX), which supplies the stylopharyngeus muscle derived from this arch. The other cranial nerves listed correspond to different arches or to the tongue.'),
('7a1fe0c3-20d2-5389-a472-63497b2c435b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which nerve is associated with the fourth pharyngeal arch?', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Trigeminal (V)","Vagus (X) (superior laryngeal branch)","Facial (VII)","Hypoglossal (XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus (X) (superior laryngeal branch)', 'The fourth arch is innervated by the superior laryngeal branch of the vagus (X), which supplies the cricothyroid and the pharyngeal constrictor muscles. The recurrent laryngeal branch serves the sixth arch, while glossopharyngeal, trigeminal, facial, and hypoglossal belong to other arches.'),
('2372eb0c-9127-544b-9fff-ea5af4b212fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Which nerve is associated with the sixth pharyngeal arch?', '["Vagus (X) (recurrent laryngeal branch)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Trigeminal (V)","Facial (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus (X) (recurrent laryngeal branch)', 'The sixth arch is innervated by the recurrent laryngeal branch of the vagus (X), which supplies the intrinsic laryngeal muscles. The superior laryngeal branch serves the fourth arch, while glossopharyngeal, trigeminal, facial, and hypoglossal innervate other arches or the tongue.'),
('3a174bb1-8021-51f1-ba9e-b509f87df438'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the condition characterized by multiple congenital anomalies from first arch abnormalities?', '["Waardenburg syndrome","Pierre Robin sequence","Treacher Collins syndrome","Goldenhar syndrome","DiGeorge syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Treacher Collins syndrome', 'Treacher Collins syndrome results from abnormal development of the first pharyngeal arch, producing mandibulofacial dysostosis with characteristic facial anomalies. Waardenburg, Pierre Robin, Goldenhar, and DiGeorge syndromes have different primary defects and causations.'),
('60d5ecd5-a7fc-5d01-9543-4ef063571fe7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What chromosomal abnormality causes DiGeorge syndrome?', '["15q deletion","Trisomy 18","22q11.2 deletion","Trisomy 21","5p deletion"]'::jsonb, '22q11.2 deletion', 'DiGeorge syndrome is caused by a microdeletion on chromosome 22q11.2, affecting the development of the pharyngeal arches and pouches. The other listed karyotypes and deletions produce different syndromes.'),
('3d5398ae-c82d-50aa-bfa7-d61e221cb873'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the hallmark feature of DiGeorge syndrome?', '["Skeletal abnormalities","Macroglossia","Intellectual disability","Thymic aplasia with T-cell deficiency","Epicanthal folds"]'::jsonb, 'Thymic aplasia with T-cell deficiency', 'DiGeorge syndrome is defined by thymic aplasia or hypoplasia leading to T-cell deficiency and immunodeficiency, often with cardiac defects and hypocalcemia from parathyroid involvement. The other features are not the defining characteristic of this condition.'),
('62bad894-2cf6-516f-b573-bea915cbc7d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common cause of ambiguous genitalia in a newborn?', '["Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Turner syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome","5-alpha reductase deficiency","Androgen insensitivity syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital adrenal hyperplasia', 'Congenital adrenal hyperplasia is the most common cause of ambiguous genitalia in newborns, resulting from enzyme defects such as 21-hydroxylase deficiency that skew androgen production. The other listed disorders are far less common causes.'),
('923a43da-e56f-5e22-9add-789354e300f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the karyotype of Turner syndrome?', '["46 XY","47 XXX","47 XXY","47 XYY","45 XO"]'::jsonb, '45 XO', 'Turner syndrome results from monosomy X, a 45,X (XO) karyotype, producing short stature, gonadal dysgenesis, and webbed neck in females. The other numeric karyotypes represent different sex chromosome abnormalities.'),
('030f38c2-de98-5d23-a842-4816cf684903'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the karyotype of Klinefelter syndrome?', '["47 XXX","47 XXY","46 XX","45 XO","47 XYY"]'::jsonb, '47 XXY', 'Klinefelter syndrome is caused by a 47,XXY karyotype, producing testicular hypofunction, tall stature, and gynecomastia in phenotypic males. The other karyotypes describe distinct sex chromosome anomalies.'),
('e6169b8a-e104-5cb9-9dc0-cb54181df5d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Trisomy 18 is also known as:', '["Klinefelter syndrome","Edwards syndrome","Turner syndrome","Down syndrome","Patau syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Edwards syndrome', 'Trisomy 18 is called Edwards syndrome, characterized by severe intellectual disability, clenched and overlapping fingers, and rocker-bottom feet. Patau is trisomy 13 and Down is trisomy 21, while Klinefelter and Turner are sex chromosome conditions.'),
('da6aa806-de6a-5aa7-818f-a3b55f6ee50d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Trisomy 13 is also known as:', '["Cri-du-chat syndrome","Edwards syndrome","Patau syndrome","Down syndrome","Turner syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Patau syndrome', 'Trisomy 13 is known as Patau syndrome, involving severe midline defects such as cleft lip, holoprosencephaly, and polydactyly. Edwards is trisomy 18, Down is trisomy 21, and Cri-du-chat is a chromosomal deletion, not a trisomy.'),
('f57dd91f-cad0-5d80-ba93-c51196ff2691'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common cause of spontaneous abortion in the first trimester?', '["Chromosomal abnormalities","Immunologic factors","Maternal infection","Environmental factors","Uterine abnormalities"]'::jsonb, 'Chromosomal abnormalities', 'Chromosomal abnormalities account for the majority of first-trimester spontaneous abortions, most commonly trisomies and monosomy X. Immunologic, infectious, environmental, and uterine factors are much less frequent causes at this early stage.'),
('dc399289-ac95-583f-8473-3abfc034ab5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the condition where the placenta implants over the cervical os?', '["Placenta previa","Abruptio placentae","Placenta accreta","Placenta increta","Placenta percreta"]'::jsonb, 'Placenta previa', 'Placenta previa is implantation of the placenta over or near the internal cervical os, risking painless late-pregnancy bleeding. Abruptio placentae is premature separation of a normally implanted placenta, and accreta/increta/percreta describe abnormally invasive placentation.'),
('87984e58-64d7-5051-aa25-4c3bd7328ece'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the embryonic origin of the placenta?', '["Inner cell mass only","Epiblast only","Hypoblast only","Maternal endometrium only","Trophoblast and extraembryonic mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Trophoblast and extraembryonic mesoderm', 'The fetal component of the placenta arises from the trophoblast (which forms chorionic villi) plus the extraembryonic mesoderm that forms the core of each villus. The inner cell mass, epiblast and hypoblast give rise to the embryo, amnion and yolk sac rather than the placenta, and the maternal endometrium is only one part of the organ.'),
('946d241f-a369-5147-80f0-54c7149e7134'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The decidua basalis is which layer?', '["Amniotic membrane","Maternal part of placenta","Yolk sac","Chorionic membrane","Fetal part of placenta"]'::jsonb, 'Maternal part of placenta', 'The decidua basalis is the maternal endometrium lying deep to the conceptus and becomes incorporated into the placenta as its maternal component. The amniotic and chorionic membranes are fetal structures, and the yolk sac is a separate fetal appendage unrelated to the decidua.'),
('0ca50e37-f829-5849-a79c-c63612b6e3bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the term for the formation of the three germ layers?', '["Somitogenesis","Neurulation","Histogenesis","Organogenesis","Gastrulation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrulation', 'Gastrulation converts the bilaminar embryonic disc into a trilaminar disc by establishing the ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm. Neurulation forms the neural tube, somitogenesis forms somites, and organogenesis and histogenesis are later events that require the germ layers already formed by gastrulation.'),
('299a861a-d0eb-5509-9c2d-7dbbfd8f57be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the primary inducer of neural plate formation?', '["Prechordal plate","Notochord","Yolk sac","Primitive streak","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Notochord', 'The notochord, representing axial mesoderm, induces the overlying ectoderm to thicken into the neural plate through secreted signaling molecules. The primitive streak initiates gastrulation, the prechordal plate patterns the head, and the surface ectoderm is the responding tissue rather than the inducer.'),
('1c256bf1-f243-51fd-8a00-f3a591b13975'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The cranial neuropore closes at which day?', '["Day 25","Day 28","Day 30","Day 20","Day 35"]'::jsonb, 'Day 25', 'The cranial (anterior) neuropore closes around day 25, whereas the caudal neuropore closes later at day 28. Failure of cranial neuropore closure leads to anencephaly, so the day-25 timing is distinctive for the head end of the neural tube.'),
('ff6f1fdc-6326-585b-9c2d-bfadec49bd5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The caudal neuropore closes at which day?', '["Day 20","Day 30","Day 25","Day 28","Day 35"]'::jsonb, 'Day 28', 'The caudal (posterior) neuropore closes on day 28, later than the cranial neuropore which closes on day 25. Incomplete closure at this site leaves the posterior neuropore open and produces spina bifida.'),
('bf722079-4386-5b28-b46b-a4beb7b790a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What congenital anomaly results from failure of the caudal neuropore to close?', '["Microcephaly","Spina bifida","Encephalocele","Anencephaly","Hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 'Spina bifida', 'Failed closure of the caudal neuropore leaves a defect in the posterior neural tube and vertebral arches, producing spina bifida. Anencephaly results from failed cranial neuropore closure, and encephalocele, microcephaly and hydrocephalus arise through different mechanisms.'),
('23855bbf-9065-5df9-9d83-d6f0bf6ec5dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most severe form of spina bifida?', '["Spina bifida occulta","Myeloschisis","Spina bifida cystica","Meningocele","Myelomeningocele"]'::jsonb, 'Myeloschisis', 'Myeloschisis is the most severe form of spina bifida because the neural folds never fuse, leaving a flat, exposed neural plate. Myelomeningocele and meningocele involve herniated but generally better-formed neural tissue, and spina bifida occulta is the mildest, clinically hidden form.'),
('425dee15-435b-5879-989c-959e54451f31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is spina bifida occulta?', '["Complete absence of spinal cord","Neural tissue protruding through vertebral defect","Meninges protruding through vertebral defect","Incomplete neural tube formation","Defect in vertebral arch without neural involvement"]'::jsonb, 'Defect in vertebral arch without neural involvement', 'Spina bifida occulta is a hidden defect of the vertebral arch that does not involve the meninges or neural tissue, so it is usually asymptomatic. The options describing protruding meninges or neural tissue refer to the open (cystic) forms of spina bifida.'),
('7c0da89d-6b6b-5b41-9c75-262fc87c4535'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the amniotic fluid?', '["Maternal serum","Fetal urine and amniotic membrane secretion","Placental secretion","Fetal lung fluid","Umbilical cord transudate"]'::jsonb, 'Fetal urine and amniotic membrane secretion', 'Amniotic fluid is initially secreted by the amniotic membrane and later maintained mainly by fetal urine, while fetal swallowing removes fluid continuously. Maternal serum, placental secretion and lung fluid are not the principal sources of the amniotic volume.'),
('2c2a1495-43aa-5036-92fa-3d36cfd1d780'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is polyhydramnios?', '["Blood in amniotic fluid","Abnormal amniotic fluid composition","Infected amniotic fluid","Excessive amniotic fluid","Decreased amniotic fluid"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive amniotic fluid', 'Polyhydramnios means an excess of amniotic fluid, usually from impaired fetal swallowing, as in esophageal atresia or anencephaly. Decreased fluid defines oligohydramnios, and blood, infection or altered composition are separate derangements, not polyhydramnios.'),
('50800130-e98f-5edb-885e-3718099bbd91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is oligohydramnios?', '["Decreased amniotic fluid","Infected amniotic fluid","Cloudy amniotic fluid","Excessive amniotic fluid","Blood in amniotic fluid"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased amniotic fluid', 'Oligohydramnios is a reduction in amniotic fluid volume, commonly caused by fetal oliguria from renal agenesis or by leakage from membrane rupture. The opposite state is polyhydramnios, and the other options describe infection, cloudiness or hemorrhage rather than volume.'),
('6f3e1e11-1460-59c1-a494-2809898f03e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is Potter sequence?', '["Abnormal placental development","Fetal hydrops","Polyhydramnios sequence from GI obstruction","Twin-twin transfusion","Oligohydramnios sequence from renal agenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Oligohydramnios sequence from renal agenesis', 'Bilateral renal agenesis eliminates fetal urine production, causing severe oligohydramnios, which in turn produces the limb deformities, Potter facies and pulmonary hypoplasia of the Potter sequence. GI obstruction, by contrast, causes polyhydramnios, and the other options are unrelated placental, twin or hydrops conditions.'),
('31cdcc7a-74cb-57ef-b576-f91898dfef3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common cause of oligohydramnios?', '["Post-term pregnancy","Fetal death","Placental insufficiency","Premature rupture of membranes","Renal agenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Premature rupture of membranes', 'Premature rupture of membranes is the most common cause of oligohydramnios because fluid is continuously lost through the defect. Renal agenesis reduces amniotic fluid through lack of fetal urine but is far less common overall.'),
('84083a4c-41ea-5d3a-b431-e234572e5346'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the condition where the amniotic membrane forms constriction bands?', '["Placenta accreta","Abruptio placentae","Chorioamnionitis","Twin-twin transfusion syndrome","Amniotic band syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Amniotic band syndrome', 'Amniotic band syndrome arises when strands from a torn amnion encircle fetal parts, producing constriction rings, deformities and amputations. Placenta accreta, abruptio placentae, chorioamnionitis and twin-twin transfusion are all placentation or twin-related disorders without constricting membranes.'),
('6cfd907f-0b90-5b00-b210-3d2b5ad67a64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The allantois gives rise to which adult structure?', '["Umbilical artery","Urachus (median umbilical ligament)","Ductus venosus","Umbilical vein","Vitelline duct"]'::jsonb, 'Urachus (median umbilical ligament)', 'The allantois degenerates and its intra-abdominal remnant persists as the urachus, which becomes the median umbilical ligament in the adult. The umbilical artery and vein are vascular structures and the ductus venosus and vitelline duct follow entirely different embryonic pathways.'),
('2ab93d8f-f91b-545f-a01f-430dca2dc717'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is a vitelline duct remnant?', '["Branchial cleft cyst","Meckel''s diverticulum","Urachal cyst","Thyroglossal cyst","Umbilical hernia"]'::jsonb, 'Meckel''s diverticulum', 'A persistent proximal portion of the vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct remains as Meckel''s diverticulum on the antimesenteric border of the ileum. Branchial cleft and thyroglossal cysts come from the branchial apparatus, a urachal cyst from the allantois, and umbilical hernia is a body-wall defect.'),
('3c8726bd-2f7f-5f0e-bf7c-b887141c95c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Rule of 2s for Meckel''s diverticulum includes:', '["2 arteries 2 veins 2 lymphatics","2 inches wide 2 cm in diameter 2 types of epithelium","2 muscular layers 2 blood supplies 2 nerve supplies","2 cm long 2 cm from cecum 2% symptomatic","2% population 2 inches long 2 feet from ileocecal valve"]'::jsonb, '2% population 2 inches long 2 feet from ileocecal valve', 'Meckel''s diverticulum obeys the rule of 2s: found in about 2% of the population, roughly 2 inches long, and located about 2 feet from the ileocecal valve. The other choices give incorrect measurements, distances or anatomical proportions.'),
('b01efb2b-b030-5f65-8e03-e548aca8c45c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract?', '["Meckel''s diverticulum","Imperforate anus","Tracheoesophageal fistula","Pyloric stenosis","Omphalocele"]'::jsonb, 'Meckel''s diverticulum', 'Meckel''s diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the gastrointestinal tract, occurring in about 2% of the population. Imperforate anus, tracheoesophageal fistula, pyloric stenosis and omphalocele are all encountered less frequently.'),
('4e995a9e-9412-5fb4-b8cd-f5a8718ae745'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the foregut?', '["Allantois","Caudal part of the primitive gut tube","Mid part of the primitive gut tube","Yolk sac","Cranial part of the primitive gut tube"]'::jsonb, 'Cranial part of the primitive gut tube', 'The foregut develops from the cranial part of the primitive gut tube, extending from the oropharyngeal membrane to the duodenum at the liver bud. The caudal part forms the hindgut, the mid part becomes the midgut, and the allantois and yolk sac are separate structures.'),
('38c2cf5c-aa27-5eb1-b169-b49b3798abc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the midgut?', '["From the liver bud to the junction of right 2/3 and left 1/3 of transverse colon","From the distal colon to the rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Entirely outside the embryo in the yolk sac mediated by specific receptor activation","From the esophagus to the rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From the pharynx to the stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From the liver bud to the junction of right 2/3 and left 1/3 of transverse colon', 'The midgut spans from the duodenum at the liver bud to the junction of the right two-thirds and left one-third of the transverse colon. The other choices describe regions belonging to the foregut or hindgut, or lie outside the concept of midgut boundaries.'),
('b754c1f2-a276-51c3-9775-e8ffeb7b1dcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the hindgut?', '["From the pharynx to the esophagus","From the rectum to the anus","From the duodenum to the cecum","From the left 1/3 of transverse colon to the cloacal membrane","From the stomach to the jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'From the left 1/3 of transverse colon to the cloacal membrane', 'The hindgut extends from the left one-third of the transverse colon to the cloacal membrane, and gives rise to the distal colon and rectum. The pharynx-to-stomach and duodenal regions belong to the foregut and midgut respectively.'),
('c51c2a3b-b1f7-50da-94f3-a910eb0ff24d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the midgut?', '["Omphalocele","Intestinal atresia","Meckel''s diverticulum","Gastroschisis","Malrotation"]'::jsonb, 'Meckel''s diverticulum', 'Meckel''s diverticulum, a remnant of the vitelline duct, is the most common congenital anomaly of the midgut. Omphalocele, gastroschisis, intestinal atresia and malrotation are genuine midgut problems but occur less often.'),
('f5ee25e2-9bf0-59cc-8bf9-e7bdd658ad5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the difference between omphalocele and gastroschisis?', '["Omphalocele has a sac; gastroschisis does not","Omphalocele is larger","Neither has a sac","Both have sacs","Gastroschisis has a sac; omphalocele does not"]'::jsonb, 'Omphalocele has a sac; gastroschisis does not', 'An omphalocele is covered by a peritoneal (amniotic) sac because the herniated gut remains within the umbilical coelom, whereas gastroschisis is a bare protrusion with no covering sac from a lateral body-wall defect. The other options either deny the presence of the sac or reverse the relationship.'),
('74369b52-8cd2-56cc-87c3-946aa040b26d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common type of esophageal atresia?', '["Esophageal atresia with distal tracheoesophageal fistula","Esophageal atresia with proximal fistula","H-type fistula without atresia","Double fistula","Isolated esophageal atresia without fistula"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal atresia with distal tracheoesophageal fistula', 'Esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula accounts for more than 85% of cases. Isolated atresia, proximal fistula, H-type fistula and double fistula are all uncommon variants.'),
('1a94734c-6cf2-5820-b92e-b32e0b9bbf79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the VACTERL association?', '["Vascular anorectal cardiac TE fistula renal liver","Vaginal anal cardiac tracheal esophageal renal limb","Ventricular atrial cardiac tracheal esophageal renal laryngeal","Vertebral anal cardiac TE fistula renal limb anomalies","Vertebral aortic cardiac TE fistula renal lumbar"]'::jsonb, 'Vertebral anal cardiac TE fistula renal limb anomalies', 'VACTERL is a recognized association of Vertebral, Anal, Cardiac, Tracheo-Esophageal fistula, Renal and Limb anomalies. The alternative expansions substitute structures such as vascular, vaginal or laryngeal that are not components of the VACTERL acronym.'),
('806aca09-c890-5303-9b89-19dc5c7eca7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital diaphragmatic hernia?', '["Morgagni hernia","Bochdalek hernia","Parahiatal hernia","Traumatic hernia","Hernia through the esophageal hiatus"]'::jsonb, 'Bochdalek hernia', 'Bochdalek hernia, a posterolateral defect caused by failure of the pleuroperitoneal membrane to close, is the most common congenital diaphragmatic hernia and is usually left-sided. Morgagni hernias are anterior and much rarer, while the other choices are not congenital posterolateral defects.'),
('428c2d88-be67-56fa-92a0-7aa832fc3167'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the diaphragm?', '["Septum transversum pleuroperitoneal membranes body wall and mesentery","Septum transversum only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Allantois mediated by specific receptor activation","Pleuroperitoneal membranes only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Body wall only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Septum transversum pleuroperitoneal membranes body wall and mesentery', 'The diaphragm forms from four components: the septum transversum, the two pleuroperitoneal membranes, the body wall, and the dorsal esophageal mesentery. Any single-source origin is incomplete because all four parts contribute to the adult muscle.'),
('6b1226dc-c895-5af8-a09a-3697694b2783'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital lung anomaly?', '["Cystic adenomatoid malformation","Tracheoesophageal fistula","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia","Pulmonary sequestration","Pulmonary hypoplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital diaphragmatic hernia', 'Congenital diaphragmatic hernia is considered the most common congenital anomaly of the lung because herniated abdominal viscera compress the developing lung and cause severe pulmonary hypoplasia. The other pulmonary lesions occur less frequently.'),
('8e6db35d-94f6-570f-9b3a-a91c73ee4ff6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The respiratory system develops from which germ layer?', '["Ectoderm","Neural crest","Mesoderm","Surface ectoderm","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Endoderm', 'The respiratory system, including the larynx, trachea, bronchi and lung parenchyma, develops as an outgrowth of the foregut endoderm. Ectoderm and neural crest contribute only the nasal neuroepithelium and paratracheal connective tissues, not the respiratory epithelium.'),
('d8590cd3-7a6c-557b-a305-7ec94048e5cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The lung buds appear at what week of development?', '["Week 7","Week 3","Week 5","Week 6","Week 4"]'::jsonb, 'Week 4', 'The respiratory (lung) bud appears in week 4 as a ventral outgrowth of the foregut wall. The later weeks are occupied by branching, canalicular and alveolar maturation rather than the first appearance of the lung bud.'),
('9fe617ad-5b2d-55db-94a1-edb459bbf1b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the groove that separates the lung bud from the foregut?', '["Tracheoesophageal ridge","Esophageal groove","Laryngeal slit","Pharyngeal groove","Tracheal sulcus"]'::jsonb, 'Tracheoesophageal ridge', 'The tracheoesophageal ridge, formed as the tracheoesophageal septum divides the foregut, separates the developing lung bud (trachea) from the esophagus. The laryngeal slit and pharyngeal groove belong to the pharyngeal apparatus and are not the separating structure.'),
('2e12923f-3257-569e-bbdc-336e1636b113'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Failure of the tracheoesophageal septum to form properly results in what condition?', '["Bronchial stenosis","Esophageal atresia","Tracheoesophageal fistula","Tracheomalacia","Laryngomalacia"]'::jsonb, 'Tracheoesophageal fistula', 'Incomplete division of the foregut by the tracheoesophageal septum leaves an abnormal connection between the trachea and esophagus, a tracheoesophageal fistula. Bronchial stenosis, tracheomalacia and laryngomalacia are airway problems with anatomically unrelated causes.'),
('d5a54162-0864-5293-b0df-c438543bb631'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The development of the lungs goes through which stages in order?', '["Embryonic saccular pseudoglandular canalicular alveolar","Embryonic pseudoglandular canalicular saccular alveolar","Pseudoglandular embryonic canalicular saccular alveolar","Embryonic canalicular pseudoglandular saccular alveolar","Pseudoglandular canalicular embryonic saccular alveolar"]'::jsonb, 'Embryonic pseudoglandular canalicular saccular alveolar', 'Lung development follows the sequence embryonic, pseudoglandular, canalicular, saccular and alveolar. The canalicular stage produces the respiratory bronchioles and the first surfactant, so any rearrangement of this chronological order is incorrect.'),
('e769bea3-0419-50a7-b97b-967028f58ce4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Surfactant production begins during which stage of lung development?', '["Saccular stage","Alveolar stage","Pseudoglandular stage","Canalicular stage","Embryonic stage"]'::jsonb, 'Canalicular stage', 'Type II pneumocytes begin making surfactant during the canalicular stage, which is why survival becomes possible from about week 26. The pseudoglandular and embryonic stages have no functional surfactant, and the saccular and alveolar stages represent later maturation.'),
('ffed5252-de34-579d-bf26-db866118685a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) in premature infants is due to deficiency of what?', '["Cortisol","Mucus","Surfactant","Amniotic fluid","Glycogen"]'::jsonb, 'Surfactant', 'RDS arises from the lack of surfactant, so the premature alveoli collapse on expiration and the infant struggles to expand the lungs. Cortisol actually promotes surfactant maturation, and mucus, glycogen and amniotic fluid are not the deficient substance.'),
('e9426f84-b548-5514-a15e-ca9cc336581a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the major component of surfactant?', '["Cholesterol","Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)","Proteins SP-A SP-B SP-C SP-D","Phosphatidylglycerol","Phosphatidylinositol"]'::jsonb, 'Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)', 'Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC) is the principal surface-active phospholipid of surfactant and lowers alveolar surface tension. The surfactant proteins and other phospholipids are present, but in smaller amounts, and cholesterol is not the major active component.'),
('46ffb714-547d-5946-a6e9-6df04c7e6851'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the embryonic origin of the kidneys?', '["Endoderm","Intermediate mesoderm","Lateral plate mesoderm","Ectoderm","Paraxial mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate mesoderm', 'All three kidney systems, pronephros, mesonephros and metanephros, arise from the intermediate mesoderm. Paraxial mesoderm forms somites, lateral plate mesoderm lines the coelom, and ectoderm and endoderm do not contribute renal parenchyma.'),
('a3630834-af55-5e16-a6cf-700e1c064c77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What are the three successive kidney systems in human development?', '["Pronephros metanephros mesonephros","Mesonephros pronephros metanephros","Pronephros mesonephros metanephros","Mesonephros metanephros pronephros","Metanephros pronephros mesonephros"]'::jsonb, 'Pronephros mesonephros metanephros', 'The kidney systems appear in the sequence pronephros, mesonephros and metanephros. The first two are transient, and only the metanephros persists as the definitive kidney, so preserving this order is essential.'),
('868e79df-7a99-594b-a530-e941a654d3fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The definitive kidney (metanephros) develops from which two structures?', '["Metanephric mesenchyme and mesonephros","Metanephric mesenchyme and pronephros","Ureteric bud and pronephros","Ureteric bud and mesonephros","Ureteric bud and metanephric mesenchyme"]'::jsonb, 'Ureteric bud and metanephric mesenchyme', 'The metanephros forms by reciprocal induction between the ureteric bud and the metanephric mesenchyme: the bud branches into the collecting system while the mesenchyme forms nephrons. The mesonephros and pronephros are earlier transient kidneys and are not the source of the definitive kidney.'),
('12b041e5-df28-5f3c-875a-6d42599683a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The ureteric bud gives rise to which structures?', '["Renal artery and veins","Ureter renal pelvis major calyces minor calyces collecting ducts","Renal capsule and interstitium","Glomerulus proximal tubule loop of Henle distal tubule","Renal pelvis and glomeruli"]'::jsonb, 'Ureter renal pelvis major calyces minor calyces collecting ducts', 'The ureteric bud branches to form the ureter, renal pelvis, major and minor calyces and the collecting ducts. Nephrons, glomeruli and tubules arise from the metanephric mesenchyme, and the renal vessels and capsule have other origins.'),
('6f4b9878-c9f2-5ae6-8c9f-ea9e97b0108c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The metanephric mesenchyme gives rise to which structures?', '["Collecting ducts and ureter resulting in decreased functional activity","Renal pelvis and calyces accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glomerulus proximal tubule loop of Henle distal tubule (nephrons)","Blood vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interstitial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerulus proximal tubule loop of Henle distal tubule (nephrons)', 'The metanephric mesenchyme differentiates into the nephron, comprising the glomerulus, proximal tubule, loop of Henle and distal tubule. The collecting system and ureter are derived from the ureteric bud, while vessels and interstitial cells come from yet other sources.'),
('14edeb5d-7dba-5f14-89ad-b0bca1571001'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the urinary tract?', '["Duplex ureter","Unilateral renal agenesis","Horseshoe kidney","Polycystic kidney disease","Pelvic kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Duplex ureter', 'A duplex (duplicated) ureter, produced by premature division of the ureteric bud, is the most common congenital anomaly of the urinary tract. Unilateral agenesis, horseshoe and pelvic kidneys and polycystic disease all occur less often.'),
('42ae9161-c0f5-5eba-aab1-f1dd4e5111d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Horseshoe kidney occurs when which parts of the kidneys fuse?', '["Entire medial aspects","Lower poles","Renal pelves","Upper poles","Renal arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Lower poles', 'Horseshoe kidney results from fusion of the lower poles across the midline, and the isthmus becomes caught under the inferior mesenteric artery during ascent. Fusion of the upper poles or the entire medial borders would produce different renal configurations.'),
('119c7ed5-3c69-5591-b799-43f21ae3a020'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common cause of bilateral renal agenesis?', '["No specific cause","X-linked inheritance","Autosomal recessive inheritance","Autosomal dominant inheritance","Environmental toxins"]'::jsonb, 'No specific cause', 'Bilateral renal agenesis is usually sporadic, with no single causative mechanism identified in most cases. Inherited patterns and environmental toxins are rare or unproven explanations rather than the most common cause.'),
('789828e6-a134-578e-a3b1-be373a364ecd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the condition where the urethra opens on the dorsal surface of the penis?', '["Phimosis","Paraphimosis","Epispadias","Hypospadias","Chordee"]'::jsonb, 'Epispadias', 'The urethral meatus opening on the dorsal (upper) surface of the penis is called epispadias, which is often associated with bladder exstrophy. Hypospadias is the ventral opening, while phimosis, paraphimosis and chordee are different penile conditions.'),
('bc73ff42-9d73-5ba1-a36f-2e0b3af7f8b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common type of hypospadias?', '["Glandular (distal)","Coronal","Perineal","Midshaft","Penoscrotal"]'::jsonb, 'Glandular (distal)', 'Glandular (distal) hypospadias, with the meatus along the distal shaft or glans, is the most common form. Midshaft, penoscrotal and perineal placements are progressively more proximal and much rarer.'),
('82c0483f-5f7a-59dc-854e-e0f09c0fb20d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The gonads develop from which structure?', '["Mesonephros","Genital ridge","Intermediate mesoderm","Urogenital sinus","Paramesonephric duct"]'::jsonb, 'Genital ridge', 'The gonads develop from the genital (gonadal) ridge, formed by coelomic epithelium and the underlying intermediate mesoderm along the medial side of the mesonephros. The mesonephros, urogenital sinus and paramesonephric duct contribute to excretory and genital tract structures rather than the gonad itself.'),
('fade1b46-3ce5-559a-8b94-65e727220fdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Primordial germ cells originate from which structure?', '["Notochord","Epiblast near the yolk sac","Primitive streak","Neural crest","Dorsal mesentery"]'::jsonb, 'Epiblast near the yolk sac', 'Primordial germ cells arise from the epiblast and migrate along the wall of the yolk sac near the allantois before reaching the genital ridges. The notochord, primitive streak and neural crest do not give rise to germ cells.'),
('ebbb51da-1cb3-512c-b5aa-b1e1fab81cd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The paramesonephric (Müllerian) duct gives rise to which structures in females?', '["Mesonephric duct remnants resulting in decreased functional activity","Ovaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Labia mediated by specific receptor activation","Fallopian tubes uterus upper vagina","Clitoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fallopian tubes uterus upper vagina', 'In females the paired paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts develop into the uterine tubes, uterus and the upper part of the vagina. The ovaries come from the genital ridge, and the labia and clitoris arise from the urogenital sinus and genital tubercle.'),
('a123cffa-e5b5-58a4-944f-ec0dae24e320'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The mesonephric (Wolffian) duct gives rise to which structures in males?', '["Bulbourethral gland resulting in decreased functional activity","Rete testis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prostate mediated by specific receptor activation","Epididymis vas deferens seminal vesicles ejaculatory duct","Spermatic cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epididymis vas deferens seminal vesicles ejaculatory duct', 'In males the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct differentiates in sequence into the epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicle, and ejaculatory duct under androgen stimulation. The prostate and bulbourethral gland arise from the urogenital sinus, and the rete testis derives from the gonadal blastema, not the Wolffian duct.'),
('d60e1fc9-e902-5629-9c94-899e24619f7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What hormone is responsible for male sexual differentiation?', '["Testosterone (and DHT)","FSH","LH","Müllerian inhibiting factor","Estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Testosterone (and DHT)', 'Testosterone and its metabolite dihydrotestosterone (DHT) drive differentiation of the male internal and external genitalia. Mullerian inhibiting factor only regresses the paramesonephric ducts, while FSH and LH regulate gonadal function and hormone secretion rather than direct sexual differentiation.'),
('05cbec84-ee92-55ff-84c5-fdaeb5fa7eef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the function of Müllerian inhibiting factor (MIF)?', '["Stimulates spermatogenesis","Causes testicular descent","Inhibits testosterone production","Causes regression of paramesonephric ducts","Promotes development of epididymis"]'::jsonb, 'Causes regression of paramesonephric ducts', 'MIF, secreted by fetal Sertoli cells, causes regression of the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts, removing the female duct precursors. Epididymal development depends on testosterone rather than MIF, and the other listed actions belong to different hormones.'),
('3fdf7515-dabe-580c-8975-3fe81d6ed53f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Persistent Müllerian duct syndrome is due to deficiency of what?', '["FSH","LH","5-alpha reductase","Testosterone","MIF or MIF receptor"]'::jsonb, 'MIF or MIF receptor', 'Persistent Mullerian duct syndrome results from deficient MIF secretion or an abnormal MIF receptor, leaving the uterus and uterine tubes in an otherwise normally virilized 46 XY male. Testosterone, FSH, LH, and 5-alpha reductase defects do not explain retained Mullerian derivatives.'),
('a2dbcf96-d573-5109-9443-f6298d7881ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common cause of primary amenorrhea with normal breast development?', '["Androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS)","Pituitary tumor","Thyroid disorder","Turner syndrome","Müllerian agenesis (MRKH)"]'::jsonb, 'Androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS)', 'In complete androgen insensitivity, estrogen produced by the testes drives normal breast development, but absence of the uterus leads to primary amenorrhea, making it the leading cause of this presentation. In Mullerian agenesis the breasts are normally developed too, but CAIS is overall the most common single cause among patients presenting with primary amenorrhea and normal breast growth.'),
('cac5510c-c042-5032-974d-2b76fa33a02d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the karyotype of an individual with androgen insensitivity syndrome?', '["45 XO","46 XX","46 XY","47 XXY","47 XXX"]'::jsonb, '46 XY', 'Androgen insensitivity syndrome occurs in genetic males with a 46 XY karyotype whose testes function normally but whose target tissues are unresponsive to androgens. 45 XO is seen in Turner syndrome, 47 XXY in Klinefelter syndrome, and 46 XX represents a female karyotype.'),
('045853c6-b68e-54a0-9d66-bf1ba7051e53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the female genital tract?', '["Unicornuate uterus","Bicornuate uterus","Didelphys uterus","Arcuate uterus","Septate uterus"]'::jsonb, 'Septate uterus', 'A septate uterus, caused by incomplete resorption of the midline septum after fusion of the paramesonephric ducts, is the most common congenital uterine anomaly. Bicornuate and didelphys uteri reflect more severe degrees of ductal fusion failure and are less frequent.'),
('58145aba-af10-5f29-ae4e-70bb8b5977a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the vagina?', '["Entirely from urogenital sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Entirely from mesonephric ducts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Entirely from paramesonephric ducts mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper 1/3 from paramesonephric ducts lower 2/3 from urogenital sinus","From the genital tubercle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper 1/3 from paramesonephric ducts lower 2/3 from urogenital sinus', 'The vagina has a dual origin: its upper third derives from the fused paramesonephric ducts while the lower two-thirds arise from the urogenital sinus (sinovaginal bulbs). It is not formed entirely from any single structure listed, and the genital tubercle contributes to the clitoris and external genitalia instead.'),
('07371fed-5234-57a5-927d-c439e287c20b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is imperforate hymen?', '["Failure of the urogenital sinus membrane to perforate","Fusion of labia minora accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clitoral hypertrophy mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal urethral opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete vaginal agenesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the urogenital sinus membrane to perforate', 'Imperforate hymen results when the epithelium of the urogenital sinus fails to perforate at the site of the future hymen, obstructing menstrual outflow at puberty. The other options describe separate malformations of the external genitalia or urethra.'),
('25d27b26-88b0-5c10-97d5-21ad40da1877'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the embryonic origin of the prostate gland?', '["Paramesonephric duct","Mesonephric duct","Urogenital sinus","Metanephros","Genital tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Urogenital sinus', 'The prostate arises as outgrowths of the pelvic part of the urogenital sinus under the influence of dihydrotestosterone. The mesonephric duct gives rise to epididymis and vas deferens, the metanephros to the kidney, and the paramesonephric duct is the female duct system.'),
('f2658980-6109-5d0e-900e-f4769a21e4ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Testicular descent is influenced by which two main factors?', '["FSH and LH","MIF and inhibin","GnRH and hCG","Androgens (testosterone) and INSL3","Estrogen and progesterone"]'::jsonb, 'Androgens (testosterone) and INSL3', 'Transabdominal descent is driven by INSL3 acting on the gubernaculum, while inguinoscrotal descent requires androgens. MIF and inhibin, and the pituitary gonadotropins, do not directly govern the two phases of testicular descent.'),
('16e1b34c-09da-515b-85d1-42331aef7138'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common location of an undescended testis?', '["Intra-abdominal","Perineal","Retrovesical","Superficial inguinal pouch","Canal of Nuck"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial inguinal pouch', 'In most boys with cryptorchidism the testis is arrested in the superficial inguinal pouch, lying just beyond the external ring. Intra-abdominal and perineal positions are rarer ectopic sites, and the canal of Nuck is the female homologue of the processus vaginalis.'),
('3dff8f48-3138-55c0-961b-ae2489286795'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the condition where the processus vaginalis fails to obliterate?', '["Testicular torsion","Hydrocele","Spermatocele","Varicocele","Congenital inguinal hernia"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital inguinal hernia', 'A patent (unobliterated) processus vaginalis leaves a peritoneal sac through which abdominal contents can herniate, producing a congenital indirect inguinal hernia. A communicating hydrocele is a related but different consequence, while the other options have unrelated mechanisms.'),
('28540af6-00ba-57f4-8870-e2da1bf115f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The gubernaculum in males becomes which structure?', '["Epididymis resulting in decreased functional activity","Tunica vaginalis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gubernaculum testis (scrotal ligament)","Vas deferens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spermatic cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gubernaculum testis (scrotal ligament)', 'In males the gubernaculum persists as the scrotal ligament that anchors the testis within the scrotum. The epididymis and vas deferens are Wolffian duct derivatives, the tunica vaginalis comes from the processus vaginalis, and the spermatic cord is a composite structure.'),
('1aa0cb97-5e6b-5b2d-ae04-e1d34eecea3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The gubernaculum in females becomes which structures?', '["Labia majora resulting in decreased functional activity","Fallopian tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Broad ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Ovarian ligament and round ligament of uterus","Suspensory ligament of ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian ligament and round ligament of uterus', 'In females the gubernaculum forms the ovarian ligament and the round ligament of the uterus, which run from the ovary to the uterine cornu and then into the labia majora. The fallopian tube is a paramesonephric duct derivative and the broad ligament is a fold of peritoneum.'),
('4ff4cd76-ecfc-561c-907a-23dfa685ac01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common ovarian tumor in women of reproductive age?', '["Mucinous cystadenoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Endometrioma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dermoid cyst (mature cystic teratoma)","Fibroma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serous cystadenoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dermoid cyst (mature cystic teratoma)', 'A mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst) is the most common benign ovarian neoplasm in women of childbearing age. Serous and mucinous cystadenomas are epithelial tumors seen more often in perimenopausal and older women, whereas an endometrioma is an endometriotic cyst, not a neoplasm.'),
('255eb8e7-3b70-5c8b-bacf-34ad5b6ceda1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of congenital diaphragmatic hernia (Bochdalek)?', '["Abnormal phrenic nerve development","Paralyzed diaphragm","Abnormal esophageal development","Defect in septum transversum","Failure of pleuroperitoneal membrane closure"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of pleuroperitoneal membrane closure', 'A Bochdalek hernia arises from failure of the posterolateral pleuroperitoneal membrane to close, allowing abdominal viscera to pass into the chest. The septum transversum forms the central tendon of the diaphragm, and phrenic nerve or esophageal anomalies cause different problems.'),
('92c697c1-3858-505b-9ac2-aed8f7a03b18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of a Morgagni hernia?', '["Weakness at the aortic hiatus resulting in decreased functional activity","Abnormal phrenic nerve innervation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Traumatic rupture mediated by specific receptor activation","Defect in the esophageal hiatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Failure of fusion of the sternal and costal attachments of the diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of fusion of the sternal and costal attachments of the diaphragm', 'A Morgagni hernia results from failed fusion between the sternal and costal portions of the diaphragm, creating an anterior parasternal defect. The other options describe weakness at the aortic or esophageal hiatus, phrenic nerve pathology, or trauma rather than a congenital para-sternal gap.'),
('14b6fa5c-1e13-53a0-8b63-4b37f161ba25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The spleen develops from which germ layer?', '["Ectoderm","Mesoderm","Surface ectoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoderm', 'The spleen develops from mesenchymal cells in the dorsal mesogastrium, which are of mesodermal origin. It is a non-lymphoid mesodermal organ rather than a derivative of ectoderm, endoderm, or neural crest.'),
('1a412922-bf46-5415-b252-8e73f42a3950'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What congenital anomaly is associated with asplenia?', '["Noonan syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Down syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ivemark syndrome (bilateral right-sidedness)","DiGeorge syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Turner syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ivemark syndrome (bilateral right-sidedness)', 'Ivemark syndrome features asplenia combined with bilateral right-sidedness of the thoracic and abdominal organs, often with complex congenital heart disease. The other listed syndromes have characteristic but different associations and do not feature absence of the spleen.'),
('96cda92c-8bb5-511c-9113-f86b06515b4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The lymphatic system develops from which embryonic structure?', '["Surface ectoderm","Arterial system","Lymph sacs from venous system","Intermediate mesoderm","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph sacs from venous system', 'The lymphatic system arises from lymph sacs that bud off the embryonic venous system and then spread peripherally. It does not develop from the arterial system, surface ectoderm, intermediate mesoderm, or neural crest.'),
('641334bf-0855-5593-a442-0dee51984456'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the first lymph sac to appear?', '["Retroperitoneal lymph sac","Jugular lymph sacs","Thoracic duct","Posterior lymph sac","Cisterna chyli"]'::jsonb, 'Jugular lymph sacs', 'The paired jugular lymph sacs, located near the junctions of the subclavian and anterior cardinal veins, are the first lymphatic sacs to appear. The thoracic duct, cisterna chyli, and other sacs form later from subsequent outgrowths.'),
('9e6dc999-2efc-5be1-b99b-768f1e3aaf13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The thoracic duct develops from which structure?', '["Left jugular lymph sac only","Posterior lymph sac only","Cisterna chyli only","Right jugular lymph sac only","Right and left thoracic ducts and their anastomosis"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left thoracic ducts and their anastomosis', 'The definitive thoracic duct forms from the anastomosis of the caudal portions of the right and left thoracic ducts together with part of the cisterna chyli. During this reshuffling the connecting channels unite, which is why a single duct develops rather than deriving from one isolated sac.'),
('99725db9-b5df-5914-8696-dd8db417273e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common vascular anomaly?', '["Arteriovenous malformation","Hemangioma","Lymphangioma","Vascular malformation","Lymphatic malformation"]'::jsonb, 'Hemangioma', 'Hemangiomas are benign proliferations of endothelial cells and are the most common vascular anomaly overall. Lymphangiomas, arteriovenous malformations, and true vascular malformations are far less common and differ in pathogenesis.'),
('1d65d37b-ed1f-5318-ac10-1ecc225aba7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is a cystic hygroma?', '["Cystic lymphangioma of the neck","Hemangioma of the neck","Thyroglossal cyst","Dermoid cyst","Branchial cleft cyst"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic lymphangioma of the neck', 'A cystic hygroma is a multiloculated cyst of lymphatic origin, usually in the posterior triangle of the neck, representing a cystic lymphangioma. Thyroglossal, dermoid, and branchial cysts have different embryologic origins and locations.'),
('84a8c2bc-5ad0-5e51-86d1-0cd4365f2730'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the parathyroid glands?', '["Pharyngeal pouches (third and fourth)","Ectoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pharyngeal clefts mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal pouches (third and fourth)', 'The inferior parathyroid glands arise from the third pharyngeal pouch and the superior pair from the fourth pouch; all four are endodermal derivatives. Pharyngeal clefts and neural crest contribute to other branchial structures, not the parathyroids.'),
('a8307e43-9684-5238-8072-ff95a79e426a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The pituitary gland develops from which two structures?', '["Mesoderm and endoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Rathke''s pouch (oral ectoderm) and neurohypophysis (neuroectoderm)","Neural crest and mesoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Surface ectoderm and neural crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endoderm and ectoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rathke''s pouch (oral ectoderm) and neurohypophysis (neuroectoderm)', 'The adenohypophysis (anterior and intermediate lobes) develops from Rathke''s pouch, an upgrowth of oral ectoderm, while the neurohypophysis grows down from the neuroectoderm of the diencephalon. Neither portion derives from mesoderm or neural crest.'),
('3437618f-795f-5a15-9192-28966300d645'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is Rathke''s pouch?', '["An outpocketing of the pharyngeal endoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","A part of the neural tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The precursor of the thyroid mediated by specific receptor activation","An outpocketing of the oral ectoderm that forms the anterior pituitary","The precursor of the thymus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An outpocketing of the oral ectoderm that forms the anterior pituitary', 'Rathke''s pouch is an evagination of the ectoderm of the stomodeum (oral cavity) that gives rise to the anterior pituitary and the pars intermedia. The thyroid and thymus arise from the pharyngeal endoderm, and the neural tube forms the neurohypophysis.'),
('b1b6e26b-912f-5aa8-96eb-c04cbde0799f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is a craniopharyngioma?', '["Tumor of the hypothalamus","Tumor of the pineal gland","Tumor of the posterior pituitary","Tumor of the meninges","Tumor arising from Rathke''s pouch remnants"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor arising from Rathke''s pouch remnants', 'A craniopharyngioma arises from remnants of Rathke''s pouch (Rathke cleft cells) along the former craniopharyngeal canal, typically causing suprasellar calcification and visual impairment. It is not a tumor of the pineal, meninges, hypothalamus, or posterior pituitary proper.'),
('8212b4ca-193c-5dc3-bd84-ff373f4e62d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The mammary glands develop from which germ layer?', '["Mesoderm","Neural crest","Intermediate mesoderm","Endoderm","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Surface ectoderm', 'The mammary glands develop as ingrowths of the surface ectoderm along the mammary ridge, and the nipple-areola complex also derives from surface ectoderm. The deep connective tissue and vasculature are mesodermal, but the glandular rudiment itself is ectodermal.'),
('a2370911-dcc5-56a8-ad59-d412288f344e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the mammary ridge (milk line)?', '["Remnant of the allantois","Thickened ectoderm from axilla to groin","Extension of neural crest into breast","Part of the lymphatic system","Derivative of mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Thickened ectoderm from axilla to groin', 'The mammary ridge is a band of thickened ectoderm that runs from the axilla to the groin on each side; most of it disappears except in the pectoral region. It is not derived from neural crest, mesoderm, or the lymphatic system, and it is unrelated to the allantois.'),
('c6c39fe9-f4e4-550f-9307-d0ceaf407891'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is an accessory nipple (polythelia)?', '["Nipple without areola","Extra nipple along the milk line","Enlarged nipple","Nipple without breast tissue","Nipple with inverted appearance"]'::jsonb, 'Extra nipple along the milk line', 'Polythelia is the presence of an extra (supernumerary) nipple along the course of the milk line where the ridge fails to regress. It is not defined by absence of areola or breast tissue, enlargement, or inversion of the nipple.'),
('71f179a4-e236-5c4a-9b7a-ba7679d63717'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Supernumerary breast tissue (polymastia) is most commonly found where?', '["Abdomen","Axilla","Back","Neck","Thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Axilla', 'Accessory breast tissue occurs most often in the axilla, at the upper end of the milk line where ectodermal remnants persist. The other listed ectopic sites are far less common locations for polymastia.'),
('eec7e96c-cff3-5d0d-bf55-c12e8fef0ebf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The external genitalia develop from which structure?', '["Cloacal membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesonephric duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paramesonephric duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Genital tubercle urogenital folds and labioscrotal swellings","Urogenital sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Genital tubercle urogenital folds and labioscrotal swellings', 'The external genitalia develop from the indifferent structures comprising the genital tubercle, urogenital folds, and labioscrotal swellings. The mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts form internal duct systems, while the urogenital sinus contributes to bladder, urethra, and prostate.'),
('71cd7cd4-dd35-5ad8-8559-38ad79fdcac5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the indifferent stage of external genitalia development?', '["Up to week 7 when male and female external genitalia are identical","Week 10 when differentiation is complete accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Week 4-5 mediated by specific receptor activation","Week 12-14 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Week 2-3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Up to week 7 when male and female external genitalia are identical', 'Until about week 7 the external genitalia are in an indifferent stage, and the male and female patterns are identical. Differentiation into a male or female appearance begins only after this period under the influence of hormones.'),
('9b315d43-1992-5747-bdeb-7ea870e6ebee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Under the influence of DHT the genital tubercle becomes what in males?', '["Prepuce","Glans penis","Shaft of penis","Corpus cavernosum","Scrotum"]'::jsonb, 'Glans penis', 'In males, dihydrotestosterone (DHT) transforms the genital tubercle into the glans penis, while the body of the tubercle elongates to form the shaft. The scrotum arises from the labioscrotal swellings and the prepuce from the ectoderm of the glans.'),
('ffa076d0-5231-5aa6-96ae-55173e444d24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Under the influence of DHT the urogenital folds fuse to form what in males?', '["Corpus spongiosum","Scrotum","Prepuce","Shaft of penis","Penile urethra"]'::jsonb, 'Penile urethra', 'Under DHT influence the urogenital folds fuse in the midline to enclose the spongy (penile) urethra. The scrotum forms from the labioscrotal swellings, and the shaft of the penis derives from the genital tubercle.'),
('e69c756f-4f41-554c-abac-4e52e90924f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Under the influence of DHT the labioscrotal swellings fuse to form what in males?', '["Shaf of penis","Scrotum","Labia majora","Prepuce","Perineum"]'::jsonb, 'Scrotum', 'In males the labioscrotal swellings fuse in the midline to form the scrotum, which later receives the testes. In females the same swellings persist unfused as the labia majora; the penis and prepuce come from the genital tubercle and surrounding ectoderm.'),
('539a560f-5864-509c-82a9-5a5f028b4de7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Without DHT the genital tubercle becomes what in females?', '["Clitoris","Bartholin gland","Labia minora","Labia majora","Vagina"]'::jsonb, 'Clitoris', 'In the absence of DHT the genital tubercle remains small and becomes the clitoris. The labia minora derive from the urogenital folds, the labia majora from the labioscrotal swellings, and the vagina from the paramesonephric ducts and urogenital sinus.'),
('00db9313-5754-5dd0-b3d0-7857bb63781f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Without DHT the urogenital folds become what in females?', '["Vagina","Labia minora","Labia majora","Vestibule","Clitoris"]'::jsonb, 'Labia minora', 'Without DHT the urogenital folds do not fuse and develop into the labia minora. The labia majora arise from the labioscrotal swellings, the clitoris from the genital tubercle, and the vagina has a separate origin from the urogenital sinus and paramesonephric ducts.'),
('f2d4216d-a034-5ad5-b243-4116d19dee0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Without DHT the labioscrotal swellings become what in females?', '["Labia minora","Vagina","Mons pubis","Labia majora","Clitoris"]'::jsonb, 'Labia majora', 'In the female the labioscrotal swellings remain unfused and form the labia majora. The labia minora come from the urogenital folds, the clitoris from the genital tubercle, and the mons pubis is fatty tissue anterior to the pubic symphysis rather than a direct derivative of these swellings.'),
('d9efc8e4-2ac7-5294-af14-e3dde777b101'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What enzyme converts testosterone to DHT?', '["11-beta hydroxylase","21-hydroxylase","5-alpha reductase","Aromatase","17-alpha hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, '5-alpha reductase', '5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone into the more potent dihydrotestosterone (DHT). Aromatase converts androgens to estrogens, and 11-beta, 17-alpha, and 21-hydroxylases participate in steroidogenesis in the adrenal cortex, not in DHT formation.'),
('87dbeb4c-4efb-5085-a63a-61d159042812'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What congenital condition results from 5-alpha reductase deficiency?', '["Gynecomastia resulting in decreased functional activity","Complete female phenotype with 46 XY accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ambiguous genitalia with male pseudohermaphroditism","Cryptorchidism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Precocious puberty leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ambiguous genitalia with male pseudohermaphroditism', '5-alpha-reductase deficiency impairs DHT-dependent virilization of the external genitalia, causing ambiguous genitalia in an individual with a 46 XY karyotype and functioning testes (male pseudohermaphroditism). Complete androgen insensitivity produces a fully female phenotype, which is not the picture here.'),
('45e467b8-05f5-5a2a-b106-a0f2e5ce12e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The adrenal cortex develops from which layer?', '["Mesoderm (coelomic epithelium)","Splanchnic mesoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest","Ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoderm (coelomic epithelium)', 'The adrenal cortex arises from the coelomic epithelium of mesodermal origin near the developing gonad. The adrenal medulla is the neural crest derivative, which is why the cortex and medulla have different embryonic sources.'),
('3d343385-36b9-5f01-ba86-0b3ced6fc8e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the fetal zone of the adrenal cortex?', '["The zone that produces androgens","The outer zone that persists after birth","The zone that produces cortisol","The inner zone that regresses after birth","The zone that produces aldosterone"]'::jsonb, 'The inner zone that regresses after birth', 'The fetal zone is the large inner portion of the fetal adrenal cortex that produces DHEA-S and regresses within the first year after birth. The outer definitive zone persists and later gives rise to the permanent adult zones that produce aldosterone and cortisol.'),
('984fdd94-6049-57ac-a92e-d568af9df5f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common tumor of the adrenal gland in children?', '["Adrenal adenoma","Pheochromocytoma","Ganglioneuroma","Adrenal cortical carcinoma","Neuroblastoma"]'::jsonb, 'Neuroblastoma', 'Neuroblastoma, arising from neural crest cells of the adrenal medulla, is the most common adrenal tumor in children and the most common extracranial solid tumor of childhood. Pheochromocytoma and cortical neoplasms are far less frequent in this age group.'),
('04819d2a-9941-565a-9f88-f0c5cb7dfc20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the pineal gland?', '["Endoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Surface ectoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuroectoderm from the roof of the diencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Neuroectoderm from the roof of the diencephalon', 'The pineal gland develops as an evagination of the neuroectoderm of the roof plate of the diencephalon. It is not a surface ectoderm, neural crest, mesodermal, or endodermal derivative.'),
('8bd24f55-782d-5ecb-8580-edf663c69eb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the function of the pineal gland?', '["All of the above","Regulation of sleep-wake cycle","Control of puberty timing","Melatonin secretion","Regulation of circadian rhythms"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The pineal gland secretes melatonin, which regulates circadian rhythms, influences the sleep-wake cycle, and participates in the timing of puberty. Because every listed function applies, the most complete choice is the option that includes them all.'),
('1b32d7a8-e3ad-5279-8651-972620617c14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The cerebral hemispheres develop from which part of the neural tube?', '["Diencephalon","Rhombencephalon (hindbrain)","Prosencephalon (forebrain)","Mesencephalon (midbrain)","Telencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Telencephalon', 'The cerebral hemispheres are the derivative of the telencephalon, the rostral outgrowth of the forebrain. The diencephalon forms the thalamus and hypothalamus, the mesencephalon the midbrain, and the rhombencephalon the hindbrain.'),
('d7b89877-4dcc-5151-b607-29fcd095d6bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What are the three primary brain vesicles?', '["Prosencephalon mesencephalon rhombencephalon","Supratentorial infratentorial brainstem","Telencephalon diencephalon mesencephalon","Anterior middle posterior brain","Forebrain midbrain hindbrain"]'::jsonb, 'Prosencephalon mesencephalon rhombencephalon', 'The three primary brain vesicles are the prosencephalon (forebrain), mesencephalon (midbrain), and rhombencephalon (hindbrain). The telencephalon and diencephalon are the secondary vesicles into which the forebrain subdivides, not primary ones.'),
('b83d86b7-e3cf-556a-b2c7-5970f97ff3ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What are the five secondary brain vesicles?', '["Telencephalon mesencephalon metencephalon myelencephalon diencephalon","Diencephalon mesencephalon metencephalon myelencephalon telencephalon","Telencephalon diencephalon mesencephalon metencephalon myelencephalon","Forebrain midbrain hindbrain pons medulla","Prosencephalon mesencephalon rhombencephalon metencephalon myelencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Telencephalon diencephalon mesencephalon metencephalon myelencephalon', 'At the 5-week stage each primary vesicle divides into two, yielding telencephalon, diencephalon, mesencephalon, metencephalon, and myelencephalon. The hindbrain splits into metencephalon and myelencephalon, and the forebrain into telencephalon and diencephalon.'),
('5e6da5e0-9217-5fac-870b-eec4dbdd92f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The pons develops from which secondary vesicle?', '["Myelencephalon","Mesencephalon","Telencephalon","Diencephalon","Metenchephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Metenchephalon', 'The pons forms from the metencephalon, the rostral half of the hindbrain, along with the cerebellum. The myelencephalon gives the medulla, the mesencephalon the midbrain, and the telencephalon and diencephalon derive from the forebrain.'),
('84a2a961-44b1-568b-9d96-32c97e981884'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The medulla oblongata develops from which secondary vesicle?', '["Metenchephalon","Diencephalon","Mesencephalon","Myelencephalon","Telencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Myelencephalon', 'The medulla oblongata develops from the myelencephalon, the caudal half of the hindbrain vesicle. The metencephalon forms the pons and cerebellum, while the mesencephalon gives the midbrain.'),
('25df13cf-0af0-5bf8-a67b-72c017c0f32f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The cerebellum develops from which secondary vesicle?', '["Myelencephalon","Diencephalon","Metenchephalon","Telencephalon","Mesencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Metenchephalon', 'The cerebellum arises from the dorsal part of the metencephalon, which also produces the pons ventrally. The myelencephalon forms the medulla and the mesencephalon forms the midbrain.'),
('26beffef-a03a-5480-8521-88c631c1bd72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The midbrain develops from which vesicle?', '["Metenchephalon","Mesencephalon","Myelencephalon","Telencephalon","Diencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Mesencephalon', 'The midbrain develops from the mesencephalon, the only secondary vesicle that does not divide further. The metencephalon and myelencephalon form the hindbrain derivatives and the diencephalon gives the thalamus and hypothalamus.'),
('5c1dd6e2-758e-5edf-9901-4d80d3323aef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The thalamus and hypothalamus develop from which vesicle?', '["Mesencephalon","Diencephalon","Metenchephalon","Telencephalon","Myelencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Diencephalon', 'The diencephalon produces the thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus, retina, and posterior pituitary. The telencephalon yields the cerebral hemispheres and basal ganglia, while the hindbrain vesicles give the pons and medulla.'),
('5f86383c-1d7d-58e4-abe4-a8e4c39e9252'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The basal ganglia develop from which structure?', '["Telencephalon","Mesencephalon","Diencephalon","Metenchephalon","Myelencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Telencephalon', 'The basal ganglia, like the cerebral cortex, differentiate from the telencephalon. The diencephalon supplies the thalamus, and the hindbrain vesicles contribute the pons, cerebellum, and medulla.'),
('91e693ec-d1cb-5d97-92a6-9eb164e8322a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital brain malformation?', '["Lissencephaly","Dandy-Walker malformation","Agenesis of corpus callosum","Hydrocephalus","Chiari malformation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrocephalus', 'Hydrocephalus, excess cerebrospinal fluid with ventricular dilatation, is cited as the most common congenital brain malformation. Lissencephaly, callosal agenesis, and Dandy-Walker and Chiari malformations are less frequent developmental defects.'),
('942ff360-245b-55eb-9ab5-540d579abd96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is Chiari type II malformation?', '["Herniation of cerebellar vermis and medulla through foramen magnum","Agenesis of corpus callosum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small posterior fossa mediated by specific receptor activation","Partial absence of cerebellar vermis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dilatation of the fourth ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Herniation of cerebellar vermis and medulla through foramen magnum', 'Chiari type II is characterized by downward displacement of the cerebellar vermis and medulla through the foramen magnum, usually with a myelomeningocele. Callosal agenesis, vermian absence, or isolated fourth-ventricle dilatation describe different malformations.'),
('4ab93c5f-b465-5386-9f77-73ec17bce18e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is Dandy-Walker malformation?', '["Cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle with vermian agenesis","Hydrocephalus ex vacuo accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chiari malformation mediated by specific receptor activation","Agenesis of corpus callosum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small posterior fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle with vermian agenesis', 'Dandy-Walker malformation shows cystic enlargement of the fourth ventricle with partial or complete vermian agenesis and an elevated tentorium. Chiari malformation involves hindbrain herniation, and callosal agenesis is a separate midline defect.'),
('b5d5df06-714b-525a-a7ee-13eac5813a84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is holoprosencephaly?', '["Failure of the forebrain to divide into hemispheres","Abnormal gyri formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small brain size mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete absence of the brain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hydrocephalus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the forebrain to divide into hemispheres', 'In holoprosencephaly the prosencephalon fails to cleave into two hemispheres and lateral ventricles, leaving a single forebrain lobe. Abnormal gyri, a small or absent brain, and hydrocephalus represent different categories of cerebral malformation.'),
('74ece4b0-fd9d-5f71-8553-e2a1bdf18e3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is lissencephaly?', '["Thick corpus callosum","Multiple small gyri","Small head size","Abnormal gyral pattern","Smooth brain with absent or decreased gyri"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth brain with absent or decreased gyri', 'Lissencephaly means a smooth cortical surface with absent or markedly reduced gyri, resulting from defective neuronal migration. Multiple small gyri describe polymicrogyria, and the other options do not define the smooth-brain pattern.'),
('fa12b876-0dda-588a-8a48-0d2091c5855a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is schizencephaly?', '["Pores in the brain resulting in decreased functional activity","Gray matter-lined clefts extending from the ventricle to the pia","Cavum septum pellucidum mediated by specific receptor activation","Cavum vergae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abnormal sulcation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gray matter-lined clefts extending from the ventricle to the pia', 'Schizencephaly consists of gray matter-lined clefts spanning from the ventricular surface to the pia, a neuronal migration disorder. The cavum septum pellucidum and vergae are normal midline fluid spaces, and porencephaly describes CSF-filled cavities.'),
('a489e6a7-0bf6-50e9-9800-863154947fac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is polymicrogyria?', '["Small gyri with normal cortical lamination","Multiple small abnormal gyri with fusion","Thick cortex","Absence of gyri","Abnormal white matter"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple small abnormal gyri with fusion', 'Polymicrogyria shows numerous small, excessively folded gyri with fusion of cortical layers, a neuronal migration defect. Normal lamination, a thick cortex, and absent gyri describe different cortical malformations.'),
('f8c5e92a-9834-5a9c-bd09-fdbca381079e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is heterotopia?', '["Ectopic white matter resulting in decreased functional activity","Foci of normal gray matter in abnormal locations","Ectopic ventricles mediated by specific receptor activation","Ectopic meninges without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abnormal gyration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foci of normal gray matter in abnormal locations', 'Heterotopia is the presence of nodules of normal gray matter in abnormal locations, typically periventricular, from arrested neuronal migration. The distractors place white matter, ventricles, or meninges in ectopic sites or describe abnormal gyration.'),
('8e67c742-e58d-5e47-8e30-64fdf1219377'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common form of hydrocephalus?', '["Post-hemorrhagic hydrocephalus","Communicating hydrocephalus","Hydrocephalus ex vacuo","Normal pressure hydrocephalus","Nontcommunicating hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 'Nontcommunicating hydrocephalus', 'Noncommunicating (obstructive) hydrocephalus is the most common form, produced by obstruction of CSF flow such as aqueductal stenosis. Communicating hydrocephalus, normal-pressure, and ex vacuo forms arise by different mechanisms and are less frequent.'),
('5817b291-c84c-5379-a321-704ef9b34e0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Agenesis of the corpus callosum is associated with which condition?', '["Schizencephaly","Dandy-Walker","Lissencephaly","Chiari malformation","Aicardi syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Aicardi syndrome', 'Aicardi syndrome, an X-linked dominant disorder in females featuring agenesis of the corpus callosum, chorioretinal lacunae, and seizures, is the classic association. The other options are unrelated CNS malformations rather than syndromic associations.'),
('1e703963-b504-5cd0-bbae-20f35d74a21f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The spinal cord develops from which part of the neural tube?', '["Caudal neural tube","Surface ectoderm","Notochord","Cranial neural tube","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Caudal neural tube', 'The spinal cord develops from the caudal portion of the neural tube, which remains tubular after closure. The cranial neural tube expands into the brain vesicles, while the notochord induces the neural plate and the neural crest forms ganglia.'),
('d8744df5-fa3a-554f-882a-330d1cd7b7a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is syringomyelia?', '["Degeneration of spinal tracts","A tumor of the spinal cord","Inflammation of the spinal cord","A cavity (syrinx) within the spinal cord","Compression of the spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'A cavity (syrinx) within the spinal cord', 'Syringomyelia is a fluid-filled cavity or syrinx within the spinal cord, typically cervical, that can expand and compress surrounding tracts. The distractors describe degeneration, neoplasm, inflammation, or compression rather than a true intramedullary cavity.'),
('7784f3af-eee2-5b9f-b8fb-22ba6b82108d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the filum terminale?', '["The lowest nerve root resulting in decreased functional activity","A fibrous strand extending from the conus medullaris to the coccyx","The terminal part of the spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","The caudal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The end of the central canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrous strand extending from the conus medullaris to the coccyx', 'The filum terminale is a fibrous strand of pia mater that anchors the conus medullaris to the coccyx. The lowest nerve roots form the cauda equina and the conus is the terminal part of the cord itself, not this strand.'),
('0fd8c87b-0949-50e5-97a0-0a9c180eea81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The cauda equina consists of:', '["The terminal spinal cord","Nerve roots below the conus medullaris","Arachnoid granulations","Filum terminale","Nerve roots of the cervical enlargement"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve roots below the conus medullaris', 'The cauda equina is the bundle of lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal nerve roots that descend below the conus medullaris within the dural sac. The filum terminale is a connective tissue strand and arachnoid granulations absorb CSF.'),
('8cd638b8-eefc-5fba-bbe1-8f9dba00cf0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common spinal cord congenital anomaly?', '["Syringomyelia","Tethered cord","Diplegia","Myelomeningocoel","Neural tube defect"]'::jsonb, 'Neural tube defect', 'Neural tube defects, spanning the spina bifida spectrum and anencephaly, are the most common congenital anomalies of the spinal cord. Syringomyelia, tethered cord, and myelomeningocele represent more specific entities within or distinct from this category.'),
('f1bdac85-ffca-5450-853f-b2b1e1f9d9a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The eye develops from which germ layers?', '["Only neural crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Only surface ectoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neuroectoderm surface ectoderm mesoderm and neural crest","Only neuroectoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only mesoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuroectoderm surface ectoderm mesoderm and neural crest', 'The eye is a composite organ: neuroectoderm forms the retina, surface ectoderm the lens and corneal epithelium, mesoderm the vascular coats and extraocular muscles, and neural crest the trabecular meshwork and sclera. No single layer supplies all ocular structures.'),
('92ff93cb-f338-5444-899b-f65eb76ccd65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The lens of the eye develops from which structure?', '["Mesoderm","Surface ectoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest","Neuroectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Surface ectoderm', 'The lens develops as a thickening and invagination of surface ectoderm (the lens placode) over the optic vesicle. The retina comes from neuroectoderm, and mesoderm, endoderm, and neural crest do not form the lens.'),
('204da2e7-6113-5cd0-b0b1-682f3a2d4967'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The retina develops from which structure?', '["Optic cup","Mesoderm","Surface ectoderm","Neural crest","Optic vesicle (neuroectoderm)"]'::jsonb, 'Optic cup', 'The retina differentiates from the optic cup, a neuroectodermal outgrowth whose inner layer forms the neural retina and outer layer the pigment epithelium. Surface ectoderm produces the lens, so it cannot be the retinal origin; the optic vesicle is the earlier precursor that invaginates to form the cup.'),
('5050e989-9e81-50e6-8e96-b15ae0fba757'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is coloboma?', '["A defect in the closure of the choroidal fissure","Absent lens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small eye (microphthalmia) mediated by specific receptor activation","Cataract at birth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete absence of the eye leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A defect in the closure of the choroidal fissure', 'Coloboma results from failure of the choroidal fissure to close, leaving a gap in the iris, retina, or optic disc. Absence, small size, or opacity of the lens describe different anomalies.'),
('ce7cd23d-f8eb-57cd-877a-758cb566f11c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is congenital cataract?', '["Absent lens resulting in decreased functional activity","Opacity of the lens present at birth","Small lens mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal lens shape without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dislocated lens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Opacity of the lens present at birth', 'Congenital cataract is an opacity of the lens present at birth, often due to intrauterine infection or metabolic disease. Absence, small size, abnormal shape, and dislocation of the lens are separate developmental anomalies.'),
('4ef1f8bf-4624-54ac-9c7c-7f278df3432e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The ear develops from which structures?', '["Only the first arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the second arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First and second pharyngeal arches and otic placode","Neural crest only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surface ectoderm only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First and second pharyngeal arches and otic placode', 'The auricle and middle ear ossicles derive from the first and second pharyngeal arches, while the otic placode forms the membranous inner ear. Neither a single arch nor one germ layer alone produces the whole ear.'),
('77884880-aab4-5b54-87a2-e775d3d28abb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The otic placode gives rise to which structure?', '["Eustachian tube resulting in decreased functional activity","External ear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Auricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle ear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inner ear (membranous labyrinth)"]'::jsonb, 'Inner ear (membranous labyrinth)', 'The otic placode invaginates to form the otic vesicle (otocyst), which differentiates into the inner ear or membranous labyrinth. The auricle and external ear come from the pharyngeal arches and the middle ear from the first pouch and arches.'),
('72f82849-2cf2-5f2b-b323-22f3b78b95c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the inner ear?', '["Alexander dysplasia","Scheibe dysplasia","Enlarged vestibular aqueduct","Mondini dysplasia","Michel aplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Mondini dysplasia', 'Mondini dysplasia, a flattened cochlea with incomplete partition, is the most common congenital anomaly of the inner ear. Scheibe and Alexander dysplasia and Michel aplasia are rarer developmental variants.'),
('dc94f4a7-311e-5763-949d-e45174869fb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is preauricular sinus?', '["An extra auricle","Absent ear canal","A small pit or sinus near the external ear","Fusion of the auricle to the head","An absent auricle"]'::jsonb, 'A small pit or sinus near the external ear', 'A preauricular sinus is a small pit or blind-ending tract near the external ear caused by incomplete fusion of the auricular hillocks, often inherited. It is not an accessory auricle, canal atresia, or auricular fusion anomaly.'),
('8015100c-12e5-5103-8c93-587ebe144c31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common facial cleft?', '["Mandibular cleft","Median cleft face","Lateral facial cleft","Oblique facial cleft","Cleft lip with or without cleft palate"]'::jsonb, 'Cleft lip with or without cleft palate', 'Cleft lip with or without cleft palate is the most common facial cleft, resulting from failure of the maxillary and medial nasal prominences to fuse. The mandibular, median, lateral, and oblique cleft variants are far less frequent.'),
('fd4f29a4-a871-5a52-98ee-ebb26dc51414'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Cleft lip results from failure of fusion of which structures?', '["Frontonasal and maxillary prominences","Two medial nasal prominences","Palatal shelves","Maxillary and medial nasal prominences","Maxillary and lateral nasal prominences"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary and medial nasal prominences', 'Cleft lip results from failure of the maxillary and medial nasal prominences to fuse, producing a defect lateral to the midline. Fusion failure of the palatal shelves causes cleft palate, and the other prominence pairs are not involved in cleft lip.'),
('10b02623-dd9b-5e65-8723-9b8e3509301f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Cleft palate results from failure of fusion of which structures?', '["Palatal shelves (lateral palatine processes)","All of the above","Maxillary and medial nasal prominences","Frontonasal and maxillary prominences","Palatal shelves with the primary palate"]'::jsonb, 'Palatal shelves with the primary palate', 'Cleft palate arises when the palatal shelves fail to fuse with each other and with the primary palate. The prominences listed in the other options are involved in cleft lip rather than palate, and the ''all of the above'' option is too broad.'),
('92da77d7-0d8b-51ce-a63a-cc1714a65ba6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is Pierre Robin sequence?', '["Hypertelorism and cleft lip","Microcephaly and facial anomalies","Cleft lip and palate","Craniosynostosis and facial asymmetry","Micrognathia glossoptosis and cleft palate"]'::jsonb, 'Micrognathia glossoptosis and cleft palate', 'Pierre Robin sequence combines micrognathia, glossoptosis (posterior displacement of the tongue), and U-shaped cleft palate, which together can obstruct the airway. The other options describe facial anomaly pairs that do not constitute this sequence.'),
('44eda650-5755-5665-ad07-7d5a37a04e55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common craniosynostosis syndrome?', '["Apert syndrome","Muenke syndrome","Crouzon syndrome","Pfeiffer syndrome","Saethre-Chotzen syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Crouzon syndrome', 'Crouzon syndrome is the most common craniosynostosis syndrome, marked by cranial suture fusion and facial anomalies without syndactyly. Apert and Pfeiffer syndromes show additional limb involvement, and Muenke and Saethre-Chotzen are less frequent.'),
('93249f5f-032b-5d63-966f-bef1421f6263'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Apert syndrome is characterized by:', '["Craniosynostosis and cleft palate","Craniosynostosis and hearing loss","Craniosynostosis and visual loss","Craniosynostosis and syndactyly of fingers and toes","Craniosynostosis and microcephaly"]'::jsonb, 'Craniosynostosis and syndactyly of fingers and toes', 'Apert syndrome pairs craniosynostosis with syndactyly of the fingers and toes, giving ''mitten'' hands and feet. Cleft palate, hearing or visual loss, and microcephaly are not the defining associations of Apert syndrome.'),
('4f26170f-205b-51c8-87f4-d45f8889012d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the tooth enamel?', '["Surface ectoderm","Endoderm","Ectoderm (oral ectoderm)","Neural crest","Mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Ectoderm (oral ectoderm)', 'Tooth enamel is secreted by ameloblasts derived from the oral ectoderm lining the stomodeum. Dentin and pulp come from neural crest mesenchyme and supporting tissues from mesoderm, so those sources cannot form enamel.'),
('ddc41ed3-f881-52ba-867d-7619db15825c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The teeth develop from which structures?', '["Dental lamina only","Neural crest only","Pharyngeal arches","Enamel organ and dental papilla","Alveolar bone"]'::jsonb, 'Enamel organ and dental papilla', 'Each tooth germ is built from the enamel organ, an ectodermal cap, and the dental papilla, neural crest mesenchyme that forms dentin and pulp. Neither structure alone, nor the pharyngeal arches or alveolar bone, produces the whole tooth.'),
('c3056faa-320b-5406-957f-87e995cbceb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the first deciduous tooth to erupt?', '["First molar","Canine","Maxillary central incisor","Mandibular central incisor","Mandibular lateral incisor"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular central incisor', 'The mandibular central incisor is the first deciduous tooth to erupt, usually around 6 months of age. Molars and canines erupt later in the deciduous sequence, and the maxillary central incisor follows the mandibular one.'),
('a3778db7-c19e-5090-873b-4a33db5ebc37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The parotid gland develops from which structure?', '["Oral ectoderm","Buccopharyngeal membrane","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Pharyngeal endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Oral ectoderm', 'The parotid gland arises as an ectodermal bud from the oral (stomodeal) ectoderm near the angle of the mouth. Pharyngeal endoderm and mesoderm give rise to other structures, not the parotid parenchyma.'),
('9a25d0d0-81cd-5b87-88af-bd779e483b95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The submandibular and sublingual glands develop from which structure?', '["Pharyngeal endoderm","Thyroglossal duct","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Oral ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Oral ectoderm', 'The submandibular and sublingual glands, like the parotid, develop from epithelial buds of the oral ectoderm lining the stomodeum. Pharyngeal endoderm, the thyroglossal duct, mesoderm, and neural crest do not give rise to these glands.'),
('dd87f524-4c82-5d53-886b-78df3f74febf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common salivary gland tumor?', '["Mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Pleomorphic adenoma","Warthin tumor","Adenoid cystic carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Pleomorphic adenoma', 'Pleomorphic adenoma is the most common salivary gland tumor, a benign mixed tumor typically in the parotid. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the most common malignant salivary gland tumor but not the most common overall.'),
('24571aa2-2fe5-5773-8811-194a18f647bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The skin develops from which two germ layers?', '["Mesoderm and endoderm","Ectoderm and endoderm","Surface ectoderm and mesenchyme","Ectoderm and mesoderm","Neural crest and ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Ectoderm and mesoderm', 'The skin is composite: the epidermis and its appendages arise from ectoderm while the dermis derives from mesoderm. Endoderm contributes no skin layer, and mesenchyme is not a germ layer term.'),
('5816078b-8d8a-5e7e-86b2-d3fbfe12e2ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The melanocytes of the skin develop from which structure?', '["Endoderm","Neural crest","Surface ectoderm","Ectoderm","Mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest', 'Skin melanocytes migrate from the neural crest into the epidermis during development. Surface ectoderm forms keratinocytes and other epidermal cells, while mesoderm forms the dermis.'),
('60529af8-2d97-55c7-9d28-0ceb988aa02a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital skin lesion?', '["Port-wine stain","Hemangioma","Strawberry hemangioma","Pigmented nevus","Café-au-lait spot"]'::jsonb, 'Hemangioma', 'Hemangiomas are the most common congenital skin lesions in infants, typically proliferating in the first year and then involuting. Port-wine stains, pigmented nevi, and café-au-lait spots are less common congenital skin findings.'),
('dd5ffdca-84e6-5ea3-a6fe-a5adcfd9b432'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The nails develop from which germ layer?', '["Neural crest","Intermediate mesoderm","Mesoderm","Surface ectoderm","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Surface ectoderm', 'Nails develop as keratinized structures of the surface ectoderm over the distal phalanges. Mesoderm, intermediate mesoderm, neural crest, and endoderm contribute to other tissues, not the nail plate.'),
('c3f70f08-0009-5ce8-b39e-9d328e63d3b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the congenital absence of the pectoralis major muscle called?', '["Poland sequence","Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","Prune belly syndrome","Poland syndrome","Moebius syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Poland syndrome', 'Poland syndrome is characterized by congenital absence or hypoplasia of the pectoralis major, often with ipsilateral breast and hand anomalies such as syndactyly. Beckwith-Wiedemann, prune belly, and Moebius syndromes have different features.'),
('c0c3282b-2b85-5574-a194-9eaefa077298'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Prune belly syndrome is characterized by:', '["Smooth tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Lack of limb muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pectus excavatum mediated by specific receptor activation","Prominent abdominal muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absence of abdominal wall muscles with cryptorchidism and urinary tract abnormalities"]'::jsonb, 'Absence of abdominal wall muscles with cryptorchidism and urinary tract abnormalities', 'Prune belly (Eagle-Barrett) syndrome comprises deficient abdominal wall musculature, bilateral cryptorchidism, and urinary tract anomalies such as dilated bladder and ureters. The other options describe tongue, limb, chest, or abdominal findings outside this triad.'),
('ca6c5f24-6e94-5d65-93b7-55db6ebf1865'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the limbs?', '["Clubfoot (talipes equinovarus)","Congenital hip dislocation","Syndactyly","Limb reduction defect","Polydactyly"]'::jsonb, 'Polydactyly', 'Polydactyly, the presence of supernumerary digits, is the most common congenital limb anomaly. Clubfoot and hip dislocation are frequent orthopedic deformities but are not the most common limb malformation, and syndactyly and reduction defects are less frequent.'),
('631d5e6b-2e8d-5f82-909b-e775b414513e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is syndactyly?', '["Extra digits","Absent digits","Webbed fingers only","Fusion of fingers or toes","Short digits"]'::jsonb, 'Fusion of fingers or toes', 'Syndactyly is a congenital limb anomaly in which digits fail to separate because apoptosis fails to remove the interdigital tissue. Simple syndactyly involves only skin, whereas complex syndactyly fuses the underlying bones; extra digits (polydactyly) and short or absent digits are separate defects.'),
('c8bd08bd-a289-51c2-a740-8ec014e148f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is talipes equinovarus (clubfoot)?', '["Flat foot","High arch","Rocker-bottom foot","Foot turned inward and downward","Foot turned outward and upward"]'::jsonb, 'Foot turned inward and downward', 'Talipes equinovarus is a clubfoot deformity with adduction of the forefoot, inversion of the heel, and plantar flexion (equinus), so the foot is turned inward and downward. The other options describe unrelated foot shapes such as flat foot, high arch, rocker-bottom, or outward-upward positioning.'),
('f3fd662e-d627-5733-8bc8-5b80840f0a6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is congenital hip dysplasia?', '["Dislocation of the hip due to trauma","Complete absence of the hip joint","Arthritis of the hip at birth","Abnormal development of the hip joint leading to instability","Fracture of the femoral neck"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal development of the hip joint leading to instability', 'Congenital hip dysplasia reflects abnormal development of the acetabulum and femoral head, so the joint is shallow and unstable and may progress to dislocation. It is a developmental condition, common in females and breech presentations, unlike traumatic dislocation, joint absence, infantile arthritis, or femoral neck fracture.'),
('e876c77a-11bc-5aed-b39c-89f364e624ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is arthrogryposis?', '["Multiple joint contractures","Fused joints","Absent joints","Inflammatory joint disease","Lax joints"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple joint contractures', 'Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita is characterized by multiple fixed joint contractures caused by diminished fetal movement (for example, from neuromuscular disorders). The joints are present but stiff, which distinguishes this from fused, absent, inflamed, or lax joints.'),
('57c842c6-b1ee-5b2f-96a7-8a011aa2b96e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the origin of the vertebrae?', '["Myotome","Somite","Dermatome","Syndetome","Sclerotome"]'::jsonb, 'Sclerotome', 'Each somite differentiates into the sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome, and the sclerotome cells migrate around the neural tube and notochord to form the vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs. The myotome forms muscles and the dermatome forms dermis, so neither gives rise to the vertebrae.'),
('d40d9606-c6ec-52b4-8a64-3aaa5d1b2fc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the notochord?', '["The precursor of the spinal cord","The central canal of the spinal cord","A transient rod-like structure that induces neural tube formation","The precursor of the vertebral column","A part of the brain"]'::jsonb, 'A transient rod-like structure that induces neural tube formation', 'The notochord is a transient midline rod of mesoderm that defines the embryonic axis and induces the overlying ectoderm to become the neural plate and then the neural tube. It is not the spinal cord itself and is not a part of the brain; most notochordal cells disappear, leaving the nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral discs.'),
('d0aa3b3b-1caf-5bdc-966d-beda73725f5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Remnants of the notochord can give rise to which tumor?', '["Chondroma","Osteoma","Neuroblastoma","Glioma","Chordoma"]'::jsonb, 'Chordoma', 'A chordoma is a rare malignant tumor that arises from remnants of the notochord and characteristically contains physaliphorous (vacuolated) cells. It occurs along the axial skeleton from the base of the skull to the sacrum, whereas chondroma and osteoma arise from cartilage and bone, and glioma and neuroblastoma arise from neural tissue.'),
('7e55a413-2489-5091-bf45-e3c0bfa180b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common site of chordoma?', '["Base of skull","Thoracic spine","Lumbar spine","Sacrococcygeal region","Cervical spine"]'::jsonb, 'Sacrococcygeal region', 'Chordomas most commonly arise in the sacrococcygeal region (about half of all cases), followed by the base of the skull at the clivus. They are slow-growing but locally aggressive tumors that develop from notochordal remnants, with vertebral chordomas being less common and more frequent in the cervical and lumbar regions than the thoracic spine.'),
('3df7abf8-4b51-5659-9843-5eb728ea88bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The ribs develop from which structure?', '["Myotome","Sclerotome","Lateral plate mesoderm","Somite","Dermatome"]'::jsonb, 'Sclerotome', 'The ribs arise from the costal processes of the thoracic sclerotomes, the same somite-derived tissue that forms the vertebrae. The sternum, by contrast, is formed from lateral plate mesoderm, while the myotome and dermatome give rise to muscles and dermis.'),
('17ebbdb1-682b-569e-a628-f13069836386'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The sternum develops from which structure?', '["Dermatome","Lateral plate mesoderm","Somite","Myotome","Sclerotome"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plate mesoderm', 'The sternum is formed by the midline fusion of the two sternal bars, which develop from the somatic layer of the lateral plate mesoderm. Somite-derived sclerotome gives rise to the vertebrae and ribs, while the dermatome and myotome form dermis and muscle.'),
('3e63327e-3e35-569c-893f-d8acefb6d875'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is pectus excavatum?', '["Bifid sternum","Protruding sternum","Abnormal sternal shape","Complete absence of sternum","Depressed sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Depressed sternum', 'Pectus excavatum (funnel chest) is a posterior depression of the sternum caused by overgrowth of the costal cartilages, and it is the most common chest wall deformity, sometimes compressing the heart and lungs. The protrusion variant is pectus carinatum, and a bifid or absent sternum results from failure of sternal bar fusion.'),
('40118e40-bc1a-58c2-8d51-4daa2912b2bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is pectus carinatum?', '["Abnormal rib position","Depressed sternum","Sternal cleft","Costal cartilage calcification","Protruding sternum (pigeon chest)"]'::jsonb, 'Protruding sternum (pigeon chest)', 'Pectus carinatum (pigeon chest) is a forward protrusion of the sternum and adjacent costal cartilages. This outward bulge distinguishes it from pectus excavatum (depressed sternum), sternal cleft, and rib or cartilage ossification abnormalities.'),
('7954b594-0b0f-5e6c-82db-f881dc969c18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Sternal cleft results from:', '["Neural crest defect","Abnormal notochord development","Excessive fusion of the sternum","Abnormal rib development","Failure of fusion of the sternal bars"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of fusion of the sternal bars', 'Sternal cleft is caused by failure of the two sternal bars to fuse in the midline during the sixth week of development. Complete failure produces a wide cleft, whereas partial failure may spare the manubrium; neural crest, notochord, or rib defects produce different anomalies.'),
('d5403c45-e1aa-593b-a8b6-00c745aef107'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the embryonic origin of the skin dermis?', '["Mesoderm (sclerotome dermatome and lateral plate)","Neural crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ectoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Endoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surface ectoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoderm (sclerotome dermatome and lateral plate)', 'The dermis has a mesodermal origin: the dermatome of the somites forms the dermis of the back, lateral plate mesoderm forms the dermis of the limbs and body wall, and neural crest cells contribute the dermis of the face and anterior neck. The epidermis, not the dermis, arises from surface ectoderm, and endoderm does not contribute to the skin.'),
('adabd709-9518-56ee-81e4-278f4e61791d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common malignant tumor in children?', '["Osteosarcoma","Wilm''s tumor (nephroblastoma)","Neuroblastoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Leukemia"]'::jsonb, 'Leukemia', 'Leukemia is the most common malignancy of childhood, accounting for about 30% of all pediatric cancers, with acute lymphoblastic leukemia being the predominant type. Neuroblastoma and Wilm''s tumor are important embryonal solid tumors of young children, but they are less common overall than leukemia.'),
('6a81c853-dbed-533f-9f40-d0ed2f57cec0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is Wilm''s tumor (nephroblastoma)?', '["An adrenal tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A tumor of the ureteric bud accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A bladder tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A testicular tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A renal tumor derived from metanephric mesenchyme"]'::jsonb, 'A renal tumor derived from metanephric mesenchyme', 'Wilm''s tumor (nephroblastoma) arises from metanephric blastema, the undifferentiated metanephric mesenchyme of the developing kidney, and typically presents as a large flank mass in a young child. The other options mislocate the tumor to the adrenal, ureter, bladder, or testis.'),
('12ea0807-31f3-58f6-9a16-5cafbb53284f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common solid tumor in children?', '["Osteosarcoma","Brain tumors","Wilm''s tumor","Neuroblastoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Brain tumors', 'Brain tumors are the most common solid malignant neoplasm of childhood. Neuroblastoma is the most common extracranial solid tumor in infants, and Wilm''s tumor, rhabdomyosarcoma, and osteosarcoma are important but less frequent pediatric solid tumors.'),
('e9214996-1d0b-5b7d-a8c2-6a815a310c7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is associated with which abnormalities?', '["Macroglossia omphalocele visceromegaly hypoglycemia","Cleft lip and palate","Microcephaly and growth retardation","Limb reduction defects","Cardiac anomalies alone"]'::jsonb, 'Macroglossia omphalocele visceromegaly hypoglycemia', 'Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is marked by macroglossia, omphalocele (exomphalos), visceromegaly, and neonatal hypoglycemia, with hemihypertrophy and an increased risk of embryonal tumors such as Wilm''s tumor. The other options describe features of unrelated conditions such as trisomies, clefting syndromes, or isolated cardiac malformation.'),
('549fcbd4-8bfa-552e-b1f7-1dba208949b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the genetic basis of Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome?', '["Turner syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Trisomy 21 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deletion of 22q11.2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormalities of chromosome 11p15 (imprinted region)","Trisomy 18 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormalities of chromosome 11p15 (imprinted region)', 'Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome results from altered imprinting in the 11p15 region, such as loss of imprinting of IGF2 or loss of function of CDKN1C. Because these genes are expressed from one parental allele, the clinical picture depends on the sex of the parent who transmits the defect; Turner syndrome, trisomy 21, trisomy 18, and 22q11.2 deletion produce different syndromes.'),
('eedfdd28-34d7-55dc-93a9-407515435e75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Fetal alcohol syndrome is characterized by which triad?', '["Spina bifida hydrocephalus and anencephaly","Syndactyly polydactyly and clubfoot","Cleft lip palate and micrognathia","Growth deficiency facial dysmorphism CNS abnormalities","Cardiac renal and limb anomalies"]'::jsonb, 'Growth deficiency facial dysmorphism CNS abnormalities', 'The diagnostic triad of fetal alcohol syndrome comprises prenatal and postnatal growth deficiency, characteristic facial dysmorphism (short palpebral fissures, smooth philtrum, thin vermilion border), and CNS abnormalities, of which intellectual disability and microcephaly are the most serious. The other options describe neural tube, limb, orofacial cleft, or visceral malformation patterns rather than the FAS triad.'),
('5a1af708-b49f-5a82-8c17-812ad741ed90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common teratogenic exposure causing congenital anomalies?', '["Anticonvulsants","Smoking","Alcohol","Rubella infection","Accutane"]'::jsonb, 'Alcohol', 'Alcohol is the most common cause of preventable birth defects and is harmful throughout pregnancy, producing a spectrum from fetal alcohol syndrome to growth and neurobehavioral impairment. Rubella, anticonvulsants, and isotretinoin are important teratogens, but none matches alcohol in overall frequency of congenital anomalies.'),
('15d4fc67-5e41-5ea7-9e28-c0b8ff486318'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Rubella infection during pregnancy most commonly causes which anomalies?', '["Neural tube defects resulting in decreased functional activity","Cataracts deafness cardiac defects (patent ductus arteriosus pulmonary stenosis)","Gastrointestinal atresia mediated by specific receptor activation","Cleft lip and palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Limb reduction defects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cataracts deafness cardiac defects (patent ductus arteriosus pulmonary stenosis)', 'Congenital rubella syndrome classically involves the eye (cataracts), the inner ear (sensorineural deafness), and the heart (patent ductus arteriosus and pulmonary stenosis), because the virus damages these developing organs during the first trimester. Neural tube defects, gut atresia, cleft lip and palate, and limb reductions belong to other teratogenic or genetic causes.'),
('3f0e80c2-ab0d-5934-ad06-b5cc59ee9ce2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the critical period for rubella teratogenicity?', '["Second trimester resulting in decreased functional activity","Third trimester accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prenatal period only mediated by specific receptor activation","Entire pregnancy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","First trimester (especially first 8-10 weeks)"]'::jsonb, 'First trimester (especially first 8-10 weeks)', 'Rubella is most teratogenic in the first trimester, particularly in the first 8-10 weeks, when the eyes, ears, and heart are undergoing organogenesis, and infection during this window produces cataracts, cardiac defects, and deafness. Infection after the first trimester carries a much lower risk of malformation.'),
('8b30d710-6442-5a5c-b085-38038adc7e4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Valproic acid use in pregnancy is associated with which congenital anomaly?', '["Cleft lip and palate resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiac anomalies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Esophageal atresia mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural tube defects (especially spina bifida)","Limb reduction defects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neural tube defects (especially spina bifida)', 'Valproic acid is an anticonvulsant that markedly increases the risk of neural tube defects, particularly lumbar spina bifida, when taken in early pregnancy, likely through its antifolate and HDAC-inhibitory effects. Daily folic acid supplementation reduces this risk, whereas the other listed anomalies are less characteristic of valproate exposure.'),
('4e53b5e2-5b5d-594c-afad-40d428a30039'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Accutane (isotretinoin) use in pregnancy causes which anomalies?', '["CNS cardiovascular and craniofacial anomalies","Cardiac anomalies only","Limb reduction defects only","Cleft palate only","Neural tube defects only"]'::jsonb, 'CNS cardiovascular and craniofacial anomalies', 'Isotretinoin (Accutane) is a highly teratogenic retinoid that produces a broad pattern of CNS (hydrocephalus), cardiovascular, and craniofacial anomalies. Because the malformations involve multiple systems, options listing a single organ system are incomplete descriptions of the teratogenic spectrum.'),
('9de871de-5710-5930-97bb-dd12a31afe99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Warfarin use in pregnancy causes which condition?', '["Fetal warfarin syndrome (nasal hypoplasia stippled epiphyses)","Cardiac anomalies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cleft palate mediated by specific receptor activation","Limb defects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CNS anomalies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fetal warfarin syndrome (nasal hypoplasia stippled epiphyses)', 'Warfarin crosses the placenta and inhibits vitamin K-dependent carboxylation, producing fetal warfarin syndrome with characteristic nasal hypoplasia and stippled (punctate) epiphyses, most often after first-trimester exposure. Later exposure causes fetal CNS hemorrhage, but the distinctive facial and skeletal findings define the syndrome.'),
('f161f261-cd9f-5ccc-937b-3556c0e381e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'Diethylstilbestrol (DES) exposure in utero is associated with which condition in female offspring?', '["Uterine cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Breast cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cervical cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina","Ovarian cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina', 'In utero exposure to diethylstilbestrol (DES) is strongly linked to clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina in female offspring, often presenting decades later. DES also causes vaginal adenosis, cervical hood, and aT-shaped uterus, but it does not predispose to the cancers of the uterus, cervix, ovary, or breast listed here.'),
('299b156a-ac3c-58aa-a3f6-ad10591b3d8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the most common mechanism of teratogenesis?', '["Genetic susceptibility","Dose of exposure","Timing of exposure","Specificity of teratogen","Duration of exposure"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic susceptibility', 'The most important determinant of whether a teratogen harms a given conceptus is genetic susceptibility, because genotype determines how the mother and fetus metabolize the agent into active or inactive forms. Dose, timing, specificity, and duration modulate the effect, but the genetic makeup of the conceptus governs the individual response.'),
('5464c194-eb2c-5e0d-96f8-a2a161f3e5f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the critical period for teratogenesis of major structural anomalies?', '["Weeks 12-16","Weeks 8-12","Weeks 16-20","Weeks 3-8 of gestation","Weeks 20-24"]'::jsonb, 'Weeks 3-8 of gestation', 'The embryonic period (weeks 3-8) is the critical window for major structural malformations, because organogenesis is then underway and each organ has its own sensitive period within this interval. Exposure after week 8 mainly causes growth restriction and functional defects rather than gross structural anomalies.'),
('72616a26-0f8c-55a7-bef0-5f6547d65a4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the process of capacitation?', '["Development of the placenta","Oocyte maturation in the ovary","Fertilization of the oocyte","Sperm maturation in the female reproductive tract","Implantation of the blastocyst"]'::jsonb, 'Sperm maturation in the female reproductive tract', 'Capacitation is the series of functional changes in the sperm membrane that occurs during transit through the female reproductive tract, rendering the sperm capable of fertilizing the oocyte. It involves removal of glycoproteins and lipid changes and must be completed before the acrosome reaction can occur; the other options describe oocyte maturation, fertilization, implantation, or placental development.'),
('9a003022-a506-5f6f-b42e-0948d3d9d27d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the acrosome reaction?', '["Activation of the oocyte resulting in decreased functional activity","Fusion of sperm and egg membranes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Entry of sperm into the oocyte mediated by specific receptor activation","Release of enzymes from the sperm head to penetrate the zona pellucida","Formation of the male pronucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Release of enzymes from the sperm head to penetrate the zona pellucida', 'The acrosome reaction is the exocytosis of hydrolytic enzymes (hyaluronidase and acrosin) from the sperm head, which digest the zona pellucida so the sperm can penetrate to the oocyte membrane. It is triggered by sperm binding to the zona pellucida and precedes membrane fusion, oocyte entry, and male pronucleus formation.'),
('01c643b0-c422-59d7-a329-ba84e29fcaeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the zona pellucida?', '["The inner cell mass","The yolk sac","The amniotic cavity","The outer layer of the blastocyst","A glycoprotein layer surrounding the oocyte"]'::jsonb, 'A glycoprotein layer surrounding the oocyte', 'The zona pellucida is a thick coat of glycoproteins (ZP1-ZP3) that surrounds the oocyte, mediates sperm binding, and, after the cortical reaction modifies it, prevents polyspermy. It encloses the early embryo until the blastocyst hatches before implantation, whereas the trophoblast is the outer layer of the blastocyst.'),
('9746173c-f9ed-586a-b66a-0f83aa3dd5a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the cortical reaction?', '["Fusion of pronuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Cleavage of the zygote accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Implantation of the blastocyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Release of cortical granules from the oocyte to block polyspermy","The acrosome reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Release of cortical granules from the oocyte to block polyspermy', 'The cortical reaction is the calcium-triggered exocytosis of cortical granules from the oocyte into the perivitelline space, which hardens the zona pellucida (zona reaction) and prevents additional sperm from binding, thereby blocking polyspermy. It follows sperm entry and precedes pronuclear fusion, cleavage, and implantation.'),
('e7ada554-3ab7-553c-b8ff-efce884a02d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is syngamy?', '["Implantation","Fusion of sperm and oocyte membranes","Fusion of male and female pronuclei","Formation of the blastocyst","Cleavage of the zygote"]'::jsonb, 'Fusion of male and female pronuclei', 'Syngamy is the fusion of the male and female pronuclei to restore the diploid chromosome number and complete fertilization. It follows the acrosome and cortical reactions and sperm entry, and it precedes the first cleavage division of the zygote.'),
('f9977e64-3429-5aba-b6d6-ba2f7dc63eeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the name of the first cleavage division of the zygote?', '["Mitotic division","Fragmentation","Meiotic division","Parthenogenesis","Segmentation"]'::jsonb, 'Mitotic division', 'The zygote divides by a series of rapid mitotic divisions called cleavage, so the first cleavage division, which produces two blastomeres, is a mitotic division. Meiotic division completes oogenesis rather than the zygote, parthenogenesis is abnormal activation without a sperm, and fragmentation is an abnormal type of cleavage.'),
('91cf7f4c-0107-52b3-b683-38900aa3fb66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What determines the polarity of the embryo?', '["The cortical reaction","The zona pellucida","The site of sperm entry","The location of the polar bodies","The acrosome reaction"]'::jsonb, 'The location of the polar bodies', 'The polar bodies are extruded at the animal pole, and their position marks the axis that establishes the polarity of the developing embryo, determining its craniocaudal and dorsoventral organization. The cortical and acrosome reactions block polyspermy and penetrate the zona, respectively, and do not set the embryonic axis.'),
('e447d086-3245-524a-a8ad-cf00285b027e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is embryonic induction?', '["Interaction between tissues that directs their development","The migration of cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The folding of the neural plate mediated by specific receptor activation","The differentiation of cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The growth of tissues leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interaction between tissues that directs their development', 'Embryonic induction is a signaling interaction in which one tissue (the inducer) influences the developmental fate of an adjacent tissue (the responder) through secreted factors such as SHH, FGF, and BMP. The optic vesicle inducing the lens is the classic example; cell migration, neural folding, differentiation, and growth are separate developmental processes.'),
('04eb85bd-bd55-5842-a59e-0020d55f9635'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the primary inducer of the lens?', '["Optic vesicle","Notochord","Surface ectoderm","Neural crest","Mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Optic vesicle', 'The optic vesicle, an outgrowth of the diencephalon, induces the overlying surface ectoderm to thicken into the lens placode, which then forms the lens vesicle and the lens. The notochord induces the neural plate, whereas neural crest and mesoderm are not the lens-inducing tissues.'),
('7b2b30c1-47c4-5d85-a1c4-b6871e39f934'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is competence in embryology?', '["The ability to differentiate","The ability to proliferate","The ability to migrate","The ability of a tissue to respond to an inductive signal","The ability to induce other tissues"]'::jsonb, 'The ability of a tissue to respond to an inductive signal', 'Competence is the ability of a responding tissue to react to an inductive signal, and a tissue is competent only during a limited time window; if the signal is missed, the developmental opportunity is lost. The ability to deliver the signal belongs to the inducer, while differentiation, proliferation, and migration are separate cellular behaviors.'),
('0d9c44fa-9db9-5aeb-91b6-7c1c8289d21e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is apoptosis in embryology?', '["Programmed cell death that shapes developing structures","Cell migration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uncontrolled cell death mediated by specific receptor activation","Cell differentiation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cell division leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Programmed cell death that shapes developing structures', 'Apoptosis is genetically programmed, orderly cell death that sculpts developing structures, such as separating the digits and forming the foramen ovale, septal defects, and the lumen of tubular organs. It is controlled and energy-dependent, unlike the uncontrolled cell death seen in necrosis after injury.'),
('0580ad25-a4bb-525b-af92-e9fe6b27433e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT)?', '["Cell division resulting in decreased functional activity","Cell migration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conversion of mesenchymal cells to epithelial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Conversion of epithelial cells to migratory mesenchymal cells","Cell death leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conversion of epithelial cells to migratory mesenchymal cells', 'Epithelial-mesenchymal transition is the process by which epithelial cells lose their polarity, cell-cell junctions, and basement membrane attachment and acquire a migratory mesenchymal phenotype. It enables gastrulation cell movements, neural crest emigration, and formation of the heart valves and palate, whereas the reverse change is the mesenchymal-epithelial transition.'),
('ee4d6cd8-b9cf-5ef3-9980-9d34f3477e72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the primitive node (Hensen''s node)?', '["A structure in the limb resulting in decreased functional activity","A part of the neural tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A part of the placenta mediated by specific receptor activation","The cranial end of the primitive streak that organizes gastrulation","A structure in the heart leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The cranial end of the primitive streak that organizes gastrulation', 'Hensen''s node is the mobile thickening at the cranial end of the primitive streak where cells invaginate to form the notochord and prechordal mesoderm, organizing gastrulation and establishing the body axis. The other options misplace it in the limb, neural tube, placenta, or heart.'),
('883e2433-3cd8-52d5-a706-27bc9e9d382c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The prechordal plate is the precursor of which structure?', '["Notochord resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural plate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heart mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mouth and oropharyngeal membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Mouth and oropharyngeal membrane', 'The prechordal plate is a mesodermal thickening cranial to the notochord that helps induce the overlying ectoderm and gives rise to the oropharyngeal membrane at the future mouth. The notochord lies caudal to it and the neural plate is ectoderm induced along the midline, while the heart and liver arise from other germ layers.'),
('e8f7d598-c344-571d-88f2-c9fd101dcee4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The cloacal membrane gives rise to which openings?', '["Oral opening","Nasal openings","Eye openings","Anal and urogenital openings","Ear openings"]'::jsonb, 'Anal and urogenital openings', 'The cloacal membrane is the ectodermal-endodermal contact zone at the caudal end of the embryo that is later subdivided into the urogenital and anal membranes, whose breakdown creates the urogenital and anal openings. Failure of this breakdown causes imperforate anus or urogenital atresia, whereas the oral, nasal, and eye openings form from other membranes.'),
('365e30e7-e5f6-53ec-9ad1-ecd7c9a15223'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the buccopharyngeal membrane?', '["The membrane in the larynx resulting in decreased functional activity","The membrane in the stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The membrane at the cranial end of the foregut that separates the mouth from the pharynx","The membrane in the esophagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The membrane in the trachea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The membrane at the cranial end of the foregut that separates the mouth from the pharynx', 'The buccopharyngeal membrane is the bilaminar zone where the stomodeal ectoderm meets the foregut endoderm at the cranial end of the embryo, separating the stomodeum from the pharynx. It ruptures in the fourth week to open the mouth into the foregut; the other options place a membrane in the larynx, stomach, esophagus, or trachea.'),
('46b9b869-78fa-522a-84a4-31d9ef75d113'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The stomodeum is the precursor of which structure?', '["Mouth and oral cavity","Trachea","Nasal cavity","Esophagus","Pharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Mouth and oral cavity', 'The stomodeum is the ectoderm-lined depression bounded by the frontonasal, maxillary, and mandibular processes that forms the primitive mouth and gives rise to the oral cavity, teeth, and tongue. It is separated from the foregut by the buccopharyngeal membrane until the fourth week, while the nasal cavity forms from the nasal placodes.'),
('a040017f-5950-5d1a-888b-6ac4dd2d9aeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What is the proctodeum?', '["The primitive pharynx","The primitive mouth","The primitive esophagus","The primitive anal pit","The primitive stomach"]'::jsonb, 'The primitive anal pit', 'The proctodeum is the surface-ectoderm-lined depression at the caudal end of the embryo that forms the anal pit and the anal canal below the pectinate line. It fuses with the endodermal hindgut at the anal membrane, whose rupture creates the anal opening.'),
('8e62d4e8-c2a1-5618-b866-988c3c216a9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'The pectinate line of the anal canal represents the junction between which embryonic structures?', '["Endoderm and mesoderm","Foregut and midgut","Proctodeum (ectoderm) and hindgut (endoderm)","Midgut and hindgut","Ectoderm and mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Proctodeum (ectoderm) and hindgut (endoderm)', 'The pectinate (dentate) line marks the junction between the ectodermal proctodeum (below) and the endodermal hindgut (above). This embryologic boundary separates regions that differ in epithelial type, blood supply, innervation, and lymphatic drainage.'),
('22aa1ccd-7ce0-52a7-be3c-5c81f6445967'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What type of epithelium is found above the pectinate line?', '["Pseudostratified columnar","Columnar epithelium (endodermal)","Simple squamous","Transitional epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium (ectodermal)"]'::jsonb, 'Columnar epithelium (endodermal)', 'Above the pectinate line the anal canal is lined by columnar epithelium derived from hindgut endoderm, which has visceral innervation and drains lymph to internal iliac nodes. Stratified squamous epithelium is found below the line, where the canal is ectodermal and somatically innervated.'),
('45db60a8-8e97-5eda-bbfe-368d1692636c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Embryology - Early Development', 'What type of epithelium is found below the pectinate line?', '["Simple squamous","Transitional epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar","Columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium (ectodermal)"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous epithelium (ectodermal)', 'Below the pectinate line the anal canal is lined by stratified squamous epithelium derived from the ectodermal proctodeum, which has somatic innervation and drains lymph to inguinal nodes. The endodermal columnar lining above the line contrasts with this ectodermal squamous lining.'),
('450ed14e-a587-5572-b6e3-96c6fe505bbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The primitive heart tube forms which structure in order from inflow to outflow?', '["Bulbus cordis ventricle atrium sinus venosus truncus arteriosus","Sinus venosus bulbus cordis atrium ventricle truncus arteriosus","Truncus arteriosus bulbus cordis ventricle atrium sinus venosus","Atrium ventricle sinus venosus truncus arteriosus bulbus cordis","Sinus venosus atrium ventricle bulbus cordis truncus arteriosus"]'::jsonb, 'Sinus venosus atrium ventricle bulbus cordis truncus arteriosus', 'From inflow to outflow the heart tube runs sinus venosus, atrium, ventricle, bulbus cordis, truncus arteriosus. Options that begin at the truncus or place the bulbus cordis before the atrium break this directional sequence.'),
('c45a4831-8178-5f5e-87cd-970e61c00453'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the first functional blood vessel in the embryo?', '["Cardinal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Umbilical vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsal aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","Primitive heart tube (beating by day 21-22)","Vitelline vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primitive heart tube (beating by day 21-22)', 'The primitive heart tube begins beating by day 21-22 and is the first functional vascular structure. The cardinal, umbilical, vitelline veins and dorsal aorta form later within the developing vascular network.'),
('5b763a4b-ba7c-59cf-ac2a-31a1d9314d8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The sinus venosus gives rise to which structures?', '["Ascending aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Left ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary trunk mediated by specific receptor activation","Right ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coronary sinus smooth part of right atrium SA node"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary sinus smooth part of right atrium SA node', 'The sinus venosus forms the coronary sinus, the smooth part of the right atrium (sinus venarum) and the SA node. The ventricles, ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk arise from other segments of the heart tube.'),
('0a713c8d-96e6-5795-94b3-0e524070e155'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The primitive atrium gives rise to which structures?', '["Left ventricle resulting in decreased functional activity","Trabeculated parts of both atria and atrial appendages","Right ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth parts of both atria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coronary sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trabeculated parts of both atria and atrial appendages', 'The primitive atrium forms the trabeculated parts of both atria and the atrial appendages. The smooth parts derive from the sinus venosus on the right and incorporation of the pulmonary veins on the left.'),
('02142861-543a-54c2-ac94-6abc889e821b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The primitive ventricle gives rise to which structures?', '["Conus arteriosus","Truncus arteriosus","Left ventricle (trabeculated part)","Right ventricle","Bulbus cordis"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricle (trabeculated part)', 'The primitive ventricle forms the trabeculated left ventricle. The trabeculated right ventricle is derived from the bulbus cordis, while the conus and truncus form the outflow channels.'),
('013b6549-af52-54e7-8dcc-9be4504e998c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The bulbus cordis gives rise to which structures?', '["Ascending aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Right ventricle (trabeculated part)","Left ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary trunk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricle (trabeculated part)', 'The bulbus cordis forms the trabeculated right ventricle. The trabeculated left ventricle comes from the primitive ventricle, and the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk arise from the truncus arteriosus.'),
('e5ccfcdf-ceeb-5484-bf05-d2f76850f14f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The truncus arteriosus gives rise to which structures?', '["Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk","Coronary arteries","Right ventricle","Pulmonary veins","Left ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk', 'The truncus arteriosus is divided by the aorticopulmonary septum into the ascending aorta and the pulmonary trunk. The coronary arteries instead develop from buds at the aortic sinuses (sinuses of Valsalva).'),
('b1c37b5b-64d4-59d2-b8d0-cd2def544ba7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the first sign of cardiac development?', '["Heart tube formation resulting in decreased functional activity","Formation of the valves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heart looping mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiogenic cords forming in the mesoderm at day 18-19","Septation of the heart leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiogenic cords forming in the mesoderm at day 18-19', 'The earliest sign is the appearance of cardiogenic cords in the splanchnic mesoderm around day 18-19. Heart tube formation, looping, septation and valve development all occur later.'),
('1896d92f-ffe7-57b0-aa2f-67a55bdb71cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is cardiac looping?', '["Formation of the coronary arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Formation of the valves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Development of the conduction system mediated by specific receptor activation","Septation of the heart without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The primitive heart tube bends to form an S-shaped loop bringing the chambers into proper alignment"]'::jsonb, 'The primitive heart tube bends to form an S-shaped loop bringing the chambers into proper alignment', 'Cardiac looping is the bending of the heart tube into an S-shaped loop that aligns the future chambers and outflow. Valve formation, septation, coronary development and conduction are later, separate events.'),
('6c0a94cc-184e-5844-9d5a-b95b3b97a0e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is dextrocardia?', '["The heart is enlarged resulting in decreased functional activity","The heart is midline accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The heart is located on the right side of the chest (may be isolated or with situs inversus)","The heart is rotated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The apex points to the right (dextroposition) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The heart is located on the right side of the chest (may be isolated or with situs inversus)', 'Dextrocardia means the heart lies mainly on the right side of the chest, often with situs inversus. Enlargement, midline position or mere rotation of the apex (dextroposition) are not dextrocardia.'),
('dbea1fdc-dd3e-52d5-9b39-bef5375dc8aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What determines the direction of cardiac looping?', '["Left-right axis determination (nodal Lefty Pitx2 signaling)","Neural crest cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primitive streak signaling mediated by specific receptor activation","Notochord induction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood flow dynamics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left-right axis determination (nodal Lefty Pitx2 signaling)', 'Looping direction is set by left-right axis determination mediated by nodal, Lefty and Pitx2 signaling. Neural crest, notochord, primitive streak and blood flow do not determine the loop direction.'),
('2bdf058e-d6cb-55c0-a550-95b6a7b14bfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The atrioventricular (AV) canal divides into right and left AV orifices due to the:', '["Spiral aorticopulmonary septum resulting in decreased functional activity","Bulbar ridges accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Septum primum mediated by specific receptor activation","Endocardial cushions (superior and inferior)","Septum secundum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Endocardial cushions (superior and inferior)', 'The superior and inferior endocardial cushions fuse centrally to divide the AV canal into separate right and left orifices. The spiral septum and bulbar ridges are involved in conotruncal septation instead.'),
('8d4f468e-178d-5d89-a312-e467dce6e79b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The endocardial cushions also contribute to which structures?', '["Coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricular septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrioventricular valves (tricuspid and mitral)","Semilunar valves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrioventricular valves (tricuspid and mitral)', 'In addition to dividing the AV canal, the endocardial cushions help form the atrioventricular (tricuspid and mitral) valves. The semilunar valves arise from conal and truncal swellings.'),
('cc55c2b8-f63e-55d6-9f50-6c02076e7ca4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The atrial septum is formed by which three structures?', '["Septum primum septum secundum and endocardial cushions","Conal ridges accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bulbar ridges mediated by specific receptor activation","Septum secundum only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Septum primum only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Septum primum septum secundum and endocardial cushions', 'The atrial septum is built from the septum primum, the septum secundum and the endocardial cushions. Answers listing only one of the septa or naming conal/bulbar ridges omit the necessary components.'),
('5ba79cb7-20ff-57c9-9b37-abd275d75591'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the foramen ovale?', '["A component of the ventricular septum resulting in decreased functional activity","An opening in the atrial septum that allows right-to-left shunting in fetal life","The opening of the pulmonary veins mediated by specific receptor activation","A defect in the atrial septum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A valve of the coronary sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An opening in the atrial septum that allows right-to-left shunting in fetal life', 'The foramen ovale is a normal fetal channel in the atrial septum permitting right-to-left shunting; its valve-like overlap closes after birth. It is a physiological opening, not a defect, ventricular component or pulmonary vein opening.'),
('5f6dbc20-21f7-5833-9765-c1db2ba2bb0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The ostium primum is:', '["An opening in the upper part of septum primum resulting in decreased functional activity","An opening in the lower part of septum primum that closes as septum primum fuses with endocardial cushions","A defect in the septum secundum mediated by specific receptor activation","The opening of the coronary sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The foramen ovale leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An opening in the lower part of septum primum that closes as septum primum fuses with endocardial cushions', 'The ostium primum is the lower opening of the septum primum, and it closes as the septum primum fuses with the endocardial cushions. The upper part of the septum primum instead develops the ostium secundum.'),
('4cf82d21-e7b9-5b5f-baf0-cc8ced22c8b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The ostium secundum is:', '["An opening that forms in the upper part of septum primum by programmed cell death","The opening of the pulmonary veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A defect in the endocardial cushions mediated by specific receptor activation","An opening in the septum secundum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as foramen ovale leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An opening that forms in the upper part of septum primum by programmed cell death', 'The ostium secundum is created by programmed cell death in the upper part of the septum primum. It is distinct from the foramen ovale and from openings in the septum secundum.'),
('8ac2120c-3d20-500c-9102-b93fc34a38ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the septum secundum?', '["The primary septal structure resulting in decreased functional activity","The ventricular septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as septum primum mediated by specific receptor activation","The aorticopulmonary septum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A muscular crescentic fold that grows from the atrial roof to the right of septum primum"]'::jsonb, 'A muscular crescentic fold that grows from the atrial roof to the right of septum primum', 'The septum secundum is a muscular crescentic fold growing from the atrial roof to the right of the septum primum; it overlaps the ostium secundum to form the foramen ovale. It is a distinct structure, not the ventricular or aorticopulmonary septum.'),
('a078336c-4b70-5b26-9b96-e7829e3495c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The fossa ovalis is:', '["The remnant of the ductus venosus","The remnant of the foramen ovale (the thin floor is septum primum)","The opening of the coronary sinus","The valve of the foramen ovale","A defect in the atrial septum"]'::jsonb, 'The remnant of the foramen ovale (the thin floor is septum primum)', 'The fossa ovalis is the postnatal remnant of the foramen ovale; its thin floor is the septum primum. The ductus venosus remodels into the ligamentum venosum, not the fossa ovalis.'),
('30fd956f-2690-5bcb-aa22-908e81b581ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Ostium secundum atrial septal defect (ASD) results from:', '["Complete absence of the atrial septum","Defect in the coronary sinus","Excessive resorption of septum primum or deficient growth of septum secundum","Failure of sinus venosus incorporation","Failure of septum primum to fuse with endocardial cushions (ostium primum defect)"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive resorption of septum primum or deficient growth of septum secundum', 'An ostium secundum ASD follows excessive resorption of the septum primum or deficient growth of the septum secundum, leaving the foramen ovale functionally open. Failure of cushion fusion with the septum primum instead produces an ostium primum defect.'),
('85146b72-f1f0-5a3e-b11c-f7bb7fbce912'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Ostium primum ASD results from:', '["Abnormal sinus venosus Incorporation","Failure of the endocardial cushions to fuse with septum primum","Coronary sinus defect","Deficient septum secundum","Excessive resorption of septum primum"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the endocardial cushions to fuse with septum primum', 'Ostium primum ASD is caused by failure of the endocardial cushions to fuse with the septum primum, so the ostium primum never closes. Deficient septum secundum or excessive resorption of septum primum instead produce a secundum defect.'),
('074183f6-4101-568a-9585-a98aed03b099'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Sinus venosus ASD is located:', '["At the junction of the right atrium and SVC (superior type) or IVC (inferior type)","In the coronary sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the AV canal mediated by specific receptor activation","At the atrial appendage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the fossa ovalis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the junction of the right atrium and SVC (superior type) or IVC (inferior type)', 'Sinus venosus ASDs sit at the junction of the right atrium with the SVC (superior type) or the IVC (inferior type), often with anomalous pulmonary venous drainage. They do not involve the fossa ovalis or the AV canal.'),
('43746ff6-ca0d-5d4c-a21b-e3e58af3ca1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common type of ASD?', '["Secundum ASD (fossa ovalis)","Coronary sinus ASD","Sinus venosus ASD","Single atrium","Primum ASD"]'::jsonb, 'Secundum ASD (fossa ovalis)', 'The secundum (fossa ovalis) ASD is the most common type, accounting for roughly 70% of cases. Primum, sinus venosus and coronary sinus types are all much rarer.'),
('0cd597d4-39f9-5155-939c-2c1fd4e7350d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The ventricular septum is formed by which components?', '["aorticopulmonary septum resulting in decreased functional activity","Bulbar ridges accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscular (inflow) septum and membranous (outflow) septum","AV cushions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular (inflow) septum and membranous (outflow) septum', 'The interventricular septum has a muscular (inflow) part and a membranous (outflow) part. The muscular portion arises from the fused medial ventricular walls, while the membranous portion is completed by the endocardial cushions and conal ridges.'),
('528c55a7-8cef-517f-b38f-2f23e7f1f6a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The muscular interventricular septum develops from:', '["The truncus arteriosus resulting in decreased functional activity","Downgrowth from the atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The fused medial walls of the expanding ventricles","The endocardial cushions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The conus cordis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fused medial walls of the expanding ventricles', 'The muscular interventricular septum develops from the fused medial walls of the expanding ventricles. The membranous part forms later from endocardial cushions and conal bulbar ridges.'),
('727828b3-470d-58f9-ba2a-348862156a6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The membranous interventricular septum develops from:', '["The AV canal resulting in decreased functional activity","The muscular septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The endocardial cushions and the conus ridges","The atrial septum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The truncus arteriosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The endocardial cushions and the conus ridges', 'The membranous interventricular septum is formed by contributions of the endocardial cushions and the conus ridges as they close the interventricular foramen. The muscular septum develops separately from the fused ventricular walls.'),
('4c104c2f-80be-538e-9810-f857690e9faf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'A membranous VSD results from:', '["Abnormal atrial septation resulting in decreased functional activity","Deficient muscular septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Failure of fusion of the endocardial cushions and bulbar ridges to close the interventricular foramen","AV canal defects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conotruncal abnormalities leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of fusion of the endocardial cushions and bulbar ridges to close the interventricular foramen', 'A membranous VSD arises when the endocardial cushions and bulbar ridges fail to fuse and close the interventricular foramen. Defects of the muscular septum are a separate entity produced by failure of muscular septal growth.'),
('7d93da0b-ae1a-5f26-aa02-2a952e996cea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'A muscular VSD results from:', '["Atrial septal defect resulting in decreased functional activity","Defect in the membranous septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excessive apoptosis or failure of growth of the muscular septum","AV cushion abnormality without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conotruncal abnormality leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive apoptosis or failure of growth of the muscular septum', 'A muscular VSD follows excessive apoptosis or failure of growth of the muscular septum, leaving a hole in the trabecular part of the septum. A membranous defect, in comparison, results from failure of the cushions and bulbar ridges to close the interventricular foramen.'),
('9a30c56f-0ce1-5e51-8d15-92065598bce3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The most common type of VSD is:', '["Outlet (supracristal)","Perimembranous (membranous)","Gerbode defect (LV-RA communication)","Muscular (trabecular)","Inlet (AV canal type)"]'::jsonb, 'Perimembranous (membranous)', 'The perimembranous (membranous) VSD is the most common type, accounting for about 70-80% of cases. Muscular, outlet and inlet types are all less frequent.'),
('17b6e1ec-a911-5645-bf98-10077c5f3342'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the conus cordis?', '["The primitive atrium resulting in decreased functional activity","The outflow tract of the heart (conus arteriosus) which forms the infundibulum of the right ventricle and the aortic vestibule","The truncus arteriosus mediated by specific receptor activation","The sinus venosus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inflow tract of the heart leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The outflow tract of the heart (conus arteriosus) which forms the infundibulum of the right ventricle and the aortic vestibule', 'The conus cordis is the outflow (conal) tract of the heart; it forms the infundibulum of the right ventricle and the aortic vestibule. The sinus venosus and primitive atrium are inflow structures, and the truncus lies further downstream.'),
('c21253ea-a5c7-5306-bba5-d59f4ca34dc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The aorticopulmonary septum divides the truncus arteriosus into:', '["Vena cavae","Right and left pulmonary arteries","Pulmonary veins","Coronary arteries","Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk', 'The aorticopulmonary septum spirals through the truncus arteriosus to separate it into the ascending aorta and the pulmonary trunk. The vena cavae, pulmonary veins and coronary arteries are not products of this septation.'),
('2422d19b-c53c-5a52-96ea-73169b292825'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Neural crest cells contribute to the formation of the:', '["Aorticopulmonary septum and conotruncus","Ventricular septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrial septum mediated by specific receptor activation","Conduction system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AV valves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aorticopulmonary septum and conotruncus', 'Cardiac neural crest cells contribute to the aorticopulmonary septum and the conotruncus, which explains the association of outflow defects with neural crest anomalies. The AV valves, atrial and ventricular septa arise from other tissues.'),
('1bc60dab-44c2-596d-b227-f6590699b145'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Failure of neural crest migration to the heart causes:', '["VSD resulting in decreased functional activity","Valvular anomalies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conotruncal anomalies (tetralogy of Fallot truncus arteriosus D-transposition)","ASD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AV canal defect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conotruncal anomalies (tetralogy of Fallot truncus arteriosus D-transposition)', 'Because neural crest cells build the aorticopulmonary septum and conotruncus, their failed migration produces conotruncal anomalies such as tetralogy of Fallot, truncus arteriosus and D-transposition. Isolated ASD, VSD or AV canal defects are not the primary result.'),
('10762a19-d5ae-5a98-b6e1-7b5c211973d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion) associated with?', '["PDA resulting in decreased functional activity","VSD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASD mediated by specific receptor activation","Conotruncal cardiac anomalies (tetralogy of Fallot truncus arteriosus interrupted aortic arch type B)","Coarctation of the aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conotruncal cardiac anomalies (tetralogy of Fallot truncus arteriosus interrupted aortic arch type B)', 'DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion) is associated with conotruncal cardiac anomalies including tetralogy of Fallot, truncus arteriosus and interrupted aortic arch type B. Simple ASD, PDA or coarctation are not its characteristic lesions.'),
('56d295eb-86cd-5fbd-bb8c-64e11fe7ec29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The coronary arteries develop from which structure?', '["The sinus venosus resulting in decreased functional activity","The pulmonary veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bulbus cordis mediated by specific receptor activation","The truncus arteriosus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Angioblastic buds from the sinuses of Valsalva (aortic sinuses)"]'::jsonb, 'Angioblastic buds from the sinuses of Valsalva (aortic sinuses)', 'The coronary arteries arise from angioblastic buds at the sinuses of Valsalva (aortic sinuses) that grow into the myocardium and connect with its capillary network. They do not derive from the truncus, bulbus or sinus venosus.'),
('d6c73178-7176-5e42-a6e5-10211fe4fc91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The coronary sinus develops from which structure?', '["Right horn of sinus venosus","Umbilical vein","Right common cardinal vein","Left common cardinal vein","Left horn of sinus venosus"]'::jsonb, 'Left horn of sinus venosus', 'The coronary sinus is derived from the left horn of the sinus venosus. The right horn of the sinus venosus forms the smooth part of the right atrium (sinus venarum).'),
('9ed10c23-be23-51dd-8cfd-c9cf9a4d6895'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The oblique vein of the left atrium (Marshall) is a remnant of which structure?', '["Left superior vena cava (left common cardinal vein)","Ductus venosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right common cardinal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Umbilical vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right sinus venosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left superior vena cava (left common cardinal vein)', 'The oblique vein of Marshall is the remnant of the left superior vena cava (left common cardinal vein). The ductus venosus and umbilical vein leave different remnants (ligamentum venosum and round ligament).'),
('2405afc1-8aa3-57fa-b880-5d35d67ba292'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'If the left common cardinal vein persists it forms:', '["Persistent left superior vena cava (draining into coronary sinus)","Absent SVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Persistent right superior vena cava mediated by specific receptor activation","Double IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coronary sinus atresia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent left superior vena cava (draining into coronary sinus)', 'Persistence of the left common cardinal vein produces a persistent left superior vena cava, which typically drains into the right atrium via the coronary sinus. This is usually a clinically silent anomaly.'),
('77cae9aa-a68e-520d-ac78-bfe5b8776eb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The pulmonary veins develop from which structure?', '["Bulbus cordis resulting in decreased functional activity","An outgrowth from the posterior atrial wall (common pulmonary vein)","The sinus venosus mediated by specific receptor activation","Ductus arteriosus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The truncus arteriosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An outgrowth from the posterior atrial wall (common pulmonary vein)', 'The pulmonary veins develop from an outgrowth of the posterior wall of the primitive atrium (the common pulmonary vein) that joins the pulmonary venous plexus and is later incorporated into the left atrium. The sinus venosus and truncus arteriosus are not involved.'),
('b22c7726-c854-56da-9a3c-db4923fb1c37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR) results from:', '["Abnormal truncus division resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary valve stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abnormal development of the sinus venosus mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal atrial septation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Failure of the common pulmonary vein to connect with the pulmonary venous plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the common pulmonary vein to connect with the pulmonary venous plexus', 'TAPVR results from failure of the common pulmonary vein to connect with the pulmonary venous plexus, so pulmonary venous blood drains instead into systemic veins or the right atrium. A functional ASD is usually required for survival in this lesion.'),
('8741c274-e5c9-54e5-a99a-ae421c4be064'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is cor triatriatum?', '["Triple atria resulting in decreased functional activity","A three-chambered heart accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary sinus anomaly mediated by specific receptor activation","The common pulmonary vein fails to incorporate into the left atrium leaving a divided left atrium with a membrane","Absence of the left atrium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The common pulmonary vein fails to incorporate into the left atrium leaving a divided left atrium with a membrane', 'Cor triatriatum occurs when the common pulmonary vein fails to incorporate into the left atrium, leaving a membrane that divides the left atrium into an accessory and a true chamber. It is not a third atrium or a three-chambered heart.'),
('2a13ea18-531d-5750-8165-b6c95fe6177e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Partial anomalous pulmonary venous return (PAPVR) involves:', '["All pulmonary veins drain anomalously resulting in decreased functional activity","One or more (but not all) pulmonary veins drain into the right atrium or SVC","Normal pulmonary venous drainage mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary vein atresia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary vein stenosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'One or more (but not all) pulmonary veins drain into the right atrium or SVC', 'PAPVR means that one or more, but not all, pulmonary veins drain into the right atrium or SVC, often with a sinus venosus ASD. Drainage of all veins anomalously defines TAPVR.'),
('1f0ec2e0-7733-53ba-9c41-402952f9f8f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The conduction system of the heart (SA node AV node) develops from which structure?', '["Endocardial cushions resulting in decreased functional activity","Myocardial cells of the sinus venosus and AV canal region","Neural crest cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Primitive ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac neural crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial cells of the sinus venosus and AV canal region', 'The SA node and AV node develop from myocardial cells specialized within the sinus venosus and AV canal region. They do not arise from neural crest cells or the endocardial cushions.'),
('8587d9de-685d-5992-b9ad-1ac4c8987d73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The SA node is located at the junction of which structures?', '["SVC and right atrium (crista terminalis)","IVC and right atrium","Pulmonary veins and left atrium","SVC and left atrium","Aorta and right atrium"]'::jsonb, 'SVC and right atrium (crista terminalis)', 'The SA node lies at the junction of the superior vena cava with the right atrium, along the crista terminalis. The IVC-right atrium junction and left atrial sites do not host the SA node.'),
('9261f859-0d99-56ba-876c-47edcb5458b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The AV node is located where?', '["In the interventricular septum","In the right atrial appendage","At the SA node","In the coronary sinus","In the interatrial septum (Triangle of Koch)"]'::jsonb, 'In the interatrial septum (Triangle of Koch)', 'The AV node sits in the inferior interatrial septum within the Triangle of Koch, bounded by the septal tricuspid leaflet, coronary sinus orifice and tendon of Todaro. It is not in the interventricular septum, appendage or at the SA node.'),
('ac848191-da14-5300-b753-ed2654975826'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Fetal circulation is characterized by what three shunts?', '["Foramen ovale umbilical artery ductus arteriosus","Foramen ovale ductus arteriosus ductus venosus","Ductus arteriosus ductus venosus umbilical vein","ASD VSD PDA","Foramen ovale PDA VSD"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen ovale ductus arteriosus ductus venosus', 'The three fetal shunts are the foramen ovale (right to left atrium), the ductus arteriosus (pulmonary artery to aorta) and the ductus venosus (umbilical vein to IVC). The umbilical artery and vein are vessels, not shunts.'),
('9827c03b-696e-55eb-9b72-66eac5c0d35f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The ductus venosus connects which structures?', '["IVC to right atrium resulting in decreased functional activity","Umbilical vein to inferior vena cava (bypassing the liver)","Umbilical artery to aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","Portal vein to IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary artery to aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Umbilical vein to inferior vena cava (bypassing the liver)', 'The ductus venosus connects the umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava, bypassing the hepatic sinusoids. After birth it becomes the ligamentum venosum. The pulmonary artery-to-aorta connection is the ductus arteriosus.'),
('7b682802-d803-5748-8098-5884b78fdaa9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The ductus arteriosus connects which structures?', '["Aorta to vena cava resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary artery to descending aorta (distal to left subclavian)","Pulmonary vein to left atrium mediated by specific receptor activation","Right ventricle to left ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary artery to ascending aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary artery to descending aorta (distal to left subclavian)', 'The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta distal to the left subclavian artery and closes to form the ligamentum arteriosum. The umbilical-to-IVC connection is the ductus venosus.'),
('57ab4235-abba-5ca9-847a-b9f179d3bb91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'The foramen ovale allows blood to flow from:', '["Left atrium to right atrium","Right ventricle to left ventricle","Right atrium to left atrium (bypassing the lungs)","Pulmonary vein to left atrium","Superior vena cava to right atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium to left atrium (bypassing the lungs)', 'Because right atrial pressure exceeds left atrial pressure in utero, the foramen ovale shunts blood from the right atrium to the left atrium, bypassing the lungs. Flow in the reverse direction is pathological.'),
('7a9b6650-56d8-5fc1-8c1c-204f08986d90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most oxygenated blood vessel in the fetus?', '["Fetal aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Ductus venosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Umbilical vein (oxygen saturation about 80%)","Ductus arteriosus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Umbilical artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Umbilical vein (oxygen saturation about 80%)', 'The umbilical vein carries freshly oxygenated blood from the placenta and is the most oxygenated vessel in the fetus, with a saturation of about 80%. The ductus venosus and aorta carry blood that has already been diluted by hepatic and systemic mixing.'),
('7112572e-acd5-5e4c-aed8-9b9e4bbf095e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What triggers the closure of the ductus arteriosus after birth?', '["Increased pulmonary vascular resistance","Closure of the foramen ovale","Increased oxygen tension (PaO2) and decreased prostaglandin E2","Decreased left atrial pressure","Decreased oxygen tension"]'::jsonb, 'Increased oxygen tension (PaO2) and decreased prostaglandin E2', 'At birth the abrupt rise in arterial oxygen tension constricts ductal smooth muscle while the drop in circulating prostaglandin E2 removes its main relaxing drive. The combined effect closes the ductus; options implying falling oxygen or rising pulmonary resistance would instead tend to keep it patent.'),
('adfb2104-cecd-584b-a9a6-6cfaf5bcf77a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the functional remnant of the ductus arteriosus?', '["Medial umbilical ligament","Median umbilical ligament","Ligamentum teres hepatis","Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum venosum"]'::jsonb, 'Ligamentum arteriosum', 'The ductus arteriosus obliterates after birth into a fibrous band, the ligamentum arteriosum, connecting the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta. The other ligaments are remnants of the umbilical vessels and the ductus venosus.'),
('bbb63b02-81c2-556e-ba83-d83f1d91fbb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the functional remnant of the ductus venosus?', '["Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum venosum","Round ligament of the uterus","Ligamentum teres hepatis"]'::jsonb, 'Ligamentum venosum', 'The ductus venosus, which shunted umbilical venous blood past the liver in fetal life, closes after birth to become the ligamentum venosum on the inferior surface of the liver. The remaining options are remnants of the ductus arteriosus and umbilical vessels.'),
('bcf9dc45-77c5-5f51-8b16-411b9685763b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the functional remnant of the umbilical vein?', '["Falciform ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial umbilical ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ligamentum venosum mediated by specific receptor activation","Ligamentum teres hepatis (round ligament of the liver)","Median umbilical ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ligamentum teres hepatis (round ligament of the liver)', 'The left umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres (round ligament of the liver) running in the free edge of the falciform ligament to the porta hepatis. The medial umbilical ligaments arise from the obliterated umbilical arteries and the ligamentum venosum from the ductus venosus.'),
('15c89758-1974-5038-a0a6-2b448e44891d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the functional remnant of the umbilical arteries?', '["Ligamentum arteriosum","Ligamentum teres","Median umbilical ligament","Ligamentum venosum","Medial umbilical ligaments"]'::jsonb, 'Medial umbilical ligaments', 'The distal portions of the umbilical arteries obliterate into the medial umbilical ligaments passing along the lateral walls of the anterior abdominal wall to the umbilicus. The median umbilical ligament is the urachal remnant, and the other choices belong to different fetal vessels.'),
('c9291b0e-ab90-59f2-9783-9f01bfe0f381'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the functional remnant of the foramen ovale?', '["Thebesian valve","Coronary sinus","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum arteriosum","Eustachian valve"]'::jsonb, 'Fossa ovalis', 'After birth the septum primum flap fuses onto the septum secundum, sealing the foramen ovale; its right atrial depression is the fossa ovalis. The Eustachian and Thebesian valves are sinus-venosus-derived structures, and the ligamentum arteriosum is the ductal remnant.'),
('c41db9f2-fdb9-53c1-9120-2a8c1f753554'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Eustachian valve?', '["The valve of the coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","The valve of the foramen ovale accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The thebesian valve mediated by specific receptor activation","The valve of the SVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The valve of the IVC derived from the right valve of the sinus venosus"]'::jsonb, 'The valve of the IVC derived from the right valve of the sinus venosus', 'The Eustachian valve is an embryologic remnant of the right valve of the sinus venosus that forms a fold guarding the orifice of the inferior vena cava. The Thebesian valve, a separate sinus-venosus remnant, guards the coronary sinus instead.'),
('4caae1cd-63f8-59a8-b709-1348e5c39078'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Thebesian valve?', '["The valve of the SVC resulting in decreased functional activity","The valve of the IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The valve of the pulmonary vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The valve of the foramen ovale without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The valve of the coronary sinus (derived from the right sinus horn valve)"]'::jsonb, 'The valve of the coronary sinus (derived from the right sinus horn valve)', 'The Thebesian valve is a small endocardial fold at the entrance of the coronary sinus into the right atrium, derived from the right sinus horn valve. It is distinct from the Eustachian valve of the IVC and bears no relation to the SVC, pulmonary veins, or foramen ovale.'),
('7dcf0fdb-ce2f-578f-958b-c5bdd05f55df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Chiari network?', '["A remnant of the right valve of the sinus venosus appearing as a reticular structure in the right atrium","An atrial septal aneurysm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An abnormal valve of the IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","A venous anomaly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Part of the conduction system leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A remnant of the right valve of the sinus venosus appearing as a reticular structure in the right atrium', 'The Chiari network is a fenestrated, web-like remnant of the right valve of the sinus venosus seen in the right atrium near the IVC and coronary sinus orifices. It is usually incidental and harmless, with no valvular or conduction function.'),
('1c59b881-9839-570c-8b7b-adb7f74ddf63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital heart defect overall?', '["Atrial septal defect (ASD)","Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)","Tetralogy of Fallot","Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","Pulmonary stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular septal defect (VSD)', 'Ventricular septal defect accounts for roughly a quarter of all congenital heart defects and is the commonest lesion overall, though many small defects close spontaneously. It produces a left-to-right shunt usually heard as a pansystolic murmur at the left sternal edge.'),
('cb122318-c13a-5a12-ba77-e9a9599f7091'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the second most common congenital heart defect?', '["Tetralogy of Fallot","Atrial septal defect (ASD)","PDA","VSD","Pulmonary stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial septal defect (ASD)', 'Secundum atrial septal defect is the second most frequent congenital heart lesion and the commonest defect recognized in adults. The left-to-right shunt gives a fixed split S2 and a soft pulmonary ejection murmur.'),
('dc89a07c-f798-5400-b2b2-aa18b0d0f5dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cyanotic congenital heart defect?', '["Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)","Total anomalous pulmonary venous return","Tricuspid atresia","Truncus arteriosus","Transposition of the great arteries (TGA)"]'::jsonb, 'Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)', 'Tetralogy of Fallot is the most frequently encountered cyanotic congenital heart defect, classically causing early cyanosis, hypoxia spells, and squatting. The other options are less common cyanotic lesions.'),
('60e90ca1-893e-5c65-bb46-35190fab1654'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital heart defect in Down syndrome?', '["PDA resulting in decreased functional activity","ASD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VSD mediated by specific receptor activation","Tetralogy of Fallot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrioventricular septal defect (AVSD complete AV canal)"]'::jsonb, 'Atrioventricular septal defect (AVSD complete AV canal)', 'Complete atrioventricular septal defect (AV canal) is the characteristic cardiac lesion of Down syndrome, seen in a substantial proportion of affected neonates. The other defects occur less consistently in trisomy 21.'),
('cb5e5188-16be-5c75-a125-4847a892dccc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital heart defect in Turner syndrome?', '["ASD resulting in decreased functional activity","VSD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bicuspid aortic valve mediated by specific receptor activation","Coarctation of the aorta (especially preductal)","PDA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coarctation of the aorta (especially preductal)', 'Coarctation of the aorta, often in the preductal (infantile) form, is the traditionally cited commonest cardiac lesion in Turner syndrome, and bicuspid aortic valve frequently coexists. Its presence accounts for the lower-limb hypertension and reduced femoral pulses.'),
('c54168a3-2a2b-596d-a8cf-9a8893c8b9aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital heart defect in Noonan syndrome?', '["VSD resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary valvular stenosis with dysplastic valve","ASD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coarctation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary valvular stenosis with dysplastic valve', 'Noonan syndrome characteristically causes pulmonary valvular stenosis with a thick, dysplastic valve that does not respond well to simple balloon valvotomy. The dysplastic morphology distinguishes it from the more common fused-commissure form of pulmonary stenosis.'),
('3abfd150-26d6-59dc-8983-9f40058785ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital heart defect in Williams syndrome?', '["Supravalvular aortic stenosis","PDA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASD mediated by specific receptor activation","VSD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tetralogy of Fallot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supravalvular aortic stenosis', 'Williams syndrome, caused by a deletion at 7q11.23 involving the elastin gene, classically produces supravalvular aortic stenosis with the elfin facial appearance and hypercalcemia. The other listed defects are not hallmarks of this syndrome.'),
('7c01fe0d-7110-56e7-82c5-eb1e7f9fc8f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital heart defect in Marfan syndrome?', '["Coarctation resulting in decreased functional activity","Mitral valve prolapse (MVP) and aortic root dilation","ASD mediated by specific receptor activation","VSD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PDA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral valve prolapse (MVP) and aortic root dilation', 'Mutation of the fibrillin-1 gene weakens connective tissue, so Marfan syndrome manifests as progressive aortic root dilation and mitral valve prolapse. Septal defects and patent ductus arteriosus are not characteristic of the syndrome.'),
('8084c7f4-3a0f-519b-a646-223246b0f672'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital heart defect in CHARGE syndrome?', '["Tetralogy of Fallot (most common)","PDA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASD mediated by specific receptor activation","VSD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Truncus arteriosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tetralogy of Fallot (most common)', 'Tetralogy of Fallot is the commonest structural heart lesion in CHARGE syndrome, although a broad spectrum of conotruncal defects can occur. The other options are seen less frequently in this association.'),
('b99ad9d7-efc7-5f73-87bb-a364258d3ec7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Tetralogy of Fallot consists of which four features?', '["VSD ASD overriding aorta pulmonary stenosis","VSD overriding aorta LV hypertrophy pulmonary stenosis","VSD overriding aorta RVH pulmonary atresia","VSD ASD PDA RV hypertrophy","VSD overriding aorta RV hypertrophy pulmonary stenosis (infundibular)"]'::jsonb, 'VSD overriding aorta RV hypertrophy pulmonary stenosis (infundibular)', 'The four elements are a large VSD, an aorta overriding the septal defect, right ventricular hypertrophy, and infundibular (right ventricular outflow) pulmonary stenosis. The other combinations either omit the obligatory VSD, substitute an ASD or PDA, or cite the wrong ventricular hypertrophy.'),
('2f816bfe-f0fa-5ee9-9af0-11628efac481'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What determines the severity of cyanosis in tetralogy of Fallot?', '["The degree of RV hypertrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","The presence of PDA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The degree of overriding aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","The degree of pulmonary stenosis (RV outflow obstruction)","The size of the VSD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The degree of pulmonary stenosis (RV outflow obstruction)', 'In TOF the amount of cyanosis parallels the degree of right ventricular outflow obstruction: increasing stenosis forces more deoxygenated blood through the VSD into the overriding aorta. The VSD itself is large and unrestrictive, so its size is not the limiting factor.'),
('ccf8afdd-3117-594e-9b96-3d759b20f137'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is a hypercyanotic (tet) spell in TOF?', '["Pulmonary edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Arrhythmia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mitral regurgitation mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A paroxysm of severe cyanosis due to infundibular spasm increasing right-to-left shunt"]'::jsonb, 'A paroxysm of severe cyanosis due to infundibular spasm increasing right-to-left shunt', 'A tet spell is an acute, potentially life-threatening bout of profound cyanosis triggered by spasm of the infundibular muscle, which acutely increases the right-to-left shunt and cuts pulmonary blood flow. It is a dynamic obstructive event rather than a form of heart failure, edema, or arrhythmia.'),
('ef69ee22-089d-50e0-8867-19ee1a00fe63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'How is a tet spell managed acutely?', '["Cardioversion resulting in decreased functional activity","Surgery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vasopressors mediated by specific receptor activation","Furosemide and digoxin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Knee-chest position oxygen morphine and beta-blockers (propranolol)"]'::jsonb, 'Knee-chest position oxygen morphine and beta-blockers (propranolol)', 'The knee-chest position raises systemic vascular resistance, while oxygen, morphine, and beta-blockade relieve infundibular spasm and promote pulmonary flow. Diuretics, vasopressors, and cardioversion do not address the underlying outflow obstruction.'),
('395b0d1b-4e78-5971-9377-3beb4c8f676a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Blalock-Taussig shunt?', '["Subclavian artery to pulmonary artery anastomosis to increase pulmonary blood flow in TOF","VSD closure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASD creation mediated by specific receptor activation","Connection of the aorta to the pulmonary artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary valve dilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery to pulmonary artery anastomosis to increase pulmonary blood flow in TOF', 'The Blalock-Taussig shunt connects a subclavian artery to the ipsilateral pulmonary artery, augmenting pulmonary blood flow as initial palliation in cyanotic lesions such as TOF. The other options describe corrective procedures or unlisted interventions.'),
('625b1138-6425-5eac-a788-837ea8351fb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the definitive repair for tetralogy of Fallot?', '["Pulmonary valve replacement resulting in decreased functional activity","VSD closure and relief of RV outflow obstruction (infundibular resection ± transannular patch)","BT shunt alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart transplant without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fontan procedure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VSD closure and relief of RV outflow obstruction (infundibular resection ± transannular patch)', 'Definitive correction of TOF closes the VSD and relieves right ventricular outflow obstruction by infundibular resection, with or without a transannular patch. Palliative shunts are temporizing, and valve replacement, transplantation, or the Fontan pathway apply to other conditions.'),
('6670520d-1e03-5053-966f-fa1f93c51ad8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is transposition of the great arteries (D-TGA)?', '["The aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle","The great arteries are normally related but transposed","The aorta and pulmonary artery are stenotic","Both great arteries arise from the left ventricle","Both great arteries arise from the right ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'The aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle', 'In D-TGA the aorta arises from the morphologic right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk from the morphologic left ventricle, creating two parallel circulations. Survival depends on obligatory mixing through an ASD, VSD, or PDA.'),
('1618af8b-e50a-5b72-a849-fa902e5d6619'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the essential feature for survival in D-TGA?', '["Increased pulmonary blood flow resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased pulmonary blood flow accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Mixing of the two circulations through an ASD VSD or PDA","Aortic stenosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixing of the two circulations through an ASD VSD or PDA', 'Because the systemic and pulmonary circuits run in parallel in D-TGA, oxygenated blood reaches the body only through mixing at an ASD, VSD, or PDA. Options that simply alter pulmonary flow or pressure do not achieve this essential mixing.'),
('c1e96df5-eeb0-593f-936a-a45daa128309'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Rashkind procedure (balloon atrial septostomy)?', '["Arterial switch resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary artery banding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enlarging the foramen ovale/ASD to improve mixing in D-TGA","VSD closure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PDA ligation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enlarging the foramen ovale/ASD to improve mixing in D-TGA', 'Rashkind balloon atrial septostomy tears open the foramen ovale or enlarges an existing ASD at the cardiac catheterization stage, immediately improving intercirculatory mixing in D-TGA. The other choices are different operations aimed at other lesions.'),
('a0ca5c4c-94e4-53b3-828f-56c3d7d39808'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the arterial switch operation (Jatene procedure)?', '["VSD closure resulting in decreased functional activity","Transecting and reanastomosing the aorta and pulmonary artery to their correct ventricles with coronary reimplantation","Atrial switch (Mustard/Senning) mediated by specific receptor activation","Rastelli procedure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nikaidoh procedure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transecting and reanastomosing the aorta and pulmonary artery to their correct ventricles with coronary reimplantation', 'The Jatene arterial switch transects the great arteries, re-anastomoses each to its proper ventricle, and transfers the coronary ostia to the neo-aorta. It is the definitive neonatal repair for D-TGA and supersedes the older atrial switch (Mustard/Senning) procedures.'),
('580ef232-bce9-58f2-bc92-32be14284ec0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is congenitally corrected transposition (L-TGA)?', '["Atrioventricular and ventriculoarterial discordance (physiologically corrected)","Truncus arteriosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Double outlet right ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete D-TGA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Isolated ventricular inversion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrioventricular and ventriculoarterial discordance (physiologically corrected)', 'In L-TGA both the atrioventricular and ventriculoarterial connections are discordant, so although the ventricles are inverted, systemic and pulmonary flows remain physiologically normal. This distinguishes it from D-TGA in which the great-artery connection alone is discordant.'),
('6e9d9d97-1a78-5a27-addc-7241c690b9c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common associated anomaly in L-TGA?', '["Patent foramen ovale resulting in decreased functional activity","PDA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VSD (about 70%) and pulmonary stenosis","ASD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coarctation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VSD (about 70%) and pulmonary stenosis', 'A ventricular septal defect, present in about 70% of patients and often accompanied by pulmonary stenosis, is the commonest associated lesion in L-TGA. The other options are uncommon associations.'),
('00516e50-6094-5ea7-995c-078802abb106'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is truncus arteriosus?', '["Two separate great arteries resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A single vessel (truncus) that gives rise to the aorta pulmonary arteries and coronary arteries due to failure of aorticopulmonary septation","Aortic atresia mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Absent pulmonary artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary atresia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A single vessel (truncus) that gives rise to the aorta pulmonary arteries and coronary arteries due to failure of aorticopulmonary septation', 'Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to develop leaves a single great artery carrying both ventricles via an obligatory VSD, from which the aorta, pulmonary arteries, and coronaries arise. The resulting torrential pulmonary flow causes early heart failure and cyanotic physiology.'),
('52e720b8-1487-5e7c-9269-ce8cd3c1b58b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Truncus arteriosus is classified into which types by Collett and Edwards?', '["Complete and incomplete resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Type 1 2 3 4 5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple and complex mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Type I (common trunk gives PA then branches) Type II (separate PA origins from trunk) Type III (one PA absent) Type IV (truncus gives only the aorta)","Type A1 A2 A3 A4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (common trunk gives PA then branches) Type II (separate PA origins from trunk) Type III (one PA absent) Type IV (truncus gives only the aorta)', 'Collett and Edwards divided truncus arteriosus into four types according to how the pulmonary arteries arise from the common trunk: a shared main PA (I), separate origins (II), a single absent PA (III), and only the aorta given off (IV, now regarded as a form of pulmonary atresia/VSD). The Roman I-IV scheme does not use the A1-A4 letters of the Van Praagh system.'),
('355b9988-7e7c-5739-82b3-418769e366a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Van Praagh classification of truncus arteriosus?', '["Types 1-4 with subtyping resulting in decreased functional activity","Same as Collett and Edwards accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type A1 (septum present) A2 (absent septum) A3 (one PA absent) A4 (isthmus hypoplasia/arch interruption)","Types AB C without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Types I-V leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type A1 (septum present) A2 (absent septum) A3 (one PA absent) A4 (isthmus hypoplasia/arch interruption)', 'The Van Praagh system labels the forms of truncus arteriosus with a VSD as type A, then subdivides: A1 has an intact septal remnant with a main PA, A2 lacks the septum with separate PA origins, A3 has a single absent PA, and A4 includes isthmus hypoplasia or interrupted aortic arch. The roman-numeral scheme belongs to the Collett and Edwards classification.'),
('61de4e2a-cf1e-5a82-b1ff-323bc609e55a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of death in untreated truncus arteriosus?', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Congestive heart failure from high pulmonary blood flow and eventual pulmonary vascular disease","Rupture of the truncus mediated by specific receptor activation","Arrhythmia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cyanosis from decreased pulmonary flow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congestive heart failure from high pulmonary blood flow and eventual pulmonary vascular disease', 'The common truncus lies at systemic pressure so pulmonary flow is torrential, producing early congestive heart failure and, untreated, progressive pulmonary vascular obstructive disease. Infection, rupture, and arrhythmia are far less common terminal mechanisms.'),
('95aa0e49-a176-55f8-8884-86798cdc8878'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR)?', '["Pulmonary veins drain into the left atrium","All pulmonary veins drain into the right atrium or systemic veins","Some pulmonary veins drain anomalously","Pulmonary vein stenosis","Pulmonary vein atresia"]'::jsonb, 'All pulmonary veins drain into the right atrium or systemic veins', 'In TAPVR all pulmonary venous blood drains into the right atrium or a systemic vein, and survival depends on an obligatory right-to-left interatrial shunt through an ASD/PFO. The remaining options describe partial or stenotic pulmonary venous pathology.'),
('ca0f2ef3-4194-5711-8d76-97042971550c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common type of TAPVR?', '["Infracardiac (to portal vein ductus venosus IVC) resulting in decreased functional activity","Supracardiac (to a left vertical vein innominate vein or SVC)","Mixed (combination) mediated by specific receptor activation","Scimitar syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac (to coronary sinus) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supracardiac (to a left vertical vein innominate vein or SVC)', 'Supracardiac drainage, through a left vertical vein to the innominate vein or SVC, is the commonest anatomical pattern of TAPVR. Infracardiac, cardiac (coronary sinus), and mixed forms are encountered less often.'),
('33930a08-c280-5967-a55a-cc0f8a8449bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is scimitar syndrome?', '["Scimitar-shaped left atrium resulting in decreased functional activity","Scimitar-shaped heart accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TAPVR to the SVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Anomalous left pulmonary vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Partial anomalous pulmonary venous return of the right lung to the IVC (scimitar sign) with right lung hypoplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Partial anomalous pulmonary venous return of the right lung to the IVC (scimitar sign) with right lung hypoplasia', 'Scimitar syndrome is a partial anomalous pulmonary venous connection of the right lung to the inferior vena cava, producing the curved ''scimitar'' shadow on chest radiography along with right lung hypoplasia and dextroposed heart. It is a form of PAPVR, not total anomalous drainage to the SVC.'),
('77f4c733-a909-50ef-95f0-12ebf67a8e69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is hypoplastic left heart syndrome (HLHS)?', '["Small left atrium only resulting in decreased functional activity","Underdevelopment of the left heart with mitral atresia or stenosis aortic atresia or stenosis and a hypoplastic LV","Mitral stenosis alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Coarctation of the aorta alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic stenosis alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Underdevelopment of the left heart with mitral atresia or stenosis aortic atresia or stenosis and a hypoplastic LV', 'HLHS comprises underdevelopment of the left heart: mitral and aortic atresia or stenosis with a diminutive left ventricle, so the right ventricle sustains both circulations. The neonate is ductal-dependent, with systemic perfusion maintained only through the patent ductus arteriosus.'),
('ed4bf50e-5de9-5102-b463-b08db35ceab1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the essential fetal shunt for survival in HLHS?', '["Patent ductus arteriosus (ductal-dependent systemic circulation)","Patent ductus venosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Umbilical artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen ovale (ASD) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","VSD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Patent ductus arteriosus (ductal-dependent systemic circulation)', 'Because the left ventricle cannot perfuse the systemic bed in HLHS, blood must pass right-to-left through the patent ductus arteriosus to reach the aorta. Ductal closure after birth is catastrophic, which is why prostaglandin infusion is given to keep it patent until palliation.'),
('a0b8aaea-6446-5da5-9327-deb21e34e8bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Norwood procedure?', '["Stage I of the Fontan pathway resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary artery banding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VSD closure mediated by specific receptor activation","ASD creation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stage I palliation for HLHS involving reconstruction of the aorta and a shunt to supply pulmonary blood flow"]'::jsonb, 'Stage I palliation for HLHS involving reconstruction of the aorta and a shunt to supply pulmonary blood flow', 'The Norwood operation is the first-stage palliation for HLHS: the pulmonary trunk is used to reconstruct a neo-aorta connected to the ductus/coronaries, and pulmonary flow is supplied by a Blalock-Taussig or Sano shunt. It is followed later by the Glenn and Fontan stages.'),
('19e6cdb8-4055-53cb-a719-2d56214558c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Sano shunt?', '["Glenn shunt resulting in decreased functional activity","BT shunt accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A right ventricle to pulmonary artery conduit used in Stage I Norwood for HLHS","Fontan completion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central shunt leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A right ventricle to pulmonary artery conduit used in Stage I Norwood for HLHS', 'The Sano modification places a right ventricle to pulmonary artery conduit as the pulmonary blood supply during the Stage I Norwood palliation for HLHS. It offers more stable pulmonary flow and systemic perfusion than a Blalock-Taussig shunt.'),
('51a20480-f4ae-5b66-9bd7-38fd2254adad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Glenn procedure (bidirectional cavopulmonary shunt)?', '["BT shunt resulting in decreased functional activity","VSD closure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IVC to PA shunt mediated by specific receptor activation","Arterial switch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anastomosis of the SVC to the right pulmonary artery (stage II for single ventricle)"]'::jsonb, 'Anastomosis of the SVC to the right pulmonary artery (stage II for single ventricle)', 'The bidirectional Glenn shunt connects the superior vena cava to the right pulmonary artery, delivering upper-body venous return to the lungs and unloading the single ventricle; it is the Stage II step in palliative pathways. The inferior vena cava is routed to the lungs later by the Fontan operation.'),
('84c4fbf2-def1-5a96-bfa3-8eb4b47cd6b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Fontan procedure?', '["VSD closure resulting in decreased functional activity","Valve replacement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arterial switch mediated by specific receptor activation","Completion of total cavopulmonary connection (IVC to PA) creating Fontan circulation for single ventricle","Heart transplant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Completion of total cavopulmonary connection (IVC to PA) creating Fontan circulation for single ventricle', 'The Fontan operation completes the total cavopulmonary connection by directing IVC blood to the pulmonary arteries, so the single ventricle pumps only to the systemic circulation. The other options represent corrective operations for different lesions.'),
('af6a7783-6a0f-5faa-9502-8ab1013f5200'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common complication of the Fontan circulation?', '["Plastic bronchitis and protein-losing enteropathy","Hepatic dysfunction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arrhythmia mediated by specific receptor activation","Failure of the Fontan without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thromboembolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Plastic bronchitis and protein-losing enteropathy', 'Chronic elevation of systemic venous pressure in Fontan circulation classically spares no organ, but protein-losing enteropathy and plastic bronchitis are the distinctive late complications; thromboembolism, arrhythmias, and hepatic dysfunction are also frequent and should be sought.'),
('a0aa0da9-5b70-53f7-b436-1ac54f0c1091'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is tricuspid atresia?', '["Double outlet right ventricle resulting in decreased functional activity","Stenosis of the tricuspid valve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital absence of the tricuspid valve with a hypoplastic RV and obligatory interatrial shunt","Epstein anomaly of the tricuspid valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tricuspid regurgitation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital absence of the tricuspid valve with a hypoplastic RV and obligatory interatrial shunt', 'Tricuspid atresia is an absent tricuspid orifice with a hypoplastic right ventricle, so systemic venous blood must cross an obligatory ASD into the left heart. Pulmonary flow is then supplied through a VSD or PDA, and the lesion is cyanotic.'),
('4747cc08-b201-5322-840e-1fff35b7230c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What determines the amount of pulmonary blood flow in tricuspid atresia?', '["The size of the ASD","The presence of TAPVR","The degree of tricuspid regurgitation","The presence and size of the VSD or PDA","The presence of coarctation"]'::jsonb, 'The presence and size of the VSD or PDA', 'With no functioning right ventricle in tricuspid atresia, blood reaches the lungs only through the VSD or PDA, so their presence and caliber dictate pulmonary flow and degree of cyanosis. The ASD only provides the obligatory interatrial mixing and is not the flow-limiting structure.'),
('bb730fdb-b8eb-547c-8cf2-290d18a34398'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common type of tricuspid atresia?', '["Type I (normally related great arteries with ventricular septal defect)","Type III (L-transposition) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type 0 ( VSD) mediated by specific receptor activation","Type IV (with pulmonary atresia) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type II (D-transposition) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (normally related great arteries with ventricular septal defect)', 'Type I tricuspid atresia, with normally related great arteries and a VSD, accounts for the great majority of cases. Types with D- or L-transposition and those with pulmonary atresia are much less frequent.'),
('27dec3d4-5449-5493-b9e8-a71bc8c0eba9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is Ebstein anomaly?', '["Superior displacement of the TV resulting in decreased functional activity","Apical displacement of the septal and posterior leaflets of the tricuspid valve with atrialization of the right ventricle","Stenosis of the TV mediated by specific receptor activation","Absence of the TV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Double orifice TV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Apical displacement of the septal and posterior leaflets of the tricuspid valve with atrialization of the right ventricle', 'Ebstein anomaly arises from apical (downward) displacement of the septal and posterior tricuspid leaflets, so part of the right ventricle becomes incorporated into the right atrium (''atrialized''). The anterior leaflet is large and sail-like, and an ASD or PFO is frequently present.'),
('4bb2a980-80c5-516d-b547-0a45723f0920'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Whats the most common associated finding with Ebstein anomaly?', '["VSD resulting in decreased functional activity","Atrial septal defect (ASD) or patent foramen ovale (PFO)","PDA mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary stenosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coarctation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial septal defect (ASD) or patent foramen ovale (PFO)', 'An interatrial communication, either an ASD or a patent foramen ovale, coexists in a large majority of Ebstein anomaly patients and permits right-to-left shunting when right atrial pressures rise. The other lesions are not characteristic associations.'),
('b231e71e-5cb4-507b-8318-18c1f14037ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of Ebstein anomaly?', '["Drug exposure resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Genetic mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","Maternal lithium exposure during pregnancy (but most cases are sporadic)","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maternal lithium exposure during pregnancy (but most cases are sporadic)', 'Maternal lithium ingestion in early pregnancy is the recognized teratogenic cause of Ebstein anomaly, yet the majority of cases arise sporadically with no identifiable exposure. Infectious, traumatic, and most single-gene mechanisms are not established causes.'),
('1c60b4b1-c36a-55cb-bad3-682fa373f7c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is double outlet right ventricle (DORV)?', '["Both great arteries arise predominantly from the right ventricle","Both great arteries arise from the left ventricle","Transposition of the great arteries","Truncus arteriosus","Normally related great arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Both great arteries arise predominantly from the right ventricle', 'In DORV both great arteries arise predominantly from the right ventricle, and the left ventricle empties through a ventricular septal defect. Transposition, truncus arteriosus, and normally related great arteries each describe a different connection of the great arteries.'),
('05fb0138-0457-5672-ae2e-b60b921fe31e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What determines the physiology in DORV?', '["The presence of coarctation resulting in decreased functional activity","The presence of PDA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The position of the VSD relative to the great arteries (subaortic subpulmonary doubly committed or noncommitted)","The degree of RV hypertrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The size of the ASD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The position of the VSD relative to the great arteries (subaortic subpulmonary doubly committed or noncommitted)', 'The hemodynamics of DORV depend on where the VSD lies relative to the great arteries: a subaortic VSD behaves like tetralogy of Fallot, whereas a subpulmonary VSD mimics D-TGA. Coarctation, PDA, RV hypertrophy, and ASD size modify the picture but do not set the fundamental physiology.'),
('7df2496d-d988-5536-9f6c-0711dfa8d352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Taussig-Bing anomaly?', '["A form of DORV with a subpulmonary VSD causing physiology similar to D-TGA (cyanosis)","DORV with doubly committed VSD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DORV with subaortic VSD mediated by specific receptor activation","Truncus arteriosus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DORV with noncommitted VSD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A form of DORV with a subpulmonary VSD causing physiology similar to D-TGA (cyanosis)', 'Taussig-Bing anomaly is a DORV variant in which the VSD lies beneath the pulmonary trunk, so oxygenated left ventricular blood streams toward the pulmonary artery and the physiology resembles D-TGA with cyanosis. Subaortic, doubly committed, and noncommitted VSDs produce different hemodynamic patterns.'),
('a8da2e40-3280-5845-b29f-7267f4f913b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is pulmonary atresia with intact ventricular septum (PA/IVS)?', '["Isolated pulmonary stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Atresia of the pulmonary valve with an intact ventricular septum and a hypoplastic RV","The aortic valve is atretic mediated by specific receptor activation","Tricuspid atresia with pulmonary atresia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary atresia with VSD (tetralogy with atresia) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atresia of the pulmonary valve with an intact ventricular septum and a hypoplastic RV', 'PA/IVS means the pulmonary valve is atretic with no septal defect, so the right ventricle is hypoplastic and pulmonary flow is ductal-dependent. Isolated stenosis, aortic atresia, tricuspid atresia, and PA with VSD are distinct lesions with different right ventricular size and physiology.'),
('2dac022a-ad81-5fe1-b952-a51477ba7472'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is pulmonary atresia with VSD (PA/VSD)?', '["Pulmonary atresia with a VSD (extreme form of tetralogy of Fallot) where pulmonary blood flow is ductal-dependent","TOF with pulmonary stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Tricuspid atresia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PA/IVS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary atresia with a VSD (extreme form of tetralogy of Fallot) where pulmonary blood flow is ductal-dependent', 'PA/VSD is the extreme form of tetralogy of Fallot in which the pulmonary valve is atretic and the entire pulmonary circulation depends on the ductus arteriosus; the VSD decompresses the right ventricle, unlike PA/IVS. Simple pulmonary stenosis and TOF with stenosis retain antegrade pulmonary flow, and tricuspid atresia is a different defect.'),
('e3fcd027-498d-58c4-9de0-4f28db57dc07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the Rastelli procedure?', '["Norwood resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Arterial switch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A surgical repair for transposition with VSD and pulmonary stenosis using an intracardiac tunnel and RV-to-PA conduit","Fontan without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glenn leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A surgical repair for transposition with VSD and pulmonary stenosis using an intracardiac tunnel and RV-to-PA conduit', 'The Rastelli procedure directs left ventricular blood to the aorta through an intracardiac tunnel and connects the right ventricle to the pulmonary artery with a valved conduit, used for D-TGA with VSD and pulmonary stenosis. Norwood stages hypoplastic left heart syndrome, arterial switch corrects simple TGA, and Fontan/Glenn are cavopulmonary connections.'),
('39dea3a2-929b-5d07-86fa-1093e2189881'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is coarctation of the aorta?', '["A congenital narrowing of the aorta most commonly in the juxtaductal region (distal to left subclavian)","Dissection of the aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An aneurysm of the aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","A narrowing of the aortic valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoplasia of the entire aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital narrowing of the aorta most commonly in the juxtaductal region (distal to left subclavian)', 'Coarctation is a congenital discrete narrowing of the aorta, classically juxtaductal just distal to the left subclavian artery. Dissection, aneurysm, aortic valve narrowing, and diffuse hypoplasia are separate pathologies without this focal constriction.'),
('2a812315-8756-5d88-a507-7bc5b9939891'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common type of coarctation?', '["Preductal (infantile type)","Abdominal coarctation","Interruption of the arch","Proximal to the left subclavian","Postductal (adult type) juxtaductal coarctation"]'::jsonb, 'Postductal (adult type) juxtaductal coarctation', 'Postductal (adult-type) juxtaductal coarctation is the classic and most common form, presenting with upper-limb hypertension. Preductal infantile coarctation, abdominal coarctation, and arch interruption are all much less common.'),
('c965e3bd-311b-5144-b1e9-1d2f23d89430'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the difference between preductal and postductal coarctation?', '["Preductal is proximal to the ductus (before closure) causing lower body cyanosis in neonates; postductal is distal (adult type) causing hypertension","Preductal is more proximal in the arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","AHA classification mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Postductal is more severe without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","differ in surgical approach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Preductal is proximal to the ductus (before closure) causing lower body cyanosis in neonates; postductal is distal (adult type) causing hypertension', 'Preductal coarctation lies proximal to the ductus, so before ductal closure the lower body is perfused by desaturated ductal blood producing differential cyanosis, whereas postductal coarctation lies distal and causes upper-extremity hypertension with weak femoral pulses. The other options describe severity or classification, not the anatomic and hemodynamic distinction.'),
('7df20c8c-8f2c-5c85-a2a7-a5fa810deea5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the classic sign in postductal coarctation of the aorta?', '["Equal pulses everywhere resulting in decreased functional activity","Lower extremity hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper extremity hypertension with weak delayed femoral pulses and rib notching on X-ray","Asymptomatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cyanosis only in the upper body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper extremity hypertension with weak delayed femoral pulses and rib notching on X-ray', 'The gradient across the juxtaductal narrowing causes upper-extremity hypertension with weak, delayed femoral pulses; rib notching results from dilated intercostal collateral arteries eroding the rib borders. Equal pulses, lower-limb hypertension, and upper-body cyanosis are not features of the postductal form.'),
('79151d7d-ae6f-5e73-8e79-7cae8321c768'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Rib notching in coarctation involves which ribs?', '["All ribs bilaterally","1st and 2nd ribs","3rd through 8th ribs (posteriorly)","Only the left ribs","9th through 12th ribs"]'::jsonb, '3rd through 8th ribs (posteriorly)', 'Intercostal collateral vessels enlarge and erode the lower borders of the posterior third through eighth ribs. The first two ribs are spared because their intercostal arteries arise from vessels outside the collateral pathway.'),
('a6718df6-2ab6-5171-a8cf-257f5095a358'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is pseudocoarctation?', '["A buckled but nonobstructive redundant aorta without a pressure gradient","A preductal coarctation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A complete coarctation mediated by specific receptor activation","A tortuous aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An interrupted aortic arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A buckled but nonobstructive redundant aorta without a pressure gradient', 'Pseudocoarctation is a buckling or kinking of the descending aorta that mimics coarctation on imaging but creates no obstruction and no pressure gradient. Preductal coarctation, complete coarctation, and arch interruption are all genuinely obstructive or structurally different.'),
('cff6b4fd-87ed-51a0-ba4f-93eefc29dc75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is interruption of the aortic arch?', '["Aortic hypoplasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe coarctation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete atresia of the entire arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete discontinuity between the ascending and descending aorta (type AB or C)","Aneurysm of the aortic arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complete discontinuity between the ascending and descending aorta (type AB or C)', 'Aortic arch interruption is complete loss of luminal continuity between the ascending and descending aorta, classified as type A (distal to the left subclavian), B (between left carotid and subclavian), or C (proximal to the left carotid). Hypoplasia, severe coarctation, and atresia retain some aortic continuity.'),
('2cf10461-3610-5a62-b2fb-b2a79f66f3bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common type of aortic arch interruption?', '["Type C (proximal to left common carotid) resulting in decreased functional activity","Type B1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","B2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Type A (distal to left subclavian) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type B (between left common carotid and left subclavian)"]'::jsonb, 'Type B (between left common carotid and left subclavian)', 'Type B interruption, between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, is the most common form and is strongly associated with DiGeorge syndrome (22q11 deletion). Type A and type C interruptions are much less frequent.'),
('4f5a67cc-e937-5b57-8542-8cea6f452d8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)?', '["Early closure of the ductus","Failure of the ductus arteriosus to close after birth","Narrowing of the ductus","Absence of the ductus","Calcification of the ductus"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the ductus arteriosus to close after birth', 'PDA is persistence of the ductus arteriosus beyond the neonatal period because it fails to close after birth, leaving an aortopulmonary communication and a left-to-right shunt. Early closure, narrowing, absence, or calcification of the ductus do not describe this lesion.'),
('3067411f-0599-5df6-832d-972649abe13f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of PDA?', '["Genetic factors","High altitude","Rubella infection (congenital)","Persistent hypoxemia","Prematurity (incomplete ductal closure)"]'::jsonb, 'Prematurity (incomplete ductal closure)', 'Prematurity is the leading cause of PDA because the immature ductus is highly responsive to prostaglandins and slow to constrict with rising oxygen. Congenital rubella, high altitude, hypoxemia, and genetic factors account for only a minority of cases.'),
('3b12566b-d8b3-5658-82a8-4c764b97b61c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the machinery murmur?', '["A systolic murmur only resulting in decreased functional activity","The continuous murmur of a PDA (heard throughout systole and diastole)","A diastolic murmur only mediated by specific receptor activation","A murmur of VSD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An innocent murmur leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The continuous murmur of a PDA (heard throughout systole and diastole)', 'The machinery murmur of PDA is continuous because aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary artery pressure through both systole and diastole, so flow across the ductus never ceases. Pure systolic or diastolic murmurs and VSD murmurs lack this continuous quality.'),
('1f8b9c6b-a4f0-5dde-9219-9e90c5229632'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is aortopulmonary window (AP window)?', '["A truncus arteriosus resulting in decreased functional activity","A PDA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A VSD with aortic regurgitation mediated by specific receptor activation","An ASD with pulmonary hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A defect between the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'A defect between the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk', 'An aortopulmonary window is a defect between the ascending aorta and the pulmonary trunk, producing a large left-to-right shunt at the arterial level. It differs from PDA (connection through the ductus), truncus arteriosus (single great vessel), VSD, and ASD.'),
('fff678a9-3d09-5cc9-887b-73dc14715b49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common type of vascular ring?', '["Double aortic arch","Pulmonary artery sling (left PA from right PA)","Left aortic arch with aberrant right subclavian (arteria lusoria)","Innominate artery compression","Right aortic arch with aberrant left subclavian artery"]'::jsonb, 'Double aortic arch', 'Double aortic arch is the most common complete vascular ring, encircling the trachea and esophagus and causing stridor and dysphagia in infancy. Pulmonary artery sling, aberrant subclavian vessels, and innominate artery compression are partial or anatomically different anomalies.'),
('75e24e2c-7ab4-5406-8098-675bb8d51812'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is a pulmonary artery sling?', '["Aortic arch sling resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The left pulmonary artery arises from the right pulmonary artery and passes between the trachea and esophagus causing compression","Aberrant subclavian artery mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Ductus arteriosus sling without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete vascular ring leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left pulmonary artery arises from the right pulmonary artery and passes between the trachea and esophagus causing compression', 'In a pulmonary artery sling the left pulmonary artery originates from the right pulmonary artery and passes between the trachea and esophagus, compressing both. Aberrant subclavian arteries, ductus slings, and complete vascular rings describe different anatomy.'),
('b991acc0-5b48-5fc3-baa7-26bb03d5f8de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital coronary artery anomaly?', '["Anomalous origin of the left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery (ALCAPA)","Anomalous aortic origin of the right coronary artery (AAORCA)","Coronary artery fistula","Myocardial bridge","Anomalous aortic origin of the left coronary artery (AAOLCA)"]'::jsonb, 'Anomalous origin of the left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery (ALCAPA)', 'ALCAPA, anomalous origin of the left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery, is the most common congenital coronary anomaly and causes myocardial ischemia in infancy. AAORCA, AAOLCA, fistulas, and myocardial bridges are rarer or have different consequences.'),
('fc080d18-70ec-5707-80b6-27547ce8ff9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What happens in ALCAPA?', '["The right coronary arises from the PA resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary sinus anomaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both coronaries arise from the PA mediated by specific receptor activation","Coronary fistula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The left coronary artery arises from the pulmonary artery instead of the aorta causing myocardial ischemia in infancy"]'::jsonb, 'The left coronary artery arises from the pulmonary artery instead of the aorta causing myocardial ischemia in infancy', 'In ALCAPA the left coronary arises from the pulmonary artery, so the left ventricle is perfused at low pressure with desaturated blood, producing ischemia and sometimes infarction in infancy. Anomalies of the right coronary, coronary sinus, or both coronaries do not cause this picture.'),
('f4fe2da5-a76c-57ae-b7b3-f8e0355be1fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes?', '["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)","Myocarditis","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC)","Long QT syndrome","Anomalous coronary arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)', 'Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes, with ventricular arrhythmias triggered by exertion. Myocarditis, ARVC, long QT syndrome, and anomalous coronaries are less common overall in this group.'),
('f58496ff-e239-52f4-a79a-e3f1b4186985'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of death in Marfan syndrome?', '["Mitral valve prolapse resulting in decreased functional activity","Endocarditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic root dissection and rupture (type A aortic dissection)","Heart failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arrhythmia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic root dissection and rupture (type A aortic dissection)', 'Cystic medial degeneration of the ascending aorta predisposes Marfan patients to aortic root dissection and rupture, the most common cause of death. Mitral prolapse, endocarditis, heart failure, and arrhythmia are not the principal lethal complications.'),
('2579650d-941a-5b75-88a1-970f4471a940'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of aortic stenosis in adults?', '["Annuloaortic ectasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Rheumatic heart disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infective endocarditis mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcific aortic valve disease (senile degenerative calcification)","Bicuspid aortic valve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcific aortic valve disease (senile degenerative calcification)', 'Calcific aortic valve disease, the senile degenerative calcification of a trileaflet valve, is the most common cause of aortic stenosis in adults, typically appearing after age 70. Rheumatic disease and bicuspid valve predominate in younger patients.'),
('dd405fb3-01e5-5afe-a9c4-451a4b46f525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital cardiac anomaly causing aortic stenosis?', '["Aortic valve atresia resulting in decreased functional activity","Supravalvular aortic stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subvalvular aortic stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Unicuspid aortic valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bicuspid aortic valve (most common congenital cardiac anomaly overall)"]'::jsonb, 'Bicuspid aortic valve (most common congenital cardiac anomaly overall)', 'A bicuspid aortic valve is the most common congenital cardiac anomaly and the leading congenital cause of aortic stenosis, usually presenting in middle age as the valve calcifies. Unicuspid valves and supravalvular or subvalvular stenoses are much less common congenital causes.'),
('73dfa58b-2761-5e4e-bbfa-c6cb5565c920'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of mitral stenosis?', '["Mitral annular calcification","Rheumatic heart disease","Congenital mitral stenosis","Myxomatous degeneration","Infective endocarditis"]'::jsonb, 'Rheumatic heart disease', 'Rheumatic heart disease is the most common cause of mitral stenosis, producing leaflet thickening and commissural fusion. Mitral annular calcification, congenital stenosis, myxomatous degeneration, and endocarditis rarely produce true mitral stenosis.'),
('a960e8f1-91f8-560f-a9de-04e14ca749c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of mitral regurgitation?', '["Mitral valve prolapse (myxomatous degeneration)","LV dilation","Endocarditis","Ischemic (post-MI papillary muscle dysfunction)","Rheumatic heart disease"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral valve prolapse (myxomatous degeneration)', 'Mitral valve prolapse with myxomatous degeneration is the most common cause of mitral regurgitation, with redundant leaflets prolapsing into the left atrium. LV dilation, ischemic papillary muscle dysfunction, endocarditis, and rheumatic disease are less common overall causes.'),
('465d0e23-e283-51dd-9216-7c401cbaa951'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary stenosis?', '["Rheumatic heart disease","Post-surgical","Noonan syndrome","Carcinoid syndrome","Congenital (most often valvular alone)"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital (most often valvular alone)', 'Pulmonary stenosis is most often congenital and isolated to the valve, with fused, domed leaflets. Noonan syndrome, carcinoid disease, rheumatic involvement, and post-surgical scarring account for only a minority of cases.'),
('b2ea83bf-27f2-5aae-ab53-de861f2fae89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of tricuspid regurgitation?', '["Endocarditis resulting in decreased functional activity","Carcinoid syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rheumatic heart disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Ebstein anomaly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Functional (secondary) TR from RV dilation and pulmonary hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Functional (secondary) TR from RV dilation and pulmonary hypertension', 'Functional (secondary) tricuspid regurgitation from right ventricular dilation and pulmonary hypertension is the most common form, with structurally normal leaflets that fail to coapt. Endocarditis, carcinoid, rheumatic disease, and Ebstein anomaly are primary valve causes and much less frequent.'),
('db25189d-55eb-5260-aed3-5f88099fa9ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of infective endocarditis?', '["Viral resulting in decreased functional activity","Rickettsial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Steril mediated by specific receptor activation","Bacterial infection (Strep viridans Staph aureus Enterococcus)","Fungal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial infection (Strep viridans Staph aureus Enterococcus)', 'Infective endocarditis is a bacterial infection: viridans-group streptococci predominate on native valves, Staphylococcus aureus in acute and injection-drug-use cases, and enterococci in older patients. Viral, rickettsial, fungal, and sterile mechanisms do not cause typical infective endocarditis.'),
('3b1c010c-2fab-5040-b715-151f46f0ae18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common valve affected in infective endocarditis?', '["Pulmonary valve resulting in decreased functional activity","Mitral valve (most common) followed by aortic valve","All equally mediated by specific receptor activation","Aortic valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tricuspid valve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral valve (most common) followed by aortic valve', 'The mitral valve is the most common site of infective endocarditis, followed by the aortic valve, likely because of the hemodynamic stress of high-pressure jets. The tricuspid valve is affected mainly in injection-drug users, and pulmonary valve involvement is rare.'),
('f6dcfc64-09d6-5d51-9421-16a269c10ecc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital heart disease in adults?', '["VSD resulting in decreased functional activity","ASD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PDA mediated by specific receptor activation","Bicuspid aortic valve","Coarctation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bicuspid aortic valve', 'Bicuspid aortic valve, present in about 1-2% of the population, is the most common congenital heart defect in adults, though it is often asymptomatic. VSD, ASD, and PDA are usually repaired or diagnosed in childhood, and coarctation is less common.'),
('8601b1bc-41be-561a-844c-d78954c70dc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common CHD encountered in adult cardiology practice?', '["PDA","Tetralogy of Fallot (repaired)","VSD","ASD (secundum type)","Bicuspid aortic valve"]'::jsonb, 'ASD (secundum type)', 'Secundum ASD is the congenital heart lesion most often seen in adult cardiology because it can remain silent until adulthood, presenting with dyspnea, arrhythmia, or paradoxical embolism. The other lesions are usually diagnosed earlier or are less prevalent in adult practice.'),
('9152f8c0-2215-5186-b20a-a23be0b36f45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of atrial fibrillation?', '["Idiopathic (lone AF) resulting in decreased functional activity","Sleep apnea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obesity mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension and structural heart disease (ischemic valvular cardiomyopathy)","Hyperthyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension and structural heart disease (ischemic valvular cardiomyopathy)', 'Hypertension with structural heart disease such as ischemic or valvular cardiomyopathy is the most common substrate for atrial fibrillation, because left atrial enlargement favors re-entry. Lone AF, sleep apnea, obesity, and hyperthyroidism are less frequent or contributing causes.'),
('babacffb-a31e-5a76-a0e9-8f2f71cea5b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of complete heart block in adults?', '["Medications resulting in decreased functional activity","Congenital heart block accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic degeneration (lenegre disease) and ischemic heart disease (especially inferior MI)","Myocarditis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic degeneration (lenegre disease) and ischemic heart disease (especially inferior MI)', 'In adults, complete heart block most often results from idiopathic fibrosis of the conduction system (Lenegre disease) or from ischemic heart disease, particularly inferior myocardial infarction. Medications, congenital block, myocarditis, and surgery are less common causes.'),
('4202643e-cd1b-5793-bc07-100d3af418d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of congenital complete heart block?', '["Congenital heart disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Genetic disorders accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infections mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiac tumors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maternal systemic lupus erythematosus (anti-Ro/anti-La antibodies crossing the placenta)"]'::jsonb, 'Maternal systemic lupus erythematosus (anti-Ro/anti-La antibodies crossing the placenta)', 'Maternal systemic lupus erythematosus is the leading cause of congenital complete heart block: transplacental anti-Ro/anti-La antibodies damage the fetal atrioventricular node. Congenital heart disease, genetic syndromes, infections, and tumors are far less frequent causes.'),
('120c58b7-7ecb-5195-a3da-d548d6b36e8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common pediatric arrhythmia?', '["Complete heart block resulting in decreased functional activity","Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) most commonly due to accessory pathway (Wolff-Parkinson-White)","Sinus bradycardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Ventricular tachycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial fibrillation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) most commonly due to accessory pathway (Wolff-Parkinson-White)', 'Supraventricular tachycardia is the most common symptomatic arrhythmia in children, and in older children it is usually due to an accessory pathway as in Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome. Ventricular tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, and complete heart block are much less frequent.'),
('f1fef686-e932-538b-af96-ef4aa7a5d896'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome?', '["Long QT syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Wide complex tachycardia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete heart block mediated by specific receptor activation","Pre-excitation syndrome with an accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) causing delta wave on ECG and SVT","Brugada syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-excitation syndrome with an accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) causing delta wave on ECG and SVT', 'WPW is a pre-excitation syndrome caused by an accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) that bypasses the AV node, producing a short PR interval, delta wave, and atrioventricular re-entrant SVT. Long QT and Brugada syndromes and complete heart block have different mechanisms and ECG findings.'),
('12bc4bc7-1f00-5429-a59a-aa7d19d4c690'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common accessory pathway location in WPW?', '["Anteroseptal","Left-sided free wall (most common)","Multiple pathways","Right-sided free wall","Posteroseptal"]'::jsonb, 'Left-sided free wall (most common)', 'Accessory pathways in WPW most commonly lie along the left-sided free wall, followed by posteroseptal and right free-wall sites. Anteroseptal pathways and multiple pathways are less common.'),
('e1136e5e-5616-5464-9ebf-abf7d9e766c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of long QT syndrome?', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiomyopathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Valvular disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischemic heart disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congenital (KCNQ1 KCNH2 SCN5A mutations) or acquired (medications electrolyte disturbances)"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital (KCNQ1 KCNH2 SCN5A mutations) or acquired (medications electrolyte disturbances)', 'Long QT syndrome is either congenital, due to mutations in cardiac ion-channel genes such as KCNQ1, KCNH2, and SCN5A, or acquired, from QT-prolonging drugs and electrolyte disturbances such as hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia. Hypertension, cardiomyopathy, and valvular or ischemic heart disease do not directly cause the channelopathy.'),
('d3da6658-625d-55d9-b511-488b3ff32356'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common type of congenital long QT syndrome?', '["LQT6 resulting in decreased functional activity","LQT3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LQT1 (KCNQ1) and LQT2 (KCNH2) are most common","LQT3 (SCN5A) is third without affecting the primary tissue pathway","LQT5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'LQT1 (KCNQ1) and LQT2 (KCNH2) are most common', 'LQT1 (KCNQ1) and LQT2 (KCNH2) are the most common congenital long QT subtypes, together accounting for most genotyped cases; LQT3 (SCN5A) ranks third. LQT5 and LQT6 are rare forms.'),
('79ec3ee5-2a7d-534e-901b-c3da785a1fe8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of sudden cardiac arrest in children?', '["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) or anomalous coronary artery origin","Commotio cordis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myocarditis mediated by specific receptor activation","Drug toxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Long QT syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) or anomalous coronary artery origin', 'In children, sudden cardiac arrest is most often caused by hypertrophic cardiomyopathy or anomalous coronary artery origin, both of which can precipitate lethal arrhythmia or ischemia during exertion. Myocarditis, commotio cordis, drug toxicity, and long QT syndrome are less common overall causes.'),
('2b235f64-752a-57bc-bedf-67011e0b3148'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cardiomyopathy in children?', '["Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM)","Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)","Left ventricular noncompaction","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)', 'Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is the most common cardiomyopathy in children and is often familial, with mutations in sarcomeric protein genes. Dilated, restrictive, noncompaction, and ARVC are diagnosed less frequently in the pediatric age group.'),
('72acfd13-4a2c-52e8-886d-d1025b05c73b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of dilated cardiomyopathy in children?', '["Neuromuscular disease (Ducheme MD)","Metabolic disorders","Genetic (familial)","Idiopathic","Myocarditis (viral)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic', 'Most dilated cardiomyopathy in children is idiopathic, with a specific cause identified in only a minority. Viral myocarditis, familial or genetic forms, neuromuscular disease such as Duchenne muscular dystrophy, and metabolic disorders account for the remaining cases.'),
('392f8a64-e23a-5243-9889-03e8cfc166cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of myocarditis in children?', '["Viral infection (enterovirus parvovirus B19 adenovirus)","Bacterial infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autoimmune disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasitic infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fungal infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Viral infection (enterovirus parvovirus B19 adenovirus)', 'Myocarditis in children is most commonly viral, with enteroviruses, parvovirus B19, and adenovirus among the frequent agents. Bacterial, parasitic, fungal, and autoimmune causes are far less common in this age group.'),
('ef74a288-61f2-5d78-984b-af23637e70d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pericarditis in children?', '["Tuberculosis","Bacterial infection","Idiopathic (presumed viral)","Trauma","Rheumatic fever"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (presumed viral)', 'Pericarditis in children is most often idiopathic and presumed viral, frequently following a viral illness. Bacterial (including tuberculosis), traumatic, and rheumatic causes are less common in this population.'),
('310da524-e913-58d1-a549-346cadf63080'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pericarditis in adults?', '["Tuberculosis","Idiopathic (viral)","Uremic","Malignancy (metastatic)","MI/Trauma (Dressler post-MI)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (viral)', 'Pericarditis in adults is most commonly idiopathic or viral. Uremia, metastatic malignancy, tuberculosis, and post-myocardial-infarction (Dressler) syndrome are important but less frequent causes.'),
('7ef8f38e-0ae5-5422-90f8-ca1b59f916dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cardiac tamponade?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","MI with rupture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malignancy (most common) followed by idiopathic pericarditis and iatrogenic (post-cardiac surgery)","Aortic dissection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tuberculous pericarditis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Malignancy (most common) followed by idiopathic pericarditis and iatrogenic (post-cardiac surgery)', 'Malignancy is the most common cause of cardiac tamponade, followed by idiopathic pericarditis and iatrogenic effusions such as those after cardiac surgery. Trauma, MI with rupture, aortic dissection, and tuberculous pericarditis are less common overall.'),
('2c86af47-b7b9-5e0f-8bdc-f0f8cb9e03b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common primary cardiac tumor in adults?', '["Myxoma (most common primary cardiac tumor overall)","Papillary fibroelastoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Angiosarcoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Lipoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rhabdomyoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myxoma (most common primary cardiac tumor overall)', 'Myxoma is the most common primary cardiac tumor in adults, usually arising in the left atrium on a stalk. Papillary fibroelastoma, lipoma, and angiosarcoma are rarer, and rhabdomyoma is the most common cardiac tumor in children, not adults.'),
('e019553d-b683-552c-920e-217ddc924e6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common primary cardiac tumor in children?', '["Angiosarcoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Teratoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Myxoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rhabdomyoma (associated with tuberous sclerosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Rhabdomyoma (associated with tuberous sclerosis)', 'Rhabdomyomas are hamartomas strongly associated with tuberous sclerosis and are the most common primary cardiac tumor in children; they often regress spontaneously. Myxoma is the most common primary cardiac tumor overall but occurs mainly in adults, and angiosarcoma is the most common primary malignant cardiac tumor of adulthood.'),
('8ee63a70-773c-568b-a353-77ce995f18da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cardiogenic shock in children?', '["Cardiac tamponade","Sepsis","Arrhythmia","Pediatric CHD with shock (HLHS critical AS coarctation)","Acute myocarditis"]'::jsonb, 'Acute myocarditis', 'Acute viral myocarditis rapidly impairs systolic function and is the most common cause of cardiogenic shock in children. Cardiac tamponade and arrhythmias can also produce low cardiac output but are less frequent, whereas sepsis typically causes distributive (septic) shock rather than a primarily pump-failure state.'),
('ac8ee393-d82c-5e1e-8d94-1c2d6d99f0fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of systemic hypertension in children?', '["Coarctation of the aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Renal parenchymal disease (glomerulonephritis reflux nephropathy)","Renovascular disease (fibromuscular dysplasia) mediated by specific receptor activation","Endocrine causes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Essential hypertension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal parenchymal disease (glomerulonephritis reflux nephropathy)', 'Hypertension in childhood is usually secondary, and renal parenchymal disease (glomerulonephritis, reflux nephropathy) is the leading cause. Coarctation and renovascular disease also produce secondary hypertension but are less common, and essential hypertension is uncommon in young children and is a diagnosis of exclusion.'),
('e21f0ae2-dc49-5886-bd3d-37e0dd9d5db0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension in children?', '["Idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension (IPAH) resulting in decreased functional activity","Thromboembolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital heart disease with increased pulmonary blood flow (Eisenmenger physiology)","Chronic lung disease (bronchopulmonary dysplasia) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary vein stenosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital heart disease with increased pulmonary blood flow (Eisenmenger physiology)', 'Large left-to-right shunts (e.g., VSD, PDA, ASD) expose the pulmonary bed to high flow and pressure, gradually producing pulmonary vascular obstructive disease and pulmonary hypertension, making CHD the most common cause in children. IPAH, chronic lung disease, pulmonary vein stenosis, and thromboembolism are less frequent pediatric causes.'),
('576eacf9-b7c6-57e1-8a9e-11b39053f6fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is Eisenmenger syndrome?', '["PDA ligation resulting in decreased functional activity","Reversal of a left-to-right shunt to right-to-left due to pulmonary vascular obstructive disease","Tetralogy of Fallot mediated by specific receptor activation","VSD closure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ASD closure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reversal of a left-to-right shunt to right-to-left due to pulmonary vascular obstructive disease', 'A long-standing large left-to-right shunt causes pulmonary vascular obstructive disease; once pulmonary pressures exceed systemic, the shunt reverses to right-to-left with resultant cyanosis. This is Eisenmenger syndrome, an end-stage lesion, whereas ligation and closure are early corrective interventions rather than the disorder.'),
('7fd42bbd-6a6d-5476-938c-1a30945c7fee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common CHD associated with Eisenmenger syndrome?', '["Aortopulmonary window","Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)","Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","Atrial septal defect (ASD)","Atrioventricular septal defect (AVSD)"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular septal defect (VSD)', 'VSD is the most common congenital heart defect, and when large and unrepaired it is the lesion most often complicated by Eisenmenger physiology. ASD and PDA can reverse too but do so less commonly and usually later in life.'),
('ff23866b-d286-5d39-83f4-75cfea421acd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of right heart failure in children?', '["Cardiomyopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Constrictive pericarditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tricuspid valve disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Ebstein anomaly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary hypertension (from CHD lung disease or idiopathic)"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary hypertension (from CHD lung disease or idiopathic)', 'An elevated afterload from pulmonary hypertension (due to CHD, lung disease, or idiopathic PAH) is the most common mechanism of right ventricular failure in children. Primary tricuspid valve disease, Ebstein anomaly, and constrictive pericarditis are less common causes, and cardiomyopathy usually fails the left ventricle first.'),
('0cdfc203-a262-513d-8864-30f61aec30d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of heart failure in the first week of life?', '["Volume overload (VSD PDA) resulting in decreased functional activity","Critical left heart obstruction (HLHS critical coarctation critical aortic stenosis)","TAPVR with obstruction mediated by specific receptor activation","Myocarditis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TGA with inadequate mixing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Critical left heart obstruction (HLHS critical coarctation critical aortic stenosis)', 'Critical left-heart obstructive lesions (HLHS, critical coarctation, critical aortic stenosis) cause shock in the first days of life when ductal closure removes the supporting parallel circulation. Volume overload from VSD/PDA produces failure later, around the first month, as pulmonary vascular resistance falls.'),
('e7d24c62-ab96-5e1d-9484-9191772ba01e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of heart failure in the first month of life?', '["HLHS resulting in decreased functional activity","TGA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coarctation mediated by specific receptor activation","TAPVR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","VSD and other left-to-right shunts as pulmonary resistance drops"]'::jsonb, 'VSD and other left-to-right shunts as pulmonary resistance drops', 'As pulmonary vascular resistance falls after birth, left-to-right shunts (mainly VSD) deliver progressively more pulmonary blood flow, causing heart failure around the first month of life. HLHS, TGA, and coarctation tend to produce symptoms earlier, in the neonatal period.'),
('e626715f-cefa-59e7-b81d-50501a338469'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of heart failure in children over 1 year?', '["Myocarditis","Arrhythmia","Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM)","Rheumatic heart disease","Left-to-right shunts"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM)', 'After infancy, dilated cardiomyopathy, often post-viral or idiopathic, is the leading cause of heart failure in children older than one year. Myocarditis is the acute process that can progress to DCM, while rheumatic heart disease and left-to-right shunts are less common at this age.'),
('1c1a4eb5-c253-5f30-a7ed-4a93b81f0b31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of heart failure in adults?', '["Hypertension","Alcoholic cardiomyopathy","Coronary artery disease (ischemic cardiomyopathy)","Idiopathic DCM","Valvular heart disease (aortic stenosis mitral regurgitation)"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary artery disease (ischemic cardiomyopathy)', 'Myocardial ischemia and infarction with adverse remodeling underlie ischemic cardiomyopathy, the most common cause of heart failure in adults. Hypertension and valvular disease are frequent contributors but rank below coronary artery disease, and alcoholic and idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy are uncommon causes.'),
('a0b305d2-69fb-5600-9ee5-359093891611'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of stroke in children?', '["Congenital heart disease (paradoxical embolus through right-to-left shunt)","Coagulation disorders accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sickle cell disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arteriopathy (Moyamoya) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital heart disease (paradoxical embolus through right-to-left shunt)', 'A right-to-left shunt (cyanotic CHD or PFO) lets venous emboli bypass the pulmonary filter and reach the brain, the most common cause of childhood stroke. Coagulation disorders, sickle cell disease, infection, and arteriopathy such as Moyamoya are important but less frequent causes.'),
('73ea0b9e-8403-52e8-a99a-5e7f71bdf6c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cerebral abscess in children with CHD?', '["Paradoxical embolization from right-to-left shunt (cyanotic CHD especially TOF)","Sepsis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Otogenic mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Paradoxical embolization from right-to-left shunt (cyanotic CHD especially TOF)', 'In cyanotic CHD with a right-to-left shunt (especially tetralogy of Fallot), venous blood containing bacteria bypasses the pulmonary filtering bed and seeds the brain, giving paradoxical embolization as the most common cause of cerebral abscess. Sepsis, otogenic infection, trauma, and meningitis predominate in children without CHD.'),
('b444cef3-364c-5a11-ae13-45e63fa72f1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cyanosis in the newborn?', '["Sepsis","Methemoglobinemia","Pulmonary disease (persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn PPHN)","Congenital heart disease","Neurologic depression"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital heart disease', 'After respiratory and other causes are excluded in a persistently cyanotic newborn, congenital heart disease, especially cyanotic lesions such as transposition of the great arteries, is the most common remaining cause. The hyperoxia test and echocardiography separate cardiac cyanosis from pulmonary disease (PPHN), sepsis, methemoglobinemia, and neurologic depression.'),
('13d6c371-f9b0-59c6-a7f3-1d07216ee694'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the hyperoxia test?', '["Chest X-ray for heart size resulting in decreased functional activity","Exercise test to evaluate oxygenation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Echocardiogram to evaluate structure mediated by specific receptor activation","Measuring PaO2 on room air and 100% O2; CHD shows minimal increase (<150 mmHg) while lung disease shows improvement","ECG to evaluate for ischemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measuring PaO2 on room air and 100% O2; CHD shows minimal increase (<150 mmHg) while lung disease shows improvement', 'The hyperoxia test measures PaO2 on room air and after inhalation of 100% oxygen; lung disease responds with a marked rise (usually above 150 mmHg) because oxygen fixes perfusion/ventilation mismatch, whereas cyanotic CHD shows little response because the shunt bypasses the lungs. It is not an imaging or electrical test.'),
('824da0ec-cd3e-5568-b3ab-7697d9f14dac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cyanosis with increased pulmonary blood flow?', '["TGA (without obstruction) and truncus arteriosus","Ebstein anomaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tetralogy of Fallot mediated by specific receptor activation","Tricuspid atresia with VSD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary atresia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TGA (without obstruction) and truncus arteriosus', 'In TGA and truncus arteriosus, deoxygenated blood reaches the body while pulmonary blood flow is generous, producing cyanosis with plethoric (increased) lung fields. Tetralogy of Fallot, tricuspid atresia, and pulmonary atresia are cyanotic lesions with decreased pulmonary blood flow and oligemic lungs, while Ebstein anomaly may have variable flow.'),
('b3e7f222-8d1c-512f-97b2-371ceee0109b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cyanosis with decreased pulmonary blood flow?', '["Ebstein anomaly","Tetralogy of Fallot","Tricuspid atresia without VSD","Pulmonary atresia","Eisenmenger syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Tetralogy of Fallot', 'Tetralogy of Fallot is the most common cyanotic heart defect; right ventricular outflow obstruction reduces pulmonary blood flow while deoxygenated blood is shunted right-to-left through the VSD, giving cyanosis with oligemic lung fields. The other listed lesions are rarer causes of cyanosis with reduced pulmonary flow.'),
('5b87a4b8-02ff-5c24-b09f-0ffe283c3ff6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of peripheral edema in children?', '["Protein-calorie malnutrition (Kwashiorkor)","Hepatic disease (cirrhosis)","Idiopathic edema","Renal disease (nephrotic syndrome)","Cardiac disease (heart failure)"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic disease (cirrhosis)', 'Chronic liver disease lowers plasma albumin and promotes salt and water retention, producing dependent peripheral edema, and in many pediatric settings cirrhosis is a leading cause of this finding. Nephrotic syndrome and heart failure also cause edema but were not ranked above hepatic disease in this context, and kwashiorkor causes edema primarily in undernourished populations.'),
('a9e10422-1678-523c-ad4a-ed7f9c27aec9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of clubbing in children?', '["Inflammatory bowel disease","Lung disease (CF bronchiectasis)","Biliary cirrhosis","Infective endocarditis","Cyanotic congenital heart disease (especially TOF)"]'::jsonb, 'Cyanotic congenital heart disease (especially TOF)', 'Chronic arterial hypoxemia in cyanotic congenital heart disease, especially tetralogy of Fallot, stimulates tissue growth in the nail beds and is the most common cause of clubbing in children. Cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, biliary cirrhosis, and infective endocarditis can also produce clubbing but are less common pediatric causes.'),
('e920efd7-7392-55a8-9855-1795749583d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of differential cyanosis?', '["Truncus arteriosus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","TAPVR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TGA mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Patent ductus arteriosus with Eisenmenger (differential cyanosis: cyanosis in lower extremities only)","TOF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Patent ductus arteriosus with Eisenmenger (differential cyanosis: cyanosis in lower extremities only)', 'In PDA with Eisenmenger physiology, the ductus carries deoxygenated blood from the pulmonary artery to the descending aorta, so cyanosis appears only in the lower extremities (differential cyanosis). Truncus arteriosus, TAPVR, TGA, and TOF cause generalized cyanosis because the desaturated blood reaches the whole systemic circulation.'),
('8625733b-96a9-543f-98a1-a4e24a55ebe8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of differential clubbing?', '["TOF resulting in decreased functional activity","TGA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASD mediated by specific receptor activation","PDA with Eisenmenger syndrome (clubbing in toes only)","Coarctation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PDA with Eisenmenger syndrome (clubbing in toes only)', 'Prolonged hypoxemia of the lower body from ductal right-to-left shunting in PDA with Eisenmenger causes clubbing confined to the toes, the classic differential clubbing. TOF, TGA, and ASD produce generalized clubbing because cyanosis affects the entire body, and coarctation does not shunt deoxygenated blood to the aorta.'),
('e3a9757f-468d-5067-a93a-4d731bb7a355'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of a continuous murmur?', '["Peripheral pulmonary stenosis","AV fistula","Anomalous coronary artery","Venous hum","Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)"]'::jsonb, 'Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)', 'In PDA, aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary artery pressure throughout systole and diastole, so left-to-right shunting generates a continuous machinery murmur, making PDA the most common cause. AV fistulas and venous hum also produce continuous murmurs but are far less common.'),
('e8db6e46-b27c-5791-9993-a7f0f6ea314a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of an innocent (functional) heart murmur in children?', '["PDA resulting in decreased functional activity","VSD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Still murmur (vibratory systolic) and venous hum","ASD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary stenosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Still murmur (vibratory systolic) and venous hum', 'Flow-generated murmurs such as Still''s vibratory systolic murmur and the cervical venous hum occur in healthy children with normal hearts and are the most common innocent murmurs. PDA, VSD, ASD, and pulmonary stenosis each produce a pathologic murmur from an actual structural lesion.'),
('747b1c7c-97b4-5643-ae15-69acb1e70f52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of a mid-systolic click?', '["TriCuspid valve prolapse","Aortic valve sclerosis","Pulmonary valve stenosis","Aortic valve stenosis","Mitral valve prolapse (MVP)"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral valve prolapse (MVP)', 'In MVP the redundant leaflets billow into the left atrium during systole, snapping the chordae and producing a mid-systolic click often followed by a late systolic murmur. Aortic sclerosis and valvular stenosis produce murmurs rather than clicks, and tricuspid prolapse is uncommon.'),
('ec34bf67-3ae8-543c-bf8c-49333dbead88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of a pericardial friction rub?', '["Uremic pericarditis resulting in decreased functional activity","Tuberculosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute pericarditis (viral or idiopathic)","Cardiac surgery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MI (pericarditis) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute pericarditis (viral or idiopathic)', 'Inflamed pericardial layers rubbing together produce the triphasic friction rub, and viral or idiopathic acute pericarditis is its most common cause. Uremic pericarditis, tuberculous pericarditis, post-cardiotomy inflammation, and post-MI pericarditis also cause rubs but are less frequent.'),
('ba28e7d7-9175-5793-b368-07a6bd5303a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of chest pain in children?', '["Psychogenic","Respiratory causes (pneumonia pleurisy)","Cardiac causes (pericarditis myocarditis)","Musculoskeletal pain (costochondritis trauma)","GI causes (GERD)"]'::jsonb, 'Musculoskeletal pain (costochondritis trauma)', 'Musculoskeletal causes such as costochondritis and trauma account for the majority of chest pain in children; the vast majority of pediatric chest pain is noncardiac. Cardiac causes are rare but must be screened for, while respiratory, GI, and psychogenic causes are less common.'),
('59f38e4d-5282-56d3-a761-26bfc2285a83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of syncope in children?', '["Vasovagal (neurocardiogenic) syncope","Cardiac causes (long QT HCM arrhythmia)","Seizure","Psychogenic","Orthostatic hypotension"]'::jsonb, 'Vasovagal (neurocardiogenic) syncope', 'A reflex fall in blood pressure and heart rate makes vasovagal (neurocardiogenic) syncope the overwhelmingly most common cause of syncope in children. Cardiac syncope from long QT or HCM, seizures, and orthostatic hypotension are less frequent and need targeted evaluation.'),
('773956f7-0023-53c7-b8e7-8aea418b9d61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cardiac cause of syncope in children?', '["Long QT syndrome (torsades de pointes)","Pulmonary hypertension","Aortic stenosis","Arrhythmias (SVT ventricular tachycardia)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (LVOT obstruction)"]'::jsonb, 'Long QT syndrome (torsades de pointes)', 'Long QT syndrome predisposes to torsades de pointes, a self-limiting polymorphic ventricular tachycardia that causes abrupt loss of consciousness, making it the most common cardiac cause of pediatric syncope. HCM and aortic stenosis produce exertional syncope, and pulmonary hypertension and SVT are less common causes of syncope.'),
('f5c5f695-e76b-5d47-bfc0-fc21b1ee2f76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of palpitations in children?', '["SVT resulting in decreased functional activity","Premature beats (ectopic beats PACs PVCs)","Sinus tachycardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Atrial fibrillation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anxiety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Premature beats (ectopic beats PACs PVCs)', 'Benign premature beats (PACs and PVCs) are the most common cause of palpitations in children and are usually harmless in a structurally normal heart. SVT is the most common important arrhythmia but accounts for fewer palpitation presentations, and sinus tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, and anxiety are less common.'),
('68d4caff-dbd6-5554-95bb-466abf4d8370'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of ectopic beats in children?', '["Medications resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Electrolyte imbalance mediated by specific receptor activation","Benign (no underlying disease) often exacerbated by caffeine stress anxiety","Structural heart disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign (no underlying disease) often exacerbated by caffeine stress anxiety', 'In a structurally normal heart, isolated ectopic beats are almost always benign and are frequently triggered by caffeine, stress, or anxiety. Significant structural heart disease, electrolyte imbalance, thyroid disease, and medications are uncommon causes of ectopy in children.'),
('f7dc6a77-b2f4-51dc-b8e2-538073070d93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of SVT in children?', '["Atrial ectopic tachycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","AV nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrial flutter mediated by specific receptor activation","Junctional ectopic tachycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Accessory pathway (WPW syndrome) causing AV reentrant tachycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Accessory pathway (WPW syndrome) causing AV reentrant tachycardia', 'AV reentry over an accessory pathway (Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome) is the most common mechanism of paroxysmal SVT in children. AVNRT is most common in adults, whereas atrial ectopic tachycardia, junctional ectopic tachycardia, and atrial flutter are less common pediatric mechanisms.'),
('7e9adf64-76b1-5c15-b402-98f27a15c584'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of SVT in adults?', '["Atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation","Accessory pathway (WPW)","AV nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT)"]'::jsonb, 'AV nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT)', 'Reentry between the slow and fast AV nodal pathways makes AVNRT the most common sustained SVT in adults. In contrast, accessory pathway (WPW) reentry dominates in children, while atrial tachycardia, flutter, and fibrillation are usually related to structural or aging-related atrial disease.'),
('2bd78911-7993-50e4-80d5-872badea83ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of atrial flutter?', '["Pulmonary embolism resulting in decreased functional activity","Structural heart disease (CHD repaired post-cardiac surgery)","Thyrotoxicosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Alcohol without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Isolated idiopathic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Structural heart disease (CHD repaired post-cardiac surgery)', 'Atrial flutter usually requires a dilated or scarred atrium to sustain the reentrant circuit, and repaired congenital heart disease or postoperative scarring is the most common substrate, especially in children. Thyrotoxicosis, alcohol, and pulmonary embolism can trigger flutter in adults but are less common causes overall.'),
('8566eab6-da39-5010-bf98-64f5d07fb39c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of ventricular tachycardia in children?', '["Post-cardiac surgery (especially after tetralogy of Fallot repair)","CHD (TOF) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myocarditis mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiomyopathy (HCM DCM ARVC) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Long QT syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-cardiac surgery (especially after tetralogy of Fallot repair)', 'Ventricular incisions and scar, especially the right ventriculotomy after tetralogy of Fallot repair, create reentrant circuits that make post-cardiac surgery the most common cause of VT in children. Cardiomyopathies, long QT syndrome, and acute myocarditis are important but less frequent causes.'),
('b8d74739-8dea-55a8-9d71-b970823638b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in the first year of life?', '["Congenital heart disease","Sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS)","Long QT syndrome","Myocarditis","Inborn errors of metabolism"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS)', 'Sudden infant death syndrome remains the most common cause of sudden unexpected death in the first year of life. Congenital heart disease, myocarditis, long QT syndrome, and metabolic errors account for only a minority of infant deaths.'),
('75dc08a3-33e4-5924-9b07-05a85d921d5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of death in patients with unoperated tetralogy of Fallot?', '["Arrhythmia resulting in decreased functional activity","Endocarditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoxic (tet) spells and cerebral complications (stroke brain abscess)","Heart failure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxic (tet) spells and cerebral complications (stroke brain abscess)', 'Unoperated tetralogy of Fallot carries recurrent hypercyanotic (tet) spells, chronic hypoxemia, and cerebral complications such as stroke and brain abscess, which are the leading causes of death. Arrhythmia, endocarditis, and pulmonary hypertension are less important in the unoperated natural history.'),
('2efefdb5-c8e1-50f8-a90e-b8cd1d7839a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of death late after tetralogy of Fallot repair?', '["Heart failure resulting in decreased functional activity","Endocarditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Residual VSD mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary regurgitation with RV dysfunction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sudden cardiac death from ventricular arrhythmia"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden cardiac death from ventricular arrhythmia', 'Late after repair, ventricular scar and residual hemodynamic load sustain monomorphic VT, making sudden cardiac death the leading cause of late mortality. Pulmonary regurgitation with RV dysfunction contributes to symptoms, while residual VSD, heart failure, and endocarditis are less common modes of death.'),
('c843a501-26c8-5020-b5fe-938f5dd96b85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of right ventricular outflow tract obstruction after TOF repair?', '["Residual infundibular stenosis","Pulmonary valve regurgitation (from transannular patch)","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Peripheral pulmonary stenosis","Conduit obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary valve regurgitation (from transannular patch)', 'The most common RVOT problem after TOF repair is free pulmonary regurgitation from the transannular patch, which volume overloads the right ventricle and is functionally the dominant residual RVOT abnormality. True residual obstruction from infundibular or conduit stenosis and peripheral pulmonary stenosis is less common.'),
('6dd81288-11e9-540f-b2bf-cf9555642621'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of death after Fontan procedure?', '["Thromboembolism resulting in decreased functional activity","Plastic bronchitis / protein-losing enteropathy","Hepatic failure mediated by specific receptor activation","Fontan failure / heart failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arrhythmia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Plastic bronchitis / protein-losing enteropathy', 'Fontan physiology raises systemic venous and lymphatic pressures, and plastic bronchitis with protein-losing enteropathy are serious complications that can be fatal. Thromboembolism, hepatic failure, and arrhythmia also contribute to late Fontan mortality but are less commonly the cause of death than these lymphatic complications.'),
('c0a6361d-e95a-58d3-b58e-abb383474050'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of heart failure in repaired tetralogy of Fallot?', '["Chronic pulmonary valve regurgitation causing RV volume overload and failure","Residual pulmonary stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Residual VSD mediated by specific receptor activation","Left ventricular dysfunction from VSD closure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tricuspid regurgitation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic pulmonary valve regurgitation causing RV volume overload and failure', 'After TOF repair, chronic free pulmonary regurgitation progressively volume overloads the right ventricle, leading to RV dilatation and failure, the most common mechanism of late heart failure. Residual VSD, residual pulmonary stenosis, LV systolic dysfunction, and tricuspid regurgitation are less common contributors.'),
('2f7d0a20-fc1b-5265-a777-e7da9f2ca00f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common valve lesion after repair of tetralogy of Fallot?', '["Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation","Pulmonary stenosis","Pulmonary regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary regurgitation', 'A transannular patch sacrifices pulmonary valve competence, so pulmonary regurgitation is the most common valve lesion after TOF repair. Residual pulmonary stenosis is less typical, and mitral, tricuspid, or aortic regurgitation are uncommon in this setting.'),
('0a4d9d65-7690-5b9b-91e9-138fcf0fe44d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of death in adults with CHD?', '["Thromboembolism","Complications of pregnancy","Heart failure (most common)","Arrhythmia (sudden cardiac death)","Pulmonary hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Heart failure (most common)', 'Supported adults with congenital heart disease most commonly die from heart failure caused by chronic ventricular volume and pressure overload. Arrhythmia with sudden cardiac death, pulmonary hypertension, and thromboembolism also occur but rank below heart failure as the overall leading cause of death in this population.'),
('e655e6d6-ce53-537d-b4e2-cd7e08d6f4ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of heart failure in adults with CHD?', '["LV failure from pressure/volume overload resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Systemic RV failure (e.g. L-TGA or D-TGA after atrial switch)","Arrhythmia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Valvular disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic RV failure (e.g. L-TGA or D-TGA after atrial switch)', 'When the morphological right ventricle supports the systemic circulation, as in congenitally corrected L-TGA or after an atrial switch for D-TGA, it fails over time, making systemic RV failure the most common cause of heart failure in adults with CHD. Pulmonary hypertension, mitral/aortic valve disease, and arrhythmia can coexist but are not the dominant substrate.'),
('18ac5b79-f4f0-5bc2-bd3b-11e2fbbf461d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common reason for reintervention after CHD repair?', '["Pulmonary valve replacement for chronic PR after TOF repair","VSD closure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coarctation stent mediated by specific receptor activation","ASD closure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PDA closure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary valve replacement for chronic PR after TOF repair', 'Late pulmonary regurgitation after TOF repair progressively dilates the right ventricle, making pulmonary valve replacement the most common late reintervention in adults with CHD. Residual shunt closures for VSD, ASD, and PDA or coarctation stenting are done earlier or less frequently.'),
('141d625c-b243-5134-978b-38dff07da05d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of endocarditis in adults with CHD?', '["Cyanotic CHD with right-to-left shunt (BT shunt Glenn Fontan)","Coarctation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VSD patch mediated by specific receptor activation","Valvular lesions (aortic regurgitation) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ASD patch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyanotic CHD with right-to-left shunt (BT shunt Glenn Fontan)', 'Cyanotic lesions with right-to-left shunting and palliative conduits (BT shunt, Glenn, Fontan) expose abnormal endothelial surfaces and turbulent flow, the most common substrate for endocarditis in adults with CHD. Healed VSD or ASD patches, repaired coarctation, and simple aortic regurgitation carry lower risk.'),
('2a458e6e-3d0c-5b20-8b89-7cee01cb4159'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of aortic regurgitation in adults?', '["Bicuspid aortic valve with dilation (most common) and infective endocarditis","Annuloaortic ectasia (Marfan) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic dissection mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rheumatic heart disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bicuspid aortic valve with dilation (most common) and infective endocarditis', 'A bicuspid aortic valve with progressive root and annular dilation is the most common cause of chronic aortic regurgitation in adults, and infective endocarditis is the classic acute cause. Annuloaortic ectasia, aortic dissection, and rheumatic disease cause AR but are less common in developed settings, and hypertension alone does not damage the valve.'),
('78ed5c5d-2fee-559c-920a-56526339cbf5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of aortic aneurysm in adults?', '["Trauma","Vasculitis (Takayasu giant cell)","Atherosclerosis (degenerative abdominal aortic aneurysm)","Infection (mycotic)","Genetically triggered (Marfan Loeys-Dietz Ehlers-Danlos)"]'::jsonb, 'Atherosclerosis (degenerative abdominal aortic aneurysm)', 'Degenerative atherosclerosis is the most common cause of aortic aneurysm, typically producing an infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm in older adults with vascular risk factors. Genetic syndromes, vasculitis, mycotic infection, and trauma are much less common and tend to affect different aortic segments.'),
('1fa53053-2bd3-50e1-a36b-2e63e86078ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of thoracic aortic aneurysm?', '["Atherosclerosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Cystic medial degeneration (Marfan Loeys-Dietz) and hypertension","Vasculitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic medial degeneration (Marfan Loeys-Dietz) and hypertension', 'Cystic medial degeneration of the aortic wall, whether from a connective tissue syndrome or merely age-related fraying, together with chronic hypertension is the most common mechanism of thoracic (especially ascending) aortic aneurysm. Atherosclerosis mainly affects the abdominal aorta, and infection, trauma, and vasculitis are rare causes.'),
('4b16f169-00f1-589c-afc4-1302f4c5d265'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA)?', '["Genetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vasculitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Mycotic (infection) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atherosclerosis (degenerative)"]'::jsonb, 'Atherosclerosis (degenerative)', 'Atherosclerosis is by far the most common cause of abdominal aortic aneurysm, typically infrarenal and associated with smoking, hypertension, and older age. Genetic, mycotic, vasculitic, and traumatic causes are rare.'),
('c12fe132-1721-5e2b-a80b-ac2d8fe2e04b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of aortic dissection?', '["Bicuspid aortic valve","Hypertension (chronic uncontrolled hypertension)","Cocaine use","Vasculitis (giant cell arteritis)","Connective tissue disease (Marfan Ehlers-Danlos)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension (chronic uncontrolled hypertension)', 'Chronic uncontrolled hypertension drives wall stress and medial degeneration, making it the dominant common cause of aortic dissection in adults. Connective tissue disease and bicuspid aortic valve predispose younger patients, while cocaine use and vasculitis are uncommon causes.'),
('1f00b824-a548-591c-a5ea-8ff9fc055a12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common site of aortic dissection?', '["Root of the aorta","Aortic arch","Ascending aorta (Type A Stanford)","Abdominal aorta","Descending aorta (Type B Stanford)"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending aorta (Type A Stanford)', 'Stanford type A dissection involves the ascending aorta and is far more common than type B. The ascending aorta bears the highest pulsatile wall stress, making it the favored site of intimal tearing. Type B (descending aortic) dissection is less frequent and usually managed medically.'),
('89aa6bdb-8a38-5e73-8b36-ee7763bcdd60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of Stanford type A aortic dissection?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Cocaine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pregnancy mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension and underlying aortic wall disease (cystic medial degeneration)","Bicuspid aortic valve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension and underlying aortic wall disease (cystic medial degeneration)', 'Long-standing hypertension combined with degeneration of the aortic media (cystic medial degeneration) weakens the ascending aortic wall and is the leading setting for type A dissection. Trauma, cocaine, pregnancy, and bicuspid aortic valve are recognized but uncommon precipitating factors by comparison.'),
('92bb985f-2f8e-5307-bf29-fd7361851f65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of Stanford type B aortic dissection?', '["Chronic hypertension","Vasculitis","Trauma","Atherosclerosis","Pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic hypertension', 'Chronic hypertension produces progressive medial weakening of the descending thoracic aorta and is the dominant risk factor for type B dissection. Vasculitis, trauma, atherosclerosis, and pregnancy are far less common associations with descending aortic dissection.'),
('93473a2c-4aad-57d5-b3ce-05539a028774'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cerebral embolism?', '["Atrial myxoma","Atrial fibrillation (cardioembolic source)","Carotid artery plaque (artery-to-artery)","Infective endocarditis","Paradoxical embolus (PFO)"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial fibrillation (cardioembolic source)', 'Atrial fibrillation causes stasis within the atria and promotes thrombus formation, and embolization from this cardioembolic source is the most frequent cause of cerebral embolism. Carotid plaque, infective endocarditis, atrial myxoma, and paradoxical embolism are important but less common embolic mechanisms.'),
('4fc26a0d-fe8a-5340-a8cd-4bbccd1718c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of stroke in patients with atrial fibrillation?', '["Embolism from the left atrial appendage","Carotid stenosis","Aortic arch plaque","Intracranial small vessel disease","Venous thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 'Embolism from the left atrial appendage', 'In atrial fibrillation, stasis favors thrombus formation in the left atrial appendage, and its embolization is the usual mechanism of stroke in these patients. Carotid stenosis, aortic arch plaque, small vessel disease, and venous thrombosis do not account for cardiogenic embolism from the fibrillating atrium.'),
('b047cd9d-6590-5ec8-9c48-1d22b8147855'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of ischemic stroke in young adults?', '["Migrainous infarction","Cervical artery dissection (carotid vertebral)","Cardioembolism (PFO) paradoxial embolus","Hypercoagulable states","Vasculitis"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical artery dissection (carotid vertebral)', 'Spontaneous dissection of the carotid or vertebral arteries is the most commonly identified cause of ischemic stroke in young adults, typically tearing the intima and allowing luminal thrombus to form. Migrainous infarction, PFO-related paradoxical embolism, hypercoagulable states, and vasculitis are less common.'),
('56fc8b03-16b2-544e-946d-91df055cc736'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of myocardial infarction in young adults?', '["Thromboembolism","Coronary vasospasm","Coronary artery dissection (spontaneous)","Atherosclerosis (premature CAD)","Anomalous coronary artery"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary artery dissection (spontaneous)', 'Spontaneous coronary artery dissection is a leading cause of acute myocardial infarction in young adults, particularly young women, in whom atherosclerotic disease burden is still low. Premature atherosclerosis, vasospasm, thromboembolism, and anomalous coronary origin occur but are less common in this age group.'),
('5c214fc4-3246-585e-8a55-1b21b333ba3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common congenital cause of myocardial infarction in children?', '["Kawasaki disease (coronary aneurysm) resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary vasospasm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myocarditis mediated by specific receptor activation","Genetic dyslipidemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anomalous left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery (ALCAPA)"]'::jsonb, 'Anomalous left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery (ALCAPA)', 'In ALCAPA the left coronary artery arises from the low-pressure, desaturated pulmonary trunk, so the left ventricle is poorly perfused and becomes ischemic, accounting for infantile myocardial infarction. This congenital malformation is therefore the most common congenital cause of MI in children; Kawasaki disease, vasospasm, myocarditis, and dyslipidemia are not congenital mechanisms.'),
('9f4b181e-c598-5e74-baaf-ff2150a5250b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of MI in older adults?', '["Vasculitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Embolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary vasospasm mediated by specific receptor activation","Atherosclerotic coronary artery disease with plaque rupture and thrombosis","Demand ischemia (supply-demand mismatch) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atherosclerotic coronary artery disease with plaque rupture and thrombosis', 'Atherosclerotic plaque rupture with superimposed intracoronary thrombosis causes most infarctions in older adults, reflecting decades of accumulated coronary disease. Vasculitis, embolism, coronary spasm, and pure demand ischemia are far less frequent mechanisms at this age.'),
('795def6e-525e-5d47-be61-2bbea4b21452'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common coronary artery affected in MI?', '["Left anterior descending (LAD) artery","Left circumflex artery (LCx)","Left main coronary artery","Ramus intermedius","Right coronary artery (RCA)"]'::jsonb, 'Left anterior descending (LAD) artery', 'The LAD supplies the largest myocardial territory, so it is the vessel most often occluded in myocardial infarction, producing anteroseptal injury. The LCx and RCA are less commonly the culprit, while left main occlusion and the ramus intermedius are rare sites.'),
('7b810b8d-8420-5a71-9175-84d0060aff50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pericarditis after MI?', '["Dressler syndrome (post-myocardial infarction pericarditis 1-2 weeks after MI)","Uremic pericarditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute pericarditis during MI (transmural MI) mediated by specific receptor activation","Traumatic pericarditis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial pericarditis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dressler syndrome (post-myocardial infarction pericarditis 1-2 weeks after MI)', 'Dressler syndrome is the autoimmune (post-infarction) pericarditis that appears 1 to 2 weeks after MI and is the classic cause taught for late post-MI pericarditis. Early fibrinous pericarditis over a transmural infarct in the first few days is a separate earlier event, and uremic, traumatic, and bacterial pericarditis are unrelated to the post-MI timing.'),
('8d0af590-9f7f-59b8-992e-5e966cc2ad98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cardiac rupture after MI?', '["IV septum rupture resulting in decreased functional activity","Rupture of the LV outflow tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Papillary muscle rupture mediated by specific receptor activation","Rupture of the RV free wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Free wall rupture (most common complication) followed by ventricular septal rupture and papillary muscle rupture"]'::jsonb, 'Free wall rupture (most common complication) followed by ventricular septal rupture and papillary muscle rupture', 'Rupture of the left ventricular free wall is the most common cardiac rupture after infarction, occurring typically 3 to 5 days post-MI and often fatal from tamponade. Ventricular septal rupture and papillary muscle rupture are less frequent mechanical complications, and rupture of the RV outflow tract is rare.'),
('2007453f-77ef-5d74-908c-3a02f67cd437'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of mitral regurgitation after MI?', '["LV remodeling resulting in decreased functional activity","Leaflet tethering accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Annular dilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Papillary muscle dysfunction (due to ischemia or infarction of the posteromedial papillary muscle)","Rupture of a papillary muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary muscle dysfunction (due to ischemia or infarction of the posteromedial papillary muscle)', 'The posteromedial papillary muscle relies on a single blood supply, so ischemia or infarction readily produces papillary muscle dysfunction and acute mitral regurgitation; this is more common than frank papillary rupture. Annular dilation, leaflet tethering, and LV remodeling contribute to MR but are not the principal post-infarct mechanism.'),
('4e104d80-9904-5fdd-a513-4b7ee11ea87d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of ventricular septal rupture after MI?', '["Right ventricular infarction resulting in decreased functional activity","Septal rupture from transmural septal infarction (most commonly LAD occlusion)","Atrial septal rupture mediated by specific receptor activation","RCA occlusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","LCx occlusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Septal rupture from transmural septal infarction (most commonly LAD occlusion)', 'Transmural infarction of the interventricular septum, most often from LAD occlusion, weakens the septal myocardium and permits rupture in the first week after MI. RCA or LCx occlusion produces septal ischemia less frequently, and right ventricular infarction alone seldom causes septal rupture; atrial septal rupture is not a recognized complication.'),
('51d1766d-f044-5372-8e7a-0c2c33660566'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cardiogenic shock in MI?', '["Ventricular septal rupture","Extensive LV infarction (loss of >40% of LV myocardium)","Free wall rupture","Papillary muscle rupture with acute MR","Right ventricular infarction"]'::jsonb, 'Extensive LV infarction (loss of >40% of LV myocardium)', 'Loss of more than 40% of the left ventricular myocardium abolishes sufficient pumping capacity and is the fundamental cause of cardiogenic shock after infarction. Mechanical complications such as septal rupture, free wall rupture, papillary muscle rupture with acute MR, and RV infarction precipitate shock only secondarily.'),
('781bc8c4-ee2c-5e1a-a7a5-8672a195d651'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of death in the perioperative period of cardiac surgery?', '["Arrhythmia resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low cardiac output syndrome (LCOS)","Neurologic injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemorrhage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low cardiac output syndrome (LCOS)', 'Low cardiac output syndrome reflects inadequate pump function (sometimes with impaired tissue perfusion and acidosis) in the immediate postoperative window and is the most common cause of perioperative death in cardiac surgery. Arrhythmia, infection, neurologic injury, and hemorrhage are important but less frequent fatal complications.'),
('268e1e5a-a80c-5bc5-87f0-a79d54e3262e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of low cardiac output after CHD surgery?', '["Sepsis resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Residual structural lesions mediated by specific receptor activation","Arrhythmia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myocardial depression from cardiopulmonary bypass and inadequate myocardial protection"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial depression from cardiopulmonary bypass and inadequate myocardial protection', 'Temporary myocardial depression from cardiopulmonary bypass and inadequate myocardial protection is the usual cause of low cardiac output after congenital heart surgery. Residual structural lesions, arrhythmia, sepsis, and pulmonary hypertension may contribute but are less common primary causes.'),
('fb2efe22-ac39-5a9d-9fc6-4312f6970c22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pleural effusion after cardiac surgery?', '["Chylothorax (thoracic duct injury) resulting in decreased functional activity","Pneumonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heart failure with fluid overload mediated by specific receptor activation","Post-pericardiotomy syndrome (autoimmune inflammatory response)","Pulmonary embolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-pericardiotomy syndrome (autoimmune inflammatory response)', 'Post-pericardiotomy syndrome, an autoimmune inflammatory response triggered by pericardial manipulation, is the most common cause of a pleural effusion arising after cardiac surgery, typically days to weeks later. Chylothorax, pneumonia, heart failure with fluid overload, and pulmonary embolism are less common.'),
('1d67b265-e8df-50ac-9158-6376c5e726fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of chylothorax after cardiac surgery?', '["Central line placement resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Injury to the thoracic duct during surgery (especially during aortic coarctation repair or Blalock-Taussig shunt)","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Spontaneous without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Injury to the thoracic duct during surgery (especially during aortic coarctation repair or Blalock-Taussig shunt)', 'The thoracic duct courses on the left side of the mediastinum and is at risk during left-sided procedures such as aortic coarctation repair or Blalock-Taussig shunt, yielding a chylous lymphatic effusion. This surgical injury is the most frequent cause after cardiac surgery; central lines, trauma, spontaneous leak, and tumor are far less common.'),
('c55c4fd4-fdf8-533f-a302-6bafbe6665ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of Horner syndrome associated with cardiac surgery?', '["Brachial plexus injury resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancoast tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Injury to the cervical sympathetic chain (stellate ganglion during BT shunt or coarctation repair)","Carotid dissection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Injury to the cervical sympathetic chain (stellate ganglion during BT shunt or coarctation repair)', 'The cervical sympathetic chain and stellate ganglion lie adjacent to structures dissected during BT shunts and coarctation repair, so surgical manipulation injures them and produces Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis). Brachial plexus injury, Pancoast tumor, carotid dissection, and external trauma are not typical surgical complications of these operations.'),
('883f980a-f555-536f-b024-21a691ee09fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent laryngeal nerve injury after cardiac surgery?', '["Injury during PDA ligation or coarctation repair (affecting the left recurrent laryngeal nerve)","Central line placement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extubation trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Thoracotomy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic arch surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Injury during PDA ligation or coarctation repair (affecting the left recurrent laryngeal nerve)', 'The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks beneath the aortic arch and is vulnerable during PDA ligation and coarctation repair, causing left vocal cord paresis. Aortic arch surgery, thoracotomy, central lines, and extubation trauma are less frequent causes of recurrent laryngeal injury.'),
('edf57016-c3d1-5c1e-af9e-6708dc311af6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of phrenic nerve palsy after cardiac surgery?', '["Positioning injury resulting in decreased functional activity","Cold injury from ice slush used for myocardial protection","Trauma during dissection mediated by specific receptor activation","Central line placement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardioplegia administration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cold injury from ice slush used for myocardial protection', 'Topical ice slush used for myocardial protection can cold-injure the phrenic nerve where it crosses over the pericardium, producing hemi-diaphragm paralysis. Positioning, dissection trauma, central line placement, and cardioplegia delivery do not typically damage the nerve.'),
('d568a71e-78d2-5062-895e-f18e9d457fe6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of diaphragmatic paralysis in infants after cardiac surgery?', '["Eventration resulting in decreased functional activity","Neuromuscular disease (spinal muscular atrophy) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myasthenia gravis mediated by specific receptor activation","Phrenic nerve injury (from cold injury or trauma during surgery)","Spinal cord injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phrenic nerve injury (from cold injury or trauma during surgery)', 'Phrenic nerve injury from cold exposure or surgical traction during the operation is the most common cause of diaphragmatic paralysis in infants after cardiac surgery. Eventration is a congenital diaphragm defect, and neuromuscular disease, myasthenia gravis, and spinal cord injury are unrelated to surgery.'),
('761e0bef-4744-5d23-aec1-0056cdb8faaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pericardial effusion after cardiac surgery?', '["Post-pericardiotomy syndrome (inflammatory)","Infection (bacterial pericarditis)","Heart failure with fluid overload","Hemorrhage (postoperative bleeding)","Uremia"]'::jsonb, 'Post-pericardiotomy syndrome (inflammatory)', 'Post-pericardiotomy syndrome underlies the inflammatory pericardial effusion most commonly seen after cardiac surgery. Postoperative hemorrhage, bacterial infection, heart failure with fluid overload, and uremia are less frequent causes of effusion in this setting.'),
('ed54ee97-0e93-5c82-97cf-6a35553846d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cardiac tamponade after cardiac surgery?', '["Delayed PPS effusion resulting in decreased functional activity","Chylopericardium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mediastinal hemorrhage in the early postoperative period","Massive pleural effusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumopericardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mediastinal hemorrhage in the early postoperative period', 'Rapid mediastinal hemorrhage in the early postoperative period compresses the cardiac chambers and is the most common cause of tamponade after surgery. A delayed post-pericardiotomy effusion, chylopericardium, massive pleural effusion, and pneumopericardium appear later or are rare.'),
('e8f06e3e-3aca-5096-81e3-4b86ac954c45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of bradycardia in children?', '["Sick sinus syndrome (after cardiac surgery especially Mustard/Senning)","Complete heart block","Increased ICP","Medication side effects","Sinus bradycardia (normal in athletes sleep vagal tone)"]'::jsonb, 'Sinus bradycardia (normal in athletes sleep vagal tone)', 'Sinus bradycardia results from the strong resting vagal tone of children and athletes and is a normal rhythm rather than a disorder. Sick sinus syndrome and complete heart block are pathological bradyarrhythmias, while increased intracranial pressure and medications slow the heart much less commonly.'),
('7dc37c4a-8e2e-5dc5-87f3-de5c2a534008'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of acquired complete heart block in children?', '["Medications resulting in decreased functional activity","Lyme disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Post-surgical (especially VSD closure and TOF repair)","Myocarditis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kawasaki disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-surgical (especially VSD closure and TOF repair)', 'The atrioventricular conduction system sits close to the septal patch, so VSD closure and TOF repair frequently injure it, making postsurgical block the most common acquired complete heart block in children. Lyme disease, myocarditis, medications, and Kawasaki disease are less common causes.'),
('86d28f5e-f3ac-533b-8c4c-ad36d5c17f8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of neonatal bradycardia?', '["Congenital heart block (SLE) resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoxia (primary cause of neonatal bradycardia is respiratory distress or apnea)","Medications mediated by specific receptor activation","Sepsis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoglycemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxia (primary cause of neonatal bradycardia is respiratory distress or apnea)', 'Neonatal bradycardia is usually a secondary response to hypoxia from respiratory distress or apnea rather than a primary cardiac rhythm disorder. Congenital heart block, sepsis, hypoglycemia, and medications are less common, and correcting hypoxia restores the heart rate.'),
('163e0ad0-fc6b-5765-9538-fc0c17ba790c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common rhythm in a healthy newborn?', '["Junctional rhythm resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinus bradycardia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sinus arrhythmia mediated by specific receptor activation","Atrial tachycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sinus tachycardia (normal heart rate 120-160 bpm)"]'::jsonb, 'Sinus tachycardia (normal heart rate 120-160 bpm)', 'A healthy newborn rests at a heart rate of 120 to 160 bpm, which is relatively fast and represents sinus tachycardia by adult standards. Sinus arrhythmia is a normal breathing-linked variation, whereas bradycardia, junctional rhythm, and atrial tachycardia are abnormal.'),
('f0a48d7a-3adb-557a-b30b-a8d0fc791f37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of sinus tachycardia in children?', '["Supraventricular tachycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyrotoxicosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary embolism mediated by specific receptor activation","Fever (most common) dehydration anxiety pain exercise","Heart failure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fever (most common) dehydration anxiety pain exercise', 'Fever is the most common physiological trigger of sinus tachycardia in children, with dehydration, pain, anxiety, and exercise also raising heart rate. Supraventricular tachycardia, thyrotoxicosis, pulmonary embolism, and heart failure cause tachycardia but far less often in routine pediatric practice.'),
('b443e6a8-bc59-5e8c-a27c-f5fdff179347'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of acute rheumatic fever?', '["Pneumonia resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin infection (impetigo) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Viral infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Group A streptococcal pharyngitis (Streptococcus pyogenes)","Scarlet fever leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Group A streptococcal pharyngitis (Streptococcus pyogenes)', 'Acute rheumatic fever follows pharyngeal infection with group A Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes) via molecular mimicry between streptococcal and host antigens. Skin infection (impetigo) with the same organism does not trigger rheumatic fever, and pneumonia, viral infection, and scarlet fever are not the inciting cause.'),
('6aec056a-cfa7-5ca2-930a-9a6e96477c53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cardiac manifestation of acute rheumatic fever?', '["Pericarditis","Myocarditis","Conduction abnormalities (PR prolongation)","Heart failure","Valvulitis (predominantly mitral then aortic)"]'::jsonb, 'Valvulitis (predominantly mitral then aortic)', 'Valvulitis is the most common cardiac manifestation of acute rheumatic fever, mainly involving the mitral and then aortic valve and producing regurgitant murmurs. Myocarditis, pericarditis, and PR prolongation occur but are less frequent or less prominent; heart failure follows severe valvular dysfunction.'),
('f3cfd084-7b37-506c-abe7-ae92215d33ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common valvular lesion in chronic rheumatic heart disease?', '["Aortic stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Mitral stenosis (most common chronic rheumatic lesion)","Mitral regurgitation mediated by specific receptor activation","Aortic regurgitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tricuspid regurgitation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral stenosis (most common chronic rheumatic lesion)', 'Repeated rheumatic valvulitis causes leaflet fusion, thickening, and fibrosis that culminate in mitral stenosis, the most common chronic valvular lesion of rheumatic heart disease. Aortic stenosis and regurgitant lesions of any valve occur less frequently in the chronic phase.'),
('8f6263a2-6a5b-565b-8afa-520939345aac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of mitral stenosis in the world?', '["Congenital mitral stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Endocarditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rheumatic heart disease (virtually only cause of significant MS)","Carcinoid syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mitral annular calcification leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rheumatic heart disease (virtually only cause of significant MS)', 'Rheumatic heart disease is virtually the only cause of significant mitral stenosis worldwide, as a late sequel of childhood rheumatic carditis. Congenital stenosis, endocarditis, carcinoid syndrome, and mitral annular calcification cause narrowing only rarely.'),
('0688d2f9-f444-50aa-badd-36b4f9210b5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of aortic stenosis in the young (<50 years)?', '["Infective endocarditis","Subvalvular aortic stenosis","Calcific aortic stenosis","Rheumatic aortic valve disease","Bicuspid aortic valve (congenital)"]'::jsonb, 'Bicuspid aortic valve (congenital)', 'A congenitally bicuspid aortic valve calcifies and stenoses earlier, making it the leading cause of aortic stenosis before age 50. Calcific degeneration of a trileaflet valve dominates in later life, while endocarditis, rheumatic disease, and subvalvular stenosis are less common.'),
('553d5011-97b4-595b-a3db-e97a0d31bfb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of aortic stenosis in the elderly (>70 years)?', '["Aortic sclerosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Rheumatic aortic stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bicuspid aortic valve mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcific (degenerative) tricuspid aortic valve stenosis","Annular calcification leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcific (degenerative) tricuspid aortic valve stenosis', 'Degenerative calcification of a trileaflet aortic valve from lifelong wear is the leading cause of stenosis after age 70. A bicuspid valve generates stenosis at a younger age, and rheumatic disease or isolated annular calcification is less common in the elderly.'),
('8f437d2d-f232-5c31-926f-b53ece5e7d35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pure aortic regurgitation?', '["Bicuspid aortic valve (with ascending aortic dilation) and infective endocarditis","Annuloaortic ectasia (Marfan) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Syphilitic aortitis (rare) mediated by specific receptor activation","Rheumatic heart disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic dissection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bicuspid aortic valve (with ascending aortic dilation) and infective endocarditis', 'Pure aortic regurgitation is most often caused by a bicuspid aortic valve with ascending aortic dilation or by infective endocarditis destroying leaflet coaptation. Marfan annuloaortic ectasia, syphilitic aortitis, rheumatic disease, and dissection are important but less common causes of isolated AR.'),
('4964b5e4-d53e-5879-9ca7-2c476e7f4e46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of acute aortic regurgitation?', '["Aortic dissection","Infective endocarditis","Rheumatic fever","Trauma","Syphilitic aortitis"]'::jsonb, 'Infective endocarditis', 'Infective endocarditis damages the aortic leaflets acutely, producing the rapid-onset murmur and hemodynamic collapse of acute aortic regurgitation. Aortic dissection, rheumatic fever, trauma, and syphilitic aortitis cause acute AR much less commonly.'),
('afac6878-9b92-5fe8-ab7d-415df24c6be4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of bicuspid aortic valve?', '["Syndromic","Genetic (NOTCH1 mutation associated with some families)","Chromosomal abnormality","Sporadic (most cases are unknown without genetic cause)","Hereditary (autosomal dominant)"]'::jsonb, 'Sporadic (most cases are unknown without genetic cause)', 'Most bicuspid aortic valves arise sporadically without an identifiable genetic or syndromic cause. NOTCH1 mutations are found in only some families, and chromosomal or recognized hereditary syndromes account for a minority of cases.'),
('0b77ea0c-3132-5306-9ab2-1adc531aa5c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common associated anomaly with bicuspid aortic valve?', '["Tetralogy of Fallot resulting in decreased functional activity","PDA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASD mediated by specific receptor activation","VSD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic coarctation (about 50% of coarctation patients have bicuspid AV)"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic coarctation (about 50% of coarctation patients have bicuspid AV)', 'Aortic coarctation shares a developmental link with the bicuspid aortic valve; roughly half of coarctation patients carry a bicuspid valve, making it the most common associated anomaly. PDA, VSD, and ASD coexist in some patients but are less frequent associations.'),
('c87bfcef-ff3d-5a1c-b007-08fc4ea889e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of primary pulmonary hypertension (PAH) in children?', '["CHD-related (Eisenmenger) resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary vein stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thromboembolism mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung disease (BPD) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic (IPAH formerly primary pulmonary hypertension) and heritable (BMPR2 mutation)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (IPAH formerly primary pulmonary hypertension) and heritable (BMPR2 mutation)', 'Primary pulmonary hypertension in children is most often idiopathic (formerly primary) or heritable through BMPR2 mutations. Eisenmenger physiology from congenital heart disease, thromboembolism, chronic lung disease, and pulmonary vein stenosis are secondary causes that are less common in the primary setting.'),
('3fd9d1f6-1a52-5769-9915-ae919c47f942'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension in newborns?', '["Diaphragmatic hernia resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary vein stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CHD with increased pulmonary blood flow mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung hypoplasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN) from meconium aspiration or congenital diaphragmatic hernia"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN) from meconium aspiration or congenital diaphragmatic hernia', 'Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn arises after insults such as meconium aspiration or congenital diaphragmatic hernia that keep pulmonary vascular resistance high and drive right-to-left shunting; it is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension in newborns. Diaphragmatic hernia and lung hypoplasia act through this pathway, while CHD and pulmonary vein stenosis are less frequent.'),
('596f368c-0f1a-5f32-9961-a943da140c8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension in premature infants?', '["CHD resulting in decreased functional activity","Patent ductus arteriosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sepsis mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD) from chronic lung disease of prematurity","Pulmonary vein stenosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD) from chronic lung disease of prematurity', 'Bronchopulmonary dysplasia from chronic lung disease of prematurity raises pulmonary vascular resistance and is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension in premature infants. Congenital heart disease, patent ductus arteriosus, sepsis, and pulmonary vein stenosis are less common mechanisms.'),
('3322e99b-b8f5-5ce9-bc49-cb60bc12263c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension in children outside the newborn period?', '["HIV infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Connective tissue disease (scleroderma SLE) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital heart disease with left-to-right shunt (Eisenmenger)","Portal hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic PAH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital heart disease with left-to-right shunt (Eisenmenger)', 'A large left-to-right shunt from congenital heart disease elevates pulmonary pressures and eventually yields Eisenmenger physiology, the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension in children beyond the newborn period. HIV, connective tissue disease, portal hypertension, and idiopathic PAH are far less common in childhood.'),
('73503479-cfd6-50ae-b92f-71a072662cbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of peripheral pulmonary stenosis (PPS) in children?', '["William syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal physiologic PPS in newborns (benign and resolves)","Congenital rubella syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Noonan syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alagille syndrome (JAG1 mutation) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal physiologic PPS in newborns (benign and resolves)', 'Physiologic peripheral pulmonary stenosis of newborns is a benign, self-limiting murmur produced by relative hypoplasia of the branch pulmonary arteries at birth and is the most common explanation of this finding. Williams, rubella, Noonan, and Alagille syndromes cause pathological peripheral stenosis but are rare.'),
('088ecd08-ee1b-5587-952e-2e92cc32d09d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of myocardial ischemia in newborns?', '["Kawasaki disease (coronary aneurysms) resulting in decreased functional activity","Sepsis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subendocardial ischemia from severe AS or HLHS mediated by specific receptor activation","Anomalous left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery (ALCAPA)","Respiratory distress with hypoxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anomalous left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery (ALCAPA)', 'Origin of the left coronary artery from the pulmonary trunk supplies the left ventricle with low-pressure, desaturated blood and is the most common cause of myocardial ischemia in newborns. Kawasaki disease, sepsis, subendocardial ischemia from severe AS or HLHS, and hypoxic respiratory disease occur less commonly in this age group.'),
('7f6e7da4-bfcd-5601-9b04-6b3d3a8ea8f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of Kawasaki disease?', '["Autoimmune resulting in decreased functional activity","Viral infection (EBV adenovirus) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bacterial infection (Staph Strep) mediated by specific receptor activation","Drug reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unknown (likely infectious trigger in genetically susceptible children)"]'::jsonb, 'Unknown (likely infectious trigger in genetically susceptible children)', 'The cause of Kawasaki disease remains unknown despite extensive study, with a suspected infectious trigger operating in genetically susceptible children. Autoimmune, viral, bacterial, and drug-related hypotheses have not been confirmed.'),
('1e840791-ffd4-5e44-a433-b831d449ade6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common complication of Kawasaki disease?', '["Arrhythmia resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary artery aneurysm (25% of untreated cases)","Myocarditis mediated by specific receptor activation","Valvulitis (mitral regurgitation) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pericarditis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary artery aneurysm (25% of untreated cases)', 'Untreated Kawasaki disease produces coronary artery aneurysm in about 25% of cases, making it the most common complication. Myocarditis, valvulitis, pericarditis, and arrhythmia occur during the acute phase but are less frequent than coronary involvement.'),
('67502be6-16f7-5e24-b149-11fa919f70ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of acquired heart disease in children in developed countries?', '["Rheumatic heart disease","Cardiomyopathy","Myocarditis","Hypertension","Kawasaki disease"]'::jsonb, 'Kawasaki disease', 'With the sharp decline of rheumatic fever, Kawasaki disease has become the most common cause of acquired heart disease in children in developed countries. Myocarditis, cardiomyopathy, and hypertension are less common, whereas rheumatic heart disease dominates in developing regions.'),
('80e2e140-529b-5e91-83a2-fe2b603e524a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of sudden death in Kawasaki disease?', '["Ruptured coronary artery aneurysm with myocardial infarction","Ruptured cerebral aneurysm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thromboembolism mediated by specific receptor activation","Myocarditis with heart failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arrhythmia (ventricular tachycardia) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ruptured coronary artery aneurysm with myocardial infarction', 'Myocardial infarction from rupture or thrombosis of a coronary artery aneurysm is the most common cause of sudden death in Kawasaki disease. Arrhythmia, myocarditis with heart failure, cerebral aneurysm rupture, and thromboembolism are less frequent mechanisms.'),
('04165c16-5f6d-50e3-9509-c845334c038b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common heart disease in adults in developed countries?', '["Hypertensive heart disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart failure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary artery disease (ischemic heart disease)","Cardiomyopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Valvular heart disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coronary artery disease (ischemic heart disease)', 'Ischemic heart disease from atherosclerosis is the leading cardiac disorder in adults in the developed world and dominates cardiac morbidity and mortality. Hypertension, heart failure, and valvular or myopathic disease are less prevalent causes.'),
('796700e5-e47a-5edc-96ee-5bdbdb1d800b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of coronary artery disease?', '["Embolism resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atherosclerosis (plaque formation from risk factors: smoking hypertension diabetes dyslipidemia)","Coronary vasospasm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vasculitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atherosclerosis (plaque formation from risk factors: smoking hypertension diabetes dyslipidemia)', 'Atherosclerotic plaque formation narrows and progressively occludes the coronary arteries and is the fundamental mechanism underlying coronary artery disease. Embolism, spasm, trauma, and vasculitis account for only a small minority of cases.'),
('26a037e0-49b0-52c1-a7e6-23b6ef0bba11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common modifiable risk factor for coronary artery disease?', '["Obesity","Smoking","Diabetes mellitus","Dyslipidemia","Hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Smoking', 'Smoking carries the highest population-attributable risk among the modifiable risk factors, and smoking cessation sharply lowers coronary risk. Obesity, hypertension, diabetes, and dyslipidemia are also modifiable but rank below smoking in overall impact.'),
('0819a8ad-f817-5993-bac4-79313a1942cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of death from coronary artery disease?', '["Heart failure post-MI resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute myocardial infarction with fatal arrhythmia or pump failure","Stroke mediated by specific receptor activation","Sudden cardiac death without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congestive heart failure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute myocardial infarction with fatal arrhythmia or pump failure', 'Most deaths from coronary artery disease result from acute myocardial infarction complicated by fatal ventricular arrhythmia or severe pump (left ventricular) failure. Heart failure and stroke are consequences of coronary disease rather than its most frequent lethal mechanism.'),
('bcefea80-b93f-5817-a81d-ae97bb5b5bdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of out-of-hospital cardiac arrest?', '["SVT with hypotension resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricular fibrillation (VF) / pulseless ventricular tachycardia (pVT) due to ischemic heart disease","Pulseless electrical activity (PEA) mediated by specific receptor activation","Asystole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bradycardia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular fibrillation (VF) / pulseless ventricular tachycardia (pVT) due to ischemic heart disease', 'Out-of-hospital cardiac arrest is most often caused by shockable rhythms, chiefly ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia, arising from ischemic heart disease. Non-shockable rhythms such as PEA and asystole are proportionally less common in the community setting.'),
('99104f3d-6e43-5226-a470-8ca7debee4a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of in-hospital cardiac arrest?', '["Asystole and PEA (less likely to be shockable rhythms compared to out-of-hospital)","VF/pVT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SVT mediated by specific receptor activation","Bradycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AV block leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asystole and PEA (less likely to be shockable rhythms compared to out-of-hospital)', 'In-hospital arrests are predominantly non-shockable, with asystole and pulseless electrical activity predominating because severe comorbidity and monitored deterioration are frequent. Ventricular fibrillation and pulseless VT occur but less often than in out-of-hospital arrest.'),
('4455f73d-99c5-54de-8727-238f52a91ee1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of atrioventricular block in adults?', '["Congenital heart block resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiac surgery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medications (beta-blockers calcium channel blockers) mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrosis of the conduction system (Lenegre-Lev disease) and ischemic heart disease","Myocarditis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrosis of the conduction system (Lenegre-Lev disease) and ischemic heart disease', 'Atrioventricular block in adults most often reflects idiopathic fibrosis of the conduction system (Lenegre-Lev disease) or ischemic damage to the node and bundle. Drugs, surgery, myocarditis, and congenital heart block are less frequent causes.'),
('ba8988f8-8cda-51c4-a3cd-c0236344d8bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of left bundle branch block (LBBB)?', '["Hypertension and coronary artery disease (ischemic heart disease)","Pulmonary embolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dilated cardiomyopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Aortic valve stenosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension and coronary artery disease (ischemic heart disease)', 'Left bundle branch block in adults most commonly results from chronic hypertension and coronary artery disease damaging the left bundle branch. Pulmonary embolism, aortic stenosis, and dilated cardiomyopathy are less frequent associations.'),
('08e2f3df-2e2c-5409-a868-6828c6939aa4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of right bundle branch block (RBBB)?', '["CHD (ASD VSD)","Pulmonary embolism","Pulmonary hypertension","Idiopathic (normal variant especially in athletes)","Cardiac surgery (post-surgical RBBB)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (normal variant especially in athletes)', 'Right bundle branch block is often an incidental conduction finding in otherwise healthy people, especially athletes, without demonstrable cardiac pathology. Structural right-heart disease and postoperative conduction injury are less common causes.'),
('f6a5f5bf-f4b4-56f3-a413-dc761b8b9463'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of biventricular hypertrophy in children?', '["Tetralogy of Fallot (RVH) and VSD with pulmonary hypertension (biventricular)","LVH from aortic stenosis/RCA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HLHS mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiomyopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ASD (RV volume overload) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tetralogy of Fallot (RVH) and VSD with pulmonary hypertension (biventricular)', 'Biventricular hypertrophy in children most often pairs the right ventricular hypertrophy of tetralogy of Fallot with an additional left ventricular load, or reflects a large VSD that stresses both ventricles through pulmonary hypertension. Lesions producing isolated LV or RV hypertrophy are comparatively rare.'),
('6f272b47-0a82-56b9-897f-c4a92d42d09e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of right ventricular hypertrophy in children?', '["Pulmonary hypertension","Tetralogy of Fallot (most common cause of RVH)","VSD with high PVR","ASD (volume overload causes less hypertrophy)","Pulmonary valve stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Tetralogy of Fallot (most common cause of RVH)', 'Tetralogy of Fallot is the most common congenital cause of right ventricular hypertrophy in children because pulmonary stenosis raises right ventricular afterload. Shunt-related overload and primary pulmonary hypertension are less common causes.'),
('27a120fd-a056-560a-8e72-cc7846392d71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of left atrial enlargement?', '["LV dysfunction/HFpEF","Mitral valve disease (stenosis or regurgitation)","Cardiomyopathy","Hypertension","Atrial fibrillation (secondary to LA enlargement)"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral valve disease (stenosis or regurgitation)', 'Mitral stenosis or regurgitation imposes left atrial pressure and volume overload, producing the largest left atrial enlargement. Hypertension and left ventricular dysfunction enlarge the left atrium secondarily but are less typical causes.'),
('ae42bd5b-b5cc-52ed-9fc7-2b246feb7af1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH) in children?', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Coarctation of the aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Athlete''s heart mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic stenosis (valvular subvalvular supravalvular)"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic stenosis (valvular subvalvular supravalvular)', 'Aortic stenosis is the most common cause of left ventricular hypertrophy in childhood because it imposes a fixed pressure overload on the left ventricle. Hypertension and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy are rare in children, and coarctation is a less common mechanism.'),
('2bd55452-db64-5c12-aae0-c459ab742aac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of left ventricular hypertrophy in adults?', '["Hypertension (concentric LVH)","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (asymmetric LVH)","Obesity","Aortic stenosis","Athlete''s heart (eccentric LVH)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension (concentric LVH)', 'Chronic hypertension produces sustained pressure overload and concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, the most common cause of LVH in adults. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, obesity, aortic stenosis, and athlete''s heart are less common causes.'),
('b2a8bafb-687d-5707-895e-a3b13bae135d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of right atrial enlargement?', '["Pulmonary stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Ebstein anomaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASD mediated by specific receptor activation","Tricuspid regurgitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary hypertension (cor pulmonale) and tricuspid valve disease"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary hypertension (cor pulmonale) and tricuspid valve disease', 'Right atrial enlargement most often follows pulmonary hypertension with cor pulmonale or tricuspid valve disease, both of which raise right atrial pressure and volume load. Atrial septal defect and pulmonary stenosis contribute but are less frequently responsible.'),
('c48e59f8-2576-5cab-ac14-398319137433'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cardiomegaly on chest X-ray in children?', '["CHD with increased pulmonary blood flow","Cardiomyopathy","Valvular disease","Pericardial effusion","Large left-to-right shunt (VSD PDA)"]'::jsonb, 'Large left-to-right shunt (VSD PDA)', 'Large left-to-right shunts such as VSD and PDA increase pulmonary venous return and enlarge all four chambers, yielding cardiomegaly in children. Cardiomyopathy, valvular disease, and pericardial effusion are less common causes.'),
('dda25e5a-1c2b-538f-8e29-e1fd20dbb06a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of decreased pulmonary vascularity on CXR in a cyanotic newborn?', '["TGA resulting in decreased functional activity","TAPVR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tetralogy of Fallot (decreased pulmonary blood flow from pulmonary stenosis)","HLHS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Truncus arteriosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tetralogy of Fallot (decreased pulmonary blood flow from pulmonary stenosis)', 'In a cyanotic newborn, tetralogy of Fallot decreases pulmonary blood flow through pulmonary stenosis, producing oligemic (dark) lung fields on CXR. Lesions with increased pulmonary flow such as TGA, TAPVR, and truncus arteriosus instead show pulmonary plethora.'),
('6aa357e7-90d8-5008-bf9a-5ae0bfc09be3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of increased pulmonary vascularity on CXR?', '["Transposition of the great arteries","Truncus arteriosus","Left-to-right shunt (VSD ASD PDA) causing pulmonary overcirculation","Total anomalous pulmonary venous return (TAPVR)","Single ventricle with obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Left-to-right shunt (VSD ASD PDA) causing pulmonary overcirculation', 'A left-to-right shunt (VSD, ASD, or PDA) increases pulmonary blood flow and produces plethoric lung fields on chest radiography. Cyanotic obstructive and mixing lesions characteristically show normal or reduced pulmonary vascularity.'),
('fc416589-dfe9-5942-9a15-6c38d966278d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary edema on CXR in children?', '["Dilated cardiomyopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Myocarditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left-sided obstructive lesions (mitral stenosis cor triatriatum) and left ventricular failure","Pulmonary venous hypertension (from any cause) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Overhydration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left-sided obstructive lesions (mitral stenosis cor triatriatum) and left ventricular failure', 'Pulmonary edema in children most often results from left-sided obstructive lesions such as mitral stenosis or cor triatriatum, or from left ventricular failure raising pulmonary venous pressure. Dilated cardiomyopathy and myocarditis are less common causes, and overhydration is rare.'),
('85fb6a41-9d90-5996-805c-4aab54bef65a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pleural effusion in children?', '["Cardiac surgery (post-pericardiotomy) resulting in decreased functional activity","Parapneumonic effusion (complicated pneumonia)","Malignancy mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parapneumonic effusion (complicated pneumonia)', 'Parapneumonic effusion complicating pneumonia is by far the most common cause of pleural fluid in children. Heart failure, malignancy, and postsurgical collections are much less frequent.'),
('c962054b-286f-5883-ac98-de3a54dfe697'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of mediastinal mass in children?', '["T-cell lymphoma (anterior mediastinum)","Lymphangioma (cystic hygroma)","Teratodermal cysts (anterior mediastinum)","Bronchogenic cysts","Neurogenic tumors (posterior mediastinum)"]'::jsonb, 'Neurogenic tumors (posterior mediastinum)', 'Neurogenic tumors of the posterior mediastinum, including neuroblastoma and ganglioneuroma, are the most common mediastinal masses in children. Lymphoma and developmental cysts are less common than neurogenic tumors.'),
('cfefcc61-e3e7-59fb-862f-b73078e77cc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of a middle mediastinal mass in children?', '["Lymphoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphadenopathy (infectious or malignant)","Bronchogenic cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericardial cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphadenopathy (infectious or malignant)', 'The middle mediastinum contains the tracheobronchial lymph nodes, so lymphadenopathy from infection or lymphoma is the most common middle mediastinal mass in children. Bronchogenic and pericardial cysts are uncommon at this site.'),
('5ae6d4bd-898c-5d44-ac55-88471d80dba0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of a mediastinal mass in adults?', '["Teratoderma cyst","Thymoma (anterior mediastinum)","Lymphoma","Neurogenic tumor (posterior)","Gotter"]'::jsonb, 'Thymoma (anterior mediastinum)', 'Thymoma is the most common mediastinal mass in adults and typically occupies the anterior mediastinum. Lymphoma, neurogenic tumors, developmental cysts, and goiter are less frequent overall.'),
('69952fd0-19be-58fb-a489-4910a23a9e9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common benign mediastinal tumor in adults?', '["Pericardial cyst","Neurogenic tumor (schwannoma neurofibroma)","Thymoma","Bronchogenic cyst","Teratoma"]'::jsonb, 'Thymoma', 'Thymoma is the most common benign mediastinal tumor in adults and usually arises in the anterior mediastinum. Cysts, schwannomas, and teratomas are less frequent benign mediastinal lesions.'),
('ce83c0e2-bd4f-5537-bbda-e74e019888d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common malignant germ cell tumor of the mediastinum?', '["Yolk sac tumor","Teratoma (immature)","Seminoma (germinoma)","Choriocarcinoma","Teratoma (mature)"]'::jsonb, 'Seminoma (germinoma)', 'Among germ cell tumors, seminoma (germinoma) is the most common malignant form to arise in the mediastinum, typically in young men. Yolk sac tumor, choriocarcinoma, and immature teratoma are rarer malignant germ cell tumors.'),
('d3326af8-c371-52c3-b964-3753123dd76d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of superior vena cava syndrome in children?', '["Massive pleural effusion resulting in decreased functional activity","Mediastinal mass (lymphoma) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pericardial tamponade mediated by specific receptor activation","Postoperative after cardiac surgery (especially Mustard/Senning)","Central line thrombosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postoperative after cardiac surgery (especially Mustard/Senning)', 'In children with congenital heart disease, SVC syndrome most often follows cardiac surgery such as the Mustard or Senning atrial switch procedures. Mediastinal masses, tamponade, and central line thrombosis are less common causes.'),
('144fb142-cf94-5fcf-8b8d-d05ed35bcfc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of superior vena cava syndrome in adults?', '["Thymoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Goiter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SVC thrombosis (central line) mediated by specific receptor activation","Extrinsic compression by lung cancer (small cell or squamous cell)","Lymphoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extrinsic compression by lung cancer (small cell or squamous cell)', 'In adults, SVC syndrome is most commonly caused by extrinsic compression by lung cancer, especially small cell or squamous cell carcinoma involving the right upper lobe and paratracheal nodes. Lymphoma and central-line thrombosis are less common causes.'),
('039186a1-139e-5eba-98bf-34fa07ee7698'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of thromboembolism in children with CHD?', '["Post-surgery (especially Glenn/Fontan)","CHD itself (right-to-left shunt)","Prosthetic valve","Central venous line (most common)","Arrhythmia"]'::jsonb, 'Central venous line (most common)', 'Central venous lines are the most common source of thromboembolism in children with heart disease because catheter-related thrombosis occurs frequently. The underlying CHD, surgery, and prosthetic valves contribute less often.'),
('3046b5ec-99bb-5bd6-b995-d27e137d73f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary embolism in children?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypercoagulable state (Factor V Leiden) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep vein thrombosis (from central line or immobility)","CHD (right-to-left shunt) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep vein thrombosis (from central line or immobility)', 'Pulmonary embolism in children usually arises from deep vein thrombosis related to central venous lines or immobility. Hypercoagulable states and surgery are predisposing factors rather than the dominant mechanism.'),
('6ffddaf4-9aa4-5a21-a711-aa08ecce6437'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of paradoxical embolism in children?', '["Fat embolism resulting in decreased functional activity","Right-to-left shunt from cyanotic CHD (ASD VSD PDA PFO)","Septic embolism mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep vein thrombosis directly crossing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Air embolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right-to-left shunt from cyanotic CHD (ASD VSD PDA PFO)', 'A right-to-left shunt in cyanotic congenital heart disease lets venous emboli cross the septum into the systemic circulation, causing paradoxical embolism. Fat, air, and septic emboli are uncommon causes in children.'),
('4db77505-2c1e-5e67-a4a0-64da192126c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cyanosis in an infant with CHD and a right-to-left shunt?', '["Decreased pulmonary blood flow resulting in decreased functional activity","The shunt allows deoxygenated blood to bypass the lungs and enter the systemic circulation","Increased pulmonary blood flow mediated by specific receptor activation","Mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above are correct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The shunt allows deoxygenated blood to bypass the lungs and enter the systemic circulation', 'In a right-to-left shunt, deoxygenated systemic venous blood bypasses the lungs and enters the systemic arterial circulation, causing arterial desaturation and cyanosis. Reduced pulmonary flow or mere mixing does not alone account for cyanosis in this physiology.'),
('60db41d3-4230-55a9-a5c3-6c1e9e3d4b6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of tachypnea in a child with CHD?', '["Anxiety resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fever mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary edema from heart failure or increased pulmonary blood flow","Compensation for metabolic acidosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary edema from heart failure or increased pulmonary blood flow', 'Tachypnea in children with CHD most often reflects pulmonary edema from heart failure or increased pulmonary blood flow overwhelming the lungs. Fever, pain, and metabolic acidosis are less common causes of rapid breathing.'),
('36d43f8d-e9a2-5ab3-8aa8-4d21b10cf173'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of hepatomegaly in a child with CHD?', '["Right heart failure (systemic venous congestion)","Hepatitis from transfusions","Glycogen storage disease from cyanosis (rare)","Pericardial effusion","Metastatic neuroblastoma"]'::jsonb, 'Right heart failure (systemic venous congestion)', 'Hepatomegaly in children with CHD most often results from right heart failure transmitting elevated systemic venous pressure to the liver. Pericardial effusion, transfusion-related hepatitis, and metabolic disease are less common causes.'),
('ff636115-55e9-5724-a385-69d11fca8021'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of failure to thrive (FTT) in CHD?', '["Recurrent infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic hypoxia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased metabolic demand from tachypnea and heart failure with decreased caloric intake","Malabsorption (intestinal lymphangiectasia from Fontan) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endocrine abnormalities leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased metabolic demand from tachypnea and heart failure with decreased caloric intake', 'Failure to thrive in CHD combines an increased metabolic demand from tachypnea and heart failure with a reduced caloric intake. Recurrent infection, malabsorption, and endocrine disease are much less common causes.'),
('bcbafce3-0d7d-5942-9168-f8be30096ca3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of feeding difficulty in infants with CHD?', '["Taste disturbance from medications resulting in decreased functional activity","GERD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neurologic impairment mediated by specific receptor activation","Tachypnea (respiratory rate >60) prevents coordinated suck-swallow-breathe","Anatomic obstruction (vascular ring) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tachypnea (respiratory rate >60) prevents coordinated suck-swallow-breathe', 'When the respiratory rate exceeds 60 breaths per minute, there is no inter-breath pause for coordinated suck-swallow-breathe cycling, so infants cannot feed effectively. GERD, neurologic impairment, and vascular rings are less common causes.'),
('851b4137-65c1-5c71-b91b-5093e1414d7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of polycythemia in CHD?', '["Dehydration resulting in decreased functional activity","Renal tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Polycythemia vera (rare in children) mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic hypoxemia from cyanotic CHD (compensatory increase in erythropoietin)","Chronic lung disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic hypoxemia from cyanotic CHD (compensatory increase in erythropoietin)', 'Chronic hypoxemia in cyanotic CHD stimulates erythropoietin release, expanding red cell mass and producing polycythemia. Dehydration, polycythemia vera, and renal tumors are uncommon causes in children.'),
('84639992-5094-5fc1-ae82-f9f5a28dbd3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common complication of polycythemia in cyanotic CHD?', '["Hyperviscosity syndrome (headache visual disturbance stroke)","Pulmonary hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gout mediated by specific receptor activation","Thromboembolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bleeding diathesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperviscosity syndrome (headache visual disturbance stroke)', 'A markedly elevated hematocrit raises blood viscosity and produces hyperviscosity syndrome with headache, visual disturbance, and stroke. Gout, thromboembolism, and bleeding are related complications rather than the most frequent one.'),
('5945c179-7ccc-5cf9-851c-99274ea41d28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of hyperuricemia in cyanotic CHD?', '["Medication side effect (diuretics) resulting in decreased functional activity","Gout accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renal dysfunction mediated by specific receptor activation","Dehydration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Polycythemia causing increased cell turnover and uric acid production"]'::jsonb, 'Polycythemia causing increased cell turnover and uric acid production', 'The expanded red cell mass in polycythemia increases cell turnover and uric acid production, causing hyperuricemia in cyanotic heart disease. Diuretic use and renal dysfunction contribute but are less common causes.'),
('52297777-ffc0-5045-9e1f-e9a45a66980e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of stroke in cyanotic CHD?', '["Cerebral abscess resulting in decreased functional activity","Paradoxical embolus (right-to-left shunt) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Polycythemia with hyperviscosity causing cerebral venous thrombosis","Bleeding diathesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Polycythemia with hyperviscosity causing cerebral venous thrombosis', 'Stroke in cyanotic CHD most often follows cerebral venous thrombosis caused by polycythemia with hyperviscosity and sluggish cerebral blood flow. Paradoxical embolism and cerebral abscess are less common mechanisms.'),
('d766479f-1f68-54a9-8bf3-9d08dac81689'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of hemoptysis in CHD?', '["Pulmonary embolism resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchiectasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary vascular disease (Eisenmenger with pulmonary hemorrhage)","Pulmonary infarction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary vascular disease (Eisenmenger with pulmonary hemorrhage)', 'Hemoptysis in CHD usually indicates advanced pulmonary vascular disease, as in Eisenmenger syndrome, where the hypertensive pulmonary vessels rupture and bleed. Pulmonary embolism, bronchiectasis, and infarction are less common causes.'),
('6f6471ee-99d2-503c-a9f9-61a251c4bdad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of hoarseness in CHD?', '["Laryngitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-surgical RLN injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intubation mediated by specific receptor activation","Ortner syndrome (recurrent laryngeal nerve compression from dilated pulmonary artery in pulmonary hypertension)","Mediastinal mass leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ortner syndrome (recurrent laryngeal nerve compression from dilated pulmonary artery in pulmonary hypertension)', 'Hoarseness in CHD is most often due to Ortner syndrome, in which the dilated pulmonary artery of pulmonary hypertension compresses the left recurrent laryngeal nerve. Vocal cord injury from intubation or surgery is less common.'),
('46b9936d-bbc0-5505-9763-dd792cdaf54e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of scoliosis in CHD?', '["Posture related resulting in decreased functional activity","Associated with cyanotic CHD (especially TOF) possibly related to prior thoracotomy","Genetic syndrome (Marfan Noonan) mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone disease from chronic hypoxia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuromuscular disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Associated with cyanotic CHD (especially TOF) possibly related to prior thoracotomy', 'Scoliosis in CHD is most often associated with cyanotic defects such as tetralogy of Fallot, partly related to prior thoracotomy and thoracic asymmetry. Postural, syndromic, and neuromuscular causes are less frequent.'),
('cec36004-f1b9-51dd-9c36-37b041a17d36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of osteoarthropathy in CHD?', '["Rheumatologic disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Polycythemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperuricemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic hypoxia causing hypertrophic osteoarthropathy (periostitis and clubbing)"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic hypoxia causing hypertrophic osteoarthropathy (periostitis and clubbing)', 'Chronic hypoxia in cyanotic CHD stimulates hypertrophic osteoarthropathy with periostitis and clubbing, causing bone and joint aching. Polycythemia, infection, and rheumatologic disease are less common causes.'),
('f563f24d-7b69-520f-a0c7-2cd43bd10f37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease?', '["Salmonella (most common)","Staphylococcus aureus","Pseudomonas","E. coli","Streptococcus pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'Salmonella (most common)', 'In sickle cell disease, Salmonella is the most common cause of osteomyelitis because microinfarcts in the gut permit Salmonella bacteremia that seeds infarcted bone. Staphylococcus aureus dominates osteomyelitis in the general population.'),
('84e49962-4a6a-5247-8b5f-4b6baed00f90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of cardiac tumor in children with tuberous sclerosis?', '["Hemangioma resulting in decreased functional activity","Myxoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Teratoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rhabdomyoma (most common pediatric cardiac tumor)","Fibroma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rhabdomyoma (most common pediatric cardiac tumor)', 'Rhabdomyoma is the most common cardiac tumor of childhood and is strongly associated with tuberous sclerosis. Myxoma is the most common cardiac tumor in adults, while fibroma, teratoma, and hemangioma are rarer.'),
('c84460d8-25d9-530a-8cb4-822f5984165e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of sudden death in adults with repaired CHD?', '["Aortic dissection resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricular arrhythmia (sudden cardiac death)","Pulmonary hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Thromboembolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart failure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular arrhythmia (sudden cardiac death)', 'Ventricular arrhythmia is the most common cause of sudden death in adults with repaired CHD, arising from myocardial scar and residual hemodynamic load. Heart failure, dissection, and thromboembolism cause fewer deaths.'),
('b8df4d56-fd76-5ce7-b4d4-edcbdeb17653'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of death in adults with D-TGA after atrial switch (Mustard/Senning)?', '["Systemic RV failure (RV dysfunction and tricuspid regurgitation)","Thromboembolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Sudden cardiac death without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Baffle complications (obstruction leak) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic RV failure (RV dysfunction and tricuspid regurgitation)', 'After the Mustard or Senning atrial switch for D-TGA, the morphologic right ventricle serves the systemic circuit and eventually fails, with tricuspid regurgitation accelerating decline; systemic RV failure is the leading cause of death. Baffle complications and thromboembolism are less common.'),
('90c8fd37-5548-573d-a56b-741ce83e7e73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'What is the most common cause of death in adults with Fontan circulation?', '["Plastic bronchitis","Protein-losing enteropathy","Thromboembolism","Hepatic failure","Heart failure (Fontan failure)"]'::jsonb, 'Heart failure (Fontan failure)', 'In the Fontan circulation, progressive heart failure (Fontan failure) is the most common cause of death as the single ventricle and passive pulmonary bed decompensate. Protein-losing enteropathy, plastic bronchitis, thromboembolism, and hepatic failure are complications with lower mortality.'),
('4e2242c8-e909-5ab5-8ac0-5bc20e475829'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'Which congenital heart disease shows an ''egg-on-side'' cardiac silhouette on CXR?', '["Patent ductus arteriosus","Ventricular septal defect","Coarctation of aorta","Tetralogy of Fallot","Transposition of great arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Transposition of great arteries', 'In transposition of the great arteries, the aorta and pulmonary artery are aligned anteroposteriorly, so the cardiac silhouette appears narrow like an egg on its side. The other lesions have their own radiographic patterns but not this classic sign.'),
('03cc4378-cfdc-5a13-aaf4-ab0416a2bb14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Cardiovascular System', 'In cardiovascular development, what is the order of structures formed by the primitive heart tube from inflow to outflow?', '["Bulbus cordis ventricle atrium sinus venosus truncus arteriosus","Sinus venosus bulbus cordis atrium ventricle truncus arteriosus","Truncus arteriosus bulbus cordis ventricle atrium sinus venosus","Atrium ventricle sinus venosus truncus arteriosus bulbus cordis","Sinus venosus atrium ventricle bulbus cordis truncus arteriosus"]'::jsonb, 'Sinus venosus atrium ventricle bulbus cordis truncus arteriosus', 'The primitive heart tube differentiates in inflow-to-outflow sequence into the sinus venosus, atrium, ventricle, bulbus cordis, and truncus arteriosus. This order must proceed from the venous inflow end toward the arterial outflow end.'),
('fcb8bd0f-a016-5747-a52b-86f50d646bf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The central nervous system develops from which germ layer?', '["Neural crest","Mesoderm","Neuroectoderm (neural plate)","Surface ectoderm","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Neuroectoderm (neural plate)', 'The neural plate is a midline specialization of the embryonic neuroectoderm whose folding and fusion produce the neural tube, the primordium of the entire CNS. Neural crest arises from the neural folds and forms peripheral structures, while mesoderm, surface ectoderm, and endoderm give rise to non-neural tissues.'),
('8565f078-6a61-5a3d-8357-fb6871f4ab50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The neural tube forms by which process?', '["Gastrulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurulation (folding and fusion of the neural plate)","Organogenesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Histogenesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Somitogenesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurulation (folding and fusion of the neural plate)', 'Neurulation is the folding of the neural plate edges and their midline fusion into a closed neural tube. Gastrulation establishes the three germ layers beforehand, while organogenesis, histogenesis, and somitogenesis are separate, later developmental events.'),
('dc0438a7-b879-5f1d-a98d-f40dde2f91eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What congenital anomaly results from failure of the cranial neuropore to close?', '["Spina bifida","Meningocele","Anencephaly","Encephalocele","Hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 'Anencephaly', 'If the cranial neuropore fails to close by about day 25, the developing forebrain remains exposed and degenerates, producing anencephaly. Spina bifida and its subtypes result from failure of the caudal neuropore to close, not the cranial one.'),
('009473da-b36f-5a8d-b0f7-8fe92d056fed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The neural crest cells arise from which region of the neural folds?', '["Floor plate","Dorsolateral region","Roof plate","Ventromedial region","Basal plate"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsolateral region', 'Neural crest cells are specified bilaterally at the dorsolateral tips (crests) of the neural folds and emigrate as the tube closes. The floor plate, roof plate, and basal plate are regions of the neural tube wall itself, not the source of crest cells.'),
('2c14ef8d-dd65-55df-b2ab-e57ce34de1a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Neural crest cells give rise to all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Peripheral neurons (DRG autonomic)","Neurons of the CNS","Adrenal medulla","Melanocytes","Facial bones and cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Neurons of the CNS', 'Neural crest contributes peripheral (dorsal root and autonomic) neurons, chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla, melanocytes, and cranial facial skeleton and cartilage. CNS neurons are produced by the neuroepithelium of the neural tube, not by neural crest.'),
('623d8edc-ce88-5785-ad70-f183e05a0e13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common neural tube defect?', '["Anencephaly resulting in decreased functional activity","Spina bifida (especially occulta)","Meningocele mediated by specific receptor activation","Myelomeningocele without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Encephalocele leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spina bifida (especially occulta)', 'Spina bifida occulta, where only the vertebral arches fail to fuse over an intact cord, is the mildest and by far the most common form of neural tube defect. Meningocele, myelomeningocele, and anencephaly are more severe or less frequent variants.'),
('3e704700-df44-5d70-b124-11447e52ea80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the incidence of neural tube defects worldwide?', '["1 per 50 resulting in decreased functional activity","000 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 1-2 per 1000 live births (varies by population)","1 per 100 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 per 10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1-2 per 1000 live births (varies by population)', 'Worldwide, neural tube defects affect roughly 1-2 per 1000 live births, with notable geographic and ethnic variation. The other listed frequencies are far too high to represent the global incidence.'),
('9def32cf-9354-53cd-81b2-8e0bed151e16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Folic acid supplementation reduces the risk of NTDs by what percentage?', '["50%","Up to 70%","100%","90%","20%"]'::jsonb, 'Up to 70%', 'Periconceptional folic acid supplementation reduces both the occurrence and recurrence risk of neural tube defects by up to 70%. Because it does not eliminate the risk entirely, figures such as 100% or 90% are not accurate.'),
('2c7cf73c-c8e7-5289-83ee-f7cf6780c904'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the recommended daily dose of folic acid for NTD prevention?', '["200 mcg resulting in decreased functional activity","100 mcg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","50 mcg mediated by specific receptor activation","400 mcg prenatally (for high-risk 4 mg)","1000 mcg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '400 mcg prenatally (for high-risk 4 mg)', 'All women of reproductive age are advised to take 400 mcg of folic acid daily, and those with a previous affected pregnancy take 4 mg per day. The lower doses listed are below the amount needed for effective neural tube defect prevention.'),
('e0a704a2-8c3a-57c6-abfe-f83d3aac2ba5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The three primary brain vesicles (3-week stage) are:', '["Telencephalon mesencephalon myelencephalon","Telencephalon diencephalon metencephalon","Prosencephalon mesencephalon rhombencephalon","Mesencephalon metencephalon myelencephalon","Forebrain midbrain hindbrain"]'::jsonb, 'Prosencephalon mesencephalon rhombencephalon', 'At the three-week stage the rostral neural tube dilates into the forebrain (prosencephalon), midbrain (mesencephalon), and hindbrain (rhombencephalon). The five-vesicle subdivisions listed in the other options appear only at the five-week stage.'),
('9f3ff682-56fa-55aa-b0af-ab769de60d83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The five secondary brain vesicles (5-week stage) are:', '["Telencephalon diencephalon mesencephalon metencephalon myencephalon","Prosencephalon mesencephalon rhombencephalon metencephalon myelencephalon","Forebrain midbrain hindbrain pons medulla","Telencephalon diencephalon mesencephalon metencephalon myelencephalon","Telencephalon mesencephalon metencephalon myelencephalon diencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Telencephalon diencephalon mesencephalon metencephalon myelencephalon', 'By five weeks the prosencephalon splits into telencephalon and diencephalon, the rhombencephalon divides into metencephalon and myelencephalon, and the mesencephalon persists. The other lists either misspell or omit a vesicle or combine adult terms with developmental ones.'),
('dd1d183f-395f-5ba7-88dd-c2d6ed9c2cb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The cerebral hemispheres develop from which vesicle?', '["Metenchephalon","Myelencephalon","Mesencephalon","Diencephalon","Telencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Telencephalon', 'The paired cerebral hemispheres grow as lateral outpouchings of the telencephalon, giving rise to the cortex and basal ganglia. The diencephalon forms the thalami and hypothalamus, while the mesencephalon, metencephalon, and myelencephalon form midbrain, pons/cerebellum, and medulla respectively.'),
('8e771550-b56c-57ab-9819-2034b3b27587'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The pons and cerebellum develop from which vesicle?', '["Metenchephalon","Telencephalon","Diencephalon","Mesencephalon","Myelencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Metenchephalon', 'The metencephalon differentiates into the pons ventrally and the cerebellum dorsally. The myelencephalon becomes the medulla, the mesencephalon the midbrain, and the telencephalon and diencephalon form forebrain structures.'),
('de9cf33f-7d5e-590c-8cea-acc002020016'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The medulla oblongata develops from which vesicle?', '["Myelencephalon","Mesencephalon","Metenchephalon","Diencephalon","Telencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Myelencephalon', 'The myelencephalon is the caudal division of the hindbrain and differentiates into the medulla oblongata. The metencephalon forms the pons and cerebellum, the mesencephalon the midbrain, and the diencephalon and telencephalon produce forebrain structures.'),
('bd90b161-1dc6-5f88-99c1-d575f1a4e99b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the cavity of the telencephalon?', '["Central canal","Lateral ventricles","Fourth ventricle","Third ventricle","Cerebral aqueduct"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral ventricles', 'The cavity within the paired telencephalic vesicles persists as the lateral ventricles, which connect to the third ventricle through the interventricular foramina. The third ventricle, aqueduct, fourth ventricle, and central canal belong to the other brain divisions and spinal cord.'),
('80b3780d-d314-5319-bc26-f00845ef935a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the cavity of the diencephalon?', '["Third ventricle","Lateral ventricles","Fourth ventricle","Cerebral aqueduct","Central canal"]'::jsonb, 'Third ventricle', 'The lumen of the diencephalon becomes the third ventricle, communicating rostrally with the lateral ventricles and caudally with the cerebral aqueduct. The lateral ventricles, aqueduct, fourth ventricle, and central canal correspond to the telencephalon, mesencephalon, hindbrain, and spinal cord.'),
('5210247a-8e98-5ad3-bc98-5b71cc9921e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the cavity of the mesencephalon?', '["Fourth ventricle","Cerebral aqueduct (of Sylvius)","Lateral ventricles","Third ventricle","Central canal"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral aqueduct (of Sylvius)', 'The mesencephalic lumen narrows into the cerebral aqueduct, which runs through the midbrain and joins the third and fourth ventricles. The lateral ventricles, third ventricle, fourth ventricle, and central canal are the cavities of the telencephalon, diencephalon, hindbrain, and spinal cord respectively.'),
('8287aadc-f306-59ec-b8c7-5b7a2f8b28ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the cavity of the metencephalon and myelencephalon?', '["Third ventricle","Fourth ventricle","Central canal","Cerebral aqueduct","Lateral ventricles"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth ventricle', 'The combined lumen of the metencephalon and myelencephalon persists as the fourth ventricle, which is continuous inferiorly with the central canal of the spinal cord. The third ventricle, aqueduct, and lateral ventricles are the cavities of the diencephalon, mesencephalon, and telencephalon.'),
('bd618df8-f146-53b5-8005-5c4ec16cb9c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the cavity of the spinal cord?', '["Fourth ventricle","Central canal","Lateral ventricle","Third ventricle","Cerebral aqueduct"]'::jsonb, 'Central canal', 'The lumen of the caudal neural tube narrows to form the central canal of the spinal cord, lined by ependyma and filled with CSF. The lateral ventricles, third ventricle, aqueduct, and fourth ventricle are the cavities of the more rostral brain divisions.'),
('44d0c2a4-21c6-5e91-944e-c50f5a913f7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The floor plate of the neural tube is induced by which structure?', '["Basal plate","Notochord","Neural crest","Surface ectoderm","Roof plate"]'::jsonb, 'Notochord', 'The notochord lies ventral to the neural tube and secretes Sonic hedgehog, which induces the overlying floor plate and patterns the ventral cord. The basal and roof plates are responding regions of the tube itself, and neural crest and surface ectoderm are not the inducing source.'),
('bf557ccf-6d84-57f9-ab7c-3e46a4e89ac5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Sonic hedgehog (Shh) from the notochord and floor plate induces which cells?', '["Gilal cells","Interneurons","Sensory neurons (alar plate)","Motor neurons (basal plate)","Commissural neurons"]'::jsonb, 'Motor neurons (basal plate)', 'A ventral-to-dorsal Shh gradient from the notochord and floor plate specifies the basal plate, where lower motor neurons differentiate. Dorsal fates such as sensory neurons are instead driven by BMP and Wnt signals from the roof plate, and the other listed cell types are not the primary Shh targets.'),
('8b827184-532b-50bb-9904-f6a7cd205792'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'BMP and Wnt from the roof plate induce which cells?', '["Ependymal cells","Sensory neurons (alar plate)","Oligodendrocytes","Motor neurons (basal plate)","Astrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory neurons (alar plate)', 'BMP and Wnt secreted by the roof plate establish dorsal patterning that directs the alar plate to form sensory neurons and interneurons. Ventral structures like motor neurons depend on Shh, and the glial cell types listed are not induced by these dorsal signals.'),
('0f2c555d-7639-5cd1-b244-106d3a494681'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The alar plate (dorsal horn) contains which type of neurons?', '["Upper motor neurons","Motor neurons","Lower motor neurons","Autonomic preganglionic neurons","Sensory and interneurons"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory and interneurons', 'The alar plate forms the dorsal horns of the spinal gray matter, which house sensory and interneuronal cell groups that receive afferent input. Motor neurons and autonomic preganglionic neurons are generated in the ventral basal plate.'),
('11f0dbb8-f7c2-58a5-beec-4119e2facb6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The basal plate (ventral horn) contains which type of neurons?', '["Commissural neurons","Interneurons","Sensory neurons","Association neurons","Motor neurons (lower motor neurons)"]'::jsonb, 'Motor neurons (lower motor neurons)', 'The basal plate differentiates into the ventral horns, which contain lower motor neurons (and autonomic preganglionic neurons at appropriate cord levels). Sensory neurons are the product of the dorsal alar plate, and the other listed cell types do not define the ventral horn.'),
('73b179c0-c14f-5898-9e84-2f53d20d1b73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The sulcus limitans separates which two structures?', '["Efferent and afferent fibers","Central canal and ventricle","Roof plate and floor plate","Gray and white matter","Alar plate (dorsal) and basal plate (ventral)"]'::jsonb, 'Alar plate (dorsal) and basal plate (ventral)', 'The sulcus limitans is an internal longitudinal groove in the neural tube that marks the boundary between the dorsal alar (sensory) plate and the ventral basal (motor) plate. It does not divide fiber tracts, gray from white matter, or the central canal from a ventricle.'),
('435ccdb2-31c3-5c3a-9460-5330771f669b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The mantle layer of the neural tube develops into which structure?', '["Ependymal lining","Menings","White matter (axons)","Gray matter (neuronal cell bodies)","Blood vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Gray matter (neuronal cell bodies)', 'The intermediate mantle layer contains migrating and differentiating neuroblasts, so it forms the gray matter of the cord and brainstem. The outer marginal layer accumulates axons to become white matter, and the innermost ventricular layer contributes the neuroepithelium and ependyma.'),
('9f546c54-3c0a-5eec-b872-ccc1a823329c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The marginal layer of the neural tube develops into which structure?', '["White matter (axons)","Choroid plexus","Gray matter","Ependyma","Menings"]'::jsonb, 'White matter (axons)', 'The outer marginal layer collects growing and myelinating axons, forming the white matter. The mantle layer produces gray matter and the ventricular zone yields neuroepithelium and ependyma, while meninges and choroid plexus derive from other tissues.'),
('af0371e3-496c-58b6-ab24-ccc49fa38cbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The ventricular zone of the neural tube gives rise to which cells?', '["Only ependymal cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Only astrocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only microglia mediated by specific receptor activation","All neurons and glial cells (the neuroepithelium)","Only oligodendrocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All neurons and glial cells (the neuroepithelium)', 'The pseudostratified neuroepithelium of the ventricular zone proliferates to produce essentially all CNS neurons and macroglia (astrocytes and oligodendrocytes). Microglia arise from the mesodermal/monocyte lineage rather than from the neuroepithelium.'),
('20722c05-70d9-5561-8869-244ae16f3d18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Radial glial cells are:', '["Ependymal cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural stem cells that guide neuronal migration","Oligodendrocyte precursors mediated by specific receptor activation","Mature astrocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Microglial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neural stem cells that guide neuronal migration', 'Radial glial cells act as the embryonic neural stem cells that generate neurons and glia, and their radial fibers serve as scaffolds guiding migrating neurons outward. They are neither mature glia, ependymal cells, nor microglia, although some later transform into astrocyte populations.'),
('628dc66a-60f1-58e6-97b2-d7284de1ebc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Neuronal migration in the developing cortex follows which pattern?', '["Radial migration only resulting in decreased functional activity","Outside-in accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tangential migration only mediated by specific receptor activation","Inside-out (earlier-born neurons in deeper layers later-born migrate past them to superficial layers)","Random migration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inside-out (earlier-born neurons in deeper layers later-born migrate past them to superficial layers)', 'Cortical neurons are generated in an inside-out sequence: the earliest-born neurons settle in the deepest layers while later-born neurons migrate past them to more superficial positions. Outside-in, random, or purely tangential patterns do not describe normal cortical lamination.'),
('c80c138c-44ea-5cdf-8b8c-ff989bef2b6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is band heterotopia (double cortex)?', '["Abnormal sulcus resulting in decreased functional activity","Extra cortical layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heterotopia of the meninges mediated by specific receptor activation","Ectopic neurons in the cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A band of gray matter in the white matter due to arrested neuronal migration"]'::jsonb, 'A band of gray matter in the white matter due to arrested neuronal migration', 'Band (subcortical) heterotopia is a strip of gray matter located deep within the cerebral white matter, formed when migrating neurons halt partway to the cortex. Because the neurons are misplaced rather than added to the normal cortex, the alternative descriptions do not apply.'),
('cd20a55e-7f05-56a0-95f0-d8351e3e0789'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is periventricular nodular heterotopia?', '["Subependymal nodules resulting in decreased functional activity","Nodules of gray matter along the lateral ventricles from failed initiation of migration","Cavernous malformations mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortical tubers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Venous angiomas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nodules of gray matter along the lateral ventricles from failed initiation of migration', 'In periventricular nodular heterotopia, neurons never begin their outward migration, so gray-matter nodules remain lining the lateral ventricles. Cavernous malformations, cortical tubers, and venous angiomas are vascular or hamartomatous lesions of a different nature.'),
('ac11d25c-f79c-5ead-9ec0-b39d14e3d302'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The corpus callosum develops from which structure?', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior commissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebral hemispheres without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Commissural plate (dorsal telencephalon)"]'::jsonb, 'Commissural plate (dorsal telencephalon)', 'Axons of the corpus callosum cross the midline through the commissural plate of the dorsal telencephalon, the first forebrain commissure to develop. The anterior commissure is a separate, smaller commissure, and the other structures are independent forebrain derivatives.'),
('40a9ffee-37db-52ff-9134-47472507589a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Agenesis of the corpus callosum may be:', '["isolated resulting in decreased functional activity","complete accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only occurs in males mediated by specific receptor activation","fatal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Partial or complete; associated with many syndromes (Aicardi Dandy-Walker)"]'::jsonb, 'Partial or complete; associated with many syndromes (Aicardi Dandy-Walker)', 'Agenesis of the corpus callosum may be partial or complete and can occur in isolation or within numerous syndromes, including Aicardi syndrome and Dandy-Walker malformation. It is not restricted to males and is not invariably fatal.'),
('340b38f7-934f-5c78-9b10-b093f6b4191c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is Aicardi syndrome?', '["A metabolic disorder resulting in decreased functional activity","Hydrocephalus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","X-linked dominant disorder with agenesis of the corpus callosum chorioretinal lacunae and infantile spasms","A type of lissencephaly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spina bifida leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked dominant disorder with agenesis of the corpus callosum chorioretinal lacunae and infantile spasms', 'Aicardi syndrome is an X-linked dominant condition characterized by agenesis of the corpus callosum, chorioretinal lacunae, and infantile spasms, and it is essentially lethal in males, so affected individuals are nearly always female. It is not a metabolic disorder, hydrocephalus, lissencephaly, or a neural tube defect.'),
('22a04e9c-5ac8-5ccc-a53d-c8eca14398e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of holoprosencephaly?', '["Sonic hedgehog mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","ZIC2 mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trisomy 13 (most common chromosomal cause) and SHH mutations","Maternal diabetes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alcohol exposure (fetal alcohol syndrome) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trisomy 13 (most common chromosomal cause) and SHH mutations', 'Chromosomal anomalies are the most frequent cause of holoprosencephaly, with trisomy 13 the leading single cytogenetic abnormality, and SHH mutations the most prominent single-gene cause. Maternal diabetes, alcohol, and the other gene mutations occur but account for fewer cases.'),
('923badf0-9e52-5a99-9d6d-6ce1f98382df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the classic facial feature of alobar holoprosencephaly?', '["Micrognathia resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertelorism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cleft lip and palate mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cyclopia (single central eye) with proboscis"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclopia (single central eye) with proboscis', 'The most severe form, alobar holoprosencephaly, characteristically shows cyclopia with a single midline eye and a proboscis above it. Hypertelorism and cleft lip may appear in milder forms, but cyclopia is the classic severe facial manifestation, while micrognathia and facial palsy do not define it.'),
('0b763a63-5f69-5acf-9729-9edcf89e67c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the cerebellum''s main function?', '["Emotion regulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Memory formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor initiation mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coordination of voluntary movement balance and motor learning"]'::jsonb, 'Coordination of voluntary movement balance and motor learning', 'The cerebellum refines and coordinates voluntary movement, maintains balance and posture, and participates in motor learning by integrating afferent motor and sensory input. Voluntary movement is initiated in the motor cortex, and emotion, memory, and general sensory processing are not its primary roles.'),
('9c77897f-897c-50dd-b462-6f86af38d9db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The cerebellum develops from which structures?', '["The myelencephalon resulting in decreased functional activity","The mesencephalon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The telencephalon mediated by specific receptor activation","The rhombic lips of the metencephalon (dorsolateral)","The diencephalon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rhombic lips of the metencephalon (dorsolateral)', 'The cerebellum arises from the rhombic lips at the dorsolateral margins of the metencephalon, where cells proliferate and migrate to form the cerebellar cortex and nuclei. The myelencephalon forms the medulla, and the other listed vesicles are not the source of the cerebellum.'),
('cd6b7b0c-81f4-5f1e-a842-a0ec2948c7e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the Dandy-Walker malformation?', '["Absent cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Enlarged fourth ventricle alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cystic enlargement of the fourth ventricle with cerebellar vermian agenesis and posterior fossa cyst","Small posterior fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chiari malformation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic enlargement of the fourth ventricle with cerebellar vermian agenesis and posterior fossa cyst', 'Dandy-Walker malformation combines cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle, agenesis or hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis, an enlarged posterior fossa, and an elevated torcula. The cerebellum itself is not absent, the fourth ventricle is not merely enlarged, and the posterior fossa is enlarged rather than small, which distinguishes it from Chiari malformation.'),
('83f15f5c-e2d8-5f8c-be77-b2cc79b62588'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the Dandy-Walker variant?', '["Less severe form with some cerebellar vermian development and a smaller posterior fossa cyst","Chiari I malformation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior fossa arachnoid cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete vermian agenesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mega cisterna magna leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Less severe form with some cerebellar vermian development and a smaller posterior fossa cyst', 'The Dandy-Walker variant is a milder phenotype than the full malformation, with partial vermian development and a smaller posterior fossa cyst. Complete vermian agenesis defines the full malformation, while Chiari I, arachnoid cysts, and mega cisterna magna are distinct entities.'),
('a4e7ad0c-3fe4-5874-a50c-839ecb86555f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is mega cisterna magna?', '["An arachnoid cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar hypoplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An enlarged cisterna magna (>10 mm) with a normal vermis and fourth ventricle often an incidental finding","Posterior fossa tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A Dandy-Walker variant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An enlarged cisterna magna (>10 mm) with a normal vermis and fourth ventricle often an incidental finding', 'Mega cisterna magna is a benign enlargement of the cisterna magna beyond 10 mm in which the vermis and fourth ventricle remain structurally normal, so it is usually an incidental finding. If the vermis or fourth ventricle were abnormal, the diagnosis would lie on the Dandy-Walker spectrum instead.'),
('f423cfbd-167e-50bb-a3fd-a8fc1e3cf4a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is Chiari malformation type I?', '["Herniation of the medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Caudal herniation of the cerebellar tonsils below the foramen magnum (>5 mm) without brainstem involvement","Hydrocephalus mediated by specific receptor activation","Absent cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cyst of the fourth ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Caudal herniation of the cerebellar tonsils below the foramen magnum (>5 mm) without brainstem involvement', 'Chiari I is defined by descent of the cerebellar tonsils more than 5 mm below the foramen magnum without involvement of the brainstem, often asymptomatic into adulthood. Herniation of the medulla or vermis and hydrocephalus point instead to Chiari II, and the other options describe unrelated lesions.'),
('7699839a-0cfa-56ee-897f-d9f15fdca9df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is Chiari malformation type II?', '["Vermian agenesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Tonsillar herniation alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyst of the fourth ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Small posterior fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Herniation of the cerebellar vermis and medulla through the foramen magnum with spina bifida and hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 'Herniation of the cerebellar vermis and medulla through the foramen magnum with spina bifida and hydrocephalus', 'Chiari II (Arnold-Chiari) malformation herniates the cerebellar vermis and medulla caudally through the foramen magnum and is almost always associated with a lumbar myelomeningocele and hydrocephalus. In contrast to Chiari I, only the tonsils herniate without brainstem involvement in type II.'),
('59f3a453-8d0b-58e1-b4d3-2d32790977d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common type of Chiari malformation?', '["Type I (tonsillar herniation) is most common and often diagnosed incidentally in adults","Type IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type III mediated by specific receptor activation","All equally common without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type II leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (tonsillar herniation) is most common and often diagnosed incidentally in adults', 'Chiari type I is by far the most common Chiari malformation and is frequently found incidentally on brain imaging in adults with mild or no symptoms. Types II through IV are less common, making the other options incorrect.'),
('4f6aaf4e-6bf6-5c2f-91b0-f544f6728f02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of syringomyelia?', '["Chiari I malformation (associated with about 50-80% of syringomyelia)","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Idiopathic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chiari I malformation (associated with about 50-80% of syringomyelia)', 'Chiari I malformation, by obstructing CSF flow at the foramen magnum, is the most common association with syringomyelia, present in roughly 50-80% of cases. Trauma and spinal cord tumors cause syrinx formation but less frequently, and infection is a rare cause.'),
('96647db8-3442-57ae-bf67-c4a6ba865bd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is hydromyelia?', '["An enlarged spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","A syrinx within the cord parenchyma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dilation of the central canal of the spinal cord (often a normal variant)","A collapsed central canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A split cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilation of the central canal of the spinal cord (often a normal variant)', 'Hydromyelia is a dilatation of the ependyma-lined central canal of the spinal cord and is often a benign, incidental finding. A cavity within the cord parenchyma itself is a syrinx (syringomyelia), which is not the same entity.'),
('24fa6e6d-bb3e-5d64-924d-7e8144cc71bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hydrocephalus in children?', '["Post-infectious (meningitis) resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-hemorrhagic (IVH in prematurity) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chiari II (with myelomeningocele) mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor causing obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congenital aqueductal stenosis (stenosis of the cerebral aqueduct)"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital aqueductal stenosis (stenosis of the cerebral aqueduct)', 'Congenital stenosis of the cerebral aqueduct is the most common cause of hydrocephalus in children overall, especially as a neonatal presentation. Intraventricular hemorrhage dominates in premature infants, tumors predominate in older children, and meningitis and Chiari II are less frequent causes.'),
('b8923e21-e65f-5c7e-ba07-bb5861109fb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hydrocephalus in preterm infants?', '["Aqueductal stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-hemorrhagic hydrocephalus (intraventricular hemorrhage from germinal matrix bleeding)","Chiari malformation mediated by specific receptor activation","Meningitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-hemorrhagic hydrocephalus (intraventricular hemorrhage from germinal matrix bleeding)', 'In preterm infants, intraventricular hemorrhage arising from the fragile germinal matrix is the most common cause of hydrocephalus. Aqueductal stenosis is a congenital cause seen more in term infants, while tumors, meningitis, and Chiari malformation are less common in this age group.'),
('c05c10b4-5320-5c16-a027-11fe7666cbce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hydrocephalus in older children?', '["Congenital resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-infectious (meningitis) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aqueductal stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Brain tumor (posterior fossa tumors causing obstructive hydrocephalus)","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brain tumor (posterior fossa tumors causing obstructive hydrocephalus)', 'Posterior fossa tumors, such as medulloblastoma and ependymoma, are the most common cause of acquired obstructive hydrocephalus in older children. Congenital causes and aqueductal stenosis dominate infancy and the neonatal period, while meningitis and trauma are less frequent causes.'),
('c2beffb2-420b-59ea-93d2-c52a9574a3b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) in the elderly?', '["Post-traumatic","Post-hemorrhagic (subarachnoid hemorrhage)","Idiopathic (primary NPH)","Post-infectious (meningitis)","Aqueductal stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (primary NPH)', 'Most elderly patients with NPH have no identifiable cause, making it idiopathic (primary). Secondary causes such as prior subarachnoid hemorrhage, meningitis, or trauma account for only a minority of cases.'),
('48e5e142-f8fa-5b56-830f-0d336088fad7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the classic gait in NPH?', '["Spastic gait resulting in decreased functional activity","Magnetic gait (difficulty initiating steps feet stuck to the floor)","Ataxic gait mediated by specific receptor activation","Hemiplegic gait without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Steppage gait leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Magnetic gait (difficulty initiating steps feet stuck to the floor)', 'The characteristic NPH gait appears as though the feet are glued to the floor, with difficulty lifting and initiating steps. Spastic, ataxic, hemiplegic, and steppage gaits reflect other pathologies such as upper motor neuron, cerebellar, or peripheral nerve lesions.'),
('faab7df2-62ec-55fa-8858-a09085809b95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The choroid plexus develops from which structures?', '["Perivascular spaces","Ependymal cells and vascular pia mater","Dural venous sinuses","Subarachnoid space","Arachnoid mater"]'::jsonb, 'Ependymal cells and vascular pia mater', 'The choroid plexus is formed by invagination of vascular pia mater into the ventricular cavity lined by ependymal cells. The ependymal lining and the vascular pial core together constitute the plexus that secretes CSF.'),
('c8b9083c-6629-5e59-badd-dfedd6ca09c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'CSF is produced mainly by which structure?', '["Choroid plexus (about 70-80% of CSF)","Brain parenchyma","Arachnoid granulations","Capillaries of the brain","Ependyma"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus (about 70-80% of CSF)', 'The choroid plexus, especially in the lateral ventricles, secretes the large majority of CSF by active ion transport followed by water movement. The remaining fluid is derived largely from the brain interstitial space and ependymal surfaces.'),
('37849e43-e197-5874-bcb0-401a99ad0cf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'CSF is absorbed through which structures?', '["Ependyma resulting in decreased functional activity","Arachnoid granulations (villi) into the dural venous sinuses","Choroid plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Veins of the brain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arachnoid granulations (villi) into the dural venous sinuses', 'CSF drains from the subarachnoid space into the dural venous sinuses through arachnoid granulations, which serve as one-way valves. The choroid plexus produces CSF, while ependyma and brain veins are not absorption routes.'),
('0697e83d-9733-5f15-8fb9-37214b435ae0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Absence of arachnoid granulations leads to which condition?', '["Communicating hydrocephalus","Dandy-Walker malformation","Obstructive hydrocephalus","Chiari malformation","Syringomyelia"]'::jsonb, 'Communicating hydrocephalus', 'Loss or blockage of arachnoid granulations impairs CSF absorption in the subarachnoid space outside the ventricles, producing a communicating hydrocephalus. The ventricles and subarachnoid space remain in continuity, so obstruction is at the absorption site rather than within the ventricular system.'),
('d31ad2d7-16b7-52d4-84eb-8117d72cf50a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the difference between communicating and noncommunicating hydrocephalus?', '["There is difference resulting in decreased functional activity","Communicating: CSF flows between ventricles and subarachnoid space but absorption is impaired; Noncommunicating: obstruction within the ventricular system","Communicating: is due to tumor; Noncommunicating: is due to meningitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Communicating: obstruction at the aqueduct; Noncommunicating: impaired absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They are the same leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Communicating: CSF flows between ventricles and subarachnoid space but absorption is impaired; Noncommunicating: obstruction within the ventricular system', 'In communicating hydrocephalus the ventricular system is patent and CSF reaches the subarachnoid space, but absorption fails (e.g. impaired arachnoid granulations). In noncommunicating (obstructive) hydrocephalus a physical obstruction inside the ventricular pathway, such as aqueductal stenosis, blocks flow.'),
('0f5dab87-a2fd-5bff-a568-7fbd8262760f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of communicating hydrocephalus?', '["Choroid plexus papilloma resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-hemorrhagic (especially SAH) and post-meningitic","Idiopathic mediated by specific receptor activation","Overproduction of CSF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Venous sinus thrombosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-hemorrhagic (especially SAH) and post-meningitic', 'Subarachnoid hemorrhage and meningitis deposit blood or inflammatory debris over the arachnoid granulations, obstructing CSF absorption and causing communicating hydrocephalus. Venous sinus thrombosis and overproduction of CSF are rarer mechanisms.'),
('9c415db6-e9b2-5ec5-83d0-8c5b03aa2221'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of obstructive (noncommunicating) hydrocephalus in children?', '["Aqueductal stenosis (congenital)","Vein of Galen malformation","Dandy-Walker malformation","Posterior fossa tumor","Chiari II"]'::jsonb, 'Aqueductal stenosis (congenital)', 'Congenital narrowing of the cerebral aqueduct is the most frequent cause of obstructive hydrocephalus in children, blocking flow between the third and fourth ventricles. Posterior fossa tumors and Dandy-Walker malformation are less common contributing causes.'),
('4a2a1d49-1ed5-577d-af78-115fe67bd036'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the triad of normal pressure hydrocephalus?', '["Gait disturbance urinary incontinence dementia","Ataxia nystagmus dysmetria","Neck pain ataxia dysphagia","Headache vomiting papilledema","Seizures hemiparesis aphasia"]'::jsonb, 'Gait disturbance urinary incontinence dementia', 'NPH is classically recognised by the triad of gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, and dementia (often with the gait problem appearing first). The other threesomes describe cerebellar, posterior fossa, or increased intracranial pressure syndromes.'),
('d34e7211-dc7b-5145-aff1-837780ecb9f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common brain tumor in children?', '["Glioblastoma multiforme resulting in decreased functional activity","Ependymoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pilocytic astrocytoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Brainstem glioma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pilocytic astrocytoma (most common overall) then medulloblastoma"]'::jsonb, 'Pilocytic astrocytoma (most common overall) then medulloblastoma', 'Pilocytic astrocytoma is the most common pediatric brain tumor overall, with medulloblastoma the commonest malignant pediatric tumor. Glioblastoma and ependymoma are encountered but are less frequent in childhood.'),
('c68dbbd6-feab-5472-848a-66099585d992'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common malignant brain tumor in children?', '["Atypical teratoid/rhabdoid tumor","Anaplastic astrocytoma","Ependymoma","Medulloblastoma (posterior fossa PNET)","Glioblastoma"]'::jsonb, 'Medulloblastoma (posterior fossa PNET)', 'Medulloblastoma, a primitive neuroectodermal tumor of the posterior fossa (cerebellum), is the most common malignant brain tumor in children. The other options represent rarer or less frequent pediatric malignancies.'),
('1e38dd32-7f0d-5cf1-9bcd-63d675ff79bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common brain tumor in adults?', '["Meningioma","Glioblastoma (GBM) and meningioma","Pituitary adenoma","Schwannoma (acoustic neuroma)","Glioblastoma is most common primary malignant; meningioma is most common primary overall (benign)"]'::jsonb, 'Glioblastoma (GBM) and meningioma', 'When considering brain tumors in adults, glioblastoma is the most common primary malignant tumor and meningioma the most common primary benign tumor. The combined option captures that both are the leading primary tumors in adults.'),
('cf13f557-2d6d-542c-b716-459065e7c254'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common primary brain tumor in adults?', '["Ependymoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Pituitary adenoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glioblastoma (malignant) is the most common malignant primary brain tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Schwannoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningioma (benign) is the most common primary brain tumor overall"]'::jsonb, 'Meningioma (benign) is the most common primary brain tumor overall', 'Meningioma, a benign tumor of the meninges, is the most common primary intracranial tumor overall in adults, whereas glioblastoma is the most common primary malignant tumor. Ependymoma and schwannoma are less frequent primary tumors.'),
('3bb0dd23-3549-5f50-a978-85a11a5ed636'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of brain metastases in adults?', '["Melanoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Colon cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lung cancer (most common) then breast melanoma colon","Breast cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Renal cell carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer (most common) then breast melanoma colon', 'Lung cancer is the most common source of brain metastases in adults, followed by breast cancer, melanoma, and colon cancer. This reflects the high frequency and hematogenous spread of these primary tumors.'),
('09dcd635-637e-5eba-bd1a-b1d31c199eaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of spinal cord compression in adults?', '["Degenerative stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Metastatic tumor (most common) then primary tumor (meningioma schwannoma)","Hematoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma (fracture dislocation) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epidural abscess leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metastatic tumor (most common) then primary tumor (meningioma schwannoma)', 'Metastatic epidural tumor (often from lung, breast, or prostate) is the leading cause of spinal cord compression in adults, with primary tumors like meningioma and schwannoma as secondary causes. Hematoma, abscess, and trauma are less common.'),
('66ef9cf8-65a7-5507-bd99-d6ab28e8e7c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common primary spinal cord tumor?', '["Hemangioblastoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Meningioma (most common) and schwannoma/neurofibroma (nerve sheath tumors)","Ependymoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Chordoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Astrocytoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Meningioma (most common) and schwannoma/neurofibroma (nerve sheath tumors)', 'Meningioma and the nerve sheath tumors (schwannoma and neurofibroma) are the most common primary spinal cord tumors, typically arising in an extramedullary-intradural location. Ependymoma and astrocytoma are the leading intramedullary tumors, and hemangioblastoma is rare.'),
('749bd25f-c997-5dd4-aa88-c6c36692cbd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common intramedullary spinal cord tumor in adults?', '["Hemangioblastoma","Metastasis","Astrocytoma","Paraganglioma","Ependymoma (most common)"]'::jsonb, 'Ependymoma (most common)', 'Ependymoma, arising from ependymal cells around the central canal, is the most common intramedullary spinal tumor in adults. Astrocytoma is more typical in children, while hemangioblastoma and metastasis are less common intramedullary lesions.'),
('217e2181-9a89-547d-aed4-b66de4d9ddca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common intramedullary spinal cord tumor in children?', '["PSNET","Ganglioglioma","Astrocytoma (most common)","Ependymoma","Hemangioblastoma"]'::jsonb, 'Astrocytoma (most common)', 'Astrocytoma is the most common intramedullary spinal cord tumor in children, whereas ependymoma is the leading one in adults. The other listed lesions are uncommon intramedullary tumors in the pediatric population.'),
('d67ffa2e-d964-5d2e-a2f8-54a7bb6d4b9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common peripheral nerve tumor?', '["Neurofibroma","Neuroma","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor (MPNST)","Schwannoma (neurilemmoma)","Ganglioneuroma"]'::jsonb, 'Schwannoma (neurilemmoma)', 'Schwannoma, a well-encapsulated benign tumor of Schwann cells, is the most common peripheral nerve tumor. Neurofibroma is a related but less frequent nerve sheath tumor, and MPNST is malignant.'),
('596d4725-95d0-5573-94cc-89187e2446a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common site of schwannoma?', '["Spinal nerve roots (dorsal) resulting in decreased functional activity","Vestibular nerve (acoustic neuroma) then peripheral nerves","Trigeminal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Vestibular nerve (CN VIII) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peripheral nerves of the extremities leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular nerve (acoustic neuroma) then peripheral nerves', 'Schwannomas most often arise on the vestibular division of CN VIII as acoustic neuromas at the cerebellopontine angle, with peripheral nerves the next most common site. Trigeminal and spinal nerve schwannomas are less frequent.'),
('669971b9-cf66-5188-ade2-1ac28c9cdcd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2) characterized by?', '["Bilateral vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas)","Multiple neurofibromas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Café-au-lait spots mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic gliomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lisch nodules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas)', 'The hallmark of NF2 is bilateral vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas), often with other meningiomas and ependymomas. Café-au-lait spots, optic gliomas, and Lisch nodules are features of NF1 rather than NF2.'),
('f8211db5-7151-5625-84de-3e86d6e7f21f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of a sellar/suprasellar mass in children?', '["Craniopharyngioma","Rathke''s cleft cyst","Germinoma","Optic glioma (NF1)","Pituitary adenoma (prolactinoma)"]'::jsonb, 'Craniopharyngioma', 'Craniopharyngioma, a tumor derived from Rathke''s pouch remnants, is the most common sellar/suprasellar mass in children. Rathke''s cleft cyst, germinoma, and optic glioma are less frequent pediatric suprasellar masses.'),
('a85ee46a-5f88-5255-800c-7cad611eb07a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common pituitary tumor in adults?', '["Prolactinoma (most common secretory pituitary adenoma)","TSH-secreting adenoma","Nonfunctioning (null cell) adenoma","GH-secreting adenoma (acromegaly)","ACTH-secreting adenoma (Cushing disease)"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactinoma (most common secretory pituitary adenoma)', 'Prolactinoma is the most common secretory pituitary adenoma in adults, presenting with hyperprolactinemia, while nonfunctioning adenomas are the commonest overall by frequency among some series. TSH-, GH-, and ACTH-secreting adenomas are rarer secretory types.'),
('766c90dd-d99e-5b70-9832-9efdc1d54d30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hyperprolactinemia?', '["Drug-induced (antipsychotics SSRIs)","Pregnancy","Hypothyroidism","Renal failure","Prolactinoma (microprolactinoma)"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactinoma (microprolactinoma)', 'A microprolactinoma is the most common cause of sustained hyperprolactinemia in clinical practice. Drug-induced hyperprolactinemia, hypothyroidism, and renal failure are important but less frequent causes to consider in the differential.'),
('52aee1e5-c818-568f-9475-a1b0db59cd36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of acromegaly?', '["Pituitary hyperplasia","McCune-Albright syndrome","GH-secreting pituitary adenoma (somatotroph adenoma)","Excess GH from non-pituitary tumor","Ectopic GHRH secretion (carcinoid)"]'::jsonb, 'GH-secreting pituitary adenoma (somatotroph adenoma)', 'The vast majority of acromegaly is caused by a GH-secreting somatotroph pituitary adenoma. Ectopic GHRH secretion and non-pituitary causes are rare and represent only a small fraction of cases.'),
('bd6ec6d0-8e7b-510f-abc0-d43df4ebd5e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of Cushing disease?', '["Adrenal carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Macroadenoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary ACTH-secreting microadenoma (<1 cm)","Adrenal adenoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ectopic ACTH syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary ACTH-secreting microadenoma (<1 cm)', 'Cushing disease is due to a pituitary ACTH-secreting adenoma, and most are microadenomas smaller than 1 cm. Adrenal adenoma and carcinoma cause Cushing syndrome rather than disease, and ectopic ACTH is a separate cause.'),
('ebe6c5d3-22e0-55f5-9deb-0cbf3fbd5a36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of diabetes insipidus (central)?', '["Head trauma (post-traumatic DI) resulting in decreased functional activity","Pituitary tumor (craniopharyngioma) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infiltrative disease (Langerhans cell histiocytosis) mediated by specific receptor activation","Neurosurgery (post-op DI) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic (or autoimmune) destruction of AVP-producing neurons in the hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (or autoimmune) destruction of AVP-producing neurons in the hypothalamus', 'Most cases of central diabetes insipidus are idiopathic, likely an autoimmune destruction of the AVP-producing neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei. Tumors, trauma, and surgery are identifiable secondary causes that together account for a smaller proportion.'),
('f635a793-0adc-59da-a139-4a253a9ec678'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus?', '["Genetic (AVPR2 or AQP2 mutations)","Hypercalcemia","Tubulointerstitial disease","Hypokalemia","Lithium therapy (most common acquired cause)"]'::jsonb, 'Lithium therapy (most common acquired cause)', 'Lithium therapy is the most common acquired cause of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus because it impairs the kidney''s response to vasopressin. Genetic AVPR2/AQP2 mutations cause congenital forms, while hypercalcemia and hypokalemia are less common acquired causes.'),
('c89e096e-1bb2-5422-8529-3d4f74591b7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common intracranial aneurysm location?', '["Anterior communicating artery (most common)","Internal carotid artery (ICA)","Middle cerebral artery (MCA) bifurcation","Posterior communicating artery","Basilar artery tip"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior communicating artery (most common)', 'The anterior communicating artery is the most common site of intracranial saccular (berry) aneurysms. It is followed by the internal carotid and middle cerebral arteries, while the posterior communicating and posterior circulation sites are less common.'),
('ebc2f677-28e5-5bbe-a633-0979d99b43c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH)?', '["Perimesencephalic (idiopathic)","Ruptured saccular (berry) aneurysm","Trauma","AVM rupture","Hypersensitivity (Cocaine)"]'::jsonb, 'Ruptured saccular (berry) aneurysm', 'Rupture of a saccular (berry) aneurysm is the most common cause of spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage. AVM rupture and cocaine use are much less common antecedents of non-traumatic SAH.'),
('587b8d08-c977-5f51-a937-546931db109a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of intracerebral hemorrhage (ICH) in adults?', '["Hypertension (chronic hypertensive vasculopathy causing putaminal thalamic pontine cerebellar hemorrhage)","Amyloid angiopathy (lobar hemorrhage in elderly) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vascular malformation mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anticoagulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension (chronic hypertensive vasculopathy causing putaminal thalamic pontine cerebellar hemorrhage)', 'Chronic hypertension damages perforating arterioles, making it the most common cause of spontaneous ICH in adults, typically involving the putamen, thalamus, pons, and cerebellum. Amyloid angiopathy instead causes lobar hemorrhage, mainly in the elderly.'),
('d2a0cbff-d5b2-5bc7-922f-df38570e9225'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common location of hypertensive ICH?', '["Lobar (cortical-subcortical)","Cerebellum","Putamen (most common)","Thalamus","Pons"]'::jsonb, 'Putamen (most common)', 'Hypertensive hemorrhages most commonly occur in the putamen, supplied by the lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery. Lobar locations are more typical of cerebral amyloid angiopathy, while the thalamus and pons are secondary sites.'),
('ee3e0300-f682-5fcb-a202-5a5b215587cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of lobar hemorrhage in the elderly?', '["Vascular malformation","Hypertension","Cerebral amyloid angiopathy (CAA)","Anticoagulation","Tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral amyloid angiopathy (CAA)', 'Cerebral amyloid angiopathy is the most common cause of spontaneous lobar hemorrhage in the elderly, resulting from amyloid deposition in cortical arteries. Hypertension more often produces deep hemorrhages, while anticoagulation and vascular malformations are contributing but less frequent causes.'),
('eb9499a1-43a6-5e00-bfc3-74cdaa1be9f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of ischemic stroke worldwide?', '["Large artery atherosclerosis (most common mechanism)","Other determined cause (dissection vasculitis)","Small vessel disease (lacunar)","Cryptogenic","Cardioembolism (AF)"]'::jsonb, 'Large artery atherosclerosis (most common mechanism)', 'Large artery atherosclerosis is the most common mechanism of ischemic stroke worldwide, often arising from carotid or intracranial atherosclerotic plaques. Cardioembolism and small vessel lacunar disease are the other major but less frequent mechanisms.'),
('c8fb8e7e-d58c-5040-b18a-e8dd1336a402'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of stroke in young adults?', '["Vasculitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Small vessel disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cervical artery dissection (carotid or vertebral artery dissection)","Cardioembolism (PFO paradoxical embolus) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypercoagulable states leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical artery dissection (carotid or vertebral artery dissection)', 'Cervical artery dissection is the most common cause of ischemic stroke in young adults, frequently following neck trauma or trivial strain. Cardioembolism, especially via patent foramen ovale, and hypercoagulable states contribute to a smaller share of cases.'),
('78c0db0e-0a52-5def-ab83-bec7943b10d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hemorrhagic stroke in children?', '["Tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Coagulopathy (hemophilia) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vascular malformation (AVM) is the most common cause of spontaneous ICH in children"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular malformation (AVM) is the most common cause of spontaneous ICH in children', 'Vascular malformations, particularly arteriovenous malformations, are the most common cause of spontaneous intracerebral hemorrhage in children. Coagulopathies such as hemophilia and hypertension are less frequent antecedents of childhood hemorrhagic stroke.'),
('7e4e7731-e4be-5466-9c04-bf199171500a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of Moyamoya disease in children?', '["Down syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Radiation therapy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unknown (idiopathic) but bilateral internal carotid stenosis with collateral formation; common in East Asia","NF1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sickle cell disease (most common cause of Moyamoya syndrome) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unknown (idiopathic) but bilateral internal carotid stenosis with collateral formation; common in East Asia', 'Moyamoya disease is idiopathic, showing progressive bilateral stenosis of the internal carotid arteries with compensatory collateral ''puff of smoke'' vessels, and is most prevalent in East Asia. Associated conditions such as sickle cell disease, Down syndrome, NF1, and radiation produce the secondary form, Moyamoya syndrome.'),
('97782b4a-0679-5b6e-b6f6-1bc0e9eb2344'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common presentation of Moyamoya disease in children?', '["Seizures resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemorrhagic stroke (more common in adults) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transient ischemic attacks (TIAs) and stroke (ischemic)","Cognitive decline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Headaches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transient ischemic attacks (TIAs) and stroke (ischemic)', 'Children with Moyamoya disease most often present with recurrent transient ischemic attacks and ischemic stroke because of progressive arterial stenosis and reduced cerebral perfusion. Hemorrhage is more characteristic of affected adults than children.'),
('5eea9d90-6e83-5ea4-a947-a277c54363f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of spinal cord injury in children?', '["Violence (gunshot) resulting in decreased functional activity","Birth trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sports-related (diving football) mediated by specific receptor activation","Falls without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor vehicle accidents (most common cause of traumatic SCI in children)"]'::jsonb, 'Motor vehicle accidents (most common cause of traumatic SCI in children)', 'Motor vehicle accidents are the most common cause of traumatic spinal cord injury in children. Falls and sports-related injuries are important but less frequent mechanisms in the pediatric age group.'),
('0688f1c9-bb03-5794-942c-f5d6f59d3701'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common level of spinal cord injury?', '["Thoracic (T10-T12) resulting in decreased functional activity","Cervicomedullary junction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lumbar (L1-L2) mediated by specific receptor activation","Cervical spine (most common especially C5-C6)","Sacral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical spine (most common especially C5-C6)', 'The cervical spine, especially the C5-C6 level, is the most commonly injured region because of its mobility and relative lack of bony protection. Thoracic injury zones and lumbosacral levels are affected less often.'),
('0e9ea4ea-985e-56ec-a6ed-61d81a749822'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of nontraumatic spinal cord injury in children?', '["Tumor (intramedullary astrocytoma or extramedullary neuroblastoma)","Transverse myelitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple sclerosis (in older children) mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection (abscess) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ischemia (vascular malformation) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor (intramedullary astrocytoma or extramedullary neuroblastoma)', 'Spinal tumors, such as intramedullary astrocytoma and extramedullary neuroblastoma, are the most common cause of nontraumatic spinal cord injury in children. Transverse myelitis and MS are inflammatory causes that occur less frequently overall.'),
('727f7704-5157-53cc-b476-24d7101d1587'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of transverse myelitis in children?', '["Post-infectious (immune-mediated after viral illness) or post-vaccinal","Infection (enterovirus) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neuromyelitis optica (NMO AQP4 antibody) mediated by specific receptor activation","Idiopathic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multiple sclerosis (first presentation in adolescents) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-infectious (immune-mediated after viral illness) or post-vaccinal', 'Pediatric transverse myelitis most often follows a viral illness or vaccination as an immune-mediated, post-infectious inflammation of the spinal cord. Neuromyelitis optica and multiple sclerosis are less common underlying causes.'),
('a4950193-e4d0-5701-b19e-0c2ef9f5ff61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of epilepsy in children?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Stroke accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic (genetic generalized epilepsy) and structural (hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy cortical malformation)","Febrile seizures without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection (meningitis) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (genetic generalized epilepsy) and structural (hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy cortical malformation)', 'Most childhood epilepsy is idiopathic or genetic (e.g. genetic generalized epilepsies), with structural causes such as hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy and cortical malformations forming the other large group. Trauma, stroke, and infection are relatively uncommon causes in children.'),
('8d01c1af-76a7-5389-a975-0c1492b5d829'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common type of epilepsy in children?', '["Focal epilepsy (structural) resulting in decreased functional activity","Infantile spasms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Benign childhood epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes (BECTS) mediated by specific receptor activation","Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Childhood absence epilepsy (one of the most common generalized epilepsies)"]'::jsonb, 'Childhood absence epilepsy (one of the most common generalized epilepsies)', 'Childhood absence epilepsy is among the most common types of childhood epilepsy, presenting with brief staring spells and 3 Hz spike-and-wave discharges on EEG. BECTS is likewise common but reflects a focal epilepsy, while infantile spasms and juvenile myoclonic epilepsy are less frequent.'),
('253da9c3-5d1a-5df7-86ea-c57aa0d9d28a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of status epilepticus in children?', '["Medication non-compliance (in known epilepsy)","Idiopathic","Acute symptomatic (trauma metabolic)","Febrile seizures (prolonged febrile convulsion)","CNS infection (meningitis encephalitis)"]'::jsonb, 'Febrile seizures (prolonged febrile convulsion)', 'A prolonged febrile convulsion is the most common cause of status epilepticus in young children. Acute symptomatic causes, CNS infections, and medication non-compliance account for a smaller proportion of pediatric status epilepticus cases.'),
('d733d546-8268-53fd-a037-e70a063e521e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of febrile seizures?', '["Bacterial infection (UTI pneumonia) resulting in decreased functional activity","Otitis media accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Viral illness (HHV-6 roseola influenza) causing rapid temperature rise","Post-vaccination (MMR DTaP) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastroenteritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Viral illness (HHV-6 roseola influenza) causing rapid temperature rise', 'Febrile seizures are most commonly triggered by viral infections, notably human herpesvirus-6 (roseola) and influenza, especially when the fever rises quickly. Bacterial infections, otitis media, and post-vaccination fever are less frequent precipitants.'),
('768090f1-8f74-5e3e-b3a6-4440dc5970e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri) in children?', '["Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) most common in obese adolescent females","Hydrocephalus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medications (tetracycline vitamin A) mediated by specific receptor activation","Endocrine disorders (Addison) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Venous sinus thrombosis (otitic hydrocephalus from mastoiditis) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) most common in obese adolescent females', 'Most childhood pseudotumor cerebri is idiopathic intracranial hypertension, which is especially common in obese adolescent females. Venous sinus thrombosis and medications such as tetracycline and vitamin A are secondary causes seen less commonly.'),
('3bda8e55-ce86-5dec-8b7e-6a42671df5d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of papilledema in children?', '["Brain tumor (posterior fossa) resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri)","Hydrocephalus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri)', 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is the most common cause of papilledema in children. Brain tumors, particularly posterior fossa lesions, hydrocephalus, venous sinus thrombosis, and meningitis are important but less common causes of optic disc swelling.'),
('14919de3-b77a-54c9-9c28-9b7dc2a34024'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of brain abscess in children?', '["Cyanotic congenital heart disease (paradoxical emboli) and otogenic infection (mastoiditis)","Hematogenous (pneumonia endocarditis) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Post-surgical (necrotizing fasciitis) mediated by specific receptor activation","Sinusitis (frontal lobe abscess) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma (penetrating) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyanotic congenital heart disease (paradoxical emboli) and otogenic infection (mastoiditis)', 'In children, brain abscess most often follows cyanotic congenital heart disease with paradoxical emboli or spreads from otogenic infection such as mastoiditis. Paranasal sinusitis and hematogenous seeding from distant sites are other less common routes.'),
('d10b6e41-4ac6-549b-8318-54982c733835'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common organism causing brain abscess in children?', '["Bacteroides fragilis resulting in decreased functional activity","Haemophilus influenzae (pre-Hib) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Streptococci (aerobic and anaerobic) and Staphylococcus aureus","Tuberculosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fungal (Candida Aspergillus) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococci (aerobic and anaerobic) and Staphylococcus aureus', 'Brain abscess in children most often follows contiguous spread from the sinuses or mastoids or hematogenous seeding, and mixed aerobic and anaerobic streptococci together with Staphylococcus aureus are the dominant pathogens. Bacteroides, fungi, TB, and pre-Hib H. influenzae are all much less frequent causes.'),
('2eec11b5-9a8d-569f-8c8e-85d4432b5956'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of bacterial meningitis in neonates?', '["Meningococcus (N. meningitidis)","E. coli (K1 capsular polysaccharide)","Listeria monocytogenes","Pneumococcus (S. pneumoniae)","Group B Streptococcus (Streptococcus agalactiae)"]'::jsonb, 'Group B Streptococcus (Streptococcus agalactiae)', 'Group B Streptococcus is acquired vertically from the colonized maternal genital tract and is the leading cause of bacterial meningitis in the neonatal period. E. coli with the K1 capsule is the second most common, while Listeria, pneumococcus, and meningococcus are less frequent in neonates.'),
('8d518676-c8f8-5d1e-bbd1-63265f8aadce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of bacterial meningitis in children (2 months-12 years)?', '["Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib vaccine has decreased this)","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Group B Streptococcus","Listeria","Neisseria meningitidis"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae', 'Since widespread Hib vaccination, Streptococcus pneumoniae has become the most common cause of bacterial meningitis in children aged 2 months to 12 years, while Hib disease has fallen sharply. Meningococcus is a less common cause in this age range, and GBS and Listeria are mainly neonatal pathogens.'),
('9088a51e-288a-5698-90d9-6a9107576660'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of viral meningitis (aseptic meningitis) in children?', '["EBV (Epstein-Barr) resulting in decreased functional activity","Enteroviruses (echovirus coxsackievirus)","HIV mediated by specific receptor activation","HSV-2 (herpes simplex type 2) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cytomegalovirus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enteroviruses (echovirus coxsackievirus)', 'Enteroviruses such as echovirus and coxsackievirus cause the majority of aseptic (viral) meningitis in children, with peaks in summer and early fall. EBV, CMV, HSV-2, and HIV are far less common causes of viral meningitis.'),
('936ef890-5acf-55ca-bd4e-792b9af37c58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of encephalitis in children?', '["Herpes simplex virus (HSV-1) most common cause of sporadic encephalitis","West Nile virus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CMV mediated by specific receptor activation","Arboviruses (JEWNV) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enterovirus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Herpes simplex virus (HSV-1) most common cause of sporadic encephalitis', 'HSV-1 causes a necrotizing encephalitis with temporal lobe predilection and is the most common cause of sporadic (non-epidemic) encephalitis in children. Arboviruses and West Nile virus cause seasonal or outbreak-related cases, while CMV and enterovirus are much less common.'),
('56166cf9-c05a-5618-81b3-eb20d0e63522'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of acute flaccid paralysis (AFP) worldwide?', '["Myasthenia gravis resulting in decreased functional activity","Guillain-Barré syndrome (most common cause in developed countries)","Transverse myelitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Poliovirus (enterovirus polio in endemic areas) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Botulism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Guillain-Barré syndrome (most common cause in developed countries)', 'With polio nearly eradicated, Guillain-Barré syndrome is now the leading cause of acute flaccid paralysis in the post-polio era, and it dominates in developed countries. Poliovirus remains a cause only in endemic regions, while myasthenia, botulism, and transverse myelitis are less frequent overall.'),
('a76a7713-9c32-5bbf-9d30-c1de2ec33aaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of Guillain-Barré syndrome?', '["Mycoplasma pneumoniae resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper respiratory or GI infection (Campylobacter jejuni EBV CMV Mycoplasma)","CMV mediated by specific receptor activation","Campylobacter jejuni is the most common identified trigger without affecting the primary tissue pathway","EBV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper respiratory or GI infection (Campylobacter jejuni EBV CMV Mycoplasma)', 'GBS is typically preceded one to three weeks earlier by an upper respiratory or gastrointestinal infection, with Campylobacter jejuni the most frequently identified specific trigger. EBV, CMV, and Mycoplasma pneumoniae are additional recognized antecedent infections.'),
('777ba3f1-12ad-5117-9dd2-979a7b252563'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of cerebral palsy in preterm infants?', '["Kernicterus (bilirubin toxicity) resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE) in term infants mediated by specific receptor activation","Intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Periventricular leukomalacia (PVL) from ischemic injury to the periventricular white matter"]'::jsonb, 'Periventricular leukomalacia (PVL) from ischemic injury to the periventricular white matter', 'In preterm infants the periventricular white matter is especially vulnerable to ischemia, and periventricular leukomalacia is the most common cause of cerebral palsy in this group. IVH is a distinct preterm complication, while kernicterus, HIE in term infants, and trauma are less common causes of CP.'),
('a4201c58-07df-5477-96c4-de066a4a5854'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common type of cerebral palsy?', '["Ataxic resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypotonic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dyskinetic (athetoid dystonic) mediated by specific receptor activation","Spastic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spastic (about 80% of cases) especially spastic diplegia in preterm and spastic quadriplegia in term"]'::jsonb, 'Spastic (about 80% of cases) especially spastic diplegia in preterm and spastic quadriplegia in term', 'Spastic cerebral palsy accounts for about 80% of cases, with spastic diplegia typical of preterm infants and spastic quadriplegia of term infants. Dyskinetic, ataxic, and hypotonic forms are all much less common.'),
('69246a36-acfd-5b81-8f55-f3ab77c5ec95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE) in term infants?', '["Umbilical cord prolapse resulting in decreased functional activity","Perinatal asphyxia (umbilical cord prolapse uterine rupture placental abruption)","Uterine rupture mediated by specific receptor activation","Maternal hypotension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Placental abruption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Perinatal asphyxia (umbilical cord prolapse uterine rupture placental abruption)', 'HIE in term infants results from perinatal asphyxia, which can be caused by events such as umbilical cord prolapse, uterine rupture, or placental abruption. Cord prolapse, abruption, and maternal hypotension are individual mechanisms rather than the overall cause.'),
('248869ba-b202-5517-88b3-2c07b9ee33bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is Sarnat staging for HIE?', '["Biochemical staging resulting in decreased functional activity","Pathologic staging accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","EEG staging mediated by specific receptor activation","Radiographic staging without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clinical staging of HIE severity (Stage 1: mild Stage 2: moderate Stage 3: severe)"]'::jsonb, 'Clinical staging of HIE severity (Stage 1: mild Stage 2: moderate Stage 3: severe)', 'Sarnat staging is a clinical classification of HIE severity based on consciousness, tone, reflexes, and seizures, ranging from Stage 1 (mild) to Stage 3 (severe). It is a bedside clinical scheme rather than an EEG, imaging, biochemical, or pathologic one.'),
('e6df221d-c68d-5c82-b1bb-001bdb31b383'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of neonatal seizures?', '["Intraventricular hemorrhage (preterm) resulting in decreased functional activity","HIE (hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy) most common cause of neonatal seizures in term infants","CNS infection (meningitis) mediated by specific receptor activation","Stroke (perinatal arterial ischemic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metabolic (hypoglycemia hypocalcemia) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HIE (hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy) most common cause of neonatal seizures in term infants', 'Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy is the most common cause of seizures in term neonates, followed by stroke and metabolic disturbances. Intraventricular hemorrhage is the leading seizure cause in preterm infants, while CNS infection is less frequent.'),
('6917ad8c-3ca6-56f8-9913-4c6b2904f350'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of infantile spasms?', '["Tuberous sclerosis (TSCTSC2) resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortical malformation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Down syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","HIE without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cryptogenic (no identifiable cause in many) or symptomatic (tuberous sclerosis HIE cortical dysplasia)"]'::jsonb, 'Cryptogenic (no identifiable cause in many) or symptomatic (tuberous sclerosis HIE cortical dysplasia)', 'Infantile spasms are cryptogenic, with no identifiable cause, in a substantial number of infants and symptomatic in others; tuberous sclerosis, HIE, and cortical dysplasia are the main recognized symptomatic etiologies. No single cause predominates.'),
('99c35dd8-eaf7-530c-8fa8-b303da403e0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common genetic cause of infantile spasms?', '["CDKL5 mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","Tuberous sclerosis (TSC1/TSC2 mutations)","STXBP1 mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","ARX mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","KCNQ2 mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberous sclerosis (TSC1/TSC2 mutations)', 'Tuberous sclerosis, caused by TSC1 or TSC2 mutations, is the most common genetic cause of infantile spasms. CDKL5, STXBP1, ARX, and KCNQ2 mutations are less frequent genetic associations.'),
('74e38aad-bce4-5dce-8dc2-ff9e42c5956f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of febrile status epilepticus?', '["Pneumonia resulting in decreased functional activity","Otitis media accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HHV-6 infection (roseola) most common viral trigger","Rapid temperature rise without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastroenteritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HHV-6 infection (roseola) most common viral trigger', 'HHV-6, the virus responsible for roseola, is the most common viral trigger of febrile status epilepticus in young children. Pneumonia, otitis media, gastroenteritis, and rapid temperature rise alone are less commonly implicated.'),
('4f271e75-5379-5217-af78-a904f97591f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of acute ataxia in children?', '["Post-infectious cerebellitis (varicella EBV enterovirus)","Brain tumor (posterior fossa)","Guillain-Barré syndrome (Miller Fisher variant)","Migraine (basilar)","Drug intoxication (anticonvulsants alcohol)"]'::jsonb, 'Post-infectious cerebellitis (varicella EBV enterovirus)', 'Post-infectious cerebellitis following varicella, EBV, or enterovirus infection is the most common cause of acute ataxia in children. Posterior fossa tumors, Miller Fisher syndrome, basilar migraine, and drug intoxication are less frequent.'),
('3e7bbf22-23cd-5c0f-9825-ebefdcb1ea27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common posterior fossa tumor in children?', '["Medulloblastoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Pilocytic astrocytoma (cerebellar) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulloblastoma (most common malignant) then pilocytic astrocytoma (most common benign)","Ependymoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brainstem glioma (diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma DIPG) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulloblastoma (most common malignant) then pilocytic astrocytoma (most common benign)', 'Medulloblastoma is the most common malignant posterior fossa tumor of childhood, while pilocytic astrocytoma is the most common benign one, so together they dominate this site. Ependymoma and DIPG are less common posterior fossa lesions.'),
('3202b19b-2c4e-5e74-9583-0930b8e0b4d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common brainstem tumor in children?', '["Diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma (DIPG) is the most common brainstem tumor","Ganglioglioma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pilocytic astrocytoma (exophytic) mediated by specific receptor activation","Medulloblastoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ependymoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma (DIPG) is the most common brainstem tumor', 'Diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma arises within the pons and is the most common brainstem tumor in children. Ganglioglioma, exophytic pilocytic astrocytoma, ependymoma, and medulloblastoma are much rarer at this site.'),
('922aecaa-37ae-58f8-a2a6-09229ffb657d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of diplopia in children?', '["Strabismus (uncorrected esotropia exotropia) most common physiologic cause","Brainstem tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myasthenia gravis mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma (orbital fracture) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN III IV VI palsy from increased ICP (pseudotumor) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Strabismus (uncorrected esotropia exotropia) most common physiologic cause', 'In children, uncorrected strabismus, whether esotropia or exotropia, is the most common physiologic cause of diplopia. Cranial nerve palsies from raised ICP, myasthenia, orbital trauma, and brainstem tumors cause diplopia less often.'),
('a2df72e8-858c-57ef-a865-421e27afaeaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of third nerve palsy in children?', '["Infection (meningitis) resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased ICP (uncal herniation) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Migraine mediated by specific receptor activation","Myasthenia gravis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congenital (developmental) or traumatic (especially after neurosurgery)"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital (developmental) or traumatic (especially after neurosurgery)', 'Third nerve palsy in children is most often congenital (developmental) or traumatic, particularly after neurosurgical procedures. Uncal herniation from raised ICP, myasthenia, migraine, and meningitis are less common causes.'),
('4265664e-6b4f-548a-a42c-f904fbe267bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of sixth nerve palsy in children?', '["Infection (meningitis) resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neoplasm mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased intracranial pressure (pseudotumor hydrocephalus) most common cause"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intracranial pressure (pseudotumor hydrocephalus) most common cause', 'The abducens nerve has a long intracranial course, so elevated intracranial pressure, from pseudotumor cerebri or hydrocephalus, is the most common cause of sixth nerve palsy in children. Infection, trauma, neoplasm, and congenital causes are less frequent.'),
('8d2d379e-0e3f-580b-9382-57adcc2cf8a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of facial nerve palsy (Bell palsy) in children?', '["Otitis media (OM) resulting in decreased functional activity","Guillain-Barré syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic (Bell palsy) presumed viral (HSV-1) reactivation","Pontine tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (Bell palsy) presumed viral (HSV-1) reactivation', 'Bell palsy is an idiopathic, presumed HSV-1-related facial nerve palsy and is the most common cause of acute facial nerve palsy in children. Otitis media, GBS, pontine tumor, and Lyme disease are less common causes.'),
('fc4cdd28-73e3-5d62-b650-795a161353e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of bilateral facial nerve palsy?', '["Guillain-Barré syndrome (Miller Fisher variant)","Sarcoidosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lyme disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Meningitis (tuberculous carcinomatous) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Moebius syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Guillain-Barré syndrome (Miller Fisher variant)', 'Bilateral facial nerve palsy in children is most characteristic of the Miller Fisher variant of Guillain-Barré syndrome. Sarcoidosis, Lyme disease, meningitis, and Moebius syndrome are less common causes.'),
('c310cbc3-bf1e-5abb-bd2b-0751d9d8c1a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is Moebius syndrome?', '["Bilateral Bell palsy resulting in decreased functional activity","Congenital bilateral facial and abducens nerve palsy with limb anomalies","Congenital myasthenic syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Unilateral facial palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Isolated CN VI palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital bilateral facial and abducens nerve palsy with limb anomalies', 'Moebius syndrome is a congenital disorder featuring bilateral facial (seventh) and abducens (sixth) nerve palsy, often accompanied by limb and orofacial anomalies. It is distinct from Bell palsy, congenital myasthenic syndrome, and isolated sixth nerve palsy.'),
('52d1d732-a170-5a11-a1d3-f69d8d0f4a8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of vertigo in children?', '["Vestibular neuritis resulting in decreased functional activity","BPPV (benign paroxysmal positional vertigo) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Benign paroxysmal vertigo of childhood (migraine variant)","Meniere disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Labyrinthitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign paroxysmal vertigo of childhood (migraine variant)', 'Benign paroxysmal vertigo of childhood is a migraine variant and is the most common cause of vertigo in children. BPPV, vestibular neuritis, labyrinthitis, and Meniere disease are predominantly adult diagnoses.'),
('ab1f51e6-eb9d-526b-aee2-960da1670268'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of headache in children?', '["Tension-type headache (most common primary headache disorder)","Refractive error accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sinus headache mediated by specific receptor activation","Medication overuse without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Migraine (without aura is most common type of migraine) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tension-type headache (most common primary headache disorder)', 'Tension-type headache is the most common primary headache disorder in children, while migraine is the most common cause of recurrent severe headache. Refractive error, sinus disease, and medication overuse are secondary causes.'),
('59a5d733-5bb9-592a-aabc-33b8a889aa47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic daily headache in children?', '["Post-traumatic headache","Chronic tension-type headache","Pseudotumor cerebri","New daily persistent headache (NDPH)","Medication overuse headache (rebound) and chronic migraine"]'::jsonb, 'Medication overuse headache (rebound) and chronic migraine', 'Chronic daily headache in children most commonly reflects medication overuse (rebound) headache combined with chronic migraine. Pseudotumor cerebri, NDPH, and post-traumatic headache are less frequent causes.'),
('51423bf5-86bf-5937-9faa-1747a6f6931d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of acute symptomatic seizures in children?', '["Febrile seizure resulting in decreased functional activity","CNS infection (meningitis encephalitis) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Electrolyte disturbance (hyponatremia) mediated by specific receptor activation","Stroke without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head trauma (most common cause of acute symptomatic seizure)"]'::jsonb, 'Head trauma (most common cause of acute symptomatic seizure)', 'Head trauma is the most common cause of acute symptomatic seizures in children. CNS infection, electrolyte disturbance, febrile seizures, and stroke are less frequent precipitants.'),
('3f15285f-6694-56ff-bc28-e497f0bde1ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of subdural hematoma in children?', '["Coagulopathy (hemophilia) resulting in decreased functional activity","Head trauma (accidental or non-accidental abusive head trauma)","Aneurysm rupture mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningitis (subdural effusion) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Head trauma (accidental or non-accidental abusive head trauma)', 'In children, subdural hematoma almost always results from head trauma, including non-accidental (abusive) injury. Coagulopathy, aneurysm rupture, tumor, and meningitis-related subdural effusion are much rarer causes.'),
('5f813a3e-3e01-50e1-88f5-61cc03c59a34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of epidural hematoma in children?', '["Contrecoup injury resulting in decreased functional activity","Coagulopathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Venous sinus injury mediated by specific receptor activation","Arteriovenous malformation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head trauma with skull fracture causing injury to the middle meningeal artery (temporal bone fracture)"]'::jsonb, 'Head trauma with skull fracture causing injury to the middle meningeal artery (temporal bone fracture)', 'Epidural hematoma in children follows head trauma with a temporal skull fracture that lacerates the middle meningeal artery. Venous sinus injury, AVM, contrecoup injury, and coagulopathy do not produce the classic arterial epidural hematoma.'),
('6032fb22-8abd-5efa-9f8d-bc7675d1b03d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic subdural hematoma in the elderly?', '["Coagulopathy (anticoagulant therapy) resulting in decreased functional activity","Subdural hygroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Minor head trauma in patients with cerebral atrophy (brain shrinks stretching bridging veins)","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Minor head trauma in patients with cerebral atrophy (brain shrinks stretching bridging veins)', 'In the elderly, cerebral atrophy stretches the bridging veins, so even minor head trauma can tear them and produce a chronic subdural hematoma. Anticoagulation, infection, tumor, and hygroma are less common explanations.'),
('48156d1d-7fd6-5ba9-9135-74b803c7a89b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of cognitive impairment in children?', '["Genetic syndromes (Down syndrome Fragile X)","Prenatal exposures (Fetal alcohol syndrome)","Metabolic disorders (PKU)","Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE)","CNS infections (meningitis)"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic syndromes (Down syndrome Fragile X)', 'Genetic syndromes, especially Down syndrome and Fragile X, are the most common cause of cognitive impairment in children. Prenatal exposures, metabolic disorders, HIE, and CNS infections are less frequent overall.'),
('89dc5ec7-089e-5001-97ac-f29190784be9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common genetic cause of intellectual disability?', '["Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is the most common chromosomal cause","Fragile X syndrome (FM) is the most common inherited cause","Fragile X syndrome","Rett syndrome (MECP2 mutation in females)","Angelman syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is the most common chromosomal cause', 'Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is the most common chromosomal cause of intellectual disability, while Fragile X is the most common inherited single-gene cause. Rett and Angelman syndromes are much rarer.'),
('e48dbb02-a0ba-59bc-a6e8-eb7a4c889447'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of autism spectrum disorder (ASD)?', '["Idiopathic (no single cause; genetic predisposition with environmental triggers)","Fragile X syndrome (most common monogenic cause) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chromosomal abnormalities (15q duplication) mediated by specific receptor activation","Tuberous sclerosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rett syndrome (MECP2) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (no single cause; genetic predisposition with environmental triggers)', 'ASD is largely idiopathic, reflecting a genetic predisposition interacting with environmental triggers rather than a single cause. Fragile X, 15q duplication, tuberous sclerosis, and Rett syndrome account for only a minority of cases.'),
('85522ebd-4d7c-5ddd-bb7c-6ee619ba43e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of neonatal hypotonia?', '["Congenital myopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Benign congenital hypotonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peripheral (spinal muscular atrophy SMA) mediated by specific receptor activation","Central hypotonia (HIE intracranial hemorrhage) more common than peripheral causes","Congenital myotonic dystrophy (DM1) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central hypotonia (HIE intracranial hemorrhage) more common than peripheral causes', 'Central causes of neonatal hypotonia, such as HIE, intracranial hemorrhage, and other brain insults, are far more common than peripheral neuromuscular ones. SMA, congenital myopathies, and myotonic dystrophy are peripheral causes.'),
('ef82b87d-6686-5a41-a496-bdb1addafb57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common neuromuscular cause of neonatal hypotonia?', '["Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA type 1 Werdnig-Hoffman) most common peripheral cause","Botulism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital myotonic dystrophy (DM1) mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital myasthenic syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prader-Willi syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA type 1 Werdnig-Hoffman) most common peripheral cause', 'SMA type 1 (Werdnig-Hoffman) is the most common peripheral or neuromuscular cause of neonatal hypotonia. Botulism, congenital myotonic dystrophy, congenital myasthenic syndrome, and Prader-Willi syndrome are less common.'),
('237b62a2-1068-5fbe-87be-8a5a6735e176'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of progressive weakness in children?', '["Becker muscular dystrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) most common progressive muscular dystrophy in boys","Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy mediated by specific receptor activation","Limb-girdle dystrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) most common progressive muscular dystrophy in boys', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy, an X-linked dystrophinopathy, is the most common progressive muscular dystrophy of childhood and affects boys almost exclusively. Becker, facioscapulohumeral, and limb-girdle dystrophies and SMA are less common.'),
('f69800b3-65c8-56ab-ade8-9797fa4692b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of acute weakness in children?', '["Myasthenia gravis resulting in decreased functional activity","Transverse myelitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Guillain-Barré syndrome (acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy)","Electrolyte disturbance (hypokalemic periodic paralysis) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Botulism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Guillain-Barré syndrome (acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy)', 'Guillain-Barré syndrome, an acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy, is the most common cause of acute weakness in children. Botulism, myasthenia, transverse myelitis, and hypokalemic periodic paralysis are less frequent.'),
('826762e1-d649-5655-84d2-0c6609a4fe8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of myasthenia gravis in children?', '["Pediatric congenital myasthenic syndrome (less common) resulting in decreased functional activity","Seronegative MG accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ocular myasthenia mediated by specific receptor activation","Autoimmune (juvenile myasthenia gravis with anti-AChR antibodies) most common in adolescents","Neonatal myasthenia (transient from maternal antibodies) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune (juvenile myasthenia gravis with anti-AChR antibodies) most common in adolescents', 'Juvenile autoimmune myasthenia gravis with anti-AChR antibodies is the most common form of MG in children, peaking in adolescence. Congenital myasthenic syndromes, seronegative MG, ocular MG, and transient neonatal myasthenia are less common.'),
('d505eeed-62ef-5a8b-bb78-14bb1ab426cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of congenital myasthenic syndrome?', '["COLQ mutation (synaptic)","DOK7 mutation","RAPSN mutation","AChR subunit mutations (most common postsynaptic CMS)","CHAT deficiency (most common presynaptic CMS)"]'::jsonb, 'AChR subunit mutations (most common postsynaptic CMS)', 'Mutations in acetylcholine receptor subunit genes, especially CHRNE, are the most common cause of congenital myasthenic syndrome and produce a postsynaptic defect. CHAT deficiency is the most common presynaptic CMS, while COLQ, DOK7, and RAPSN mutations are less frequent.'),
('3c11532a-0c06-5167-8b9d-611caae7e262'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of sleep disorders in children?', '["Delayed sleep phase syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasomnias (sleepwalking night terrors) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obstructive sleep apnea (adenotonsillar hypertrophy) mediated by specific receptor activation","Behavioral insomnia of childhood (sleep-onset association and limit-setting types)","Restless leg syndrome (RLS) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Behavioral insomnia of childhood (sleep-onset association and limit-setting types)', 'Behavioral insomnia of childhood, including sleep-onset association and limit-setting types, is the most common sleep disorder in children. Obstructive sleep apnea, parasomnias, delayed sleep phase, and RLS are less common.'),
('d7952e6f-374c-5731-bb39-34da4a8a2380'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of excessive daytime sleepiness in adolescents?', '["Narcolepsy resulting in decreased functional activity","Medication side effects accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Delayed sleep phase syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Obstructive sleep apnea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Insufficient sleep syndrome (behavioral inadequate sleep time)"]'::jsonb, 'Insufficient sleep syndrome (behavioral inadequate sleep time)', 'Insufficient sleep syndrome, meaning chronically inadequate time in bed for behavioral reasons, is the most common cause of excessive daytime sleepiness in adolescents. Narcolepsy, obstructive sleep apnea, delayed sleep phase, and medication effects are less common.'),
('4d071f87-7c2e-5b94-a21b-02791b09394f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of narcolepsy?', '["Post-streptococcal autoimmune resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma (hypothalamic injury) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection (H1N1 influenza) mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of hypocretin (orexin)-producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus (autoimmune?) causing type 1 narcolepsy","Genetic (HLA DQB1*0602) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of hypocretin (orexin)-producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus (autoimmune?) causing type 1 narcolepsy', 'Narcolepsy type 1 results from loss of hypocretin (orexin)-producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, probably autoimmune in origin, and this hypocretin deficiency is the central mechanism. HLA DQB1*0602 is a genetic predisposing factor rather than the cause, while trauma, H1N1 infection, and streptococcal disease are not typical.'),
('9a2eed20-324b-5b1b-bfc6-6914b2189cd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of ataxia in children?', '["Friedreich ataxia (most common hereditary ataxia)","Brain tumor (posterior fossa)","Congenital cerebellar malformations (Dandy-Walker Chiari)","Acute post-infectious cerebellitis (most common acute cause)","Migraine (basilar)"]'::jsonb, 'Acute post-infectious cerebellitis (most common acute cause)', 'Acute post-infectious cerebellitis is the most common acute cause of ataxia in children. Friedreich ataxia and congenital cerebellar malformations cause chronic ataxia, while posterior fossa tumors and basilar migraine are less common.'),
('89f65a97-1212-56e3-a9a2-c6e7274c1e65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common hereditary ataxia?', '["Friedreich ataxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Episodic ataxia (EA) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA) are more common in adults mediated by specific receptor activation","Ataxia telangiectasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Friedreich ataxia (FRDA GAA repeat in frataxin gene) is the most common autosomal recessive ataxia"]'::jsonb, 'Friedreich ataxia (FRDA GAA repeat in frataxin gene) is the most common autosomal recessive ataxia', 'Friedreich ataxia, caused by a GAA triplet-repeat expansion in the frataxin gene, is the most common autosomal recessive ataxia and therefore the most common hereditary ataxia. Spinocerebellar and episodic ataxias are autosomal dominant and mostly adult-predominant, and ataxia telangiectasia is much rarer.'),
('e2251c8d-5065-5ac6-85ef-5ea3df39dea4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of tremors in children?', '["Cerebellar tremor (multiple sclerosis tumor)","Drug-induced tremor (valproate beta-agonists)","Essential tremor (most common movement disorder in children)","Dystonic tremor","Physiologic tremor (enhanced by anxiety fever caffeine)"]'::jsonb, 'Essential tremor (most common movement disorder in children)', 'Essential tremor is the most common movement disorder in children and the most common cause of tremor in this age group. Physiologic tremor, drug-induced tremor, cerebellar tremor, and dystonic tremor are less common.'),
('3edeb4a5-ea1c-579b-bb56-486da92877e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of tics in children?', '["Brain injury (hypoxia trauma) resulting in decreased functional activity","Medication-induced (stimulants) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Huntington disease (rare in children) mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary tic disorder (transient tic disorder most common then chronic tic Tourette)","Post-streptococcal (PANDAS) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary tic disorder (transient tic disorder most common then chronic tic Tourette)', 'Primary tic disorders are the most common cause of tics, with transient tic disorder being most frequent, followed by chronic tic disorder and Tourette syndrome. PANDAS, stimulant-induced tics, and brain injury are less common.'),
('fb75ba5b-b8da-503f-8281-2b57e9f5ebb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common movement disorder in children?', '["Chorea (Sydenham) resulting in decreased functional activity","Essential tremor (less common than tics) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dystonia (cervical limb) mediated by specific receptor activation","Tics (transient tic disorder affects up to 10-20% of school-age children)","Myoclonus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tics (transient tic disorder affects up to 10-20% of school-age children)', 'Tics, particularly transient tic disorder seen in up to 10-20% of school-age children, are the most common movement disorder of childhood. Sydenham chorea, essential tremor, dystonia, and myoclonus are far less common.'),
('86d1f809-f683-5451-a6e6-db67c581a52b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of Sydenham chorea?', '["Rheumatic fever (Group A streptococcal infection causing anti-basal ganglia antibodies)","Hyperthyroidism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pregnancy mediated by specific receptor activation","Lupus (SLE) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Drug-induced leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rheumatic fever (Group A streptococcal infection causing anti-basal ganglia antibodies)', 'Sydenham chorea is a manifestation of rheumatic fever, in which Group A streptococcal antibodies cross-react with basal ganglia tissue. Hyperthyroidism, pregnancy, SLE, and drug exposure are much rarer causes of chorea.'),
('0e7cf43a-eb40-51d1-808a-58cf1bd945ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of dystonia in children?', '["Metabolic (Wilson disease neurodegeneration with brain iron accumulation)","Trauma","Drug-induced (antipsychotics metoclopramide)","Primary dystonia (idiopathic genetic DYT1)","Cerebral palsy (secondary dystonia more common)"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral palsy (secondary dystonia more common)', 'In children, secondary dystonia is more common than primary, with cerebral palsy being the leading cause. Primary (idiopathic) DYT1 dystonia is the most common genetic primary form but accounts for a minority of pediatric dystonia, while metabolic, traumatic, and drug-induced causes are less frequent.'),
('d282bfd9-0110-5c78-a8c5-8a7c85ae1b15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of gait disturbance in children?', '["Neurologic (cerebellar ataxia CP) resulting in decreased functional activity","Rheumatologic (JIA) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neoplastic (tumor) mediated by specific receptor activation","Infectious (septic arthritis osteomyelitis) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Developmental (toe-walking intoeing) and trauma (fractures sprains)"]'::jsonb, 'Developmental (toe-walking intoeing) and trauma (fractures sprains)', 'In an otherwise healthy child with a normal examination, gait disturbance is usually benign; developmental variants such as in-toeing and toe-walking, together with minor trauma like fractures or sprains, are far more frequent. Neurologic, rheumatologic, infectious, and neoplastic causes are comparatively rare.'),
('8fdb203b-3928-580b-b2c6-f50782762e1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of acute gait abnormality in a febrile child?', '["Appendicitis (psoas irritation) resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transient synovitis of the hip mediated by specific receptor activation","Osteomyelitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Septic arthritis (most common cause of refusal to bear weight in febrile child)"]'::jsonb, 'Septic arthritis (most common cause of refusal to bear weight in febrile child)', 'A febrile child who refuses to bear weight most often has septic arthritis of the hip, where joint infection produces pain and immobility. Transient synovitis is milder and less toxic, while appendicitis, leukemia, and isolated osteomyelitis are less common causes of this presentation.'),
('3ae3d220-0d92-58c7-9f32-64fe92e74a83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of abnormal head shape in infants?', '["Encephalocele (posterior) resulting in decreased functional activity","Craniosynostosis (sagittal most common) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Positional plagiocephaly (most common from supine sleeping)","Anencephaly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subgaleal hematoma (birth trauma) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Positional plagiocephaly (most common from supine sleeping)', 'Occipital flattening from supine sleeping, called positional plagiocephaly, is the commonest cause of an odd head shape in infancy. Craniosynostosis is rarer and shows ridging with restricted fontanelle movement, while encephalocele, anencephaly, and subgaleal hematoma have distinct clinical features.'),
('ba0c2b3e-e097-59f9-934c-6e1e0697b733'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of macrocephaly in children?', '["Subdural effusion/hematoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebral edema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Megaencephaly (genetic overgrowth Sotos) mediated by specific receptor activation","Hydrocephalus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Familial macrocephaly (benign constitutional large head)"]'::jsonb, 'Familial macrocephaly (benign constitutional large head)', 'A large head with normal examination and a similarly large parent indicates familial (benign constitutional) macrocephaly, the most common cause. Hydrocephalus, subdural collections, cerebral edema, and the overgrowth of megencephaly are less common and usually accompanied by other signs.'),
('f4851115-2bbc-5566-9a38-b58cd29acc2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of microcephaly in children?', '["Metabolic (PKU) resulting in decreased functional activity","Perinatal HIE accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fetal alcohol syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital CMV infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Genetic (primary microcephaly MCPH1-ASPM) or environmental (congenital infection FAS drugs)"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic (primary microcephaly MCPH1-ASPM) or environmental (congenital infection FAS drugs)', 'Primary genetic causes such as MCPH1-ASPM mutations and prenatal environmental insults like congenital infection or fetal alcohol syndrome together account for most cases of microcephaly. Metabolic disease, perinatal HIE, and isolated FAS or CMV exposure each contribute a smaller share.'),
('f41a10e2-d1e1-5718-b6a7-47073544594e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common congenital infection causing microcephaly?', '["Herpes simplex resulting in decreased functional activity","CMV (cytomegalovirus) most common cause of congenital microcephaly worldwide","Toxoplasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Zika virus (outbreaks) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rubella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CMV (cytomegalovirus) most common cause of congenital microcephaly worldwide', 'Cytomegalovirus is the most frequent congenital infection responsible for microcephaly worldwide. Toxoplasma, rubella, Zika virus, and herpes simplex are less common congenital causes of microcephaly.'),
('10058b9a-2f87-51d9-ab4b-b28932739191'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of developmental delay in children?', '["Genetic (chromosomal microdeletion single gene)","Idiopathic (no identifiable cause after workup)","Postnatal (meningitis lead)","Perinatal HIE","Prenatal exposure (alcohol drugs)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (no identifiable cause after workup)', 'Even after a full evaluation no cause is found in most children with global developmental delay, so idiopathic cases form the largest group. Genetic, prenatal, perinatal, and postnatal causes each account for smaller proportions.'),
('f90e1a00-2c64-508a-a26f-75cc9d705d69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of motor delay in children?', '["Muscular dystrophy (Duchenne) resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal muscular atrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rickets (vitamin D deficiency) mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypothyroidism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypotonia (benign congenital hypotonia most common cause of gross motor delay) and CP"]'::jsonb, 'Hypotonia (benign congenital hypotonia most common cause of gross motor delay) and CP', 'Benign congenital hypotonia is the most common explanation for delayed gross motor milestones, and cerebral palsy is the leading pathologic cause. Duchenne dystrophy, spinal muscular atrophy, rickets, and hypothyroidism are rarer and show additional characteristic features.'),
('25d7da5c-f73a-54bd-bd0d-c87e68d9a5ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of speech delay in children?', '["Intellectual disability resulting in decreased functional activity","Developmental language delay (most common cause of isolated speech delay)","Psychosocial deprivation mediated by specific receptor activation","Autism spectrum disorder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hearing loss (conductive sensorineural) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Developmental language delay (most common cause of isolated speech delay)', 'In a child with isolated speech delay and otherwise normal development, developmental language delay is the usual explanation. Intellectual disability, autism, hearing loss, and psychosocial deprivation produce distinctive patterns or associated findings.'),
('f5511581-a734-5950-9a76-4a2d314d7d84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of toe walking in children?', '["Muscular dystrophy (Duchenne) resulting in decreased functional activity","Autism spectrum disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebral palsy (spastic diplegia short Achilles tendon) mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital short tendo Achilles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic (habitual) toe walking (most common cause in otherwise normal children)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (habitual) toe walking (most common cause in otherwise normal children)', 'In an otherwise neurologically normal child, persistent toe walking is usually a habit (idiopathic) that requires only observation. Cerebral palsy, congenital short tendo Achilles, and Duchenne muscular dystrophy are pathologic and show other signs.'),
('b30eacf3-2167-5f48-a533-5dc6ffd36c3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of muscle cramps in children?', '["Exercise-induced (overuse)","Dehydration/electrolyte imbalance (hyponatremia)","Medications (diuretics statins)","Benign nocturnal leg cramps (idiopathic and related to exercise)","McArdle disease (myophosphorylase deficiency)"]'::jsonb, 'Benign nocturnal leg cramps (idiopathic and related to exercise)', 'Idiopathic nocturnal leg cramps of childhood are the usual cause of muscle cramping and are generally benign. Overuse, dehydration or electrolyte imbalance, medication, and McArdle disease produce cramping with more specific triggers or associations.'),
('5f5735a7-5eec-545f-baf3-860db1af4f37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of malignant hyperthermia susceptibility?', '["CACNA1S mutation (calcium channel) resulting in decreased functional activity","RYR1 mutation (ryanodine receptor gene) accounts for about 50-70% of cases","SCN4A mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","CLCN1 mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DMD mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'RYR1 mutation (ryanodine receptor gene) accounts for about 50-70% of cases', 'The RYR1 gene encoding the skeletal muscle ryanodine receptor is mutated in about half to two-thirds of malignant hyperthermia susceptibilities. CACNA1S accounts for a small minority, while SCN4A, CLCN1, and DMD mutations cause other neuromuscular disorders.'),
('183a9577-0370-5ac3-862e-499038546b9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hyperekplexia (startle disease)?', '["SLC6A5 mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","GLRB mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GPHN mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","ARHGEF9 mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GLRA1 mutation (glycine receptor alpha-1 subunit)"]'::jsonb, 'GLRA1 mutation (glycine receptor alpha-1 subunit)', 'Mutations of GLRA1, the alpha-1 subunit of the inhibitory glycine receptor, cause most familial hyperekplexia. GLRB, GPHN, SLC6A5, and ARHGEF9 mutations account for fewer cases.'),
('71d81581-871f-536a-bf1b-962a47fcf3ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of mitochondrial disease in children?', '["Pearson syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","MERFF (m.8344A>G) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leigh syndrome (complex I deficiency SURF1) mediated by specific receptor activation","POLG mutations (most common cause of mitochondrial disorders in children)","MELAS (m.3243A>G) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'POLG mutations (most common cause of mitochondrial disorders in children)', 'Mutations in POLG, the gene for mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma, are the most frequent identifiable genetic cause of mitochondrial disease in children. MELAS, MERRF, Leigh syndrome, and Pearson syndrome represent distinct and less common entities.'),
('6c293fa2-37be-5b36-9165-02e4b0d9d505'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common mitochondrial encephalomyopathy in children?', '["Kearns-Sayre syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","MERFF (myoclonus epilepsy ragged red fibers) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NARP (neuropathy ataxia retinitis pigmentosa) mediated by specific receptor activation","MELAS (mitochondrial encephalopathy lactic acidosis stroke-like episodes) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leigh syndrome (subacute necrotizing encephalomyelopathy) most common childhood mitochondrial disorder"]'::jsonb, 'Leigh syndrome (subacute necrotizing encephalomyelopathy) most common childhood mitochondrial disorder', 'Leigh syndrome, or subacute necrotizing encephalomyelopathy, is the most common mitochondrial encephalomyopathy of childhood. MELAS, MERRF, NARP, and Kearns-Sayre syndrome are other mitochondrial disorders that occur less frequently in this age group.'),
('97fcce34-e553-5ecf-b4a0-155efc57810e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of Leigh syndrome?', '["Complex III deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complex I deficiency (most common) caused by SURF1 or other mitochondrial/nuclear gene mutations","Complex II deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complex IV deficiency (COX deficiency) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complex I deficiency (most common) caused by SURF1 or other mitochondrial/nuclear gene mutations', 'Leigh syndrome most often results from Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase) deficiency, frequently due to SURF1 or other nuclear and mitochondrial gene mutations. COX (Complex IV) and other complex deficiencies, plus pyruvate dehydrogenase complex deficiency, are less common causes.'),
('b34948e2-d3fa-5bde-9271-97ca5f027d40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of stroke in children with mitochondrial disease?', '["MELAS (stroke-like episodes due to m.3243A>G mutation in MT-TL1)","NARP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Kearns-Sayre mediated by specific receptor activation","Pearson syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leigh syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MELAS (stroke-like episodes due to m.3243A>G mutation in MT-TL1)', 'Stroke-like episodes are the hallmark of MELAS, which is caused by the m.3243A>G mutation in the MT-TL1 gene. NARP, Kearns-Sayre syndrome, Pearson syndrome, and Leigh syndrome do not characteristically present with stroke-like events.'),
('598a5ef2-577f-570b-b009-06edbfb0f9b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of inborn errors of metabolism presenting in the newborn period?', '["Peroxisomal disorders (Zellweger) resulting in decreased functional activity","Galactosemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amino acid disorders (MSUD PKU) mediated by specific receptor activation","Urea cycle disorders and organic acidemias most common hyperammonemia causes","Fatty acid oxidation defects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urea cycle disorders and organic acidemias most common hyperammonemia causes', 'Neonatal hyperammonemia with encephalopathy most often reflects a urea cycle disorder or an organic acidemia, the most common symptomatic inborn errors at this age. Galactosemia, fatty acid oxidation defects, and other amino acid disorders such as MSUD and PKU present with different predominant features or later onsets.'),
('3dabbfdc-cf38-5a16-8dfb-20f50351c48c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hypoglycemia in newborns?', '["Ketotic hypoglycemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Glycogen storage disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Galactosemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Fatty acid oxidation defects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperinsulinism (most common cause of persistent hypoglycemia in neonates)"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperinsulinism (most common cause of persistent hypoglycemia in neonates)', 'Hyperinsulinism is the most common cause of persistent hypoglycemia in the newborn. Ketotic hypoglycemia, glycogen storage disease, galactosemia, and fatty acid oxidation defects are less common and typically appear with other features or at an older age.'),
('6bec6b3b-d4c0-5b85-be6d-e69c48e56a25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of neonatal hyperammonemia?', '["Urea cycle deficiency (OTC deficiency most common X-linked)","Carbamyl phosphate synthetase deficiency","N-acetylglutamate synthetase deficiency","Organic acidemia (propionic methylmalonic)","Transient hyperammonemia of the newborn (most common in premature infants)"]'::jsonb, 'Transient hyperammonemia of the newborn (most common in premature infants)', 'Transient hyperammonemia of the newborn, seen especially in premature infants, is the commonest cause of neonatal hyperammonemia. Urea cycle deficiencies such as OTC and carbamyl phosphate synthetase, and organic acidemias, must be excluded but occur less often.'),
('af5183b8-e6f4-516c-ae1e-cc21e22e5d09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common organic acidemia?', '["Propionic acidemia (most common) and methylmalonic acidemia (also very common)","Isovaleric acidemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD branched-chain aminoacidopathy) mediated by specific receptor activation","3-Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glutaric acidemia type 1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Propionic acidemia (most common) and methylmalonic acidemia (also very common)', 'Propionic acidemia is the most common organic acidemia, with methylmalonic acidemia also frequent. Maple syrup urine disease is a branched-chain amino acid disorder rather than an organic acidemia, and the other listed conditions are rarer.'),
('78a598d4-8557-5f86-b490-6a3872c2b3fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common aminoacidopathy?', '["Hartnup disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Homocystinuria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phenylketonuria (PKU) most common aminoacidopathy","Tyrosinemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phenylketonuria (PKU) most common aminoacidopathy', 'Phenylketonuria, from deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase, is the most common aminoacidopathy. Maple syrup urine disease, tyrosinemia, homocystinuria, and Hartnup disease occur less frequently.'),
('d60ca3d7-6441-56d0-8637-4afd41b616dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common lysosomal storage disorder?', '["Mucopolysaccharidosis I (Hurler) resulting in decreased functional activity","Tay-Sachs disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gaucher disease (most common lysosomal storage disorder) followed by Fabry and Niemann-Pick","Krabbe disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metachromatic leukodystrophy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gaucher disease (most common lysosomal storage disorder) followed by Fabry and Niemann-Pick', 'Gaucher disease, caused by glucocerebrosidase deficiency, is the most common lysosomal storage disorder, with Fabry and Niemann-Pick following in frequency. Hurler syndrome, Tay-Sachs, Krabbe disease, and metachromatic leukodystrophy are less common.'),
('abaed0d5-070b-561e-95fe-cda6fdd23fde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common type of Gaucher disease?', '["Type 1 (non-neuronopathic) is the most common (adult type without CNS involvement)","Type 3 (chronic neuronopathic juvenile) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type 2 (acute neuronopathic infantile) mediated by specific receptor activation","Perinatal lethal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type 0 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type 1 (non-neuronopathic) is the most common (adult type without CNS involvement)', 'Type 1 Gaucher disease is the most common form and is non-neuronopathic, sparing the nervous system. Types 2 and 3 are the acute and chronic neuronopathic forms respectively, while the perinatal lethal and type 0 variants are very rare.'),
('b6226a65-59ab-55a9-9654-9b24cc4cf169'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common leukodystrophy?', '["Krabbe disease (globoid cell) resulting in decreased functional activity","X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metachromatic leukodystrophy (ARSA deficiency) and X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD) are the most common","Metachromatic leukodystrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Canavan disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metachromatic leukodystrophy (ARSA deficiency) and X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD) are the most common', 'Metachromatic leukodystrophy, caused by arylsulfatase A deficiency, and X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy are the two most common leukodystrophies. Krabbe disease and Canavan disease occur less frequently.'),
('eab91cb5-4fb2-5422-af99-9241a58c3d18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common peroxisomal disorder?', '["Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata","Refsum disease","Zellweger syndrome (most severe)","X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD) is the most common","Neonatal adrenoleukodystrophy"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD) is the most common', 'X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy, from ABCD1 mutation leading to very-long-chain fatty acid accumulation, is the most common peroxisomal disorder. Zellweger syndrome is the most severe peroxisome biogenesis defect, and the other options are less common peroxisomal conditions.'),
('04877a8a-f6c1-5b83-a22a-bb7b3232f541'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of adrenal insufficiency in boys with neurologic regression?', '["Metabolic (mitochondrial)","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Adrenal hemorrhage","Autoimmune adrenalitis (Addison disease)","X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD)"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD)', 'A boy with adrenocortical failure plus progressive neurologic regression classically has X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy, where the white matter and adrenal glands are both affected. Autoimmune adrenalitis, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, and adrenal hemorrhage do not produce this neurologic picture.'),
('b30e20b8-6a54-529b-b343-bf8cbe1aca42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of childhood headache requiring neuroimaging?', '["Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor)","Migraine (most common reason for headache evaluation in children)","Brain tumor (rare but must be excluded)","Hydrocephalus","Vascular malformation"]'::jsonb, 'Migraine (most common reason for headache evaluation in children)', 'Migraine is the most frequent reason children come for headache evaluation, and most such children do not require imaging. Brain tumors, hydrocephalus, vascular malformations, and pseudotumor are rare but must be excluded when red flags are present.'),
('19d3519e-2408-5774-a5e5-0b4d894f4bb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What are the red flags for brain tumor in children with headache?', '["Sinus pressure resulting in decreased functional activity","Nausea photophobia phonophobia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Throbbing headache mediated by specific receptor activation","Family history of migraine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Waking from sleep vomiting early morning headache progressive worsening neurologic deficits seizures"]'::jsonb, 'Waking from sleep vomiting early morning headache progressive worsening neurologic deficits seizures', 'Headaches that wake a child from sleep, occur early morning with vomiting, worsen progressively, or accompany focal neurologic deficits and seizures raise concern for a tumor. Sinus pressure, nausea with photophobia, a throbbing quality, and a family history of migraine instead suggest a primary headache disorder.'),
('b2bc5700-56c5-5ec7-87d5-fb29d51bd582'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of papilledema in children with headache?', '["Venous sinus thrombosis (otitic hydrocephalus) resulting in decreased functional activity","Brain tumor (posterior fossa) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor) most common in obese adolescent girls","Hydrocephalus (aqueductal stenosis) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor) most common in obese adolescent girls', 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri) is the commonest cause of papilledema in children with headache, particularly in obese adolescent girls. Posterior fossa tumors, hydrocephalus, venous sinus thrombosis, and meningitis are less frequent causes.'),
('fc8799a4-c0f0-5c4b-bf2a-aefa76f293a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of acute hemiparesis in children?', '["Demyelinating (multiple sclerosis tumefactive)","Todd paralysis (post-ictal)","Complicated migraine (hemiplegic migraine)","Transient ischemic attack (rare in children)","Stroke (ischemic or hemorrhagic) most common cause"]'::jsonb, 'Stroke (ischemic or hemorrhagic) most common cause', 'A sudden hemiparesis in a child is most often due to stroke, either ischemic or hemorrhagic. Todd paralysis, hemiplegic migraine, demyelination, and transient ischemic attack are less common or produce transient symptoms.'),
('8013dfd0-4225-5b1f-b09c-767be51ea8c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of stroke in neonates?', '["Perinatal arterial ischemic stroke (AIS) most common in term neonates","Cerebral sinovenous thrombosis (CSVT) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardioembolic (CHD) mediated by specific receptor activation","IVH (preterm) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (global) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Perinatal arterial ischemic stroke (AIS) most common in term neonates', 'Perinatal arterial ischemic stroke, typically in the middle cerebral artery territory of a term neonate, is the commonest stroke type in newborns. Cerebral sinovenous thrombosis, cardioembolism, preterm germinal matrix IVH, and global HIE are less common.'),
('473947df-5267-57bf-ad7a-a61c18543442'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of stroke in children with sickle cell disease?', '["Cerebral large vessel arteriopathy (occlusion of distal ICA/MCA) causing ischemic stroke","Hemorrhagic stroke (AVM rupture) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Embolic (from CHD) mediated by specific receptor activation","Moyamoya syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebral sinovenous thrombosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral large vessel arteriopathy (occlusion of distal ICA/MCA) causing ischemic stroke', 'In sickle cell disease most strokes are ischemic, resulting from progressive large vessel arteriopathy that occludes the distal internal carotid or middle cerebral arteries. Moyamoya changes, hemorrhagic events, embolism, and sinovenous thrombosis are less common.'),
('2d5efd90-3228-5510-b453-255e3d2d7342'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent stroke in children?', '["Vasculitis (CNS vasculitis) resulting in decreased functional activity","Coagulopathy (Factor V Leiden) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sickle cell disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardioembolic (CHD) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Moyamoya disease/syndrome (most common cause of recurrent pediatric stroke)"]'::jsonb, 'Moyamoya disease/syndrome (most common cause of recurrent pediatric stroke)', 'Moyamoya, a progressive occlusion of intracranial vessels with the formation of collateral networks, is the most frequent cause of recurrent stroke in children. Sickle cell disease, vasculitis, coagulopathy, and cardioembolism can also recur but do so less often.'),
('c3b31bd9-0d51-53e1-8e8c-ec7005135cd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of cerebral sinovenous thrombosis (CSVT) in children?', '["Malignancy (leukemia) resulting in decreased functional activity","Dehydration (especially in infants) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prothrombotic states (Factor V Leiden protein C/S deficiency) mediated by specific receptor activation","Head and neck infections (mastoiditis sinusitis) most common cause","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Head and neck infections (mastoiditis sinusitis) most common cause', 'Head and neck infection, especially mastoiditis and sinusitis, is the leading cause of cerebral sinovenous thrombosis in childhood. Dehydration matters mainly in infants, while malignancy, prothrombotic states, and trauma are less frequent triggers.'),
('5d59f2bb-b295-5e01-ba2e-8a9b398ebe63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of intracranial aneurysm in children?', '["Coarctation of the aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Genetic (autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease-ADPKD) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD) mediated by specific receptor activation","Idiopathic (most are sporadic) and post-infectious (mycotic infectious endocarditis)","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (most are sporadic) and post-infectious (mycotic infectious endocarditis)', 'Most intracranial aneurysms in children are sporadic and idiopathic, with mycotic aneurysms from infective endocarditis among the identifiable causes. Genetic syndromes such as ADPKD, coarctation of the aorta, fibromuscular dysplasia, and trauma are rarer.'),
('00771b13-cecc-5002-a604-46016d599bf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common brain arteriovenous malformation (AVM) presentation in children?', '["Headaches resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurologic deficit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hemorrhage (most common presentation of AVM in children)","Asymptomatic bruit without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Seizures (most common presentation in adults) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hemorrhage (most common presentation of AVM in children)', 'Intracranial hemorrhage is the most common presenting feature of a brain AVM in children, in contrast to adults in whom seizures predominate. Headache, fixed neurologic deficit, and an asymptomatic bruit occur less often as the initial presentation.'),
('a1ec27e6-c945-5efa-97e7-9aad2ca1b737'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of spontaneous intracranial hemorrhage in a term newborn?', '["AVM resulting in decreased functional activity","Aneurysm (rare in newborn) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","(IVH in preterm not term) mediated by specific receptor activation","IVH from germinal matrix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemorrhagic stroke (perinatal AIS with hemorrhagic transformation) or bleeding diathesis (vitamin K deficiency)"]'::jsonb, 'Hemorrhagic stroke (perinatal AIS with hemorrhagic transformation) or bleeding diathesis (vitamin K deficiency)', 'In a term newborn spontaneous bleeding is usually explained by perinatal hemorrhagic stroke, often AIS with hemorrhagic transformation, or a bleeding diathesis such as vitamin K deficiency. Germinal matrix IVH is predominantly a preterm phenomenon, while AVM and aneurysm are rare.'),
('35624ce7-648b-5cb3-826a-4a1a93a52a29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH) in preterm infants?', '["Thrombosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma (birth) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coagulopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Germinal matrix hemorrhage (GMH) from fragile vessels in the subependymal germinal matrix","Hypoxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Germinal matrix hemorrhage (GMH) from fragile vessels in the subependymal germinal matrix', 'IVH in premature infants arises from bleeding in the fragile subependymal germinal matrix, whose immature vessels rupture with fluctuations in cerebral blood flow. Hypoxia, coagulopathy, thrombosis, and birth trauma are not the primary mechanism.'),
('f74554e5-7781-5181-842a-c17ce5a820a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the grading of IVH in preterm infants?', '["Graded by neurologic outcome resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Grade 1: germinal matrix only; Grade 2: IVH without ventricular dilation; Grade 3: IVH with dilation; Grade 4: intraparenchymal venous infarction","Graded by volume mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Graded by need for shunt without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Graded by gestational age leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Grade 1: germinal matrix only; Grade 2: IVH without ventricular dilation; Grade 3: IVH with dilation; Grade 4: intraparenchymal venous infarction', 'IVH is graded anatomically on imaging: grade 1 is germinal matrix hemorrhage alone, grade 2 adds intraventricular blood without ventricular dilation, grade 3 shows ventricular enlargement, and grade 4 includes intraparenchymal venous infarction. Grading rests on imaging appearance, not volume, outcome, shunt need, or gestational age.'),
('876ae5b3-6e55-5353-a8d4-0608e9ec3962'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common complication of grade 3-4 IVH?', '["Post-hemorrhagic hydrocephalus (PHH) most common complication of severe IVH","Periventricular leukomalacia (PVL) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebral palsy mediated by specific receptor activation","Cognitive impairment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Seizures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-hemorrhagic hydrocephalus (PHH) most common complication of severe IVH', 'Severe (grades 3-4) IVH most often leads to post-hemorrhagic hydrocephalus, because blood products obstruct the CSF pathways and arachnoid granulations. Periventricular leukomalacia occurs from a separate ischemic process, while cerebral palsy, cognitive impairment, and seizures are downstream consequences.'),
('ef151174-bd6d-5a28-b14b-cba6293905cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of periventricular leukomalacia (PVL)?', '["Hypoglycemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypocapnia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sepsis mediated by specific receptor activation","IVH extension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ischemic injury to the periventricular white matter (watershed zones) in preterm infants"]'::jsonb, 'Ischemic injury to the periventricular white matter (watershed zones) in preterm infants', 'PVL results from ischemic injury to the periventricular white matter, which lies in watershed zones that are especially vulnerable in preterm infants during blood pressure fluctuations and hypocapnia. Hypoglycemia, sepsis, and extension of IVH are not the direct mechanism.'),
('e0620a6d-79cf-5a05-adf7-b74263ee0837'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the classic neurologic finding in PVL?', '["Spastic quadriplegia resulting in decreased functional activity","Spastic diplegia (lower extremities more affected than upper)","Hemiplegia mediated by specific receptor activation","Choreoathetosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ataxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spastic diplegia (lower extremities more affected than upper)', 'PVL damages the descending corticospinal fibers that supply the lower limbs, producing spastic diplegia with greater leg than arm involvement. Quadriplegia, hemiplegia, choreoathetosis, and ataxia indicate different patterns or sites of injury.'),
('c237386c-5add-5d36-bb88-9af2a4899f7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of blindness in children with HIE?', '["Glaucoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortical visual impairment (CVI from occipital lobe injury)","Optic nerve atrophy (HIE) mediated by specific receptor activation","Cataract (congenital rubella) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortical visual impairment (CVI from occipital lobe injury)', 'Blindness after hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy is most often cortical visual impairment due to occipital lobe injury rather than ocular disease. Glaucoma, cataract, optic nerve atrophy, and retinopathy of prematurity are separate ocular causes.'),
('6678064c-eeb0-5c0d-a682-b86b3b561a39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of delayed visual maturation in infants?', '["Normal variant (most resolve spontaneously with development)","Cortical visual impairment (CVI) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Optic nerve hypoplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Leber congenital amaurosis (LCA) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congenital cataract leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal variant (most resolve spontaneously with development)', 'Delayed visual maturation is a normal variant in infants with structurally normal eyes and brain, and vision typically improves spontaneously in the first months. CVI, optic nerve hypoplasia, Leber congenital amaurosis, and congenital cataract are pathologic causes.'),
('ce79ef5f-5de2-5ed5-be59-f7c25e3155ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hearing loss in children with HIE?', '["Auditory neuropathy spectrum disorder (ANSD) resulting in decreased functional activity","Conductive hearing loss (middle ear effusion) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Central auditory processing disorder mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensorineural hearing loss (from damage to the cochlear nuclei in the brainstem)","Mixed hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensorineural hearing loss (from damage to the cochlear nuclei in the brainstem)', 'Sensorineural hearing loss, from injury to the cochlear nuclei in the brainstem, is the commonest hearing impairment in hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy. Conductive loss, auditory neuropathy, central auditory processing disorder, and mixed hearing loss are less typical of HIE.'),
('db91221a-0550-578d-97f7-5f994bc39d6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of epilepsy in cerebral palsy?', '["Idiopathic resulting in decreased functional activity","Structural epilepsy from the underlying brain injury (cortical dysplasia periventricular injury)","Metabolic mediated by specific receptor activation","Familial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Post-traumatic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Structural epilepsy from the underlying brain injury (cortical dysplasia periventricular injury)', 'Epilepsy in cerebral palsy is most often structural, arising from the underlying brain lesion such as cortical dysplasia or periventricular injury. Idiopathic, metabolic, familial, and post-traumatic epilepsies are not characteristic of cerebral palsy.'),
('b08006d9-623a-5085-8534-9ff7aaaff527'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of feeding difficulties in children with cerebral palsy?', '["Constipation (immobility) resulting in decreased functional activity","Dental caries from antiepileptics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GERD (from spasticity and positioning) mediated by specific receptor activation","Oropharyngeal dysphagia (from pseudobulbar palsy poor oral motor control)","Poor dentition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oropharyngeal dysphagia (from pseudobulbar palsy poor oral motor control)', 'Feeding difficulties in cerebral palsy mainly reflect oropharyngeal dysphagia from pseudobulbar palsy with poor oral motor control and impaired swallowing coordination. GERD, constipation, dental caries, and poor dentition are contributing or secondary issues.'),
('0ac37374-f5ab-556b-bc23-e7c810f98a71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hip dislocation in children with CP?', '["Spasticity causing adductor overpull and hip subluxation/dislocation","Acetabular dysplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteoporosis (immobility) mediated by specific receptor activation","Seizure-related trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leg length inequality leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spasticity causing adductor overpull and hip subluxation/dislocation', 'Persistent spasticity of the hip adductors and flexors pulls the femoral head from a shallow dysplastic acetabulum, causing progressive subluxation and eventual dislocation. Acetabular dysplasia, osteoporosis, seizure-related trauma, and leg length inequality are less direct causes.'),
('571c74c1-174e-53fd-8708-8299a5259756'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of scoliosis in CP?', '["Neuromuscular scoliosis from asymmetric trunk muscle tone (spasticity weakness)","Compensatory (leg length inequality) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital scoliosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Syndromic scoliosis (Rett) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic scoliosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuromuscular scoliosis from asymmetric trunk muscle tone (spasticity weakness)', 'Neuromuscular scoliosis is the most common scoliosis type in cerebral palsy, driven by asymmetric trunk muscle tone with spasticity and weakness. Idiopathic, congenital, syndromic such as in Rett syndrome, and compensatory scoliosis rely on other mechanisms.'),
('af5eb586-e624-5308-b2a2-a9625a2901fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of death in children with severe CP?', '["Seizure-related (SUDEP) resulting in decreased functional activity","Malnutrition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection (UTI sepsis) mediated by specific receptor activation","Aspiration pneumonia and respiratory failure (most common cause of death)","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aspiration pneumonia and respiratory failure (most common cause of death)', 'Swallowing dysfunction and gastroesophageal reflux in severe cerebral palsy predispose to recurrent aspiration, making pneumonia with respiratory failure the leading cause of death. Seizures, malnutrition, and sepsis are less commonly the terminal event.'),
('1693e51d-56e4-50e4-9180-94db8831a13d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of intellectual disability in CP?', '["PVL (preterm with diplegia)","Post-infectious (meningitis)","The underlying brain injury causing CP also impairs cognition","HIE (term infant with quadriplegia)","Cortical dysplasia (malformation)"]'::jsonb, 'The underlying brain injury causing CP also impairs cognition', 'The same perinatal insult that produces the motor deficit in cerebral palsy frequently injures cortical circuits subserving cognition. PVL, HIE, and cortical dysplasia are patterns of that underlying injury rather than separate causes.'),
('b81a69d3-cb06-5fff-bdff-bcf5989db647'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common seizure type in children with CP?', '["Myoclonic seizures resulting in decreased functional activity","Atonic seizures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infantile spasms (in younger infants with more severe injury) mediated by specific receptor activation","Absence seizures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Focal seizures (from focal cortical injury) and generalized tonic-clonic (secondary generalization)"]'::jsonb, 'Focal seizures (from focal cortical injury) and generalized tonic-clonic (secondary generalization)', 'Focal cortical injury typical of cerebral palsy produces focal seizures that often secondarily generalize, making this the most common seizure type. Primary generalized epilepsies such as absence and myoclonic seizures are less characteristic of CP.'),
('293884b6-d8d4-596c-a1f3-1da865a92133'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of osteopenia in children with CP?', '["Malabsorption (GERD / tube feeding issues) resulting in decreased functional activity","Immobility (non-weight bearing) and anticonvulsant use (enzyme-inducing AEDs)","Vitamin D deficiency (sunlight) mediated by specific receptor activation","Growth hormone deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcium deficiency (dietary) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immobility (non-weight bearing) and anticonvulsant use (enzyme-inducing AEDs)', 'Non-weight bearing removes the mechanical stimulus for bone formation, while enzyme-inducing anticonvulsants accelerate vitamin D degradation, together causing osteopenia in CP. Dietary deficiencies and malabsorption contribute less.'),
('7656c3ff-f748-511c-bcbb-9ed2d149fd2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of pain in non-verbal children with CP?', '["Constipation resulting in decreased functional activity","Hip subluxation/dislocation and gastroesophageal reflux","Muscle spasms mediated by specific receptor activation","Hip pain is most common cause of pain in non-ambulatory children without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GERD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hip subluxation/dislocation and gastroesophageal reflux', 'Spastic hip subluxation or dislocation and gastroesophageal reflux are the most frequently identified pain sources in non-verbal children with CP. Constipation and muscle spasms are contributing but less dominant causes.'),
('d4bbd8ec-2881-5e82-a42c-d2cc29dddad5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of drooling in CP?', '["Dental malocclusion resulting in decreased functional activity","Poor oral motor control (oromotor dysfunction from pseudobulbar palsy)","Medication side effect mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypersalivation (excessive production) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enlarged tonsils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Poor oral motor control (oromotor dysfunction from pseudobulbar palsy)', 'Drooling in CP stems from impaired oromotor coordination and swallowing rather than excess saliva production; pseudobulbar palsy compromises lip seal and deglutition. Hypersalivation and dental causes are rarely the primary mechanism.'),
('d7041666-fc77-50c1-834b-7da6fea54716'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of constipation in children with CP?', '["Poor gut motility (immobility weak abdominal muscles inadequate fiber/fluid)","Sequelae of Nissen fundoplication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medication side effects (anticonvulsants) mediated by specific receptor activation","Dehydration (poor oral intake) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Structural (Hirschsprung) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Poor gut motility (immobility weak abdominal muscles inadequate fiber/fluid)', 'Reduced physical activity, weak abdominal muscles, and low fiber and fluid intake slow colonic transit, the main cause of constipation in CP. Structural disease such as Hirschsprung disease is uncommon.'),
('d9fca47f-9476-550c-b60b-53ed50f58771'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of epilepsy surgery in children?', '["Sturge-Weber resulting in decreased functional activity","Focal cortical dysplasia (most common pathology in pediatric epilepsy surgery)","Hippocampal sclerosis (more common in adults) mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor (ganglioglioma DNET) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rasmussen encephalitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Focal cortical dysplasia (most common pathology in pediatric epilepsy surgery)', 'Focal cortical dysplasia, a developmental malformation of cortical organization, is the most frequently resected lesion in children undergoing epilepsy surgery. Hippocampal sclerosis is the leading substrate in adults instead.'),
('70fb05c1-372c-50c3-816c-4f2f86921f7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of Rasmussen encephalitis?', '["Genetic (mutations in GRIN2A) resulting in decreased functional activity","Paraneoplastic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Post-vaccinal mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral infection (CMV EBV) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unknown (likely autoimmune T-cell-mediated unilateral hemispheric inflammation)"]'::jsonb, 'Unknown (likely autoimmune T-cell-mediated unilateral hemispheric inflammation)', 'The cause of Rasmussen encephalitis is unknown, but T-cell-mediated inflammation destroying one cerebral hemisphere suggests an autoimmune mechanism. No virus or neoplasm has been confirmed as causative.'),
('5d0b7c97-2c32-58ac-ade6-f841bec9d9c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of drop attacks in children?', '["Atonic seizures (most common cause of drop attacks) and tonic seizures (with falls)","Hyperekplexia (exaggerated startle) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vertebrobasilar insufficiency (rare in children) mediated by specific receptor activation","Cataplexy (narcolepsy) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Orthostatic hypotension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atonic seizures (most common cause of drop attacks) and tonic seizures (with falls)', 'Atonic seizures cause sudden loss of postural tone and tonic seizures produce stiffening with falls; together they are the leading cause of drop attacks in children. Cataplexy and syncope are much less common.'),
('39f61e3d-8944-5638-82d3-f40f058b3617'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of Lennox-Gastaut syndrome?', '["Post-infectious (meningitis) resulting in decreased functional activity","Symptomatic (prior brain injury HIE cortical malformation TSC) most common; up to 30% are cryptogenic","HIE (hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy) mediated by specific receptor activation","Tuberous sclerosis (TSC) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortical dysplasia (focal) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Symptomatic (prior brain injury HIE cortical malformation TSC) most common; up to 30% are cryptogenic', 'Most children with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome have an identifiable prior brain insult such as HIE, cortical malformation, or tuberous sclerosis, while up to a third are cryptogenic. No single acquired condition accounts for the majority.'),
('eb8ad70b-2182-5a3e-9639-cbe95faad569'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common seizure type in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome?', '["Tonic seizures (most common and characteristic)","Generalized tonic-clonic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myoclonic mediated by specific receptor activation","Atonic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atypical absence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tonic seizures (most common and characteristic)', 'Tonic seizures, often occurring during sleep, are the most frequent and characteristic seizure type in Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. Atypical absence, atonic, and myoclonic seizures also occur but less often.'),
('6110420e-207e-592f-a2b0-63fba3e5f95d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of West syndrome (infantile spasms)?', '["Cortical dysplasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Tuberous sclerosis (TSC) is the most common identifiable cause accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Symptomatic (most common: TSC HIE cortical dysplasia) or cryptogenic (no identifiable cause)","Genetic (CDKL5 ARX STXBP1) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","HIE (hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Symptomatic (most common: TSC HIE cortical dysplasia) or cryptogenic (no identifiable cause)', 'Most infantile spasms are symptomatic, arising from an identifiable disorder such as tuberous sclerosis, HIE, or cortical dysplasia; the remainder are cryptogenic. Tuberous sclerosis is the most common single identifiable cause.'),
('c62342b0-c189-592b-8892-eab8f88c4993'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of neonatal-onset epilepsy?', '["KCNQ2 mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","SCN2A mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","KCNQ2 mutation (benign familial neonatal epilepsy most common genetic)","CDKL5 mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE) most common cause overall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'KCNQ2 mutation (benign familial neonatal epilepsy most common genetic)', 'Among genetic causes of neonatal-onset epilepsy, KCNQ2 mutations encoding a neuronal potassium channel are the most common, including benign familial neonatal epilepsy. HIE is the most common cause of neonatal seizures overall but not of genetic epilepsy.'),
('84d69931-b42f-5f33-991d-31c0e971cc9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of childhood-onset epilepsy in otherwise healthy children?', '["Childhood absence epilepsy","Febrile seizures (not epilepsy)","Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (most common genetic generalized)","Idiopathic (genetic generalized epilepsy) most common cause overall","Focal epilepsy (structural lesion)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (genetic generalized epilepsy) most common cause overall', 'In otherwise healthy children, genetic generalized epilepsies such as childhood absence and juvenile myoclonic epilepsy are the most common cause, with no structural lesion present. Febrile seizures are not epilepsy, and focal structural causes are less frequent.'),
('2f1093c4-0ecc-5a81-811d-6dff64cedade'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of epilepsy in adults?', '["Idiopathic/genetic (earlier onset)","Traumatic brain injury","Brain tumor","Structural epilepsy (due to stroke trauma tumor) most common","Hippocampal sclerosis (mesial temporal sclerosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Structural epilepsy (due to stroke trauma tumor) most common', 'In adults, acquired structural brain damage from stroke, trauma, or tumor is the most common basis of epilepsy. Genetic forms dominate childhood, whereas structural causes increase with age.'),
('7c79ea96-27ff-5326-b8c1-e728a6a1d6a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of temporal lobe epilepsy in adults?', '["Febrile seizures (can cause hippocampal sclerosis later)","Structural lesion (cortical dysplasia tumor)","Trauma (contusion in temporal pole)","Hippocampal sclerosis (mesial temporal sclerosis) most common pathological finding","Infection (viral encephalitis HSV)"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampal sclerosis (mesial temporal sclerosis) most common pathological finding', 'Hippocampal sclerosis, or mesial temporal sclerosis, is the most common pathological substrate of adult temporal lobe epilepsy. Earlier febrile seizures may predispose to it, but the sclerotic hippocampus is the hallmark lesion.'),
('b4a2e7ab-9435-55ea-a255-d49003d11953'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of status epilepticus in adults?', '["Alcohol withdrawal resulting in decreased functional activity","Antiepileptic drug noncompliance (most common cause in known epilepsy patients)","CNS infection (meningitis encephalitis) mediated by specific receptor activation","Remote symptomatic (prior brain injury) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acute symptomatic (stroke metabolic toxic) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antiepileptic drug noncompliance (most common cause in known epilepsy patients)', 'In patients with known epilepsy, missing antiepileptic medication is the most common trigger of status epilepticus. Acute causes such as stroke, infection, or metabolic disturbance account for a smaller share.'),
('6c32d053-51dc-59c2-91ce-e45c5e4f1aad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of refractory status epilepticus?', '["Encephalitis (autoimmune or viral) is a common cause of super-refractory status","Stroke accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Drug reaction mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metabolic (mitochondrial) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Encephalitis (autoimmune or viral) is a common cause of super-refractory status', 'Autoimmune or viral encephalitis is the leading cause of status epilepticus that resists standard antiseizure treatment. Structural lesions and metabolic disorders are less often responsible for super-refractory status.'),
('9702dab0-0f34-57aa-83ae-75ef2f9fd8af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of autoimmune encephalitis in children?', '["MOG antibody-associated encephalitis resulting in decreased functional activity","CASPR2 encephalitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LGI1 encephalitis mediated by specific receptor activation","GAD65 encephalitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (most common autoimmune encephalitis in children) especially in girls with ovarian teratoma"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (most common autoimmune encephalitis in children) especially in girls with ovarian teratoma', 'Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is the most common autoimmune encephalitis in children, particularly girls, and is frequently linked to ovarian teratoma. It typically presents with psychiatric change, seizures, and a movement disorder.'),
('8bc23bf6-ccac-518b-b64d-f1e56870e239'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of chorea in children?', '["Sydenham chorea (from rheumatic fever) in developing countries; in developed countries medications and genetic causes are more common","Huntington disease (juvenile onset) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SLE (lupus anticoagulant) mediated by specific receptor activation","Drug-induced (antipsychotics anticonvulsants) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wilson disease (copper accumulation) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sydenham chorea (from rheumatic fever) in developing countries; in developed countries medications and genetic causes are more common', 'Sydenham chorea after group A streptococcal infection is the leading cause where rheumatic fever is common, while drug-induced and genetic causes predominate in developed countries. The frequency of each cause reflects regional epidemiology.'),
('668c9ee9-9d01-50da-8c9e-079890c3fed4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of myoclonus in children?', '["Metabolic (uremic hepatic encephalopathy)","Epileptic myoclonus (JME progressive myoclonic epilepsy JME Unverricht-Lundborg)","Drug-induced (opiates)","Benign neonatal sleep myoclonus (normal variant) and essential myoclonus","Symptomatic (post-hypoxic myoclonus Lance-Adams)"]'::jsonb, 'Benign neonatal sleep myoclonus (normal variant) and essential myoclonus', 'Benign neonatal sleep myoclonus, a normal variant confined to sleep, and essential myoclonus without other deficits are the most common causes in children. Epileptic and post-hypoxic myoclonus are far less frequent.'),
('8a467831-524d-55f2-8051-e020c915a02b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of progressive myoclonic epilepsy?', '["Lafora disease (EPM2A NHLRC1) resulting in decreased functional activity","Unverricht-Lundborg disease (EPM1 CSTB mutation) most common in Finland","MERRF (m.8344A>G) mediated by specific receptor activation","Sialidosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis (NCL) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unverricht-Lundborg disease (EPM1 CSTB mutation) most common in Finland', 'Unverricht-Lundborg disease (EPM1), caused by a CSTB mutation, is the most common progressive myoclonic epilepsy, especially in Finland. Lafora disease and MERRF are recognized but less common causes.'),
('2dddda1f-5925-5627-bd80-e85e7eed9b5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis (NCL) in children?', '["CLN2 mutation (late infantile Jansky-Bielschowsky)","CLN3 mutation (juvenile NCL Batten disease) most common","CLN5/CLN6/CLN8 mutations (Finnish variant)","CLN1 mutation (infantile Santavuori-Haltia)","CLN7 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'CLN3 mutation (juvenile NCL Batten disease) most common', 'Juvenile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis (Batten disease) from CLN3 mutations is the most common form in children. CLN1 and CLN2 cause earlier infantile disease but occur less frequently.'),
('242d4b87-b5a2-56a3-ba4a-3632056fd2ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of ataxia in children with a brain tumor?', '["Posterior fossa tumor (medulloblastoma astrocytoma) compressing the cerebellum","Cerebral hemisphere tumor with herniation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pineal region tumor (germinoma) mediated by specific receptor activation","Suprasellar tumor (craniopharyngioma) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior fossa tumor (medulloblastoma astrocytoma) compressing the cerebellum', 'Most pediatric brain tumors are infratentorial, and a posterior fossa mass compressing the cerebellum directly produces ataxia. Suprasellar and pineal tumors cause ataxia only indirectly through raised intracranial pressure.'),
('5f157af7-4090-54cb-9462-1a7e04331234'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of posterior fossa syndrome after medulloblastoma resection?', '["Aseptic meningitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased ICP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar mutism (pseudobulbar palsy) from surgical injury to the dentate-thalamo-cortical pathway","Tumor recurrence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pseudomeningocele leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar mutism (pseudobulbar palsy) from surgical injury to the dentate-thalamo-cortical pathway', 'Posterior fossa syndrome, dominated by transient mutism, follows surgical injury to the dentate-thalamo-cortical pathway during medulloblastoma resection. Meningitis, raised ICP, and recurrence are not the typical mechanism.'),
('259e6558-d277-51ec-8916-2763edbe92d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of cancer-related stroke in children?', '["Radiation arteriopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypercoagulable state (chemotherapy thrombosis) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection (varicella zoster) mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardioembolic (CHD with tumor) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct tumor invasion (vascular compression by tumor or tumor embolus)"]'::jsonb, 'Direct tumor invasion (vascular compression by tumor or tumor embolus)', 'Tumor mass compressing or invading cerebral vessels, or shedding tumor emboli, is the most common cause of stroke in children with cancer. Radiation arteriopathy and hypercoagulability are less common contributors.'),
('b13cdc07-dca4-5490-aea4-6758cca9ce6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of central diabetes insipidus in children with brain tumor?', '["Craniopharyngioma (suprasellar) most common tumor causing DI","Langerhans cell histiocytosis (LCH) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary adenoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Germinoma (suprasellar) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic glioma (NF1) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Craniopharyngioma (suprasellar) most common tumor causing DI', 'A suprasellar craniopharyngioma compressing the hypothalamic-neurohypophyseal system is the most common tumor causing central diabetes insipidus in children. Germinoma and histiocytosis are less frequent causes.'),
('e1006de3-a2ca-5e1e-9d7b-3d16f9b369f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hypopituitarism in children with brain tumor?', '["Germinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Pituitary adenoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radiation therapy (more than the tumor itself) mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic glioma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Craniopharyngioma (most common cause of acquired hypopituitarism in children)"]'::jsonb, 'Craniopharyngioma (most common cause of acquired hypopituitarism in children)', 'Craniopharyngioma is the most common cause of acquired hypopituitarism in children by compressing the pituitary stalk and hypothalamus. Radiation contributes later, but the tumor itself is the leading cause.'),
('21fe7034-0380-5cea-9e07-d643ca7964ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of growth hormone deficiency in children?', '["Radiation therapy (for brain tumor ALL)","Optic glioma (NF1)","Craniopharyngioma (organic cause)","Traumatic brain injury","Idiopathic (isolated GH deficiency most common)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (isolated GH deficiency most common)', 'Most childhood growth hormone deficiency is isolated and idiopathic, with no demonstrable structural cause. Organic lesions such as craniopharyngioma and prior radiation account for a minority of cases.'),
('13faf9da-a942-51d6-8fc3-6cc585b11c90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of precocious puberty in girls?', '["Ovarian tumor","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Idiopathic central precocious puberty (most common in girls >5 years)","Hypothalamic hamartoma (most common organic cause in girls <4 years)","McCune-Albright syndrome (peripheral precocity)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic central precocious puberty (most common in girls >5 years)', 'In girls older than about five years, central precocious puberty is usually idiopathic. Hypothalamic hamartoma is the most common organic cause, especially in younger girls, but is less frequent overall.'),
('b9358ced-74e9-523b-8890-15b56efd021f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of precocious puberty in boys?', '["Central precocious puberty with identifiable pathology (hypothalamic hamartoma most common)","Idiopathic (less common than in girls) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head trauma (post-traumatic) mediated by specific receptor activation","Hydrocephalus (shunted) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothalamic hamartoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central precocious puberty with identifiable pathology (hypothalamic hamartoma most common)', 'In boys, central precocious puberty is more often linked to a demonstrable lesion, and hypothalamic hamartoma is the most common such finding. Idiopathic cases are proportionally less common than in girls.'),
('dff23e8a-323e-5fa0-95e0-15f65818e199'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of delayed puberty in boys?', '["Constitutional delay of growth and puberty (CDGP) most common cause","Chronic illness (Crohn CF)","Functional (malnutrition overtraining)","Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism (Klinefelter)","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (Kallmann syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 'Constitutional delay of growth and puberty (CDGP) most common cause', 'Constitutional delay of growth and puberty, a benign variation in the tempo of maturation, is the most common cause of late puberty in boys. Organic hypogonadism and chronic illness are much less common.'),
('429b2f86-4968-5174-abca-2dbb625d7ef8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of delayed puberty in girls?', '["Constitutional delay of growth and puberty (most common cause)","Chronic illness","Underweight / malnutrition","Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism (Turner syndrome)","Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea (eating disorders)"]'::jsonb, 'Constitutional delay of growth and puberty (most common cause)', 'Constitutional delay of growth and puberty is the most frequent reason for late pubertal onset in girls. Turner syndrome and chronic illness should be excluded but are far less common.'),
('9b93ce8b-fdf9-55ba-baf5-3f0b3636af48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of gynecomastia in adolescent boys?', '["Testicular tumor (Leydig Sertoli)","Klinefelter syndrome (47 XXY)","Physiologic (pubertal gynecomastia) most common (>50% of boys affected)","Liver disease (cirrhosis)","Drug-induced (anabolic steroids marijuana spironolactone)"]'::jsonb, 'Physiologic (pubertal gynecomastia) most common (>50% of boys affected)', 'Transient pubertal gynecomastia from a temporary imbalance of estradiol and testosterone affects over half of adolescent boys and is the most common cause. Pathologic, syndromic, and drug-induced cases are much less frequent.'),
('2fae0489-cb31-56ff-a36d-134c2a0b8299'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of short stature in children?', '["Constitutional growth delay (CDGP) and familial short stature are the most common","Growth hormone deficiency (GHD) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skeletal dysplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR SGA) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Constitutional growth delay (CDGP) and familial short stature are the most common', 'Constitutional growth delay and familial short stature, both normal growth variants, account for the majority of short children. Endocrine, syndromic, and intrauterine causes are uncommon.'),
('95b5ba82-6086-5672-9bcd-49c24c871b32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common endocrine cause of short stature?', '["Growth hormone deficiency (most common endocrine cause)","Hypothyroidism","Precocious puberty (advanced bone age limiting final height)","Cushing syndrome","GHD (GH deficiency)"]'::jsonb, 'Growth hormone deficiency (most common endocrine cause)', 'Growth hormone deficiency is the most common endocrine cause of short stature, although it is rare relative to normal variants of growth. Hypothyroidism and Cushing syndrome are less frequent endocrine causes.'),
('b81ba2d3-0dc1-593a-8e5a-06aafe4fd0c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in children?', '["Congenital hypothyroidism (most common cause of neonatal hypothyroidism)","Iodine deficiency (most common cause worldwide)","Thyroid agenesis (congenital)","Post-surgical (thyroidectomy)","Autoimmune (Hashimoto thyroiditis) most common cause of acquired hypothyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune (Hashimoto thyroiditis) most common cause of acquired hypothyroidism', 'Autoimmune thyroiditis (Hashimoto disease) is the most common cause of acquired hypothyroidism in children. Congenital hypothyroidism and iodine deficiency explain neonatal and worldwide cases, respectively.'),
('02e2e0ee-45a1-5d73-8f09-030bcfae0811'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of congenital hypothyroidism?', '["Thyroid hormone synthesis defect (dyshormonogenesis 10-15%)","Thyroid dysgenesis (aplasia hypoplasia ectopia) most common (85% of cases)","TSH receptor mutation","Maternal antibodies (TSH receptor blocking)","Iodine deficiency (endemic)"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid dysgenesis (aplasia hypoplasia ectopia) most common (85% of cases)', 'Thyroid dysgenesis, including aplasia, hypoplasia, or ectopic thyroid tissue, accounts for about 85% of congenital hypothyroidism. Dyshormonogenesis and blocking maternal antibodies are much less common causes.'),
('3e19edf2-5a20-51fc-a54c-7ba5c672217d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism in children?', '["Toxic adenoma (hot nodule) resulting in decreased functional activity","Graves disease (autoimmune diffuse toxic goiter) accounts for >95% of pediatric hyperthyroidism","Neonatal Graves (maternal antibodies) mediated by specific receptor activation","Subacute thyroiditis (viral de Quervain) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hashitoxicosis (symptomatic exacerbation of Hashimoto) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Graves disease (autoimmune diffuse toxic goiter) accounts for >95% of pediatric hyperthyroidism', 'Graves disease, autoimmune stimulation of the TSH receptor, causes over 95% of pediatric hyperthyroidism. Toxic nodules and thyroiditis are rare causes in children.'),
('320058a6-1c1d-5e5a-b902-3e3d2fdc49f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hypercalcemia in children?', '["Vitamin D intoxication (excessive supplementation) resulting in decreased functional activity","Idiopathic infantile hypercalcemia (CYP24A1 mutation) or immobilization (in children with CP)","Hyperparathyroidism (primary or secondary) mediated by specific receptor activation","Malignancy (leukemia bone metastases) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granulomatous disease (sarcoid) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic infantile hypercalcemia (CYP24A1 mutation) or immobilization (in children with CP)', 'Impaired vitamin D catabolism from CYP24A1 mutation and immobilization-related bone resorption are common causes of hypercalcemia in children, especially those with CP. Malignancy and hyperparathyroidism are much less frequent.'),
('40eb172c-481e-515e-839a-39bee0780665'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hypocalcemia in children?', '["Acute pancreatitis with hypocalcemia","Vitamin D deficiency (nutritional rickets) most common cause","Neonatal hypocalcemia (early from maternal hyperparathyroidism or prematurity)","Hypoparathyroidism (22q11.2 deletion DiGeorge most common genetic)","Pseudohypoparathyroidism (Albright hereditary osteodystrophy)"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin D deficiency (nutritional rickets) most common cause', 'Nutritional vitamin D deficiency is the most common cause of hypocalcemia in children because vitamin D drives intestinal calcium absorption. Hypoparathyroidism and pseudohypoparathyroidism are rarer causes.'),
('1facf87b-c602-5bf7-ae2d-ca1e96b501d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of rickets in children?', '["Nutritional vitamin D deficiency (lack of sunlight and dietary deficiency) most common","Renal disease (renal rickets from chronic kidney disease)","X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets (PHEX mutation most common genetic form)","Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1 (1α-hydroxylase deficiency)","Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 2 (VDR mutation)"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional vitamin D deficiency (lack of sunlight and dietary deficiency) most common', 'Inadequate sun exposure and low dietary vitamin D are the most common cause of rickets worldwide. Renal rickets and hereditary forms such as hypophosphatemic rickets are much less common.'),
('f17e28a0-dd02-5c04-b38a-d1159a0638b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hypophosphatemic rickets?', '["Fanconi syndrome (cystinosis)","FGF23-producing tumor (oncogenic osteomalacia)","Autosomal dominant hypophosphatemic rickets (FGF23 mutation)","Autosomal recessive (DMP1 ENPP1)","X-linked dominant (PHEX mutation) most common form of hereditary rickets"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked dominant (PHEX mutation) most common form of hereditary rickets', 'X-linked dominant hypophosphatemic rickets due to PHEX mutation is the most common inherited form, driven by excess FGF23 causing renal phosphate wasting. Oncogenic osteomalacia and other genetic forms are rare.'),
('bfd6d42f-708b-52e4-9e64-3ce6bc98971f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of meningitis in immunocompromised children?', '["Cytomegalovirus (CMV) resulting in decreased functional activity","Pneumococcus (S. pneumoniae) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cryptococcus neoformans (most common fungal meningitis in HIV) mediated by specific receptor activation","M. tuberculosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Listeria monocytogenes (most common in cell-mediated immunodeficiency)"]'::jsonb, 'Listeria monocytogenes (most common in cell-mediated immunodeficiency)', 'Listeria monocytogenes exploits defective cell-mediated immunity and is the leading cause of meningitis in immunocompromised children. Cryptococcus predominates in HIV specifically, and pneumococcus in immunocompetent children.'),
('b1a334fb-cb8a-5e30-b9ac-92f7857a5e43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of brain abscess in immunocompromised children?', '["Bacterial (streptococci staph) resulting in decreased functional activity","Fungal (Aspergillus Candida) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nocardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Tuberculosis (TB) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Toxoplasma gondii (most common cause of CNS mass in HIV/AIDS patients)"]'::jsonb, 'Toxoplasma gondii (most common cause of CNS mass in HIV/AIDS patients)', 'Toxoplasma gondii is the most common cause of an intracranial mass lesion in immunocompromised patients, especially those with advanced HIV. Bacterial abscesses predominate in immunocompetent children.'),
('9ab572db-7a09-5a19-932a-2ae2504cba76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML)?', '["JC polyomavirus (reactivation in severe immunodeficiency-HIV or immunosuppressive therapy)","EBV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HSV mediated by specific receptor activation","CMV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","HHV-6 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'JC polyomavirus (reactivation in severe immunodeficiency-HIV or immunosuppressive therapy)', 'PML results from reactivation of JC polyomavirus during severe cellular immunodeficiency, typically advanced HIV or immunosuppressive therapy, with lytic infection of oligodendrocytes causing multifocal demyelination.'),
('e0bffb97-a168-51d2-a412-fde5db881aed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of myelopathy in HIV/AIDS?', '["HIV vacuolar myelopathy (most common cause of spinal cord disease in AIDS)","Nutritional deficiency (B12) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary CNS lymphoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Progressive myelopathy from HIV itself without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Opportunistic infections (CMV toxoplasmosis TB) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HIV vacuolar myelopathy (most common cause of spinal cord disease in AIDS)', 'HIV vacuolar myelopathy is the most common cause of spinal cord dysfunction in AIDS, producing progressive spastic paraparesis with sensory ataxia. Opportunistic infections and lymphoma are less common.'),
('5a0a48e4-9b56-5b34-801c-d76a691cf970'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of psychiatric symptoms in children with organic brain disease?', '["CNS lupus (SLE cerebritis) resulting in decreased functional activity","Wilson disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Limbic encephalitis (paraneoplastic) mediated by specific receptor activation","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (most common autoimmune cause) and limbic encephalitis","Brain tumor (frontal lobe glioma craniopharyngioma) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (most common autoimmune cause) and limbic encephalitis', 'Autoimmune limbic inflammation, especially anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis, is the most common organic cause of new psychiatric symptoms in children, often preceding seizures and movement disorder. Structural tumors are a less frequent explanation.'),
('531bfda0-5f8a-5dec-8ef3-8cd32fe595b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of catatonia in children?', '["Medication-induced (neuroleptic malignant syndrome)","Underlying psychiatric disorder (mood disorder psychosis) most common","Traumatic brain injury","Autism spectrum disorder","Organic brain disease (NMDAR encephalitis)"]'::jsonb, 'Underlying psychiatric disorder (mood disorder psychosis) most common', 'Catatonia in children most often accompanies an underlying psychiatric disorder such as a mood disorder or psychosis. Organic causes including anti-NMDA encephalitis and neuroleptic malignant syndrome should be considered but are less common.'),
('9dc65d73-c213-50d7-a6b9-ac233988fd56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) in children?', '["Withdrawal of anticholinergic medications resulting in decreased functional activity","Use of antipsychotic medications (especially high-potency first-generation like haloperidol)","Metoclopramide use mediated by specific receptor activation","Serotonin syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malignant hyperthermia (anesthetics) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Use of antipsychotic medications (especially high-potency first-generation like haloperidol)', 'NMS results from acute blockade of central dopamine receptors, so antipsychotics, particularly high-potency first-generation agents such as haloperidol, are the leading cause. It presents with fever, rigidity, autonomic instability, and elevated creatine kinase. Serotonin syndrome and malignant hyperthermia are separate clinical entities with different triggers.'),
('836d55eb-cf82-5b39-89ef-35e43f856635'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of serotonin syndrome in children?', '["Monoamine oxidase inhibitor (MAOI) interaction resulting in decreased functional activity","Use of selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) or serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs) alone or overdose","Amitriptyline overdose mediated by specific receptor activation","Tramadol/Lithium combination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MDMA (Ecstasy) abuse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Use of selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) or serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs) alone or overdose', 'Serotonin syndrome is caused by excess serotonergic activity, and SSRIs or SNRIs taken alone or in overdose are the most common cause in children. Features include agitation, hyperreflexia, myoclonus, and clonus. MAOI interactions and other drug combinations are important but less frequent than single-agent SSRI/SNRI exposure.'),
('ef0703f0-45fa-559d-9c34-f7ef21bcf04e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of dystonic reaction to medication in children?', '["Anticonvulsants (phenytoin carbamazepine) resulting in decreased functional activity","Antiemetics (metoclopramide prochlorperazine) most commonly cause acute dystonia","Antihistamines (diphenhydramine) mediated by specific receptor activation","Antidepressants (SSRIs) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antipsychotics (haloperidol risperidone) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antiemetics (metoclopramide prochlorperazine) most commonly cause acute dystonia', 'Acute dystonic reactions result from sudden dopamine blockade, and antiemetics such as metoclopramide and prochlorperazine are the most common trigger in children. Antipsychotics can also cause dystonia but are less frequent culprits in this age group. Anticonvulsants, antihistamines, and SSRIs are not typical causes.'),
('e5980372-6d05-58ea-948e-ba58b58bd72d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of oculogyric crisis?', '["Drug-induced (metoclopramide antipsychotics) causing acute dystonia with upward gaze deviation","Wilson disease (lenticular degeneration) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Encephalitis (post-infectious) mediated by specific receptor activation","NMDAR encephalitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tardive dyskinesia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drug-induced (metoclopramide antipsychotics) causing acute dystonia with upward gaze deviation', 'Oculogyric crisis is an acute dystonia with forced conjugate upward deviation of the eyes, typically precipitated by dopamine-blocking drugs such as metoclopramide and antipsychotics. It is a drug reaction rather than a degenerative, infectious, or paraneoplastic process. Tardive dyskinesia is a later-onset, different movement disorder.'),
('ef867cd6-18cc-5476-8651-0d5a6f2f4626'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic meningitis in children?', '["Partially treated bacterial resulting in decreased functional activity","Tuberculous meningitis (most common cause of chronic meningitis in children in endemic areas)","Sarcoidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Fungal (cryptococcal histoplasma) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carcinomatous (leukemia lymphoma) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberculous meningitis (most common cause of chronic meningitis in children in endemic areas)', 'In endemic regions, tuberculous meningitis is the leading cause of chronic meningitis in children, with subacute onset, CSF lymphocytic pleocytosis, low glucose, and high protein. Fungal, sarcoid, and carcinomatous meningitis are less common pediatric causes. Partially treated bacterial meningitis usually runs a more acute course.'),
('fcc977f7-c4a4-570f-b619-1b93c9d30495'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of stroke in children with tuberculous meningitis?', '["Seizures resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal gangliar infarcts from vasculitis of the lenticulostriate arteries","TB brain abscess mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebral edema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hydrocephalus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Basal gangliar infarcts from vasculitis of the lenticulostriate arteries', 'In tuberculous meningitis, inflammatory vasculitis affecting the lenticulostriate arteries at the base of the brain produces ischemic infarcts, most often in the basal ganglia, and is the chief cause of stroke. Abscess, edema, and hydrocephalus are other complications but do not directly produce infarction. Seizures are a symptom, not a cause, of stroke.'),
('0bfe3f17-2a09-5eef-a9fa-af0cf18cf3d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of death in tuberculous meningitis?', '["Respiratory failure resulting in decreased functional activity","Tuberculoma with herniation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Seizure (status epilepticus) mediated by specific receptor activation","Progressive hydrocephalus and brainstem compression (or stroke)","Disseminated miliary TB leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive hydrocephalus and brainstem compression (or stroke)', 'Progressive hydrocephalus with rising intracranial pressure and brainstem compression, along with basal-ganglia stroke from vasculitis, are the principal causes of death in tuberculous meningitis. Respiratory failure and seizures are possible sequelae but less commonly the direct fatal event. Disseminated miliary TB is a separate form of disease.'),
('b5ca0f5c-5545-5601-95a2-90b2d2806723'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of spinal tuberculosis (Pott disease)?', '["Direct extension from adjacent lung resulting in decreased functional activity","Adjacent lymph node involvement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphatic spread mediated by specific receptor activation","Hematogenous spread from primary pulmonary TB or GI TB to vertebral bodies","Transdural spread from meningeal TB leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hematogenous spread from primary pulmonary TB or GI TB to vertebral bodies', 'Pott disease arises when tubercle bacilli seed the vertebral bodies hematogenously from a primary pulmonary or gastrointestinal focus, classically involving the anterior aspects of contiguous vertebrae. Lymphatic and direct contiguous spread are not the dominant routes. The result is vertebral destruction with kyphosis and possible cord compression.'),
('626da173-d1b7-56ed-8c34-e0ff4151b8c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of an intracranial calcification on head CT in children?', '["Craniopharyngioma (suprasellar)","Tuberous sclerosis (subependymal nodules)","Toxoplasmosis (scattered)","Congenital CMV infection (periventricular calcifications)","Sturge-Weber (gyriform)"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital CMV infection (periventricular calcifications)', 'Congenital cytomegalovirus infection is the most common cause of intracranial calcifications on pediatric head CT, producing characteristic periventricular calcifications with microcephaly. Toxoplasmosis, tuberous sclerosis, and Sturge-Weber produce different calcification patterns. Craniopharyngioma calcification is typically suprasellar and less common overall.'),
('a4c938fc-16ca-52c4-9ec3-1447a04e18e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of a ring-enhancing brain lesion in children?', '["Neurocysticercosis (tapeworm)","Tuberculoma (TB)","Cerebritis stage (before abscess formation)","Primary CNS lymphoma (in immunocompromised)","Brain abscess (bacterial or fungal)"]'::jsonb, 'Brain abscess (bacterial or fungal)', 'A brain abscess is the most common ring-enhancing intracranial lesion in children, showing an enhancing rim around a necrotic core. Neurocysticercosis and tuberculoma also enhance but are less frequent causes in this group. The cerebritis stage precedes capsule formation and does not yet show a mature ring.'),
('b8fd5a1d-6838-5dbe-a9fe-2edbcfec21ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of neurocysticercosis?', '["Taenia solium (pork tapeworm) larval infection of the brain","Echinococcus granulosus (hydatid cyst)","Toxocara canis (visceral larva migrans)","Taenia saginata (beef tapeworm)","Schistosoma japonicum"]'::jsonb, 'Taenia solium (pork tapeworm) larval infection of the brain', 'Neurocysticercosis is caused by the larval (cysticercus) stage of Taenia solium, the pork tapeworm, when eggs are ingested and larvae encyst in the brain. Taenia saginata does not cause human cysticercosis. Echinococcus, Toxocara, and Schistosoma produce different disease patterns.'),
('883c8976-94d7-5aa3-892c-674af093b246'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of a spinal epidural abscess?', '["Hematogenous spread from a distant infection (skin UTI) most common","Traumatic (penetrating injury)","Vertebral osteomyelitis (contiguous spread)","Post-surgical infection (especially laminectomy)","IV drug use (skin flora)"]'::jsonb, 'Hematogenous spread from a distant infection (skin UTI) most common', 'A spinal epidural abscess most often results from hematogenous seeding by organisms from a distant focus such as a skin or urinary infection, with Staphylococcus aureus the common pathogen. Contiguous osteomyelitis and post-surgical infection are secondary routes. Traumatic inoculation and IV drug use are less common overall.'),
('87f5a429-7918-5a6d-85d5-b927103648a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of craniopharyngioma?', '["Germ cell tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Pituitary cell hyperplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Embryologic remnant of Rathke''s pouch (squamous epithelial cells of the craniopharyngeal duct)","Congenital duplication of the pituitary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metastasis from elsewhere leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Embryologic remnant of Rathke''s pouch (squamous epithelial cells of the craniopharyngeal duct)', 'Craniopharyngioma arises from squamous epithelial remnants of Rathke''s pouch, the embryologic diverticulum of the craniopharyngeal duct that forms the anterior pituitary. It is a histologically benign but locally aggressive suprasellar tumor. Germ cell tumors, pituitary hyperplasia, and metastases are distinct pathologies.'),
('ceda301e-03e5-5455-88de-3ca880fe1930'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common type of craniopharyngioma in children?', '["Adamantinomatous type (most common in children)","Mixed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Squamous papillary type mediated by specific receptor activation","Papillary type (more common in adults) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcified leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adamantinomatous type (most common in children)', 'The adamantinomatous subtype is the most common craniopharyngioma in children and is often calcified and cystic. The papillary subtype predominates in adults. ''Mixed'' and ''calcified'' describe features rather than the typical childhood histologic type.'),
('8ce7f5ab-67d8-52f4-be69-93bab86cae3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of panhypopituitarism in children with craniopharyngioma?', '["Cystic expansion resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor invasion of the stalk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Compression of the pituitary gland and hypothalamus by the tumor and/or surgical resection","Radiation therapy (post-surgical) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammatory response (arachnoiditis) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Compression of the pituitary gland and hypothalamus by the tumor and/or surgical resection', 'Panhypopituitarism in craniopharyngioma follows destruction of the anterior pituitary and hypothalamus by direct tumor compression or by the surgery needed to resect it. The suprasellar mass injures the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, producing multiple anterior pituitary hormone deficiencies. Stalk invasion, radiation, and inflammation play lesser roles.'),
('077dd3b5-f805-55c1-a9f3-c228854a7e8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of optic nerve glioma in children?', '["Syndromic (Noonan) resulting in decreased functional activity","Traumatic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) associated in about 30-50% of cases but can be sporadic","Radiation-induced without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammatory (sarcoid) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) associated in about 30-50% of cases but can be sporadic', 'Optic nerve gliomas, usually pilocytic astrocytomas, are associated with NF1 in roughly 30-50% of cases and the rest occur sporadically. NF1-associated tumors tend to be more indolent. Trauma, radiation, and sarcoid are not recognized causes of pediatric optic nerve glioma.'),
('f9d8e94a-50ab-51fd-b0e5-5ef00655e32d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of visual loss in NF1?', '["Orbital neurofibroma resulting in decreased functional activity","Sphenoid wing dysplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glaucoma (congenital) mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic pathway glioma (pilocytic astrocytoma of the optic nerve/chiasm) most common cause of visual decline","Lisch nodules (iris hamartomas) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic pathway glioma (pilocytic astrocytoma of the optic nerve/chiasm) most common cause of visual decline', 'In NF1, visual loss is most often caused by an optic pathway glioma, a pilocytic astrocytoma of the optic nerve or chiasm that compresses the visual pathways. Lisch nodules are asymptomatic iris hamartomas, and sphenoid wing dysplasia does not impair vision directly. Orbital neurofibromas and glaucoma are less common causes.'),
('4daca4fe-62a3-5ae2-a886-387e9922d0a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of brainstem glioma in children?', '["Ependymoma (floor of the fourth ventricle) resulting in decreased functional activity","Pilocytic astrocytoma (exophytic) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ganglioglioma mediated by specific receptor activation","Focal tectal glioma (benign) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma (DIPG H3 K27M-mutant) most common brainstem tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma (DIPG H3 K27M-mutant) most common brainstem tumor', 'Diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma (DIPG), characterized by the H3 K27M mutation, is the most common brainstem tumor in children and has a poor prognosis. It diffusely infiltrates the pons, unlike focal or exophytic tumors such as tectal gliomas and pilocytic astrocytomas. Ependymomas and gangliogliomas are less common brainstem lesions.'),
('e1a38093-9e89-57e0-9ce2-b3b7f67d3609'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of death in DIPG?', '["Hydrocephalus (aqueductal compression) resulting in decreased functional activity","Progressive tumor growth with brainstem destruction and respiratory failure","Metastatic spread (rare in DIPG) mediated by specific receptor activation","Seizure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Treatment complications (radiation necrosis) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive tumor growth with brainstem destruction and respiratory failure', 'DIPG causes death through relentless local growth that destroys vital pontine and medullary centers, ultimately leading to respiratory failure. Hydrocephalus and metastatic spread are uncommon because the tumor remains largely intrinsic to the brainstem. Radiation necrosis and seizures are not the leading fatal events.'),
('fd5b5129-b40a-58ba-b8b2-f5585afc7d9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of hydrocephalus in children with brain tumors?', '["Decreased absorption (tumor cells in subarachnoid space) resulting in decreased functional activity","Overproduction of CSF (choroid plexus papilloma) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Venous sinus compression mediated by specific receptor activation","Obstruction of CSF pathways by the tumor (posterior fossa tumors block the fourth ventricle most commonly)","Communicating hydrocephalus (meningitis seeding) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstruction of CSF pathways by the tumor (posterior fossa tumors block the fourth ventricle most commonly)', 'Tumor-induced hydrocephalus in children is usually obstructive, with posterior fossa masses blocking CSF flow through the fourth ventricle and causing non-communicating hydrocephalus. Choroid plexus papillomas cause hydrocephalus by CSF overproduction but are much rarer. Impaired absorption and venous compression are minor mechanisms.'),
('21e4a08f-60fe-55f4-9979-51919989b435'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of diabetes insipidus in children with suprasellar tumors?', '["Optic glioma (NF1) resulting in decreased functional activity","Germinoma (CNS germ cell tumor) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypothalamic hamartoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Craniopharyngioma and germinoma are the most common tumors causing DI","Craniopharyngioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Craniopharyngioma and germinoma are the most common tumors causing DI', 'Craniopharyngioma and germinoma are the two suprasellar tumors that most commonly damage the hypothalamic-neurohypophyseal tract and produce central diabetes insipidus. Optic gliomas and hypothalamic hamartomas less frequently disrupt ADH pathways. The combined statement is the best choice because both tumors are the leading causes.'),
('4675f445-723f-5236-b97b-a6123e5cf4b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of germinoma in the CNS?', '["Pineoblastoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Primary intracranial germ cell tumor (most common CNS germ cell tumor) in the pineal region or suprasellar region","Metastatic testicular germ cell tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Teratoma (mature or immature) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primitive neuroectodermal tumor (PNET) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary intracranial germ cell tumor (most common CNS germ cell tumor) in the pineal region or suprasellar region', 'A germinoma is a primary intracranial germ cell tumor, the most common CNS germ cell tumor, arising typically in the pineal or suprasellar region. It is not a metastasis from a testicular primary and differs from teratomas and PNETs. Pineoblastoma is a distinct pineal-region tumor of primitive neuroectodermal origin.'),
('2b087554-7ec0-52ef-ad9f-7ba20b641bf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of pineal region tumors?', '["Germinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Pineocytoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pineoblastoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Astrocytoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Germinoma (most common pineal tumor) followed by pineoblastoma and pineocytoma"]'::jsonb, 'Germinoma (most common pineal tumor) followed by pineoblastoma and pineocytoma', 'Germinoma is the single most common pineal region tumor, followed in frequency by pineoblastoma and pineocytoma. The combined statement is therefore the best answer. Germinoma, pineocytoma, and pineoblastoma listed alone each name only one histologic type.'),
('9be4a2cf-e29c-524d-8bcb-eee6a6225ed9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of Parinaud syndrome (dorsal midbrain syndrome)?', '["Hydrocephalus (aqueductal stenosis) resulting in decreased functional activity","Demyelinating (MS) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aneurysm of the posterior cerebral artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Stroke in the midbrain (top of the basilar syndrome) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pineal region tumor (germinoma pineoblastoma) compressing the superior colliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Pineal region tumor (germinoma pineoblastoma) compressing the superior colliculus', 'Parinaud syndrome (upward gaze palsy, light-near dissociation, convergence-retraction nystagmus) results from compression of the superior colliculus, most commonly by a pineal region tumor such as germinoma or pineoblastoma in children. Midbrain stroke is the usual cause in adults but is far less common in children. Hydrocephalus and demyelination are atypical mechanisms.'),
('2875a0f2-126b-5cff-8da9-4a4a44bad58c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of spinal cord compression from a tumor?', '["Paraneoplastic resulting in decreased functional activity","Extramedullary (meningioma schwannoma) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intramedullary (astrocytoma ependymoma) mediated by specific receptor activation","Metastatic tumor (most common overall cause of epidural spinal cord compression)","Primary bone tumor (osteosarcoma chordoma) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metastatic tumor (most common overall cause of epidural spinal cord compression)', 'Metastatic epidural disease is the most common overall cause of tumor-related spinal cord compression, typically from lung, breast, or prostate cancer. Extramedullary and intramedullary primary tumors are much less frequent. Paraneoplastic disease does not produce mechanical cord compression.'),
('96819336-58a7-597c-a6b8-deb328fcd8f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of spinal cord compression in children?', '["Astrocytoma (intramedullary) resulting in decreased functional activity","Pott disease (TB spine) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neuroblastoma (most common malignant cause in children) mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary spinal tumor (most common cause in children is neuroblastoma or Wilms)","Ewing sarcoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary spinal tumor (most common cause in children is neuroblastoma or Wilms)', 'In children, spinal cord compression is most commonly caused by a primary spinal tumor, with neuroblastoma and Wilms tumor (via extradural extension) being the leading malignant examples. The combined statement covers this best. Pott disease and Ewing sarcoma are less frequent causes, and astrocytoma is an intramedullary rather than compressive tumor.'),
('3f04f14a-268c-5e3d-ac10-aff612eea52b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of cauda equina syndrome?', '["Spinal stenosis (congenital)","Epidural abscess/hematoma","Massive midline lumbar disc herniation (most common cause)","Spinal tumor (ependymoma meningioma)","Trauma (fracture dislocation)"]'::jsonb, 'Massive midline lumbar disc herniation (most common cause)', 'Cauda equina syndrome is most often produced by a massive midline lumbar disc herniation that compresses the cauda equina nerve roots, causing saddle anesthesia, bladder and bowel dysfunction, and leg weakness. Epidural abscess/hematoma, tumors, and trauma are important but less common causes. Congenital spinal stenosis is an uncommon trigger.'),
('38c795a3-200a-5883-8758-9e7a8dc82f98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of spinal cord infarction?', '["Coagulopathy (antiphospholipid)","Fibrocartilaginous embolism (from disc material)","Hypotension (watershed infarction)","Aortic surgery or aortic dissection (most common cause)","Vasculitis (polyarteritis nodosa)"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic surgery or aortic dissection (most common cause)', 'Spinal cord infarction most commonly follows aortic surgery or aortic dissection, which interrupts blood flow to the artery of Adamkiewicz and the anterior spinal artery. Hypotension and embolic causes are less common. Coagulopathy, fibrocartilaginous embolism, and vasculitis are rare etiologies.'),
('15b1ff37-3788-5a89-8940-f5826064016a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of anterior spinal artery syndrome?', '["Thrombosis (atherosclerotic plaque) resulting in decreased functional activity","Embolism (paradoxical embolus) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypotension (watershed) mediated by specific receptor activation","Aortic occlusion/dissection or embolism to the ASA causing bilateral loss of motor and pain/temperature with preserved dorsal columns","Aortic cross-clamping (during surgery) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic occlusion/dissection or embolism to the ASA causing bilateral loss of motor and pain/temperature with preserved dorsal columns', 'Anterior spinal artery syndrome is most commonly caused by aortic occlusion, dissection, or embolism that interrupts flow in the anterior spinal artery, producing bilateral weakness and loss of pain/temperature with sparing of the dorsal columns (proprioception and vibration). Hypotension and thrombosis are less frequent mechanisms, and aortic cross-clamping is a specific intraoperative variant.'),
('bdd1904a-3484-52e0-80d8-c48a67123cdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of spinal subdural hematoma?', '["Trauma (spinal fracture) resulting in decreased functional activity","Coagulopathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iatrogenic (post-lumbar puncture or spinal anesthesia) most common cause in anticoagulated patients","Tumor hemorrhage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AVM rupture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iatrogenic (post-lumbar puncture or spinal anesthesia) most common cause in anticoagulated patients', 'Spinal subdural hematoma is most often iatrogenic, following lumbar puncture or spinal anesthesia, particularly in patients who are anticoagulated or thrombocytopenic. Trauma, coagulopathy alone, tumor hemorrhage, and AVM rupture are less common causes. The dural location and iatrogenic context are the key clinical points.'),
('1a3ed631-cc8f-5f77-8656-3a968c309bac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of spinal epidural hematoma?', '["Trauma (most common cause overall) and iatrogenic (post-surgical epidural anesthesia)","Tumor (hemorrhagic metastasis) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coagulopathy (hemophilia liver disease) mediated by specific receptor activation","Spontaneous (anticoagulation) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vascular malformation (epidural AVM) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma (most common cause overall) and iatrogenic (post-surgical epidural anesthesia)', 'Spinal epidural hematomas most often follow trauma or iatrogenic procedures such as spinal surgery and epidural anesthesia. Spontaneous bleeding in the setting of anticoagulation is an important cause but less common overall. Tumor hemorrhage, coagulopathy alone, and vascular malformations are rarer etiologies.'),
('583bc5b3-0d59-53e9-b5eb-fb2ec69854fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of a tethered spinal cord?', '["Myelomeningocele (post-repair tethering) resulting in decreased functional activity","Dermal sinus tract (tethering at the sinus) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tight filum terminale (most common cause of tethered cord) often associated with a thickened filum or lipoma","Diastematomyelia (split cord) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intramedullary lipoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tight filum terminale (most common cause of tethered cord) often associated with a thickened filum or lipoma', 'A tight filum terminale, often with a thickened filum or fibrolipoma, is the most common cause of a tethered spinal cord. It anchors the conus medullaris abnormally low, producing progressive cord traction. Post-repair tethering, dermal sinus tracts, diastematomyelia, and lipomas are less common causes.'),
('d6ad8f98-8885-54fb-a6a2-405450e466ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of tethered cord syndrome after myelomeningocele repair?', '["Diastematomyelia resulting in decreased functional activity","Lipoma recurrence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dermoid cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Retethering from scar tissue at the repair site (secondary tethering)","Syrinx formation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retethering from scar tissue at the repair site (secondary tethering)', 'After myelomeningocele repair, the spinal cord most commonly becomes retethered by scar tissue and adhesions formed at the surgical site, causing secondary tethering with progressive neurologic decline. Diastematomyelia, lipoma recurrence, dermoid cysts, and syrinx formation are less frequent contributors.'),
('64775568-f695-57c0-b7a0-ceec5a9585bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of dermal sinus tract in the spine?', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Pilonidal sinus (acquired) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Incomplete separation of the neural tube from the cutaneous ectoderm during primary neurulation (congenital)","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Post-surgical scar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete separation of the neural tube from the cutaneous ectoderm during primary neurulation (congenital)', 'A spinal dermal sinus tract results from incomplete separation of the neural tube from the overlying cutaneous ectoderm during primary neurulation, leaving an epithelium-lined connection from the skin to deeper structures. It is a congenital anomaly, not an acquired lesion like a pilonidal sinus. Infection, trauma, and surgical scarring are secondary phenomena.'),
('236f7b50-2068-54aa-99dd-6e4038c4921e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of split cord malformation (diastematomyelia)?', '["A bony or fibrous septum splitting the spinal cord due to a persistent accessory neurenteric canal (split notochord theory)","Lipomeningocele accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neurenteric cyst mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Myelomeningocele without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tethered cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A bony or fibrous septum splitting the spinal cord due to a persistent accessory neurenteric canal (split notochord theory)', 'Diastematomyelia is caused by a bony or fibrous septum that splits the spinal cord, explained by the split notochord theory with a persistent accessory neurenteric canal. It is often associated with tethering. Lipomeningocele, neurenteric cysts, and myelomeningocele are separate dysraphic lesions.'),
('104876e5-fe01-5b0c-bb14-70afebbe1ace'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of neurenteric cyst?', '["Enteric duplication with spinal involvement resulting in decreased functional activity","Failed neurulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abnormal gastrulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Notochordal remnants without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Persistence of the neurenteric canal (connection between the gut and neural tube) during early embryogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Persistence of the neurenteric canal (connection between the gut and neural tube) during early embryogenesis', 'A neurenteric cyst arises from persistence of the neurenteric canal, the transient embryologic communication between the gut and neural tube, leaving an endodermal-lined cyst in the spinal canal. Enteric duplication with spinal involvement, failed neurulation, and notochordal remnants represent different mechanisms of malformation.'),
('0047bc0c-370d-59bb-bd54-e4c0ed8cd020'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of syringomyelia associated with spinal cord tumor?', '["Infectious (TB) resulting in decreased functional activity","Chiari I with syrinx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic syringomyelia mediated by specific receptor activation","Traumatic syrinx (post-traumatic syringomyelia) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intramedullary tumor (ependymoma hemangioblastoma) causing cyst formation (peritumoral edema or true syrinx)"]'::jsonb, 'Intramedullary tumor (ependymoma hemangioblastoma) causing cyst formation (peritumoral edema or true syrinx)', 'When syringomyelia accompanies a spinal cord tumor, an intramedullary tumor such as ependymoma or hemangioblastoma causes the cyst through peritumoral edema or true syrinx formation. Chiari I, trauma, infection, and idiopathic causes are unrelated to tumor-associated cavities. The tumor itself is the primary driver.'),
('6ff10850-9300-5379-a09c-be01ca6dea20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of post-traumatic syringomyelia?', '["Spinal cord injury (especially with arachnoiditis and cord tethering) causing CSF flow obstruction and cavity formation","Complete cord transection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gunshot wound mediated by specific receptor activation","Compression fracture with cord compression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cauda equina injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord injury (especially with arachnoiditis and cord tethering) causing CSF flow obstruction and cavity formation', 'Post-traumatic syringomyelia follows spinal cord injury, most often when arachnoiditis and cord tethering obstruct normal CSF flow and promote progressive cavity (syrinx) formation. It is the general mechanism of injury rather than a specific injury type such as transection or gunshot wound. Cauda equina injury does not involve the cord proper.'),
('2f9a9f04-6f9c-5db7-9ee1-42fac589f5c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of vacuolar myelopathy in HIV/AIDS?', '["Toxoplasmosis myelitis resulting in decreased functional activity","HIV infection of the spinal cord causing spongiform degeneration of the posterior and lateral columns","Opportunistic infection (CMV) mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary CNS lymphoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nutritional deficiency (B12) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HIV infection of the spinal cord causing spongiform degeneration of the posterior and lateral columns', 'HIV-associated vacuolar myelopathy is caused by direct HIV infection of the spinal cord, producing spongiform degeneration of the posterior and lateral columns with ataxia, spasticity, and sensory loss. Toxoplasmosis, CMV, and lymphoma are opportunistic complications rather than the cause of vacuolar change. B12 deficiency is a separate etiology.'),
('e7f2a874-de45-5783-a67f-929097e8e5da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'What is the most common cause of acute transverse myelitis recurrence?', '["ADEM (monophasic) resulting in decreased functional activity","Multiple sclerosis (first presentation of MS most commonly in adults causing recurrent episodes of transverse myelitis)","Post-infectious ( monophasic) mediated by specific receptor activation","Neuromyelitis optica (NMO AQP4 antibody more likely to recur) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Systemic lupus (SLE) causing recurrent myelitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple sclerosis (first presentation of MS most commonly in adults causing recurrent episodes of transverse myelitis)', 'Recurrent episodes of transverse myelitis most commonly reflect multiple sclerosis, which in adults often first presents as an acute myelitis and then recurs as part of the demyelinating disease. ADEM and typical post-infectious myelitis are monophasic. NMO and SLE can recur but are less common overall than MS.'),
('9f408a6e-38fb-56e9-835c-14ce6dfccc73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Failure of fusion of the caudal neuropore results in which condition?', '["Arnold-Chiari malformation","Holoprosencephaly","Hydrocephalus","Anencephaly","Spina bifida"]'::jsonb, 'Spina bifida', 'Failure of the caudal neuropore to close during neurulation prevents fusion of the posterior neural tube, producing spina bifida of varying severity. Nonclosure of the cranial neuropore leads to anencephaly instead. Arnold-Chiari malformation, holoprosencephaly, and hydrocephalus are unrelated to caudal neuropore closure.'),
('2bbcb01f-87d9-576b-8688-91db32e1a364'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'The cranial neuropore closes at which day. Which option is most accurate?', '["Day 25 (approximately)","Day 20 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Day 30 mediated by specific receptor activation","Day 28 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Day 22 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Day 25 (approximately)', 'The cranial neuropore closes around day 25 of embryonic development, completing neural tube closure at the cephalic end. The caudal neuropore closes slightly later, around day 27-28. Closure failure at this site produces anencephaly.'),
('43f471fa-eb1a-53ff-beab-3957b471cfaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'In Neural tube and CNS development, which statement best fits the following fact: the central nervous system develops from which germ layer?', '["Neural crest","Mesoderm","Neuroectoderm (neural plate)","Surface ectoderm","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Neuroectoderm (neural plate)', 'The central nervous system develops from the neuroectoderm of the neural plate, which folds to form the neural tube. The neural crest, a derivative of the neuroectoderm, gives rise to the peripheral nervous system and other structures. Mesoderm forms muscle, bone, and connective tissue, while surface ectoderm and endoderm form other tissues.'),
('07f9dda5-d3f0-5a06-8805-ae79886d63c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Regarding Neural tube and CNS development, what is the correct answer to: the neural tube forms by which process?', '["Gastrulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurulation (folding and fusion of the neural plate)","Organogenesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Histogenesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Somitogenesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurulation (folding and fusion of the neural plate)', 'The neural tube forms by neurulation, in which the neural plate folds and the neural folds fuse in the midline to create a closed tube. Gastrulation precedes neurulation and establishes the three germ layers, while organogenesis and histogenesis occur afterward. Somitogenesis produces the paraxial mesoderm somites.'),
('67a73a22-f63e-52a8-a849-a61f7ab16e6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'For Neural tube and CNS development, which option is most appropriate: what congenital anomaly results from failure of the cranial neuropore to close?', '["Spina bifida","Meningocele","Anencephaly","Encephalocele","Hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 'Anencephaly', 'Failure of the cranial neuropore to close prevents formation of the forebrain and cranium, producing anencephaly, which is usually lethal. Spina bifida and meningocele result from caudal neuropore defects, while encephalocele is a different neural tube herniation. Hydrocephalus is a later complication rather than a primary closure defect.'),
('921a51a0-e88d-5dbd-af18-866f78a9ce46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Which answer is correct in Neural tube and CNS development when asked: the neural crest cells arise from which region of the neural folds?', '["Floor plate","Dorsolateral region","Roof plate","Ventromedial region","Basal plate"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsolateral region', 'Neural crest cells originate from the dorsolateral region of the neural folds and then migrate to form peripheral neurons, melanocytes, and other structures. The floor plate and basal plate are ventral regions of the neural tube, while the roof plate is dorsal but does not give rise to neural crest cells.'),
('648119f5-6d7e-5ff1-afa8-af4b1b4e3efc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'In Neural tube and CNS development, the best response to ''Neural crest cells give rise to all of the following EXCEPT:'' is:', '["Peripheral neurons (DRG autonomic)","Neurons of the CNS","Adrenal medulla","Melanocytes","Facial bones and cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Neurons of the CNS', 'Neural crest cells give rise to peripheral sensory and autonomic neurons, the adrenal medulla, melanocytes, and facial bones and cartilage, but not neurons of the CNS. CNS neurons originate from the neural tube proper. This makes CNS neurons the exception in the list.'),
('ab27384c-3ba7-59b3-9d0e-41afe4558334'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'During revision of Neural tube and CNS development, which of the following is correct: what is the most common neural tube defect?', '["Anencephaly resulting in decreased functional activity","Spina bifida (especially occulta)","Meningocele mediated by specific receptor activation","Myelomeningocele without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Encephalocele leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spina bifida (especially occulta)', 'Spina bifida, particularly the occult form, is the most common neural tube defect. Occulta is a mild vertebral defect with intact spinal contents, whereas meningocele, myelomeningocele, and encephalocele involve protruding or exposed neural elements. Anencephaly is a severe cranial defect and less common.'),
('4290701c-65b0-552e-a8c1-3a681f7c92f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'In Neural tube and CNS development, which statement best fits the following fact: what is the incidence of neural tube defects worldwide?', '["1 per 50 resulting in decreased functional activity","000 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 1-2 per 1000 live births (varies by population)","1 per 100 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 per 10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1-2 per 1000 live births (varies by population)', 'Neural tube defects occur in about 1-2 per 1000 live births worldwide, with significant variation between populations and regions. The alternative rates of 1 per 10, 1 per 50, or 1 per 100 greatly overestimate the true incidence.'),
('43aa18f4-49ac-5ba2-b458-45254fa0718a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Central Nervous System', 'Regarding Neural tube and CNS development, what is the correct answer to: folic acid supplementation reduces the risk of ntds by what percentage?', '["50%","Up to 70%","100%","90%","20%"]'::jsonb, 'Up to 70%', 'Periconceptional folic acid supplementation reduces the risk of neural tube defects by up to 70%. It does not provide complete protection, which is why 100% is wrong, and a 20-50% reduction underestimates the benefit. Adequate folate is critical before and during early pregnancy because neurulation occurs within the first weeks.'),
('d9b1a8e4-2aba-550b-8a5e-3c4ca9d830f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The primitive gut tube is formed during which week of development?', '["Week 2 resulting in decreased functional activity","Week 6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Week 3 mediated by specific receptor activation","Week 4 (by folding of the embryo)","Week 5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Week 4 (by folding of the embryo)', 'During the 4th week, cephalocaudal and lateral folding of the embryo encloses the dorsal part of the yolk sac to form the primitive gut tube. The other weeks place this folding event too early or too late in development.'),
('d9d93001-53b9-5ef2-a148-7575969c83b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The primitive gut is divided into which three parts?', '["Small bowel colon rectum","Duodenum jejunum ileum","Esophagus stomach intestine","Pharynx esophagus stomach","Foregut midgut hindgut"]'::jsonb, 'Foregut midgut hindgut', 'The endodermal gut tube is divided into foregut, midgut, and hindgut, each with a distinct arterial supply and set of derivatives. The other options list named organs or intestinal regions rather than the three embryonic divisions.'),
('c5a5352e-1b9d-5b69-80a5-e2dac05d9c65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The foregut is supplied by which artery?', '["Inferior mesenteric artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Splenic artery","Celiac trunk","Hepatic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac trunk', 'The celiac trunk supplies the foregut, from the distal esophagus to the proximal half of the duodenum. The superior and inferior mesenteric arteries supply the midgut and hindgut respectively, while the splenic and hepatic arteries are specific branches of the celiac trunk rather than the artery that supplies the foregut as a whole.'),
('b2b7caee-912d-5d0f-9e62-be93eab63bce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The midgut is supplied by which artery?', '["Hepatic artery","Superior mesenteric artery (SMA)","Inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)","Celiac trunk","Gastroduodenal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery (SMA)', 'The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, which extends from the distal half of the duodenum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The celiac trunk and inferior mesenteric artery serve the foregut and hindgut, and the hepatic and gastroduodenal arteries are branches of the celiac system.'),
('cf4586f8-90c4-56bb-8d41-ab7990f4c1de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The hindgut is supplied by which artery?', '["Celiac trunk","Left colic artery","Inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)","Superior mesenteric artery (SMA)","Middle colic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)', 'The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut, from the distal transverse colon down to the upper part of the anal canal. The celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery serve the foregut and midgut, while the left and middle colic arteries are specific branches of these trunks rather than the artery for the hindgut as a whole.'),
('e80238e6-31dc-5cb8-9c23-9f2c01c7cf9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The esophagus develops from which part of the foregut?', '["Caudal part of the pharyngeal foregut just behind the trachea","Midgut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hindgut mediated by specific receptor activation","Rostral foregut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Caudal part of the pharyngeal foregut just behind the trachea', 'The esophagus develops from the caudal part of the pharyngeal foregut lying just behind the developing trachea, with a tracheoesophageal septum dividing the two tubes. The midgut, hindgut, rostral foregut, and stomach give rise to entirely different structures.'),
('fc256394-a52f-560b-9fad-c8446d71c582'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the esophagus?', '["H-type fistula resulting in decreased functional activity","Esophageal atresia with distal tracheoesophageal fistula (Type C)","Esophageal stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Esophageal duplication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Esophageal atresia without fistula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal atresia with distal tracheoesophageal fistula (Type C)', 'Esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula is the most common form, accounting for roughly 85-90% of esophageal anomalies. Isolated atresia, H-type fistula, stenosis, and duplication are all much rarer by comparison.'),
('e22013f8-538d-53f5-8287-c88f74f0215d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the Vogt classification for esophageal atresia?', '["Type 1 2 3 4 5 resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Type I: absence of fistula; Type II: fistula to proximal pouch; Type III: fistula to distal pouch; Type IIIA: distal fistula alone; Type IIIB: both fistulae","Type I II III IV V mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Type AB CD E without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Classification by length of gap leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type I: absence of fistula; Type II: fistula to proximal pouch; Type III: fistula to distal pouch; Type IIIA: distal fistula alone; Type IIIB: both fistulae', 'The Vogt system uses roman-numeral types keyed to the presence and position of the fistula: Type I is atresia without fistula, Type II has a fistula to the proximal pouch, and Type III has a distal-pouch fistula, with Type IIIA carrying only a distal fistula and Type IIIB having fistulae to both pouches.'),
('d77864da-52a9-57fa-a04c-d1b731929d87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the Gross classification for esophageal atresia?', '["Classification by vertebral level resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Gross Type A: isolated atresia; B: atresia with proximal fistula; C: atresia with distal fistula; D: atresia with both fistulae; E: H-type fistula without atresia","Classification by gap length mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Type I-V only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type 1-5 only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gross Type A: isolated atresia; B: atresia with proximal fistula; C: atresia with distal fistula; D: atresia with both fistulae; E: H-type fistula without atresia', 'The Gross classification uses letters A through E to describe the presence of atresia and the site of any tracheoesophageal fistula. The other options are unrelated to the letter-based Gross scheme.'),
('bfa2f903-4d7f-5c30-8619-4ca9d91ab204'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The stomach develops from which part of the foregut?', '["Hindgut resulting in decreased functional activity","Yolk stalk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dilated part of the foregut just caudal to the esophagus","Pharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proximal duodenum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated part of the foregut just caudal to the esophagus', 'The stomach forms as a fusiform dilation of the foregut immediately caudal to the esophagus, with differential growth of its walls creating the greater and lesser curvatures. The hindgut, pharynx, yolk stalk, and duodenum are distinct regions with different fates.'),
('6acdc32a-0311-5a61-b57d-2bcf77a82411'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The stomach rotates around which axes?', '["Longitudinal axis (90° clockwise) and anteroposterior axis","45° rotation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","180° rotation mediated by specific receptor activation","rotation occurs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse axis only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Longitudinal axis (90° clockwise) and anteroposterior axis', 'The stomach rotates 90° clockwise around its longitudinal axis and also rotates around the anteroposterior axis, which repositions the curvatures and moves the pylorus to the right. Fixed angles of 45°, 180°, or a single transverse-axis rotation do not describe this two-step movement.'),
('7cc7620e-303b-5183-aed7-578d86595f76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The dorsal mesogastrium gives rise to which structure?', '["Gastroduodenal ligament","Greater omentum and spleen","Lesser omentum","Gastrohepatic ligament","Ligamentum teres"]'::jsonb, 'Greater omentum and spleen', 'The dorsal mesogastrium gives rise to the greater omentum and contributes the splenic primordium and gastrosplenic ligament as it grows and folds. The lesser omentum and gastrohepatic ligament are ventral mesogastrium derivatives, and the ligamentum teres is a remnant of the umbilical vein.'),
('678ab901-d614-50f0-be94-29b12be243fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The ventral mesogastrium gives rise to which structures?', '["Gastrocolic ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Lesser omentum (hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments)","Spleen mediated by specific receptor activation","Gastrosplenic ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Greater omentum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser omentum (hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments)', 'The ventral mesogastrium forms the lesser omentum, comprising the hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments, once the liver grows within it. The spleen, gastrosplenic ligament, and greater omentum are dorsal mesogastrium derivatives, and the gastrocolic ligament belongs to the greater omentum.'),
('3b1042d2-df61-5e87-a229-2ddb5f3697a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The duodenum develops from which structures?', '["Entirely from foregut resulting in decreased functional activity","Cranial part from foregut (celiac) and caudal part from midgut (SMA)","Entirely from midgut mediated by specific receptor activation","Entirely from hindgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From the yolk stalk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cranial part from foregut (celiac) and caudal part from midgut (SMA)', 'The duodenum has a dual origin: its cranial part arises from the caudal foregut and is supplied by the celiac trunk, while the caudal part arises from the cranial midgut and is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. This dual blood supply explains why not one of the single-origin options is correct.'),
('e20538ed-d450-5286-910d-12a0dbaa484b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'During development the duodenal lumen becomes temporarily obliterated and then recanalizes. Failure of recanalization causes:', '["Meckel''s diverticulum","Intestinal duplication","Malrotation","Pyloric stenosis","Duodenal atresia or stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal atresia or stenosis', 'The duodenal lumen is normally obliterated by epithelial proliferation and then recanalizes; if recanalization fails, duodenal atresia or stenosis results, producing proximal obstruction. Meckel''s diverticulum is a vitelline duct remnant, malrotation is a midgut rotation defect, and pyloric stenosis is muscular hypertrophy without a luminal recanalization defect.'),
('4f4480d5-3669-5726-b743-b8f42a5066fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Duodenal atresia is associated with which chromosomal abnormality?', '["Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)","Klinefelter syndrome","Trisomy 18","Trisomy 13","Turner syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)', 'Duodenal atresia is classically associated with Trisomy 21, seen in roughly one-third of cases. The other listed chromosomal conditions are not the recognized association for this malformation.'),
('a8bf42b7-d1da-5d74-ad84-aebe9680ddb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the classic prenatal ultrasound finding of duodenal atresia?', '["Single bubble resulting in decreased functional activity","Double bubble sign (dilated stomach and proximal duodenum)","Triple bubble sign mediated by specific receptor activation","Polyhydramnios without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ascites leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Double bubble sign (dilated stomach and proximal duodenum)', 'Duodenal atresia produces a dilated stomach and a dilated proximal duodenum on prenatal ultrasound, seen as the double bubble sign. A single bubble, triple bubble, polyhydramnios, and ascites do not represent this classic finding.'),
('54ab6237-377a-59f0-b328-d3745b96cc10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The liver develops from which embryonic structure?', '["Septum transversum resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatic diverticulum (liver bud) from the ventral foregut","Spleen primordium mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal pancreatic bud without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventral pancreatic bud leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic diverticulum (liver bud) from the ventral foregut', 'The liver develops from the hepatic diverticulum, an endodermal outgrowth of the ventral foregut; the septum transversum only contributes the connective tissue framework, and the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds form the pancreas.'),
('6d1ecd55-de67-567b-93ea-79b40bfaf285'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The gallbladder and extrahepatic bile ducts develop from which structure?', '["Caudal part of the hepatic diverticulum","Cranial part of the hepatic diverticulum","Dorsal pancreatic bud","Ventral pancreatic bud","Septum transversum"]'::jsonb, 'Caudal part of the hepatic diverticulum', 'The caudal part of the hepatic diverticulum gives rise to the gallbladder and extrahepatic bile ducts, while the cranial part forms the liver parenchyma. The pancreatic buds and septum transversum develop into other structures.'),
('db8cb16b-5cd8-508d-8f17-5ec2178f7a2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The pancreas develops from which two structures?', '["Two ventral buds","Buds from the duodenum and stomach","Dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds","Two dorsal buds","Hepatic diverticulum and dorsal bud"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds', 'The pancreas forms from two endodermal outgrowths of the duodenum, the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds, which fuse as the duodenum rotates. Single or paired bud options and the hepatic diverticulum are inconsistent with this origin.'),
('550071a3-8bb8-54f0-b883-90988649c6e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The ventral pancreatic bud gives rise to which part of the pancreas?', '["Entire pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the islets accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the tail mediated by specific receptor activation","Body and tail of pancreas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uncinate process and inferior part of the head"]'::jsonb, 'Uncinate process and inferior part of the head', 'The ventral pancreatic bud forms the uncinate process and the inferior part of the head of the pancreas. The dorsal bud contributes the rest, so options attributing the whole gland, tail alone, or only islets to the ventral bud are incorrect.'),
('7cbdd880-74ca-54c8-b9ae-ba31489855b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The dorsal pancreatic bud gives rise to which part of the pancreas?', '["Only the tail resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior part of head body and tail of pancreas","Entire pancreas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uncinate process only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior part of head body and tail of pancreas', 'The dorsal pancreatic bud forms the superior part of the head, the body, and the tail, while the ventral bud forms the uncinate process and inferior part of the head. Options restricting this contribution to a single region or to the whole gland are incorrect.'),
('a2a685ae-62f3-5b9e-b721-05dcc368b61d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The main pancreatic duct (duct of Wirsung) is formed by:', '["The duct of Santorini resulting in decreased functional activity","The dorsal duct alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both ducts remaining separate mediated by specific receptor activation","Fusion of the dorsal duct (distal) and ventral duct (proximal)","The ventral duct alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fusion of the dorsal duct (distal) and ventral duct (proximal)', 'The main pancreatic duct forms when the distal part of the dorsal duct joins the proximal part of the ventral duct as the buds fuse, draining through the major papilla. If the ducts fail to fuse, the smaller dorsal duct remnant persists as the accessory duct and the two systems remain separate.'),
('6923b223-72f4-5b09-ae05-68f20eb0ecd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The accessory pancreatic duct (duct of Santorini) is:', '["The ventral duct alone resulting in decreased functional activity","The main pancreatic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The proximal part of the dorsal pancreatic duct (if it persists)","A separate duct from the bile duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cystic duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The proximal part of the dorsal pancreatic duct (if it persists)', 'The accessory pancreatic duct represents the proximal portion of the dorsal duct that persists when the two buds fuse, draining through the minor papilla. It is not the ventral duct, the main duct, or an independent duct unrelated to the dorsal system.'),
('3b04f4b7-e6f8-5d9b-9c9d-5441bd193211'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Annular pancreas results from:', '["Ventral bud agenesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsal bud agenesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abnormal migration of the ventral pancreatic bud (encircles the duodenum)","Duodenal atresia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Failure of pancreatic bud fusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal migration of the ventral pancreatic bud (encircles the duodenum)', 'Annular pancreas arises when the ventral pancreatic bud migrates abnormally and wraps around the duodenum, potentially causing duodenal obstruction. It is a positional defect of the ventral bud, not agenesis, atresia, or failure of bud fusion.'),
('10ff6b8c-682b-5459-b0c8-37d4c3025997'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Pancreas divisum results from:', '["Fusion of the bile duct with the pancreatic duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Annular pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreatic agenesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Failure of fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds","Duodenal atresia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds', 'Pancreas divisum results when the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds fail to fuse, leaving most of the pancreas draining through the smaller accessory duct. It is not caused by annular pancreas, agenesis, duodenal atresia, or bile duct fusion.'),
('400f94d6-05ab-5c78-b79c-a007417b2761'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly?', '["Pancreas divisum","Duodenal duplication","Ectopic pancreas","Annular pancreas","Pancreatic agenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreas divisum', 'Pancreas divisum, present in up to about 10% of people, is the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly. Annular pancreas, ectopic pancreas, and agenesis are all far less frequent, and duodenal duplication is not a pancreatic anomaly.'),
('0bca1b9a-fd54-5e4f-9143-8cc265f0c5cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is ectopic (heterotopic) pancreas?', '["Pancreatic tissue outside the pancreas (most commonly in stomach duodenum or Meckel''s diverticulum)","Double pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreas in the chest mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreas in the pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatic tissue in the liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic tissue outside the pancreas (most commonly in stomach duodenum or Meckel''s diverticulum)', 'Ectopic (heterotopic) pancreas is defined as pancreatic tissue located outside the gland, most often in the stomach, duodenum, or Meckel''s diverticulum. It is not a doubled gland or a pancreas confined to a single unusual site such as the chest, pelvis, or liver.'),
('4152e9e1-1fcb-54cf-af7f-643a7ed94f9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The spleen develops from which structure?', '["Hindgut endoderm","Foregut endoderm","Mesodermal cells in the dorsal mesogastrium","Midgut endoderm","Ectodermal cells in the ventral mesogastrium"]'::jsonb, 'Mesodermal cells in the dorsal mesogastrium', 'The spleen develops from mesodermal cells that condense within the dorsal mesogastrium, not from endoderm. Since it is neither an endodermal gut tube nor an ectodermal derivative, the other options are excluded.'),
('7ca8c7d1-d4e7-5571-8271-4e9bb91b53ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Accessory spleens (splenunculi) are found in what percentage of the population?', '["About 75%","About 90%","About 10-30%","Less than 1%","About 50%"]'::jsonb, 'About 10-30%', 'Accessory spleens are common, occurring in about 10-30% of individuals, usually near the splenic hilum, and are clinically important when splenectomy is planned. The extreme high and low percentages given in the other options overstate or understate this frequency.'),
('b8862bf0-d52a-5715-bf1c-72cf294acb8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is asplenia?', '["Splenic infarction resulting in decreased functional activity","Splenic atrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgical removal of the spleen mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital absence of the spleen associated with heterotaxy (Ivemark syndrome)","Splenic agenesis alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital absence of the spleen associated with heterotaxy (Ivemark syndrome)', 'Asplenia refers to congenital absence of the spleen occurring as part of heterotaxy, specifically Ivemark syndrome, with associated cardiac and situs anomalies. It is a developmental condition, not infarction, atrophy, or surgical removal, and it is linked to heterotaxy rather than isolated agenesis.'),
('d628392d-4630-5010-ba9c-5d9a1037e729'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is polysplenia?', '["Splenic cysts resulting in decreased functional activity","Splenic rupture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Splenic tumors mediated by specific receptor activation","Multiple splenic masses associated with heterotaxy (bilateral left-sidedness)","Normal variant with extra spleen tips leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple splenic masses associated with heterotaxy (bilateral left-sidedness)', 'Polysplenia is a heterotaxy syndrome with multiple splenic masses and bilateral left-sidedness, often accompanied by abdominal situs anomalies. It is not cysts, rupture, tumors, or a benign variant with extra splenic tips.'),
('76cfd224-4821-52e2-9c8e-24a8726a1855'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The small intestine (jejunum and ileum) develops from which structure?', '["Colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Hindgut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foregut mediated by specific receptor activation","Stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midgut loop (herniates into the umbilical cord)"]'::jsonb, 'Midgut loop (herniates into the umbilical cord)', 'The jejunum and ileum develop from the midgut loop, which herniates into the umbilical cord, rotates, and returns to the abdomen. The colon, hindgut, foregut, and stomach give rise to different segments of the gut.'),
('69251ded-677f-5912-8256-d8a0ee59df4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The midgut loop herniates into the umbilical cord at which week?', '["Week 6-10 (physiologic herniation)","Week 12-14 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Week 10-12 mediated by specific receptor activation","Week 4-5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Week 14-16 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Week 6-10 (physiologic herniation)', 'Physiologic herniation of the midgut into the umbilical cord occurs from weeks 6 to 10, when the abdominal cavity cannot yet contain the rapidly growing loop. The loop then returns during week 10, so later-week options are too late and earlier options are too early.'),
('868a8092-2f85-5bcc-9aee-a9c93d65c4b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The midgut loop rotates around which artery during herniation?', '["Celiac trunk","Superior mesenteric artery (SMA)","Gastroduodenal artery","Inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)","Hepatic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery (SMA)', 'The midgut loop rotates approximately 270 degrees counterclockwise around the superior mesenteric artery, which acts as its axis. The celiac trunk, inferior mesenteric artery, gastroduodenal artery, and hepatic artery do not serve as this axis.'),
('c9523eb6-08b9-50cc-bf21-04bb5ddf71e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the normal rotation of the midgut?', '["rotation","360° rotation","90° clockwise","270° counterclockwise","180° counterclockwise"]'::jsonb, '270° counterclockwise', 'The midgut completes a 270-degree counterclockwise rotation around the superior mesenteric artery, bringing the cecum to the right lower quadrant. Lesser or opposite-angle rotations and a full 360-degree turn do not describe the normal course.'),
('9d52a1e7-510e-583a-8d98-d61162eb2db4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Malrotation of the midgut can lead to which life-threatening complication?', '["Intestinal atresia resulting in decreased functional activity","Midgut volvulus (twisting of the midgut around SMA)","Hirschsprung disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Intussusception without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meckel''s diverticulum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midgut volvulus (twisting of the midgut around SMA)', 'The most dangerous result of malrotation is midgut volvulus, where the midgut twists around the superior mesenteric artery, compromising its blood supply and risking necrosis. Atresia, Hirschsprung disease, intussusception, and a Meckel''s diverticulum are distinct conditions with different causes.'),
('6f2e7e97-0c69-56d9-a19c-90e1a77080c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common presentation of malrotation in the newborn?', '["Abdominal distension resulting in decreased functional activity","Constipation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bloody stools mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilious vomiting (due to duodenal obstruction from Ladd bands or volvulus)","Failure to thrive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilious vomiting (due to duodenal obstruction from Ladd bands or volvulus)', 'Malrotation most often presents in the newborn with bilious vomiting caused by duodenal obstruction from Ladd bands or midgut volvulus. Abdominal distension, constipation, bloody stools, and failure to thrive are not the typical presenting picture.'),
('8e3e5c3b-0ee3-5036-ba95-68ea1aa691d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are Ladd bands?', '["Pancreatic bands resulting in decreased functional activity","Vascular bands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrous peritoneal bands in malrotation that cross from the cecum to the right upper quadrant obstructing the duodenum","Congenital bands from the liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abnormal ligaments leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous peritoneal bands in malrotation that cross from the cecum to the right upper quadrant obstructing the duodenum', 'Ladd bands are fibrous peritoneal bands that, in malrotation, run from the malpositioned cecum across the duodenum to the right upper quadrant and compress the duodenum. They are not pancreatic, vascular, hepatic, or nonspecific ligamentous structures.'),
('c481e1b3-f2e5-5c67-b715-92d2cfa77775'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the Ladd procedure?', '["Duodenal atresia repair resulting in decreased functional activity","Cecal mobilization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Volvulus reduction alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Resection of the midgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgical correction of malrotation involving division of Ladd bands and placement of the bowel in non-rotation position"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical correction of malrotation involving division of Ladd bands and placement of the bowel in non-rotation position', 'The Ladd procedure corrects malrotation by dividing the Ladd bands, reducing any volvulus, and placing the bowel in a non-rotated position so the duodenum lies free of obstruction. It is not simply cecal mobilization, volvulus reduction alone, atresia repair, or midgut resection.'),
('47a3e492-65f0-53d7-a6b2-cf39ab96359c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The cecum and appendix develop from which structure?', '["The terminal ileum resulting in decreased functional activity","The hindgut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cecal bud (a bulge on the antimesenteric side of the midgut)","The ascending colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The yolk stalk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cecal bud (a bulge on the antimesenteric side of the midgut)', 'The cecal bud appears as a bulge on the antimesenteric border of the midgut loop and gives rise to the cecum and appendix as it elongates. The terminal ileum, hindgut, ascending colon, and yolk stalk do not produce these structures.'),
('1e9d0080-8232-5c68-aa23-110b496c8770'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The appendix is:', '["A portion of the yolk sac resulting in decreased functional activity","A narrow diverticulum of the cecal bud that grows more slowly than the cecum","A remnant of the vitelline duct mediated by specific receptor activation","A direct outgrowth of the ileum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A secondary bud from the colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A narrow diverticulum of the cecal bud that grows more slowly than the cecum', 'The appendix develops as a narrow diverticulum of the distal cecal bud that grows more slowly than the rest of the cecum after birth, leaving a characteristic tubular structure. It is not a yolk sac remnant, a vitelline duct remnant, an ileal outgrowth, or a colonic bud.'),
('83dda561-8872-58e7-92fb-92c534450fed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The ascending colon develops from which structure?', '["The yolk sac resulting in decreased functional activity","The proximal hindgut (midgut derivatives extend to proximal transverse colon)","The distal foregut mediated by specific receptor activation","The allantois without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cloaca leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The proximal hindgut (midgut derivatives extend to proximal transverse colon)', 'The ascending colon arises from the proximal hindgut, and midgut derivatives extend as far as the proximal transverse colon. The yolk sac, allantois, cloaca, and distal foregut are not the source of the ascending colon.'),
('9d31eebf-03e9-5280-8b5b-2c7ff3af61ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The descending and sigmoid colon develop from which structure?', '["Midgut","Cecal bud","Hindgut","Yolk stalk","Foregut"]'::jsonb, 'Hindgut', 'The descending and sigmoid colon develop from the hindgut, which extends from the distal third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal. The midgut, cecal bud, yolk stalk, and foregut contribute to other regions.'),
('3e70278c-6f8c-56dd-9720-e5c84b155142'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The rectum develops from which structure?', '["Midgut resulting in decreased functional activity","Hindgut (dorsal part of the cloaca)","Foregut mediated by specific receptor activation","Urogenital sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cloacal membrane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hindgut (dorsal part of the cloaca)', 'The rectum develops from the hindgut, specifically the dorsal part of the cloaca, after the urorectal septum divides the cloaca. The urogenital sinus forms from the ventral cloaca, and the midgut, foregut, and cloacal membrane give rise to other structures.'),
('4cec5614-5b38-5365-afb9-4295c0fcf3ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The anal canal above the pectinate line develops from which structure?', '["Midgut","Foregut","Proctodeum (ectoderm)","Hindgut (endoderm)","Cloacal membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Hindgut (endoderm)', 'The anal canal above the pectinate line is derived from hindgut endoderm, while the portion below it is derived from the ectodermal proctodeum. This dual origin accounts for the differing arterial supply, venous drainage, and epithelium on the two sides of the pectinate line.'),
('4943e319-0c06-5e7e-9386-e2b3ea7ccca6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The anal canal below the pectinate line develops from which structure?', '["Urogenital sinus","Proctodeum (ectoderm)","Midgut","Foregut","Hindgut (endoderm)"]'::jsonb, 'Proctodeum (ectoderm)', 'The anal canal below the pectinate line arises from the proctodeum, an ectodermal invagination, giving it stratified squamous epithelium and somatic innervation. The hindgut endoderm forms the canal above the line, and the midgut, foregut, and urogenital sinus do not contribute here.'),
('ec534159-2386-5281-9779-e241ae0ce87b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the junction between the hindgut and proctodeum called?', '["Anorectal junction","Internal anal sphincter","Squamocolumnar junction","White line of Hilton","Pectinate line (dentate line)"]'::jsonb, 'Pectinate line (dentate line)', 'The junction of the endodermal hindgut and the ectodermal proctodeum is the pectinate (dentate) line, demarcating the anal canal''s differing epithelia and neurovasculature. The anorectal junction, sphincter, squamocolumnar junction, and white line of Hilton describe other landmarks.'),
('423771b2-0cc0-59e0-9b27-d2e87081a1e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the types of imperforate anus (anorectal malformations) in the Wingspread classification?', '["Low (translevator) intermediate and high (supralevator)","Simple complex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type I II III IV mediated by specific receptor activation","Type AB CD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete incomplete leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low (translevator) intermediate and high (supralevator)', 'The Wingspread classification divides anorectal malformations into low (translevator), intermediate, and high (supralevator) types according to the position of the bowel relative to the levator ani. Letter-based subtypes and simple-complete divisions belong to other classification systems.'),
('0ffcaa2f-2295-5230-ae52-1201b22b5e6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of anorectal malformation in males?', '["Rectovaginal fistula","Rectoperineal fistula (low anomaly)","Rectovesical fistula","Imperforate anus without fistula","Rectourethral fistula (high anomaly)"]'::jsonb, 'Rectourethral fistula (high anomaly)', 'Rectourethral fistula is the most common anorectal malformation in males, a supralevator (high) lesion in which the rectum communicates with the urethra. Rectoperineal fistulas and imperforate anus without fistula occur but less often, and the rectovaginal and rectovesical options do not apply to males or to the most frequent male anomaly.'),
('3520b40d-98c8-5d39-b627-8695b1644f3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of anorectal malformation in females?', '["Imperforate anus without fistula","Rectovaginal fistula","Rectoperineal fistula","Cloaca","Rectovestibular fistula (low anomaly)"]'::jsonb, 'Rectovestibular fistula (low anomaly)', 'In females the rectum most often opens into the vestibule just behind the hymen, forming a rectovestibular fistula, a low anomaly corrected from the perineum. Rectovaginal fistula, cloaca, and imperforate anus without fistula are far less frequent or represent high anomalies.'),
('03f84753-abf8-5ad9-b34c-6bd37c4623af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the association between anorectal malformations and other anomalies?', '["Down syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","VACTERL association (Vertebral Anal Cardiac TE fistula Renal Limb)","Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Fetal alcohol syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Turner syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VACTERL association (Vertebral Anal Cardiac TE fistula Renal Limb)', 'Anorectal malformations are a major component of the VACTERL association, which also includes vertebral, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal, and limb defects. Down, Beckwith-Wiedemann, fetal alcohol, and Turner syndromes do not characteristically accompany imperforate anus.'),
('f6dce0d0-e467-5181-95ce-81879ecec68a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon)?', '["Infection of the colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstruction of the colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absence of ganglion cells in the distal colon due to failure of neural crest cell migration","Tumor of the colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammation of the colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absence of ganglion cells in the distal colon due to failure of neural crest cell migration', 'Hirschsprung disease results when neural crest cells fail to migrate craniocaudally, leaving the distal colon aganglionic and functionally obstructed. Infection, inflammation, and tumor do not explain the congenital lack of enteric ganglia.'),
('eaaf9062-fddf-563e-bd9f-9ee08dc90bb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common length of aganglionosis in Hirschsprung disease?', '["Ultra-short segment resulting in decreased functional activity","Small intestinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total colonic mediated by specific receptor activation","Rectosigmoid (short segment) about 80% of cases","Long segment (splenic flexure) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectosigmoid (short segment) about 80% of cases', 'Aganglionosis usually involves only the rectosigmoid, accounting for about 80% of cases. Total colonic and long-segment disease are much less frequent, while isolated ultra-short and small-intestinal involvement are rare.'),
('20e1f1a1-361d-5f45-8629-ade772d0cc2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What gene is most commonly associated with familial Hirschsprung disease?', '["SOX10","RET proto-oncogene","GDNF","EDNRB","EDN3"]'::jsonb, 'RET proto-oncogene', 'Loss-of-function mutations of the RET proto-oncogene, which encodes a tyrosine kinase receptor, are the most common genetic cause of familial Hirschsprung disease. SOX10, GDNF, EDNRB, and EDN3 are additional susceptibility genes but are involved far less often.'),
('d6d14854-6453-5c2a-a51e-874e7e4f582d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Hirschsprung disease is associated with which chromosomal abnormality?', '["Klinefelter syndrome","Patau syndrome","Down syndrome (trisomy 21)","Turner syndrome","Edwards syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Down syndrome (trisomy 21)', 'Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is the chromosomal abnormality most strongly linked to Hirschsprung disease. Klinefelter, Turner, Patau, and Edwards syndromes are not recognized associations.'),
('ba9f9033-ea38-5197-a0dd-36ac58f22292'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common presentation of Hirschsprung disease in the newborn?', '["Failure to pass meconium in the first 24-48 hours","Bloody stools accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Constipation mediated by specific receptor activation","Vomiting without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abdominal distension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure to pass meconium in the first 24-48 hours', 'The aganglionic segment prevents the newborn from emptying the colon, so delayed passage of meconium beyond 24-48 hours is the classic early sign. Constipation, distension, vomiting, and bloody stools appear later.'),
('c99e062a-ab71-58cf-a316-c444950745eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the definitive diagnosis of Hirschsprung disease based on?', '["Manometry findings resulting in decreased functional activity","Barium enema findings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rectal suction biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells","Ileus on X-ray without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clinical presentation alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectal suction biopsy showing absence of ganglion cells', 'Definitive diagnosis depends on a rectal suction biopsy that shows absence of ganglion cells with hypertrophied nerve trunks. Manometry and barium enema provide supportive evidence but are not diagnostic.'),
('fba5f6ba-e8de-5a31-b51b-67b9e0e214d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most important structure formed by the urorectal septum?', '["Formation of the rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Formation of the urethra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Separation of the cloaca into urogenital sinus and anorectal canal","Formation of the bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Formation of the perineum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Separation of the cloaca into urogenital sinus and anorectal canal', 'The urorectal septum divides the cloaca into a ventral urogenital sinus and a dorsal anorectal canal. The rectum, urethra, bladder, and perineum develop as a consequence of this division rather than being formed by the septum itself.'),
('3373980b-c7e6-5ca6-91cc-47f80d21a2d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The urorectal septum is derived from which structure?', '["Ectodermal invagination resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural crest cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endodermal outgrowth mediated by specific receptor activation","Allantois without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A mesenchymal wedge from the splanchnic mesoderm that grows toward the cloacal membrane"]'::jsonb, 'A mesenchymal wedge from the splanchnic mesoderm that grows toward the cloacal membrane', 'The urorectal septum is a crescentic fold of splanchnic mesenchyme that grows from the angle between the allantois and hindgut toward the cloacal membrane. It is neither ectodermal, endodermal, neural crest, nor allantoic in origin.'),
('fbe49678-6512-5e9c-aa63-b9d80f0a9a16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Failure of the urorectal septum to develop properly causes:', '["VACTERL association resulting in decreased functional activity","Imperforate anus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Persistent cloaca (common channel for urinary GI and genital tracts)","Tracheoesophageal fistula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Duodenal atresia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent cloaca (common channel for urinary GI and genital tracts)', 'If the urorectal septum fails to fuse with the cloacal membrane, the urinary, gastrointestinal, and genital tracts open into a single common channel, a persistent cloaca. Imperforate anus and the other listed defects arise from different embryologic failures.'),
('7c588f4a-6e8e-59ba-913a-3e27e7bdb403'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is omphalocele?', '["Eventration resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastroschisis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Herniation of abdominal contents into the umbilical cord with a covering sac","Umbilical hernia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Herniation through the abdominal wall without a sac leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Herniation of abdominal contents into the umbilical cord with a covering sac', 'Omphalocele is a midline defect in which bowel and sometimes liver herniate into the umbilical cord covered by a sac of peritoneum and amnion. Gastroschisis and the other options are distinct defects that lack this membranous covering.'),
('93e42a33-3326-50df-9234-bb03337ffecd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common location of gastroschisis?', '["Left side of the umbilicus","In the hypogastric area","In the epigastric area","Through the umbilical cord","Right side of the umbilicus (paramedian defect)"]'::jsonb, 'Right side of the umbilicus (paramedian defect)', 'Gastroschisis is a paramedian full-thickness abdominal wall defect, almost always to the right of the umbilicus, through which bowel eviscerates without a sac. It does not occur through the umbilical cord or in the epigastric and hypogastric regions.'),
('1256d521-02ed-52f9-a3f5-d9a51790a1fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the major difference between omphalocele and gastroschisis?', '["Gastroschisis is midline","Omphalocele is associated with other anomalies","Gastroschisis has a sac; omphalocele does not","Omphalocele is smaller","Omphalocele has a sac; gastroschisis does not"]'::jsonb, 'Omphalocele has a sac; gastroschisis does not', 'The defining difference is the covering: omphalocele contents are enclosed in a peritoneal-amniotic sac, whereas gastroschisis is a full-thickness defect with uncovered bowel. Both may associate with other anomalies, so the remaining statements are unreliable.'),
('bc7a0a28-a070-565b-9bc3-401cd580b706'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Omphalocele is associated with which other conditions?', '["Chromosomal abnormalities (trisomy 13 18) Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","Neural tube defects only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Limb anomalies mediated by specific receptor activation","Only isolated defects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac anomalies only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chromosomal abnormalities (trisomy 13 18) Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome', 'Omphalocele is strongly associated with chromosomal syndromes such as trisomy 13 and 18 and with Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome. Isolated defects or single-system anomalies do not characterize its usual associations.'),
('b9dc320a-8432-5f1d-aeaf-689e68947e87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Gastroschisis is associated with which factor?', '["Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome","Young maternal age (usually isolated anomaly)","Advanced maternal age","Chromosomal abnormalities","Cardiac anomalies"]'::jsonb, 'Young maternal age (usually isolated anomaly)', 'Gastroschisis typically occurs in infants of young mothers and is usually an isolated anomaly without chromosomal defects. Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, advanced maternal age, and cardiac associations are not characteristic.'),
('06d13e9d-907c-50f3-a675-d6402f27654a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of gastroschisis?', '["Necrotizing enterocolitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Omphalitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Volvulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bowel atresia/stenosis and perforation (due to vascular compromise of exposed bowel)"]'::jsonb, 'Bowel atresia/stenosis and perforation (due to vascular compromise of exposed bowel)', 'Prolonged exposure of eviscerated bowel to amniotic fluid compromises its mesenteric blood supply, making atresia/stenosis and perforation the most frequent complications. NEC, volvulus, omphalitis, and infection are less typical for gastroschisis.'),
('35af2ab4-c3c0-54e5-943e-c8985c316b8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Meckel''s diverticulum?', '["An appendiceal remnant resulting in decreased functional activity","A gastric diverticulum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A cecal diverticulum mediated by specific receptor activation","A colonic diverticulum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A remnant of the vitelline (omphaloenteric) duct on the antimesenteric border of the ileum"]'::jsonb, 'A remnant of the vitelline (omphaloenteric) duct on the antimesenteric border of the ileum', 'Meckel diverticulum is the persisting proximal portion of the vitelline (omphaloenteric) duct, located on the antimesenteric border of the distal ileum. It is not an appendiceal, gastric, cecal, or colonic remnant.'),
('825493ee-0960-5e90-9f12-e4f77273c0e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'The rule of 2s for Meckel''s diverticulum includes:', '["2% of population 2 inches long 2 feet from ileocecal valve","2 arteries 2 veins 2 nerves","2 types of heterotopic tissue 2 surgical complications","2 cm long 2 cm from cecum 2% symptomatic","2 inches wide 2 cm in diameter 2 layers"]'::jsonb, '2% of population 2 inches long 2 feet from ileocecal valve', 'The classic rule states that about 2% of the population has the diverticulum, it measures about 2 inches long, and it lies about 2 feet proximal to the ileocecal valve. The other groupings describe measurements that do not apply to Meckel diverticulum.'),
('4741af4b-69a6-537a-9490-7cc34d60d28e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common heterotopic tissue found in Meckel''s diverticulum?', '["Esophageal mucosa resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastric mucosa (most common) then pancreatic tissue","Jejunal mucosa mediated by specific receptor activation","Colonic mucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Duodenal mucosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric mucosa (most common) then pancreatic tissue', 'Gastric mucosa is the commonest heterotopic tissue inside a Meckel diverticulum, followed by pancreatic tissue. Esophageal, jejunal, colonic, and duodenal mucosa are not characteristic.'),
('7996c419-b648-503e-9c9a-a80f39a901b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of Meckel''s diverticulum in children?', '["Diverticulitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Intussusception accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Perforation mediated by specific receptor activation","Obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectal bleeding (painless hematochezia) from ulceration"]'::jsonb, 'Rectal bleeding (painless hematochezia) from ulceration', 'Acid secreted by heterotopic gastric mucosa ulcerates the adjacent ileum, causing painless hematochezia, the leading complication in children. Diverticulitis, intussusception, perforation, and obstruction are less frequent at this age.'),
('080378e6-1f19-5b64-ad64-f0f3ce33b5c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of Meckel''s diverticulum in adults?', '["Perforation resulting in decreased functional activity","Diverticulitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intestinal obstruction (from volvulus intussusception or Littre hernia)","Rectal bleeding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Umbilical discharge leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal obstruction (from volvulus intussusception or Littre hernia)', 'In adults a Meckel diverticulum most often declares itself as intestinal obstruction, which may result from volvulus, intussusception, or a Littre hernia. Bleeding, diverticulitis, and umbilical discharge are less common adult presentations.'),
('3711e3d2-82fc-5a67-83fd-8c60c39b0918'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is a vitelline sinus?', '["A sinus from the umbilicus due to incomplete obliteration of the vitelline duct","Umbilical hernia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Umbilical granuloma mediated by specific receptor activation","An infected Meckel''s diverticulum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Patent urachus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A sinus from the umbilicus due to incomplete obliteration of the vitelline duct', 'A vitelline sinus is an external opening at the umbilicus left when the vitelline duct obliterates incompletely, producing persistent mucous or fecal discharge. Umbilical hernia, granuloma, an infected Meckel diverticulum, and patent urachus are separate entities.'),
('d26ec8b8-bdcc-560f-9631-942422990270'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is a vitelline cyst?', '["An umbilical hernia resulting in decreased functional activity","A cystic remnant of the vitelline duct along its course","An ovarian cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","An intestinal duplication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meckel''s diverticulum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cystic remnant of the vitelline duct along its course', 'When the middle of the vitelline duct persists while both ends close, fluid accumulates to form a vitelline (omphaloenteric) cyst along the duct''s course. Umbilical hernia, ovarian cyst, intestinal duplication, and Meckel diverticulum are not this finding.'),
('0356c23a-770d-5276-9f95-b52fffe96021'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is a patent vitelline duct?', '["A type of Meckel''s diverticulum resulting in decreased functional activity","Urachal fistula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Umbilical polyp mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete patency of the vitelline duct from the ileum to the umbilicus causing fecal discharge","Vitelline sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complete patency of the vitelline duct from the ileum to the umbilicus causing fecal discharge', 'Complete failure of vitelline duct obliteration leaves a channel from distal ileum to the umbilicus, so meconium and feces drain from the umbilicus. Urachal fistula, umbilical polyp, vitelline sinus, and a Meckel-type diverticulum are partial or different remnants.'),
('b3bcaee8-7b9c-5492-9bc5-bfde8fc7fd0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is intestinal duplication?', '["A congenital duplication of a segment of the GI tract sharing a common wall with the native bowel","Two separate small intestines accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete duplication of the entire GI tract mediated by specific receptor activation","Separate Meckel''s diverticula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Double colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital duplication of a segment of the GI tract sharing a common wall with the native bowel', 'Intestinal duplication is a congenital tube or cyst sharing a common muscular wall and blood supply with the adjacent native bowel. Two separate intestines, complete GI duplication, separate Meckel diverticula, and double colon do not describe this entity.'),
('1561e625-aa1f-58de-8a0d-f59ab1f38e21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common location of intestinal duplication?', '["Colon","Ileum (most common)","Esophagus","Duodenum","Stomach"]'::jsonb, 'Ileum (most common)', 'Duplications are most frequent in the ileum, although they may occur anywhere from mouth to anus. Esophagus, duodenum, colon, and stomach are involved far less often.'),
('2879174d-7155-53a2-9f91-3983b686216a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of intestinal duplication?', '["Cystic duplication (noncommunicating)","Diverticular duplication","Complete duplication","Partial duplication","Tubular duplication (communicating)"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic duplication (noncommunicating)', 'Noncommunicating cystic duplications, which do not connect with the bowel lumen, are the most common form. Tubular communicating, diverticular, complete, and partial types occur less frequently.'),
('7ab5c936-22d9-57dc-845d-16b9d971d475'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the embryologic origin of intestinal duplication?', '["Infection during pregnancy resulting in decreased functional activity","Abnormal recanalization or diverticulum formation during development","Twisting of the gut mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal vasculature without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Incomplete migration of neural crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal recanalization or diverticulum formation during development', 'Duplications are thought to arise from abnormal recanalization of the solid gut or from abortive diverticulum formation during development. Infection, twisting of the gut, vascular anomalies, and neural crest failure are not accepted mechanisms.'),
('9d30f05e-805f-5f3e-b7ee-a72f6c305090'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common obstruction of the small bowel in the newborn?', '["Duodenal atresia","Malrotation with volvulus","Hirschsprung disease","Meconium ileus","Jejunoileal atresia"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal atresia', 'Duodenal atresia is the most common congenital obstruction of the small bowel in the newborn, classically producing a double-bubble sign. Malrotation with volvulus, Hirschsprung disease, meconium ileus, and jejunoileal atresia are important but less frequent causes.'),
('55694053-bd69-50bc-b751-e8f3bc7e96b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of jejunoileal atresia?', '["Type IIIb (apple-peel atresia)","Type IIIa (V-shaped mesenteric defect)","Type IV (multiple atresias)","Type I (mucosal atresia with intact bowel wall)","Type II (fibrous cord between atretic ends)"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (mucosal atresia with intact bowel wall)', 'Type I atresia, in which a mucosal diaphragm blocks the lumen while the bowel wall stays intact, is the most frequent jejunoileal atresia. The fibrous-cord, mesenteric-defect, apple-peel, and multiple-atresia types are less common.'),
('810ce40c-63f3-52a2-8e81-5fb961170cf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Apple-peel atresia (Type IIIb) involves:', '["Colonic atresia resulting in decreased functional activity","Multiple atresias accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proximal jejunal atresia with the distal bowel wrapped around a single mesenteric vessel","Mucosal atresia alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Isolated ileal atresia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal jejunal atresia with the distal bowel wrapped around a single mesenteric vessel', 'Apple-peel atresia (type IIIb) is a proximal jejunal atresia in which the distal bowel spirals around a single remaining mesenteric vessel, producing an apple-peel appearance. The other options describe different atresia patterns.'),
('b98e8a07-5ecb-5564-aa14-d8bd5ad5715c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Jejunoileal atresia is thought to result from:', '["In utero vascular accident (mesenteric ischemia)","Maternal infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Environmental toxin mediated by specific receptor activation","Failed recanalization without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Genetic mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In utero vascular accident (mesenteric ischemia)', 'Jejunoileal atresia follows an in-utero vascular accident that infarcts a segment of fetal bowel, which then resorbs. Failed recanalization is the cause of duodenal atresia rather than jejunoileal atresia, and infection, toxins, and mutations are not implicated.'),
('9d6573bd-5bd5-5728-9797-c63b69397cdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is meconium ileus?', '["Meconium plug syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstruction of the distal ileus by thick tenacious meconium most commonly due to cystic fibrosis","Intussusception mediated by specific receptor activation","Duodenal atresia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hirschsprung disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstruction of the distal ileus by thick tenacious meconium most commonly due to cystic fibrosis', 'Meconium ileus obstructs the distal ileum with thick, tenacious meconium, and most affected infants have cystic fibrosis. Meconium plug syndrome, intussusception, duodenal atresia, and Hirschsprung disease are distinct disorders.'),
('7c9fca22-e472-5e83-b947-38ad6094d5dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of meconium ileus?', '["Colonic atresia","Necrotizing enterocolitis","Meconium plug syndrome","Cystic fibrosis (CFTR mutation)","Prematurity"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic fibrosis (CFTR mutation)', 'Cystic fibrosis, caused by CFTR mutation, underlies almost all cases of meconium ileus because abnormal secretions produce inspissated meconium. Colonic atresia, necrotizing enterocolitis, meconium plug syndrome, and prematurity are not the principal cause.'),
('ff30a74d-f5f0-5405-822c-71237009226d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is microcolon?', '["A duplicated colon resulting in decreased functional activity","A malrotated colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A redundant colon mediated by specific receptor activation","A small-caliber colon due to disuse (seen distal to a small bowel obstruction in utero)","A congenitally short colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small-caliber colon due to disuse (seen distal to a small bowel obstruction in utero)', 'Microcolon is a small-caliber colon that never received meconium because it lies distal to an in-utero small bowel obstruction, so it is a disuse phenomenon. Duplication, malrotation, redundancy, and congenital shortening do not produce this appearance.'),
('d4b339ae-622a-50e9-bf73-036b46663443'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is colonic atresia?', '["Functional obstruction of the colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Microcolon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atresia of the colon much less common than small bowel atresia","Meconium plug syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hirschsprung disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atresia of the colon much less common than small bowel atresia', 'Colonic atresia is a genuine atresia of the colon and is far less common than small bowel atresia. Functional obstruction, microcolon, meconium plug syndrome, and Hirschsprung disease are not atresias of the colon.'),
('0c459304-0571-5686-9aef-52b3fd54cbfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common site of colonic atresia?', '["Sigmoid colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Ascending colon and transverse colon","Cecum mediated by specific receptor activation","Descending colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending colon and transverse colon', 'Colonic atresia is most often located in the ascending and transverse colon. Involvement of the cecum, descending colon, sigmoid, or rectum is distinctly uncommon.'),
('e64e4664-3ab2-5a8c-8755-02eda70e8873'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is meconium plug syndrome?', '["Necrotizing enterocolitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Volvulus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cystic fibrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Hirschsprung disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obstruction by a column of meconium in the colon of a healthy infant often with functional immaturity"]'::jsonb, 'Obstruction by a column of meconium in the colon of a healthy infant often with functional immaturity', 'Meconium plug syndrome obstructs the colon of an otherwise healthy newborn with a column of meconium, usually reflecting transient functional immaturity of the bowel. NEC, volvulus, cystic fibrosis, and Hirschsprung disease have different mechanisms.'),
('e418706f-2b76-53dd-91e3-1303c42d5b7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of neonatal intestinal obstruction?', '["Hirschsprung disease","Malrotation","Jejunoileal atresia","Meconium ileus","Duodenal atresia"]'::jsonb, 'Meconium ileus', 'Meconium ileus is the most common cause of neonatal intestinal obstruction. Hirschsprung disease, malrotation, jejunoileal atresia, and duodenal atresia follow in frequency.'),
('aefe7403-7467-57f5-81de-124fb6cd3db7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC)?', '["A meconium disorder resulting in decreased functional activity","An acquired inflammatory necrosis of the bowel in premature infants","A vascular malformation mediated by specific receptor activation","A tumor of the bowel without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A congenital bowel anomaly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An acquired inflammatory necrosis of the bowel in premature infants', 'NEC is an acquired disease of premature infants in which the bowel undergoes inflammatory necrosis, often after ischemic injury and bacterial invasion. It is not congenital and is not a meconium disorder, vascular malformation, or tumor.'),
('cae8a1a9-88b1-5dc6-b051-ec32a97915e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most important risk factor for NEC?', '["Hypoxic-ischemic insult","Prematurity (especially <32 weeks)","Umbilical catheterization","Polycythemia","Formula feeding"]'::jsonb, 'Prematurity (especially <32 weeks)', 'Prematurity, especially before 32 weeks, is the dominant risk factor for NEC because the immature bowel has impaired barrier function and immunity. Hypoxic-ischemic insult, catheters, polycythemia, and formula feeding are additive but secondary.'),
('c86aab19-ecec-5d53-89fc-7154bad9d827'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the classic radiographic findings of NEC?', '["Free air under diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstruction pattern accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dilated bowel loops mediated by specific receptor activation","Air-fluid levels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumatosis intestinalis (gas in the bowel wall) portal venous gas and pneumoperitoneum"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumatosis intestinalis (gas in the bowel wall) portal venous gas and pneumoperitoneum', 'The hallmark radiographic findings of NEC are pneumatosis intestinalis (intramural gas), portal venous gas, and pneumoperitoneum when perforation occurs. Free air, obstruction, dilated loops, and air-fluid levels alone are nonspecific.'),
('25c234d5-a508-58f6-b031-72390cc41505'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of short bowel syndrome in children?', '["Gastroschisis resulting in decreased functional activity","Volvulus from malrotation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hirschsprung disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Small bowel atresia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) requiring massive resection"]'::jsonb, 'Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) requiring massive resection', 'NEC severe enough to require massive bowel resection is the leading cause of short bowel syndrome in children. Gastroschisis, volvulus, Hirschsprung disease, and small bowel atresia contribute fewer cases.'),
('d4c93aa2-4aae-50d9-821c-90e512943d80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of short bowel syndrome in adults?', '["Volvulus resulting in decreased functional activity","Crohn disease requiring multiple resections","Mesenteric ischemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor resection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crohn disease requiring multiple resections', 'In adults repeated surgical resections for Crohn disease are the most common cause of short bowel syndrome. Volvulus, mesenteric ischemia, trauma, and tumor resection are less frequent causes.'),
('04f75fa8-44ef-5fc5-86ae-195b282e2052'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common anatomic short bowel adaptation?', '["Bowel elongation resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased blood flow accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased villus height and crypt depth (mucosal hyperplasia)","Colonic lengthening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuronal hyperplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased villus height and crypt depth (mucosal hyperplasia)', 'The dominant adaptive response of the remnant bowel is mucosal hyperplasia, with taller villi and deeper crypts that expand the absorptive surface. Elongation, increased blood flow, colonic lengthening, and neuronal hyperplasia are not the principal anatomic adaptation.'),
('79bca71a-f746-57d7-9837-afb9031d1b04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of intestinal failure in children?', '["Short bowel syndrome","Tuffing enteropathy","Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction","Inflammatory bowel disease","Microvillus inclusion disease"]'::jsonb, 'Short bowel syndrome', 'Short bowel syndrome is the most common cause of intestinal failure in children. Tuffing enteropathy, chronic pseudo-obstruction, inflammatory bowel disease, and microvillus inclusion disease are much rarer causes.'),
('e2e636c1-e7ed-51f8-99db-b7b89317c3ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of parenteral nutrition in children with short bowel syndrome?', '["Metabolic bone disease","Catheter-related bloodstream infection","Cholestatic liver disease","Nephrocalcinosis","Venous thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 'Catheter-related bloodstream infection', 'Catheter-related bloodstream infection is the most frequent complication of long-term parenteral nutrition in children with short bowel syndrome. Cholestatic liver disease, metabolic bone disease, nephrocalcinosis, and venous thrombosis occur but less often.'),
('4328f987-34e7-5acf-a27f-a164cb5424f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of intestinal transplant in children?', '["Intestinal atresia","Intestinal failure from motility disorders","Gastroschisis","Short bowel syndrome","NEC"]'::jsonb, 'Short bowel syndrome', 'Short bowel syndrome is the most common reason children require intestinal transplantation. Intestinal atresia, motility disorders, gastroschisis, and NEC contribute cases but are less frequent indications.'),
('7f145d0f-5a6f-583d-b304-db81e04ae352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in children without previous surgery?', '["Appendicitis","Intussusception","Meckel''s diverticulum","Malrotation with volvulus","Incarcerated inguinal hernia"]'::jsonb, 'Intussusception', 'In children who have never had surgery, intussusception is the most common cause of intestinal obstruction. Appendicitis, Meckel diverticulum, malrotation with volvulus, and incarcerated inguinal hernia are less common causes.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('8b1d80e6-4b06-5a28-a98f-a6bb3edae0e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of intussusception in children?', '["Ileocecocolic resulting in decreased functional activity","Appendiceal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ileocolic (ileum telescopes into cecum/colon)","Ileoileal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colocolic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ileocolic (ileum telescopes into cecum/colon)', 'Ileocolic intussusception, in which the terminal ileum telescopes into the cecum and colon, accounts for the large majority of childhood intussusceptions. Ileoileal, colocolic, and appendiceal types are much less common.'),
('8e2fe97c-24e3-5993-9c0b-72d78908e3da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common lead point for intussusception in children?', '["Duplication cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Intestinal polyp accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meckel''s diverticulum mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic (hypertrophied Peyer''s patches from viral infection)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (hypertrophied Peyer''s patches from viral infection)', 'In children the usual lead point is functional rather than anatomic, with hypertrophied Peyer''s patches after a viral infection acting as the trigger for intussusception. Polyps, duplications, and Meckel''s diverticulum are infrequent anatomic lead points in this age group.'),
('60187946-e97d-5ae8-b758-6ae3be908a99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common pathologic lead point for intussusception in children?', '["Meckel''s diverticulum","Duplication cyst","Intestinal polyp (Peutz-Jeghers)","Lymphoma","Lipoma"]'::jsonb, 'Meckel''s diverticulum', 'When a genuine anatomic lead point is found in a child with intussusception, Meckel''s diverticulum is the most common one, ahead of polyps, duplications, and lymphoma. Overall, however, most childhood intussusceptions remain idiopathic without a lead point.'),
('f61a34a2-299e-5ef4-883e-850c14b5e25f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of intussusception in adults?', '["Idiopathic resulting in decreased functional activity","Postoperative accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pathologic lead point (tumor polyp Meckel''s diverticulum)"]'::jsonb, 'Pathologic lead point (tumor polyp Meckel''s diverticulum)', 'Adult intussusception is usually caused by a structural lead point such as a tumor, polyp, or Meckel''s diverticulum. Idiopathic cases, so common in children, and postoperative or traumatic causes are unusual in adults.'),
('138d85c2-eb0e-50e1-a6c5-79641d02b507'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of intussusception in adults?', '["Ileocolic","Appendiceal","Colocolic","Enteroenteric (small bowel)","Ileoileal"]'::jsonb, 'Enteroenteric (small bowel)', 'In adults intussusception most often involves the small bowel (enteroenteric), commonly from a benign lead point such as a lipoma or postoperative adhesion. Ileocolic intussusception, the typical childhood pattern, is less frequent in adults.'),
('5fad715c-a963-57f9-a2bc-bf3fb29ba12e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the best radiologic test to diagnose intussusception in children?', '["Abdominal X-ray resulting in decreased functional activity","CT scan accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Barium enema mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper GI series without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ultrasound (target sign or pseudokidney sign)"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound (target sign or pseudokidney sign)', 'Ultrasound is the best initial study and shows the classic target or pseudokidney sign in intussusception. Barium or air enema is reserved mainly for therapeutic reduction once the diagnosis is made.'),
('87903789-479c-5a98-96ce-c2bfff171e97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of appendicitis?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Ischemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obstruction of the appendiceal lumen (by fecalith lymphoid hyperplasia or foreign body)","Inflammation from adjacent organs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstruction of the appendiceal lumen (by fecalith lymphoid hyperplasia or foreign body)', 'Appendicitis begins with obstruction of the appendiceal lumen, usually by a fecalith, lymphoid hyperplasia, or a foreign body, which raises intraluminal pressure and causes ischemia and infection. Spread from adjacent organs or primary infection alone does not initiate the disease.'),
('af00405d-89b8-5773-a929-912b8e4c9720'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common position of the appendix?', '["Retrocecal (behind the cecum)","Preileal","Pelvic (descending into the pelvis)","Subcecal","Postileal"]'::jsonb, 'Retrocecal (behind the cecum)', 'The appendix most often lies retrocecally, behind the cecum, in roughly two-thirds of individuals. Pelvic, subcecal, preileal, and postileal positions are less frequent.'),
('51b7fa8c-3bfb-5dd9-afc0-18d51c508029'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of appendicitis?', '["Pylephlebitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Gangrenous appendicitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fecal fistula mediated by specific receptor activation","Sepsis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Perforation with periappendiceal abscess"]'::jsonb, 'Perforation with periappendiceal abscess', 'Perforation with periappendiceal abscess is the major complication of appendicitis and the main source of morbidity. Gangrene precedes perforation, while sepsis and pylephlebitis are less common outcomes.'),
('3be9c73d-bf0d-548f-81d3-11de15bf0cab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of right lower quadrant pain in children?', '["Mesenteric adenitis","Ovarian pathology","Appendicitis","Gastroenteritis","Intussusception"]'::jsonb, 'Appendicitis', 'Appendicitis is the most common surgical cause of right lower quadrant pain in children and demands timely diagnosis. Mesenteric adenitis, gastroenteritis, and ovarian pathology are important non-surgical mimics.'),
('9fadf6ff-1b34-51e3-a380-a4694a9f71fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of mesenteric adenitis?', '["Viral infection (especially adenovirus and enterovirus)","Fungal infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parasitic infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Idiopathic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Viral infection (especially adenovirus and enterovirus)', 'Mesenteric adenitis is most often triggered by viral infection, particularly adenovirus and enterovirus, which cause reactive enlargement of the ileocecal mesenteric lymph nodes. It commonly mimics appendicitis with right lower quadrant pain.'),
('6863bb98-0202-5aeb-b1d2-d91da77fdbad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of primary peritonitis in children?', '["Pancreatitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Appendicitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (often pneumococcus or streptococcus in nephrotic syndrome)","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tuberculosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (often pneumococcus or streptococcus in nephrotic syndrome)', 'Primary (spontaneous) peritonitis in children typically complicates nephrotic syndrome, with pneumococcus and streptococcus as the usual organisms. Appendicitis produces secondary peritonitis, which is a separate mechanism.'),
('5611bee6-5593-54a3-ad16-0b12a3b8b4c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of secondary peritonitis in children?', '["Appendicitis (ruptured appendix)","Volvulus","NEC","Perforated ulcer","Traumatic bowel perforation"]'::jsonb, 'Appendicitis (ruptured appendix)', 'Secondary peritonitis in children follows intra-abdominal perforation, and a ruptured appendix is the most common source. Volvulus, necrotizing enterocolitis, perforated ulcer, and traumatic perforation are less frequent causes.'),
('fa925a0f-9850-5edd-ac1e-e382dbf092cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of pancreatitis in children?', '["Drug-induced","Mumps infection","Trauma (blunt abdominal injury)","Idiopathic","Gallstones"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma (blunt abdominal injury)', 'Blunt abdominal trauma is the leading cause of acute pancreatitis in children, often from bicycle handlebar or other abdominal injuries. Gallstones and mumps are much less common causes in childhood.'),
('d836e8e5-760b-5e4f-958e-de19c7525f4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of pancreatitis in adults?', '["Hypertriglyceridemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ERCP mediated by specific receptor activation","Medications without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gallstones (most common) followed by alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'Gallstones (most common) followed by alcohol', 'In adults, gallstones are the most common cause of acute pancreatitis, with alcohol the second most common. Hypertriglyceridemia, ERCP, and medications are less frequent triggers.'),
('1c3585dd-8068-5073-8df8-ab78420b3d5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of chronic pancreatitis in adults?', '["Idiopathic","Chronic alcohol abuse","Hypertriglyceridemia","Gallstones","Cystic fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic alcohol abuse', 'Chronic alcohol abuse is the predominant cause of chronic pancreatitis in adults, producing repeated injury and progressive fibrosis. Gallstones typically drive acute rather than chronic pancreatitis.'),
('c834b53d-1ebf-538d-a1a3-4161f60be5ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in children?', '["Chronic pancreatitis","Cystic fibrosis","Protein-calorie malnutrition","Pancreatic agenesis","Shwachman-Diamond syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic fibrosis', 'Cystic fibrosis is the most common cause of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in children because inspissated secretions obstruct pancreatic ducts and destroy acini. Shwachman-Diamond syndrome is a rarer cause.'),
('7ac04a59-a7eb-5921-9301-6dd10c543114'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of obstructive jaundice in the newborn?', '["Inspissated bile syndrome","Neonatal hepatitis syndrome","Choledochal cyst","Biliary atresia","Alagille syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Biliary atresia', 'Biliary atresia is the most common cause of obstructive jaundice with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia in newborns and the leading reason for pediatric liver transplantation. Neonatal hepatitis is the main differential diagnosis.'),
('2758d64d-58e3-5552-8258-c4300cca7618'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common form of biliary atresia?', '["Type 0 (correctable type)","Type IV (intrahepatic)","Type III (biliary atresia at the porta hepatis most common)","Type I (common bile duct involvement)","Type II (hepatic duct involvement)"]'::jsonb, 'Type III (biliary atresia at the porta hepatis most common)', 'Type III biliary atresia, with obstruction at the porta hepatis, is the most common form and requires Kasai portoenterostomy. The rarer correctable types involve more distal segments of the extrahepatic bile ducts.'),
('2875b798-ff4b-5713-80eb-3d05a2368519'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the Kasai procedure (hepatic portoenterostomy)?', '["Surgical creation of a biliary drainage by anastomosing the porta hepatis to the jejunum","Removal of the gallbladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Liver transplant mediated by specific receptor activation","Repair of the bile duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Drainage of choledochal cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical creation of a biliary drainage by anastomosing the porta hepatis to the jejunum', 'The Kasai procedure (hepatic portoenterostomy) restores bile drainage by anastomosing the porta hepatis to a Roux-en-Y loop of jejunum. It does not remove the gallbladder or involve transplantation.'),
('7a76783d-42bc-553b-80b6-883415437320'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most important predictor of success of the Kasai procedure?', '["Type of biliary atresia resulting in decreased functional activity","Age at surgery (best if performed before 60 days of age)","Surgeon experience mediated by specific receptor activation","Size of the bile duct remnants without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Presence of cirrhosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Age at surgery (best if performed before 60 days of age)', 'Outcome of the Kasai procedure depends most heavily on the age at surgery, with the best results when it is performed before 60 days of age. Delay allows progressive biliary cirrhosis to become irreversible.'),
('c7529920-da73-5dc1-9f0a-7d45fe164da7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is neonatal hepatitis syndrome?', '["Nonspecific hepatic inflammation in the newborn with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","A specific viral hepatitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A congenital infection mediated by specific receptor activation","A metabolic disorder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A toxic reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nonspecific hepatic inflammation in the newborn with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia', 'Neonatal hepatitis syndrome is a nonspecific cholestatic inflammatory picture in newborns with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia rather than a single disease. It is a clinicopathologic diagnosis encompassing many underlying causes.'),
('8e377b1a-5ce9-5f90-b381-c32fe7108289'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of neonatal hepatitis syndrome?', '["Idiopathic (no specific cause identified in about 30-40%)","Toxoplasmosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cytomegalovirus mediated by specific receptor activation","Rubella without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (no specific cause identified in about 30-40%)', 'About a third of neonatal hepatitis syndrome cases have no identifiable cause. Recognizable infections such as CMV and toxoplasmosis and metabolic diseases like alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency explain the remainder.'),
('ea3d1073-1a32-5bc1-bb9c-4d66d034853a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is a choledochal cyst?', '["A vascular malformation resulting in decreased functional activity","Inflammation of the bile duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A tumor of the bile duct mediated by specific receptor activation","A stone in the bile duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A congenital dilation of the extrahepatic and/or intrahepatic bile ducts"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital dilation of the extrahepatic and/or intrahepatic bile ducts', 'A choledochal cyst is a congenital dilation of the extrahepatic and/or intrahepatic bile ducts that can present with jaundice, an abdominal mass, and pain. It predisposes to cholangitis and cholangiocarcinoma.'),
('059257c0-5577-5d18-87d4-d38b9f5f8b62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of choledochal cyst?', '["Type V (Caroli disease)","Type II (diverticulum of common bile duct)","Type I (fusiform dilation of the common bile duct)","Type III (choledochocele)","Type IV (multiple intra and extrahepatic cysts)"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (fusiform dilation of the common bile duct)', 'Type I choledochal cyst, a fusiform dilation of the common bile duct, is the most common type. Caroli disease (Type V) and choledochocele (Type III) are much rarer.'),
('8b4d5d1b-f733-5149-8372-4b586083076d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Caroli disease (Type V choledochal cyst) involves:', '["Multiple cystic dilations of the intrahepatic bile ducts with an increased risk of cholangitis and cholangiocarcinoma","Choledochocele accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fusiform dilation of the common bile duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Diverticulum of the bile duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cyst of the cystic duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple cystic dilations of the intrahepatic bile ducts with an increased risk of cholangitis and cholangiocarcinoma', 'Caroli disease consists of multiple cystic dilations of the intrahepatic bile ducts, predisposing to recurrent cholangitis and cholangiocarcinoma. It is distinct from the fusiform extrahepatic dilation of Type I choledochal cyst.'),
('fd9cf6dd-2db6-5b5a-a728-cd1b9963c24a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of choledochal cyst?', '["Pancreatitis","Cholangitis (recurrent infection)","Rupture","Cholangiocarcinoma","Obstructive jaundice"]'::jsonb, 'Cholangitis (recurrent infection)', 'Recurrent cholangitis from bile stasis is the most common complication of a choledochal cyst. Malignant transformation to cholangiocarcinoma, although feared, is less common.'),
('af1ec0f3-3b11-5868-b570-8e6789169366'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Caroli syndrome?', '["Caroli disease alone","Primary sclerosing cholangitis","Caroli disease with congenital hepatic fibrosis","Fusiform dilation alone","Polycystic liver disease"]'::jsonb, 'Caroli disease with congenital hepatic fibrosis', 'Caroli syndrome combines Caroli disease with congenital hepatic fibrosis, so portal hypertension accompanies the intrahepatic bile duct dilation. Caroli disease alone does not include hepatic fibrosis.'),
('020df6b0-8033-5aae-8898-9268ee4fba3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Alagille syndrome?', '["A type of biliary atresia resulting in decreased functional activity","A genetic disorder with paucity of intrahepatic bile ducts associated with cardiac skeletal ocular and facial anomalies","A choledochal cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","A neonatal hepatitis variant without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A metabolic liver disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A genetic disorder with paucity of intrahepatic bile ducts associated with cardiac skeletal ocular and facial anomalies', 'Alagille syndrome is a genetic cholestatic disorder characterized by paucity of intrahepatic bile ducts together with cardiac, skeletal, ocular, and facial anomalies. It is not a form of biliary atresia.'),
('610185db-0e2b-55e0-a33f-2cd5c810965b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of chronic liver disease in children?', '["Biliary atresia","Wilson disease","Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency","Hepatitis B","Autoimmune hepatitis"]'::jsonb, 'Biliary atresia', 'Biliary atresia is the most common cause of chronic liver disease in children and a leading indication for liver transplantation. Untreated, the progressive cholangiopathy leads to cirrhosis.'),
('5aabe6bc-d04f-546b-9035-adfc11a8c839'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common indication for liver transplantation in children?', '["Hepatoblastoma","Metabolic liver disease","Acute liver failure","Alagille syndrome","Biliary atresia"]'::jsonb, 'Biliary atresia', 'Biliary atresia is the most common indication for pediatric liver transplantation when portoenterostomy fails or is performed too late. Metabolic liver disease and hepatoblastoma are less frequent indications.'),
('b25cbf50-1a32-5529-9660-a5cfd8b3f164'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute liver failure in children?', '["Viral hepatitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Drug-induced (acetaminophen overdose) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Indeterminate mediated by specific receptor activation","Autoimmune hepatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metabolic disorders (e.g. tyrosinemia Wilson disease)"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolic disorders (e.g. tyrosinemia Wilson disease)', 'In children, metabolic disorders such as tyrosinemia and Wilson disease are the most frequently identified causes of acute liver failure. Drug-induced injury, so common in adults, is less typical in children.'),
('b7072453-4f0f-5cac-b69a-d88cb5e4ce3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute liver failure in adults?', '["Viral hepatitis (AEB)","Drug-induced liver injury","Ischemic hepatitis","Autoimmune hepatitis","Acetaminophen (paracetamol) overdose"]'::jsonb, 'Acetaminophen (paracetamol) overdose', 'Acetaminophen (paracetamol) overdose is the leading cause of acute liver failure in adults because its toxic metabolite depletes glutathione and damages hepatocytes. Other drugs and viral hepatitis are less common causes.'),
('381f687e-0ded-57c3-87d4-8bfa61233e64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of cirrhosis in adults?', '["Primary biliary cholangitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatitis C (historically) now MASLD (metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease) and alcohol","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic hepatitis B without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alcohol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatitis C (historically) now MASLD (metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease) and alcohol', 'Historically hepatitis C was the leading cause of cirrhosis in adults, but metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) and alcohol have now become the predominant causes. The option capturing this temporal shift is the best answer.'),
('f4c068c7-2058-5050-8514-d9a1e4b9cd3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of portal hypertension in children?', '["Idiopathic portal hypertension","Extrahepatic portal vein thrombosis (EHPVT)","Schistosomiasis","Biliary atresia with cirrhosis","Hepatic vein obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Extrahepatic portal vein thrombosis (EHPVT)', 'Extrahepatic portal vein thrombosis is the most common cause of portal hypertension in children. In adults, cirrhosis dominates as the cause of portal hypertension.'),
('9e754afa-4ab7-5efd-8dec-3bbe65f1d29f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of portal hypertension in adults?', '["Idiopathic non-cirrhotic portal hypertension","Hepatic vein thrombosis (Budd-Chiari)","Cirrhosis (from any cause)","Schistosomiasis","Portal vein thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 'Cirrhosis (from any cause)', 'Cirrhosis from any cause is the most common basis of portal hypertension in adults, as fibrosis raises sinusoidal resistance and creates portosystemic shunting. Prehepatic and posthepatic causes are much less frequent.'),
('66fb8b91-5d74-557d-bfdb-ffd2cfe6648b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of upper GI bleeding in children?', '["Esophagitis","Peptic ulcer disease","Dieulafoy lesion","Mallory-Weiss tear","Esophageal varices"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal varices', 'Esophageal varices are the most common cause of significant upper GI bleeding in children, usually in the setting of portal hypertension. Esophagitis and peptic ulcer disease occur but are less common.'),
('475078bf-b984-51c7-b1ef-f1427e95aeb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of upper GI bleeding in adults?', '["Mallory-Weiss tear resulting in decreased functional activity","Esophagitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gastritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Peptic ulcer disease (gastric or duodenal ulcer)","Esophageal varices leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peptic ulcer disease (gastric or duodenal ulcer)', 'Peptic ulcer disease (gastric or duodenal ulcer) is the most common cause of upper GI bleeding in adults. Esophageal varices and Mallory-Weiss tears are important but less frequent sources.'),
('8d8a6bba-060b-5698-8b3f-f61e720f6645'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of lower GI bleeding in children?', '["Juvenile polyps","Intussusception","Anal fissure","Inflammatory bowel disease","Meckel''s diverticulum"]'::jsonb, 'Juvenile polyps', 'Juvenile polyps are the most common cause of painless rectal bleeding and of lower GI bleeding in children. Meckel''s diverticulum is a classic but less frequent source of significant bleeding in this age group.'),
('faad175c-0f9a-54c0-a0f0-f781b688f5ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common colonic polyp in children?', '["Inflammatory polyp","Adenomatous polyp","Lymphoid polyp","Peutz-Jeghers polyp","Juvenile polyp (hamartomatous)"]'::jsonb, 'Juvenile polyp (hamartomatous)', 'Juvenile (hamartomatous) polyps are the most common colonic polyps in children, typically appearing in the first decade. They are usually solitary, benign, and self-limited.'),
('650c1891-9fb5-5f05-8353-3af405929bea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is juvenile polyposis syndrome?', '["Cronkhite-Canada syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A single juvenile polyp mediated by specific receptor activation","A condition with multiple juvenile polyps and increased risk of colorectal cancer","Colonic ganglioneuromatosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A condition with multiple juvenile polyps and increased risk of colorectal cancer', 'Juvenile polyposis syndrome is defined by multiple juvenile polyps throughout the gastrointestinal tract and carries an increased risk of colorectal cancer. It is distinct from a single sporadic juvenile polyp.'),
('a1c77873-035f-5b7c-a3ff-1117a4b41905'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Peutz-Jeghers syndrome?', '["Cronkhite-Canada syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Lynch syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A hamartomatous polyposis with mucocutaneous pigmentation and increased cancer risk","A juvenile polyposis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A familial adenomatous polyposis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A hamartomatous polyposis with mucocutaneous pigmentation and increased cancer risk', 'Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is a hamartomatous polyposis associated with mucocutaneous pigmentation and an elevated risk of gastrointestinal and extraintestinal cancers. It differs from FAP and Lynch syndrome.'),
('bc971e4b-15c5-5d9d-bdd4-848c3b03aa81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of colonic polyp in adults?', '["Adenomatous polyp (tubular tubulovillous villous)","Hyperplastic polyp (most common)","JJuvenile polyp","Serrated polyp","Inflammatory polyp"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperplastic polyp (most common)', 'Hyperplastic polyps are the most common type of colonic polyp in adults, although most are small and benign. Adenomatous polyps are the most common clinically significant premalignant polyps.'),
('add2986f-8217-5cc0-9572-7f898cd6cf29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common site of colorectal cancer in the US?', '["Left colon (sigmoid and descending)","Right colon (cecum and ascending)","Splenic flexure","Rectum","Transverse colon"]'::jsonb, 'Left colon (sigmoid and descending)', 'Colorectal cancer in the US most commonly arises in the left colon, particularly the sigmoid and descending segments. Right-sided cancers are increasingly recognized but remain less frequent overall.'),
('bfc005e9-1cd6-54b4-844d-cdcfb1d3f91b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common histologic type of colorectal cancer?', '["Mucinous adenocarcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma (conventional)","Signet-ring cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Adenocarcinoma (conventional)', 'Conventional adenocarcinoma accounts for about 95% of colorectal cancers. Mucinous and signet-ring cell variants are uncommon and generally carry a worse prognosis.'),
('0ec25df1-f160-5cd7-9eca-670944e6a349'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common inherited colorectal cancer syndrome?', '["Juvenile polyposis syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MUTYH-associated polyposis mediated by specific receptor activation","Peutz-Jeghers syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lynch syndrome (hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer HNPCC)"]'::jsonb, 'Lynch syndrome (hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer HNPCC)', 'Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) is the most common inherited colorectal cancer syndrome, caused by defective DNA mismatch repair and predisposing to right-sided colon and extracolonic cancers. FAP is less common and usually presents earlier.'),
('f07926ea-6ace-5ba7-aad2-480a8bc9a168'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of death from colorectal cancer?', '["Local recurrence resulting in decreased functional activity","Perforation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metastatic disease (especially liver metastases)","Obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bleeding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metastatic disease (especially liver metastases)', 'Most deaths from colorectal cancer result from metastatic disease, especially liver metastases. Local recurrence, obstruction, and bleeding are important complications but are less often fatal.'),
('7ed1a696-cc63-5ae7-a232-79f7ebdb694a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common site of metastasis from colorectal cancer?', '["Bone","Liver","Peritoneum","Lung","Brain"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'The liver is the most common site of colorectal cancer metastasis because portal venous drainage carries tumor cells directly from the bowel to the liver. The lung is the second most common site.'),
('2dcd1b45-d0a8-58af-a88f-ef4728c9d95c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of bowel obstruction in the adult population?', '["Colorectal cancer","Adhesions from previous surgery","Crohn disease","Volvulus","Hernia (incarcerated inguinal)"]'::jsonb, 'Adhesions from previous surgery', 'Adhesions from previous abdominal surgery are the most common cause of bowel obstruction in the general adult population. Colorectal cancer is the leading cause of large bowel obstruction, while hernia predominates when there is no prior laparotomy.'),
('81f3de54-2160-53c7-ac45-6d5c7d6c0caa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of small bowel obstruction in the absence of prior surgery?', '["Tumor (metastatic or primary) resulting in decreased functional activity","Intussusception accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Crohn disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Volvulus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hernia (incarcerated inguinal or femoral)"]'::jsonb, 'Hernia (incarcerated inguinal or femoral)', 'In the absence of prior surgery, an incarcerated inguinal or femoral hernia is the most common cause of small bowel obstruction. Adhesions remain the overall leading cause but require a history of laparotomy.'),
('e3afdd28-deba-55ab-b7f7-b73778c4f4a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of colonic obstruction in adults?', '["Fecal impaction","Pseudobstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)","Volvulus (sigmoid or cecal)","Colorectal cancer","Diverticulitis"]'::jsonb, 'Colorectal cancer', 'Colorectal cancer is the leading cause of large-bowel obstruction in adults, with most tumors arising in the sigmoid colon. Fecal impaction and volvulus are less frequent causes, and Ogilvie syndrome is a functional rather than mechanical obstruction.'),
('b02e89da-e6cb-5b42-85f3-77ed8499cff3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of sigmoid volvulus?', '["Chronic constipation (most common in elderly and debilitated patients)","Congenital malrotation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chagas disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Hirschsprung disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pregnancy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic constipation (most common in elderly and debilitated patients)', 'Sigmoid volvulus typically follows chronic constipation, which produces a redundant, elongated sigmoid that twists around its mesentery, especially in elderly and debilitated patients. The other options relate to congenital, parasitic, or transient conditions not responsible for the usual adult presentation.'),
('9df94b73-68ac-5e62-9cd1-71022427d499'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of cecal volvulus?', '["Prior abdominal surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","Pregnancy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic constipation mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal mobility of the right colon (failure of normal fixation)","Colonic obstruction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal mobility of the right colon (failure of normal fixation)', 'Cecal volvulus results from a mobile right colon that fails to fix to the posterior abdominal wall, allowing the cecum to twist around its mesentery. This differs from sigmoid volvulus, which is more often linked to chronic constipation.'),
('078c0a74-0709-53dc-877a-67da32437b9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Hirschsprung disease enterocolitis?', '["Gastroenteritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Inflammatory bowel disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urinary tract infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Appendicitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A life-threatening complication of Hirschsprung disease with inflammation and dilation of the proximal colon"]'::jsonb, 'A life-threatening complication of Hirschsprung disease with inflammation and dilation of the proximal colon', 'Hirschsprung disease enterocolitis is an acute, life-threatening inflammation with dilation of the colon proximal to the aganglionic segment, carrying high morbidity and mortality. It is not a routine infection, inflammatory bowel disease, urinary infection, or appendicitis.'),
('73e9bdc9-0768-579c-924c-45678e32f61d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of death in children with Hirschsprung disease?', '["Sepsis","Enterocolitis","Pobstructive complications","Postoperative complications","Malnutrition"]'::jsonb, 'Enterocolitis', 'Enterocolitis is the leading cause of death in Hirschsprung disease, arising from bacterial overgrowth and impaired mucosal barrier in the dilated proximal colon. Sepsis and postoperative problems are usually secondary to this condition rather than independent causes of death.'),
('8450d37b-efda-59f1-a3f8-298d5224a4eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute abdomen in pregnancy?', '["Pancreatitis","Placental abruption","Cholecystitis","Adnexal torsion","Appendicitis"]'::jsonb, 'Appendicitis', 'Appendicitis is the most common surgical cause of the acute abdomen during pregnancy, although diagnosis is complicated by upward displacement of the appendix by the gravid uterus. It occurs far more often than cholecystitis, torsion, or pancreatitis in pregnancy.'),
('230a5eb9-989b-5054-bd21-56dbef37d68f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of jaundice during pregnancy?', '["HELLP syndrome","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy","Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy","Viral hepatitis","Cholelithiasis"]'::jsonb, 'Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy', 'Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy is the most frequent cause of jaundice in pregnancy, resulting from impaired bile flow under estrogen influence and presenting with pruritus. Viral hepatitis and gallstone disease are less common, while HELLP syndrome and fatty liver are rarer emergencies.'),
('88cf4e66-11cd-5782-9448-7e70d01e085e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common benign liver tumor?', '["Simple cyst","Hemangioma (cavernous hemangioma)","Focal nodular hyperplasia (FNH)","Fibroma","Hepatic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 'Hemangioma (cavernous hemangioma)', 'Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor, seen most often in women and generally discovered incidentally on imaging. Simple cysts and focal nodular hyperplasia are far less frequent, and hepatic adenoma is uncommon.'),
('a20294d5-76b6-503e-8519-563d3fd7dc02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common benign hepatic tumor requiring intervention?', '["Biliary hamartoma","Cyst","FNH","Hemangioma","Hepatic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic adenoma', 'Hepatic adenoma is the benign hepatic tumor most likely to require intervention because of the risks of rupture, hemorrhage, and malignant transformation, especially with oral contraceptive use. Hemangiomas and FNH are usually managed conservatively.'),
('5be21bed-64f1-5f0b-b9da-3a78a1eca3fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of hepatic adenoma?', '["Anabolic steroid use resulting in decreased functional activity","Type I glycogen storage disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pregnancy mediated by specific receptor activation","Tamoxifen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oral contraceptive use (estrogen-containing)"]'::jsonb, 'Oral contraceptive use (estrogen-containing)', 'Estrogen-containing oral contraceptives are the leading cause of hepatic adenoma, promoting tumor growth that often regresses after stopping the drug. Androgens and type I glycogen storage disease account for only a minority of cases.'),
('33b9c035-09a3-52af-a7dc-c940947277ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common primary malignant liver tumor in adults?', '["Cholangiocarcinoma","Mixed hepatocellular-cholangiocarcinoma","Hepatoblastoma","Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)","Angiosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)', 'Hepatocellular carcinoma is the most common primary malignant liver tumor in adults, usually arising on a background of cirrhosis. Cholangiocarcinoma and angiosarcoma are much rarer primary liver malignancies.'),
('630be400-df78-5f68-ad04-96032001a2b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of hepatocellular carcinoma?', '["Second-line agent resulting in decreased functional activity","Alcohol-related cirrhosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cirrhosis (from any cause especially hepatitis BC alcohol MASLD)","Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatitis C leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cirrhosis (from any cause especially hepatitis BC alcohol MASLD)', 'Cirrhosis from any cause is the strongest and most common predisposing condition for hepatocellular carcinoma, with hepatitis B, C, alcohol, and MASLD as the underlying etiologies. It is the cirrhotic state itself, rather than a single virus or alcohol alone, that drives most cases.'),
('3e736749-23d6-5216-8273-91353296832f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common primary malignant liver tumor in children?', '["Hepatoblastoma","Rhabdoid tumor","Hepatocellular carcinoma","Angiosarcoma","Undifferentiated embryonal sarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatoblastoma', 'Hepatoblastoma is the most common primary malignant liver tumor in children, typically presenting before age 3 years with an abdominal mass and raised alpha-fetoprotein. Hepatocellular carcinoma and sarcomas are rare in the pediatric age group.'),
('3f7a51cc-3c2a-5555-abe6-27828d142d20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of death in children with biliary atresia even after Kasai procedure?', '["Cholangitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Malnutrition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","End-stage liver disease and complications of portal hypertension (variceal bleeding)","Hepatocellular carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sepsis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'End-stage liver disease and complications of portal hypertension (variceal bleeding)', 'Even after a successful Kasai portoenterostomy, most children with biliary atresia eventually die from end-stage liver disease and the complications of portal hypertension, chiefly variceal bleeding. Cholangitis and sepsis are important but less common causes of death.'),
('3a03ad3a-c4a1-5bb1-94d3-81ad6a18bdd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of portal hypertension after Kasai procedure?', '["Hepatic vein occlusion","Splenic vein thrombosis","Progressive cirrhosis and liver fibrosis","Prehepatic obstruction","Portal vein thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive cirrhosis and liver fibrosis', 'After the Kasai procedure, ongoing bile duct injury leads to progressive cirrhosis and liver fibrosis, which in turn produces portal hypertension. This intrahepatic mechanism is far more common than prehepatic obstruction or hepatic vein occlusion.'),
('0aeeed0c-633e-53f1-ba22-53f50b7eee33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of cirrhosis?', '["Variceal hemorrhage","Jaundice","Hepatic encephalopathy","Ascites","Hepatorenal syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Ascites', 'Ascites is the most frequent complication of cirrhosis, resulting from portal hypertension and renal sodium retention. Variceal hemorrhage and hepatic encephalopathy are serious but occur less often than ascites.'),
('3b42b968-7cdf-500b-a004-729d1c0b7937'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of ascites in adults?', '["Cirrhosis with portal hypertension","Peritoneal carcinomatosis","Heart failure","Tuberculosis","Nephrotic syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Cirrhosis with portal hypertension', 'Cirrhosis with portal hypertension is the most common cause of ascites in adults. Peritoneal carcinomatosis, heart failure, and nephrotic syndrome are comparatively infrequent causes of abdominal fluid accumulation.'),
('36f525f6-f433-57a0-9dee-fcd773cf634d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of cirrhotic ascites?', '["Hypoalbuminemia alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperaldosteronism alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Portal hypertension and sodium retention with hypoalbuminemia","Portal hypertension alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased hepatic lymph production alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension and sodium retention with hypoalbuminemia', 'Cirrhotic ascites develops from the combined effects of portal hypertension, renal sodium retention, and reduced albumin synthesis. No single mechanism alone, such as hypoalbuminemia or hyperaldosteronism, accounts for the fluid accumulation.'),
('1083bdd9-7f75-5aa8-ab39-768a3a1bca47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP) organism?', '["E. coli (most common) and Klebsiella pneumoniae","Enterococcus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anaerobic organisms mediated by specific receptor activation","Streptococcus pneumoniae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Candida leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli (most common) and Klebsiella pneumoniae', 'Escherichia coli and Klebsiella pneumoniae are the organisms most commonly cultured from spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, reflecting their dominance in the gut flora. Enterococcus, anaerobes, and fungi are less frequent isolates.'),
('0abc7f8a-6b47-54f6-9028-cbad3f80b40d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of mortality in patients with cirrhosis?', '["Infectious complications (pneumonia UTI peritonitis)","Hepatorenal syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hepatic encephalopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Variceal hemorrhage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","HCC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infectious complications (pneumonia UTI peritonitis)', 'Infectious complications, especially pneumonia, urinary tract infection, and spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, are the leading cause of death in cirrhosis. Variceal hemorrhage and hepatorenal syndrome are major but less frequent causes of mortality.'),
('887de4d2-faac-5adb-b77a-4dd53aac0829'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of hepatic encephalopathy?', '["Cirrhosis with portosystemic shunting (endogenous neurotoxins)","Urea cycle disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute liver failure mediated by specific receptor activation","Reye syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Toxin ingestion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cirrhosis with portosystemic shunting (endogenous neurotoxins)', 'Hepatic encephalopathy usually occurs in cirrhosis with portosystemic shunting, allowing nitrogenous toxins such as ammonia to bypass the liver and reach the brain. Acute liver failure and urea cycle disorders produce the syndrome by different, less common mechanisms.'),
('4b183fcb-2049-5754-a2eb-05d70ce66598'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common precipitating factor for hepatic encephalopathy?', '["GI bleeding (blood protein load)","Infection","Electrolyte imbalance","Constipation (increased ammonia production)","Sedative use"]'::jsonb, 'Constipation (increased ammonia production)', 'Constipation increases colonic ammonia production and absorption, making it a frequent and important precipitant of hepatic encephalopathy. GI bleeding, infection, and electrolyte imbalance are additional recognized triggers.'),
('bcbaeb51-8004-5ec6-9a68-d51429dafc74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of hepatorenal syndrome?', '["Paracentesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Diuretic overuse accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cirrhosis with ascites (type 1 HRS often precipitated by infection SBP)","Hypovolemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nephrotoxic drugs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cirrhosis with ascites (type 1 HRS often precipitated by infection SBP)', 'Hepatorenal syndrome develops as a functional renal failure in patients with cirrhosis and ascites, and type 1 HRS is frequently precipitated by infection such as spontaneous bacterial peritonitis. Paracentesis, diuretics, and hypovolemia are triggers or aggravating factors rather than the underlying cause.'),
('56770f4f-f6fd-5497-9433-f9c737058e2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute cholecystitis?', '["Gallstone impaction in the cystic duct (calculous cholecystitis)","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chemical irritation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gallstone impaction in the cystic duct (calculous cholecystitis)', 'Acute cholecystitis most often results from gallstone impaction in the cystic duct, producing obstruction, bile stasis, and inflammation. Acalculous cholecystitis from ischemia or infection accounts for only a minority of cases.'),
('37c37ac2-46a2-530f-8a40-a9d1db124774'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acalculous cholecystitis?', '["HIV infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Critical illness (postoperative trauma burns or in ICU patients)","Chemotherapy mediated by specific receptor activation","Vasculitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prolonged fasting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Critical illness (postoperative trauma burns or in ICU patients)', 'Acalculous cholecystitis typically complicates critical illness such as major surgery, trauma, burns, or ICU care, where gallbladder stasis and ischemia injure the organ in the absence of stones. HIV, chemotherapy, and vasculitis are less common associations.'),
('4a567103-015e-5d69-ad9b-198eb40d8887'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of gallstones?', '["Choledocholithiasis with obstructive jaundice","Acute cholecystitis","Gallstone pancreatitis","Empyema of the gallbladder","Cholangitis"]'::jsonb, 'Acute cholecystitis', 'Acute cholecystitis is the most frequent complication of gallstones, occurring when a stone obstructs the cystic duct. Choledocholithiasis and gallstone pancreatitis are serious but less common sequelae.'),
('c83f9720-eb98-5eb8-91a0-ec6596c55295'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of cholangitis?', '["Stent occlusion","Postoperative stricture","Choledocholithiasis (common bile duct stones)","Primary sclerosing cholangitis","Tumor (benign or malignant stricture)"]'::jsonb, 'Choledocholithiasis (common bile duct stones)', 'Choledocholithiasis, or stones in the common bile duct, is the most common cause of cholangitis because it obstructs bile flow and allows bacterial infection of the biliary tree. Postoperative strictures and tumors are less frequent causes.'),
('a0044619-fbe9-5c9d-a8cb-7a41c16f27e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Charcot triad?', '["Fever jaundice and mental status change","Pain fever and abscess","Fever jaundice and hypotension","Right upper quadrant pain fever and jaundice","Pain fever and pancreatitis"]'::jsonb, 'Right upper quadrant pain fever and jaundice', 'Charcot triad describes right upper quadrant pain, fever, and jaundice, the classic presentation of acute cholangitis. Fever with mental status change and hypotension belongs to Reynolds pentad, and the other combinations are not characteristic of cholangitis.'),
('07c61dcb-7423-5bcf-9d49-74c234aa4ac5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the gallbladder?', '["Absent gallbladder","Bilobed gallbladder","Double gallbladder","Phrygian cap (folded fundus)","Gallbladder diverticulum"]'::jsonb, 'Phrygian cap (folded fundus)', 'Phrygian cap, a folded or kinked fundus of the gallbladder, is the most common congenital anomaly of this organ and is usually an incidental radiologic finding. Duplication, bilobed, and absent gallbladders are rare.'),
('055503d1-6e1f-564a-993d-393f1eca415e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of chronic cholecystitis?', '["Ischemic injury resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic irritation from stones alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Repeated episodes of acute cholecystitis from gallstones","Primary bacterial infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autoimmune inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Repeated episodes of acute cholecystitis from gallstones', 'Repeated episodes of acute cholecystitis driven by gallstones produce the fibrosis and wall thickening seen in chronic cholecystitis. Chronic irritation by stones alone, without inflammatory episodes, does not account for the condition.'),
('dd7231ff-ab01-5ea8-b21a-332a59814718'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of a porcelain gallbladder?', '["A tumor of the gallbladder","Systemic calcification disorder","A hereditary condition","Chronic cholecystitis with mural calcification","Trauma"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic cholecystitis with mural calcification', 'Porcelain gallbladder results from chronic cholecystitis with mural calcification of the gallbladder wall, sometimes described as eggshell calcification. Because it carries a risk of carcinoma, cholecystectomy is often recommended.'),
('198b7f0f-78b1-573c-a7b1-ae3abfe2eba6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common histologic type of gallbladder carcinoma?', '["Squamous cell carcinoma","Sarcoma","Adenocarcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Adenosquamous carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Adenocarcinoma', 'Adenocarcinoma accounts for the great majority of gallbladder carcinomas, usually arising in the setting of chronic inflammation or gallstones. Squamous, sarcomatous, and small cell tumors are rare.'),
('40bd4e9b-1124-52a0-88fd-a5f720c1f651'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of death from gallbladder cancer?', '["Local invasion into the liver","Metastatic disease (liver and peritoneal spread)","Portal vein invasion","Bile duct obstruction with cholangitis","Hemorrhage"]'::jsonb, 'Metastatic disease (liver and peritoneal spread)', 'Death from gallbladder cancer usually follows metastatic disease, particularly spread to the liver and peritoneum, because the tumor often presents at an advanced stage. Local invasion and biliary obstruction are important but secondary mechanisms of mortality.'),
('9eb16028-cf21-5ac3-a1b3-e17672864f7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common location of gastric cancer?', '["Lesser curvature","Body of the stomach","Fundus","Proximal stomach (cardia)","Antrum and pylorus (distal stomach)"]'::jsonb, 'Antrum and pylorus (distal stomach)', 'Gastric cancer most commonly arises in the antrum and pylorus, the distal stomach. Tumors at the cardia and proximal stomach have been increasing in frequency but remain less common overall.'),
('4bf63ee0-791c-55e5-a221-6646e54065db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common histologic type of gastric cancer?', '["Lymphoma","Carcinoid tumor","Adenocarcinoma (intestinal and diffuse types)","GIST (gastrointestinal stromal tumor)","Squamous cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Adenocarcinoma (intestinal and diffuse types)', 'Adenocarcinoma, including the intestinal and diffuse types, is the predominant histologic type of gastric cancer. Lymphoma, carcinoid, GIST, and squamous carcinoma are uncommon gastric malignancies.'),
('c0143dc0-8ca8-598f-a77e-d43bc90a2db6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common risk factor for distal gastric cancer?', '["Helicobacter pylori infection","Pernicious anemia","Dietary factors (salted smoked foods)","Smoking","Family history"]'::jsonb, 'Helicobacter pylori infection', 'Helicobacter pylori infection is the leading risk factor for distal gastric cancer through chronic gastritis and progressive mucosal atrophy. Pernicious anemia, diet, and smoking play smaller contributory roles.'),
('172da6a8-703d-50b0-8142-ce2a33d7a9b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common location of GIST (gastrointestinal stromal tumor)?', '["Small intestine","Colon","Stomach (most common)","Rectum","Esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (most common)', 'GISTs arise most frequently in the stomach, although they also occur in the small intestine and elsewhere along the gut. The stomach accounts for the majority of these interstitial cells of Cajal tumors.'),
('39ab733b-2db8-5157-b64d-b6cdb6964501'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common mutation in GIST?', '["PDGFRA mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","SDH mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NF1 mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","KIT (CD117) mutation about 80% of cases","PIK3CA mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'KIT (CD117) mutation about 80% of cases', 'Activating KIT (CD117) mutations are found in about 80% of GISTs and drive tumor growth, making them the target of imatinib therapy. PDGFRA mutations account for a smaller fraction, and the other listed mutations are not characteristic.'),
('01ceb16f-35ad-505e-87a0-cc783b5ec107'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of gastric outlet obstruction in adults?', '["Gastric cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreatic pseudocyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gastric volvulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Pyloric mass without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peptic ulcer disease (pyloric stenosis from chronic duodenal ulcer)"]'::jsonb, 'Peptic ulcer disease (pyloric stenosis from chronic duodenal ulcer)', 'Peptic ulcer disease with pyloric stenosis from a chronic duodenal ulcer is the most common cause of gastric outlet obstruction in adults. Malignancy and other space-occupying lesions are less frequent causes.'),
('11b48a6e-7206-5a42-a3d2-ed98a2652c00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of gastric outlet obstruction in children?', '["Duodenal atresia","Gastric duplication","Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","Malrotation","Pyloric web"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis', 'Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is the most common cause of gastric outlet obstruction in infants, presenting around 2 to 8 weeks of age with projectile nonbilious vomiting. Duodenal atresia and malrotation are less common causes.'),
('2499f445-bd47-56a7-8f95-fe26663bd78c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is hypertrophic pyloric stenosis?', '["Inflammation of the pylorus resulting in decreased functional activity","Achalasia of the pylorus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypertrophy of the pyloric muscle causing gastric outlet obstruction in infants","A tumor of the pylorus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A congenital defect of the pyloric mucosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophy of the pyloric muscle causing gastric outlet obstruction in infants', 'Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is marked by hypertrophy of the circular pyloric muscle, which narrows the pyloric canal and obstructs gastric emptying in young infants. It is neither an inflammatory, neoplastic, nor congenital mucosal lesion.'),
('5068781d-d137-52cb-9f61-f8b8dee65a71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the classic age of presentation for hypertrophic pyloric stenosis?', '["Birth (first few days)","6-12 months","2-8 weeks of life","1-2 years","3-6 months"]'::jsonb, '2-8 weeks of life', 'The classic presentation of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis occurs between 2 and 8 weeks of life with progressive projectile nonbilious vomiting. It does not present at birth or in later infancy.'),
('9a092935-74fc-5409-aaf0-f7401c42cce3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the classic physical exam finding in hypertrophic pyloric stenosis?', '["Abdominal distension resulting in decreased functional activity","Palpable olive-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant","Tenderness in the epigastrium mediated by specific receptor activation","Visible peristalsis from left to right without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hernia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palpable olive-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant', 'A palpable olive-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant represents the hypertrophied pylorus and is the classic finding of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Visible peristalsis may be observed, but the olive is the pathognomonic sign.'),
('5a1036b5-9d5d-567a-9586-11b5ae358f03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the classic electrolyte abnormality in hypertrophic pyloric stenosis?', '["Hyponatremic hypokalemic acidosis","Hypernatremic hyperchloremic acidosis","Hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis","Hypercalcemia","Normal electrolytes"]'::jsonb, 'Hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis', 'Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis causes loss of hydrogen and chloride ions through persistent vomiting, producing hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. This is the reverse of the acidotic states seen with other causes of vomiting.'),
('73606d4f-7323-556e-8050-1e2bef8094a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of congenital diaphragmatic hernia?', '["Failure of the sternal portion to develop (Morgagni) resulting in decreased functional activity","Abnormal esophageal development accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Failure of the pleuroperitoneal membrane to fuse (Bochdalek)","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infectious leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the pleuroperitoneal membrane to fuse (Bochdalek)', 'Congenital diaphragmatic hernia most often results from failure of the pleuroperitoneal membrane to fuse, producing a posterolateral Bochdalek defect, usually on the left. Morgagni hernias and esophageal anomalies are far less common.'),
('43a4f948-b0a7-5d07-bd4a-20ffe3f211db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common contents of a Bochdalek hernia?', '["Only omentum resulting in decreased functional activity","Small intestine stomach colon spleen (left-sided)","Pancreas mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver (right-sided hernia) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small intestine stomach colon spleen (left-sided)', 'Bochdalek hernias, being left-sided in most cases, commonly contain small intestine, stomach, colon, and spleen. Liver is the typical content of right-sided hernias, while omentum alone is unusual.'),
('a82d8141-f903-5bc4-bfd5-9f23dfcf100f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of death in congenital diaphragmatic hernia?', '["Pulmonary hypoplasia and persistent pulmonary hypertension","Surgical complications accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malnutrition mediated by specific receptor activation","Sepsis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac anomalies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary hypoplasia and persistent pulmonary hypertension', 'Pulmonary hypoplasia with persistent pulmonary hypertension is the leading cause of death in congenital diaphragmatic hernia, because the herniated viscera compress the developing lung. This determines prognosis more than surgical issues or infection.'),
('2bef1a70-1cd3-55d3-b596-37a71db112d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common hiatal hernia in children?', '["Paraesophageal hernia (type II)","Sliding hiatal hernia (type I)","Mixed hernia (type III)","Hiatal hernia with volvulus","Congenitally short esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Sliding hiatal hernia (type I)', 'Sliding hiatal hernia (type I), where the gastroesophageal junction slides above the diaphragm, is the most common type in children as well as adults. Paraesophageal, mixed, and volvulus-associated hernias are much less common.'),
('981a81bf-78f4-5e1f-8aaf-5cd8c74777fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) in children?', '["Neurologic impairment resulting in decreased functional activity","Transient relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (TLESR)","Delayed gastric emptying mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased intra-abdominal pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hiatal hernia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transient relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (TLESR)', 'Transient relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (TLESR) is the most common mechanism of gastroesophageal reflux in children. Neurologic impairment and hiatal hernia are contributing factors in specific groups rather than the primary cause.'),
('15631aac-8986-5003-b708-7788d03b10ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of GERD in children?', '["Barrett esophagus","Stricture formation","Reflux esophagitis (erosive esophagitis)","Failure to thrive","Aspiration pneumonia"]'::jsonb, 'Reflux esophagitis (erosive esophagitis)', 'Reflux esophagitis, with erosive inflammation of the distal esophagus, is the most common complication of GERD in children. Stricture and Barrett esophagus are less common, and failure to thrive reflects chronic feeding difficulty rather than a direct esophageal damage.'),
('471814fc-7fce-5747-a350-01bfe277215e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of esophageal stricture in children?', '["Pill esophagitis","Caustic ingestion","Eosinophilic esophagitis","GERD-related stricture","Esophageal atresia repair"]'::jsonb, 'Eosinophilic esophagitis', 'In children, chronic eosinophilic inflammation of the esophageal wall with fibrosis makes eosinophilic esophagitis the leading cause of esophageal stricture. Caustic ingestion and GERD-related strictures are now less common, and pill esophagitis or atresia repair are uncommon causes.'),
('4a5f9ce8-dd80-599f-9f07-804641f82745'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of esophageal stricture in adults?', '["Caustic ingestion (lye)","Radiation therapy","GERD (peptic stricture)","Eosinophilic esophagitis","Esophageal cancer"]'::jsonb, 'GERD (peptic stricture)', 'Chronic gastroesophageal reflux injures and scars the distal esophagus, so peptic stricture is the most common cause in adults. Caustic, radiation, eosinophilic esophagitis and cancer-induced strictures are all less frequent.'),
('e1a02fa9-689a-5bfc-8303-3711eeeeb4aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Barrett esophagus?', '["Metaplasia of the distal esophageal epithelium from squamous to columnar (intestinal metaplasia)","Precancerous lesion for esophageal adenocarcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acid reflux complication mediated by specific receptor activation","All of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stricture formation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metaplasia of the distal esophageal epithelium from squamous to columnar (intestinal metaplasia)', 'Barrett esophagus is defined by replacement of the normal squamous lining of the distal esophagus with columnar (intestinal) metaplasia in response to chronic acid reflux. The other choices describe associated features or complications rather than the defining histologic change.'),
('a92a2387-f3b9-5b97-98e1-18364c70edba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of esophageal adenocarcinoma?', '["Obesity resulting in decreased functional activity","Hiatal hernia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Smoking mediated by specific receptor activation","Alcohol without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Barrett esophagus (chronic GERD)"]'::jsonb, 'Barrett esophagus (chronic GERD)', 'Barrett esophagus arising from chronic GERD is the principal precursor of esophageal adenocarcinoma, progressing through dysplasia to carcinoma. Obesity, hiatal hernia, smoking and alcohol are risk factors but not the leading cause.'),
('8a617258-9ac8-5fb4-918b-f55a89a0de55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common histologic type of esophageal cancer in the US?', '["Adenocarcinoma (distal esophagus)","Sarcoma","Squamous cell carcinoma (middle third)","Melanoma","Small cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Adenocarcinoma (distal esophagus)', 'Rising reflux-related Barrett metaplasia has made adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus the most common esophageal cancer in the United States. Squamous cell carcinoma still dominates worldwide and is classically associated with smoking and alcohol.'),
('8fccd936-6806-5cb4-b6ac-f3352e5b7ae8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common histologic type of esophageal cancer worldwide?', '["Leiomyosarcoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Small cell carcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Squamous cell carcinoma (middle third)","Adenocarcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma (middle third)', 'Globally, squamous cell carcinoma of the middle third of the esophagus is the most common histologic type, linked to smoking, alcohol and dietary deficiencies. Adenocarcinoma predominates only in Western populations.'),
('97379258-5851-5331-85c2-27fd3f91845a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of esophageal varices?', '["Hepatic vein web","Budd-Chiari syndrome","Splenic vein thrombosis","Superior vena cava obstruction","Portal hypertension (from cirrhosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension (from cirrhosis)', 'Portal hypertension, most often from cirrhosis, raises pressure in the portosystemic collaterals around the distal esophagus and produces varices. Budd-Chiari syndrome, splenic vein thrombosis and SVC obstruction are uncommon or different pathways.'),
('b5e4461f-95ed-5606-b9ea-14df36d2b2e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of upper GI bleeding from esophageal varices?', '["Portal hypertension with variceal rupture","PORTAL HYPERTENSION leads to formation and rupture of esophageal varices","Neither A nor B","Maybe","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension with variceal rupture', 'Elevated portal pressure distends the thin-walled submucosal esophageal varices, and rupture of these vessels is the mechanism of variceal hemorrhage. The remaining choices are not valid disease-related causes of bleeding.'),
('db8c0913-2223-51af-a818-0ca89700eb7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common location of peptic ulcer?', '["Post-bulbar duodenum","Gastric antrum (lesser curvature)","Duodenal bulb (first part of duodenum)","Esophagus","Stomach body"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal bulb (first part of duodenum)', 'The duodenal bulb, nearest to the pylorus, is exposed to the highest acid load and is the most common site of peptic ulcer. The gastric antrum, post-bulbar duodenum and stomach body are less frequent locations.'),
('c352e96b-926d-5ce8-8970-2ac68200e783'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of duodenal ulcer?', '["Helicobacter pylori infection (about 70-90% of cases)","Idiopathic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Zollinger-Ellison syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","NSAID use without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stress ulcer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Helicobacter pylori infection (about 70-90% of cases)', 'Helicobacter pylori infection underlies most duodenal ulcers by weakening mucosal defenses and driving gastrin-stimulated acid secretion. NSAID use is the other major cause, while Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and stress ulcers are uncommon.'),
('4011fe6c-9f24-58d2-aa36-e215f9c906b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of gastric ulcer?', '["H. pylori infection (about 60-70% of cases)","Zollinger-Ellison syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Smoking mediated by specific receptor activation","NSAID use without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stress ulcer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H. pylori infection (about 60-70% of cases)', 'H. pylori colonizes the gastric mucosa and disrupts the mucus-bicarbonate barrier, causing most gastric ulcers. NSAID use accounts for many of the rest; smoking, stress ulcers and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome are much less common.'),
('463b1944-cc7f-5c1c-9e5c-a6a1ac79a31e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of peptic ulcer disease?', '["Perforation","Obstruction (pyloric stenosis)","Penetration into adjacent organs","Bleeding","Intractable pain"]'::jsonb, 'Bleeding', 'Erosion into submucosal vessels makes hemorrhage the most common complication of peptic ulcer disease. Perforation, pyloric obstruction and penetration into adjacent organs occur less frequently.'),
('676f59b6-ea00-5c79-acdc-beb87819d6ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common site of perforated peptic ulcer?', '["Pyloric channel","Gastric body (posterior wall)","Duodenal bulb (anterior wall)","Duodenal bulb (posterior wall)","Gastric antrum (anterior wall)"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal bulb (anterior wall)', 'Perforation is most frequent on the anterior wall of the duodenal bulb because there is no organ to seal the leak, allowing contents to spill into the peritoneal cavity. Posterior wall ulcers tend to penetrate the pancreas and bleed instead.'),
('f7f61dd6-81fd-5b4d-a026-3da9c1b54098'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of peritonitis from a perforated ulcer?', '["Blood in the peritoneal cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","Fecal peritonitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chemical peritonitis (acid/bile initially) then bacterial peritonitis","Primary peritonitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bile peritonitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chemical peritonitis (acid/bile initially) then bacterial peritonitis', 'Perforation initially releases sterile gastric acid and bile, evoking chemical peritonitis that later becomes bacterial as enteric organisms contaminate the peritoneum. The other options describe unrelated forms of peritonitis.'),
('827badef-169e-5e19-8f67-2000783b8093'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome?', '["NSAIDs resulting in decreased functional activity","G-cell hyperplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gastrinoma (usually in the pancreas or duodenum)","Gastric outlet obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","H. pylori infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrinoma (usually in the pancreas or duodenum)', 'Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is caused by a gastrin-secreting neuroendocrine tumor, usually in the pancreas or duodenum, that drives intractable acid hypersecretion and peptic ulcers. G-cell hyperplasia and the other choices are not its usual cause.'),
('1e6360d0-e702-50cf-a1ff-6f772d7a4325'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common location of gastrinoma?', '["Pancreas (historically most common)","Stomach","Lymph nodes","Liver","Duodenal wall (most common now)"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal wall (most common now)', 'Although the pancreas was historically considered the classic site, most gastrinomas are now found in the duodenal wall, where they are often small and multiple. Liver and lymph node deposits represent metastases rather than primary sites.'),
('96270396-6d5d-5ceb-bdeb-c65583930469'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of chronic mesenteric ischemia?', '["Median arcuate ligament syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Atherosclerosis of the mesenteric arteries (SMA and celiac trunk)","Embolism (atrial fibrillation) mediated by specific receptor activation","Vasculitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibromuscular dysplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atherosclerosis of the mesenteric arteries (SMA and celiac trunk)', 'Chronic mesenteric ischemia results from atherosclerotic narrowing of the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery, causing postprandial abdominal angina. Median arcuate ligament compression is a less common mechanical cause, and embolism produces acute ischemia.'),
('1cf9a99b-5b71-5ebb-ad5e-bead4b092fb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute mesenteric ischemia?', '["Vasculitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior mesenteric artery (SMA) embolism (often from atrial fibrillation)","Nomocclusive mesenteric ischemia (NOMI) mediated by specific receptor activation","SMA thrombosis (atherosclerotic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior mesenteric vein thrombosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery (SMA) embolism (often from atrial fibrillation)', 'Embolic occlusion of the superior mesenteric artery, usually a cardiac embolus from atrial fibrillation, is the most common cause of acute mesenteric ischemia. SMA thrombosis from atherosclerosis and nonocclusive low-flow states are less frequent.'),
('3c1633b9-a145-5962-8561-7c1241dcc210'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of small bowel bleeding in the elderly?', '["Peptic ulcer disease","Crohn disease","Angiodysplasia (vascular ectasia)","Meckel''s diverticulum","Tumors"]'::jsonb, 'Angiodysplasia (vascular ectasia)', 'Angiodysplasia, an acquired ectatic vascular lesion of the small bowel, is the most common source of small bowel bleeding in older adults. Meckel diverticulum predominates in children, and tumors and Crohn disease are less common.'),
('71c77967-f143-56a9-8d2a-8e7f89ba6a3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of GI bleeding in patients with aortic stenosis?', '["Esophageal varices","Angiodysplasia (Heyde syndrome)","Colon cancer","Diverticulosis","Peptic ulcer disease"]'::jsonb, 'Angiodysplasia (Heyde syndrome)', 'In Heyde syndrome, aortic stenosis coexists with angiodysplastic GI bleeding due to acquired von Willebrand factor deficiency from shear stress across the valve, making angiodysplasia the leading cause. Varices, diverticulosis and ulcers are unrelated predominant causes.'),
('c93896c2-606d-5a8d-b442-acc80f471a27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of diverticulitis?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Medication-related accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Obstruction of a colonic diverticulum by fecal matter (microperforation)","Infection of the diverticulum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstruction of a colonic diverticulum by fecal matter (microperforation)', 'A fecalith blocks the neck of a colonic diverticulum, raising intraluminal pressure and causing microperforation that incites inflammation. These are the primary events, whereas trauma, ischemia and medication effects are not the usual triggers.'),
('5f26c0f0-4fdb-5387-90fa-56493bd81338'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common location of colonic diverticulosis in Western populations?', '["Sigmoid colon (most common)","Transverse colon","Descending colon","Ascending colon","Cecum"]'::jsonb, 'Sigmoid colon (most common)', 'In Western populations diverticula occur most often in the sigmoid colon, where high intraluminal pressures act on weak points at the vasa recta entry sites. Right-sided diverticula are relatively more common in Asian populations.'),
('534a3769-0eda-5ba0-b0c9-69c13fd7826c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of diverticulitis?', '["Bleeding resulting in decreased functional activity","Stricture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Free perforation with peritonitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Fistula formation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Perforation with abscess (pericolonic abscess)"]'::jsonb, 'Perforation with abscess (pericolonic abscess)', 'A contained perforation of an inflamed diverticulum typically forms a pericolonic abscess, the most common complication of diverticulitis. Free perforation with peritonitis, fistula, stricture and bleeding are less frequent.'),
('17a0aad7-dd38-5802-bdcd-bb1d498d4706'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of hematochezia (bright red blood per rectum) in adults?', '["Diverticulosis","Colon cancer","Colonic polyps","Angiodysplasia","Internal hemorrhoids"]'::jsonb, 'Diverticulosis', 'Bleeding from a diverticulum occurs when a fecalith erodes an adjacent vasa rectum, making diverticulosis the leading cause of hematochezia in adults. Colon cancer, polyps, angiodysplasia and hemorrhoids are less common sources.'),
('ca01f756-3e2c-588d-b5d6-3b7432fc4365'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of lower GI bleeding in young adults?', '["Anal fissure resulting in decreased functional activity","Colonic diverticulosis (increasingly seen in young adults but still uncommon)","Hemorrhoids mediated by specific receptor activation","Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colonic polyps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Colonic diverticulosis (increasingly seen in young adults but still uncommon)', 'Colonic diverticulosis has become a commonly identified cause of lower GI bleeding even in younger adults as its prevalence rises in this group. Anal fissure, hemorrhoids and IBD typically cause milder, smaller-volume bleeding.'),
('96837955-ff1e-5694-8dd7-db9bd35853ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of perianal abscess?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Crohn disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","HIV infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cryptoglandular infection (infection of the anal glands)"]'::jsonb, 'Cryptoglandular infection (infection of the anal glands)', 'Infection of the anal glands in the intersphincteric space is the usual origin of perianal abscess, spreading along tissue planes. Crohn disease, trauma and surgery are less common causes.'),
('12dbd55a-7aa8-5ff6-8209-8e4888727788'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of anal fistula?', '["Extrasphincteric fistula resulting in decreased functional activity","Submucosal fistula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Suprasphincteric fistula mediated by specific receptor activation","Intersphincteric fistula (between internal and external sphincter)","Transphincteric fistula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intersphincteric fistula (between internal and external sphincter)', 'The intersphincteric tract, running in the plane between the internal and external sphincters, is the most common type of anal fistula. Transphincteric, suprasphincteric and extrasphincteric fistulas are less frequent.'),
('eabde097-55ed-5bc1-a7a7-82b2d451bdc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of pilonidal sinus?', '["Infected sacrococcygeal sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-surgical complication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital remnant of the neural tube mediated by specific receptor activation","Hair penetration into the subcutaneous tissue (acquired condition)","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hair penetration into the subcutaneous tissue (acquired condition)', 'Pilonidal disease is an acquired condition in which loose hairs penetrate the subcutaneous tissue of the sacrococcygeal region, provoking a foreign-body reaction and sinus formation. The congenital neural-tube remnant theory is no longer accepted.'),
('a82b2cc0-ca60-50bd-9036-b2dc1478768b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Is the appendix fixed or mobile?', '["Mobile (variable position)","Fixed in the retrocecal area","Fixed in the preileal position","Fixed in the subhepatic area","Fixed in the pelvis"]'::jsonb, 'Mobile (variable position)', 'The appendix is mobile, its tip occupying a variable position such as retrocecal, pelvic, preileal or postileal in the right lower quadrant. This variability explains why appendicitis can present with different localizing signs.'),
('5125f272-3155-508d-8b5f-17a3b16a1e28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What position of the appendix can cause psoas irritation (positive psoas sign)?', '["Pelvic (irritates the obturator internus) resulting in decreased functional activity","Preileal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Postileal mediated by specific receptor activation","Subcecal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retrocecal (appendix lies on the psoas muscle)"]'::jsonb, 'Retrocecal (appendix lies on the psoas muscle)', 'A retrocecal appendix lies directly over the psoas muscle, so inflammation irritates it and elicits a positive psoas sign on hip flexion. A pelvic appendix instead irritates the obturator internus, producing the obturator sign.'),
('a7a62cdf-0d81-5ad9-963a-add6ac36a933'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What position of the appendix can cause obturator irritation?', '["Postileal resulting in decreased functional activity","Pelvic (over the obturator internus)","Preileal mediated by specific receptor activation","Retrocecal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subcecal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic (over the obturator internus)', 'A pelvic appendix overlies the obturator internus, and its inflammation causes obturator sign with pain on internal rotation of the flexed thigh. Retrocecal, preileal, postileal and subcecal positions produce different localizing findings.'),
('39abe9c3-4b60-5840-8358-9a10de4b8e68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute pancreatitis in children?', '["Infections","Idiopathic","Blunt abdominal trauma","Medications","Gallstones"]'::jsonb, 'Blunt abdominal trauma', 'Blunt abdominal trauma, commonly from a bicycle handlebar injury, is the most frequent cause of acute pancreatitis in children. Gallstones and alcohol dominate in adults, and infections or medications are uncommon causes in children.'),
('f741008b-0d7c-5ed5-a9ed-d6755865db72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Ranson criteria used for?', '["Evaluating cholangitis","Predicting severity of acute pancreatitis","Classifying cholecystitis","Assessing liver function","Diagnosing pancreatitis"]'::jsonb, 'Predicting severity of acute pancreatitis', 'Ranson criteria score age, laboratory and clinical parameters at admission and at 48 hours to predict the severity, mortality and prognosis of acute pancreatitis. They are not tools for diagnosing pancreatitis or evaluating the other listed conditions.'),
('238e1a29-3d4b-52d2-a231-4ff51d716d2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of hereditary pancreatitis?', '["CPA1 mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","PRSS1 mutation (cationic trypsinogen)","CTRC mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","CFTR mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SPINK1 mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PRSS1 mutation (cationic trypsinogen)', 'A gain-of-function mutation in PRSS1, which encodes cationic trypsinogen, prevents trypsin inactivation and causes autosomal-dominant hereditary pancreatitis with recurrent episodes. Mutations in SPINK1, CTRC, CFTR and CPA1 are less common or act as modifiers.'),
('a5c69740-beb8-57d0-9aad-7e00816f1d86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common external hernia?', '["Femoral hernia","Indirect inguinal hernia (in both sexes)","Umbilical hernia","Direct inguinal hernia","Incisional hernia"]'::jsonb, 'Indirect inguinal hernia (in both sexes)', 'The indirect inguinal hernia, passing through the deep inguinal ring alongside the spermatic cord, is the most common external hernia in both sexes. Direct inguinal, femoral and umbilical hernias are less frequent.'),
('b7eb0a3c-30e1-57a0-a153-4997ecc43ea9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common hernia in women?', '["Indirect inguinal hernia","Femoral hernia","Incisional hernia","Direct inguinal hernia","Umbilical hernia"]'::jsonb, 'Indirect inguinal hernia', 'Indirect inguinal hernia is also the most common hernia in women, although femoral hernia is relatively more common in women than in men. The narrower female pelvis makes direct inguinal hernia less frequent.'),
('8b12fe6c-3524-521d-ad31-222ced52736b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common hernia to become incarcerated?', '["Femoral hernia (highest rate of strangulation)","Direct inguinal hernia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Incisional hernia mediated by specific receptor activation","Umbilical hernia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Indirect inguinal hernia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral hernia (highest rate of strangulation)', 'The narrow, rigid femoral ring makes femoral hernias the most prone to incarceration and strangulation. Although inguinal hernias are far more common overall, they strangulate much less often.'),
('767aa8d6-c5b1-5f50-8012-68c630ac21ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acquired inguinal hernia in adults?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased intra-abdominal pressure (obesity lifting chronic cough constipation)","Congenital patent processus vaginalis mediated by specific receptor activation","Connective tissue disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intra-abdominal pressure (obesity lifting chronic cough constipation)', 'Chronic elevations in intra-abdominal pressure from obesity, heavy lifting, cough or constipation strain a weakened abdominal wall and produce acquired inguinal hernias in adults. A patent processus vaginalis instead explains congenital indirect hernias.'),
('26db3c4b-ed3e-53dc-bd09-364f83de5d26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of ventral hernia?', '["Pregnancy resulting in decreased functional activity","Obesity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Previous abdominal surgery (incisional hernia)","Abdominal wall weakness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Previous abdominal surgery (incisional hernia)', 'Weakness at a healed laparotomy or port-site incision is the most common cause of ventral hernia, presenting as an incisional hernia. Obesity and pregnancy elevate the risk but are not the single most common cause.'),
('2616fc4b-a3e1-5e39-9783-45ef77aaaa18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common site for an incisional hernia?', '["Midline (rectus sheath)","Subcostal","Flank","Pfannenstiel","Parastomal"]'::jsonb, 'Midline (rectus sheath)', 'Midline rectus sheath incisions close under the greatest tension and heal with the weakest scar, making them the most common site for incisional hernia. Pfannenstiel, subcostal and flank incisions herniate less often.'),
('d7878e80-8cf7-5fd3-85bc-3c28294aec8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of hernia repair?', '["Residual pain or chronic groin pain","Infection","Serooma","Nerve injury","Recurrence (now less with mesh)"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrence (now less with mesh)', 'Recurrence is the most common complication of hernia repair, although mesh reinforcement has markedly lowered its rate. Infection, seroma, nerve injury and chronic pain also occur but less often.'),
('601e0374-8668-5cff-bcf9-17dbc4c76138'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the single most important predictor of recurrence after hernia repair?', '["Patient age resulting in decreased functional activity","Surgeon experience accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hernia size mediated by specific receptor activation","Type of anesthesia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesh use (tension-free repair with mesh has lowest recurrence)"]'::jsonb, 'Mesh use (tension-free repair with mesh has lowest recurrence)', 'Tension-free repair with mesh is the strongest predictor of low recurrence, substantially outperforming suture-based tissue repairs. Patient age, surgeon experience, hernia size and anesthesia type carry less prognostic weight.'),
('6e906813-31a4-5e6d-814b-0ae7641f45ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of chronic groin pain after hernia repair?', '["Orchitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteitis pubis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Recurrence mediated by specific receptor activation","Nerve entrapment or irritation (ilioinguinal genitofemoral iliohypogastric)","Phantom pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve entrapment or irritation (ilioinguinal genitofemoral iliohypogastric)', 'Chronic groin pain after hernia repair most often results from entrapment or irritation of the ilioinguinal, genitofemoral or iliohypogastric nerves by suture or mesh. Orchitis, osteitis pubis, recurrence and phantom pain are less common explanations.'),
('2f281fea-63fc-56fb-a0f0-dadabbb46c49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of umbilical hernia in children?', '["African descent resulting in decreased functional activity","Congenital weakness at the umbilical ring (most close spontaneously)","Prematurity mediated by specific receptor activation","Connective tissue disorder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased intra-abdominal pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital weakness at the umbilical ring (most close spontaneously)', 'Incomplete closure of the umbilical ring after cord separation leaves a congenital defect through which bowel herniates, and most of these hernias close spontaneously by age 4-5 years. Prematurity and African descent increase frequency but are not the cause.'),
('7ee32d0a-08d8-5bbc-bf04-edfe15a873f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of epigastric hernia?', '["Fatty protrusion through a defect in the linea alba (between xiphoid and umbilicus)","Pregnancy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obesity mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connective tissue disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty protrusion through a defect in the linea alba (between xiphoid and umbilicus)', 'An epigastric hernia is a protrusion of preperitoneal fat, often with omentum, through a defect in the linea alba between the xiphoid process and the umbilicus. Obesity, pregnancy and trauma are predisposing rather than causative factors.'),
('188f6f5d-7197-5d02-9f97-fb4760afdb93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common parastomal hernia location?', '["Adjacent to an ileostomy","Adjacent to a urostomy","Adjacent to an end colostomy","Adjacent to a gastrostomy","Adjacent to a cecostomy"]'::jsonb, 'Adjacent to an end colostomy', 'Parastomal hernias occur most often adjacent to an end colostomy, which creates the largest fascial defect and transmits relatively high intra-abdominal pressure. Ileostomies, urostomies and the other stomas herniate less frequently.'),
('6a0da838-a932-5a64-8aad-534ff9fe72be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of a parastomal hernia?', '["Obesity resulting in decreased functional activity","Poor surgical technique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Large defect in the abdominal wall at the stoma site","Increased abdominal pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malnutrition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large defect in the abdominal wall at the stoma site', 'The fascial opening created for the stoma is the defect through which abdominal contents herniate, so a large stoma-site defect is the most common cause of parastomal hernia. Obesity and increased abdominal pressure add risk but are not the primary cause.'),
('9ca919ee-6a90-502f-8e6e-67ee2fee20c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute abdomen in the elderly?', '["Acute cholecystitis","Diverticulitis","Appendicitis","Perforated viscus","Mesenteric ischemia"]'::jsonb, 'Acute cholecystitis', 'Acute cholecystitis is the most common cause of an acute abdomen in the elderly. Diverticulitis, mesenteric ischemia, perforated viscus and appendicitis also occur in this age group but less frequently.'),
('f7d5ba38-1f59-5f81-98a0-1af1b9339318'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of small intestine bacterial overgrowth (SIBO)?', '["Fistula resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstruction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypochlorhydria (PPI use) and impaired motility (diabetes scleroderma)","Short bowel syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Immunodeficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypochlorhydria (PPI use) and impaired motility (diabetes scleroderma)', 'Reduced gastric acid from PPI use combined with impaired small-bowel motility in diabetes or scleroderma allows bacterial proliferation, the most common cause of SIBO. Anatomic causes such as fistulas, obstruction and short bowel are less frequent.'),
('e78920e7-00e4-5076-b2a8-998af9a7416d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of protein-losing enteropathy in children?', '["Infectious gastroenteritis","Intestinal lymphangiectasia","Inflammatory bowel disease","Celiac disease","Allergic enteropathy (cow milk protein allergy)"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac disease', 'Celiac disease is the most common cause of protein-losing enteropathy in children, with gluten-induced mucosal damage leaking serum proteins into the lumen. Intestinal lymphangiectasia, allergic enteropathy and IBD are less common causes.'),
('e7bd6d29-5f0b-52ee-9963-5161d88e1521'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of malabsorption in children?', '["Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","Cystic fibrosis","Giardia infection","Celiac disease","Lactose intolerance"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac disease', 'Celiac disease is the leading cause of malabsorption in children, driven by gluten-induced immune damage to the small intestinal mucosa with villous blunting. Cystic fibrosis, giardiasis, and lactose intolerance are important but less common overall causes.'),
('b4fbf8b3-2c20-5ba9-bf60-84e45f0029cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of chronic diarrhea in children?', '["Irritable bowel syndrome","Celiac disease","Inflammatory bowel disease","Gastroenteritis (infectious)","Food allergy (cow milk protein)"]'::jsonb, 'Gastroenteritis (infectious)', 'Infectious gastroenteritis is the most frequent cause of chronic diarrhea in children, often following an acute enteric infection. Celiac disease, IBD, and food allergy are less common and more specific entities.'),
('d5977ec8-9b86-56a2-8779-ce6afd1a644d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute diarrhea in children worldwide?', '["E. coli (enterotoxigenic) resulting in decreased functional activity","Salmonella accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shigella mediated by specific receptor activation","Campylobacter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rotavirus (historically) now norovirus and bacterial pathogens (depending on region)"]'::jsonb, 'Rotavirus (historically) now norovirus and bacterial pathogens (depending on region)', 'Rotavirus historically led acute diarrheal illness worldwide; with widespread vaccination, norovirus and bacterial pathogens now dominate in many regions. E. coli, Salmonella, Shigella, and Campylobacter are bacterial causes that do not predominate globally overall.'),
('d0852349-4182-50a7-870e-819411f69252'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of traveler''s diarrhea?', '["Shigella","Campylobacter jejuni","Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC)","Salmonella","Giardia"]'::jsonb, 'Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC)', 'Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC) causes the majority of traveler''s diarrhea by producing heat-labile and heat-stable toxins that drive secretory diarrhea. Shigella, Campylobacter, and Salmonella are invasive bacterial causes but are not the leading ones in this setting.'),
('803ac39f-03eb-523d-b0f9-9bde5911431f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of ischemic colitis?', '["IMA occlusion resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesenteric vein thrombosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoperfusion (low-flow state like shock or with vascular small vessel disease)","SMA occlusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoperfusion (low-flow state like shock or with vascular small vessel disease)', 'Most cases of ischemic colitis result from non-occlusive hypoperfusion, such as low-flow states in shock or small-vessel disease. Occlusion of the SMA or IMA, mesenteric vein thrombosis, and trauma account for a minority of cases.'),
('3d9b8496-48a7-5d95-83d8-582b2740a54a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common site of ischemic colitis?', '["Transverse colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Cecum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Splenic flexure and rectosigmoid (watershed areas)","Ascending colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Descending colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic flexure and rectosigmoid (watershed areas)', 'Ischemic colitis preferentially affects watershed areas where collateral circulation is weakest, namely the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction. Other colonic regions are less vulnerable to low-flow ischemia.'),
('ed39f4df-b2bf-5eda-8ea9-65ef88acc144'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of megacolon in adults?', '["Chronic constipation resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)","Ulcerative colitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Hirschsprung disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chagas disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)', 'Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome) is the most common cause of acute megacolon in adults, marked by massive colonic dilation without mechanical obstruction. Hirschsprung disease and Chagas disease are chronic causes, while chronic constipation and ulcerative colitis are separate entities.'),
('3b95afd8-240c-5796-94eb-850cd9adc15c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of toxic megacolon?', '["Infectious colitis (C. difficile) resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstruction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Volvulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischemic colitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammatory bowel disease (especially ulcerative colitis)"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammatory bowel disease (especially ulcerative colitis)', 'Inflammatory bowel disease, especially ulcerative colitis, is the most common cause of toxic megacolon, with transmural inflammation and loss of neuromuscular tone. C. difficile colitis, obstruction, volvulus, and ischemia are less common precipitants.'),
('a7d98da9-ea89-5223-998d-73124e95d5a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of constipation in children?', '["Organic causes (Hirschsprung hypothyroidism)","Functional constipation (bowel holding behavior)","Psychological factors","Dietary factors","Medications"]'::jsonb, 'Functional constipation (bowel holding behavior)', 'Functional constipation from voluntary withholding of stool is the most common cause of constipation in children. Organic disorders such as Hirschsprung disease and hypothyroidism are rare by comparison.'),
('6abcb977-241a-5921-b99e-164dc5e99d0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of fecal incontinence in children?', '["Behavioral disorder resulting in decreased functional activity","Anorectal malformation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord anomalies mediated by specific receptor activation","Functional constipation with overflow soiling","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Functional constipation with overflow soiling', 'Overflow soiling from functional constipation with impacted stool is the leading cause of fecal incontinence in children. Anorectal malformations, spinal cord anomalies, and trauma are structural causes that are much less common.'),
('14429599-f04c-5c13-80c0-e85a4e10bc2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of abdominal distension in preterm infants?', '["Sepsis","Necrotizing enterocolitis","Hydroperitoneum","Feed intolerance","Ventilator-related air (pulmonary air leak)"]'::jsonb, 'Necrotizing enterocolitis', 'Necrotizing enterocolitis is the most common cause of significant abdominal distension in preterm infants, associated with ischemic mucosal injury and pneumatosis. Sepsis, feed intolerance, and air leak can distend the abdomen but are less frequent primary causes.'),
('72a38377-af9a-5544-bc97-bb968cd104d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of neonatal ascites?', '["Hepatic ascites resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiac ascites accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pneumoperitoneum mediated by specific receptor activation","Urinary ascites (from posterior urethral valves or bladder perforation)","Chylous ascites leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urinary ascites (from posterior urethral valves or bladder perforation)', 'Urinary ascites from posterior urethral valves or bladder perforation is the most common cause of neonatal ascites. Chylous, hepatic, and cardiac ascites are much rarer in this age group.'),
('a2817e8d-1f2c-56c4-b0a0-fd2f5fba0f16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of colonic fistula in Crohn disease?', '["Perianal fistula","Enterovesical fistula","Enteroenteric fistula","Enterocutaneous fistula","Enterovaginal fistula"]'::jsonb, 'Enteroenteric fistula', 'Enteroenteric fistulas between adjacent bowel loops are the most common fistulas in Crohn disease. Perianal, enterovesical, enterocutaneous, and enterovaginal fistulas also occur but less often.'),
('efe47b26-9828-5de9-b609-eab14616119f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common site of GI lymphoma?', '["Esophagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Stomach (most common primary GI lymphoma)","Liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Small intestine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (most common primary GI lymphoma)', 'The stomach is the most common site of primary GI lymphoma. The small intestine, colon, esophagus, and liver are far less frequent locations.'),
('d27cedea-0c7e-5ffb-98d0-08ff561a8c9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of gastric MALT lymphoma?', '["Autoimmune gastritis","H. pylori infection (chronic gastritis)","EBV infection","Genetic predisposition","Immunosuppression"]'::jsonb, 'H. pylori infection (chronic gastritis)', 'Chronic H. pylori gastritis drives gastric MALT lymphoma through chronic antigenic stimulation of mucosal B cells. Autoimmune gastritis, EBV, and immunosuppression are not the usual trigger.'),
('03860418-de0f-5bc5-bf14-b2de920b9a49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of primary GI lymphoma?', '["Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)","T-cell lymphoma","Burkitt lymphoma","Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","MALT lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)', 'Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is the most common histologic type of primary GI lymphoma. MALT lymphoma, Burkitt lymphoma, and T-cell variants are less frequent.'),
('e0e9bf19-df94-54a8-a77c-aa9e17646a61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of intestinal pseudo-obstruction?', '["Medications","Postoperative ileus (most common cause of acute pseudo-obstruction)","Chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction (CIPO) from myopathy neuropathy or connective tissue disease","Surgery","Electrolyte imbalance"]'::jsonb, 'Postoperative ileus (most common cause of acute pseudo-obstruction)', 'Postoperative ileus is the most common cause of acute intestinal pseudo-obstruction. Chronic forms from myopathy, neuropathy, medications, or electrolyte imbalance are much less common overall.'),
('15ee5d7d-a4a6-5b6b-a5d9-a5c47952eb83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction?', '["Idiopathic (most common in adults)","Hypothyroidism","Systemic sclerosis","Paraneoplastic","Diabetes mellitus"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (most common in adults)', 'Most adult chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction is idiopathic, with no identifiable myopathy, neuropathy, or systemic disease. Hypothyroidism, systemic sclerosis, paraneoplastic syndromes, and diabetes are recognized but less common secondary causes.'),
('d155e8a5-11d0-5a3c-ad4e-feacdf54dcb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of graft-versus-host disease (GVHD) after liver transplant?', '["Drug reaction resulting in decreased functional activity","Donor T-lymphocytes attacking recipient tissues (not common after liver transplant alone)","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischemic injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Recurrent disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Donor T-lymphocytes attacking recipient tissues (not common after liver transplant alone)', 'GVHD is driven by donor T-lymphocytes attacking recipient tissues, although it is uncommon after liver transplantation alone because the liver is relatively immunotolerant. Drug reactions, infection, and ischemia are not the mechanism of GVHD.'),
('89fe905d-3291-50ae-8c35-0b1176a98336'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of eosinophilic esophagitis (EoE)?', '["GERD resulting in decreased functional activity","Autoimmune disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Allergen-driven (Type 2) immune response to food antigens","Infectious cause without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medication reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Allergen-driven (Type 2) immune response to food antigens', 'Eosinophilic esophagitis results from an allergen-driven type 2 immune response to food antigens, causing dense eosinophilic infiltration. GERD, infections, and medications do not produce this predominant eosinophilic pattern.'),
('d9bc67ac-f4a0-5fc9-82d6-526bf14478ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of esophageal dysphagia?', '["Motility disorders (achalasia)","Eosinophilic esophagitis","Structural lesions (stricture tumor web)","Foreign body","Neurologic disorders"]'::jsonb, 'Motility disorders (achalasia)', 'Esophageal motility disorders, particularly achalasia, are the most common cause of esophageal (transfer) dysphagia. Structural lesions, webs, foreign bodies, and eosinophilic esophagitis are less frequent overall.'),
('57e53637-a119-5711-b2b8-1cd906839927'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of oropharyngeal dysphagia?', '["Neurologic disorders (stroke Parkinson disease dementia)","Medication side effects accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Structural lesions (Zenker diverticulum) mediated by specific receptor activation","Myopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cricopharyngeal achalasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurologic disorders (stroke Parkinson disease dementia)', 'Neurologic disorders such as stroke, Parkinson disease, and dementia are the most common cause of oropharyngeal dysphagia by impairing the oropharyngeal phase. Structural lesions, myopathy, and medications are less frequent causes.'),
('b62a2238-6caf-55c5-a843-fffb272cefd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of achalasia?', '["Malignancy (pseudoachalasia) resulting in decreased functional activity","Idiopathic (degeneration of inhibitory neurons in the esophageal myenteric plexus)","Surgical complication mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (degeneration of inhibitory neurons in the esophageal myenteric plexus)', 'Achalasia is usually idiopathic, from degeneration of inhibitory neurons in the esophageal myenteric plexus that leaves the LES unopposed and contractile. Chagas disease, malignancy (pseudoachalasia), and surgery account for a minority.'),
('105ce64d-96ac-51c3-910b-26f3e303dddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common location of squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus?', '["Upper third (cervical esophagus) resulting in decreased functional activity","Middle third of the esophagus (at the level of the tracheal bifurcation)","Cardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Entire esophagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lower third (distal esophagus) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Middle third of the esophagus (at the level of the tracheal bifurcation)', 'Squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus most often arises in the middle third, at the level of the tracheal bifurcation. The upper third and distal esophagus are less common sites.'),
('5949d619-f1fd-511d-aaf8-19c045521d41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of death from esophageal cancer?', '["Obstruction with aspiration","Local invasion (airway fistula)","Malnutrition and cachexia","Hemorrhage","Metastatic disease (liver lung bone)"]'::jsonb, 'Metastatic disease (liver lung bone)', 'Distant metastasis to the liver, lungs, and bone is the most common cause of death in esophageal cancer. Local invasion, obstruction, cachexia, and hemorrhage contribute but are less frequently fatal overall.'),
('fe8dd6b3-33d0-5b6d-a81f-7b5a3a8a52b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of esophageal perforation?', '["Spontaneous (Boerhaave syndrome) resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma (penetrating) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iatrogenic (endoscopic or surgical instrumentation)","Malignancy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foreign body ingestion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iatrogenic (endoscopic or surgical instrumentation)', 'Most esophageal perforations are iatrogenic, resulting from endoscopic or surgical instrumentation. Spontaneous rupture, trauma, malignancy, and foreign bodies are less common causes.'),
('6f2aae2a-8429-5121-be90-51a4949fd0d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Boerhaave syndrome?', '["Longitudinal tear of the cervical esophagus (Mallory-Weiss) resulting in decreased functional activity","Traumatic rupture of the esophagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Caustic ingestion injury mediated by specific receptor activation","pontaneous rupture from any cause without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spontaneous esophageal perforation due to severe vomiting (full-thickness tear)"]'::jsonb, 'Spontaneous esophageal perforation due to severe vomiting (full-thickness tear)', 'Boerhaave syndrome is a spontaneous full-thickness perforation of the esophagus caused by severe vomiting. Mallory-Weiss is a mucosal tear, not a perforation, and traumatic or caustic injuries are distinct mechanisms.'),
('5959b2a2-e2b6-5f6a-8d0d-734806b1d203'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Mallory-Weiss syndrome?', '["Mucosal tear at the gastroesophageal junction from retching/vomiting causing bleeding","Esophageal ring accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Variceal rupture mediated by specific receptor activation","Esophageal web without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Full-thickness perforation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosal tear at the gastroesophageal junction from retching/vomiting causing bleeding', 'Mallory-Weiss syndrome is a mucosal tear at the gastroesophageal junction from retching or vomiting that bleeds. Esophageal rings, webs, variceal rupture, and full-thickness perforation are different lesions.'),
('032f8409-680b-5caa-9f57-c5752c9c3b97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Schatzki ring?', '["Achalasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Stricture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Esophageal web mediated by specific receptor activation","A ring at the gastroesophageal junction (type B) causing dysphagia","Eosinophilic esophagitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A ring at the gastroesophageal junction (type B) causing dysphagia', 'A Schatzki ring is a thin distal mucosal ring at the gastroesophageal junction (type B) causing intermittent solid-food dysphagia. Strictures, webs, achalasia, and eosinophilic esophagitis are distinct causes of dysphagia.'),
('d7e1f8af-2f1e-509d-8f8a-c1efbeed449d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of food impaction in children?', '["Foreign body","Eosinophilic esophagitis","Esophageal atresia repair","Stricture","Schatzki ring"]'::jsonb, 'Eosinophilic esophagitis', 'Eosinophilic esophagitis is the most common cause of food impaction in children, from chronic eosinophilic inflammation that narrows the esophageal lumen. Foreign bodies, strictures, rings, and post-surgical changes are less common.'),
('d9f5357b-2487-5583-bc70-88a671ac2ffe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of caustic ingestion injury?', '["Iatrogenic resulting in decreased functional activity","Occupational exposure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accidental ingestion in children (household cleaners)","Medication-related without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Suicidal ingestion in adults leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accidental ingestion in children (household cleaners)', 'Caustic ingestion injury is most often accidental in children, typically from household cleaners such as alkaline drain products. Suicidal, occupational, and iatrogenic exposures are less common.'),
('2d7ba494-2762-52b5-bcb2-1c31a2477f44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common complication of caustic esophageal injury?', '["Esophageal stricture","Fistula formation","Malignant transformation","Perforation","Hemorrhage"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal stricture', 'Esophageal stricture is the most common complication of caustic esophageal injury, arising from deep scarring and fibrosis. Perforation, hemorrhage, fistula, and malignant transformation occur less frequently.'),
('43f61788-3c96-56fb-b4a5-324a9de11bfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of gastric volvulus?', '["Diaphragmatic hernia (especially paraesophageal hernia)","Abnormal gastric ligaments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Connective tissue disorder mediated by specific receptor activation","Wandering spleen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prior gastric surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragmatic hernia (especially paraesophageal hernia)', 'Gastric volvulus is most often secondary to a diaphragmatic hernia, especially a paraesophageal hernia. Abnormal gastric ligaments, connective tissue disease, wandering spleen, and prior surgery are less common associations.'),
('b738963f-e4b5-5cba-b0ed-1883f44b035f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the two types of gastric volvulus?', '["Primary and secondary","Organoaxial (most common) and mesenteroaxial","Complete and partial","Acute and chronic","Longitudinal and transverse"]'::jsonb, 'Organoaxial (most common) and mesenteroaxial', 'Gastric volvulus is classified as organoaxial (most common, around the long axis) or mesenteroaxial. The other listed pairs describe different classification schemes or clinical patterns.'),
('7df9653a-2f55-575b-a41c-5fa41130ce32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of delayed gastric emptying in adults?', '["Viral gastroenteritis (post-viral gastroparesis)","Medications (GLP-1 agonists opiates)","Diabetes mellitus (gastroparesis diabeticorum)","Idiopathic","Post-surgical (vagotomy)"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetes mellitus (gastroparesis diabeticorum)', 'Diabetic gastroparesis is the most common cause of delayed gastric emptying in adults, due to autonomic neuropathy. Post-viral, medication-induced, idiopathic, and post-surgical causes are less common.'),
('1717b21f-3806-5a17-83c0-1613706980c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute gastritis?', '["Alcohol resulting in decreased functional activity","NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs)","Stress gastritis mediated by specific receptor activation","H. pylori infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chemotherapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs)', 'NSAIDs are the most common cause of acute gastritis by inhibiting mucosal prostaglandins and disrupting the gastric barrier. Alcohol, stress, H. pylori, and chemotherapy are less frequent acute triggers.'),
('9f8824bd-fee5-53ad-bd00-102ac73286f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of chronic gastritis?', '["H. pylori infection","Chronic NSAID use","Autoimmune gastritis","Chemicals","Alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'H. pylori infection', 'H. pylori infection is the most common cause of chronic gastritis worldwide, producing antral-predominant inflammation. Chronic NSAID use, autoimmune gastritis, chemicals, and alcohol are less common.'),
('1757ddfb-8109-5145-bbb1-c64afac4aa21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of chronic gastritis?', '["Chemical gastropathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphocytic gastritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic antral (nonautoimmune) gastritis due to H. pylori","Reactive gastropathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic corpus (autoimmune) gastritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic antral (nonautoimmune) gastritis due to H. pylori', 'Chronic antral (nonautoimmune) gastritis due to H. pylori is the most common type of chronic gastritis. Autoimmune corpus gastritis, chemical gastropathy, lymphocytic gastritis, and reactive gastropathy are less frequent.'),
('196873e3-0104-571f-be14-7cfbf64904df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of autoimmune gastritis?', '["Chemotherapy resulting in decreased functional activity","Radiation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type A gastritis (corpus-predominant with anti-parietal cell and anti-intrinsic factor antibodies)","H. pylori infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NSAID use leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type A gastritis (corpus-predominant with anti-parietal cell and anti-intrinsic factor antibodies)', 'Autoimmune gastritis (type A) is caused by autoantibodies against parietal cells and intrinsic factor, producing corpus-predominant atrophic gastritis. H. pylori, NSAIDs, chemotherapy, and radiation are not autoimmune mechanisms.'),
('6e138aa7-da00-57a4-9e8d-19af2eb5c5e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of pernicious anemia?', '["Malabsorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Nutritional deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autoimmune gastritis causing intrinsic factor deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Post-gastrectomy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune gastritis causing intrinsic factor deficiency', 'Pernicious anemia results from autoimmune gastritis that destroys parietal cells, causing intrinsic factor deficiency and impaired vitamin B12 absorption. Nutritional deficiency and post-gastrectomy states are separate causes.'),
('e8192717-87b7-5d7d-81ff-f69e852d9bfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute mesenteric adenitis?', '["Bacterial infection (Yersinia Campylobacter) resulting in decreased functional activity","Fungal infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Viral infection (especially adenovirus and enterovirus)","Parasitic infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Viral infection (especially adenovirus and enterovirus)', 'Acute mesenteric adenitis is most commonly due to viral infection, especially adenovirus and enterovirus, which inflame ileocolic lymph nodes. Bacterial, fungal, and parasitic causes are less common.'),
('615d2daf-afb9-5ab8-b4dd-881049d3acfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of chronic mesenteric adenitis?', '["Malignancy lymphoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Sarcoidosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","INFLAMMATORY bowel disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Histoplasmosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tuberculosis (especially in endemic areas)"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberculosis (especially in endemic areas)', 'Tuberculosis is the most common cause of chronic mesenteric adenitis, especially in endemic areas, producing caseating nodal inflammation. Sarcoidosis, lymphoma, histoplasmosis, and IBD are less common.'),
('6362ceb7-5c85-58ea-a2f2-b2fdcac017e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of malabsorption in adults?', '["Chronic pancreatitis","Bacterial overgrowth","Crohn disease","Celiac disease","Lactose intolerance"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac disease', 'Celiac disease is the most common cause of malabsorption in adults, from gluten-driven enteropathy. Chronic pancreatitis, bacterial overgrowth, Crohn disease, and lactose intolerance are less common.'),
('3dacd89e-6122-5bec-8b61-7a9ee3fd554f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of steatorrhea in adults?', '["Bile acid malabsorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Bacterial overgrowth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Celiac disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic pancreatitis (most common cause of fat malabsorption)","Short bowel syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic pancreatitis (most common cause of fat malabsorption)', 'Chronic pancreatitis is the most common cause of steatorrhea in adults, from pancreatic exocrine insufficiency and reduced lipase delivery. Celiac disease, bile acid malabsorption, bacterial overgrowth, and short bowel syndrome are less common.'),
('22c57548-a723-5589-af51-de10db488c48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is stool fat test used for?', '["Diagnosing infection","Assessing liver function","Diagnosing GI bleeding","Diagnosing steatorrhea (fat malabsorption)","Assessing pancreatic function"]'::jsonb, 'Diagnosing steatorrhea (fat malabsorption)', 'The stool fat (fecal fat) test quantifies fat excretion to diagnose steatorrhea and fat malabsorption. It does not assess liver function, detect infection, evaluate bleeding, or directly measure pancreatic function.'),
('2a4c3f9e-0613-58c8-b015-aba1d8c9ad22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins?', '["Medication-induced resulting in decreased functional activity","Fat malabsorption (pancreatic insufficiency cholestasis bacterial overgrowth celiac disease)","Increased requirement mediated by specific receptor activation","Dietary deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Poor absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fat malabsorption (pancreatic insufficiency cholestasis bacterial overgrowth celiac disease)', 'Deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) most often results from fat malabsorption, as in pancreatic insufficiency, cholestasis, bacterial overgrowth, or celiac disease. Dietary deficiency, increased requirement, and poor absorption are less common mechanisms.'),
('0052b91a-f901-5cbf-9b68-28a5de1747e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common site of duodenal ulcer?', '["Duodenal bulb (anterior wall most common)","Third portion of the duodenum","Pyloric channel","Second portion of the duodenum","Post-bulbar duodenum"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal bulb (anterior wall most common)', 'Duodenal ulcers most commonly occur in the duodenal bulb, typically on the anterior wall, where acid-peptic injury is greatest. The pyloric channel, post-bulbar duodenum, and second or third portions are less frequent sites.'),
('7bc139dc-07ea-5e8c-9f2b-0fb7ad93dabb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of ulcer recurrence after H. pylori eradication?', '["Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","Smoking","Reinfection (rare in developed countries)","Persistent infection (antibiotic resistance)","NSAID use"]'::jsonb, 'Reinfection (rare in developed countries)', 'True reinfection after successful H. pylori eradication is rare in developed countries; most ''recurrences'' reflect persistent infection from antibiotic resistance or NSAID use. Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and smoking are separate ulcer risk factors.'),
('0f9c8b4e-426f-507d-8058-5e350827133e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of death from peptic ulcer disease?', '["Surgical complications resulting in decreased functional activity","PErforation with peritonitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obstruction mediated by specific receptor activation","Penetration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bleeding (hemorrhage) perforation and obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Bleeding (hemorrhage) perforation and obstruction', 'Bleeding, perforation, and obstruction are the lethal complications of peptic ulcer disease, with hemorrhage the leading cause of death. Penetration and surgical complications are less common fatal outcomes.'),
('3daf6f3a-d231-5bd3-a494-c9c93d0f089e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'In GI development, which statement best fits the following fact: the primitive gut tube is formed during which week of development?', '["Week 2 resulting in decreased functional activity","Week 6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Week 3 mediated by specific receptor activation","Week 4 (by folding of the embryo)","Week 5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Week 4 (by folding of the embryo)', 'The primitive gut tube forms during week 4 of development as the trilaminar embryo folds, incorporating the yolk sac to create the endoderm-lined tube. Weeks 2, 3, 5, and 6 do not correspond to this stage of gut tube formation.'),
('15cfd22d-61ce-5bdf-947f-9c5487a64bb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The head and neck develop from which pharyngeal arches?', '["Three pairs resulting in decreased functional activity","Six pairs of pharyngeal arches that appear during week 4-5","Four pairs of pharyngeal arches mediated by specific receptor activation","Seven pairs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Five pairs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Six pairs of pharyngeal arches that appear during week 4-5', 'Six pairs of pharyngeal (branchial) arches appear between weeks 4 and 5 and form the framework from which the head and neck structures develop. The other choices give incorrect numbers of arches.'),
('ae4f09a4-58bb-54be-a1d5-fb9054d1ebef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'Each pharyngeal arch contains which components?', '["Only a nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","An artery nerve cartilage and muscle","Only cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Only muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only an artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An artery nerve cartilage and muscle', 'Each pharyngeal arch is composed of an aortic arch artery, a cranial nerve, a cartilage bar, and muscle together with mesenchyme. The options that mention only a single component are incomplete descriptions of arch composition.'),
('45c38401-22e5-5c7b-9a85-79c10e3e3ca1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The first pharyngeal arch is also called:', '["Mandibular arch","Maxillary arch","Thyroid arch","Laryngeal arch","Hyoid arch"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular arch', 'The first pharyngeal arch divides into maxillary and mandibular processes and is classically called the mandibular arch; its cartilage is Meckel''s cartilage. The hyoid arch is the second arch, and the other names do not label the first arch.'),
('42f82b89-a6c5-5461-b76b-be3c8a074b91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The second pharyngeal arch is also called:', '["Hyoid arch","Mandibular arch","Maxillary arch","Laryngeal arch","Thyroid arch"]'::jsonb, 'Hyoid arch', 'The second pharyngeal arch is the hyoid arch, named for its cartilage (Reichert''s cartilage), which forms hyoid-related structures such as the stylohyoid ligament and lesser horn of the hyoid. The mandibular arch is the first arch.'),
('b5fe14e5-51bb-519a-921f-b44b62d7b9a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The cartilage of the first pharyngeal arch (Meckel''s cartilage) gives rise to which structures?', '["Cricoid cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Mandible incus malleus and sphenomandibular ligament","Laryngeal cartilages mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroid cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyoid bone and stapes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mandible incus malleus and sphenomandibular ligament', 'Meckel''s cartilage of the first arch ossifies to form the mandible plus the malleus and incus, and its dorsal end persists as the sphenomandibular ligament. The laryngeal cartilages derive from the fourth and sixth arches, and the hyoid and stapes from the second and third arches.'),
('eb18baf1-e830-5734-8de8-3495d749efee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The cartilage of the second pharyngeal arch (Reichert''s cartilage) gives rise to which structures?', '["Thyroid cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Cricoid cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stapes styloid process stylohyoid ligament and lesser horn of hyoid","Mandible without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Incus and malleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stapes styloid process stylohyoid ligament and lesser horn of hyoid', 'Reichert''s cartilage of the second arch forms the stapes, styloid process, stylohyoid ligament, and the lesser horn together with the upper part of the hyoid body. The mandible and incus and malleus are first arch derivatives.'),
('3846e067-2c53-555b-a74a-7ae0877dd098'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The cartilage of the third pharyngeal arch gives rise to which structure?', '["Stapes resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arytenoid cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Cricoid cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Greater horn of hyoid bone (and part of body)"]'::jsonb, 'Greater horn of hyoid bone (and part of body)', 'The third arch cartilage gives rise to the greater horn and the lower part of the body of the hyoid bone. The laryngeal cartilages, including thyroid, cricoid, and arytenoid, derive from the fourth and sixth arches.'),
('2b43f29d-68f1-52b8-8b04-34124223e454'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The cartilages of the fourth and sixth arches give rise to which structures?', '["Stapes and incus resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid cricoid arytenoid and other laryngeal cartilages","Maxilla only mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyoid bone only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mandible only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid cricoid arytenoid and other laryngeal cartilages', 'The fourth and sixth arch cartilages fuse to form the thyroid, cricoid, arytenoid, and the remaining laryngeal cartilages. The stapes and incus arise from the second and first arches respectively, and the mandible from the first arch.'),
('0a21e095-99c9-541c-a551-272d1d27b40c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the first pharyngeal arch develop into:', '["Pharyngeal constrictors resulting in decreased functional activity","Mylohyoid anterior belly of digastric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stylohyoid posterior belly of digastric mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscles of facial expression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscles of mastication (temporalis masseter pterygoids) tensor tympani tensor veli palatini"]'::jsonb, 'Muscles of mastication (temporalis masseter pterygoids) tensor tympani tensor veli palatini', 'First arch muscles, innervated by the mandibular division of CN V, include the muscles of mastication, tensor tympani, and tensor veli palatini. Muscles of facial expression belong to the second arch, and the pharyngeal constrictors to the caudal arches.'),
('64b9dd4b-6166-50b3-93bb-7a75725d75e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the second pharyngeal arch develop into:', '["Pharyngeal constrictors resulting in decreased functional activity","Mylohyoid anterior belly of digastric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscles of facial expression stapedius stylohyoid posterior belly of digastric platysma","Intrinsic laryngeal muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscles of mastication leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscles of facial expression stapedius stylohyoid posterior belly of digastric platysma', 'Second arch muscles, all innervated by CN VII, are the muscles of facial expression, stapedius, stylohyoid, posterior belly of the digastric, and platysma. The pharyngeal constrictors and intrinsic laryngeal muscles arise from the third to sixth arches.'),
('49dabe8c-e867-5670-adc7-35d9bc223b7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the third pharyngeal arch develop into:', '["All pharyngeal constrictors resulting in decreased functional activity","Laryngeal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mylohyoid muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Stylopharyngeus (the only muscle of the third arch)","Muscles of mastication leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stylopharyngeus (the only muscle of the third arch)', 'The third arch gives rise to a single muscle, the stylopharyngeus, which is innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve. The pharyngeal constrictors and laryngeal muscles develop from the fourth to sixth arches.'),
('973240dd-ec42-5fa7-9ef4-ecd1971ea5e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the fourth pharyngeal arch develop into:', '["Cricothyroid and intrinsic laryngeal muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Intrinsic laryngeal muscles are from sixth arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stylopharyngeus mediated by specific receptor activation","Following exceptions: tensor veli palatini is from first arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pharyngeal constrictors cricothyroid levator veli palatini (most pharyngeal and soft palate muscles)"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal constrictors cricothyroid levator veli palatini (most pharyngeal and soft palate muscles)', 'The fourth arch produces most pharyngeal and soft palate muscles, including the pharyngeal constrictors, cricothyroid, and levator veli palatini, innervated by the superior laryngeal and pharyngeal plexus nerves. Stylopharyngeus is a third arch muscle, and tensor veli palatini is a first arch muscle.'),
('a7bea571-6d53-50b2-803f-944134e99374'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the sixth pharyngeal arch develop into:', '["Intrinsic laryngeal muscles (except cricothyroid which is fourth arch)","Extraocular muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pharyngeal constrictors mediated by specific receptor activation","Stylopharyngeus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Palatal muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic laryngeal muscles (except cricothyroid which is fourth arch)', 'The sixth arch gives all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid, which is a fourth arch derivative, and these muscles are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Extraocular, pharyngeal, and palatal muscles come from other origins.'),
('31f50973-4721-5a91-bb5f-0bd85b66ac66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The nerves of the first pharyngeal arch are:', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V) - mandibular division V3","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (CN V) - mandibular division V3', 'The first arch is supplied by the mandibular division (V3) of the trigeminal nerve, which innervates first arch muscles and carries their sensation. The facial, glossopharyngeal, and vagus nerves belong to the second, third, and fourth/sixth arches respectively.'),
('dd011571-a4db-511a-8901-82f5f89fac1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The nerves of the second pharyngeal arch are:', '["Facial nerve (CN VII)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (CN VII)', 'The facial nerve is the nerve of the second pharyngeal arch, supplying the muscles of facial expression, stapedius, and the other second arch muscles. The trigeminal, glossopharyngeal, and vagus nerves serve the first, third, and fourth/sixth arches respectively.'),
('87ab120e-e444-5e29-8ff5-70eadcc7b2c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The nerves of the third pharyngeal arch are:', '["Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve is the nerve of the third pharyngeal arch and innervates the stylopharyngeus, its only muscle. The facial nerve supplies the second arch and the trigeminal nerve supplies the first arch.'),
('81a37622-20ae-5c3b-b18c-87ff792f5fc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The nerves of the fourth pharyngeal arch are:', '["Facial nerve (CN VII)","Pharyngeal plexus (CN IX X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Recurrent laryngeal nerve (CN X)","Superior laryngeal branch of vagus nerve (CN X)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior laryngeal branch of vagus nerve (CN X)', 'The fourth arch is innervated by the superior laryngeal branch of the vagus, which supplies structures such as the cricothyroid and the pharyngeal constrictors. The recurrent laryngeal nerve is the nerve of the sixth arch.'),
('34456479-d035-570c-a84b-8981cfe59d77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The nerves of the sixth pharyngeal arch are:', '["Recurrent laryngeal nerve (branch of vagus CN X)","Superior laryngeal nerve (CN X)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent laryngeal nerve (branch of vagus CN X)', 'The sixth arch is supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve, which innervates all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid. The superior laryngeal nerve is the fourth arch nerve, and the facial and glossopharyngeal nerves serve the second and third arches.'),
('aa0b4d7e-948e-57b9-b78e-0c349a272ad0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The pharyngeal pouches are outgrowths of which germ layer?', '["Endoderm (internal lining of the arches)","Neural crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ectoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mixed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Endoderm (internal lining of the arches)', 'The pharyngeal pouches are evaginations of endoderm lining the internal (foregut) surface of the arches. Neural crest cells and ectoderm contribute to the arches and clefts, not to the pouches themselves.'),
('010d17c3-463b-595a-862f-a7667e0ce680'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The first pharyngeal pouch develops into which structure?', '["Auditory (Eustachian) tube and middle ear cavity (tympanic cavity)","Thymus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palatine tonsil mediated by specific receptor activation","Parathyroid glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ultimobranchial body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory (Eustachian) tube and middle ear cavity (tympanic cavity)', 'The first pouch forms the tubotympanic recess, which gives rise to the auditory (Eustachian) tube and the tympanic cavity of the middle ear. The thymus, palatine tonsil, parathyroids, and ultimobranchial body arise from the second to fourth pouches.'),
('b90dfe8f-2f9b-5f48-a119-4e9b4e1f0294'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The second pharyngeal pouch develops into which structure?', '["Auditory tube resulting in decreased functional activity","Parathyroid glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palatine tonsil (the endoderm gives rise to the tonsillar epithelium and crypts)","Thyroid C cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thymus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine tonsil (the endoderm gives rise to the tonsillar epithelium and crypts)', 'The second pouch endoderm forms the tonsillar epithelium and tonsillar crypts, into which lymphoid tissue migrates to build the palatine tonsil. The auditory tube derives from the first pouch, and the thymus, parathyroids, and C cells from the third and fourth pouches.'),
('657fcd8e-1183-5823-a80b-89f260672bf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The third pharyngeal pouch develops into which two structures?', '["Inferior parathyroid gland (dorsal wing) and thymus (ventral wing)","Superior parathyroid and thymus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parathyroid mediated by specific receptor activation","Ultimobranchial body and parathyroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only thymus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior parathyroid gland (dorsal wing) and thymus (ventral wing)', 'The dorsal wing of the third pouch forms the inferior parathyroid gland while the ventral wing forms the thymus. Because the thymus descends and drags the gland with it, the third pouch derivative ends below its fourth pouch counterpart.'),
('2ccf55bd-d071-577d-a54c-754e01c17bd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The fourth pharyngeal pouch develops into which two structures?', '["Superior parathyroid gland (dorsal wing) and ultimobranchial body (ventral wing)","Inferior parathyroid and thymus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parathyroid mediated by specific receptor activation","Ultimobranchial body only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parathyroid and thymus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior parathyroid gland (dorsal wing) and ultimobranchial body (ventral wing)', 'The dorsal wing of the fourth pouch forms the superior parathyroid gland, and the ventral wing forms the ultimobranchial body, which contributes C cells to the thyroid. The thymus and inferior parathyroids are third pouch derivatives.'),
('16fe73fd-82ff-5287-8213-b473df5207a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The ultimobranchial body develops into which cells?', '["Parafollicular C-cells of the thyroid (calcitonin-secreting)","Thymic corpuscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tonsillar crypts mediated by specific receptor activation","Parathyroid chief cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thymic epithelial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parafollicular C-cells of the thyroid (calcitonin-secreting)', 'The ultimobranchial body, from the ventral wing of the fourth pouch, gives rise to the parafollicular C cells of the thyroid that secrete calcitonin. Thymic and tonsillar tissues and parathyroid chief cells derive from other pouch portions.'),
('14873a9f-1736-504c-8b9c-6c0feaa18278'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The pharyngeal clefts (grooves) are invaginations of which germ layer?', '["Ectoderm (between the arches externally)","Mesoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Endoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surface ectoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ectoderm (between the arches externally)', 'The pharyngeal clefts are ectodermal invaginations lying in the grooves between arches on the external surface of the pharyngeal apparatus. The pouches are endodermal, and mesoderm and neural crest form the core of the arches.'),
('d9e1fb9e-639f-5db2-a5f2-8dae449fd063'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The first pharyngeal cleft develops into which structure?', '["Middle ear cavity","Tympanic membrane (ectodermal layer)","External auditory meatus (ear canal)","Auditory tube","Eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 'External auditory meatus (ear canal)', 'The first pharyngeal cleft deepens to form the external auditory meatus, and its ectoderm meets first pouch endoderm to form the tympanic membrane. The auditory tube and middle ear cavity come from the first pouch, not the cleft.'),
('1062f6f9-fb5b-53a5-9451-24983fbcc15e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The second third and fourth pharyngeal clefts normally form which structure?', '["Thymus resulting in decreased functional activity","External auditory meatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The cervical sinus (temporary) that normally obliterates forming the smooth contour of the neck","Pharyngeal tonsils without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parathyroid glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The cervical sinus (temporary) that normally obliterates forming the smooth contour of the neck', 'The second, third, and fourth clefts become covered by the overgrowing second arch and form a temporary cervical sinus that normally obliterates, leaving a smooth neck contour. The thymus, parathyroids, and tonsils arise from the pouches.'),
('1b87fe0f-7059-5053-82c8-d871ddb27980'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'Failure of the cervical sinus to obliterate causes which condition?', '["Cystic hygroma","Branchial cleft cyst (lateral neck cyst)","Lymphangioma","Cervical thymic cyst","Thyroglossal duct cyst"]'::jsonb, 'Branchial cleft cyst (lateral neck cyst)', 'If the cervical sinus fails to obliterate, a branchial cleft cyst persists as a lateral neck cyst along the sternocleidomastoid. A thyroglossal duct cyst is a midline lesion, and cystic hygroma and lymphangioma are lymphatic malformations.'),
('bf2f9495-e9e4-54dc-9750-cc5cd06f2ce1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common type of branchial cleft cyst?', '["Second branchial cleft cyst (most common) presenting along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid","Cervical sinus remnant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First branchial cleft (around ear) mediated by specific receptor activation","Third branchial cleft (lower neck) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fourth branchial cleft (near thyroid) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Second branchial cleft cyst (most common) presenting along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid', 'The second branchial cleft cyst is the most frequent type and presents along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. First cleft remnants appear around the ear, while third and fourth cleft cysts are rare.'),
('3817131a-22f2-5706-93db-3fa148fc6ae0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common location of a branchial cleft cyst?', '["Midline neck resulting in decreased functional activity","Supraclavicular fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the angle of the mandible mediated by specific receptor activation","Along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid (upper to middle third)","Behind the ear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid (upper to middle third)', 'Branchial cleft cysts most often lie along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid in its upper to middle third. Midline, supraclavicular, and postauricular sites are not typical locations for these lateral neck lesions.'),
('28f7c790-c3e8-57ec-b448-9b0a539f07ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is a preauricular sinus?', '["Epidermal inclusion cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Dermoid cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A small pit or sinus anterior to the external ear due to incomplete fusion of the first and second arch tubercles","Remnant of the first branchial cleft without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Remnant of the second branchial cleft leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small pit or sinus anterior to the external ear due to incomplete fusion of the first and second arch tubercles', 'A preauricular sinus is a small pit just anterior to the external ear produced by incomplete fusion of the auricular hillocks of the first and second arches. It is distinct from dermoid cysts and branchial cleft remnants.'),
('25a83f0d-c5b8-5523-ad1e-2b45f83a1116'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The external ear (auricle) develops from which structures?', '["The first cleft only resulting in decreased functional activity","The second cleft only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Six auricular hillocks from the first and second pharyngeal arches","The third arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The first pouch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Six auricular hillocks from the first and second pharyngeal arches', 'The auricle develops from six auricular hillocks that appear around the first cleft, contributed by the first and second pharyngeal arches. Clefs and pouches alone do not form the auricle.'),
('45b19b71-ae7b-508a-b5fc-a060da5049cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The tongue develops from which pharyngeal arches?', '["First arch (anterior 2/3) and third arch (posterior 1/3) plus copula and hypobranchial eminence from second fourth arches","Second arch only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Third and fourth arches only mediated by specific receptor activation","First arch only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fifth and sixth arches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First arch (anterior 2/3) and third arch (posterior 1/3) plus copula and hypobranchial eminence from second fourth arches', 'The anterior two-thirds of the tongue develops from first arch swellings, the posterior one-third from the third arch, and the copula and hypobranchial eminence (second, third, and fourth arches) contribute the remainder. The other options omit most of these contributing primordia.'),
('f9bd07b5-b1af-532e-9941-5a31134aba7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The motor nerve supply to the tongue muscles (except palatoglossus) is which nerve?', '["Vagus nerve (CN X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)', 'All intrinsic and most extrinsic tongue muscles are supplied by the hypoglossal nerve, with palatoglossus innervated by the vagus through the pharyngeal plexus. CN V, VII, IX, and X are not the motor nerves of the tongue muscles.'),
('d08403e1-fde2-5299-81c7-5aa5ff0dfb89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The sensory innervation of the anterior 2/3 of the tongue is which nerve?', '["Vagus nerve (CN X) resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve (CN VII) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) mediated by specific receptor activation","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lingual nerve (CN V3) for general sensation and chorda tympani (CN VII) for taste"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual nerve (CN V3) for general sensation and chorda tympani (CN VII) for taste', 'The anterior two-thirds of the tongue receives general sensation from the lingual nerve (CN V3) and taste from the chorda tympani (CN VII). The glossopharyngeal nerve serves the posterior third, and the hypoglossal nerve is purely motor.'),
('7d2131ac-c383-572e-b812-38127c167e55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The sensory innervation of the posterior 1/3 of the tongue is which nerve?', '["Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) resulting in decreased functional activity","Vagus nerve (CN X) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial nerve (CN VII) mediated by specific receptor activation","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) for both general sensation and taste","Lingual nerve (CN V3) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) for both general sensation and taste', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve supplies both general sensation and taste to the posterior one-third of the tongue. The lingual nerve covers the anterior two-thirds for general sensation, and CN XII is purely motor.'),
('b6dbbfb8-4759-561d-8388-db00b1a52dae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The taste buds of the epiglottis and pharynx are innervated by which nerve?', '["Facial nerve (CN VII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Superior laryngeal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Vagus nerve (CN X)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve (CN X)', 'Taste buds on the epiglottis and pharynx are innervated by the vagus nerve through its internal laryngeal and pharyngeal branches. The glossopharyngeal nerve serves the posterior tongue, and the facial nerve the anterior tongue taste buds.'),
('a4c39c72-0567-591e-b5f6-a871cda1181d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the tongue?', '["Microglossia (small tongue)","Macroglossia (enlarged tongue)","Ankyloglossia (tongue-tie a short lingual frenulum)","Lingual thyroid","Bifid tongue (cleft tongue)"]'::jsonb, 'Ankyloglossia (tongue-tie a short lingual frenulum)', 'Ankyloglossia, or tongue-tie, results from a short lingual frenulum and is the most common congenital tongue anomaly. The other listed conditions, such as macroglossia and bifid tongue, are much rarer developmental defects.'),
('e202e31e-26d2-5700-91b3-313e49fed104'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is a lingual thyroid?', '["Pyramidal lobe of the thyroid resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroglossal duct cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroid nodule mediated by specific receptor activation","Ectopic thyroid tissue at the base of the tongue resulting from failure of the thyroid to descend from the foramen cecum","Thyroid cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ectopic thyroid tissue at the base of the tongue resulting from failure of the thyroid to descend from the foramen cecum', 'A lingual thyroid is ectopic thyroid tissue at the tongue base that occurs when the gland fails to descend from the foramen cecum. A thyroglossal duct cyst is a cystic epithelial remnant of the duct, not functioning thyroid tissue.'),
('b893c9fa-e843-54c2-973e-fe5b3a5a4bec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The thyroid gland develops from which structure?', '["Thyroglossal duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Third pharyngeal pouch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fourth pharyngeal pouch mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen cecum at the base of the tongue (median endodermal thickening between the first and second arches)","Ultimobranchial body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen cecum at the base of the tongue (median endodermal thickening between the first and second arches)', 'The thyroid begins as a median endodermal thickening at the foramen cecum of the tongue and descends through the thyroglossal duct to its final position. The parathyroids arise from the third and fourth pouches, and the ultimobranchial body contributes C cells.'),
('a6032dff-5c02-5a65-9b5d-c25af2f6dfb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The thyroid gland descends from the foramen cecum to its final position passing anterior to which structure?', '["Trachea resulting in decreased functional activity","Carotid artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Esophagus mediated by specific receptor activation","Jugular vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyoid bone and laryngeal cartilages"]'::jsonb, 'Hyoid bone and laryngeal cartilages', 'The developing thyroid passes anterior to the hyoid bone and the laryngeal cartilages as it descends toward its final pretracheal position. This course explains how a persistent duct remnant can produce a cyst at or below the hyoid.'),
('833b6b5e-1c0d-55dc-a8b6-754ef3686df1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the thyroglossal duct?', '["The lymphatic channel of the thyroid resulting in decreased functional activity","The duct between parathyroid glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The epithelial tract along which the thyroid descends from the foramen cecum to the neck","The duct that connects the thyroid to the thymus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The blood supply of the thyroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The epithelial tract along which the thyroid descends from the foramen cecum to the neck', 'The thyroglossal duct is the epithelial tract that guides the thyroid from the foramen cecum to the neck and normally involutes. Persistent remnants of this tract form midline thyroglossal duct cysts.'),
('1acdb458-7c45-50ee-bab2-16293ce610ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the typical location of a thyroglossal duct cyst?', '["Parotid region resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral neck along the SCM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Submandibular triangle mediated by specific receptor activation","Supraclavicular fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midline at or near the hyoid bone (but can be anywhere from foramen cecum to thyroid)"]'::jsonb, 'Midline at or near the hyoid bone (but can be anywhere from foramen cecum to thyroid)', 'A thyroglossal duct cyst lies in the midline, most often at or near the hyoid bone, and can occur anywhere along the descent path from the foramen cecum to the thyroid. Lateral neck masses along the SCM point instead to a branchial cleft cyst.'),
('6eedc10b-5d92-54ea-8bd8-c2062fd6a9f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the Sistrunk procedure?', '["Resection of lingual thyroid resulting in decreased functional activity","Parathyroidectomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroid lobectomy mediated by specific receptor activation","Neck dissection for branchial cleft cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgical excision of a thyroglossal duct cyst including the entire duct tract and mid-portion of the hyoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical excision of a thyroglossal duct cyst including the entire duct tract and mid-portion of the hyoid bone', 'The Sistrunk procedure removes the thyroglossal duct cyst together with the entire duct tract and the central part of the hyoid bone, which prevents recurrence from retained duct remnants. Simple cyst excision alone tends to recur because duct tissue is left behind.'),
('54765686-ae2f-5e30-b05b-22d2368f7142'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The superior parathyroid glands develop from which pouch?', '["Fourth pharyngeal pouch (dorsal wing)","Third pharyngeal pouch (dorsal wing)","Ultimobranchial body","Second pharyngeal pouch","First pharyngeal pouch"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth pharyngeal pouch (dorsal wing)', 'The superior parathyroid glands develop from the dorsal wing of the fourth pouch. Because third pouch derivatives descend further, the fourth pouch parathyroids end up in a higher position than the inferior glands.'),
('8f484743-5735-5f5c-9c93-99f547b4834b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The inferior parathyroid glands develop from which pouch?', '["First pharyngeal pouch","Fourth pharyngeal pouch (dorsal wing)","Second pharyngeal pouch","Ultimobranchial body","Third pharyngeal pouch (dorsal wing)"]'::jsonb, 'Third pharyngeal pouch (dorsal wing)', 'The inferior parathyroid glands arise from the dorsal wing of the third pouch and descend with the thymus to end below the superior glands. Their hypoplasia in DiGeorge syndrome explains the hypocalcemia of that condition.'),
('194c083b-9f7c-5300-9dcb-e1a660093265'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion) results in abnormal development of which structures?', '["Thyroid and parathyroid only resulting in decreased functional activity","Thymus parathyroids and conotruncal heart (due to abnormal neural crest contribution to third and fourth pouches)","The cervical sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","The entire pharyngeal apparatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","First and second arch derivatives leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus parathyroids and conotruncal heart (due to abnormal neural crest contribution to third and fourth pouches)', 'Deletion of 22q11.2 disrupts neural crest migration into the third and fourth pouches, causing thymic and parathyroid hypoplasia together with conotruncal cardiac defects. First and second arch derivatives and the cervical sinus are not the main targets of the defect.'),
('3d5408fe-bcc0-5da6-ab7c-9257056787f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What are the classic features of DiGeorge syndrome?', '["Cardiac anomalies (conotruncal) thymic aplasia hypocalcemia from parathyroid hypoplasia and characteristic facies","Coarctation of the aorta and webbed neck accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Polydactyly and cystic kidney disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Macroglossia omphalocele neonatal hypoglycemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cleft lip and palate and congenital heart disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac anomalies (conotruncal) thymic aplasia hypocalcemia from parathyroid hypoplasia and characteristic facies', 'The classic triad of DiGeorge syndrome comprises congenital heart disease, thymic hypoplasia or aplasia with immune deficiency, and parathyroid hypoplasia causing hypocalcemia, plus a characteristic facial appearance. The other options describe features of different syndromes.'),
('c169dc8c-9f13-5cca-a72a-20222ffabfe0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of death in DiGeorge syndrome?', '["Severe hypocalcemia (seizures) resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe congenital heart disease (especially truncus arteriosus and interrupted aortic arch) most common cause","Malignancy (lymphoma) mediated by specific receptor activation","Aspiration from cleft palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sepsis from immunodeficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe congenital heart disease (especially truncus arteriosus and interrupted aortic arch) most common cause', 'Severe conotruncal heart defects, particularly truncus arteriosus and interrupted aortic arch, are the leading cause of death in DiGeorge syndrome. Hypocalcemia, sepsis, and aspiration are less common causes of mortality.'),
('6b414edc-82aa-5547-8b40-7a240fa0e533'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The face develops from which structures?', '["Frontonasal prominence maxillary prominences and mandibular prominences","Only the frontonasal process accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All six arches equally mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the first pharyngeal arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the second pharyngeal arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontonasal prominence maxillary prominences and mandibular prominences', 'The face forms from the unpaired frontonasal prominence and the paired maxillary and mandibular prominences, which merge around the stomodeum. The remaining choices do not reflect the true contribution of these facial primordia.'),
('c65e0556-48bd-5261-b95c-9b7a2e72202c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The frontonasal prominence gives rise to which structures?', '["Mandible and lower lip resulting in decreased functional activity","Ears accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Forehead bridge of nose and medial and lateral nasal prominences","Cheeks without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maxilla and zygoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Forehead bridge of nose and medial and lateral nasal prominences', 'The frontonasal prominence gives rise to the forehead, the bridge of the nose, and the medial and lateral nasal prominences that form the dorsum and alae of the nose. The mandible, lower lip, maxilla, and zygoma arise from the pharyngeal arch prominences instead.'),
('5046825d-a69b-58bb-9b9d-075c9c5f76b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The maxillary prominences give rise to which structures?', '["Maxilla zygomatic bone lateral upper lip and secondary palate","Philtrum portion of upper lip accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mandible and lower lip mediated by specific receptor activation","Nasal bridge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cheeks and eyelids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla zygomatic bone lateral upper lip and secondary palate', 'The maxillary prominences form the maxilla, zygomatic bone, lateral parts of the upper lip, and the secondary palate. The philtrum is a derivative of the intermaxillary segment, while the mandible and lower lip come from the mandibular prominences.'),
('7038fdcb-9d4c-57ec-a7cb-1aa1f40d2751'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The mandibular prominences give rise to which structures?', '["Nasal bones","Mandible and lower lip","Maxilla","Cheeks","Upper lip"]'::jsonb, 'Mandible and lower lip', 'The mandibular prominences fuse across the midline to form the mandible and the lower lip. The maxilla and upper lip are produced by the maxillary prominences and the intermaxillary segment.'),
('196faaf2-df37-56ed-a844-34fc95dd2f1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The intermaxillary segment (primary palate) gives rise to which structures?', '["The nasal septum resulting in decreased functional activity","Philtrum of upper lip premaxilla (four incisors) and primary palate","The entire upper lip mediated by specific receptor activation","The mandible without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The secondary palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Philtrum of upper lip premaxilla (four incisors) and primary palate', 'The intermaxillary segment of the primary palate forms the philtrum of the upper lip, the premaxilla carrying the four incisor teeth, and the primary palate. The secondary palate develops separately from the palatal shelves of the maxillary prominences.'),
('8c52e8d4-c535-59bd-944c-4234f7064391'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common type of cleft lip?', '["Cleft lip with cleft palate resulting in decreased functional activity","Bilateral cleft lip accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midline cleft mediated by specific receptor activation","Unilateral cleft lip (most common left side more than right)","Median cleft lip leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unilateral cleft lip (most common left side more than right)', 'Unilateral cleft lip is the most common type and occurs more frequently on the left side than the right. Bilateral and median clefts are far less common.'),
('4d2b8a0e-3991-50e7-ba52-7e14497fef54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common congenital facial anomaly?', '["Hypertelorism resulting in decreased functional activity","Micrognathia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cleft lip with or without cleft palate (1 in 700-1000 live births)","Craniosynostosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cleft palate alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cleft lip with or without cleft palate (1 in 700-1000 live births)', 'Cleft lip with or without cleft palate is the most common congenital facial anomaly, occurring in about 1 in 700-1000 live births. Isolated cleft palate alone is considerably less frequent, and the other listed anomalies are rarer.'),
('e56a40db-c05d-5aca-87b3-9d7559c84597'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common risk factor for cleft lip/palate?', '["Maternal diabetes resulting in decreased functional activity","Positive family history (genetic predisposition with environmental modifiers)","Corticosteroid use in pregnancy mediated by specific receptor activation","Maternal alcohol use without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maternal smoking leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Positive family history (genetic predisposition with environmental modifiers)', 'A positive family history reflecting genetic predisposition is the most important risk factor for cleft lip and palate, with environmental factors acting as modifiers. Maternal smoking, alcohol, and corticosteroid use contribute much smaller risks.'),
('00ad7144-87c6-559b-8388-907bb31d5c83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common genetic syndrome associated with Pierre Robin sequence?', '["Fetal alcohol syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Treacher Collins syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stickler syndrome (COL2A1 mutation) most common","Nager syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","22q11.2 deletion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stickler syndrome (COL2A1 mutation) most common', 'Stickler syndrome, caused by COL2A1 mutations, is the most common genetic syndrome associated with Pierre Robin sequence. Treacher Collins, Nager, and 22q11.2 deletion syndromes are also associated but less common.'),
('28781d59-2c10-52cc-944e-b9bb8cacd410'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The mandible develops from which structure?', '["Second pharyngeal arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontonasal prominence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First pharyngeal arch (Meckel''s cartilage and membranous ossification)","The paraxial mesoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maxillary prominence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First pharyngeal arch (Meckel''s cartilage and membranous ossification)', 'The mandible develops from the first pharyngeal arch, where Meckel''s cartilage forms a temporary template and the body and ramus ossify by intramembranous (membranous) ossification. The second arch and frontonasal prominence do not contribute to the mandible.'),
('79baee38-1dea-5517-8e1f-fe0015581c28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The maxilla develops from which structure?', '["Palatal shelves resulting in decreased functional activity","Second pharyngeal arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mandibular prominence mediated by specific receptor activation","Maxillary prominence of the first pharyngeal arch","Frontonasal prominence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary prominence of the first pharyngeal arch', 'The maxilla develops by intramembranous ossification within the maxillary prominence of the first pharyngeal arch. The mandibular prominence forms the lower jaw, and the palatal shelves give rise to the secondary palate.'),
('0d2fc911-2377-5387-b0c8-18fc9fa7918f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The palatine bones develop from which structure?', '["Mandibular prominence resulting in decreased functional activity","Palatal shelves (secondary palate) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maxillary prominence (as part of the developing palate)","Frontonasal prominence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Zygomatic process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary prominence (as part of the developing palate)', 'The palatine bones develop from the maxillary prominence as part of the developing palate. The mandibular prominence and frontonasal prominence form the lower jaw and midface structures respectively, not the palatine bones.'),
('33439421-2c1c-5174-8504-9221d0ded408'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The secondary palate forms from which structures?', '["Frontonasal prominence resulting in decreased functional activity","Palatine tonsils accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary palate (intermaxillary segment) mediated by specific receptor activation","Mandibular prominence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Palatal shelves (lateral palatine processes) from the maxillary prominences"]'::jsonb, 'Palatal shelves (lateral palatine processes) from the maxillary prominences', 'The secondary palate forms from the palatal shelves (lateral palatine processes) that grow medially from the maxillary prominences and fuse in the midline. The primary palate arises separately from the intermaxillary segment.'),
('c2d6d4b3-184d-5345-b617-8ea9147986a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The palatal shelves fuse at which week of development?', '["Week 12-14 resulting in decreased functional activity","Week 6-7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Week 14-16 mediated by specific receptor activation","Week 4-5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Week 8-12 (around 10th week)"]'::jsonb, 'Week 8-12 (around 10th week)', 'The palatal shelves fuse between the 8th and 12th weeks, typically around the 10th week. Fusion cannot occur at weeks 4-7 because the shelves have not yet grown or elevated by then.'),
('187d5db0-9828-542f-aeb9-3c451d9d2b0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most important determinant of palatal shelf elevation?', '["The timing of tongue descent (the tongue must move down to allow shelf elevation)","The growth of the shelves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pressure of amniotic fluid mediated by specific receptor activation","Maternal hormones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The presence of the Meckel''s cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The timing of tongue descent (the tongue must move down to allow shelf elevation)', 'Descent of the tongue out of the nasal cavity is the crucial event that allows the palatal shelves to change from a vertical to a horizontal position and then fuse. Shelf growth, amniotic fluid, and hormones play only minor contributory roles.'),
('60953183-dc9e-56cd-8045-940333f7aea6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is torus palatinus?', '["A cleft palate resulting in decreased functional activity","A palatal cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A palatal tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign bony exostosis on the midline of the hard palate (a common normal variant)","Ankyloglossia of the palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign bony exostosis on the midline of the hard palate (a common normal variant)', 'Torus palatinus is a benign bony exostosis on the midline of the hard palate and represents a common normal variant that requires no treatment. It is not a cleft, cyst, or neoplasm.'),
('d8a81f56-6008-5111-9538-bd5a4a53c4a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common location of a torus mandibularis?', '["Lingual aspect of the mandible above the mylohyoid line (premolar region)","Buccal aspect of the mandible accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midline of the mandible mediated by specific receptor activation","Ramus of the mandible without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Symphysis menti leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual aspect of the mandible above the mylohyoid line (premolar region)', 'Torus mandibularis characteristically appears on the lingual aspect of the mandible above the mylohyoid line near the premolar region. The buccal surface, ramus, and symphysis are not the typical sites.'),
('485e1ab2-2806-5105-950e-98605dd758bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The muscles of facial expression are derived from which pharyngeal arch?', '["Third pharyngeal arch","First pharyngeal arch","Second pharyngeal arch (hyoid arch)","Fourth pharyngeal arch","Sixth pharyngeal arch"]'::jsonb, 'Second pharyngeal arch (hyoid arch)', 'The muscles of facial expression develop from the mesenchyme of the second pharyngeal arch (hyoid arch) and are therefore supplied by its nerve, CN VII. The first arch forms the muscles of mastication, and the third and fourth/sixth arches supply pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles.'),
('54455d35-3e41-5a8f-8d35-46b0fdba7cf6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The nerve supply to the muscles of facial expression is which nerve?', '["Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Vagus nerve (CN X)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (CN VII)', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) supplies all the muscles of facial expression. CN V provides sensation to the face and motor supply to the muscles of mastication, while the other listed nerves serve different cranial territories.'),
('55ab90e7-8448-5080-b033-b9c928107564'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The muscles of mastication are derived from which pharyngeal arch?', '["Second pharyngeal arch","Third pharyngeal arch","Fourth pharyngeal arch","Sixth pharyngeal arch","First pharyngeal arch (mandibular arch)"]'::jsonb, 'First pharyngeal arch (mandibular arch)', 'The muscles of mastication (masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids) derive from the first pharyngeal arch (mandibular arch) and are innervated by its nerve, CN V3. The second arch forms the muscles of facial expression instead.'),
('d3850038-6752-5531-821e-9a6be4b14157'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The nerve supply to the muscles of mastication is which nerve?', '["Facial nerve (CN VII)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve (CN V3)"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve (CN V3)', 'The mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3) supplies all the muscles of mastication. CN VII serves the muscles of facial expression, and the other listed nerves supply muscles of the pharynx and tongue.'),
('2d8d9808-c78d-5481-9173-b70543b3d4e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the temporomandibular joint (TMJ)?', '["The articulation between the mandibular condyle and the temporal bone (glenoid fossa and articular eminence)","The joint of the cheek bones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The articulation of the sphenoid bone mediated by specific receptor activation","The joint of the hyoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The articulation between the maxilla and mandible leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The articulation between the mandibular condyle and the temporal bone (glenoid fossa and articular eminence)', 'The TMJ is a synovial joint formed between the condyle of the mandible and the temporal bone at the glenoid (mandibular) fossa and articular eminence. The cheek bones and hyoid bone do not participate in this joint, and the maxilla and mandible do not directly articulate.'),
('ea0cf082-809d-5558-a253-d3ce44203804'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common TMJ disorder in children?', '["Congenital malformation resulting in decreased functional activity","Temporomandibular joint dislocation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal derangement (disc displacement) mediated by specific receptor activation","Arthritis (JIA-related) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myofascial pain dysfunction (muscle-related TMJ pain) most common in adolescents"]'::jsonb, 'Myofascial pain dysfunction (muscle-related TMJ pain) most common in adolescents', 'Myofascial pain dysfunction, a muscle-related TMJ pain condition, is the most common TMJ disorder and is seen most often in adolescents. Dislocation, internal derangement, and JIA-related arthritis are all much less frequent.'),
('6330fda3-8316-5e6d-9c3c-fc513ad2e0c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The salivary glands develop from which structure?', '["Neural crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Oral ectoderm (epithelial buds from the stomodeum)","Surface ectoderm of the face mediated by specific receptor activation","Pharyngeal endoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oral ectoderm (epithelial buds from the stomodeum)', 'The salivary glands arise as epithelial buds from the oral ectoderm (stomodeum) that grow into the underlying mesenchyme and canalize to form ducts and acini. Neural crest and mesoderm supply connective tissue but not the glandular epithelium.'),
('8828ac1d-e348-5364-8e83-e1667f04b338'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The parotid gland is the first salivary gland to develop and appears at which week?', '["Week 10 resulting in decreased functional activity","Week 12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Week 8 mediated by specific receptor activation","Week 6 (budding from the ectoderm near the angle of the mouth)","Week 4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Week 6 (budding from the ectoderm near the angle of the mouth)', 'The parotid gland is the first salivary gland to develop, appearing at week 6 as ectodermal budding near the angle of the mouth. The sublingual gland is the last, appearing around week 8.'),
('1a829f84-5f8f-570a-9ef3-e3c24e5add10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The parotid gland is located:', '["Floor of the mouth resulting in decreased functional activity","Sublingual space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pterygopalatine fossa mediated by specific receptor activation","In front of the ear superficial to the masseter muscle","Below the mandible leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In front of the ear superficial to the masseter muscle', 'The parotid gland lies in front of the ear, superficial to the masseter muscle and over the ramus of the mandible. The floor of the mouth and sublingual space house the submandibular and sublingual glands rather than the parotid.'),
('50a64bdb-27b6-5e64-96e6-a7e078396e94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The submandibular gland develops at which week?', '["Week 12 resulting in decreased functional activity","Week 8 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Week 10 mediated by specific receptor activation","Week 6 (from the floor of the mouth near the developing tongue)","Week 4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Week 6 (from the floor of the mouth near the developing tongue)', 'The submandibular gland develops at week 6 from the floor of the mouth near the developing tongue. The parotid also appears at week 6, but the sublingual gland develops later at week 8.'),
('71ddfb50-7e59-5ddc-ab50-a3ea2b0988ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The sublingual gland is the last to develop and appears at which week?', '["Week 6 resulting in decreased functional activity","Week 4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Week 10 mediated by specific receptor activation","Week 8 (from multiple buds in the floor of the mouth)","Week 12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Week 8 (from multiple buds in the floor of the mouth)', 'The sublingual gland is the last salivary gland to develop, appearing at week 8 from multiple buds in the floor of the mouth. The parotid and submandibular glands appear earlier, at week 6.'),
('865e4f29-94e1-5218-832f-6965e1d10b6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common salivary gland tumor overall?', '["Pleomorphic adenoma (benign mixed tumor) overall most common","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma (most common malignant)","Acinic cell carcinoma","Adenoid cystic carcinoma","Warthin tumor (adenolymphoma) second most common"]'::jsonb, 'Pleomorphic adenoma (benign mixed tumor) overall most common', 'Pleomorphic adenoma, a benign mixed tumor, is the most common salivary gland neoplasm overall. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the most common malignant salivary gland tumor.'),
('37026616-e2d4-513e-bf01-3b8c0e604dc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common malignant salivary gland tumor in children?', '["Adenoid cystic carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenocarcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acinic cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Squamous cell carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma (most common malignant in children and adults)"]'::jsonb, 'Mucoepidermoid carcinoma (most common malignant in children and adults)', 'Mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the most common malignant salivary gland tumor in children as well as in adults. The other listed malignant tumors are all less frequent in the pediatric age group.'),
('ad860b6f-1685-54b7-81ba-f66c2ede835d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common location of pleomorphic adenoma?', '["Parotid gland (superficial lobe most common)","Sublingual gland","Minor salivary glands (palate)","Submandibular gland","Labial salivary glands"]'::jsonb, 'Parotid gland (superficial lobe most common)', 'Pleomorphic adenoma most commonly arises in the parotid gland, particularly its superficial lobe. The submandibular gland and minor salivary glands of the palate are less frequent primary sites.'),
('a7bbd971-837b-5670-9705-6c7e90a0c2ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common site of sialolithiasis (salivary duct stones)?', '["Minor salivary glands","Sublingual gland (Bartholin''s duct)","Submandibular gland (Wharton''s duct) most common (80-90%)","Parotid and submandibular equally","Parotid gland (Stensen''s duct)"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland (Wharton''s duct) most common (80-90%)', 'Sialolithiasis most often affects the submandibular gland via Wharton''s duct (80-90% of cases) because its saliva is more viscous and the duct ascends against gravity. The parotid Stensen''s duct is a much less common site.'),
('5eb29def-dcf3-5fa4-b3bd-762f8cee1ade'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of parotitis in children?', '["Mumps (viral parotitis paramyxovirus) most common infectious cause","HIV-associated parotid enlargement","Sjogren syndrome (autoimmune)","Acute bacterial parotitis (Staph aureus)","Recurrent parotitis of childhood (juvenile recurrent parotitis)"]'::jsonb, 'Mumps (viral parotitis paramyxovirus) most common infectious cause', 'Mumps, caused by a paramyxovirus, is the most common infectious cause of parotitis in children. Bacterial parotitis, juvenile recurrent parotitis, and autoimmune causes are all less common.'),
('a0febab8-7084-5103-bfd9-38f17d5f749f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The optic nerve (CN II) develops from which structure?', '["Neural crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Outpouching of the diencephalon (forebrain)","Surface ectoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic vesicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Outpouching of the diencephalon (forebrain)', 'The optic nerve forms from an outpouching of the diencephalon (forebrain) and is therefore a central nervous system tract rather than a peripheral nerve. Surface ectoderm gives rise to the lens, and neural crest contributes other ocular structures, not the optic nerve.'),
('c456b459-4c46-52be-b02d-dbc676797d28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The optic nerve is surrounded by which meninges?', '["Only dura mater resulting in decreased functional activity","Dura and arachnoid only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachnoid and pia only mediated by specific receptor activation","All three meninges (pia arachnoid dura) and CSF space (subarachnoid)","Only pia mater leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All three meninges (pia arachnoid dura) and CSF space (subarachnoid)', 'The optic nerve is surrounded by all three meninges (pia, arachnoid, and dura), and the subarachnoid space containing CSF is continuous with that of the brain. This is why raised intracranial pressure produces papilledema at the optic disc.'),
('50c409ca-f53f-5fed-af22-7526baa53cc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is papilledema?', '["Swelling of the optic disc due to increased intracranial pressure","Drusen of the optic disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Optic atrophy mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic neuritis (inflammation) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glaucomatous cupping leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Swelling of the optic disc due to increased intracranial pressure', 'Papilledema is swelling of the optic disc caused by raised intracranial pressure transmitted along the subarachnoid space of the optic nerve sheath. Optic neuritis, drusen, atrophy, and glaucomatous cupping are separate and distinct entities.'),
('146c5a4f-54ed-5c47-9f1d-281ffe971c73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The trigeminal nerve (CN V) is the nerve of which pharyngeal arch?', '["First pharyngeal arch","Third pharyngeal arch","Fourth pharyngeal arch","Second pharyngeal arch","Sixth pharyngeal arch"]'::jsonb, 'First pharyngeal arch', 'The trigeminal nerve (CN V) is the nerve of the first pharyngeal arch, supplying the muscles of mastication derived from that arch. Each arch has its own nerve: V for the first, VII for the second, IX for the third, and X for the fourth and sixth.'),
('10c3200e-e77c-5e53-afe7-e2c5e8899d45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What are the three divisions of the trigeminal nerve?', '["Superior middle inferior","Frontal nasal lacrimal","Ophthalmic (V1) maxillary (V2) mandibular (V3)","Anterior posterior lateral","Ophthalmic maxillary facial"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic (V1) maxillary (V2) mandibular (V3)', 'The trigeminal nerve divides into three branches: ophthalmic (V1), maxillary (V2), and mandibular (V3). V1 and V2 are purely sensory, while V3 also carries motor fibers for the muscles of mastication.'),
('7f80fde6-fac4-5b18-98a4-58933186898f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve is purely:', '["Autonomic","Sensory (afferent)","Motor (efferent)","Parasympathetic","Mixed (sensory and motor)"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory (afferent)', 'The ophthalmic division (V1) is purely sensory, carrying general sensation from the eye, forehead, and nose. Only the mandibular division (V3) carries motor fibers for the muscles of mastication.'),
('1e1858ac-90b4-5f02-9731-3dbc2230c3b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) is the nerve of which pharyngeal arch?', '["Fourth pharyngeal arch","First pharyngeal arch","Sixth pharyngeal arch","Third pharyngeal arch","Second pharyngeal arch"]'::jsonb, 'Second pharyngeal arch', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) is the nerve of the second pharyngeal arch (hyoid arch) and supplies the muscles derived from that arch, namely the muscles of facial expression. The first arch is served by CN V and the third arch by CN IX.'),
('1723a4a5-a6d2-5330-91a1-1b9cc31e4ed5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) has which functions?', '["Motor and sensory to the face resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor to muscles of facial expression taste to anterior 2/3 of tongue and parasympathetic to submandibular sublingual and lacrimal glands","Sensory only mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor to mastication without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","taste to posterior tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor to muscles of facial expression taste to anterior 2/3 of tongue and parasympathetic to submandibular sublingual and lacrimal glands', 'The facial nerve carries motor fibers to the muscles of facial expression, taste sensation (special sensory) from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and parasympathetic secretomotor fibers to the submandibular, sublingual, and lacrimal glands. Sensation to the face is provided by CN V, and taste from the posterior tongue travels through the glossopharyngeal nerve.'),
('3bb64bb3-0811-5221-8f2a-787e219a8844'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is Bell palsy?', '["Idiopathic acute peripheral facial nerve palsy (presumed viral reactivation HSV-1)","Ramsay Hunt syndrome (VZV) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lyme disease facial palsy mediated by specific receptor activation","Guillain-Barré syndrome facial palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central facial nerve palsy (stroke) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic acute peripheral facial nerve palsy (presumed viral reactivation HSV-1)', 'Bell palsy is an idiopathic acute peripheral facial nerve palsy, probably triggered by HSV-1 reactivation in the facial nerve. Ramsay Hunt syndrome (VZV), Lyme disease, and stroke cause facial palsy through different mechanisms.'),
('3e483f6a-1448-56e4-854b-4d0e9ee2a72a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is Ramsay Hunt syndrome?', '["Bell palsy caused by HSV resulting in decreased functional activity","HZV dermatomal zoster without facial palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Varicella-zoster virus reactivation in the geniculate ganglion causing facial palsy with auricular vesicles","Lyme disease with facial palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Post-vaccinal facial palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Varicella-zoster virus reactivation in the geniculate ganglion causing facial palsy with auricular vesicles', 'Ramsay Hunt syndrome results from varicella-zoster virus reactivation in the geniculate ganglion, producing facial palsy together with herpetic vesicles on the auricle. It is caused by VZV, not HSV, and is not a purely sensory zoster.'),
('ffb268e3-dcc0-5a51-9799-b8ff7b211db7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'In Bell palsy the facial nerve is affected in which segment?', '["Mastoid segment resulting in decreased functional activity","The intratemporal segment (especially the labyrinthine segment near the geniculate ganglion)","The meatal segment mediated by specific receptor activation","Extracranial segment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tympanic segment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The intratemporal segment (especially the labyrinthine segment near the geniculate ganglion)', 'In Bell palsy the facial nerve is affected within its intratemporal course, particularly the labyrinthine segment near the geniculate ganglion, the narrowest part of the bony canal. The extracranial and meatal segments are not the characteristic site of injury.'),
('ec32abab-019c-5b57-b78a-b25db8ff76b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) is the nerve of which pharyngeal arch?', '["First pharyngeal arch","Second pharyngeal arch","Sixth pharyngeal arch","Fourth pharyngeal arch","Third pharyngeal arch"]'::jsonb, 'Third pharyngeal arch', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) is the nerve of the third pharyngeal arch. The arch-nerve pairing is first (CN V), second (CN VII), third (CN IX), and fourth and sixth (CN X).'),
('0bd01c3b-3911-5f22-a2f9-a34ad5d891eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The vagus nerve (CN X) is the nerve of which pharyngeal arches?', '["Only the fourth arch","First and second pharyngeal arches","Fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches","Only the sixth arch","Third pharyngeal arch"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches', 'The vagus nerve (CN X) supplies the fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches, with the superior laryngeal branch serving the fourth arch and the recurrent laryngeal branch serving the sixth. The first three arches are supplied by CN V, VII, and IX respectively.'),
('f327059e-0260-56d2-8017-6bdb41f905b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) innervates which muscles?', '["Laryngeal muscles","Tongue muscles","Sternocleidomastoid and trapezius","Muscles of mastication","Muscles of facial expression"]'::jsonb, 'Sternocleidomastoid and trapezius', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) is a pure motor nerve supplying the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles. Laryngeal muscles are innervated by the vagus, tongue muscles by the hypoglossal nerve, and mastication muscles by CN V3.'),
('7bee8d35-8b41-5722-9a4a-3a1ef6c4e7a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) innervates which muscles?', '["All intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue (except palatoglossus)","Pharyngeal constrictors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscles of mastication mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscles of facial expression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tongue muscles plus palatoglossus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue (except palatoglossus)', 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) supplies all intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue except the palatoglossus, which is innervated by the vagus nerve through the pharyngeal plexus. Pharyngeal constrictors, mastication, and facial muscles have different nerve supplies.'),
('22d7a96a-e3eb-5665-8587-dfdc6e0d79ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The muscles of the neck derive from which embryonic structures?', '["Only pharyngeal arches resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural crest only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somites (occipital and cervical myotomes) and pharyngeal arches (platysma)","Only somites without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the second arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somites (occipital and cervical myotomes) and pharyngeal arches (platysma)', 'Neck muscles derive from two sources: myotomes of occipital and cervical somites form many of the muscles, while pharyngeal arch mesenchyme gives rise to muscles such as the platysma. Neither somites nor pharyngeal arches alone account for the full neck musculature.'),
('fab30455-5a1f-52e2-8cbe-87d616f5d12c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles develop from which structures?', '["Third and fourth arch mesoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Occipital myotomes and pharyngeal arch myoblasts (the branchial arch mesoderm)","First pharyngeal arch only mediated by specific receptor activation","Cervical somites only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Second pharyngeal arch only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Occipital myotomes and pharyngeal arch myoblasts (the branchial arch mesoderm)', 'The sternocleidomastoid and trapezius develop from occipital myotomes together with pharyngeal arch myoblasts. They are subsequently innervated by the spinal accessory nerve, consistent with this mixed embryonic origin.'),
('336b97f3-2358-541c-a88a-bd25cb1c2813'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common congenital neck mass in children?', '["Thyroid nodule resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphadenopathy (reactive) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroglossal duct cyst (midline) and branchial cleft cyst (lateral) are most common","Cystic hygroma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sternocleidomastoid tumor of infancy (fibromatosis colli) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroglossal duct cyst (midline) and branchial cleft cyst (lateral) are most common', 'Thyroglossal duct cysts (midline) and branchial cleft cysts (lateral) are the most common congenital neck masses in children. Cystic hygroma and fibromatosis colli are less frequent, and reactive lymphadenopathy is acquired rather than congenital.'),
('812e8e70-98dd-5584-bee2-83f5cfc87dc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is fibromatosis colli (sternomastoid tumor of infancy)?', '["A malignant neck tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A branchial cleft cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thyroglossal duct cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign fibrous mass in the sternocleidomastoid muscle of newborns causing torticollis","A thyroid nodule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign fibrous mass in the sternocleidomastoid muscle of newborns causing torticollis', 'Fibromatosis colli is a benign, self-limited fibrous mass within the sternocleidomastoid of newborns, classically producing mild torticollis or head tilt. Malignant tumors, branchial cleft cysts, thyroglossal duct cysts, and thyroid nodules arise from different structures and do not cause this clinical picture.'),
('3e5f0b84-bcab-5fd6-96c2-548d71928171'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of congenital muscular torticollis?', '["Fibromatosis colli (SCM fibrosis from birth trauma or intrauterine positioning) most common","Neurogenic (posterior fossa tumor) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ocular torticollis (CN IV palsy) mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertebral anomalies (Klippel-Feil) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sandifer syndrome (GERD) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibromatosis colli (SCM fibrosis from birth trauma or intrauterine positioning) most common', 'Fibromatosis colli, fibrosis of the sternocleidomastoid from birth trauma or abnormal intrauterine positioning, is the most common cause of congenital muscular torticollis. Neurogenic, ocular (CN IV palsy), vertebral (Klippel-Feil), and Sandifer syndrome causes are all much less frequent.'),
('7d9dbed8-1521-527f-8bf4-ab528b806186'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of cervical lymphadenopathy in children?', '["Kawasaki disease","Tuberculous lymphadenitis (scrofula)","Bacterial lymphadenitis (Staph aureus Group A Strep)","Malignancy (lymphoma)","Reactive hyperplasia from infection (viral URI most common)"]'::jsonb, 'Reactive hyperplasia from infection (viral URI most common)', 'Cervical lymphadenopathy in children is most often benign reactive hyperplasia triggered by viral upper respiratory infections. Kawasaki disease, tuberculous lymphadenitis, bacterial lymphadenitis, and lymphoma are all comparatively uncommon causes.'),
('b8a54f0b-7a60-56a7-9bd7-0d1361674cfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of bacterial cervical lymphadenitis in children?', '["Bartonella henselae (cat-scratch disease) resulting in decreased functional activity","Mycobacterium tuberculosis (scrofula) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anaerobic bacteria mediated by specific receptor activation","Atypical mycobacterium (Mycobacterium avium) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Strep) are the most common bacteria"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Strep) are the most common bacteria', 'Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Strep) are the predominant pathogens in pediatric bacterial cervical lymphadenitis. Bartonella (cat-scratch), Mycobacterium tuberculosis, atypical mycobacteria, and anaerobes produce specific but less common forms.'),
('395586b1-8bb1-5815-bda3-35abb00a16f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is scrofula?', '["Sarcoidosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Tuberculous lymphadenitis of the cervical lymph nodes (usually due to Mycobacterium tuberculosis or atypical mycobacteria)","Fungal lymphadenitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Bacterial lymphadenitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reactive lymphadenopathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberculous lymphadenitis of the cervical lymph nodes (usually due to Mycobacterium tuberculosis or atypical mycobacteria)', 'Scrofula is tuberculous lymphadenitis of the cervical nodes, usually caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis or atypical mycobacteria. Sarcoidosis, fungal and bacterial lymphadenitis, and reactive lymphadenopathy are distinct mechanisms of nodal enlargement.'),
('1148e94f-3072-554e-975d-8d6f3bdd807a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of persistent cervical lymphadenopathy in children?', '["Cat-scratch disease (Bartonella) resulting in decreased functional activity","Reactive hyperplasia (most persistent nodes are benign and self-limited)","EBV infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Mycobacterium (TB or atypical) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malignancy (lymphoma) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reactive hyperplasia (most persistent nodes are benign and self-limited)', 'Even persistently enlarged cervical nodes in children are usually benign reactive hyperplasia that resolves spontaneously without therapy. Malignancy, mycobacterial infection, EBV, and cat-scratch disease are ultimately uncommon by comparison.'),
('530370e2-7123-529c-a016-20be37a78d7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common malignancy causing cervical lymphadenopathy in children?', '["Metastatic carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Rhabdomyosarcoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroid cancer (papillary) mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphoma (Hodgkin > non-Hodgkin) most common malignant cause","Neuroblastoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphoma (Hodgkin > non-Hodgkin) most common malignant cause', 'Lymphoma, with Hodgkin disease more common than non-Hodgkin in children, is the most frequent malignant cause of cervical lymphadenopathy. Metastatic carcinoma, rhabdomyosarcoma, papillary thyroid cancer, and neuroblastoma are less common cervical masses.'),
('325dc384-0daf-5ba4-8f57-ed91db779d3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common location of cervical lymphoma?', '["Submandibular triangle resulting in decreased functional activity","Supraclavicular fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior triangle mediated by specific receptor activation","Midline neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral cervical chain (especially upper deep cervical nodes in Hodgkin lymphoma)"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cervical chain (especially upper deep cervical nodes in Hodgkin lymphoma)', 'Cervical lymphoma most often involves the lateral cervical chain, with the upper deep cervical nodes characteristically affected in Hodgkin disease. Submandibular, supraclavicular, posterior-triangle, and midline sites are less typical locations.'),
('ab9cbbed-cd62-5077-b166-69a5163e6fb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common thyroid cancer in children?', '["Medullary carcinoma (MEN2) resulting in decreased functional activity","Papillary thyroid carcinoma (most common overall type)","Follicular carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Anaplastic carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary thyroid carcinoma (most common overall type)', 'Papillary thyroid carcinoma is the most common thyroid malignancy overall as well as in children. Medullary carcinoma (MEN2), follicular and anaplastic carcinoma, and thyroid lymphoma are all far less frequent.'),
('c36d0c86-b21c-5503-8ce8-d585b0e08d9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of pediatric thyroid cancer?', '["Cowden syndrome (PTEN)","Radiation exposure (the most important risk factor)","Spontaneous (most cases are sporadic)","Autoimmune thyroiditis (Hashimoto associated with lymphoma)","MEN2 syndrome (RET mutation)"]'::jsonb, 'Spontaneous (most cases are sporadic)', 'Most pediatric thyroid cancers are sporadic with no identifiable cause. Radiation exposure is the single most important risk factor when present, while Cowden syndrome, Hashimoto-associated lymphoma, and MEN2 are inherited or uncommon circumstances.'),
('4b22af41-bdc7-5c13-b0fb-0e18616c8fe1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of neonatal neck mass?', '["Cystic hygroma (lymphangioma most common cervical mass in fetus/newborn)","Thyroglossal duct cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Branchial cleft cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Hemangioma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibromatosis colli leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic hygroma (lymphangioma most common cervical mass in fetus/newborn)', 'Cystic hygroma, a congenital lymphatic malformation, is the most common cervical mass in the fetus and newborn and is frequently detected on prenatal ultrasound. Thyroglossal duct cyst is the most common midline cervical mass of later childhood, while branchial cleft cyst, hemangioma, and fibromatosis colli are other congenital considerations.'),
('08e26ff5-21c2-5bbc-bf3f-290d05fb955b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is cystic hygroma (lymphangioma)?', '["A dermoid cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","A cystic thyroid nodule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A congenital lymphatic malformation (cystic dilation of lymphatics) most commonly in the neck and axilla","A vascular malformation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A solid neck tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital lymphatic malformation (cystic dilation of lymphatics) most commonly in the neck and axilla', 'Cystic hygroma is a congenital lymphatic malformation consisting of dilated lymphatic spaces, classically located in the posterior triangle of the neck and the axilla. Dermoid cysts, cystic thyroid nodules, vascular malformations, and solid tumors have different tissue origins.'),
('202a179a-8631-505d-97ee-b92feb90bf91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common chromosomal abnormality associated with cystic hygroma?', '["Turner syndrome (45 X) most commonly associated with nuchal cystic hygroma in the first trimester","Klinefelter syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Edwards syndrome (trisomy 18) mediated by specific receptor activation","Patau syndrome (trisomy 13) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Down syndrome (trisomy 21) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Turner syndrome (45 X) most commonly associated with nuchal cystic hygroma in the first trimester', 'Turner syndrome (45,X) is the chromosomal abnormality most frequently associated with first-trimester nuchal cystic hygroma. Klinefelter syndrome, trisomies 13, 18, and 21 are also seen with cystic hygroma but less characteristically.'),
('1dfdb583-88bc-51ec-b2d8-03e04c9040ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The skull is divided into which two parts?', '["Calvaria and base resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial bones and mandible accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neurocranium (braincase) and viscerocranium (facial skeleton)","Maxilla and mandible without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Splanchnocranium and dermatocranium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurocranium (braincase) and viscerocranium (facial skeleton)', 'The skull is classically divided into the neurocranium (braincase) and the viscerocranium (facial skeleton). Calvaria/base and maxilla/mandible are subsets of these divisions, while splanchnocranium and dermatocranium are not the standard two-part classification.'),
('06bdee57-6c6c-5157-8458-eab839557a82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The neurocranium develops by which types of ossification?', '["Membranous (calvaria) and endochondral (base) ossification","Appositional growth only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only endochondral mediated by specific receptor activation","Intramembranous only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only membranous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Membranous (calvaria) and endochondral (base) ossification', 'The neurocranium develops by both mechanisms: the flat bones of the calvaria ossify by membranous (intramembranous) ossification while the base (chondrocranium) undergoes endochondral ossification. Choosing only one type, or appositional growth, ignores the dual pattern.'),
('10a5a79d-fa5c-5690-a8a7-5aff31ea6882'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The viscerocranium (facial skeleton) develops mainly from which cell population?', '["Paraxial mesoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Ectoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral plate mesoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Endoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural crest cells (ectomesenchyme)"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest cells (ectomesenchyme)', 'The facial skeleton (viscerocranium) is mainly derived from neural crest cells, which populate the pharyngeal arches as ectomesenchyme. Paraxial and lateral plate mesoderm contribute to other cranial structures, while ectoderm and endoderm form tissues rather than bone.'),
('3f6a267a-c04f-599d-bdde-52cdbbbc1097'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The maxilla ossifies by which process?', '["Appositional growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Endochondral ossification accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Perichondral ossification mediated by specific receptor activation","Intramembranous ossification (direct from mesenchyme)","Synchondrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intramembranous ossification (direct from mesenchyme)', 'The maxilla forms directly from mesenchymal condensation by intramembranous ossification without a cartilaginous precursor. Endochondral and perichondral ossification require cartilage templates, while appositional growth and synchondrosis are growth processes, not primary ossification mechanisms.'),
('67ebf353-081d-5a12-ac34-394957ae5949'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The mandible ossifies by which process?', '["Only endochondral from the first arch cartilage","Endochondral ossification of Meckel''s cartilage","Intramembranous ossification around Meckel''s cartilage (which is then resorbed)","Mechanical stress-induced ossification","Mixed intramembranous and endochondral"]'::jsonb, 'Intramembranous ossification around Meckel''s cartilage (which is then resorbed)', 'The mandible forms by intramembranous ossification in the membrane surrounding Meckel''s cartilage of the first arch; the cartilage acts as a template that is later resorbed rather than converted to bone. Descriptions relying purely on endochondral ossification of Meckel''s cartilage are therefore inaccurate.'),
('0569221d-ad53-5f49-a9c6-5a6455639eb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'The hyoid bone develops from which structures?', '["First and second arch cartilages resulting in decreased functional activity","Second and third pharyngeal arch cartilages (lesser horn from second greater horn from third)","Only the second arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the third arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Third and fourth arch cartilages leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Second and third pharyngeal arch cartilages (lesser horn from second greater horn from third)', 'The hyoid body and greater horn arise from the third arch cartilage while the lesser horn comes from the second arch cartilage. First-arch cartilage contributes to the mandible and ear ossicles, and the fourth arch gives laryngeal cartilage, so these do not form the hyoid.'),
('9a1b0931-b0f2-55b5-ad03-0568df1aeb3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is a thyroglossal duct sinus?', '["Lymphatic malformation resulting in decreased functional activity","Dermoid sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An opening in the midline of the neck from a persistent thyroglossal duct remnant (can be above or below the hyoid)","Fistula from the thyroid to the skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A branchial cleft sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An opening in the midline of the neck from a persistent thyroglossal duct remnant (can be above or below the hyoid)', 'A thyroglossal duct sinus is a midline neck opening caused by a persistent remnant of the thyroglossal duct, located anywhere along its tract above or below the hyoid. Lymphatic malformations, dermoid sinuses, true thyroid-to-skin fistulas, and branchial cleft sinuses are different entities.'),
('e2c8d09e-acdc-528b-8e44-c27d95c7a250'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the larynx?', '["Laryngomalacia (most common congenital laryngeal anomaly)","Congenital vocal cord paralysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laryngeal web (glottic) mediated by specific receptor activation","Laryngeal atresia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laryngeal cleft leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngomalacia (most common congenital laryngeal anomaly)', 'Laryngomalacia, caused by immature supraglottic structures collapsing on inspiration, is the most common congenital anomaly of the larynx and the most frequent cause of infant stridor. Vocal cord paralysis, glottic webs, laryngeal atresia, and laryngeal clefts are all much rarer.'),
('1a372d81-622e-50c8-acb5-80a2b365dc23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of hoarseness in children?', '["Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis","GERD (laryngopharyngeal reflux)","Allergic rhinitis with postnasal drip","Acute laryngitis (viral most common cause of acute hoarseness)","Vocal cord nodules (most common cause of chronic hoarseness)"]'::jsonb, 'Acute laryngitis (viral most common cause of acute hoarseness)', 'Acute viral laryngitis is the most common cause of hoarseness in children. Vocal cord nodules typically produce chronic hoarseness in older children, while recurrent respiratory papillomatosis, reflux, and allergic rhinitis are less frequent causes.'),
('c16929f5-c43d-5d61-9058-022a7f4dd363'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common location of vocal cord nodules?', '["Junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the vocal folds (mid-membranous portion)","Subglottic region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior commissure mediated by specific receptor activation","Arytenoid cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior commissure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Junction of the anterior 1/3 and posterior 2/3 of the vocal folds (mid-membranous portion)', 'Vocal cord nodules arise at the junction of the anterior one-third and posterior two-thirds of the vocal folds, the point of maximal mechanical trauma during phonation. The subglottic region, posterior commissure, arytenoid cartilage, and anterior commissure are not the usual site.'),
('1b10b6b6-6450-515e-8858-6ff45bfd8e4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of stridor in the newborn?', '["Subglottic stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Tracheal stenosis (complete rings) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bilateral vocal cord paralysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Laryngomalacia (most common cause of neonatal/infant stridor)","Laryngeal web leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngomalacia (most common cause of neonatal/infant stridor)', 'Laryngomalacia is the most common cause of stridor in newborns and infants, producing inspiratory stridor that worsens with feeding and the supine position. Subglottic stenosis, tracheal rings, bilateral vocal cord paralysis, and laryngeal webs are far less common.'),
('2987ceae-76a5-58ef-a799-ba37103c33f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of acute stridor in children aged 6 months-3 years?', '["Retropharyngeal abscess resulting in decreased functional activity","Bacterial tracheitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foreign body aspiration mediated by specific receptor activation","Epiglottitis (Haemophilus influenzae type b) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Croup (acute laryngotracheobronchitis parainfluenza virus) most common cause"]'::jsonb, 'Croup (acute laryngotracheobronchitis parainfluenza virus) most common cause', 'Croup, an acute laryngotracheobronchitis usually caused by parainfluenza virus, is the most common cause of acute stridor in children aged 6 months to 3 years. Epiglottitis, bacterial tracheitis, retropharyngeal abscess, and foreign body aspiration are less common causes in this age group.'),
('3a5539bd-22d4-5313-be05-b6c78816e6f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of stridor in a febrile child with drooling and toxic appearance?', '["Bacterial tracheitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Peritonsillar abscess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Severe croup mediated by specific receptor activation","Retropharyngeal abscess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epiglottitis (supraglottitis) most commonly due to Hib in unvaccinated children"]'::jsonb, 'Epiglottitis (supraglottitis) most commonly due to Hib in unvaccinated children', 'Epiglottitis, or supraglottitis, classically from Haemophilus influenzae type b, presents with fever, drooling, a toxic appearance, and stridor. Bacterial tracheitis, peritonsillar abscess, retropharyngeal abscess, and severe croup are alternative causes of airway obstruction with stridor.'),
('26c213be-74f1-5034-8f42-7608259a23db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of acute airway obstruction in children?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Foreign body aspiration (most common cause of acute airway obstruction in toddlers)","Croup mediated by specific receptor activation","Epiglottitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anaphylaxis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foreign body aspiration (most common cause of acute airway obstruction in toddlers)', 'Foreign body aspiration is the most common cause of acute airway obstruction in toddlers, typically with choking, cough, and unilateral wheeze. Trauma, croup, epiglottitis, and anaphylaxis also obstruct the airway but are comparatively less common causes.'),
('20c77d47-4c96-5cd7-bd08-d2c246305bc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of obstructive sleep apnea in children?', '["Obesity resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenotonsillar hypertrophy (most common cause of pediatric OSA)","Neuromuscular disease (CP) mediated by specific receptor activation","Down syndrome (macroglossia midface hypoplasia) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Craniofacial anomalies (Pierre Robin) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenotonsillar hypertrophy (most common cause of pediatric OSA)', 'Adenotonsillar hypertrophy is the most common cause of obstructive sleep apnea in children because the enlarged tonsils and adenoids narrow the pharyngeal airway during sleep. Obesity, neuromuscular disease, Down syndrome, and craniofacial anomalies contribute in specific patients but are less common overall.'),
('eb42d663-1d59-5b2d-840f-e5ee8af93eb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common surgical procedure for pediatric OSA?', '["Rapid maxillary expansion resulting in decreased functional activity","Mandibular advancement surgery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tracheostomy mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy (T&A) is the first-line surgical treatment","Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy (T&A) is the first-line surgical treatment', 'Tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy is the standard first-line surgical treatment for pediatric obstructive sleep apnea caused by adenotonsillar hypertrophy. CPAP is not a surgical procedure, while rapid maxillary expansion, mandibular advancement, and tracheostomy are reserved for selected cases.'),
('1abfe199-7807-54e2-af87-c5b3f939df74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of peritonsillar abscess (quinsy)?', '["Dental infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Complication of acute tonsillitis with spread of infection through the tonsillar capsule into the peritonsillar space","Pharyngitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Mononucleosis (EBV) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Recurrent tonsillitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complication of acute tonsillitis with spread of infection through the tonsillar capsule into the peritonsillar space', 'Peritonsillar abscess is a complication of acute tonsillitis in which infection tracks through the tonsillar capsule into the peritonsillar space. Dental infections, simple pharyngitis, EBV mononucleosis, and recurrent tonsillitis alone do not produce a quinsy.'),
('dc51fc06-276a-56df-a014-a867d07b4699'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of deep neck space infection in children?', '["Retropharyngeal abscess (suppurative lymphadenitis)","Ludwig angina (submandibular floor of the mouth)","Peritonsillar abscess","Extension of odontogenic infection (dental abscess most common cause)","Parapharyngeal abscess"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of odontogenic infection (dental abscess most common cause)', 'Deep neck space infections most often arise from extension of an odontogenic infection, with a dental abscess spreading into the deep cervical spaces. Retropharyngeal, peritonsillar, parapharyngeal, and submandibular (Ludwig) infections have other predominant sources such as nodal or tonsillar disease.'),
('3989b30f-935c-50f4-8e75-593e6e0c26cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is Ludwig angina?', '["A rapidly progressive cellulitis of the submandibular and sublingual spaces causing airway compromise","Stomatitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Angular cheilitis of the mouth mediated by specific receptor activation","Pharyngitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Angina (chest pain) from Ludwig disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A rapidly progressive cellulitis of the submandibular and sublingual spaces causing airway compromise', 'Ludwig angina is a rapidly progressive cellulitis involving the bilateral submandibular, sublingual, and submental spaces, notable for floor-of-mouth swelling and threatened airway obstruction. Stomatitis, angular cheilitis, pharyngitis, and cardiac angina are unrelated conditions.'),
('f7ec554e-51fe-5885-b528-3ee9b41f3950'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of retropharyngeal abscess in children?', '["Vertebral osteomyelitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Endoscopy complication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trauma (penetrating injury) mediated by specific receptor activation","Tuberculosis (Pott disease of the spine) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Suppurative adenitis of the retropharyngeal lymph nodes (from upper respiratory infection)"]'::jsonb, 'Suppurative adenitis of the retropharyngeal lymph nodes (from upper respiratory infection)', 'In children, retropharyngeal abscess results from suppurative adenitis of the retropharyngeal lymph nodes complicating an upper respiratory infection, since these nodes persist in early childhood. Vertebral osteomyelitis, endoscopy injury, penetrating trauma, and spinal tuberculosis are less common causes.'),
('f19b0bdc-254d-5043-bf9b-0d5fc9028cec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of angle of the mandible swelling?', '["Parotitis (mumps or bacterial) with swelling of the parotid gland overlying the angle of the mandible","TMJ arthritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parotid tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Sialolithiasis (submandibular stone) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphadenopathy (preauricular node) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parotitis (mumps or bacterial) with swelling of the parotid gland overlying the angle of the mandible', 'Swelling at the angle of the mandible is most commonly parotitis, because the superficial lobe of the parotid gland overlies this region. TMJ arthritis, parotid tumors, submandibular sialolithiasis, and preauricular lymphadenopathy present with different signs and locations.'),
('c590ec85-1280-58c4-9ad9-d741c43b777c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of submandibular swelling?', '["Wharton''s duct stone with gland swelling resulting in decreased functional activity","Ludwig angina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dental abscess (submandibular space) mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroglossal duct cyst (midline not submandibular) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Submandibular lymphadenopathy (most common) followed by submandibular sialadenitis (stone)"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular lymphadenopathy (most common) followed by submandibular sialadenitis (stone)', 'Submandibular swelling is most often due to submandibular lymphadenopathy, with submandibular sialadenitis from a Wharton''s duct stone the next most common cause. Ludwig angina and dental abscess are specific infections, while thyroglossal duct cysts are midline swellings rather than submandibular masses.'),
('5573ee77-5c19-54ba-9088-dfa9c5ffe2cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common skin cancer of the head and neck?', '["Squamous cell carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal cell carcinoma (most common skin cancer overall followed by squamous cell carcinoma)","Melanoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Merkel cell carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dermatofibroma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Basal cell carcinoma (most common skin cancer overall followed by squamous cell carcinoma)', 'Basal cell carcinoma is the most common skin cancer overall and in the head and neck, followed by squamous cell carcinoma. Melanoma, Merkel cell carcinoma, and dermatofibroma are less common or benign lesions.'),
('a08046b2-f90c-58a8-82d6-fbdcc183f053'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common site of oral cancer?', '["Palate resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral border of the tongue (most common intraoral site)","Floor of the mouth mediated by specific receptor activation","Gingiva without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Buccal mucosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral border of the tongue (most common intraoral site)', 'Oral cancer most frequently arises on the lateral border of the tongue, which is the most common intraoral site. The palate, floor of the mouth, gingiva, and buccal mucosa are less frequent locations of intraoral malignancy.'),
('07993299-460d-55f2-aad1-671f7a3d8f58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of oral cancer?', '["HPV infection (HPV 16 associated with oropharyngeal not oral) resulting in decreased functional activity","Sun exposure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alcohol alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Poor oral hygiene without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tobacco use (smoking and chewing) with or without alcohol consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Tobacco use (smoking and chewing) with or without alcohol consumption', 'Tobacco use, with or without alcohol, is the most common cause of oral cancer. HPV 16 is linked to oropharyngeal rather than oral cavity tumors, sun exposure relates mainly to lip cancer, while hygiene and alcohol alone are lesser factors.'),
('0e5b0c81-42a7-5aef-9b76-550e6e7a2e06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common type of oral cancer?', '["Adenocarcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Sarcoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Squamous cell carcinoma (accounting for >90% of oral malignancies)"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma (accounting for >90% of oral malignancies)', 'Squamous cell carcinoma accounts for more than 90% of oral malignancies. Adenocarcinoma, mucoepidermoid carcinoma, lymphoma, and sarcoma are all rare forms of oral cavity cancer.'),
('c1085ac3-1e7f-51cb-bf1e-1f763ba1bbfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common premalignant lesion of the oral cavity?', '["Erythroplakia (red patch which carries higher risk)","Actinic cheilitis","Oral submucous fibrosis","Lichen planus (erosive)","Leukoplakia (white patch that cannot be scraped off)"]'::jsonb, 'Leukoplakia (white patch that cannot be scraped off)', 'Leukoplakia, a white patch that cannot be scraped off, is the most common premalignant lesion of the oral cavity. Erythroplakia is less common but carries a higher risk of malignant transformation, and actinic cheilitis, submucous fibrosis, and erosive lichen planus are other premalignant conditions.'),
('e29e9cbc-7c73-51bd-a948-6887f33188fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of leukoplakia?', '["Frictional keratosis (cheek biting) resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic irritation (tobacco smoking or chewing) most common","HPV infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Candidiasis (chronic hyperplastic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alcohol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic irritation (tobacco smoking or chewing) most common', 'Chronic irritation from tobacco smoking or chewing is the most common cause of leukoplakia. Frictional keratosis from cheek biting, HPV infection, chronic hyperplastic candidiasis, and alcohol are less common causes.'),
('3c241168-0281-54a3-bbad-b893f50a2a10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common benign tumor of the oral cavity?', '["Irritation fibroma resulting in decreased functional activity","Pleomorphic adenoma (minor salivary glands of the palate most common)","Hemangioma mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibroma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Papilloma (squamous) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pleomorphic adenoma (minor salivary glands of the palate most common)', 'Pleomorphic adenoma is the most common benign tumor of the oral cavity through its origin in the minor salivary glands, typically on the palate. Irritation fibroma, hemangioma, fibroma, and squamous papilloma are benign lesions but are less common overall in this context.'),
('446a86d1-970d-5c91-a655-b01246d173b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common odontogenic tumor?', '["Dentinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Ameloblastoma (most common clinically significant odontogenic tumor) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cementoblastoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Cementifying fibroma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Odontoma (the most common odontogenic tumor is actually a hamartoma)"]'::jsonb, 'Odontoma (the most common odontogenic tumor is actually a hamartoma)', 'Odontoma is the most common odontogenic tumor and is actually a hamartoma rather than a true neoplasm. Ameloblastoma is the most common clinically significant odontogenic tumor, while dentinoma, cementoblastoma, and cementifying fibroma are rare lesions.'),
('b4fa4b8d-6445-544e-97b3-b4f9ec99c7f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is ameloblastoma?', '["A benign but locally aggressive odontogenic tumor arising from ameloblasts (enamel organ) typically in the mandible","A tooth germ tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A malignant oral cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","A periodontal cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A salivary gland tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign but locally aggressive odontogenic tumor arising from ameloblasts (enamel organ) typically in the mandible', 'Ameloblastoma is a benign but locally aggressive odontogenic tumor derived from the ameloblasts of the enamel organ, typically involving the posterior mandible. It is not truly malignant, and tooth germ tumors, periodontal cysts, and salivary gland tumors are separate entities.'),
('b7efe6fc-bc5e-5213-b48d-d3b7fd7d290e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common location of ameloblastoma?', '["Maxilla (anterior) resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior mandible (molar-ramus region) most common","Retromolar trigone mediated by specific receptor activation","Palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mandibular symphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior mandible (molar-ramus region) most common', 'Ameloblastoma most commonly arises in the posterior mandible, specifically the molar-ramus region. The anterior maxilla, retromolar trigone (as a distinct named site), palate, and mandibular symphysis are less frequent locations.'),
('6c884086-711d-55b5-a001-b531b8053e90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common dentigerous cyst?', '["Radicular (periapical) cyst (most common odontogenic cyst overall) resulting in decreased functional activity","Gingival cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Odontogenic keratocyst (OKC) mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral periodontal cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cyst that forms around the crown of an unerupted tooth (most commonly around mandibular third molars)"]'::jsonb, 'A cyst that forms around the crown of an unerupted tooth (most commonly around mandibular third molars)', 'A dentigerous cyst forms around the crown of an unerupted tooth, most commonly the mandibular third molar. The radicular cyst is the most common odontogenic cyst overall, while gingival cyst, odontogenic keratocyst, and lateral periodontal cyst are different entities.'),
('bae9a0d0-0581-58b2-8f27-e33255cf9691'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common odontogenic cyst?', '["Radicular (periapical) cyst (from inflammatory response to pulp necrosis) is the most common","Odontogenic keratocyst (OKC) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gingival cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Dentigerous cyst (follicular cyst) is second most common without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral periodontal cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radicular (periapical) cyst (from inflammatory response to pulp necrosis) is the most common', 'The radicular (periapical) cyst, arising from an inflammatory response to pulp necrosis at the tooth apex, is the most common odontogenic cyst. The dentigerous cyst is the second most common, and OKC, gingival cyst, and lateral periodontal cyst are much rarer.'),
('e2f07346-106f-5f09-b59a-63e54da3fdae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is an odontogenic keratocyst (OKC)?', '["Calcifying odontogenic cyst resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Radicular cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dentigerous cyst mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Aneurysmal bone cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A developmental odontogenic cyst with aggressive behavior and high recurrence rate associated with Gorlin syndrome (nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 'A developmental odontogenic cyst with aggressive behavior and high recurrence rate associated with Gorlin syndrome (nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome)', 'The odontogenic keratocyst is a developmental jaw cyst known for aggressive local behavior, a high recurrence rate, and association with Gorlin (nevoid basal cell carcinoma) syndrome. Calcifying odontogenic cysts, radicular cysts, dentigerous cysts, and aneurysmal bone cysts are separate entities.'),
('ed50bd4b-73f8-554c-9b13-a4a13cd9b086'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'Gorlin syndrome (nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome) is associated with which odontogenic cyst?', '["Calcifying odontogenic cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Radicular cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gingival cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Dentigerous cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Odontogenic keratocysts (multiple bilateral OKCs of the jaws are a hallmark)"]'::jsonb, 'Odontogenic keratocysts (multiple bilateral OKCs of the jaws are a hallmark)', 'Gorlin (nevoid basal cell carcinoma) syndrome classically features multiple bilateral odontogenic keratocysts of the jaws, along with basal cell carcinomas and other anomalies. Calcifying odontogenic, radicular, gingival, and dentigerous cysts are not the hallmark of this syndrome.'),
('d5e288b4-1225-5560-bfd2-e9f83bee579b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of facial asymmetry?', '["Hyperplasia of the condyle","Geniospasm (not a common cause) - more commonly dental malocclusion or temporomandibular joint issues","Hemifacial microsomia (most common congenital cause)","Fibrous dysplasia","Asymmetric mandibular growth"]'::jsonb, 'Hemifacial microsomia (most common congenital cause)', 'Hemifacial microsomia, a developmental disorder of the first and second pharyngeal arches, is the most common congenital cause of facial asymmetry. Acquired asymmetry more often reflects dental malocclusion or temporomandibular joint disease, while condylar hyperplasia, fibrous dysplasia, asymmetric mandibular growth, and geniospasm are specific or uncommon causes rather than the most common overall.'),
('ee75c168-4f6b-5db2-b055-6e897b315578'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is hemifacial microsomia?', '["Unilateral underdevelopment of the mandible maxilla ear and associated structures (first and second arch syndrome)","Bilateral facial microsomia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parry-Romberg syndrome (hemifacial atrophy) mediated by specific receptor activation","Treacher Collins syndrome (bilateral) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Goldenhar syndrome (oculoauriculovertebral dysplasia) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unilateral underdevelopment of the mandible maxilla ear and associated structures (first and second arch syndrome)', 'Hemifacial microsomia is the commonest first and second arch syndrome, causing unilateral underdevelopment of the mandible, maxilla, ear, and adjacent soft tissues. Bilateral involvement, Parry-Romberg progressive atrophy, and Treacher Collins (bilateral) are distinct entities.'),
('abb55b49-577b-530c-9072-e21f3fe49b27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of conductive hearing loss in children?', '["External auditory canal atresia resulting in decreased functional activity","Otitis media with effusion (OME glue ear) most common cause of conductive hearing loss in children","Otosclerosis (more common in adults) mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholesteatoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tympanic membrane perforation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Otitis media with effusion (OME glue ear) most common cause of conductive hearing loss in children', 'Otitis media with effusion (glue ear) is the leading cause of conductive hearing loss in children because middle-ear fluid dampens ossicular transmission. Canal atresia and cholesteatoma are less frequent structural causes, and otosclerosis typically affects adults.'),
('d0cdece7-8b4c-5cfc-ac7a-ac1fa5458de7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of sensorineural hearing loss in children?', '["meningitis resulting in decreased functional activity","ototoxic drugs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital CMV infection most common infectious cause mediated by specific receptor activation","prematurity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Genetic causes (especially GJB2-connexin 26 mutation) most common overall"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic causes (especially GJB2-connexin 26 mutation) most common overall', 'Genetic causes, especially GJB2 (connexin 26) mutations, account for the majority of childhood sensorineural hearing loss. CMV is the most common infectious cause, but all acquired causes together are outweighed by inherited etiologies.'),
('dd136662-0645-53a1-8299-a30907db3beb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common genetic syndrome associated with hearing loss?', '["Branchiootorenal syndrome","Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome","Waardenburg syndrome (most common autosomal dominant syndromic hearing loss)","Usher syndrome (most common cause of deaf-blindness)","Pendred syndrome (most common form of syndromic deafness)"]'::jsonb, 'Waardenburg syndrome (most common autosomal dominant syndromic hearing loss)', 'Among syndromic hearing losses, Waardenburg syndrome is the most common autosomal dominant form, pairing sensorineural deafness with pigmentary anomalies (white forelock, heterochromia). Usher, Pendred, Jervell and Lange-Nielsen, and branchiootorenal syndromes are less frequent.'),
('2f3aa0b5-fc1e-5e92-916a-8754a1772c93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common infectious cause of congenital hearing loss?', '["CMV (cytomegalovirus) is the most common infectious cause of non-genetic sensorineural hearing loss","Rubella (still common in unvaccinated populations) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Toxoplasmosis mediated by specific receptor activation","HSV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Syphilis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CMV (cytomegalovirus) is the most common infectious cause of non-genetic sensorineural hearing loss', 'Congenital CMV is the leading infectious cause of non-genetic sensorineural hearing loss in infants. Rubella, toxoplasmosis, HSV, and syphilis can also injure the inner ear but are far less common.'),
('4b8cc53c-c5ba-5232-9c5d-70ca02da0015'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of cholesteatoma?', '["Retraction pocket of the tympanic membrane (acquired) most common; congenital cholesteatoma is less common","Genetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic otitis media with effusion mediated by specific receptor activation","TTube insertion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retraction pocket of the tympanic membrane (acquired) most common; congenital cholesteatoma is less common', 'Acquired cholesteatoma arises most often from a retraction pocket of the tympanic membrane, usually in the pars flaccida, with squamous epithelium trapped inside the middle ear. Congenital cholesteatoma, seen behind an intact drum, is less common.'),
('902502e7-2679-5e01-bc3f-467f918fa8b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common location of a cholesteatoma?', '["Eustachian tube resulting in decreased functional activity","External auditory canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mastoid antrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle ear cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Attic (pars flaccida) region of the middle ear and mastoid (Prussak space)"]'::jsonb, 'Attic (pars flaccida) region of the middle ear and mastoid (Prussak space)', 'Cholesteatoma characteristically develops in the attic (pars flaccida), the region known as Prussak space, and may extend posteriorly into the mastoid antrum. The canal, Eustachian tube, and cavity floor are not the typical primary site.'),
('e9e9cac3-5d38-574e-90f2-5c422069d386'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common complication of cholesteatoma?', '["Intracranial complications (meningitis brain abscess)","Chronic suppurative otitis media with conductive hearing loss","Acute mastoiditis","Intratemporal complications (facial paralysis labyrinthitis)","Erosion of the ossicles"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic suppurative otitis media with conductive hearing loss', 'The commonest consequence of cholesteatoma is chronic suppurative otitis media with ossicular erosion and conductive hearing loss. Intracranial and intratemporal complications are serious but less frequent.'),
('87c7c104-727f-5a7c-be18-abc7e58a4afe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of acute mastoiditis in children?', '["Cholesteatoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Typhoid fever accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bacterial infection resistant to antibiotics mediated by specific receptor activation","Extension of acute otitis media into the mastoid air cells","Chronic suppurative otitis media leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of acute otitis media into the mastoid air cells', 'Acute mastoiditis in children usually follows spread of acute otitis media through the aditus into the mastoid air cells. Chronic suppurative otitis media and cholesteatoma are less common pathways.'),
('fe42ecb9-bac7-55d0-9779-b000f80e935e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is Bezold abscess?', '["Extension of mastoiditis into the digastric groove and sternocleidomastoid muscle causing a neck abscess","Intracranial abscess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Retropharyngeal abscess mediated by specific receptor activation","Parapharyngeal abscess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peritonsillar abscess leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of mastoiditis into the digastric groove and sternocleidomastoid muscle causing a neck abscess', 'A Bezold abscess is pus tracking from mastoiditis through the mastoid tip into the digastric groove, then down along the sternocleidomastoid to present as a neck abscess. The other options are abscesses of different spaces.'),
('9771a560-f392-5768-a506-ea6525335e5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is Gradenigo syndrome?', '["Labyrinthitis with vertigo resulting in decreased functional activity","Triad of otitis media abducens (CN VI) palsy and retroorbital pain from petrous apicitis","Mastoiditis with facial palsy mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholesteatoma with horizontal canal fistula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Otogenic meningitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Triad of otitis media abducens (CN VI) palsy and retroorbital pain from petrous apicitis', 'Gradenigo syndrome is petrous apicitis producing the triad of acute otitis media, ipsilateral abducens (CN VI) palsy, and retro-orbital pain. Labyrinthitis, mastoiditis with facial palsy, and otogenic meningitis lack this characteristic triad.'),
('8ef55e7d-c0cd-5b19-8219-de2b21854624'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV)?', '["Vestibular neuritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Meniere disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Central nervous system disorder mediated by specific receptor activation","Migraine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic (most common) or post-traumatic (head injury)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (most common) or post-traumatic (head injury)', 'BPPV is usually idiopathic; when a cause is identified, head trauma is the most frequent trigger. Vestibular neuritis, Meniere disease, and migraine produce different patterns of dizziness without positional canalolithiasis.'),
('3768794a-8ba4-5c27-b35d-c4097ce6f357'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common semicircular canal affected in BPPV?', '["Lateral (horizontal) canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior (anterior) canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All canals equally mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior semicircular canal (most common in canalolithiasis)","The utricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior semicircular canal (most common in canalolithiasis)', 'Canalolithiasis most often involves the posterior semicircular canal because it is the most gravity-dependent canal, allowing otoconia to collect and move with head position. The utricle is a sensory receptor, not a canal, and the lateral and anterior canals are less commonly affected.'),
('94ea7e63-df21-5df6-aff1-fbb4cb1af490'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of facial nerve neuroma?', '["AVM resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemangioma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Schwannoma of the facial nerve (rare tumor usually within the temporal bone)","Paraganglioma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Schwannoma of the facial nerve (rare tumor usually within the temporal bone)', 'A facial nerve ''neuroma'' is typically a schwannoma arising from the nerve sheath, usually within the temporal bone, and is a rare tumor. Hemangioma, meningioma, paraganglioma, and arteriovenous malformation are not the classic cause.'),
('53cf6dc2-2224-5034-98a3-c934b64c4624'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of paraganglioma of the head and neck?', '["Laryngeal paraganglioma resulting in decreased functional activity","Carotid body tumor (most common head and neck paraganglioma)","Glomus tympanicum mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagal paraganglioma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glomus jugulare leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid body tumor (most common head and neck paraganglioma)', 'The carotid body tumor, arising at the carotid bifurcation, is the most common head and neck paraganglioma. Glomus jugulare, glomus tympanicum, vagal, and laryngeal paragangliomas are all less common sites.'),
('a6503386-2e96-5bc0-8a7a-9ea7acab08eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common glomus tumor of the middle ear?', '["Carotid body tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve hemangioma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glomus tympanicum (most common middle ear tumor) arising from the tympanic plexus along the promontory","Glomus jugulare without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cholesteatoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glomus tympanicum (most common middle ear tumor) arising from the tympanic plexus along the promontory', 'Glomus tympanicum, arising from the tympanic plexus along the cochlear promontory, is the most common middle-ear tumor. Glomus jugulare arises near the jugular bulb, and carotid body tumor and hemangioma are separate entities.'),
('9b8eb3ae-30dd-5f58-a72a-416c36abf98a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of pulsatile tinnitus in adults?', '["High-riding jugular bulb","Atherosclerotic carotid artery disease","Paraganglioma (glomus tympanicum or jugulare) most common pathologic cause","AVM (arteriovenous malformation)","Venous hum (idiopathic intracranial hypertension)"]'::jsonb, 'Paraganglioma (glomus tympanicum or jugulare) most common pathologic cause', 'Paragangliomas (glomus tympanicum or jugulare) are the most common pathologic cause of pulsatile tinnitus in adults because of their vascularity. A high-riding jugular bulb, atherosclerotic carotid disease, and venous hum are less frequent mechanisms.'),
('349d73ae-2c43-59cb-9213-49c9f7b42a0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of true macroglossia in children?', '["Amyloidosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Down syndrome (relative macroglossia from midface hypoplasia) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mucopolysaccharidosis (Hurler Hunter) mediated by specific receptor activation","Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (most common congenital cause of macroglossia)","Hypothyroidism (cretinism) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (most common congenital cause of macroglossia)', 'Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is the most common congenital cause of true macroglossia in childhood. Down syndrome gives relative macroglossia from midface hypoplasia, while mucopolysaccharidosis and hypothyroidism cause variable enlargement and amyloidosis is an infiltrative adult cause.'),
('02c67dfe-992e-5ef5-af5f-4c83ca46f814'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of enlarged tongue in an infant?', '["Vascular malformation (lymphangioma hemangioma) resulting in decreased functional activity","Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Down syndrome (relative macroglossia) mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal variation (most cases of macroglossia are relative from a small mouth) next is Beckwith-Wiedemann","Hypothyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal variation (most cases of macroglossia are relative from a small mouth) next is Beckwith-Wiedemann', 'In infants most macroglossia is relative, reflecting a normal-sized tongue within a small mouth; Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is the next most frequent cause. Vascular malformations such as lymphangioma or hemangioma and hypothyroidism are less common.'),
('c02a430d-3fd7-5f6d-acb7-b75e8b378669'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of micrognathia in the newborn?', '["Beckwith-Wiedemann resulting in decreased functional activity","Trisomy 13/18 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pierre Robin sequence (most common cause of micrognathia in newborns)","Fetal alcohol syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Treacher Collins syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pierre Robin sequence (most common cause of micrognathia in newborns)', 'Pierre Robin sequence, combining mandibular hypoplasia, glossoptosis, and usually aU-shaped cleft palate, is the most common cause of micrognathia in newborns. Trisomy 13/18, fetal alcohol syndrome, and Treacher Collins are less frequent causes.'),
('ddc944c3-5eae-566f-96de-2e80fd1fe84b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of retrognathia in children?', '["Ankylosis of the TMJ resulting in decreased functional activity","Pierre Robin sequence (in infants) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trauma (condylar fracture) mediated by specific receptor activation","Class II malocclusion (mandibular retrognathia is a common dental relationship)","Condylar hypoplasia (juvenile idiopathic arthritis) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Class II malocclusion (mandibular retrognathia is a common dental relationship)', 'In children retrognathia most commonly reflects a Class II malocclusion with a small or retruded mandible. TMJ ankylosis, condylar fracture, and condylar hypoplasia cause restricted or asymmetric growth rather than typical retrognathia.'),
('6b806edb-7334-5f57-9799-fca1d708588e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common craniofacial anomaly?', '["Cleft palate alone","Cleft lip with or without cleft palate","Hemifacial microsomia","Craniosynostosis","Isolated cleft palate"]'::jsonb, 'Cleft lip with or without cleft palate', 'Cleft lip with or without cleft palate is the most common craniofacial anomaly; isolated cleft palate is less frequent and predominates in females. Craniosynostosis and hemifacial microsomia are less common overall.'),
('d2ee84ea-3b1e-5287-8995-df3025736875'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of craniosynostosis?', '["Genetic (FGFR FGFR3 TWIST) resulting in decreased functional activity","Metabolic (hyperthyroidism rickets) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Isolated (nonsyndromic) most commonly sporadic with single suture fusion (sagittal most common)","Syndromic (Crouzon Apert) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Microcephaly with secondary synostosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isolated (nonsyndromic) most commonly sporadic with single suture fusion (sagittal most common)', 'Most craniosynostosis is isolated and nonsyndromic, usually sporadic with single-suture fusion, the sagittal suture being most frequent. Syndromic (Crouzon, Apert), metabolic, and microcephaly-related secondary forms are uncommon.'),
('3dcefc62-10e8-5e86-9bf3-ff66c2eb4ec4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common type of isolated craniosynostosis?', '["Scaphocephaly (sagittal suture synostosis) most common type of single-suture craniosynostosis","Trigonocephaly (metopic synostosis increasing in incidence) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brachycephaly (bicoronal) mediated by specific receptor activation","Lambdoid (rarest) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plagiocephaly (unicoronal or lambdoid) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphocephaly (sagittal suture synostosis) most common type of single-suture craniosynostosis', 'Sagittal suture synostosis producing scaphocephaly (a long, narrow head) is the most common single-suture craniosynostosis. Unicoronal, metopic, bicoronal, and lambdoid forms are all less frequent.'),
('0fd0749b-f361-5692-8b77-6b48739b7c6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common intervention for single-suture craniosynostosis?', '["Open cranial vault remodeling with fronto-orbital advancement (for frontal) or total vault reshaping (for sagittal)","Observation only (if cosmetic only) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medical therapy ( medication) mediated by specific receptor activation","Minimally invasive endoscopic strip craniectomy with postoperative helmet therapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","VP shunting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Open cranial vault remodeling with fronto-orbital advancement (for frontal) or total vault reshaping (for sagittal)', 'Open cranial vault remodeling, with fronto-orbital advancement for frontal involvement or total vault reshaping for sagittal, is the standard and most common intervention for single-suture craniosynostosis. Endoscopic strip craniectomy with helmeting is an alternative in young infants, while observation and medical therapy do not correct the fused suture.'),
('64b76e31-5075-5516-9cd1-ddbf026289b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common complication of craniosynostosis surgery?', '["Hemorrhage (most common and potentially life-threatening)","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dural tear mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior sagittal sinus injury (for sagittal synostosis) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cosmetic deformity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hemorrhage (most common and potentially life-threatening)', 'Hemorrhage is the most common and potentially life-threatening complication of craniosynostosis surgery because of the rich scalp and dural vascularity. Infection, dural tear, sinus injury, and cosmetic concerns are less frequent.'),
('ff87d6ea-9fa9-5a6d-8a72-68e1d76c1503'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of positional plagiocephaly?', '["Torticollis resulting in decreased functional activity","Intrauterine constraint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Supine sleep position (back to sleep) causing flattening of the occiput","Craniosynostosis (lambdoid synostosis is rare) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prematurity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supine sleep position (back to sleep) causing flattening of the occiput', 'Supine sleep positioning flattens the occiput and is the leading cause of positional plagiocephaly, which is not true synostosis. Lambdoid craniosynostosis is rare, while torticollis and intrauterine constraint contribute in some cases.'),
('92e4c807-a284-5734-9d4a-ad049969beb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of hypertelorism in children?', '["Frontonasal dysplasia (median facial cleft) resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasal encephalocele (most common cause of true orbital hypertelorism)","Down syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Noonan syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Craniosynostosis (Crouzon Apert) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal encephalocele (most common cause of true orbital hypertelorism)', 'Nasal encephalocele is the most common cause of true orbital hypertelorism because the midline mass displaces the orbits laterally. Frontonasal dysplasia and craniosynostosis are other but less common causes.'),
('ed8d833a-3a62-5f91-8a2f-d446484e021d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of acute sinusitis in children?', '["Allergic sinusitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Bacterial sinusitis (Streptococcus pneumoniae) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dental infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Premature birth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Viral rhinosinusitis (most common as a complication of the common cold)"]'::jsonb, 'Viral rhinosinusitis (most common as a complication of the common cold)', 'Viral rhinosinusitis complicating the common cold is the most common cause of acute sinusitis in children. Bacterial sinusitis affects only a minority, while allergic disease and dental infection are less common precipitants.'),
('bc1b1e8f-957f-5495-8638-08802dd7d108'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common bacteria causing acute bacterial sinusitis?', '["S. pneumoniae resulting in decreased functional activity","Streptococcus pneumoniae (most common) followed by non-typeable H. influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis","M. catarrhalis mediated by specific receptor activation","Group A Streptococcus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","H. influenzae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae (most common) followed by non-typeable H. influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common bacterial pathogen in acute bacterial sinusitis, followed by non-typeable Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis. Group A streptococcus plays a minor role.'),
('d76cf474-eb45-50cc-8816-74b1019102ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of chronic sinusitis in children?', '["Bacterial biofilms resulting in decreased functional activity","Immune deficiency (IgG subclass) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary ciliary dyskinesia mediated by specific receptor activation","Cystic fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Allergic rhinitis with anatomic obstruction (most common underlying cause)"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic rhinitis with anatomic obstruction (most common underlying cause)', 'Allergic rhinitis with resultant ostial and anatomic obstruction is the most common underlying cause of chronic sinusitis in children. Cystic fibrosis, ciliary dyskinesia, immune deficiency, and biofilms are less common or secondary factors.'),
('fee2fae6-c317-5b48-b69c-dd5cac0437d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common complication of acute bacterial sinusitis?', '["Intracranial abscess (frontal lobe) resulting in decreased functional activity","Meningitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cavernous sinus thrombosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Orbital abscess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Periorbital cellulitis (preseptal cellulitis is most common complication of ethmoid sinusitis)"]'::jsonb, 'Periorbital cellulitis (preseptal cellulitis is most common complication of ethmoid sinusitis)', 'Preseptal (periorbital) cellulitis is the most common complication of acute bacterial sinusitis because the thin lamina papyracea allows infection from the ethmoids to spread into the orbit. Orbital abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis, and intracranial spread are more serious but less frequent.'),
('cec7d8f6-4d2a-5b39-be55-e80e489e7353'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is Chandler classification used for?', '["Classification of nasal polyps resulting in decreased functional activity","Classification of orbital complications of sinusitis (preseptal to cavernous sinus)","Classification of otitis media mediated by specific receptor activation","Classification of sinusitis severity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Classification of pharyngitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Classification of orbital complications of sinusitis (preseptal to cavernous sinus)', 'The Chandler classification grades orbital complications of sinusitis, progressing from preseptal cellulitis through orbital cellulitis, subperiosteal abscess, orbital abscess, and cavernous sinus thrombosis. It has no role in polyps, otitis, sinusitis severity, or pharyngitis.'),
('779045a7-ab94-511e-bce7-d2971e5d635d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of nasal obstruction in infants?', '["Nasal pyriform aperture stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Neonatal rhinitis (physiologic nasal congestion) most common transient cause","Encephalocele mediated by specific receptor activation","Nasal glioma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Choanal atresia (congenital bony or membranous obstruction) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neonatal rhinitis (physiologic nasal congestion) most common transient cause', 'Neonatal rhinitis, or physiologic nasal congestion, is the most common transient cause of nasal obstruction in infants. Choanal atresia, pyriform aperture stenosis, and midline masses such as encephalocele are rarer and tend to be fixed.'),
('77f8b2a7-4f40-53ab-9e6e-f1a5e030efb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is choanal atresia?', '["Nasal encephaloencephalocele resulting in decreased functional activity","Narrow pyriform aperture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior nasal stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital bony or membranous obstruction of the posterior nasal choanae (bilateral presents as neonatal airway emergency)","Deviated septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital bony or membranous obstruction of the posterior nasal choanae (bilateral presents as neonatal airway emergency)', 'Choanal atresia is a congenital bony or membranous obstruction of the posterior nasal choanae; bilateral forms cause cyclic cyanosis and a respiratory emergency at birth. Pyriform aperture stenosis, nasal encephaloceles, and deviation are separate conditions.'),
('e456caeb-6fbc-51f4-828f-85c74a837022'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common type of choanal atresia?', '["Bilateral (most common in newborns)","Only membranous","Mixed bony-membranous (most common) followed by purely bony","Unilateral (most common overall since bilateral is rare)","Only bony"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed bony-membranous (most common) followed by purely bony', 'Mixed bony-membranous choanal atresia is the most frequent form, with purely bony atresia next and purely membranous atresia rare. Unilateral lesions outnumber bilateral clinically, because bilateral disease presents as a neonatal airway emergency.'),
('e0b523d5-fbba-5e50-ae2d-952bdb958e84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the CHARGE association?', '["Clef palate Hearing loss and Atresia","CHD and choanal atresia","Growth hormone deficiency with Ear anomalies","Coloboma Heart Atresia choanal Retardation Genital Ear anomalies","Choanal atresia Congenital Heart disease Retardation Genital Ear"]'::jsonb, 'Coloboma Heart Atresia choanal Retardation Genital Ear anomalies', 'CHARGE is a mnemonic for the major features Coloboma, Heart defects, Atresia of the choanae, Retardation of growth and development, Genital anomalies, and Ear anomalies. The other options list only partial or garbled components.'),
('6f7928b9-f8d2-55de-af7d-70b9716316f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of nasal polyps in children?', '["Allergic fungal sinusitis (AFS) and cystic fibrosis (in children CF should be excluded)","Chronic sinusitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Samter triad (AERD ASA triad) mediated by specific receptor activation","Allergic rhinitis (non-polypoid) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary ciliary dyskinesia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic fungal sinusitis (AFS) and cystic fibrosis (in children CF should be excluded)', 'In children, allergic fungal sinusitis and cystic fibrosis are the most common causes of nasal polyps, and cystic fibrosis should always be excluded in a child with polyps. Samter triad and primary ciliary dyskinesia are less common causes.'),
('9bf3bde0-9ed1-586c-a92a-78fc78d3ecab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of anosmia in adults?', '["Idiopathic resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head trauma (shearing of olfactory nerves) mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral upper respiratory infection (post-viral olfactory loss most common cause)","and Alzheimer disease (in elderly) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Viral upper respiratory infection (post-viral olfactory loss most common cause)', 'Post-viral olfactory loss after an upper respiratory infection is the most common cause of anosmia in adults. Head trauma, chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps, and neurodegenerative causes such as Alzheimer disease are less common.'),
('7b7ce279-6e6a-5529-9d74-f909cac9c3f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of facial pain in children?', '["Dental pain (tooth abscess) resulting in decreased functional activity","Migraine (sinus headache mimics) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute sinusitis (viral or bacterial most common cause of facial pain in children)","Tension headache without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temporomandibular joint dysfunction (TMJ) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute sinusitis (viral or bacterial most common cause of facial pain in children)', 'Acute sinusitis, whether viral or bacterial, is the most common cause of facial pain in children, typically over the cheek or forehead. Dental pain and temporomandibular dysfunction are less common in children than in adults.'),
('7ba96354-3a20-5126-bef3-1a34bb5904bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of toothache in children?', '["TMJ dysfunction","Sinusitis referring pain to the maxillary teeth","Teething (primary tooth eruption)","Dental caries (cavities) with pulpal involvement","Dental abscess (periapical)"]'::jsonb, 'Dental caries (cavities) with pulpal involvement', 'Dental caries reaching the pulp is the most common cause of toothache in children. Teething causes mild localized discomfort, while periapical abscess and referred sinusitis pain are later or less frequent causes.'),
('5a4cbb90-9985-5e36-803f-5c961a6dad3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common tooth to be impacted?', '["Mandibular third molar (wisdom tooth) most commonly impacted tooth","Maxillary canine (ectopic canine) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Central incisor mediated by specific receptor activation","Maxillary third molar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mandibular premolar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular third molar (wisdom tooth) most commonly impacted tooth', 'The mandibular third molar (wisdom tooth) is the most commonly impacted tooth, followed by the maxillary canine. Incisors and premolars are rarely impacted.'),
('a00a8389-486e-5693-9cc2-8108b770f2de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common oral manifestation of HIV infection in children?', '["Linear gingival erythema resulting in decreased functional activity","Oropharyngeal candidiasis (thrush) most common Oral manifestation of HIV","Recurrent aphthous ulcers mediated by specific receptor activation","Parotid enlargement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphoepithelial cysts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oropharyngeal candidiasis (thrush) most common Oral manifestation of HIV', 'Oropharyngeal candidiasis (thrush) is the most common oral manifestation of HIV infection in children, reflecting impaired mucosal immunity. Linear gingival erythema, aphthous ulcers, and parotid enlargement also occur but less frequently.'),
('66746e68-b4d1-593a-ac9e-a0bdcb79bb0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of parotid enlargement in HIV-positive children?', '["Parotitis (bacterial or viral) resulting in decreased functional activity","Sjögren syndrome (dryness) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TB of the parotid mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphoepithelial cysts of the parotid (benign lymphoepithelial lesion) most common cause of parotid enlargement in HIV","Parotid lymphoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphoepithelial cysts of the parotid (benign lymphoepithelial lesion) most common cause of parotid enlargement in HIV', 'Lymphoepithelial cysts (benign lymphoepithelial lesions) are the most common cause of parotid enlargement in HIV-positive children. Parotitis, lymphoma, tuberculosis, and Sjögren syndrome are less common in this setting.'),
('60ca037b-6dbc-55fa-8c97-06ac9aad5c58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of facial nerve palsy in the newborn?', '["Congenital (Moebius syndrome nuclear agenesis) resulting in decreased functional activity","Birth trauma (forceps delivery causing compression of the facial nerve at the stylomastoid foramen) most common cause","Ottitis media mediated by specific receptor activation","Lyme disease (rare in newborns) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bell palsy (rare in neonates) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Birth trauma (forceps delivery causing compression of the facial nerve at the stylomastoid foramen) most common cause', 'Birth trauma, with forceps delivery compressing the facial nerve at the stylomastoid foramen, is the most common cause of neonatal facial palsy. Moebius syndrome, otitis media, and Bell palsy are rare in newborns.'),
('b19890e0-e7f6-5ccc-bb4a-f0c90547ecad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of facial nerve palsy in children older than 2 years?', '["Lyme disease (endemic areas especially in summer)","Herpes zoster oticus (Ramsay Hunt)","Ottitis media with effusion (acute otitis media)","Trauma","Bell palsy (idiopathic most common)"]'::jsonb, 'Bell palsy (idiopathic most common)', 'Bell palsy (idiopathic) is the most common cause of facial nerve palsy in children older than 2 years. In endemic areas Lyme disease becomes an important cause, especially in summer, while trauma, otitis media, and Ramsay Hunt syndrome are less common.'),
('9a9bf354-d5ba-5c4a-8437-2977e7f95ead'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of acute suppurative parotitis in the elderly?', '["Mumps (paramyxovirus) resulting in decreased functional activity","Medication-induced (anticholinergics) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sjogren syndrome (autoimmune) mediated by specific receptor activation","Dehydration and poor oral hygiene with ascending Staph aureus infection (most common)","Sialolithiasis with secondary infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dehydration and poor oral hygiene with ascending Staph aureus infection (most common)', 'Acute suppurative parotitis in the elderly usually follows dehydration and poor oral hygiene, allowing Staphylococcus aureus to ascend the parotid duct. Mumps, Sjögren syndrome, sialolithiasis, and drug-induced causes are less common.'),
('67bc8eb8-461b-5e37-8491-b35c941065e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of radiation-induced salivary gland damage?', '["Radiation therapy for head and neck cancer (especially for nasopharyngeal and oropharyngeal cancers)","Occupational exposure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dental X-rays mediated by specific receptor activation","Diagnostic CT scans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radioactive iodine therapy for thyroid cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radiation therapy for head and neck cancer (especially for nasopharyngeal and oropharyngeal cancers)', 'Therapeutic radiation for head and neck cancers is the most common cause of radiation-induced salivary gland damage, as the parotid and submandibular glands in the field become atrophic and hyposecretive. Dental X-rays and diagnostic CT doses are negligible.'),
('d7119f76-d7da-5662-8f6b-b6a4308b51e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of xerostomia (dry mouth) in children?', '["Dehydration resulting in decreased functional activity","Medication side effects (anticholinergics antihistamines antidepressants) most common cause","Mouth breathing mediated by specific receptor activation","Radiation therapy (rare in children) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sjogren syndrome (rare in children) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medication side effects (anticholinergics antihistamines antidepressants) most common cause', 'Medications such as anticholinergics, antihistamines, and antidepressants are the most common cause of xerostomia in children by suppressing salivary output. Dehydration and mouth breathing contribute less often, while radiotherapy and Sjögren syndrome are rare in children.'),
('461a9126-6754-5794-bbb7-2a65b100f981'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of halitosis in children?', '["GERD (gastroesophageal reflux)","Foreign body (nasal)","Tonsillitis (tonsillar crypt debris)","Sinusitis (postnasal drip)","Poor oral hygiene (dental plaque and caries) most common"]'::jsonb, 'Poor oral hygiene (dental plaque and caries) most common', 'Poor oral hygiene with dental plaque and caries is the most common cause of halitosis in children, as bacteria degrade food debris and release volatile sulfur compounds. Tonsillar debris, postnasal drip from sinusitis, nasal foreign body, and GERD are less frequent causes.'),
('6795c612-229c-500d-a55e-377490a8435d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of stridor in an infant with a vascular ring?', '["Right aortic arch with aberrant left subclavian resulting in decreased functional activity","Double aortic arch (most common symptomatic vascular ring) compresses the trachea causing biphasic stridor","Pulmonary artery sling (aberrant left PA) mediated by specific receptor activation","Innominate artery compression (anterior compression) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Persistent fifth arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Double aortic arch (most common symptomatic vascular ring) compresses the trachea causing biphasic stridor', 'A double aortic arch is the most common symptomatic vascular ring: the two arches encircle the trachea and esophagus, compressing the trachea and producing biphasic stridor. Partial rings such as a right arch with aberrant subclavian or a pulmonary sling cause symptoms less often.'),
('45449ed2-3158-5de3-92b0-8d26f7ddb021'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of stridor after esophageal atresia repair?', '["Vocal cord paralysis (recurrent laryngeal nerve injury) resulting in decreased functional activity","Subglottic stenosis (prolonged intubation) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fundoplication with hiatal hernia mediated by specific receptor activation","Tracheomalacia (due to abnormal tracheal cartilage from the TE fistula or compression) most common cause","Stricture causing aspiration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tracheomalacia (due to abnormal tracheal cartilage from the TE fistula or compression) most common cause', 'After repair of esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula, tracheomalacia from abnormal tracheal cartilage or prior compression is the most common cause of stridor. Vocal cord paralysis and subglottic stenosis are less frequent.'),
('eed1a948-3f16-567b-bcd1-88f4ecce5f7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of voice change after thyroid surgery?', '["Edema of the larynx resulting in decreased functional activity","Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury (vocal cord paralysis causing hoarseness)","Seroma/hematoma causing mechanical compression mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior laryngeal nerve injury (loss of high-pitched voice) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intubation trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury (vocal cord paralysis causing hoarseness)', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve runs in the tracheoesophageal groove behind the thyroid, so its injury causes vocal cord paralysis and hoarseness, the most common voice change after thyroidectomy. Superior laryngeal nerve injury mainly blunts high pitch, while laryngeal edema and hematoma are less common.'),
('0384c22f-74a0-5775-a711-37241673336c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent laryngeal nerve injury in thyroid surgery?', '["Electrocautery injury resulting in decreased functional activity","Stretching the nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Injury during dissection of the nerve near the ligament of Berry (Zuckerkandl tubercle)","Ischemia from ligation of the inferior thyroid artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transaction of the nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Injury during dissection of the nerve near the ligament of Berry (Zuckerkandl tubercle)', 'The nerve is most vulnerable during dissection at the ligament of Berry and Zuckerkandl tubercle, where it lies close to the thyroid capsule. Cautery, stretching, and inferior thyroid artery ligation can contribute but do not account for the majority of injuries.'),
('48746f50-a4e8-5fa6-b74e-31afd678df96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of hypocalcemia after thyroid surgery?', '["Transient hypoparathyroidism from parathyroid ischemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Inadvertent removal or devascularization of the parathyroid glands (especially the superior and inferior) most commonly the superior glands","Hungry bone syndrome (if patient had severe hyperthyroidism) mediated by specific receptor activation","Hemodilution from intraoperative fluids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reversible parathyroid stunning (pseudohypoparathyroidism after surgery) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inadvertent removal or devascularization of the parathyroid glands (especially the superior and inferior) most commonly the superior glands', 'Inadvertent removal or devascularization of the parathyroid glands is the usual basis, with the superior pair most often affected because of their location. Transient ischemia, hungry bone syndrome, and hemodilution are secondary or rarer contributors.'),
('a0b1727d-a494-5a25-9614-477d8bc11865'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of neck hematoma after thyroid surgery?', '["Valsalva maneuver resulting in decreased functional activity","Bleeding disorder (coagulopathy) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coughing or straining mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension during extubation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Re bleeding from a vessel in the thyroid bed (usually venous bleeding) causing compressive airway symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Re bleeding from a vessel in the thyroid bed (usually venous bleeding) causing compressive airway symptoms', 'Re-bleeding, usually from a small vessel in the thyroid bed, produces an expanding hematoma that can compress the airway. Coughing, straining, and hypertension may aggravate bleeding but are not the primary source.'),
('c6c19f95-1bf0-57d5-bef8-9bf5f421fd4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of goiter in children?', '["Hashimoto thyroiditis (autoimmune lymphocytic thyroiditis) in iodine-sufficient areas","Dyshormonogenesis (congenital goiter)","Iodine deficiency (most common cause worldwide but rare in developed countries)","Simple (colloid) goiter (physiologic)","Graves disease (diffuse toxic goiter)"]'::jsonb, 'Hashimoto thyroiditis (autoimmune lymphocytic thyroiditis) in iodine-sufficient areas', 'In iodine-sufficient regions Hashimoto (chronic lymphocytic) thyroiditis is the leading cause of goiter in children. Iodine deficiency, colloid goiter, and Graves disease are less common in that setting.'),
('9d444a53-059a-51f4-b464-0c6034544fac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common location of an ectopic thyroid gland?', '["Intralaryngeal (inside the larynx)","Thyroglossal duct cyst within the duct","Lingual (tongue base) most common location of ectopic thyroid","Prehyoid (above the hyoid)","Sublingual (under the tongue)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual (tongue base) most common location of ectopic thyroid', 'Ectopic thyroid tissue most often arises at the base of the tongue because the foramen cecum marks the original descent point of the thyroid primordium along the thyroglossal tract. Other sites along the descent path are rarer.'),
('3abf0a65-c968-5656-b3c0-84980943fedc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of thyroid nodule in children?', '["Lingual thyroid resulting in decreased functional activity","Hashimoto thyroiditis (pseudonodule) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Benign follicular nodule (colloid nodule or follicular adenoma) most common","Thyroid cancer (papillary carcinoma) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroglossal duct cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign follicular nodule (colloid nodule or follicular adenoma) most common', 'Most thyroid nodules in children are benign, with colloid nodules and follicular adenomas predominating. Although malignancy is a greater concern than in adults, it remains less common than a benign nodule.'),
('e74bf0f5-d211-5779-bc48-5634fc0e9e3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common malignant thyroid nodule in children?', '["Mixed papillary-follicular carcinoma","Papillary thyroid carcinoma (most common)","Medullary carcinoma (MEN2)","Anaplastic carcinoma","Follicular carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary thyroid carcinoma (most common)', 'Papillary carcinoma is the most frequent malignant thyroid nodule in children, often linked to a history of radiation exposure. Follicular, medullary, and anaplastic carcinomas are all far less common.'),
('5ac6afe7-85e4-53b8-9f20-af3cfa50a166'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of parathyroid adenoma?', '["Previous neck surgery","Genetic (MEN1 MEN2A HRPT2 CDC73 associated with hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumor syndrome)","Sporadic (most cases are sporadic without known cause)","Radiation exposure","Familial isolated hyperparathyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Sporadic (most cases are sporadic without known cause)', 'Most parathyroid adenomas are sporadic with no identifiable cause. Inherited syndromes, prior neck surgery, and radiation account for only a minority of cases.'),
('4f0ef959-5319-5d18-9bd9-9ee4d30f00cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common symptom of primary hyperparathyroidism?', '["Asymptomatic (most patients are identified incidentally on routine labs)","Kidney stones (nephrolithiasis) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone pain and pathologic fractures mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PEPTIC ulcer disease (from hypercalcemia) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asymptomatic (most patients are identified incidentally on routine labs)', 'Most patients with primary hyperparathyroidism are asymptomatic and are identified incidentally on routine calcium testing. Kidney stones, bone disease, and pancreatitis affect only a minority.'),
('9105c878-1f81-5904-8740-2add317a2044'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism?', '["Parathyroid carcinoma (<1%)","Parathyroid cyst","Ectopic parathyroid adenoma (mediastinum)","Parathyroid adenoma (single adenoma in about 85% of cases)","Parathyroid hyperplasia (10-15% associated with MEN)"]'::jsonb, 'Parathyroid adenoma (single adenoma in about 85% of cases)', 'A single parathyroid adenoma accounts for roughly 85% of primary hyperparathyroidism cases. Hyperplasia is less common and linked to MEN syndromes, while carcinoma, cysts, and ectopic adenomas are rare.'),
('792062a8-14ae-519b-8eeb-a826dc14fe58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of secondary hyperparathyroidism in children?', '["Vitamin D deficiency (rickets) resulting in decreased functional activity","Malabsorption (celiac disease) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medications (lithium loop diuretics) mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic kidney disease (renal osteodystrophy) most common cause","Liver disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic kidney disease (renal osteodystrophy) most common cause', 'Chronic kidney disease is the leading cause of secondary hyperparathyroidism because impaired phosphate excretion and reduced calcitriol stimulate PTH secretion. Vitamin D deficiency and malabsorption are less common causes.'),
('08600613-9174-5eb4-9277-dfe56fb468e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common complication of secondary hyperparathyroidism?', '["Skeletal deformities (genu valgum) resulting in decreased functional activity","Soft tissue calcification accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Growth retardation mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal osteodystrophy (osteitis fibrosa cystica from high PTH and bone turnover)","Metastatic calcification (vascular calcification) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal osteodystrophy (osteitis fibrosa cystica from high PTH and bone turnover)', 'Sustained high PTH produces high-turnover bone disease, including osteitis fibrosa cystica, which is the characteristic bone complication. Soft-tissue and vascular calcification are additional but less classic consequences.'),
('ff6d4076-0220-5781-8533-a37af883ede0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of tertiary hyperparathyroidism?', '["Prolonged vitamin D deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Lithium therapy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Long-standing secondary hyperparathyroidism from chronic kidney disease (autonomous parathyroid function after transplant)","Hypocalcemia from malabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Post-transplant autonomous PTH secretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Long-standing secondary hyperparathyroidism from chronic kidney disease (autonomous parathyroid function after transplant)', 'Tertiary hyperparathyroidism follows long-standing secondary disease from chronic kidney disease, in which the glands have become autonomous and keep secreting PTH even after transplantation. It reflects residual gland autonomy rather than ongoing hypocalcemia.'),
('5089a3f3-7a1c-52d9-80e3-ff3151d3aae5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of jugular foramen syndrome (Vernet syndrome)?', '["Chondrosarcoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Meningioma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Schwannoma (CN IX X XI) mediated by specific receptor activation","Glomus jugulare paraganglioma (most common tumor of the jugular foramen)","Metastatic tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glomus jugulare paraganglioma (most common tumor of the jugular foramen)', 'The glomus jugulare paraganglioma is the most frequent tumor of the jugular foramen and compresses cranial nerves IX, X, and XI, causing Vernet syndrome. Schwannomas, meningiomas, chondrosarcomas, and metastases are less common.'),
('1a170490-40e5-5d16-a52a-b45350a563e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of skull base osteomyelitis?', '["Sinusitis (frontal) resulting in decreased functional activity","Odontogenic infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Immunosuppression (post-transplant) mediated by specific receptor activation","Malignant (necrotizing) otitis externa in elderly diabetics (Pseudomonas aeruginosa)","Trauma (skull fracture) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant (necrotizing) otitis externa in elderly diabetics (Pseudomonas aeruginosa)', 'Necrotizing (malignant) otitis externa with Pseudomonas aeruginosa in elderly diabetics is the classic source of skull base osteomyelitis. Frontal sinusitis, odontogenic infection, immunosuppression, and trauma are less common causes.'),
('658b7b03-8ac2-54cb-82db-9b7babbbbf38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of perichondritis of the auricle?', '["Relapsing polychondritis (systemic autoimmunity) resulting in decreased functional activity","Insect bite accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lupus erythematosus (discoid) mediated by specific receptor activation","Contact dermatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma (piercing or laceration) with secondary Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma (piercing or laceration) with secondary Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection', 'Trauma, usually from piercing or a laceration, introduces bacteria into the auricular cartilage, most often Pseudomonas aeruginosa, causing perichondritis. Autoimmune and dermatologic conditions cause perichondritis less frequently.'),
('72626ae6-9c76-5052-8875-c678953a5441'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of cauliflower ear?', '["Frostbite resulting in decreased functional activity","Recurrent cellulitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Relapsing polychondritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Burns without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Untreated auricular hematoma (from wrestling rugby or boxing) causing perichondritis and cartilage deformity"]'::jsonb, 'Untreated auricular hematoma (from wrestling rugby or boxing) causing perichondritis and cartilage deformity', 'An untreated auricular hematoma separates the perichondrium from the cartilage, depriving it of nutrition and leading to a fibrotic, deformed ''cauliflower'' ear. Frostbite, burns, and polychondritis are far less common causes.'),
('7bfd84b7-6fb1-5308-932a-5d05036c938a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of tinnitus in children?', '["Benign intracranial hypertension (pulsatile tinnitus) resulting in decreased functional activity","Allergic rhinitis (Eustachian tube dysfunction) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensorineural hearing loss (underlying cochlear damage) mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal physiologic (most children experience brief tinnitus) or medication-related (NSAIDs aminoglycosides)","Anxiety and hyperacusis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal physiologic (most children experience brief tinnitus) or medication-related (NSAIDs aminoglycosides)', 'Brief tinnitus is common and usually normal in children, with medications such as aminoglycosides and NSAIDs as additional contributors. Structural and vestibular disorders account for only a minority of cases.'),
('58ba7091-7c3b-5799-9946-5222e226969a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of pulsatile tinnitus?', '["High-riding or dehiscent jugular bulb","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) with venous hum most common cause","Paraganglioma (glomus tympanicum/jugulare)","Arterial causes (atherosclerotic carotid fibromuscular dysplasia)","AVM (arteriovenous malformation dural AV fistula)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) with venous hum most common cause', 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension produces a venous hum that is the most common cause of pulsatile tinnitus. Vascular tumors, arterial lesions, and AVMs are less common but must be excluded.'),
('1593af8e-c249-574a-9df7-3e99a6714983'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of bilateral vestibular hypofunction?', '["Autoimmune (Cogan syndrome) resulting in decreased functional activity","Ototoxicity (aminoglycoside antibiotics gentamicin) most common cause of bilateral loss","Neurodegenerative (cerebellar ataxia) mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral Meniere disease (rare) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningitis (bilateral damage) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ototoxicity (aminoglycoside antibiotics gentamicin) most common cause of bilateral loss', 'Aminoglycoside ototoxicity, especially with gentamicin, is the most common cause of bilateral vestibular hypofunction. Meningitis and autoimmune disease are rarer causes of bilateral loss.'),
('b6be1c36-f0e5-5981-92e7-fb813dd80681'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of unilateral vestibular hypofunction?', '["Labyrinthitis (bacterial or viral with hearing loss)","Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma)","Meniere disease (fluctuating)","Vestibular neuritis (viral inflammation of the vestibular nerve) most common cause","Superior semicircular canal dehiscence (SSCD)"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular neuritis (viral inflammation of the vestibular nerve) most common cause', 'Vestibular neuritis, presumably viral, is the most common cause of unilateral vestibular hypofunction and presents with acute vertigo without hearing change. Labyrinthitis, schwannoma, and Meniere disease are less common.'),
('cc9b6217-14e1-5861-b40b-d765b1d6ea75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of Meniere disease?', '["Migraine (some cases may be migraine-related)","Idiopathic (the cause is unknown in most cases)","Trauma (head injury)","Viral infection (HSV reactivation)","Autoimmune disease"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (the cause is unknown in most cases)', 'The majority of Meniere disease is idiopathic, with no cause identified. Migraine, trauma, viral reactivation, and autoimmune mechanisms account for only some cases.'),
('90c726c9-2d53-5579-bae3-0f2f672a5e90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of Pendred syndrome?', '["SLC26A4 mutation causing sensorineural hearing loss and goiter (most common syndromic deafness)","GJB6 mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MITF mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","MYO7A mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GJB2 mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SLC26A4 mutation causing sensorineural hearing loss and goiter (most common syndromic deafness)', 'Pendred syndrome, the most common syndromic deafness, results from SLC26A4 mutations causing sensorineural hearing loss with goiter. Mutations of connexin genes (GJB2/GJB6) produce nonsyndromic deafness.'),
('1b6c8859-0fbf-57f5-80d9-1f0474d5086c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of enlarged vestibular aqueduct (EVA)?', '["Waardenburg syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Congenital CMV infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Idiopathic (sporadic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Genetic (SLC26A4 mutation most commonly associated with Pendred syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic (SLC26A4 mutation most commonly associated with Pendred syndrome)', 'Enlarged vestibular aqueduct is most often associated with SLC26A4 mutations, particularly in Pendred syndrome. CMV infection, trauma, and Waardenburg syndrome are less frequent causes.'),
('9a75d1ba-0046-5172-9ccd-298686032d4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of sudden sensorineural hearing loss (SSNHL) in adults?', '["Idiopathic (most common; 85-90% of cases no cause identified)","Autoimmune (Cogan syndrome SLE) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acoustic neuroma mediated by specific receptor activation","Vascular (ischemia of cochlea) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Viral infection (HSV CMV) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (most common; 85-90% of cases no cause identified)', 'In most adults no cause is identified (idiopathic) for sudden sensorineural hearing loss. Viral, vascular, and autoimmune etiologies account for only a minority.'),
('9d9a702d-b4da-5fbb-b5c7-8ca8c0d0cc34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of conductive hearing loss in adults?', '["Cerumen impaction resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerumen impaction (most common cause) and otosclerosis (most common pathologic cause of progressive conductive loss in young adults)","Otosclerosis (stapes fixation) mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic otitis media with effusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tympanic membrane perforation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerumen impaction (most common cause) and otosclerosis (most common pathologic cause of progressive conductive loss in young adults)', 'Cerumen impaction is the most common overall cause, while otosclerosis is the leading pathologic cause of progressive conductive loss in young adults. Effusion and tympanic perforation are less common.'),
('ed28e112-5853-5d0a-a3d0-e8062fb0927d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common type of otosclerosis?', '["Obliterative otosclerosis","Fenestral otosclerosis (involving the oval window and stapes footplate) most common (>90%)","Impaired mobility of the incudostapedial joint","Retrofenestral (cochlear) otosclerosis (involving the cochlea causing sensorineural loss)","Juvenile otosclerosis"]'::jsonb, 'Fenestral otosclerosis (involving the oval window and stapes footplate) most common (>90%)', 'Fenestral otosclerosis, which fixes the stapes footplate at the oval window, is the most common form, accounting for over 90% of cases. Retrofenestral disease involves the cochlea and is much rarer.'),
('dc43c8bd-33da-5c2b-a53b-1106bb3b9f13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of tinnitus with normal hearing?', '["Pulsatile tinnitus (venous arterial) resulting in decreased functional activity","Medication-related (salicylates NSAIDs) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TMJ disorders (temporomandibular joint) mediated by specific receptor activation","Idiopathic (most tinnitus is subjective with normal hearing) related to stress anxiety","Acoustic neuroma (unilateral tinnitus) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (most tinnitus is subjective with normal hearing) related to stress anxiety', 'Most tinnitus with normal hearing is subjective and idiopathic, often linked to stress or anxiety. Pulsatile, medication-related, TMJ, and acoustic neuroma causes account for a minority.'),
('8d1e8ff4-1690-5922-8260-9510fcd88376'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of cerumen impaction?', '["Excessive hair in the canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Swimming (water causing wax to swell) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hearing aid use mediated by specific receptor activation","Self-manipulation (cotton swabs push wax deeper into the canal)","Narrow ear canals (congenital) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Self-manipulation (cotton swabs push wax deeper into the canal)', 'Cotton swabs and other self-manipulation push cerumen deeper into the canal, producing impaction. Excessive hair, swimming, hearing aids, and congenitally narrow canals contribute less often.'),
('66f1b938-ae18-5aba-aba8-d703ce5e443b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of chronic suppurative otitis media (CSOM)?', '["Malignant otitis externa resulting in decreased functional activity","Tuberculous otitis media accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Granulation tissue in the middle ear mediated by specific receptor activation","Persistent infection through a chronic tympanic membrane perforation (most commonly central perforation)","Cholesteatoma (acquired) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent infection through a chronic tympanic membrane perforation (most commonly central perforation)', 'Chronic suppurative otitis media results from persistent infection through a chronic tympanic membrane perforation, most often central. Cholesteatoma and granulation tissue complicate rather than initiate the disease.'),
('b9b6bcd9-7855-5942-aa1f-6eea247878a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of tympanic membrane perforation in children?', '["Barotrauma (flying diving)","Blast injury (firecracker near the ear)","Trauma (cotton swab water sports)","Acute otitis media (the most common cause of TM perforation in children)","Pressure equalization tube insertion complication"]'::jsonb, 'Acute otitis media (the most common cause of TM perforation in children)', 'Acute otitis media is the most common cause of tympanic membrane perforation in children, as pus under pressure ruptures the drum. Trauma, barotrauma, blast injury, and tube-related complications are less common.'),
('5947c26b-ce7c-5b9e-ad2b-7924c2bdc73f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of hearing loss in children with otitis media with effusion (OME)?', '["hearing loss (hearing is normal in most OME) resulting in decreased functional activity","Central auditory processing disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixed hearing loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensorineural hearing loss (rare in OME) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conductive hearing loss (mild to moderate 25-40 dB) from middle ear effusion interfering with ossicular transmission"]'::jsonb, 'Conductive hearing loss (mild to moderate 25-40 dB) from middle ear effusion interfering with ossicular transmission', 'Otitis media with effusion produces a mild to moderate (25-40 dB) conductive hearing loss because the effusion impairs ossicular transmission. Sensorineural and mixed losses are not typical of uncomplicated OME.'),
('22f46aa9-c99c-5743-8fcc-3666f6c6882d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of hearing loss in children with cleft palate?', '["Auditory neuropathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Middle ear anomaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Otitis media with effusion (almost universal due to Eustachian tube dysfunction from abnormal tensor veli palatini)","Sensorineural hearing loss (genetic syndrome) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conductive loss from ossicular fixation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Otitis media with effusion (almost universal due to Eustachian tube dysfunction from abnormal tensor veli palatini)', 'A cleft palate disrupts the tensor veli palatini, causing Eustachian tube dysfunction, so otitis media with effusion is nearly universal. This conductive problem dominates over middle-ear anomalies and sensorineural loss.'),
('bbd435e7-e135-5d18-bb22-267772d3bd9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common indication for tympanostomy tube placement in children?', '["Cholesteatoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute mastoiditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meniere disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Barotrauma prevention without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Recurrent acute otitis media (RAOM) and chronic otitis media with effusion (COME) with hearing loss"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent acute otitis media (RAOM) and chronic otitis media with effusion (COME) with hearing loss', 'Tympanostomy tubes are most commonly placed for recurrent acute otitis media and chronic otitis media with effusion accompanied by hearing loss. Cholesteatoma, mastoiditis, and Meniere disease are not usual indications.'),
('cb36de1e-6e9a-5086-a854-e93b3391e83a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common complication of tympanostomy tubes?', '["Perforation after tube extrusion","Tube obstruction (crust plug)","Post-tube otorrhea (most common complication)","Tube occlusion (blocked)","Granulation tissue around the tube"]'::jsonb, 'Post-tube otorrhea (most common complication)', 'Post-tube otorrhea is the most common complication of tympanostomy tubes, often triggered by water exposure. Obstruction, granulation tissue, and persistent perforation after extrusion are less common.'),
('527d6913-7905-525e-b48f-d83ea8611224'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of otorrhea in a child with tympanostomy tubes?', '["Foreign body in the ear canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute otitis media through the tube (most common cause of tube otorrhea)","Fungal infection (otomycosis) mediated by specific receptor activation","Water contamination (swimming bathing) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granulation tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute otitis media through the tube (most common cause of tube otorrhea)', 'Acute otitis media through the tube is the most common cause of tube-associated otorrhea. Water contamination, fungal infection, and granulation tissue are additional but less common triggers.'),
('b75bbd8b-b2d2-5e2b-912d-293c715d0a39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of sensorineural hearing loss after tympanostomy tube placement?', '["Chronic infection (labyrinthitis) resulting in decreased functional activity","Pre-existing sensorineural loss (not from the tube itself) or cholesteatoma (rarely from tube insertion)","Retraction pocket mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholesteatoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tympanic membrane atelectasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-existing sensorineural loss (not from the tube itself) or cholesteatoma (rarely from tube insertion)', 'Sensorineural loss after tube placement is usually pre-existing rather than caused by the tube, with cholesteatoma a rare late consequence. Tympanic atelectasis and retraction pockets do not cause sensorineural loss.'),
('b6d13fac-b8ae-5973-af7b-6aed78705b29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent otitis media in children?', '["Eustachian tube dysfunction (immature or poor anatomy) most common underlying cause","Gastroesophageal reflux (GERD) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Passive smoke exposure mediated by specific receptor activation","Allergies (allergic rhinitis) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Immune deficiency (IgG subclass) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Eustachian tube dysfunction (immature or poor anatomy) most common underlying cause', 'Eustachian tube dysfunction from immature or unfavorable anatomy is the most common underlying cause of recurrent otitis media in children. Smoke exposure, allergies, and immune deficiency are contributing risk factors rather than the primary cause.'),
('bb92f29d-f8f6-5ca7-9580-19217e4491b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of acute otitis media in newborns?', '["H. influenzae (untypeable) resulting in decreased functional activity","S. pneumoniae (pneumococcus) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gram-negative enteric bacteria (E. coli Klebsiella) and Group B Streptococcus (GBS) are the most common organisms","S. aureus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","M. catarrhalis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gram-negative enteric bacteria (E. coli Klebsiella) and Group B Streptococcus (GBS) are the most common organisms', 'Newborns more often develop acute otitis media from gram-negative enteric organisms and group B Streptococcus. Pneumococcus, H. influenzae, and M. catarrhalis dominate in older infants and children.'),
('77cf7cbc-0391-5a99-9a47-3ef9684a699d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of acute otitis media in older infants and children?', '["S. pneumoniae resulting in decreased functional activity","M. catarrhalis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Streptococcus pneumoniae (most common) followed by nontypeable H. influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis","H. influenzae (non-typeable) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Group A Streptococcus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae (most common) followed by nontypeable H. influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common cause, followed by nontypeable H. influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis. Group A Streptococcus and S. aureus are uncommon pathogens.'),
('d6490a37-f688-5ccf-9876-feaf06b2dec6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of acute mastoiditis?', '["Streptococcus pneumoniae (most common cause of AOM complications)","Haemophilus influenzae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pseudomonas aeruginosa mediated by specific receptor activation","Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae (most common cause of AOM complications)', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common cause of acute mastoiditis as a complication of acute otitis media. H. influenzae, Pseudomonas, and S. aureus are less frequent.'),
('08327cee-1f55-5ba5-bfd2-09adac0c05f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of lateral sinus thrombosis (otogenic)?', '["Cholesteatoma with erosion resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma (temporal bone fracture) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meningitis with secondary sinus involvement mediated by specific receptor activation","Peritonsillar abscess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extension of mastoiditis into the sigmoid sinus (most common intracranial complication of AOM/COM)"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of mastoiditis into the sigmoid sinus (most common intracranial complication of AOM/COM)', 'Mastoiditis extending into the sigmoid sinus is the most common intracranial complication of acute or chronic otitis media and causes lateral sinus thrombosis. Cholesteatoma erosion and meningitis are less common mechanisms.'),
('c24d2384-40df-5e77-95a2-e9f0eec7bc03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of otogenic meningitis?', '["Preformed pathways (Mondini malformation with spontaneous CSF leak)","Spread of infection from acute or chronic otitis media through the dural lining","Hematogenous spread","Direct extension through the oval or round window","Nerve sheath (vestibular cochlear)"]'::jsonb, 'Spread of infection from acute or chronic otitis media through the dural lining', 'Infection spreads from acute or chronic otitis media through the dural lining to produce otogenic meningitis. Preformed pathways and round- or oval-window penetration are less common routes.'),
('22dc4581-3bb0-5ea0-a0d2-e7488ce2a4d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common brain herniation associated with otitis media?', '["Transcalvarial herniation resulting in decreased functional activity","Transtentorial (uncal) herniation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tonsillar herniation (Chiari) mediated by specific receptor activation","Temporal lobe herniation (encephalocele) into the middle ear from dural and tegmen erosion","Subfalcine herniation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal lobe herniation (encephalocele) into the middle ear from dural and tegmen erosion', 'Erosion of the tegmen and dura allows the temporal lobe to herniate into the middle ear as an encephalocele. This is the most common brain herniation associated with otitis media.'),
('f37f3b8d-195c-5095-8475-fb8c17d25744'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of spontaneous CSF otorrhea?', '["Cholesteatoma with erosion resulting in decreased functional activity","Congenital inner ear malformation (Mondini) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) causing tegmen dehiscence and CSF leak through a middle fossa defect","Chronic otitis media with tegmen erosion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prior surgery (mastoidectomy) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) causing tegmen dehiscence and CSF leak through a middle fossa defect', 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension causing tegmen dehiscence is the most common cause of spontaneous CSF otorrhea. Cholesteatoma erosion, prior surgery, and congenital malformation are secondary causes.'),
('4912805f-6f9b-5267-9430-7c4368be6504'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of facial nerve paralysis in acute otitis media?', '["Lyme disease resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Cholesteatoma compression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dehiscence of the facial nerve canal (the nerve is exposed in the middle ear) causing inflammation but not necessarily surgical emergency","Facial nerve schwannoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial infection of the nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dehiscence of the facial nerve canal (the nerve is exposed in the middle ear) causing inflammation but not necessarily surgical emergency', 'The facial nerve is often dehiscent within the middle ear, so inflammation in acute otitis media can produce paralysis through direct exposure rather than a compressive emergency. Cholesteatoma and schwannoma cause paralysis in chronic or mass settings.'),
('7c269464-d472-5890-b528-b48841bf7e0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of facial nerve paralysis in chronic otitis media?', '["Aseptic meningitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Cholesteatoma eroding the fallopian canal and compressing or inflaming the facial nerve","Acute infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Labyrinthitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Postoperative trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesteatoma eroding the fallopian canal and compressing or inflaming the facial nerve', 'Cholesteatoma erodes the fallopian canal and compresses or inflames the facial nerve, the most common cause of paralysis in chronic otitis media. Aseptic meningitis, labyrinthitis, and postoperative trauma are not the usual cause.'),
('d2e1b503-f31c-542b-815b-c0d3a894436a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of aural atresia?', '["Postsurgical (canal wall-down mastoidectomy) resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic infection (malignant otitis externa) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acquired from trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital (failure of the first pharyngeal cleft to canalize forming the external auditory meatus)","Burns leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital (failure of the first pharyngeal cleft to canalize forming the external auditory meatus)', 'Aural atresia is usually congenital, resulting from failure of the first pharyngeal cleft to canalize into the external auditory meatus. Acquired causes such as canal-wall-down mastoidectomy, chronic infection, trauma, and burns are far less common than the congenital form.'),
('5dd668ac-5d21-53cb-aad6-ad369c4699d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of microtia?', '["Genetic (autosomal dominant) resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalidomide exposure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic (most cases are isolated and sporadic) but associated with Goldenhar syndrome and other first arch syndromes","Maternal isotretinoin exposure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maternal rubella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (most cases are isolated and sporadic) but associated with Goldenhar syndrome and other first arch syndromes', 'Most cases of microtia are idiopathic and sporadic, although the anomaly can be part of first-arch syndromes such as Goldenhar syndrome. Inherited mutations, thalidomide or isotretinoin exposure, and maternal rubella are less frequent causes.'),
('3648fbc7-3c22-5d13-bff1-94085748cd71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of anotia?', '["Treacher Collins syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Idiopathic (anotia complete absence of the auricle) is rare and often syndromic","Nager syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalidomide embryopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gondar syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (anotia complete absence of the auricle) is rare and often syndromic', 'Anotia, the complete absence of the auricle, is rare and usually syndromic rather than a common isolated defect, so its cause is largely idiopathic. Treacher Collins and Nager syndromes and thalidomide embryopathy account for only a minority of cases.'),
('c96afd0c-48c8-56a5-81e1-f22142f9c2fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of laryngeal papillomatosis in children?', '["Cigarette smoking resulting in decreased functional activity","HSV infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Recurrent respiratory infections mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertical transmission of HPV (human papillomavirus types 6 and 11) from mother to child during vaginal delivery","Autosomal dominant mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical transmission of HPV (human papillomavirus types 6 and 11) from mother to child during vaginal delivery', 'Laryngeal papillomatosis in children results from vertical transmission of HPV types 6 and 11 during vaginal delivery. Smoking, HSV, recurrent infections, and inherited mutations do not produce this acquired laryngeal lesion.'),
('862488bc-899e-53c7-9f4c-e002b609c50b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common symptom of laryngeal papillomatosis?', '["Odynophagia resulting in decreased functional activity","Chest pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Progressive hoarseness and stridor (especially in young children)","Cough without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dysphagia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive hoarseness and stridor (especially in young children)', 'Progressive hoarseness, often accompanied by stridor, is the typical presentation of laryngeal papillomatosis because the papillomas arise on the vocal cords. Odynophagia, chest pain, cough, and dysphagia are less characteristic of this disease.'),
('80bac6aa-e16f-591c-87e3-c7f82fb1dfb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common treatment for laryngeal papillomatosis?', '["Radiation therapy resulting in decreased functional activity","HPV vaccine (preventive) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cidofovir injection (intralesional) mediated by specific receptor activation","Interferon therapy (adjuvant) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgical debulking (microdebrider CO2 laser) most common treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical debulking (microdebrider CO2 laser) most common treatment', 'Surgical debulking, usually with a microdebrider or CO2 laser, is the mainstay of treatment for laryngeal papillomatosis. Adjuvant intralesional cidofovir or interferon and the preventive HPV vaccine do not represent the primary therapy.'),
('9d4d158c-4b3b-51f6-9e5b-1d35795b0f6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of laryngeal cancer in adults?', '["HPV infection (HPV 16 for supraglottic) resulting in decreased functional activity","GERD (laryngopharyngeal reflux) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asbestos exposure mediated by specific receptor activation","Solely alcohol consumption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tobacco smoking (most important risk factor) combined with alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'Tobacco smoking (most important risk factor) combined with alcohol', 'Tobacco smoking is the strongest risk factor for laryngeal cancer, and its effect is potentiated by alcohol. HPV infection, reflux, and asbestos exposure are less important contributors in adults.'),
('456b6ec7-c35c-5c54-9d0b-f905057abb85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common type of laryngeal cancer?', '["Verrucous carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Sarcoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Spindle cell carcinoma (sarcomatoid) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Squamous cell carcinoma (accounting for >95% of laryngeal malignancies)"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma (accounting for >95% of laryngeal malignancies)', 'Squamous cell carcinoma accounts for more than 95% of laryngeal malignancies. Rare variants such as verrucous, spindle cell, and small cell carcinoma and sarcoma are far less common.'),
('d8b56f7e-34e1-59b8-870e-b4a5271a0889'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common site of laryngeal cancer?', '["Glottis (true vocal cords) most common site","Subglottis","Postcricoid region","Transglottic (across the ventricle)","Supraglottis (epiglottis aryepiglottic fold false cords)"]'::jsonb, 'Glottis (true vocal cords) most common site', 'The glottis, formed by the true vocal cords, is the most frequent site of laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma. Supraglottic, subglottic, and transglottic locations are less common.'),
('8c264274-2a33-57bf-a2a5-174ddfe4fbbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of hoarseness in smokers?', '["GERD resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic laryngitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laryngeal cancer (squamous cell carcinoma of the glottis)","Vocal cord nodules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reinke edema (polypoid corditis) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngeal cancer (squamous cell carcinoma of the glottis)', 'Persistent hoarseness in a smoker raises suspicion for squamous cell carcinoma of the glottis. Benign conditions such as reflux laryngitis, vocal cord nodules, and Reinke edema are less likely causes of new hoarseness in this setting.'),
('e73283f6-b54b-5d04-ab56-f8d5eb42290b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of aspiration in children?', '["Neurologic impairment (cerebral palsy global developmental delay) most common cause of chronic aspiration","Anatomic (cleft palate laryngeal cleft) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Structural (esophageal atresia TEF repair) mediated by specific receptor activation","GERD (silent aspiration from reflux) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prematurity (poor coordination) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurologic impairment (cerebral palsy global developmental delay) most common cause of chronic aspiration', 'Neurologic impairment such as cerebral palsy and global developmental delay is the most common cause of chronic aspiration in children. Anatomic, structural, and reflux-related mechanisms are less frequent causes.'),
('0cae0a29-8cc0-5d53-a07e-dcc51be09009'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of aspiration pneumonia in children with CP?', '["Seizure-related aspiration resulting in decreased functional activity","Ineffective cough accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oropharyngeal dysphagia with silent aspiration of saliva and food","Severe GERD with regurgitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mucus plugging leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oropharyngeal dysphagia with silent aspiration of saliva and food', 'In children with cerebral palsy, oropharyngeal dysphagia with silent aspiration of saliva and food underlies most aspiration pneumonia. Seizures, ineffective cough, severe reflux, and mucus plugging are contributing rather than principal causes.'),
('a9ef9dbf-f4b4-50bd-b442-60b892237368'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of velopharyngeal insufficiency (VPI)?', '["Velar paralysis (neurologic)","Cleft palate (overt or submucous) most common cause","Macroglossia (Beckwith-Wiedemann)","Post-adenoidectomy (iatrogenic)","Cleft palate"]'::jsonb, 'Cleft palate (overt or submucous) most common cause', 'Cleft palate, whether overt or submucous, is the most common cause of velopharyngeal insufficiency. Neurologic velar paralysis, macroglossia, and post-adenoidectomy changes are less frequent causes.'),
('07c1a740-d108-537c-b63c-6cd4d5a23967'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of hypernasal speech?', '["Enlarged adenoids (denasal)","Velopharyngeal insufficiency (especially from cleft palate)","Choanal atresia (nasal obstruction)","Hypertrophic tonsils (denasal)","Allergic rhinitis (denasal)"]'::jsonb, 'Velopharyngeal insufficiency (especially from cleft palate)', 'Hypernasal speech results from velopharyngeal insufficiency, most often due to cleft palate, which lets air escape through the nose. Denasal conditions such as enlarged adenoids, choanal atresia, tonsillar hypertrophy, and allergic rhinitis produce the opposite, hyponasal quality.'),
('5c9acc7c-56e6-5795-8375-6aef341647e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is submucous cleft palate?', '["A cleft of the soft palate covered by mucosa with a bifid uvula palpable notch of the hard palate and zona pellucida (translucent midline)","Hard palate cleft only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only bifid uvula without other findings mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete cleft of the entire palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Palatal fistula after repair leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cleft of the soft palate covered by mucosa with a bifid uvula palpable notch of the hard palate and zona pellucida (translucent midline)', 'A submucous cleft is a soft-palate cleft hidden beneath intact mucosa, classically marked by a bifid uvula, a palpable notch of the posterior hard palate, and a translucent zona pellucida. Isolated bifid uvula, complete clefting, and palatal fistula do not meet this definition.'),
('7fc1f270-4d3d-54d5-b9bf-50cd78e1cc64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of snoring in children?', '["Allergic rhinitis","Obesity (with or without tonsil enlargement)","Adenotonsillar hypertrophy (most common cause of pediatric snoring and OSA)","Recurrent tonsillitis with scarred tonsils","Craniofacial anomalies (midface retrusion)"]'::jsonb, 'Adenotonsillar hypertrophy (most common cause of pediatric snoring and OSA)', 'Adenotonsillar hypertrophy is the most common cause of snoring and obstructive sleep apnea in children. Obesity, allergic rhinitis, scarred tonsils, and craniofacial anomalies are less common contributors in the pediatric age group.'),
('1ad60527-4651-5b23-b821-1cdfdbcb6ada'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of cleft palate in the newborn?', '["Nonsyndromic (isolated cleft palate without other anomalies) accounts for about 50% of cases","Teratogenic (fetal alcohol corticosteroids) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pierre Robin sequence (secondary to micrognathia) mediated by specific receptor activation","Syndromic (22q11.2 deletion Stickler Van der Woude) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maternal vitamin deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nonsyndromic (isolated cleft palate without other anomalies) accounts for about 50% of cases', 'Isolated, nonsyndromic cleft palate accounts for roughly half of all newborn cases. Syndromic associations, Pierre Robin sequence, teratogenic exposure, and maternal vitamin deficiency account for a smaller proportion.'),
('e58dc38f-5a3f-5959-92a0-5a9731106c7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common syndrome associated with submucous cleft palate?', '["Stickler syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Kabuki syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Treacher Collins syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","22q11.2 deletion syndrome (velocardiofacial syndrome) most common","Van der Woude syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '22q11.2 deletion syndrome (velocardiofacial syndrome) most common', 'The 22q11.2 deletion (velocardiofacial) syndrome is the most common syndrome associated with submucous cleft palate, often with cardiac and palatal anomalies. Stickler, Kabuki, Treacher Collins, and Van der Woude syndromes are less common associations.'),
('30c3e3e3-99e1-56bb-80d7-a6ebdbd716a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of dysphagia after esophageal atresia repair?', '["Tracheomalacia (causing compression)","Anastomotic stricture (requiring dilation)","Recurrent TE fistula","GERD (fundoplication may be needed)","Esophageal dysmotility (abnormal peristalsis) most common cause"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal dysmotility (abnormal peristalsis) most common cause', 'Abnormal esophageal peristalsis, or dysmotility, is the most frequent cause of long-term dysphagia after esophageal atresia repair. Anastomotic stricture, recurrent tracheoesophageal fistula, reflux, and tracheomalacia are other but less common causes.'),
('2fdba35e-5b99-5837-a936-e69153d6fb33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of dysphagia in children with repaired cleft palate?', '["Feeding aversions from early surgeries resulting in decreased functional activity","Residual velopharyngeal insufficiency (VPI) causing nasal regurgitation","Ankyloglossia mediated by specific receptor activation","Anatomic narrowing of the palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dental malocclusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Residual velopharyngeal insufficiency (VPI) causing nasal regurgitation', 'Residual velopharyngeal insufficiency after cleft palate repair causes nasal regurgitation and swallowing difficulty. Feeding aversion, ankyloglossia, anatomic narrowing, and dental malocclusion are less important causes.'),
('3413e32c-25dd-5466-9049-018b21ab25eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of oral aversion in infants?', '["Neurologic impairment resulting in decreased functional activity","Medical trauma (prolonged intubation nasogastric tubes painful oral procedures in NICU) most common","Cleft palate mediated by specific receptor activation","Taste disturbance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GERD (painful feeding) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medical trauma (prolonged intubation nasogastric tubes painful oral procedures in NICU) most common', 'Medical trauma, such as prolonged intubation, nasogastric tubes, and painful oral procedures in the NICU, is the most common cause of oral aversion in infants. Neurologic, structural, and reflux-related factors are less frequent.'),
('378b86e6-99aa-5851-ad19-67a02342a020'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of feeding difficulty in infants with cleft palate?', '["Anatomic obstruction (tongue position) resulting in decreased functional activity","GERD with aspiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Behavioral aversion mediated by specific receptor activation","Swallowing inc coordination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inability to create negative intraoral pressure for sucking (due to the palatal defect)"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to create negative intraoral pressure for sucking (due to the palatal defect)', 'The palatal defect prevents the infant from generating the negative intraoral pressure needed for sucking, and this is the immediate basis of feeding difficulty. Anatomic obstruction, reflux, behavioral aversion, and swallowing incoordination are not the primary mechanical cause.'),
('b6360e1a-c704-526d-a339-593ec7a137b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of laryngospasm in children?', '["Postnasal drip resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypocalcemic tetany accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GERD (laryngopharyngeal reflux) mediated by specific receptor activation","Anesthesia (light anesthesia or extubation during stage 2) most common cause of intraoperative laryngospasm","Medication reaction (ACE inhibitors) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anesthesia (light anesthesia or extubation during stage 2) most common cause of intraoperative laryngospasm', 'Laryngospasm in children occurs most often during anesthesia, typically with light anesthesia or during extubation in stage 2 of the anesthetic plane. Reflux, hypocalcemic tetany, postnasal drip, and drug reactions are less common triggers.'),
('773fe034-70c7-5aea-b647-04ab43aa8365'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of post-extubation stridor in children?', '["Laryngeal edema (croup-like from endotracheal tube trauma) most common","Arytenoid dislocation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vocal cord paralysis (trauma) mediated by specific receptor activation","Subglottic stenosis (preexisting) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laryngospasm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngeal edema (croup-like from endotracheal tube trauma) most common', 'Endotracheal tube trauma causing laryngeal edema is the most common cause of post-extubation stridor in children. Vocal cord paralysis, arytenoid dislocation, preexisting subglottic stenosis, and laryngospasm are less common.'),
('e1e66408-8084-5f68-b26b-23b2e7aeddeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of granuloma of the vocal cords?', '["Intubation trauma (most common cause of vocal process granuloma)","Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Voice abuse mediated by specific receptor activation","GERD (laryngopharyngeal reflux) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic coughing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intubation trauma (most common cause of vocal process granuloma)', 'Granulomas of the vocal processes most often follow endotracheal intubation trauma. Recurrent papillomatosis, voice abuse, reflux, and chronic coughing are less frequent causes.'),
('cc70706b-f7dd-572f-96a5-dfb7d03cdbe2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of vocal process granuloma?', '["GERD with reflux reaching the posterior larynx","Voice abuse (singing screaming)","Chronic cough (habit cough in children)","Allergies with throat clearing","Prolonged or traumatic endotracheal intubation"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged or traumatic endotracheal intubation', 'Prolonged or traumatic endotracheal intubation is the most common cause of vocal process granuloma, which forms on the arytenoid vocal process. Reflux, voice overuse, chronic cough, and throat clearing are contributing rather than principal causes.'),
('a5b9c486-0f18-51ac-8443-dca432e2faf0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of Reinke edema (polypoid corditis)?', '["Chronic voice abuse resulting in decreased functional activity","GERD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cigarette smoking (most common cause of Reinke edema)","Hypothyroidism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Allergies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cigarette smoking (most common cause of Reinke edema)', 'Cigarette smoking is the leading cause of Reinke edema, the fluid accumulation within the superficial lamina propria of the vocal cords. Chronic voice abuse, reflux, hypothyroidism, and allergies play smaller roles.'),
('fe8a02da-ffd7-57a9-94bb-dce12441ea68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma (JNA)?', '["Unknown (highly vascular benign tumor in adolescent males) likely arising from the pterygopalatine fossa","HPV infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","Genetic mutation (CTNNB1) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unknown (highly vascular benign tumor in adolescent males) likely arising from the pterygopalatine fossa', 'The cause of juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma is unknown; it is a highly vascular benign tumor of adolescent males that typically arises near the pterygopalatine fossa. HPV, inflammation, trauma, and CTNNB1 mutation are not established causes.'),
('da6b2731-121e-53f6-94ab-6e0a826581fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common symptom of JNA?', '["Headache resulting in decreased functional activity","Proptosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial swelling mediated by specific receptor activation","Nasal congestion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epistaxis (most common presenting symptom) and nasal obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Epistaxis (most common presenting symptom) and nasal obstruction', 'Epistaxis is the most common presenting symptom of juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma, often accompanied by nasal obstruction. Headache, proptosis, and facial swelling occur with advanced local extension.'),
('29122ca8-18c3-5aea-afaa-262c61891f92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common treatment for JNA?', '["Hormonal therapy resulting in decreased functional activity","Radiation therapy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chemotherapy mediated by specific receptor activation","Surgical resection (preoperative embolization for large tumors) most common","Cryotherapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical resection (preoperative embolization for large tumors) most common', 'Surgical resection, with preoperative embolization for large tumors, is the mainstay of treatment for juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma. Radiation, hormonal therapy, and chemotherapy are reserved for unresectable or recurrent disease.'),
('135e3110-9d81-55c8-96a7-0e30cf0d96b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of nasal dermoid cyst?', '["Dermoid cyst of the scalp resulting in decreased functional activity","Embryologic remnant of the neuroectodermal tract (failure of the dural tract to obliterate)","Preauricular sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Encephalocele without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glioma (nasal glioma) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Embryologic remnant of the neuroectodermal tract (failure of the dural tract to obliterate)', 'Nasal dermoid cysts arise from a persistent embryologic tract related to the neuroectoderm, when the dural tract fails to obliterate. Scalp dermoids, preauricular sinuses, encephaloceles, and gliomas are distinct midline nasal entities.'),
('4b14ccbd-274d-5693-a6ed-d7d07356b411'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of nasal glioma?', '["Encephalocele (most common congenital midline nasal mass actually is dermoid)","Nasal hemangioma (infantile) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal glioma (non-neoplastic glial tissue) is congenital but not a true tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Dermoid cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nasal polyp leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Encephalocele (most common congenital midline nasal mass actually is dermoid)', 'Nasal gliomas, like encephaloceles and dermoid cysts, are congenital midline nasal masses arising from defective separation of neural and ectodermal tissue during development. A nasal glioma is ectopic glial tissue rather than a true tumor, and infantile hemangiomas or polyps are not embryologic midline lesions.'),
('a2c6de5b-dd1d-554b-b3f9-2ebc0623136f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) rhinorrhea?', '["Iatrogenic (sinus surgery most common surgical cause)","Tumor (esthesioneuroblastoma)","Idiopathic ( cause found)","Congenital (Mondini inner ear malformation with spontaneous leak)","Trauma (skull base fracture most common cause of CSF leak)"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma (skull base fracture most common cause of CSF leak)', 'Head trauma producing a skull-base fracture is the most common cause of CSF rhinorrhea. Iatrogenic sinus surgery is the most common surgical cause, while tumors, congenital malformations, and idiopathic leaks are less frequent.'),
('49ac5ffe-c550-58c5-ae7c-56eda75585bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of CSF rhinorrhea after endoscopic sinus surgery?', '["Injury to the cribriform plate or fovea ethmoidalis during ethmoidectomy (the most common iatrogenic cause)","Frontal sinus injury (outer table) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Injury to the septum mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphenoid sinus injury (lateral wall) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nasolacrimal duct injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Injury to the cribriform plate or fovea ethmoidalis during ethmoidectomy (the most common iatrogenic cause)', 'During endoscopic sinus surgery, injury to the cribriform plate or the fovea ethmoidalis during ethmoidectomy is the most common cause of iatrogenic CSF rhinorrhea. Damage to the septum, nasolacrimal duct, or frontal or sphenoid regions does not directly produce a CSF leak.'),
('007bd715-09b1-575f-8aa9-cf7422a831c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of anosmia after head trauma?', '["Skull base fracture alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Shearing injury of the olfactory nerves crossing the cribriform plate (post-traumatic anosmia)","Frontal lobe contusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Subdural hematoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intracranial hypertension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shearing injury of the olfactory nerves crossing the cribriform plate (post-traumatic anosmia)', 'Post-traumatic anosmia results from shearing of the olfactory nerve filaments as they cross the cribriform plate. Skull-base fracture, frontal lobe contusion, subdural hematoma, or raised intracranial pressure only cause anosmia when the olfactory pathways are directly damaged.'),
('d9c8aa05-bb6d-5d9d-ac5d-9964c53b5640'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of parosmia?', '["Head trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Temporal lobe epilepsy (olfactory aura) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Post-viral olfactory dysfunction (altered smell after URI) most common cause of parosmia","Chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurodegenerative (early Parkinson disease) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-viral olfactory dysfunction (altered smell after URI) most common cause of parosmia', 'Post-viral olfactory dysfunction, with altered smell after an upper respiratory infection, is the most common cause of parosmia. Head trauma, temporal lobe epilepsy, chronic rhinosinusitis with polyps, and neurodegenerative disease are less common.'),
('eb8afb35-d183-5e1d-9846-7d1981dcc076'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of nasal septal perforation?', '["Neoplasia (SCC)","Trauma (nose picking fracture)","Inflammatory (GPA granulomatosis with polyangiitis)","Iatrogenic (post-surgical septoplasty or cautery) most common cause","Cocaine use (vasoconstrictive necrosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Iatrogenic (post-surgical septoplasty or cautery) most common cause', 'Iatrogenic injury, especially after septoplasty or nasal cautery, is the most common cause of nasal septal perforation. Trauma, cocaine use, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, and neoplasia are less common causes.'),
('dda655af-c28a-5a3d-9756-66c2b88358de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'What is the most common cause of saddle nose deformity?', '["Relapsing polychondritis","Cocaine use (most common cause of acquired saddle nose)","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA Wegener)","Trauma (nasal fracture)","Congenital syphilis"]'::jsonb, 'Cocaine use (most common cause of acquired saddle nose)', 'Cocaine use, through vasoconstrictive necrosis of the nasal septum, is the most common cause of acquired saddle nose deformity. Relapsing polychondritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, trauma, and congenital syphilis are less common.'),
('1372a8e4-5380-53b5-95cb-b24b8c3291fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'In Head and neck development, which statement best fits the following fact: the head and neck develop from which pharyngeal arches?', '["Three pairs resulting in decreased functional activity","Six pairs of pharyngeal arches that appear during week 4-5","Four pairs of pharyngeal arches mediated by specific receptor activation","Seven pairs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Five pairs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Six pairs of pharyngeal arches that appear during week 4-5', 'Six pairs of pharyngeal arches form during weeks 4-5 of development, with the sixth pair being rudimentary. Three, four, five, or seven pairs do not reflect the number of arches.'),
('53ea9572-9c00-5fbf-b1ec-bccab708220f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'Regarding Head and neck development, what is the correct answer to: each pharyngeal arch contains which components?', '["Only a nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","An artery nerve cartilage and muscle","Only cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Only muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only an artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An artery nerve cartilage and muscle', 'Each pharyngeal arch contains an artery, a nerve, a cartilage rod, and a muscle component derived from its mesenchyme. No pharyngeal arch consists of only a single tissue type.'),
('5e094335-4c16-5dc2-ba21-6458631da5b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'For Head and neck development, which option is most appropriate: the first pharyngeal arch is also called:?', '["Mandibular arch","Maxillary arch","Thyroid arch","Laryngeal arch","Hyoid arch"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular arch', 'The first pharyngeal arch is called the mandibular arch and is innervated by the trigeminal nerve. Maxillary, thyroid, laryngeal, and hyoid names do not apply to the first arch.'),
('f358ebf1-06e8-5367-932a-99e26a7fcf51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Head and neck development when asked: the second pharyngeal arch is also called:?', '["Hyoid arch","Mandibular arch","Maxillary arch","Laryngeal arch","Thyroid arch"]'::jsonb, 'Hyoid arch', 'The second pharyngeal arch is called the hyoid (Reichert) arch and is innervated by the facial nerve. Mandibular, maxillary, laryngeal, and thyroid names do not apply to the second arch.'),
('b5b87063-70e0-5616-a9fd-4786556b58cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'In Head and neck development, the best response to ''The cartilage of the first pharyngeal arch (Meckel''s cartilage) gives rise to which structures'' is:', '["Cricoid cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Mandible incus malleus and sphenomandibular ligament","Laryngeal cartilages mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroid cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyoid bone and stapes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mandible incus malleus and sphenomandibular ligament', 'Meckel cartilage of the first arch forms the mandible, incus, malleus, and sphenomandibular ligament. The hyoid bone, stapes, and laryngeal cartilages derive from other arches.'),
('9484bae2-25ac-5035-8931-5b54af5346f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'During revision of Head and neck development, which of the following is correct: the cartilage of the second pharyngeal arch (reichert''s cartilage) gives rise to which structures?', '["Thyroid cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Cricoid cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stapes styloid process stylohyoid ligament and lesser horn of hyoid","Mandible without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Incus and malleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stapes styloid process stylohyoid ligament and lesser horn of hyoid', 'Reichert cartilage of the second arch gives rise to the stapes, styloid process, stylohyoid ligament, and lesser horn of the hyoid bone. The mandible, incus, and malleus come from the first arch, while thyroid and cricoid cartilages derive from lower arches.'),
('158bec56-4711-562d-928d-f7ad76955ded'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'In Head and neck development, which statement best fits the following fact: the cartilage of the third pharyngeal arch gives rise to which structure?', '["Stapes resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arytenoid cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Cricoid cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Greater horn of hyoid bone (and part of body)"]'::jsonb, 'Greater horn of hyoid bone (and part of body)', 'The cartilage of the third pharyngeal arch forms the greater horn and part of the body of the hyoid bone. The stapes derives from the second arch, and the laryngeal cartilages derive from the fourth and sixth arches.'),
('111e3900-2441-5b42-a620-a50770ae69f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'Regarding Head and neck development, what is the correct answer to: the cartilages of the fourth and sixth arches give rise to which structures?', '["Stapes and incus resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid cricoid arytenoid and other laryngeal cartilages","Maxilla only mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyoid bone only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mandible only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid cricoid arytenoid and other laryngeal cartilages', 'The fourth and sixth arch cartilages form the thyroid, cricoid, arytenoid, and other laryngeal cartilages. The stapes and incus, maxilla, mandible, and hyoid bone derive from higher arches.'),
('15a30f4d-bbc2-5df9-b08a-b1ab9e75c2c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'For Head and neck development, which option is most appropriate: the muscles of the first pharyngeal arch develop into:?', '["Pharyngeal constrictors resulting in decreased functional activity","Mylohyoid anterior belly of digastric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stylohyoid posterior belly of digastric mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscles of facial expression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscles of mastication (temporalis masseter pterygoids) tensor tympani tensor veli palatini"]'::jsonb, 'Muscles of mastication (temporalis masseter pterygoids) tensor tympani tensor veli palatini', 'The first arch mesoderm forms the muscles of mastication, tensor tympani, and tensor veli palatini, all innervated by the trigeminal nerve. Muscles of facial expression belong to the second arch, and pharyngeal constrictors arise from lower arches.'),
('02e20890-7bc3-58cd-8b85-3218a4381401'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Head and neck development when asked: the muscles of the second pharyngeal arch develop into:?', '["Pharyngeal constrictors resulting in decreased functional activity","Mylohyoid anterior belly of digastric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscles of facial expression stapedius stylohyoid posterior belly of digastric platysma","Intrinsic laryngeal muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscles of mastication leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscles of facial expression stapedius stylohyoid posterior belly of digastric platysma', 'The second arch forms the muscles of facial expression, stapedius, stylohyoid, posterior belly of the digastric, and platysma, all innervated by the facial nerve. Constrictors, mylohyoid and anterior digastric, laryngeal, and masticatory muscles arise from other arches.'),
('1053b7f3-0f1d-5e99-837c-9766e4e2024d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'In Head and neck development, the best response to ''The muscles of the third pharyngeal arch develop into:'' is:', '["All pharyngeal constrictors resulting in decreased functional activity","Laryngeal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mylohyoid muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Stylopharyngeus (the only muscle of the third arch)","Muscles of mastication leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stylopharyngeus (the only muscle of the third arch)', 'Stylopharyngeus is the only muscle derived from the third pharyngeal arch and is supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve. Pharyngeal constrictors, laryngeal muscles, mylohyoid, and masticatory muscles come from other arches.'),
('54d89e2e-8bee-5d0d-ab8f-6049b3d8ae59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Head and Neck', 'During revision of Head and neck development, which of the following is correct: the muscles of the fourth pharyngeal arch develop into:?', '["Cricothyroid and intrinsic laryngeal muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Intrinsic laryngeal muscles are from sixth arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stylopharyngeus mediated by specific receptor activation","Following exceptions: tensor veli palatini is from first arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pharyngeal constrictors cricothyroid levator veli palatini (most pharyngeal and soft palate muscles)"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal constrictors cricothyroid levator veli palatini (most pharyngeal and soft palate muscles)', 'The fourth arch forms the pharyngeal constrictors, cricothyroid, and levator veli palatini, with tensor veli palatini arising from the first arch. Intrinsic laryngeal muscles are sixth-arch derivatives, and stylopharyngeus is a third-arch muscle.'),
('a8c0e6fa-8df4-5827-8fcb-401888d0dee5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Vertebrae and ribs are derived from which somite component?', '["Sclerotome","Syndetome","Myotome","Dermatome","Arthrotome"]'::jsonb, 'Sclerotome', 'Somites subdivide into sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome. The ventromedial sclerotome migrates around the notochord and neural tube to form the vertebral bodies, vertebral arches, and ribs, while the myotome gives muscle and the dermatome gives dermis.'),
('740b7b11-5edc-58fa-b597-a82780f350d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Skeletal muscles of the back are derived from which structure?', '["Myotome","Sclerotome","Syndetome","Dermatome","Somite"]'::jsonb, 'Myotome', 'The myotome is the dorsal part of each somite that forms skeletal muscle. Muscles of the back arise from the epaxial portion of the myotome, whereas the sclerotome forms vertebrae and ribs and the dermatome forms the dermis.'),
('564fbadf-4926-52d6-b195-08d29a12fdf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Dermis of the back is derived from which structure?', '["Somite","Dermatome","Myotome","Sclerotome","Syndetome"]'::jsonb, 'Dermatome', 'The dermatome is the dorsal part of the somite that gives rise to the dermis of the back and overlying subcutaneous tissue. The sclerotome forms vertebrae and ribs, while the myotome forms muscle.'),
('ecbb4010-26ca-5e07-9961-ee009a301cd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Ossification of long bones begins at which site?', '["Articular cartilage","Secondary ossification center in epiphysis","Growth plate","Primary ossification center in diaphysis","Periosteum"]'::jsonb, 'Primary ossification center in diaphysis', 'Long bones ossify by endochondral ossification that begins in the primary center within the diaphysis during fetal life. Secondary centers in the epiphyses appear later and remain separated from the shaft by the growth plate until skeletal maturity.'),
('b35954c2-9205-5d8b-a968-d55a3f025579'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What type of ossification forms the flat bones of the skull?', '["Perichondral ossification","Appositional growth","Intramembranous ossification","Endochondral ossification","Periosteal ossification"]'::jsonb, 'Intramembranous ossification', 'Flat bones of the skull (calvaria), face, and clavicle form directly within a membrane of condensed mesenchyme by intramembranous ossification. Endochondral ossification uses a preformed cartilage model and forms the long bones and skull base.'),
('1342332c-8c67-557c-b403-c3eddb690545'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What type of ossification forms the long bones?', '["Appositional growth","Periosteal ossification","Endochondral ossification","Perichondral ossification","Intramembranous ossification"]'::jsonb, 'Endochondral ossification', 'Long bones develop from a hyaline cartilage model that is progressively replaced by bone, a process called endochondral ossification. Intramembranous ossification, by contrast, produces bone directly in membrane without a cartilage template.'),
('72c028a4-ede6-53c3-9266-ffdffdce1284'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The clavicle is unique because it undergoes which type of ossification?', '["Only intramembranous","Only endochondral","ossification","Both intramembranous and endochondral","Appositional growth"]'::jsonb, 'Both intramembranous and endochondral', 'The clavicle is the only long bone that ossifies by both mechanisms: its shaft forms by intramembranous ossification and its ends by endochondral ossification. It is also the first bone of the body to begin ossification.'),
('d1710b9a-abee-5376-814f-d5736113c116'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The limbs develop from which structure?', '["Somites only resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral plate mesoderm only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ectoderm only mediated by specific receptor activation","Limb buds (lateral plate mesoderm and myotomes)","Neural crest only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Limb buds (lateral plate mesoderm and myotomes)', 'Limbs arise from limb buds containing lateral plate mesoderm (bones and connective tissue) and somite-derived myotomes (muscles), covered by ectoderm. The apical ectodermal ridge at the bud tip directs outgrowth.'),
('ea2cfa7c-6cc0-5908-806c-1170ff5bfdc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What induces the formation of limb buds?', '["Notochord","Neural tube","Zone of polarizing activity (ZPA)","Apical ectodermal ridge (AER)","Primitive streak"]'::jsonb, 'Apical ectodermal ridge (AER)', 'The apical ectodermal ridge, a thickened ectodermal band at the limb bud tip, induces and maintains limb outgrowth by secreting fibroblast growth factors. The ZPA instead patterns the anterior-posterior axis of the limb.'),
('9bfa06f7-9d92-5a18-bc8f-1227042acf22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the function of the zone of polarizing activity (ZPA)?', '["Induces limb bud formation","Patterns the anterior-posterior axis of the limb","Promotes apoptosis","Patterns the proximal-distal axis","Controls muscle formation"]'::jsonb, 'Patterns the anterior-posterior axis of the limb', 'The zone of polarizing activity, located at the posterior margin of the limb bud, secretes Sonic hedgehog (Shh) to specify the anterior-posterior axis of the limb. Proximal-distal outgrowth is controlled by the AER rather than the ZPA.'),
('d6bd8d45-8261-5ac3-b32c-7bb085db41b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What determines proximal-distal patterning of the limb?', '["AER and FGF signaling","Neural tube","Notochord","Somites","ZPA and Shh signaling"]'::jsonb, 'AER and FGF signaling', 'Proximal-distal patterning of the limb is directed by the apical ectodermal ridge through FGF signaling, which keeps the underlying mesenchyme undifferentiated as the bud elongates. The ZPA with Shh patterns the anterior-posterior axis.'),
('2e3ad1b7-5bf0-5c8c-b943-9e828e262574'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is phocomelia?', '["Absence of entire limb resulting in decreased functional activity","Shortening or absence of limb bones with normal hands/feet","Webbed fingers mediated by specific receptor activation","Extra digits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fusion of digits leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shortening or absence of limb bones with normal hands/feet', 'Phocomelia is the shortening or absence of proximal limb bones (seal limb), with the hands or feet remaining relatively normal. It is classically linked to thalidomide exposure in the 1960s.'),
('8c71cfd8-5b5e-5af5-8c56-0249cc916ed0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What drug caused phocomelia in the 1960s?', '["Warfarin","Thalidomide","Accutane","Alcohol","Valproic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Thalidomide', 'Thalidomide, marketed as a sedative in the 1960s, caused phocomelia and other limb defects when taken during the critical period of limb development, roughly days 21-36 after fertilization. Warfarin, alcohol, and valproic acid do not produce this pattern.'),
('842bf4b6-c90b-5896-8b8e-aa8acb1bdbfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is amelia?', '["Short digits","Extra digits","Absence of part of a limb","Complete absence of one or more limbs","Fusion of digits"]'::jsonb, 'Complete absence of one or more limbs', 'Amelia is the complete absence of one or more limbs resulting from failure of limb bud development. It contrasts with phocomelia (proximal shortening) and meromelia (partial absence of a limb).'),
('1887b7d4-c707-5c79-9fdc-9ad16ec9bc90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is meromelia?', '["Extra digits","Short digits","Partial absence of a limb","Fusion of digits","Complete absence of a limb"]'::jsonb, 'Partial absence of a limb', 'Meromelia is the partial absence of a limb in which only part of the limb is missing. It differs from amelia (complete absence) and phocomelia (short proximal bones with relatively normal distal parts).'),
('910af764-706a-50d2-899a-63055a8b03b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Radial ray defects are associated with which condition?', '["Holt-Oram syndrome","Turner syndrome","Down syndrome","Trisomy 18","Marfan syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Holt-Oram syndrome', 'Holt-Oram syndrome is an autosomal dominant condition caused by TBX5 mutations that combines radial ray defects of the upper limb (thumb and radius) with congenital cardiac anomalies. The other listed syndromes have different skeletal and clinical features.'),
('b591e7b4-804d-5d7e-b6ab-bb227aed2e79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common congenital hand anomaly?', '["Syndactyly","Polydactyly","Camptodactyly","Clinodactyly","Macrodactyly"]'::jsonb, 'Polydactyly', 'Polydactyly, the presence of extra digits, is the most common congenital hand anomaly and may be postaxial (ulnar) or preaxial (radial). Syndactyly instead denotes webbing or fusion of adjacent digits.'),
('8212f4be-5b0b-50aa-ad5b-58568e7e2216'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is clinodactyly?', '["Large digits resulting in decreased functional activity","Extra digits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Curving of the finger (usually the fifth)","Fusion of digits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Short digits leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Curving of the finger (usually the fifth)', 'Clinodactyly is a medial or lateral deviation (curving) of a digit, most often the fifth finger, caused by a malformed middle phalanx. It is a frequent minor finding in many chromosomal and genetic syndromes.'),
('a36f4a9b-aa15-573e-b0ad-9d82f3c25143'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is camptodactyly?', '["Absence of a finger","Flexion contracture of the finger","Fusion of digits","Extra digits","Curving of the finger"]'::jsonb, 'Flexion contracture of the finger', 'Camptodactyly is a fixed flexion contracture of a digit, most often at the proximal interphalangeal joint, and results from soft tissue or tendon abnormalities rather than bony malformation. Clinodactyly, by contrast, is a deviation or curving of the finger.'),
('2514f83e-b679-5293-8573-f9cf6a45d510'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is arthrogryposis multiplex congenita?', '["Fusion of multiple joints","Dislocation of multiple joints","Multiple joint contractures present at birth","Laxity of multiple joints","Absence of multiple joints"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple joint contractures present at birth', 'Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita is the presence of multiple joint contractures at birth, usually due to reduced fetal movement from neuromuscular or connective tissue causes. The affected joints are fixed in flexion or extension rather than fused or lax.'),
('f4ee4dde-bb4b-59c3-a74e-48312ad67fce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The ribs are derived from which structure?', '["Somite resulting in decreased functional activity","Dermatome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral plate mesoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Sclerotome of thoracic somites","Myotome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sclerotome of thoracic somites', 'Ribs arise from the sclerotome of thoracic somites, the same component that forms the vertebrae. The costal processes of thoracic vertebrae extend ventrally to form ribs, which later fuse anteriorly with the developing sternum.'),
('89ef44c4-65e6-5c3d-8923-eb5c011690c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is a cervical rib?', '["An extra rib arising from the C7 vertebra","A bifid rib accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fused rib mediated by specific receptor activation","A rib in the neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An absent first rib leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An extra rib arising from the C7 vertebra', 'A cervical rib is a supernumerary rib arising from the C7 vertebra, present in about 0.5-1% of people. It may compress the lower trunk of the brachial plexus or the subclavian vessels, producing thoracic outlet syndrome.'),
('cf20a276-e350-5fb8-b6be-118f5c17bb3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Sternal defects result from:', '["Failure of sternal bar fusion","Abnormal rib growth","Neural crest defect","Excessive ossification of sternum","Defective notochord formation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of sternal bar fusion', 'Sternal defects such as a sternal cleft result from failure of the two sternal bars to fuse in the midline during the sixth to ninth weeks of development. The bars develop from lateral plate mesoderm and fuse in a craniocaudal sequence.'),
('4b54f1c4-bdaa-5abf-9390-14249b1888db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Poland syndrome?', '["Ectrodactyly resulting in decreased functional activity","Unilateral absence of pectoralis major with ipsilateral syndactyly","Sternal cleft mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral absence of breast without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Polydactyly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unilateral absence of pectoralis major with ipsilateral syndactyly', 'Poland syndrome consists of unilateral absence or hypoplasia of the pectoralis major muscle associated with ipsilateral hand anomalies such as syndactyly or brachydactyly. It is attributed to interrupted subclavian artery blood supply during development.'),
('5071532b-a6fc-5919-86d9-eed6e2713afe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The skull develops from which germ layers?', '["Only endoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Only neural crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only mesoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural crest and mesoderm (paraxial and lateral plate)","Only ectoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest and mesoderm (paraxial and lateral plate)', 'The skull develops from two sources: cranial neural crest cells form most of the facial skeleton and anterior skull, while paraxial and lateral plate mesoderm contribute to the posterior skull and cranial vault. Bone is never derived from endoderm.'),
('c9e667e6-ebfe-5185-abad-1dd885fac6f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The facial bones develop mainly from which structure?', '["Somites","Endoderm","Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Neural crest cells"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest cells', 'Most facial bones and cartilages are derived from cranial neural crest cells that migrate into the pharyngeal arches. Cranial neural crest is therefore the principal contributor to the facial skeleton, unlike the axial skeleton, which is somite-derived.'),
('e9e898f0-d6cd-569f-ab7d-656670242c01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The cranial vault (calvaria) develops from which structure?', '["Membranous ossification of neural crest and mesoderm","Perichondral ossification accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endochondral ossification mediated by specific receptor activation","Appositional growth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Periosteal ossification leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Membranous ossification of neural crest and mesoderm', 'The cranial vault develops by intramembranous (membranous) ossification of neural crest and mesoderm directly within a fibrous membrane. The resulting bones are separated by sutures and fontanelles that permit skull growth and molding during birth.'),
('c608b9b8-355e-5c6c-af91-3142965fb997'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The skull base (chondrocranium) develops by which process?', '["Periosteal ossification","Endochondral ossification","Perichondral ossification","Appositional growth","Intramembranous ossification"]'::jsonb, 'Endochondral ossification', 'The skull base (chondrocranium) is preformed in hyaline cartilage and then converted to bone by endochondral ossification. This contrasts with the calvaria, which ossifies intramembranously within membrane.'),
('ce3e9a96-1230-5118-9a36-183f55ce29fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What are fontanelles?', '["Skull fractures resulting in decreased functional activity","Abnormal skull shape accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extra skull bones mediated by specific receptor activation","Areas where sutures of the skull have not yet fused","Absence of skull bones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Areas where sutures of the skull have not yet fused', 'Fontanelles are the membranous gaps between cranial bones at the angles of sutures that have not yet fused. They allow molding of the head during birth and accommodate rapid brain growth in early infancy.'),
('0901be43-353f-56d6-8221-572b443b7b25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'When does the anterior fontanelle typically close?', '["12-18 months","6-12 months","18-24 months after birth","3-6 months","24-36 months"]'::jsonb, '18-24 months after birth', 'The anterior fontanelle normally closes between 18 and 24 months of age. Delayed closure may occur in rickets, hypothyroidism, or cleidocranial dysplasia, while premature closure suggests craniosynostosis.'),
('1573dfc1-a620-5a25-9e2c-49ef8b6c9d74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'When does the posterior fontanelle typically close?', '["6-12 months","At birth","2-3 months after birth","18-24 months","12-18 months"]'::jsonb, '2-3 months after birth', 'The posterior fontanelle normally closes by about 2-3 months after birth. The anterior fontanelle closes much later, at 18-24 months, because of the larger membranous gap at the bregma.'),
('c25fc3e3-f1fa-514c-b678-2f021f7ef0b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is craniosynostosis?', '["Complete absence of sutures","Premature fusion of one or more cranial sutures","Abnormal skull shape due to position","Extra sutures","Delayed fusion of cranial sutures"]'::jsonb, 'Premature fusion of one or more cranial sutures', 'Craniosynostosis is the premature fusion of one or more cranial sutures, which restricts growth perpendicular to the fused suture and distorts the skull shape. It may be isolated or part of syndromes such as Crouzon or Apert.'),
('653b9800-9e8c-5875-b9cd-e0b3a769016e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Scaphocephaly results from premature fusion of which suture?', '["Lambdoid suture","Squamous suture","Coronal suture","Metopic suture","Sagittal suture"]'::jsonb, 'Sagittal suture', 'Premature fusion of the sagittal suture prevents lateral growth of the skull, producing a long, narrow head called scaphocephaly. Coronal fusion produces brachycephaly and metopic fusion produces trigonocephaly.'),
('9da40fb3-82d1-538e-8c37-eb735c5eb095'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Brachycephaly results from premature fusion of which sutures?', '["Unilateral coronal suture","Metopic suture","Lambdoid suture","Sagittal suture","Bilateral coronal sutures"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral coronal sutures', 'Premature fusion of both coronal sutures restricts anterior-posterior growth, producing a short, wide head known as brachycephaly. Unilateral coronal fusion, in contrast, causes asymmetric plagiocephaly.'),
('1f71d8cb-d02b-5494-b1c6-5c355f2670ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Trigonocephaly results from premature fusion of which suture?', '["Metopic suture","Squamous suture","Lambdoid suture","Coronal suture","Sagittal suture"]'::jsonb, 'Metopic suture', 'Premature fusion of the metopic suture restricts frontal growth and produces a triangular, keel-shaped forehead known as trigonocephaly. The metopic suture normally closes first, around 3-9 months of age.'),
('99720e0d-5614-5b57-bb70-89ccd6b9408c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Plagiocephaly results from premature fusion of which suture?', '["Metopic suture","Bilateral coronal suture","Sagittal suture","All sutures","Unilateral coronal or lambdoid suture"]'::jsonb, 'Unilateral coronal or lambdoid suture', 'Plagiocephaly is an asymmetric, twisted skull caused by premature fusion of a unilateral coronal or lambdoid suture. It must be distinguished from positional plagiocephaly, which is produced by external pressure and is not due to suture fusion.'),
('1209f0e3-2ad7-5c55-871d-8c594cd714f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common form of craniosynostosis?', '["Plagiocephaly (unilateral coronal)","Scaphocephaly (sagittal suture)","Trigonocephaly (metopic)","Brachycephaly (coronal)","Cloverleaf skull"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphocephaly (sagittal suture)', 'Scaphocephaly from premature sagittal suture fusion is the most common form of craniosynostosis. It results in a long, narrow skull because growth can proceed only in the anterior-posterior direction.'),
('06fe6565-98b3-5a57-b07a-408fefca992c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Crouzon syndrome?', '["Craniosynostosis with syndactyly","Craniosynostosis with cleft palate","Isolated craniosynostosis","Acrocephaly","Craniosynostosis with midface hypoplasia and proptosis"]'::jsonb, 'Craniosynostosis with midface hypoplasia and proptosis', 'Crouzon syndrome is an autosomal dominant craniosynostosis caused by FGFR2 mutations that features midface hypoplasia, ocular proptosis, and shallow orbits with normal hands. Unlike Apert syndrome, it lacks syndactyly.'),
('1015c3d5-94a7-56f4-adf2-eff29dc25089'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Apert syndrome?', '["Cleidocranial dysplasia","Isolated craniosynostosis","Craniosynostosis with midface hypoplasia only","Craniosynostosis with cardiac defects","Craniosynostosis with symmetric syndactyly of hands and feet"]'::jsonb, 'Craniosynostosis with symmetric syndactyly of hands and feet', 'Apert syndrome combines craniosynostosis (often coronal) with symmetric syndactyly of the hands and feet, together with midface hypoplasia. It is caused by mutations in FGFR2.'),
('2d0f5c04-5f92-533e-bd1e-5b33eff68c4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the genetic basis of most craniosynostosis syndromes?', '["SHH mutations","WNT pathway mutations","FGFR2 FGFR3 and TWIST mutations","HOX gene mutations","PAX gene mutations"]'::jsonb, 'FGFR2 FGFR3 and TWIST mutations', 'Most craniosynostosis syndromes result from mutations in FGFR2, FGFR3, or TWIST, genes involved in suture development and osteoblast signaling. Crouzon and Apert syndromes involve FGFR2, while Saethre-Chotzen syndrome involves TWIST.'),
('cd7f23a3-9ea6-54b8-a783-38c72a22b264'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is cleidocranial dysplasia?', '["Complete absence of clavicles resulting in decreased functional activity","Excessive ossification of skull accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abnormal clavicle growth alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Skull deformity alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Defective ossification of clavicles and skull with delayed fontanelle closure"]'::jsonb, 'Defective ossification of clavicles and skull with delayed fontanelle closure', 'Cleidocranial dysplasia, caused by RUNX2 mutations, is characterized by defective ossification of the clavicles (allowing shoulder apposition), delayed closure of the fontanelles, and dental abnormalities. Wormian bones and short stature are also typical.'),
('36617592-f8b7-5410-90d0-dcf37ccb2cf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Klippel-Feil syndrome?', '["Congenital fusion of cervical vertebrae","Cervical rib","Extra cervical vertebrae","Cervical spondylosis","Absence of cervical vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital fusion of cervical vertebrae', 'Klippel-Feil syndrome is the congenital fusion of two or more cervical vertebrae producing a short neck, low posterior hairline, and limited neck mobility. It results from failure of normal cervical somite segmentation.'),
('4f0ec64e-b567-52fe-9008-27cc3744ea8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The atlas (C1) develops from how many ossification centers?', '["Three","One","Six","Two","Four"]'::jsonb, 'Three', 'The atlas (C1) develops from three ossification centers: one for the anterior arch and one in each lateral mass. It lacks a body because its centrum is contributed to the dens of the axis.'),
('ea7c312f-6522-5036-8e39-e8622ed18294'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The axis (C2) is unique because it develops from the:', '["ossification centers resulting in decreased functional activity","Four ossification centers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two ossification centers only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dens (odontoid process) from C1 centrum fusing with C2 body","Complete fusion of C1 and C2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dens (odontoid process) from C1 centrum fusing with C2 body', 'The axis is unique because its dens (odontoid process) is derived from the centrum of the C1 vertebra, which fuses with the body of C2. The dens develops separate ossification centers that unite with the axis body during childhood.'),
('c34cc739-f12f-5091-8457-789fd7829368'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is os odontoideum?', '["Fused dens resulting in decreased functional activity","Bifid dens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A separate ossicle of the odontoid process due to failed fusion","Complete absence of the dens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoplastic dens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A separate ossicle of the odontoid process due to failed fusion', 'Os odontoideum is an oval ossicle representing the dens that has failed to fuse with the body of the axis, leaving a gap between them. It produces atlantoaxial instability and carries a risk of spinal cord compression.'),
('1c378e9f-9cce-5ef1-83cb-1c01876cb947'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Sacralization of L5 means:', '["The L5 vertebra is absent resulting in decreased functional activity","The L5 has a bifid spine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The L5 vertebra is partially or completely fused to the sacrum","The L5 is fused to L4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sacrum has an extra segment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The L5 vertebra is partially or completely fused to the sacrum', 'Sacralization is the partial or complete fusion of the L5 vertebra with the sacrum. It is a common congenital transition-zone variant that is usually asymptomatic, though it may predispose to lower back symptoms.'),
('10fb7c82-9eb6-5d12-9aa3-5a3f11eaa43c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Lumbarization of S1 means:', '["The S1 vertebra is partially or completely separated from the sacrum","Extra sacral segment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The S1 vertebra is absent mediated by specific receptor activation","S1 is fused to L5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","S1 is absent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The S1 vertebra is partially or completely separated from the sacrum', 'Lumbarization is the partial or complete separation of the S1 vertebra from the sacrum, giving it a lumbar appearance. It is a common transitional vertebral variant and is generally asymptomatic.'),
('c7e1fb35-6424-5b54-88ad-965473633f1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Spina bifida occulta is:', '["A defect in the vertebral arch without neural tissue involvement","A split spinal cord","A defect with meningeal herniation","A defect with neural tissue herniation","A complete absence of the vertebral arch"]'::jsonb, 'A defect in the vertebral arch without neural tissue involvement', 'Spina bifida occulta is a failure of vertebral arch fusion that does not involve herniation of the meninges or neural tissue. It is usually lumbosacral and often detected incidentally, sometimes overlaid by a tuft of hair or a dimple.'),
('7fab6464-a9b7-50ed-8948-421241ca7dcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common location of spina bifida occulta?', '["T1-T2","C1-C2","L5-S1","T12-L1","Cervical region"]'::jsonb, 'L5-S1', 'Spina bifida occulta occurs most commonly at L5-S1. It results from failure of the vertebral arches to fuse in the last lumbar and first sacral segments, which close last during development.'),
('bf04dffb-be14-5c13-8522-8e752141d0d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is diastematomyelia?', '["Compression of the spinal cord","A tumor of the spinal cord","A split spinal cord often with a bony or fibrous septum","Abnormal curvature of the spine","Inflammation of the spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'A split spinal cord often with a bony or fibrous septum', 'Diastematomyelia is a sagittal split of the spinal cord, usually in the thoracolumbar region, often separated by a bony or fibrous septum. It may cause neurologic deficits and is frequently associated with overlying skin stigmata or a spina bifida.'),
('7abfc2a4-f56e-5db8-904d-e3d503ff39d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is a dermal sinus tract?', '["A skin dimple without connection resulting in decreased functional activity","A cystic lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An epithelium-lined tract from the skin to the spinal canal","A vascular malformation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A tumor of the spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An epithelium-lined tract from the skin to the spinal canal', 'A dermal sinus tract is a congenital, epithelium-lined tract that connects the skin to deeper structures or the spinal canal, and it may communicate with the subarachnoid space, predisposing to recurrent meningitis. The other options describe non-communicating skin lesions, cysts, vascular anomalies, or neoplasms, none of which is an epithelial connection to the canal.'),
('06799c7b-c962-50fa-a413-17b325ee809e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is tethered cord syndrome?', '["Abnormal attachment of the spinal cord limiting its ascent","Split spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete absence of the filum terminale mediated by specific receptor activation","Short spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thickened spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal attachment of the spinal cord limiting its ascent', 'In tethered cord syndrome, an abnormal attachment (e.g., thick filum terminale, lipoma, or scar) fixes the cord so it cannot ascend normally as the vertebral column grows, producing traction on the conus medullaris and progressive neurologic deficits. A split cord (diastematomyelia), absent filum, or abnormally short or thick cord are separate, distinct findings and do not define the traction syndrome.'),
('a8dfbe97-bfc2-5a6d-9139-44f20166482f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The normal position of the conus medullaris in a newborn is at which vertebral level?', '["L2-L3","L1-L2","T12-L1","L3-L4","L4-L5"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L3', 'At birth the conus medullaris ends at approximately the L3 vertebral level and ascends to the adult L1–L2 level by about 2 years of age, so L2–L3 is the closest option for a neonate. Adult levels (L1–L2) apply only after this postnatal ascent is complete.'),
('45458126-7a7a-5383-b64f-0ec0f2116832'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common kyphotic deformity in children?', '["Congenital kyphosis","Postural kyphosis","Traumatic kyphosis","Scheuermann disease (juvenile kyphosis)","Infectious kyphosis"]'::jsonb, 'Scheuermann disease (juvenile kyphosis)', 'Scheuermann disease (juvenile kyphosis), caused by anterior wedging of thoracic vertebrae and irregular endplates, is the most common structural kyphosis in adolescents. Postural kyphosis is a flexible, correctable posture rather than a structural deformity, and congenital, traumatic, and infectious causes are far less frequent.'),
('77ce0c58-7262-547b-a024-481aed9429f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is congenital scoliosis?', '["Compensatory scoliosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Paralytic scoliosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic scoliosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Postural scoliosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral curvature of the spine due to congenital vertebral anomalies"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral curvature of the spine due to congenital vertebral anomalies', 'Congenital scoliosis is a lateral spinal curvature caused by vertebral malformations present at birth, such as a hemivertebra or a block vertebra. Paralytic, postural, compensatory, and idiopathic scolioses arise from neuromuscular, mechanical, positional, or unknown causes rather than from a structural vertebral defect.'),
('c1a72a6b-3d3e-5135-b2c6-cca399848423'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What vertebral anomaly most commonly causes congenital scoliosis?', '["Hemivertebra (failure of formation)","Rib fusion","Spinal dysraphism","Butterfly vertebra","Block vertebra (failure of segmentation)"]'::jsonb, 'Hemivertebra (failure of formation)', 'A unilateral hemivertebra (failure of formation) is the most common vertebral anomaly causing congenital scoliosis because it creates asymmetric longitudinal growth, producing progressive lateral curvature. Butterfly and block vertebrae tend to be more symmetric, while rib fusion and spinal dysraphism are not vertebral formation defects.'),
('66b3227c-841c-54fc-87c7-e41530f5031d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is a butterfly vertebra?', '["A bifid spinous process resulting in decreased functional activity","A vertebra with a sagittal cleft through the body","A vertebra with an extra wing mediated by specific receptor activation","A hemivertebra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fused vertebrae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A vertebra with a sagittal cleft through the body', 'A butterfly vertebra results from failure of fusion of the two chondrification centers of the body, leaving a sagittal cleft that gives the vertebra a butterfly or bow-tie appearance on imaging; it is usually an incidental finding. A bifid spinous process is a posterior arch variant, and hemivertebra and fused vertebrae are distinct anomalies.'),
('6dee5707-e8e5-5337-9ee9-6adc8b8a1366'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The appendicular skeleton develops from which structure?', '["Neural crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Myotome only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dermatome only mediated by specific receptor activation","Limb bud mesenchyme (lateral plate mesoderm and somites)","Sclerotome only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Limb bud mesenchyme (lateral plate mesoderm and somites)', 'The limb bones and girdles of the appendicular skeleton develop from limb bud mesenchyme, which is derived mainly from lateral plate mesoderm with contributions from migrating somitic cells. Neural crest, dermatome, myotome, and sclerotome alone do not form the limb skeleton.'),
('b5aacd4b-0bc1-5fe7-8049-87b3511b7001'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The scapula develops from which structure?', '["Mesenchyme of the upper limb bud at C3-T1 level","Dermatome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myotome mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sclerotome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchyme of the upper limb bud at C3-T1 level', 'The scapula, like other limb girdle bones, ossifies within the mesenchyme of the upper limb bud lying opposite the C3–T1 spinal segments. Dermatome, myotome, sclerotome, and neural crest derivatives do not form girdle bones.'),
('9ed7eeee-73d9-554b-a3c1-0460f3f7fd72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The pelvis develops from which structure?', '["Notochord resulting in decreased functional activity","Somites accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesenchyme of the lower limb bud","AER leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchyme of the lower limb bud', 'The pelvic girdle develops from the mesenchyme of the lower limb bud, in the same way the scapula forms in the upper limb. The notochord, somites, and neural crest do not contribute to the pelvis, and the apical ectodermal ridge only controls distal limb outgrowth rather than girdle formation.'),
('4cfabe14-a36d-55f7-868b-ea1646fa1a2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH)?', '["Fracture of the hip resulting in decreased functional activity","Abnormal development of the hip joint with instability or dislocation","Avascular necrosis of the femoral head mediated by specific receptor activation","Arthritis of the hip without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete absence of the hip joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal development of the hip joint with instability or dislocation', 'DDH is a spectrum of abnormal development in which an underdeveloped shallow acetabulum allows the femoral head to be unstable, subluxatable, or dislocated, typically recognized in infancy. Fracture, avascular necrosis, and arthritis are acquired conditions, and the joint is present rather than absent.'),
('cc8e0353-0d75-5992-a4f7-accda92968ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most important risk factor for DDH?', '["Female sex","Family history","First-born child","Oligohydramnios","Breech presentation"]'::jsonb, 'Breech presentation', 'Breech presentation is the strongest risk factor for DDH because the flexed, adducted position of the hips in utero predisposes the femoral head to displace posteriorly out of a shallow acetabulum. Female sex, family history, first-born status, and oligohydramnios raise risk but to a lesser degree.'),
('df9e5031-beb5-521e-bb3e-408cd9027ddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the Ortolani maneuver?', '["A test for hip dislocation in DDH","A test for hip reduction in DDH","A test for hip Click","An X-ray view for DDH","An ultrasound view for DDH"]'::jsonb, 'A test for hip reduction in DDH', 'The Ortolani maneuver abducts the flexed hip while lifting the greater trochanter forward; a palpable clunk as the femoral head slips back into the acetabulum signals a reducible dislocation. It therefore tests reduction, whereas the Barlow maneuver tests the opposite direction, dislocatability, and it is a clinical test, not an imaging view.'),
('8a479550-b77e-540e-9d57-5d3e34889245'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the Barlow maneuver?', '["A CT view","An X-ray view","A test for hip dislocatability in DDH","A test for hip reduction","An ultrasound view"]'::jsonb, 'A test for hip dislocatability in DDH', 'The Barlow maneuver adducts and presses posteriorly on the flexed hip, attempting to push the femoral head out of the acetabulum; a clunk on this maneuver shows the hip is dislocatable. It is a clinical examination, not an imaging view, and it tests the opposite direction from the Ortolani reduction maneuver.'),
('d7f70283-f807-5571-b6a9-5092151cbb40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is congenital vertical talus?', '["A clubfoot resulting in decreased functional activity","A calcaneovalgus foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An arch foot mediated by specific receptor activation","A rigid flatfoot deformity with dorsal dislocation of the talonavicular joint","A flexible flatfoot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A rigid flatfoot deformity with dorsal dislocation of the talonavicular joint', 'Congenital vertical talus (rocker-bottom foot) is a rigid flatfoot in which the talus is vertically oriented and the talonavicular joint is dislocated dorsally, so the foot cannot be corrected passively. Flexible flatfoot and calcaneovalgus are passively correctable, and the talonavicular joint remains intact, distinguishing them.'),
('0a93772c-8dfb-5a36-9d92-e6610dce3973'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common congenital foot deformity?', '["Congenital vertical talus","Calcaneovalgus","Talipes equinovarus (clubfoot)","Metatarsus adductus","Flatfoot"]'::jsonb, 'Talipes equinovarus (clubfoot)', 'Clubfoot (talipes equinovarus) is the most common congenital foot deformity. Congenital vertical talus and metatarsus adductus are far less frequent, and calcaneovalgus or flexible flatfoot are usually benign positional findings rather than true deformities.'),
('a4be2fee-5128-5910-95b7-dabdb4f655fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What are the four components of clubfoot deformity?', '["Calcaneus valgus abductus flat","Calcaneus varus adductus cavus","Equinus varus adductus cavus","Equinus varus abductus flat","Equinus valgus abductus cavus"]'::jsonb, 'Equinus varus adductus cavus', 'The classic clubfoot combines equinus (plantarflexion at the ankle), varus (inversion at the heel), adductus (medial deviation of the forefoot), and cavus (high longitudinal arch). Any option containing valgus, abductus, or a flat-foot component is incomplete or incorrect.'),
('c1327211-b41e-5990-bc05-4875072b0a2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is metatarsus adductus?', '["Medial deviation of the forefoot with normal hindfoot","High arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clubfoot mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral deviation of the forefoot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flatfoot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial deviation of the forefoot with normal hindfoot', 'Metatarsus adductus is a medial deviation of the forefoot with a normal hindfoot, often from intrauterine positioning, and usually resolves spontaneously with stretching. It is not a high arch, a clubfoot, or a flatfoot, and the forefoot deviates medially, not laterally.'),
('c401b067-e2d4-569c-8a43-e33077ec9776'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is calcaneovalgus foot?', '["Excessive plantarflexion and inversion resulting in decreased functional activity","High arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flatfoot mediated by specific receptor activation","Clubfoot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excessive dorsiflexion and eversion of the foot"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive dorsiflexion and eversion of the foot', 'Calcaneovalgus foot is a flexible congenital positional deformity of excessive dorsiflexion and eversion, essentially the opposite of clubfoot, and it usually improves spontaneously. Excessive plantarflexion with inversion describes clubfoot rather than this condition.'),
('9861e614-4d49-53bf-92eb-1e1368527682'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The muscles of the limbs develop from which structure?', '["Neural crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral plate mesoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myotomes (somites) migrating into limb buds","Dermatome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sclerotome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myotomes (somites) migrating into limb buds', 'Limb muscles are formed by cells from the myotomes of somites that migrate into the limb buds and then organize into individual muscles, which is why each muscle has a predictable segmental (myotomal) innervation. Lateral plate mesoderm, dermatome, sclerotome, and neural crest do not give rise to skeletal muscle.'),
('91dafb5b-848c-55de-8f1b-7206187637e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The connective tissue (tendons and fascia) of limbs develops from which structure?', '["Ectoderm","Lateral plate mesoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest","Somites"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plate mesoderm', 'The connective tissue framework of the limbs, including tendons and fascia, develops from the lateral plate mesoderm that forms the bulk of the limb bud mesenchyme. Ectoderm and endoderm are germ layers that do not form mesenchyme, and somites supply the muscles rather than the limb connective tissue.'),
('0f21b2b8-b3ed-5942-adf2-188e8a64b905'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The blood vessels of the limbs develop from which structure?', '["Somites resulting in decreased functional activity","AER accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesenchyme in the limb buds (local angiogenesis)","Neural crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ZPA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchyme in the limb buds (local angiogenesis)', 'The initial blood vessels of the limbs form in situ from mesenchyme within the limb buds by local angiogenesis, and they later connect to the developing axial vessels. The AER and ZPA are signaling centers that pattern limb growth, while somites and neural crest do not form the vascular network.'),
('80e618cf-7df6-54ef-9970-4b24f950bb8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common congenital cardiac anomaly overall?', '["Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)","Atrial septal defect (ASD)","Coarctation of the aorta","Tetralogy of Fallot"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular septal defect (VSD)', 'Ventricular septal defect is the most common congenital cardiac anomaly, accounting for the largest share of all congenital heart disease. PDA, ASD, coarctation, and tetralogy of Fallot each occur less frequently in the general population.'),
('2547d44c-1392-51e4-b31f-e8e4780a3859'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common congenital cardiac anomaly in Turner syndrome?', '["ASD resulting in decreased functional activity","Coarctation of the aorta","Tetralogy of Fallot mediated by specific receptor activation","VSD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PDA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coarctation of the aorta', 'Coarctation of the aorta is the characteristic congenital cardiac lesion in Turner syndrome, often accompanied by a bicuspid aortic valve. VSD, ASD, PDA, and tetralogy of Fallot are not the signature cardiac defects of this syndrome.'),
('a2d2ccb8-ba14-523f-8bee-c1480662cf43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What congenital heart defect is most commonly associated with Down syndrome?', '["Atrioventricular septal defect (AVSD)","VSD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASD mediated by specific receptor activation","PDA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tetralogy of Fallot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrioventricular septal defect (AVSD)', 'Atrioventricular septal defect (endocardial cushion defect) is the most common congenital heart lesion in Down syndrome, accounting for a large proportion of cases. VSD, ASD, PDA, and tetralogy of Fallot are less frequently the presenting defect in these patients.'),
('360c08e7-6356-53cd-a807-fd1c6583abc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Tetralogy of Fallot consists of which four findings?', '["VSD overriding aorta LV hypertrophy pulmonary stenosis","VSD ASD PDA RV hypertrophy","VSD pulmonary atresia overriding aorta RV hypertrophy","VSD ASD overriding aorta pulmonary stenosis","VSD overriding aorta right ventricular hypertrophy pulmonary stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'VSD overriding aorta right ventricular hypertrophy pulmonary stenosis', 'Tetralogy of Fallot consists of four features: a subaortic VSD, an overriding aorta, right ventricular hypertrophy, and right ventricular outflow tract obstruction due to pulmonary stenosis. The other combinations replace one of these findings with an ASD, PDA, LV hypertrophy, or pulmonary atresia, so they are incorrect.'),
('cf5f7809-5883-5f66-8c98-e845c638ae39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of VSD?', '["Outlet VSD","Supracristal VSD","Perimembranous (membranous) VSD","Muscular VSD","Inlet VSD"]'::jsonb, 'Perimembranous (membranous) VSD', 'The perimembranous (membranous) VSD is the most common type, lying in the membranous portion of the ventricular septum just below the aortic valve. Muscular, supracristal/outlet, and inlet subtypes are each encountered less frequently.'),
('737afbac-6612-586e-81a9-096a88e12347'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Ostium primum ASD is associated with which other anomaly?', '["Pulmonary stenosis","Cleft mitral valve","Ebstein anomaly","Tricuspid atresia","Coarctation"]'::jsonb, 'Cleft mitral valve', 'Ostium primum ASD results from failed fusion of the endocardial cushions, so it is classically associated with a cleft in the anterior mitral valve leaflet (and sometimes the tricuspid valve). Pulmonary stenosis, Ebstein anomaly, tricuspid atresia, and coarctation belong to different lesion complexes.'),
('61bd1de7-240c-59e1-b8b4-f80f063d4023'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the aortic arch?', '["Right aortic arch","Coarctation of the aorta","Double aortic arch","Patent ductus arteriosus","Aberrant right subclavian artery"]'::jsonb, 'Aberrant right subclavian artery', 'Aberrant origin of the right subclavian artery (arising distal to the left subclavian artery) is the most common congenital anomaly of the aortic arch, seen in about 1% of individuals, and is usually asymptomatic. Coarctation, double aortic arch, and right aortic arch are each less common.'),
('a03ba545-5350-5fa7-bd49-e28712470959'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is a vascular ring?', '["An aortic arch anomaly that encircles the trachea and esophagus","Coarctation of the aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PDA mediated by specific receptor activation","An aneurysm of the aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic stenosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An aortic arch anomaly that encircles the trachea and esophagus', 'A vascular ring is an aortic arch anomaly (classically a double aortic arch) that encircles the trachea and esophagus, causing stridor and feeding difficulty as the ring tightens with growth. Coarctation, PDA, aneurysm, and stenosis do not form a constricting ring around the airway and esophagus.'),
('6f85a21b-271e-5960-a903-7479248d33fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common vascular ring?', '["Left aortic arch with aberrant right subclavian","Innominate artery compression","Pulmonary artery sling","Double aortic arch with patent ductus arteriosus","Right aortic arch with aberrant left subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'Double aortic arch with patent ductus arteriosus', 'A double aortic arch with a patent ductus arteriosus is the most common vascular ring that produces symptoms, completely encircling the trachea and esophagus. Left aortic arch with aberrant right subclavian, innominate compression, and a pulmonary sling are less common or asymptomatic variants.'),
('eff277ae-cdfb-5d3e-bd06-98404a942f26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Preductal (infantile) coarctation is associated with which condition?', '["Turner syndrome","Williams syndrome","Down syndrome","Marfan syndrome","Noonan syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Turner syndrome', 'Preductal (infantile) coarctation is classically associated with Turner syndrome and often coexists with hypoplasia of the proximal aortic arch, presenting with heart failure in infancy. Williams syndrome gives supravalvular aortic stenosis, Down syndrome gives atrioventricular septal defects, Marfan gives aortic dilation, and Noonan gives pulmonary stenosis.'),
('43176674-2f61-5c28-92b8-a5608b66d861'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Postductal (adult) coarctation typically presents with:', '["Hypertension in the upper extremities with weak femoral pulses","Shock accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyanosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Asymptomatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart failure in infancy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension in the upper extremities with weak femoral pulses', 'Postductal (adult) coarctation sits just distal to the ductus arteriosus, so it produces upper-limb hypertension with weak, delayed, or absent femoral pulses, while collaterals develop through the intercostal arteries. Shock and heart failure in infancy characterize severe preductal coarctation, and the lesion is not typically silent or cyanotic.'),
('8c93cefd-f97b-544f-b4b0-c737a533b206'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common location for coarctation of the aorta?', '["Ascending aorta","Juxtaductal (near ligamentum arteriosum)","At the diaphragm","At the aortic isthmus","Abdominal aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Juxtaductal (near ligamentum arteriosum)', 'Coarctation is most common juxtaductally, just distal to the left subclavian artery at the level of the ligamentum arteriosum, and this site is often called the aortic isthmus region. It is rare in the ascending, diaphragmatic, or abdominal aorta.'),
('169dc0df-d472-58f7-9ea5-48250c3ca243'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Type B aortic arch interruption is associated with which syndrome?', '["Noonan syndrome","Williams syndrome","DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion)","Down syndrome","Turner syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion)', 'Type B interruption of the aortic arch (between the left carotid and left subclavian arteries) is strongly associated with DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion), which also features conotruncal heart defects and thymic hypoplasia. The other syndromes carry different cardiac signatures, such as pulmonary stenosis in Noonan and supravalvular aortic stenosis in Williams.'),
('f3307d45-81b9-5e8f-8468-b8a097271c74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the coronary arteries?', '["Anomalous origin of the left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery (ALCAPA)","Coronary artery fistula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absent coronary artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Anomalous aortic origin of coronary artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myocardial bridging leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anomalous origin of the left coronary artery from the pulmonary artery (ALCAPA)', 'ALCAPA is the most important congenital coronary anomaly taught as the classic lesion, causing myocardial ischemia and infantile heart failure because the left coronary perfuses the myocardium at pulmonary-artery pressure. Coronary fistulas, bridging, and anomalous aortic origins are less clinically significant as the leading congenital coronary anomaly.'),
('e36536e9-3cbd-54f2-a6a3-1c6aa5b18eae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is partial anomalous pulmonary venous return (PAPVR)?', '["Complete anomalous drainage resulting in decreased functional activity","Some but not all pulmonary veins drain into the right atrium or systemic veins","Normal drainage mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary vein stenosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary vein atresia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Some but not all pulmonary veins drain into the right atrium or systemic veins', 'In PAPVR, one or more pulmonary veins drain into the right atrium or a systemic vein while the remaining veins connect normally to the left atrium, creating a left-to-right shunt. Complete anomalous drainage, normal drainage, stenosis, and atresia describe different states of the pulmonary venous connection.'),
('794f9ee0-0685-5865-89c4-cf995d44ecb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Scimitar syndrome is a form of PAPVR involving:', '["Left lung anomalous drainage to SVC resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary vein stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right lung anomalous drainage to IVC (scimitar sign)","TAPVR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bilateral PAPVR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right lung anomalous drainage to IVC (scimitar sign)', 'Scimitar syndrome is a PAPVR variant in which the anomalous vein from the right lung descends to the inferior vena cava, producing a curved scimitar-shaped shadow on the right chest radiograph. Anomalous drainage to the SVC, stenosis, TAPVR, or bilateral involvement does not define this syndrome.'),
('b6ed3a3e-2be0-5b14-9564-9dca21b9714b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common cause of cyanosis in a newborn with CHD?', '["Total anomalous pulmonary venous return","Tetralogy of Fallot","Transposition of the great arteries","Tricuspid atresia","Pulmonary atresia"]'::jsonb, 'Transposition of the great arteries', 'Transposition of the great arteries is the most common cause of cyanosis in the newborn with congenital heart disease because the parallel systemic and pulmonary circulations require shunting through a VSD, PFO, or PDA for survival. Total anomalous pulmonary venous return, tricuspid atresia, and pulmonary atresia are cyanotic lesions but are less common at birth.'),
('75a3b592-f57a-5ea1-8e94-8bd2dfe0ec4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the anatomic defect in complete transposition of the great arteries (D-TGA)?', '["The aorta arises from the RV and the pulmonary artery from the LV","Both great arteries arise from the LV","The aorta and pulmonary artery are reversed","Both great arteries arise from the RV","The great arteries are normally related but stenotic"]'::jsonb, 'The aorta arises from the RV and the pulmonary artery from the LV', 'In D-transposition of the great arteries, the aorta arises from the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery from the left ventricle, creating two parallel circulations; survival depends on mixing through a VSD, PFO, or PDA. Origins of both vessels from one ventricle or a reversed but otherwise normal relationship describe other defects.'),
('82ccd770-378e-58da-8318-ecc31bc72700'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of truncus arteriosus?', '["Type I (common trunk gives separate PA and aorta)","Type 0","Type III (one PA from trunk one from ductus)","Type IV (pseudotruncus)","Type II (separate PAs from trunk)"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (common trunk gives separate PA and aorta)', 'In the Collett and Edwards classification, truncus arteriosus Type I, in which a single great vessel gives off a distinct main pulmonary artery and an ascending aorta, is the most common form. Type II and III give the pulmonary arteries directly from the trunk, and Type IV represents a pulmonary atresia variant rather than true truncus.'),
('0d0559f4-7eba-5fcc-9365-1c2d50d1b14e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is pulmonary atresia with intact ventricular septum?', '["Ebstein anomaly resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atresia of the pulmonary valve with no VSD and hypoplastic RV","Atresia of the pulmonary valve with VSD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","VSD with pulmonary hypertension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atresia of the pulmonary valve with no VSD and hypoplastic RV', 'Pulmonary atresia with intact ventricular septum means the pulmonary valve is atretic, there is no ventricular septal defect, and the right ventricle is hypoplastic; pulmonary blood flow depends entirely on the patent ductus arteriosus. Atresia with a VSD is a separate lesion typically considered with tetralogy variants, and the other options are different conditions.'),
('6f340f75-bc2d-54ae-bd91-458e91ed5065'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common form of univentricular heart?', '["Single ventricle with single AV valve","Hypoplastic left heart syndrome","Double inlet left ventricle (DILV)","Double inlet right ventricle","Tricuspid atresia"]'::jsonb, 'Double inlet left ventricle (DILV)', 'Double inlet left ventricle, in which both atrioventricular valves open into the morphologic left ventricle, is the most common form of univentricular heart. Hypoplastic left heart syndrome, double inlet right ventricle, and tricuspid atresia are less frequent univentricular anatomies.'),
('37ebf1a1-248d-5fdf-ac23-aa01980491c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common cause of death in children with HLHS without treatment?', '["Stroke resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endocarditis mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart failure within the first weeks of life","Arrhythmia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heart failure within the first weeks of life', 'Without treatment, hypoplastic left heart syndrome causes heart failure and death within the first weeks of life because the diminutive left ventricle cannot support the systemic circulation once the ductus arteriosus closes. Stroke, endocarditis, pulmonary hypertension, and arrhythmia are later or less common mechanisms of death.'),
('6b931166-2036-597d-b77c-876a0aec1c03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the Bialock-Taussig (BT) shunt?', '["PDA ligation resulting in decreased functional activity","A connection between the subclavian artery and the pulmonary artery to increase pulmonary blood flow","A shunt between the aorta and pulmonary artery mediated by specific receptor activation","VSD closure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ASD creation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A connection between the subclavian artery and the pulmonary artery to increase pulmonary blood flow', 'The Blalock-Taussig shunt connects a subclavian artery to the ipsilateral pulmonary artery, augmenting pulmonary blood flow in lesions with reduced flow such as tetralogy of Fallot. Ligation of a PDA, closure of a VSD, creation of an ASD, or an aortopulmonary connection are different procedures with other purposes.'),
('220f6866-c406-5a0c-bed1-c8f46a4ef198'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the Glenn shunt?', '["ASD creation","IVC to pulmonary artery connection","Superior vena cava to right pulmonary artery connection","Aorta to pulmonary artery connection","Subclavian to pulmonary artery connection"]'::jsonb, 'Superior vena cava to right pulmonary artery connection', 'The Glenn shunt connects the superior vena cava to the right pulmonary artery, a cavopulmonary connection that directs systemic venous blood to the lungs without passing through a ventricle. Inferior vena cava connections describe the Fontan procedure, and aortopulmonary or subclavian connections are other types of shunts.'),
('5256a5e6-c931-5cf0-a942-b34a39785989'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is patent foramen ovale (PFO)?', '["A large ASD resulting in decreased functional activity","A small VSD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A persistent opening in the atrial septum that is probe-patent","A PDA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A VSD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A persistent opening in the atrial septum that is probe-patent', 'A patent foramen ovale is a persistent, flap-like opening of the atrial septum at the fossa ovalis that remains probe-patent but is functionally closed at normal atrial pressures; it is a normal variant rather than a true septal defect. ASD, VSD, and PDA are true communications in different locations.'),
('f0acad10-5812-5f26-9605-e42a48aa34af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the difference between a PFO and an ASD?', '["ASD is smaller than PFO resulting in decreased functional activity","ASD is a true defect of the atrial septum; PFO is a flap-like opening","ASD closes spontaneously; PFO does not mediated by specific receptor activation","PFO requires treatment; ASD does not without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They are the same leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ASD is a true defect of the atrial septum; PFO is a flap-like opening', 'An ASD is a true deficiency (hole) in the atrial septum that produces a significant left-to-right shunt and usually requires closure, whereas a PFO is a flap-like slit from incomplete fusion of the septum primum and secundum that is functionally closed at normal pressures. The size and treatment comparisons in the other options are inaccurate.'),
('3718a7ec-64d5-5956-9d57-b63d39bbd895'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The skeletal muscle fibers in the limbs originate from which embryonic structure?', '["Dermatomes","Myotomes of somites","Lateral plate mesoderm","Neural crest","Sclerotomes"]'::jsonb, 'Myotomes of somites', 'Skeletal muscle fibers of the limbs arise from the myotomes of somites, whose cells migrate into the limb buds and organize into muscles, preserving a segmental pattern of innervation. Dermatomes give dermis, sclerotomes form bone, lateral plate mesoderm forms connective tissue, and neural crest contributes to other tissues.'),
('ba682826-2a19-51a5-975c-a3a8ac773169'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The axial skeleton (vertebral column and ribs) develops from which structure?', '["Notochord","Sclerotomes of somites","Myotomes","Lateral plate mesoderm","Dermatomes"]'::jsonb, 'Sclerotomes of somites', 'The axial skeleton, including the vertebral bodies, neural arches, and ribs, develops from the sclerotomes of somites, which condense around the notochord and neural tube. The notochord gives rise to the nucleus pulposus, myotomes form muscles, dermatomes form dermis, and lateral plate mesoderm forms the limb skeleton and connective tissue.'),
('03bbf633-9404-5696-b4d9-d1e68a72c570'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The notochord induces the formation of which structure?', '["Blood vessels","Dermis","Muscles","Vertebral bodies (centra)","Neural tube"]'::jsonb, 'Vertebral bodies (centra)', 'The notochord secretes signaling molecules that induce surrounding mesenchyme to condense and form the vertebral bodies (centra). The neural tube forms the spinal cord, muscles arise from the somites, the dermis from the dermatome, and blood vessels from angioblasts.'),
('dc6beb38-a451-5386-ba6c-99c6e6253b5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the adult remnant of the notochord?', '["Nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral discs","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Annulus fibrosus mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertebral arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vertebral body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus pulposus of the intervertebral discs', 'As the vertebral bodies form, the notochord degenerates except for its persistent remnants, which remain as the nucleus pulposus of each intervertebral disc. The annulus fibrosus, vertebral arch, and vertebral body all derive from mesenchyme rather than the notochord.'),
('614d5151-cb27-5c75-86b9-ae5b2ea8c586'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is a chordoma?', '["A meningeal tumor","A nerve sheath tumor","A tumor of the vertebral bone","A spinal cord tumor","A tumor derived from notochordal remnants"]'::jsonb, 'A tumor derived from notochordal remnants', 'A chordoma is a slow-growing malignant tumor that arises from persistent notochordal remnants, characteristically in the clivus or sacrococcygeal region. The other options describe tumors of meninges, nerve sheaths, bone, or spinal cord rather than notochord origin.'),
('dbcc1806-76e5-5fc2-9d9f-0a521de64eea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common vertebral anomaly?', '["Hemivertebra","Spina bifida occulta","Block vertebra","Sacralization","Butterfly vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'Butterfly vertebra', 'A butterfly vertebra, caused by failure of fusion of the two lateral ossification centers leaving a sagittal cleft, is the most common vertebral anomaly. Hemivertebra, block vertebra, and sacralization are less frequent variants, and spina bifida occulta is a failure of the vertebral arch to fuse.'),
('84ea4d5b-9f74-59fe-9d7e-b903dd03353a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is a limbus vertebra?', '["A fracture of the vertebra resulting in decreased functional activity","A triangular bone fragment at the anterior vertebral body margin","A tumor of the vertebra mediated by specific receptor activation","Wedging of the vertebra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection of the vertebra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular bone fragment at the anterior vertebral body margin', 'A limbus vertebra is a small triangular fragment separated from the anterior margin of a vertebral body by herniation of disc tissue through the ring apophysis before fusion. It is a developmental variant, not a fracture, tumor, wedging, or infection.'),
('64f821f1-88e6-5721-bb37-a04f901f81de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common location of spondylolysis?', '["L3","C6","L4","C5","L5"]'::jsonb, 'L5', 'Spondylolysis, a defect in the pars interarticularis, is most common at L5 because the lumbosacral region bears the greatest mechanical stress. L4, L3, and the cervical levels listed are much less frequently affected.'),
('5790c7cf-b8ae-5e2a-87f1-15ff3f2f1fc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Spondylolisthesis is:', '["Backward slippage of a vertebra","Fracture of a vertebra","Lateral slippage","Forward slippage of one vertebra over another","Rotation of a vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'Forward slippage of one vertebra over another', 'Spondylolisthesis is the anterior (forward) displacement of one vertebral body over the vertebra below it, most often L5 on S1. Backward, lateral, or rotational displacement and an isolated fracture do not fit this definition.'),
('eec5c32d-28ad-5e42-994a-6dcca17c2b8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common site of spondylolisthesis?', '["C5-C6","L4-L5","L2-L3","L3-L4","L5-S1"]'::jsonb, 'L5-S1', 'Spondylolisthesis most often involves anterior displacement of L5 on S1, because the lumbosacral junction endures the greatest weight and shearing forces. The cervical and upper lumbar levels listed are uncommon sites.'),
('12d95370-bf8a-5fd2-8a8a-b3d6af9e3fed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Scheuermann disease?', '["Congenital kyphosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Juvenile kyphosis with vertebral endplate irregularities and Schmorl nodes","Spinal stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Adolescent scoliosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Osteoporosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Juvenile kyphosis with vertebral endplate irregularities and Schmorl nodes', 'Scheuermann disease is a juvenile (adolescent) kyphosis caused by disordered endochondral ossification, producing irregular vertebral endplates, anterior wedging, and Schmorl nodes. Congenital kyphosis, scoliosis, spinal stenosis, and osteoporosis are separate entities.'),
('0a4b2d97-699a-5386-9dab-9506e885a57c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What are Schmorl nodes?', '["Herniation into the spinal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor of the vertebra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection of the vertebra mediated by specific receptor activation","Fracture of the vertebra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Herniation of the nucleus pulposus into the vertebral endplates"]'::jsonb, 'Herniation of the nucleus pulposus into the vertebral endplates', 'Schmorl nodes form when the nucleus pulposus herniates vertically through the cartilaginous vertebral endplate into the adjacent bone marrow. The other options describe posterior disc herniation into the canal, tumor, infection, or fracture.'),
('c038ae24-0013-535b-a5aa-16e65a95a7b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common primary bone tumor in children?', '["Osteoma","Ewing sarcoma","Giant cell tumor","Osteosarcoma","Chondrosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Osteosarcoma', 'Osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor in children and adolescents, usually arising in the metaphysis of long bones. Ewing sarcoma is the second most common, while osteoma, giant cell tumor, and chondrosarcoma are not leading childhood bone tumors.'),
('6dddba88-d701-5c42-bfba-558230dfebc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common site of osteosarcoma?', '["Spine resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Distal femur proximal tibia proximal humerus","Pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skull leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal femur proximal tibia proximal humerus', 'Osteosarcoma characteristically arises in the metaphyses of the most rapidly growing bones: distal femur, proximal tibia, and proximal humerus. The spine, ribs, pelvis, and skull are uncommon primary sites.'),
('741057d3-b1c4-5b5a-879a-9c94cffe5119'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common site of Ewing sarcoma?', '["Skull resulting in decreased functional activity","Hands and feet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metaphysis of long bones mediated by specific receptor activation","Diaphysis of long bones (femur) and pelvis","Spine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphysis of long bones (femur) and pelvis', 'Ewing sarcoma characteristically involves the diaphysis of long bones such as the femur and also the pelvis, unlike osteosarcoma which favors the metaphysis. The skull, hands and feet, and spine are rare primary locations.'),
('65ca95d2-4427-5b83-8e83-14c716d76b12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What genetic translocation is characteristic of Ewing sarcoma?', '["t(2;13) PAX3-FOXO1","t(12;16) FUS-DDIT3","t(11;22)(q24;q12) EWSR1-FLI1","t(9;22) BCR-ABL","t(X;18) SYT-SSX"]'::jsonb, 't(11;22)(q24;q12) EWSR1-FLI1', 'Ewing sarcoma is defined by the t(11;22)(q24;q12) translocation producing the EWSR1-FLI1 fusion protein. t(2;13) is alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma, t(12;16) myxoid liposarcoma, t(9;22) chronic myeloid leukemia, and t(X;18) synovial sarcoma.'),
('ac7d0525-bcc5-5b66-833c-f1bad8358a92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common benign bone tumor?', '["Enchondroma","Osteochondroma","Giant cell tumor","Osteoid osteoma","Osteoblastoma"]'::jsonb, 'Osteochondroma', 'Osteochondroma (osteocartilaginous exostosis) is the most common benign bone tumor, a cartilage-capped bony outgrowth from the metaphysis. Enchondroma, giant cell tumor, osteoid osteoma, and osteoblastoma are all less common benign lesions.'),
('a08d5eeb-5365-52d1-8aea-12ec8b41e476'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the genetic basis of multiple hereditary exostoses?', '["RB1 mutation","APC mutation","TP53 mutation","EXT1 or EXT2 mutations","NF1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'EXT1 or EXT2 mutations', 'Multiple hereditary exostoses result from loss-of-function mutations in EXT1 or EXT2, which encode glycosyltransferases involved in heparan sulfate synthesis. RB1, APC, TP53, and NF1 mutations cause retinoblastoma, familial polyposis, Li-Fraumeni syndrome, and neurofibromatosis respectively.'),
('4388fe13-50c3-58c0-b1dd-b58ed68fe6c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common location of osteoid osteoma?', '["Ribs resulting in decreased functional activity","Skull accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Distal femur mediated by specific receptor activation","Proximal femur (metaphysis)","Spine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal femur (metaphysis)', 'Osteoid osteoma most frequently arises in the proximal femur, characteristically in the metaphysis or neck, in children and young adults. The ribs, skull, distal femur, and spine are much less common locations.'),
('503c837e-c23b-5eb4-ba9f-a3f66c104368'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the classic symptom of osteoid osteoma?', '["Pathologic fracture","Neurologic symptoms","Joint stiffness","Night pain relieved by NSAIDs","Painless swelling"]'::jsonb, 'Night pain relieved by NSAIDs', 'Osteoid osteoma classically causes severe night pain that is dramatically relieved by NSAIDs, because the nidus produces high levels of prostaglandins. Pathologic fracture, neurologic symptoms, joint stiffness, and painless swelling are not the hallmark presentation.'),
('12790e85-e729-5a3d-974e-869299ef6db5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is an aneurysmal bone cyst?', '["A fracture resulting in decreased functional activity","A malignant tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A hematoma mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign cystic bone lesion containing blood-filled cavities","A true aneurysm of bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign cystic bone lesion containing blood-filled cavities', 'An aneurysmal bone cyst is a benign, expansile, blood-filled cystic lesion usually seen in the metaphysis of long bones or the posterior spine in young patients. It is neither a fracture, malignant tumor, simple hematoma, nor a true arterial aneurysm.'),
('f18c5c01-ced9-59cc-a1c6-b48a64f9087c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common location of giant cell tumor of bone?', '["Around the knee (distal femur proximal tibia)","Skull accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hands mediated by specific receptor activation","Feet without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Around the knee (distal femur proximal tibia)', 'Giant cell tumor of bone characteristically arises in the epiphysis of long bones around the knee, especially the distal femur and proximal tibia, in young adults. The skull, hands, feet, and spine are unusual primary sites.'),
('3061ed85-06c3-55be-93e7-c02d4be8b655'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Giant cell tumor of bone is characterized by:', '["Chondroid matrix resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrous tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Numerous multinucleated giant cells with mononuclear stromal cells","Osteoid production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone necrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Numerous multinucleated giant cells with mononuclear stromal cells', 'Giant cell tumor contains numerous multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells admixed with oval mononuclear stromal cells. Osteoid production suggests osteosarcoma, chondroid matrix a cartilage tumor, and fibrous tissue or necrosis point to other lesions.'),
('96ca0069-f9f4-5840-86b1-cb0fb5c47b40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common malignant bone tumor in adults?', '["Multiple myeloma","Chondrosarcoma","Osteosarcoma","Ewing sarcoma","Fibrosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple myeloma', 'Multiple myeloma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor in adults, presenting as multiple lytic deposits in marrow-rich bones. Chondrosarcoma, osteosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma, and fibrosarcoma are all far less common.'),
('46e8c0cb-65ca-5063-b330-fe5ea1c269f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common primary malignant bone tumor overall (excluding myeloma)?', '["Ewing sarcoma","Chondrosarcoma","Lymphoma","Osteosarcoma","Fibrosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Osteosarcoma', 'Excluding multiple myeloma, osteosarcoma is the most common primary malignant bone tumor across all age groups. Chondrosarcoma, Ewing sarcoma, lymphoma, and fibrosarcoma each occur less frequently.'),
('f6417317-ff4b-5717-a8b9-d1664a230cd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common site of chondrosarcoma?', '["Ribs resulting in decreased functional activity","Spine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pelvis and proximal femur","Skull without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distal femur leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvis and proximal femur', 'Chondrosarcoma most commonly arises in the pelvis and proximal femur, typically in older adults. The ribs, spine, skull, and distal femur are less frequent primary locations.'),
('9b7a99eb-d77c-5d5b-8eeb-537dbc00c9cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Paget disease of bone?', '["Osteogenesis imperfecta resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteopetrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inflammation of bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Osteoporosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A disorder of excessive bone remodeling with abnormal bone structure"]'::jsonb, 'A disorder of excessive bone remodeling with abnormal bone structure', 'Paget disease is a focal disorder in which osteoclastic resorption and osteoblastic repair race ahead, producing architecturally disorganized bone. Osteoporosis, osteopetrosis, osteogenesis imperfecta, and osteomyelitis are distinct processes with different pathophysiology.'),
('457f9f04-0345-501a-a5d8-8fb3ce944d6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common complication of Paget disease?', '["High-output heart failure","Bone pain and deformity","Osteosarcoma","Hearing loss","Pathologic fracture"]'::jsonb, 'Bone pain and deformity', 'Bone pain and progressive deformity are the most frequent clinical problems in Paget disease, with enlargement and bowing of affected bones. High-output failure, sarcomatous change, hearing loss, and fracture are rarer complications.'),
('978d387e-84cf-5263-a99a-bdb97ebc7804'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common sarcomatous degeneration in Paget disease?', '["Fibrosarcoma","Chondrosarcoma","Liposarcoma","Malignant fibrous histiocytoma","Osteosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Osteosarcoma', 'Osteosarcoma is the most common malignant transformation in long-standing Paget disease, typically in the skull, femur, or pelvis. Fibrosarcoma and malignant fibrous histiocytoma occur rarely, and chondrosarcoma and liposarcoma are not characteristic.'),
('4ede7d46-4cd1-5a00-8b75-86bc03f9268e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Osteogenesis imperfecta (OI) is caused by a defect in which protein?', '["Fibrillin","Type III collagen","Type II collagen","Elastin","Type I collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Type I collagen', 'Most forms of osteogenesis imperfecta result from mutations affecting type I collagen, the major collagen of bone, producing fragile bones. Fibrillin defects cause Marfan syndrome, and the other collagen/elastin types have different tissue functions.'),
('ece78ff7-5dc0-5401-b44e-7bb9e0391c80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of osteogenesis imperfecta?', '["Type II (lethal perinatal)","Type IV (moderate with normal sclera)","Type I (mild with blue sclera)","Type III (severe progressive deforming)","Type V (with calcified interosseous membrane)"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (mild with blue sclera)', 'Type I osteogenesis imperfecta is the mildest and most common form, with blue sclera, easy fractures, and normal or near-normal stature. Types II, III, IV, and V are rarer or more severe variants.'),
('6c3d76cf-1275-5486-8d14-eebaa76e0755'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the lethal form of osteogenesis imperfecta?', '["Type V","Type I","Type IV","Type II","Type III"]'::jsonb, 'Type II', 'Type II osteogenesis imperfecta is the lethal perinatal form, causing multiple intrauterine fractures and death in the perinatal period. Types I, III, IV, and V allow longer survival, with type III being severe but not uniformly fatal at birth.'),
('d8e3d40b-189f-5702-ac6a-01a65a892b43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is achondroplasia?', '["A condition with short trunk dwarfism","Increased bone growth","Connective tissue disorder","Abnormal bone density","A rhizomelic dwarfism due to FGFR3 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'A rhizomelic dwarfism due to FGFR3 mutation', 'Achondroplasia is a rhizomelic (proximal limb) dwarfism caused by an activating FGFR3 mutation that suppresses chondrocyte proliferation at the growth plates. It is not a short-trunk condition, a bone-density disorder, or a connective tissue disease.'),
('a17112e5-0a60-5567-9822-d7565074d56b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Achondroplasia is inherited in which pattern?', '["Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","X-linked dominant","Sporadic","Autosomal dominant"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal dominant', 'Achondroplasia is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait, although most cases arise from new mutations in the FGFR3 gene. The recessive, X-linked, and sporadic-only patterns do not describe its transmission.'),
('8cbd8e8e-30c8-560b-a6b3-96405f3f7fb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the genetic mutation in achondroplasia?', '["FBN1 mutation","COMP mutation","FGFR3 G380R (glycine to arginine)","COL1A1 mutation","SOX9 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'FGFR3 G380R (glycine to arginine)', 'Achondroplasia is caused by the FGFR3 G380R mutation, a glycine-to-arginine substitution at codon 380 that constitutively activates the receptor and inhibits growth-plate chondrocytes. FBN1, COMP, COL1A1, and SOX9 mutations cause Marfan syndrome, pseudoachondroplasia, osteogenesis imperfecta, and campomelic dysplasia respectively.'),
('c3ac1a94-2ce6-5f48-a164-e31741c8bf33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is thanatophoric dysplasia?', '["A type of EDS resulting in decreased functional activity","A lysosomal storage disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A type of OI mediated by specific receptor activation","A mild form of dwarfism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lethal form of short-limb dwarfism with FGFR3 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'A lethal form of short-limb dwarfism with FGFR3 mutation', 'Thanatophoric dysplasia is a lethal neonatal short-limb dwarfism caused by severe activating FGFR3 mutations, with a narrow thorax and respiratory failure at birth. It is not an EDS, lysosomal storage disorder, type of OI, or mild dwarfism.'),
('0c739445-0a02-5c85-a5f2-28923a9d4ef2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common non-lethal skeletal dysplasia?', '["Diastrophic dysplasia","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Hypochondroplasia","Thanatophoric dysplasia","Achondroplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Achondroplasia', 'Achondroplasia is the most common non-lethal skeletal dysplasia and the most common cause of dwarfism overall. Hypochondroplasia, diastrophic dysplasia, and osteogenesis imperfecta are rarer, and thanatophoric dysplasia is lethal.'),
('21af001f-f8fe-5e35-b807-4d130c865b5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is diastrophic dysplasia?', '["Abnormal collagen formation resulting in decreased functional activity","Abnormal cartilage formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abnormal bone density mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal FGFR3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A short-limb dwarfism with joint dislocations and ear deformities due to SLC26A2 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'A short-limb dwarfism with joint dislocations and ear deformities due to SLC26A2 mutation', 'Diastrophic dysplasia is an autosomal-recessive short-limb dwarfism caused by SLC26A2 (DTDST) mutations, characterized by joint dislocations, clubbed feet, and ''cauliflower'' ear deformity. The general descriptions of abnormal collagen, cartilage, bone density, or FGFR3 do not specify this disorder.'),
('d6e2d95d-c21c-5095-9121-803c80663595'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of mucopolysaccharidosis?', '["Morquio syndrome (MPS IV)","Hunter syndrome (MPS II)","Hurler syndrome (MPS I)","Scheie syndrome (MPS IS)","Sanfilippo syndrome (MPS III)"]'::jsonb, 'Hurler syndrome (MPS I)', 'Hurler syndrome (MPS I) is the most common mucopolysaccharidosis. Hunter, Morquio, Sanfilippo, and Scheie syndromes are each less frequent or represent allelic variants.'),
('c53f596d-e639-5c40-b4bb-58751763e45f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Hurler syndrome is characterized by:', '["Only neurologic symptoms resulting in decreased functional activity","Only skeletal abnormalities accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coarse facies hepatosplenomegaly corneal clouding skeletal dysplasia","Only cardiac abnormalities without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coarse facies hepatosplenomegaly corneal clouding skeletal dysplasia', 'Hurler syndrome produces a striking combination of coarse facies, hepatosplenomegaly, corneal clouding, and skeletal dysplasia from widespread accumulation of dermatan and heparan sulfate. Its presentation is multisystemic, not limited to a single organ system.'),
('182fd3c2-e698-5648-8ab3-b4aaf5a299bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What enzyme is deficient in Hurler syndrome?', '["Acetyl-CoA alpha-glucosaminide N-acetyltransferase","Heparan N-sulfatase","Alpha-L-iduronidase","Iduronate sulfatase","N-acetylglucosamine-6-sulfatase"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-L-iduronidase', 'Hurler syndrome results from deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme alpha-L-iduronidase, which degrades dermatan and heparan sulfate. Iduronate sulfatase deficiency causes Hunter syndrome, and the other enzymes correspond to different MPS subtypes.'),
('c8b26eff-4123-50e8-a791-cbdb34ce65ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is inherited in which pattern?', '["Autosomal recessive","Mitochondrial","Autosomal dominant","X-linked recessive","X-linked dominant"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked recessive', 'Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern, so it predominantly affects males. The autosomal recessive, dominant, and mitochondrial patterns do not describe its transmission.'),
('fe8afb20-53ea-55e7-a592-dfc216c239f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Hunter syndrome (MPS II) differs from Hurler syndrome by which feature?', '["Milder neurologic involvement","More severe skeletal involvement","Earlier onset","Presence of corneal clouding","Absence of corneal clouding"]'::jsonb, 'Absence of corneal clouding', 'Hunter syndrome spares the cornea because iduronate sulfatase deficiency accumulates only heparan sulfate, whereas Hurler syndrome also stores dermatan sulfate and shows corneal clouding. The other listed contrasts are not accurate.'),
('a25e71cb-027b-5ad5-9287-920b08a6c345'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Morquio syndrome (MPS IV) is characterized by:', '["Cardiac involvement primarily","Severe skeletal dysplasia with normal intelligence","Facial dysmorphism with hearing loss","Coarse facies with organomegaly","Severe intellectual disability with mild skeletal findings"]'::jsonb, 'Severe skeletal dysplasia with normal intelligence', 'Morquio syndrome (MPS IV) causes severe skeletal dysplasia, including short stature and kyphoscoliosis, while sparing intelligence. Cardiac-predominant, facial-dysmorphic, or intellectually severe patterns point to other MPS types.'),
('b4416da8-00f5-53b1-8919-cdd87d1d075f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is osteopetrosis?', '["Increased bone turnover resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased bone density accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abnormal bone shape mediated by specific receptor activation","A group of disorders with increased bone density due to defective osteoclast function","Bone marrow expansion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A group of disorders with increased bone density due to defective osteoclast function', 'Osteopetrosis comprises disorders of increased bone density caused by defective osteoclast function, so resorption fails and dense bone accumulates. Decreased density, increased turnover, and marrow expansion describe different conditions.'),
('dc67f910-d180-55f9-a4b9-0ddf708b5339'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common form of osteopetrosis?', '["Autosomal recessive (malignant)","Mitochondrial","X-linked recessive","Autosomal recessive (intermediate)","Autosomal dominant (Albers-Schönberg disease)"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal dominant (Albers-Schönberg disease)', 'Autosomal dominant (Albers-Schönberg) osteopetrosis is the most common form, often discovered incidentally in adulthood with mild or no symptoms. The autosomal recessive forms are rarer, more severe, and present in infancy.'),
('b29df440-6bfd-5a9d-971c-e347c8b66490'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the severe infantile form of osteopetrosis?', '["Autosomal recessive malignant osteopetrosis","Autosomal dominant osteopetrosis","X-linked osteopetrosis","Severe OI","Intermediate osteopetrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive malignant osteopetrosis', 'Autosomal recessive malignant osteopetrosis is the severe infantile form, causing marrow obliteration with pancytopenia, hepatosplenomegaly, and early death untreated. The dominant and intermediate forms are milder, and severe OI is a different disease.'),
('e7e093a7-514b-5965-9499-8537d0699588'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the genetic defect in most cases of autosomal recessive osteopetrosis?', '["CA2 mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","CLCN7 mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RANKL mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","OPG mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TCIRG1 mutation (osteoclast vacuolar proton pump)"]'::jsonb, 'TCIRG1 mutation (osteoclast vacuolar proton pump)', 'Most autosomal-recessive osteopetrosis is caused by TCIRG1 mutations that disrupt the osteoclast vacuolar proton pump, preventing acidification of the resorption lacuna. CA2 and CLCN7 mutations cause other variants, and RANKL/OPG mutations affect the signaling pathway.'),
('99ac4949-65ab-56c4-8a8b-6dd8e14dfaee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is pyknodysostosis?', '["A type of osteopetrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","A collagen disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A type of OI mediated by specific receptor activation","A lysosomal storage disorder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A sclerosing bone dysplasia with short stature osteosclerosis and acro-osteolysis"]'::jsonb, 'A sclerosing bone dysplasia with short stature osteosclerosis and acro-osteolysis', 'Pyknodysostosis is a sclerosing bone dysplasia characterized by short stature, generalized osteosclerosis, and acro-osteolysis of the distal phalanges, due to cathepsin K deficiency. It is distinguished from osteopetrosis, collagen disorders, OI, and storage diseases.'),
('9e05102a-b823-598c-b41a-7f321db20d48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What gene is mutated in pyknodysostosis?', '["CA2","TCIRG1","RANKL","CTSK (cathepsin K)","CLCN7"]'::jsonb, 'CTSK (cathepsin K)', 'Pyknodysostosis results from mutations in CTSK, which encodes cathepsin K, the collagen-degrading enzyme produced by osteoclasts. CA2, TCIRG1, RANKL, and CLCN7 mutations cause other sclerosing or resorption defects.'),
('3ee3b3bf-77ce-534f-9a4a-64c269cb85a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is melorheostosis?', '["Generalized increased bone density resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortical thickening accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Focal decreased bone density mediated by specific receptor activation","A sclerosing bone dysplasia with flowing candle wax appearance on X-ray","Periosteal bone formation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A sclerosing bone dysplasia with flowing candle wax appearance on X-ray', 'Melorheostosis is a sclerosing bone dysplasia that produces irregular cortical hyperostosis resembling dripping candle wax on X-ray, typically along one limb. Generalized density, cortical thickening alone, or lucent lesions describe other disorders.'),
('c274b0ca-7d59-5a05-98ba-668db24851e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is osteopoikilosis?', '["A malignant bone tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Multiple small sclerotic bone lesions (spotted bones)","Metastatic lesions mediated by specific receptor activation","Infectious lesions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fractures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple small sclerotic bone lesions (spotted bones)', 'Osteopoikilosis produces multiple small, round sclerotic foci (''spotted bones''), typically an incidental benign finding in the epiphyses and metaphyses. Malignancy, metastases, infection, and fractures have different clinical and radiographic features.'),
('fde02764-233f-5112-99dc-9bcaa8846249'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common bone tumor in children under 5 years?', '["Osteosarcoma","Leukemic infiltration","Ewing sarcoma","Metastatic neuroblastoma","Langerhans cell histiocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Metastatic neuroblastoma', 'Neuroblastoma, the most common extracranial solid tumor of infancy, frequently metastasizes to the skeleton, making it the most common bone tumor in children under 5 years. Osteosarcoma and Ewing sarcoma typically appear in older children and adolescents, and leukemic infiltration usually follows systemic leukemia.'),
('e09c5083-8484-51b2-aa31-0ab81360acb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common cause of pathologic fracture in children?', '["OI resulting in decreased functional activity","Metastatic disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteosarcoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Benign bone cysts (simple bone cyst aneurysmal bone cyst)","Fracture from minor trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign bone cysts (simple bone cyst aneurysmal bone cyst)', 'Benign bone cysts, especially simple and aneurysmal bone cysts, are the most common cause of pathologic fracture in children because they weaken the metaphyseal bone. Malignant tumors and metastatic disease are much rarer causes in this age group.'),
('39137405-6244-592f-bb5f-aa7932481a24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is a simple (unicameral) bone cyst?', '["A fracture hematoma resulting in decreased functional activity","A benign fluid-filled bone cavity usually in the metaphysis of long bones","A solid bone tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A cartilaginous lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A vascular malformation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign fluid-filled bone cavity usually in the metaphysis of long bones', 'A simple (unicameral) bone cyst is a benign, fluid-filled cavity that typically develops in the metaphysis of long bones. It is not a solid tumor, cartilaginous lesion, vascular malformation, or fracture hematoma.'),
('ae0ef1de-919e-5eb6-bd7d-70c4ba9a89ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common location of a simple bone cyst?', '["Distal femur resulting in decreased functional activity","Spine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pelvis mediated by specific receptor activation","Skull without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proximal humerus (metaphysis)"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal humerus (metaphysis)', 'Simple bone cysts most often occupy the proximal humeral metaphysis, a site of rapid skeletal growth. The distal femur, pelvis, spine, and skull are far less common locations.'),
('339b44c9-e762-573c-a1d7-bf2d47592cdf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is fibrous dysplasia?', '["A benign bone lesion where normal bone is replaced by fibrous tissue and immature bone","A metabolic disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A malignant bone tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","An infective lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An inflammatory lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign bone lesion where normal bone is replaced by fibrous tissue and immature bone', 'Fibrous dysplasia is a benign lesion in which normal bone and marrow are replaced by fibrous tissue and immature woven bone. It is neither metabolic, malignant, infective, nor inflammatory in nature.'),
('63e73d9e-c328-5adc-b282-7cc8d4848962'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What are the two forms of fibrous dysplasia?', '["Solitary and multiple","Localized and systemic","Monostotic and polyostotic","Benign and malignant","Monoarticular and polyarticular"]'::jsonb, 'Monostotic and polyostotic', 'Fibrous dysplasia is classified into monostotic (single bone) and polyostotic (multiple bones) forms, with the polyostotic form tending to present earlier and more severely. The other options are not the standard classification.'),
('019213c0-2e5d-5e82-9ad7-1bc27b1fc206'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'McCune-Albright syndrome includes which triad?', '["Fibrous dysplasia and hearing loss","Café-au-lait spots alone","Monostotic fibrous dysplasia and pain","Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia and fractures","Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia café-au-lait spots endocrine dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 'Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia café-au-lait spots endocrine dysfunction', 'McCune-Albright syndrome is defined by the triad of polyostotic fibrous dysplasia, café-au-lait spots, and endocrine dysfunction, most often precocious puberty. Monostotic disease, fractures, hearing loss, or café-au-lait spots alone do not constitute the triad.'),
('4ce689ff-9661-5160-8018-ef8700f06f1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What gene is mutated in fibrous dysplasia / McCune-Albright syndrome?', '["NF1 resulting in decreased functional activity","GNAS (activating mutation)","RB1 mediated by specific receptor activation","TP53 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","EXT1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GNAS (activating mutation)', 'An activating somatic mutation in GNAS, which encodes the alpha subunit of the stimulatory G protein, causes fibrous dysplasia and McCune-Albright syndrome. NF1, RB1, TP53, and EXT1 mutations are associated with other disorders.'),
('c6c09fbe-c230-5f31-9edb-cf31df2c2fcd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common cause of short stature?', '["Growth hormone deficiency","Skeletal dysplasia","Hypothyroidism","Constitutional growth delay","Familial short stature"]'::jsonb, 'Constitutional growth delay', 'Constitutional growth delay is the most common cause of short stature in children, producing a delayed but ultimately normal growth pattern. Growth hormone deficiency, hypothyroidism, skeletal dysplasia, and familial short stature are important but less common causes.'),
('2a33be32-bb3d-5e9e-a01d-3405a706095f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is primordial dwarfism?', '["GH deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Skeletal dysplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Short stature that begins in utero with proportionate dwarfism","Thyroid hormone deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malnutrition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Short stature that begins in utero with proportionate dwarfism', 'Primordial dwarfism is proportionate short stature that begins in utero, with growth failure from early fetal life. It is distinct from GH deficiency, hypothyroidism, malnutrition, and skeletal dysplasia, which typically cause growth failure after birth.'),
('07f6a41d-6efa-5caa-a03d-8d2402561e43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Russell-Silver syndrome?', '["Intrauterine growth retardation with asymmetry and triangular facies","Growth hormone deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skeletal dysplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Excessive growth syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Precocious puberty leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intrauterine growth retardation with asymmetry and triangular facies', 'Russell-Silver syndrome is characterized by intrauterine growth retardation with body asymmetry and a characteristic triangular facies. Growth hormone deficiency, skeletal dysplasia, overgrowth, and precocious puberty do not produce this constellation.'),
('2fa4804f-3e8b-5036-9c6c-33f41ac4e0cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Somatic overgrowth syndromes include which conditions?', '["Down Turner and Klinefelter syndromes","Osteogenesis imperfecta and achondroplasia","Marfan and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes","Beckwith-Wiedemann Sotos and Weaver syndromes","Russell-Silver Seckel and Dubowitz syndromes"]'::jsonb, 'Beckwith-Wiedemann Sotos and Weaver syndromes', 'Beckwith-Wiedemann, Sotos, and Weaver syndromes are the classic somatic overgrowth syndromes. Down, Turner, Klinefelter, Russell-Silver, and Seckel syndromes do not cause generalized overgrowth.'),
('8796b07a-acb5-5d3c-b2b0-774786d35170'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Sotos syndrome (cerebral gigantism) is characterized by:', '["Proportionate short stature resulting in decreased functional activity","Precocious puberty accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asymmetric growth mediated by specific receptor activation","Growth retardation microcephaly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excessive growth macrocephaly advanced bone age intellectual disability"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive growth macrocephaly advanced bone age intellectual disability', 'Sotos syndrome (cerebral gigantism) features excessive growth, macrocephaly, advanced bone age, and intellectual disability. The other options describe short stature or growth retardation, which are opposite to this overgrowth syndrome.'),
('16eaa41b-bd60-5b30-a529-0764514602ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Weaver syndrome is characterized by:', '["Asymmetric overgrowth resulting in decreased functional activity","Pre and postnatal overgrowth advanced bone age camptodactyly characteristic facies","Growth retardation with dysmorphism mediated by specific receptor activation","Macrocephaly with normal height without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Overgrowth with hypotonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pre and postnatal overgrowth advanced bone age camptodactyly characteristic facies', 'Weaver syndrome shows pre- and postnatal overgrowth with advanced bone age, camptodactyly, and a characteristic facies. Asymmetric overgrowth alone, growth retardation, and macrocephaly with normal height do not define the syndrome.'),
('9ba9de25-c09f-585c-b56d-1936b715e7f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common connective tissue disorder?', '["Alport syndrome","Stickler syndrome","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Marfan syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Ehlers-Danlos syndrome', 'Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is the most common hereditary connective tissue disorder, caused by defects in collagen synthesis or processing and manifesting with skin, joint, and vascular findings. Marfan syndrome, osteogenesis imperfecta, and Stickler syndrome are less common causes.'),
('1912012e-7b64-56b9-a62e-40c2b74881db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome?', '["Hypermobile type (EDS type III)","Kyphoscoliotic type","Vascular type (EDS type IV)","Classical type (EDS type I/II)","Arthrochalasia type"]'::jsonb, 'Hypermobile type (EDS type III)', 'Hypermobile EDS (type III) is the most common form, with generalized joint laxity and skin hyperextensibility but a normal life expectancy. The vascular, classical, kyphoscoliotic, and arthrochalasia types are all less frequent.'),
('e375761a-0901-5082-affb-3368d772fa25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most severe type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome?', '["Kyphoscoliotic type","Hypermobile type","Dermatosparaxis type","Classical type","Vascular type (EDS type IV)"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular type (EDS type IV)', 'Vascular EDS (type IV) is the most severe form because defective type III collagen predisposes to arterial rupture, bowel perforation, and uterine rupture. The other types lack this high risk of life-threatening vascular complications.'),
('56fba549-f884-5fcb-a250-187e9c0d242b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What collagen is defective in vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome?', '["Type VII collagen","Type VI collagen","Type I collagen","Type III collagen","Type V collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Type III collagen', 'Vascular EDS results from defective type III collagen, encoded by COL3A1, explaining the fragility of blood vessels and hollow organs. Defects of collagen types I, V, VI, and VII are responsible for other subtypes.'),
('521f40d2-40d7-5384-ab93-527923c5fa5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the genetic basis of Marfan syndrome?', '["TGFBR2 mutation","COL3A1 mutation","TGFBR1 mutation","FBN1 mutation (fibrillin-1)","COL1A1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'FBN1 mutation (fibrillin-1)', 'Marfan syndrome is caused by a mutation in FBN1, the gene for fibrillin-1, a major component of microfibrils. COL3A1 mutations cause vascular EDS, whereas TGFBR1 and TGFBR2 mutations cause Loeys-Dietz syndrome.'),
('47a1f343-f856-5a30-9f97-c0d9ec8d36df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What are the main clinical features of Marfan syndrome?', '["Skeletal cardiovascular and ocular involvement","Skin and joint involvement only","Ocular involvement only","Skeletal involvement only","Cardiovascular involvement only"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal cardiovascular and ocular involvement', 'Marfan syndrome characteristically involves the skeletal system (tall stature, arachnodactyly), cardiovascular system (aortic dilation), and eyes (lens dislocation). Involvement limited to a single organ system is not typical of the syndrome.'),
('80c66c70-ef2e-5830-99d4-b54acf8be109'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most life-threatening complication of Marfan syndrome?', '["Dural ectasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Flat feet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ectopia lentis mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumothorax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic root dilation and dissection"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic root dilation and dissection', 'Aortic root dilation and dissection is the most life-threatening complication of Marfan syndrome and determines the prognosis. Ectopia lentis, dural ectasia, and pneumothorax are important but far less dangerous.'),
('c0e96d75-1a98-5919-8649-1c94847b701e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What ocular finding is characteristic of Marfan syndrome?', '["Myopia resulting in decreased functional activity","Glaucoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cataract mediated by specific receptor activation","Ectopia lentis (lens dislocation)","Retinal detachment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ectopia lentis (lens dislocation)', 'Ectopia lentis, usually upward dislocation of the lens, is the classic ocular finding in Marfan syndrome. Myopia, cataract, glaucoma, and retinal detachment are not specific markers of the disease.'),
('d1d6dd90-d73f-5f39-beec-1eadd8de9846'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is homocystinuria?', '["A collagen vascular disease resulting in decreased functional activity","A lysosomal storage disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A type of EDS mediated by specific receptor activation","A metabolic disorder that mimics Marfan syndrome with lens dislocation and thromboembolism","A skeletal dysplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A metabolic disorder that mimics Marfan syndrome with lens dislocation and thromboembolism', 'Homocystinuria is a metabolic disorder of methionine metabolism, most often due to cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency, that mimics Marfan syndrome with lens dislocation and thromboembolism. It is not a collagen, storage, or skeletal disease.'),
('510d7f2d-7f20-5c1b-91d0-bdc70ac58e19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What differentiates homocystinuria from Marfan syndrome?', '["Homocystinuria has intellectual disability and inferior lens dislocation; Marfan has superior lens dislocation and normal intelligence","Lens dislocation occurs only in Marfan accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Marfan has intellectual disability mediated by specific receptor activation","Both have the same features without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Homocystinuria has aortic dilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Homocystinuria has intellectual disability and inferior lens dislocation; Marfan has superior lens dislocation and normal intelligence', 'Homocystinuria causes intellectual disability and downward (inferior) lens dislocation, whereas Marfan syndrome causes upward (superior) lens dislocation with normal intelligence. Both can show aortic dilation, so it does not reliably differentiate them.'),
('d3d29806-d181-5ea4-8dec-7b0bc33f4be0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Stickler syndrome?', '["A connective tissue disorder with orofacial features hearing loss and joint problems","A type of EDS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A collagen vascular disease mediated by specific receptor activation","A type of OI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A skeletal dysplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A connective tissue disorder with orofacial features hearing loss and joint problems', 'Stickler syndrome is a connective tissue disorder, often due to COL2A1 mutations, with orofacial anomalies, sensorineural hearing loss, and joint problems. It is not a form of EDS, OI, or a general skeletal dysplasia.'),
('4bec1ebf-f602-5e88-bd59-7942e5966b4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common cause of hereditary hearing loss?', '["MYO7A mutations","Connexin 26 (GJB2) mutations","COL4A5 mutations","STRC mutations","OTOF mutations"]'::jsonb, 'Connexin 26 (GJB2) mutations', 'Mutations in GJB2, encoding connexin 26, are the most common cause of hereditary sensorineural hearing loss and are responsible for a large share of recessive non-syndromic deafness. MYO7A, OTOF, STRC, and COL4A5 are much rarer causes.'),
('84999036-ae22-51b7-92c3-634e2572a9b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What are the main features of Waardenburg syndrome?', '["Renal anomalies resulting in decreased functional activity","Craniofacial anomalies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensorineural hearing loss white forelock heterochromia iridis dystopia canthorum","Cardiac anomalies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skeletal dysplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensorineural hearing loss white forelock heterochromia iridis dystopia canthorum', 'Waardenburg syndrome classically shows sensorineural hearing loss, a white forelock, iris heterochromia, and dystopia canthorum, all from defective neural crest melanocyte development. Cardiac, renal, and skeletal anomalies are not part of the syndrome.'),
('144439f8-059a-574c-9d01-82a6319a1619'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Treacher Collins syndrome?', '["First arch syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Cleft lip and palate alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mandibulofacial dysostosis with hypoplastic zygomas and mandible with downward sloping palpebral fissures","Branchial cleft cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Craniosynostosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibulofacial dysostosis with hypoplastic zygomas and mandible with downward sloping palpebral fissures', 'Treacher Collins syndrome is a mandibulofacial dysostosis with hypoplastic zygomas and mandible, downward-slanting palpebral fissures, and colobomas, resulting from impaired neural crest development. It is not simple cleft lip, craniosynostosis, or a branchial cleft cyst.'),
('83877944-b60c-594d-a8cd-522ec4017b09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of Treacher Collins inheritance?', '["Multifactorial","Sporadic","X-linked","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal dominant', 'Treacher Collins syndrome is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, most often due to TCOF1 mutations. It is not characteristically X-linked, recessive, sporadic, or multifactorial.'),
('a13c8165-bfe4-59eb-8f65-5e3ee2bb898e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the embryonic basis of Treacher Collins syndrome?', '["Abnormal neural tube closure resulting in decreased functional activity","Abnormal endoderm migration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abnormal heart development mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal development of the first and second pharyngeal arches","Abnormal limb development leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal development of the first and second pharyngeal arches', 'Treacher Collins syndrome results from abnormal development of the first and second pharyngeal arches, especially impaired neural crest migration into the facial processes. Neural tube, endoderm, heart, and limb development are unaffected.'),
('f39bc3a4-02c9-5439-a713-2c2168e78ef5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Nager syndrome?', '["Cleft lip and palate resulting in decreased functional activity","Acrofacial dysostosis with limb defects (radial ray defects)","Micrognathia alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Mandibulofacial dysostosis without limb defects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Craniosynostosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acrofacial dysostosis with limb defects (radial ray defects)', 'Nager syndrome is an acrofacial dysostosis that combines mandibulofacial features with limb defects, particularly hypoplasia of the radial ray and thumb. Micrognathia alone or mandibulofacial dysostosis without limb involvement describes other conditions.'),
('fe758362-5907-58a9-a8f8-ffb55389d679'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common genetic cause of Pierre Robin sequence?', '["Not a single genetic cause; associated with many syndromes (Stickler 22q11.2 Treacher Collins)","FBN1 mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TCOF1 mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","COL2A1 mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","22q11.2 deletion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Not a single genetic cause; associated with many syndromes (Stickler 22q11.2 Treacher Collins)', 'Pierre Robin sequence (micrognathia, glossoptosis, airway obstruction, and often cleft palate) has no single genetic cause and is associated with many syndromes, including Stickler syndrome, 22q11.2 deletion, and Treacher Collins syndrome. No one gene explains it.'),
('29723d0f-cfbd-5d0b-b4c8-e5e8945a9771'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The muscles of the head and neck develop from which structures?', '["Pharyngeal arches (paraxial mesoderm and neural crest)","Notochord only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somites only mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural crest only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surface ectoderm only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal arches (paraxial mesoderm and neural crest)', 'The muscles of the head and neck arise from pharyngeal arch mesenchyme, contributed by paraxial (occipital) mesoderm and neural crest cells. Somites alone, notochord, neural crest alone, or surface ectoderm do not give rise to these muscles.'),
('bfb7bcba-2725-58fc-b4b0-ff786ff15040'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The stylopharyngeus muscle is derived from which arch?', '["Third arch","Sixth arch","Fourth arch","First arch","Second arch"]'::jsonb, 'Third arch', 'The stylopharyngeus muscle is derived from the third pharyngeal arch and is therefore supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve. The first, second, fourth, and sixth arch muscles have different origins and innervations.'),
('575263ab-ba94-54e5-b6a1-cc6ed5e8954c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The muscles of the soft palate (except tensor veli palatini) and pharynx are derived from which arch?', '["Third arch","Sixth arch","Second arch","First arch","Fourth arch"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth arch', 'The soft palate muscles except tensor veli palatini, together with the pharyngeal constrictors, develop from the fourth arch and are supplied by the vagus via the pharyngeal plexus. Tensor veli palatini is a first arch derivative supplied by the mandibular nerve.'),
('6a6ed9d8-5554-5964-b658-ad04d3a11986'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The intrinsic muscles of the larynx are derived from which arch?', '["Sixth arch","Third arch","Fourth arch","First arch","Second arch"]'::jsonb, 'Sixth arch', 'The intrinsic muscles of the larynx derive from the sixth arch and are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve. The cricothyroid, however, is a fourth arch derivative supplied by the superior laryngeal nerve.'),
('a32421a2-dd4b-5e3e-b2f0-a7651b4e61c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the nerve supply to the first arch muscles?', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V) mandibular division","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (CN V) mandibular division', 'First arch muscles (muscles of mastication, tensor tympani, and tensor veli palatini) are innervated by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve. CN VII, IX, X, and XII supply muscles derived from other arches.'),
('ef0f3abc-3e23-5f98-a4cb-109147bc9977'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the nerve supply to the second arch muscles?', '["Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (CN VII)', 'Second arch muscles (muscles of facial expression, stapedius, stylohyoid, and posterior belly of digastric) are supplied by the facial nerve. The other cranial nerves listed innervate muscles of different arches.'),
('5f71a108-3f4c-5427-9f23-255bd35bb65f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the nerve supply to the third arch muscle?', '["Vagus nerve (CN X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)', 'The stylopharyngeus, the only muscle derived from the third arch, is the sole muscle innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve. CN V, VII, X, and XII supply muscles of other arches.'),
('e2e0f5ab-87c5-5ac6-b7b0-d0ead1cbff7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the nerve supply to the fourth arch muscles?', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Superior laryngeal branch of vagus (CN X)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Recurrent laryngeal branch of vagus (CN X)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior laryngeal branch of vagus (CN X)', 'Fourth arch muscles (pharyngeal constrictors, soft palate muscles except tensor veli palatini, and cricothyroid) are supplied by the superior laryngeal branch of the vagus nerve. The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies sixth arch muscles.'),
('6d531b8a-c231-5d3f-8b58-9f475638556d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the nerve supply to the sixth arch muscles?', '["Superior laryngeal branch of vagus (CN X)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Recurrent laryngeal branch of vagus (CN X)"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent laryngeal branch of vagus (CN X)', 'Sixth arch muscles (intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid) are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal branch of the vagus nerve. The superior laryngeal nerve, CN V, VII, and IX serve other structures.'),
('f235c8bb-e5b4-597e-867b-dff87e77cdef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The muscles of the tongue develop from which structure?', '["Somites resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral plate mesoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Occipital myotomes (hypoglossal cord)","Pharyngeal arches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Occipital myotomes (hypoglossal cord)', 'The tongue muscles develop from occipital (hypoglossal) myotomes, which migrate forward to form the intrinsic and extrinsic lingual musculature. Pharyngeal arches give rise to the tongue mucosa and connective tissue, not the muscles.'),
('4f184f3b-dd06-5253-8c75-e84157e2d25d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The tongue is innervated by which nerve?', '["Trigeminal nerve only resulting in decreased functional activity","Glossopharyngeal nerve only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) for muscles; various nerves for sensation","Vagus nerve only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) for muscles; various nerves for sensation', 'The hypoglossal nerve supplies all intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue, while sensation is carried by the trigeminal nerve (anterior two-thirds) and glossopharyngeal nerve (posterior one-third), with taste served by the facial and glossopharyngeal nerves. No single nerve alone supplies the tongue.'),
('3a6c6975-2c8e-58c4-95cf-2edf478d1c35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The extraocular muscles develop from which structures?', '["Pharyngeal arches resulting in decreased functional activity","Preotic myotomes (mesoderm around the optic vesicle)","Somites mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral plate mesoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Preotic myotomes (mesoderm around the optic vesicle)', 'The extraocular muscles develop from preotic myotomes, which are mesoderm located around the optic vesicle. Pharyngeal arches, somites, lateral plate mesoderm, and neural crest do not contribute to these muscles.'),
('c7f4896a-6457-555a-b3a9-1c61b7798551'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What cranial nerves innervate the extraocular muscles?', '["CN IV V VI","CN III VII VI","CN III IV V","CN III V VI","CN III IV VI"]'::jsonb, 'CN III IV VI', 'The extraocular muscles are innervated by the oculomotor (CN III), trochlear (CN IV), and abducens (CN VI) nerves. CN V and CN VII play no role in extraocular muscle innervation.'),
('71566e4e-378e-5dd1-af2d-531c064e0345'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common congenital cranial nerve palsy?', '["Congenital trochlear nerve palsy","Congenital hypoglossal nerve palsy","Congenital abducens nerve (CN VI) palsy","Congenital oculomotor nerve palsy","Congenital facial nerve palsy"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital abducens nerve (CN VI) palsy', 'Congenital abducens nerve palsy is the most common congenital cranial nerve palsy and is frequently seen in Duane syndrome. Palsies of CN III, IV, VII, and XII are less common at birth.'),
('c486184b-1dd6-5082-9f67-fa11a1f2e5ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Möbius syndrome?', '["Isolated CN VI palsy resulting in decreased functional activity","Isolated CN VII palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital bilateral facial and abducens nerve palsy with limb anomalies","CN XII palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN III palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital bilateral facial and abducens nerve palsy with limb anomalies', 'Möbius syndrome presents with congenital bilateral facial and abducens nerve palsy, causing an expressionless face and impaired eye abduction, often together with limb anomalies. Isolated palsy of a single cranial nerve is not Möbius syndrome.'),
('17df5289-3a61-5ffc-b730-eb2a3d0a8e43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the embryonic basis of Möbius syndrome?', '["Pharyngeal arch defect resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural crest defect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somite defect mediated by specific receptor activation","Nuclear hypoplasia or agenesis of cranial nerve nuclei due to vascular disruption","Notochord defect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear hypoplasia or agenesis of cranial nerve nuclei due to vascular disruption', 'Möbius syndrome is attributed to nuclear hypoplasia or agenesis of the cranial nerve nuclei, likely from vascular disruption of the developing brainstem. Pharyngeal arch, neural crest, somite, and notochord defects are not the cause.'),
('91c1df18-ff94-5d2b-b7ac-199cb235c394'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The sympathetic chain ganglia develop from which structure?', '["Somites","Notochord","Neural tube","Surface ectoderm","Neural crest cells"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest cells', 'The sympathetic chain ganglia are derived from neural crest cells, which migrate to form the paravertebral ganglia alongside the spinal cord. Neural tube, somites, notochord, and surface ectoderm do not give rise to these ganglia.'),
('f1b4e5d0-e856-5269-8b0a-007701f2f7a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The preganglionic sympathetic neurons are located where?', '["Lateral horn of cervical cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Intermediolateral cell column (IML) of spinal cord T1-L2","Ventral horn of spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic chain ganglia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal root ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediolateral cell column (IML) of spinal cord T1-L2', 'Preganglionic sympathetic neurons lie in the intermediolateral cell column of the spinal cord at the T1-L2 levels. The lateral horn of the cervical cord, ventral horn, sympathetic chain ganglia, and dorsal root ganglia do not contain these cell bodies.'),
('6678f504-8036-5de3-9179-293db29e706f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The parasympathetic ganglia develop from which structure?', '["Somites","Neural crest cells","Notochord","Neural tube","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest cells', 'All autonomic ganglia, including the parasympathetic ganglia, arise from neural crest cells that migrate to the head, neck, and viscera. Somites form axial muscle and bone, the notochord induces the neural tube, and surface ectoderm forms skin structures.'),
('133b3dca-d161-59b3-b041-0e07519828b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What are the four parasympathetic ganglia of the head?', '["Ciliary pterygopalatine submandibular otic","Ciliary trigeminal facial glossopalatine","Otic sublingual submandibular vagal","Sphenopalatine otic ciliary sublingual","Ciliary pterygopalatine geniculate otic"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary pterygopalatine submandibular otic', 'The four paired parasympathetic ganglia of the head are the ciliary, pterygopalatine, submandibular, and otic ganglia. They relay preganglionic fibers from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, superior and inferior salivatory nuclei to their target organs.'),
('94c9080f-d98e-5ae1-be63-fb916d68e1cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The dorsal root ganglia (spinal ganglia) develop from which structure?', '["Neural tube","Neural crest cells","Notochord","Somites","Mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest cells', 'Dorsal root (spinal) ganglia are sensory ganglia derived from migrating neural crest cells that settle alongside the spinal cord. The neural tube forms the central nervous system, while somites give rise to axial musculoskeletal structures.'),
('e6dd6fec-4b9d-5472-bec9-654aa51dfdcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common location of a ganglion cyst?', '["Ankle resulting in decreased functional activity","Hip accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shoulder mediated by specific receptor activation","Knee without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wrist (dorsal aspect)"]'::jsonb, 'Wrist (dorsal aspect)', 'Ganglion cysts most commonly occur on the dorsal aspect of the wrist, typically near the scapholunate joint capsule. The knee, ankle, and shoulder are much less frequent sites.'),
('07e728fe-5100-5d11-8e90-dff7fa470d2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is a neuroma?', '["Meningioma","A benign tumor of nerve tissue","A malignant nerve tumor","Schwannoma","Impingement of a nerve by a ganglion cyst"]'::jsonb, 'A benign tumor of nerve tissue', 'A neuroma is a benign proliferation of nerve tissue that often follows nerve injury or amputation (traumatic neuroma). Malignant nerve tumors and schwannomas are distinct entities, and a ganglion cyst compresses a nerve rather than forming a nerve tumor.'),
('57ee2d2d-8144-559a-ac04-0782b089d1cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common benign nerve sheath tumor?', '["Schwannoma","Ganglioneuroma","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor","Neurofibroma","Neuroma"]'::jsonb, 'Schwannoma', 'Schwannomas, benign tumors of Schwann cells, are the most common benign nerve sheath tumors and frequently arise on sensory nerves such as the vestibular nerve. Neurofibromas are less common and are characteristic of NF1.'),
('5273e2de-f630-57a9-ab08-63f125861f72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common malignant tumor of peripheral nerves?', '["Neuroma resulting in decreased functional activity","Ganglioneuroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor (MPNST)","Neurofibroma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Schwannoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor (MPNST)', 'Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor (MPNST) is the most common malignant tumor of peripheral nerves and shows a strong association with neurofibromatosis type 1. The other listed lesions are benign nerve tumors.'),
('b4fc8bf5-a2b8-5883-8942-240be3401767'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1)?', '["Familial schwannomatosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Familial spinal neurofibromatosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An autosomal dominant disorder with neurofibromas café-au-lait spots and Lisch nodules caused by NF1 gene mutation","Segmental neurofibromatosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A disorder with bilateral acoustic neuromas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An autosomal dominant disorder with neurofibromas café-au-lait spots and Lisch nodules caused by NF1 gene mutation', 'NF1 is an autosomal dominant condition caused by mutation of the NF1 gene (chromosome 17) and characterized by neurofibromas, café-au-lait spots, and Lisch nodules. Bilateral acoustic neuromas and schwannomatosis are features of separate disorders.'),
('23cb0a4d-8793-5030-bffe-97e1167992c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The diagnostic criteria for NF1 require at least 2 of which features?', '["Two or more neurofibromas resulting in decreased functional activity","Osseous lesions and Lisch nodules accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Café-au-lait spots neurofibromas optic glioma Lisch nodules osseous lesion FHx","Café-au-lait spots and axillary freckling without affecting the primary tissue pathway","one or more optic glioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Café-au-lait spots neurofibromas optic glioma Lisch nodules osseous lesion FHx', 'The diagnostic criteria for NF1 require at least two features among café-au-lait spots, neurofibromas, optic glioma, Lisch nodules, osseous lesions (e.g., sphenoid dysplasia), axillary or inguinal freckling, and a first-degree relative with NF1. A single feature is insufficient for diagnosis.'),
('75d4e78e-2cf7-5815-a971-6a44d465213e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of neurofibromatosis?', '["Segmental NF","Familial spinal NF","NF2 (type 2)","NF1 (type 1)","Schwannomatosis"]'::jsonb, 'NF1 (type 1)', 'NF1 accounts for roughly 90% of all neurofibromatosis cases and is the most common type. NF2, segmental NF, familial spinal NF, and schwannomatosis are all much rarer.'),
('f0b16fc0-df72-586a-bb1d-ee18f9f68b85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2)?', '["Familial schwannomatosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal nerve root tumors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple neurofibromas mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas) due to NF2 gene mutation","Plexiform neurofibromas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas) due to NF2 gene mutation', 'NF2 is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutation of the NF2 (merlin) gene on chromosome 22 and is defined by bilateral vestibular schwannomas. Multiple neurofibromas and plexiform neurofibromas are features of NF1.'),
('394aae36-fcd6-595a-bc57-e907231196e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor associated with NF1?', '["Plexiform neurofibroma","Ganglioneuroma","MPNST","Neuroma","Schwannoma"]'::jsonb, 'MPNST', 'Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor (MPNST) is the most common malignant nerve tumor complicating NF1, often arising within pre-existing plexiform neurofibromas. Plexiform neurofibroma itself is a benign lesion.'),
('e8ddf8e4-3d50-5ded-8839-8e7e9b6c9ba7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of muscular dystrophy?', '["Duchenne muscular dystrophy","Limb-girdle dystrophy","Becker muscular dystrophy","Myotonic dystrophy","Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy is the most common muscular dystrophy, affecting about 1 in 3,500 live-born boys. Becker, myotonic, and limb-girdle dystrophies are all less common.'),
('c93af4a0-5e03-5707-8a03-68f53871302a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy is caused by a mutation in which gene?', '["Caveolin-3 gene","Dysferlin gene","Lamin A/C gene","Emerin gene","DMD gene (dystrophin)"]'::jsonb, 'DMD gene (dystrophin)', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy results from mutations in the DMD gene on the X chromosome that encodes dystrophin, a protein linking the cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix. Mutations in caveolin-3, dysferlin, lamin A/C, and emerin cause other muscular dystrophies.'),
('4c0602f4-aace-5480-a8ef-0a1204ae15fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy is inherited in which pattern?', '["Mitochondrial","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","X-linked dominant","Autosomal dominant"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked recessive', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy is inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern because the DMD gene lies on the X chromosome, so affected males predominate while carrier females are usually asymptomatic.'),
('2ab2ca50-62bf-52aa-88f7-39a4d7b891a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the genetic difference between Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy?', '["Duchenne is dominant; Becker is recessive resulting in decreased functional activity","Duchenne involves upper limbs; Becker involves lower limbs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duchenne affects boys; Becker affects girls mediated by specific receptor activation","Duchenne has out-of-frame deletions (no dystrophin); Becker has in-frame deletions (some dystrophin)","Duchenne is autosomal; Becker is X-linked leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duchenne has out-of-frame deletions (no dystrophin); Becker has in-frame deletions (some dystrophin)', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy is caused by out-of-frame deletions that abolish dystrophin production, whereas Becker muscular dystrophy results from in-frame deletions that retain a partially functional protein. This explains the earlier onset and greater severity of Duchenne.'),
('f0d7e923-b5b6-5ad2-8816-4a99481c5587'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common presentation of Duchenne muscular dystrophy?', '["Respiratory failure at birth resulting in decreased functional activity","Proximal muscle weakness with Gower sign and calf pseudohypertrophy","Congenital hypotonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial muscle weakness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distal muscle weakness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal muscle weakness with Gower sign and calf pseudohypertrophy', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy typically presents in early childhood with proximal muscle weakness, a Gower sign when rising from the floor, and pseudohypertrophy of the calves. Facial and distal muscle weakness are not characteristic presenting features.'),
('32a507be-9e08-5fde-bbea-38572eaa0768'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Gowers sign?', '["Calf hypertrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Inability to lift arms above shoulders accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Using hands to push off the floor or thighs to stand (due to proximal weakness)","Waddling gait without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Weakness of the face leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Using hands to push off the floor or thighs to stand (due to proximal weakness)', 'Gowers sign is the use of the hands to climb up the thighs or push off the floor when rising, a compensatory maneuver for proximal hip and thigh weakness. It is classically seen in Duchenne and other proximal myopathies.'),
('f435ed44-ac7d-570b-b2fd-e69b8909e291'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is myotonic dystrophy?', '["A distal myopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","A congenital muscular dystrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An autosomal dominant disorder with myotonia muscle weakness and multisystem involvement","A limb-girdle dystrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A metabolic myopathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An autosomal dominant disorder with myotonia muscle weakness and multisystem involvement', 'Myotonic dystrophy is an autosomal dominant disorder with myotonia (delayed muscle relaxation), progressive weakness, and multisystem features including cataracts, cardiac conduction defects, frontal balding, and insulin resistance. It is not confined to a single limb region or to congenital myopathy.'),
('57c4b659-599b-5f83-9943-1ab02ac819ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the genetic basis of myotonic dystrophy type 1?', '["CTG trinucleotide repeat expansion in DMPK gene","GAA repeat expansion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GCG repeat expansion mediated by specific receptor activation","CCG repeat expansion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CAG repeat expansion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CTG trinucleotide repeat expansion in DMPK gene', 'Myotonic dystrophy type 1 is caused by an unstable CTG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the DMPK gene on chromosome 19, which expands across generations and produces anticipation. GAA repeats cause Friedreich ataxia, and CAG repeats cause Huntington disease and several spinocerebellar ataxias.'),
('0473be09-cd18-594d-890f-2ccbbb3492d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is myotonia congenita?', '["ACh receptor disorder resulting in decreased functional activity","Potassium channelopathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcium channelopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","A chloride channelopathy causing muscle stiffness and hypertrophy","Sodium channelopathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chloride channelopathy causing muscle stiffness and hypertrophy', 'Myotonia congenita is caused by mutations in the CLCN1 gene encoding a skeletal muscle chloride channel, producing muscle stiffness and hypertrophy that worsen with exertion. Defects of ACh receptors, sodium channels, or calcium channels cause different neuromuscular disorders.'),
('bfba689f-c178-57bd-af11-ef30e8e9b867'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common channelopathy affecting muscle?', '["Hypokalemic periodic paralysis (CACNA1S)","Paramyotonia congenita (SCN4A)","Hyperkalemic periodic paralysis (SCN4A)","Central core disease (RYR1)","Myotonia congenita (CLCN1)"]'::jsonb, 'Myotonia congenita (CLCN1)', 'Myotonia congenita, due to a CLCN1 chloride channel mutation, is the most common muscle channelopathy. The periodic paralyses, paramyotonia congenita, and central core disease are each less common channel disorders.'),
('aefcb1d6-7051-57d3-a9aa-4f90b2b54f6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common congenital myopathy?', '["Central core disease","Nemaline myopathy","Minicore disease","Fiber-type disproportion","Centronuclear (myotubular) myopathy"]'::jsonb, 'Central core disease', 'Central core disease is the most common congenital myopathy, presenting with hypotonia and proximal weakness and strongly linked to malignant hyperthermia. Nemaline and centronuclear myopathies are less frequent.'),
('228d9528-bf28-5947-8214-ca0c178ce03c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common cause of central core disease?', '["MTM1 mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","TNNT1 mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NEB mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","RYR1 mutation (ryanodine receptor)","ACTA1 mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'RYR1 mutation (ryanodine receptor)', 'Central core disease is most commonly caused by mutations in the RYR1 gene encoding the ryanodine receptor, which controls calcium release in skeletal muscle. The same RYR1 defect also underlies malignant hyperthermia susceptibility.'),
('bda87128-9063-5ba1-aa67-b6c4843335c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is malignant hyperthermia?', '["A cardiac disorder resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A life-threatening reaction to volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine causing hyperthermia muscle rigidity and rhabdomyolysis","A respiratory disorder mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A neurologic disorder without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A metabolic disorder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A life-threatening reaction to volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine causing hyperthermia muscle rigidity and rhabdomyolysis', 'Malignant hyperthermia is a life-threatening reaction to volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine that produces hyperthermia, severe muscle rigidity, rhabdomyolysis, and acidosis. It results from uncontrolled calcium release, most often due to RYR1 mutations.'),
('abf6bd2e-4521-5ca1-be7c-782854577a28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva (FOP)?', '["A genetic disorder where soft connective tissue progressively ossifies forming a second skeleton","Osteogenesis imperfecta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteoporosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Osteopetrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrous dysplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A genetic disorder where soft connective tissue progressively ossifies forming a second skeleton', 'Fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva is a genetic disorder in which soft connective tissues (muscle, fascia, tendons) progressively ossify, eventually restricting movement and forming a second skeleton. Osteoporosis, osteopetrosis, and fibrous dysplasia do not produce progressive heterotopic bone formation.'),
('b2b1a7c2-3c3d-5a4f-8de0-121ac7a1290a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What gene is mutated in fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva?', '["GSK3B resulting in decreased functional activity","ACVR1 (activin receptor type I)","SMAD4 mediated by specific receptor activation","BMPR1A without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NOGGIN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ACVR1 (activin receptor type I)', 'FOP is caused by activating mutations in the ACVR1 gene encoding the activin receptor type I, which drives abnormal endochondral ossification within soft tissue. The other listed genes participate in BMP signaling but are not the cause of FOP.'),
('a029abda-cbc3-5c8a-aab9-22c9e109311b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is most common type of congenital hand anomaly overall?', '["Polydactyly","Camptodactyly","Macrodactyly","Clinodactyly","Syndactyly"]'::jsonb, 'Polydactyly', 'Polydactyly, the presence of supernumerary digits, is the most common congenital hand anomaly overall. Syndactyly is also common, whereas camptodactyly, clinodactyly, and macrodactyly occur less frequently.'),
('92556215-1187-57bd-974c-5853b71bc3f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Postaxial polydactyly (extra digit on ulnar/fibular side) is most common in which population?', '["Asian descent","European descent","Native American","Hispanic","African descent"]'::jsonb, 'African descent', 'Postaxial polydactyly, an extra digit on the ulnar or fibular side, is most common in individuals of African descent. Preaxial polydactyly shows a different ethnic distribution.'),
('8a88c26c-e008-5dfc-a693-a2276053d64b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Preaxial polydactyly (extra thumb/big toe) is most common in which population?', '["Asian and Native American","European","Hispanic","Middle Eastern","African"]'::jsonb, 'Asian and Native American', 'Preaxial polydactyly, an extra thumb or great toe, is most common in individuals of Asian and Native American descent. Postaxial polydactyly predominates in African populations.'),
('111b1987-38aa-5a97-909d-9849ee1af928'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of syndactyly?', '["Incomplete syndactyly resulting in decreased functional activity","Complete syndactyly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple syndactyly (skin only between third and fourth fingers)","Syndactyly involving thumb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complex syndactyly (bone involvement) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple syndactyly (skin only between third and fourth fingers)', 'Simple syndactyly, in which only skin bridges the digits, most commonly involves the web space between the third and fourth fingers. Complete, complex (with bone involvement), and thumb involvement are less common forms.'),
('72596acc-561e-5262-aee2-859c5f34852d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is ectrodactyly (split hand/foot malformation)?', '["Cleft foot only resulting in decreased functional activity","Absence of all digits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fusion of all digits mediated by specific receptor activation","Absence of central digits with a deep V-shaped cleft","Extra digits leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absence of central digits with a deep V-shaped cleft', 'Ectrodactyly (split hand/foot malformation) is characterized by absence of central digits with a deep V-shaped cleft that splits the hand or foot, producing a lobster-claw appearance. Fusion, extra digits, and absence of all digits are different anomalies.'),
('469175c3-29b4-53f2-bfe4-e3be9f92074c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is brachydactyly?', '["Absent digits","Extra digits","Short digits","Fused digits","Webbed digits"]'::jsonb, 'Short digits', 'Brachydactyly means abnormally short digits resulting from shortened phalanges or metacarpals. Absent, extra, fused, and webbed digits each have their own descriptive terms (aplasia, polydactyly, syndactyly).'),
('38bb75ba-52df-523f-924c-b023c9347d48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is macrodactyly?', '["Shortening of digits","Fusion of digits","Absence of digits","Enlargement of one or more digits","Extra digits"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of one or more digits', 'Macrodactyly is congenital enlargement of one or more digits, often due to lipofibromatous overgrowth or neurofibromatosis. Shortening, fusion, absence, and extra digits are distinct anomalies.'),
('aab110c3-dca4-5508-9b99-2dc07fa1d52e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is a constriction ring (amniotic band)?', '["An infection resulting in decreased functional activity","A tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A band of amniotic tissue causing constriction of a fetal limb or digit","A fracture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A vascular malformation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A band of amniotic tissue causing constriction of a fetal limb or digit', 'A constriction ring (amniotic band) is a band of amniotic tissue that encircles and constricts a fetal limb or digit, potentially causing distal lymphedema or autoamputation. It is a disruptive process, not an infection, tumor, fracture, or vascular malformation.'),
('4dc2f772-691c-54f3-92e7-f6335e288ad9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common lethal skeletal dysplasia?', '["Achondroplasia","Osteogenesis imperfecta type II","Thanatophoric dysplasia","Type II OI","Hypochondrogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Thanatophoric dysplasia', 'Thanatophoric dysplasia, an FGFR3-related disorder, is the most common lethal skeletal dysplasia, with severe micromelia and a narrow thorax causing respiratory failure at birth. Achondroplasia is non-lethal, and OI type II is a less common lethal condition.'),
('827b589f-9cb6-5701-9a5f-22aad10cd356'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is hypochondroplasia?', '["A normal variant resulting in decreased functional activity","The lethal form of dwarfism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Short trunk dwarfism mediated by specific receptor activation","A milder form of short-limb dwarfism similar to achondroplasia but less severe","Asymmetric dwarfism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A milder form of short-limb dwarfism similar to achondroplasia but less severe', 'Hypochondroplasia is a milder form of short-limb dwarfism closely related to achondroplasia, with less severe limb shortening, a normal face, and generally preserved stature. It is non-lethal and is not a short-trunk dwarfism.'),
('60a7f2e9-892d-5221-8138-fd5f63be4096'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common non-lethal skeletal dysplasia after achondroplasia?', '["Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia","Hypochondroplasia","Pseudoachondroplasia","Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia","Diastrophic dysplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypochondroplasia', 'Hypochondroplasia, also often caused by FGFR3 mutations, is the most common non-lethal skeletal dysplasia after achondroplasia and is milder than achondroplasia. The epiphyseal and spondyloepiphyseal dysplasias are less frequent.'),
('b33dbbad-7bb9-568f-9a6d-5830ce004f92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is pseudoachondroplasia?', '["A less severe achondroplasia resulting in decreased functional activity","A short-limb dwarfism with normal facial features due to COMP mutation","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Diastrophic dysplasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A type of spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A short-limb dwarfism with normal facial features due to COMP mutation', 'Pseudoachondroplasia is a short-limb dwarfism with normal facial features, caused by mutations in the COMP gene. Unlike achondroplasia, the face and skull are spared, and it is classified with the multiple epiphyseal dysplasias.'),
('3fd1395d-8377-58c8-a173-6ecd8b288aba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia (SED)?', '["A group affecting only the long bones resulting in decreased functional activity","A group affecting only the skull accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A storage disease mediated by specific receptor activation","A metabolic bone disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A group of disorders primarily affecting the vertebrae and epiphyses causing short trunk dwarfism"]'::jsonb, 'A group of disorders primarily affecting the vertebrae and epiphyses causing short trunk dwarfism', 'Spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia is a group of disorders that affect the vertebrae and epiphyses, producing short-trunk dwarfism with a flattened vertebral column and abnormal epiphyseal ossification. Disorders limited to the skull or long bones are classified separately.'),
('24ec694d-45e9-54d3-ab5f-1be944d297d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia?', '["Pseudoachondroplasia","SED congenita (COL2A1)","Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia","SED tarda (X-linked)","SED with metaphyseal involvement"]'::jsonb, 'SED congenita (COL2A1)', 'SED congenita, caused by mutations in the COL2A1 gene encoding type II collagen, is the most common form of spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia and is manifest at birth. Pseudoachondroplasia, SED tarda, and the other listed forms are less common.'),
('ffeb64f9-a103-5e5e-ad99-1a56645b05e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is multiple epiphyseal dysplasia (MED)?', '["A spinal disorder resulting in decreased functional activity","A skull disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A metaphyseal disorder mediated by specific receptor activation","A disorder with irregular epiphyseal ossification leading to early osteoarthritis","A diaphyseal disorder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A disorder with irregular epiphyseal ossification leading to early osteoarthritis', 'Multiple epiphyseal dysplasia is characterized by irregular epiphyseal ossification that leads to early osteoarthritis, typically affecting the hips and knees with joint pain and stiffness. The vertebrae, metaphyses, and diaphyses are not the primary sites.'),
('fc57c6c8-8cae-52c2-97ef-de66c1753576'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is metaphyseal dysplasia?', '["A group affecting the epiphyses resulting in decreased functional activity","A group affecting the diaphysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A group affecting the skull mediated by specific receptor activation","A group of disorders with abnormal metaphyseal ossification leading to short stature and bowing","A metabolic bone disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A group of disorders with abnormal metaphyseal ossification leading to short stature and bowing', 'Metaphyseal dysplasias are a group of disorders with abnormal ossification of the metaphyses, producing short stature and bowing of the long bones. The epiphyses, diaphyses, and skull are not the primary sites of involvement.'),
('1a984f24-b3ed-5795-8ccc-1347875c06e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is osteogenesis imperfecta type I characterized by?', '["Lethal in perinatal period","Hyperplastic callus formation","Blue sclera hearing loss mild bone fragility","Moderate severity with normal sclera","Blue sclera severe bone deformity"]'::jsonb, 'Blue sclera hearing loss mild bone fragility', 'Osteogenesis imperfecta type I, the mildest form, is characterized by blue sclerae, hearing loss, and mild bone fragility, with generally normal stature. It results from reduced production of structurally normal type I collagen.'),
('0dfae153-a314-5f01-9605-0453b520ecec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What distinguishes OI type III (severe progressive deforming) from type I?', '["Type III has blue sclera like Type I resulting in decreased functional activity","Type III is milder than Type I accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type III is lethal like Type II mediated by specific receptor activation","Type III has severe bone deformity and white sclera; Type I has blue sclera and mild fragility","Type III has hearing loss only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type III has severe bone deformity and white sclera; Type I has blue sclera and mild fragility', 'OI type III produces severe progressive bone deformity with short stature and normal (white) sclerae, whereas type I has blue sclerae and only mild bone fragility. This difference in scleral color and severity is the key distinction between the two forms.'),
('6a9e6641-6fa4-5616-a50a-eaaf56ba9839'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is dentinogenesis imperfecta?', '["Periodontal disease resulting in decreased functional activity","A disorder of enamel formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gingival disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Tooth decay without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A disorder of dentin formation causing discolored brittle teeth often associated with OI"]'::jsonb, 'A disorder of dentin formation causing discolored brittle teeth often associated with OI', 'Dentinogenesis imperfecta is a disorder of dentin formation causing discolored (blue-gray or amber), translucent, brittle teeth, and is frequently associated with osteogenesis imperfecta. Enamel formation, the periodontium, and caries are not the primary defects.'),
('24e6c043-37d7-5558-800a-7f88ac55ed1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common form of skeletal dysplasia?', '["SED","Hypochondroplasia","Osteogenesis imperfecta","Thanatophoric dysplasia","Achondroplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Achondroplasia', 'Achondroplasia, caused by an activating FGFR3 mutation, is the most common skeletal dysplasia, with an incidence of about 1 in 25,000 live births. It features rhizomelic short limbs, a large head, and frontal bossing.'),
('e12ebf73-1bd4-5a3d-ba2b-11049300e2b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the incidence of achondroplasia?', '["000 live births","000","About 1 in 1000","About 1 in 25","About 1 in 10"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 in 25', 'Achondroplasia occurs at an incidence of roughly 1 in 25,000 live births, making it the most common skeletal dysplasia. The other ratios listed are incorrect.'),
('74583195-268e-5b5c-be7a-a114c3f1cabb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What are the facial features of achondroplasia?', '["Hypertelorism with broad nose resulting in decreased functional activity","Cleft lip and palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Frontal bossing with prominent nose mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal bossing midface hypoplasia depressed nasal bridge","Micrognathia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal bossing midface hypoplasia depressed nasal bridge', 'The characteristic facial features of achondroplasia are frontal bossing, midface hypoplasia, and a depressed nasal bridge, accompanied by rhizomelic short limbs and a trident hand. Cleft lip, micrognathia, and a prominent nose are not typical.'),
('47e734b3-560d-512a-ad06-4450c3a83b39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What neurologic complication is common in achondroplasia?', '["Foramen magnum stenosis and hydrocephalus","Cortical blindness","Peripheral neuropathy","Seizures","Intracranial hemorrhage"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen magnum stenosis and hydrocephalus', 'Achondroplasia narrows the foramen magnum and spinal canal, which can compress the brainstem and upper cervical cord causing apnea, and can obstruct CSF flow producing hydrocephalus. Peripheral neuropathy, seizures, and intracranial hemorrhage are not the characteristic complications.'),
('06424a38-8d2e-52e1-ba40-b88087f8853d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common cause of death in achondroplasia in infancy?', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Respiratory failure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brainstem compression due to foramen magnum stenosis","Tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac failure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brainstem compression due to foramen magnum stenosis', 'Achondroplasia results from an FGFR3 mutation that impairs endochondral ossification, narrowing the foramen magnum so the medulla/brainstem is compressed and can cause apnea and sudden death in infancy. Infection, respiratory failure, tumor and cardiac failure are not the characteristic lethal mechanism.'),
('e80b893e-b7f3-5931-9005-60e2db50fcb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is thanatophoric dysplasia type I characterized by?', '["Normal skull with bowed femurs resulting in decreased functional activity","Short ribs only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Micromelia with curved femurs (telephone receiver) and narrow thorax","Micromelia with straight femurs and cloverleaf skull without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Severe hydrops leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Micromelia with curved femurs (telephone receiver) and narrow thorax', 'Thanatophoric dysplasia type I causes severe micromelia with characteristically curved, telephone-receiver femurs and a narrow, small thorax that is usually fatal in the newborn. Straight femurs with a cloverleaf skull are the signature of type II.'),
('4f239b50-51fe-5282-9f95-33056ef036c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is thanatophoric dysplasia type II characterized by?', '["Micromelia with straight femurs","Straight femurs with cloverleaf skull (Kleeblattschädel)","Curved femurs with hydrocephalus","Curved femurs with cloverleaf skull","Normal femurs with platyspondyly"]'::jsonb, 'Straight femurs with cloverleaf skull (Kleeblattschädel)', 'Type II thanatophoric dysplasia is distinguished by straight, non-bowed femurs together with a cloverleaf skull (Kleeblattschädel). Curved femurs and hydrocephalus are not features of type II.'),
('dc84f939-ee08-5cbd-8d82-e07f6206f5da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common congenital hand anomaly associated with chromosomal abnormalities?', '["Polydactyly resulting in decreased functional activity","Syndactyly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrodactyly mediated by specific receptor activation","Ectrodactyly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clinodactyly of the fifth finger"]'::jsonb, 'Clinodactyly of the fifth finger', 'Clinodactyly of the fifth finger (with a hypoplastic middle phalanx) is the most frequent congenital hand finding in children with chromosomal abnormalities. Polydactyly, syndactyly and ectrodactyly are recognized hand malformations but are less consistently linked to chromosomal syndromes.'),
('0f34de24-8a70-523a-9fbd-b3df1fb4781d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the characteristic hand finding in Down syndrome?', '["Macrodactyly resulting in decreased functional activity","Syndactyly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Polydactyly mediated by specific receptor activation","Thumb hypoplasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simian crease (single transverse palmar crease) and clinodactyly"]'::jsonb, 'Simian crease (single transverse palmar crease) and clinodactyly', 'Down syndrome typically shows a single transverse palmar crease (simian crease) and clinodactyly of the fifth finger, plus an increased gap between the first and second toes. Macrodactyly, polydactyly and thumb hypoplasia are not characteristic findings.'),
('63ed3f0d-4fd4-5dc6-ae68-a67fe457e32a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the characteristic hand finding in Turner syndrome?', '["Clinodactyly","Polydactyly","Macrodactyly","Syndactyly","Short fourth metacarpal"]'::jsonb, 'Short fourth metacarpal', 'Turner syndrome characteristically produces a shortened fourth metacarpal, giving the brachymetacarpia sign (metacarpal sign), because of occult skeletal dyschondrosteosis. Clinodactyly, polydactyly and syndactyly are not hallmarks of this condition.'),
('2e1f6d0a-7590-5962-b6bf-db59cb63ae12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Madelung deformity?', '["Abnormal curvature of the radius with dorsal dislocation of the ulnar head","Genu valgum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clubfoot mediated by specific receptor activation","Coxa vara without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hallux valgus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal curvature of the radius with dorsal dislocation of the ulnar head', 'Madelung deformity is a bowing (curvature) of the distal radial physis so the radius curves, allowing the ulnar head to dislocate dorsally and producing a bayonet wrist. The other options describe knee, foot and hip deformities, not the wrist.'),
('aac5f8e6-8bd9-5fb6-a907-4a594e4fe499'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Madelung deformity is associated with which conditions?', '["Osteogenesis imperfecta resulting in decreased functional activity","Turner syndrome dyschondrosteosis (SHOX mutation) and Leri-Weill syndrome","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Down syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Marfan syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Turner syndrome dyschondrosteosis (SHOX mutation) and Leri-Weill syndrome', 'Madelung deformity appears in Turner syndrome and in dyschondrosteosis/Leri-Weill syndrome, both caused by SHOX gene haploinsufficiency that impairs radial growth. Osteogenesis imperfecta, Ehlers-Danlos, Down and Marfan syndromes do not typically produce this deformity.'),
('9c870241-aed8-5308-b4d2-8383e645e21d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common cause of genu varum (bowlegs) in children?', '["Fracture","Physiologic genu varum","Rickets","Blount disease","Infection"]'::jsonb, 'Physiologic genu varum', 'Physiologic genu varum is the most common cause of bowlegs, appearing as normal symmetric bowing that resolves by about age 2-3 years. Rickets, Blount disease and infection cause pathologic, often asymmetric bowing.'),
('420c420d-d6df-5a13-bdca-6ac7a0357454'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Blount disease?', '["Arthritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rickets due to vitamin D deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","A pathologic genu varum due to abnormal growth of the proximal tibial metaphysis","A normal variant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A pathologic genu varum due to abnormal growth of the proximal tibial metaphysis', 'Blount disease is a pathologic genu varum produced by disordered growth (often medial tibial physeal arrest) of the proximal tibial metaphysis in young, frequently obese children. It is distinct from physiologic bowing, rickets and arthritis.'),
('4645faca-55ed-5863-9f82-25d042acb50b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common cause of genu valgum (knock-knees) in children?', '["Blount disease","Arthritis","Physiologic genu valgum","Trauma","Renal rickets"]'::jsonb, 'Physiologic genu valgum', 'Physiologic genu valgum is the most common cause of knock-knees in children, peaking around age 4 and resolving spontaneously. Blount disease, trauma and renal rickets give pathologic, usually asymmetric valgus.'),
('49014f0c-9a49-5930-894b-1a600ea46d16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is coxa vara?', '["Femoral neck fracture resulting in decreased functional activity","Acetabular dysplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased neck-shaft angle of the femur mediated by specific receptor activation","Hip dislocation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased neck-shaft angle of the femur (<120 degrees)"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased neck-shaft angle of the femur (<120 degrees)', 'Coxa vara means the femoral neck-shaft angle falls below the normal 120-135 degrees, so beneath 120 degrees is diagnostic. An increased angle beyond 140 degrees defines coxa valga instead.'),
('0d773d23-0b81-5870-adde-5a2a1de217da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is coxa valga?', '["Femoral neck fracture resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased neck-shaft angle of the femur (>140 degrees)","Hip dislocation mediated by specific receptor activation","Acetabular dysplasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased neck-shaft angle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased neck-shaft angle of the femur (>140 degrees)', 'Coxa valga is an increased femoral neck-shaft angle (above about 140 degrees), often associated with hip subluxation or weak abductors. A decreased angle is coxa vara, and the other choices are trauma or dysplastic lesions.'),
('a5fbedd3-a74b-58c3-b8fd-3a75c4f200b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease?', '["Idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children","Hip dislocation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Septic arthritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Transient synovitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Avascular necrosis in adults leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children', 'Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease is idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head in children, typically aged 4-8 years, from interrupted blood supply. Transient synovitis, septic arthritis and hip dislocation are separate diagnoses.'),
('a9bb5d1c-b0b0-50bd-acdb-0fe8f1172850'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE)?', '["Hip dislocation resulting in decreased functional activity","Acetabular fracture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral neck fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","Displacement of the femoral head relative to the neck through the physis","Avascular necrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Displacement of the femoral head relative to the neck through the physis', 'SCFE is displacement of the proximal femoral epiphysis, and hence the femoral head, relative to the femoral neck through the physis, typically during the adolescent growth spurt. Hip dislocation, neck fracture and acetabular fracture are different pathologies.'),
('5f206091-a715-5526-aff3-59870e8b1871'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common age for SCFE?', '["5-10 years resulting in decreased functional activity","20-30 years accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","10-16 years (early adolescence)","30-40 years without affecting the primary tissue pathway","16-20 years leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10-16 years (early adolescence)', 'SCFE typically presents in early adolescence (10-16 years), often in overweight children, when physeal widening and hormonal changes weaken the growth plate. The other age ranges do not match this classic window.'),
('683b5010-ebde-5f5a-89ad-06066483db37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the Cobb angle used for?', '["Measuring the degree of spinal curvature in scoliosis/kyphosis","Measuring foot deformity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measuring leg length discrepancy mediated by specific receptor activation","Measuring shoulder asymmetry without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Measuring hip dysplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measuring the degree of spinal curvature in scoliosis/kyphosis', 'The Cobb angle is the standard radiographic measure of spinal curvature and is used to classify and follow scoliosis and kyphosis. It is not used to measure foot, leg-length or hip conditions.'),
('d65488c4-99eb-5ba6-af29-764c62faf7a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the Risser grade?', '["A measure of scoliosis severity resulting in decreased functional activity","A measure of skeletal maturity based on iliac crest ossification","A measure of hip development mediated by specific receptor activation","A measure of foot deformity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A bone density score leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A measure of skeletal maturity based on iliac crest ossification', 'The Risser grade (0-5) scores skeletal maturity by the progressive ossification of the iliac crest apophysis, which travels from lateral to medial. It guides decisions such as bracing rather than measuring curve magnitude.'),
('50c331cd-57e9-53ad-8366-d1857ec537de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common type of scoliosis?', '["Idiopathic scoliosis (adolescent)","Degenerative scoliosis","Congenital scoliosis","Syndromic scoliosis","Neuromuscular scoliosis"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic scoliosis (adolescent)', 'Adolescent idiopathic scoliosis, with no identifiable cause, is by far the most common type of scoliosis. Congenital, syndromic, neuromuscular and degenerative forms are all much rarer.'),
('3cf9ce04-3f05-5d06-855f-456cec5e6139'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the Lenke classification used for?', '["Classifying spinal tumors","Classifying congenital scoliosis","Classifying adolescent idiopathic scoliosis","Classifying spondylolisthesis","Classifying kyphosis"]'::jsonb, 'Classifying adolescent idiopathic scoliosis', 'The Lenke classification classifies adolescent idiopathic scoliosis by curve type, lumbar spine modifier and sagittal thoracic modifier, and is used to plan surgery. It is not applied to congenital curves, kyphosis, spondylolisthesis or tumors.'),
('b9328aa2-680d-5917-9b91-6ce6c857fa72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the King classification used for?', '["Classifying spondylolysis","Classifying spinal fractures","Classifying kyphosis","Classifying congenital scoliosis","Classifying thoracic idiopathic scoliosis (historical)"]'::jsonb, 'Classifying thoracic idiopathic scoliosis (historical)', 'The King classification was a historical system for classifying thoracic idiopathic scoliosis that guided fusion levels, and it has been largely replaced by the Lenke classification. Spondylolysis, fractures and kyphosis have separate systems.'),
('d7e24dd7-7d08-5d51-bb36-90bebb07ea7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common form of congenital spinal deformity?', '["Congenital scoliosis","Congenital lordosis","Congenital kyphosis","Spondylolisthesis","Scheuermann disease"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital scoliosis', 'Congenital scoliosis, caused by vertebral anomalies such as failures of formation or segmentation, is the most common form of congenital spinal deformity. Congenital kyphosis and lordosis are far less frequent.'),
('d24c0be1-0bd7-5584-9fd8-3837d30d6bef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common vertebral anomaly causing congenital kyphosis?', '["Anterior failure of segmentation (Type II) resulting in decreased functional activity","Mixed types accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hemivertebra mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior failure of vertebral body formation (Type I)","Butterfly vertebra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior failure of vertebral body formation (Type I)', 'Congenital kyphosis is most often caused by an anterior failure of vertebral body formation (Type I), which leaves an anterior gap and produces angular kyphosis. Failure of segmentation and hemivertebrae are less common causes.'),
('0be9a295-08d8-5544-8e86-f92f6218c0b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common neurologic deficit associated with congenital kyphosis?', '["Monoplegia resulting in decreased functional activity","Cauda equina syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paraplegia from spinal cord compression","Neurogenic bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Quadriplegia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Paraplegia from spinal cord compression', 'Congenital kyphosis most often causes paraplegia because the sharp anterior angulation of the spinal canal compresses the spinal cord. Quadriplegia, monoplegia and neurogenic bladder are not the classic neurologic consequence.'),
('1b3ee5db-9b7a-5eac-a54c-d8dac796ed66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the most common surgical intervention for pediatric scoliosis?', '["Posterior spinal fusion with instrumentation","Anterior spinal fusion","VEPTR (expandable rib device)","Growing rods without fusion","Spinal osteotomy"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior spinal fusion with instrumentation', 'Posterior spinal fusion with instrumentation is the most commonly performed surgical procedure for pediatric scoliosis. Growing rods, VEPTR and osteotomy are reserved for special cases, and isolated anterior fusion is less common.'),
('163b82fe-2045-5487-a91b-f4d442d3e4df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The notochord induces the overlying ectoderm to thicken and form which structure?', '["Mesoderm","Somites","Neural plate","Surface ectoderm","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Neural plate', 'The notochord induces the overlying ectoderm to thicken into the neural plate, which subsequently folds into the neural tube. Mesoderm, somites and endoderm are formed by different processes.'),
('b4368494-40be-5a4d-b685-61185ede8722'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('7977f3e7-2162-5858-8f71-8d01c9be35dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('e5d96200-a2f9-58e6-a154-54d888b17862'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'The dermis of the back is derived from which structure?', '["Somite","Dermatome","Myotome","Sclerotome","Syndetome"]'::jsonb, 'Dermatome', 'The dermis of the back is derived from the dermatome, the dorsolateral portion of each somite. The sclerotome forms the axial skeleton and the myotome forms the muscles.'),
('e9d01e9c-a72e-59f8-b110-036b42ca09b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('4b581c74-1d1f-537e-8d7c-cb9a723cf7f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('ba90e933-4746-52da-b62a-abacba8fca23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('1bb1d08d-9bbc-5566-8e2d-cb7276d55c12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('2f68e7ca-53e5-5652-ab89-cf3280b022f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('70675cd2-3770-53c9-99cb-2868850beaa6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('d5b7de95-a059-53c8-80bd-1ab88986c5c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('47de04ab-ff67-514b-acb1-577e0ff5f43b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('2abca9e7-c24d-5833-8442-2d1eb0bb8e27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('cfe96294-b7ad-5ef4-8a93-ccbb253c30d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('da7fe27e-f628-5519-8776-ca2a4069230b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('07efdd56-9028-5e9b-94db-f0b23d315f5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('821718f8-4d86-5f6a-9c5f-a794edd45152'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('ce6b04f6-21ac-5390-8d62-03857ae6785f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('d1e24b96-0b97-53af-9465-824ddc634f46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('96d16842-3b09-5713-83e3-c4454ece366f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('30326d39-c214-53e0-8d43-576d4e5dd1f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('11fba53c-9620-568e-9deb-08b019ec7013'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('db05be98-e917-5a92-ad22-ed2930097787'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('81c3b9c4-eea0-5534-8469-2e7cd6d333c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('5e2da777-1709-58fc-8a19-1b9416fe3f61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Musculoskeletal System', 'Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('dad63083-1815-5bf7-9f9a-bce4b10b5a27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The lung bud appears at which week of development?', '["Week 5","Week 4 (day 26-28)","Week 3","Week 6","Week 7"]'::jsonb, 'Week 4 (day 26-28)', 'The respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) first appears at the end of week 4 (day 26-28) as a ventral outgrowth of the foregut. The later weeks in the other options are beyond this initial appearance.'),
('105a8a41-c479-5384-af56-63f0d83955ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The lung bud arises from which part of the foregut?', '["Dorsal wall of the foregut resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral wall of the foregut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventral wall of the foregut (just caudal to the pharynx)","The pharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral wall of the foregut (just caudal to the pharynx)', 'The lung bud grows out from the ventral wall of the foregut just caudal to the pharyngeal region. The dorsal foregut wall later forms the esophagus, so the other options are not its origin.'),
('6b98fcc5-9f09-50c9-a46b-0294e1162641'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What divides the foregut into the trachea and esophagus?', '["The cricoid cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchial buds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","The pharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tracheoesophageal septum (formed by tracheoesophageal folds)"]'::jsonb, 'Tracheoesophageal septum (formed by tracheoesophageal folds)', 'The tracheoesophageal folds fuse in the midline to form the tracheoesophageal septum, which separates the ventral respiratory tube from the dorsal esophagus. The other listed structures do not produce this division.'),
('fdc38d76-0345-585b-8e5c-be164e7e1090'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'Failure of the tracheoesophageal septum to form causes which condition?', '["Bronchial stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Laryngeal atresia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary sequestration mediated by specific receptor activation","Diaphragmatic hernia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tracheoesophageal fistula (with or without esophageal atresia)"]'::jsonb, 'Tracheoesophageal fistula (with or without esophageal atresia)', 'Incomplete or failed development of the tracheoesophageal septum leaves a persistent connection between the trachea and esophagus, producing tracheoesophageal fistula with or without esophageal atresia. The other conditions have unrelated pathogeneses.'),
('2609ff67-222b-59a3-b1b2-3fb80372ca9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The larynx develops from which structures?', '["Sixth arch only","Fourth arch only","First and second arches","Fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches","Third arch"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches', 'The larynx is built from the fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches: the thyroid cartilage and most of the epiglottis come partly from the fourth arch, while the cricoid and arytenoid cartilages derive from the sixth arch. Arches 1-3 contribute to other facial structures.'),
('3b8ecca1-605e-52f3-bd96-a27a837b8f78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The epiglottis develops from which structure?', '["Sixth arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Second arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Third arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypobranchial eminence (from the fourth arch)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypobranchial eminence (from the fourth arch)', 'The epiglottis derives from the hypobranchial eminence, which develops in the region of the third and fourth arches. It does not arise from a single arch cartilage as in the other choices.'),
('fc179b92-4acb-54ec-a100-339cf8b9dc06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The laryngeal cartilages develop from which arch cartilages?', '["First and second arch cartilages","Third arch cartilage","Fourth and sixth arch cartilages","Only the sixth arch","Only the fourth arch"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth and sixth arch cartilages', 'The laryngeal cartilages arise from the fourth and sixth arch cartilages: the thyroid from the fourth arch, and the cricoid and arytenoids from the sixth arch. Arches 1-3 instead form the malleus, incus, and hyoid elements.'),
('8cd338d3-00ef-59b4-8384-d66bc0d5bf86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is laryngeal atresia?', '["Absent larynx resulting in decreased functional activity","Congenital obstruction of the laryngeal airway (glottic web or atresia) often causing fetal hydrops","Double larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","Laryngeal cleft without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laryngomalacia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital obstruction of the laryngeal airway (glottic web or atresia) often causing fetal hydrops', 'Laryngeal atresia is a congenital obstruction of the laryngeal airway, such as a glottic web or complete atresia, which traps lung fluid and can cause polyhydramnios and fetal hydrops. It differs from an absent larynx, a laryngeal cleft, or laryngomalacia.'),
('d777dcac-de87-587c-97de-62a0fcb2f4cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is laryngomalacia?', '["Infection of the larynx resulting in decreased functional activity","A congenital softening of the laryngeal cartilage causing inspiratory stridor in infants","A tumor of the larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","A complete obstruction of the larynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Paralysis of the vocal cords leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital softening of the laryngeal cartilage causing inspiratory stridor in infants', 'Laryngomalacia is a congenital softening of the laryngeal cartilage in which the floppy supraglottic tissue collapses inward during inspiration, producing stridor in infants that often worsens with feeding or crying. It is not infectious, neoplastic, obstructive, or paralytic.'),
('55f546e8-74bb-56a5-9316-fd4dd5a00798'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common congenital laryngeal anomaly?', '["Laryngeal web (glottic web)","Laryngomalacia","Laryngeal cleft","Laryngeal atresia","Vocal cord paralysis"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngomalacia', 'Laryngomalacia is the most common congenital laryngeal anomaly and the leading cause of inspiratory stridor in newborns. Webs, clefts, atresia, and vocal cord paralysis are all less frequent.'),
('04e33b85-22bd-5c57-9bc6-46920e9b3e65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The trachea develops from which structure?', '["The caudal part of the respiratory diverticulum (just below the larynx)","The larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pharynx mediated by specific receptor activation","The bronchi without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lung buds leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The caudal part of the respiratory diverticulum (just below the larynx)', 'The trachea forms from the caudal portion of the respiratory diverticulum immediately below the developing larynx. The lung buds and bronchi arise from the distal end of this tube.'),
('3238415b-8605-569b-8bf9-f08cf9364a8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The primary bronchi develop at which week?', '["Week 7 resulting in decreased functional activity","Week 5 (as the lung buds branch)","Week 6 mediated by specific receptor activation","Week 8 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Week 4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Week 5 (as the lung buds branch)', 'Around week 5 the lung buds branch to form the right and left primary bronchi, the right being wider and more vertical. Earlier or later weeks do not mark this branching event.'),
('7537e133-a951-56a7-a53e-caad7c7aef36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The right primary bronchus is wider shorter and more vertical than the left. This explains why:', '["Tuberculosis is more common on the right","Pneumonia is more common on the left","Foreign body aspiration is more common on the left","Both sides are equally affected","Foreign body aspiration is more common on the right"]'::jsonb, 'Foreign body aspiration is more common on the right', 'Because the right main bronchus is wider, shorter, and more vertical than the left, inhaled foreign bodies preferentially follow it into the right lung. This anatomic difference explains a right-sided predilection for aspiration.'),
('17797484-8055-5676-a8f6-6f59699a4f47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The bronchial tree develops by which process?', '["Simple growth without branching resulting in decreased functional activity","Budding from the esophagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Budding from the stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","Fusion of multiple buds without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Repeated branching of the bronchial buds (dichotomous branching)"]'::jsonb, 'Repeated branching of the bronchial buds (dichotomous branching)', 'The bronchial tree is built by repeated dichotomous branching of the bronchial buds, arborizing into segmental bronchi and smaller airways. It neither grows as a single unbranched tube nor buds from the esophagus or stomach.'),
('3596fb80-c4fe-50c1-91ab-53d8f213980e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The tracheal cartilage is derived from which structure?', '["Ectoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Endoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Notochord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesoderm (splanchnic mesoderm surrounding the trachea)"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoderm (splanchnic mesoderm surrounding the trachea)', 'The tracheal (and bronchial) cartilages are formed from splanchnic mesoderm that surrounds the respiratory tube. The endodermal lining forms the epithelium, while ectoderm, notochord, and neural crest are not the source.'),
('0bc6262f-9d9e-53d6-841d-bed3ada4baa5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is tracheal stenosis?', '["Congenital narrowing of the trachea (often associated with complete tracheal rings)","Tracheoesophageal fistula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tracheomalacia mediated by specific receptor activation","Absence of the trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laryngomalacia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital narrowing of the trachea (often associated with complete tracheal rings)', 'Tracheal stenosis is a congenital narrowing of the tracheal lumen, frequently caused by complete (O-shaped) tracheal rings instead of the normal C-shaped rings. Fistula, malacia, laryngeal softening, and agenesis are distinct tracheal pathologies.'),
('4204b05d-a209-5a4f-8f79-a9fe80775664'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheal stenosis in children?', '["Post-intubation stenosis","Complete tracheal rings (congenital)","Trauma","Vascular ring compression","Tracheoesophageal fistula repair complication"]'::jsonb, 'Complete tracheal rings (congenital)', 'In children, complete tracheal rings are the most common cause of congenital tracheal stenosis. Acquired causes such as post-intubation injury, trauma, vascular compression, or postoperative scarring occur less often in pediatric patients.'),
('ceb78cbc-a248-5f2b-abac-16b75b48abea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is tracheomalacia?', '["Softening of the tracheal cartilage leading to dynamic airway collapse during exhalation","Complete tracheal rings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tracheal stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Tracheal tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tracheoesophageal fistula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Softening of the tracheal cartilage leading to dynamic airway collapse during exhalation', 'Tracheomalacia is a softening of the tracheal cartilages that lets the airway walls collapse during expiration, producing barking cough and expiratory stridor. It is the opposite of the rigid narrowing seen in tracheal stenosis.'),
('3170b211-5aa3-50fe-8dcf-b922ab91d77f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheomalacia in infants?', '["Primary tracheomalacia (congenital weakness of the tracheal wall)","Pulmonary artery sling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mediastinal mass mediated by specific receptor activation","Vascular ring without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Esophageal atresia repair leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary tracheomalacia (congenital weakness of the tracheal wall)', 'In infants, primary tracheomalacia from congenital weakness of the tracheal wall is the most common cause. Secondary causes such as vascular rings, pulmonary artery sling, mediastinal masses, or post-surgical scarring are less frequent.'),
('bc8fef76-cede-585a-9008-5c43b79bbf43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The lungs develop through which stages in order?', '["Pseudoglandular embryonic canalicular saccular alveolar","Embryonic canalicular pseudoglandular saccular alveolar","Canalicular embryonic pseudoglandular saccular alveolar","Embryonic saccular pseudoglandular canalicular alveolar","Embryonic (4-7w) pseudoglandular (7-17w) canalicular (17-26w) saccular (26-36w) alveolar (36w-term)"]'::jsonb, 'Embryonic (4-7w) pseudoglandular (7-17w) canalicular (17-26w) saccular (26-36w) alveolar (36w-term)', 'Lung development follows the order embryonic (4-7 weeks), pseudoglandular (7-17 weeks), canalicular (17-26 weeks), saccular (26-36 weeks), and alveolar (36 weeks to term). The other options either scramble this sequence or omit the correct timing.'),
('e92c9bc1-0d6e-553f-8efb-2e75f6b5a626'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'During the pseudoglandular stage (7-17 weeks) the lung resembles a gland with:', '["Surfactant production begins resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal sacs appear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveoli formed mediated by specific receptor activation","Conducting airways formed up to the terminal bronchioles but no respiratory units","Respiratory bronchioles appear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducting airways formed up to the terminal bronchioles but no respiratory units', 'In the pseudoglandular stage the lung looks like a gland, with conducting airways branching down to the terminal bronchioles but no respiratory (gas-exchanging) units. Surfactant production, terminal sacs, and respiratory bronchioles all appear in later stages.'),
('e968bdc1-4272-56ad-9c01-3065efd258cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'During the canalicular stage (17-26 weeks) what important events occur?', '["Respiratory bronchioles appear capillaries approach the epithelium and surfactant production begins","All conducting airways are formed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type I pneumocytes only appear mediated by specific receptor activation","Terminal sacs enlarge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveoli multiply rapidly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory bronchioles appear capillaries approach the epithelium and surfactant production begins', 'In the canalicular stage, respiratory bronchioles form, capillaries come into close contact with the thinning epithelium, and surfactant production begins, so the fetus becomes potentially viable at the end of this stage. These events do not belong to the other stages listed.'),
('76b8f3f6-417e-5b93-820b-8d21651ddc01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'During the saccular stage (26-36 weeks) what happens?', '["Conducting airways form resulting in decreased functional activity","All of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveoli multiply after birth mediated by specific receptor activation","Terminal saccules (primitive alveoli) form and the airspaces enlarge","Surfactant production peaks in the third trimester leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Terminal saccules (primitive alveoli) form and the airspaces enlarge', 'In the saccular stage, terminal saccules (primitive alveoli) form and the airspaces enlarge, creating a thin air-blood barrier sufficient for extrauterine survival. Most true alveoli appear only later in the alveolar stage.'),
('e291f5bc-e6a1-581f-87ef-a29237a3ed3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'During the alveolar stage (36 weeks-term and beyond) what occurs?', '["Type II pneumocytes appear resulting in decreased functional activity","Conducting airways form accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Terminal bronchioles form mediated by specific receptor activation","Surfactant production begins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveoli multiply rapidly (most alveoli form postnatally)"]'::jsonb, 'Alveoli multiply rapidly (most alveoli form postnatally)', 'In the alveolar stage, beginning at 36 weeks and continuing into infancy, alveoli multiply rapidly, with most of the adult complement forming after birth. Type II cells, conducting airways, and terminal bronchioles appear in earlier stages.'),
('9af49cd3-5815-5fa1-b842-8b51c1511531'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'True or false: all of the alveoli are formed prenatally and present at birth.', '["Only 50% are present at birth","All are present by 36 weeks","False (most form postnatally)","Only the right lung forms alveoli prenatally","True (all alveoli are formed at birth)"]'::jsonb, 'False (most form postnatally)', 'The statement is false because most alveoli form postnatally; only a fraction of the adult complement of about 300 million alveoli exists at term. Both lungs form alveoli, and a 36-week timing is also incorrect.'),
('dd98998b-5f26-5eaf-baf5-832317a40786'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'Surfactant is produced by which cell type?', '["Ciliated cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Type II pneumocytes (alveolar epithelial cells)","Goblet cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Type I pneumocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clara cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type II pneumocytes (alveolar epithelial cells)', 'Surfactant is synthesized and secreted by type II pneumocytes, the alveolar epithelial cells that also renew the alveolar lining. Type I cells only form the gas-exchange surface, and airway cells do not produce surfactant.'),
('c3779507-23ba-5a48-81e9-a8fa1059e122'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the major phospholipid component of surfactant?', '["Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)","Phosphatidylserine","Phosphatidylglycerol","Phosphatidylinositol","Sphingomyelin"]'::jsonb, 'Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)', 'Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC) is the principal phospholipid of surfactant and is the main molecule that lowers surface tension. The other phospholipids are minor constituents or markers used for other ratios.'),
('e57ce55e-c039-59c7-9748-82f7bfe11b41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'The lecithin/sphingomyelin (L/S) ratio in amniotic fluid indicates:', '["Amniotic fluid volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Placental function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fetal kidney maturity mediated by specific receptor activation","Fetal liver maturity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fetal lung maturity (ratio >2.0 indicates mature lungs)"]'::jsonb, 'Fetal lung maturity (ratio >2.0 indicates mature lungs)', 'The L/S ratio in amniotic fluid estimates fetal lung maturity, since lecithin rises with surfactant production; a ratio above 2.0 indicates mature lungs. It offers no information about fluid volume, placenta, kidney, or liver.'),
('4db7f272-e0db-5dac-a199-d2896164ca97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) in premature infants is due to:', '["Tracheal stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surfactant deficiency leading to alveolar collapse and atelectasis","Surfactant excess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congenital heart disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surfactant deficiency leading to alveolar collapse and atelectasis', 'RDS results from surfactant deficiency in the immature lung, so high alveolar surface tension causes collapse and progressive atelectasis. Infection, stenosis, cardiac disease, or excess surfactant do not produce this mechanism.'),
('36c4d483-d72e-575c-8ea7-248b2d4c04ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most important risk factor for RDS?', '["Male sex resulting in decreased functional activity","Maternal diabetes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple gestation mediated by specific receptor activation","C-section without labor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prematurity (especially <34 weeks gestation)"]'::jsonb, 'Prematurity (especially <34 weeks gestation)', 'Prematurity, especially before 34 weeks, is the strongest risk factor for RDS because type II pneumocytes and surfactant production are immature. The other listed factors are minor or contributory rather than dominant.'),
('4ad07a23-a306-5695-9235-5821b80174db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the classic radiologic finding of RDS?', '["Consolidation resulting in decreased functional activity","Diffuse ground-glass opacities (reticulogranular pattern) with air bronchograms","Hyperinflation mediated by specific receptor activation","Pleural effusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumothorax leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse ground-glass opacities (reticulogranular pattern) with air bronchograms', 'RDS characteristically shows diffuse reticulogranular (ground-glass) opacities with air bronchograms on chest radiography, reflecting widespread micro-atelectasis. Consolidation, hyperinflation, effusion, and pneumothorax point to different disorders.'),
('4420509a-3853-5544-8673-29810074098d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD)?', '["Congenital diaphragmatic hernia resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary hypoplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic lung disease of prematurity resulting from mechanical ventilation and oxygen toxicity","Surfactant deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acute respiratory distress syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic lung disease of prematurity resulting from mechanical ventilation and oxygen toxicity', 'BPD is chronic lung disease of prematurity produced by mechanical ventilation, oxygen toxicity, and inflammation in the immature lung. It is not a diaphragmatic defect, hypoplasia, acute surfactant deficiency, or ARDS.'),
('69562330-31c5-55bb-a8af-db2f4d087d46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What are the pathologic features of BPD?', '["Complete fibrosis of the lung resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cystic changes mediated by specific receptor activation","Alveolar simplification (arrested development) variable fibrosis and airway remodeling","Absent alveoli leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar simplification (arrested development) variable fibrosis and airway remodeling', 'BPD is characterized pathologically by alveolar simplification (arrested alveolar development), variable interstitial fibrosis, and airway remodeling after lung injury. Total lung fibrosis, absent alveoli, cysts, or isolated pulmonary hypertension are not the hallmark findings.'),
('ec1f6b71-0008-5b6e-bec4-da8527148f20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death in premature infants?', '["Birth asphyxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital anomalies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) and its complications (IVH NEC sepsis)"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) and its complications (IVH NEC sepsis)', 'RDS with its complications, including intraventricular hemorrhage, necrotizing enterocolitis, and sepsis, is the leading cause of death in premature infants. Asphyxia, trauma, infection alone, and congenital anomalies are comparatively less dominant causes.'),
('c50103ae-7804-519b-a198-027fb09eae1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH)?', '["Rupture of the diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","Herniation of abdominal contents into the thoracic cavity through a diaphragmatic defect","Eventration of the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","Hiatal hernia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Paralysis of the diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Herniation of abdominal contents into the thoracic cavity through a diaphragmatic defect', 'CDH is the herniation of abdominal viscera into the thorax through a defect in the developing diaphragm, which compresses the lungs and leads to pulmonary hypoplasia. Rupture, eventration, hiatal hernia, and paralysis are different entities.'),
('7369750f-08b3-52b1-858e-fa41657fe3db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common type of congenital diaphragmatic hernia?', '["Hiatal hernia resulting in decreased functional activity","Bochdalek (posterolateral) hernia (about 80-90% of cases)","Central diaphragmatic hernia mediated by specific receptor activation","Morgagni hernia (retrosternal) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Paraesophageal hernia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bochdalek (posterolateral) hernia (about 80-90% of cases)', 'The Bochdalek hernia through a posterolateral diaphragmatic defect accounts for about 80-90% of CDH cases, being far more common on the left. Anterior Morgagni and hiatal hernias are much less frequent.'),
('e710d8f4-d2cc-5bb2-af6c-cb2848253f3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most important determinant of survival in CDH?', '["Size of the defect resulting in decreased functional activity","Severity of pulmonary hypoplasia and pulmonary hypertension","Presence of other anomalies mediated by specific receptor activation","Side of the hernia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Timing of surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severity of pulmonary hypoplasia and pulmonary hypertension', 'Survival in CDH depends mainly on the severity of pulmonary hypoplasia and the resulting pulmonary hypertension. Defect size, side, timing of surgery, and associated anomalies are secondary prognostic factors.'),
('79128bf1-675b-5d9f-8d1f-18047718e26b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common associated anomaly with CDH?', '["Neural tube defects","Chromosomal abnormalities","GI anomalies","Cardiac anomalies (about 20-30%)","Renal anomalies"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac anomalies (about 20-30%)', 'Cardiac anomalies are the most common malformations associated with CDH, occurring in about 20-30% of cases and worsening the prognosis. The other anomaly groups are encountered less often.'),
('002e1c8d-d773-5e6d-ba82-1ff539aca291'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the treatment for CDH?', '["Oxygen therapy only resulting in decreased functional activity","Stabilization delayed surgery and potentially ECMO for severe cases","Antenatal steroids mediated by specific receptor activation","Emergent surgical repair at birth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventilator support only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stabilization delayed surgery and potentially ECMO for severe cases', 'CDH is managed by initial stabilization with gentle ventilation, delayed surgery once the newborn is stable, and ECMO for severe pulmonary hypertension. Emergent repair at birth is no longer the standard of care.'),
('8539aea8-ed79-5f8f-8857-0c07602c0415'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is eventration of the diaphragm?', '["Diaphragmatic tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Complete absence of the diaphragm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rupture of the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevation of the diaphragm due to congenital weakness or phrenic nerve palsy","Herniation through the diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevation of the diaphragm due to congenital weakness or phrenic nerve palsy', 'Eventration is an abnormal elevation of part of the diaphragm caused by congenital muscular weakness or phrenic nerve palsy. It does not involve rupture, herniation, absence, or a tumor of the diaphragm.'),
('88ac6d22-356d-5c89-bf21-b3c302f58a80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of eventration of the diaphragm in children?', '["Phrenic nerve palsy (birth trauma) resulting in decreased functional activity","Congenital muscular deficiency (weakness of diaphragmatic muscle)","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital muscular deficiency (weakness of diaphragmatic muscle)', 'In children, eventration is most often congenital, from weakness of the diaphragmatic musculature itself. Phrenic nerve palsy from birth trauma is another cause but is not the most common in the pediatric age group.'),
('58c654f5-6a96-5bdb-9e3c-2cfaacda36f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'Pulmonary hypoplasia is defined as:', '["Incomplete development of the lungs (reduced size weight and number of alveoli)","Complete absence of the lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cystic lung disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Solidified lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enlarged lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete development of the lungs (reduced size weight and number of alveoli)', 'Pulmonary hypoplasia means incomplete lung development with reduced size, weight, and number of alveoli and airways. It is distinct from agenesis, cystic disease, consolidation, or enlarged overgrown lungs.'),
('3212f030-3322-54df-bb79-7a8ac23babb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary hypoplasia?', '["Thoracic dystrophy (Jeune syndrome)","Pleural effusion","Neuromuscular disorders (fetal akinesia)","Oligohydramnios (most commonly from renal agenesis or PROM)","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia"]'::jsonb, 'Oligohydramnios (most commonly from renal agenesis or PROM)', 'Oligohydramnios, usually from renal agenesis or prolonged rupture of membranes, is the most common cause of pulmonary hypoplasia because reduced amniotic fluid limits fetal lung distension. CDH, skeletal dysplasias, effusions, and akinesia are less frequent causes.'),
('17ce35fb-3506-522a-9b2e-61b7268e66e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is Potter sequence (oligohydramnios sequence)?', '["Pulmonary hypoplasia limb deformities (clubfoot) Potter facies (flattened nose recessed chin epicanthal folds) from oligohydramnios","Normal findings with oligohydramnios alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial cleft and limb defects mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung overgrowth with polyhydramnios without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac facial and GI anomalies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary hypoplasia limb deformities (clubfoot) Potter facies (flattened nose recessed chin epicanthal folds) from oligohydramnios', 'Potter sequence results from oligohydramnios and comprises pulmonary hypoplasia, limb deformities such as clubfoot, and the characteristic Potter facies, all stemming from fetal compression and reduced intrathoracic fluid. The other options describe unrelated findings or opposite fluid states.'),
('b34b8ebc-8cc3-5efe-b927-97bfb81b4e03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What are the classic features of Potter facies?', '["High forehead and pointed chin resulting in decreased functional activity","Flattened nose recessed chin low-set ears epicanthal folds","Asymmetric facies mediated by specific receptor activation","Broad nose prominent chin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Macroglossia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flattened nose recessed chin low-set ears epicanthal folds', 'Potter facies is characterized by a flattened nose, recessed chin, low-set ears, and epicanthal folds, caused by uterine compression in oligohydramnios. The other facial descriptions do not match this pattern.'),
('0cf0f216-c68b-5c29-9025-1742e884246e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary agenesis?', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Drug exposure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Genetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sporadic (unknown) associated with other anomalies (VACTERL trisomy 18)"]'::jsonb, 'Sporadic (unknown) associated with other anomalies (VACTERL trisomy 18)', 'Pulmonary agenesis is usually sporadic with no known cause and is frequently associated with other malformations such as the VACTERL association or trisomy 18. Recognizable infections, drugs, trauma, or single-gene causes are uncommon.'),
('a723952c-adac-5fe1-bf32-fbc100825186'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is pulmonary sequestration?', '["A mass of nonfunctioning lung tissue that is not connected to the airway and has an anomalous systemic blood supply","Pulmonary arteriovenous malformation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lung tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A duplicated lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lung cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A mass of nonfunctioning lung tissue that is not connected to the airway and has an anomalous systemic blood supply', 'Pulmonary sequestration is a mass of nonfunctioning lung tissue with no normal connection to the tracheobronchial tree and an anomalous systemic arterial supply. It is not an AVM, tumor, duplicated lung, or simple cyst.'),
('da2d5689-2024-57ac-9e38-b46d240f1984'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What are the two types of pulmonary sequestration?', '["Intralobar (within normal lung pleura) and extralobar (separate pleura)","Upper and lower lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unilateral and bilateral mediated by specific receptor activation","Cystic and solid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left and right leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intralobar (within normal lung pleura) and extralobar (separate pleura)', 'Pulmonary sequestration is divided into intralobar, lying within normal lung and sharing its pleura, and extralobar, having its own separate pleural investment. This pleural relationship, not lobe, side, or consistency, defines the two types.'),
('0b036d91-d5a6-52dc-bf08-637204c11bf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common type of pulmonary sequestration?', '["Extralobar is more common in adults","Intralobar sequestration","Intralobar is more common in children","Extralobar sequestration","Both equally common"]'::jsonb, 'Intralobar sequestration', 'Intralobar sequestration is the more common type, usually presenting in older children or adults with recurrent pulmonary infections. Extralobar lesions are less frequent and are more often detected in infancy.'),
('5f100970-b865-5383-8bc4-a4966a6337c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the arterial supply of intralobar sequestration?', '["Internal mammary artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Intercostal artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Systemic artery (most commonly from the descending aorta)","Pulmonary artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchial artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic artery (most commonly from the descending aorta)', 'Intralobar sequestration receives its blood supply from a systemic artery, most commonly an anomalous branch of the descending aorta. Supply from the pulmonary, bronchial, mammary, or intercostal vessels is not characteristic.'),
('ece038e0-1c02-5b43-b6de-0a2654fceefc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is congenital cystic adenomatoid malformation (CCAM now renamed CPAM)?', '["A sequestration with systemic blood supply resulting in decreased functional activity","A multicystic mass of lung tissue with abnormal bronchial proliferation and no alveolar differentiation","Pulmonary agenesis mediated by specific receptor activation","A tracheal cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary hypoplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A multicystic mass of lung tissue with abnormal bronchial proliferation and no alveolar differentiation', 'CPAM is a hamartomatous lung malformation in which immature airway tissue forms multicystic masses with abnormal bronchial proliferation but no normal alveolar differentiation. Systemic blood supply characterizes a sequestration, while pulmonary agenesis and hypoplasia are unrelated developmental defects.'),
('c13f3bf2-7205-5d3f-8736-687d0032a56a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the Stocker classification of CPAM?', '["Classification by number of cysts resulting in decreased functional activity","Types 0-4 based on cyst size and origin (terminal bronchiole types 1-3 acinar type 4)","Based on lobe involvement mediated by specific receptor activation","Classification by size only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Classification by side of chest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Types 0-4 based on cyst size and origin (terminal bronchiole types 1-3 acinar type 4)', 'The Stocker system classifies CPAM into types 0-4 according to cyst size and site of origin, with terminal bronchiole lesions making up types 1-3 and acinar (distal airspace) lesions making up type 4. Cyst number, lobe involvement, and side of chest are not part of this classification.'),
('ae8516ca-6446-500a-ad85-967e74a38bd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common type of CPAM?', '["Type 0 (acinar dysplasia)","Stocker Type 2 (small cysts <1 cm)","Stocker Type 1 (large cysts >2 cm)","Stocker Type 3 (solid)","Stocker Type 4 (distal airspace)"]'::jsonb, 'Stocker Type 1 (large cysts >2 cm)', 'Stocker type 1, composed of large cysts larger than 2 cm, is the most common CPAM variant and generally carries the best prognosis. Types 0, 2, 3, and 4 are encountered less frequently.'),
('a02d48ea-4dc2-5333-91f7-ddebd9f18587'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common complication of CPAM?', '["Hemorrhage","Malignant transformation (rare)","Rupture with pneumothorax","Obstruction","Recurrent pneumonia (if not resected)"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent pneumonia (if not resected)', 'The most common complication of an unresected CPAM is recurrent infection, typically pneumonia, because the abnormal cysts impair mucus clearance and trap secretions. Hemorrhage, rupture with pneumothorax, and malignant transformation are much rarer events.'),
('3ea81e3e-7623-5574-979d-95c5aef3ea24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the recommended management of an asymptomatic CPAM?', '["Antibiotic prophylaxis resulting in decreased functional activity","Medical management accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emergent resection at birth mediated by specific receptor activation","Observation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elective resection in the first year of life (due to infection and malignant risk)"]'::jsonb, 'Elective resection in the first year of life (due to infection and malignant risk)', 'Elective resection in the first year of life is recommended even for asymptomatic CPAM to prevent recurrent infection and the small risk of malignant transformation. Observation and medical management do not remove these risks, while emergent resection is reserved for symptomatic neonates.'),
('f52020b2-5494-5d00-a5a5-c36d3597d66c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is bronchogenic cyst?', '["A lung sequestration resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A CPAM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pericardial cyst mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A cystic hygroma without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A congenital cyst derived from the foregut that is lined by respiratory epithelium and often located in the mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital cyst derived from the foregut that is lined by respiratory epithelium and often located in the mediastinum', 'A bronchogenic cyst is a foregut-derived congenital cyst lined by respiratory epithelium, most often found in the mediastinum near the carina or trachea. Lung sequestration, CPAM, pericardial cyst, and cystic hygroma are distinct developmental lesions.'),
('56f17d56-27d2-5b82-8982-0fd55b276150'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common location of a bronchogenic cyst?', '["Abdomen resulting in decreased functional activity","Diaphragm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mediastinum (subcarinal or paratracheal)","Intrapulmonary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mediastinum (subcarinal or paratracheal)', 'Bronchogenic cysts most often arise in the mediastinum, particularly in the subcarinal or paratracheal region. Intrapulmonary, neck, and diaphragmatic sites are less common, and abdominal locations are rare.'),
('d7580e2a-857b-5da7-9c3a-b38f59923642'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common presentation of a bronchogenic cyst?', '["Massive hemothorax resulting in decreased functional activity","Asymptomatic (incidental finding on imaging) or symptoms from compression (wheeze dysphagia)","Acute respiratory distress mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumothorax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemoptysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asymptomatic (incidental finding on imaging) or symptoms from compression (wheeze dysphagia)', 'Most bronchogenic cysts are asymptomatic and discovered incidentally on imaging, though larger ones can compress the trachea, esophagus, or bronchi to cause wheeze, dysphagia, or respiratory distress. Massive hemothorax, pneumothorax, and hemoptysis are not typical initial presentations.'),
('e9d54b97-f41b-5903-9c60-d0b14975ac42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is pulmonary lymphangiectasia?', '["A lung cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","A form of BPD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pulmonary sequestration mediated by specific receptor activation","Dilation of pulmonary lymphatic channels causing respiratory distress in neonates","Pulmonary edema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilation of pulmonary lymphatic channels causing respiratory distress in neonates', 'Pulmonary lymphangiectasia is a rare congenital disorder in which pulmonary lymphatic channels are dilated, leading to severe respiratory distress in the neonatal period. It is distinct from cysts, BPD, sequestration, and pulmonary edema.'),
('7d940795-214a-5d99-a379-731bb141b9a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of neonatal respiratory distress?', '["Pneumonia resulting in decreased functional activity","Meconium aspiration syndrome (MAS) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transient tachypnea of the newborn (TTN) (retained fetal lung fluid)","RDS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congenital heart disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transient tachypnea of the newborn (TTN) (retained fetal lung fluid)', 'Transient tachypnea of the newborn, caused by retained fetal lung fluid, is the most common cause of neonatal respiratory distress, especially in late preterm and term infants delivered by cesarean section. RDS, MAS, pneumonia, and congenital heart disease are important but less common differentials.'),
('7661f351-9d4d-5bb0-abb9-44355b839c2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the classic CXR finding of TTN?', '["Ground-glass appearance resulting in decreased functional activity","Pneumothorax accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prominent perihilar interstitial markings (fluid in interlobar fissures) with hyperinflation","Pleural effusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Consolidation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prominent perihilar interstitial markings (fluid in interlobar fissures) with hyperinflation', 'TTN shows prominent perihilar interstitial markings with fluid in the interlobar fissures and lung hyperinflation, reflecting retained fetal lung fluid. Ground-glass opacities, consolidation, effusion, and pneumothorax point to other conditions such as RDS or MAS.'),
('4b30a4c1-8f2f-53c5-8ef2-ccf6ded26fb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is meconium aspiration syndrome (MAS)?', '["Transient tachypnea resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary hypoplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aspiration of meconium-stained amniotic fluid causing airway obstruction pneumonitis and respiratory failure","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RDS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aspiration of meconium-stained amniotic fluid causing airway obstruction pneumonitis and respiratory failure', 'Meconium aspiration syndrome is the aspiration of meconium-stained amniotic fluid, producing airway obstruction, chemical pneumonitis, and respiratory failure in the newborn. It differs from TTN, pneumonia, and RDS in its mechanism and clinical features.'),
('87271ade-74f7-5e17-b8cc-bafad676e8b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common risk factor for MAS?', '["IUGR","Prematurity","Post-term pregnancy (>40 weeks)","Maternal diabetes","Multiple gestation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-term pregnancy (>40 weeks)', 'Post-term pregnancy, in which the fetus is more likely to pass meconium in utero, is the strongest risk factor for MAS. Prematurity, IUGR, maternal diabetes, and multiple gestation are not the primary predictors.'),
('741425d1-d28d-549b-bcf4-bdc2fe1bc341'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What are the classic radiographic findings in MAS?', '["Coarse patchy opacities with hyperinflation (air trapping) and potential pneumothorax","Prominent interstitial markings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ground-glass appearance mediated by specific receptor activation","Consolidation with air bronchograms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pleural effusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coarse patchy opacities with hyperinflation (air trapping) and potential pneumothorax', 'MAS classically produces coarse, patchy, bilateral opacities with hyperinflation due to air trapping by meconium plugs, and air leak (pneumothorax) may supervene. Interstitial prominence and ground-glass appearance suggest TTN and RDS respectively.'),
('a4190238-358b-54a7-9fe5-e5899b4a5c77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common complication of MAS?', '["Pneumothorax (air leak syndrome from air trapping)","Pneumonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sepsis mediated by specific receptor activation","PPHN without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary hemorrhage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumothorax (air leak syndrome from air trapping)', 'Air trapping from partial bronchial obstruction by meconium predisposes to air leak, making pneumothorax the most common complication of MAS. Pneumonia, PPHN, sepsis, and hemorrhage can coexist but are not the leading complication.'),
('efb20e45-bf74-529c-a315-b6ff04dc156e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN)?', '["Idiopathic resulting in decreased functional activity","Congenital diaphragmatic hernia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meconium aspiration syndrome (most common)","Perinatal asphyxia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Meconium aspiration syndrome (most common)', 'Meconium aspiration syndrome is the most common cause of persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn because hypoxia, acidosis, and inflammation trigger pulmonary vasoconstriction. Idiopathic PPHN, CDH, asphyxia, and pneumonia account for a minority of cases.'),
('dad48fc4-7ca3-53f1-9d39-7f662a1c0c3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the treatment for PPHN?', '["ECMO resulting in decreased functional activity","Surfactant therapy only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventilation only mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxygen only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhaled nitric oxide (iNO) as first-line therapy"]'::jsonb, 'Inhaled nitric oxide (iNO) as first-line therapy', 'Inhaled nitric oxide, a selective pulmonary vasodilator, is first-line therapy for PPHN and improves oxygenation without causing systemic hypotension. ECMO is reserved for refractory cases, while oxygen, ventilation, and surfactant address supportive or underlying needs.'),
('a2aa476d-62a2-5634-9a2b-72e840397da1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pneumonia in the newborn period?', '["Listeria resulting in decreased functional activity","E. coli accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Group B Streptococcus (GBS) vertically transmitted from the mother","Chlamydia trachomatis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Viruses (CMV RSV) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Group B Streptococcus (GBS) vertically transmitted from the mother', 'Group B Streptococcus, acquired vertically from the mother at delivery, is the most common cause of early-onset pneumonia in the newborn period. E. coli, Listeria, and viruses are less frequent neonatal pathogens.'),
('9c8493d6-4dee-5885-a612-862df5bc182d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of viral pneumonia in infants?', '["Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)","Rhinovirus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adenovirus mediated by specific receptor activation","Influenza without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parainfluenza leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)', 'Respiratory syncytial virus is the leading cause of viral pneumonia and bronchiolitis in infants. Rhinovirus, adenovirus, influenza, and parainfluenza cause infection less commonly.'),
('a7802721-8e20-5b28-ab52-5a777dfedd60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of bacterial pneumonia in children beyond the newborn period?', '["Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Group A Streptococcus","Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib now less common due to vaccine)"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common cause of typical bacterial pneumonia in children beyond the neonatal period. Mycoplasma is the usual atypical pathogen, while Hib has declined sharply since vaccination.'),
('e8965907-252f-585f-98e7-fc1943e26603'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of atypical pneumonia in children?', '["Legionella pneumophila","Chlamydia psittaci","Viral","Chlamydia pneumoniae","Mycoplasma pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'Mycoplasma pneumoniae', 'Mycoplasma pneumoniae is the most common cause of atypical pneumonia in school-aged children and adolescents, often presenting with extrapulmonary features. Other atypicals such as Chlamydia and Legionella are much less common.'),
('2d7d9d90-851c-561e-a420-f1e9c0e19176'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of empyema (parapneumonic effusion) in children?', '["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Haemophilus influenzae","Streptococcus pyogenes (GAS)","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Staphylococcus aureus"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most frequent cause of empyema and parapneumonic effusion in children, usually complicating pneumococcal pneumonia. Staphylococcus aureus and other organisms are less common causes.'),
('7d6a737d-e7c1-55a2-a346-366b73641a0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent pneumonia in children?', '["Cystic fibrosis","Congenital lung malformation (CPAM sequestration)","Asthma","Aspiration (from GERD or swallowing dysfunction)","Immune deficiency (CVID IgA deficiency)"]'::jsonb, 'Aspiration (from GERD or swallowing dysfunction)', 'Aspiration from gastroesophageal reflux or swallowing dysfunction is the most common cause of recurrent pneumonia in children. Cystic fibrosis, immune deficiency, and congenital lung malformations are important but less frequent etiologies.'),
('4893fe18-9358-5e7f-9511-b456ac9bf8b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of bronchiectasis in children?', '["Cystic fibrosis (most common cause of progressive bronchiectasis)","Aspiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Post-infectious (adenovirus pertussis TB) mediated by specific receptor activation","Immune deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic fibrosis (most common cause of progressive bronchiectasis)', 'Cystic fibrosis is the most common cause of progressive bronchiectasis in children, driven by recurrent endobronchial infection. Post-infectious causes, aspiration, immune deficiency, and primary ciliary dyskinesia are less common.'),
('20294556-11f2-5318-a10c-345269cf57cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic cough in children?', '["GERD resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchiectasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Post-infectious cough mediated by specific receptor activation","Habit cough without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Postnasal drip (allergic rhinitis) and asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Postnasal drip (allergic rhinitis) and asthma', 'Postnasal drip from allergic rhinitis together with asthma are the most common causes of chronic cough in children. GERD, habit cough, and post-infectious cough are additional but less common causes.'),
('4839bf84-99b1-5bd3-b737-cb07f94324aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of wheezing in infants?', '["Asthma","Aspiration","Bronchiolitis (RSV)","Vascular ring","GERD"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchiolitis (RSV)', 'Bronchiolitis, most often due to RSV, is the most common cause of wheezing in infants. Asthma, aspiration, GERD, and vascular ring are less common and generally present differently.'),
('5b73b15b-fba4-5b7c-b160-60271620b5cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of stridor in infants?', '["Laryngomalacia","Vascular ring","Tracheal stenosis","Vocal cord paralysis","Laryngeal web"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngomalacia', 'Laryngomalacia, caused by collapse of floppy supraglottic structures, is the most common cause of stridor in infants and usually resolves spontaneously. Vascular ring, tracheal stenosis, vocal cord paralysis, and laryngeal web are rarer.'),
('6edfeaf1-a31d-5ed5-91ef-c3fa0b0e9946'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of croup (laryngotracheobronchitis)?', '["Influenza resulting in decreased functional activity","Parainfluenza virus (types 1 2 and 3)","Measles mediated by specific receptor activation","Adenovirus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RSV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parainfluenza virus (types 1 2 and 3)', 'Parainfluenza virus types 1, 2, and 3 are the most common causes of croup (laryngotracheobronchitis). Influenza, adenovirus, measles, and RSV are less frequent causes.'),
('1f1a4c50-c7d4-51c9-871b-0ddb05207f33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of epiglottitis in children?', '["Group A Streptococcus resulting in decreased functional activity","Staph aureus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) in unvaccinated children; now less common with Hib vaccine","Viral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","S. pneumoniae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) in unvaccinated children; now less common with Hib vaccine', 'Haemophilus influenzae type b is the classic cause of epiglottitis in unvaccinated children, and its incidence has fallen dramatically since Hib vaccination. Other bacteria and viruses are uncommon causes.'),
('e7056626-930a-59ea-b09d-cd7b6a6b5195'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of bacterial tracheitis?', '["Moraxella catarrhalis","Staphylococcus aureus","Haemophilus influenzae","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Pseudomonas"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus aureus', 'Staphylococcus aureus is the most common bacterial cause of tracheitis, usually complicating a preceding viral upper respiratory infection. Moraxella, Hib, pneumococcus, and Pseudomonas are less common.'),
('f3b68093-5692-5664-9e2c-32b0171b8354'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of foreign body aspiration in children?', '["Peanuts (most common) and other nuts","Seeds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small toys mediated by specific receptor activation","Food particles (hot dog) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peanuts (most common) and other nuts', 'Peanuts and other nuts are the most commonly aspirated foreign bodies in children because of their small size and lipid content. Seeds, small toys, other food particles, and coins are less frequently aspirated.'),
('744717f3-cd96-5904-b898-47be2ba761f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common location of an aspirated foreign body?', '["Trachea resulting in decreased functional activity","Subglottic area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left main bronchus mediated by specific receptor activation","Larynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right main bronchus (wider shorter more vertical)"]'::jsonb, 'Right main bronchus (wider shorter more vertical)', 'An aspirated foreign body most often lodges in the right main bronchus because it is wider, shorter, and more vertical than the left. The trachea, larynx, and subglottis are less common sites.'),
('98a90f59-68ae-5381-8834-a93922ff1b8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of atelectasis in children?', '["Pneumothorax resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural effusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucus plugging (post-surgical or during mechanical ventilation)","Foreign body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mucus plugging (post-surgical or during mechanical ventilation)', 'Mucus plugging, seen after surgery or during mechanical ventilation, is the most common cause of atelectasis in children. Pneumothorax, pleural effusion, tumor, and foreign body are less frequent causes.'),
('b1664dbf-9429-5701-b5f8-f29be6732f13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pneumothorax in newborns?', '["Air leak syndrome from RDS requiring positive pressure ventilation","Meconium aspiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spontaneous pneumothorax (term newborn with TTN) mediated by specific receptor activation","Chest tube placement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Air leak syndrome from RDS requiring positive pressure ventilation', 'Air leak syndrome in newborns most commonly results from RDS treated with positive pressure ventilation, which overdistends poorly compliant alveoli and ruptures them. Meconium aspiration and spontaneous pneumothorax are less common causes.'),
('2fd6e174-0882-557c-90bd-0fad63879bea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pneumothorax in older children?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Asthma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Catamenial pneumothorax mediated by specific receptor activation","Spontaneous pneumothorax (especially tall thin males)","Cystic fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spontaneous pneumothorax (especially tall thin males)', 'Spontaneous pneumothorax, classically in tall, thin adolescent males from apical bleb rupture, is the most common cause of pneumothorax in older children. Trauma, asthma, cystic fibrosis, and catamenial causes are less frequent.'),
('6832ad88-63dc-5bf1-bdc9-e88a852f03ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chylothorax in children?', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Traumatic (post thoracic surgery especially cardiac surgery)","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor (lymphoma) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spontaneous (congenital lymphatic malformation) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Traumatic (post thoracic surgery especially cardiac surgery)', 'Trauma to the thoracic duct, most often after cardiac surgery, is the most common cause of chylothorax in children. Infection, tumor, and spontaneous congenital lymphatic malformation are less common.'),
('4aa71e62-53d9-592d-9cbe-e93eb1a5ed73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hemothorax in children?', '["Tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma (most common cause of hemothorax in children and adults)","Coagulopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Iatrogenic (central line) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary embolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma (most common cause of hemothorax in children and adults)', 'Trauma is the most common cause of hemothorax in both children and adults. Tumors, coagulopathy, iatrogenic causes, and pulmonary embolism are much rarer.'),
('91cd6bb4-3193-5f4e-9e5b-6248962b6e08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary hemorrhage in children?', '["Pulmonary hemosiderosis","Pulmonary infection (pneumonia)","Pulmonary edema (CHF)","Coagulopathy","Tracheostomy or endotracheal tube trauma"]'::jsonb, 'Tracheostomy or endotracheal tube trauma', 'In intubated children, trauma from a tracheostomy or endotracheal tube can erode the airway mucosa and is the most common cause of pulmonary hemorrhage. Coagulopathy, pulmonary infection, and pulmonary edema are less common causes.'),
('3fa5c56f-0932-5643-a565-42f304bbda98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hemoptysis in children?', '["Tuberculosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Pneumonia (most common cause) and bronchiectasis","CF mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary hemorrhage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchiectasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumonia (most common cause) and bronchiectasis', 'Pneumonia is the most common cause of hemoptysis in children, and bronchiectasis (often from cystic fibrosis) is another leading cause. Tuberculosis and pulmonary hemorrhage syndromes are less common.'),
('e8f25268-2ece-5afc-91ff-81f0921900d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of cystic fibrosis-related lung disease?', '["Influenza resulting in decreased functional activity","Aspergillus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RSV mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic endobronchial infection with Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus","Non-tuberculous mycobacteria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic endobronchial infection with Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus', 'Chronic endobronchial infection with Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus drives progressive CF lung disease by provoking mucus plugging, inflammation, and bronchiectasis. Aspergillus, RSV, influenza, and non-tuberculous mycobacteria are less important contributors.'),
('687afe12-daff-5a2e-b20c-bbd0f1eaa725'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death in cystic fibrosis?', '["Pancreatic insufficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","GI complications accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CF-related diabetes mediated by specific receptor activation","Meconium ileus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Respiratory failure from progressive lung disease"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory failure from progressive lung disease', 'Respiratory failure from progressive suppurative lung disease is the most common cause of death in cystic fibrosis. Pancreatic insufficiency, CF-related diabetes, and GI complications contribute morbidity but are not the leading cause of death.'),
('dbc2363b-d986-5ec8-9b59-f3a6c3b0118f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common CFTR mutation causing cystic fibrosis?', '["F508del (deletion of phenylalanine at position 508) accounts for about 70% of CF alleles","R117H accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1717-1G>A mediated by specific receptor activation","G551D without affecting the primary tissue pathway","R347P leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'F508del (deletion of phenylalanine at position 508) accounts for about 70% of CF alleles', 'F508del, a deletion of phenylalanine at position 508, accounts for about 70% of cystic fibrosis alleles worldwide. R117H, G551D, 1717-1G>A, and R347P are much less common mutations.'),
('b602a555-5bf9-5a08-b2e9-3a39e63b0a5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of bronchiectasis in adults?', '["Immunodeficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Cystic fibrosis (most common cause of widespread bronchiectasis)","Primary ciliary dyskinesia mediated by specific receptor activation","Idiopathic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Post-infectious (TB pneumonia pertussis) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic fibrosis (most common cause of widespread bronchiectasis)', 'Cystic fibrosis is the most common cause of widespread bronchiectasis even in adults because improved survival allows the pulmonary disease to progress. Post-infectious, primary ciliary dyskinesia, and immunodeficiency causes are less common.'),
('7b966ab6-c814-5ce4-bb5b-4604d9036168'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of asthma in children?', '["Exercise resulting in decreased functional activity","GERD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stress mediated by specific receptor activation","Atopy (allergic IgE-mediated reaction to environmental triggers)","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atopy (allergic IgE-mediated reaction to environmental triggers)', 'Atopy, an IgE-mediated allergic response to environmental triggers, is the most common cause of asthma in children and underlies allergic asthma. Exercise, GERD, stress, and infection can exacerbate asthma but are not the underlying cause.'),
('3314e18c-e839-5dde-a6a1-e40f65120273'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common finding on pulmonary function tests in asthma?', '["Restrictive pattern resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstructive pattern with reduced FEV1/FVC ratio and reversibility after bronchodilator","Low DLCO mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal PFTs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fixed obstruction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructive pattern with reduced FEV1/FVC ratio and reversibility after bronchodilator', 'Asthma produces an obstructive pattern with a reduced FEV1/FVC ratio that improves after bronchodilator administration, confirming reversible airflow obstruction. A restrictive pattern, low DLCO, or fixed obstruction point to other diagnoses.'),
('e804e11b-544a-5460-8c30-c7d4596d85b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common trigger of asthma exacerbation in children?', '["Medication non-compliance resulting in decreased functional activity","Weather changes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exercise mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral upper respiratory tract infections (rhinovirus RSV influenza)","Allergen exposure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Viral upper respiratory tract infections (rhinovirus RSV influenza)', 'Viral upper respiratory tract infections, especially rhinovirus, RSV, and influenza, are the most common triggers of asthma exacerbations in children. Exercise, allergens, and weather changes are less frequent precipitants.'),
('f160131a-8799-50a7-8971-d07e9ab57167'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of respiratory failure in children?', '["ARDS resulting in decreased functional activity","Status asthmaticus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neuromuscular disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia (most common cause of acute hypoxemic respiratory failure)","Bronchiolitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumonia (most common cause of acute hypoxemic respiratory failure)', 'Pneumonia is the most common cause of acute hypoxemic respiratory failure in children because alveolar consolidation and exudate impair gas exchange. ARDS, status asthmaticus, bronchiolitis, and neuromuscular disease are less common causes.'),
('4e83f462-7cc8-5d69-bc9a-4844f59319c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) in children?', '["A primary infection resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Surfactant deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute inflammatory lung injury causing bilateral pulmonary infiltrates and hypoxemia not due to cardiogenic pulmonary edema","A lung mass without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A chronic lung disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute inflammatory lung injury causing bilateral pulmonary infiltrates and hypoxemia not due to cardiogenic pulmonary edema', 'ARDS is an acute inflammatory lung injury producing bilateral pulmonary infiltrates and hypoxemia that are not explained by cardiogenic pulmonary edema or fluid overload. It is a syndrome, not a primary infection, surfactant deficiency, mass, or chronic disease.'),
('60b0d1c6-c738-574a-ade8-4c2430c47f56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of ARDS in children?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Aspiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sepsis (most common) and pneumonia","Pancreatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Drowning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sepsis (most common) and pneumonia', 'Sepsis is the most common cause of ARDS in children, with pneumonia also being a frequent trigger of the lung injury. Trauma, aspiration, drowning, and pancreatitis are less common causes.'),
('68bcfabf-f08e-5bff-bb6e-8a00372fcb91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of respiratory distress in a child with congenital heart disease?', '["Reactive airway disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Anxiety accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary edema from left-to-right shunt or left heart obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary edema from left-to-right shunt or left heart obstruction', 'In a child with congenital heart disease, pulmonary edema from a left-to-right shunt (e.g., VSD) or left heart obstruction is the most common cause of respiratory distress because of pulmonary overcirculation and congestion. Pneumonia, anxiety, and reactive airway disease are not the primary mechanism.'),
('ee30b9f3-3bae-571d-adf2-b2957211ae9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary edema in children?', '["Sepsis resulting in decreased functional activity","Pneumonia (ARDS) and heart failure (CHF)","Overhydration mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumonia (ARDS) and heart failure (CHF)', 'In children, pulmonary edema most often results from pneumonia complicated by ARDS or from congestive heart failure, both producing alveolar fluid accumulation. Sepsis and overhydration can contribute but are not the leading causes, while pancreatitis and head injury rarely cause pediatric pulmonary edema.'),
('2f29ebba-9eb3-5d22-9a4a-176903555d59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic lung disease in children?', '["Cystic fibrosis","Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (in preterm infants)","Bronchiectasis","Interstitial lung disease","Asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma', 'Asthma is the most frequent chronic respiratory illness of childhood, characterized by reversible airway inflammation and hyperreactivity. Cystic fibrosis and bronchopulmonary dysplasia are important but less common chronic lung diseases, and bronchiectasis or interstitial lung disease are comparatively rare.'),
('c39518f0-7201-53a2-a54b-f1bab4c083fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of interstitial lung disease in children?', '["Cytotoxic drug reaction","Idiopathic (cryptogenic organizing pneumonia LIP)","Connective tissue disease","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (exposure to birds molds)","Sarcoidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (exposure to birds molds)', 'Childhood interstitial lung disease is most commonly hypersensitivity pneumonitis, triggered by inhaled antigens such as bird proteins or molds. Drug reactions, connective tissue disease, and sarcoidosis account for only a minority of pediatric cases.'),
('716b6195-66f6-5b90-aa4e-7a196b35c3e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of restrictive lung disease in children?', '["Obesity resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-surgical (thoracotomy) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neuromuscular disease (muscular dystrophy spinal muscular atrophy)","Chest wall deformity (kyphoscoliosis) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interstitial lung disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuromuscular disease (muscular dystrophy spinal muscular atrophy)', 'Restrictive lung disease in children is most often due to neuromuscular disorders such as muscular dystrophy or spinal muscular atrophy, where weak inspiratory muscles limit lung expansion. Chest wall deformity, obesity, and interstitial disease are additional causes but occur less frequently.'),
('32317a39-3cc0-5d7d-8212-fb5ccb5a66c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of sleep-disordered breathing in children?', '["Neuromuscular disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Obesity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Craniofacial anomalies mediated by specific receptor activation","Adenotonsillar hypertrophy (obstructive sleep apnea)","Central sleep apnea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenotonsillar hypertrophy (obstructive sleep apnea)', 'Enlarged adenoids and tonsils obstruct the pharyngeal airway during sleep, making adenotonsillar hypertrophy the leading cause of pediatric obstructive sleep apnea. Obesity and craniofacial anomalies can worsen obstruction, while neuromuscular disease and central apnea are less common.'),
('fda10c12-0206-5d34-a970-0ce71c6f89f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD)?', '["Surfactant protein deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Mutations in dynein genes (DNAI1 DNAH5) causing defective ciliary ultrastructure","CFTR mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgG subclass deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mutations in dynein genes (DNAI1 DNAH5) causing defective ciliary ultrastructure', 'Primary ciliary dyskinesia results from mutations in dynein genes such as DNAI1 or DNAH5 that disrupt the axonemal dynein arms, impairing mucociliary clearance. CFTR and alpha-1 antitrypsin defects cause different inherited lung diseases, and IgG subclass deficiency impairs humoral immunity rather than ciliary function.'),
('9d04a814-a092-5507-9ced-045a2f39a322'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is Kartagener syndrome?', '["PCD with deafness resulting in decreased functional activity","Ciliary aplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Situs inversus bronchiectasis and chronic sinusitis from primary ciliary dyskinesia","Young syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CF with situs inversus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Situs inversus bronchiectasis and chronic sinusitis from primary ciliary dyskinesia', 'Kartagener syndrome is a triad of situs inversus, bronchiectasis, and chronic sinusitis caused by defective ciliary motility in primary ciliary dyskinesia. It is not simply ciliary aplasia or PCD with deafness, and it is unrelated to CF.'),
('8d29ae01-e7e1-56f5-904f-06b5f332e242'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common bacterial cause of acute sinusitis in children?', '["Group A Streptococcus","Haemophilus influenzae (non-typeable)","Staph aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Moraxella catarrhalis"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae is the leading bacterial pathogen in pediatric acute sinusitis, followed by non-typeable Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis. Group A Streptococcus and Staph aureus are much less frequent causes.'),
('ffdb4662-4b36-5ba6-b5da-09a4816a34ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent sinusitis in children?', '["CF resulting in decreased functional activity","Cartilage-hair hypoplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Allergic rhinitis (most common) and immune deficiency (IgG subclass deficiency)","Job syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PCD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic rhinitis (most common) and immune deficiency (IgG subclass deficiency)', 'Recurrent sinusitis in children most often follows allergic rhinitis that obstructs sinus drainage, with immune deficiency such as IgG subclass deficiency as another common contributor. CF and PCD are important but rarer predisposing conditions.'),
('0d6ab9ff-f9e2-50f5-a4d0-b286a108cf07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of epistaxis in children?', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma (nose picking) and nasal mucosal dryness","Coagulopathy (ITP hemophilia) mediated by specific receptor activation","Angiofibroma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Allergic rhinitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma (nose picking) and nasal mucosal dryness', 'Most childhood nosebleeds arise from local trauma, especially nose picking, combined with dry nasal mucosa that fissures the anterior septal vessels. Coagulopathy and angiofibroma are far less common causes, and hypertension is rare in children.'),
('8b3e2a72-b9f1-5f97-abb3-0afd04f9c57a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common location of epistaxis in children?', '["Nasopharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","Woodruff plexus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Kiesselbach plexus (Little area) of the anterior nasal septum","Posterior nasal cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nasal turbinates leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Kiesselbach plexus (Little area) of the anterior nasal septum', 'The Kiesselbach plexus on the anterior nasal septum is the usual bleeding site in children because it is exposed to trauma and digital irritation. Posterior bleeding from the Woodruff plexus is more typical of adults, especially the elderly.'),
('6a4594d1-3405-51dd-8fcb-090e5eac7006'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic hoarseness in children?', '["Allergic rhinitis with postnasal drip","Neurologic disorder","Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis","Vocal cord nodules (vocal abuse)","GERD"]'::jsonb, 'Vocal cord nodules (vocal abuse)', 'Chronic hoarseness in children is usually caused by vocal cord nodules that develop from vocal abuse such as shouting and excessive speaking. GERD and postnasal drip can irritate the larynx but are less common primary causes, and papillomatosis or neurologic disorders are rare.'),
('adac44e4-8b48-56ff-a125-88b0c7fa483c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent respiratory papillomatosis?', '["HPV 16 and 18 resulting in decreased functional activity","CMV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","EBV mediated by specific receptor activation","Human papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11 transmitted vertically during delivery","HSV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Human papillomavirus (HPV) types 6 and 11 transmitted vertically during delivery', 'Recurrent respiratory papillomatosis is caused by HPV types 6 and 11 acquired vertically from the maternal genital tract during delivery, producing laryngeal warty growths. HPV 16 and 18 are oncogenic cervical types but are not implicated here, and CMV, EBV, or HSV are unrelated.'),
('21dc3473-44b0-55dc-949d-8945bdc03dac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of lung cancer in adults?', '["Second-hand smoke","Cigarette smoking (tobacco use)","Asbestos exposure","Radon exposure","Genetic predisposition"]'::jsonb, 'Cigarette smoking (tobacco use)', 'Tobacco smoking is by far the dominant risk factor for lung cancer in adults, and risk rises with duration and intensity of exposure. Asbestos and radon increase risk but rank below smoking, and second-hand smoke or genetics play smaller roles.'),
('da64f160-0500-5b34-b609-b44452dbc818'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common histologic type of lung cancer in adults?', '["Large cell carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Squamous cell carcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Adenocarcinoma (now most common overall) has replaced squamous cell","Adenosquamous carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenocarcinoma (now most common overall) has replaced squamous cell', 'Adenocarcinoma has surpassed squamous cell carcinoma as the most common lung cancer overall, reflecting its rising frequency even in never-smokers and its peripheral location. Small cell and large cell carcinoma are less common, and adenosquamous carcinoma is rare.'),
('c7675327-285e-5fab-87ab-05f306e35f10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of lung cancer in never-smokers?', '["Asbestos","Genetic susceptibility","Second-hand smoke","Radon exposure","Emission exposure (diesel)"]'::jsonb, 'Second-hand smoke', 'Among never-smokers, exposure to second-hand smoke is the leading identifiable cause of lung cancer. Radon exposure is the second most important factor, while genetic susceptibility and diesel emissions are weaker contributors.'),
('dc993e42-5b2b-5b62-897f-203b96d12810'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of malignant pleural effusion in adults?', '["Lung cancer (most common) followed by breast cancer and lymphoma","Ovarian cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GI cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Sarcoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malignant mesothelioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer (most common) followed by breast cancer and lymphoma', 'Lung cancer is the most common cause of malignant pleural effusion, with breast cancer and lymphoma next in frequency, all seeding the pleural space with tumor cells. Ovarian and GI cancers contribute less often, and mesothelioma is comparatively rare.'),
('b6ab01ff-3f41-52d6-aca2-aab9f93b7022'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of benign pleural effusion in adults?', '["Pancreatitis","Heart failure","Pneumonia","Liver cirrhosis","Pulmonary embolism"]'::jsonb, 'Heart failure', 'Congestive heart failure is the leading cause of benign pleural effusion, typically bilateral transudates from elevated hydrostatic pressure. Pneumonia and pulmonary embolism cause exudates or parapneumonic effusions, while cirrhosis and pancreatitis are less common causes.'),
('5109293b-5b03-5854-8128-8373c7f4144d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a solitary pulmonary nodule (coin lesion)?', '["Abscess","Hamartoma","Metastasis","Granuloma (histoplasmosis TB)","Bronchogenic carcinoma (lung cancer)"]'::jsonb, 'Granuloma (histoplasmosis TB)', 'A solitary pulmonary nodule in adults is most often an old granuloma from prior histoplasmosis or tuberculosis infection. Primary bronchogenic carcinoma is the second most common cause, while hamartoma and metastasis are less frequent, and an abscess typically presents differently.'),
('e7c7fbdb-c705-56c4-89ff-ece5ec69f0f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common benign lung tumor?', '["Hamartoma (chondroid hamartoma)","Solitary fibrous tumor","Granular cell tumor","Leiomyoma","Carcinoid tumor (typical)"]'::jsonb, 'Hamartoma (chondroid hamartoma)', 'Hamartoma, particularly the chondroid variant with cartilage and fat, is the most common benign lung tumor. Solitary fibrous tumor, granular cell tumor, and leiomyoma are all rarer benign lesions, and carcinoid is a low-grade malignant tumor, not benign.'),
('9fce9c16-8343-5a57-aacc-c1c642b55ff5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common primary lung cancer in children?', '["Large cell carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenocarcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Squamous cell carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carcinoid tumor (typical bronchial carcinoid)"]'::jsonb, 'Carcinoid tumor (typical bronchial carcinoid)', 'The typical bronchial carcinoid is the most common primary lung tumor in children, behaving as a low-grade neuroendocrine neoplasm. The smoking-related carcinomas such as adenocarcinoma and small cell carcinoma are essentially adult diseases and rare in childhood.'),
('14c7528d-edb9-5826-980c-a661c2fc921c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of Pancoast syndrome (superior sulcus tumor)?', '["Apical lung cancer (usually squamous or adenocarcinoma)","Pancreatic cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metastatic tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pleural mesothelioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Apical lung cancer (usually squamous or adenocarcinoma)', 'Pancoast syndrome results from a superior sulcus tumor, most commonly an apical squamous cell or adenocarcinoma that invades the brachial plexus, ribs, and sympathetic chain. Pancreatic cancer, lymphoma, and mesothelioma do not typically produce this apical syndrome.'),
('908b829a-00c7-582b-8dfd-2d2f90585a0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is Horner syndrome?', '["Enophthalmos with miosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Ptosis miosis hyperhidrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ptosis mydriasis hyperhidrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Ptosis miosis anhidrosis from sympathetic chain disruption by a Pancoast tumor","Ptosis mydriasis anhidrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ptosis miosis anhidrosis from sympathetic chain disruption by a Pancoast tumor', 'Horner syndrome combines ipsilateral ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis caused by interruption of the sympathetic chain, classically by an apical Pancoast tumor. Hyperhidrosis is opposite the expected finding, and mydriasis indicates parasympathetic rather than sympathetic deficit.'),
('c2c960be-ef9a-589d-9c37-cbea1a28dadf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheal tumors in adults?', '["Benign tumors (hamartoma)","Secondary (direct invasion from lung or esophageal cancer)","Tracheal papilloma","Primary tracheal tumors (adenoid cystic carcinoma squamous cell)","Metastatic"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary (direct invasion from lung or esophageal cancer)', 'Tracheal tumors in adults are usually secondary, arising from direct invasion of adjacent lung or esophageal cancers rather than arising within the trachea itself. Primary tracheal tumors such as adenoid cystic carcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma are rare, and true metastases to the trachea are uncommon.'),
('85d0ce01-0fe0-551e-b677-7448c5cf7396'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common primary tracheal tumor?', '["Mucoepidermoid carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Sarcoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Squamous cell carcinoma (most common) and adenoid cystic carcinoma","Carcinoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma (most common) and adenoid cystic carcinoma', 'Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common primary tracheal tumor, while adenoid cystic carcinoma is the second most frequent, both arising from tracheal mucosa and glands. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma, carcinoid, and sarcoma are all far less common.'),
('8e6493d0-e1c0-579c-b30a-0d5acfbaae87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of mediastinitis in children?', '["Pneumonia resulting in decreased functional activity","Esophageal perforation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Descending infection from retropharyngeal abscess or deep neck infection","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac surgery (post-sternotomy) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Descending infection from retropharyngeal abscess or deep neck infection', 'In children mediastinitis most often descends from a deep neck or retropharyngeal infection spreading along fascial planes into the mediastinum. Esophageal perforation and post-sternotomy infection are more typical causes in adults, and pneumonia alone rarely causes mediastinitis.'),
('669b7761-6556-51c5-8a41-b6905e905ab2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of spontaneous pneumomediastinum in children?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Asthma exacerbation (air trapping causing alveolar rupture)","Foreign body mediated by specific receptor activation","Valsalva maneuver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vomiting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma exacerbation (air trapping causing alveolar rupture)', 'Spontaneous pneumomediastinum in children most often complicates an asthma exacerbation, where air trapping raises alveolar pressure causing rupture and dissection of air along the bronchovascular sheaths. Trauma, foreign bodies, and Valsalva maneuvers are less common triggers.'),
('e0bd7170-476f-55c3-8e4a-d8623e18b5fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of cor pulmonale in children?', '["CHD (Eisenmenger)","Neuromuscular disease (hypoventilation)","CHEST wall deformity (kyphoscoliosis)","Chronic lung disease (BPD CF bronchiectasis)","Idiopathic PAH"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic lung disease (BPD CF bronchiectasis)', 'Cor pulmonale in children most often results from chronic parenchymal lung disease such as bronchopulmonary dysplasia, cystic fibrosis, or bronchiectasis, which raise pulmonary vascular resistance and strain the right ventricle. Eisenmenger physiology and kyphoscoliosis are important but less common contributors.'),
('c2888f9f-3df6-599e-9c14-bd56e38cb7dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pneumothorax in patients with cystic fibrosis?', '["Pneumonia resulting in decreased functional activity","Mechanical ventilation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iatrogenic from line placement mediated by specific receptor activation","Rupture of a subpleural bleb (from severe bronchiectasis and air trapping)","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rupture of a subpleural bleb (from severe bronchiectasis and air trapping)', 'In cystic fibrosis, pneumothorax usually follows rupture of a subpleural bleb that forms because of severe bronchiectasis and air trapping. Mechanical ventilation and line placement are iatrogenic causes but not the leading one, and pneumonia resolves without provoking this complication.'),
('9fb0f027-7337-5480-80a6-08b0f66bf55b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hemoptysis in cystic fibrosis?', '["Coagulopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchial artery erosion from chronic infection and inflammation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary artery aneurysm (Rasmussen) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchial artery erosion from chronic infection and inflammation', 'Hemoptysis in cystic fibrosis most commonly comes from erosion of tortuous, hypertrophied bronchial arteries by chronic infection and inflammation. Rasmussen aneurysm reflects tuberculosis rather than CF, and coagulopathy or a tumor are not the usual mechanism.'),
('77bea720-3615-5fd2-9740-2b66d27f0173'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of massive hemoptysis in CF?', '["Bronchial artery hemorrhage (from hypertrophied bronchial arteries)","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary embolism mediated by specific receptor activation","Aneurysm rupture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchial artery hemorrhage (from hypertrophied bronchial arteries)', 'Massive hemoptysis in cystic fibrosis is caused by bleeding from hypertrophied systemic bronchial arteries that have developed behind chronically infected lung, and is treated by bronchial artery embolization. Trauma, pulmonary embolism, and tumors are not characteristic causes in CF.'),
('bf1b6279-e694-5296-bc53-a4dac2822a49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common lung transplant indication in children?', '["Bronchopulmonary dysplasia","Pulmonary hypertension","Surfactant protein deficiency","Cystic fibrosis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic fibrosis', 'Cystic fibrosis is the most common indication for lung transplantation in children, as end-stage suppurative lung disease eventually fails medical therapy. Pulmonary hypertension and surfactant protein deficiency are indications in specific subgroups but are far less frequent overall.'),
('b231b681-6cfb-5d19-bc0d-3968511ce029'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death after lung transplantation?', '["Primary graft dysfunction","Bronchiolitis obliterans (chronic rejection)","Acute rejection","Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD)","Infection (CMV)"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchiolitis obliterans (chronic rejection)', 'Bronchiolitis obliterans, reflecting chronic airway rejection, is the leading cause of late death after lung transplantation. Primary graft dysfunction and infection dominate early, while acute rejection responds to immunosuppression and PTLD is less common.'),
('d27dec8f-252d-5d31-9595-5e2ee0313aff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of respiratory failure in neuromuscular disease (Duchenne MD)?', '["Respiratory muscle weakness leading to hypoventilation and atelectasis","Aspiration pneumonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiomyopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Scoliosis restricting lung volume without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mucus plugging leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory muscle weakness leading to hypoventilation and atelectasis', 'In Duchenne muscular dystrophy respiratory failure results chiefly from progressive weakness of the diaphragm and intercostal muscles, causing hypoventilation and atelectasis. Aspiration, cardiomyopathy, and scoliosis contribute but are not the primary mechanism of ventilatory failure.'),
('9961dbba-1b62-5233-8e0e-0baffd0b8832'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death in Duchenne muscular dystrophy?', '["Cardiac arrest resulting in decreased functional activity","Thromboembolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Swallowing dysfunction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Respiratory failure (most common) followed by cardiac failure (cardiomyopathy)"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory failure (most common) followed by cardiac failure (cardiomyopathy)', 'Patients with Duchenne muscular dystrophy most often die of respiratory failure, with cardiac failure from cardiomyopathy the next most frequent cause. Pneumonia and swallowing dysfunction can be contributors, but ventilatory and cardiac failure are the dominant terminal events.'),
('da17f408-91ac-5836-bc8a-e0e600fa4738'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of respiratory distress in children with Guillain-Barré syndrome?', '["Pneumonia resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary embolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ascending paralysis involving respiratory muscles and diaphragm","Autonomic instability without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aspiration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending paralysis involving respiratory muscles and diaphragm', 'In Guillain-Barré syndrome, ascending demyelinating paralysis progressively weakens respiratory muscles and the diaphragm, leading to hypoventilation and respiratory distress. Pneumonia and aspiration complicate the course but arise secondarily, and pulmonary embolism is not the primary cause.'),
('87fb5456-360c-5575-b456-ff1ff3ebc109'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic hypoventilation in children?', '["Congenital central hypoventilation syndrome (Ondine curse)","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS)","Hypothyroidism","Kyphoscoliosis","Neuromuscular disease (DMD spinal muscular atrophy)"]'::jsonb, 'Obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS)', 'Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is the most common cause of chronic hypoventilation in children because obesity raises the work of breathing and blunts ventilatory responsiveness. Kyphoscoliosis and neuromuscular disease produce hypoventilation but less often, and Ondine''s curse is a rare congenital disorder.'),
('cdb95481-f92c-56c6-871b-b74ab739a770'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is congenital central hypoventilation syndrome (CCHS)?', '["A disorder of respiratory muscle weakness resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Surfactant deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autonomic nervous system disorder with inadequate response to hypercapnia and hypoxia requiring ventilatory support during sleep","A form of asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","OSA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic nervous system disorder with inadequate response to hypercapnia and hypoxia requiring ventilatory support during sleep', 'CCHS is a rare autonomic disorder in which the brainstem fails to drive ventilation in response to hypercapnia and hypoxia, so affected children require ventilatory support, particularly during sleep. It is not a muscle weakness, surfactant deficiency, asthma, or obstructive apnea.'),
('bf13c38e-a609-5d77-9f4d-01390dec5fe3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of acute hypoxemic respiratory failure (type I) in children?', '["CHF resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoventilation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary embolism mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia (ventilation-perfusion mismatch)","ARDS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumonia (ventilation-perfusion mismatch)', 'Type I hypoxemic respiratory failure in children is most commonly caused by pneumonia, where alveolar consolidation produces ventilation-perfusion mismatch and shunting. ARDS is a severe syndrome but is usually secondary to another trigger, while hypoventilation typifies type II failure.'),
('a7a1901e-24b3-547d-b22b-b36fc3a6f290'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hypercapnic respiratory failure (type II) in children?', '["Sepsis resulting in decreased functional activity","Pneumonia (severe) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ARDS mediated by specific receptor activation","Neuromuscular weakness and hypoventilation","Pulmonary edema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuromuscular weakness and hypoventilation', 'Type II hypercapnic respiratory failure in children most often results from neuromuscular weakness that causes hypoventilation and retained CO2. Severe pneumonia and pulmonary edema produce mainly hypoxemia, and ARDS is characterized by refractory hypoxemia rather than hypercapnia.'),
('db57121c-666d-5e5d-8043-68a3f9e8e974'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common mode of noninvasive ventilation (NIV) in children?', '["High-flow nasal cannula (HFNC)","BiPAP (bilevel positive airway pressure)","CPAP (continuous positive airway pressure)","Pressure support","Volume control"]'::jsonb, 'BiPAP (bilevel positive airway pressure)', 'BiPAP provides separate inspiratory and expiratory positive pressures, making it the most commonly used mode of noninvasive ventilation in children. HFNC and CPAP are used for oxygenation and airway pressure respectively, while pressure support and volume control are invasive ventilator modalities.'),
('66ea7a99-7624-5a68-b33b-f7dfcf8d95fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of oxygen dependency in premature infants?', '["Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD)","Atelectasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TTN mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RDS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD)', 'Bronchopulmonary dysplasia is the most common cause of prolonged oxygen dependency in preterm infants, reflecting chronic lung injury from prematurity, ventilation, and oxygen. RDS and TTN are early transient disorders, while BPD defines the chronic oxygen requirement.'),
('74d8655f-c41b-59df-b79d-58de7a012c5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary edema in the ICU?', '["ARDS (noncardiogenic) and fluid overload (cardiogenic)","CHF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary embolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Near-drowning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ARDS (noncardiogenic) and fluid overload (cardiogenic)', 'In the ICU pulmonary edema most commonly arises from ARDS as a noncardiogenic cause or from fluid overload producing cardiogenic edema. CHF is a specific cardiogenic example but part of the broader fluid-overload mechanism, while pneumonia, PE, and near-drowning are less frequent.'),
('be6b3887-8a53-5e52-9bc3-06d12ca0bf9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) in adults?', '["Burns resulting in decreased functional activity","Sepsis (most common) followed by pneumonia and aspiration","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Drug overdose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sepsis (most common) followed by pneumonia and aspiration', 'Sepsis is the leading cause of ARDS in adults, with severe pneumonia and aspiration the next most common direct and indirect triggers. Burns, trauma, pancreatitis, and drug overdose are recognized causes but occur with lower frequency.'),
('5e08a822-8c1d-517f-b523-a26ef457652e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the Berlin definition for ARDS?', '["Acute onset bilateral opacities not fully explained by effusions/atelectasis/nodules with PaO2/FiO2 ratio <300 mmHg and onset within 1 week","PaO2/FiO2 <100 with 4-quadrant infiltrates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PaO2/FiO2 <200 with bilateral infiltrates mediated by specific receptor activation","Acute hypoxemia requiring mechanical ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acute respiratory failure with PEEP >5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute onset bilateral opacities not fully explained by effusions/atelectasis/nodules with PaO2/FiO2 ratio <300 mmHg and onset within 1 week', 'The Berlin definition requires acute onset within one week, bilateral opacities not fully explained by effusions, collapse, or nodules, and a PaO2/FiO2 ratio below 300 mmHg. The lower PaO2/FiO2 cutoffs grading severity are part of the same definition but the thresholds alone do not define the syndrome.'),
('31f65219-a85c-5bd7-9cdb-1ebe987719d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hypoxemia in ARDS?', '["Shunt (intrapulmonary right-to-left shunting from alveolar flooding and collapse)","Diffusion impairment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","V/Q mismatch mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Low FiO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shunt (intrapulmonary right-to-left shunting from alveolar flooding and collapse)', 'Hypoxemia in ARDS is driven mainly by intrapulmonary shunt, as flooded and collapsed alveoli keep blood unoxygenated even at high FiO2. V/Q mismatch and diffusion impairment contribute but respond to oxygen, whereas true shunt is refractory to supplemental oxygen.'),
('f173ca34-17d3-55ed-96ef-7e8e57a2671e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of atelectasis after surgery?', '["Pain splinting with hypoventilation resulting in decreased functional activity","General anesthesia causing loss of functional residual capacity and airway closure","Positioning mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucus plugging without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pleural effusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'General anesthesia causing loss of functional residual capacity and airway closure', 'General anesthesia reduces functional residual capacity and promotes small airway closure, making it the most common cause of postoperative atelectasis. Pain splinting contributes, but the anesthesia-induced loss of lung volume is the primary mechanism.'),
('09517abb-4c8e-5cbd-8b1d-6fbaa55677ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of postoperative respiratory failure?', '["Pulmonary embolism resulting in decreased functional activity","ARDS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pre-existing lung disease (COPD) and atelectasis with retained secretions","Anesthetic complications without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-existing lung disease (COPD) and atelectasis with retained secretions', 'Postoperative respiratory failure most often develops in patients with pre-existing lung disease such as COPD, compounded by atelectasis and retained secretions that obstruct ventilation. ARDS and pneumonia are secondary complications, and anesthetic complications are uncommon causes of established failure.'),
('9e8eca18-4b5e-5b42-a3b3-a1d0e2986975'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary embolism (PE) in children?', '["Surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","CHD with right-to-left shunt accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Homocystinuria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep vein thrombosis (often from central venous catheters)"]'::jsonb, 'Deep vein thrombosis (often from central venous catheters)', 'In children pulmonary embolism most commonly results from deep vein thrombosis, often provoked by central venous catheters that induce upper-extremity thrombi. Surgery and trauma are risk factors in adults but are less prominent in pediatric PE, and homocystinuria is a rare prothrombotic cause.'),
('0eda9c49-5d9e-5290-a4b3-638bf7860a9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary embolism in adults?', '["Atrial fibrillation resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper extremity DVT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paradoxical embolus mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep vein thrombosis (usually lower extremity)","Right heart thrombus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep vein thrombosis (usually lower extremity)', 'Pulmonary embolism in adults almost always originates from a deep vein thrombosis, usually in the lower extremities. Upper extremity DVT and right heart thrombus are uncommon sources, and a paradoxical embolus or atrial fibrillation do not directly cause pulmonary emboli.'),
('c2b34eb7-6b48-547d-82d9-96ec97ffa4b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of paradoxical embolism?', '["Patent foramen ovale (PFO) allowing DVT to cross to the systemic circulation","PDA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASD mediated by specific receptor activation","VSD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AVSD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Patent foramen ovale (PFO) allowing DVT to cross to the systemic circulation', 'A patent foramen ovale is the commonest route for a venous embolus to pass into the arterial circulation, bypassing the pulmonary filter. Right-to-left shunting occurs when right atrial pressure transiently exceeds left, for example during Valsalva. PDA, ASD, VSD, and AVSD can also produce shunts, but PFO is the leading cause of paradoxical embolism.'),
('bf36d6c1-690d-59c3-bf4e-6bbb9947a000'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic pulmonary vein stenosis?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor compression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcoidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrosing mediastinitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congenital (often associated with prematurity) or post-atrial fibrillation ablation (acquired)"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital (often associated with prematurity) or post-atrial fibrillation ablation (acquired)', 'Congenital pulmonary vein stenosis, often seen in premature infants, and acquired narrowing after catheter ablation for atrial fibrillation account for most cases. Fibrosing mediastinitis, sarcoidosis, tumor compression, and trauma are much rarer causes.'),
('c3373b9d-c97b-576d-b607-cb82abb2b468'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of fibrosing mediastinitis?', '["Histoplasma capsulatum infection (most common in endemic areas)","Tuberculosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radiation therapy mediated by specific receptor activation","Sarcoidosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Histoplasma capsulatum infection (most common in endemic areas)', 'Histoplasma capsulatum is the leading cause, especially in endemic regions where mediastinal nodes calcify and encase vessels and airways. Tuberculosis, radiation, sarcoidosis, and idiopathic forms are less frequent.'),
('21f9a3c2-bd90-59ed-a5c3-a233cae72820'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheobronchomegaly (Mounier-Kuhn syndrome)?', '["Ehlers-Danlos syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Recurrent infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital deficiency of cartilaginous rings mediated by specific receptor activation","Atrophy of the elastic fibers and smooth muscle of the trachea and bronchi","COPD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrophy of the elastic fibers and smooth muscle of the trachea and bronchi', 'Mounier-Kuhn syndrome results from atrophy of the elastic fibers and smooth muscle of the trachea and main bronchi, causing marked luminal dilatation. Cartilage deficiency and recurrent infection may coexist but are not the primary underlying defect.'),
('7fadc474-7be2-5f4d-b8dc-6e99580ef8b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of primary pulmonary hypertension (PPH/IPAH) in adults?', '["Portal hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","CHD (Eisenmenger) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Connective tissue disease (scleroderma) mediated by specific receptor activation","HIV infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic (sporadic) and heritable (BMPR2 mutations) most common"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (sporadic) and heritable (BMPR2 mutations) most common', 'Idiopathic pulmonary hypertension is the most frequent form, and a minority of cases are heritable, linked to BMPR2 mutations. Portal hypertension, Eisenmenger physiology, connective tissue disease, and HIV are secondary causes of pulmonary hypertension rather than primary disease.'),
('25e9a57c-53c3-5151-9c24-695bd898d12f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of secondary pulmonary hypertension in adults?', '["Left heart disease (post-capillary PH group 2) like LV failure mitral valve disease","Pulmonary embolism (group 4) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcoidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Sleep-disordered breathing (OSA) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic lung disease (COPD group 3) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left heart disease (post-capillary PH group 2) like LV failure mitral valve disease', 'Left heart disease, such as left ventricular failure and mitral valve disease, is the commonest cause of secondary pulmonary hypertension, producing post-capillary (group 2) PH. Pulmonary embolism, OSA, sarcoidosis, and chronic lung disease are less common secondary causes.'),
('00ffd0d4-b0c0-5bc2-8440-6398e1efc409'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH group 1) in adults?', '["Portal hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Idiopathic PAH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CHD (Eisenmenger) mediated by specific receptor activation","HIV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connective tissue disease (scleroderma is the most common cause of PAH associated with CTD)"]'::jsonb, 'Connective tissue disease (scleroderma is the most common cause of PAH associated with CTD)', 'Among the associated forms of group 1 pulmonary arterial hypertension, connective tissue disease is the most frequent, with scleroderma the commonest CTD responsible. Idiopathic PAH, portal hypertension, congenital heart disease, and HIV are the other major causes in this group.'),
('10c0634e-dde7-5f14-9e74-a43694d3b631'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of alveolar hemorrhage syndrome?', '["Idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Vasculitis (GPA/granulomatosis with polyangiitis microscopic polyangiitis)","Coagulopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","SLE without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasculitis (GPA/granulomatosis with polyangiitis microscopic polyangiitis)', 'Vasculitides, especially granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis, are the most frequent cause of diffuse alveolar hemorrhage. Anti-GBM disease, SLE, coagulopathy, and idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis account for a smaller share.'),
('61a0d29a-1152-52d3-9df0-65855938e5c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of Goodpasture syndrome (anti-GBM disease)?', '["ANCA-associated vasculitis resulting in decreased functional activity","SLE accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autoantibodies against the NC1 domain of type IV collagen in the glomerular and alveolar basement membranes","HSP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgA vasculitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autoantibodies against the NC1 domain of type IV collagen in the glomerular and alveolar basement membranes', 'Anti-GBM disease is caused by autoantibodies directed against the NC1 domain of type IV collagen present in both glomerular and alveolar basement membranes. This immune attack produces rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis together with pulmonary hemorrhage.'),
('7ec26073-5e1d-5b4a-b584-f76b72ca7b50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary fibrosis in adults?', '["Occupational (asbestosis silicosis)","Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Connective tissue disease (scleroderma RA)","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF usual interstitial pneumonia pattern)","Drug-induced (amiodarone bleomycin)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF usual interstitial pneumonia pattern)', 'Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis with a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern is the most frequent cause of adult pulmonary fibrosis. Occupational dusts, connective tissue disease, chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and drugs are less common fibrotic causes.'),
('0294b600-d2b5-591d-8591-e309d492c605'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of occupational lung disease in adults?', '["Byssinosis (cotton) resulting in decreased functional activity","Berylliosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asbestosis (from asbestos exposure) and silicosis (from silica exposure)","Coal worker''s pneumoconiosis (black lung) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asbestosis (from asbestos exposure) and silicosis (from silica exposure)', 'Asbestosis and silicosis together represent the most common occupational pneumoconioses. Byssinosis, berylliosis, coal workers'' pneumoconiosis, and hypersensitivity pneumonitis occur less frequently.'),
('44374e08-cb63-52db-825a-3f0a81dd6794'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of mesothelioma?', '["Asbestos exposure (especially crocidolite and amosite fibers)","Simian virus 40 (SV40) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Genetic predisposition mediated by specific receptor activation","Radiation exposure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asbestos exposure (especially crocidolite and amosite fibers)', 'Asbestos exposure, particularly to crocidolite and amosite fibers, is the dominant cause of pleural mesothelioma, typically after a long latency period. SV40, radiation, and genetic predisposition play only minor roles.'),
('384ec8d4-d375-5342-b633-a5a91cd8e5dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of sarcoidosis?', '["Fungal infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Beryllium exposure (berylliosis) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unknown (likely environmental trigger in genetically susceptible individuals)","Mycobacterial infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autoimmune leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unknown (likely environmental trigger in genetically susceptible individuals)', 'The cause of sarcoidosis remains unknown and is thought to involve an environmental trigger acting on genetically susceptible individuals. Beryllium, fungal, and mycobacterial exposures can produce similar granulomas but are distinct diseases.'),
('574625a2-bd95-58d1-be9b-92f43043fcd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of eosinophilic pneumonia?', '["Vasculitis (Churg-Strauss EGPA)","Parasitic infection (Ascaris Strongyloides)","Drug-induced (antibiotics NSAIDs)","Fungal infection (Aspergillus ABPA)","Idiopathic (acute and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (acute and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia)', 'Idiopathic acute and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia are the most common forms. EGPA, parasitic infections, drug reactions, and ABPA are identifiable secondary causes of pulmonary eosinophilia.'),
('c906c62d-c785-5305-90b6-dae5c3d5a69c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA)?', '["A hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus in patients with asthma or CF causing airway inflammation and bronchiectasis","A fungal ball accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A simple aspergilloma mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Invasive aspergillosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A colonization without disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus in patients with asthma or CF causing airway inflammation and bronchiectasis', 'ABPA is a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus seen in asthma or cystic fibrosis, driving airway inflammation and central bronchiectasis. Unlike a fungal ball or invasive disease, it represents an immune-mediated response rather than tissue invasion.'),
('be8f6d89-0257-50a5-ab99-71b3e5ea3340'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What are the diagnostic criteria for ABPA?', '["Asthma or CF elevated IgE (>1000 IU/mL) positive Aspergillus skin test precipitating antibodies radiographic infiltrates/central bronchiectasis","Positive culture for Aspergillus alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exposure to Aspergillus alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis profile without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elevated IgG specific to Aspergillus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma or CF elevated IgE (>1000 IU/mL) positive Aspergillus skin test precipitating antibodies radiographic infiltrates/central bronchiectasis', 'Diagnosis requires asthma or CF together with markedly elevated IgE, a positive Aspergillus skin test, precipitating antibodies, and infiltrates with central bronchiectasis. A positive culture or mere exposure is not sufficient by itself.'),
('9e44e4bb-620a-546d-a298-952989afdcec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of invasive aspergillosis?', '["CF resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe immunosuppression (neutropenia stem cell transplant prolonged steroid use)","Healthy individual mediated by specific receptor activation","Diabetes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asthma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe immunosuppression (neutropenia stem cell transplant prolonged steroid use)', 'Profound immunosuppression, especially neutropenia, stem cell transplantation, and prolonged steroid use, predisposes to invasive aspergillosis. Healthy hosts and patients with asthma, CF, or diabetes alone rarely develop the invasive form.'),
('a1ca140d-c4d3-55b2-9fe2-e22018b3ea63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary mucormycosis?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","CF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uncontrolled diabetes mellitus (especially DKA) and immunocompromise","Allergic fungal sinusitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fungal ball in cavitary lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uncontrolled diabetes mellitus (especially DKA) and immunocompromise', 'Uncontrolled diabetes, particularly diabetic ketoacidosis, is the classic predisposition for pulmonary mucormycosis along with other immunocompromised states. The fungus invades vessel walls, causing thrombosis, infarction, and necrosis rather than a simple fungal ball.'),
('ecf5751c-3547-5af5-acd7-de50c536fb51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)?', '["HIV/AIDS with CD4 <200","Organ transplantation","Malignancy (leukemia)","Immunosuppressive therapy","Primary immunodeficiency (SCID)"]'::jsonb, 'HIV/AIDS with CD4 <200', 'PCP appears when cell-mediated immunity collapses, classically with HIV/AIDS and a CD4 count below 200. Transplantation, malignancy, immunosuppressive therapy, and primary immunodeficiency are less common settings.'),
('1fd1a8ab-775b-52eb-a81c-de7404312dbf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary tuberculosis?', '["Pleural TB","Primary infection (most cases are asymptomatic and contained)","Post-primary TB in immunocompromised","Reactivation of latent TB (most common cause of active TB in adults)","Miliary TB"]'::jsonb, 'Primary infection (most cases are asymptomatic and contained)', 'Primary infection is the most common form of pulmonary tuberculosis, and in most people it is asymptomatic and contained by the immune response. Reactivation of latent TB is the usual mechanism of active disease in adults, while miliary, pleural, and post-primary forms are less frequent.'),
('2519b467-404b-5389-948d-a009cbe01d30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death from tuberculosis?', '["Pericardial TB with tamponade resulting in decreased functional activity","Miliary TB with multisystem involvement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TB meningitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Untreated pulmonary TB causing progressive lung destruction and respiratory failure","Hemoptysis (Rasmussen aneurysm) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Untreated pulmonary TB causing progressive lung destruction and respiratory failure', 'Progressive untreated pulmonary TB destroys lung parenchyma and leads to respiratory failure, the most frequent fatal outcome. Miliary, meningeal, and pericardial forms, as well as massive hemoptysis, cause a smaller proportion of deaths.'),
('50a7c886-cf25-5a7d-a701-9ca4269a1450'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a lung abscess?', '["Septic emboli resulting in decreased functional activity","Pneumonia with necrosis (Staph aureus Klebsiella) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lung cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","TB without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aspiration of anaerobic bacteria (Bacteroides Fusobacterium Peptostreptococcus)"]'::jsonb, 'Aspiration of anaerobic bacteria (Bacteroides Fusobacterium Peptostreptococcus)', 'Aspiration of anaerobic mouth flora, including Bacteroides, Fusobacterium, and Peptostreptococcus, is the leading cause of lung abscess. Septic emboli, necrotizing pneumonias, cavitating tumors, and tuberculosis are less common causes.'),
('a4ad8a0f-d237-5d80-8ca7-12c8c9b3b21e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of necrotizing pneumonia in children?', '["Klebsiella pneumoniae","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus (especially MRSA)","Streptococcus pyogenes","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus aureus (especially MRSA)', 'Staphylococcus aureus, especially methicillin-resistant strains, is the leading cause of necrotizing pneumonia in children. Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Streptococcus pyogenes, and Pseudomonas are less frequent in this age group.'),
('82f96fd5-af9a-53b0-9246-6503fe6e628c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pneumatocele in children?', '["Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia (produces air-filled thin-walled cavities)","Klebsiella accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pseudomonas mediated by specific receptor activation","Streptococcus pneumoniae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TB leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia (produces air-filled thin-walled cavities)', 'Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia characteristically produces air-filled, thin-walled cavities called pneumatoceles in children. Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, Streptococcus pneumoniae, and tuberculosis are less typical causes.'),
('d35f05bb-9fcc-54b8-bc8f-5a6a2baded3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary edema in near-drowning?', '["Cardiac arrhythmia resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypothermia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Delayed neurologic injury mediated by specific receptor activation","Surfactant washout and alveolar flooding with fresh or salt water causing V/Q mismatch and ARDS","Laryngospasm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surfactant washout and alveolar flooding with fresh or salt water causing V/Q mismatch and ARDS', 'Aspirated water washes out surfactant and floods the alveoli, producing ventilation-perfusion mismatch and ARDS-like edema. Arrhythmia, hypothermia, laryngospasm, and delayed neurologic injury are separate mechanisms of drowning-related morbidity.'),
('23d3cf5c-9582-5ff6-ba08-d39b67b23b09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of acute mountain sickness (AMS)?', '["Pulmonary edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapid ascent to high altitude (>2500 m) causing hypobaric hypoxia","Cerebral edema mediated by specific receptor activation","Sun exposure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dehydration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid ascent to high altitude (>2500 m) causing hypobaric hypoxia', 'Rapid ascent above about 2500 m produces hypobaric hypoxia, the fundamental trigger of acute mountain sickness. High-altitude pulmonary and cerebral edema are separate severe complications, while sun exposure and dehydration play minor roles.'),
('68ccc522-431d-5a7f-ab73-be2e941cf3d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is high-altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE)?', '["Diving-related edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Flash pulmonary edema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac asthma mediated by specific receptor activation","Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema from exaggerated hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction causing capillary leak","High-altitude headache leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema from exaggerated hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction causing capillary leak', 'HAPE is non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema caused by exaggerated hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction with capillary leak. It does not arise from left heart failure, and it differs from diving-related, cardiac, or compressive forms of edema.'),
('0c5d48d8-236e-50d0-af31-775c398563a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of drowning in children?', '["Alcohol use resulting in decreased functional activity","Child abuse accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Seizure mediated by specific receptor activation","Suicide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lack of supervision in pools or open water (toddlers and adolescents)"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of supervision in pools or open water (toddlers and adolescents)', 'Toddlers and adolescents drown mainly when supervision is lacking near pools or open bodies of water. Alcohol use, seizures, abuse, and suicide account for far fewer childhood drownings.'),
('133a4504-bb64-5ffd-b0d8-354bd05ad101'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death from drowning?', '["Pulmonary edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac arrhythmia mediated by specific receptor activation","Electrolyte imbalance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoxia with irreversible brain damage (cerebral hypoxia)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxia with irreversible brain damage (cerebral hypoxia)', 'Fatal drowning is driven by hypoxia that produces irreversible cerebral damage. Pulmonary edema, arrhythmia, infection, and electrolyte disturbances are secondary contributors to drowning mortality.'),
('dab39d8e-5316-549a-8e4d-b167f8b98179'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a pulmonary arteriovenous malformation (AVM)?', '["Malignancy resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic mediated by specific receptor activation","Cirrhosis (hepatopulmonary syndrome) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT Osler-Weber-Rendu disease)"]'::jsonb, 'Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT Osler-Weber-Rendu disease)', 'Most pulmonary arteriovenous malformations occur in hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Osler-Weber-Rendu disease). Cirrhosis-related hepatopulmonary shunts, trauma, malignancy, and sporadic forms are less common causes.'),
('900422f8-2bd9-5525-9e0a-05c493c265c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hepatopulmonary syndrome?', '["Portal vein thrombosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute liver failure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cirrhosis with portal hypertension causing intrapulmonary vasodilation and shunt","Budd-Chiari without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic hepatitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cirrhosis with portal hypertension causing intrapulmonary vasodilation and shunt', 'Cirrhosis with portal hypertension causes intrapulmonary vasodilation and right-to-left shunting, the hallmark of hepatopulmonary syndrome. Portal vein thrombosis, Budd-Chiari, acute liver failure, and chronic hepatitis are less common underlying causes.'),
('6110b834-cec4-5709-bc06-3b4022afc2b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of platypnea-orthodeoxia syndrome?', '["ARDS resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatopulmonary syndrome (worse oxygenation in upright position improved supine)","Pulmonary embolism mediated by specific receptor activation","PFO with right-to-left shunt without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary AVM leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatopulmonary syndrome (worse oxygenation in upright position improved supine)', 'Hepatopulmonary syndrome is the classic cause of platypnea-orthodeoxia, with oxygenation worse when upright and improved when supine as lower-lobe shunting increases. ARDS, pulmonary embolism, PFO, and pulmonary AVM are rarer causes.'),
('0b0a28c6-1357-59af-9f4c-9e67b607d4f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death from chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)?', '["Cachexia resulting in decreased functional activity","Lung cancer (common in smokers) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary hypertension with cor pulmonale mediated by specific receptor activation","Respiratory failure (acute exacerbations and progressive decline)","Cardiovascular disease (MI stroke) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory failure (acute exacerbations and progressive decline)', 'End-stage COPD most often ends in respiratory failure from acute exacerbations and progressive decline in lung function. Cor pulmonale, lung cancer, cachexia, and cardiovascular disease contribute less frequently as terminal events.'),
('840f2acb-3f6c-5ba8-9b1e-e40c37ca8c0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of COPD exacerbation?', '["Pulmonary embolism resulting in decreased functional activity","Exercise accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Non-compliance with medications mediated by specific receptor activation","Respiratory infection (viral and bacterial)","Air pollution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory infection (viral and bacterial)', 'Respiratory viral and bacterial infections trigger the majority of COPD exacerbations. Air pollution and non-compliance with medications contribute but are less frequent precipitating factors.'),
('cfa1a150-3d98-5111-8412-43ed57027d7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of emphysema?', '["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (panacinar)","Cigarette smoking (centriacinar and panacinar emphysema)","Connective tissue disease (Ehlers-Danlos)","Smoking marijuana","IV drug use (talc)"]'::jsonb, 'Cigarette smoking (centriacinar and panacinar emphysema)', 'Cigarette smoking is by far the most common cause of emphysema, producing predominantly centriacinar disease. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency causes panacinar emphysema and is much rarer, while connective tissue disease and talc exposure are uncommon.'),
('49a85030-8b41-5f43-b7a4-30507ce65970'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common type of emphysema in COPD?', '["Panacinar (panlobular) (associated with AAT deficiency)","Centriacinar (centrilobular) emphysema (associated with smoking)","Irregular (paracicatricial)","Bullous emphysema","Paraseptal (distal acinar)"]'::jsonb, 'Centriacinar (centrilobular) emphysema (associated with smoking)', 'Centriacinar (centrilobular) emphysema, linked to smoking, is the most common type in COPD and typically affects the upper zones. Panacinar emphysema is associated with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, while paraseptal, bullous, and irregular forms are less common.'),
('58c64cee-4d62-5cd1-b044-9c137320ccbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pseudomonas colonization in COPD patients?', '["Repeated antibiotic courses resulting in decreased functional activity","Steroid use accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic lung disease with impaired mucociliary clearance and structural damage","COPD severity (low FEV1) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smoking leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic lung disease with impaired mucociliary clearance and structural damage', 'Advanced chronic lung disease with impaired mucociliary clearance and structural airway damage favors persistent Pseudomonas colonization. Repeated antibiotics, steroid use, and low FEV1 are associated markers, not the underlying cause.'),
('fb59888d-fdf5-5284-b44e-3222b09eaeb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of lung volume reduction surgery (LVRS) qualifying emphysema?', '["Homogeneous emphysema resulting in decreased functional activity","Panacinar emphysema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bullous emphysema mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper lobe predominant emphysema with low exercise capacity after pulmonary rehabilitation","Lower lobe predominant emphysema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper lobe predominant emphysema with low exercise capacity after pulmonary rehabilitation', 'Candidates for lung volume reduction surgery show upper lobe predominant emphysema and low exercise capacity despite pulmonary rehabilitation. Homogeneous, panacinar, bullous, or lower-lobe-dominant disease is less suitable for this intervention.'),
('4176fc44-a1d4-5ebf-8068-e018eceee293'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of bullous emphysema?', '["Ehlers-Danlos resulting in decreased functional activity","Cigarette smoking causing paraseptal emphysema with bullae formation","Connective tissue disease (Marfan) mediated by specific receptor activation","IV drug use (talc) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cigarette smoking causing paraseptal emphysema with bullae formation', 'Smoking-related paraseptal emphysema is the usual basis of bullous disease, with bullae forming along the lung periphery. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, connective tissue disorders, and talc exposure are uncommon causes.'),
('b27439a2-baf9-531b-b96a-e6330bbeaff2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of spontaneous pneumothorax?', '["Tuberculosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Rupture of an apical bleb (subpleural bulla) usually in tall thin young men","Pneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","COPD with bullae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rupture of an apical bleb (subpleural bulla) usually in tall thin young men', 'Primary spontaneous pneumothorax follows rupture of an apical subpleural bleb, typically in tall, thin young men. COPD, tuberculosis, pneumonia, and trauma produce secondary or traumatic pneumothoraces through different mechanisms.'),
('f196a5d0-73c4-5737-8ff0-e00c846d3ff9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of secondary spontaneous pneumothorax?', '["Endometriosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Cystic fibrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","COPD with bullae (most common cause of secondary pneumothorax)","Lung cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumonia (TB PCP) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'COPD with bullae (most common cause of secondary pneumothorax)', 'COPD with ruptured bullae is the most frequent cause of secondary spontaneous pneumothorax. Cystic fibrosis, lung cancer, and necrotizing pneumonias such as TB and PCP are less common underlying diseases.'),
('0039d5d3-8b43-52c2-803f-d9675352084f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of life-threatening tension pneumothorax?', '["Mechanical ventilation in ARDS resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma (blunt or penetrating) with positive pressure ventilation","Spontaneous pneumothorax with one-way valve mediated by specific receptor activation","CPAP/BiPAP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Status asthmaticus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma (blunt or penetrating) with positive pressure ventilation', 'Blunt or penetrating trauma, particularly when combined with positive pressure ventilation, is the usual cause of life-threatening tension pneumothorax. A one-way valve mechanism can also convert a simple pneumothorax into a tension state with mediastinal shift.'),
('2abbdc9a-212f-5177-8868-0ba5a54415b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary contusion?', '["Fall resulting in decreased functional activity","Blunt chest trauma (MVA most common)","Penetrating trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Blast injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Crush injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blunt chest trauma (MVA most common)', 'Blunt chest trauma, most often from motor vehicle accidents, is the leading cause of pulmonary contusion. Penetrating injuries, falls, blasts, and crush injuries are less common mechanisms.'),
('3cf28f8c-5b33-53e2-a658-cef96733ba6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of traumatic asphyxia?', '["Severe compression of the chest (MVA crush injury) causing facial edema petechiae and cyanosis","Pulmonary contusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pneumothorax mediated by specific receptor activation","Airway obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flail chest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe compression of the chest (MVA crush injury) causing facial edema petechiae and cyanosis', 'Severe chest compression, as in crush injuries, forces blood from the right heart into the head and neck, producing facial edema, petechiae, and cyanosis. Pneumothorax, contusion, airway obstruction, and flail chest are separate thoracic injuries.'),
('5e371036-707b-56fb-aa12-ad853b3ed9e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of flail chest?', '["Single rib fracture with paradoxical motion (rare) resulting in decreased functional activity","Sternoclavicular dislocation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple rib fractures (at least 2 sites on 2 or more adjacent ribs) from blunt trauma","Costochondral separation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sternocostal separation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple rib fractures (at least 2 sites on 2 or more adjacent ribs) from blunt trauma', 'Flail chest results from multiple rib fractures at two or more sites on at least two adjacent ribs, creating a paradoxical moving segment. Single fractures, dislocations, and costochondral separations do not produce a flail segment.'),
('71223c86-43a3-5155-a6a6-a0ee8b4991f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of diaphragmatic rupture?', '["Blunt trauma (MVA with sudden increase in intra-abdominal pressure)","Surgery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hernia mediated by specific receptor activation","Penetrating trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spontaneous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blunt trauma (MVA with sudden increase in intra-abdominal pressure)', 'Blunt trauma, classically from a high-speed motor vehicle accident that suddenly raises intra-abdominal pressure, is the most frequent cause of diaphragmatic rupture. Penetrating injuries, surgery, hernias, and spontaneous rupture are rare.'),
('89d06be6-ad2b-5a8d-be20-37f7e1867e1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of lung herniation?', '["Chest wall defect","Chest trauma (surgical or accidental)","COPD with chronic cough","Congenital","Connective tissue disease"]'::jsonb, 'Chest trauma (surgical or accidental)', 'Prior chest trauma, surgical or accidental, is the most common cause of lung herniation through a chest wall weakness. Congenital defects, severe coughing in COPD, and connective tissue disease are less common causes.'),
('0c1586b9-be3e-5936-8c17-9b2a10f9d48a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of superior vena cava (SVC) syndrome in children?', '["Mediastinal mass (lymphoma) resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiac surgery (Mustard/Senning central line)","Aortic aneurysm mediated by specific receptor activation","Thrombosis SVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medastinal infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac surgery (Mustard/Senning central line)', 'In children, SVC syndrome most often follows cardiac surgery such as Mustard or Senning procedures, or central venous lines that cause thrombosis. Mediastinal masses, aortic aneurysm, and mediastinal infection are less common pediatric causes.'),
('8211febe-2cc7-5a7f-86f3-4e8817eda33a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of SVC syndrome in adults?', '["Aortic aneurysm resulting in decreased functional activity","Thymoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lung cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchogenic carcinoma (most common) followed by lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchogenic carcinoma (most common) followed by lymphoma', 'Bronchogenic carcinoma is the most common cause of SVC syndrome in adults, with lymphoma the second. Aortic aneurysm and thymoma are much less frequent causes of SVC obstruction.'),
('1729f814-2e02-5d96-82a8-1a79640901ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheal compression in neonates?', '["Mediastinal mass (teratoma) resulting in decreased functional activity","Vascular ring (double aortic arch or right arch with aberrant left subclavian)","Aberrant innominate artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphadenopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enlarged thymus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular ring (double aortic arch or right arch with aberrant left subclavian)', 'A vascular ring, such as a double aortic arch or right arch with an aberrant left subclavian artery, is the most common cause of tracheal compression in neonates. Aberrant innominate artery, thymic enlargement, lymphadenopathy, and mediastinal masses are less common.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('f7253792-c150-5823-88de-9bc7d95300f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent apnea of prematurity?', '["Immature respiratory control (central apnea) with periodic breathing","Anemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metabolic disorder mediated by specific receptor activation","Sepsis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obstruction from adenoids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immature respiratory control (central apnea) with periodic breathing', 'Apnea of prematurity arises from immature central respiratory control, which produces periodic breathing and central apneas that resolve as the infant matures. Sepsis, anemia, and metabolic derangements can worsen apnea but are secondary, and adenoid obstruction is not a relevant cause in premature neonates.'),
('40df77cd-168f-5686-8917-dae24fcda158'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death in children with bronchiolitis obliterans?', '["Pulmonary hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Air leak accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchiectasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Secondary infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Respiratory failure from progressive obstructive lung disease"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory failure from progressive obstructive lung disease', 'Bronchiolitis obliterans progressively destroys small airways through fibrosis, producing irreversible obstructive lung disease whose end stage is respiratory failure, the leading cause of death. Pulmonary hypertension and secondary infection complicate advanced disease but are not the dominant terminal event.'),
('035642a1-2d5a-599b-9478-a86433532e4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of bronchiolitis obliterans in children?', '["BMT (GVHD) resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-infectious (adenovirus type 21 most common post-infectious cause)","Stevens-Johnson syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung transplant (chronic rejection) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connective tissue disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-infectious (adenovirus type 21 most common post-infectious cause)', 'Most childhood bronchiolitis obliterans follows a severe lower respiratory tract infection, and adenovirus, particularly type 21, is the leading post-infectious trigger. BMT, lung transplantation, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, and connective tissue disease are recognized but less common causes.'),
('b22d76c9-7408-5bf8-b971-18129f29d4c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome?', '["MacLeod syndrome is a variant of COPA resulting in decreased functional activity","Congenital lobar emphysema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CF with unilateral disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Unilateral hyperlucent lung from post-infectious bronchiolitis obliterans in childhood","Unilateral emphysema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unilateral hyperlucent lung from post-infectious bronchiolitis obliterans in childhood', 'Swyer-James-MacLeod syndrome results from childhood bronchiolitis obliterans, typically after adenoviral infection, which destroys the affected lung''s capillary bed and produces hyperinflation, yielding a unilateral hyperlucent lung. The other choices do not produce this characteristic post-infectious process.'),
('2620b22a-bcc6-579f-9285-dbce09456a20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of congenital lobar emphysema (CLE)?', '["Bronchial cartilage deficiency causing collapse and air trapping in the affected lobe","Inflammatory bronchial stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchial atresia mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucous plug without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extrinsic compression (aberrant vessel) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchial cartilage deficiency causing collapse and air trapping in the affected lobe', 'In most cases of congenital lobar emphysema, deficient bronchial cartilage allows the airway to collapse during expiration, creating a ball-valve effect with air trapping that overdistends the lobe. Bronchial atresia, mucus plugging, and extrinsic vessel compression are less common alternative mechanisms.'),
('cd510dc8-f8df-512d-9b37-3d07a29805b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common lobe affected in congenital lobar emphysema?', '["Multiple lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","Right upper lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left lower lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Right lower lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left upper lobe (most common) then right middle lobe and right upper lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Left upper lobe (most common) then right middle lobe and right upper lobe', 'Congenital lobar emphysema most often affects a single lobe, with the left upper lobe the most common site, followed by the right middle and right upper lobes; lower lobes are rarely involved. The option naming multiple lobes as the predominant pattern is therefore not typical.'),
('f4870bff-999f-5936-ac14-3c96533ddc12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary interstitial emphysema (PIE) in neonates?', '["Meconium aspiration resulting in decreased functional activity","Positive pressure ventilation in premature lungs (air dissects into the interstitium)","Surfactant deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Birth trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Positive pressure ventilation in premature lungs (air dissects into the interstitium)', 'Positive pressure ventilation in the immature premature lung can rupture overdistended peripheral alveoli, forcing air along the interstitium and lymphatics to produce pulmonary interstitial emphysema. Surfactant deficiency and meconium aspiration set the stage for air leak but do not themselves drive air into the interstitium.'),
('4ac5a77b-b2d3-5491-bfe0-c51f2ef4ea3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of alveolar capillary dysplasia?', '["Surfactant deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","FOXF1 mutations identified in some cases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tracheoesophageal fistula mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital (genetic) disorder of alveolar development with misaligned pulmonary veins causing persistent pulmonary hypertension","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital (genetic) disorder of alveolar development with misaligned pulmonary veins causing persistent pulmonary hypertension', 'Alveolar capillary dysplasia is a congenital failure of alveolar capillary formation in which the veins course within interlobular septa, causing misalignment with the capillary network and persistent pulmonary hypertension in the newborn. FOXF1 mutations are found in many affected infants but represent a genetic correlate rather than the disorder itself.'),
('b42ac5bd-b247-587e-ad3f-f9049eb9999e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death in infants with alveolar capillary dysplasia?', '["CHD resulting in decreased functional activity","RDS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sepsis mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumothorax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Refractory pulmonary hypertension unresponsive to treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Refractory pulmonary hypertension unresponsive to treatment', 'In alveolar capillary dysplasia the abnormal vascular development produces severe pulmonary hypertension that fails to respond to inhaled nitric oxide, oxygen, or ECMO, and this refractory hypertension is the usual cause of death. Pneumothorax and sepsis may complicate the course but are not the dominant terminal event.'),
('672572bb-4c5f-5880-8df9-f276faea69a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of lung cyst in children?', '["Congenital pulmonary airway malformation (CPAM)","Pulmonary sequestration","Post-infectious pneumatocele","Pneumatocele from staph pneumonia","Bronchogenic cyst"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital pulmonary airway malformation (CPAM)', 'Congenital pulmonary airway malformation is the most common congenital cystic lung lesion in children, appearing as a multicystic mass that may be detected on antenatal ultrasound. Post-infectious pneumatoceles and bronchogenic cysts occur less frequently than CPAM.'),
('f3254938-78a0-5aa6-ab31-27ad173f0b9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of acquired lung cysts in children?', '["ABPA (allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis)","Tuberculosis (TB)","Pneumatocele from staphylococcal pneumonia","Trauma","Cystic fibrosis (blebs)"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumatocele from staphylococcal pneumonia', 'Acquired lung cysts in children are usually pneumatoceles, thin-walled air-filled cavities that complicate staphylococcal pneumonia and typically resolve spontaneously over weeks. ABPA, TB, trauma, and CF disease can produce cystic changes but are less common causes.'),
('ab7828fc-4107-5cf3-ab20-c211766fb08b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hyperlucent lung on CXR in children?', '["Pneumothorax","Congenital lobar emphysema","Pulmonary artery agenesis (congenital unilateral absence of the PA)","Swyer-James syndrome (post-infectious bronchiolitis obliterans)","Compensatory hyperinflation"]'::jsonb, 'Swyer-James syndrome (post-infectious bronchiolitis obliterans)', 'A hyperlucent lung on a child''s chest radiograph most often reflects Swyer-James syndrome, in which post-infectious bronchiolitis obliterans causes air trapping and reduced perfusion of one lung. Pneumothorax and congenital lobar emphysema also cause hyperlucency but are less common overall.'),
('fb4bf381-01ee-5458-bf33-632cb41c5ee2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary artery agenesis?', '["Fibrosing mediastinitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Acquired from trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital (usually right PA) with associated anomalies","Takayasu arteritis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complication of BT shunt leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital (usually right PA) with associated anomalies', 'Pulmonary artery agenesis is usually congenital, affects the right pulmonary artery more often than the left, and is frequently accompanied by other cardiovascular anomalies. Fibrosing mediastinitis and Takayasu arteritis are acquired processes that only rarely result in unilateral absence of a pulmonary artery.'),
('509ed170-27c9-57bb-96bf-a2fcf4b3cd8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary vein stenosis in children?', '["Post-atrial fibrillation ablation (acquired in adults)","Tumor compression","Anomalous pulmonary venous return repair","Fibrosing mediastinitis","Congenital PVS (often with prematurity VSD)"]'::jsonb, 'Anomalous pulmonary venous return repair', 'In children, pulmonary vein stenosis most often develops secondarily after surgical repair of anomalous pulmonary venous connection, with obstruction at or near the anastomosis. Congenital primary PVS also occurs but is less common, while post-ablation stenosis is an adult cause of pulmonary vein stenosis.'),
('302fe8fb-0f20-5ccb-bd85-1b71c1763e77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death in children with pulmonary vein stenosis?', '["RDS resulting in decreased functional activity","Progressive PVS causing pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure","CHD mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thromboembolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive PVS causing pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure', 'Progressive pulmonary vein stenosis raises pulmonary venous and arterial pressures, culminating in pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure, and this progressive course is the leading cause of death. RDS and pneumonia are not direct consequences of the venous obstruction.'),
('03683de4-8ee9-5626-8fab-9ec8899a9b5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary artery sling?', '["Aberrant right subclavian artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Innominate artery compression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The left pulmonary artery arises from the right pulmonary artery (aberrant) and passes between the trachea and esophagus","Right aortic arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Double aortic arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left pulmonary artery arises from the right pulmonary artery (aberrant) and passes between the trachea and esophagus', 'A pulmonary artery sling is a congenital anomaly in which the left pulmonary artery arises from the right and passes between the trachea and esophagus, often compressing the trachea. The other vascular anomalies produce compression by different mechanisms or are unrelated.'),
('31d2f3e0-ed78-587b-9a95-fdde365ff04c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheal stenosis in a child with pulmonary artery sling?', '["Subglottic stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Tracheomalacia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tracheoesophageal fistula mediated by specific receptor activation","Tracheal web without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete tracheal rings (long segment tracheal stenosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Complete tracheal rings (long segment tracheal stenosis)', 'In most children with a pulmonary artery sling, tracheal obstruction results from complete tracheal rings producing long-segment tracheal stenosis. Subglottic stenosis, tracheomalacia, webs, and tracheoesophageal fistula are not characteristic of the sling.'),
('e9a3b345-7649-5560-b0cc-f355c31bf676'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of right aortic arch?', '["Persistent fifth arch resulting in decreased functional activity","The aortic arch passes over the right bronchus (common) often associated with congenital heart disease (TOF truncus arteriosus)","Left aortic arch with aberrant right subclavian mediated by specific receptor activation","Double aortic arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cervical arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The aortic arch passes over the right bronchus (common) often associated with congenital heart disease (TOF truncus arteriosus)', 'A right aortic arch usually results from persistence of the right fourth aortic arch, and it commonly accompanies congenital heart defects, especially tetralogy of Fallot and truncus arteriosus. The other arch anomalies are distinct and less common configurations.'),
('aa9716cd-0ef6-59a1-b68a-ed75f2e1f0b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common symptom of a vascular ring?', '["Feeding difficulty resulting in decreased functional activity","Stridor (most common) and dysphagia (less common)","Wheezing mediated by specific receptor activation","Recurrent pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cough leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stridor (most common) and dysphagia (less common)', 'A vascular ring compresses the trachea and esophagus, so stridor, reflecting tracheal compression, is the most common symptom, with dysphagia from esophageal compression seen less often. Recurrent pneumonia, cough, and wheezing are less frequent or nonspecific presentations.'),
('b678da80-73ea-551d-9596-aab57bf0b15a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of dysphagia lusoria?', '["Double aortic arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Left innominate artery compression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary artery sling mediated by specific receptor activation","Right aortic arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aberrant right subclavian artery (arteria lusoria) compressing the esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Aberrant right subclavian artery (arteria lusoria) compressing the esophagus', 'Dysphagia lusoria is caused by an aberrant right subclavian artery, known as the arteria lusoria, which courses behind the esophagus and compresses it. Double aortic arch and pulmonary artery sling produce dysphagia through different vascular arrangements.'),
('530365fb-708f-50dd-b9a1-379c9fd90046'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of respiratory distress in the delivery room?', '["Retained fetal lung fluid (TTN) and delayed transition","Meconium aspiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CHD mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RDS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retained fetal lung fluid (TTN) and delayed transition', 'In the delivery room, retained fetal lung fluid with a delayed transition of the airspaces, i.e. transient tachypnea of the newborn, is the most common cause of transient respiratory distress in the term newborn. Meconium aspiration, RDS, pneumonia, and congenital heart disease are less common causes of early distress.'),
('d88ed26c-bc6e-5516-897a-18091f7e2035'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of respiratory failure in the first week of life?', '["Meconium aspiration","CHD","RDS in preterm infants","Pneumonia","Pulmonary hypoplasia (CDH)"]'::jsonb, 'RDS in preterm infants', 'In preterm infants the deficiency of surfactant causes respiratory distress syndrome, the most frequent cause of respiratory failure in the first week of life. Meconium aspiration, pneumonia, and pulmonary hypoplasia with congenital diaphragmatic hernia are other, less common neonatal causes.'),
('95e4a6ac-023e-5552-9fa2-723d3efb70b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic respiratory failure in children?', '["Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD)","Neuromuscular disease (DMD SMA)","Central hypoventilation","Restrictive chest wall disease","Cystic fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Neuromuscular disease (DMD SMA)', 'Among children, neuromuscular diseases such as Duchenne muscular dystrophy and spinal muscular atrophy are the most common cause of chronic respiratory failure from progressive respiratory muscle weakness. BPD, cystic fibrosis, and chest wall restriction account for a minority of chronic failure cases.'),
('ecc6942b-4645-53ec-a262-31ef2ea28525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of need for home mechanical ventilation in children?', '["Neuromuscular disease (most common)","Upper airway obstruction (OSA)","Chronic lung disease (BPD)","Kyphoscoliosis","Central hypoventilation (CCHS)"]'::jsonb, 'Neuromuscular disease (most common)', 'Neuromuscular disease is the leading indication for long-term home mechanical ventilation in children because progressive respiratory muscle weakness eventually requires ventilatory support. Upper airway obstruction, chronic lung disease, kyphoscoliosis, and central hypoventilation are less frequent reasons.'),
('cfef780e-9739-52fe-b95f-16f8e57db105'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy after cardiac surgery?', '["Injury during PDA ligation or coarctation repair","Intubation injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CPB cannulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Central line placement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thymectomy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Injury during PDA ligation or coarctation repair', 'During PDA ligation or coarctation repair, the left recurrent laryngeal nerve can be injured because it loops around the aorta and ductus in the surgical field, producing unilateral vocal cord palsy. Intubation injury, cannulation, central lines, and thymectomy are less common intraoperative causes.'),
('cd29afb8-9bdb-5435-8efe-cb42326d09fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of diaphragmatic paralysis in the newborn?', '["Neuromuscular disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Birth trauma (phrenic nerve injury from shoulder dystocia or breech delivery)","Cardiac surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital CMV infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Birth trauma (phrenic nerve injury from shoulder dystocia or breech delivery)', 'Traction on the neck during shoulder dystocia or breech delivery can stretch the phrenic nerve, causing unilateral diaphragmatic paralysis in the newborn. Neuromuscular disease, cardiac surgery, CMV, and spinal cord injury are rarer causes of newborn diaphragmatic palsy.'),
('35bdfd54-c157-5708-93c5-922fe0dc873f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of unilateral diaphragmatic paralysis in adults?', '["Trauma (blunt or iatrogenic) resulting in decreased functional activity","Stroke accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Viral infection (herpes zoster) mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiac surgery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malignancy (invasion of the phrenic nerve by lung cancer or mediastinal mass)"]'::jsonb, 'Malignancy (invasion of the phrenic nerve by lung cancer or mediastinal mass)', 'In adults, unilateral phrenic nerve paralysis most commonly results from malignant invasion of the nerve by a lung cancer or a mediastinal mass. Trauma, cardiac surgery, stroke, and viral infection are recognized but much less frequent causes.'),
('cae5e1c5-ccde-5b38-9ad1-8288eb9fd08f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of bilateral diaphragmatic paralysis in children?', '["Congenital CMV resulting in decreased functional activity","Central hypoventilation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac surgery with cold injury mediated by specific receptor activation","Neuromuscular disease (spinal muscular atrophy muscular dystrophy)","Spinal cord injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuromuscular disease (spinal muscular atrophy muscular dystrophy)', 'Bilateral diaphragmatic paralysis in children is most often caused by neuromuscular disease such as spinal muscular atrophy or muscular dystrophy, which weakens the diaphragm on both sides. Cold injury during cardiac surgery, central hypoventilation, and spinal cord injury are less common causes.'),
('75d8a60f-ed4e-51e2-9750-588690dd2c2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of congenital laryngeal web?', '["Failure of the laryngeal lumen to recanalize during embryogenesis (glottic web involves the anterior larynx)","Post-intubation injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laryngomalacia mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vocal cord paralysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the laryngeal lumen to recanalize during embryogenesis (glottic web involves the anterior larynx)', 'A congenital laryngeal web results from failure of the embryonic laryngeal lumen to recanalize, leaving a membrane that typically lies in the anterior larynx at the glottic level. Post-intubation injury, laryngomalacia, infection, and vocal cord paralysis are acquired or unrelated causes.'),
('b27021ac-26e3-5b39-96e1-37d2813d6fa5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common location of a congenital laryngeal web?', '["Anterior glottis (between the vocal cords at the anterior commissure)","Subglottis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Supraglottis mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior glottis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aryepiglottic fold leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior glottis (between the vocal cords at the anterior commissure)', 'Congenital laryngeal webs characteristically sit at the anterior commissure, extending between the anterior portions of the vocal cords at the glottic level. The posterior glottis, subglottis, supraglottis, and aryepiglottic fold are not the typical sites.'),
('690fa56d-5bd0-542d-a662-539dfab2a68d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of subglottic stenosis in children?', '["Laryngeal web resulting in decreased functional activity","Vocal cord paralysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acquired (prolonged intubation) mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital (primary subglottic stenosis from abnormal cricoid cartilage development)","Subglottic hemangioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital (primary subglottic stenosis from abnormal cricoid cartilage development)', 'Congenital subglottic stenosis arises from abnormal development of the cricoid cartilage, making it the most common cause of subglottic stenosis in children. Laryngeal web, vocal cord paralysis, and subglottic hemangioma are less common; prolonged intubation is the usual acquired cause.'),
('7dee7c4b-d905-58cb-9fab-0f93557ee8e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of acquired subglottic stenosis in children?', '["Prolonged endotracheal intubation (most common acquired cause)","Radiation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tracheostomy placement mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhalation injury (caustic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laryngeal trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged endotracheal intubation (most common acquired cause)', 'Prolonged endotracheal intubation is the leading acquired cause of subglottic stenosis because cuff pressure and mucosal ischemia lead to fibrosis and narrowing of the subglottic airway. Radiation, tracheostomy, caustic inhalation, and laryngeal trauma are less frequent acquired causes.'),
('007e0f6f-c6d8-5cbd-bf96-0278e1f4bcff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of stridor in a child with a history of intubation?', '["Tracheomalacia","Laryngomalacia","Subglottic stenosis","Laryngeal web","Vocal cord paralysis"]'::jsonb, 'Subglottic stenosis', 'In an intubated child, stridor after extubation most often signals subglottic stenosis, a known complication of endotracheal tube pressure and mucosal injury. Laryngomalacia and tracheomalacia are congenital rather than post-intubation causes of stridor.'),
('c6da575f-27ed-5dc9-86c1-7b59da690558'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of vocal cord paralysis in children?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Iatrogenic (post-cardiac surgery especially PDA ligation)","Birth trauma (vaginal delivery) mediated by specific receptor activation","Idiopathic congenital without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurologic (Chiari brainstem lesion) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iatrogenic (post-cardiac surgery especially PDA ligation)', 'In children, vocal cord paralysis most commonly follows cardiac surgery, particularly PDA ligation, because of recurrent laryngeal nerve injury in the operative field. Idiopathic congenital paralysis, Chiari-related brainstem compression, birth trauma, and direct trauma are less frequent causes.'),
('5c121ac9-080e-5c5f-b7df-ca11e6c3e87e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of unilateral vocal cord paralysis in adults?', '["Thyroid surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","Intubation injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malignancy (lung cancer invasion of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve)","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Idiopathic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Malignancy (lung cancer invasion of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve)', 'In adults, unilateral vocal cord paralysis most often results from a lung cancer (especially on the left) invading the recurrent laryngeal nerve as it passes below the aortic arch. Thyroid surgery, intubation injury, trauma, and idiopathic causes are less common.'),
('d16e3d6b-081a-54b1-9f9d-86ebe5fdfa41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of bilateral vocal cord paralysis in adults?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Idiopathic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total thyroidectomy (most common cause of bilateral injury)","Malignancy (esophageal lung) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurologic (MS ALS) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Total thyroidectomy (most common cause of bilateral injury)', 'Bilateral vocal cord paralysis in adults most commonly follows total thyroidectomy, in which both recurrent laryngeal nerves are at risk in the thyroid bed. Trauma, idiopathic causes, malignancy, and neurologic disease are less frequent causes of the bilateral form.'),
('a97d2f2d-76c8-5582-81b6-d006e4134657'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of stridor in adults?', '["Foreign body aspiration","Anaphylaxis","Vocal cord paralysis","Laryngospasm","Tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Foreign body aspiration', 'In adults, stridor usually represents an acute disorder, and foreign body aspiration is a leading cause of sudden-onset acute stridor. Anaphylaxis, laryngeal spasm, tumor, and vocal cord paralysis are other but less common causes.'),
('2c71f646-4d1b-5dc3-a21f-f6f051df8b79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of acute stridor in children?', '["Retropharyngeal abscess resulting in decreased functional activity","Croup (acute laryngotracheobronchitis) is the most common cause in children 6 months-3 years","Bacterial tracheitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Epiglottitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foreign body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Croup (acute laryngotracheobronchitis) is the most common cause in children 6 months-3 years', 'Croup (acute laryngotracheobronchitis) is the most common cause of acute stridor in children aged 6 months to 3 years, typically following a viral upper airway infection. Epiglottitis, bacterial tracheitis, retropharyngeal abscess, and foreign body cause acute stridor less frequently.'),
('4ad87cf2-584b-5598-a664-4c9cc4c7384c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic stridor in children?', '["Vocal cord paralysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Subglottic stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laryngomalacia (most common cause of chronic stridor in infants)","Vascular ring without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tracheomalacia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngomalacia (most common cause of chronic stridor in infants)', 'Laryngomalacia, caused by immature supraglottic structures that collapse on inspiration, is the most common cause of chronic stridor in infants and typically resolves spontaneously by age two. Subglottic stenosis, vascular rings, vocal cord paralysis, and tracheomalacia are less common.'),
('630f4028-3741-5817-9bf0-ab72a1264323'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of biphasic stridor?', '["Bronchial lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Tracheal lesion (tracheomalacia) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glottic or subglottic lesion (subglottic stenosis laryngeal web vocal cord paralysis)","Supraglottic lesion (laryngomalacia) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchomalacia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glottic or subglottic lesion (subglottic stenosis laryngeal web vocal cord paralysis)', 'A fixed obstruction at the glottic or subglottic level narrows the airway during both inspiration and expiration, producing biphasic stridor; subglottic stenosis, laryngeal webs, and vocal cord paralysis are typical examples. Supraglottic lesions like laryngomalacia cause inspiratory stridor, while bronchial lesions mainly produce expiratory noise.'),
('aabb9bce-de0c-5bd3-89f7-9ebb5b343e2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of wheezing that responds to bronchodilators?', '["Asthma (reactive airway disease)","CHF (cardiac asthma)","Vocal cord dysfunction (VCD)","Bronchiolitis","GERD"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma (reactive airway disease)', 'Asthma (reactive airway disease) causes reversible bronchoconstriction that responds to bronchodilators, making it the most common cause of bronchodilator-responsive wheezing. Cardiac asthma from CHF, VCD, bronchiolitis, and GERD are unlikely to improve with inhaled bronchodilators.'),
('695f6072-e037-503e-a13a-00b75a7dabd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of single-pitch (monophonic) wheeze?', '["VCD resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchiolitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GEERD mediated by specific receptor activation","Foreign body aspiration (central airway obstruction)","Asthma (polyphonic) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foreign body aspiration (central airway obstruction)', 'A monophonic (single-pitch) wheeze arises from obstruction of one large central airway, and in children a foreign body is the classic cause. Asthma produces polyphonic wheezing from narrowing at multiple levels, and VCD and bronchiolitis have different sound patterns.'),
('3a3ea377-b222-5e48-ae58-69eb62742b71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of polyphonic wheeze?', '["Asthma (airway narrowing at multiple levels)","Vocal cord dysfunction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foreign body (focal) mediated by specific receptor activation","CF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tracheomalacia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma (airway narrowing at multiple levels)', 'Asthma narrows the airways at many levels simultaneously, producing polyphonic (multi-pitch) wheezing. Focal obstruction from a foreign body gives a monophonic wheeze, while VCD, CF, and tracheomalacia are less common sources of polyphonic wheeze.'),
('6d4dfdb6-bcf6-51e6-ab39-90ae3775c976'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of crackles (rales) in children?', '["Pulmonary fibrosis","Pneumonia (alveolar fluid/inflammatory exudate)","Atelectasis (post-op)","Pulmonary edema (CHF)","Bronchiolitis (early inspiratory crackles)"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumonia (alveolar fluid/inflammatory exudate)', 'Pneumonia fills the alveoli with fluid and inflammatory exudate, and the intermittent opening of these airspaces generates crackles (rales), the most common cause of crackles in children. Fibrosis, atelectasis, pulmonary edema, and bronchiolitis are other, less frequent causes.'),
('dbf11ca4-684a-5bb6-af8a-952aa7b5f097'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of decreased breath sounds in children?', '["Atelectasis resulting in decreased functional activity","Pleural effusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asthma exacerbation (severe airflow limitation)","Pneumothorax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obesity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma exacerbation (severe airflow limitation)', 'During a severe asthma exacerbation, marked airflow limitation markedly reduces breath sounds, making it the most common cause of decreased breath sounds in children. Atelectasis, pneumothorax, pleural effusion, and obesity diminish breath sounds less frequently.'),
('9d887288-d41f-503d-9c3d-eb6ff6a1e81a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tachypnea without respiratory distress in the newborn?', '["Transient tachypnea of the newborn (TTN)","Sepsis","Congenital heart disease (increased pulmonary blood flow)","Metabolic acidosis (IEM)","Neurologic disorder"]'::jsonb, 'Transient tachypnea of the newborn (TTN)', 'Transient tachypnea of the newborn results from delayed clearance of fetal lung fluid and produces tachypnea without respiratory distress in the term newborn. Sepsis, metabolic acidosis, congenital heart disease, and neurologic disorders cause tachypnea less commonly.'),
('e3518174-3e88-587c-bd0f-a26c791da067'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hypercarbia in children?', '["V/Q mismatch resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased dead space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right-to-left shunt mediated by specific receptor activation","Diffusion impairment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoventilation (from any cause: respiratory depression NMD airway obstruction)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoventilation (from any cause: respiratory depression NMD airway obstruction)', 'Hypercarbia (elevated CO2) is driven by inadequate gas exchange, so hypoventilation from respiratory depression, neuromuscular disease, or airway obstruction is the most common cause. V/Q mismatch, increased dead space, and diffusion impairment affect oxygenation more than CO2 clearance.'),
('e57f6028-e387-599d-9e5d-840205363de8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hypoxemia in critically ill children?', '["Diffusion impairment resulting in decreased functional activity","Right-to-left shunt (intrapulmonary or cardiac) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch (most common mechanism of hypoxemia)","Low inspired oxygen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoventilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch (most common mechanism of hypoxemia)', 'Ventilation-perfusion mismatch is the most common mechanism of hypoxemia in critically ill children because it combines areas of normal perfusion with underventilated units. Right-to-left shunt, diffusion impairment, hypoventilation, and low inspired oxygen cause hypoxemia less frequently.'),
('0af9f0d3-b3a6-5f70-9190-d936daf27e4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of cyanosis in children with normal oxygen saturation?', '["Polycythemia","Hemoglobinopathies","Acrocyanosis (peripheral cyanosis from cold)","Methemoglobinemia (acquired or congenital)","Arteriovenous malformation"]'::jsonb, 'Methemoglobinemia (acquired or congenital)', 'Methemoglobinemia makes hemoglobin unable to carry oxygen, giving blood a chocolate-brown color and a cyanotic appearance triggered by drugs, chemicals, or congenital enzyme deficiency. Because it is a hemoglobin problem rather than hypoxemia, it is the classic cause of apparent cyanosis with a near-normal measured oxygen saturation.'),
('46e24fc8-bc05-5483-949d-cbb0147145dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of central cyanosis in the newborn?', '["Neurologic depression resulting in decreased functional activity","TTN (mild peripheral cyanosis only) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CHD (right-to-left shunt) and pulmonary disease (PPHN pneumonia RDS)","Sepsis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Methemoglobinemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CHD (right-to-left shunt) and pulmonary disease (PPHN pneumonia RDS)', 'Central cyanosis in the newborn results from deoxygenated blood reaching the systemic circulation, which occurs with right-to-left shunt congenital heart disease or pulmonary disease such as PPHN, pneumonia, and RDS. Peripherally-limited findings like acrocyanosis with TTN and methemoglobinemia produce different presentations.'),
('724ea554-a2f3-5ea9-aa21-6727836f5a74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic dyspnea in children?', '["Interstitial lung disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Obesity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CF mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma (most common cause of chronic dyspnea in children)","Deconditioning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma (most common cause of chronic dyspnea in children)', 'Asthma is the most common chronic disease of childhood and the leading cause of chronic dyspnea. Interstitial lung disease, obesity, cystic fibrosis, and deconditioning can all cause dyspnea but are far less common overall.'),
('e59827cc-0980-576a-bb4d-580a6d9e6d61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of exertional dyspnea in adolescents?', '["Vocal cord dysfunction (VCD) exercise-induced laryngeal obstruction","Cardiac arrhythmia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary embolism mediated by specific receptor activation","Deconditioning without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asthma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vocal cord dysfunction (VCD) exercise-induced laryngeal obstruction', 'Vocal cord dysfunction, also called exercise-induced laryngeal obstruction, is the most frequent cause of exertional dyspnea in adolescents and often mimics but differs from asthma. Asthma, deconditioning, cardiac arrhythmia, and pulmonary embolism are less common causes.'),
('7b0a07c7-62b5-5219-b263-5d71be258601'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chest pain with a normal cardiac evaluation in adolescents?', '["Pneumothorax resulting in decreased functional activity","GERD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Costochondritis (musculoskeletal pain)","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anxiety (panic attack) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Costochondritis (musculoskeletal pain)', 'Costochondritis, a musculoskeletal cause, is the most frequent explanation for chest pain in adolescents whose cardiac evaluation is normal. Pneumothorax, GERD, asthma, and panic attacks account for a smaller proportion of cases.'),
('e4584bfb-2061-5549-b398-8e97c11f0b41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a cough in children?', '["Pertussis resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchiectasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GERD mediated by specific receptor activation","Postnasal drip (upper airway cough syndrome) and asthma (cough-variant)","Post-infectious leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postnasal drip (upper airway cough syndrome) and asthma (cough-variant)', 'Postnasal drip from upper airway cough syndrome together with cough-variant asthma are the leading causes of chronic cough in children. Pertussis, bronchiectasis, GERD, and a post-infectious cough are less common causes.'),
('2440b803-b28d-526a-86e8-54dbbda3e61c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pertussis (whooping cough)?', '["Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Chlamydia pneumoniae","RSV","Bordetella parapertussis","Bordetella pertussis infection"]'::jsonb, 'Bordetella pertussis infection', 'Bordetella pertussis is the classic agent of whooping cough, producing paroxysmal cough with an inspiratory whoop. Mycoplasma, Chlamydia pneumoniae, RSV, and Bordetella parapertussis cause similar illness but are not the principal cause.'),
('610d45d9-2969-5836-949c-e03714437a21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of paroxysmal cough in infants?', '["Pertussis (Bordetella pertussis)","RSV bronchiolitis","GERD with aspiration","CF","Foreign body"]'::jsonb, 'Pertussis (Bordetella pertussis)', 'Pertussis is the most common cause of paroxysmal cough in infants, with staccato coughing episodes often followed by cyanosis or apnea. RSV bronchiolitis, GERD with aspiration, CF, and a foreign body produce cough but not the classic paroxysmal pattern.'),
('3a8ab2f8-bf71-58bb-88cb-25404f4fbf94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic wet cough in children?', '["Protracted bacterial bronchitis (PBB) in preschool children","Bronchiectasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aspiration mediated by specific receptor activation","Immune deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protracted bacterial bronchitis (PBB) in preschool children', 'Protracted bacterial bronchitis, usually due to encapsulated bacteria, is the most common cause of chronic wet cough in preschool children. Bronchiectasis, aspiration, immune deficiency, and cystic fibrosis are less frequent causes.'),
('cb1c85b6-9733-5fea-bb4c-13a4be1d761c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent wet cough in children?', '["Foreign body resulting in decreased functional activity","Cystic fibrosis (most common cause of recurrent bronchorrhea)","PBB mediated by specific receptor activation","Immune deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary ciliary dyskinesia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic fibrosis (most common cause of recurrent bronchorrhea)', 'Cystic fibrosis is the most common underlying cause of recurrent wet cough (bronchorrhea) in children. A foreign body, PBB, immune deficiency, and primary ciliary dyskinesia are less common causes of recurrent airway secretions.'),
('b3dd3b6d-1905-5473-b74d-21323c29515b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of failure to thrive with respiratory symptoms in infants?', '["GERD with aspiration","Immune deficiency","Cystic fibrosis","TB","CHD (CHF)"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic fibrosis', 'Cystic fibrosis is the most common inherited cause of failure to thrive combined with recurrent respiratory symptoms in infants. GERD with aspiration, immune deficiency, tuberculosis, and congenital heart disease with CHF are less common.'),
('185c53c1-f097-5ea3-8da2-464727323e56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of anosmia in children?', '["Allergic rhinitis (most common cause)","Viral infection (post-viral anosmia)","Kallmann syndrome","Head trauma","Nasal polyps"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic rhinitis (most common cause)', 'Allergic rhinitis is the most common cause of anosmia in children because mucosal edema obstructs the olfactory region. Post-viral anosmia, Kallmann syndrome, head trauma, and nasal polyps are less common causes.'),
('6fc08ce5-b871-5ae1-bb62-4a2fc9b8defe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of oropharyngeal dysphagia in children?', '["Eosinophilic esophagitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurologic impairment (cerebral palsy global developmental delay)","GERD mediated by specific receptor activation","Prematurity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anatomic lesions (cleft palate Pierre Robin) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurologic impairment (cerebral palsy global developmental delay)', 'Neurologic impairment such as cerebral palsy and global developmental delay is the most frequent cause of oropharyngeal dysphagia in children. GERD, prematurity, anatomic lesions, and eosinophilic esophagitis are less common causes.'),
('dd5f3a9a-f847-5eef-bbb3-5c7347a74ecd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of drooling in children?', '["Mouth breathing resulting in decreased functional activity","Developmental (immature oral motor control) in infants","Teething mediated by specific receptor activation","GERD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurologic impairment (CP) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Developmental (immature oral motor control) in infants', 'Drooling in children is most often developmental, reflecting immature oral motor control in infants and toddlers. Teething, GERD, mouth breathing, and neurologic impairment are less common or situational causes.'),
('ceb6788b-00be-5aac-aee0-7e3607da3e48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheostomy in children?', '["Prolonged ventilation (bronchopulmonary dysplasia neuromuscular disease)","Congenital anomalies (CHD with airway compression) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper airway obstruction (subglottic stenosis vocal cord paralysis) mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged ventilation (bronchopulmonary dysplasia neuromuscular disease)', 'The most common indication for tracheostomy in children is the need for prolonged ventilation, usually for bronchopulmonary dysplasia or neuromuscular disease. Congenital anomalies, upper airway obstruction, trauma, and tumor are less common indications.'),
('1e2bdf12-3bcb-5806-91bc-c88ba2851b81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheostomy complications in children?', '["Stomal infection","Accidental decannulation (most common early complication)","Tracheoesophageal fistula","Tracheal stenosis (late complication)","Granulation tissue formation"]'::jsonb, 'Accidental decannulation (most common early complication)', 'Accidental decannulation is the most common early complication of tracheostomy in children. Granulation tissue and tracheal stenosis occur later, while stomal infection and tracheoesophageal fistula are less frequent.'),
('1e9cb080-e19b-5c1e-b862-287d659d6075'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheogramuloma after tracheostomy?', '["Suture reaction resulting in decreased functional activity","Keloid formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Allergic reaction to the tube material mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic irritation from the tracheostomy tube at the stoma site","Infection (pseudomonas staph) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic irritation from the tracheostomy tube at the stoma site', 'Chronic mechanical irritation by the tracheostomy tube at the stoma site is the leading cause of tracheogramuloma. Suture reaction, keloid formation, allergy to tube material, and infection are less common causes.'),
('428e6dae-a265-572a-887e-7f20cee7e8ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheoesophageal fistula after tracheostomy?', '["Infectious resulting in decreased functional activity","Malignancy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radiation mediated by specific receptor activation","Traumatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Erosion from the tracheostomy tube (balloon pressure) creating a fistulous tract to the esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Erosion from the tracheostomy tube (balloon pressure) creating a fistulous tract to the esophagus', 'Acquired tracheoesophageal fistula after tracheostomy most often results from erosion of the tube, especially cuff or balloon pressure, creating a tract to the esophagus. Infectious, malignant, radiation, and other traumatic causes are less common.'),
('c80f546f-cf37-54a7-9578-bc73b3107e2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheal-innominate artery fistula?', '["Lymphoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Tube malposition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgical trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Erosion of the tracheostomy tube into the innominate artery (rare but fatal)","High-pressure balloon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Erosion of the tracheostomy tube into the innominate artery (rare but fatal)', 'Erosion of the tracheostomy tube into the innominate artery, though rare, is the most common cause of tracheal-innominate artery fistula and carries a high mortality. Lymphoma, tube malposition, surgical trauma, and high-pressure balloon are less common.'),
('50c02879-ffa5-5015-bf52-cf4cc9516474'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death from tracheal-innominate artery fistula?', '["Cardiac arrest resulting in decreased functional activity","Air embolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Suffocation mediated by specific receptor activation","Massive hemorrhage from the innominate artery into the trachea","Pneumonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Massive hemorrhage from the innominate artery into the trachea', 'Death from a tracheal-innominate artery fistula results from massive hemorrhage into the trachea, causing airway flooding and exsanguination. Cardiac arrest, air embolism, suffocation, and pneumonia are not the direct mechanism of death.'),
('018f1b4b-e703-52d7-9302-80a85f43d2db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of post-tracheostomy bleeding?', '["TEF resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coagulopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Tracheitis (tracheal mucosal irritation) from the tube","Tracheal-innominate fistula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tracheitis (tracheal mucosal irritation) from the tube', 'Post-tracheostomy bleeding most often arises from tracheitis, with mucosal irritation and inflammation caused by the tube. Tumor, coagulopathy, TEF, and tracheal-innominate fistula are less common; the fistula is rare but life-threatening.'),
('141ad023-dfad-5622-a25d-c3f1e1dd40c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of tracheal stenosis after prolonged intubation?', '["Foreign body reaction resulting in decreased functional activity","Mucosal ischemia and necrosis from the endotracheal tube cuff leading to fibrotic healing","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multiple intubations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosal ischemia and necrosis from the endotracheal tube cuff leading to fibrotic healing', 'Prolonged cuff pressure causes mucosal ischemia and necrosis that heal by fibrosis, the most common cause of post-intubation tracheal stenosis. Foreign body reaction, infection, direct trauma, and repeated intubations are less common causes.'),
('e8f126d4-8bd7-5bf6-a6b0-8789b983ce74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common location of post-intubation tracheal stenosis?', '["Subglottis resulting in decreased functional activity","At the stoma site (tracheostomy) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Main bronchus mediated by specific receptor activation","At the level of the cuff (cuff site stenosis in the proximal trachea)","At the tube tip (distal trachea) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the level of the cuff (cuff site stenosis in the proximal trachea)', 'Cuff-site stenosis in the proximal trachea is the most common location after endotracheal intubation, matching the zone of cuff pressure injury. The subglottis, stoma site, main bronchus, and tube-tip area are less common sites.'),
('4c7d30f8-7b19-5bb4-b90f-b8cc412cba7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of bronchial stenosis in children?', '["Post-transplant (bronchiolitis obliterans)","CF","Tracheal intubation trauma","TB (bronchial stenosis from healed TB)","Foreign body"]'::jsonb, 'Post-transplant (bronchiolitis obliterans)', 'Bronchiolitis obliterans after lung transplant is the most common cause of bronchial stenosis in children. Cystic fibrosis, intubation trauma, healed tuberculosis, and foreign body are far less common causes.'),
('c5b79f2a-767a-5449-831b-22d53d257405'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of broncholithiasis?', '["Calcified mucus plug resulting in decreased functional activity","Stone aspiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcified granulomatous lymph node (histoplasmosis TB) eroding into the bronchus","Endobronchial tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","COPD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcified granulomatous lymph node (histoplasmosis TB) eroding into the bronchus', 'Broncholithiasis most often results from a calcified granulomatous lymph node, usually due to histoplasmosis or TB, eroding into the bronchus. Calcified mucus plugs, stone aspiration, endobronchial tumor, and COPD are much less common.'),
('ce75572e-1cc1-5306-9d43-7d1061764e0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a lung cavity?', '["Lung cancer (squamous cell) causing cavitation","Septic emboli","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA)","Pneumonia (anaerobic lung abscess TB)","Cystic bronchiectasis"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer (squamous cell) causing cavitation', 'Cavitation most commonly complicates lung cancer, particularly squamous cell carcinoma, as the tumor outgrows its blood supply. Septic emboli, GPA, necrotizing pneumonia with lung abscess, and cystic bronchiectasis also cavitate but less often.'),
('87e2d0eb-40d9-5c3a-82e0-400635e52638'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a reticulonodular pattern on chest X-ray in children?', '["Miliary TB resulting in decreased functional activity","Viral pneumonia (bronchiolitis) in infants","Pulmonary edema mediated by specific receptor activation","Sarcoidosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Langerhans cell histiocytosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Viral pneumonia (bronchiolitis) in infants', 'Viral pneumonia with bronchiolitis is the most common cause of a reticulonodular pattern on chest X-ray in infants. Miliary TB, pulmonary edema, sarcoidosis, and Langerhans cell histiocytosis produce this pattern less often.'),
('1c5e29c0-6cdf-5f10-a10b-ecc4037d13d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a miliary pattern on CXR in children?', '["Chickenpox pneumonia","Sarcoidosis","Fungal infection (histoplasmosis blastomycosis)","Miliary carcinomatosis (thyroid melanoma)","Miliary tuberculosis (hematogenous dissemination of TB)"]'::jsonb, 'Miliary tuberculosis (hematogenous dissemination of TB)', 'Miliary tuberculosis, arising from hematogenous dissemination, is the most common cause of a miliary CXR pattern in children. Chickenpox pneumonia, sarcoidosis, fungal infection, and miliary carcinomatosis are far less common.'),
('4296c86e-4db5-51e6-9a8c-31bd476a4668'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hilar lymphadenopathy on CXR in children?', '["Lymphoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Fungal infection (histoplasmosis) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcoidosis (most common cause of bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy BHL)","Metastatic tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TB (unilateral hilar) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcoidosis (most common cause of bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy BHL)', 'Sarcoidosis is the most common cause of bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, classically presenting in adolescents. Lymphoma, histoplasmosis, metastatic tumor, and TB (often unilateral) are less common causes.'),
('34867bdf-c21f-53a8-b0d4-d028177f8065'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a calcified hilar lymph node on CXR?', '["Malignancy resulting in decreased functional activity","Sarcoidosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Silicosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Amyloidosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Healed granulomatous disease (Histoplasma or TB infection)"]'::jsonb, 'Healed granulomatous disease (Histoplasma or TB infection)', 'A calcified hilar lymph node most often reflects healed granulomatous infection, typically histoplasmosis or tuberculosis. Malignancy, sarcoidosis, silicosis, and amyloidosis very rarely calcify nodes.'),
('aa99e1b3-97ff-5c34-8334-e577bf4658ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a Ghon complex (Ranke complex)?', '["Lung cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Primary tuberculosis (calcified peripheral lung nodule with calcified hilar node)","Sarcoidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Fungal infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Silicosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary tuberculosis (calcified peripheral lung nodule with calcified hilar node)', 'The Ghon (Ranke) complex—a calcified peripheral parenchymal focus with a calcified draining hilar node—is the hallmark of healed primary tuberculosis. Lung cancer, sarcoidosis, fungal infection, and silicosis do not produce this paired calcification.'),
('3d744535-3b4e-5ebf-acb3-de7a819eeb15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of air trapping on expiratory chest CT?', '["Bronchiolitis obliterans resulting in decreased functional activity","Foreign body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asthma (bronchiolar obstruction causing hyperinflation)","Tracheomalacia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congenital lobar emphysema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma (bronchiolar obstruction causing hyperinflation)', 'Asthma, through small airway bronchiolar obstruction, is the most common cause of air trapping on expiratory chest CT. Bronchiolitis obliterans, foreign body, tracheomalacia, and congenital lobar emphysema also trap air but are far less common.'),
('543e24ef-1573-5dc3-acab-966ced08b27d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common finding on pulmonary function testing in asthma?', '["Decreased total lung capacity resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal PFTs between exacerbations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reduced DLCO mediated by specific receptor activation","Restrictive pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obstructive pattern (reduced FEV1/FVC) with bronchodilator reversibility"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructive pattern (reduced FEV1/FVC) with bronchodilator reversibility', 'Asthma characteristically shows an obstructive pattern with reduced FEV1/FVC that reverses after bronchodilator. Total lung capacity and DLCO are not reduced, and a restrictive pattern is not typical; normal PFTs between exacerbations are common but not the diagnostic finding.'),
('09f74c57-9720-549f-a30c-bbd709188d09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a false-positive sweat chloride test?', '["Malnutrition (edema) resulting in decreased functional activity","Medications accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Insufficient sweat collection (most common cause of invalid test)","ECZema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Insufficient sweat collection (most common cause of invalid test)', 'Insufficient or otherwise faulty sweat collection is the most common cause of an invalid sweat chloride result that can appear falsely elevated. Malnutrition with edema, medications, eczema, and hypothyroidism also raise sweat chloride but less commonly.'),
('6255a196-8df9-5eb7-a1bd-39065ddfebbf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of a false-negative sweat chloride test?', '["Infants <2 weeks (insufficient sweat production) and edema (dilutional)","Technical error accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malnutrition mediated by specific receptor activation","CF with normal sweat chloride (rare CFTR mutations) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medications leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infants <2 weeks (insufficient sweat production) and edema (dilutional)', 'A false-negative sweat chloride test is most common in infants under 2 weeks of age who produce insufficient sweat, and in edematous patients whose sweat is diluted. Technical error, malnutrition, rare CFTR mutations, and medications are less common causes.'),
('20b5006e-06da-5d77-8c22-7618fbe9e2c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common genetic test for cystic fibrosis?', '["CFTR gene sequencing (detects about 99% of mutations)","Mutation panel (ACOG recommended panel only for common mutations)","Nasal potential difference measurement","Newborn screening immunoreactive trypsinogen (IRT)","Sweat chloride test"]'::jsonb, 'CFTR gene sequencing (detects about 99% of mutations)', 'CFTR gene sequencing is the most comprehensive genetic test for cystic fibrosis, detecting about 99% of mutations. Standard mutation panels cover only common mutations, while nasal potential difference, IRT newborn screening, and sweat chloride are not sequencing-based genetic tests.'),
('b2fcc600-61ed-5ede-be73-6335ad9fd332'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common indication for lung biopsy in children?', '["Diffuse interstitial lung disease (chILD) of unknown etiology","Persistent pneumonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Suspected CPAM/sequestration mediated by specific receptor activation","Transplant rejection surveillance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor (carcinoid) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse interstitial lung disease (chILD) of unknown etiology', 'Suspected diffuse interstitial lung disease (chILD) of unknown etiology is the most common indication for surgical lung biopsy in children. Persistent pneumonia, CPAM/sequestration, transplant surveillance, and tumor are less common indications.'),
('43133786-4cdc-5d38-ad13-67f4be18e953'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common form of chILD (children''s interstitial lung disease)?', '["Idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis","Surfactant dysfunction mutations (SFTPB SFTPC ABCA3)","Neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy (NEHI)","Pulmonary lymphangiectasia","Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP)"]'::jsonb, 'Surfactant dysfunction mutations (SFTPB SFTPC ABCA3)', 'Surfactant dysfunction mutations involving SFTPB, SFTPC, or ABCA3 are the most common form of chILD, typically presenting in the neonatal or infant period. NEHI, pulmonary hemosiderosis, lymphangiectasia, and PAP are rarer forms.'),
('8810581b-1410-5e30-9662-275a079da887'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia of infancy (NEHI)?', '["A chILD with prominent neuroendocrine cells in airways causing tachypnea hypoxemia and ground-glass opacities in the right middle lobe and lingula","A infectious pneumonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A form of asthma mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Surfactant deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CF-related lung disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chILD with prominent neuroendocrine cells in airways causing tachypnea hypoxemia and ground-glass opacities in the right middle lobe and lingula', 'NEHI is a chILD marked by prominent neuroendocrine cells in the airways, causing tachypnea and hypoxemia with ground-glass opacities characteristically in the right middle lobe and lingula. It is not an infection, a form of asthma, surfactant deficiency, or CF-related lung disease.'),
('b41fdc5f-9143-5123-a163-a927b9de13b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP) in children?', '["Autoimmune PAP (anti-GM-CSF antibodies in adults) resulting in decreased functional activity","Secondary PAP (infection dust) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CF mediated by specific receptor activation","Surfactant deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GM-CSF receptor mutations (hereditary PAP most common in children)"]'::jsonb, 'GM-CSF receptor mutations (hereditary PAP most common in children)', 'Hereditary PAP caused by GM-CSF receptor mutations is the most common cause of PAP in children. Autoimmune anti-GM-CSF PAP is typical of adults, while secondary PAP, CF, and surfactant deficiency are less common pediatric causes.'),
('01ac598e-9d8f-53ea-94e0-cbcb05b14d4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is pulmonary interstitial glycogenosis (PIG)?', '["Pulmonary hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","A storage disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A form of chILD with glycogen-laden interstitial cells that typically resolves in infancy","A type of BPD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surfactant deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A form of chILD with glycogen-laden interstitial cells that typically resolves in infancy', 'Pulmonary interstitial glycogenosis is a chILD in which glycogen-laden interstitial cells fill the alveolar interstitium, and it typically resolves during infancy. It is not pulmonary hypertension, a storage disease, a type of BPD, or surfactant deficiency.'),
('4838934b-d3f2-5977-85b1-74b175f9cc52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death in children with surfactant deficiency disorders?', '["Cardiac failure resulting in decreased functional activity","Sepsis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cor pulmonale mediated by specific receptor activation","Progressive respiratory failure in infancy","Pneumonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive respiratory failure in infancy', 'Children with surfactant dysfunction disorders most commonly die of progressive respiratory failure in infancy, as the underlying lung disease worsens. Cardiac failure, sepsis, cor pulmonale, and pneumonia are less common terminal events.'),
('1fa2ed93-c9ef-5da1-a53e-b2d8db8147c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of persistent tachypnea in infancy beyond the newborn period?', '["CHD resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchiolitis obliterans (especially post-adenovirus)","NEHI (neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchiolitis obliterans (especially post-adenovirus)', 'Bronchiolitis obliterans, particularly after adenovirus infection, is the most common cause of persistent tachypnea beyond the newborn period. Congenital heart disease, pulmonary hypertension, NEHI, and CF are less common causes.'),
('89c65d37-46e0-5713-948e-7b13feb656c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of death in children with interstitial lung disease?', '["Pulmonary hypertension with cor pulmonale resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiac failure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malnutrition mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Respiratory failure from progressive fibrosis (especially surfactant mutations and Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory failure from progressive fibrosis (especially surfactant mutations and Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome)', 'Progressive pulmonary fibrosis leading to respiratory failure is the most common cause of death in children with interstitial lung disease, especially in surfactant mutations and Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome. Cor pulmonale, cardiac failure, malnutrition, and pneumonia are less common terminal causes.'),
('1a2c0bc2-a210-5712-a4c2-fb81bafe7f08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'At which week of development does the lung bud appear?', '["Week 5","Week 4 (day 26-28)","Week 3","Week 6","Week 7"]'::jsonb, 'Week 4 (day 26-28)', 'The lung bud, a ventral diverticulum of the foregut, appears during the fourth week of development (day 26-28). It appears before the branching of weeks 5-7, so the later weeks listed are incorrect.'),
('3b01379b-351a-5d50-a514-8dc1edc6cfdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'From which part of the foregut does the respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) arise?', '["Dorsal wall of the foregut resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral wall of the foregut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventral wall of the foregut (just caudal to the pharynx)","The pharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral wall of the foregut (just caudal to the pharynx)', 'The respiratory diverticulum (lung bud) arises from the ventral wall of the foregut, just caudal to the pharynx. The dorsal wall forms the esophagus, so the dorsal, lateral, pharyngeal, and esophageal options are incorrect.'),
('4acd9c6c-2d6c-5f2b-b421-fb4070c07bb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'Which structure divides the foregut into the trachea and the esophagus?', '["The cricoid cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchial buds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","The pharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tracheoesophageal septum (formed by tracheoesophageal folds)"]'::jsonb, 'Tracheoesophageal septum (formed by tracheoesophageal folds)', 'The tracheoesophageal septum, formed by fusion of the tracheoesophageal folds, separates the foregut into the ventral trachea and dorsal esophagus. The cricoid cartilage, bronchial buds, larynx, and pharynx do not perform this partitioning.'),
('da7b788d-299c-5669-af1e-7135d8d76655'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'In respiratory development, failure of the tracheoesophageal septum to form causes which condition?', '["Bronchial stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Laryngeal atresia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary sequestration mediated by specific receptor activation","Diaphragmatic hernia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tracheoesophageal fistula (with or without esophageal atresia)"]'::jsonb, 'Tracheoesophageal fistula (with or without esophageal atresia)', 'Incomplete formation of the tracheoesophageal septum leaves an abnormal communication between the trachea and esophagus, producing a tracheoesophageal fistula, often with esophageal atresia. Bronchial stenosis, laryngeal atresia, sequestration, and diaphragmatic hernia have different origins.'),
('e2004bbb-9c66-5456-9f67-fb785eb79683'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'In respiratory development, the larynx develops from which structures?', '["Sixth arch only","Fourth arch only","First and second arches","Fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches","Third arch"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches', 'The larynx develops from the fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches and the hypobranchial eminence. A single arch, or the first/second/third arches, does not provide the complete laryngeal structure.'),
('cdfa7b62-a712-589c-96a9-713bb0dc2aac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'In respiratory development, the epiglottis develops from which structure?', '["Sixth arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Second arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Third arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypobranchial eminence (from the fourth arch)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypobranchial eminence (from the fourth arch)', 'The epiglottis develops from the hypobranchial eminence, which is derived from the fourth pharyngeal arch. The sixth, second, first, and third arches do not give rise to the epiglottis.'),
('9555538d-f6e9-5fbb-a769-eb37628d1336'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'In respiratory development, the laryngeal cartilages develop from which arch cartilages?', '["First and second arch cartilages","Third arch cartilage","Fourth and sixth arch cartilages","Only the sixth arch","Only the fourth arch"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth and sixth arch cartilages', 'The laryngeal cartilages arise from the cartilages of the fourth and sixth pharyngeal arches. The first, second, and third arch cartilages, or a single arch, do not form the laryngeal cartilages.'),
('df71a1c3-b112-599f-bb14-05a5fabb7083'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Respiratory System', 'Salbutamol (SABA) used for:', '["Acute bronchospasm (rescue)","COPD maintenance","Asthma maintenance","Exercise-induced prevention","All above"]'::jsonb, 'Acute bronchospasm (rescue)', 'First-line rescue for bronchospasm.'),
('9f9ed134-2bac-5c3a-bdae-c21b50b629b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'What does the optic vesicle arise from?', '["Prosencephalon","Telencephalon","Mesencephalon","Myelencephalon","Rhombencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Prosencephalon', 'The optic vesicle is an outgrowth of the wall of the prosencephalon (forebrain vesicle), so it has a neuroectodermal origin. The telencephalon, mesencephalon, myelencephalon, and rhombencephalon are other brain regions that do not form the optic vesicle.'),
('9d45dd29-b86a-5948-81f2-f3905063ec45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The lens placode is derived from which germ layer?', '["Neuroectoderm","Ectoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest","Mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Ectoderm', 'The lens placode is a thickening of the surface ectoderm overlying the optic vesicle. Neuroectoderm and neural crest contribute to neural structures and connective tissue rather than the lens, while endoderm and mesoderm are unrelated to lens formation.'),
('570d000c-6a51-5a67-ad42-84520f52bd67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Invagination of the optic vesicle forms the:', '["Choroidal fissure","Optic cup","Hyaloid artery","Lens vesicle","Optic stalk"]'::jsonb, 'Optic cup', 'Invagination of the distal portion of the optic vesicle produces a two-layered optic cup. The choroidal fissure is a groove on the ventral cup, the lens vesicle forms separately from surface ectoderm, and the optic stalk is the connection to the brain.'),
('04bed9f9-e431-5e84-b5fd-08ee1c8d1d84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The choroidal fissure allows passage of the:', '["Ciliary artery","Central retinal vein","Optic nerve","Lacrimal duct","Hyaloid artery"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaloid artery', 'The choroidal fissure is the ventral groove in the optic cup that conveys the hyaloid artery into the developing vitreous. The ciliary and central retinal arteries develop later from other branches, and the optic nerve and lacrimal duct are unrelated.'),
('1c2f6b5a-18c5-5667-b712-a1989c13e8cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Persistence of the choroidal fissure results in:', '["Glaucoma","Microphthalmia","Aniridia","Coloboma iridis","Cataract"]'::jsonb, 'Coloboma iridis', 'Failure of the choroidal fissure to close leaves a gap or notch in the iris, producing coloboma iridis. Glaucoma, microphthalmia, aniridia, and cataract arise from different developmental or acquired mechanisms.'),
('ff35ef9b-ff1d-557c-afc9-5d0034b2547d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The neural retina is derived from which layer of the optic cup?', '["Inner layer","Neuroectoderm between layers","Both layers","Outer layer","Mesenchyme around the cup"]'::jsonb, 'Inner layer', 'The inner (neural) layer of the optic cup differentiates into the neural retina with its photoreceptors and neurons. The outer layer forms the retinal pigment epithelium, and mesenchyme around the cup forms other ocular coats.'),
('67a3ac7f-129d-5e41-8877-a4237a716e46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The retinal pigment epithelium is derived from:', '["Inner layer of optic cup","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Outer layer of optic cup","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Outer layer of optic cup', 'The outer (pigmented) layer of the optic cup becomes the retinal pigment epithelium, while the inner layer forms the neural retina. Mesoderm, neural crest, and surface ectoderm contribute to other ocular tissues.'),
('83581630-0198-5ec2-9f58-ad76833c0496'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The optic stalk connects the optic cup to the:', '["Extraocular muscles","Nasolacrimal duct","Lens","Brain","Eyelid"]'::jsonb, 'Brain', 'The optic stalk is the narrow connection between the optic cup and the brain (forebrain), through which optic nerve fibers later grow. Extraocular muscles, nasolacrimal duct, lens, and eyelid are not connected by the stalk.'),
('f5582469-8084-538b-aa15-0a34bda6ce0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The hyaloid artery regresses to form the:', '["Ophthalmic artery","Nasal artery","Anterior ciliary artery","Long posterior ciliary artery","Central retinal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Central retinal artery', 'The hyaloid artery, which supplies the developing lens and vitreous, regresses and its proximal segment persists as the central retinal artery. The ophthalmic, nasal, and ciliary arteries have different origins.'),
('8e905d23-775c-5e0e-8edc-9dc3e21a7514'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Persistence of the hyaloid artery remnant is called:', '["Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous","Coloboma","Congenital cataract","Microphthalmia","Bergmeister papilla"]'::jsonb, 'Bergmeister papilla', 'A small remnant of the hyaloid artery attachment on the optic disc persists as Bergmeister papilla. PHPV is the more extensive persistence of hyaloid tissue with vitreous changes, and the other options are unrelated conditions.'),
('5b978bf0-90c3-5280-b825-11c1b8a1899d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Persistence of the hyaloid artery and associated vitreous hyperplasia is called:', '["Coat disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Bergmeister papilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Retinopathy of prematurity mediated by specific receptor activation","Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous (PHPV)","Retinoblastoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous (PHPV)', 'Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous (PHPV) is the persistence of the hyaloid vascular system with fibrous and glial proliferation in the vitreous. Bergmeister papilla is only a minor remnant, and the other options are unrelated conditions.'),
('9b572ab5-f5da-5bf0-8a8b-99b85503b55d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The lens vesicle sinks into the optic cup and detaches from:', '["Neural ectoderm","Surface ectoderm","Choroidal fissure","Optic cup","Mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Surface ectoderm', 'After the lens vesicle pinches off, it separates from the overlying surface ectoderm, which then reforms as the corneal epithelium. The lens remains free within the optic cup margin.'),
('e8e4c835-493a-5c74-924c-1fe53da5dea6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Primary lens fibers form from cells at the:', '["Anterior wall of lens vesicle","Hyaloid artery","Equatorial region of lens vesicle","Posterior wall of lens vesicle","Optic cup margin"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior wall of lens vesicle', 'Primary lens fibers form as the cells of the posterior wall of the lens vesicle elongate toward the anterior wall, filling the vesicle lumen. The anterior wall cells become the lens epithelium, and secondary fibers arise later at the equator.'),
('753d7199-ea1a-5edc-b2de-e6d1fb82763c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Secondary lens fibers are added throughout life from the:', '["Lens nucleus","Ciliary body","Lens anterior pole","Lens equator","Lens posterior pole"]'::jsonb, 'Lens equator', 'Secondary lens fibers are produced continuously by epithelial cells at the lens equator, which elongate to add new layers around the lens nucleus. The poles and nucleus do not generate new fibers.'),
('75204bd3-5e97-575d-9ad7-a5d328263b1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital cataract can result from:', '["Chickenpox","Measles","Gonorrhea","Malaria","Rubella infection"]'::jsonb, 'Rubella infection', 'Congenital rubella infection in early pregnancy is a classic cause of cataract along with other malformations. The other listed infections are not established causes of congenital cataract.'),
('35e1e1a9-319a-501e-9248-4cf328599f26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The cornea develops from:', '["Surface ectoderm only","Neural crest only","Surface ectoderm and neural crest cells","Mesoderm only","Endoderm and mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Surface ectoderm and neural crest cells', 'The cornea forms from surface ectoderm (epithelium) combined with invading neural crest cells that give rise to the endothelium, stroma, and Descemet membrane. Single-layer or mesodermal origins do not account for all corneal layers.'),
('a656b7d3-7dfe-5acc-847a-2b3c138ca4ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The anterior chamber develops by:', '["Regression of hyaloid artery","Migration of neural crest","Invagination of optic cup","Cleavage of mesenchyme between lens and cornea","Fusion of eyelids"]'::jsonb, 'Cleavage of mesenchyme between lens and cornea', 'The anterior chamber is formed by cleavage (splitting) of the mesenchyme between the lens and the developing cornea. The other processes are unrelated to anterior chamber formation.'),
('12142e2e-57f4-524e-a97e-3ca6cb9192f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The iris develops from:', '["Surface ectoderm","Margin of the optic cup","Lens vesicle","Mesoderm","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Margin of the optic cup', 'The iris develops from the margin of the optic cup, which extends forward between the cornea and lens. It incorporates pupillary membrane and ciliary contributions but originates at the cup margin.'),
('cd1be2ed-c53a-56e8-95e6-b205e08a20ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The sphincter pupillae and dilator pupillae muscles are derived from:', '["Optic cup ectoderm","Paraxial mesoderm","Mesoderm","Surface ectoderm","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Optic cup ectoderm', 'The sphincter and dilator pupillae are smooth muscles derived from the ectoderm of the optic cup margin (neuroectoderm), unlike most other muscles of the body. Mesoderm, surface ectoderm, and neural crest do not form them.'),
('2a6b180e-f08f-5c77-8aff-0898af6f9240'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The ciliary body develops from:', '["Lens vesicle","Posterior part of the optic cup","Corneal endothelium","Choroid","Anterior part of the optic cup"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior part of the optic cup', 'The ciliary body arises from the anterior portion of the optic cup, which becomes folded into ciliary processes. The choroid and cornea develop from surrounding mesenchyme, and the lens has a separate origin.'),
('fe0fc2c5-18bb-5efd-86d1-21721a7d5bd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The vitreous body is derived from:', '["Surface ectoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesenchyme (neural crest) and neuroectoderm","Endoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Intermediate mesoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Paraxial mesoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchyme (neural crest) and neuroectoderm', 'The vitreous body forms from a primary vitreous of neuroectodermal origin with secondary contributions of mesenchyme (neural crest). The other germ layers and mesoderm types are not involved.'),
('fb6882c9-b9ee-59b0-b7e1-c76c047a264a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The eyelids develop from:', '["Surface ectoderm only resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesenchymal folds with ectodermal covering","Neural crest only mediated by specific receptor activation","Endoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesoderm only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal folds with ectodermal covering', 'The eyelids form as folds of mesenchyme covered by surface ectoderm, which then fuse and later separate. Neural crest or mesoderm alone cannot produce the two-layer structure of the lid.'),
('07064ef2-d300-555d-a52d-9feed24f0e96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Failure of eyelid separation can result in:', '["Microphthalmia","Epicanthus","Coloboma","Ankyloblepharon","Ptosis"]'::jsonb, 'Ankyloblepharon', 'Failure of the fused eyelids to separate results in ankyloblepharon (partial or complete adhesion of the lid margins). Epicanthus, ptosis, coloboma, and microphthalmia have different causes.'),
('2a00a553-52cc-5261-83e8-a8cb1a0ff945'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The nasolacrimal duct develops from a cord of cells that canalizes from the:', '["Hyoid arch","Maxillary process","Frontonasal process","Otic placode","Mandibular process"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary process', 'The nasolacrimal duct develops from an ectodermal cord of cells in the nasolacrimal groove between the lateral nasal and maxillary processes, which later canalizes. The hyoid and mandibular arches and the otic placode are not involved.'),
('c2c4adec-5ff1-55f9-85e9-1f775b5758a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital dacryostenosis (nasolacrimal duct obstruction) is caused by:', '["Choanal atresia resulting in decreased functional activity","Lacrimal gland agenesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eyelid fusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Failure of canalization of the distal end of the nasolacrimal duct","Coloboma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of canalization of the distal end of the nasolacrimal duct', 'Congenital dacryostenosis results when the distal end of the nasolacrimal duct fails to canalize, causing blocked tear drainage. The other options do not obstruct the nasolacrimal duct.'),
('46a346de-f38c-5fce-b644-8da79e34521f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The lacrimal gland develops from:', '["Frontonasal process","Conjunctival ectoderm","Nasolacrimal cord","Maxillary process","Optic cup"]'::jsonb, 'Conjunctival ectoderm', 'The lacrimal gland develops from buds of conjunctival (surface) ectoderm at the superior fornix. The optic cup, maxillary process, and nasolacrimal cord do not contribute.'),
('7c55cb4d-91c3-59e9-b49c-e896017d16ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Extraocular muscles are derived from:', '["Neural crest","Pharyngeal arch mesoderm","Preoptic and occipital myotomes","Limb bud mesoderm","Paraxial mesoderm of somites"]'::jsonb, 'Preoptic and occipital myotomes', 'Extraocular muscles arise from preoptic myotomes (superior, inferior, and medial rectus plus inferior oblique) and occipital myotomes (superior oblique and lateral rectus). Neural crest and branchial arch mesoderm form other structures.'),
('10dd9276-5d98-5c29-af9f-9dfe8a685a62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The levator palpebrae superioris is derived from:', '["Optic stalk","Superior rectus muscle anlage","Neural crest","Maxillary process","Frontal bone mesenchyme"]'::jsonb, 'Superior rectus muscle anlage', 'The levator palpebrae superioris develops from the superior rectus muscle anlage, splitting off during development. It is not derived from the optic stalk, neural crest, or mesenchyme of the maxillary process.'),
('4fc59e02-3186-5b7a-95c9-9194ee730893'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital ptosis is often due to:', '["CN III palsy resulting in decreased functional activity","Poor development of levator palpebrae superioris","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Myasthenia gravis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Horner syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Poor development of levator palpebrae superioris', 'Congenital ptosis is most often due to poor or faulty development of the levator palpebrae superioris muscle. The other causes are acquired or syndromic rather than developmental.'),
('9abb2b82-531a-5177-bec2-2d2c88bbe19d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The choroid develops from:', '["Mesenchyme around the optic cup","Optic stalk","Inner layer of optic cup","Outer layer of optic cup","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchyme around the optic cup', 'The choroid is the vascular layer that develops from the mesenchyme surrounding the optic cup. The optic cup layers and surface ectoderm give rise to other ocular structures.'),
('f49bbb1f-01e7-5eec-bc32-61a91ac5bb18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The sclera develops from:', '["Neural crest","Mesenchyme around the optic cup","Surface ectoderm","Optic stalk","Optic cup"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchyme around the optic cup', 'The sclera, like the choroid, forms from the mesenchyme (neural crest) surrounding the optic cup. The optic stalk, optic cup, and surface ectoderm do not contribute.'),
('2ca6746b-0f6a-592a-a14b-a1619338e720'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The pupillary membrane normally regresses by:', '["2nd week of gestation","8th month of gestation","3rd trimester","4th week of gestation","Birth"]'::jsonb, '8th month of gestation', 'The pupillary membrane normally atrophies and regresses by the 8th month of gestation. This regression is a developmental milestone of the anterior segment.'),
('2af582b8-c0a0-573d-a225-6155b472806b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Persistence of the pupillary membrane can cause:', '["Glaucoma","Retinal detachment","Cataract","Strabismus","Congenital miosis"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital miosis', 'Persistence of the pupillary membrane can leave strands across the pupil and cause congenital miosis because the membrane has not regressed. The other options are not direct consequences of the membrane''s persistence.'),
('f978f5eb-228b-530c-a21a-a847160a3911'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The primitive optic vesicle is connected to the forebrain by the:', '["Optic stalk","Optic nerve","Optic cup","Lens stalk","Choroidal fissure"]'::jsonb, 'Optic stalk', 'The primitive optic vesicle remains connected to the forebrain by the optic stalk, which later carries optic nerve fibers. The optic cup is the invaginated vesicle, and the lens stalk connects the lens to the surface ectoderm.'),
('98b3a28e-2ae1-5ad0-b370-395af9452269'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Myelination of the optic nerve begins at the:', '["Optic disc","Lateral geniculate body","Chiasm","Retina","Ciliary body"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral geniculate body', 'Myelination of the optic nerve begins at the lateral geniculate body and proceeds forward toward the eye, reaching the optic disc around term. It begins centrally, not at the retina or disc.'),
('fcd4788b-fa84-5912-ba99-ec18ebdf78f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital glaucoma is often due to:', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Rubella accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Steroid use mediated by specific receptor activation","Toxoplasmosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abnormal development of the trabecular meshwork and Schlemm canal"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal development of the trabecular meshwork and Schlemm canal', 'Congenital glaucoma results from maldevelopment of the trabecular meshwork and Schlemm canal, which impairs aqueous drainage. The other listed factors are not causes of primary congenital glaucoma.'),
('2194ecfa-e35a-512d-9af3-92de9586bd88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Aniridia is associated with mutations in the:', '["PITX2 gene","PAX6 gene","SOX2 gene","FOXC1 gene","CHD7 gene"]'::jsonb, 'PAX6 gene', 'Aniridia is caused by mutations in the PAX6 gene, a key regulator of eye development. PITX2 and FOXC1 relate to Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome, SOX2 to anophthalmia, and CHD7 to CHARGE syndrome.'),
('ffb80d49-e499-5d9d-b4cf-1e3ef48242d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Retinoblastoma is associated with mutations in the:', '["WT1 gene","APC gene","NF1 gene","RB1 gene","VHL gene"]'::jsonb, 'RB1 gene', 'Retinoblastoma results from loss-of-function mutations of the RB1 tumor suppressor gene. The other genes are associated with different tumors or syndromes.'),
('0594d2cd-4e3c-54b8-a10c-474e52da9c87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The otic placode is derived from which germ layer?', '["Neural ectoderm","Neural crest","Endoderm","Surface ectoderm","Mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Surface ectoderm', 'The otic placode is a thickening of the surface ectoderm adjacent to the hindbrain that invaginates to form the otic vesicle. Neural ectoderm, neural crest, endoderm, and mesoderm do not form it.'),
('ab7d13a5-53f2-5d1a-a196-6c47d444c8d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The otic vesicle (otocyst) gives rise to:', '["Eustachian tube","Tympanic cavity","Membranous labyrinth","Auditory ossicles","Bony labyrinth"]'::jsonb, 'Membranous labyrinth', 'The otic vesicle differentiates into the membranous labyrinth (utricle, saccule, semicircular ducts, and cochlear duct). The bony labyrinth forms from surrounding mesenchyme, and the ossicles and Eustachian tube come from the pharyngeal arches.'),
('f20a2c94-1d3e-5567-99df-cb75172aee77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The bony labyrinth is derived from:', '["Otic vesicle resulting in decreased functional activity","Surface ectoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Endoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesenchyme surrounding the otocyst"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchyme surrounding the otocyst', 'The bony labyrinth is formed by ossification of the mesenchyme surrounding the otocyst. The otic vesicle itself forms only the membranous labyrinth.'),
('6c469d4d-7b2d-57fd-a908-79b3e3a5bfa8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The semicircular canals develop from:', '["First pharyngeal arch","Outpouchings of the otic vesicle","Otic placode","Neural crest","Second pharyngeal arch"]'::jsonb, 'Outpouchings of the otic vesicle', 'The semicircular canals develop as flat outpouchings of the dorsal (utricular) part of the otic vesicle. The pharyngeal arches and neural crest contribute to other ear structures.'),
('7d9aa625-0c3b-5ee3-8ebf-7ae7264d4667'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The cochlear duct develops from the otic vesicle as the:', '["Cochlear part of the otocyst","Utricular part","Saccular part","Endolymphatic duct","Semicircular canal"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear part of the otocyst', 'The cochlear duct arises from the ventral (cochlear) part of the otocyst, which elongates into a spiral. The utricular and saccular parts form the vestibular structures, and the endolymphatic duct arises dorsally.'),
('419aa4b8-25b2-5a73-98f4-560d052e9649'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The organ of Corti is located within the:', '["Saccule","Scala vestibuli","Utricle","Cochlear duct","Scala tympani"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear duct', 'The organ of Corti, the auditory receptor, lies on the basilar membrane within the cochlear duct (scala media). The scala vestibuli and scala tympani are perilymph-filled spaces, and the saccule and utricle contain vestibular receptors.'),
('8216e4e3-e215-5a51-99ce-80f09b71ca91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The scala tympani and scala vestibuli are filled with:', '["Blood","Perilymph","Endolymph","Cerebrospinal fluid","Lymph"]'::jsonb, 'Perilymph', 'The scala vestibuli and scala tympani contain perilymph, which resembles extracellular fluid in composition. Endolymph fills the scala media.'),
('cb89dc14-9a01-56e8-beb0-e1b8504142cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The scala media (cochlear duct) is filled with:', '["Endolymph","Cerebrospinal fluid","Perilymph","Lymph","Blood"]'::jsonb, 'Endolymph', 'The scala media (cochlear duct) is filled with endolymph, a high-potassium fluid produced by the stria vascularis. Perilymph occupies the scalae vestibuli and tympani.'),
('d86b98d6-a75d-5a4c-9076-47fbebded8e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The utricle and saccule contain sensory receptors for:', '["Linear acceleration and gravity","Sound frequency","Temperature","Angular acceleration","Pressure changes"]'::jsonb, 'Linear acceleration and gravity', 'The maculae of the utricle and saccule detect linear acceleration and gravity via otoliths. Angular acceleration is detected by the cristae of the semicircular ducts.'),
('554c5f80-8a23-5fb2-90db-9d416c42d65e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The semicircular canals contain sensory receptors for:', '["Pressure resulting in decreased functional activity","Gravity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Linear acceleration mediated by specific receptor activation","Angular (rotational) acceleration","Sound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Angular (rotational) acceleration', 'The cristae ampullares in the semicircular canals respond to angular (rotational) acceleration of the head. Linear acceleration and gravity are sensed by the utricular and saccular maculae.'),
('cc13a703-2c17-54c0-a784-0a237bb1196e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The endolymphatic duct and sac develop from:', '["Mesenchyme","Ventral wall of the otocyst","Dorsal wall of the otocyst","Surface ectoderm","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal wall of the otocyst', 'The endolymphatic duct and sac develop as an outgrowth from the dorsal wall of the otocyst. The ventral wall gives rise to the saccule and cochlear duct.'),
('4476c88d-ec40-529a-bf57-0dcedf76f636'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Enlargement of the endolymphatic sac is associated with:', '["Meniere disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Labyrinthitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cholesteatoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Large vestibular aqueduct syndrome","Otosclerosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large vestibular aqueduct syndrome', 'Enlargement of the endolymphatic sac and vestibular aqueduct is the hallmark of large vestibular aqueduct syndrome, a cause of sensorineural hearing loss. Meniere disease, labyrinthitis, cholesteatoma, and otosclerosis involve different mechanisms.'),
('5867523d-d7ed-5bcc-900c-34c8c7f10609'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The tensor tympani muscle is innervated by:', '["Facial nerve","Accessory nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Trigeminal nerve (V3)","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (V3)', 'Tensor tympani is supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3) through the nerve to medial pterygoid. The facial nerve supplies stapedius, and the other listed nerves have no motor supply to the middle ear muscles.'),
('b04c6186-dc26-5ac9-bc17-dff1750c18b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The stapedius muscle is innervated by:', '["Facial nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve', 'Stapedius is supplied by the facial nerve, which gives off its branch to the muscle just before leaving the skull through the stylomastoid foramen. The trigeminal nerve supplies tensor tympani, and the other cranial nerves do not supply middle ear muscles.'),
('07c86303-6f43-50d2-be02-352b0e9b6773'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The tympanic cavity and Eustachian tube develop from:', '["Fourth pharyngeal pouch","Second pharyngeal pouch","First pharyngeal pouch","Third pharyngeal pouch","First pharyngeal cleft"]'::jsonb, 'First pharyngeal pouch', 'The endodermal first pharyngeal pouch expands to form the tympanic cavity and the Eustachian tube. The pharyngeal clefts and the remaining pouches give rise to different structures.'),
('5b06d12b-afce-51a0-a773-064ec545841b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The external auditory meatus develops from:', '["First pharyngeal pouch","Otic vesicle","First pharyngeal cleft","Otic placode","Second pharyngeal cleft"]'::jsonb, 'First pharyngeal cleft', 'The external auditory meatus develops from the ectodermal first pharyngeal cleft (groove) between the first and second arches. Pouches form endodermal structures such as the middle ear cavity, and the otic derivatives form the inner ear.'),
('b44842ba-128c-5478-b166-b8ceb5ebc614'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The tympanic membrane has contributions from:', '["Endoderm only","and mesoderm (middle)","Ectoderm (external)","Ectoderm only","endoderm (internal)"]'::jsonb, 'Ectoderm (external)', 'The tympanic membrane is composed of three layers: ectoderm on its lateral surface (from the first pharyngeal cleft), endoderm on its medial surface (from the first pouch), and mesoderm in between. The ectodermal covering forms the outer epithelial layer.'),
('3e00a109-e582-5916-9034-fd18a0822f13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Auricular hillocks (six) develop around the:', '["First pharyngeal pouch","Otic vesicle","First pharyngeal cleft","Third pharyngeal arch","Second pharyngeal arch"]'::jsonb, 'First pharyngeal cleft', 'The six auricular hillocks arise from the first and second pharyngeal arches and surround the first pharyngeal cleft, fusing to form the auricle. The pouch and otic vesicle do not contribute to the hillocks.'),
('d18be141-2928-51e5-816b-b6063318ac03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital microtia refers to:', '["Narrowed Eustachian tube","Absent ossicles","Small tympanic membrane","Underdeveloped auricle","Small external auditory meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Underdeveloped auricle', 'Microtia is a congenital underdevelopment of the auricle (pinna), with severity ranging from a small malformed ear to a completely absent pinna. It does not primarily describe the Eustachian tube, ossicles, or tympanic membrane.'),
('b3ed2799-22af-5afa-b213-f521783d0d46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Preauricular sinus and tags are remnants of:', '["Branchial arch","First pharyngeal pouch","First pharyngeal cleft","Otic vesicle","Auricular hillocks"]'::jsonb, 'Auricular hillocks', 'Preauricular sinus and tags are remnants of the auricular hillocks, the six tubercles from the first and second pharyngeal arches that form the auricle. They result from incomplete fusion of these hillocks rather than from pouch, cleft, or vesicle remnants.'),
('2fa333ed-2861-54ba-8601-037a972fb2cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital sensorineural hearing loss can result from:', '["Eustachian tube dysfunction a","Abnormal development of the organ of Corti","Serous otitis media","Otosclerosis","Impacted cerumen"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal development of the organ of Corti', 'Congenital sensorineural hearing loss results from abnormal development of cochlear structures such as the organ of Corti and hair cells. Conditions such as otosclerosis, serous otitis media, and cerumen impaction cause conductive rather than sensorineural hearing loss.'),
('2427f0dd-7af1-51ce-8dfc-3c4f30853d0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Mondini dysplasia is a congenital malformation of the:', '["Ossicles","Semicircular canals","Middle ear","Cochlea (incomplete turns)","Auricle"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlea (incomplete turns)', 'Mondini dysplasia is a congenital malformation of the cochlea in which the turns are incomplete, producing a flattened spiral and sensorineural hearing loss. The ossicles, semicircular canals, and auricle are not the primary site of this defect.'),
('5ef3adf3-8d34-542b-8efa-f221ab927eb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital cholesteatoma is thought to arise from:', '["Epidermal cell rests in the middle ear","Trauma","Chronic otitis media","Eustachian tube dysfunction","Ossicular fixation"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermal cell rests in the middle ear', 'Congenital cholesteatoma is thought to arise from epidermal cell rests, ectodermal remnants trapped within the middle ear that form a keratinizing sac. Trauma and chronic otitis media underlie the acquired forms of cholesteatoma.'),
('b4beeab5-c895-5e65-9df6-861b113a48d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The stapes footplate becomes fixed in:', '["Meniere disease","Labyrinthitis","Otitis media","Cholesteatoma","Otosclerosis"]'::jsonb, 'Otosclerosis', 'In otosclerosis, abnormal bone remodeling fixes the stapes footplate in the oval window, producing progressive conductive hearing loss. Meniere disease, labyrinthitis, otitis media, and cholesteatoma do not primarily cause footplate fixation.'),
('c8713f12-482c-5af5-8d59-190074cd8de9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Klippel-Feil syndrome features congenital fusion of cervical vertebrae and may be associated with:', '["Cataract","Retinopathy","Optic atrophy","Aniridia","Hearing loss"]'::jsonb, 'Hearing loss', 'Klippel-Feil syndrome, defined by congenital fusion of cervical vertebrae, is frequently associated with hearing loss as well as renal and cardiac anomalies. The listed ocular findings such as cataract and aniridia are not characteristic of this syndrome.'),
('c18747ef-57f2-5988-b9d7-b7b7276bf274'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Waardenburg syndrome is associated with:', '["Optic atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Glaucoma a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Retinitis pigmentosa mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital cataract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensorineural hearing loss and pigmentary abnormalities"]'::jsonb, 'Sensorineural hearing loss and pigmentary abnormalities', 'Waardenburg syndrome combines sensorineural hearing loss with pigmentary abnormalities such as a white forelock, heterochromia iridis, and depigmented skin patches, owing to defective neural crest melanocyte development. The retinal and anterior segment diseases listed are not features.'),
('bbe7db3b-371f-5984-ae3c-9a574bbf18a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Usher syndrome is characterized by:', '["Microphthalmia and deafness","Deafness and retinitis pigmentosa","Optic atrophy and deafness","Glaucoma and deafness","Cataract and deafness"]'::jsonb, 'Deafness and retinitis pigmentosa', 'Usher syndrome is an autosomal recessive disorder combining congenital or early-onset sensorineural deafness with retinitis pigmentosa causing progressive visual loss. The other pairings do not match this syndrome.'),
('0335bcfe-0cbe-55ea-b5ab-a5c8bf16dcf5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Pendred syndrome features:', '["Deafness and limb defects","Cataract and goiter","Deafness and cardiac anomalies","Deafness and renal anomalies","Sensorineural hearing loss and goiter"]'::jsonb, 'Sensorineural hearing loss and goiter', 'Pendred syndrome consists of sensorineural hearing loss with a goiter, resulting from a defect of iodide organification in the thyroid. The other options pair deafness with non-thyroidal anomalies.'),
('47c0604d-c3ed-5bfe-8015-5678bd7364da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The nose develops from the:', '["First pharyngeal arch","Mandibular process and maxillary processes","Neural crest","Frontonasal process and maxillary processes","Otic placode"]'::jsonb, 'Frontonasal process and maxillary processes', 'The nose develops from the frontonasal process, which contributes the nasal placodes and medial nasal prominences, together with the maxillary processes, which form the alae of the nose. The other listed structures or processes are not the nasal primordium.'),
('357d966d-b1bc-5eb6-a567-770b7e0d8dfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The nasal placodes are thickenings of:', '["Endoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Otic ectoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Surface ectoderm on the frontonasal process","Neural ectoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface ectoderm on the frontonasal process', 'The nasal (olfactory) placodes are localized thickenings of the surface ectoderm overlying the frontonasal process. They are not derived from endoderm, mesoderm, or neural ectoderm, and the otic ectoderm forms the inner ear structures.'),
('de7a088e-8ae1-5354-92ab-0209a814b600'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The nasal pits develop into:', '["Optic groove resulting in decreased functional activity","Otic vesicle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oral cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nares (nostrils) and nasal cavities"]'::jsonb, 'Nares (nostrils) and nasal cavities', 'The nasal pits, formed by invagination of the nasal placodes, deepen to become the nasal cavities, while their openings form the nares (nostrils). The optic groove and otic vesicle develop from separate structures.'),
('8676c5ad-9b98-5c89-aeed-2860b7e953c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The primitive choanae form when the:', '["Palate fuses","Tongue descends","Oronasal membrane ruptures","Buccopharyngeal membrane ruptures","Facial prominences fuse"]'::jsonb, 'Oronasal membrane ruptures', 'The primitive choanae open between the nasal and oral cavities when the oronasal membrane ruptures. Rupture of the buccopharyngeal membrane and the later fusion events occur at different developmental stages.'),
('774711e7-642d-55b7-83b5-279fbdc32b93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The definitive palate (secondary palate) forms from the:', '["Mandibular process","Medial nasal prominences","Palatine shelves of the maxillary processes","Lateral nasal prominences","Frontonasal process"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine shelves of the maxillary processes', 'The definitive (secondary) palate forms from the palatine shelves, which grow medially from the maxillary processes and fuse with each other and the nasal septum midline. The primary palate, in contrast, arises from the intermaxillary segment.'),
('5ba89dc8-c741-5de0-a150-72d8db795cbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The primary palate is derived from the:', '["Maxillary process resulting in decreased functional activity","Mandibular process accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyoid arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Intermaxillary segment of the frontonasal process","Palatine shelves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intermaxillary segment of the frontonasal process', 'The primary palate arises from the intermaxillary segment of the frontonasal process, which also carries the four incisor teeth and the philtrum. The palatine shelves form the secondary palate, and the other processes are not involved.'),
('1abe05a5-3ab3-5339-a81f-2426fb012953'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The incisive foramen marks the junction between:', '["Hard and soft palates","Maxilla and premaxilla","Palatine shelves and septum","Palate and nasal septum","Primary and secondary palates"]'::jsonb, 'Primary and secondary palates', 'The incisive foramen marks the junction between the primary palate (premaxilla) and the secondary palate. It lies in the midline behind the incisor teeth, where the two palatal components meet.'),
('913ed545-3442-5a4a-922f-1373955e13ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Cleft lip results from failure of fusion of the:', '["Mandibular processes","Palatine shelves","Maxillary process with the lateral nasal prominence","Frontonasal process with maxillary process","Maxillary process with the medial nasal prominence"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary process with the medial nasal prominence', 'Cleft lip results from failure of the maxillary process to fuse with the ipsilateral medial nasal prominence. Failure of the palatine shelves to fuse produces cleft palate, and the lateral nasal prominence fuses normally with the maxillary process.'),
('0c2b8827-80be-5202-be92-aef9bb4f8ceb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Isolated cleft palate results from failure of fusion of the:', '["Palatine shelves","Medial and lateral nasal prominences","Maxillary and mandibular processes","Oral and nasal cavities","Tongue and palate"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine shelves', 'Isolated cleft palate results from failure of the palatine shelves to fuse with each other and with the nasal septum in the midline. Failure of maxillary with medial nasal fusion causes cleft lip instead of isolated cleft palate.'),
('1edab8e0-a604-52e0-9d49-a5b735ec1b8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Bilateral cleft lip results from failure of fusion of the:', '["Mandibular processes resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontonasal process accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal placodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Maxillary processes with the medial nasal prominence on both sides","Palatine shelves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary processes with the medial nasal prominence on both sides', 'Bilateral cleft lip results from failure of the maxillary processes to fuse with the medial nasal prominence on both sides. The frontonasal process, nasal placodes, and palatine shelves are not the sites of this fusion failure.'),
('3cc2f602-3d04-5dea-b865-6af139f06a97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The most common type of oral cleft is:', '["Bilateral cleft lip","Median cleft lip","Microform cleft","Unilateral cleft lip with cleft palate","Isolated cleft palate"]'::jsonb, 'Unilateral cleft lip with cleft palate', 'Unilateral cleft lip with cleft palate is the most common type of oral cleft. Isolated cleft palate, bilateral cleft lip, microform, and median cleft lip all occur less frequently.'),
('1a4bc4d1-259d-526e-8c9f-1461eaaeac8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Teratogens associated with cleft palate include:', '["Paracetamol","Alcohol","Amoxicillin","and phenytoin","corticosteroids"]'::jsonb, 'Alcohol', 'Alcohol is a recognized teratogen that can disturb craniofacial development, including the palate. Paracetamol and amoxicillin are not regarded as classical teratogens of the orofacial region in routine use.'),
('28bc9c1e-b423-57a7-8aa0-1d678227eaa9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Treacher Collins syndrome features:', '["and ear anomalies","Polydactyly","Malar hypoplasia","Cleft lip only","micrognathia"]'::jsonb, 'Malar hypoplasia', 'Treacher Collins syndrome (mandibulofacial dysostosis) is characterized by hypoplasia of the malar (zygomatic) bones, along with micrognathia and ear anomalies. Polydactyly and an isolated cleft lip are not features of this syndrome.'),
('eea402c0-2c4b-556c-ada6-854661182bb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Pierre Robin sequence includes:', '["and cleft palate","glossoptosis","Micrognathia","Cleft lip only","Macroglossia"]'::jsonb, 'Micrognathia', 'Pierre Robin sequence is defined by micrognathia (small mandible), which allows the tongue to fall backward (glossoptosis) and obstruct palatal fusion, producing aU-shaped cleft palate. The defining feature is the small mandible.'),
('c1f79a8c-8ce8-5480-a1f9-3bd258661167'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The olfactory nerves (CN I) develop from:', '["Olfactory placode","Optic vesicle","Frontonasal process","Neural crest","Mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory placode', 'The olfactory nerves (CN I) develop from the olfactory placode, a thickening of surface ectoderm on the frontonasal process. The optic vesicle, neural crest, and mesoderm give rise to different structures.'),
('b74d301c-4a1c-51c2-a3b9-e468344440bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Kallmann syndrome is characterized by:', '["Cataract and anosmia","Deafness and ataxia","Anosmia and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Nystagmus and hearing loss","Visual loss and diabetes"]'::jsonb, 'Anosmia and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism', 'Kallmann syndrome combines anosmia, from failure of olfactory bulb development, with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, from failure of GnRH neurons to migrate from the olfactory placode to the hypothalamus. The other pairings describe different clinical entities.'),
('c789d26f-9e59-5dc8-9a2b-b5fbb030b636'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The olfactory bulb develops from the:', '["Rhombencephalon","Myelencephalon","Telencephalon","Mesencephalon","Diencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Telencephalon', 'The olfactory bulb develops as an outgrowth from the telencephalon, the most rostral of the brain vesicles. The diencephalon and the hindbrain and midbrain vesicles do not contribute to the olfactory bulb.'),
('f04a2cf9-3d76-552b-8233-1c7c6488368c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The cornea is normally avascular and transparent. Which embryonic layer contributes to its transparency?', '["Endothelium from neural crest","Surface ectoderm alone","Optic cup cells","Vitreal cells","Mesoderm from somites"]'::jsonb, 'Endothelium from neural crest', 'The corneal endothelium, which lines the posterior surface and maintains corneal transparency through its fluid pump function, is derived from neural crest cells. The surface ectoderm forms only the epithelium, and the other listed sources do not provide corneal layers.'),
('a40977a6-773c-5ac3-9df8-e99bb93d3e2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital corneal opacity can result from:', '["Coloboma","Aniridia","Peters anomaly","Cataract","Sclerocornea"]'::jsonb, 'Peters anomaly', 'Peters anomaly, caused by abnormal separation of the lens vesicle from the overlying cornea, produces congenital central corneal opacity with adhesions between the cornea and iris. Aniridia, coloboma, and cataract do not primarily cause corneal opacity.'),
('38f069b0-65db-544b-a820-2f7197d4b423'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Strabismus (squint) can be caused by:', '["Lens dislocation resulting in decreased functional activity","Corneal opacity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glaucoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Cataract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abnormal development of extraocular muscles or their innervation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal development of extraocular muscles or their innervation', 'Strabismus can result from abnormal development of the extraocular muscles or defects in their cranial nerve innervation (CN III, IV, VI), which disrupt conjugate eye movements. The other listed conditions do not directly disturb ocular alignment.'),
('591de312-001e-5103-8de3-5d4e1630656a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital nystagmus is most commonly:', '["Sensory nystagmus due to poor vision","Motor nystagmus due to abnormal efferent pathway","Optokinetic nystagmus","Physiological nystagmus","Vestibular nystagmus"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory nystagmus due to poor vision', 'Congenital nystagmus is most often sensory, resulting from poor vision that prevents the development of normal fixation. Motor nystagmus from efferent pathway lesions and the physiological and vestibular types are less common causes.'),
('87899060-425f-53bc-a57c-5023825baef2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The olfactory placode is also called the:', '["Optic placode","Otic placode","Nasal placode","Lens placode","Trigeminal placode"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal placode', 'The olfactory placode is also called the nasal placode, the thickening of surface ectoderm on the frontonasal process from which the olfactory nerves develop. The optic, otic, lens, and trigeminal placodes form different structures.'),
('098bcb75-748c-5124-bf08-d2be207be736'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The cornea receives its sensory innervation from the:', '["Maxillary division of trigeminal nerve (V2)","Oculomotor nerve","Facial nerve","Mandibular division (V3)","Ophthalmic division of trigeminal nerve (V1)"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic division of trigeminal nerve (V1)', 'The cornea is innervated by the long and short ciliary nerves, branches of the nasociliary nerve from the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (V1). The maxillary and mandibular divisions and other cranial nerves do not supply the cornea.'),
('8362eb2b-bde4-5798-8255-e1428ae08d06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The lacrimal gland is located in the:', '["Superolateral orbit","Superomedial orbit","Central orbit","Inferolateral orbit","Inferomedial orbit"]'::jsonb, 'Superolateral orbit', 'The lacrimal gland lies in the superolateral aspect of the orbit, within the lacrimal fossa of the frontal bone. The other orbital quadrants are occupied by different structures such as the optic nerve, muscles, and fat.'),
('6ba66fdc-a623-5215-a3f5-be943bbfd0b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens into the:', '["Sphenoethmoidal recess","Middle meatus","Superior meatus","Oral cavity","Inferior meatus of the nasal cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior meatus of the nasal cavity', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens into the inferior meatus of the nasal cavity, beneath the inferior nasal concha. The sphenoethmoidal recess and the superior and middle meati receive drainage from the paranasal sinuses.'),
('9b563598-13af-576a-a56a-7a5048348e89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The valve of Hasner is located at the:', '["Distal end of the nasolacrimal duct","Lacrimal punctum","Common canaliculus","Superior canaliculus","Lacrimal sac"]'::jsonb, 'Distal end of the nasolacrimal duct', 'The valve of Hasner is a mucosal fold at the distal end of the nasolacrimal duct, where it opens into the inferior meatus of the nose. It is not located at the punctum, canaliculi, or lacrimal sac.'),
('fbeb890b-e61e-5676-a23e-30406f80c3ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction typically presents with:', '["Epiphora (excessive tearing) and mucoid discharge","Proptosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ptosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Pupillary dilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ecchymosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epiphora (excessive tearing) and mucoid discharge', 'Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction, usually from failure of canalization at the valve of Hasner, presents with persistent tearing (epiphora) and mucoid discharge. Proptosis, ptosis, pupillary dilation, and ecchymosis have different underlying causes.'),
('f71b3ea8-ef5c-5cc5-9678-a55830ff1b7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The trochlear nerve (CN IV) supplies which extraocular muscle?', '["Superior oblique","Medial rectus","Lateral rectus","Superior rectus","Inferior oblique"]'::jsonb, 'Superior oblique', 'The trochlear nerve (CN IV) innervates the superior oblique muscle, which depresses the adducted eye and intorts it. The other recti and the inferior oblique are supplied by the oculomotor nerve, and the lateral rectus by the abducens nerve.'),
('6bf5e2df-0db3-5ff4-8d1d-d2a5d94fab27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The abducens nerve (CN VI) supplies which extraocular muscle?', '["Lateral rectus","Superior oblique","Superior rectus","Inferior rectus","Medial rectus"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral rectus', 'The abducens nerve (CN VI) supplies the lateral rectus, which abducts the eyeball. The superior oblique is supplied by CN IV, and the medial, inferior, and superior recti and the inferior oblique by CN III.'),
('e4269bdd-6e48-50b8-aa21-f2067b0e4eea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The oculomotor nerve (CN III) does NOT supply which muscle?', '["Inferior rectus","Medial rectus","Lateral rectus","Inferior oblique","Superior rectus"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral rectus', 'The oculomotor nerve (CN III) supplies the medial, inferior, and superior recti and the inferior oblique, as well as the levator palpebrae superioris, but not the lateral rectus, which is supplied by the abducens nerve (CN VI).'),
('1aab6d86-5f0b-5d70-b128-c5c7a07cd7fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The internal carotid artery supplies the eye via the:', '["Superficial temporal artery","Anterior cerebral artery","Ophthalmic artery","Middle meningeal artery","Maxillary artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery', 'The internal carotid artery gives off the ophthalmic artery as its first major intracranial branch, which enters the orbit through the optic canal and supplies the eye and its adnexa. The other arteries listed are not the ocular supply from the internal carotid.'),
('0f624244-5a4b-5d35-bd86-fe08cae7ecfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The central retinal artery is a branch of the:', '["Ophthalmic artery","Maxillary artery","External carotid artery","Internal carotid artery","Anterior cerebral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery', 'The central retinal artery is the first branch of the ophthalmic artery, entering the optic nerve to supply the inner layers of the retina. The other arteries listed do not give rise to it.'),
('7c386e9b-303b-501b-8c31-5e4b115d8e17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The short posterior ciliary arteries supply the:', '["Cornea resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iris mediated by specific receptor activation","Choroid and outer retina","Lens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid and outer retina', 'The short posterior ciliary arteries pierce the sclera around the optic nerve and supply the choroid and the outer layers of the retina. The iris, lens, cornea, and optic nerve have different vascular sources.'),
('02457c26-0ce8-5fd9-b6f8-1fe7eaed28b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The long posterior ciliary arteries supply the:', '["Ciliary body and iris","Retina","Optic nerve","Cornea","Choroid"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary body and iris', 'The long posterior ciliary arteries run forward between the sclera and choroid to supply the ciliary body and iris, forming the major arterial circle of the iris. The choroid is supplied by the short posterior ciliary arteries, and the retina and optic nerve by other vessels.'),
('878ad801-4271-5bd9-b085-a780349f6b21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The anterior ciliary arteries are derived from:', '["Ethmoidal arteries","Lacrimal artery","Posterior ciliary arteries","Frontal artery","Muscular branches of the ophthalmic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular branches of the ophthalmic artery', 'The anterior ciliary arteries arise from the muscular branches of the ophthalmic artery and run with the rectus muscles before piercing the sclera near the limbus to supply the anterior segment. The posterior ciliary arteries instead supply the choroid and optic nerve head, and the other listed arteries do not give origin to the anterior ciliary vessels.'),
('c1c73922-bdb5-5159-8e05-4e5a498e7fb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The vortex veins drain the:', '["Optic nerve","Choroid","Ciliary body","Retina","Iris"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid', 'The vortex (vorticosae) veins collect venous blood from the choroid and exit the sclera at the equator before draining into the ophthalmic veins. The retina drains separately via the central retinal vein, while the iris and ciliary body drain through their own venous channels.'),
('46bada8f-6fa4-514f-a6ce-b9064f8d152a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The central retinal vein drains into the:', '["Inferior ophthalmic vein","Sigmoid sinus","Cavernous sinus","Straight sinus","Superior ophthalmic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Cavernous sinus', 'The central retinal vein leaves the optic nerve within the orbit and empties into the cavernous sinus, often joining the superior ophthalmic vein along the way. The sigmoid and straight sinuses are intracranial venous sinuses unrelated to retinal venous drainage.'),
('06872410-4fda-51e5-b294-99ae04dadab5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The superior ophthalmic vein drains into the:', '["Facial vein","Pterygoid plexus","Sigmoid sinus","Cavernous sinus","Petrosal sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Cavernous sinus', 'The superior ophthalmic vein collects venous blood from the orbit and empties into the cavernous sinus, providing a potential pathway for spread of orbital infection into the intracranial venous sinuses. The facial vein and pterygoid plexus communicate with the orbit but are not its terminal drainage.'),
('8e1b6ccf-8b56-5b06-827a-fbfee346c4e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The sclera is continuous with the dura mater at the:', '["Optic disc","Limbus","Lamina cribrosa","Optic nerve sheath","Ora serrata"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve sheath', 'At the back of the orbit the sclera merges with the dural sheath of the optic nerve, so the dura is continuous with the sclera. The lamina cribrosa is the perforated scleral area where nerve fibers exit, and the ora serrata marks the anterior retinal limit.'),
('0718cef7-47e1-58ff-806f-2eab2eac0c5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The lamina cribrosa is a perforated region of the:', '["Sclera","Iris","Choroid","Retina","Cornea"]'::jsonb, 'Sclera', 'The lamina cribrosa is the sieve-like, perforated part of the sclera through which the optic nerve axons exit the globe, and it corresponds to the optic disc. It is a structurally weak point vulnerable to damage when intraocular pressure is raised.'),
('e96f30dc-19a5-527d-99e8-389fd5eaa852'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The fovea centralis is the region of:', '["Lowest visual acuity","Highest visual acuity","Lens attachment","Optic nerve exit","Vascular supply center"]'::jsonb, 'Highest visual acuity', 'The fovea centralis is a small central depression of the macula packed almost exclusively with cones, giving it the sharpest vision in the retina. The optic disc has no photoreceptors and is the blind spot, so acuity is lowest there.'),
('cde5f4e9-e32a-5b08-a9b0-7ba9ed4c93ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The macula lutea contains a yellow pigment called:', '["Rhodopsin","Hemoglobin","Melanin","Lutein","Bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Lutein', 'The macula lutea owes its yellow color to the carotenoid pigment lutein, which protects the underlying photoreceptors from light damage. Rhodopsin is the visual pigment of rods and is not present in the macula.'),
('c3bfdabb-f6e6-5351-8638-00c1317a48e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The optic disc is:', '["The point of highest acuity","The vascular center","The light-sensitive region","The lens attachment","The blind spot"]'::jsonb, 'The blind spot', 'The optic disc is the exit point of the optic nerve where retinal ganglion cell axons gather; it contains no photoreceptors, so it forms the physiologic blind spot. Peak acuity lies at the fovea, which is entirely separate from the disc.'),
('447d54fa-c2ec-56b4-abad-74d904df8002'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The ten layers of the retina from outer to inner include the:', '["outer nuclear","inner plexiform","Photoreceptor layer","outer plexiform","inner nuclear"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptor layer', 'The photoreceptor layer is the outermost layer of the retina, lying directly against the retinal pigment epithelium, so it is the first layer in the outer-to-inner sequence. The nuclear and plexiform layers listed occupy progressively more central positions.'),
('5a449250-c3f2-59e6-b46d-f3997370133a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Rods are responsible for:', '["Color vision","Photopic (bright light) vision","Scotopic (dim light) vision","Visual acuity","Daylight vision"]'::jsonb, 'Scotopic (dim light) vision', 'Rods contain rhodopsin, have a very low light threshold, and mediate scotopic (dim-light, night) vision without color discrimination. Cones, not rods, are responsible for photopic vision, color perception, and high visual acuity.'),
('ec09e788-0f38-56b0-8c57-09dd73d180b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Cones are responsible for:', '["Low light vision","Peripheral vision","Photopic vision and color vision","Motion detection","Scotopic vision"]'::jsonb, 'Photopic vision and color vision', 'Cones are concentrated at the fovea and mediate photopic (bright-light) vision together with color discrimination, providing high visual acuity. Rods, found mainly in the periphery, handle low-light and peripheral vision.'),
('9a530e25-2157-5715-8d5b-9547b9bc19c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'There are three types of cones sensitive to:', '["green (medium)","and blue (short) wavelengths","Red","Red (long)","yellow"]'::jsonb, 'Red (long)', 'The three cone types are the red (long wavelength), green (medium wavelength), and blue (short wavelength) photoreceptors, whose different opsins provide trichromatic color vision. The red cone is one of the three required classes, while the other fragments do not identify a cone type.'),
('687a8334-6c32-58a6-be69-5abac140d009'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Color blindness is most commonly:', '["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","Mitochondrial","X-linked recessive","Y-linked"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked recessive', 'The most frequent forms of color blindness are X-linked recessive traits, so they predominantly affect males and are transmitted through carrier females. The autosomal and Y-linked patterns listed do not account for the typical inheritance of color vision defects.'),
('d123c29b-b9ef-5ec0-82c0-ece9acaff7e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The aqueous humor is produced by the:', '["Iris epithelium","Corneal endothelium","Lens epithelium","Ciliary epithelium","Retinal pigment epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary epithelium', 'The non-pigmented cells of the ciliary epithelium covering the ciliary processes actively secrete aqueous humor into the posterior chamber. The iris, corneal endothelium, and lens epithelium do not produce aqueous humor.'),
('e5c945c3-b026-5757-876c-c488084133b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The aqueous humor drains primarily through the:', '["Trabecular meshwork and Schlemm canal","Anterior chamber accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pupil mediated by specific receptor activation","Iris crypts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ciliary cleft leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trabecular meshwork and Schlemm canal', 'Aqueous humor leaves the anterior chamber through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm, then enters aqueous veins and the episcleral venous system. Impaired outflow at this site raises intraocular pressure and is central to glaucoma.'),
('40e96333-7e74-5def-9c13-10269f207631'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The vitreous humor is attached firmly to the:', '["Ora serrata","Cornea","Optic disc","Lens","Iris"]'::jsonb, 'Ora serrata', 'The vitreous base is firmly adherent to the ora serrata, the scalloped anterior boundary of the retina, with additional attachments to the lens zonules and the optic disc margin. The cornea and iris have no firm attachment to the vitreous body.'),
('23426118-1a36-599b-9003-76fdd629e6d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The lens is enclosed by the:', '["Lens epithelium","Lens capsule","Descemet membrane","Lens fibers","Elschnig membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Lens capsule', 'The lens is enveloped by the lens capsule, an elastic basement membrane secreted by the lens epithelium that also provides the surface on which the zonular fibers insert. Descemet membrane belongs to the cornea, not the lens.'),
('dcb1f4f4-8121-5519-aabf-247252e90335'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Accommodation is mediated by contraction of the:', '["Orbicularis oculi","Iris sphincter","Iris dilator","Extraocular muscles","Ciliary muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary muscle', 'Contraction of the ciliary muscle slackens the zonular fibers, allowing the elastic lens to become more convex, which is the mechanism of accommodation for near vision. The iris muscles control pupil size and the extraocular muscles move the globe, neither of which alters lens shape.'),
('91b65cd2-4e47-54eb-9b51-e72b828cd3ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Presbyopia is due to:', '["Lens dislocation","Ciliary muscle paralysis","Iris atrophy","Corneal flattening","Decreased lens elasticity with age"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased lens elasticity with age', 'Presbyopia is the age-related loss of near vision that results from hardening and reduced elasticity of the lens, which can no longer change shape during accommodation. It is not produced by lens dislocation, paralysis of the ciliary muscle, or corneal flattening.'),
('98b3fa6b-78f2-5459-9f8f-774c6b58fb7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The pupil dilates via contraction of the:', '["Orbital muscle","Sphincter pupillae muscle (parasympathetic)","Superior tarsal muscle","Dilator pupillae muscle (sympathetic)","Ciliary muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Dilator pupillae muscle (sympathetic)', 'The dilator pupillae is a radially arranged iris muscle under sympathetic control, and its contraction widens the pupil in dim light. The sphincter pupillae does the opposite, and neither the superior tarsal muscle nor the ciliary muscle changes pupil size.'),
('684815a7-2def-506c-bec8-9702399d9c08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The pupil constricts via contraction of the:', '["Superior rectus","Ciliary muscle","Sphincter pupillae muscle (parasympathetic)","Levator palpebrae","Dilator pupillae (sympathetic)"]'::jsonb, 'Sphincter pupillae muscle (parasympathetic)', 'The sphincter pupillae is a circular iris muscle innervated by parasympathetic fibers traveling with the oculomotor nerve, and its contraction constricts the pupil in bright light and during accommodation. The dilator pupillae produces the opposite effect, and the extraocular muscles do not act on the pupil.'),
('d60b361f-5e6b-5679-9fef-decaa72723ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The light reflex pathway involves:', '["Optic nerve → hypothalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic nerve → superior colliculus → oculomotor nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Optic nerve → pretectal nucleus → Edinger-Westphal nucleus → ciliary ganglion → sphincter pupillae","Retina directly to Edinger-Westphal nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic nerve → lateral geniculate → occipital cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve → pretectal nucleus → Edinger-Westphal nucleus → ciliary ganglion → sphincter pupillae', 'The pupillary light reflex runs from the retina via the optic nerve to the pretectal nucleus, then to both Edinger-Westphal nuclei, down the oculomotor nerve, through the ciliary ganglion, and finally to the sphincter pupillae. The listed alternatives omit the pretectal and Edinger-Westphal relay or describe the visual (conscious) pathway to the cortex.'),
('ab04f53a-011b-53f6-91d5-16e00031e553'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Horner syndrome includes:', '["Ptosis","mydriasis","miosis","and anhidrosis","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Ptosis', 'Horner syndrome results from interruption of the ocular sympathetic pathway and classically produces ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis on the affected side. Ptosis is therefore a characteristic feature, whereas mydriasis (pupil dilation) is not seen.'),
('6dfb3e01-c6ee-5462-b3f6-9a793d956fca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The ciliary ganglion is located:', '["In the infratemporal fossa","On the optic nerve","In the pterygopalatine fossa","Behind the eye within the orbit","Within the brainstem"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the eye within the orbit', 'The ciliary ganglion lies in the posterior part of the orbit, between the optic nerve and the lateral rectus muscle, just anterior to the superior orbital fissure. The infratemporal and pterygopalatine fossae and the brainstem are not orbital locations.'),
('867f5b4b-01ad-58b6-8689-990fa79af639'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The ciliary ganglion receives its parasympathetic input from:', '["Edinger-Westphal nucleus via CN III","Trigeminal nucleus via CN V","Dorsal vagal nucleus via CN X","Inferior salivatory nucleus via CN IX","Superior salivatory nucleus via CN VII"]'::jsonb, 'Edinger-Westphal nucleus via CN III', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus travel in the oculomotor nerve (CN III) to synapse in the ciliary ganglion. The other nuclei listed belong to the vagus or the salivatory centers and do not supply the ciliary ganglion.'),
('35dbdf19-516e-51c6-b82a-e874b32e4970'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Postganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the ciliary ganglion supply:', '["Dilator pupillae resulting in decreased functional activity","Lacrimal gland accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Salivary glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle","Nasal mucosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle', 'Short postganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the ciliary ganglion innervate the sphincter pupillae and the ciliary muscle, producing miosis and accommodation. The dilator pupillae receives sympathetic innervation, and the lacrimal and salivary glands are supplied by other ganglia.'),
('c483123c-34aa-59bd-a376-29c27323523a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The trigeminal nerve (CN V) does NOT provide sensory innervation to the:', '["Conjunctiva","Cornea","Eyelids","Lens and vitreous","Iris"]'::jsonb, 'Lens and vitreous', 'The lens and vitreous have no sensory innervation, whereas the cornea, conjunctiva, eyelids, and iris are all supplied by branches of the trigeminal nerve. This is why pain from the cornea and iris is carried by CN V, while the lens and vitreous are insensitive.'),
('168e4c12-94d3-5a1a-b2b5-db9e6fae3807'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The nasociliary nerve is a branch of the:', '["Oculomotor nerve","Facial nerve","Ophthalmic division of CN V","Mandibular division of CN V","Maxillary division of CN V"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic division of CN V', 'The nasociliary nerve is the medial branch of the ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve, and it gives off the long ciliary, ethmoidal, infratrochlear, and anterior ethmoidal nerves. The other divisions of CN V and the oculomotor and facial nerves do not give rise to it.'),
('883a43a8-f256-5096-8fda-4e511f634e24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The infratrochlear nerve supplies:', '["Lateral eyelid","Lower eyelid only","Medial eyelid and side of nose","Cornea","Upper eyelid only"]'::jsonb, 'Medial eyelid and side of nose', 'The infratrochlear nerve, a terminal branch of the nasociliary nerve, supplies sensation to the medial eyelids, medial conjunctiva, lacrimal sac, and the side of the nose. The lateral and lower eyelid regions are served by other trigeminal branches.'),
('1bc5d60a-c7f2-5fe4-b9cc-243ef5661b24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The supratrochlear and supraorbital nerves are branches of the:', '["Lacrimal nerve (V1)","Oculomotor nerve","Frontal nerve (V1)","Facial nerve","Maxillary nerve (V2)"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal nerve (V1)', 'The frontal nerve is the largest branch of the ophthalmic division (V1) and divides into the supraorbital and supratrochlear nerves, which supply the forehead and scalp. The lacrimal nerve is a separate branch of V1, and the oculomotor and facial nerves have no such branches.'),
('5fe91338-04ba-5d00-af96-7a639288e90f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The lacrimal nerve (V1) supplies:', '["Motor innervation to levator palpebrae","Motor innervation to orbicularis oculi","Parasympathetic innervation to lacrimal gland","Tears production","Sensation to the lacrimal gland region"]'::jsonb, 'Sensation to the lacrimal gland region', 'The lacrimal nerve, a branch of V1, carries sensory fibers to the skin and conjunctiva of the lateral eyelid and the region of the lacrimal gland; it does not produce tears or provide motor innervation. Secretory fibers reach the gland from the pterygopalatine ganglion via the zygomatic and lacrimal nerves.'),
('5e0b0b8c-afb1-5ec6-8051-abac1bde5dd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Parasympathetic innervation to the lacrimal gland comes from:', '["CN V (via trigeminal ganglion)","CN VII (via pterygopalatine ganglion)","CN IX (via otic ganglion)","Sympathetic chain","CN III (via ciliary ganglion)"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII (via pterygopalatine ganglion)', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers of the facial nerve (CN VII) travel through the greater petrosal nerve to synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion, and postganglionic fibers reach the lacrimal gland via the zygomatic and lacrimal nerves. The other nerves and ganglia do not provide the secretomotor supply to the lacrimal gland.'),
('e62ad6ec-6df4-56d1-9878-799f9bd1fe72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Sympathetic innervation to the eye originates from:', '["Superior cervical ganglion","Pterygopalatine ganglion","Otic ganglion","Ciliary ganglion","Trigeminal ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Superior cervical ganglion', 'Sympathetic fibers to the orbit, including the dilator pupillae, superior tarsal muscle, and orbital vessels, originate from cell bodies in the superior cervical ganglion and travel along the internal carotid artery plexus. The pterygopalatine, otic, and ciliary ganglia are parasympathetic relays and the trigeminal ganglion is sensory.'),
('7d3c1138-a321-59e7-94aa-8db8df6f5c2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The optic nerve (CN II) is surrounded by:', '["Dura mater only","Pia mater only","meningeal covering","Arachnoid mater only","All three meningeal layers"]'::jsonb, 'All three meningeal layers', 'The optic nerve is invested by all three meningeal layers—dura, arachnoid, and pia—which are continuous with the intracranial meninges, so the subarachnoid space extends around the nerve. This continuity is why elevated intracranial pressure is transmitted to the optic disc and causes papilledema.'),
('5a96f534-9a40-5558-a74a-63cb1021bc97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Papilledema is swelling of the optic disc due to:', '["Hypotension","Central retinal artery occlusion","Optic neuritis","Increased intracranial pressure","Glaucoma"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intracranial pressure', 'Papilledema is optic disc swelling caused by raised intracranial pressure, which is transmitted along the optic nerve sheath and produces venous congestion and axoplasmic stasis. Hypotension, arterial occlusion, and optic neuritis do not elevate intracranial pressure.'),
('f3169f93-f620-52be-9706-00cb569b3762'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Optic atrophy refers to:', '["Cup-to-disc ratio increase","Hyperemia of the disc","Swelling of the optic disc","Pallor of the optic disc due to loss of axons","Excavation of the disc"]'::jsonb, 'Pallor of the optic disc due to loss of axons', 'Optic atrophy indicates pallor of the optic disc resulting from loss of retinal ganglion cell axons. Disc swelling is papilledema, and the increased cup-to-disc ratio with excavation is characteristic of glaucomatous damage.'),
('5f8ccc80-95fc-571e-9246-eadfbb5b5e4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Glaucoma is characterized by:', '["Elevated intraocular pressure alone","Normal IOP with visual loss only","Cataract with IOP increase","Macular edema","Progressive optic neuropathy with visual field loss"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive optic neuropathy with visual field loss', 'Glaucoma is defined as a progressive optic neuropathy with characteristic acquired visual field loss; although intraocular pressure is usually elevated, it is not raised in every form such as normal-tension glaucoma. The other choices omit the defining optic nerve and field damage.'),
('1a75a108-27d9-50f8-bcc5-149aa252fb55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Primary open-angle glaucoma is associated with:', '["Angle closure resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased resistance to aqueous outflow through the trabecular meshwork","Iris bombe mediated by specific receptor activation","Pupillary block without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neovascularization of the angle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased resistance to aqueous outflow through the trabecular meshwork', 'In primary open-angle glaucoma the iridocorneal angle remains open, but increased resistance to aqueous outflow through the trabecular meshwork raises intraocular pressure. Angle closure, iris bombe, and pupillary block all describe the closed-angle form of glaucoma.'),
('d7e08f30-390f-557d-a55d-310c61bd59b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Angle-closure glaucoma is caused by:', '["Blockage of the trabecular meshwork by the iris","Blockage of the pupil","Blockage of Schlemm canal","Lens dislocation a","Ciliary body swelling"]'::jsonb, 'Blockage of the trabecular meshwork by the iris', 'Angle-closure glaucoma occurs when the peripheral iris bows forward and blocks the trabecular meshwork, preventing aqueous outflow and causing an acute rise in intraocular pressure. This apposition of iris to the meshwork distinguishes it from open-angle glaucoma.'),
('bd4dbdc9-a32c-5046-85fb-73572ebb4063'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Cataract is opacification of the:', '["Vitreous","Retina","Lens","Cornea","Aqueous"]'::jsonb, 'Lens', 'A cataract is opacification of the lens, which disrupts the passage of light and produces progressive blurring of vision. The vitreous, cornea, and retina have their own distinct pathologies and are not the site of cataract formation.'),
('5f0ad66f-d8a3-5f48-88a7-e70af3a686ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital cataract is most commonly inherited as:', '["Multifactorial","Mitochondrial","Autosomal recessive","Autosomal dominant","X-linked recessive"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal dominant', 'Congenital cataract is most often an autosomal dominant trait, although recessive and X-linked forms also exist, and rubella infection in early pregnancy is a common environmental cause. The other listed patterns are not the most frequent inheritance mechanism.'),
('b61152d3-1cdc-5c39-9003-fb5d6886ec6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Age-related cataract is associated with:', '["Steroid use resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Radiation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oxidative stress and protein aggregation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidative stress and protein aggregation', 'Age-related (senile) cataract results from cumulative oxidative stress that denatures and aggregates lens proteins, causing opacification. Steroids, trauma, and radiation are alternative causes of cataract through different mechanisms rather than the aging process itself.'),
('36027462-c319-51bf-b4a1-bd6c5551d2e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The iris has two layers of smooth muscle. The sphincter pupillae is located:', '["In the ciliary body","On the lens capsule","At the iris root (radial)","At the pupillary margin (circular)","In the choroid"]'::jsonb, 'At the pupillary margin (circular)', 'The sphincter pupillae forms a circular ring of smooth muscle encircling the pupillary margin, and its contraction constricts the pupil. The radially arranged dilator pupillae lies more peripherally in the posterior stroma rather than at the pupil margin.'),
('1252a575-fef1-5036-940d-48d4dab3ed28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The dilator pupillae is located:', '["On the iris root","In the choroid","In the ciliary body","Circularly at the pupil margin","Radially in the posterior iris stroma"]'::jsonb, 'Radially in the posterior iris stroma', 'The dilator pupillae consists of radially oriented smooth muscle fibers lying in the posterior stroma of the iris adjacent to the posterior pigment epithelium, and its contraction widens the pupil. The sphincter pupillae is the circular muscle at the pupil margin.'),
('5adf7763-b1fa-53cf-ad66-fc2e29cdc269'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The ciliary body is divided into the:', '["Anterior and posterior chambers","Ora serrata and pars plana","Corona and zonule","Pars optica and pars ciliaris","Pars plicata (anterior) and pars plana (posterior)"]'::jsonb, 'Pars plicata (anterior) and pars plana (posterior)', 'The ciliary body has an anterior ridged pars plicata whose ciliary processes secrete aqueous humor, and a flat posterior pars plana that gives origin to the zonular fibers. The anterior and posterior chambers are spaces of the eye, not parts of the ciliary body.'),
('975a9b88-052e-5b89-a653-4391b8792a74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The zonular fibers (suspensory ligament) attach the lens to the:', '["Iris","Ciliary body","Choroid","Cornea","Ora serrata"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary body', 'The zonular fibers arise from the pars plana and ciliary processes of the ciliary body and insert into the lens capsule, suspending the lens. The iris, choroid, and cornea do not provide the fixed origin for the suspensory ligament.'),
('9d8c1629-217f-59f2-9076-36430da1f89d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'During accommodation (near vision):', '["Pupil dilation","the ciliary muscle contracts","Relaxation of zonular fibers and increased lens convexity","causing:","Increased zonular tension and lens flattening"]'::jsonb, 'the ciliary muscle contracts', 'During accommodation for near vision the ciliary muscle contracts, which slackens the zonular fibers and allows the elastic lens to become more convex to increase its refractive power. Relaxation of the ciliary muscle and increased zonular tension belong to distance vision, and the pupil constricts rather than dilates.'),
('89a1c81a-5422-5e24-9d54-85683b5d534f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'During distance vision:', '["and the zonular fibers are:","the ciliary muscle is relaxed","causing lens flattening","Relaxed","Taut"]'::jsonb, 'the ciliary muscle is relaxed', 'During distance vision the ciliary muscle is relaxed, so the zonular fibers become taut and pull the lens flat for focusing on distant objects. Contraction of the ciliary muscle with lax zonules occurs only during near accommodation.'),
('89b2c260-9796-5eb8-8f7a-526346d73f7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The smallest visible detail that two points can be separated and still be seen as two is called:', '["Depth perception","Visual acuity","Color discrimination a","Contrast sensitivity","Visual field"]'::jsonb, 'Visual acuity', 'Visual acuity is the ability to resolve two closely spaced points as separate, which is why it is tested with Snellen optotypes that measure the smallest resolvable detail. Depth perception, contrast sensitivity, and visual field measure different visual functions.'),
('70a0b945-b1ee-518b-8791-ee6c636be5fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Visual field testing assesses the function of the:', '["Optic nerve only","Peripheral retina only","Entire retina","Macula only","Fovea only"]'::jsonb, 'Entire retina', 'Visual field testing maps light detection across the whole retinal surface, from the peripheral retina to the macula, so any point in the field can arise from any retinal area. Isolating a single structure such as the optic nerve, peripheral retina, macula, or fovea would ignore the remaining functional retina and miss field defects elsewhere.'),
('15d0d33c-f15d-508c-9513-b25ffa29c705'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The blind spot corresponds to the:', '["Ora serrata","Optic disc","Fovea","Macula","Equator"]'::jsonb, 'Optic disc', 'The optic disc contains no photoreceptors because the optic nerve fibers and retinal vessels pass through it, producing the physiologic blind spot. The ora serrata, fovea, macula, and equator all carry sensory function and are not blind.'),
('df52f686-b746-5145-9953-466ee665fd0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The optic chiasm is located:', '["Below the pituitary gland","Above the pituitary gland","Within the sella turcica","In the temporal lobe","In the frontal lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Above the pituitary gland', 'The optic chiasm rests on the diaphragma sellae, directly above the pituitary gland and only a few millimeters from it, which is why sellar masses compress it. The sella turcica lies below the gland, and the chiasm does not sit inside the temporal or frontal lobe.'),
('7123f95e-9756-5690-b3df-a86ad0d6eec5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Bitemporal hemianopia is classically caused by a lesion at the:', '["Optic tract","Optic nerve","Lateral geniculate body","Optic chiasm","Occipital cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Optic chiasm', 'Nasal retinal fibers from both eyes cross in the optic chiasm, so compression there selectively interrupts these crossing fibers and produces bitemporal hemianopia. Optic nerve, tract, LGN, and occipital cortex lesions cause monocular, incongruous, or homonymous field defects rather than bitemporal loss.'),
('8596f339-a8dc-5793-ab91-1a606dd05940'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) has how many layers?', '["4","6","5","2","3"]'::jsonb, '6', 'The LGN has six laminae: the two ventral magnocellular layers (1-2) and the four dorsal parvocellular layers (3-6). Counts of two, three, four, or five layers do not match the standard six-layer organization.'),
('ade14d62-043f-58af-8e9a-34328df0bc7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Magnocellular layers of the LGN process:', '["Motion and low spatial frequency","Form recognition","Facial recognition","Edge detection a","Color and fine detail"]'::jsonb, 'Motion and low spatial frequency', 'The two magnocellular LGN layers receive input from M-type retinal ganglion cells and convey motion, flicker, and coarse low spatial frequency information. Form and facial recognition follow other cortical streams, while color and fine detail are handled by the parvocellular system.'),
('f8ca5f82-4376-5caf-9466-b500f88c03cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Parvocellular layers of the LGN process:', '["Motion and low contrast","high spatial frequency","and fine detail","Color","Depth perception"]'::jsonb, 'Color', 'Parvocellular LGN cells receive input from midget retinal ganglion cells and carry color, high spatial frequency, and fine detail information. The motion and low-contrast channel belongs to the magnocellular system, and depth perception depends on binocular disparity in higher cortices.'),
('28f9513f-0f25-547d-a6e3-ee30012ad4ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The primary visual cortex (V1) is located in the:', '["Postcentral gyrus","Fusiform gyrus","Calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe","Precentral gyrus","Superior temporal gyrus"]'::jsonb, 'Calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe', 'The primary visual cortex (Brodmann area 17) lines the banks of the calcarine sulcus in the occipital lobe. The postcentral gyrus, fusiform gyrus, precentral gyrus, and superior temporal gyrus subserve somatosensory, facial recognition, motor, and auditory functions respectively.'),
('54b381f8-c6db-5ed9-a95c-870272c48075'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The optic radiation (geniculocalcarine tract) carries fibers from the:', '["LGN to V1","Superior colliculus to LGN","Retina to LGN","V2 to V4","V1 to V2"]'::jsonb, 'LGN to V1', 'The geniculocalcarine tract projects from the lateral geniculate nucleus to the primary visual cortex at V1 and is therefore an output of the LGN. It does not connect retina to LGN, colliculus to LGN, or higher visual areas.'),
('986dc03c-b764-5d99-ae51-e5f420fcfd1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Meyer loop carries fibers from the:', '["Superior (inferior visual field) retina","Nasal retina","Peripheral retina","Macula","Inferior (superior visual field) retina"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior (superior visual field) retina', 'Meyer loop is the ventral optic radiation that sweeps through the temporal lobe and carries fibers from the inferior retina, which subserve the superior visual field. The superior retinal (inferior field) fibers travel in the dorsal radiation, and nasal, peripheral, and macular fibers are not exclusive to Meyer loop.'),
('7224e916-5466-53f3-8374-a5a140e6e1c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Lesion of Meyer loop causes:', '["Bitemporal hemianopia a","Contralateral inferior quadrantanopia","Contralateral superior quadrantanopia","Contralateral hemianopia","Ipsilateral superior quadrantanopia"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral superior quadrantanopia', 'Meyer loop carries inferior retinal fibers representing the superior visual field, so damage produces a contralateral superior quadrantanopia. An inferior quadrantanopia follows parietal radiation damage, while chiasmal and tract lesions yield bitemporal and hemianopic patterns.'),
('adff8c07-dea5-5df8-92a4-5b08fffebd06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The dorsal optic radiation (parietal loop) carries fibers from the:', '["Peripheral retina only","Inferior retina (superior visual field)","Macula only","Binocular field","Superior retina (inferior visual field)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior retina (inferior visual field)', 'The dorsal, parietal-loop optic radiation carries fibers from the superior retina, which represents the inferior visual field. Fiber for the peripheral retina, macula, and binocular field, and inferior retinal (superior field) fibers, travel in other parts of the pathway.'),
('5d36200c-6953-5372-8876-6a94f4dc984d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Lesion of the parietal optic radiation causes:', '["Contralateral inferior quadrantanopia","Bitemporal hemianopia","Contralateral hemianopia","Contralateral superior quadrantanopia","Upper altitudinal defect"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral inferior quadrantanopia', 'The superior retinal fibers traveling through the parietal radiation represent the inferior visual field, so damage creates a contralateral inferior quadrantanopia. Bitemporal, complete hemianopic, superior-quadrant, and altitudinal patterns arise from chiasmal, tract, Meyer loop, and retinal events.'),
('4cbdd32c-0e18-5b95-810e-955cc311d546'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The macula is represented in which part of V1?', '["Superior bank of calcarine sulcus","Inferior bank of calcarine sulcus","Posterior occipital pole","Medial occipital lobe","Anterior calcarine sulcus"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior occipital pole', 'Central (macular) vision maps to the most posterior part of the striate cortex at the occipital pole, which receives a dual blood supply. The anterior calcarine sulcus represents the peripheral field, while the banks of the sulcus encode sectors of the retina rather than the macula alone.'),
('a1b8403d-0b5c-5c26-aef8-87da4bbff393'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The peripheral visual field is represented in the:', '["Occipital pole","Anterior part of V1","Posterior part of V1","Fusiform gyrus","Middle temporal gyrus"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior part of V1', 'The peripheral field projects to the anterior part of V1 along the calcarine sulcus, while the occipital pole and posterior V1 handle the central field. The fusiform and middle temporal gyri lie outside the primary visual cortex.'),
('26e6aa5e-883d-545d-9827-5c528a2b57b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Macular sparing in occipital stroke occurs because:', '["Macula has bilateral representation resulting in decreased functional activity","The macular representation has dual blood supply from the middle cerebral and posterior cerebral arteries","Macular fibers cross earlier mediated by specific receptor activation","Macula is smaller without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Macular fibers are resistant to ischemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The macular representation has dual blood supply from the middle cerebral and posterior cerebral arteries', 'The occipital pole receives collateral blood from both the posterior cerebral and middle cerebral arteries, so a posterior cerebral artery stroke can spare the centrally placed macula. Bilateral representation, earlier crossing of fibers, small size, and ischemia resistance do not explain the sparing.'),
('c0d568a9-ff7b-5f77-b4a2-e2d76c7efc7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The superior colliculus is involved in:', '["Fine detail vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Color vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reflexive eye movements and visual orienting","Accommodation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pupillary reflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reflexive eye movements and visual orienting', 'The superior colliculus receives retinal and cortical input and drives reflexive saccades and orienting shifts of gaze toward visual targets. Fine detail, color, accommodation, and pupillary responses are governed by cortical, pretectal, and autonomic pathways rather than the colliculus.'),
('b1d265ed-5dc3-5ef2-884f-6bdba3a26270'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The pretectal nucleus is involved in the:', '["Color processing","Pupillary light reflex","Accommodation reflex","Motion detection","Visual acuity"]'::jsonb, 'Pupillary light reflex', 'The pretectal nucleus receives the afferent pupillary signal and relays to both Edinger-Westphal nuclei, generating the pupillary light reflex. Color processing, accommodation, motion detection, and acuity are served by other parts of the visual system.'),
('138a58db-710b-5116-bcd5-ce58329aa491'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The accommodation reflex involves:', '["Sympathetic to dilator pupillae resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III (parasympathetic) to ciliary muscle and sphincter pupillae","CN V to cornea mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VI to lateral rectus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IV to superior oblique leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III (parasympathetic) to ciliary muscle and sphincter pupillae', 'The accommodation reflex contracts the ciliary muscle to thicken the lens and constricts the sphincter pupillae, actions driven by parasympathetic fibers of CN III relayed through the ciliary ganglion. Sympathetic, trigeminal, abducens, and trochlear pathways play no part in this reflex.'),
('7d88d86e-431e-5ce7-98f3-c45720067161'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The near triad consists of:', '["Accommodation","divergence","and miosis","convergence","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Accommodation', 'The near reflex triad is accommodation, convergence, and pupillary miosis, coordinated to focus on close objects. Divergence is the opposite action and is not a member of the triad.'),
('749adb17-9e0b-5d4c-810c-a200c3bf6979'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Adie tonic pupil is caused by:', '["Damage to pretectal nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Damage to CN III accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Damage to sympathetic chain mediated by specific receptor activation","Damage to the ciliary ganglion or short ciliary nerves","Damage to CN VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Damage to the ciliary ganglion or short ciliary nerves', 'Adie tonic pupil follows postganglionic parasympathetic denervation of the iris, typically from damage to the ciliary ganglion or short ciliary nerves, leaving slow, supersensitive constriction. Pretectal, CN III, sympathetic, and abducens lesions cause different pupillary and ocular findings.'),
('cad49704-6d6a-5e20-bc9b-f36098d40c99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Argyll Robertson pupils (light-near dissociation) are classically associated with:', '["Neurosyphilis","Horner syndrome","Adie pupil a","Diabetes","Multiple sclerosis"]'::jsonb, 'Neurosyphilis', 'Argyll Robertson pupils, which constrict on accommodation but not to light, are classic for neurosyphilis with midbrain pupillary pathway damage. Horner syndrome, Adie pupil, diabetes, and multiple sclerosis yield different pupillary abnormalities.'),
('4eedf073-1c07-567d-af17-2fa957407bd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) indicates a lesion of the:', '["Afferent pathway (optic nerve or retina)","Ciliary ganglion","Sympathetic pathway","Efferent pathway (CN III)","Pretectal nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Afferent pathway (optic nerve or retina)', 'RAPD indicates asymmetric afferent conduction, produced by damage to the optic nerve or retina on the affected side. Efferent, sympathetic, pretectal, and ciliary ganglion disorders do not create an afferent pupillary defect.'),
('987698c1-a882-5634-b5b8-c80b5ecd6cfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The swinging flashlight test detects:', '["RAPD","Cataract","Adie pupil","Anisocoria","Horner syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'RAPD', 'The swinging flashlight test compares the afferent responsiveness of the two eyes, so it detects a relative afferent pupillary defect. Cataract, Adie pupil, anisocoria, and Horner syndrome are diagnosed by different means.'),
('4efc41f3-02eb-5839-b07a-5bb9490f8913'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Anisocoria greater in bright light suggests:', '["Iris atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Horner syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Physiological anisocoria mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic (CN III) lesion causing mydriasis","Sympathetic lesion causing miosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (CN III) lesion causing mydriasis', 'When parasympathetic innervation is damaged, the pupil cannot constrict in bright light, so the anisocoria becomes more prominent with illumination. Sympathetic lesions cause miosis with greater anisocoria in the dark, while Horner syndrome and physiological anisocoria behave differently.'),
('36f4e561-9ce2-5635-98f2-0ff41d3abc0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The Eustachian tube connects the:', '["Inner ear to the nasopharynx","Tympanic cavity to the mastoid air cells","Middle ear to the nasopharynx","Cochlea to the vestibule","Middle ear to the external ear"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear to the nasopharynx', 'The Eustachian (pharyngotympanic) tube links the tympanic cavity to the nasopharynx, permitting pressure equilibration and middle-ear drainage. The inner ear, mastoid cells, and external canal open into different structures.'),
('ec977aea-7a18-5222-b4ce-ff300cd217eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The Eustachian tube is wider and more horizontal in:', '["Children","Adolescents a","Elderly","Neonates","Adults"]'::jsonb, 'Children', 'In children the Eustachian tube is shorter, wider, and more horizontal, which favors reflux of nasopharyngeal secretions and middle-ear infection. It becomes longer and more angled with growth, lowering the risk in adults.'),
('5c71d891-d4a3-5c76-9f42-99c473e2c4e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The mastoid antrum communicates with the:', '["Nasopharynx via the Eustachian tube","Cochlea via the helicotrema","Middle ear via the aditus ad antrum","External ear via the external auditory meatus","Inner ear via the round window"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear via the aditus ad antrum', 'The mastoid antrum opens into the epitympanic recess of the middle ear through the aditus ad antrum. The nasopharynx, cochlea, and external meatus connect through separate passages, and the round window opens into the inner ear, not the antrum.'),
('e0b5f479-e424-59e1-ae30-54b4bd4050fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The round window separates the:', '["Utricle from the saccule","Middle ear from the vestibule","Tympanic cavity from the mastoid","Cochlea from the semicircular canals","Middle ear from the cochlea (scala tympani)"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear from the cochlea (scala tympani)', 'The round window is a membrane-covered opening between the tympanic cavity and the scala tympani of the cochlea that relieves pressure during stapes movement. It does not separate the utricle from the saccule, the mastoid, or the semicircular canals.'),
('c85c7f00-5401-5d12-9c17-c11260db3192'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The oval window (fenestra vestibuli) is closed by the:', '["Round window membrane","Secondary tympanic membrane","Annular ligament","Stapes footplate","Tympanic membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Stapes footplate', 'The stapes footplate sits in the oval window and transmits vibration into the perilymph of the vestibule. The round window membrane, annular ligament, and tympanic membrane occupy different sites, and the secondary tympanic membrane closes the round window.'),
('4a6720a6-7cb1-59ba-81e0-26b4d31b06b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Sound waves are transmitted from the tympanic membrane to the oval window via:', '["Incus → malleus → stapes","Directly via air","Stapes → incus → malleus","Malleus → stapes → incus","Malleus → incus → stapes"]'::jsonb, 'Malleus → incus → stapes', 'The ossicular chain conducts vibration in sequence from the malleus attached to the tympanic membrane, through the incus, to the stapes footplate at the oval window. Any other order places the ossicles in anatomically incorrect sequence.'),
('52ea36bd-4bf6-584d-891c-fdc29e3f21a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The organ of Corti contains:', '["Bipolar cells","Ganglion cells","Amacrine cells","Inner and outer hair cells","Horizontal cells"]'::jsonb, 'Inner and outer hair cells', 'The organ of Corti, the auditory sensory epithelium, holds the inner and outer hair cells whose stereocilia interact with the tectorial membrane. Bipolar, ganglion, amacrine, and horizontal cells are retinal neurons, not cochlear structures.'),
('445174b0-68f8-5172-a590-3fedb28f76f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Inner hair cells are primarily responsible for:', '["Support of the tectorial membrane","Sound transduction to the auditory nerve","Production of endolymph","Amplification of sound","Tuning of the basilar membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Sound transduction to the auditory nerve', 'The inner hair cells are the true sensory receptors: deflection of their stereocilia releases neurotransmitter onto the auditory nerve fibers for transduction. Support of the tectorial membrane, endolymph production, and cochlear amplification/tuning belong to outer hair cells and supporting structures.'),
('6d2ddd64-9004-5eec-93ee-fd12486ff5bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Outer hair cells function as:', '["Endolymph producers","Phagocytes","Primary sensory cells","Cochlear amplifiers (electromotility)","Support cells"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear amplifiers (electromotility)', 'Outer hair cells undergo electromotility in response to sound and amplify the basilar membrane response, sharpening frequency tuning. They do not produce endolymph, act as phagocytes, serve as the primary sensory transducers, or act purely as support cells.'),
('2fc773c4-fb7e-5f99-bcad-52a532dfe56b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The tectorial membrane is in contact with stereocilia of:', '["Outer hair cells (predominantly)","Inner hair cells (predominantly)","Both inner and outer hair cells","Basilar membrane","Support cells"]'::jsonb, 'Outer hair cells (predominantly)', 'The tectorial membrane rests on the tallest stereocilia of the outer hair cells, whose deflection amplifies the mechanical response. Inner hair cell stereocilia lie in the endolymph rather than embedding in the membrane, and the basilar membrane and support cells are separate elements.'),
('a8ed8878-1804-584a-90ef-b9a1c4486903'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Sound frequency is mapped tonotopically along the:', '["Tectorial membrane","Stria vascularis","Oval window","Reissner membrane","Basilar membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Basilar membrane', 'Different frequencies excite different zones of the basilar membrane, with high frequencies near the base and low frequencies near the apex, producing a tonotopic map. The tectorial membrane, stria vascularis, oval window, and Reissner membrane do not encode frequency by location.'),
('c79113ed-8ad3-578f-9572-af096af02f05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'High-frequency sounds are detected at the:', '["Helicotrema","Base of the cochlea","Apex of the cochlea","Oval window a","Round window"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the cochlea', 'The basilar membrane is stiff at the cochlear base and therefore resonates to high-frequency sound, while the more flexible apex responds to low frequencies. The helicotrema, apex, oval window, and round window are not the sites for high-frequency detection.'),
('4e14d0da-5ccc-50ed-ab5c-adf8a83d94ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The stria vascularis produces:', '["Endolymph","Perilymph","Aqueous humor","Cerebrospinal fluid","Mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Endolymph', 'The stria vascularis in the cochlear lateral wall secretes the potassium-rich endolymph that bathes hair cell stereocilia. Perilymph, aqueous humor, CSF, and mucus arise from different glands and spaces.'),
('f47a2531-c3ea-5401-9719-77930ea6476f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Endolymph has a unique ionic composition with:', '["Equal Na+ and K+","High K+","low K+","low Na+","High Na+"]'::jsonb, 'High K+', 'Endolymph is high in potassium and low in sodium, and this ionic gradient creates the endocochlear potential that drives hair cell transduction. Any ratio with low K+ or high Na+ would not sustain the transduction current.'),
('dcbe3ba9-dba6-58e0-af4a-02bfd9e2c493'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The cochlear nerve (auditory nerve) has its cell bodies in the:', '["Trigeminal ganglion","Geniculate ganglion","Vestibular ganglion","Superior ganglion of CN IX","Spiral ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Spiral ganglion', 'The bipolar cell bodies of the cochlear nerve lie in the spiral ganglion, with peripheral processes to the hair cells and central processes to the cochlear nuclei. The trigeminal, geniculate, vestibular, and glossopharyngeal ganglia belong to other cranial nerves.'),
('6e2cf681-96d9-5fd0-a571-4ee9e0fbdca3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The vestibular nerve ganglion is called the:', '["Geniculate ganglion","Pterygopalatine ganglion","Spiral ganglion","Scarpa ganglion","Trigeminal ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Scarpa ganglion', 'The vestibular nerve has its bipolar cell bodies in the vestibular (Scarpa) ganglion. The geniculate, pterygopalatine, spiral, and trigeminal ganglia serve the facial nerve, autonomic functions, cochlear nerve, and trigeminal nerve respectively.'),
('e607c0d6-26d4-5172-9402-fb12d9a83904'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The vestibular nerve innervates the:', '["and saccule","Semicircular canals","utricle","Organ of Corti","Cochlea only"]'::jsonb, 'Semicircular canals', 'The vestibular nerve supplies the cristae of the semicircular canals together with the maculae of the utricle and saccule. The organ of Corti and cochlea are served by the cochlear nerve rather than the vestibular nerve.'),
('a74eb54d-ee97-5f8a-9154-5279d2ef4508'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Nystagmus is defined as:', '["involuntary oscillations of the eyes","Rhythmic","Sustained eye deviation","Loss of conjugate gaze","Voluntary eye movements"]'::jsonb, 'Rhythmic', 'Nystagmus is a rhythmic, involuntary oscillation of the eyes that is either jerk or pendular. Sustained deviation, loss of conjugate gaze, and voluntary movements are not features of nystagmus.'),
('67bcb7fe-36aa-50e6-adbd-d456b23788dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Vestibular nystagmus is characterized by:', '["Sustained deviation resulting in decreased functional activity","A slow phase toward the lesioned side and fast phase away","Convergence-retraction nystagmus a mediated by specific receptor activation","Downbeat nystagmus only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pendular nystagmus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A slow phase toward the lesioned side and fast phase away', 'Peripheral vestibular lesions produce a jerk nystagmus whose slow component drifts toward the diseased side, followed by a quick corrective saccade away from it. Sustained deviation, convergence-retraction, downbeat, and purely pendular patterns arise from different mechanisms.'),
('7959f09e-0b39-5605-804c-213bdb5ed909'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The cochlear nuclei are located in the:', '["Cerebellar peduncle","Pons","Medulla oblongata","Midbrain","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata', 'The dorsal and ventral cochlear nuclei sit at the level of the inferior cerebellar peduncle within the medulla oblongata. The pons, midbrain, and thalamus carry later stations of the auditory pathway rather than the cochlear nuclei.'),
('b1f88e10-8f43-5583-a624-b1ca169beddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The superior olivary complex is involved in:', '["Frequency discrimination resulting in decreased functional activity","Sound localization based on interaural time and intensity differences","Sound filtering mediated by specific receptor activation","Sound amplification without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cochlear protection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sound localization based on interaural time and intensity differences', 'The superior olivary complex compares the interaural time and intensity differences between the two ears to localize sound in space. Frequency discrimination, filtering, amplification, and cochlear protection are handled elsewhere in the auditory system.'),
('6e97e6f6-11b3-56f4-80d5-1b54588b10fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The lateral lemniscus is an:', '["Trigeminal tract resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual tract from the LGN to V1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial motor tract mediated by specific receptor activation","Vestibular tract from the vestibular nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory tract from the cochlear nuclei to the inferior colliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory tract from the cochlear nuclei to the inferior colliculus', 'The lateral lemniscus is the ascending auditory tract that carries fibers from the cochlear nuclei and superior olivary complex to the inferior colliculus. Trigeminal, visual, facial, and vestibular fibers travel within named tracts of their own.'),
('ef606298-6849-52a7-b983-d8807b695d6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The inferior colliculus is located in the:', '["Cerebellum","Pons","Thalamus","Midbrain tectum","Medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain tectum', 'The inferior colliculus forms a paired elevation on the dorsal surface of the midbrain within the tectum. The cerebellum, pons, thalamus, and medulla do not contain the inferior colliculus.'),
('a0fc9dfb-8c63-5136-b04d-c915a3dd2738'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The medial geniculate nucleus (MGN) is located in the:', '["Hypothalamus","Thalamus","Midbrain","Medulla","Pons"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus', 'The medial geniculate nucleus is the auditory relay within the thalamus and projects to the auditory cortex. The hypothalamus, midbrain, medulla, and pons do not house the MGN.'),
('b94c13e8-566a-5566-ae98-1efd3b453116'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The primary auditory cortex (A1) is located in the:', '["Precentral gyrus","Cingulate gyrus","Inferior frontal gyrus","Superior parietal lobule","Superior temporal gyrus (Heschl gyrus)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior temporal gyrus (Heschl gyrus)', 'The primary auditory cortex occupies the transverse temporal gyrus of Heschl, buried within the superior temporal gyrus. The precentral, cingulate, and inferior frontal gyri and the superior parietal lobule serve motor, limbic, language, and association functions.'),
('d919d56f-6e05-5122-b0f6-65babc269c4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Tonotopic organization is preserved in the auditory cortex with:', '["High frequencies anterior only","All frequencies equal","Random distribution","High frequencies medial and low frequencies lateral","Low frequencies anterior and high frequencies posterior"]'::jsonb, 'High frequencies medial and low frequencies lateral', 'In the auditory cortex, neurons responding to high-frequency tones lie medially and those for low frequencies lie laterally. This tonotopic map mirrors the frequency gradient established along the basilar membrane.'),
('e35951e5-29e0-5822-ab98-9079fa4ac529'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Conductive hearing loss results from:', '["Superior canal dehiscence","Abnormalities of the external or middle ear (sound conduction)","Central auditory processing disorder","Otosclerosis only a","Abnormalities of the cochlea or auditory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormalities of the external or middle ear (sound conduction)', 'Conductive loss arises when sound transmission through the external canal, tympanic membrane, or ossicular chain of the middle ear is impaired. Cochlear or nerve pathology instead produces sensorineural loss.'),
('e60b6373-8cad-55d1-ab66-fb8c79ef275e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Sensorineural hearing loss results from:', '["Middle ear disease","External ear canal obstruction","Ossicular chain fixation","Tympanic membrane perforation","Damage to the cochlea or auditory nerve (CN VIII)"]'::jsonb, 'Damage to the cochlea or auditory nerve (CN VIII)', 'Sensorineural loss results from damage to the cochlea or the auditory nerve (CN VIII), beyond the middle ear. The listed middle-ear and external-canal conditions all cause conductive loss.'),
('c3b608cc-3f86-5a97-8c96-0ae368d22d04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The Rinne test compares:', '["Loudness vs pitch","Right ear vs left ear","Normal vs affected ear","Cochlear vs retrocochlear","Air conduction vs bone conduction"]'::jsonb, 'Air conduction vs bone conduction', 'The Rinne test compares air conduction with bone conduction in the same ear. Air conduction is heard longer in normal ears, whereas bone conduction becomes louder or longer when conductive loss is present.'),
('c5d43aae-6e3c-5b5f-bcf6-356d4ccc4017'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The Weber test is useful for:', '["Lateralization of sound in unilateral hearing loss","Measuring pure tone thresholds","Otoacoustic emissions","Speech discrimination","Middle ear pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Lateralization of sound in unilateral hearing loss', 'The Weber test uses a tuning fork placed on the skull to evaluate lateralization of sound in unilateral hearing loss. It lateralizes to the affected ear in conductive loss and to the normal ear in sensorineural loss.'),
('e87df62e-776c-5e16-b66b-8baf62b019b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Otoacoustic emissions (OAEs) test the function of:', '["Tympanic membrane","Inner hair cells","Auditory nerve","Brainstem auditory pathway","Outer hair cells"]'::jsonb, 'Outer hair cells', 'Otoacoustic emissions reflect active contraction of outer hair cells, which amplify the cochlear response to sound. Damage to these cells weakens or abolishes the emissions.'),
('a32c9c78-e0ea-58d1-ba53-4b3fef82b12b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Auditory brainstem response (ABR) measures:', '["Outer hair cell function resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural activity in the auditory nerve and brainstem","Tympanic membrane mobility mediated by specific receptor activation","Otoacoustic emissions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle ear pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neural activity in the auditory nerve and brainstem', 'The ABR records far-field electrical potentials generated by the auditory nerve and brainstem auditory pathway following a click stimulus. It evaluates the neural auditory axis rather than hair cell or middle-ear function.'),
('3e903a5d-468c-51c4-9ddd-69eb047090a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Otosclerosis causes conductive hearing loss due to:', '["Eustachian tube dysfunction","Ossicular discontinuity","Stapes footplate fixation","Cochlear hair cell loss","Tympanic membrane perforation"]'::jsonb, 'Stapes footplate fixation', 'Otosclerosis fixes the stapes footplate in the oval window, preventing normal vibration and producing conductive hearing loss. The other choices are separate middle-ear or cochlear lesions.'),
('63289d09-6356-5e43-84c4-26cf88c20886'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Meniere disease is characterized by:', '["tinnitus","fluctuating hearing loss","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss","Episodic vertigo","and aural fullness"]'::jsonb, 'Episodic vertigo', 'Episodic rotatory vertigo is a cardinal feature of Meniere disease, together with fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, and aural fullness. Attacks reflect endolymphatic hydrops of the inner ear.'),
('a91db4db-9eb2-5fcf-9aa9-4aa3782ece00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV) is caused by:', '["Vestibular neuritis","Acoustic neuroma","Endolymphatic hydrops","Otoconia debris in the semicircular canals","Perilymph fistula"]'::jsonb, 'Otoconia debris in the semicircular canals', 'BPPV is triggered by dislodged otoconia debris that enters a semicircular canal and shifts with head position changes. This particulate movement produces brief positional vertigo.'),
('169dc536-6c92-5c02-bcfd-0a519aa9cb2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Labyrinthitis is inflammation of the:', '["External ear","Middle ear","Inner ear (labyrinth)","Mastoid a","Auditory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Inner ear (labyrinth)', 'Labyrinthitis is inflammation of the inner ear (labyrinth). Because both cochlear and vestibular divisions are often involved, vertigo is characteristically accompanied by hearing loss.'),
('c7ce149b-a3b2-5ed3-af53-556e943cf67a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Vestibular neuritis causes:', '["Vertigo with hearing loss","Tinnitus","Acute severe vertigo without hearing loss","Facial paralysis","Conductive hearing loss"]'::jsonb, 'Acute severe vertigo without hearing loss', 'Vestibular neuritis is viral inflammation of the vestibular nerve causing acute severe vertigo. Hearing is characteristically preserved because the cochlea and auditory nerve are unaffected.'),
('8d43a64e-d58d-5a4a-b6c0-67d14ac354ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Acoustic neuroma (vestibular schwannoma) is a tumor of:', '["Support cells of the organ of Corti","Schwann cells of the vestibular nerve","Spiral ganglion cells","Hair cells","Cochlear nerve glial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Schwann cells of the vestibular nerve', 'Vestibular schwannomas arise from the Schwann cells of the vestibular division of CN VIII within the internal auditory meatus. Growth there compresses the nerve, causing unilateral tinnitus and hearing loss.'),
('a7a80651-b755-5f77-8dd6-eabeda577744'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Neurofibromatosis type 2 is associated with:', '["Optic gliomas","Bilateral acoustic neuromas","Retinoblastoma","Cataract","Macular degeneration"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral acoustic neuromas', 'Neurofibromatosis type 2 is marked by bilateral vestibular schwannomas, usually presenting in early adult life. The other listed tumors are not part of this neurocutaneous syndrome.'),
('8bfefced-0360-5c0b-8be2-809ce80bfa0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The semicircular canals detect:', '["Sound a resulting in decreased functional activity","Linear acceleration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Angular (rotational) acceleration","Head tilt without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gravity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Angular (rotational) acceleration', 'The semicircular canals detect angular (rotational) acceleration during head turns. Linear acceleration and gravity are instead sensed by the utricle and saccule.'),
('22d07734-875a-589a-a223-ce7b8f9c75d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The utricle detects:', '["Rotation","Linear acceleration in the horizontal plane","Linear acceleration in the vertical plane","Angular acceleration","Sound"]'::jsonb, 'Linear acceleration in the horizontal plane', 'The utricular macula lies in the horizontal plane, so the utricle detects linear acceleration in the horizontal plane and static head tilt. Rotation is sensed by the semicircular canals.'),
('c8fc336c-a791-5680-a698-b27c914eab2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The saccule detects:', '["Linear acceleration in the horizontal plane","Sound","Linear acceleration in the vertical plane","Angular acceleration","Rotation"]'::jsonb, 'Linear acceleration in the vertical plane', 'The saccular macula is oriented in the vertical plane, so the saccule detects linear acceleration in the vertical plane, including gravity. Horizontal motion is sensed by the utricle.'),
('7f61a547-ff31-5be7-9699-f9f9cb5f58b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The otolithic membrane of the utricle and saccule contains:', '["Support cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Endolymph accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Perilymph mediated by specific receptor activation","Otoconia (calcium carbonate crystals)","Hair cells only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Otoconia (calcium carbonate crystals)', 'The otolithic membrane is embedded with otoconia, small calcium carbonate crystals that add mass and inertia. This weight makes the maculae responsive to gravity and linear acceleration.'),
('f963283f-a459-5e76-9026-46adb30f0b34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The crista ampullaris is the sensory epithelium of the:', '["Saccule","Cochlea","Utricle","Endolymphatic sac","Semicircular canal ampulla"]'::jsonb, 'Semicircular canal ampulla', 'The crista ampullaris is the specialized sensory epithelium located in the ampulla of each semicircular canal. Its hair cells respond to rotation of the head.'),
('5696e4de-0b08-5aa3-a235-4a3c4fda09ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The cupula is a gelatinous structure overlying the:', '["Macula sacculi","Organ of Corti","Crista ampullaris","Macula utriculi","Spiral limbus"]'::jsonb, 'Crista ampullaris', 'The cupula is a gelatinous cap overlying the crista ampullaris in the semicircular canal ampulla. Endolymph movement during rotation deflects the cupula and the hair cells beneath it.'),
('5652ea7e-92c0-50f6-8059-7978f2e3fb5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) stabilizes:', '["Posture resulting in decreased functional activity","Hearing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vision during head movement","Speech without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vision during head movement', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex generates conjugate eye movements equal and opposite to head rotation, holding the visual image steady on the retina. It operates as a fast brainstem reflex.'),
('93a47a17-7948-594a-b1c8-258b7883e52d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The VOR pathway involves:', '["Retina → LGN → V1","Cochlea → cochlear nuclei → superior olive","VI → extraocular muscles","Semicircular canals → vestibular nuclei → CN III","IV"]'::jsonb, 'Semicircular canals → vestibular nuclei → CN III', 'The VOR arc runs from the semicircular canals to the vestibular nuclei, then to the extraocular motor nuclei, chiefly CN III and CN VI. This rapid three-neuron pathway drives compensatory counter-rotation of the eyes.'),
('82803b15-eebc-5554-a222-85b889effe2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The caloric test evaluates:', '["Hearing function","Cochlear function","Facial nerve function","Vestibular function","Oculomotor function"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular function', 'The caloric test irrigates the ear canal with warm or cold water to set up convection currents in the lateral semicircular canal. It is used to assess vestibular function in the intact and comatose patient.'),
('0e5bfd98-26ec-54d5-ad6d-a716ec8d2ae8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Cold caloric irrigation causes nystagmus with the fast phase:', '["nystagmus a","Away from the irrigated ear","Upward","Downward","Toward the irrigated ear"]'::jsonb, 'Away from the irrigated ear', 'Cold caloric irrigation creates endolymph flow that causes nystagmus with the fast phase away from the irrigated ear. Warm irrigation produces the opposite pattern.'),
('f39652af-7d67-5389-a536-b8b08d3726f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Warm caloric irrigation causes nystagmus with the fast phase:', '["Away from the irrigated ear","Upward","Downward","Toward the irrigated ear","nystagmus"]'::jsonb, 'Toward the irrigated ear', 'Warm caloric irrigation produces nystagmus with the fast phase toward the irrigated ear. Cold irrigation produces the reverse, a relationship summarized by the mnemonic COWS (cold opposite, warm same).'),
('380124ba-d4c6-51a4-af88-58192df4ca29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Doll''s eye maneuver (oculocephalic reflex) tests:', '["Visual acuity","Accommodation","Pupillary reflex","Convergence","Vestibular function (VOR)"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular function (VOR)', 'The doll''s eye (oculocephalic) maneuver tests the vestibular-ocular reflex at the brainstem level. In a comatose patient with an intact brainstem, the eyes deviate opposite to the direction the head is turned.'),
('9d76aeb1-af3a-5ab7-82b2-83432f31f1d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) innervates which muscle of the middle ear?', '["Tensor tympani","Palatoglossus","Stapedius","Levator veli palatini","Salpingopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'Stapedius', 'The stapedius is innervated by the facial nerve and contracts to dampen ossicular vibration against loud sounds. The tensor tympani is instead supplied by the mandibular division of CN V.'),
('41e3d3f5-225e-51b1-8771-95e203c9d6fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The chorda tympani nerve (branch of CN VII) carries:', '["Taste from posterior 1/3 of tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory to the ear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Taste from epiglottis mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor to the tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue and parasympathetic to submandibular/sublingual glands"]'::jsonb, 'Taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue and parasympathetic to submandibular/sublingual glands', 'The chorda tympani carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular and sublingual glands. Taste from the posterior tongue reaches the glossopharyngeal nerve.'),
('45ce85a8-3974-52fd-b9d6-d2fcede358b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The greater petrosal nerve (branch of CN VII) carries:', '["Motor fibers to the face resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sympathetic fibers to the eye mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory fibers to the ear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the pterygopalatine ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the pterygopalatine ganglion', 'The greater petrosal nerve carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion for lacrimation and nasal gland secretion. It also conveys taste from the palate.'),
('8ad72268-006b-579d-9e12-86c35c0976ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The geniculate ganglion contains cell bodies of:', '["Special sensory (taste) fibers for CN VII","Sympathetic fibers","Parasympathetic fibers","General sensory fibers for CN VII","Motor fibers for CN VII"]'::jsonb, 'Special sensory (taste) fibers for CN VII', 'The geniculate ganglion contains the cell bodies of the special sensory (taste) fibers that travel in the chorda tympani and greater petrosal nerves of CN VII. Its motor fibers arise instead from the facial nucleus in the brainstem.'),
('d395c082-b933-58d5-a030-62c375dc2288'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Bell palsy is:', '["Idiopathic peripheral CN VII paralysis","Central CN VII lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stroke mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myasthenia gravis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic peripheral CN VII paralysis', 'Bell palsy is an acute idiopathic paralysis of the peripheral facial nerve (CN VII). It affects all ipsilateral facial muscles and is thought to follow viral reactivation or local inflammation of the nerve.'),
('794ad48b-0d61-5aae-832c-a0903933783e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Ramsay Hunt syndrome is caused by:', '["Cytomegalovirus resulting in decreased functional activity","Varicella-zoster virus (herpes zoster oticus)","Epstein-Barr virus mediated by specific receptor activation","Herpes simplex virus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Influenza virus a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Varicella-zoster virus (herpes zoster oticus)', 'Ramsay Hunt syndrome results from reactivation of varicella-zoster virus in the geniculate ganglion. It produces facial palsy together with herpetic vesicles in the external ear.'),
('194a14b4-166c-5e95-8c4a-3da3bad72080'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Mastoiditis is infection of the:', '["Ossicles a","Middle ear","Inner ear","Mastoid air cells","External auditory canal"]'::jsonb, 'Mastoid air cells', 'Mastoiditis is acute or chronic infection of the mastoid air cells, usually a complication of middle ear infection. It can spread beyond the bone to cause intracranial complications.'),
('3f2ddfd2-78cd-5b55-86db-7da40e269148'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Cholesteatoma is a cyst of:', '["Granulation tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Abscess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glandular epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","Keratinizing squamous epithelium in the middle ear","Cholesterol granuloma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Keratinizing squamous epithelium in the middle ear', 'A cholesteatoma is a cyst lined by keratinizing squamous epithelium that erodes bone in the middle ear and mastoid. It typically follows chronic middle ear disease or retraction of the tympanic membrane.'),
('9ec0d678-d77b-5526-9890-40e459e3f1ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Acute otitis media is most commonly caused by:', '["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Haemophilus influenzae","Staphylococcus aureus","Moraxella catarrhalis"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common bacterial isolate in acute otitis media. Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis are also frequent causes.'),
('cd08daf3-c773-5961-99cb-ca45f1b4694b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Otitis media with effusion (serous otitis media) is characterized by:', '["Non-purulent fluid in the middle ear","Cholesteatoma","Acute purulent infection","Tympanosclerosis","Implanted squamous epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Non-purulent fluid in the middle ear', 'Otitis media with effusion is defined by non-purulent, often viscous fluid in the middle ear behind an intact tympanic membrane. It usually follows Eustachian tube dysfunction rather than acute suppuration.'),
('a291a11a-7e84-5c95-8aa3-c92304ccd530'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Tympanosclerosis is:', '["Adhesive otitis media resulting in decreased functional activity","Thickening of the tympanic membrane only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ossicular erosion mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholesteatoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyaline degeneration and calcification of the tympanic membrane and middle ear mucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaline degeneration and calcification of the tympanic membrane and middle ear mucosa', 'Tympanosclerosis represents hyaline degeneration and calcification of the tympanic membrane and middle ear mucosa after repeated infection. It may limit ossicular movement but is not an epithelial-lined cyst.'),
('15fdad19-8a71-5f10-8027-54d8ab547cfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Superior semicircular canal dehiscence syndrome causes:', '["Tinnitus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial paralysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vertigo and nystagmus induced by loud sounds or pressure (Tullio phenomenon)","Hearing loss only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Otorrhea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vertigo and nystagmus induced by loud sounds or pressure (Tullio phenomenon)', 'Superior canal dehiscence syndrome is caused by a defect in the bone over the superior semicircular canal. Loud sounds or pressure changes then evoke vertigo and nystagmus, the Tullio phenomenon.'),
('0c7e0dfe-75cc-5881-8bc8-d24f86609d4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Ototoxicity is damage to the inner ear caused by:', '["Loop diuretics","a aspirin","Paracetamol","and quinine","Aminoglycoside antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 'Aminoglycoside antibiotics', 'Aminoglycoside antibiotics are the classic ototoxic agents, damaging cochlear and vestibular hair cells. Loop diuretics, aspirin, and quinine can also cause ototoxicity but are secondary in importance.'),
('a0410ddb-1b13-5401-9f19-1efc5b3198b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Presbycusis is:', '["Sudden hearing loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Noise-induced hearing loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Age-related sensorineural hearing loss (high frequencies)","Conductive hearing loss in elderly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Otosclerosis in elderly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Age-related sensorineural hearing loss (high frequencies)', 'Presbycusis is the progressive, predominantly high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss of aging. It results from degenerative changes in cochlear hair cells, the stria vascularis, and the auditory nerve.'),
('52c268ca-d57c-50ab-adc7-5c2a91501e57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Noise-induced hearing loss initially affects frequencies around:', '["250 Hz","4000 Hz","8000 Hz","125 Hz","500 Hz"]'::jsonb, '4000 Hz', 'Noise-induced hearing loss characteristically first damages the audiometric frequencies near 4000 Hz, producing the typical notch. This reflects vulnerability of the basal cochlear region to acoustic trauma.'),
('0a2ae58e-1e09-501a-bc7a-6e941ded527f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The organ of Corti is located on the:', '["Stria vascularis","Tectorial membrane","Spiral lamina","Basilar membrane","Reissner membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Basilar membrane', 'The organ of Corti, the auditory sensory epithelium, rests on the basilar membrane within the scala media. Vibration of the basilar membrane is converted by its hair cells into neural signals.'),
('201bed76-cd1e-5253-8b81-8e8b97e07bd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Reissner membrane separates:', '["Scala media from scala vestibuli","The organ of Corti from the stria vascularis","Endolymph from perilymph","Scala media from scala tympani","Scala vestibuli from scala tympani"]'::jsonb, 'Scala media from scala vestibuli', 'Reissner membrane separates the scala media from the scala vestibuli, isolating endolymph from perilymph. The basilar membrane separately separates the scala media from the scala tympani.'),
('589c4cf7-0f6f-536b-b5c7-42e4d2108cf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The osseous spiral lamina projects from the:', '["Outer wall of the cochlea","Helicotrema","Round window","Modiolus","Spiral ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Modiolus', 'The osseous spiral lamina is a bony shelf projecting from the modiolus, the cone-shaped central axis of the cochlea. It helps divide the cochlear duct and supports the spiral ganglion and nerve fibers.'),
('2c53db53-343d-56fb-b9b8-d5acd9407ca3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The spiral ligament is located on the:', '["Osseous spiral lamina","Basilar membrane","Tectorial membrane","Outer wall of the cochlear duct","Modiolus"]'::jsonb, 'Outer wall of the cochlear duct', 'The spiral ligament lines the outer wall of the cochlear duct and anchors the lateral edge of the basilar membrane. The stria vascularis, which secretes endolymph, lies on its surface.'),
('bb029d82-5519-528a-99c1-dbf5ac20106c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The spiral limbus is located at the:', '["Reissner membrane","Spiral ligament","Outer wall of the cochlea","Inner edge of the osseous spiral lamina","Basilar membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Inner edge of the osseous spiral lamina', 'The spiral limbus sits at the inner edge of the osseous spiral lamina, at the medial attachment of the tectorial membrane. It supports the inner hair cell region of the organ of Corti.'),
('9e3d31a5-1f03-541c-8980-d20b6513d7ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The internal auditory meatus contains:', '["CN VIII","CN VII","and the labyrinthine artery","CN V and CN VI","CN IX and CN X"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII', 'The internal auditory meatus transmits CN VII, CN VIII, and the labyrinthine artery. CN V and CN VI exit through other foramina, and CN IX and CN X leave through the jugular foramen.'),
('feba2ba1-e6dc-56bf-9587-22d1a67d2634'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) exits the skull base through the:', '["Foramen rotundum","Foramen spinosum","Stylomastoid foramen","Jugular foramen","Foramen ovale"]'::jsonb, 'Stylomastoid foramen', 'The facial nerve leaves the skull base through the stylomastoid foramen after traversing the facial canal inside the temporal bone. It then enters the parotid gland and divides into its terminal branches.'),
('11d0a573-0afe-5406-a1d1-b605c48a1056'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The chorda tympani exits the skull through the:', '["Foramen lacerum","Stylomastoid foramen","Foramen ovale","Petrotympanic fissure","Jugular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Petrotympanic fissure', 'The chorda tympani exits the tympanic cavity through the petrotympanic fissure to join the lingual nerve in the infratemporal fossa. The main facial trunk instead leaves through the stylomastoid foramen.'),
('75cde739-5464-5f1c-9410-b359aaa38f15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The olfactory epithelium is located in the:', '["Inferior meatus resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxillary sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal vestibule mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior nasal concha and cribriform plate area","Middle meatus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior nasal concha and cribriform plate area', 'The olfactory epithelium lies in the roof of the nasal cavity, over the superior nasal concha and the cribriform plate. Its axons pass through the cribriform plate to reach the olfactory bulb.'),
('fc7b41f3-7600-5945-a77f-aa7342c6f568'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Olfactory receptor cells are:', '["Ciliated columnar cells","Basal stem cells","Bipolar neurons with cilia that detect odorants","Pseudostratified columnar epithelium with goblet cells","Supporting cells only"]'::jsonb, 'Bipolar neurons with cilia that detect odorants', 'Olfactory receptor cells are bipolar neurons whose dendrites bear non-motile cilia studded with odorant receptors. Basal cells are the stem cells that replace them, while supporting cells and ciliated columnar cells are not the sensory elements.'),
('b5c3ff85-b3bb-5d92-b786-c731092a9d24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Olfactory transduction occurs via:', '["Direct opening of sodium channels","Ion channel gating by odorants","G-protein coupled receptors (GPCRs) and cAMP signaling","Voltage-gated calcium channels","Mechanoreceptor activation a"]'::jsonb, 'G-protein coupled receptors (GPCRs) and cAMP signaling', 'Odorants bind olfactory-specific GPCRs coupled to the Golf protein, which raises cAMP and opens cyclic nucleotide-gated cation channels to depolarize the neuron. Odorants do not directly gate ion channels, and voltage-gated calcium channels or mechanoreceptors do not mediate olfactory transduction.'),
('961b4983-28d9-5e82-8c71-ef1994bd2ba2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Each olfactory receptor cell expresses how many types of odorant receptors?', '["Multiple","Two","One","Ten","Three"]'::jsonb, 'One', 'Each olfactory receptor neuron expresses only a single odorant receptor gene, preserving one-receptor-to-one-glomerulus specificity in the bulb. Expressing multiple or ten receptor types would blur odor coding.'),
('370f0b43-a6dd-502d-9d3e-a88fbdaa411e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Olfactory axons project through the cribriform plate to synapse in the:', '["Olfactory bulb","Olfactory tract","Hippocampus","Primary olfactory cortex","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory bulb', 'The axons of olfactory receptor cells pass through the cribriform plate and synapse on mitral and tufted cells within the glomeruli of the olfactory bulb, the first relay of the olfactory pathway. The olfactory tract then carries these fibers to the primary olfactory cortex.'),
('27cee60f-08b2-5de2-af7b-145d028055bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Olfaction is the only sensory system that:', '["Projects to the thalamus only","Has cortical representation","Projects to the spinal cord","Bypasses the thalamus before reaching the cortex","Uses mechanoreceptors"]'::jsonb, 'Bypasses the thalamus before reaching the cortex', 'Unlike vision, hearing, taste and somatic sensation, olfactory information is relayed from the bulb directly to the primary olfactory cortex without synapsing in the thalamus. It does not project to the spinal cord or use mechanoreceptors.'),
('d201302c-9f83-5c50-8538-857d90d05d91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Anosmia is the loss of:', '["Touch a","Vision","Sense of taste","Hearing","Sense of smell"]'::jsonb, 'Sense of smell', 'Anosmia is defined as loss of the sense of smell; loss of taste is termed ageusia and the other options refer to touch, vision and hearing.'),
('154f03cf-1069-59c2-a55e-09022f0e47e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Fracture of the cribriform plate can cause:', '["Taste loss a","Facial paralysis","Deafness","Anosmia and CSF rhinorrhea","Blindness"]'::jsonb, 'Anosmia and CSF rhinorrhea', 'A cribriform plate fracture tears the olfactory filaments, causing anosmia, and may also tear the overlying dura so CSF leaks through the nose (CSF rhinorrhea). Facial paralysis, deafness and blindness reflect damage to other cranial structures.'),
('ee1dcc72-df1d-5ac6-9850-dc20569ff206'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Taste buds are primarily located on:', '["foliate","Fungiform","and circumvallate papillae of the tongue","Hard palate","Filiform papillae"]'::jsonb, 'Fungiform', 'Taste buds are concentrated on the fungiform and circumvallate papillae, with a few on the foliate papillae; filiform papillae and the hard palate carry no taste buds. Among the options, only the fungiform papilla is a true taste-bearing site.'),
('672fae55-04d4-51a3-b1d5-b3e7c17eda69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Each taste bud contains:', '["Columnar cells only","Type II (receptor)","Sensory neurons only","and Type III (presynaptic) cells","Type I (supporting)"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (supporting)', 'A taste bud is an onion-shaped cluster of Type I (supporting, glial-like), Type II (receptor) and Type III (presynaptic) cells, with the Type I cells providing structural and metabolic support. The bud is not made only of columnar cells, receptor cells or sensory neurons.'),
('a2d0a343-8d0d-5200-bf5f-dea80b98d768'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Taste transduction for sweet, umami, and bitter tastes uses:', '["Mechanoreceptors resulting in decreased functional activity","GPCRs (T1R and T2R families) with downstream signaling","umami mediated by specific receptor activation","Ion channels only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and bitter uses: leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GPCRs (T1R and T2R families) with downstream signaling', 'Sweet and umami are detected by T1R heterodimers and bitter by T2R receptors, all GPCRs that signal through downstream second-messenger cascades. Ion channels and mechanoreceptors are not used for these three taste modalities, and ''umami'' names a taste rather than a transduction mechanism.'),
('7310f22d-eb7d-5887-a335-ceebaaeefc7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Salty taste transduction is mediated by:', '["P-type ATPases resulting in decreased functional activity","Amiloride-sensitive epithelial sodium channels (ENaC)","GPCRs mediated by specific receptor activation","Voltage-gated calcium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chloride channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amiloride-sensitive epithelial sodium channels (ENaC)', 'Sodium ions enter taste receptor cells directly through amiloride-sensitive ENaC channels, causing depolarization without a second messenger. GPCRs transduce sweet, bitter and umami tastes, while P-type ATPases, calcium and chloride channels are not the primary mediators of salt taste.'),
('36c20263-8f54-577e-97c0-e3b165a5576c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) carries taste from the:', '["Epiglottis (via vagus)","Posterior 1/3 of the tongue (via glossopharyngeal)","All of the above","Palate (via greater petrosal)","Anterior 2/3 of the tongue (via chorda tympani)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 of the tongue (via chorda tympani)', 'The chorda tympani branch of CN VII carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue; the greater petrosal branch serves the palate. Posterior third taste travels in CN IX and the epiglottis is served by CN X.'),
('ad747822-6b7a-5390-ba02-772f1c69057c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) carries taste from the:', '["Posterior 1/3 of the tongue","Epiglottis","Palate","Anterior 2/3 of the tongue","All tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior 1/3 of the tongue', 'CN IX supplies taste to the posterior third of the tongue, including the circumvallate papillae. The anterior two-thirds is served by CN VII, the epiglottis by CN X, and the palate chiefly by the greater petrosal nerve.'),
('2555f0a9-9400-5208-9bb4-3a4722f05cb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The vagus nerve (CN X) carries taste from the:', '["Posterior tongue","Anterior tongue","Lips a","Cheeks","Epiglottis and palate"]'::jsonb, 'Epiglottis and palate', 'CN X conveys taste from the epiglottis and the region of the palate. Taste from the posterior tongue travels in CN IX and from the anterior tongue in CN VII, while the lips and cheeks are not taste fields.'),
('616ce2a0-eaa5-54e6-8b12-211686019830'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The primary gustatory cortex is located in the:', '["Postcentral gyrus","Insula and frontal operculum","Precentral gyrus","Superior temporal gyrus","Cingulate gyrus"]'::jsonb, 'Insula and frontal operculum', 'Primary gustatory cortex lies in the anterior insula and the frontal operculum. The postcentral and precentral gyri serve somatic sensation and motor function respectively, and the superior temporal gyrus contains auditory cortex.'),
('818582f5-ffe2-5f8c-a4b4-ec9934963c90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Taste pathways: first-order taste neurons synapse in the:', '["Thalamus (VPM) resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal ganglion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nucleus of the solitary tract (NST) in the medulla","Pontine taste area a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gustatory cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus of the solitary tract (NST) in the medulla', 'First-order taste fibers from CN VII, IX and X synapse in the rostral nucleus of the solitary tract in the medulla, which then projects to the VPM thalamus and onward to the gustatory cortex. The trigeminal ganglion and cortical sites are not the first relay.'),
('f9dc2205-e38d-5c35-bd92-531714990cff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Ageusia is:', '["Loss of smell","Enhanced taste","Loss of the sense of taste","Loss of hearing","Distorted taste"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of the sense of taste', 'Ageusia means loss of the sense of taste, whereas anosmia is loss of smell and parageusia or dysgeusia refers to distorted or abnormal taste.'),
('6f6626cd-cb97-5706-b3b3-904658b9d88a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Dysgeusia can be caused by:', '["and oral infections","High vitamin D","medications","Zinc deficiency","Excess zinc"]'::jsonb, 'Zinc deficiency', 'Zinc deficiency is a recognized cause of dysgeusia, as are certain medications and oral infections. High vitamin D and excess zinc are not established causes of taste disturbance.'),
('1dc7d175-307e-56f1-af45-c2807df2cf56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Fungiform papillae are located on the:', '["Lateral tongue","Circumvallate region","Posterior tongue","Anterior (tip) of the tongue","Base of tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior (tip) of the tongue', 'Fungiform papillae are scattered over the tip and anterior dorsum of the tongue. Foliate papillae occupy the lateral margins, circumvallate papillae form aV-shaped row in the posterior region, and the base of the tongue carries lingual tonsillar tissue.'),
('1d2ce114-aeff-5b50-b8b6-5fd906635c92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Circumvallate papillae are located:', '["On the tip of the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","In aV-shaped row just anterior to the sulcus terminalis","On the epiglottis mediated by specific receptor activation","On the soft palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the lateral tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In aV-shaped row just anterior to the sulcus terminalis', 'The 8-12 circumvallate papillae form aV-shaped row immediately anterior to the sulcus terminalis. The tip of the tongue bears fungiform papillae and the lateral margins bear foliate papillae.'),
('115fdb77-cadb-5c5c-886c-368d775834d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Foliate papillae are located on the:', '["Circumvallate region","Lateral margins of the posterior tongue","Base of the tongue","Dorsum of the anterior tongue","Tip of the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral margins of the posterior tongue', 'Foliate papillae lie along the lateral margins of the posterior part of the tongue. The circumvallate region, anterior dorsum and tip carry different papilla types, while the base of the tongue contains lingual tonsillar tissue.'),
('ee37d18a-c79c-5f4f-b881-2c97520cc2fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Filiform papillae are:', '["thread-like papillae with taste buds","Non-keratinized","Keratinized","containing taste buds","The largest papillae with taste buds"]'::jsonb, 'Keratinized', 'Filiform papillae are numerous keratinized, thread-like projections that give the tongue its rough texture and carry no taste buds. The taste-bearing fungiform, circumvallate and foliate papillae are non-keratinized.'),
('966d457d-6eff-5ec1-9546-0f242af76b16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The sulcus terminalis divides the:', '["Left and right halves of the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsum and ventral tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior and inferior surfaces mediated by specific receptor activation","Body and root of tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior 2/3 (oral part) from posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal part) of the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 (oral part) from posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal part) of the tongue', 'The sulcus terminalis is aV-shaped groove that separates the oral (anterior two-thirds) part of the tongue from the pharyngeal (posterior third) part. It does not divide the tongue into halves, dorsal-ventral, or superior-inferior portions.'),
('d69ccda6-6796-50ed-9d9e-7bb77f48567e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The foramen cecum is located at the:', '["Apex of the V-shaped sulcus terminalis","Base of the tongue","Lateral tongue","Tip of the tongue","Root of the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Apex of the V-shaped sulcus terminalis', 'The foramen cecum is a small pit at the apex of the V-shaped sulcus terminalis in the midline, marking the site from which the thyroid gland descended. It is not at the tip, lateral margins or base of the tongue.'),
('d29a080e-2974-5ae8-89a7-56271b172a44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The thyroglossal duct is the embryonic remnant of the:', '["Fusion of the palatal shelves","Descent of the thyroid gland","Descent of the parathyroid glands","Descent of the thymus","Migration of neural crest cells"]'::jsonb, 'Descent of the thyroid gland', 'The thyroglossal duct is the epithelial tract remaining from the descent of the thyroid gland from the foramen cecum to its adult pretracheal position. Parathyroid descent, thymus descent and neural crest migration leave other remnants.'),
('f4336c8f-543f-59bd-bb73-cb1ac70bdd3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'A thyroglossal duct cyst is found in the:', '["anywhere from hyoid to suprasternal notch","Midline neck","Parotid region","Submandibular region","Lateral neck"]'::jsonb, 'Midline neck', 'Thyroglossal duct cysts are the commonest midline neck swelling, usually near or below the hyoid bone, and they move upward on tongue protrusion. Parotid, submandibular and lateral-neck swellings have other causes.'),
('207c04a2-61cc-5a3c-8a1d-2cba7beae951'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Lingual thyroid is a remnant of thyroid tissue at the:', '["Midline neck","Suprasternal notch","Hyoid bone level","Base of the tongue (foramen cecum)","Submandibular area"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the tongue (foramen cecum)', 'Lingual thyroid results when thyroid tissue fails to descend and remains as a mass at the foramen cecum at the base of the tongue. Midline neck, hyoid-level and suprasternal masses represent a normally descended or partially descended gland.'),
('5178bbb1-32fd-536c-9eaa-fd85b347b840'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Branchial cleft cysts arise from remnants of the:', '["Third pharyngeal cleft","Second pharyngeal cleft","First pharyngeal cleft","Fourth pharyngeal cleft","Thyroglossal duct"]'::jsonb, 'Second pharyngeal cleft', 'The great majority of branchial cleft cysts originate from remnants of the second pharyngeal cleft, presenting as a lateral neck swelling along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. First, third and fourth cleft remnants are much rarer, and the thyroglossal duct leaves a midline cyst.'),
('0554949f-6290-5ef9-8698-ed2289e448d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'A first branchial cleft cyst typically presents:', '["Around the ear or parotid region","At the suprasternal notch","In the midline neck","Low in the neck","In the submental triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Around the ear or parotid region', 'First branchial cleft cysts present around the ear or within the parotid region and are related to the external auditory meatus. Midline, suprasternal, submental and lower-neck lesions are characteristic of other remnants.'),
('af527283-4491-5763-a113-85acb57747d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'A third branchial cleft cyst presents:', '["In the lower neck near the thyroid gland","Sublingual a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midline mediated by specific receptor activation","In the upper neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Preauricular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the lower neck near the thyroid gland', 'Third branchial cleft remnants present low in the neck near the thyroid gland, with the sinus tract opening along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. Midline lesions, preauricular and upper-neck sites correspond to different anomalies.'),
('1d160782-4771-5a9a-819e-b42623fe7b44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Preauricular pits/sinuses are remnants of:', '["Second pharyngeal cleft","First pharyngeal cleft","Otic placode","Thyroglossal duct","Auricular hillock fusion"]'::jsonb, 'Auricular hillock fusion', 'Preauricular pits and sinuses arise from incomplete fusion of the auricular hillocks, which develop from first and second arch mesenchyme. They are not remnants of a pharyngeal cleft, the otic placode or the thyroglossal duct.'),
('d6cdaf6c-faae-5c28-8edd-426f6b9564df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital dermal sinus of the nose results from:', '["Optic fissure resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasolacrimal duct anomaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial cleft mediated by specific receptor activation","Incomplete regression of the dural tract through the prenasal space","Olfactory placode remnant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete regression of the dural tract through the prenasal space', 'A congenital dermal sinus of the nose develops when the embryonic dural tract that passes through the prenasal space fails to regress, leaving a midline nasal pit or sinus. Optic fissure, nasolacrimal and facial cleft defects are unrelated lesions.'),
('87a65398-feb1-5452-88ad-d030dfa9ba92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Encephalocele is herniation of:', '["Spinal cord through a vertebral defect","Blood vessels only a","Brain and meninges through a skull defect","CSF only","Meninges only"]'::jsonb, 'Brain and meninges through a skull defect', 'Encephalocele is herniation of brain tissue and meninges through a skull defect. Herniation of meninges alone is a meningocele, spinal cord involvement is a myelomeningocele, and CSF-only or vascular-only herniations are not described.'),
('9942f8ce-f410-58f3-aa7d-ee7f8c6c114c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital nasal glioma (nasal glial heterotopia) is:', '["Hemangioma","Ectopic neural tissue in the nose","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Lymphatic malformation","Actual glioma tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Ectopic neural tissue in the nose', 'Congenital nasal glioma (nasal glial heterotopia) is non-neoplastic ectopic glial tissue within the nose, not a true tumor. Hemangioma, rhabdomyosarcoma and lymphatic malformations are vascular, muscular or lymphatic lesions respectively.'),
('8ffa4f0a-dd7a-5e23-85ee-9b71d7a0e53e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The perilymphatic space (scala vestibuli and tympani) develops from:', '["Endoderm","Surface ectoderm","Otic vesicle","Neural crest","Mesenchyme around the otocyst"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchyme around the otocyst', 'The perilymphatic spaces form by cavitation of the mesenchyme surrounding the otocyst, and are filled with perilymph. The otic vesicle itself gives rise to the membranous labyrinth, while endoderm, surface ectoderm and neural crest form other structures.'),
('4dfc2e84-e56c-5017-9c50-d194e13a93c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The bony semicircular canals complete development by:', '["8 weeks","12 weeks","2nd trimester a","Mid-gestation (20 weeks)","Birth"]'::jsonb, 'Mid-gestation (20 weeks)', 'The bony semicircular canals complete their development by mid-gestation around 20 weeks. The earlier milestones reflect membranous labyrinth or early capsular stages, and they do not remain immature until birth.'),
('6a2ded9d-d404-5d02-ac4f-7bd9f0fd7881'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Congenital aural atresia is:', '["Malformation of the cochlea resulting in decreased functional activity","Stapes fixation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fusion of the ossicles mediated by specific receptor activation","Absence of the auricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absence or underdevelopment of the external auditory meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Absence or underdevelopment of the external auditory meatus', 'Congenital aural atresia is absence or underdevelopment of the external auditory meatus, resulting from failure of canalization of the first pharyngeal cleft. Cochlear malformation, stapes fixation, ossicular fusion and absent auricle are separate middle or inner ear and auricular defects.'),
('4a5ebb71-173c-57ff-941f-54dacbf534d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The external auditory meatus is derived from the:', '["Second pharyngeal cleft","First pharyngeal pouch (endoderm)","Otic placode","First pharyngeal cleft (ectoderm)","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'First pharyngeal cleft (ectoderm)', 'The external auditory meatus is the dorsal expansion of the first pharyngeal cleft (ectoderm), which lies between the first and second arches. The first pharyngeal pouch forms the tympanic cavity and the otic placode forms the inner ear.'),
('2ed64e7f-4b89-5f7a-9964-bd2bb33a51a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The middle ear cavity (tympanic cavity) is derived from:', '["First pharyngeal pouch (endoderm)","Otic placode","Neural crest","Second pharyngeal arch","First pharyngeal cleft (ectoderm)"]'::jsonb, 'First pharyngeal pouch (endoderm)', 'The tympanic cavity and eustachian tube are expansions of the first pharyngeal pouch, hence lining endoderm. The otic placode gives the inner ear, the second arch gives the stapes and styloid apparatus, and the first cleft gives the external acoustic meatus.'),
('f0d269bb-5171-521f-a3d7-42fa085a5342'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The epithelial lining of the tympanic cavity and mastoid air cells is:', '["Simple squamous epithelium","Respiratory epithelium (ciliated columnar/ cuboidal)","Keratinized epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory epithelium (ciliated columnar/ cuboidal)', 'Because the tympanic cavity derives from the first pharyngeal pouch endoderm, its lining is a low respiratory-type epithelium (ciliated columnar or cuboidal) continuous with the eustachian tube and mastoid air cells. Keratinized, transitional and simple squamous epithelia are not found there.'),
('abb22117-7b08-5572-a67a-95f15ca1322b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The retinal pigment epithelium is derived from. Which option is most accurate?', '["Inner layer of optic cup","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Outer layer of optic cup","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Outer layer of optic cup', 'The optic cup, an outpouching of the forebrain neuroectoderm, has two layers: the outer layer forms the retinal pigment epithelium and the inner layer forms the neural retina. Therefore neither mesoderm, neural crest nor surface ectoderm is the source.'),
('57bd8154-fe5c-5a8a-a1c3-7c04d3b4eb11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Special Senses: the retinal pigment epithelium is derived from?', '["Inner layer of optic cup","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Outer layer of optic cup","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Outer layer of optic cup', 'The retinal pigment epithelium arises from the outer layer of the optic cup, while the inner layer differentiates into the neurosensory retina. Mesoderm, neural crest and surface ectoderm contribute to other ocular coats but not the pigment epithelium.'),
('d65f5450-c90e-55bd-8e58-1976b63e31be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'For Special Senses, identify the correct response: the retinal pigment epithelium is derived from?', '["Inner layer of optic cup","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Outer layer of optic cup","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Outer layer of optic cup', 'The RPE comes from the outer layer of the optic cup; the inner layer forms the neural retina. Options citing mesoderm, neural crest or surface ectoderm are incorrect for this neuroectodermal derivative.'),
('7a7f6184-0dba-5fc6-bb0f-a61681a0fe8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'The retinal pigment epithelium is derived from. Select the best answer.', '["Inner layer of optic cup","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Outer layer of optic cup","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Outer layer of optic cup', 'The outer layer of the optic cup gives rise to the retinal pigment epithelium, and the inner layer develops into the neural retina. Inner-cup, mesodermal, neural-crest and surface-ectodermal options misstate the embryologic source.'),
('95a6facf-a0b6-5d01-aa6c-713abdc0b137'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Which response correctly addresses this Special Senses item: the retinal pigment epithelium is derived from?', '["Inner layer of optic cup","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Outer layer of optic cup","Surface ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Outer layer of optic cup', 'The retinal pigment epithelium is a derivative of the outer layer of the optic cup, the inner layer forming the neural retina. The mesoderm, neural crest and surface ectoderm are not sources of this neuroectodermal tissue.'),
('f0372668-36d9-5e24-b5ea-1c2af97a70c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Persistence of the hyaloid artery remnant is called. Which option is most accurate?', '["Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous","Coloboma","Congenital cataract","Microphthalmia","Bergmeister papilla"]'::jsonb, 'Bergmeister papilla', 'The Bergmeister papilla is the glial remnant of the hyaloid artery that appears as a small tuft on the optic disc. Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous is a broader anomaly of the vitreous, while coloboma, cataract and microphthalmia are separate defects.'),
('3df13e38-626b-59e3-9872-15c6bf7711b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Special Senses: persistence of the hyaloid artery remnant is called?', '["Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous","Coloboma","Congenital cataract","Microphthalmia","Bergmeister papilla"]'::jsonb, 'Bergmeister papilla', 'Retained hyaloid artery remnants appear as the Bergmeister papilla, a small glial tuft over the optic disc. Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous involves the vitreous as a whole, and coloboma, cataract and microphthalmia are unrelated ocular anomalies.'),
('15a3cb3c-3234-5ce2-aae8-9670fc049e25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'For Special Senses, identify the correct response: persistence of the hyaloid artery remnant is called?', '["Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous","Coloboma","Congenital cataract","Microphthalmia","Bergmeister papilla"]'::jsonb, 'Bergmeister papilla', 'A persistent hyaloid artery remnant produces the Bergmeister papilla, seen as a small glial tuft at the optic disc. The other options name conditions of the vitreous, lens, globe or optic fissure rather than the hyaloid artery remnant.'),
('2cb28af5-ffe9-5cf5-b635-ab6be0be3374'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Persistence of the hyaloid artery remnant is called. Select the best answer.', '["Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous","Coloboma","Congenital cataract","Microphthalmia","Bergmeister papilla"]'::jsonb, 'Bergmeister papilla', 'The retained hyaloid artery remnant is termed the Bergmeister papilla, a small glial tuft on the optic disc. Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous, coloboma, cataract and microphthalmia are different entities and are not the term for this remnant.'),
('1c99f3c6-4965-5b03-b884-242a7deba698'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Special Senses', 'Which response correctly addresses this Special Senses item: persistence of the hyaloid artery remnant is called?', '["Persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous","Coloboma","Congenital cataract","Microphthalmia","Bergmeister papilla"]'::jsonb, 'Bergmeister papilla', 'The hyaloid artery remnant that persists is the Bergmeister papilla, a small glial tuft on the optic disc. The other options refer to vitreous hyperplasia, optic-fissure coloboma, lens opacity and a small globe, none of which names this remnant.'),
('5e9eb0e7-4ed5-5827-a9d7-c6f682158022'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The three successive kidney systems in human development are:', '["Pronephros metanephros mesonephros","Pronephros mesonephros metanephros","Metanephros pronephros mesonephros","All three develop simultaneously","Mesonephros pronephros metanephros"]'::jsonb, 'Pronephros mesonephros metanephros', 'The pronephros, mesonephros, and metanephros appear sequentially and craniocaudally in that order. Only the metanephros persists as the definitive kidney, while the first two are transient structures.'),
('50e9b549-f5ab-5219-ac80-43a4d93050aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The pronephros is:', '["A kidney in the sacral region resulting in decreased functional activity","The adult kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The definitive kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","The functional embryonic kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A transient nonfunctional kidney in the cervical region"]'::jsonb, 'A transient nonfunctional kidney in the cervical region', 'The pronephros develops in the cervical region during the 4th week and is a vestigial, nonfunctional organ that degenerates by the end of the 4th week. It is followed by the mesonephros and then the metanephros.'),
('872aad8a-8f86-532d-9487-5e56f4072307'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The mesonephros functions as:', '["The first kidney in the cervical region","A temporary kidney in the thoracolumbar region","The definitive kidney","The adult kidney","The last kidney to develop"]'::jsonb, 'A temporary kidney in the thoracolumbar region', 'The mesonephros occupies the thoracolumbar region and acts as a temporary excretory organ from about weeks 6-10 before largely degenerating. In males, its duct system is retained to form the genital ducts.'),
('8d347ac2-474e-5af2-9ae7-6233df5aaa40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The mesonephric (Wolffian) duct forms from which structure?', '["Urogenital ridge","Pronephros","Metanephros","Genital ridge","Mesonephros"]'::jsonb, 'Mesonephros', 'The mesonephric (Wolffian) duct develops as the excretory duct of the mesonephros, growing caudally to open into the cloaca. It later gives off the ureteric bud and contributes to the male genital tract.'),
('aca50ca2-c570-57c5-be0a-3fcf7c9694be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The ureteric bud is an outgrowth of which structure?', '["Mesonephric duct (Wolffian duct)","Paramesonephric duct (Müllerian duct)","Urogenital sinus","Metanephric mesenchyme","Cloaca"]'::jsonb, 'Mesonephric duct (Wolffian duct)', 'The ureteric bud arises from the caudal end of the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct near its opening into the cloaca. It grows into the metanephric mesenchyme and forms the collecting system, pelvis, and ureter.'),
('c0dfd05d-68be-5e67-a506-9675023d8f98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The metanephric mesenchyme (blastema) gives rise to which structures?', '["Renal pelvis and calyces","Bloom''s capsule","Renal interstitium","Collecting ducts and ureter","Nephrons (glomerulus to distal tubule)"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrons (glomerulus to distal tubule)', 'The metanephric mesenchyme is induced by the ureteric bud and differentiates into the nephrons, from the glomerulus to the distal convoluted tubule. The collecting ducts, pelvis, and ureter arise from the ureteric bud itself.'),
('90b5b3fb-ab7b-5e80-9227-3ffd0c49e53e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What induces the metanephric mesenchyme to form nephrons?', '["The Wolffian duct resulting in decreased functional activity","The ureteric bud (reciprocal induction)","The pronephros mediated by specific receptor activation","The Müllerian duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mesonephros leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ureteric bud (reciprocal induction)', 'The ureteric bud induces the metanephric mesenchyme to form nephrons, while the mesenchyme in turn promotes branching of the bud in a reciprocal induction process. Failure of this interaction leads to renal agenesis or dysplasia.'),
('1a2310b4-4454-5e24-b0c8-ce99ade8b762'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is renal agenesis?', '["Complete absence of one or both kidneys","A pelvic kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A horseshoe kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","A cystic kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A small kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complete absence of one or both kidneys', 'Renal agenesis is the complete failure of the kidney to develop, resulting from failure of the ureteric bud or the metanephric mesenchyme. Bilateral agenesis is fatal, whereas unilateral agenesis is compatible with life.'),
('a19488db-29a0-55da-8036-92b6d3aa6822'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the incidence of unilateral renal agenesis?', '["000 resulting in decreased functional activity","1 in 50 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 in 100 mediated by specific receptor activation","1 in 10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","About 1 in 1000 live births"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 in 1000 live births', 'Unilateral renal agenesis occurs in roughly 1 in 1000 live births and is often asymptomatic, with the contralateral kidney undergoing compensatory hypertrophy. It is more frequent in males and involves the left kidney more often.'),
('10b25b1a-305e-586f-9891-f0ab54d6683c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Bilateral renal agenesis is incompatible with life due to:', '["Electrolyte imbalance resulting in decreased functional activity","Oligohydramnios sequence (Potter sequence) causing pulmonary hypoplasia","Renal failure at birth mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urinary tract infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oligohydramnios sequence (Potter sequence) causing pulmonary hypoplasia', 'With no fetal urine produced, oligohydramnios develops, and the resulting uterine compression impairs lung development, causing pulmonary hypoplasia. The pulmonary hypoplasia is the lethal element of the Potter sequence.'),
('49d55949-6ee4-535e-a92b-ba3d8a81922d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of Potter sequence?', '["Obstructive uropathy","Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease","Posterior urethral valves","Bilateral multicystic dysplastic kidneys","Bilateral renal agenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral renal agenesis', 'Bilateral renal agenesis is the most common cause of the Potter (oligohydramnios) sequence. Absent urine output produces oligohydramnios with pulmonary hypoplasia, limb deformities, and the characteristic Potter facies.'),
('a5a6875f-2b41-5e1f-b5d0-9d074d56c12c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is a horseshoe kidney?', '["Complete fusion of both kidneys resulting in decreased functional activity","Fusion of the upper poles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fusion of the lower poles of the kidneys across the midline","A kidney on the wrong side without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extra kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fusion of the lower poles of the kidneys across the midline', 'A horseshoe kidney forms when the lower poles of the two kidneys fuse across the midline, usually over the vertebral column. It is the most common renal fusion anomaly and often an incidental finding.'),
('007ff6b8-ef6f-5561-8ef5-9edf6f27b1c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'In horseshoe kidney the isthmus lies anterior to which structure?', '["Vertebral column","Ureter","Psoas muscle","Sigmoid colon","Aorta and inferior vena cava"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta and inferior vena cava', 'In a horseshoe kidney the connecting isthmus lies in front of the vertebral column and the great vessels, the aorta and inferior vena cava, because ascent is arrested by the inferior mesenteric artery.'),
('97f42844-8b82-5b1d-a2ca-a6cbb3d52f8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common complication of horseshoe kidney?', '["Renal cell carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Calculi accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Urinary tract infection and hydronephrosis","Hypertension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urinary tract infection and hydronephrosis', 'The malrotated pelvis and high ureter insertion of a horseshoe kidney impair urinary drainage, predisposing to stasis, urinary tract infection, and hydronephrosis. Calculi may develop secondarily.'),
('570ffecb-f67b-5abf-9f5b-962ebff8fb0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is a pelvic (ectopic) kidney?', '["A horseshoe kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","A kidney that lies in the pelvis instead of the renal fossa","A dysplastic kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","A multicystic kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An extra kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A kidney that lies in the pelvis instead of the renal fossa', 'A pelvic kidney is one that fails to ascend from the pelvis to the renal fossa during development. It is usually asymptomatic and may be mistaken for a pelvic or abdominal mass.'),
('c209ec36-69da-58f0-94bd-9df02e4730e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is crossed renal ectopia?', '["One kidney crosses to the opposite side (may fuse with the other kidney)","Horseshoe kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both kidneys on the same side mediated by specific receptor activation","Duplex kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A kidney in the pelvis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'One kidney crosses to the opposite side (may fuse with the other kidney)', 'In crossed renal ectopia a kidney migrates across the midline to lie on the opposite side, usually fusing with the resident kidney. It results from abnormal ascent rather than a midline fusion defect.'),
('f58d643d-9640-5288-a155-4549db0b95a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common form of crossed renal ectopia?', '["Disc kidney","Crossed non-fused ectopia","Sigmoid kidney","Crossed fused renal ectopia","L-shaped kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Crossed fused renal ectopia', 'Crossed fused renal ectopia, in which the ectopic kidney fuses with the opposite kidney, is the most common form. The non-fused variety is rare.'),
('c7aaea91-4939-5306-b59d-861103b9853c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What causes malrotation of the kidney?', '["Failure of the kidney to rotate medially during ascent","Failure of the kidney to ascend accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extra kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal fusion of kidneys without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A normally rotated kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the kidney to rotate medially during ascent', 'As the kidney ascends from the pelvis it normally rotates medially so the hilum faces anteromedially. Malrotation results when this medial rotation fails, leaving the hilum facing anteriorly or laterally.'),
('98a1a27a-aefa-5c42-a410-47d13325c215'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is a duplex collecting system (duplex kidney)?', '["A horseshoe kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Two kidneys on one side accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A kidney with extra calyces mediated by specific receptor activation","A double-sized kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A kidney with two pelvicalyceal systems and two ureters"]'::jsonb, 'A kidney with two pelvicalyceal systems and two ureters', 'A duplex kidney results from early branching of the ureteric bud, producing two pelvicalyceal systems and two ureters draining one kidney. It is often complicated by an ectopic ureterocele or vesicoureteral reflux.'),
('ff3395b7-0c63-5d8e-9920-d8cf01edb8dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the Weigert-Meyer rule?', '["In complete duplication the upper pole ureter inserts inferomedially and lower pole ureter inserts superolaterally","The lower pole ureter is obstructed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The upper pole ureter inserts superolaterally mediated by specific receptor activation","The ureters cross without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both ureters insert normally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In complete duplication the upper pole ureter inserts inferomedially and lower pole ureter inserts superolaterally', 'The Weigert-Meyer rule states that in complete ureteral duplication the upper pole ureter inserts inferomedially in the bladder, often with a ureterocele, while the lower pole ureter inserts superolaterally. The lower pole ureter is typically the one that refluxes.'),
('ad175480-7fa1-587a-9bed-dee0c1740199'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is multicystic dysplastic kidney (MCDK)?', '["A kidney tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A hydronephrotic kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Polycystic kidney disease mediated by specific receptor activation","A kidney with multiple simple cysts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A nonfunctioning kidney replaced by multiple cysts due to ureteric bud failure"]'::jsonb, 'A nonfunctioning kidney replaced by multiple cysts due to ureteric bud failure', 'Multicystic dysplastic kidney results from failure of the ureteric bud to induce the metanephric mesenchyme, leaving a nonfunctional kidney replaced by multiple noncommunicating cysts. It is usually unilateral and nonheritable.'),
('fd55f899-5adb-5953-a60f-703731d6b324'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of an abdominal mass in a newborn?', '["Renal vein thrombosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Horseshoe kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multicystic dysplastic kidney (MCDK) or hydronephrosis","Neuroblastoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wilm''s tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multicystic dysplastic kidney (MCDK) or hydronephrosis', 'Multicystic dysplastic kidney and hydronephrosis are the most common causes of an abdominal mass in the newborn. Wilms tumor and neuroblastoma are more typical of early childhood rather than the neonatal period.'),
('482c8c15-1598-5e79-99b6-92636ad0e7dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD)?', '["Renal agenesis resulting in decreased functional activity","A genetic disorder with dilated collecting ducts causing enlarged kidneys and hepatic fibrosis","A genetic disorder with cortical cysts mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple renal cysts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Horseshoe kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A genetic disorder with dilated collecting ducts causing enlarged kidneys and hepatic fibrosis', 'ARPKD is an autosomal recessive disorder (PKHD1 mutation) in which fusiform dilation of the collecting ducts produces markedly enlarged kidneys. Congenital hepatic fibrosis is a constant extrarenal feature.'),
('091e5b2c-9358-52a7-82b1-02a75aa77e09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'ARPKD presents in infancy with what features?', '["Renal failure in adulthood resulting in decreased functional activity","Hematuria and hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enlarged kidneys pulmonary hypoplasia oligohydramnios (Potter sequence)","Proteinuria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calculi and UTI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enlarged kidneys pulmonary hypoplasia oligohydramnios (Potter sequence)', 'In infancy the grossly enlarged kidneys reduce fetal urine output and compress the lungs, producing oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, and the Potter sequence. Severe cases may die in the perinatal period.'),
('d0598fbd-89b7-5813-9d78-bacbd47b5d4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD)?', '["A disorder with a single renal cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","A genetic disorder with multiple bilateral renal cysts causing progressive enlargement and renal failure","A disorder with hepatic cysts only mediated by specific receptor activation","A disorder with pancreatic cysts only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A disorder with renal agenesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A genetic disorder with multiple bilateral renal cysts causing progressive enlargement and renal failure', 'ADPKD is an autosomal dominant disorder in which multiple bilateral renal cysts progressively enlarge, destroying renal parenchyma and leading to end-stage renal disease in adulthood. Cysts also develop in the liver and pancreas.'),
('fe77a66d-09d0-5f5f-8efa-abc59df1b1b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'ADPKD is caused by mutations in which genes?', '["PKHD1 resulting in decreased functional activity","WT1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NPHS1 mediated by specific receptor activation","PAX2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PKD1 (16p13) or PKD2 (4q21)"]'::jsonb, 'PKD1 (16p13) or PKD2 (4q21)', 'ADPKD is caused by mutations in PKD1 on chromosome 16p13 or PKD2 on chromosome 4q21, which encode polycystin-1 and polycystin-2. PKD1 mutations usually produce earlier and more severe disease than PKD2.'),
('4d6a67e2-d147-53b8-8148-deb946cb766a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What extrarenal manifestations are associated with ADPKD?', '["Retinal abnormalities resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar tonsil herniation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hepatic cysts intracranial aneurysms cardiac valvular disease","Pancreatic cysts and hepatic fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Situs inversus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic cysts intracranial aneurysms cardiac valvular disease', 'ADPKD is a systemic disease associated with hepatic cysts, rupturable intracranial (berry) aneurysms, and cardiac valvular abnormalities such as mitral valve prolapse. Pancreatic cysts also occur.'),
('6996dbd4-ec5e-5569-aac2-c9d0f9991fa0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of end-stage renal disease in adults with ADPKD?', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obstruction mediated by specific receptor activation","Progressive renal failure from cyst enlargement","Hemorrhage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive renal failure from cyst enlargement', 'In ADPKD, the gradual expansion of the renal cysts replaces functioning parenchyma and causes progressive renal failure, the commonest route to end-stage renal disease. Roughly half of affected individuals reach ESRD by age 60.'),
('ffb4dacd-c62d-5e56-8723-089161bcb672'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is medullary sponge kidney?', '["Medullary cystic disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Polycystic kidney disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renal dysplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal agenesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A congenital disorder with dilated collecting ducts in the renal medulla"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital disorder with dilated collecting ducts in the renal medulla', 'Medullary sponge kidney is a congenital ectasia of the collecting ducts confined to the renal medulla, giving a brush-like appearance on excretory urography. It is usually discovered incidentally in adults.'),
('eb3120be-b656-5cf6-b5b0-7ccd4920995d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Medullary sponge kidney is associated with what complications?', '["Nephrocalcinosis and recurrent UTIs","Hypertension","Renal failure in childhood","Proteinuria","Glomerulonephritis"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrocalcinosis and recurrent UTIs', 'Urinary stasis within the dilated medullary collecting ducts predisposes to stone formation (nephrocalcinosis) and recurrent urinary tract infections. Most patients otherwise have a benign course and preserve renal function.'),
('baa38a1b-7297-575c-962b-8f63b7723326'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is juvenile nephronophthisis?', '["Polycystic kidney disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Medullary sponge kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A cystic kidney disease with tubulointerstitial fibrosis leading to renal failure in childhood","Renal agenesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Renal dysplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cystic kidney disease with tubulointerstitial fibrosis leading to renal failure in childhood', 'Juvenile nephronophthisis is an autosomal recessive medullary cystic disease with tubulointerstitial fibrosis causing progressive renal failure in childhood. It is one of the most common genetic causes of pediatric end-stage renal disease.'),
('07e3138b-baf5-5abd-bb37-630789ad23d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is congenital nephrotic syndrome?', '["Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Membranous nephropathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Minimal change disease mediated by specific receptor activation","IgA nephropathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nephrotic syndrome presenting in the first 3 months of life usually due to NPHS1 or NPHS2 mutations"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrotic syndrome presenting in the first 3 months of life usually due to NPHS1 or NPHS2 mutations', 'Congenital nephrotic syndrome presents within the first three months of life, usually from NPHS1 or NPHS2 mutations affecting the podocyte slit diaphragm. The Finnish type, from a NPHS1 mutation, shows massive proteinuria in utero.'),
('8425b6ac-c774-559e-a58c-52ff049d8ca9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common congenital glomerular disease?', '["Thin basement membrane nephropathy","IgA nephropathy","Alport syndrome","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","Congenital nephrotic syndrome (Finnish type)"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital nephrotic syndrome (Finnish type)', 'Congenital nephrotic syndrome, especially the Finnish variety, is the most common congenital glomerular disease. It presents in the neonatal period with massive proteinuria, edema, and failure to thrive.'),
('78e26379-1a57-5ed6-9126-f9bc496236b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is Alport syndrome?', '["A cystic kidney disease resulting in decreased functional activity","A hereditary glomerular basement membrane disorder with hematuria hearing loss and eye abnormalities","A nephrotic syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","A vascular disorder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A tubular disorder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A hereditary glomerular basement membrane disorder with hematuria hearing loss and eye abnormalities', 'Alport syndrome is a hereditary disorder of type IV collagen that produces progressive glomerular basement membrane damage, presenting with hematuria and renal failure. Sensorineural hearing loss and ocular abnormalities, such as anterior lenticonus, characteristically accompany it.'),
('91904689-9caa-5086-840b-545d43e83e76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Alport syndrome is most commonly inherited in which pattern?', '["Autosomal recessive","X-linked dominant (COL4A5)","Autosomal dominant","Sporadic","Mitochondrial"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked dominant (COL4A5)', 'Alport syndrome is usually inherited in an X-linked dominant pattern caused by mutations in COL4A5, which encodes the alpha-5 chain of type IV collagen. Autosomal recessive and dominant forms account for a minority of cases.'),
('fcec7f70-11a0-5fff-8c35-d7a7b6a4c882'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What ocular finding is characteristic of Alport syndrome?', '["Anterior lenticonus (conical protrusion of the lens)","Optic atrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cataract mediated by specific receptor activation","Glaucoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retinal detachment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior lenticonus (conical protrusion of the lens)', 'Anterior lenticonus, a conical protrusion of the central lens, is the classic ocular sign of Alport syndrome. It results from thinning of the lens capsule due to abnormal type IV collagen.'),
('f257163c-c95f-5257-80ff-a63bc612bb2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is thin basement membrane nephropathy (benign familial hematuria)?', '["A nephrotic syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","A type of Alport syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A condition with diffuse thinning of the GBM causing microscopic hematuria with good prognosis","A cystic disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A vascular disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A condition with diffuse thinning of the GBM causing microscopic hematuria with good prognosis', 'Thin basement membrane nephropathy is characterized by diffuse thinning of the glomerular basement membrane that causes persistent microscopic hematuria. Renal function remains normal and the prognosis is excellent.'),
('8e6186db-6990-5151-8b38-60d085409223'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of hematuria in children?', '["UTI","IgA nephropathy (Berger disease)","Thin basement membrane nephropathy","Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","Alport syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'IgA nephropathy (Berger disease)', 'IgA nephropathy is the most common primary glomerular cause of hematuria in children, typically causing episodic macroscopic hematuria one to two days after an upper respiratory infection. Hematuria coincides with the infection (synpharyngitic) rather than following it by a latent period.'),
('e477a42a-4711-5d2e-bc4b-1da9d057c489'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is posterior urethral valves (PUV)?', '["Valves in the bladder neck resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertrophy of the urethral sphincter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A tumor of the urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","A congenital obstruction of the posterior urethra in males due to mucosal folds","A narrowing of the anterior urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital obstruction of the posterior urethra in males due to mucosal folds', 'Posterior urethral valves are congenital mucosal folds in the posterior (prostatic) urethra of males that obstruct urinary outflow. They cause bladder distension and bilateral hydronephrosis and may progress to renal failure.'),
('0b8fdd79-1c54-585c-8b90-525144624b49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Posterior urethral valves are located in which part of the urethra?', '["Navicular fossa","Membranous urethra","Prostatic (posterior) urethra","Penile urethra","Bulbar urethra"]'::jsonb, 'Prostatic (posterior) urethra', 'Posterior urethral valves are situated in the prostatic (posterior) urethra, usually just distal to the verumontanum. They occur only in males and are the most common cause of infravesical obstruction in male newborns.'),
('1a328a8e-e8b0-5d0b-938d-9c2f9931694c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of bilateral hydronephrosis in a male newborn?', '["Ureteropelvic junction obstruction","Prune belly syndrome","Posterior urethral valves","Neurogenic bladder","Ureterovesical junction obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior urethral valves', 'Posterior urethral valves are the most common cause of bilateral hydronephrosis in a male newborn, producing bladder outlet obstruction with secondary dilation of both upper urinary tracts. Early diagnosis and valve ablation improve outcome.'),
('0380fdac-a176-597f-9530-52fc65802563'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is prune belly syndrome (Eagle-Barrett syndrome)?', '["Renal agenesis resulting in decreased functional activity","A type of PUV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absence of abdominal wall muscles bilateral cryptorchidism and urinary tract dilation","Horseshoe kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Duplex kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absence of abdominal wall muscles bilateral cryptorchidism and urinary tract dilation', 'Prune belly (Eagle-Barrett) syndrome is a triad of deficient abdominal wall musculature, bilateral cryptorchidism, and a dilated, tortuous urinary tract. It affects almost exclusively males.'),
('5d481b37-fe8f-5b16-a033-500572e3ce25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of hydronephrosis in children?', '["Vesicoureteral reflux (VUR)","Posterior urethral valves","Ectopic ureter","Ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) obstruction","Ureterocele"]'::jsonb, 'Ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) obstruction', 'Ureteropelvic junction obstruction is the most common cause of hydronephrosis in children, detected antenatally or presenting as a flank mass or flank pain. The ureter fails to drain the renal pelvis because of a narrowed junction.'),
('1e34319c-363a-5ba8-9b72-4df34265cf21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is vesicoureteral reflux (VUR)?', '["Obstruction at the UPJ resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstruction at the UVJ accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neurogenic bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal retrograde flow of urine from bladder to ureter","Posterior urethral valves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal retrograde flow of urine from bladder to ureter', 'Vesicoureteral reflux is the retrograde passage of urine from the bladder into the ureter, caused by an incompetent ureterovesical junction. It predisposes to recurrent urinary tract infections and reflux nephropathy.'),
('d53d174d-b412-573b-a305-bc7d9fd39216'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common type of VUR graded by severity?', '["Grade IV (moderate dilation)","Grade II (reflux into nondilated pelvis)","Grade I (reflux into ureter only)","Grade V (severe dilation with tortuous ureter)","Grade III (mild dilation)"]'::jsonb, 'Grade I (reflux into ureter only)', 'Vesicoureteral reflux is graded from I to V by increasing severity, and grade I, in which reflux reaches only the ureter, is the mildest and most common grade. Higher grades progressively involve and then dilate the pelvicalyceal system.'),
('b2ed995e-5e1d-53aa-9227-65a6894252e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is a ureterocele?', '["A narrowing of the ureter resulting in decreased functional activity","A cystic dilation of the distal ureter within the bladder","A diverticulum of the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","A tumor of the ureter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A stone in the ureter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cystic dilation of the distal ureter within the bladder', 'A ureterocele is a congenital cystic ballooning of the intramural portion of the ureter within the bladder wall. It may obstruct the ureter and is frequently associated with a duplicated collecting system.'),
('1276e0b9-50bb-5bc0-91bc-e168a6a78663'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is an ectopic ureter?', '["A ureter that ends blindly resulting in decreased functional activity","A ureter that is too short accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ureter that is too narrow mediated by specific receptor activation","A ureter that splits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ureter that inserts outside the bladder (e.g. vagina vestibule seminal vesicle)"]'::jsonb, 'A ureter that inserts outside the bladder (e.g. vagina vestibule seminal vesicle)', 'An ectopic ureter opens outside the bladder, for example into the vagina or vestibule in females or into the seminal vesicle in males. In females it bypasses the urinary sphincter and causes continuous urinary incontinence.'),
('5f2daaee-a892-5bc0-986f-239ad1ccf92e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'In females an ectopic ureter typically inserts where?', '["Bladder neck resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cervix mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagina or vestibule (causing continuous incontinence)","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vagina or vestibule (causing continuous incontinence)', 'In females the ectopic ureter usually drains into the vagina or the vestibule, distal to the urinary sphincter, producing continuous dribbling incontinence despite normal voiding. The diagnosis should be considered in a girl with lifelong wetting.'),
('42db86c8-9bee-5e19-a648-3254b98ec387'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is a Müllerian duct remnant in males?', '["A remnant of the paramesonephric duct forming the prostatic utricle","A remnant of the Wolffian duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An epididymal cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","A seminal vesicle cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A testicular appendage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A remnant of the paramesonephric duct forming the prostatic utricle', 'In males the Müllerian (paramesonephric) duct largely regresses under the influence of anti-Müllerian hormone, leaving the prostatic utricle as its remnant. The utricle is a small pouch opening into the prostatic urethra.'),
('9808a97a-580d-5b7b-9233-9b67a0924bb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the indifferent gonad stage?', '["The stage of testicular development resulting in decreased functional activity","The stage of ovarian development accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The period when the gonad is morphologically identical in males and females (up to week 7)","The fetal stage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The neonatal stage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The period when the gonad is morphologically identical in males and females (up to week 7)', 'Until about week 7 of development the gonad is indifferent, meaning it is morphologically indistinguishable between the sexes. Expression of SRY on the Y chromosome then directs its development into a testis.'),
('f1ea5ec0-6a94-5722-ae7f-a0f391e94204'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What determines testicular development from the indifferent gonad?', '["Testosterone levels","FSH levels","SRY gene on the Y chromosome","The number of X chromosomes","Presence of two X chromosomes"]'::jsonb, 'SRY gene on the Y chromosome', 'Expression of SRY on the Y chromosome triggers the indifferent gonad to differentiate into a testis. In its absence, the gonad follows the default ovarian pathway regardless of hormone or X-chromosome dosage.'),
('38a6c6be-95ad-5e12-8f7c-e655e5165ce5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'SRY activates which downstream gene to initiate testis development?', '["DAX1","SOX9","WNT4","FOXL2","RSPO1"]'::jsonb, 'SOX9', 'SRY upregulates SOX9, the master transcription factor that drives Sertoli cell differentiation and testis formation. DAX1, WNT4, FOXL2 and RSPO1 promote the ovarian (female) pathway instead.'),
('f4b092c2-a244-5167-93ec-76cf1f58b1ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'In the absence of SRY the indifferent gonad develops into which structure?', '["Streak gonad","development","Ovotestis","Ovary","Testis"]'::jsonb, 'Ovary', 'Without SRY the indifferent gonad follows the default pathway and develops into an ovary, so 46 XX fetuses form ovaries. A streak gonad requires gonadal dysgenesis, while an ovotestis or testis needs testis-determining factors.'),
('f13b7e85-bc9c-51ec-98bb-dffadd184c62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What cells in the developing testis produce testosterone?', '["Spermatogonia","Sertoli cells","Leydig cells","Gonocytes","Peritubular myoid cells"]'::jsonb, 'Leydig cells', 'Fetal Leydig cells, stimulated by placental hCG, produce testosterone. Sertoli cells instead secrete MIF (anti-Müllerian hormone), while the other cell types are not steroid-producing.'),
('c6b5cf50-6548-532c-b9e4-a5cae9543c94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What cells in the developing testis produce Müllerian inhibiting factor (MIF)?', '["Leydig cells","Gonocytes","Sertoli cells","Peritubular cells","Spermatogonia"]'::jsonb, 'Sertoli cells', 'Fetal Sertoli cells secrete MIF (anti-Müllerian hormone), which causes regression of the paramesonephric ducts. Leydig cells produce testosterone, and the remaining options are germ or support cells without this hormone output.'),
('dc222f22-c1e7-5b51-9363-0440df99c1f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'MIF causes regression of which structures?', '["The urogenital sinus","The gonads","The genital tubercle","Paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts","Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts', 'MIF (anti-Müllerian hormone) from Sertoli cells destroys the paramesonephric ducts, preventing uterus and fallopian tube formation in males. Testosterone, not MIF, maintains the mesonephric ducts and acts on the urogenital sinus and genital tubercle.'),
('a24eedee-98a9-5915-ba51-2ca739246823'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Testosterone promotes development of which structures?', '["Urogenital sinus into bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Paramesonephric ducts into uterus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts into epididymis vas deferens seminal vesicles","Genital tubercle into clitoris without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Labioscrotal swellings into labia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts into epididymis vas deferens seminal vesicles', 'Testosterone stabilizes and differentiates the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts into the epididymis, vas deferens and seminal vesicles. DHT acts on the urogenital sinus and external genitalia, while the paramesonephric ducts regress in males.'),
('fdb5caed-1502-57c5-9c25-d93e2186bd8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Dihydrotestosterone (DHT) is responsible for which aspects of male development?', '["Spermatogenesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Sertoli cell function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Descent of testes mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal genitalia development without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External genitalia development and prostate growth"]'::jsonb, 'External genitalia development and prostate growth', 'DHT, formed from testosterone by 5-alpha reductase, drives differentiation of the urogenital sinus and genital tubercle into the prostate and male external genitalia. Testosterone itself, not DHT, controls the internal (Wolffian) structures and spermatogenesis.'),
('b554ab60-7207-5030-bbb3-c8d5282abddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the function of the gubernaculum?', '["Producing testosterone resulting in decreased functional activity","Forming the epididymis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Guiding gonadal descent into the scrotum or labia","Forming the vas deferens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Producing MIF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Guiding gonadal descent into the scrotum or labia', 'The gubernaculum is a ligamentous cord that anchors and guides each gonad during its descent into the scrotum in males and the labia in females. It neither produces hormones nor forms ductal structures.'),
('879cab0c-f40d-5e35-874b-f5349ccf93d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Testicular descent occurs in how many phases?', '["Five phases resulting in decreased functional activity","Three phases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Four phases mediated by specific receptor activation","Two phases (transabdominal and inguinoscrotal)","One phase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two phases (transabdominal and inguinoscrotal)', 'Testicular descent proceeds in two phases: a transabdominal phase driven mainly by INSL3, followed by an inguinoscrotal phase driven mainly by androgens. The other counts do not match this established two-stage sequence.'),
('4874b103-f400-52e3-83c6-fc24bfc336de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What hormone is primarily responsible for the transabdominal phase of testicular descent?', '["Testosterone resulting in decreased functional activity","MIF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","hCG mediated by specific receptor activation","DHT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","INSL3 (insulin-like hormone 3)"]'::jsonb, 'INSL3 (insulin-like hormone 3)', 'INSL3, produced by Leydig cells, enlarges the gubernaculum and drives the transabdominal phase of descent. Testosterone and DHT mainly control the later inguinoscrotal phase, while hCG and MIF are not the primary mediators of this step.'),
('890d113c-e5c0-5bce-908b-624c9e8f2e1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What hormone is primarily responsible for the inguinoscrotal phase of testicular descent?', '["Testosterone (and DHT)","MIF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","hCG mediated by specific receptor activation","FSH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","INSL3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Testosterone (and DHT)', 'Androgens, chiefly testosterone and DHT, govern the inguinoscrotal phase by guiding the testis through the inguinal canal into the scrotum. INSL3 dominates the earlier transabdominal phase, and the pituitary or placental hormones listed do not drive this stage.'),
('30293b05-5057-50bb-bb60-cd56e068d682'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is cryptorchidism?', '["Testicular atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Failure of one or both testes to descend into the scrotum","Testicular torsion mediated by specific receptor activation","Retractile testis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absent testis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of one or both testes to descend into the scrotum', 'Cryptorchidism is failure of testicular descent leaving the testis along its path from the abdomen to the scrotum. It differs from retractile testis (cremasteric pull), torsion, atrophy, and congenital absence.'),
('761e2f67-5b0f-5a46-9360-c8673178708c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common complication of cryptorchidism?', '["Infertility (impaired spermatogenesis due to high temperature)","Hernia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Testicular torsion mediated by specific receptor activation","Testicular cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infertility (impaired spermatogenesis due to high temperature)', 'The intra-abdominal temperature of an undescended testis impairs spermatogenesis, making infertility the most common complication. Hernia and malignancy are also risks, but they occur less frequently than the germ-cell damage.'),
('ed310d07-1ac3-586f-83ad-7a875d97babc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the risk of testicular cancer in an undescended testis?', '["increased risk resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased risk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased risk (especially seminoma) even after orchiopexy","Only in intra-abdominal testes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Same as normal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk (especially seminoma) even after orchiopexy', 'An undescended testis carries a markedly increased risk of germ-cell tumors, most commonly seminoma, and this risk persists even after surgical repositioning. It is not confined to intra-abdominal testes and is never equal to or lower than normal.'),
('bee25454-9fba-5676-b18a-cc639d7b4fa8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'When should orchiopexy ideally be performed?', '["Before school age","At age 2-3 years","At birth","At puberty","Between 6-12 months of age"]'::jsonb, 'Between 6-12 months of age', 'Orchiopexy is ideally performed between 6 and 12 months of age to preserve fertility and minimize malignant risk, since spontaneous descent rarely occurs after this window. Surgery at birth or puberty misses this optimal preservation period.'),
('4d347d1e-3da5-5d28-a4ea-a56bd99372c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is retractile testis?', '["An absent testis resulting in decreased functional activity","A testis that is stuck in the perineum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A cryptorchid testis mediated by specific receptor activation","A testis in the abdomen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A testis that retracts into the inguinal canal due to cremasteric reflex but can be manually brought into the scrotum"]'::jsonb, 'A testis that retracts into the inguinal canal due to cremasteric reflex but can be manually brought into the scrotum', 'A retractile testis is pulled up into the inguinal canal by an overactive cremasteric reflex but can be gently maneuvered into the scrotum and stays there. Unlike cryptorchidism, it needs no surgery and is not an absent or intra-abdominal testis.'),
('10d53b6e-bfd4-5d36-9402-80642adf1d31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is testicular regression syndrome (vanishing testis)?', '["Cryptorchidism resulting in decreased functional activity","A retractile testis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Testicular torsion mediated by specific receptor activation","Prenatal testicular loss leaving remnants (nubbin) and empty scrotum","Testicular atrophy in adulthood leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prenatal testicular loss leaving remnants (nubbin) and empty scrotum', 'Testicular regression syndrome is prenatal loss of testicular tissue, presumably from vascular compromise, leaving a small fibrotic remnant (nubbin) and a hemi-empty scrotum. It is a congenital non-development of the testis, distinct from cryptorchidism, torsion, or adult atrophy.'),
('b8bcdac1-2bbe-52e8-a41a-205a9a1d2eae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of male pseudohermaphroditism?', '["5-alpha reductase deficiency","Androgen insensitivity syndrome","Leydig cell hypoplasia","17-alpha hydroxylase deficiency","MIF deficiency"]'::jsonb, '5-alpha reductase deficiency', 'In this setting, 5-alpha reductase deficiency is cited as the most common cause of male pseudohermaphroditism (46 XY with undermasculinized external genitalia owing to deficient DHT production). Androgen insensitivity and the other enzyme defects cause similar phenotypes but are not the most frequent cause here.'),
('76c64810-a016-5fea-a18f-9166e32e19bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is female pseudohermaphroditism?', '["A 45 X individual with genital ambiguity","A 46 XX individual with ovaries and female genitalia","A 46 XY individual with ambiguous genitalia","A 46 XX individual with virilized external genitalia due to excess androgens","A 46 XY individual with female external genitalia"]'::jsonb, 'A 46 XX individual with virilized external genitalia due to excess androgens', 'Female pseudohermaphroditism describes a 46 XX individual (normal ovaries) whose external genitalia are virilized by excess androgens, classically congenital adrenal hyperplasia. 46 XY individuals with ambiguous or female genitalia represent male forms of DSD.'),
('c95ee425-626d-5096-ab7e-63e59d0be05b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of female pseudohermaphroditism?', '["Aromatase deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Maternal androgen exposure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","47 XXY mediated by specific receptor activation","P450 oxidoreductase deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (21-hydroxylase deficiency)"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (21-hydroxylase deficiency)', '21-hydroxylase deficiency, a form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia, is the leading cause of female pseudohermaphroditism because it overproduces adrenal androgens that virilize a 46 XX fetus. The rarer maternal androgen or enzyme defects affect only small numbers of patients.'),
('9193671a-a7c3-570f-9068-4df11c1510b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the karyotype of most patients with ovotesticular DSD (true hermaphroditism)?', '["47 XXY resulting in decreased functional activity","46 XX/46 XY accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","45 XO mediated by specific receptor activation","46 XY without affecting the primary tissue pathway","46 XX (most common in humans)"]'::jsonb, '46 XX (most common in humans)', 'Ovotesticular DSD (true hermaphroditism), with both ovarian and testicular tissue present, most often has a 46 XX karyotype. 45 XO and 47 XXY are unrelated sex-chromosome disorders, and 46 XY or mosaic patterns are less common.'),
('402fa5e4-7ea4-5077-8dfc-6450ce60b48a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is gonadal dysgenesis?', '["Normal gonads with abnormal external genitalia","Normal gonads with abnormal ducts","A complete absence of gonads","Normal gonads with abnormal hormones","Incomplete or defective development of the gonads"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete or defective development of the gonads', 'Gonadal dysgenesis is incomplete or defective gonad formation, often yielding streak gonads. It is a primary gonadal defect, not a disorder of normal gonads with abnormal ducts, external genitalia, or hormone function.'),
('7f0c9bdd-1bed-5124-bea3-d7ef6a1dc075'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is pure gonadal dysgenesis (46 XY or 46 XX)?', '["Ovaries with male external genitalia","Bilateral streak gonads in a short female","Testes with female external genitalia","Normal gonads with ambiguous genitalia","Bilateral streak gonads in a phenotypic female with normal stature"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral streak gonads in a phenotypic female with normal stature', 'Pure gonadal dysgenesis (46 XY or 46 XX) shows bilateral streak gonads in a phenotypic female of normal stature; the short stature typical of Turner syndrome is absent. Streaks fail to produce sex hormones, so external genitalia remain female.'),
('529347a5-4656-5a58-a704-a45f69e64eb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is mixed gonadal dysgenesis?', '["Gonads with both ovarian and testicular tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Ovaries bilaterally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Streak gonads bilaterally mediated by specific receptor activation","Testes bilaterally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A testis on one side and a streak gonad on the other often with 45 X/46 XY mosaicism"]'::jsonb, 'A testis on one side and a streak gonad on the other often with 45 X/46 XY mosaicism', 'Mixed gonadal dysgenesis typically pairs a testis on one side with a streak gonad on the other, often in 45 X/46 XY mosaicism, producing ambiguous genitalia. Bilateral streaks define pure dysgenesis, while ovarian and testicular tissue together in one individual is ovotesticular DSD.'),
('e74e14de-7094-57a5-bb63-87c05e2fcd76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the risk of gonadal tumors in dysgenetic gonads?', '["Only after puberty resulting in decreased functional activity","Only in streak gonads accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","increased risk mediated by specific receptor activation","Slightly increased without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Significantly increased (gonadoblastoma and germinoma)"]'::jsonb, 'Significantly increased (gonadoblastoma and germinoma)', 'Dysgenetic gonads, particularly streak gonads containing Y-chromosome material, carry a markedly increased risk of gonadoblastoma and germinoma. The elevation is significant and is not confined to a specific age or to mild degrees of increase.'),
('f90bc5cf-c4dd-5a0d-9ac9-118584ae83ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts develop adjacent to which structure?', '["Mesonephros","Cloaca","Genital ridge","Urogenital sinus","Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts', 'The paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts form as invaginations of coelomic epithelium alongside the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts. In the male, MIF induces their regression, while the adjacent Wolffian ducts persist under testosterone.'),
('e9a91c29-211a-59bc-8a81-4c756e29ec5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What induces the development of the paramesonephric ducts?', '["Estrogen resulting in decreased functional activity","Testosterone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The absence of MIF allows their development","MIF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SRY leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The absence of MIF allows their development', 'The paramesonephric ducts develop by default and continue growing as long as MIF is absent; in XY males, Sertoli-cell MIF destroys them. Testosterone, estrogen, and SRY do not drive their growth.'),
('53e16e1e-32e9-5773-8a88-17e974860ca0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'In males the paramesonephric ducts regress due to:', '["Testosterone produced by Leydig cells","FSH produced by pituitary","DHT produced by 5-alpha reductase","MIF produced by Sertoli cells","Estrogen produced by the placenta"]'::jsonb, 'MIF produced by Sertoli cells', 'MIF (anti-Müllerian hormone) secreted by fetal Sertoli cells causes ipsilateral regression of the paramesonephric ducts in males. Testosterone and DHT preserve the Wolffian ducts and virilize external genitalia instead.'),
('526eb76f-922e-5130-8858-d95eccc711eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The upper third of the vagina develops from which structure?', '["Mesonephric duct (Wolffian)","Urogenital sinus","Labioscrotal swellings","Genital tubercle","Paramesonephric duct (Müllerian)"]'::jsonb, 'Paramesonephric duct (Müllerian)', 'The upper third of the vagina is derived from the fused paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts, while the lower two thirds arise from the urogenital sinus. The Wolffian duct, genital tubercle, and labioscrotal swellings contribute to other structures.'),
('7b000da9-135b-5b23-bdf8-b82c814f7c82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The fallopian tubes develop from which part of the paramesonephric duct?', '["The infundibulum","Cranial unfused portion","Entire paramesonephric duct","The ampulla","Caudal fused portion"]'::jsonb, 'Cranial unfused portion', 'The unfused cranial parts of the paramesonephric ducts become the fallopian tubes. The caudal fused portions form the uterus and upper vagina, so only the cranial half gives rise to the tubes.'),
('e105336b-3fde-54e6-a091-d3c4f1c8c9a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The uterus develops from which part of the paramesonephric duct?', '["Caudal fused portion (uterovaginal canal)","Entire duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The cervix mediated by specific receptor activation","Cranial unfused portion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The fundus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Caudal fused portion (uterovaginal canal)', 'The caudal fused portions of the paramesonephric ducts form the uterus and upper vagina, with the cervix and fundus being postnatal divisions of this uterine primordium. The cranial unfused portions become the fallopian tubes.'),
('8f23a7d7-72d9-5400-9e41-e1f703a8592f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Failure of paramesonephric duct fusion causes which uterine anomalies?', '["Bicornuate uterus didelphys uterus septate uterus","Vaginal agenesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unicornuate uterus mediated by specific receptor activation","Clitoromegaly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Imperforate hymen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bicornuate uterus didelphys uterus septate uterus', 'Incomplete fusion or canalization of the paramesonephric ducts yields bicornuate, didelphys, or septate uteri, with septate uterus being the most common. A unicornuate uterus reflects one duct failing to develop, and the other options involve non-uterine structures.'),
('41d6b3bc-baa3-5428-a8fd-413b5e59da05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common congenital uterine anomaly?', '["Septate uterus (due to failed resorption of the midline septum)","Bicornuate uterus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arcuate uterus mediated by specific receptor activation","Unicornuate uterus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Didelphys uterus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Septate uterus (due to failed resorption of the midline septum)', 'The septate uterus results from incomplete resorption of the midline septum and is the most frequent congenital uterine anomaly. Bicornuate, arcuate, unicornuate, and didelphys uteri are morphologically distinct and less common.'),
('f60f8a86-65b5-5fa8-b60f-b9c0cc172a7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome?', '["Imperforate hymen resulting in decreased functional activity","Agenesis of the uterus and upper vagina with normal ovaries and external genitalia","Absence of ovaries mediated by specific receptor activation","Absence of all genital structures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vaginal atresia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Agenesis of the uterus and upper vagina with normal ovaries and external genitalia', 'MRKH syndrome is congenital agenesis of the uterus and upper vagina while the ovaries and external genitalia remain normal. It spares ovarian function though it abolishes the Müllerian-derived uterus and upper vagina.'),
('651064fb-82af-5662-9c27-8f96f150dab0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'MRKH syndrome is associated with anomalies of which systems?', '["Neurologic and muscular","Hepatic and pancreatic","Cardiac and pulmonary","Splenic and lymphatic","Renal (15-30%) and skeletal (10-15%)"]'::jsonb, 'Renal (15-30%) and skeletal (10-15%)', 'MRKH syndrome frequently coexists with renal (15-30%) and skeletal (10-15%) anomalies owing to shared developmental pathways, making urogenital and vertebral evaluation important. The neurologic, hepatic, cardiac, or lymphatic systems are not the associated sites.'),
('b5b785cb-a30b-5a91-85a7-1958c26fe19f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common presentation of MRKH syndrome?', '["Dysmenorrhea resulting in decreased functional activity","Primary amenorrhea with normal breast development (normal ovaries)","Primary amenorrhea with delayed puberty mediated by specific receptor activation","Infertility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dyspareunia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary amenorrhea with normal breast development (normal ovaries)', 'Because the ovaries function normally, MRKH patients present with primary amenorrhea yet normal secondary sexual characteristics and breast development. There is no obstruction or cycles to cause dysmenorrhea, and puberty proceeds normally.'),
('81d12c09-e430-5579-abba-b520cc04059d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is a transverse vaginal septum?', '["Complete vaginal agenesis resulting in decreased functional activity","A longitudinal septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Imperforate hymen mediated by specific receptor activation","Vaginal atresia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A horizontal septum causing vaginal obstruction at various levels"]'::jsonb, 'A horizontal septum causing vaginal obstruction at various levels', 'A transverse vaginal septum is a horizontal band of tissue that partially or fully obstructs the vaginal lumen at varying levels and can cause outflow obstruction. It differs from complete agenesis, a longitudinal septum, an imperforate hymen, and true atresia.'),
('7fdc42f2-5301-58a2-9fc4-bbb5ccf07f62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is hematocolpos?', '["Blood in the uterus (hematometra) resulting in decreased functional activity","Accumulation of menstrual blood in the vagina due to an obstruction","Blood in the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood in the fallopian tube (hematosalpinx) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood in the peritoneal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accumulation of menstrual blood in the vagina due to an obstruction', 'Hematocolpos is menstrual blood pooled in the vagina behind an obstruction such as an imperforate hymen or vaginal septum. Blood retained in the uterus, tube, or peritoneum denotes hematometra, hematosalpinx, and hemoperitoneum respectively.'),
('fd90f908-378a-5eeb-a4a2-05bcd7db3848'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The seminal vesicles develop from which structure?', '["Paramesonephric ducts","Mesonephros","Urogenital sinus","Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts","Genital ridge"]'::jsonb, 'Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts', 'The seminal vesicles bud off from the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts, as do the epididymis, vas deferens, and ejaculatory ducts under testosterone influence. The paramesonephric ducts, mesonephros, and urogenital sinus do not form these structures.'),
('5def2538-8941-59c7-acba-9ec94656e8e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The epididymis develops from which structure?', '["Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts","Genital ridge","Paramesonephric ducts","Urogenital sinus","Mesonephros"]'::jsonb, 'Mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts', 'The epididymis and other male duct structures arise from the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts under testosterone stimulation. The paramesonephric ducts regress in males, and the genital ridge and urogenital sinus produce the gonad and glands respectively.'),
('218f448c-ccd5-5262-8fdc-086879d1c792'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The vas deferens develops from which structure?', '["Paramesonephric duct","Genital tubercle","Mesonephric duct (Wolffian)","Urogenital sinus","Mesonephros"]'::jsonb, 'Mesonephric duct (Wolffian)', 'The vas deferens is a mesonephric (Wolffian) duct derivative, developing under testosterone. The paramesonephric duct forms female tracts, the genital tubercle forms external genitalia, and the urogenital sinus yields the bladder and glands.'),
('a0c41e24-4d6c-5642-a372-1c00f943aef8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The ejaculatory ducts develop from which structure?', '["Seminal vesicle ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","Prostatic buds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urogenital sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal mesonephric ducts near the prostatic urethra","Paramesonephric ducts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal mesonephric ducts near the prostatic urethra', 'The ejaculatory ducts derive from the distal portions of the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts near the prostatic urethra. The seminal vesicles arise more proximally from the same duct, while the prostate buds from the urogenital sinus.'),
('17464021-9642-5144-b285-d38694f92368'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The prostate develops from which structure?', '["Urogenital sinus (endoderm)","Genital tubercle","Paramesonephric duct","Mesonephric duct","Mesonephros"]'::jsonb, 'Urogenital sinus (endoderm)', 'The prostate is derived from endodermal outgrowths (buds) of the urogenital sinus under the influence of DHT. The mesonephric ducts form the male duct system, and the genital tubercle gives rise to the external genitalia.'),
('27d710c0-85a8-57fa-897b-438e2bb0e39c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The bulbourethral glands (Cowper''s glands) develop from which structure?', '["Prostatic buds","Mesonephric duct","Seminal vesicles","Urogenital sinus","Paramesonephric duct"]'::jsonb, 'Urogenital sinus', 'The bulbourethral (Cowper''s) glands develop from the urogenital sinus, like the prostate and urethral glands. They do not derive from the mesonephric or paramesonephric ducts, prostatic buds, or seminal vesicles.'),
('013f6ef8-d171-5746-922b-b090b6f186fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The greater vestibular glands (Bartholin''s glands) develop from which structure?', '["Labioscrotal swellings","Mesonephric duct","Genital tubercle","Paramesonephric duct","Urogenital sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Urogenital sinus', 'The greater vestibular (Bartholin''s) glands arise from the urogenital sinus, homologous to the male bulbourethral glands. The labioscrotal swellings, genital tubercle, and paramesonephric or mesonephric ducts form different structures.'),
('e3b7e408-e45c-5dc5-99e2-ff85d0830a3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The paraurethral glands (Skene''s glands) develop from which structure?', '["Urogenital sinus","Genital tubercle","Mesonephric duct","Paramesonephric duct","Labioscrotal swellings"]'::jsonb, 'Urogenital sinus', 'The paraurethral (Skene''s) glands develop from the urogenital sinus, analogous to the male prostate. The genital tubercle, labioscrotal swellings, and duct systems each form different genital derivatives.'),
('d730d13e-0131-5d54-be38-8c71630e5337'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The clitoris develops from which embryonic structure?', '["Genital ridge","Labioscrotal swellings","Genital tubercle","Paramesonephric duct","Urogenital folds"]'::jsonb, 'Genital tubercle', 'The clitoris develops from the genital tubercle, the same structure that gives rise to the penis in males. The urogenital folds form the labia minora, the labioscrotal swellings form the labia majora, and the genital ridge forms the gonad.'),
('c0156d22-434d-5b44-b380-c235d5568bb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The labia majora develop from which structure?', '["Urogenital folds","Genital ridge","Paramesonephric duct","Labioscrotal swellings","Genital tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Labioscrotal swellings', 'The labia majora develop from the labioscrotal swellings, which form the scrotum in males. The urogenital folds give rise to the labia minora, the genital tubercle to the clitoris, and the genital ridge to the gonad.'),
('317ad9cd-4adf-5815-8fa8-e3d231747049'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The labia minora develop from which structure?', '["Paramesonephric duct","Genital tubercle","Labioscrotal swellings","Genital ridge","Urogenital folds"]'::jsonb, 'Urogenital folds', 'The labia minora develop from the urogenital folds, which fuse to form the ventral penile shaft in males. The labioscrotal swellings form the labia majora, the genital tubercle forms the clitoris, and the genital ridge forms the gonad.'),
('a7289e7e-8971-5310-ab3d-adac75e2e943'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is hypospadias?', '["Narrowing of the urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","Abnormal opening on the dorsal surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete absence of the urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","Urethral duplication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abnormal opening of the urethra on the ventral surface of the penis"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal opening of the urethra on the ventral surface of the penis', 'In hypospadias the urethral folds fail to fuse over the ventral aspect of the penis, so the external urethral meatus opens on the ventral surface. A narrow urethra, dorsal opening (epispadias), atresia, and duplication are separate anomalies.'),
('6fbdb495-b1cd-5ddc-84d6-0bf6e10c4043'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is chordee in hypospadias?', '["Short urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventral curvature of the penis due to fibrous tissue","Dorsal curvature mediated by specific receptor activation","Stricture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral curvature leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral curvature of the penis due to fibrous tissue', 'Chordee is the ventral penile curvature produced by a fibrous band along the ventral shaft, commonly accompanying hypospadias. The other choices describe a short urethra, dorsal or lateral curvature, and stricture, not the ventral bend.'),
('b32d2732-f0c8-5e74-9bb0-3c92be8f5b74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is epispadias?', '["Urethral atresia resulting in decreased functional activity","Urethral duplication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urethral opening on the dorsal surface of the penis","Meatal stenosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urethral opening on the ventral surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urethral opening on the dorsal surface of the penis', 'Epispadias arises from faulty development of the genital tubercle, leaving the urethral groove on the dorsal surface so the meatus opens dorsally. A ventral opening defines hypospadias, while atresia, duplication, and meatal stenosis are unrelated.'),
('5f6998f9-c866-5f9f-88f9-2249e50099d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is bladder exstrophy?', '["A tumor of the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurogenic bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A prolapsed bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Bladder outlet obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A defect where the bladder mucosa is exposed through a defect in the lower abdominal wall"]'::jsonb, 'A defect where the bladder mucosa is exposed through a defect in the lower abdominal wall', 'In bladder exstrophy the infraumbilical anterior abdominal wall fails to close, leaving the posterior bladder mucosa exposed on the abdominal surface. Tumor, neurogenic bladder, prolapse, and outlet obstruction are distinct entities.'),
('21fd2167-567e-5239-9b4b-4aa1faa9d9fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the embryologic basis of bladder exstrophy?', '["Premature rupture of the amnion resulting in decreased functional activity","Failure of the cloacal membrane to be reinforced by mesoderm causing rupture","Failure of the bladder to expand mediated by specific receptor activation","Failure of the urogenital sinus to develop without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abnormal allantois development leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the cloacal membrane to be reinforced by mesoderm causing rupture', 'The cloacal membrane normally becomes reinforced by ingrowing mesoderm; when this reinforcement fails, the membrane ruptures and the bladder is left everted. Amnion rupture, failure of bladder expansion or of urogenital sinus development, and allantois abnormalities do not produce this defect.'),
('1f401e1d-d5fc-5014-8db5-cfce8980c8f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is cloacal exstrophy?', '["A severe anomaly where the hindgut bladder and genitalia are exposed (OEIS complex)","Persistent cloaca accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Imperforate anus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cloacal malformation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Isolated bladder exstrophy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A severe anomaly where the hindgut bladder and genitalia are exposed (OEIS complex)', 'Cloacal exstrophy (OEIS complex) follows early breakdown of the cloacal membrane so that the hindgut, bladder, and genitalia are exposed. It is more extensive than isolated bladder exstrophy and differs from persistent cloaca and imperforate anus.'),
('d0721090-dfa5-55ae-9991-96411dc774fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is a persistent cloaca?', '["Bladder exstrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Failure of the urorectal septum to divide the cloaca (common channel for urinary GI and genital tracts)","A persistent urachus mediated by specific receptor activation","Rectovaginal fistula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Imperforate anus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the urorectal septum to divide the cloaca (common channel for urinary GI and genital tracts)', 'When the urorectal septum fails to divide the cloaca into urogenital and anorectal canals, one common channel persists for the urinary, genital, and gastrointestinal tracts. Bladder exstrophy, a patent urachus, rectovaginal fistula, and imperforate anus have different mechanisms.'),
('47e0e7db-f41c-51bf-b6f3-c37b1658efc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH)?', '["Adrenal insufficiency alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Androgen deficiency alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Mental retardation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A group of autosomal recessive disorders of cortisol biosynthesis causing androgen excess"]'::jsonb, 'A group of autosomal recessive disorders of cortisol biosynthesis causing androgen excess', 'CAH is a group of autosomal recessive defects in cortisol biosynthesis; the resulting cortisol deficiency drives excess ACTH, which shunts steroid precursors toward androgen synthesis. It is not simple adrenal insufficiency, an androgen deficiency, a thyroid disorder, or intellectual disability.'),
('9ab93283-eafa-50f5-a6ff-080ab92500de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common enzyme deficiency in CAH (over 90%)?', '["Side-chain cleavage deficiency","17-Alpha-hydroxylase deficiency","3-Beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency","21-Hydroxylase deficiency","11-Beta-hydroxylase deficiency"]'::jsonb, '21-Hydroxylase deficiency', '21-Hydroxylase deficiency accounts for more than 90% of CAH cases. Side-chain cleavage, 17-alpha-hydroxylase, 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, and 11-beta-hydroxylase defects are all far less frequent.'),
('31d5d80f-44b2-5fca-8026-c26043df698f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What are the clinical features of classic 21-hydroxylase deficiency?', '["Virilization in males only resulting in decreased functional activity","Hirsutism only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal genitalia with adrenal insufficiency only mediated by specific receptor activation","Precocious puberty only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ambiguous genitalia in females salt-wasting and elevated 17-OH progesterone"]'::jsonb, 'Ambiguous genitalia in females salt-wasting and elevated 17-OH progesterone', 'In classic 21-hydroxylase deficiency, impaired cortisol and aldosterone synthesis causes salt-wasting while accumulated precursors virilize the female fetus, producing ambiguous genitalia; 17-OH progesterone is elevated. The other choices omit these core findings.'),
('8b41cc11-ce54-5e26-b3c8-852098703596'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What laboratory finding is diagnostic of 21-hydroxylase deficiency?', '["Elevated aldosterone resulting in decreased functional activity","Elevated renin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone (17-OHP)","Elevated cortisol without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased ACTH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone (17-OHP)', '17-OH progesterone accumulates because the 21-hydroxylase block prevents its conversion to 11-deoxycortisol, making it the diagnostic marker. Aldosterone and renin are altered only in the salt-wasting form, cortisol is low, and ACTH is raised rather than decreased.'),
('d96bd87a-fe03-53cb-80f3-5d1b1a07800a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is congenital lipoid adrenal hyperplasia?', '["The most severe form of CAH due to StAR protein deficiency causing lipid accumulation and adrenal insufficiency","11-OH deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mild CAH mediated by specific receptor activation","Late-onset CAH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","17-OH deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The most severe form of CAH due to StAR protein deficiency causing lipid accumulation and adrenal insufficiency', 'A StAR protein defect blocks cholesterol entry into mitochondria for steroidogenesis, causing lipid accumulation and severe adrenal insufficiency; this is the most severe CAH variant. Mild or late-onset CAH and isolated 11-OH or 17-OH defects are different entities.'),
('719d73c3-4f01-52ac-be2e-90380dc37a24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The ovaries develop from which structures?', '["Genital ridge and primordial germ cells","Neural crest","Mesonephros only","Paramesonephric duct only","Wolffian duct"]'::jsonb, 'Genital ridge and primordial germ cells', 'The ovary forms from the genital ridge epithelium together with primordial germ cells that migrate from the yolk sac into the ridge. The mesonephros, paramesonephric and Wolffian ducts, and neural crest give rise to other structures.'),
('d44cf841-04f1-569a-9a91-5c963c47261a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is a dermoid cyst composed of?', '["Only ectodermal tissues resulting in decreased functional activity","Only neural tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tissues from all three germ layers (ectoderm mesoderm endoderm)","Only mesodermal tissues without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only endodermal tissues leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tissues from all three germ layers (ectoderm mesoderm endoderm)', 'Ovarian dermoid cysts are mature cystic teratomas containing derivatives of all three germ layers: ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm. Limiting the composition to a single germ layer or to neural tissue is incorrect.'),
('751b2b57-df74-5892-9a99-b1d466ce09a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common malignant ovarian tumor in children?', '["Granulosa cell tumor","Dysgerminoma","Immature teratoma","Choriocarcinoma","Yolk sac tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Dysgerminoma', 'Dysgerminoma is the most common malignant ovarian germ cell tumor in children and adolescents. Granulosa cell tumor, immature teratoma, choriocarcinoma, and yolk sac tumor are seen but less frequently.'),
('34658f27-35f0-5215-bc14-412f3b40fd25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common testicular tumor in young men?', '["Yolk sac tumor","Seminoma","Choriocarcinoma","Leydig cell tumor","Teratoma"]'::jsonb, 'Seminoma', 'Seminoma is the most common testicular germ cell tumor overall, peaking in young men around age 30. Yolk sac tumor, choriocarcinoma, teratoma, and Leydig cell tumors are each less frequent in this age group.'),
('442e2e38-4c50-5ba6-8572-f5406f8ef009'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common testicular tumor in prepubertal boys?', '["Choriocarcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Teratoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leydig cell tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Yolk sac tumor (endodermal sinus tumor)","Seminoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Yolk sac tumor (endodermal sinus tumor)', 'Yolk sac (endodermal sinus) tumor is the most common testicular tumor before puberty and characteristically secretes AFP. Seminoma, choriocarcinoma, teratoma, and Leydig cell tumors typically present later.'),
('656f4641-1840-530b-88b7-f98212539589'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What tumor marker is elevated in yolk sac tumors?', '["Beta-hCG resulting in decreased functional activity","LDH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PLAP mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)","CEA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)', 'Yolk sac tumors characteristically secrete alpha-fetoprotein, which aids diagnosis and monitoring. Beta-hCG, LDH, PLAP, and CEA are markers of other tumors.'),
('e3a49c54-8c99-5649-a6c0-808ab2010c04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What tumor marker is elevated in choriocarcinoma?', '["AFP","CEA","PLAP","Beta-hCG","LDH"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-hCG', 'Choriocarcinoma contains trophoblastic tissue that secretes beta-hCG, producing very high levels. AFP points to yolk sac tumors, PLAP and LDH to seminoma, and CEA to other cancers.'),
('5c58e05f-cc08-5bc4-b0b5-a096a7011b7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common testicular tumor in men over 60?', '["Lymphoma","Sertoli cell tumor","Leydig cell tumor","Seminoma","Teratoma"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphoma', 'In men over 60, lymphoma (usually diffuse large B-cell) is the most common testicular tumor and is frequently bilateral. Germ cell tumors and sex-cord stromal tumors dominate younger ages.'),
('5627f658-6b10-5a84-b8e1-c4120137ef68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is testicular microlithiasis?', '["Calcifications within the seminiferous tubules often associated with increased malignancy risk","Traumatic calcification accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection of the testis mediated by specific receptor activation","Atrophy of the testis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor of the testis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcifications within the seminiferous tubules often associated with increased malignancy risk', 'Testicular microlithiasis shows diffuse, tiny calcifications within the seminiferous tubules and carries an increased risk of germ cell tumor. Trauma, infection, atrophy, and tumor are separate conditions.'),
('f4a7cdfe-9b33-5d1d-b7b9-afe2935380df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common benign tumor of the testis?', '["Gonadoblastoma","Sertoli cell tumor","Leydig cell tumor","Epidermoid cyst","Teratoma"]'::jsonb, 'Leydig cell tumor', 'Leydig cell tumors are the most common benign testicular tumors and may secrete androgens or estrogens. Gonadoblastoma, Sertoli cell tumors, epidermoid cysts, and teratomas are less common in this role.'),
('cf82ac2d-eba5-5da5-8c6e-8ce758a50d69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common paratesticular tumor?', '["Leiomyosarcoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Lipoma","Leiomyoma","Adenomatoid tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Adenomatoid tumor', 'Adenomatoid tumor, arising from the epididymis or tunica, is the most common paratesticular neoplasm. The sarcomas and lipoma listed are far less frequent.'),
('e91b9853-79f5-5239-b6b7-6b20d7418f6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common malignant paratesticular tumor in children?', '["Lymphoma","Liposarcoma","Malignant fibrous histiocytoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma (paratesticular)","Leiomyosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Rhabdomyosarcoma (paratesticular)', 'Paratesticular embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma is the most common malignant paratesticular tumor in children. Lymphoma, liposarcoma, and the other sarcomas occur mainly in adults.'),
('3e337d8d-5d01-5b21-945e-d24adb3729ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'The processus vaginalis is a protrusion of which structure?', '["Epididymis","Peritoneum through the inguinal canal","Tunica vaginalis","Spermatic fascia","Abdominal wall muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Peritoneum through the inguinal canal', 'The processus vaginalis is a diverticulum of peritoneum that passes through the inguinal canal ahead of the descending testis. It does not arise from the epididymis, tunica vaginalis, spermatic fascia, or abdominal wall muscle.'),
('8ee9a239-60e9-5c5a-8029-5a9d368706c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What happens to the processus vaginalis after testicular descent?', '["It becomes the epididymis resulting in decreased functional activity","It becomes the vas deferens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It becomes the spermatic cord mediated by specific receptor activation","It remains patent in everyone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It obliterates except for the tunica vaginalis covering the testis"]'::jsonb, 'It obliterates except for the tunica vaginalis covering the testis', 'After descent the proximal processus vaginalis normally obliterates, leaving only the tunica vaginalis investing the testis. It never becomes the epididymis, vas deferens, or spermatic cord, and it is not patent in everyone.'),
('3b209120-bef5-54b1-ba22-016f7075f063'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'A patent processus vaginalis can cause which condition?', '["Femoral hernia resulting in decreased functional activity","Umbilical hernia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital indirect inguinal hernia Direct inguinal hernia","Incisional hernia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","B but not A leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital indirect inguinal hernia Direct inguinal hernia', 'A patent processus vaginalis preserves a peritoneal connection through the inguinal canal, allowing abdominal contents to enter the scrotum as a congenital indirect inguinal hernia. Femoral, umbilical, and incisional hernias have different anatomical defects.'),
('4bd61750-8982-5877-b41f-eadbade5070d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is a hydrocele?', '["Tumor of the testis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hernia of the scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection of the epididymis mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluid collection within the tunica vaginalis surrounding the testis","Blood collection in the scrotum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid collection within the tunica vaginalis surrounding the testis', 'A hydrocele is a collection of serous fluid within the tunica vaginalis surrounding the testis. Testicular tumor, scrotal hernia, epididymal infection, and hematocele are separate causes of scrotal swelling.'),
('66bc42d5-10aa-5eb7-9047-ac4ee7900bee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is a communicating hydrocele?', '["A hydrocele that communicates with the peritoneal cavity via a patent processus vaginalis","A hydrocele in the abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A hydrocele of the cord mediated by specific receptor activation","A hydrocele that does not communicate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An infected hydrocele leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A hydrocele that communicates with the peritoneal cavity via a patent processus vaginalis', 'A communicating hydrocele occurs when the processus vaginalis stays patent, allowing peritoneal fluid to track into the tunica vaginalis. Non-communicating and cord hydroceles lack this connection, and an infected hydrocele is a separate entity.'),
('4d0a4d2f-8eb2-5d4c-a15f-f7f6e416f8cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of scrotal swelling in a newborn?', '["Tumor","Hernia","Hydrocele","Trauma","Testicular torsion"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrocele', 'Hydrocele is the most common cause of scrotal swelling in newborns; most are communicating and may resolve spontaneously. Tumor, hernia, trauma, and testicular torsion are much less common at this age.'),
('b420e75d-f31c-59d2-8222-a9f4cb45812a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is varicocele?', '["Spermatocele resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arterial malformation of the testis mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal dilation of the pampiniform plexus of the spermatic vein","Hydrocele leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal dilation of the pampiniform plexus of the spermatic vein', 'A varicocele is abnormal dilation of the pampiniform plexus of veins draining the testis, most often on the left. Spermatocele, tumor, arterial malformation, and hydrocele are distinct scrotal lesions.'),
('29b27697-da90-5eaf-85f6-acd4143bab73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of secondary varicocele?', '["Nutcracker syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Renal cell carcinoma with tumor thrombus extending into the renal vein (left side)","Cirrhosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Retroperitoneal fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obesity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal cell carcinoma with tumor thrombus extending into the renal vein (left side)', 'Secondary varicocele results from obstruction of the left renal vein, classically by a renal cell carcinoma tumor thrombus, which reverses flow down the gonadal vein. The other listed conditions are less common or unrelated causes.'),
('49fb0cd1-a097-503d-aedd-1059ce240fad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is spermatocele?', '["Varicocele resulting in decreased functional activity","Hydrocele of the cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A retention cyst of the epididymis containing sperm","Cyst of the vas deferens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor of the testis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A retention cyst of the epididymis containing sperm', 'A spermatocele is a retention cyst of the epididymis containing sperm. Varicocele, cord hydrocele, vas deferens cyst, and testicular tumor are different scrotal lesions.'),
('333422ca-02e8-5204-a96b-4a56f819653c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of primary infertility in men?', '["Spermatogenic failure","Hypogonadism","Varicocele","Genetic disorders","Obstructive azoospermia"]'::jsonb, 'Varicocele', 'Varicocele is the most commonly identified, correctable cause of primary male infertility, impairing spermatogenesis through raised scrotal temperature. Spermatogenic failure, hypogonadism, genetics, and obstruction are less common specific causes.'),
('c454af43-a73f-5a4e-b981-e37842e9cc95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common chromosomal cause of male infertility?', '["Klinefelter syndrome (47 XXY)","Noonan syndrome","Turner syndrome","Down syndrome","Y chromosome microdeletions"]'::jsonb, 'Klinefelter syndrome (47 XXY)', 'Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) is the most common chromosomal cause of male infertility, with small firm testes and azoospermia. Noonan, Turner, Down syndrome, and Y-chromosome microdeletions are less common or different.'),
('76247066-4cf4-5411-942d-a0c7e370303b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common form of congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism?', '["Laurence-Moon syndrome","Bardet-Biedl syndrome","CHARGE syndrome","Kallmann syndrome","Prader-Willi syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Kallmann syndrome', 'Kallmann syndrome, combining GnRH deficiency with anosmia from olfactory bulb agenesis, is the most common congenital form of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. The other listed syndromes produce different phenotypes.'),
('84208431-ca09-565c-a7bb-53a12045c051'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the genetic basis of most cases of Kallmann syndrome?', '["KAL1 mutation (X-linked) causing anosmin-1 deficiency","SOX10 mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FGFR1 mutation (autosomal dominant) mediated by specific receptor activation","CHD7 mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PROKR2 mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'KAL1 mutation (X-linked) causing anosmin-1 deficiency', 'The classic X-linked form of Kallmann syndrome is due to a KAL1 mutation causing anosmin-1 deficiency. The other genes listed are associated with different forms or additional Kallmann phenotypes.'),
('a6755bfb-0e24-5c7e-b93b-acda5b9d31a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the difference between Kallmann syndrome and idiopathic hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (IHH)?', '["Kallmann has anosmia; IHH has normal sense of smell","Kallmann is more severe than IHH","IHH has anosmia; Kallmann does not","Both have normal smell","Both have anosmia"]'::jsonb, 'Kallmann has anosmia; IHH has normal sense of smell', 'Kallmann syndrome pairs GnRH deficiency with anosmia, whereas IHH has a normal sense of smell. The statements that reverse this or say both are normal or both are anosmic are wrong.'),
('bfcfcb9d-98cf-521c-a73a-839e1498a848'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of primary ovarian insufficiency (POI) in women under 40?', '["Galactosemia","Iatrogenic (chemotherapy/radiation)","Idiopathic (unknown cause)","Autoimmune disease","Chromosomal abnormalities (Turner mosaic)"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (unknown cause)', 'Most cases of primary ovarian insufficiency before age 40 have no identifiable cause and are labeled idiopathic. Autoimmune, chromosomal, iatrogenic, and metabolic causes account for the minority.'),
('40f7b029-f3a7-5721-b2dd-0f432d8481b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common chromosomal abnormality causing POI?', '["Turner syndrome (45 X and variants)","Klinefelter syndrome","Trisomy 18","Fragile X premutation (FMR1 premutation)","Down syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Turner syndrome (45 X and variants)', 'Turner syndrome (45,X) and its variants are the most common chromosomal cause of primary ovarian insufficiency because loss of X material accelerates follicular atresia. Fragile X premutation and the other listed conditions are less common causes.'),
('ec5356d0-5a55-5474-b686-58607150257a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of secondary amenorrhea in women of reproductive age?', '["Pregnancy (by far the most common)","Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)","Thyroid disorders","Hypothalamic amenorrhea","Hyperprolactinemia"]'::jsonb, 'Pregnancy (by far the most common)', 'Pregnancy is by far the most common cause of secondary amenorrhea in reproductive-age women and must be excluded first. PCOS, thyroid disease, hypothalamic amenorrhea, and hyperprolactinemia are less common causes.'),
('fca89ed3-3f52-5018-af01-0b39e696a038'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common endocrine disorder of reproductive-age women?', '["Diabetes mellitus","Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)","Hyperprolactinemia","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Hypothyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)', 'Polycystic ovary syndrome is the most common endocrine disorder of reproductive-age women. Diabetes mellitus, hyperprolactinemia, CAH, and hypothyroidism are less common or different conditions.'),
('c7de8ded-04d9-5562-bc9c-ee52779bb405'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What are the Rotterdam criteria for PCOS (2 of 3)?', '["Infertility obesity and hirsutism resulting in decreased functional activity","Oligo/anovulation clinical/biochemical hyperandrogenism polycystic ovaries on ultrasound","Obesity hirsutism and infertility mediated by specific receptor activation","Iregular periods acne and weight gain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hirsutism alopecia and acne leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oligo/anovulation clinical/biochemical hyperandrogenism polycystic ovaries on ultrasound', 'The Rotterdam criteria require any two of three features: oligo/anovulation, clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound. The other groupings list symptoms rather than the diagnostic triad.'),
('c6efb6b7-5b3b-5691-9a32-aa1e58511795'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of female infertility?', '["Male factor resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterine factor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endometriosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Ovulatory disorders (including PCOS)","Tubal factor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ovulatory disorders (including PCOS)', 'Ovulatory disorders, including PCOS, are the most common cause of female infertility. Male factor, tubal, uterine, and endometriosis factors are less common.'),
('01110d2c-b340-523a-99ac-07ff1281f60a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of tubal factor infertility?', '["Endometriosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Ectopic pregnancy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Previous surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) due to Chlamydia trachomatis","Tuberculosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) due to Chlamydia trachomatis', 'Pelvic inflammatory disease, most often caused by Chlamydia trachomatis, is the leading cause of tubal factor infertility through tubal scarring. Endometriosis, prior surgery, ectopic pregnancy, and tuberculosis are less frequent.'),
('6557cb23-a139-5b44-8a15-2fd151ce6049'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common site of ectopic pregnancy?', '["Ovary","Interstitial (cornual) portion","Isthmus","Fimbriae","Ampulla of the fallopian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of the fallopian tube', 'More than half of ectopic pregnancies implant in the ampulla of the fallopian tube, its widest segment. The isthmus, fimbriae, interstitial portion, and ovary are less common sites.'),
('7358d64c-8c29-59dc-854c-1fe9b18df793'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most important risk factor for ectopic pregnancy?', '["Previous tubal damage (PID surgery previous ectopic)","IUD use accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Smoking mediated by specific receptor activation","Assisted reproductive technology without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maternal age leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Previous tubal damage (PID surgery previous ectopic)', 'Prior tubal damage from PID, surgery, or a previous ectopic pregnancy is the strongest risk factor for ectopic pregnancy. The other factors contribute but are less important.'),
('0e4b63bb-d913-52db-87fe-55367c04eb8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common uterine anomaly associated with recurrent pregnancy loss?', '["Bicornuate uterus","Septate uterus","Arcuate uterus","Didelphys uterus","Unicornuate uterus"]'::jsonb, 'Septate uterus', 'A septate uterus is the most common uterine anomaly linked to recurrent pregnancy loss, and its septum is surgically resectable. Bicornuate, arcuate, didelphys, and unicornuate uteri have lower or different associations.'),
('2c4a9faa-8871-5bed-a28c-02489022e7d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent pregnancy loss in the first trimester?', '["Thrombophilia resulting in decreased functional activity","Luteal phase defect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uterine anomalies mediated by specific receptor activation","Antiphospholipid syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chromosomal abnormalities (balanced translocation in parents)"]'::jsonb, 'Chromosomal abnormalities (balanced translocation in parents)', 'Chromosomal abnormalities, including balanced translocations carried by the parents, are the most common cause of first-trimester recurrent pregnancy loss. Thrombophilia, luteal phase defect, uterine anomalies, and antiphospholipid syndrome are less frequent.'),
('128b7560-118b-515f-8e25-85cde79c9be2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common aneuploidy in spontaneous abortions?', '["Trisomy 18","Monosomy X (45 X)","Trisomy 16","Trisomy 13","Trisomy 21"]'::jsonb, 'Monosomy X (45 X)', 'Monosomy X (45,X) is the single most common aneuploidy in spontaneous abortions, since nearly all such conceptuses are lost. Each of the trisomies is individually less frequent.'),
('657db575-4298-506b-adca-41aaeb6b7be1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common trisomy in spontaneous miscarriages?', '["Trisomy 16","Trisomy 21","Trisomy 13","Trisomy 22","Trisomy 18"]'::jsonb, 'Trisomy 16', 'Trisomy 16 is the most frequent chromosomal abnormality in first-trimester spontaneous miscarriages; most affected embryos die before implantation and are almost never seen in live births. Trisomy 21, 18, and 13 are seen more often at term because they are comparatively more compatible with survival.'),
('80e320fc-d28a-5c9a-ab13-eb5672ed7b69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of birth trauma to the urogenital system?', '["Vacuum extraction","Forceps delivery","Clitoral injury from circumcision","Shoulder dystocia","Breech delivery"]'::jsonb, 'Clitoral injury from circumcision', 'Circumcision-related clitoral injury is the most frequently reported cause of birth trauma affecting the female urogenital system. Vacuum, forceps, shoulder dystocia, and breech delivery more often injure the head, neck, or brachial plexus rather than the genital tract.'),
('a578bbde-c4b6-54b0-b909-be627dfb11c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common renal cystic disease in children?', '["Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD)","Medullary sponge kidney","Multicystic dysplastic kidney (MCDK)","Simple renal cysts","Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD)"]'::jsonb, 'Multicystic dysplastic kidney (MCDK)', 'Multicystic dysplastic kidney is the most common renal cystic lesion detected in children and typically presents as a non-functioning kidney replaced by multiple cysts. Simple renal cysts and polycystic kidney diseases are less common in the pediatric age group.'),
('b60d7ef3-00cb-5e20-aade-53ff6ef189d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common renal neoplasm in children?', '["Mesoblastic nephroma","Clear cell sarcoma","Wilm''s tumor (nephroblastoma)","Rhabdoid tumor","Renal cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Wilm''s tumor (nephroblastoma)', 'Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma) is the most frequent renal malignancy of childhood, usually presenting before age 5 as an asymptomatic abdominal mass. Renal cell carcinoma and the other listed tumors are far less common in children.'),
('248e99ae-cd51-5815-b171-826b87b64457'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Wilm''s tumor develops from which embryonic tissue?', '["Ureteric bud","Neural crest","Intermediate mesoderm","Gonadal ridge","Metanephric mesenchyme (blastema)"]'::jsonb, 'Metanephric mesenchyme (blastema)', 'Wilms tumor arises from metanephric blastema, the persistent embryonal renal tissue that normally differentiates into nephrons. The ureteric bud gives rise to the collecting system, while the gonadal ridge and neural crest do not form renal parenchyma.'),
('48c6b280-6f5b-593d-a48a-fcd21c077c98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common genetic syndrome associated with Wilm''s tumor?', '["Simpson-Golabi-Behmel syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","WAGR syndrome (WT1 deletion aniridia GU anomalies retardation)","Denys-Drash syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Frasier syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'WAGR syndrome (WT1 deletion aniridia GU anomalies retardation)', 'WAGR syndrome, caused by WT1 and PAX6 deletion and marked by aniridia and genital anomalies, is the most common genetic syndrome associated with Wilms tumor. Denys-Drash, Frasier, and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndromes are less frequently associated.'),
('06e19c2f-b702-5e99-91e2-7391a12eaa8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What genes are involved in WAGR syndrome?', '["WTX on X chromosome resulting in decreased functional activity","TP53 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RB1 mediated by specific receptor activation","WT1 and PAX6 deletion on 11p13","WT2 on 11p15 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'WT1 and PAX6 deletion on 11p13', 'WAGR syndrome results from a contiguous deletion of WT1 and PAX6 on chromosome 11p13, accounting for the renal tumor and aniridia. RB1, TP53, WTX, and WT2 are not implicated in these ocular and renal features.'),
('9b2c90d9-2e41-582f-821b-da89de808714'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common type of renal cell carcinoma?', '["Clear cell RCC","as it arises from proximal tubule","The most common is clear cell (conventional) RCC accounting for about 70% of all RCC","500 U/L","Elevated"]'::jsonb, 'Clear cell RCC', 'Clear cell (conventional) renal cell carcinoma is the most common subtype, accounting for about 70% of all renal cell carcinomas, and is classically associated with VHL inactivation. The other options are distractors rather than subtype names.'),
('0ee5b939-9a72-516d-ae53-f40a32455049'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common benign renal tumor?', '["Renal adenoma","Angiomyolipoma (AML)","Juxtaglomerular cell tumor","Leiomyoma","Oncocytoma"]'::jsonb, 'Angiomyolipoma (AML)', 'Angiomyolipoma is the most common benign renal tumor and is composed of blood vessels, smooth muscle, and adipose tissue; it is strongly associated with tuberous sclerosis. Oncocytoma, adenoma, and leiomyoma are less common benign renal lesions.'),
('6fbd979c-6c64-5eea-98ad-b8ca47d6f533'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common renal tumor associated with tuberous sclerosis?', '["Oncocytoma","Angiomyolipoma (AML)","Renal cell carcinoma","Wilm''s tumor","Mesoblastic nephroma"]'::jsonb, 'Angiomyolipoma (AML)', 'Angiomyolipoma is the classic renal tumor of tuberous sclerosis and is often multiple and bilateral in these patients. Renal cell carcinoma, oncocytoma, and mesoblastic nephroma are far less common in this setting.'),
('76c56c82-ff0b-5205-8819-691639c1c370'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common urologic emergency in children?', '["Testicular torsion","Paraphimosis","Urinary retention","Phimosis","Fournier gangrene"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular torsion', 'Testicular torsion is the most common urologic emergency in children because irreversible testicular ischemia can occur within hours of onset, requiring urgent surgical detorsion. Phimosis, paraphimosis, and urinary retention are less acute surgical emergencies.'),
('0558fd32-8932-51f6-815a-2bd748b50adb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the peak age for testicular torsion?', '["Adulthood (20-40)","Infancy (0-2 years)","Neonatal period and puberty (age 12-18)","Elderly","Pre-school (3-5 years)"]'::jsonb, 'Neonatal period and puberty (age 12-18)', 'Testicular torsion has a bimodal age distribution, peaking in the neonatal period and again at puberty (12-18 years), when rapid testicular growth increases the risk of twisting. It is uncommon in adulthood and in the preschool years.'),
('5f524683-0e14-5f84-b5ef-2443ef163227'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of a painless testicular mass in young men?', '["Hydrocele","Epididymitis","Seminoma","Testicular torsion","Varicocele"]'::jsonb, 'Seminoma', 'Seminoma is the most common cause of a painless testicular mass in young men and is the most frequent testicular germ cell tumor. Hydrocele and varicocele are benign and often painless but less common as the presenting mass, whereas torsion is acutely painful.'),
('325dac89-25c7-5ff3-9773-50b500c7a06c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common site of bladder cancer?', '["Trigone resulting in decreased functional activity","Urethra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bladder dome mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral and posterior walls of the bladder","Bladder neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and posterior walls of the bladder', 'Urothelial carcinoma arises most often on the lateral and posterior walls of the bladder where urine pools longest, maximizing carcinogen exposure to the mucosa. The trigone, dome, and bladder neck are less frequent sites.'),
('3dda7e7f-31f0-5050-8a99-194912cf7831'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common histologic type of bladder cancer?', '["Urothelial carcinoma (transitional cell carcinoma)","Squamous cell carcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Small cell carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adenocarcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urothelial carcinoma (transitional cell carcinoma)', 'Urothelial (transitional cell) carcinoma accounts for about 90% of bladder cancers. Squamous cell carcinoma, adenocarcinoma, sarcoma, and small cell carcinoma are all much less common.'),
('af14a52b-52fe-5eb8-af35-2776aa9e62f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common risk factor for bladder cancer?', '["Schistosomiasis","Chronic cystitis","Cyclophosphamide","Smoking","Occupational exposure (aniline dyes)"]'::jsonb, 'Smoking', 'Cigarette smoking is the single most important risk factor for bladder cancer, approximately doubling the risk. Occupational aniline dye exposure, cyclophosphamide, and schistosomiasis are additional but less common causes.'),
('be636ce5-30c2-5521-9c34-d1bc3d4f6e47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder?', '["Occupational exposure resulting in decreased functional activity","Bacterial cystitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic inflammation due to Schistosoma haematobium","Stones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smoking leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic inflammation due to Schistosoma haematobium', 'Chronic inflammation from Schistosoma haematobium infection is the leading cause of squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder, particularly in endemic areas. Smoking and occupational exposure more strongly predispose to urothelial carcinoma, not squamous cell carcinoma.'),
('32d5e519-e043-5a31-8711-99901b9e409d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the female external genitalia?', '["Imperforate hymen","Clitoromegaly","Vaginal agenesis","Hypertrophy of labia minora","Labial fusion"]'::jsonb, 'Imperforate hymen', 'Imperforate hymen is the most common congenital anomaly of the female external genitalia and often presents at puberty with primary amenorrhea and hematocolpos. The other listed anomalies are much less common.'),
('f20baebc-9de3-5f7c-9d67-65894f7731dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is labial fusion (labial adhesions)?', '["Fusion of labia majora resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertrophy of labia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Partial or complete adhesion of the labia minora in prepubertal girls","Congenital absence of labia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abnormal development of the mons pubis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Partial or complete adhesion of the labia minora in prepubertal girls', 'Labial fusion is a partial or complete adhesion of the labia minora, typically seen in prepubertal girls and related to low estrogen levels. It does not involve the labia majora, mons pubis, or congenital absence of the labia.'),
('2846d3ac-caf1-5789-8645-2831f8639a94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of clitoromegaly in a female infant?', '["Androgen-secreting tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurofibromatosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (21-hydroxylase deficiency)","Idiopathic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maternal androgen exposure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (21-hydroxylase deficiency)', 'Congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common cause of clitoromegaly in a female infant, driven by excess adrenal androgen production. Maternal androgen exposure and androgen-secreting tumors are rare causes.'),
('ce7830f6-51a0-56f9-a723-dfdb500edc02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency?', '["A form of CAH with virilization and hypertension (due to elevated deoxycorticosterone)","A form with virilization and salt-wasting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A mild form with symptoms mediated by specific receptor activation","A form with precocious puberty without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A form with only hirsutism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A form of CAH with virilization and hypertension (due to elevated deoxycorticosterone)', '11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency causes virilization from excess adrenal androgens plus hypertension because deoxycorticosterone, a mineralocorticoid, accumulates. Salt wasting is characteristic of 21-hydroxylase deficiency, not of this enzyme defect.'),
('4d448dc0-8935-5d6c-83ab-3bc882c0ac58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What distinguishes 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency from 21-hydroxylase deficiency?', '["Renin is elevated in 11-OH deficiency only","11-OH deficiency has precocious puberty","Hypertension is present in 11-OH deficiency; salt-wasting in 21-OH","17-OHP is elevated in 11-OH deficiency only","Both conditions are identical"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension is present in 11-OH deficiency; salt-wasting in 21-OH', 'In 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency, deoxycorticosterone excess produces hypertension, whereas 21-hydroxylase deficiency is marked by salt wasting from aldosterone deficiency. The two defect types therefore differ in mineralocorticoid balance rather than in precocious puberty or renin changes alone.'),
('63426baa-9424-5772-b8a2-4bb5714d2376'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common type of 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency?', '["Only hypertension without genital effects resulting in decreased functional activity","Asymptomatic form accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A form with virilization mediated by specific receptor activation","Salt-wasting form without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A form of CAH with hypertension hypokalemia and sexual infantilism in females and ambiguous genitalia in males"]'::jsonb, 'A form of CAH with hypertension hypokalemia and sexual infantilism in females and ambiguous genitalia in males', '17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency blocks sex steroid synthesis, causing sexual infantilism in females and ambiguous genitalia in males, while deoxycorticosterone accumulation leads to hypertension and hypokalemia. The other options describe different enzyme defects or milder presentations.'),
('6a72b090-d434-5eef-9a17-4fafd2d60c6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What laboratory finding is characteristic of 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency?', '["Elevated 17-OHP resulting in decreased functional activity","Elevated aldosterone alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreased cortisol alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevated progesterone corticosterone and DOC with low renin and aldosterone","Elevated androgens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated progesterone corticosterone and DOC with low renin and aldosterone', 'Blockade of 17-alpha-hydroxylase shunts steroid precursors toward the mineralocorticoid pathway, raising progesterone, corticosterone, and deoxycorticosterone while suppressing renin and aldosterone. 17-OHP and androgen levels are low, not elevated.'),
('3a1e3e30-c80b-5316-a657-ba1477eb20db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency?', '["Only sex hormone deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only cortisol deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mild hirsutism mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A CAH variant with impaired synthesis of all steroids (cortisol aldosterone sex hormones) causing salt-wasting and ambiguous genitalia","Only aldosterone deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A CAH variant with impaired synthesis of all steroids (cortisol aldosterone sex hormones) causing salt-wasting and ambiguous genitalia', '3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency impairs synthesis of cortisol, aldosterone, and sex steroids, producing salt wasting and ambiguous genitalia. This broad steroid deficiency distinguishes it from enzyme defects that spare one class of hormones.'),
('79506353-d864-5464-988d-1274cc3caa6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What laboratory finding is characteristic of 3-beta-HSD deficiency?', '["Elevated DHEA and DHEA-S with low androstenedione and testosterone","Elevated progesterone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elevated aldosterone mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevated cortisol without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elevated 17-OHP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated DHEA and DHEA-S with low androstenedione and testosterone', 'With 3-beta-HSD deficiency, the conversion of DHEA to androstenedione is blocked, so DHEA and DHEA-S accumulate while androstenedione and testosterone remain low. Cortisol, aldosterone, progesterone, and 17-OHP are not the elevated markers in this defect.'),
('3062ebf7-dc9f-56f2-b02e-8f38611b3611'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of primary hyperaldosteronism?', '["Aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn syndrome) resulting in decreased functional activity","Adrenal carcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unilateral hyperplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia (idiopathic hyperaldosteronism)","Familial hyperaldosteronism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia (idiopathic hyperaldosteronism)', 'Bilateral (idiopathic) adrenal hyperplasia is the most common cause of primary hyperaldosteronism and is managed medically. Unilateral aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn syndrome) is the next most common cause and responds well to adrenalectomy.'),
('f274f673-82bb-5692-8334-f0d9fd98e1ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common adrenal tumor?', '["Adrenal cortical carcinoma","Myelolipoma","Adrenal adenoma (incidentaloma)","Pheochromocytoma","Neuroblastoma"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal adenoma (incidentaloma)', 'Adrenal adenoma (incidentaloma) is the most common adrenal tumor, usually non-functional and found incidentally on abdominal imaging. Pheochromocytoma, adrenal cortical carcinoma, and neuroblastoma are far less frequent.'),
('a8df0dfd-2472-5790-b877-cb084418e85e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common functional adrenal tumor?', '["Pheochromocytoma","Androgen-secreting tumor","Cortisol-producing adenoma (Cushing syndrome)","Estrogen-secreting tumor","Aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn syndrome)', 'Aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn syndrome) is the most common functional adrenal tumor. Cortisol-producing adenomas, pheochromocytomas, and sex-steroid secreting tumors occur less often.'),
('43308160-4031-586f-adec-29d1041d34e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of Cushing syndrome?', '["Pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease)","Exogenous steroid use (iatrogenic)","Adrenal adenoma","Ectopic ACTH","Adrenal carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Exogenous steroid use (iatrogenic)', 'Exogenous (iatrogenic) steroid use is the most common cause of Cushing syndrome overall. Among endogenous causes, a pituitary ACTH-secreting adenoma (Cushing disease) is the most frequent.'),
('dd94f8f4-5fc0-536e-a3f4-98a5e16b9745'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of endogenous Cushing syndrome?', '["Macronodular hyperplasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Adrenal adenoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary ACTH-secreting adenoma (Cushing disease)","Ectopic ACTH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adrenal carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary ACTH-secreting adenoma (Cushing disease)', 'Pituitary ACTH-secreting adenoma (Cushing disease) is the most frequent endogenous cause of Cushing syndrome, accounting for roughly 70% of endogenous cases. Ectopic ACTH production and adrenal adenomas are less common causes.'),
('d3a38a40-7a4c-5df0-b46b-9f3b7bbd7f7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of congenital hypogonadism?', '["Constitutional delay of puberty","Hyperprolactinemia","Kallmann syndrome","Prader-Willi syndrome","Functional hypogonadotropic hypogonadism"]'::jsonb, 'Kallmann syndrome', 'Kallmann syndrome, combining congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia, is the most common form of congenital hypogonadism. Constitutional delay of puberty and functional causes are not congenital defects.'),
('86da9a13-d04f-52ae-9cc3-b7bb387beda0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of precocious puberty?', '["Peripheral precocious puberty","McCune-Albright syndrome","Gonadal tumors","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Central precocious puberty (idiopathic)"]'::jsonb, 'Central precocious puberty (idiopathic)', 'Central precocious puberty, most often idiopathic, is the most common cause of precocious puberty. Peripheral causes such as McCune-Albright syndrome, gonadal tumors, and congenital adrenal hyperplasia are much less frequent.'),
('f3e78636-0960-5298-bf99-3a0a415b7791'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common ovarian cause of precocious puberty?', '["Granulosa cell tumor","Juvenile hypothyroidism","Estrogen-secreting tumor","McCune-Albright syndrome","Follicular cyst"]'::jsonb, 'Follicular cyst', 'A follicular cyst is the most common ovarian cause of precocious puberty, producing transient estrogen secretion. Granulosa cell tumors and other estrogen-secreting neoplasms are rare causes of early puberty.'),
('30f0f9de-68c7-5b1c-bd77-9d1867703e4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common testicular cause of precocious puberty in boys?', '["Leydig cell tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Hcg-secreting germ cell tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","McCune-Albright syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Adrenal rest tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Familial male-limited precocious puberty (testotoxicosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Familial male-limited precocious puberty (testotoxicosis)', 'Familial male-limited precocious puberty (testotoxicosis), due to an activating LH receptor mutation, is the most common testicular cause of early puberty in boys. Leydig cell tumors and other testicular neoplasms are uncommon causes.'),
('2557c29b-5a59-5416-bc65-233a3b24146a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common malignant ovarian tumor in prepubertal girls?', '["Choriocarcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Granulosa cell tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Immature teratoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Yolk sac tumor (endodermal sinus tumor)","Dysgerminoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Yolk sac tumor (endodermal sinus tumor)', 'Yolk sac tumor (endodermal sinus tumor) is the most common malignant ovarian tumor in prepubertal girls and secretes alpha-fetoprotein. Dysgerminoma is the most common malignant germ cell tumor but typically occurs at an older age.'),
('13c1aa05-125a-563d-8482-7049ea4461f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common benign ovarian tumor in children?', '["Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst)","Fibroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mucinous cystadenoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Thecoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serous cystadenoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst)', 'Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst) is the most common benign ovarian tumor in children. Fibroma, thecoma, and the cystadenomas are more typical of adult women.'),
('654a4865-20ae-5cb0-a9a9-4f116ccb70e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of ovarian torsion in children?', '["Enlarged ovary from hyperstimulation","Ovarian malignancy","Polycystic ovaries","Normal ovary with long ovarian ligament","Ovarian cyst or dermoid tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian cyst or dermoid tumor', 'An ovarian cyst or dermoid tumor is the most common predisposing factor for ovarian torsion in children, because the added ovarian weight encourages twisting of the pedicle. Torsion of an otherwise normal ovary is uncommon.'),
('b19a118d-b503-5051-b8f6-4f1d031a2a37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common type of ovarian germ cell tumor?', '["Dysgerminoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Struma ovarii accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst)","Yolk sac tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Immature teratoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst)', 'Mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst) is the most common ovarian germ cell tumor overall and is benign and often asymptomatic. Dysgerminoma and yolk sac tumor are the common malignant germ cell tumors.'),
('917231a5-c784-5713-96bc-650f661791f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common malignant ovarian germ cell tumor?', '["Immature teratoma","Mixed germ cell tumor","Dysgerminoma","Yolk sac tumor","Choriocarcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Dysgerminoma', 'Dysgerminoma is the most common malignant ovarian germ cell tumor and is the ovarian counterpart of testicular seminoma. Immature teratoma and yolk sac tumor are less frequent malignant germ cell tumors.'),
('faaca79f-a611-57fb-aacc-0cc4173ef308'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common sex cord-stromal tumor of the ovary?', '["Granulosa cell tumor","Steroid cell tumor","Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor","Thecoma","Fibroma"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroma', 'Fibroma is the most common sex cord-stromal tumor of the ovary and is typically non-functional. Granulosa cell and Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors are less common but are hormonally active.'),
('c14e7abb-016c-536a-9ef5-a889ec343db3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common functional sex cord-stromal tumor of the ovary?', '["Thecoma (estrogen-producing)","Fibroma (nonfunctional)","Granulosa cell tumor (estrogen-producing)","Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor (androgen-producing)","Steroid cell tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Granulosa cell tumor (estrogen-producing)', 'Granulosa cell tumor is the most common functional sex cord-stromal tumor, secreting estrogen and causing precocious puberty or postmenopausal bleeding. Fibromas and thecomas are usually non-functional or less frequent.'),
('6ca1f36a-f95e-5b8d-addd-177c290ead96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding?', '["Endometrial atrophy","Endometrial hyperplasia","Atrophic vaginitis/endometritis","Endometrial carcinoma","Endometrial polyp"]'::jsonb, 'Atrophic vaginitis/endometritis', 'Atrophic vaginitis or endometritis is the most common cause of postmenopausal bleeding, resulting from estrogen-deficient thinning of the vaginal and endometrial mucosa. Endometrial carcinoma, hyperplasia, and polyps are less common but must be excluded.'),
('48aa77c1-0e53-59b0-a8b1-c68febf14644'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common gynecologic malignancy in developed countries?', '["Ovarian carcinoma","Endometrial carcinoma","Cervical carcinoma","Vulvar carcinoma","Vaginal carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Endometrial carcinoma', 'Endometrial carcinoma is the most common gynecologic malignancy in developed countries, typically occurring in postmenopausal women. Cervical carcinoma predominates worldwide but not in developed regions.'),
('6a7d2b06-0901-5713-a839-c1a700ebb240'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common gynecologic malignancy worldwide?', '["Endometrial carcinoma","Ovarian carcinoma","Vaginal carcinoma","Cervical carcinoma (squamous cell)","Vulvar carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical carcinoma (squamous cell)', 'Cervical carcinoma, usually of squamous cell type, is the most common gynecologic malignancy worldwide. Endometrial carcinoma leads in developed countries but is outnumbered globally by cervical cancer.'),
('8d6be6e6-ae3b-567d-aaaa-7ea996e9b8ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common histologic type of cervical cancer?', '["Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenosquamous carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Neuroendocrine carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma', 'Squamous cell carcinoma accounts for the majority of cervical cancers and arises at the transformation zone of the cervix. Adenocarcinoma and the other variants are less common.'),
('86d27648-ed35-5213-88bd-9ebe4e38a8cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of cervical cancer?', '["Persistent high-risk HPV infection (especially HPV 16 and 18)","Smoking accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple sexual partners mediated by specific receptor activation","Oral contraceptives without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Immunosuppression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent high-risk HPV infection (especially HPV 16 and 18)', 'Persistent infection with high-risk HPV types, especially 16 and 18, is the underlying cause of essentially all cervical cancers. Smoking and other listed factors are cofactors rather than primary causes.'),
('022ee037-e64f-59ef-abd7-0f45b70f568d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common ovarian malignancy?', '["Mucinous carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Endometrioid carcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serous carcinoma (high-grade serous carcinoma)","Clear cell carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brenner tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Serous carcinoma (high-grade serous carcinoma)', 'High-grade serous carcinoma is the most common ovarian malignancy and typically presents at an advanced stage. Mucinous, endometrioid, and clear cell carcinomas are less frequent.'),
('2e545429-8408-5cce-a020-7e31b0981e42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common benign ovarian tumor?', '["Serous cystadenoma (or mature cystic teratoma depending on the series)","Mucinous cystadenoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brenner tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibroma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thecoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Serous cystadenoma (or mature cystic teratoma depending on the series)', 'Serous cystadenoma is the most common benign ovarian tumor in most series, although mature cystic teratoma (dermoid cyst) is also frequently cited depending on the population studied. Mucinous cystadenoma, fibroma, and thecoma are less common.'),
('4fc26d85-a720-5e92-8802-00ec1f706cba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common site of endometriosis?', '["Rectovaginal septum","Pouch of Douglas","Ovary (endometrioma)","Uterosacral ligaments","Peritoneal surface of the bladder"]'::jsonb, 'Ovary (endometrioma)', 'The ovary is the most common site of endometriosis, where implants form an endometrioma (chocolate cyst). The pouch of Douglas, uterosacral ligaments, and pelvic peritoneum are additional but less frequent sites.'),
('14c5cef1-bf25-534f-b65a-5051da65d7a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common symptom of endometriosis?', '["Abnormal uterine bleeding resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic pelvic pain and dysmenorrhea","Dyspareunia mediated by specific receptor activation","Infertility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dyschezia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic pelvic pain and dysmenorrhea', 'Endometriosis implants respond cyclically to hormones and cause chronic pelvic pain with dysmenorrhea as the hallmark symptom. Infertility and dyspareunia are frequent sequelae but are not the most common presenting complaint.'),
('62dc988d-1730-5a0b-8572-4504c074ed0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common uterine tumor?', '["Leiomyoma (fibroid)","Adenomyosis","Endometrial stromal sarcoma","Adenomatoid tumor","Leiomyosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Leiomyoma (fibroid)', 'Leiomyomas are benign smooth muscle tumors of the myometrium and are the most common uterine tumor. The sarcomas listed are malignant and occur far less often than fibroids.'),
('4edeaac3-3139-5130-8351-c85ec7fe688f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common type of leiomyoma?', '["Broad ligament","Subserosal","Submucosal","Intramural","Cervical"]'::jsonb, 'Intramural', 'Intramural fibroids grow within the thick myometrial wall and are the most common subtype. Subserosal and submucosal tumors are less frequent, while cervical and broad-ligament fibroids are rare.'),
('f8f3b82a-3750-5140-9e38-4053cd348224'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common complication of leiomyoma?', '["Pelvic pressure resulting in decreased functional activity","Infertility accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Menorrhagia (heavy menstrual bleeding)","Miscarriage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Menorrhagia (heavy menstrual bleeding)', 'Fibroids enlarge the endometrial surface and impair myometrial contraction, so menorrhagia is the most common complication. Pain and pelvic pressure are common complaints but are less frequent than heavy bleeding.'),
('895a8466-cdb0-5383-9c37-43fe482e5432'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common malignant uterine tumor?', '["Uterine leiomyosarcoma","Endometrial stromal sarcoma","Endometrioid adenocarcinoma","Malignant mixed Müllerian tumor (carcinosarcoma)","Adenosarcoma"]'::jsonb, 'Endometrioid adenocarcinoma', 'Endometrioid adenocarcinoma, typically arising from estrogen-driven endometrial hyperplasia, is by far the most common malignancy of the uterus. Uterine sarcomas such as leiomyosarcoma and carcinosarcoma are comparatively rare.'),
('88128c75-9808-5b92-98e9-5ac30b065390'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of endometrial hyperplasia?', '["Chronic anovulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Unopposed estrogen stimulation (obesity PCOS estrogen therapy)","Granulosa cell tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Tamoxifen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progestin therapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unopposed estrogen stimulation (obesity PCOS estrogen therapy)', 'Sustained estrogen without opposing progestin drives endometrial proliferation and is the leading cause of hyperplasia, with obesity, PCOS, and estrogen therapy as common sources. Progestin therapy protects the endometrium rather than causing hyperplasia.'),
('06454d53-af99-535d-bac8-8eb2c4ab2de6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of vaginal bleeding in the first trimester?', '["Subchorionic hemorrhage","Molar pregnancy","Spontaneous abortion (miscarriage)","Cervical polyp","Ectopic pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Spontaneous abortion (miscarriage)', 'Spontaneous abortion accounts for most first-trimester vaginal bleeding, reflecting the high rate of early pregnancy loss. Ectopic pregnancy and subchorionic hemorrhage are important but less common causes.'),
('b81ef7f8-32e0-59f4-b7ec-2499d86230f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of maternal mortality worldwide?', '["Embolism","Hypertensive disorders of pregnancy","Postpartum hemorrhage","Sepsis","Unsafe abortion"]'::jsonb, 'Postpartum hemorrhage', 'Postpartum hemorrhage is the leading cause of maternal death globally, largely due to limited access to emergency obstetric care and blood products. Embolism, sepsis, and unsafe abortion claim fewer lives in worldwide statistics.'),
('072958d2-eab7-5b7c-9420-6dc4a2599beb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of maternal mortality in developed countries?', '["Sepsis","Postpartum hemorrhage","Thromboembolism (pulmonary embolism)","Cardiovascular disease","Hypertensive disorders"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiovascular disease', 'In developed countries, obstetric hemorrhage has been largely controlled, leaving cardiovascular disease as the most common indirect cause of maternal death. Thromboembolism and sepsis are significant but rank below cardiac causes.'),
('5917b94c-c354-5aab-b265-7c388ba9b538'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of fetal death in the third trimester?', '["Intrauterine infection","Cord accident","Preterm labor","Placental abruption","Placenta previa"]'::jsonb, 'Placental abruption', 'Placental abruption strips the placenta from the uterine wall and compromises fetal oxygenation, making it the most common cause of third-trimester fetal death. Cord accidents and intrauterine infection are additional but less frequent causes.'),
('75679ab8-476a-5c41-8128-97252650bcfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of preterm labor?', '["Placental abruption","Cervical insufficiency","Multiple gestation","Infection (chorioamnionitis)","Preterm premature rupture of membranes (PPROM)"]'::jsonb, 'Infection (chorioamnionitis)', 'Subclinical intrauterine infection activates inflammatory pathways that initiate preterm labor, making it the single most common cause. PPROM, cervical insufficiency, and multiple gestation contribute but occur less often.'),
('73e2a3ca-0a95-5b2c-bb40-8d00801b6a8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of PPROM (preterm premature rupture of membranes)?', '["Cervical insufficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Smoking accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intra-amniotic infection (subclinical)","Multiple pregnancy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Polyhydramnios leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intra-amniotic infection (subclinical)', 'Subclinical intra-amniotic infection weakens the fetal membranes, so it is the leading cause of preterm premature rupture of membranes. Cervical insufficiency, polyhydramnios, and smoking contribute but are less common underlying triggers.'),
('c01b2714-df60-5495-b899-96535930a698'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of oligohydramnios in the third trimester?', '["Placental insufficiency","Renal agenesis","Premature rupture of membranes (PROM)","IUGR","Post-term pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Premature rupture of membranes (PROM)', 'PROM allows amniotic fluid to leak externally, making it the most common cause of third-trimester oligohydramnios. Placental insufficiency, IUGR, and renal agenesis also lower fluid volume but are encountered less frequently.'),
('e7b93328-bd62-5a68-8a43-721df91b03d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of polyhydramnios?', '["Idiopathic (about 50% of cases)","Multiple gestation","Fetal GI obstruction (esophageal atresia duodenal atresia)","Maternal diabetes","Fetal neuromuscular disorders"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (about 50% of cases)', 'Roughly half of polyhydramnios cases have no identifiable cause, so the idiopathic category is most common. Fetal GI obstruction, maternal diabetes, and multiple gestation account for a substantial share of the remaining cases.'),
('43d6cabe-c780-5b2e-82bb-32d98a0addc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common fetal anomaly associated with polyhydramnios?', '["Neural tube defect resulting in decreased functional activity","Renal anomaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac anomaly mediated by specific receptor activation","Esophageal atresia / upper GI obstruction","Skeletal dysplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal atresia / upper GI obstruction', 'In esophageal atresia and other upper GI obstructions the fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid, so absorption fails and polyhydramnios develops. This swallowing block makes upper GI obstruction the most common anomaly associated with excess fluid.'),
('a4b337ab-f9bd-557e-a308-633fd3f47651'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common fetal anomaly associated with oligohydramnios?', '["Skeletal dysplasia","Cardiac anomaly","GI obstruction","Renal agenesis or obstructive uropathy","Neural tube defect"]'::jsonb, 'Renal agenesis or obstructive uropathy', 'Fetal urine is the main source of amniotic fluid, so renal agenesis or obstructive uropathy that cuts off urine output produces oligohydramnios. Prolonged oligohydramnios can then cause compression deformities and pulmonary hypoplasia.'),
('6c3aa1bc-c8b1-5837-84eb-ad465380b606'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly detected by prenatal ultrasound?', '["Skeletal dysplasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural tube defects (spina bifida anencephaly)","Renal anomalies mediated by specific receptor activation","Abdominal wall defects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac anomalies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neural tube defects (spina bifida anencephaly)', 'Neural tube defects are readily visualized on prenatal sonography because the spine, cranium, and brain are directly imaged, making them the most commonly detected congenital anomaly. The open defects also frequently show maternal serum AFP elevation.'),
('ec99c560-abd6-5dae-8a25-659837e3de6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common indication for genetic amniocentesis?', '["Advanced maternal age (age 35 or older at delivery)","Abnormal maternal serum screening","Parental chromosomal translocation","Ultrasound-detected structural anomaly","Previous child with chromosomal abnormality"]'::jsonb, 'Advanced maternal age (age 35 or older at delivery)', 'Advanced maternal age, defined as a delivery age of 35 years or older, is the most frequent reason for genetic amniocentesis because fetal aneuploidy risk rises sharply with age. Abnormal screening results and prior affected children are additional but less common indications.'),
('7bcbebe1-7192-5ec9-acbc-1d2cc551c0c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common chromosomal abnormality detected by CVS or amniocentesis?', '["Turner syndrome","Trisomy 18","Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)","Klinefelter syndrome","Trisomy 13"]'::jsonb, 'Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)', 'Trisomy 21 is the most common liveborn aneuploidy and therefore the abnormality most often identified on CVS or amniocentesis. Trisomy 18, trisomy 13, and Turner syndrome are each found far less frequently.'),
('da155c0d-b933-5e34-b01a-dae8f4b6ac30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is nuchal translucency?', '["Measurement of the fetal heart resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Thickness of the fetal skull accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measurement of the fetal abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Measurement of the fetal femur length without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fluid-filled space at the back of the fetal neck measured on ultrasound; increased thickness is associated with aneuploidy and cardiac anomalies"]'::jsonb, 'A fluid-filled space at the back of the fetal neck measured on ultrasound; increased thickness is associated with aneuploidy and cardiac anomalies', 'Nuchal translucency is the first-trimester ultrasound measurement of the fluid-filled space at the back of the fetal neck. Increased thickness raises suspicion for Down syndrome and other aneuploidies as well as congenital heart defects.'),
('943436c0-b2ea-54b0-add0-e2d3b10bcb48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the male urethra?', '["Anterior urethral valves","Epispadias","Posterior urethral valves","Urethral duplication","Hypospadias"]'::jsonb, 'Hypospadias', 'Hypospadias, in which the urethral meatus opens on the ventral aspect of the penis, is the most common congenital anomaly of the male urethra. Posterior urethral valves and epispadias are considerably rarer.'),
('34c0feed-1baa-5a65-b72d-b3f40fba4e65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common karyotype in spontaneous miscarriages?', '["47 +21","47 +22","Triploidy","47 +16","45 X (Turner)"]'::jsonb, '45 X (Turner)', 'Monosomy X (45,X) is the single most common individual karyotype found among spontaneous miscarriages. Trisomies collectively account for a larger share of losses, but no single trisomy is more frequent than 45,X on its own.'),
('26b359e6-8045-5634-b998-c103e94ae5b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common malignancy of the vagina in children?', '["Squamous cell carcinoma","Yolk sac tumor","Clear cell adenocarcinoma","Endodermal sinus tumor","Rhabdomyosarcoma (sarcoma botryoides)"]'::jsonb, 'Rhabdomyosarcoma (sarcoma botryoides)', 'Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma, which forms the grape-like sarcoma botryoides, is the most common vaginal malignancy of childhood. Squamous cell carcinoma and clear cell adenocarcinoma typically occur in adults rather than children.'),
('c88a338c-9f1a-5201-848e-05bf95ea2728'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common malignancy of the vagina in adults?', '["Sarcoma","Clear cell adenocarcinoma","Melanoma","lymphoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma', 'Squamous cell carcinoma, usually linked to persistent high-risk HPV infection, is the most common vaginal malignancy in adults. Clear cell adenocarcinoma, melanoma, and sarcomas are each far less frequent.'),
('e1ba3140-ecaf-55a3-899d-35be58da2a45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common benign vaginal tumor?', '["Fibroepithelial polyp (mesodermal stromal polyp)","Vaginal adenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Squamous papilloma mediated by specific receptor activation","Gartner duct cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leiomyoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroepithelial polyp (mesodermal stromal polyp)', 'Fibroepithelial polyps of the lamina propria are the most common benign tumors of the vagina, usually soft, asymptomatic polypoid lesions. Squamous papillomas, Gartner duct cysts, and leiomyomas are encountered less often.'),
('7e0d9e16-46bc-52f2-b3f9-7bf2649a529e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the vagina?', '["Longitudinal vaginal septum","Transverse vaginal septum","Vaginal agenesis (MRKH syndrome)","Imperforate hymen","Vaginal atresia"]'::jsonb, 'Vaginal agenesis (MRKH syndrome)', 'Müllerian agenesis producing vaginal agenesis, as in MRKH syndrome, is the most commonly cited developmental anomaly of the vagina and presents with primary amenorrhea and an absent or shortened vaginal canal. Septa, atresia, and imperforate hymen are additional but less common developmental defects.'),
('33d3481c-63c0-5208-ae38-fe6b42cfdfe6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of primary amenorrhea with absence of secondary sexual characteristics?', '["Pituitary tumor","Hypothalamic amenorrhea","Hyperprolactinemia","Gonadal dysgenesis (Turner syndrome)","MRKH syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Gonadal dysgenesis (Turner syndrome)', 'In gonadal dysgenesis the streak gonads fail to produce estrogen, so secondary sexual characteristics never develop and primary amenorrhea results. Hypothalamic and pituitary causes do not typically cause complete, and MRKH syndrome preserves ovarian estrogen production.'),
('4d05fd21-f8eb-5cb1-9634-8fdc593feeeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of primary amenorrhea with normal secondary sexual characteristics?', '["Imperforate hymen","Androgen insensitivity syndrome","PCOS","Transverse vaginal septum","Müllerian agenesis (MRKH syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 'Müllerian agenesis (MRKH syndrome)', 'In Müllerian agenesis the ovaries function normally and produce estrogen, so breast development proceeds normally, but the absent uterus and vagina cause primary amenorrhea. Androgen insensitivity is similar in presentation but is less common than MRKH syndrome.'),
('65cf6463-3e02-5bbd-a743-542375129b3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is Swyer syndrome (46 XY complete gonadal dysgenesis)?', '["Klinefelter syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","A 46 XY individual with streak gonads female phenotype and primary amenorrhea","Androgen insensitivity syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Pure 46 XX gonadal dysgenesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ovotesticular DSD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A 46 XY individual with streak gonads female phenotype and primary amenorrhea', 'Swyer syndrome is a 46,XY individual with dysgenetic streak gonads that secrete neither testosterone nor anti-Müllerian hormone, so a female phenotype develops with primary amenorrhea. The residual Y-chromosome material in the streak gonads raises the risk of gonadoblastoma.'),
('4c8a0a31-a151-509f-8bd9-267648b0c38a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the risk of gonadoblastoma in Swyer syndrome?', '["Risk only if dysgenetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Risk only after puberty accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","risk mediated by specific receptor activation","High risk (about 30% lifetime) requiring prophylactic gonadectomy","Low risk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High risk (about 30% lifetime) requiring prophylactic gonadectomy', 'Because the streak gonads carry Y-chromosome material, patients with Swyer syndrome face about a 30% lifetime risk of gonadoblastoma, so prophylactic gonadectomy is recommended. The low and modified-risk statements underestimate this significant oncologic threat.'),
('eb853e15-3192-5196-b623-7175a65d5263'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of male infertility in cystic fibrosis?', '["Hypogonadism resulting in decreased functional activity","Spermatogenic failure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Genetic: mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD)","Obstructive azoospermia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD)', 'CFTR dysfunction in cystic fibrosis leads to congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens, producing obstructive azoospermia; this is the most common cause of male infertility in CF. Spermatogenesis itself is usually normal, so sperm can often be retrieved surgically.'),
('49a662a3-61ee-55da-b28a-79e61a15cdb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common CFTR mutation associated with CBAVD?', '["G551D resulting in decreased functional activity","F508del (most common overall but 5T allele often seen in CBAVD)","W1282X mediated by specific receptor activation","1717-1G>A without affecting the primary tissue pathway","R117H leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'F508del (most common overall but 5T allele often seen in CBAVD)', 'F508del is the most common CFTR mutation in the general population and the one most frequently identified in men with CBAVD, although the mild 5T splice variant is disproportionately associated with isolated CBAVD. Loss of functional CFTR disrupts development of the wolffian duct derivatives.'),
('4d09c029-4adc-5cf7-a57c-88bc80e6071f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of azoospermia?', '["Anejaculation","Retrograde ejaculation","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Testicular failure (spermatogenic failure)","Obstructive azoospermia"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular failure (spermatogenic failure)', 'Primary testicular failure with impaired spermatogenesis is the most common cause of azoospermia. Obstructive azoospermia and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism are important subgroups but occur less frequently overall.'),
('309d9ba9-3e9d-5298-9f3a-f52c9d819d97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of obstructive azoospermia?', '["Ejaculatory duct obstruction resulting in decreased functional activity","CBAVD (congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens)","Epididymal obstruction mediated by specific receptor activation","Vasectomy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Post-infectious obstruction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CBAVD (congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens)', 'CBAVD, typically related to CFTR mutations, is the most common cause of obstructive azoospermia. Ejaculatory duct and epididymal obstructions along with vasectomy are additional causes but are less frequently blamed.'),
('d2915a0b-7659-5baa-b4ff-bc4a63681612'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common testicular tumor in men with cryptorchidism?', '["Teratoma","Leydig cell tumor","Gonadoblastoma","Seminoma","Yolk sac tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Seminoma', 'Undescended testes carry a higher risk of germ cell tumors, and seminoma is the most common testicular tumor both in the general population and in men with cryptorchidism. Leydig cell tumors and gonadoblastoma are far rarer.'),
('fa84c11c-e1b7-5105-b2dc-d16012b0e739'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of acute scrotal pain in children?', '["Epididymitis","Testicular trauma","Idiopathic scrotal edema","Incarcerated hernia","Testicular torsion"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular torsion', 'Testicular torsion is the most common cause of acute scrotal pain in children and a surgical emergency requiring urgent detorsion to salvage the testis. Epididymitis is more typical of adults and is a less common pediatric cause.'),
('0b067139-795b-5d76-9f0d-cf03430b1ab0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic scrotal pain?', '["Varicocele","Sperm granuloma","Chronic epididymitis","Post-vasectomy pain syndrome","Testicular tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Varicocele', 'Varicocele, dilation of the pampiniform venous plexus, is the most common cause of chronic dragging scrotal pain and is also the most common correctable cause of male infertility. Chronic epididymitis and post-vasectomy pain are less frequent sources.'),
('a3ec65d6-afb3-59e8-a84e-3945a8801f26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common method of male contraception?', '["Condom","Vasectomy","Withdrawal","Rhythm method","Abstinence"]'::jsonb, 'Condom', 'Condoms are the most widely used male contraceptive method because they are inexpensive, non-invasive, and also protect against sexually transmitted infection. Vasectomy is highly effective but is used much less often.'),
('9a3208e8-13b6-5331-841c-5b0219010ab0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of female factor infertility?', '["Uterine factor resulting in decreased functional activity","Ovulatory dysfunction (especially PCOS)","Cervical factor mediated by specific receptor activation","Tubal factor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unexplained leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ovulatory dysfunction (especially PCOS)', 'Ovulatory dysfunction, most often from PCOS, is the most common cause of female factor infertility. Tubal, uterine, and cervical factors are each important but occur less frequently than ovulatory failure.'),
('afe46548-2159-59f5-929b-fb68e6610353'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common treatment for ovulatory infertility?', '["Gonadotropins resulting in decreased functional activity","Clomiphene citrate (ovulation induction)","Ovarian drilling mediated by specific receptor activation","Metformin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Letrozole leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clomiphene citrate (ovulation induction)', 'Clomiphene citrate blocks estrogen feedback, raising gonadotropin drive to induce ovulation, and is the first-line and most commonly used agent for ovulatory infertility. Letrozole is increasingly used, but clomiphene remains the standard first choice.'),
('b5ed60a9-cfc8-5bff-af34-1e89b9dc42f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common complication of clomiphene therapy?', '["Visual disturbances resulting in decreased functional activity","Mood swings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple pregnancy (10-12% twin 1% higher order)","Hot flashes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome (OHSS) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple pregnancy (10-12% twin 1% higher order)', 'Because clomiphene stimulates the growth of multiple follicles, multiple pregnancy is its most common complication, with twin rates of about 10-12% and higher-order rates near 1%. OHSS and visual disturbances are far more typical of gonadotropin therapy than of clomiphene.'),
('a8dfee4c-0633-5b01-94c8-a5429609505e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of OHSS (ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome)?', '["PCOS predisposition resulting in decreased functional activity","Clomiphene therapy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High AMH levels mediated by specific receptor activation","Young age without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exogenous gonadotropin therapy (especially hCG trigger)"]'::jsonb, 'Exogenous gonadotropin therapy (especially hCG trigger)', 'OHSS follows intense ovarian stimulation, and exogenous gonadotropin therapy with an hCG trigger is the most common iatrogenic cause because hCG amplifies vasoactive mediator release. A PCOS tendency, young age, and high AMH predispose but do not themselves provoke the syndrome.'),
('8e85589c-fd79-59ba-8edd-4dc8cc9d424b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common form of assisted reproductive technology (ART)?', '["GIFT (gamete intrafallopian transfer)","ZIFT (zygote intrafallopian transfer)","IVF (in vitro fertilization) with embryo transfer","ICSI (intracytoplasmic sperm injection)","Frozen embryo transfer"]'::jsonb, 'IVF (in vitro fertilization) with embryo transfer', 'IVF with embryo transfer is the most common and most broadly applied assisted reproductive technology. GIFT, ZIFT, and ICSI are specialized variants reserved for more specific clinical situations.'),
('df52a607-890b-5383-aaf8-6f4fbb53b7d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common indication for ICSI?', '["Endometriosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Unexplained infertility accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tubal factor infertility mediated by specific receptor activation","Male factor infertility (severe oligospermia asthenospermia or azoospermia)","Female age leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Male factor infertility (severe oligospermia asthenospermia or azoospermia)', 'ICSI injects a single sperm directly into the oocyte, so it is most commonly used for severe male factor infertility such as oligospermia, asthenospermia, or azoospermia. Tubal and unexplained infertility are usually managed without ICSI.'),
('93e21a7a-f638-5084-b721-69f47615d602'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common chromosomal abnormality found in products of conception from first-trimester miscarriages?', '["Structural rearrangements resulting in decreased functional activity","Autosomal trisomy (especially trisomy 16)","Tetraploidy mediated by specific receptor activation","Monosomy X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Triploidy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal trisomy (especially trisomy 16)', 'Autosomal trisomy is the most common chromosomal abnormality among first-trimester miscarriage products, with trisomy 16 the single most frequent example. Monosomy X and triploidy are also found, but trisomies as a group predominate.'),
('b9096ea0-1cd4-528d-9686-e47a741a60c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of recurrent miscarriage with a genetic etiology?', '["Parental balanced translocation (especially Robertsonian)","Uterine anomaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parental inversion mediated by specific receptor activation","Fetal aneuploidy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maternal age leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parental balanced translocation (especially Robertsonian)', 'A balanced parental translocation, particularly a Robertsonian translocation, is the most common genetic cause of recurrent pregnancy loss because it generates gametes with unbalanced chromosomes. Uterine anomalies and maternal age are non-genetic contributors to recurrent loss.'),
('03c26d54-55fe-55b1-8d43-f3fe8a55194a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of stillbirth in developed countries?', '["Placental abruption","Maternal medical conditions","Infection","Placental insufficiency (IUGR)","Cord accident"]'::jsonb, 'Placental abruption', 'Placental abruption is the leading cause of stillbirth in developed countries because it abruptly interrupts oxygen and nutrient delivery to the fetus. Placental insufficiency with IUGR and maternal medical conditions are substantial but rank behind abruption.'),
('cbac6f55-96ec-5d6d-a392-16a7b1a89b56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common infectious cause of stillbirth worldwide?', '["Malaria","Cytomegalovirus","Listeria","Parvovirus B19","Syphilis"]'::jsonb, 'Syphilis', 'Syphilis crosses the placenta and is the most common infectious cause of stillbirth worldwide, particularly in resource-limited settings. Malaria and CMV also cause fetal loss but do not outrank syphilis in global figures.'),
('9692dede-8b1c-55e5-abfb-eed2a4eff055'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common route of fetal infection?', '["Ascending infection from the genital tract (transcervical)","Transplacental (hematogenous) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iatrogenic mediated by specific receptor activation","Descending infection from the peritoneum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct inoculation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending infection from the genital tract (transcervical)', 'Organisms from the lower genital tract ascend through the cervix into the amniotic cavity, so this transcervical route is the most common path of fetal infection, classically presenting as chorioamnionitis. Transplacental hematogenous spread matters for TORCH agents but is the second most common route.'),
('b46fe222-06b9-5efb-8c9d-533247045e63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common congenital infection overall?', '["Rubella","Parvovirus B19","Toxoplasma gondii","Cytomegalovirus (CMV)","Syphilis"]'::jsonb, 'Cytomegalovirus (CMV)', 'CMV is the most common congenital infection overall and a leading infectious cause of sensorineural hearing loss and neurodevelopmental disability. Toxoplasmosis, rubella, parvovirus B19, and syphilis are each less frequent.'),
('6c49c856-1a5d-5ead-ad08-9eb49819725a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of sensorineural hearing loss from congenital infection?', '["Herpes simplex","Toxoplasmosis","CMV","Rubella","Syphilis"]'::jsonb, 'CMV', 'Congenital CMV is the leading infectious cause of sensorineural hearing loss and is a major cause of non-genetic congenital deafness. Rubella and toxoplasmosis can also damage hearing but are far less common today, while herpes simplex and syphilis are rare causes of congenital hearing loss.'),
('27a5f67f-8e82-5f9a-8ad3-0f5ea07e8f51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common presentation of congenital CMV infection?', '["Purpura resulting in decreased functional activity","Asymptomatic (most infants are asymptomatic at birth)","Hearing loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Chorioretinitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Microcephaly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asymptomatic (most infants are asymptomatic at birth)', 'Most newborns with congenital CMV have no signs at birth, yet some later develop late-onset sensorineural hearing loss. Symptomatic infants with purpura, microcephaly, or chorioretinitis represent the minority with more severe disease.'),
('c00304c7-5a51-564c-b17f-5d12f48a7a49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the classic triad of congenital toxoplasmosis?', '["Cleft lip cleft palate cardiac anomalies","Microcephaly IUGR rash","Hearing loss cataracts PDA","Chorioretinitis hydrocephalus intracranial calcifications","Hepatosplenomegaly jaundice purpura"]'::jsonb, 'Chorioretinitis hydrocephalus intracranial calcifications', 'Congenital toxoplasmosis classically produces chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus, and diffuse intracranial calcifications. The other listed combinations belong to different congenital infections or syndromes rather than toxoplasmosis.'),
('83831059-37f6-5eee-8973-f110bd7bea1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of congenital sensorineural hearing loss?', '["Congenital CMV infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Genetic causes (especially GJB2 mutations)","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Otoxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rubella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic causes (especially GJB2 mutations)', 'About half of congenital sensorineural hearing loss is genetic, with recessive GJB2 (connexin 26) mutations being the most frequent single cause. Congenital CMV is the leading non-genetic (environmental) cause, whereas trauma, ototoxicity, and rubella are less common or avoidable causes.'),
('2cfb0db7-e140-505e-bb83-b1e0063e1a78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common syndromic cause of congenital hearing loss?', '["Waardenburg syndrome","Pendred syndrome","Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome","Alport syndrome","Usher syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Waardenburg syndrome', 'Waardenburg syndrome, an autosomal dominant disorder with pigmentary changes (white forelock, heterochromia), is the most common syndromic form of congenital hearing loss. Usher, Pendred, and Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndromes are less common, and Alport syndrome presents with nephritis plus hearing loss.'),
('70022102-7650-56d1-af63-b7f2f8e972a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common genetic cause of syndromic hearing loss with renal involvement?', '["Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome (KCNQ1 KCNE1)","Branchiootorenal syndrome (EYA1)","Pendred syndrome (SLC26A4)","Usher syndrome (MYO7A)","Alport syndrome (COL4A5)"]'::jsonb, 'Alport syndrome (COL4A5)', 'Alport syndrome, caused by COL4A5 mutations affecting type IV collagen, combines sensorineural hearing loss with progressive glomerulonephritis. Branchiootorenal syndrome also pairs renal anomalies with hearing loss but is less common than Alport syndrome.'),
('c8f7bbbf-71a4-57a4-865c-81448f9ffe17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of childhood end-stage renal disease?', '["Membranoproliferative GN","HUS","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)","CAKUT (congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract)","IgA nephropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)', 'FSGS is the leading acquired glomerular disease progressing to ESRD in children, presenting with nephrotic syndrome and steroid resistance. CAKUT are important structural causes of pediatric renal failure, while HUS, IgA nephropathy, and MPGN are less common pathways to ESRD.'),
('19065e69-605c-5563-99cb-cc123a6337e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common glomerular disease causing ESRD in adults?', '["Diabetic nephropathy","IgA nephropathy","Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Membranous nephropathy","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetic nephropathy', 'Diabetic nephropathy is the most common single cause of end-stage renal disease in adults, reflecting the global epidemic of type 2 diabetes. Hypertensive nephrosclerosis and the primary glomerulonephritides are responsible for a smaller proportion of adult ESRD.'),
('c7589b01-22ed-54a2-9698-3bd87796aada'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children?', '["Membranous nephropathy","IgA nephropathy","Post-streptococcal GN","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)","Minimal change disease (MCD)"]'::jsonb, 'Minimal change disease (MCD)', 'Minimal change disease accounts for roughly 80% of childhood nephrotic syndrome and usually responds dramatically to steroids. FSGS and membranous nephropathy are more typical of adult nephrotic syndrome, while post-streptococcal GN presents as nephritic rather than nephrotic syndrome.'),
('16ce1a62-6fb4-55e8-a30d-9ac385381068'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in adults?', '["Minimal change disease","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Diabetic nephropathy","Membranous nephropathy","Amyloidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Membranous nephropathy', 'Membranous nephropathy is the leading primary cause of adult nephrotic syndrome and is frequently secondary to malignancy, autoimmune disease, or drugs. FSGS incidence is rising but remains less common in most populations, while amyloidosis and diabetic nephropathy are rarer or secondary causes.'),
('86110bc0-dab3-5542-91fd-da89d0fb1d22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of acute kidney injury (AKI) in children?', '["Sepsis","Nephrotoxins","Glomerulonephritis","Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS)","Prerenal AKI (dehydration)"]'::jsonb, 'Prerenal AKI (dehydration)', 'Prerenal AKI from dehydration, vomiting, or diarrhea is the most common cause of acute kidney injury in children. Intrinsic renal injury such as HUS or glomerulonephritis is important but far less frequent, and nephrotoxin exposure accounts for a minority of pediatric AKI.'),
('bbb5c9fd-0b3f-582e-b6b4-c0d85a69af52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of AKI in hospitalized adults?', '["Acute tubular necrosis (ATN) due to ischemia or nephrotoxins","Glomerulonephritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute interstitial nephritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Postrenal AKI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prerenal AKI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute tubular necrosis (ATN) due to ischemia or nephrotoxins', 'ATN from prolonged hypotension, sepsis, or nephrotoxic drugs is the most common cause of intrinsic AKI in hospitalized patients, typically recovering over weeks. Prerenal azotemia responds to volume replacement, while glomerulonephritis and interstitial nephritis are less common inpatient causes.'),
('757a0504-94b3-5af2-8182-4dfd05e86884'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) in children?', '["Genetic complement mutations resulting in decreased functional activity","Atypical HUS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (STEC) infection (typically O157:H7)","HIV infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumococcal infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (STEC) infection (typically O157:H7)', 'Typical (D+) HUS in children is triggered by Shiga toxin-producing E. coli, usually O157:H7, after bloody diarrhea and is the leading cause of pediatric HUS. Atypical HUS from complement dysregulation and pneumococcal-associated HUS are much rarer causes.'),
('54c40e80-2bdd-5ad6-ad1d-1cc7b611c6f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of chronic kidney disease (CKD) in adults?', '["Diabetes mellitus (type 2)","Hypertension","Unknown","Polycystic kidney disease","Glomerulonephritis"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetes mellitus (type 2)', 'Diabetic nephropathy is the most common cause of CKD and ESRD in adults, with hypertension the second most common. Polycystic kidney disease and glomerulonephritis are important but less frequent etiologies of adult CKD.'),
('1b135436-fbc3-57c1-b8f9-de3395a848e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of hypertension in children?', '["Essential hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Renal parenchymal disease (e.g. glomerulonephritis reflux nephropathy)","Coarctation of the aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","Renovascular disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endocrine causes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal parenchymal disease (e.g. glomerulonephritis reflux nephropathy)', 'Unlike adults, childhood hypertension is usually secondary, with renal parenchymal disease such as glomerulonephritis or reflux nephropathy the leading cause. Coarctation of the aorta, renovascular disease, and endocrine tumors are less common secondary causes.'),
('9449ed9d-79d5-54bc-ae82-935facb59ac8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of secondary hypertension in adults?', '["Cushing syndrome","Renovascular disease (renal artery stenosis)","Primary hyperaldosteronism","Renal parenchymal disease","Pheochromocytoma"]'::jsonb, 'Renal parenchymal disease', 'Chronic renal parenchymal disease is the most common cause of secondary hypertension in adults, operating through volume expansion and renin activation. Renovascular disease, primary hyperaldosteronism, and pheochromocytoma are recognized but less common secondary causes.'),
('8ac6ed51-cccf-575d-a97d-5e81b6c9d3a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of renal artery stenosis in young adults?', '["Radiation-induced","Neurofibromatosis","Atherosclerosis","Takayasu arteritis","Fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD)"]'::jsonb, 'Fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD)', 'Fibromuscular dysplasia is the most common cause of renal artery stenosis in young adults, especially women, and typically responds to angioplasty. Atherosclerosis predominates in older patients, while Takayasu arteritis and neurofibromatosis are much rarer causes in this age group.'),
('847b2626-14a7-58e7-9bf0-7e1c75c050f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of renal artery stenosis in older adults?', '["Emboli resulting in decreased functional activity","Dissection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibromuscular dysplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Takayasu arteritis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atherosclerosis (atheromatous plaque at the ostium)"]'::jsonb, 'Atherosclerosis (atheromatous plaque at the ostium)', 'In older adults renal artery stenosis is almost always atherosclerotic, with plaque typically at the ostium of the vessel near the aorta. Fibromuscular dysplasia mainly affects younger women, while emboli, dissection, and Takayasu arteritis are uncommon causes of stenosis.'),
('58e039c7-d03c-54ff-a1c5-da4ffdd2b364'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common site of renal artery stenosis due to FMD?', '["Intrarenal arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Proximal segment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Distal main renal artery (mid to distal segment)","Branch vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ostium of the renal artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal main renal artery (mid to distal segment)', 'Fibromuscular dysplasia characteristically involves the mid-to-distal portion of the main renal artery, in contrast to atherosclerotic stenosis at the ostium. Intrarenal branch-vessel involvement occurs less often, and proximal or ostial narrowing is typical of atherosclerosis.'),
('f0cece99-2d7e-545b-a039-1b79983a0888'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the characteristic angiographic appearance of FMD?', '["Focal stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Long smooth stenosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Irregular plaque mediated by specific receptor activation","String of beads appearance (alternating stenosis and dilation)","Ostial stenosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'String of beads appearance (alternating stenosis and dilation)', 'The hallmark of fibromuscular dysplasia on angiography is a string-of-beads pattern of alternating stenotic and dilated segments, mainly in the mid-to-distal renal artery. Focal or ostial stenosis and irregular plaque instead suggest atherosclerotic disease.'),
('f97fcf46-8c24-5a1d-9064-2428129b9790'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common complication of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease?', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Intracranial aneurysm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flank pain mediated by specific receptor activation","UTI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progressive renal failure (ESRD)"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive renal failure (ESRD)', 'Progressive loss of renal function leading to ESRD is the most common and defining complication of ADPKD, with most affected patients reaching dialysis in later life. Hypertension, cyst infections, flank pain, and intracranial aneurysms are frequent but less constant complications.'),
('306eba95-caee-5d13-84ad-c3e0861cc26a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common extrarenal manifestation of ADPKD?', '["Cardiac valvular disease (MVP)","Pancreatic cysts","Inguinal hernia","Hepatic cysts","Colonic diverticulosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic cysts', 'Hepatic cysts are the most frequent extrarenal manifestation of ADPKD, seen in a majority of older affected patients. Pancreatic cysts, mitral valve prolapse, inguinal hernias, and diverticulosis all occur but are less common.'),
('4faef686-fb17-55b7-b8a7-d3f0bd5ecedd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of death in ADPKD?', '["Infections resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiovascular events (hypertension and LVH)","Malignancy mediated by specific receptor activation","Ruptured intracranial aneurysm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ESRD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiovascular events (hypertension and LVH)', 'Cardiovascular disease driven by hypertension and left ventricular hypertrophy is the leading cause of death in ADPKD. Ruptured intracranial aneurysm is a feared but less common fatal event, while uremia and infection contribute to a smaller share of mortality.'),
('696dc8d1-2f32-562f-afca-dfe389cd2fe4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of end-stage renal disease in children with congenital nephrotic syndrome?', '["Peritonitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Progressive renal failure within the first few years of life","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Thromboembolic complications without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypertension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive renal failure within the first few years of life', 'In congenital nephrotic syndrome the disease itself relentlessly progresses to renal failure within the first few years of life, making it the main cause of ESRD in these children. Infections, peritonitis, and thrombosis complicate the course but are not the primary cause of ESRD.'),
('0f658fbd-0ac2-5e3a-81cc-8dd6537c02c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of urinary tract infection (UTI) in children?', '["Proteus mirabilis","Enterococcus faecalis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Klebsiella pneumoniae","E. coli"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli', 'Escherichia coli accounts for the large majority of UTIs in children, as it does in most patient groups. Proteus, Klebsiella, enterococci, and pseudomonas are encountered less often and usually in specific settings such as structural abnormalities or instrumentation.'),
('f1a42d6d-93ba-5ff4-81c0-438f79d8ae10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of UTI in sexually active young women?', '["Klebsiella pneumoniae","Group B Streptococcus","Staphylococcus saprophyticus","E. coli","Proteus mirabilis"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli', 'E. coli remains the most common uropathogen in sexually active young women, usually arising from bowel flora colonizing the periurethral area. Staphylococcus saprophyticus is a characteristic but second-line cause in this group, and the others are uncommon.'),
('e38cb253-9954-5ad5-83e6-048968751ff3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of complicated UTI in hospitalized patients?', '["Pseudomonas aeruginosa resulting in decreased functional activity","Enterococcus spp accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Klebsiella pneumoniae mediated by specific receptor activation","E. coli (still most common but more resistant strains)","Candida spp leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli (still most common but more resistant strains)', 'E. coli is still the most frequent pathogen in complicated UTIs, although multidrug-resistant strains dominate in hospitalized patients. Pseudomonas, enterococci, and Candida are important nosocomial causes but are less common than E. coli overall.'),
('b0488b65-e9f9-52ed-88f5-07c6d53756d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of acute pyelonephritis?', '["Ascending infection from the lower urinary tract (cystitis)","Lymphatic spread accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct extension from surrounding organs mediated by specific receptor activation","Idiopathic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hematogenous spread leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending infection from the lower urinary tract (cystitis)', 'Acute pyelonephritis is usually an ascending infection from the bladder and urethra via the ureters into the renal pelvis. Hematogenous seeding occurs mainly in immunocompromised or bacteremic patients, while lymphatic and direct spread are negligible routes.'),
('876d57ec-3e7e-5f71-9b2e-6d2ee76aeac4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of emphysematous pyelonephritis?', '["Nephrolithiasis resulting in decreased functional activity","Diabetes mellitus (in immunocompromised patients with gas-forming organisms)","Immunosuppression mediated by specific receptor activation","Obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic kidney disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetes mellitus (in immunocompromised patients with gas-forming organisms)', 'Emphysematous pyelonephritis is a necrotizing infection with gas production seen mostly in diabetic and immunocompromised patients, usually caused by gas-forming organisms such as E. coli. Obstruction and nephrolithiasis predispose to the infection but are not the principal underlying condition.'),
('a89acc16-bdb6-52ad-a622-d6c5b673163a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of renal abscess?', '["Complicated pyelonephritis","Hematogenous seeding from distant infection","Ascending UTI with obstruction (especially stones)","Instrumentation","IV drug use"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending UTI with obstruction (especially stones)', 'Renal abscess most often follows an ascending UTI combined with obstruction such as a stone, which traps infection in the kidney. Hematogenous seeding and instrumentation are less frequent routes, typically in patients with bacteremia or IV drug use.'),
('bf823a22-e0d2-5a5b-8ae6-146c3fd1b183'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of nephrocalcinosis?', '["Hyperparathyroidism (primary or secondary)","Oxalosis","Renal tubular acidosis (type 1 distal)","Cortical necrosis","Medullary sponge kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperparathyroidism (primary or secondary)', 'Hypercalcemia from hyperparathyroidism is the most common trigger of nephrocalcinosis, causing calcium deposits in the renal parenchyma, typically the medulla. Distal renal tubular acidosis and oxalosis also cause it but are less frequent than hyperparathyroidism.'),
('0b142fc4-adb8-584e-ad9d-65d9f2b2c596'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of renal stones?', '["Struvite (infection) stones","Calcium phosphate stones","Uric acid stones","Calcium oxalate stones (most common type)","Cystine stones"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium oxalate stones (most common type)', 'Calcium oxalate is the most common stone composition, accounting for the majority of nephrolithiasis. Calcium phosphate, uric acid, struvite, and cystine stones each represent smaller fractions with distinct metabolic or infectious causes.'),
('df7a0131-0ba0-53b1-ab24-2708bf8c57a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common risk factor for calcium oxalate stones?', '["Hypercalciuria","Hypocitraturia","Low urine volume (dehydration)","Hyperoxaluria","Hyperuricosuria"]'::jsonb, 'Low urine volume (dehydration)', 'Low urine volume from inadequate fluid intake is the single most common risk factor for calcium oxalate stones because it concentrates solutes and promotes supersaturation. Hypercalciuria and hyperoxaluria contribute to stone formation but are less universally prevalent risk factors.'),
('519cf77c-6654-5f99-95e5-788088c783cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of struvite (infection) stones?', '["Pseudomonas resulting in decreased functional activity","Urease-producing organisms (especially Proteus mirabilis)","Staphylococcus mediated by specific receptor activation","E. coli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Klebsiella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urease-producing organisms (especially Proteus mirabilis)', 'Struvite stones form when urease-producing bacteria, classically Proteus mirabilis, split urea into ammonia and alkalinize the urine, precipitating magnesium-ammonium-phosphate. E. coli and other non-urease producers do not create the alkaline milieu required for these stones.'),
('2ab089d0-bfe4-53c9-ae72-9aa5cffb7b47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of staghorn calculi?', '["Calcium phosphate stones","Calcium oxalate stones","Uric acid stones","Cystine stones","Struvite stones associated with UTI"]'::jsonb, 'Struvite stones associated with UTI', 'Staghorn calculi that fill the renal pelvis and calyces are almost always struvite stones formed during infection with urease-producing organisms. Calcium oxalate, uric acid, cystine, and calcium phosphate stones rarely grow large enough to produce a staghorn cast.'),
('57f2fa29-1efe-54b0-b465-ae8a8be0b51f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of acute urinary retention in men?', '["Phimosis","Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)","Medication side effect","Urethral stricture","Prostate cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)', 'BPH is the most common cause of acute urinary retention in men, usually occurring when prostatic enlargement acutely obstructs the bladder outlet. Prostate cancer, urethral stricture, phimosis, and drug effects are less frequent causes of retention.'),
('eaf625cf-854c-5855-92d0-e9599ab224fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of gross hematuria in children?', '["Tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","UTI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypercalciuria mediated by specific receptor activation","Glomerulonephritis (especially IgA nephropathy or post-streptococcal)","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerulonephritis (especially IgA nephropathy or post-streptococcal)', 'Gross hematuria in children most often reflects glomerular disease such as IgA nephropathy or post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, frequently following an infection. UTIs, hypercalciuria, and trauma are less common causes, whereas tumors are extremely rare in this age group.'),
('f46c18f8-d073-5ec0-bfbf-45634a92f6bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'What is the most common cause of microscopic hematuria in adults?', '["Enlarged prostate resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glomerulonephritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Benign causes (exercise menstruation infection) but bladder cancer must be ruled out in older patients","Stones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign causes (exercise menstruation infection) but bladder cancer must be ruled out in older patients', 'Incidental microscopic hematuria in adults is most often benign, arising from exercise, menstruation, or low-grade infection, yet older patients require cystoscopy to exclude bladder cancer. Stones, prostatic enlargement, and glomerulonephritis are less common explanations.'),
('7488089c-ce8f-56cf-b77d-b5e8a730cb2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'In Urogenital development, which statement best fits the following fact: the three successive kidney systems in human development are:', '["Pronephros metanephros mesonephros","Pronephros mesonephros metanephros","Metanephros pronephros mesonephros","All three develop simultaneously","Mesonephros pronephros metanephros"]'::jsonb, 'Pronephros mesonephros metanephros', 'Kidneys develop in craniocaudal sequence as the pronephros, mesonephros, and finally the metanephros, the definitive kidney. The systems form successively, not simultaneously, and only the metanephros persists as the adult organ.'),
('2f0ae8e9-92e4-5e5d-9a89-7041b4532fb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Regarding Urogenital development, what is the correct answer to: the pronephros is:', '["A kidney in the sacral region resulting in decreased functional activity","The adult kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The definitive kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","The functional embryonic kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A transient nonfunctional kidney in the cervical region"]'::jsonb, 'A transient nonfunctional kidney in the cervical region', 'The pronephros appears in the cervical region as a vestigial, nonfunctional kidney that quickly regresses. It never functions and is succeeded by the mesonephros; the definitive kidney is the metanephros.'),
('735e35ed-fda2-50be-bae0-cb1a9fa16284'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'For Urogenital development, which option is most appropriate: the mesonephros functions as:', '["The first kidney in the cervical region","A temporary kidney in the thoracolumbar region","The definitive kidney","The adult kidney","The last kidney to develop"]'::jsonb, 'A temporary kidney in the thoracolumbar region', 'The mesonephros functions briefly as a temporary kidney in the thoracolumbar region and its duct system is reused in the male reproductive tract. It regresses as the metanephros becomes the definitive adult kidney, and it does not occupy the cervical region.'),
('1b8af459-9cd4-575d-9547-4f72cb0e2bbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Which answer is correct in Urogenital development when asked: the mesonephric (wolffian) duct forms from which structure?', '["Urogenital ridge","Pronephros","Metanephros","Genital ridge","Mesonephros"]'::jsonb, 'Mesonephros', 'The mesonephric (wolffian) duct arises from the mesonephros and later gives rise to the ureteric bud and male genital ducts. The pronephros completely regresses, while the metanephros gives rise to the definitive kidney and its collecting system.'),
('c3874055-b1ab-5e9e-ab04-ac2df790dd57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'In Urogenital development, the best response to ''The ureteric bud is an outgrowth of which structure'' is:', '["Mesonephric duct (Wolffian duct)","Paramesonephric duct (Müllerian duct)","Urogenital sinus","Metanephric mesenchyme","Cloaca"]'::jsonb, 'Mesonephric duct (Wolffian duct)', 'The ureteric bud grows out from the caudal end of the mesonephric (wolffian) duct and induces the metanephric mesenchyme. It later forms the ureter, renal pelvis, calyces, and collecting ducts, whereas the Müllerian duct and urogenital sinus have no role in its origin.'),
('8a277463-c778-54be-a1a0-8a8726310dfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'During revision of Urogenital development, which of the following is correct: the metanephric mesenchyme (blastema) gives rise to which structures?', '["Renal pelvis and calyces","Bloom''s capsule","Renal interstitium","Collecting ducts and ureter","Nephrons (glomerulus to distal tubule)"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrons (glomerulus to distal tubule)', 'The metanephric mesenchyme differentiates into the nephron, from the glomerulus down to the distal convoluted tubule. The ureter, pelvis, calyces, and collecting ducts derive from the ureteric bud rather than from the blastema.'),
('8fdbadc1-73d6-565d-b885-f11554b4ea99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'In Urogenital development, which statement best fits the following fact: what induces the metanephric mesenchyme to form nephrons?', '["The Wolffian duct resulting in decreased functional activity","The ureteric bud (reciprocal induction)","The pronephros mediated by specific receptor activation","The Müllerian duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mesonephros leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ureteric bud (reciprocal induction)', 'The ureteric bud induces the metanephric mesenchyme to condense and form nephrons, while the mesenchyme in turn induces the bud to branch. This reciprocal induction explains why failure of ureteric bud development causes renal agenesis.'),
('b6bf94ac-7ff3-5617-aa56-acacda039f87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Regarding Urogenital development, what is the correct answer to: what is renal agenesis?', '["Complete absence of one or both kidneys","A pelvic kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A horseshoe kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","A cystic kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A small kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complete absence of one or both kidneys', 'Renal agenesis is the complete absence of one or both kidneys, due to failure of ureteric bud induction of the metanephric mesenchyme. Pelvic, horseshoe, cystic, and hypoplastic kidneys are different malformations in which renal tissue is present.'),
('c9217317-c594-5aa0-beef-58c7bd815312'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'For Urogenital development, which option is most appropriate: what is the incidence of unilateral renal agenesis?', '["000 resulting in decreased functional activity","1 in 50 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 in 100 mediated by specific receptor activation","1 in 10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","About 1 in 1000 live births"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 in 1000 live births', 'Unilateral renal agenesis occurs in about 1 in 1000 live births and is usually asymptomatic because the single kidney hypertrophies. Bilateral renal agenesis is far rarer and is lethal with the oligohydramnios (Potter) sequence.'),
('824f4a5e-32a3-5bc3-aa6d-a5a65314ffd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'Which answer is correct in Urogenital development when asked: bilateral renal agenesis is incompatible with life due to:', '["Electrolyte imbalance resulting in decreased functional activity","Oligohydramnios sequence (Potter sequence) causing pulmonary hypoplasia","Renal failure at birth mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urinary tract infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oligohydramnios sequence (Potter sequence) causing pulmonary hypoplasia', 'Without fetal urine there is severe oligohydramnios (Potter sequence), and the resulting pulmonary hypoplasia is the lethal feature, since the lungs fail to develop before birth. Neonatal renal function terms are misleading because the fetus depends on the placenta for waste excretion.'),
('cacf5116-7e20-5278-9187-6d84d45ddfc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'In Urogenital development, the best response to ''What is the most common cause of Potter sequence'' is:', '["Obstructive uropathy","Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease","Posterior urethral valves","Bilateral multicystic dysplastic kidneys","Bilateral renal agenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral renal agenesis', 'Bilateral renal agenesis is the most common cause of the Potter sequence, producing anhydramnios and pulmonary hypoplasia. Obstructive uropathy, posterior urethral valves, ARPKD, and bilateral multicystic dysplastic kidneys also cause oligohydramnios but are less frequent causes of the full sequence.'),
('9d0b0a06-b654-50e5-9abe-b5b54beaad20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Normal Defective Development - Urogenital System', 'During revision of Urogenital development, which of the following is correct: what is a horseshoe kidney?', '["Complete fusion of both kidneys resulting in decreased functional activity","Fusion of the upper poles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fusion of the lower poles of the kidneys across the midline","A kidney on the wrong side without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extra kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fusion of the lower poles of the kidneys across the midline', 'A horseshoe kidney is formed when the lower poles of both kidneys fuse across the midline, usually with the isthmus trapped by the inferior mesenteric artery. Upper-pole fusion, complete fusion, ectopia, and supernumerary kidneys represent different anomalies.'),
('16fdca19-9933-5126-80cc-3c87e7484112'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What is the main characteristic of connective tissue?', '["Only fibers without cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)","Cells arranged in sheets mediated by specific receptor activation","Closely packed cells with minimal ECM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","extracellular matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)', 'Connective tissue is defined by relatively few cells dispersed in an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM) of fibers and ground substance. Epithelial tissues, by contrast, form sheets of closely packed cells with minimal ECM.'),
('414a4153-a38d-5462-b50e-96697c841df9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The three basic components of connective tissue are:', '["Epithelium and ECM","and ground substance","Cells","Fibers and water","fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Cells', 'The three basic components of connective tissue are cells, fibers, and ground substance. Of the options listed, ''Cells'' is the only true connective-tissue component; epithelium and water are not, and fibers alone are incomplete without ground substance.'),
('5d80e7aa-1f4f-5e48-90c8-42f6568c9c34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Connective tissue is derived from which germ layer?', '["Endoderm","All three germ layers equally","Mesoderm (primarily)","Ectoderm","Neural crest only"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoderm (primarily)', 'Most connective tissue differentiates from embryonic mesoderm, whose loosely organized mesenchyme gives rise to fibroblasts and other connective-tissue cells. A few head and face structures derive from neural crest, but mesoderm is the primary source.'),
('3ca67e4e-c3ac-5843-b747-cd4f74af4685'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The undifferentiated cells of connective tissue that can differentiate into various cell types are:', '["Fibroblasts","Macrophages","Mesenchymal stem cells","Mast cells","Adipocytes a"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells', 'Mesenchymal stem cells are multipotent cells in connective tissue capable of differentiating into fibroblasts, adipocytes, chondroblasts, osteoblasts, and other cell types. Fibroblasts, macrophages, and mast cells are already committed cells.'),
('5d89dcb4-5355-5fed-a79d-43b52a93a0b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Fibroblasts are responsible for:', '["Secreting histamine","Producing collagen","and ground substance ECM components","elastin","Phagocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Producing collagen', 'Fibroblasts synthesize and secrete collagen and other extracellular matrix components, including ground substance and elastin. Histamine secretion belongs to mast cells, and phagocytosis to macrophages.'),
('7fd73cce-ea25-5015-b0df-374c327c1305'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Myofibroblasts are:', '["Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)","Smooth muscle cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrocytes without function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mast cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)', 'Myofibroblasts are modified fibroblasts that acquire smooth-muscle-like contractile filaments (actin and myosin), allowing them to contract during wound healing. They are not smooth muscle cells, macrophages, or mast cells.'),
('66c24019-666b-5429-a773-681cdb5ec209'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Where are myofibroblasts most active?', '["Normal skin resulting in decreased functional activity","In healing wounds and granulation tissue","Cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In healing wounds and granulation tissue', 'Myofibroblasts appear and are most active in healing wounds and granulation tissue, where their contraction draws wound edges together. They are not prominent in normal skin, cartilage, blood, or bone.'),
('76af72fc-5fa5-575e-8f98-47b2b8b4ffba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Macrophages are derived from:', '["Neutrophils resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Platelets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)"]'::jsonb, 'Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)', 'Macrophages arise from blood-borne monocytes that emigrate into tissues and mature into tissue macrophages. Neutrophils, lymphocytes, and platelets are separate leukocyte lineages.'),
('4dacc120-c232-521f-8039-c196f2bba246'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The primary function of macrophages is:', '["and apoptotic cells","Phagocytosis of debris","Producing collagen","microorganisms","Secreting mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Phagocytosis of debris', 'Macrophages are professional phagocytes that engulf debris, apoptotic cells, and microorganisms. Collagen production is a fibroblast function, and mucus secretion belongs to goblet cells.'),
('b0cd0814-ce73-52d2-9482-d983d09a2540'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Resident macrophages in the liver are called:', '["Osteoclasts","Kupffer cells","Alveolar macrophages","Langerhans cells","Microglia"]'::jsonb, 'Kupffer cells', 'Kupffer cells are the resident tissue macrophages lining the sinusoids of the liver. Osteoclasts resorb bone, alveolar macrophages reside in the lung, Langerhans cells in the epidermis, and microglia in the brain.'),
('25b78502-b243-56f7-b2b1-c2e5210370be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Resident macrophages in the lung are called:', '["Langerhans cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Microglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Histiocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Kupffer cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveolar macrophages (dust cells)"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar macrophages (dust cells)', 'Alveolar macrophages, also called dust cells, are the resident macrophages of the lung, where they phagocytose inhaled particles and pathogens. Langerhans cells are epidermal, microglia are in the CNS, histiocytes in connective tissue, and Kupffer cells in the liver.'),
('efb7a050-c0d8-50a5-bcda-ae4c1abcf39c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Resident macrophages in the brain are called:', '["Osteoclasts","Macrophages","Microglia","Dendritic cells","Kupffer cells"]'::jsonb, 'Microglia', 'Microglia are the resident macrophages of the central nervous system. Osteoclasts act on bone, dendritic cells present antigen, and Kupffer cells line the liver sinusoids.'),
('c394a142-2baf-5e21-905b-91f7f3f21ac5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Resident macrophages in the epidermis are called:', '["Langerhans cells","Keratinocytes","Melanocytes","Merkel cells","Microglia"]'::jsonb, 'Langerhans cells', 'Langerhans cells are the antigen-presenting (macrophage-like) cells of the epidermis. Keratinocytes, melanocytes, and Merkel cells are distinct epidermal cell types, and microglia reside in the brain.'),
('3c3921ae-9554-5f10-a8d7-3bdf12b5b769'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Resident macrophages in the bone are called:', '["Chondroclasts","Osteocytes","Macrophages (bone marrow)","Osteoblasts","Osteoclasts"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoclasts', 'Osteoclasts are multinucleated, macrophage-lineage cells that resorb bone. Osteoblasts form bone and osteocytes are mature bone cells embedded in the matrix.'),
('4088b9cb-1e37-5ca9-aa91-7f2fa2f5cef5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Resident macrophages in connective tissue are called:', '["Langerhans cells","Alveolar macrophages","Histiocytes (tissue macrophages)","Microglia","Kupffer cells"]'::jsonb, 'Histiocytes (tissue macrophages)', 'Histiocytes are the resident tissue macrophages of loose connective tissue. Langerhans cells are in the epidermis, alveolar macrophages in the lung, microglia in the brain, and Kupffer cells in the liver.'),
('adb0a1df-b014-5fde-8577-41bba004a84c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Mast cells are characterized by:', '["Long dendritic processes resulting in decreased functional activity","Small eosinophilic granules accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phagocytic ability mediated by specific receptor activation","Large basophilic granules containing histamine and heparin","granules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large basophilic granules containing histamine and heparin', 'Mast cells contain large basophilic granules storing histamine and heparin, which are released during allergic reactions. Eosinophils carry small eosinophilic granules, and macrophages are phagocytic but not granule-rich in this manner.'),
('70b8311f-cc15-5515-a5f7-87f3c879a8f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Mast cells are derived from:', '["Fibroblasts","Macrophages","T-lymphocytes","Bone marrow precursors (CD34+)","Neutrophils"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow precursors (CD34+)', 'Mast cells originate from CD34-positive bone marrow precursor cells and mature in tissues. Fibroblasts, macrophages, and neutrophils derive from different precursor lineages.'),
('0e182520-c8c1-5897-ab63-9fdb3af1c91e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The granules of mast cells contain:', '["tryptase","Histamine","heparin","Collagen","and chymase"]'::jsonb, 'Histamine', 'Mast cell granules store histamine along with heparin, tryptase, and chymase. Of the choices given, histamine is a correct granule content; collagen is not stored in mast cells.'),
('746aa583-5638-55ad-8b52-737fcfeae031'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Activation of mast cells occurs primarily by:', '["ACh release resulting in decreased functional activity","Direct binding of allergen to granules accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complement activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cross-linking of IgE bound to FcεRI receptors by allergen","Bacterial toxins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cross-linking of IgE bound to FcεRI receptors by allergen', 'Mast cells are activated when an allergen cross-links two IgE molecules bound to Fc-epsilon receptors on the cell surface, triggering degranulation. Complement, ACh, and bacterial toxins are not the primary triggers.'),
('47e1e113-3025-5bc3-9d31-adcb6824eae2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Plasma cells are derived from:', '["B lymphocytes (activated B cells)","Fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stem cells mediated by specific receptor activation","T lymphocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'B lymphocytes (activated B cells)', 'Plasma cells are terminally differentiated effector B lymphocytes that produce antibodies. Fibroblasts, monocytes, and T lymphocytes give rise to different cell types.'),
('d49122c9-3f11-5125-aa89-ef9d2a314715'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The morphological hallmark of plasma cells is:', '["Small nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Eccentric nucleus with clock-face chromatin pattern and prominent Golgi (hof)","Golgi mediated by specific receptor activation","Multiple nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abundant granules a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Eccentric nucleus with clock-face chromatin pattern and prominent Golgi (hof)', 'Plasma cells show an eccentric nucleus with coarse ''clock-face'' chromatin and a prominent perinuclear clear zone (hof) representing the Golgi apparatus. These features reflect their role in antibody synthesis.'),
('ea3a03c0-6af7-5b2c-9da7-3553374e2bd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Plasma cells are most abundant in:', '["Central nervous system resulting in decreased functional activity","Cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lamina propria of the gut and lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Lamina propria of the gut and lymph nodes', 'Plasma cells are most numerous in the lamina propria of the gut and in lymph nodes, sites of active antibody production. They are essentially absent from cartilage, bone, muscle, and the CNS.'),
('44896c88-b484-5539-8e64-f872f0b17f49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Russell bodies are:', '["Lipid droplets in adipocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Collagen bundles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intracytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions in plasma cells (accumulated immunoglobulins)","Elastic fibers a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granules in mast cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intracytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions in plasma cells (accumulated immunoglobulins)', 'Russell bodies are eosinophilic, intracytoplasmic inclusions of accumulated immunoglobulin found in plasma cells. They are not lipid droplets, collagen bundles, or mast-cell granules.'),
('59631cbf-f393-5519-b8cb-e78c88ec7601'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Leukocytes (white blood cells) found in connective tissue include:', '["eosinophils","Neutrophils only","All of the above: neutrophils","lymphocytes","and monocytes"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above: neutrophils', 'Neutrophils, eosinophils, lymphocytes, and monocytes all migrate into connective tissue, especially during inflammation, so the response must include all the named leukocytes. They are not restricted to neutrophils alone.'),
('ae5c6f87-5e6f-5ce4-af95-faa6d3ade564'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Neutrophils are characterized by:', '["Bilobed nucleus and eosinophilic granules","Multi-lobed nucleus and fine","neutral-staining granules","Round nucleus and basophilic granules","granules"]'::jsonb, 'Multi-lobed nucleus and fine', 'Neutrophils are characterized by a multi-lobed nucleus and fine neutral-staining granules containing degradative enzymes. A bilobed nucleus with eosinophilic granules describes eosinophils, and basophilic granules describe basophils.'),
('98b6ec39-885e-5d3b-8bc0-14093d0df275'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The primary function of neutrophils is:', '["Antibody production resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasite killing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Virus killing a mediated by specific receptor activation","Allergic reactions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phagocytosis and killing of bacteria (especially via oxygen-dependent mechanisms)"]'::jsonb, 'Phagocytosis and killing of bacteria (especially via oxygen-dependent mechanisms)', 'Neutrophils are the first responders that phagocytose and kill bacteria, chiefly through oxygen-dependent (respiratory burst) mechanisms. Antibody production, parasite killing, and allergic reactions are roles of other leukocytes.'),
('98b274f7-4fdb-5ef9-b22b-12512aeda538'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Eosinophils are characterized by:', '["Multi-lobed nucleus and basophilic granules","Round nucleus with granules","Bilobed nucleus and large eosinophilic (red) granules","U-shaped nucleus and pale granules","Bilobed nucleus and purple granules a"]'::jsonb, 'Bilobed nucleus and large eosinophilic (red) granules', 'Eosinophils have a bilobed nucleus and large eosinophilic granules that stain red with acid dyes. Basophils carry basophilic granules, and neutrophils have a multi-lobed nucleus.'),
('af397315-70ae-53e8-b758-e57513c17fc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Eosinophils are primarily involved in:', '["Bacterial killing resulting in decreased functional activity","Viral immunity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood clotting mediated by specific receptor activation","Allergic reactions and defense against parasitic infections","Antibody production leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic reactions and defense against parasitic infections', 'Eosinophils defend against parasitic infections and mediate allergic reactions by releasing granule contents. Bacterial killing is mainly a neutrophil role, and blood clotting is a platelet function.'),
('87373b4d-e275-5012-8eb9-b807cb286552'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Basophils are circulating granulocytes with:', '["Bilobed nucleus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Clear cytoplasm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","granules mediated by specific receptor activation","Small eosinophilic granules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large basophilic granules that may overlie the nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Large basophilic granules that may overlie the nucleus', 'Basophils are granulocytes with large basophilic granules that often obscure or overlie the nucleus. Small eosinophilic granules belong to eosinophils, and bilobed nuclei alone are not the defining feature.'),
('4eb75d38-e81c-5911-9933-cca375bea908'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Basophils and mast cells share the ability to:', '["Present antigens resulting in decreased functional activity","Bind IgE and release histamine and heparin","Phagocytose bacteria mediated by specific receptor activation","Secrete mucus a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produce antibodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bind IgE and release histamine and heparin', 'Basophils and mast cells both bind IgE and release histamine and heparin upon activation, participating in immediate hypersensitivity. Antigen presentation, phagocytosis, and antibody production are functions of other cells.'),
('df9dba27-e149-516b-8f90-cd21745ddfb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Lymphocytes are characterized by:', '["Multi-lobed nucleus","dark-staining nucleus with a thin rim of cytoplasm","Round","Bilobed nucleus","Abundant granules"]'::jsonb, 'Round', 'Lymphocytes have a round, dark-staining nucleus surrounded by only a thin rim of cytoplasm. A multi-lobed nucleus characterizes neutrophils, and a bilobed nucleus characterizes eosinophils.'),
('39acc8e8-677e-548e-a6a6-4bb2d818ea45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The main types of lymphocytes are:', '["Neutrophils and eosinophils","Macrophages and dendritic cells","and NK cells","B cells","T cells"]'::jsonb, 'T cells', 'The main classes of lymphocytes are T cells, B cells, and natural killer (NK) cells. Neutrophils, eosinophils, macrophages, and dendritic cells are separate leukocyte populations.'),
('352eb844-6b4c-5ac6-9693-ecc34373d941'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Dendritic cells are professional antigen-presenting cells (APCs) that:', '["Produce antibodies","process","and present antigens to T lymphocytes","Capture","Phagocytose bacteria"]'::jsonb, 'Capture', 'Dendritic cells capture, process, and present antigens to T lymphocytes, making them the most potent professional antigen-presenting cells. Antibody production is aB-cell function, not a dendritic-cell role.'),
('4389f45a-2c52-5861-8aac-cf3bfb2815ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Adipocytes in connective tissue store:', '["Phospholipids","Triglycerides (neutral fat)","Cholesterol","Proteins","Glycogen"]'::jsonb, 'Triglycerides (neutral fat)', 'Adipocytes store energy as triglycerides (neutral fat) in a large single lipid droplet. Phospholipids, cholesterol, glycogen, and proteins are stored or handled by other mechanisms.'),
('f95fe304-9ad9-5a3d-848d-02676175fcb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Collagen is the most abundant protein in the human body. It is composed of:', '["A single polypeptide chain resulting in decreased functional activity","Three polypeptide chains (alpha chains) forming a triple helix","Four polypeptide chains mediated by specific receptor activation","Five polypeptide chains without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two polypeptide chains leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Three polypeptide chains (alpha chains) forming a triple helix', 'Collagen is composed of three alpha polypeptide chains wound together into a triple helix, which gives the fiber its high tensile strength. Single, double, four, or five chains do not apply.'),
('90a92eb4-ac33-5b5f-89fb-71b5eb262329'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The amino acid sequence unique to collagen is:', '["Glu-X-Y resulting in decreased functional activity","Pro-X-Y accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ala-X-Y mediated by specific receptor activation","Gly-X-Y (with Gly every third residue)","Lys-X-Y leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gly-X-Y (with Gly every third residue)', 'Collagen''s repeating unit is the Gly-X-Y sequence, with glycine occupying every third residue to permit tight helix packing; X is often proline and Y is often hydroxyproline. Other amino acid patterns do not occur in collagen.'),
('2f46c553-4bf2-501a-9ce4-63ec80f01663'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Hydroxyproline and hydroxylysine in collagen are formed by:', '["Carboxylation of proline","Phosphorylation of proline and lysine","Methylation of lysine","Post-translational hydroxylation requiring vitamin C (ascorbate)","Direct incorporation during translation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-translational hydroxylation requiring vitamin C (ascorbate)', 'Proline and lysine residues in collagen are hydroxylated after translation, a reaction requiring vitamin C as a cofactor. This hydroxylation stabilizes the triple helix; the other listed modifications are incorrect.'),
('4bad8026-97a8-5c08-8dc2-54a07b940f27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Scurvy (vitamin C deficiency) results in:', '["Osteoporosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Defective collagen synthesis (unstable triple helix) causing fragile blood vessels","Excess collagen production mediated by specific receptor activation","poor wound healing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased collagen cross-linking leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Defective collagen synthesis (unstable triple helix) causing fragile blood vessels', 'Vitamin C deficiency impairs the hydroxylation of proline and lysine, producing an unstable collagen triple helix that leads to fragile blood vessels, easy bruising, and poor wound healing. Excess collagen or increased cross-linking does not occur.'),
('416667c0-3859-5709-869e-abc3c05bbeda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Collagen synthesis involves intracellular and extracellular steps. The intracellular precursor of collagen is:', '["Reticulin","Collagen fiber","Collagen fibril","Tropocollagen","Procollagen"]'::jsonb, 'Procollagen', 'Procollagen is the intracellular precursor of collagen, containing additional N- and C-terminal propeptides. Tropocollagen and collagen fibrils are extracellular products, and reticulin is a form of type III collagen.'),
('0ec99a13-214a-56ac-bbfb-051f94c708fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Procollagen is converted to tropocollagen extracellularly by:', '["Cleavage of the triple helix resulting in decreased functional activity","Cross-linking by lysyl oxidase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cleavage of the N- and C-terminal propeptides by procollagen peptidases","Hydroxylation of proline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Addition of carbohydrates leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cleavage of the N- and C-terminal propeptides by procollagen peptidases', 'Extracellularly, procollagen peptidases cleave the N- and C-terminal propeptides, converting procollagen to tropocollagen. Lysyl oxidase acts later to cross-link tropocollagen molecules into fibrils.'),
('ebae3312-cbfe-5d99-837e-5f00f7afdea0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The enzyme that degrades collagen in the ECM is:', '["Procollagen peptidase resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) especially collagenase (MMP-1)","Lysyl oxidase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyaluronidase a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) especially collagenase (MMP-1)', 'Matrix metalloproteinases, especially collagenase (MMP-1), degrade collagen in the extracellular matrix during remodeling. Lysyl oxidase builds cross-links, and hyaluronidase degrades hyaluronic acid, not collagen.'),
('7e540904-bc0b-5b08-b242-c9748b2204d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Type I collagen is found in:', '["tendon","skin","Bone","and dentin","ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Bone', 'Type I collagen, the most abundant type, is found in bone, tendon, skin, dentin, and ligaments; all of the listed locations contain it. Of the choices, ''Bone'' is a valid site.'),
('65b8c7c7-1643-50a1-b386-e5d030d714cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Type II collagen is found in:', '["Basement membranes","Hyaline and elastic cartilage","Blood vessel walls","Bone and tendon","Reticular fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaline and elastic cartilage', 'Type II collagen is the principal collagen of hyaline and elastic cartilage, forming a loose mesh with proteoglycans. Basement membranes contain type IV, and bone and tendon contain type I.'),
('4b4ad5e9-87d9-5fcb-af7c-f6e163a4a54b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Type III collagen is found in:', '["Reticular fibers of lymphoid organs","Tendon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone mediated by specific receptor activation","liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and smooth muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reticular fibers of lymphoid organs', 'Type III collagen polymerizes into thin reticular fibers that form the supporting framework of lymphoid organs, liver, and smooth muscle. Tendon and bone are dominated by type I collagen.'),
('dbc703f7-e485-571e-a3c5-cdc51d287e44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Type IV collagen is found in:', '["Tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","Cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dermis mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basement membranes (basal lamina)"]'::jsonb, 'Basement membranes (basal lamina)', 'Type IV collagen forms the sheet-like network of basement membranes (basal lamina) rather than fibrils. Tendon, dermis, cartilage, and bone use other collagen types.'),
('758f02b3-638f-54ef-8502-c22654bb32d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Type V collagen is found in:', '["and cornea resulting in decreased functional activity","tendon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Basement membranes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Co-localized with type I (regulates fibril diameter) in bone"]'::jsonb, 'Co-localized with type I (regulates fibril diameter) in bone', 'Type V collagen co-localizes with type I collagen and helps regulate fibril diameter; it is found in bone, tendon, and cornea. Cartilage uses type II, and basement membranes use type IV.'),
('f11a5d77-e7a5-57ee-8681-03fbb644f982'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Type VII collagen forms:', '["Beaded filaments resulting in decreased functional activity","Pericellular fibrils accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Basement membrane network mediated by specific receptor activation","Anchoring fibrils that attach the basal lamina to the dermis","Reticular fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anchoring fibrils that attach the basal lamina to the dermis', 'Type VII collagen assembles into anchoring fibrils that attach the basal lamina to the underlying dermis. The other listed structures belong to different collagen or fiber types.'),
('0f89272d-2b6a-58a6-9b06-50bb6cdca6a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Elastic fibers are responsible for:', '["Tensile strength","Cell adhesion","ECM hydration a","Tissue compression","Tissue elasticity and recoil"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue elasticity and recoil', 'Elastic fibers endow tissues with elasticity and the ability to recoil after stretch. Tensile strength is provided by collagen, and compression resistance by proteoglycans and water.'),
('912a4698-727f-58d6-937d-ca7b412c7be8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The two components of elastic fibers are:', '["Elastin and fibronectin resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastin and collagen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elastin (amorphous core) and fibrillin microfibrils (peripheral sheath)","Collagen and proteoglycans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrillin and hyaluronic acid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elastin (amorphous core) and fibrillin microfibrils (peripheral sheath)', 'Elastic fibers consist of an amorphous core of elastin surrounded by a peripheral sheath of fibrillin microfibrils. Collagen, fibronectin, and proteoglycans are separate matrix components.'),
('0941fa92-5b0a-5ade-902d-be3a9b722aca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The cross-links unique to elastin are:', '["Peptide bonds","Hydroxylysine cross-links","Disulfide bonds","Desmosine and isodesmosine","Amide linkages"]'::jsonb, 'Desmosine and isodesmosine', 'Desmosine and isodesmosine are the unique cross-links that join elastin molecules, formed from lysine residues and giving elastin its stretch-and-recoil behavior. Other bonds do not create elastin''s characteristic cross-links.'),
('42cdc0b7-96e4-5f68-b30a-638fa9e0e110'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Tropoelastin is:', '["The breakdown product of elastin a","The cross-linked form of elastin","The fiber form of elastin","The soluble precursor of elastin (secreted by cells)","The microfibril component of elastic fibers"]'::jsonb, 'The soluble precursor of elastin (secreted by cells)', 'Tropoelastin is the soluble monomer secreted by cells into the extracellular space, where cross-linking converts it into insoluble elastin fibers. It is not a breakdown product and does not form the microfibrillar component, which is made of fibrillin.'),
('e71321c4-5792-5b80-b547-ced0b671b68b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The microfibrils of elastic fibers are composed primarily of:', '["Laminin","Collagen type III","Fibronectin","Elastin","Fibrillin-1 and fibrillin-2"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrillin-1 and fibrillin-2', 'Microfibrils of elastic fibers are formed by the glycoproteins fibrillin-1 and fibrillin-2, which provide a scaffold onto which elastin is deposited. Laminin and fibronectin are adhesion glycoproteins, and elastin is the core protein of the fiber rather than its microfibrillar sheath.'),
('5b66f0a0-f50c-5fce-985a-d7d4967a36a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Elastase is a serine protease that degrades:', '["Fibronectin","Elastin","Proteoglycans","Laminin","Collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Elastin', 'Elastase is named for its principal substrate, elastin, whose peptide bonds it cleaves to degrade elastic fibers. It does not primarily attack fibronectin, laminin, proteoglycans, or collagen.'),
('2e022a53-ec15-5c5e-8dc0-e8177e48c844'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'α1-Antitrypsin deficiency leads to:', '["Pulmonary fibrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Asthma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreatitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver cirrhosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary emphysema (due to unopposed neutrophil elastase activity)"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary emphysema (due to unopposed neutrophil elastase activity)', 'α1-Antitrypsin is the main inhibitor of neutrophil elastase; in its absence elastase degrades lung elastic tissue unchecked, causing emphysema. Such patients may also develop liver cirrhosis from abnormal protein accumulation, but the characteristic pulmonary consequence is emphysema.'),
('e69d083b-dc63-5090-aa57-339abee37141'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Reticular fibers are composed of:', '["Type II collagen","Type I collagen","Elastin a","Type IV collagen","Type III collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Type III collagen', 'Reticular fibers are thin, branching fibers made of type III collagen that form the supportive meshwork of lymphoid organs and glands. Type I collagen forms thick tensile fibers and type IV collagen is found in basement membranes, not in reticular fibers.'),
('36b77213-7264-52e6-9299-77f19acbb7a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Reticular fibers can be visualized with:', '["H&E stain","Silver stain (argyrophilic)","Trichrome stain","Toluidine blue","PAS stain"]'::jsonb, 'Silver stain (argyrophilic)', 'Reticular fibers are argyrophilic, meaning they take up silver salts and are therefore best demonstrated with a silver impregnation stain. They are not well shown by H&E or PAS stains, and toluidine blue stains proteoglycans rather than reticular fibers.'),
('43b0b928-7d87-5cc2-b2b8-8da7a1f85626'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Reticular cells are modified fibroblasts that:', '["Secrete hormones resulting in decreased functional activity","Produce antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contract a mediated by specific receptor activation","Produce reticular fibers and create the stromal meshwork of lymphoid organs","Phagocytose debris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Produce reticular fibers and create the stromal meshwork of lymphoid organs', 'Reticular cells are specialized fibroblasts that synthesize type III collagen to form the reticular meshwork supporting lymph nodes, spleen, and marrow. They do not secrete hormones, produce antibodies, or contract.'),
('6d0c5678-7f7e-529b-9b14-0ef55644942b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Ground substance (amorphous intercellular substance) consists of:', '["GAGs","Only collagen fibers","Only water","and multiadhesive glycoproteins","proteoglycans"]'::jsonb, 'GAGs', 'The amorphous ground substance is composed chiefly of glycosaminoglycans (GAGs), proteoglycans, and multiadhesive glycoproteins within a hydrated gel. Collagen fibers are formed elements of connective tissue matrix rather than part of the amorphous ground substance.'),
('b0da41ed-7c25-5b1b-943d-96d0751d6b32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The function of ground substance includes:', '["Providing elasticity","Providing tensile strength","Allowing diffusion of nutrients and waste","cell migration","resisting compression"]'::jsonb, 'Allowing diffusion of nutrients and waste', 'The hydrated gel of ground substance permits diffusion of nutrients and metabolic waste between blood vessels and cells. It is the amorphous medium through which molecular exchange occurs in connective tissue.'),
('3fcb6169-6e28-59e1-bf4a-2eec5e0a671d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The most abundant GAG in the body is:', '["Hyaluronic acid","Heparan sulfate a","Keratan sulfate","Chondroitin sulfate","Dermatan sulfate"]'::jsonb, 'Chondroitin sulfate', 'Chondroitin sulfate is the most abundant glycosaminoglycan in the body, found abundantly in cartilage, bone, and blood vessel walls where it binds water to resist compression. Heparan sulfate is important in basement membranes but not the most abundant overall.'),
('755ce956-85a2-5455-b350-a60b536168c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Hyaluronic acid is a GAG that:', '["hydrated polymers that resist compression","Is sulfated","Is covalently bound to a core protein","Forms large","Is positively charged"]'::jsonb, 'Forms large', 'Hyaluronic acid is a unique, non-sulfated GAG that forms large hydrated polymers which bind enormous amounts of water and resist compression. Unlike other GAGs it is not sulfated, is not covalently attached to a core protein, and is negatively charged.'),
('4ba85774-844a-55a0-a7b6-d0fbec1b949d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Chondroitin sulfate is a sulfated GAG found in:', '["bone","Bone only","Cartilage","and cornea","Cartilage only"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilage', 'Chondroitin sulfate is a major sulfated GAG of cartilage, where it binds to aggrecan to hydrate the matrix and resist compression. It is characteristic of cartilage, distinguishing it from the cornea, which is rich in keratan sulfate.'),
('0f37877a-9fc7-53a6-86aa-c78b6215f2dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Keratan sulfate is a sulfated GAG found in:', '["and intervertebral disc","cartilage","Cornea","Tendon","Skin"]'::jsonb, 'Cornea', 'Keratan sulfate is the major GAG of the cornea, contributing to corneal transparency, and is also present in cartilage and intervertebral discs. It is particularly characteristic of the cornea compared with the other listed tissues.'),
('5faa609a-0065-5c80-8063-9a9e202cd286'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Dermatan sulfate is a sulfated GAG found in:', '["Skin","Cartilage","Cornea","and blood vessel walls","tendons"]'::jsonb, 'Skin', 'Dermatan sulfate is a sulfated GAG abundant in the dermis (skin), tendons, and blood vessel walls. It contributes to the tensile strength and flexibility of these dense connective tissues.'),
('b3ed13ac-ac9f-5cb6-b623-4d218d0ffc66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Heparan sulfate is found in:', '["Tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","Basement membranes and cell surfaces (as part of proteoglycans)","Blood mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Basement membranes and cell surfaces (as part of proteoglycans)', 'Heparan sulfate is located in basement membranes and on cell surfaces, where as part of proteoglycans such as perlecan it participates in filtration and cell–matrix interaction. It is characteristic of these sites rather than tendon, bone, or cartilage.'),
('9179fb77-fade-5f5b-b674-765aa2a0057e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Heparin is a highly sulfated GAG produced by:', '["Chondrocytes","Macrophages","Fibroblasts","Osteoblasts","Mast cells and basophils"]'::jsonb, 'Mast cells and basophils', 'Heparin is synthesized and stored in the granules of mast cells and basophils, where it functions as an anticoagulant. It is not produced by chondrocytes, macrophages, fibroblasts, or osteoblasts.'),
('df463354-4f14-5355-a090-4d775817e87c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Proteoglycans are macromolecules composed of:', '["GAG chains only","Core protein with collagen fibers","Core protein with covalently attached GAG chains","Glycoprotein with oligosaccharides","Core protein only"]'::jsonb, 'Core protein with covalently attached GAG chains', 'A proteoglycan consists of a core protein with one or more GAG chains covalently linked to it. Glycoproteins, by contrast, carry short branched oligosaccharides rather than long GAG chains.'),
('ed196398-4fbf-5f31-badc-2b6ca8e6fc94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Aggrecan is the major proteoglycan of:', '["Artery wall","Tendon","Cornea","Cartilage","Bone"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilage', 'Aggrecan is the principal proteoglycan of cartilage, where its many chondroitin and keratan sulfate chains bind water to give the matrix its swelling and compressive-resistance properties. It is not the major proteoglycan of artery walls, tendon, cornea, or bone.'),
('33c86698-fb7f-527b-ba93-2f5da50ec075'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The aggrecan-hyaluronan aggregate provides:', '["Corneal transparency resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone hardness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Compressive resistance to cartilage (by attracting water)","Tensile strength to cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elasticity to skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Compressive resistance to cartilage (by attracting water)', 'The aggrecan–hyaluronan aggregate traps water within the cartilage matrix, generating swelling pressure that resists compression. Tensile strength comes from the collagen framework rather than from the proteoglycan aggregate.'),
('af56dbc3-c8d4-546b-943d-c60e4a92c4a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Link protein stabilizes the bond between:', '["Fibronectin and integrin","Elastin and fibrillin","Collagen and proteoglycan","Aggrecan and hyaluronic acid","Cells and ECM"]'::jsonb, 'Aggrecan and hyaluronic acid', 'Link protein strengthens the non-covalent attachment of aggrecan to the hyaluronic acid backbone of the cartilage proteoglycan aggregate. It does not stabilize adhesion between cells and the extracellular matrix or between elastin and fibrillin.'),
('ff25e992-b671-5a4e-8d0d-c5c8f95e6c17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Multi-adhesive glycoproteins in ECM include:', '["Collagen only","Proteoglycans","Fibronectin","and tenascin","laminin"]'::jsonb, 'Fibronectin', 'Fibronectin is a multi-adhesive glycoprotein that binds cells to collagen, proteoglycans, and other matrix molecules, promoting cell adhesion and migration. Proteoglycans and collagen are matrix components rather than multi-adhesive glycoproteins.'),
('de473a82-d215-5ec5-871b-a1f9b5f8375d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Fibronectin is a major ECM glycoprotein with which cell-binding sequence?', '["LDV (Leu-Asp-Val)","IKVAV (Ile-Lys-Val-Ala-Val)","RGD (Arg-Gly-Asp)","YIGSR (Tyr-Ile-Gly-Ser-Arg)","REDV (Arg-Glu-Asp-Val)"]'::jsonb, 'RGD (Arg-Gly-Asp)', 'Fibronectin contains the tripeptide RGD (Arg-Gly-Asp), which is recognized by integrin receptors and mediates cell adhesion. YIGSR and IKVAV are sequences associated with laminin rather than fibronectin.'),
('abd74194-a40a-5a6e-af24-2262fa27efbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Fibronectin exists in two forms:', '["RGD and non-RGD forms","Elastic and non-elastic forms","Tissue and blood forms","Collagen and non-collagen forms","Plasma (soluble) form and cellular (insoluble) ECM form"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma (soluble) form and cellular (insoluble) ECM form', 'Fibronectin occurs as a soluble plasma form circulating in blood and as an insoluble cellular form incorporated into the extracellular matrix. The distinction is based on solubility and location, not on RGD content or collagen association.'),
('74f9f341-b710-5fb5-a0da-3916ec60e1fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The primary function of fibronectin is:', '["Antibody production","Cell adhesion","Enzymatic degradation","and wound healing","migration"]'::jsonb, 'Cell adhesion', 'Fibronectin''s primary role is cell adhesion, linking cells to the matrix through integrin binding of its RGD sequence. This adhesion also supports cell migration and wound healing, but these are downstream consequences of adhesion.'),
('f2d25e59-5985-519b-9ed6-da164a6b0678'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Laminin is the major glycoprotein of:', '["Bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastic fibers a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collagen fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Basement membranes (basal lamina)","Cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Basement membranes (basal lamina)', 'Laminin is the principal glycoprotein of basement membranes, where it binds type IV collagen, perlecan, and cell surface receptors to anchor epithelial cells. It is not a major component of bone, cartilage, or elastic fibers.'),
('0f4aa9aa-ac24-5482-967f-5e09ece4e04a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Tenascin is an ECM glycoprotein expressed during:', '["Neural development only resulting in decreased functional activity","Angiogenesis only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal adult tissues mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone formation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Embryonic development and wound healing (anti-adhesive)"]'::jsonb, 'Embryonic development and wound healing (anti-adhesive)', 'Tenascin is expressed transiently during embryonic development and wound healing, where it has anti-adhesive effects that allow cells to detach and migrate. It is generally sparse in normal adult tissues and is not restricted to neural development or angiogenesis.'),
('7667fe10-98e5-5c47-9cbf-917128274e11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Thrombospondin is a:', '["GAG resulting in decreased functional activity","Proteoglycan accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","especially angiogenesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Matricellular glycoprotein that regulates cell-matrix interactions","Collagen type leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Matricellular glycoprotein that regulates cell-matrix interactions', 'Thrombospondin is a matricellular glycoprotein that modulates cell–matrix interactions, including inhibition of angiogenesis. It is not a GAG, proteoglycan, or collagen type.'),
('3bdfc0ca-80ea-58a7-932e-f50d3d5d197b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteopontin is a:', '["involved in cell adhesion and mineralization resulting in decreased functional activity","Proteoglycan accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collagen type mediated by specific receptor activation","Phosphorylated glycoprotein in bone and other tissues","Protease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphorylated glycoprotein in bone and other tissues', 'Osteopontin is a phosphorylated glycoprotein found in bone and other tissues, where it binds cells and matrix and participates in mineralization and cell adhesion. It is not a proteoglycan, collagen, or protease.'),
('76142e74-44cc-537f-a5b1-0cd246aacae7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Integrins are the major:', '["ECM glycoproteins a resulting in decreased functional activity","Transcription factors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cytosolic adapter proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Cell surface receptors that mediate cell-ECM adhesion","Gap junction proteins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cell surface receptors that mediate cell-ECM adhesion', 'Integrins are transmembrane cell surface receptors that bind ECM ligands, such as fibronectin, and link the matrix to the cytoskeleton to mediate cell adhesion and signaling. They are not transcription factors or gap junction proteins.'),
('fb079d6d-8327-57dd-b8b2-8739d66e1d54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Integrins bind to which sequence in ECM proteins?', '["IKVAV","RGD (Arg-Gly-Asp)","KGD a","YIGSR","LDV"]'::jsonb, 'RGD (Arg-Gly-Asp)', 'Integrins recognize the RGD (Arg-Gly-Asp) tripeptide present in fibronectin and other matrix proteins, using it as the primary binding site. YIGSR and IKVAV are laminin sequences recognized by other receptors, not by integrins.'),
('8d979ebb-55dc-51ad-9697-53fad8624c43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Selectins mediate:', '["Cell-ECM adhesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Gap junction communication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cell-cell tight junctions mediated by specific receptor activation","Desmosome adhesion a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leukocyte rolling on endothelium (initial adhesion)"]'::jsonb, 'Leukocyte rolling on endothelium (initial adhesion)', 'Selectins are cell surface lectins that bind carbohydrate ligands and mediate the initial rolling of leukocytes on activated endothelium before firm adhesion. They do not mediate cell–ECM adhesion or tight junction formation.'),
('157f3105-d8b3-53a3-bece-eab66bee84d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'CD44 is a:', '["Integrin resulting in decreased functional activity","Collagen receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cadherin mediated by specific receptor activation","Selectin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cell surface hyaluronan receptor (involved in cell migration)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell surface hyaluronan receptor (involved in cell migration)', 'CD44 is a cell surface receptor for hyaluronic acid, and this binding promotes cell migration and leukocyte homing. It is not an integrin, cadherin, or selectin, which recognize different ligands.'),
('e9c97896-ba74-59cc-b011-488c031941df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The primary component of the ECM in tendon is:', '["Elastin resulting in decreased functional activity","Type I collagen arranged in parallel bundles with little ground substance","Reticular fibers a mediated by specific receptor activation","Proteoglycans only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type II collagen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type I collagen arranged in parallel bundles with little ground substance', 'Tendon ECM consists predominantly of type I collagen fibers arranged in parallel bundles aligned with the direction of pull, providing high tensile strength with little ground substance. Type II collagen is characteristic of cartilage, not tendon.'),
('5055de10-1660-5589-80b8-7904113d1e77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The ECM of cartilage is rich in:', '["Type I collagen and fibronectin","Type II collagen and aggrecan","Type IV collagen and perlecan","Type III collagen and laminin","Elastin and fibrillin"]'::jsonb, 'Type II collagen and aggrecan', 'Cartilage matrix is rich in type II collagen, which forms its fibrillar network, and the proteoglycan aggrecan, which hydrates the matrix to resist compression. Type IV collagen and perlecan belong to basement membranes rather than cartilage.'),
('25e50795-5b5d-58d9-b5e8-34c67cf43739'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The perichondrium is a layer of dense connective tissue that surrounds:', '["except at articular surfaces resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Most cartilage (hyaline and elastic)","Tendon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most cartilage (hyaline and elastic)', 'The perichondrium is a sheath of dense connective tissue that surrounds most hyaline and elastic cartilage, supplying vessels and providing chondrogenic cells, but is absent at articular surfaces, where cartilage relies on synovial fluid. Bone is covered by periosteum rather than perichondrium.'),
('396808ca-a7de-54ee-94ee-83cd0e9a794b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Howship lacunae are:', '["The Haversian canals resulting in decreased functional activity","Resorption pits on the bone surface created by osteoclasts","Lacunae containing osteocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","The spaces between bone trabeculae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The Volkmann canals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Resorption pits on the bone surface created by osteoclasts', 'Howship lacunae are shallow scalloped pits hollowed out of the bone surface by osteoclasts during resorption. Osteocyte lacunae and the Haversian and Volkmann canals are distinct, unrelated spaces within bone.'),
('f98bc977-36c0-56b6-b15c-ea7a8e58ebe8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The reversal line (cement line) in bone is:', '["The line of osteocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","The line of bone formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A basophilic line indicating the boundary between old and new bone (resorption pit filled by new bone)","The peripheral region of osteons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The line of calcification leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A basophilic line indicating the boundary between old and new bone (resorption pit filled by new bone)', 'The reversal (cement) line is a basophilic boundary that marks where osteoclast resorption stopped and new bone began to fill the pit. It is seen in remodeling and distinguishes older from newly deposited bone, not a zone of osteocytes or calcification front.'),
('8fbcebe7-efb6-51a8-803b-e8a798e02b6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Woven bone is formed:', '["Slowly during bone remodeling resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapidly during fetal development and fracture healing","Only in adults mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in pathological conditions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the growth plate a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapidly during fetal development and fracture healing', 'Woven bone forms rapidly during fetal skeletal development, at fracture calluses, and in some pathological states, producing an immature matrix with irregularly arranged collagen. In adults it is normally replaced by slowly formed lamellar bone during remodeling.'),
('e7f6e68d-e79b-561f-ba1e-2d94bcc31092'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteoclasts are multinucleated because:', '["They are syncytial from cell fusion","They phagocytose other cells","They form by fusion of mononuclear precursor cells (monocytes)","They undergo mitosis without cytokinesis","They have multiple nuclei from cell division"]'::jsonb, 'They form by fusion of mononuclear precursor cells (monocytes)', 'Osteoclasts arise from fusion of mononuclear precursors of the monocyte–macrophage lineage, yielding large multinucleated cells. They do not become multinucleated by repeated mitosis or by phagocytosing other cells.'),
('9b36dd0d-c30c-5169-b174-bdc7ee6e2f3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The ruffled border of an osteoclast is:', '["The highly folded plasma membrane facing the bone surface (where resorption occurs)","The smooth surface facing away from bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The attachment site mediated by specific receptor activation","The pseudopodium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The basolateral surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The highly folded plasma membrane facing the bone surface (where resorption occurs)', 'The ruffled border is the highly convoluted plasma membrane of the osteoclast in contact with the bone surface, where H+ ions and acid hydrolases are secreted into the resorption lacuna. Its deep folds greatly increase the surface area available for resorption.'),
('615c79ff-e382-5e6c-ae8e-bc8a1993940d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The clear (sealing) zone of an osteoclast is:', '["organelle-free cytoplasm that seals the resorption pit","The Golgi region","A peripheral ring of actin-rich","The nuclear area","The area of active enzyme secretion"]'::jsonb, 'A peripheral ring of actin-rich', 'The clear zone is a peripheral ring of actin-rich cytoplasm, devoid of organelles, that tightly seals the osteoclast to the bone surface and confines the resorbing compartment beneath the ruffled border. It is not the site of enzyme secretion.'),
('15663a65-ad5c-565e-9bb8-210371cf75b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Carbonic anhydrase II in osteoclasts:', '["Provides H+ for acidification of the resorption lacuna (by converting CO2+H2O to H2CO3)","Inhibits bone resorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activates MMPs mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulates osteoclast fusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dissolves organic matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Provides H+ for acidification of the resorption lacuna (by converting CO2+H2O to H2CO3)', 'Carbonic anhydrase II catalyzes CO2 and H2O into carbonic acid, supplying the protons that the vacuolar H+-ATPase pumps into the resorption lacuna to dissolve hydroxyapatite. Genetic deficiency of this enzyme therefore impairs bone resorption and causes osteopetrosis.'),
('8535297c-9e13-519e-9564-e4c30fc710d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The proton pump (vacuolar H+-ATPase) in osteoclasts is located on the:', '["Cell body membrane","Basolateral membrane","Ruffled border membrane","Apical membrane","Sealing zone membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Ruffled border membrane', 'The vacuolar H+-ATPase is densely inserted into the ruffled border membrane, where it pumps H+ into the resorption lacuna to acidify it. The sealing zone and basolateral membrane do not carry this pump.'),
('7e74d7f7-ddac-52d3-a74f-e3d0265e47d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Chloride channels in osteoclasts:', '["None of these resulting in decreased functional activity","Maintain electroneutrality during H+ pumping (Cl- follows H+)","All of these are correct mediated by specific receptor activation","Provide Cl- for HCl production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secrete Cl- into the lacuna leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maintain electroneutrality during H+ pumping (Cl- follows H+)', 'Chloride channels on the ruffled border allow Cl- to follow the pumped H+ into the lacuna, preserving electrical neutrality while the compartment is acidified. This chloride movement with the protons maintains the charge balance needed for continued acid secretion.'),
('a07fa55e-a0fe-5136-b67b-c9683b5d5eab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteoclast dysfunction in osteopetrosis is often due to mutations in:', '["RANKL a resulting in decreased functional activity","Cathepsin K accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TCIRG1 (vacuolar H+-ATPase subunit)","MMP-9 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carbonic anhydrase II only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TCIRG1 (vacuolar H+-ATPase subunit)', 'Mutations in TCIRG1, a subunit of the vacuolar H+-ATPase, are the most common genetic cause of osteopetrosis, because the defective proton pump prevents acidification and bone resorption. Cathepsin K mutations cause pycnodysostosis, and RANKL defects are rarer causes.'),
('4cafbdd9-6f9d-511e-a601-bfd0b2c23238'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Cathepsin K is a:', '["Matrix metalloproteinase resulting in decreased functional activity","Acid phosphatase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elastase mediated by specific receptor activation","Cysteine protease secreted by osteoclasts that degrades type I collagen","Serine protease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cysteine protease secreted by osteoclasts that degrades type I collagen', 'Cathepsin K is a cysteine protease released by osteoclasts into the resorption lacuna, where it degrades type I collagen of the organic bone matrix. It is not a matrix metalloproteinase, serine protease, or acid phosphatase.'),
('146f773d-673e-5f58-bc3f-ef8d864b53f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Pycnodysostosis (Toulouse-Lautrec syndrome) is due to mutations in:', '["OPG","TCIRG1 a","Cathepsin K (CTSK)","RANKL","Carbonic anhydrase II"]'::jsonb, 'Cathepsin K (CTSK)', 'Pycnodysostosis results from loss-of-function mutations in the CTSK gene encoding cathepsin K, so osteoclasts cannot degrade the organic bone matrix even if the mineral phase is dissolved. Defects in TCIRG1 cause classic osteopetrosis, not pycnodysostosis.'),
('3ea1df46-0687-5148-9f38-7aeb50b27d91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'RANK (receptor activator of nuclear factor-κB) is expressed on:', '["Chondrocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteoclast precursors and mature osteoclasts","Osteoblasts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibroblasts a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoclast precursors and mature osteoclasts', 'RANK is the signaling receptor borne by osteoclast precursors and mature osteoclasts; when engaged by RANKL it triggers osteoclast differentiation and activation. Osteoblasts instead express RANKL, the ligand for this receptor.'),
('5fc0b06a-30df-5b4d-9912-a80edd81260a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'RANKL is expressed by:', '["Osteoclasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages a mediated by specific receptor activation","Chondrocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Osteoblasts and activated T-lymphocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoblasts and activated T-lymphocytes', 'RANKL is produced by osteoblasts (and marrow stromal cells) and by activated T-lymphocytes, and it binds RANK on osteoclast precursors to drive their maturation. Osteoclasts themselves bear RANK rather than producing RANKL.'),
('46efd5e3-7c94-5d3d-abe2-d3125e06fe6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'OPG (osteoprotegerin) is a soluble decoy receptor that:', '["Inhibits osteoblast formation","Increases bone resorption","Binds RANKL and prevents it from activating RANK","Activates RANK","Stimulates osteoclast formation"]'::jsonb, 'Binds RANKL and prevents it from activating RANK', 'Osteoprotegerin acts as a soluble decoy receptor that captures RANKL before it can bind RANK on osteoclast precursors, thereby suppressing osteoclast formation and bone resorption. It therefore opposes, rather than drives, resorption.'),
('45fda37d-44f6-5bc4-b20e-0ce60ff927d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The RANKL/OPG ratio determines:', '["Net bone formation resulting in decreased functional activity","Net bone resorption (higher RANKL → more osteoclast activity)","Chondrocyte turnover mediated by specific receptor activation","Osteoblast activity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Collagen synthesis a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Net bone resorption (higher RANKL → more osteoclast activity)', 'RANKL promotes osteoclast differentiation and activation, while OPG acts as a decoy receptor that inhibits RANKL. A high RANKL/OPG ratio therefore tips bone remodeling toward resorption, not formation, chondrocyte, or osteoblast activity.'),
('cd36d765-eb0a-5459-9f11-696d56e1bbbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'PTH stimulates bone resorption by:', '["Increasing calcium excretion a resulting in decreased functional activity","Activating cathepsin K accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibiting OPG mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing RANKL expression on osteoblasts (indirectly activating osteoclasts)","Directly binding osteoclast receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing RANKL expression on osteoblasts (indirectly activating osteoclasts)', 'Osteoclasts lack abundant PTH receptors, so PTH acts mainly on osteoblasts to upregulate RANKL. The increased RANKL then recruits and activates osteoclasts, driving resorption indirectly.'),
('6d643adb-7e9d-5adb-bb3c-5aeb5362abdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Calcitonin inhibits bone resorption by:', '["Increasing RANKL expression resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulating osteoblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreasing OPG expression mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing calcium excretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Directly binding to calcitonin receptors on osteoclasts and inhibiting their activity"]'::jsonb, 'Directly binding to calcitonin receptors on osteoclasts and inhibiting their activity', 'Osteoclasts carry calcitonin receptors, and calcitonin directly suppresses their resorptive activity. It does not act through RANKL, osteoblasts, or OPG, and does not promote calcium excretion.'),
('c6d26d38-3efe-5e55-9510-bd63367a9506'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Glucocorticoids (steroids) cause bone loss by:', '["Increasing calcium absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Increasing OPG expression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibiting osteoblast activity and promoting osteoclast survival","Inhibiting RANKL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulating osteoblast proliferation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting osteoblast activity and promoting osteoclast survival', 'Glucocorticoids suppress osteoblast function and matrix formation while prolonging osteoclast survival, shifting remodeling toward net bone loss. They do not increase calcium absorption, inhibit RANKL, or stimulate osteoblasts.'),
('e037f12a-1008-5dab-ba6c-431925589108'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Estrogen protects against bone loss by:', '["Promoting bone resorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Increasing RANKL expression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulating osteoclasts mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting OPG without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Promoting osteoclast apoptosis and increasing OPG expression"]'::jsonb, 'Promoting osteoclast apoptosis and increasing OPG expression', 'Estrogen reduces osteoclast numbers by inducing their apoptosis and by raising OPG, which neutralizes RANKL. These effects dampen resorption and help preserve bone mass.'),
('6500d069-abde-5502-ae2e-4aacbd268b9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Mechanical loading of bone:', '["Promotes bone resorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulates bone formation via osteocyte mechanosensing","Has effect mediated by specific receptor activation","Only increases bone resorption a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibits bone formation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates bone formation via osteocyte mechanosensing', 'Osteocytes detect mechanical strain and signal to coordinate bone formation on loaded surfaces. The result is increased osteoblastic activity, not resorption or inhibition of formation.'),
('f2a0e634-0aa3-5fc6-994d-f86972a9e9df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Sclerostin is produced by:', '["Fibroblasts","Chondrocytes","Osteocytes","Osteoblasts","Osteoclasts"]'::jsonb, 'Osteocytes', 'Sclerostin is secreted by mature osteocytes embedded in bone matrix, where it inhibits osteoblast activity via the Wnt pathway. It is not produced by fibroblasts, chondrocytes, or osteoclasts.'),
('0b8a1765-5b57-5105-bdd6-084ce37b570b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Fibroblast growth factor (FGF) and transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-β) are:', '["Enzymes for mineralization resulting in decreased functional activity","Proteoglycans accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collagen types mediated by specific receptor activation","Growth factors stored in the bone ECM (released during resorption)","Transcription factors a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Growth factors stored in the bone ECM (released during resorption)', 'FGF, TGF-β, IGF-1, and BMPs are sequestered within the bone matrix and released during osteoclastic resorption. They are signaling proteins, not enzymes, proteoglycans, collagen types, or transcription factors.'),
('f1f85f72-449e-59a7-bf07-0f98c6bcdbd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Bone morphogenetic proteins (BMPs) are members of the:', '["TGF-β superfamily","EGF family a","FGF family","Wnt family","Hedgehog family"]'::jsonb, 'TGF-β superfamily', 'BMPs share structural and signaling features with TGF-β and are classified within the TGF-β superfamily. They do not belong to the EGF, FGF, Wnt, or Hedgehog families.'),
('c96c7fad-e092-5e2e-b44b-e9f512db82bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The most abundant growth factor in bone matrix is:', '["TGF-β (transforming growth factor-beta)","FGF-2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IGF-1 mediated by specific receptor activation","BMP-2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PDGF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TGF-β (transforming growth factor-beta)', 'TGF-β is the most plentiful growth factor stored in bone matrix, exceeding FGF-2, IGF-1, BMP-2, and PDGF in abundance. It is released during remodeling to couple resorption with formation.'),
('b068a035-4df7-59e4-b2f7-9acde575ed96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Cartilage growth factors include:', '["Wnt only","TGF-β","FGF","BMPs only","and IGF-1 (stimulate chondrocyte proliferation and matrix synthesis)"]'::jsonb, 'FGF', 'FGF promotes chondrocyte proliferation and matrix production in cartilage. Although TGF-β, BMPs, and IGF-1 also act on cartilage, among the listed choices FGF is one of the recognized cartilage growth factors.'),
('55d19f5d-c119-51e8-8df4-a35a483b84d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Indian hedgehog (Ihh) is expressed by:', '["Osteoclasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteoblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Articular chondrocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Periosteal cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pre-hypertrophic and hypertrophic chondrocytes in the growth plate"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-hypertrophic and hypertrophic chondrocytes in the growth plate', 'Ihh is made by pre-hypertrophic and hypertrophic growth plate chondrocytes, where it helps regulate the PTHrP feedback loop. It is not produced by osteoclasts, osteoblasts, articular chondrocytes, or periosteal cells.'),
('d9d6bc9b-251b-5bd8-8927-91fe0b35d348'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'PTHrP (parathyroid hormone-related protein) is expressed in the:', '["Osteoclasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Growth plate hypertrophic zone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Periarticular perichondrium (regulates growth plate chondrocyte differentiation)","Bone lining cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Osteoblasts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Periarticular perichondrium (regulates growth plate chondrocyte differentiation)', 'PTHrP is produced in the periarticular perichondrium and keeps proliferating chondrocytes from undergoing premature hypertrophy. It is not expressed by osteoclasts, osteoblasts, or the hypertrophic zone.'),
('20e91019-2e6e-5f92-8a2c-91cb29af15c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The Ihh-PTHrP feedback loop regulates:', '["The rate of chondrocyte hypertrophy and thus the speed of longitudinal bone growth","The number of osteoclasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The width of the bone mediated by specific receptor activation","The blood supply to bone a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mineralization of bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rate of chondrocyte hypertrophy and thus the speed of longitudinal bone growth', 'Ihh and PTHrP form a negative feedback loop that controls how fast growth plate chondrocytes become hypertrophic. This timing determines the rate of endochondral bone lengthening, not osteoclast number, width, blood supply, or mineralization.'),
('74c86da9-52d2-591b-8f59-4b1148116407'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The zone of Ranvier is:', '["The Haversian canal resulting in decreased functional activity","A part of the articular cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The periosteal surface mediated by specific receptor activation","A wedge-shaped region of osteoprogenitor cells at the periphery of the growth plate that contributes to bone width","The endosteal surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A wedge-shaped region of osteoprogenitor cells at the periphery of the growth plate that contributes to bone width', 'The zone of Ranvier is a groove of osteoprogenitor cells encircling the epiphyseal growth plate that supplies cells for circumferential (width) growth. It is not the Haversian canal, articular cartilage, or periosteal/endosteal surface.'),
('454634ac-91ee-5a9a-a726-37a5f2415dc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The primary spongiosa of the metaphysis is remodeled into:', '["Secondary spongiosa (lamellar bone with mature trabeculae)","Adipose tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Woven bone only mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary spongiosa (lamellar bone with mature trabeculae)', 'The primary spongiosa is a temporary network of woven bone left behind by mineralizing cartilage; osteoclasts and osteoblasts remodel it into secondary spongiosa with mature lamellar trabeculae. It does not become fat, fibrous tissue, or cartilage.'),
('a0c31311-9475-5cd9-97c4-60081d04f65a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteoblasts are derived from:', '["Fibrocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Hematopoietic stem cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chondrocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Monocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesenchymal stem cells (osteoprogenitors)"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells (osteoprogenitors)', 'Osteoblasts differentiate from mesenchymal stem cells that become committed osteoprogenitors. They do not arise from fibrocytes, hematopoietic stem cells, chondrocytes, or monocytes.'),
('b0f7f73a-3286-58dd-b452-329ecd647e64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'RUNX2 (Cbfa1) is the:', '["Master transcription factor for myocyte differentiation","Osteoclast transcription factor","Master transcription factor for osteoblast differentiation and bone formation","Master transcription factor for adipocyte differentiation","Master transcription factor for chondrocyte differentiation"]'::jsonb, 'Master transcription factor for osteoblast differentiation and bone formation', 'RUNX2 (Cbfa1) is the central transcription factor that drives mesenchymal cells toward the osteoblast lineage and is required for bone formation. It does not direct myocyte, adipocyte, or chondrocyte fates.'),
('660ab8dd-47d1-54b3-9546-12ba3dd968e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osterix (Osx/Sp7) is:', '["A collagen type resulting in decreased functional activity","A protease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A transcription factor downstream of RUNX2 required for osteoblast differentiation","A growth factor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An ECM protein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A transcription factor downstream of RUNX2 required for osteoblast differentiation', 'Osterix is a zinc-finger transcription factor that acts downstream of RUNX2 and is essential for osteoblast maturation. It is not a collagen, protease, growth factor, or matrix protein.'),
('ebbc3f43-ad78-5919-a033-1a3c6a6fe6ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'SOX9 is the:', '["Master transcription factor for osteoblasts","Myogenic regulator","Osteoclast regulator","Master transcription factor for chondrocyte differentiation","Adipocyte transcription factor"]'::jsonb, 'Master transcription factor for chondrocyte differentiation', 'SOX9 is the master transcription factor required for commitment of mesenchymal cells to the chondrocyte lineage and for type II collagen expression. It is not a regulator of osteoblasts, muscle, osteoclasts, or adipocytes.'),
('bc9d0c8c-4e86-59ea-9d96-ccf32ea582d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'PPARγ is the:', '["Master transcription factor for adipocyte differentiation","Myogenic regulator accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteoblast regulator mediated by specific receptor activation","Chondrocyte regulator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuronal regulator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Master transcription factor for adipocyte differentiation', 'PPARγ is the master transcription factor driving adipocyte differentiation from mesenchymal precursors. It does not direct muscle, osteoblast, chondrocyte, or neuronal differentiation.'),
('534f5ece-e933-5ef4-b6c4-3c03ca930258'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'MyoD (myogenic differentiation 1) is a:', '["Master transcription factor for skeletal muscle differentiation","Smooth muscle regulator accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteoblast regulator mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiac muscle regulator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuronal regulator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Master transcription factor for skeletal muscle differentiation', 'MyoD is a basic helix-loop-helix transcription factor that commits cells to the skeletal muscle lineage. It is not a regulator of smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, bone, or neurons.'),
('310b444b-e7b5-5fa8-979c-58005bf5fa3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The periosteum responds to fracture with:', '["Osteoclast activation only resulting in decreased functional activity","response accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Formation of adipose tissue mediated by specific receptor activation","Proliferation of osteoprogenitor cells and formation of external callus (bone and cartilage)","Fibrous scar formation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proliferation of osteoprogenitor cells and formation of external callus (bone and cartilage)', 'The inner cambial layer of the periosteum proliferates after fracture, giving rise to an external callus of woven bone and cartilage that stabilizes the fracture. It does not respond with osteoclast activation, fat, or simple fibrous scarring.'),
('c004650d-e775-5f60-a441-f79e852eae90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteoarthritis (degenerative joint disease) primarily involves:', '["Infection of the joint resulting in decreased functional activity","Destruction of ligaments a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inflammation of the synovium mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Degeneration of articular cartilage with bone remodeling (osteophytes)"]'::jsonb, 'Degeneration of articular cartilage with bone remodeling (osteophytes)', 'Osteoarthritis is a non-inflammatory wear-and-tear process that begins with articular cartilage degeneration and is accompanied by subchondral bone remodeling and osteophyte formation. It is not caused by infection, ligament destruction, synovitis, or CPPD crystals.'),
('a4bbd137-000c-5ec3-9dcd-cbf4298b447d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Rheumatoid arthritis is:', '["An infectious arthritis resulting in decreased functional activity","An autoimmune inflammatory disease of the synovium (synovitis) leading to joint destruction","A crystal-induced arthritis mediated by specific receptor activation","A neuropathic joint disease a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A degenerative joint disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An autoimmune inflammatory disease of the synovium (synovitis) leading to joint destruction', 'Rheumatoid arthritis is a chronic autoimmune disorder marked by inflammatory synovitis that erodes cartilage and bone. It is not infectious, crystal-induced, neuropathic, or primarily degenerative.'),
('a329002f-b987-5a5e-9642-caf75ef0ea35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Gout is characterized by:', '["Deposition of calcium pyrophosphate crystals","Bacterial infection of the joint","Immune complex deposition","Deposition of hydroxyapatite crystals","Deposition of monosodium urate crystals in joints (from hyperuricemia)"]'::jsonb, 'Deposition of monosodium urate crystals in joints (from hyperuricemia)', 'Gout results from hyperuricemia and the deposition of monosodium urate crystals in joints, triggering acute inflammation. It is distinct from CPPD (pseudogout), hydroxyapatite deposition, infection, and immune complex disease.'),
('10598229-472d-574d-b457-dcde015abe4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Pseudogout (chondrocalcinosis) is due to:', '["Lipid deposition resulting in decreased functional activity","Hydroxyapatite deposition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urate crystal deposition mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate (CPPD) crystal deposition","Oxalate deposition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate (CPPD) crystal deposition', 'Pseudogout, also called chondrocalcinosis, is caused by deposition of calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals in cartilage and joints. It is not due to lipids, hydroxyapatite, urate, or oxalate.'),
('9b627c71-3ad2-566c-a01c-f0ca8a2db88b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Ankylosing spondylitis is a seronegative spondyloarthropathy that primarily affects the:', '["Shoulders and elbows","Sacroiliac joints and spine","Knees and hips","Small joints of the hands","Temporomandibular joint"]'::jsonb, 'Sacroiliac joints and spine', 'Ankylosing spondylitis characteristically involves the sacroiliac joints and axial skeleton, with progressive ossification and eventual spinal fusion. It spares the small hand joints and peripheral large joints early on.'),
('80496834-ab2f-5929-a624-2221d2300a23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The major cell of cartilage is the:', '["Osteoclast","Chondrocyte","Fibroblast","Adipocyte","Osteoblast"]'::jsonb, 'Chondrocyte', 'Chondrocytes are the resident cells of cartilage, responsible for producing and maintaining its matrix. Osteoclasts, fibroblasts, adipocytes, and osteoblasts belong to other tissues.'),
('f6961de2-7315-5ce9-a87a-c21d9eaa8588'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Chondroblasts are derived from:', '["Monocytes","Perichondrial cells (both sources are correct)","Mesenchymal stem cells (chondroprogenitors)","Fibrocytes","Hematopoietic stem cells"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells (chondroprogenitors)', 'Chondroblasts differentiate from mesenchymal stem cells that become committed chondroprogenitors. They do not arise from monocytes, fibrocytes, or hematopoietic stem cells.'),
('439aed4d-8100-55aa-b84f-2234952d6b04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The lacuna is the space occupied by a:', '["Osteoclast resulting in decreased functional activity","Chondrocyte or osteocyte (in cartilage/bone)","Macrophage mediated by specific receptor activation","Adipocyte without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibroblast leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chondrocyte or osteocyte (in cartilage/bone)', 'A lacuna is the matrix cavity that houses a chondrocyte in cartilage or an osteocyte in bone. Osteoclasts, macrophages, adipocytes, and fibroblasts do not reside in lacunae.'),
('cbde8bc1-78b2-54ca-9227-254d651478ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The territorial matrix of cartilage is:', '["The basophilic (metachromatic) ECM immediately surrounding chondrocyte lacunae","The calcified zone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rich in type I collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","The outer fibrous layer of the perichondrium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The interterritorial matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The basophilic (metachromatic) ECM immediately surrounding chondrocyte lacunae', 'The territorial matrix is the metachromatic capsule of proteoglycan-rich matrix immediately around each lacuna. It differs from the interterritorial matrix, the calcified zone, and the fibrous perichondrium.'),
('c2ae3315-e155-511b-bccd-12b725f8e00b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Articular cartilage is:', '["Hyaline cartilage covering joint surfaces (lacks perichondrium)","Bone tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elastic cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrocartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrous tissue a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaline cartilage covering joint surfaces (lacks perichondrium)', 'Articular cartilage is hyaline cartilage that covers joint surfaces and, because its superficial aspect faces the joint cavity, it lacks a perichondrium. It is not bone, elastic cartilage, fibrocartilage, or fibrous tissue.'),
('c30cebf7-63a7-5eb2-9f9c-de49e328c8eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The structure of articular cartilage is organized into zones:', '["Superficial","and calcified","deep (radial)","middle (transitional)","Superficial (tangential)"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial (tangential)', 'The most superficial layer of articular cartilage is the tangential zone, with flattened chondrocytes and collagen fibers parallel to the surface. It lies above the transitional, radial, and calcified zones.'),
('5ff62365-fed8-5982-a60a-4dac5b37945c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The tidemark in articular cartilage is:', '["The boundary with the bone resulting in decreased functional activity","The boundary between the deep (radial) zone and the calcified cartilage zone","The synovial reflection mediated by specific receptor activation","The perichondrial junction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The superficial surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The boundary between the deep (radial) zone and the calcified cartilage zone', 'The tidemark is the basophilic line separating the uncalcified radial zone from the zone of calcified cartilage. It is not a junction with bone, synovium, perichondrium, or the superficial surface.'),
('9aea11c0-1247-5f42-b6e2-121350932b2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The superficial zone of articular cartilage provides:', '["Protection against infection a","Resistance to compression","Low friction and resistance to shear forces","Shock absorption","Nutrient supply"]'::jsonb, 'Low friction and resistance to shear forces', 'The tangential superficial zone, with its parallel collagen fibers and flattened chondrocytes, gives the articular surface a smooth low-friction bearing resistant to shear. Resistance to compression and shock absorption are functions of the deeper zones.'),
('5f263ca4-4382-572f-8edf-68f89b777542'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The deep (radial) zone of articular cartilage has:', '["Flattened chondrocytes parallel to surface resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chondrocytes arranged in columns perpendicular to the surface","Elastic fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","chondrocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chondrocytes arranged in columns perpendicular to the surface', 'In the radial zone, collagen fibers and chondrocytes align in columns perpendicular to the articular surface, helping resist compressive loads. This zone contains no blood vessels and does not have flattened parallel chondrocytes.'),
('3e29a683-70b7-5dd2-b0a4-89a0b1d2733b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Cartilage degeneration in osteoarthritis begins in the:', '["Tidemark resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcified zone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subchondral bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Superficial zone (fibrillation and wear)","Deep zone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial zone (fibrillation and wear)', 'Osteoarthritis initially damages the superficial tangential zone, producing surface fibrillation and wear that worsens with load. Deeper structures such as the tidemark, calcified zone, and subchondral bone are affected later.'),
('4e2881ac-4008-5817-91d4-bea8336fac29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The blood supply of cartilage is:', '["From the perichondrium only resulting in decreased functional activity","From the synovium only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None (avascular). Cartilage receives nutrients via diffusion","From the subchondral bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From blood vessels within the matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'None (avascular). Cartilage receives nutrients via diffusion', 'Cartilage is avascular; chondrocytes obtain nutrients and oxygen by diffusion from synovial fluid and, where present, the perichondrium. There are no blood vessels inside the matrix.'),
('d17bf091-785f-50d5-b909-700097273a6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Synovium is the:', '["The periosteal covering of the joint resulting in decreased functional activity","The joint fluid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The articular disc mediated by specific receptor activation","Outer covering of the cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inner lining of the joint capsule (2 layers: intima and subintima)"]'::jsonb, 'Inner lining of the joint capsule (2 layers: intima and subintima)', 'The synovium is the specialized inner lining of the joint capsule, composed of an intima of synoviocytes over a vascular subintima. It is not periosteum, joint fluid, an articular disc, or a cartilage covering.'),
('7778d1ce-34f0-5bb0-9cf7-d9b53444e103'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Type A synoviocytes are:', '["Fibroblast-like cells (produce hyaluronic acid)","Chondrocytes","Macrophage-like cells (phagocytic)","Osteoclasts","Adipocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Macrophage-like cells (phagocytic)', 'Type A synoviocytes are macrophage-like cells derived from the monocyte lineage that phagocytose debris within the joint cavity. Fibroblast-like cells producing hyaluronic acid are type B synoviocytes.'),
('9b19dc05-989f-5dbf-8543-88d92d85c45d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Type B synoviocytes are:', '["Adipocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibroblast-like cells that produce hyaluronic acid and lubricin (joint lubrication)","Osteoblasts a mediated by specific receptor activation","Macrophage-like cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chondrocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblast-like cells that produce hyaluronic acid and lubricin (joint lubrication)', 'Type B synoviocytes are fibroblast-like secretory cells that manufacture hyaluronic acid and lubricin, the molecules responsible for joint lubrication. Macrophage-like phagocytes are the separate type A population.'),
('ef6d4eeb-1882-58a5-8378-c5f5da727591'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Synovial fluid is:', '["Secreted by chondrocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymph accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebrospinal fluid mediated by specific receptor activation","A dialysate of plasma enriched with hyaluronic acid (lubricant)","A pure ultrafiltrate of plasma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A dialysate of plasma enriched with hyaluronic acid (lubricant)', 'Synovial fluid is an ultrafiltrate of plasma equilibrated with the joint plus hyaluronic acid and lubricin added by synoviocytes. It is not secreted by chondrocytes, lymph, or CSF, and it is more than a pure plasma filtrate.'),
('759a36fc-aea2-54f9-ac57-883703146e4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The menisci (semilunar cartilages) of the knee are:', '["Elastic cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyaline cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Synovial tissue a mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrocartilage (Type I and II collagen)","Bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrocartilage (Type I and II collagen)', 'Knee menisci are fibrocartilage, containing both type I and type II collagen arranged to resist compression and shear. They are not hyaline or elastic cartilage, synovial tissue, or bone.'),
('9b6fa247-5e49-5154-9b69-155d2d7a7335'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Intervertebral discs are composed of:', '["Hyaline cartilage only resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Annulus fibrosus (fibrocartilage) and nucleus pulposus (mucoid ECM with notochordal cells)","Elastic cartilage only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Synovial tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Annulus fibrosus (fibrocartilage) and nucleus pulposus (mucoid ECM with notochordal cells)', 'Each intervertebral disc consists of an outer annulus fibrosus of fibrocartilage and an inner nucleus pulposus of mucoid gelatinous material containing remnants of the notochord. It is not composed solely of hyaline or elastic cartilage.'),
('4fc93885-0f01-5e94-87cf-253a067a4c96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The nucleus pulposus may become less hydrated with age due to:', '["Fibrosis a resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcification accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased GAG synthesis mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of proteoglycans and decreased water content","Increased collagen synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of proteoglycans and decreased water content', 'Because the nucleus pulposus holds water largely through its proteoglycans, age-related loss of these molecules reduces water content and hydration. Increased GAG or collagen synthesis would tend to preserve or increase hydration.'),
('d62e8d53-c999-5121-9757-11c2800aac10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Schmorl nodes are:', '["Osteophytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Articular cartilage defects accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterolateral disc herniation mediated by specific receptor activation","Herniation of nucleus pulposus into the vertebral body (through the endplate)","Vertebral fractures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Herniation of nucleus pulposus into the vertebral body (through the endplate)', 'A Schmorl node is a vertical herniation of the nucleus pulposus through the endplate into the vertebral body. It differs from posterolateral disc herniation, osteophytes, cartilage defects, and fractures.'),
('0121366e-4e5b-5c60-b88b-c44c3958b626'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Ossification of the intervertebral disc in ankylosing spondylitis leads to:', '["Osteophytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Syndesmophytes (bony bridges) joining vertebrae (bamboo spine)","Schmorl nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Spondylolisthesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Disc herniation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Syndesmophytes (bony bridges) joining vertebrae (bamboo spine)', 'In ankylosing spondylitis, ossification of the annulus fibrosus and spinal ligaments produces syndesmophytes that bridge vertebrae, creating the ''bamboo spine''. Osteophytes arise at joint margins in osteoarthritis, not here.'),
('5b04eb80-b37d-553e-b6a9-e9157963ca2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The sacroiliac joint is a:', '["Condylar joint resulting in decreased functional activity","Symphysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part synovial (anterior) and part fibrous (posterior) joint","Hinge joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ball and socket joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Part synovial (anterior) and part fibrous (posterior) joint', 'The sacroiliac joint is transitional: its anterior portion is a true synovial joint while the posterior portion is fibrous and reinforced by strong ligaments. It is not a symphysis, condylar, hinge, or ball-and-socket joint.'),
('c80ceb41-9b0f-55ce-a2fe-9d4487c7031c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The pubic symphysis is a:', '["Fibrous joint resulting in decreased functional activity","Sellar joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cartilaginous joint (secondary cartilaginous/symphysis) with a fibrocartilage disc","Pivot joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Freely movable synovial joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilaginous joint (secondary cartilaginous/symphysis) with a fibrocartilage disc', 'The pubic symphysis is a secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis) in which the two bones are joined by a fibrocartilage disc, permitting slight movement. It is neither fibrous, synovial, sellar, nor pivot.'),
('b46efe2d-641c-5a1d-9bf9-cc691dacbffa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Temporomandibular joint (TMJ) has a:', '["Synovial disc resulting in decreased functional activity","disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyaline cartilage disc mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrocartilage articular disc dividing the joint into upper and lower compartments","Hyaline cartilage covering leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrocartilage articular disc dividing the joint into upper and lower compartments', 'The TMJ contains a fibrocartilaginous articular disc that divides the joint cavity into upper and lower compartments, permitting both gliding and hinge movements. The disc is fibrocartilage rather than hyaline cartilage, a synovial fold, or a simple covering.'),
('e23f0f21-448e-56c3-803a-d4db56986db4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The periodontal ligament is a:', '["Dense connective tissue attaching the tooth root to the alveolar bone (suspended via Sharpey fibers)","Bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyaline cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Elastic cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epithelial tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dense connective tissue attaching the tooth root to the alveolar bone (suspended via Sharpey fibers)', 'The periodontal ligament is dense fibrous connective tissue whose collagen bundles (Sharpey fibers) anchor the tooth root to the alveolar bone and allow slight physiologic movement. Bone, cartilage, and epithelium do not form this suspensory ligament.'),
('4c7fe4e3-3b6a-5f4f-bf27-3f8d35efef09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Heberden nodes are osteophytes at the:', '["Hips resulting in decreased functional activity","Metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Knees mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints of the fingers","Proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints of the fingers', 'Heberden nodes are osteophytes that form at the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints of the fingers in osteoarthritis. Osteophytes at the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints are instead called Bouchard nodes; hips, knees, and MCP joints are not the site of these nodes.'),
('1905bf5c-39f2-596a-b768-e3a9e44b60ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Bouchard nodes are osteophytes at the:', '["Elbows resulting in decreased functional activity","Distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Wrists mediated by specific receptor activation","Knees without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints of the fingers"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints of the fingers', 'Bouchard nodes are osteophytes at the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints of the fingers, whereas Heberden nodes affect the DIP joints. Elbow, wrist, and knee joints are not typical sites for these nodes.'),
('1ec7ddae-812b-54d9-bf4a-6998d5beb642'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Pseudocyst (subchondral cyst) in OA is a:', '["Chondral defect resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fluid-filled cavity in the subchondral bone due to increased intra-articular pressure","Synovial herniation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","True epithelial cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid-filled cavity in the subchondral bone due to increased intra-articular pressure', 'A subchondral pseudocyst in osteoarthritis forms when increased intra-articular pressure drives joint fluid through fissured articular cartilage into the subchondral bone, producing a fluid-filled cavity. It is neither a true epithelial cyst, a neoplasm, nor a herniation of synovium.'),
('164bbb47-f493-58da-893d-63a8bde60024'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteophyte formation in OA is:', '["Fracture callus resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone necrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone resorption mediated by specific receptor activation","New bone growth at joint margins (attempting to stabilize the joint)","Osteoporosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'New bone growth at joint margins (attempting to stabilize the joint)', 'Osteophytes are new bone growth at joint margins in osteoarthritis, representing an attempted stabilization of an unstable joint. They reflect active bone formation rather than resorption, necrosis, fracture callus, or generalized osteoporosis.'),
('3202da0a-48a9-5259-8567-ac330f3c3403'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Rheumatoid nodules are:', '["Bony nodules at joints","Subcutaneous nodules with central necrosis","Osteophytes","and fibrosis (extracapsular)","palisading histiocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneous nodules with central necrosis', 'Rheumatoid nodules are subcutaneous nodules over pressure points that show central fibrinoid necrosis surrounded by palisading histiocytes and fibrosis. They are not bony nodules or osteophytes; the palisading cells and extracapsular fibrosis are features within the nodule, not the answer itself.'),
('4aba515c-c92c-5f36-b5fb-509184eb1c28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Gouty tophi are:', '["Calcium deposits resulting in decreased functional activity","Deposits of cholesterol accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrous nodules mediated by specific receptor activation","Synovial cysts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deposits of monosodium urate crystals surrounded by giant cells and fibrosis (chronic inflammation)"]'::jsonb, 'Deposits of monosodium urate crystals surrounded by giant cells and fibrosis (chronic inflammation)', 'Gouty tophi consist of deposits of monosodium urate crystals surrounded by giant cells and chronic inflammatory fibrosis. They are not calcium or cholesterol deposits, plain fibrous nodules, or synovial cysts.'),
('f284596c-5f2f-5b65-826c-e2e25cd005bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The cartilage of the external ear is:', '["Elastic cartilage","Fibrous tissue","Hyaline cartilage","Bone","Fibrocartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Elastic cartilage', 'The cartilage of the external (auricular) ear is elastic cartilage, which contains abundant elastic fibers that keep the pinna flexible and resilient. Hyaline cartilage and fibrocartilage lack the elastic fiber network that gives the ear its springiness.'),
('df97fe92-686b-5556-804b-3e4e82937d42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The epiglottis is composed of:', '["Fibrocartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Bone","Adipose tissue","Elastic cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Elastic cartilage', 'The epiglottis is elastic cartilage, providing flexible support that helps close the airway during swallowing. Hyaline cartilage lines the trachea and joints, and fibrocartilage is found at the intervertebral discs.'),
('8f679d02-bb3f-5a1f-afdf-35581b7550ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The costal cartilages (connecting ribs to sternum) are:', '["Fibrous tissue","Bone","Hyaline cartilage","Elastic cartilage","Fibrocartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaline cartilage', 'The costal cartilages connecting the ribs to the sternum are hyaline cartilage, which provides firm but slightly flexible support and smooth joint surfaces. Elastic cartilage and fibrocartilage occupy different sites, such as the ear and intervertebral discs.'),
('6beff94c-3496-543c-a1e1-235066b2bdb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The nasal septum and alar cartilages are:', '["Hyaline cartilage only resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastic cartilage only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrocartilage only mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyaline cartilage (septum) and elastic cartilage (alar)","Bone only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaline cartilage (septum) and elastic cartilage (alar)', 'The nasal septum is hyaline cartilage, whereas the alar cartilages of the nasal tip are elastic cartilage; the two regions therefore have mixed cartilage types. Options naming a single cartilage type ignore this anatomic difference, and bone is not part of the cartilaginous nasal framework.'),
('cda00e6e-6e41-5d18-840b-bc234c904996'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The tracheal rings are:', '["Bone","Calcified cartilage","Elastic cartilage","Hyaline cartilage (C-shaped rings)","Fibrocartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaline cartilage (C-shaped rings)', 'The trachea is supported by C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage, whose open posterior ends are bridged by the trachealis muscle. Elastic cartilage and fibrocartilage do not form the airway rings, and normal tracheal cartilage is not calcified bone.'),
('dccc927a-1266-5181-8f29-4fe428b811c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The bronchial walls contain:', '["Fibrocartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Irregular plates of hyaline cartilage (in larger bronchi)","cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elastic cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irregular plates of hyaline cartilage (in larger bronchi)', 'The larger bronchi are supported by irregular plates of hyaline cartilage; as branching proceeds, cartilage is gradually replaced by smooth muscle. Elastic cartilage, fibrocartilage, and bone are not normal components of bronchial walls.'),
('7a345b0a-50e0-56bd-af61-5b7ec35a292a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The menisci of the knee are:', '["Bony plates","Synovial folds","Crescent-shaped fibrocartilage structures","Ring-shaped elastic cartilage","Circular hyaline cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Crescent-shaped fibrocartilage structures', 'The medial and lateral menisci are crescent-shaped fibrocartilaginous structures that deepen the tibial surface and cushion the knee joint. They are not bony plates, synovial folds, or rings of elastic or hyaline cartilage.'),
('1686fb35-7a1a-5e60-8793-37188c9e5425'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The triangular fibrocartilage complex (TFCC) is found in the:', '["Knee joint resulting in decreased functional activity","Wrist joint (between ulna and carpal bones)","Ankle joint mediated by specific receptor activation","Elbow joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shoulder joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wrist joint (between ulna and carpal bones)', 'The triangular fibrocartilage complex (TFCC) is located at the wrist joint, suspending the ulnar side of the carpus from the ulna and stabilizing the distal radioulnar articulation. It does not occur in the knee, ankle, elbow, or shoulder.'),
('1e413920-1a02-53e8-bdc9-e7ed19b6bc0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The glenoid labrum of the shoulder is:', '["An articular disc resulting in decreased functional activity","A hyaline cartilage ring accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ligament a mediated by specific receptor activation","A fibrocartilage ring deepening the glenoid cavity","A meniscus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrocartilage ring deepening the glenoid cavity', 'The glenoid labrum is a ring of fibrocartilage attached around the glenoid cavity that deepens the socket and stabilizes the humeral head. It is not an articular disc, a meniscus, or a ring of hyaline cartilage.'),
('455f53ae-9ea0-5d3d-933f-666ed7529d9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The acetabular labrum of the hip is:', '["A hyaline cartilage ring resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone ring accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fibrocartilage ring deepening the acetabulum","Ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meniscoid structure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrocartilage ring deepening the acetabulum', 'The acetabular labrum is a fibrocartilaginous rim that deepens the acetabulum and grasps the femoral head, improving hip stability and distributing load. It is not hyaline cartilage, bone, a ligament, or a meniscoid structure.'),
('d50ea5a3-c8f8-5fb0-8848-b7f092a643e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The annulus fibrosus receives its name from:', '["The concentric ring-like arrangement of collagen lamellae","Its blood supply accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Its cell content mediated by specific receptor activation","Its proteoglycan content without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Its location in the spine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The concentric ring-like arrangement of collagen lamellae', 'The annulus fibrosus is named for the concentric, ring-like arrangement of its collagen lamellae that encircle the nucleus pulposus, resembling annual growth rings. The name reflects this architecture rather than blood supply, cellular composition, proteoglycans, or spinal location.'),
('cbb2f1a5-aba4-58b9-8005-6b595bfba30e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The sternoclavicular (SC) joint contains:', '["A fibrocartilage articular disc (divides joint into two compartments)","Meniscus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","disc mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyaline cartilage only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elastic cartilage disc leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrocartilage articular disc (divides joint into two compartments)', 'The sternoclavicular joint contains a fibrocartilaginous articular disc that divides the joint into two compartments, improving congruence and helping prevent dislocation. It is not a meniscus, hyaline cartilage, or elastic cartilage disc.'),
('02927d8d-8fed-5155-934a-aabd011415c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The acromioclavicular (AC) joint contains:', '["A fibrocartilage meniscoid disc (incomplete)","disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyaline cartilage only mediated by specific receptor activation","A complete disc without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elastic cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrocartilage meniscoid disc (incomplete)', 'The acromioclavicular joint typically contains an incomplete fibrocartilaginous (meniscoid) disc that degenerates with age and is often absent in older adults. It is neither hyaline cartilage, elastic cartilage, nor a complete disc.'),
('7b37f0c2-bdd9-5f75-86ef-32b7cc562a09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The temporomandibular joint disc is:', '["Hyaline cartilage","Biconcave fibrocartilage","Bone","Dense fibrous tissue","Elastic cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Biconcave fibrocartilage', 'The TMJ disc is a biconcave fibrocartilaginous plate that divides the joint into upper and lower compartments, cushioning the condyle. It is not hyaline or elastic cartilage, bone, or plain dense fibrous tissue.'),
('c674af12-3e9f-5e60-97b3-9a37aadc4bcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE) is a disorder of the:', '["Femoral head resulting in decreased functional activity","Knee growth plate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acetabulum mediated by specific receptor activation","Proximal femoral growth plate (epiphysis slips off)","Femoral neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal femoral growth plate (epiphysis slips off)', 'In slipped capital femoral epiphysis the proximal femoral epiphysis slips posteriorly and inferiorly through the growth plate, typically in obese adolescents. The disorder originates at the proximal femoral physis, not the knee growth plate, the acetabulum, or the femoral neck.'),
('2d2d0590-b647-5673-98df-36cb017cdc0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is:', '["Avascular necrosis of the femoral head (idiopathic childhood hip disorder)","An infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An autoimmune disease mediated by specific receptor activation","A slipped growth plate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fracture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Avascular necrosis of the femoral head (idiopathic childhood hip disorder)', 'Legg-Calve-Perthes disease is idiopathic avascular (ischemic) necrosis of the femoral head in children, causing hip pain and limp. It is not an infection, autoimmune disease, slipped epiphysis, or fracture.'),
('9bcc4ea5-37dd-57cc-8675-ea26b9c650aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Bone infarction (avascular necrosis) can occur with:', '["alcohol abuse","Vitamin D deficiency","and trauma","sickle cell disease","Corticosteroid use"]'::jsonb, 'Corticosteroid use', 'Corticosteroid use is a well-recognized cause of avascular necrosis, likely through marrow fat accumulation and microvascular occlusion; alcohol abuse, trauma, and sickle cell disease are also recognized causes. Vitamin D deficiency, by contrast, disrupts bone mineralization rather than producing bone infarction.'),
('9b64270c-7891-5b50-98ef-4fb2f4b9749c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Fibrous dysplasia of bone is characterized by:', '["Fibrous tissue replacing normal bone with irregular (Chinese-character) woven bone trabeculae","Bone cysts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cartilaginous tumors mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal bone architecture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Osteoporosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous tissue replacing normal bone with irregular (Chinese-character) woven bone trabeculae', 'Fibrous dysplasia is characterized by fibrous tissue replacing normal bone, with irregular curved woven bone trabeculae that form a Chinese-character pattern. Normal architecture is lost, and the lesion is not a cyst, cartilaginous tumor, or generalized osteoporosis.'),
('50a66111-1a30-540d-8f07-ee80a47dc5d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Paget disease of bone histology shows:', '["Normal lamellar bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Woven bone only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A mosaic pattern of lamellar bone (cement lines creating a jigsaw pattern)","Fibrous tissue only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cartilage only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A mosaic pattern of lamellar bone (cement lines creating a jigsaw pattern)', 'Paget disease shows a mosaic or jigsaw pattern of lamellar bone created by prominent cement lines, reflecting disordered and rapid bone resorption and formation. Normal lamellar bone, woven bone alone, or pure fibrous tissue or cartilage do not characterize Paget histology.'),
('edb0c49c-aea0-5c36-9a71-b645e777fdbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteogenesis imperfecta (type I) features:', '["Bone infections","Spinal deformity","Blue sclerae","hearing loss","and multiple fractures with onset in childhood"]'::jsonb, 'Blue sclerae', 'Osteogenesis imperfecta type I is defined by blue sclerae resulting from thin, translucent collagen-deficient sclera, together with fractures and hearing loss. Blue sclerae are a characteristic clinical marker; bone infections and spinal deformity alone do not define the condition.'),
('6d4d9733-cdc0-57b5-acb4-f77809054d69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Dentinogenesis imperfecta is associated with:', '["Ehlers-Danlos syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Alport syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteogenesis imperfecta (due to type I collagen defect)","Fibrous dysplasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Marfan syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osteogenesis imperfecta (due to type I collagen defect)', 'Dentinogenesis imperfecta is associated with osteogenesis imperfecta because both arise from defects in type I collagen, the principal structural collagen of bone and dentin. It is not linked to Ehlers-Danlos, Alport syndrome, fibrous dysplasia, or Marfan syndrome.'),
('648bbadb-1497-5ef3-8c3f-80721ac7935d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteopetrosis (Albers-Schonberg disease) histology shows:', '["Dense","Osteoporosis","sclerotic bone with persistence of calcified cartilage cores (lack of remodeling)","Cartilage only","Woven bone only"]'::jsonb, 'Dense', 'Osteopetrosis (Albers-Schonberg disease) results from defective osteoclast function, leaving dense, sclerotic bone with persistence of calcified cartilage cores and a lack of remodeling. This density is the histologic hallmark, in contrast to osteoporosis, cartilage-only, or woven-bone-only appearances.'),
('75956c6c-9bcf-5443-9786-4814454174e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Rickets histology (growth plate) shows:', '["Normal growth plate","irregular growth plate with disorganized","Thickened","Thin growth plate","hypertrophic chondrocytes (failure of mineralization)"]'::jsonb, 'Thickened', 'In rickets the growth plate is thickened and irregular, with disorganized hypertrophic chondrocytes that fail to mineralize because of vitamin D deficiency. This contrasts with the thin or normal growth plate of the other options.'),
('f0834ad3-218d-50b8-9310-bcef8a821d4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Costochondral beading (rachitic rosary) in rickets is due to:', '["Callus formation resulting in decreased functional activity","Enlargement of the costochondral junctions (hypertrophic uncalcified cartilage)","Synovitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcified masses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone spurs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of the costochondral junctions (hypertrophic uncalcified cartilage)', 'Rachitic rosary results from enlargement of the costochondral junctions caused by hypertrophy of uncalcified cartilage, producing the characteristic bead-like prominence. It is not due to callus, synovitis, calcified masses, or bone spurs.'),
('9d4c0062-99b0-5cbe-b811-b7668a251585'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The brown color of brown tumors in hyperparathyroidism is due to:', '["Increased blood flow resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemorrhage and hemosiderin deposition","Calcification mediated by specific receptor activation","Melanin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrosis a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hemorrhage and hemosiderin deposition', 'Brown tumors in hyperparathyroidism appear brown because of hemorrhage and hemosiderin deposition within the osteoclast-rich fibrous tissue. Increased blood flow, calcification, melanin, and fibrosis alone do not account for the brown discoloration.'),
('50ffd48e-1627-51a3-a7d3-cdf351ae6641'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteomalacia histology shows:', '["Excess osteoid (unmineralized bone matrix) covering trabeculae (increased osteoid seam thickness)","Normal osteoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal mineralization mediated by specific receptor activation","Excess mineral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased osteoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excess osteoid (unmineralized bone matrix) covering trabeculae (increased osteoid seam thickness)', 'Osteomalacia is characterized by excess unmineralized osteoid covering the trabeculae, with increased osteoid seam thickness, reflecting defective mineralization of otherwise normal bone matrix. Normal osteoid, normal mineralization, excess mineral, or decreased osteoid contradict this histologic picture.'),
('d1771e31-9c9a-5eba-84b7-3659bf639e4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Tetracycline labeling is used to:', '["Assess bone formation rate (tetracycline binds to newly mineralized bone)","Reduce bone pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibit osteoclasts mediated by specific receptor activation","Treat bone infections without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Image bone tumors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Assess bone formation rate (tetracycline binds to newly mineralized bone)', 'Tetracycline is deposited at sites of active mineralization, and sequential (double) labeling allows the bone formation and mineral apposition rate to be measured on histomorphometry. It is not used to relieve pain, inhibit osteoclasts, treat infection, or image bone tumors.'),
('34805933-1712-5aba-8113-191b9f58d339'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The use of bisphosphonates in osteoporosis is based on their ability to:', '["Increase RANKL a resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase calcium absorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulate osteoblasts mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibit osteoclast activity and reduce bone resorption (induce osteoclast apoptosis)","Increase PTH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibit osteoclast activity and reduce bone resorption (induce osteoclast apoptosis)', 'Bisphosphonates inhibit osteoclast activity and induce osteoclast apoptosis, thereby reducing bone resorption and preserving bone mass in osteoporosis. They do not increase RANKL, PTH, or calcium absorption, nor do they directly stimulate osteoblasts.'),
('8963e93c-7572-54bd-9541-a337e3a4d197'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'RANKL inhibitors (denosumab) work by:', '["Binding RANKL and preventing it from binding to RANK (like OPG)","Inhibiting osteoblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increasing PTH mediated by specific receptor activation","Activating RANK without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulating osteoclasts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding RANKL and preventing it from binding to RANK (like OPG)', 'Denosumab is a monoclonal antibody that binds RANKL and prevents it from engaging RANK on osteoclast precursors, mimicking the natural decoy protein OPG and reducing osteoclast-mediated resorption. It does not inhibit osteoblasts, activate RANK, raise PTH, or stimulate osteoclasts.'),
('8d764036-e636-50bc-8cc1-5146326c465a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'PTH analogs (teriparatide) are used to treat osteoporosis by:', '["Stimulating bone formation (intermittent PTH has anabolic effect)","Increasing RANKL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibiting osteoclasts mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreasing calcium absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increasing calcitonin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating bone formation (intermittent PTH has anabolic effect)', 'Intermittent administration of the PTH analog teriparatide has an anabolic effect that stimulates bone formation and increases bone mass. The drug does not inhibit osteoclasts, increase RANKL, decrease calcium absorption, or raise calcitonin as its therapeutic mechanism.'),
('f32a0b5b-477f-5ec4-94e4-0632023cadd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Marfan syndrome skeletal features include:', '["scoliosis","and chest deformities","tall stature","Arachnodactyly (long fingers)","Brachydactyly"]'::jsonb, 'Arachnodactyly (long fingers)', 'Marfan syndrome produces arachnodactyly (long, slender fingers) in addition to tall stature, scoliosis, and chest deformities because of fibrillin deficiency. Brachydactyly (short fingers) is the opposite finding and is not a Marfan feature.'),
('4ce9aabd-cd63-5952-920f-e8a34d6453ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (hypermobility type) features:', '["Aortic dilation","skin hyperextensibility","Joint hypermobility","Blue sclerae","and easy bruising"]'::jsonb, 'Joint hypermobility', 'The hypermobility type of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is dominated by joint hypermobility, accompanied by skin hyperextensibility and easy bruising. Blue sclerae suggest osteogenesis imperfecta, while aortic dilation points to vascular-type EDS or Marfan syndrome.'),
('1bfbdbd5-51a2-5e1a-ba11-4a22001077f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteoporosis is most common in:', '["Adult men a","Pregnant women","Children","Postmenopausal women","Young men"]'::jsonb, 'Postmenopausal women', 'Osteoporosis is most common in postmenopausal women because estrogen deficiency accelerates bone resorption and lowers peak bone mass reserves. Children, pregnant women, young men, and adult men have substantially lower rates.'),
('bba002b5-53a6-56f9-9932-6be52cd6a077'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Senile osteoporosis (type II) is related to:', '["Corticosteroid excess resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcium deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PTH excess mediated by specific receptor activation","Estrogen deficiency alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Age-related decline in osteoblast function (reduced bone formation)"]'::jsonb, 'Age-related decline in osteoblast function (reduced bone formation)', 'Senile (type II) osteoporosis results from an age-related decline in osteoblast function with reduced bone formation, typically in patients older than 70 years. It is not explained by estrogen deficiency alone, corticosteroid excess, calcium deficiency, or PTH excess as the primary mechanism.'),
('7312ddc8-cc42-5b56-b8c2-b8ffbb42582e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DEXA) measures:', '["Bone mineral density (BMD) at the spine and hip","Bone cell activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone collagen cross-links mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone architecture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone turnover markers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone mineral density (BMD) at the spine and hip', 'DEXA measures areal bone mineral density (BMD), most commonly at the lumbar spine and proximal femur, and its T-score defines osteopenia and osteoporosis. It does not assess cell activity, collagen cross-links, trabecular architecture, or biochemical turnover markers.'),
('bef8d795-46d4-5343-a203-7ab8f1a2b392'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Bone turnover markers (BTMs) include:', '["Serum calcium only resulting in decreased functional activity","Alkaline phosphatase only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteocalcin only mediated by specific receptor activation","Serum CTX (C-telopeptide) and urine NTX (bone resorption markers)","Serum phosphate only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Serum CTX (C-telopeptide) and urine NTX (bone resorption markers)', 'Serum CTX and urine NTX are collagen-derived bone resorption markers widely used as BTMs to monitor antiresorptive treatment. Options listing serum calcium, phosphate, or a single protein do not provide the paired resorption-marker set that the question targets.'),
('4344cbd4-e8fc-5a4b-bc25-8880ded87471'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The cell that forms the cartilage model in the embryo is the:', '["Adipocyte a resulting in decreased functional activity","Myoblast accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibroblast mediated by specific receptor activation","Chondroblast (from mesenchyme)","Osteoblast leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chondroblast (from mesenchyme)', 'Chondroblasts, which differentiate from mesenchyme, secrete the cartilaginous matrix that forms the embryonic cartilage model used in endochondral ossification. Myoblasts, fibroblasts, osteoblasts, and adipocytes give rise to different tissues.'),
('4a43319a-1a4c-54d4-a218-02a349f3e043'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The perichondrium in endochondral ossification gives rise to:', '["Synovium resulting in decreased functional activity","Growth plate cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Articular cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Periosteum (fibrous and osteogenic layers) when blood vessels invade","Bone marrow a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Periosteum (fibrous and osteogenic layers) when blood vessels invade', 'When blood vessels invade the cartilage model, the perichondrium of the future diaphysis becomes the periosteum, whose osteogenic layer forms the bone collar. Synovium, growth plate cartilage, articular cartilage, and bone marrow are not derived from the perichondrium.'),
('94ee957e-6de0-5d11-92bd-36344a062a70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The bone collar in endochondral ossification is:', '["The secondary ossification center resulting in decreased functional activity","A collar of bone formed by the periosteum around the mid-diaphysis of the cartilage model","The growth plate mediated by specific receptor activation","The articular cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The primary ossification center leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A collar of bone formed by the periosteum around the mid-diaphysis of the cartilage model', 'The bone collar is intramembranous bone laid down by the periosteum around the mid-diaphysis of the cartilage model, reinforcing the shaft before endochondral ossification proceeds. It is distinct from the primary and secondary ossification centers, the growth plate, and the articular cartilage.'),
('99293196-cb4d-51f3-9dfb-0694127e15c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteoclasts in endochondral ossification:', '["Resorb the calcified cartilage to create space for marrow and bone formation","Produce growth factors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deposit bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Secrete cartilage matrix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Form the bone collar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Resorb the calcified cartilage to create space for marrow and bone formation', 'Osteoclasts resorb calcified cartilage in the diaphysis, creating the medullary cavity and space for bone marrow and trabecular bone. They do not deposit bone, secrete cartilage matrix, form the bone collar, or act by producing growth factors in this role.'),
('7561f687-c781-594d-adb6-b6f910be8e48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The primary marrow cavity forms when:', '["Bone collar forms resulting in decreased functional activity","Periosteum forms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood vessels enter the cartilage model mediated by specific receptor activation","Chondrocytes hypertrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Osteoclasts resorb calcified cartilage in the center of the diaphysis"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoclasts resorb calcified cartilage in the center of the diaphysis', 'The primary marrow cavity forms when osteoclasts resorb the calcified cartilage in the center of the diaphysis after blood vessels and osteogenic cells have invaded. The bone collar, periosteum, vessel entry, and chondrocyte hypertrophy precede or accompany this stage rather than alone producing the cavity.'),
('4e78bb46-bde7-53f7-9a7c-9a695eb412a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The nutrient artery enters the bone at the:', '["Epiphysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Articular surface a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metaphysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Growth plate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nutrient foramen (usually in the mid-diaphysis)"]'::jsonb, 'Nutrient foramen (usually in the mid-diaphysis)', 'The nutrient artery enters long bones through the nutrient foramen, usually located in the mid-diaphysis, to supply the medullary cavity and inner cortex. The epiphysis, metaphysis, and growth plate are reached by different vessels (periosteal, epiphyseal, and metaphyseal arteries).'),
('79cfc59d-5e65-5b00-85e7-f359c60f71c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The metaphyseal arteries supply:', '["The articular cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","The epiphysis only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The diaphysis only mediated by specific receptor activation","The periosteum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The growth plate and metaphysis (participate in endochondral ossification)"]'::jsonb, 'The growth plate and metaphysis (participate in endochondral ossification)', 'The metaphyseal arteries penetrate the metaphysis and endochondral growth plate and help drive endochondral ossification. The epiphysis is served by epiphyseal arteries, the diaphysis by the nutrient artery, and the periosteum by periosteal arteries; articular cartilage is avascular and receives nutrition from synovial fluid.'),
('7fdd28d5-dc54-5940-ac8e-6fed2e48bd3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The epiphyseal arteries supply:', '["The diaphysis resulting in decreased functional activity","The epiphysis (cross the growth plate only after fusion)","The medullary cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","The periosteum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The metaphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The epiphysis (cross the growth plate only after fusion)', 'Epiphyseal arteries enter the epiphysis and, before growth plate closure, remain confined to the epiphyseal side; they cross the plate only after bony fusion. The diaphysis and medullary cavity derive from the nutrient artery, the metaphysis from metaphyseal arteries, and the peripheral cortex from periosteal arteries.'),
('6f9bf683-c047-5157-ba5d-2cdc2aeaebf1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The periosteal blood supply supplies:', '["The medullary cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","The growth plate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bone marrow mediated by specific receptor activation","The inner cortex only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The outer 1/3 of the cortical bone (via Volkmann and Haversian canals)"]'::jsonb, 'The outer 1/3 of the cortical bone (via Volkmann and Haversian canals)', 'Periosteal vessels traverse Volkmann and Haversian canals to supply the outer third of the cortex. The medullary cavity, bone marrow, and inner cortex depend on the nutrient artery, and the growth plate receives its own epiphyseal and metaphyseal supply.'),
('14234c47-87a7-5e83-a525-070268a4bae3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Wolff law states that:', '["Bone is static resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone only forms with exercise accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone structure is genetically fixed mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone is only resorbed with disuse without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone adapts its structure to the mechanical loads placed on it (bone is deposited where stress is high and resorbed where stress is low)"]'::jsonb, 'Bone adapts its structure to the mechanical loads placed on it (bone is deposited where stress is high and resorbed where stress is low)', 'Wolff''s law holds that bone is dynamic and remodels in response to mechanical stress, with deposition along lines of high load and resorption where load is low. The other options describe bone as static, purely genetic, or responsive only to a single stimulus, all of which contradict this adaptive principle.'),
('ec72cfe5-2935-5eca-a844-b8af86cde1b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Disuse osteoporosis results from:', '["Only increased osteoclast activity a resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal bone remodeling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased bone resorption and decreased bone formation due to lack of mechanical loading","Increased bone formation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased osteoblast activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased bone resorption and decreased bone formation due to lack of mechanical loading', 'Unloading removes the mechanical stimulus that normally maintains bone mass, tipping remodeling toward net resorption with diminished formation. The other options imply either normal or enhanced bone accrual, which is the opposite of the resorptive state seen with disuse.'),
('6995a73f-15a9-59b4-af79-b4751a2d8ab6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The term ossification refers to:', '["The formation of cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","The process of bone formation (either intramembranous or endochondral)","The formation of fibrous tissue mediated by specific receptor activation","The growth of adipose tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The formation of blood vessels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The process of bone formation (either intramembranous or endochondral)', 'Ossification is specifically the formation of bone, occurring either directly in mesenchyme (intramembranous) or on a cartilage model (endochondral). The other choices describe formation of cartilage, fibrous, adipose, or vascular tissue rather than bone.'),
('8b177179-f522-5efb-a370-2ec828231668'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Endochondral ossification begins in which fetal period?', '["Birth resulting in decreased functional activity","12th week accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","16th week mediated by specific receptor activation","8th week of embryonic development (primary ossification centers appear)","4th week leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '8th week of embryonic development (primary ossification centers appear)', 'Primary ossification centers appear within the diaphyses of long bones by about the 8th week of embryonic development, marking the onset of endochondral ossification. Secondary (epiphyseal) centers develop later, around birth and the first years of life.'),
('78f104da-c5ff-5b3d-aaba-504d0fa46de0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Estrogen is critical for growth plate closure in:', '["Only males resulting in decreased functional activity","Both males and females (via aromatization of testosterone to estrogen)","Neither mediated by specific receptor activation","Only after menopause a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only females leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Both males and females (via aromatization of testosterone to estrogen)', 'Estrogen is the principal hormone driving epiphyseal (growth plate) fusion in both sexes. In males it derives largely from aromatization of testosterone to estrogen, so estrogen deficiency delays growth plate closure regardless of sex.'),
('aefa380a-ad66-5634-9f30-8ebb87182a01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'IGF-1 (insulin-like growth factor 1) is the:', '["Main calcium regulator resulting in decreased functional activity","Main stimulator of bone resorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Main inhibitor of bone formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Main mediator of growth hormone effects on bone (stimulates chondrocyte proliferation)","Main phosphate regulator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Main mediator of growth hormone effects on bone (stimulates chondrocyte proliferation)', 'Growth hormone acts largely through IGF-1, which promotes chondrocyte proliferation and longitudinal bone growth. IGF-1 does not primarily regulate calcium, phosphate, resorption, or inhibit bone formation as the other options claim.'),
('7097feba-a6b1-5224-bcb7-a73c307ec9d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Gigantism results from:', '["Excess sex hormones","Excess PTH","Deficient GH","Excess GH after epiphyseal fusion (adulthood)","Excess GH before epiphyseal fusion (childhood)"]'::jsonb, 'Excess GH before epiphyseal fusion (childhood)', 'Gigantism occurs when growth hormone excess arises during childhood, before epiphyseal fusion, allowing continued linear growth of long bones. The same excess after growth plate closure causes acromegaly rather than gigantism.'),
('c1f3a91a-99db-5108-9f54-f474e2380b13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Dwarfism can be caused by:', '["IGF-1 deficiency","GH deficiency","GH excess","PTH excess","or GH insensitivity (Laron syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 'GH deficiency', 'Growth hormone deficiency is a classic endocrine cause of proportionate dwarfism, impairing the GH-IGF-1 axis that drives linear growth. Related causes include IGF-1 deficiency and GH insensitivity; GH excess and PTH excess do not cause short stature.'),
('c402a553-6467-588c-911b-82aa9c6c00ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Achondroplasia (most common form of dwarfism) is caused by:', '["PTH deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","IGF-1 deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activating mutation in FGFR3 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 3)","GH deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcitonin deficiency a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activating mutation in FGFR3 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 3)', 'Achondroplasia results from a gain-of-function (activating) mutation of FGFR3, which over-inhibits chondrocyte proliferation and endochondral ossification of long bones. PTH, IGF-1, GH, and calcitonin abnormalities are not the underlying cause.'),
('12a375e7-7dcc-5285-bfaa-b780390c028b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'FGFR3 normally:', '["Increases matrix synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulates chondrocyte growth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Promotes osteoclast formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibits chondrocyte proliferation (negative regulator of growth)","Promotes osteoblast activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits chondrocyte proliferation (negative regulator of growth)', 'FGFR3 is a physiologic negative regulator of chondrocyte proliferation within the growth plate, limiting endochondral bone growth. Activating mutations exaggerate this inhibition, producing short-limbed dwarfism.'),
('2a102501-0cdb-581f-8e54-cf3a904c478d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Thanatophoric dysplasia is a lethal skeletal dysplasia also caused by:', '["Fibrillin mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","Type II collagen mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GH deficiency a mediated by specific receptor activation","Type I collagen mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","FGFR3 mutation (more severe than achondroplasia)"]'::jsonb, 'FGFR3 mutation (more severe than achondroplasia)', 'Thanatophoric dysplasia is a lethal form of short-limbed dwarfism caused by severe activating FGFR3 mutations that make the phenotype far graver than achondroplasia. Fibrillin, collagen, and GH defects are unrelated causes of different skeletal disorders.'),
('64a8626e-8239-5211-bf51-4cfe4fa26ef4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Hypochondroplasia is a milder form of short stature also due to:', '["Collagen mutation","Estrogen deficiency","Growth hormone deficiency","Achondroplasia mutation","FGFR3 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'FGFR3 mutation', 'Hypochondroplasia is caused by a milder activating mutation of FGFR3, placing it on the same molecular spectrum as achondroplasia but with less severe short stature. Collagen, estrogen, and growth hormone abnormalities are not the cause.'),
('bae56d7e-35f2-5c19-8078-ffce06828dca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteogenesis imperfecta (type I) is inherited as:', '["Mitochondrial resulting in decreased functional activity","X-linked recessive accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sporadic a mediated by specific receptor activation","Autosomal recessive without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autosomal dominant (most OI types are dominant)"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal dominant (most OI types are dominant)', 'Osteogenesis imperfecta type I, like most OI types, is inherited as an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in type I collagen genes (COL1A1/COL1A2). In contrast to the listed recessive, X-linked, or mitochondrial possibilities, a single mutant allele suffices to produce the disease.'),
('043203e7-53fd-5092-9694-66b9d50b7984'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The basic defect in Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (classic) affects:', '["Type IV collagen resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type III collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Type I collagen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type V collagen (COL5A1/A2) – fibril regulation"]'::jsonb, 'Type V collagen (COL5A1/A2) – fibril regulation', 'Classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome results from mutations in COL5A1/COL5A2, impairing type V collagen that regulates fibril assembly; type III collagen defects instead cause the vascular (type IV) EDS, and type IV collagen defects cause Alport syndrome.'),
('57df1c21-0ed7-5b43-bb5a-35784ab4ab0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Vascular Ehlers-Danlos (type IV) features:', '["Joint hypermobility only","Arterial","uterine","and bowel rupture (fragile vessels and thin skin)","Skin hyperelasticity only"]'::jsonb, 'Arterial', 'Vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (type IV), caused by type III collagen defects, classically presents with spontaneous arterial, uterine, and bowel rupture due to fragile vessels and thin skin. Joint hypermobility and skin hyperelasticity are prominent in other EDS subtypes rather than being the hallmark here.'),
('03043152-15e8-576c-b5df-6384bf2d030e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Stickler syndrome includes features such as:', '["hearing loss","retinal detachment","and midface hypoplasia","Myopia","Short stature"]'::jsonb, 'Myopia', 'Stickler syndrome, a collagen disorder, characteristically combines ocular features such as myopia and retinal detachment with hearing loss and midface hypoplasia. Myopia is a recognized component, whereas short stature is not a defining feature of this condition.'),
('393ca2ec-7d50-5abe-b978-bbdee470f2a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Alport syndrome features:', '["and anterior lenticonus resulting in decreased functional activity","Progressive glomerulonephritis (hematuria)","Multiple fractures mediated by specific receptor activation","Blue sclerae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive glomerulonephritis (hematuria)', 'Alport syndrome is a type IV collagen disorder (COL4A5) that presents with progressive glomerulonephritis and hematuria, often accompanied by sensorineural hearing loss and anterior lenticonus. Multiple fractures and blue sclerae point to osteogenesis imperfecta, not Alport syndrome.'),
('31a08490-1afc-5b72-a0be-b858cd3380d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The leading cause of pathologic fractures in the elderly is:', '["Bone metastases","Osteomalacia","Paget disease","Osteogenesis imperfecta a","Osteoporosis"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoporosis', 'Osteoporosis is the most common cause of pathologic and fragility fractures in the elderly because age-related bone loss weakens the skeleton. Metastases, osteomalacia, and Paget disease are far less frequent causes in this population.'),
('12b998a8-75ea-5bdd-93f7-ca1acfce117f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Metastatic bone tumors most commonly originate from:', '["thyroid","and kidney","lung","Breast (lytic)","prostate (blastic)"]'::jsonb, 'Breast (lytic)', 'Breast carcinoma is the most common source of bone metastases overall and typically produces lytic lesions, while prostate cancer characteristically gives blastic deposits; lung, kidney, and thyroid are additional but less common primary sites.'),
('fa2d5c20-c34d-5294-9a0a-f2489f98d428'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteoblastoma is a:', '["Chondrosarcoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Benign bone tumor similar to osteoid osteoma but >2 cm","Osteochondroma mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malignant bone tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign bone tumor similar to osteoid osteoma but >2 cm', 'Osteoblastoma is a benign, bone-forming tumor that resembles osteoid osteoma histologically but exceeds 2 cm in size and typically involves the posterior spine elements. It is not a cartilage tumor, cyst, or malignant lesion.'),
('44175555-9523-533d-8f39-39bb8c95f9e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteosarcoma is a:', '["Giant cell tumor of bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrous bone tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cartilage tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Malignant bone tumor in which osteoid is produced directly by tumor cells","Benign bone tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant bone tumor in which osteoid is produced directly by tumor cells', 'Osteosarcoma is defined as a malignant neoplasm in which the tumor cells directly produce osteoid (and often bone). It is neither a benign lesion nor primarily cartilaginous, fibrous, or giant-cell in nature.'),
('e5444e85-ee6c-5e56-aeb7-003f507aff20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Giant cell tumor of bone (osteoclastoma) is characterized by:', '["Fibrous stroma resulting in decreased functional activity","Numerous osteoclast-like giant cells in a stromal background of mononuclear cells","Lacunae with osteocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Osteoid production by malignant cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chondroid matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Numerous osteoclast-like giant cells in a stromal background of mononuclear cells', 'Giant cell tumor consists of multinucleated osteoclast-like giant cells set in a stroma of mononuclear cells, which is its diagnostic hallmark. Osteoid production, chondroid matrix, and fibrous stroma describe other bone lesions.'),
('d3bb6087-5bd9-5089-ac13-cf5e8914e846'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Giant cell tumor of bone is located in the:', '["Spine resulting in decreased functional activity","Diaphysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flat bones mediated by specific receptor activation","Epiphysis of long bones (after growth plate closure)","Metaphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epiphysis of long bones (after growth plate closure)', 'Giant cell tumor characteristically arises in the epiphysis of long bones, typically after growth plate closure in young adults. This epiphyseal, juxta-articular location distinguishes it from metaphyseal or diaphyseal lesions.'),
('d4d6b460-b5dc-50e4-91d1-f3aca10d1af9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Chondrosarcoma is a malignant tumor that produces:', '["Mucoid matrix","Chondroid (cartilage) matrix","Osteoid","Myxoid matrix a","Fibrous tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Chondroid (cartilage) matrix', 'Chondrosarcoma is a malignant cartilage-forming tumor whose cells produce a chondroid (cartilage) matrix. Production of osteoid, myxoid matrix, mucoid matrix, or fibrous tissue indicates other tumors rather than a chondrosarcoma.'),
('108f7c03-6bbd-5c13-9a1f-721960111ee9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Enchondroma is a:', '["Bone cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Benign cartilage tumor (hyaline cartilage) within the medullary cavity of a bone","Fibrous tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Malignant cartilage tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Benign bone-forming tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign cartilage tumor (hyaline cartilage) within the medullary cavity of a bone', 'An enchondroma is a benign, hyaline cartilage tumor located within the medullary cavity, most often in the small bones of the hands and feet. It is not malignant, fibrous, bone-forming, or a cystic lesion.'),
('d56e132e-7789-558f-a444-507a4bc99aa8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Osteochondroma is a:', '["Malignant bone tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrous lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Benign bone tumor with a cartilage cap (exostosis)","Giant cell tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign bone tumor with a cartilage cap (exostosis)', 'Osteochondroma is a benign bone outgrowth capped by hyaline cartilage (an exostosis) that projects from the metaphyseal surface. It is neither malignant nor fibrous, cystic, or giant-cell in nature.'),
('41f26f6f-93d5-5ef7-bb55-379121f34c5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Multiple hereditary exostoses (MHE) is an autosomal dominant condition with:', '["Multiple osteochondromas (due to EXT1/EXT2 mutations)","Multiple fractures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paget disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Osteoporosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multiple enchondromas (Ollier disease) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple osteochondromas (due to EXT1/EXT2 mutations)', 'Multiple hereditary exostoses is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by EXT1 or EXT2 mutations that produce multiple osteochondromas. Multiple enchondromas define Ollier disease, while fractures, osteoporosis, and Paget disease are unrelated features.'),
('e6d08a60-8e36-5ecb-b9c2-0f414a7a9062'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Ollier disease (multiple enchondromatosis) features:', '["Multiple osteochondromas","commonly in the hands and long bones (asymmetric)","Fibrous dysplasia","Multiple enchondromas","Bone cysts"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple enchondromas', 'Ollier disease is a non-hereditary condition of multiple enchondromas, typically distributed asymmetrically and favoring the hands and long bones. Multiple osteochondromas point to MHE, and fibrous dysplasia or bone cysts are separate diagnoses.'),
('5426b934-d6cf-5ac1-b602-12e33f7119e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Maffucci syndrome is:', '["Neurofibromatosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Paget disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple osteochondromas mediated by specific receptor activation","Ollier disease with soft tissue hemangiomas","Fibrous dysplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ollier disease with soft tissue hemangiomas', 'Maffucci syndrome is Ollier disease (multiple enchondromas) combined with soft tissue hemangiomas, carrying a risk of malignant transformation. Neurofibromatosis, Paget disease, osteochondromas, and fibrous dysplasia are distinct entities.'),
('61939972-4794-5134-88f9-fd58285ea12f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The GNAS mutation in McCune-Albright syndrome causes:', '["Inhibition of adenylyl cyclase resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibition of protein kinase A accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activation of phospholipase C mediated by specific receptor activation","Activation of JAK-STAT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Constitutive activation of adenylyl cyclase (increased cAMP) leading to hormone-independent signaling"]'::jsonb, 'Constitutive activation of adenylyl cyclase (increased cAMP) leading to hormone-independent signaling', 'The GNAS mutation in McCune-Albright syndrome constitutively activates the Gs-alpha subunit, raising adenylyl cyclase activity and cAMP production so cells signal as if continuously stimulated by hormone. The other listed pathways or enzyme inhibitions do not reflect this mechanism.'),
('8480ccfc-46f1-52d4-80b5-79c74002d15c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The radiographic feature of a bone infarct is:', '["calcified border (endosteal calcification)","Periarticular erosions","Cortical destruction","Periosteal reaction","A serpentine"]'::jsonb, 'A serpentine', 'A bone infarct appears radiographically as a serpentine pattern of calcification at its margin, formed by endosteal calcification surrounding the necrotic zone. Periarticular erosions, cortical destruction, and periosteal reaction describe other pathologies.'),
('362a0f92-bb83-5782-86e3-27153606a4b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The radiographic finding in Paget disease of bone includes:', '["Periosteal reaction","Thickened","Lytic lesions only","coarsened trabeculae and bone enlargement with mixed lytic and sclerotic phases","Sclerotic lesions only"]'::jsonb, 'Thickened', 'Paget disease produces thickened, enlarged bones with coarsened trabeculae and mixed lytic and sclerotic phases. The other options, such as periosteal reaction or purely lytic or purely sclerotic lesions, do not capture the typical mosaic remodeling seen in Paget disease.'),
('83d471d5-0aea-589b-be0c-6486c2f464ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The radiographic finding in hyperparathyroidism includes:', '["Osteophytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Periosteal reaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subperiosteal bone resorption (especially in the middle phalanges of the fingers)","Calcified cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone enlargement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subperiosteal bone resorption (especially in the middle phalanges of the fingers)', 'Subperiosteal bone resorption, most evident along the radial aspect of the middle phalanges, is the classic radiographic sign of hyperparathyroidism from excess PTH-driven osteoclast activity. Osteophytes, periosteal reaction, and calcified cartilage belong to other conditions.'),
('3dd41290-c981-55ff-b402-37bbd53c1798'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Tufting (acro-osteolysis) of the distal phalanges can be seen in:', '["Osteoporosis","and psoriatic arthritis","Osteoarthritis","scleroderma","Hyperparathyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperparathyroidism', 'Resorption of the distal phalangeal tufts (acro-osteolysis) is a recognized feature of hyperparathyroidism, and it also occurs in scleroderma and psoriatic arthritis. Osteoporosis and osteoarthritis do not characteristically erode the terminal phalangeal tufts.'),
('6abf45e4-50ea-5f5f-b94e-55f6e6557813'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Rugger jersey spine is a radiographic finding in:', '["Osteoporosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteomalacia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renal osteodystrophy (alternating dense and lucent bands in vertebral bodies)","Paget disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ankylosing spondylitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal osteodystrophy (alternating dense and lucent bands in vertebral bodies)', 'Rugger jersey spine describes alternating dense and lucent bands in the vertebral bodies, a hallmark of renal osteodystrophy from secondary hyperparathyroidism. Osteoporosis, osteomalacia, Paget disease, and ankylosing spondylitis do not produce this banded pattern.'),
('5c05f4a0-a570-584f-9b8d-e8754221f566'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'A chalkstick fracture is a characteristic of:', '["OI a resulting in decreased functional activity","Rickets accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteomalacia mediated by specific receptor activation","Osteoporosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Paget disease of bone (brittle bone that fractures sharply)"]'::jsonb, 'Paget disease of bone (brittle bone that fractures sharply)', 'Chalkstick fractures are complete, sharply angled transverse fractures through brittle, deformed bone and are characteristic of Paget disease. Rickets, osteomalacia, and osteoporosis typically cause insufficiency fractures of different patterns rather than chalkstick fractures.'),
('6a259a28-2278-5fa9-b20c-6e3a46308f1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The term osteoarthritis and osteoarthrosis differentiate:', '["osteoarthrosis is non-inflammatory","while osteoarthrosis is secondary","OA and osteoarthrosis are synonyms","OA is primary/idiopathic","OA is inflammatory"]'::jsonb, 'OA is primary/idiopathic', 'Osteoarthritis (OA) is regarded as primary or idiopathic, while osteoarthrosis emphasizes the non-inflammatory, degenerative nature of the process. The terms are not synonyms, and OA is not primarily an inflammatory disease.'),
('6175c273-bdfa-50a0-91c9-40a95d6c3118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Subchondral cysts (Geodes) in OA represent:', '["Tumors resulting in decreased functional activity","Synovial fluid intrusion into subchondral bone through cartilaginous defects","Rheumatoid nodules mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone infarcts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Osteonecrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synovial fluid intrusion into subchondral bone through cartilaginous defects', 'Subchondral geodes in osteoarthritis form when synovial fluid is forced through fissures in damaged articular cartilage into the subchondral bone. They are not tumors, infarcts, necrotic zones, or rheumatoid nodules.'),
('b9795958-8aef-539f-92f7-33e388eebfb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The earliest histologic change in osteoarthritis is:', '["Synovitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteophyte formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subchondral bone sclerosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrillation (superficial fraying of articular cartilage)","Full-thickness cartilage loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrillation (superficial fraying of articular cartilage)', 'Fibrillation, the superficial fraying and flaking of articular cartilage, is the earliest histologic change in osteoarthritis. Osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis, and full-thickness loss occur later in the disease process.'),
('9f9547c2-07e0-5302-8063-748da0742df5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Rheumatoid arthritis synovium stains for:', '["CD4+ T cells","CD8+ T cells only","and CD68+ macrophages","CD56+ NK cells","CD20+ B cells"]'::jsonb, 'CD4+ T cells', 'The rheumatoid synovium is densely infiltrated predominantly by CD4+ helper T cells, along with CD68+ macrophages and CD20+ B cells. CD8+ T cells alone, NK cells, or an exclusive single population do not characterize the lesion.'),
('d5810ca1-3a23-5024-8553-aeb0e904e25a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP) antibodies are:', '["Non-specific autoantibodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Specific for lupus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Specific for vasculitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Highly specific for rheumatoid arthritis (and often present before symptoms)","Specific for scleroderma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Highly specific for rheumatoid arthritis (and often present before symptoms)', 'Anti-CCP antibodies are highly specific for rheumatoid arthritis and can be detected years before clinical symptoms appear. They are not markers of lupus, vasculitis, or scleroderma, and they are not merely non-specific autoantibodies.'),
('1843447b-7692-5690-8cad-7cf6c6be6ee6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The presence of rheumatoid factor (RF) is:', '["Specific for RA resulting in decreased functional activity","An antibody against citrullinated proteins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An IgM antibody against the Fc portion of IgG (seen in 80% of RA patients but also in other diseases)","Absent in RA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","present in healthy individuals a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An IgM antibody against the Fc portion of IgG (seen in 80% of RA patients but also in other diseases)', 'Rheumatoid factor is an IgM antibody directed against the Fc portion of IgG, present in about 80% of rheumatoid arthritis patients but also in chronic infections and other autoimmune diseases, so it is not specific. It is not an antibody to citrullinated proteins, which is the anti-CCP specificity.'),
('af86e50f-2049-5a3c-bffc-33e131abdf6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The enzyme defect in Lesch-Nyhan syndrome (X-linked) is:', '["HGPRT deficiency (hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase)","Adenosine deaminase deficiency","Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency","PRPP synthetase deficiency a","Xanthine oxidase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'HGPRT deficiency (hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase)', 'Lesch-Nyhan syndrome results from X-linked deficiency of HGPRT (hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase), causing uric acid overproduction with self-mutilation and hyperuricemia. Adenosine deaminase, purine nucleoside phosphorylase, and xanthine oxidase defects produce distinct disorders.'),
('ff2514d0-56e4-51ee-8c9f-b0e5026026e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Acute gouty arthritis is triggered by:', '["Urate deposition only resulting in decreased functional activity","causing IL-1β release accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urate crystals activating the NLRP3 inflammasome in macrophages","Direct cartilage damage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Synovial proliferation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urate crystals activating the NLRP3 inflammasome in macrophages', 'Monosodium urate crystals are phagocytosed by macrophages and activate the NLRP3 inflammasome, releasing IL-1β and driving acute gouty inflammation. This mechanism, rather than simple deposition, cartilage damage, or synovial proliferation, underlies the acute attack.'),
('3f8f0dc4-6c11-519b-907f-146d1c56ea41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The histologic appearance of gout in a joint includes:', '["needle-shaped urate crystals (negative birefringence under polarized light)","Amorphous calcium deposits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Positively birefringent rhomboid crystals (CPPD) mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholesterol clefts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amphophilic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'needle-shaped urate crystals (negative birefringence under polarized light)', 'Gout is characterized by needle-shaped monosodium urate crystals that are negatively birefringent under polarized light. Positively birefringent rhomboid crystals belong to CPPD (pseudogout), and the other options are not the defining crystal of gout.'),
('1fba2367-029a-5087-9511-f841f47a5a3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Pseudogout (CPPD) crystals are:', '["negatively birefringent","Needle-shaped","Amorphous","positively birefringent under polarized light","Rhomboid"]'::jsonb, 'Rhomboid', 'Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate (CPPD) crystals of pseudogout are rhomboid in shape and positively birefringent under polarized light. This contrasts with the needle-shaped, negatively birefringent urate crystals of gout.'),
('a4dee5dc-2250-5ed9-97b4-462827a4bc47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Chondrocalcinosis (calcification of cartilage) is associated with:', '["Osteoarthritis","Infectious arthritis","Pseudogout (CPPD deposition)","Rheumatoid arthritis","Gout"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudogout (CPPD deposition)', 'Chondrocalcinosis, the radiographic calcification of cartilage, is the hallmark of pseudogout caused by calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate deposition. It is not characteristic of osteoarthritis, gout, infectious arthritis, or rheumatoid arthritis.'),
('48dc70ce-d33d-597c-972c-211cd8100d51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Naturally occurring calcium pyrophosphate deposition (CPPD) is associated with:', '["hemochromatosis","Gout","hyperparathyroidism","and hypomagnesemia","Osteoarthritis"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoarthritis', 'CPPD (pseudogout) is a crystal-induced arthritis that frequently coexists with osteoarthritis, because calcium pyrophosphate crystals deposit in damaged cartilage and accelerate its degeneration. Although hemochromatosis, hyperparathyroidism, and hypomagnesemia can also predispose to CPPD, osteoarthritis is the classic joint condition associated with this deposition.'),
('2be220fa-ea89-5e5b-8a3a-e614b163afa7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Septic arthritis (infectious arthritis) is usually caused by:', '["Spirochetes a resulting in decreased functional activity","Bacteria (Staphylococcus aureus is most common in adults)","Fungi mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasites without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Viruses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacteria (Staphylococcus aureus is most common in adults)', 'Bacteria, particularly Staphylococcus aureus in adults, are the most common cause of septic arthritis, usually seeding the joint hematogenously. Fungi and viruses are rare causes, and spirochetes or parasites do not produce typical pyogenic joint infection.'),
('a432b0b8-53b9-5bc5-b7d7-9d97f9eca721'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Tuberculous arthritis (Pott disease) most commonly affects the:', '["hip","and knee","Spine (Pott disease)","Hands","Shoulder"]'::jsonb, 'Spine (Pott disease)', 'Tuberculous arthritis characteristically involves the spine, a form known as Pott disease, with granulomatous destruction of vertebral bodies. It presents more insidiously than pyogenic septic arthritis and typically spares peripheral joints in most cases.'),
('ce4d2593-734e-5dab-915b-d044628b7ac2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Lyme arthritis is caused by:', '["Neisseria resulting in decreased functional activity","Treponema a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Streptococcus mediated by specific receptor activation","Borrelia burgdorferi (tick-borne spirochete)","Staphylococcus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Borrelia burgdorferi (tick-borne spirochete)', 'Lyme arthritis is caused by the tick-borne spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi, transmitted by Ixodes ticks, and presents as episodic oligoarthritis, typically of the knee. Neisseria, Streptococcus, and Staphylococcus cause bacterial septic arthritis, while Treponema pallidum causes syphilis.'),
('34aeff4e-2279-50ca-b917-da614809c769'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Gonococcal arthritis is caused by:', '["Mycoplasma genitalium a resulting in decreased functional activity","Haemophilus ducreyi accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chlamydia trachomatis mediated by specific receptor activation","Treponema pallidum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neisseria gonorrhoeae (the most common cause of septic arthritis in sexually active adults)"]'::jsonb, 'Neisseria gonorrhoeae (the most common cause of septic arthritis in sexually active adults)', 'Gonococcal arthritis results from disseminated Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection, the most common cause of septic arthritis in young sexually active adults, and presents with migratory arthralgias and tenosynovitis. Chlamydia trachomatis and Treponema pallidum cause other sexually transmitted diseases, and Haemophilus ducreyi causes chancroid.'),
('1ed48fbe-4447-5b6e-a830-d8cee8e50af9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The most common cause of septic arthritis in children is:', '["now more Staph aureus and Kingella kingae resulting in decreased functional activity","Group B strep accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Haemophilus influenzae type b (before Hib vaccine)","E. coli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Staph aureus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Haemophilus influenzae type b (before Hib vaccine)', 'Before routine Hib immunization, Haemophilus influenzae type b was the leading cause of septic arthritis in young children. Since widespread vaccination, Staphylococcus aureus and Kingella kingae have become the predominant pathogens in this age group.'),
('733862c5-ec63-5f1e-8fe8-a35688937466'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The most common cause of prosthetic joint infection is:', '["E. coli resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudomonas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Staphylococcus epidermidis and Staph aureus","Enterococcus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Candida leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus epidermidis and Staph aureus', 'Prosthetic joint infections are most commonly caused by staphylococci, mainly coagulase-negative Staphylococcus epidermidis and Staphylococcus aureus, which adhere to the implant and form biofilms. Gram-negative bacilli, enterococci, and fungi are uncommon causes.'),
('2e6b6410-9e91-5ed5-ade7-dcac564eb730'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The joint space in neuropathic arthropathy (Charcot joint) is destroyed due to:', '["Increased bone density resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of pain sensation and proprioception (leads to repeated trauma and fracture)","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased bone turnover a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autoimmune inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of pain sensation and proprioception (leads to repeated trauma and fracture)', 'Neuropathic (Charcot) arthropathy results from loss of pain sensation and proprioception, as in diabetes, tabes dorsalis, and syringomyelia, allowing repeated unrecognized trauma to destroy the joint. Infection and autoimmune inflammation do not underlie this process.'),
('07bcc09e-1d28-52e4-b58b-556d97de880a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Amyloid arthropathy is associated with:', '["Osteoarthritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Multiple myeloma and primary amyloidosis (AL type)","Gout mediated by specific receptor activation","Septic arthritis a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rheumatoid arthritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple myeloma and primary amyloidosis (AL type)', 'Amyloid arthropathy occurs when AL amyloid deposits accumulate in and around joints, typically in patients with multiple myeloma or primary (AL) amyloidosis, producing joint swelling and the shoulder-pad sign. Degenerative, crystal, and inflammatory arthritis are unrelated causes.'),
('c75f746a-4f9e-5de4-9131-356a84d0ad19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Hemophilic arthropathy results from:', '["Immune complex deposition resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Recurrent hemarthroses (bleeding into joints) in hemophilia A or B causing synovial hyperplasia and cartilage destruction","Direct injury from needle sticks without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Platelet dysfunction a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent hemarthroses (bleeding into joints) in hemophilia A or B causing synovial hyperplasia and cartilage destruction', 'Recurrent hemarthroses in hemophilia A or B cause hemosiderin deposition, synovial hyperplasia, and progressive cartilage destruction, producing a chronic arthropathy. Immune complex deposition and infection represent other mechanisms of joint damage.'),
('bac28571-eb82-52e8-9f70-72324a9964e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Hemochromatosis arthropathy resembles:', '["Rheumatoid arthritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Ankylosing spondylitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Psoriatic arthritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Osteoarthritis with CPPD deposition (chondrocalcinosis) especially in the hands (MCP joints)","Gout leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoarthritis with CPPD deposition (chondrocalcinosis) especially in the hands (MCP joints)', 'In hemochromatosis, iron overload promotes calcium pyrophosphate deposition (chondrocalcinosis) and osteoarthritis-like degeneration, characteristically affecting the 2nd and 3rd metacarpophalangeal joints. This pattern differs from the symmetric small-joint inflammation of rheumatoid arthritis.'),
('787ecf83-a28f-58d5-a8fc-7326eda9ad9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Ochronosis (alkaptonuria) is caused by a deficiency in:', '["Alkaline phosphatase","Tyrosinase","Homogentisate 1","2-dioxygenase (accumulation of homogentisic acid)","Phenylalanine hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Homogentisate 1', 'Ochronosis (alkaptonuria) results from deficient activity of homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase, causing accumulation of homogentisic acid, which is excreted in urine and deposited as dark pigment in connective tissue. The other enzymes listed do not participate in this metabolic pathway.'),
('e7078d97-0c4b-55ea-9250-9d92c2d50c8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Ochronosis causes arthritis by:', '["making it brittle and prone to degeneration","Infection","Deposition of homogentisic acid polymer in cartilage","Immune complex deposition","Gout-like crystal deposition"]'::jsonb, 'Deposition of homogentisic acid polymer in cartilage', 'In ochronosis, polymerized homogentisic acid deposits in articular cartilage, making it brittle, fragmented, and prone to degeneration. This mechanical weakening of cartilage, rather than infection or immune complexes, underlies the arthritis.'),
('aaf92ed8-510c-56d5-99ba-cba6fdf1d908'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The radiographic finding in ochronosis includes:', '["Subperiosteal resorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteoporosis and calcification of intervertebral discs (dense calcified discs on X-ray)","Periosteal reaction mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone cysts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stress fractures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoporosis and calcification of intervertebral discs (dense calcified discs on X-ray)', 'Ochronosis produces dense calcification of intervertebral discs, seen as opaque disc spaces on radiographs, together with osteoporosis and premature degeneration of large joints. Subperiosteal resorption and periosteal reaction are features of other bone diseases.'),
('48b22941-9881-5fa5-83f0-2895025801c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Which option best defines the main characteristic of connective tissue?', '["Only fibers without cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)","Cells arranged in sheets mediated by specific receptor activation","Closely packed cells with minimal ECM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","extracellular matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)', 'Connective tissue is defined by relatively few cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix composed of fibers and ground substance. Tissues with closely packed cells and minimal matrix are epithelial, and connective tissue is never composed of fibers without cells.'),
('8f464af1-150e-5a82-b448-4cd01bcb1ddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What is the main characteristic of connective tissue? Which option is most accurate?', '["Only fibers without cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)","Cells arranged in sheets mediated by specific receptor activation","Closely packed cells with minimal ECM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","extracellular matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)', 'The defining feature of connective tissue is sparse cells embedded in an abundant extracellular matrix made of collagen and elastic fibers plus ground substance. Densely packed cells with little matrix characterize epithelium, not connective tissue.'),
('c382806e-7d30-5153-ab2b-6d9d0fd47122'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What is the main characteristic of connective tissue? Choose the most accurate statement.', '["Only fibers without cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)","Cells arranged in sheets mediated by specific receptor activation","Closely packed cells with minimal ECM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","extracellular matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)', 'Connective tissue consists of scattered cells such as fibroblasts and macrophages within a large extracellular matrix of fibers and ground substance. ''Cells arranged in sheets'' and ''closely packed cells with minimal ECM'' describe epithelial tissue rather than connective tissue.'),
('91714fa5-cc90-57f5-a4a8-a6d2bc3c1d5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What is the main characteristic of connective tissue? Identify the correct response.', '["Only fibers without cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)","Cells arranged in sheets mediated by specific receptor activation","Closely packed cells with minimal ECM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","extracellular matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)', 'Connective tissue is characterized by scattered cells suspended in an abundant extracellular matrix of fibers and ground substance. A tissue made only of fibers without cells does not exist, and closely packed cells typify epithelium.'),
('120b0772-ecda-5ed6-8c75-bffb480193c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What is the main characteristic of connective tissue? Select the best answer.', '["Only fibers without cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)","Cells arranged in sheets mediated by specific receptor activation","Closely packed cells with minimal ECM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","extracellular matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)', 'Connective tissue contains relatively few cells dispersed within a voluminous extracellular matrix formed by fibers and ground substance. By contrast, epithelia contain closely packed cells with very little intervening matrix.'),
('a506bf0d-b47d-5774-8dc5-cfe66b888c08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What is the main characteristic of connective tissue? Which response is correct?', '["Only fibers without cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)","Cells arranged in sheets mediated by specific receptor activation","Closely packed cells with minimal ECM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","extracellular matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells scattered within an abundant extracellular matrix (ECM)', 'Fibroblasts, macrophages, and mast cells sit scattered in a rich extracellular matrix of collagen, elastic fibers, and ground substance, which is the hallmark of connective tissue. The remaining choices describe epithelium or an impossible fiber-only arrangement.'),
('9d189b33-2302-5e1d-8415-9015ea021c77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What are the three basic components of connective tissue? Which option is most accurate?', '["Epithelium and ECM","and ground substance","Cells","Fibers and water","fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Cells', 'Connective tissue is built from three basic components: cells, fibers, and ground substance. Cells are the living component of this triad, while fibers and ground substance form the matrix; epithelium and water are unrelated.'),
('d97e0090-b691-576d-b9c8-af4fc4ce91bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The three basic components of connective tissue are: Which option is most accurate?', '["Epithelium and ECM","and ground substance","Cells","Fibers and water","fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Cells', 'The three components of connective tissue are cells, fibers, and ground substance. Of the options listed, cells is the essential living component of this triad; the other choices are incomplete fragments of the matrix or unrelated substances.'),
('2bffbbac-8980-50a5-a095-705ccb172910'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What are the three basic components of connective tissue? Choose the most accurate statement.', '["Epithelium and ECM","and ground substance","Cells","Fibers and water","fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Cells', 'Connective tissue consists of cells plus fibers and ground substance, which together form the extracellular matrix. Cells are the living element of this triad, whereas epithelium and water do not belong to this classification.'),
('b632da65-c4fe-524e-a228-f04e79aa1ac7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What are the three basic components of connective tissue? Identify the correct response.', '["Epithelium and ECM","and ground substance","Cells","Fibers and water","fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Cells', 'The three basic components of connective tissue are cells, fibers, and ground substance. Fibroblasts and other cells produce the matrix, making cells the key living component among the options presented.'),
('33b97e1a-8f1b-5e14-8443-ea2f36635481'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What are the three basic components of connective tissue? Select the best answer.', '["Epithelium and ECM","and ground substance","Cells","Fibers and water","fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Cells', 'Connective tissue comprises cells, fibers, and ground substance. Cells form the cellular component of this triad, while fibers and ground substance make up the extracellular matrix; epithelium is a different tissue type.'),
('f8f4c54d-b498-5f8d-ae7b-b39cfcdda03e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'What are the three basic components of connective tissue? Which response is correct?', '["Epithelium and ECM","and ground substance","Cells","Fibers and water","fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Cells', 'The three basic components of connective tissue are cells, fibers, and ground substance. Cells are the living component of this triad, so among the given options cells is the appropriate choice.'),
('3f7ca8ec-c865-5772-a08f-23823cce5c8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Which option correctly describes the undifferentiated cells of connective tissue that can differentiate into various cell types?', '["Fibroblasts","Macrophages","Mesenchymal stem cells","Mast cells","Adipocytes a"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells', 'Mesenchymal stem cells are the undifferentiated connective tissue cells capable of differentiating into fibroblasts, adipocytes, chondrocytes, and osteoblasts. Fibroblasts are already differentiated matrix-producing cells, and macrophages and mast cells arise from hematopoietic precursors.'),
('5fcda69d-f882-5cc4-ae23-160d0e94a123'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The undifferentiated cells of connective tissue that can differentiate into various cell types are: Which option is most accurate?', '["Fibroblasts","Macrophages","Mesenchymal stem cells","Mast cells","Adipocytes a"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells', 'Mesenchymal stem cells are the multipotent precursors of connective tissue that give rise to fibroblasts, adipocytes, chondrocytes, and osteoblasts. Fibroblasts, macrophages, and mast cells are already differentiated cell types.'),
('e0db32cb-e0c1-537c-a4be-51f8926626f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The undifferentiated cells of connective tissue that can differentiate into various cell types are: Choose the most accurate statement.', '["Fibroblasts","Macrophages","Mesenchymal stem cells","Mast cells","Adipocytes a"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells', 'Mesenchymal stem cells are undifferentiated connective tissue cells that can mature into fibroblasts, adipocytes, chondrocytes, and osteoblasts. The other listed cells are committed, differentiated forms rather than pluripotent precursors.'),
('77cffa19-8e39-51e5-afa3-5da5814fcea3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The undifferentiated cells of connective tissue that can differentiate into various cell types are: Identify the correct response.', '["Fibroblasts","Macrophages","Mesenchymal stem cells","Mast cells","Adipocytes a"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells', 'Mesenchymal stem cells serve as the source of multiple connective tissue cell types, including fibroblasts, adipocytes, and osteoblasts. Fibroblasts, macrophages, and mast cells are descended from these precursors and are already specialized.'),
('d4cc231e-2166-53a2-a380-cdab8798796d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The undifferentiated cells of connective tissue that can differentiate into various cell types are: Select the best answer.', '["Fibroblasts","Macrophages","Mesenchymal stem cells","Mast cells","Adipocytes a"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells', 'Mesenchymal stem cells are the undifferentiated connective tissue cells that can generate diverse cell types such as fibroblasts, chondrocytes, and osteoblasts. Fibroblasts and macrophages are already differentiated and cannot act as precursors.'),
('8cffc67d-efa2-57ef-b00b-ab7fc71c4131'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The undifferentiated cells of connective tissue that can differentiate into various cell types are: Which response is correct?', '["Fibroblasts","Macrophages","Mesenchymal stem cells","Mast cells","Adipocytes a"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells', 'Mesenchymal stem cells are the multipotent connective tissue precursors that differentiate into fibroblasts, adipocytes, chondrocytes, and osteoblasts. The remaining options represent mature, lineage-committed cells rather than undifferentiated stem cells.'),
('348ce978-437d-50f9-b1ef-417963676706'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Fibroblasts are responsible for: Which option is most accurate?', '["Secreting histamine","Producing collagen","and ground substance ECM components","elastin","Phagocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Producing collagen', 'Fibroblasts synthesize collagen and other extracellular matrix components, including elastin and ground substance. Histamine is secreted by mast cells, and phagocytosis is carried out by macrophages.'),
('ff52c240-62b7-5d47-93ee-a2630c7901ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Fibroblasts are responsible for: Choose the most accurate statement.', '["Secreting histamine","Producing collagen","and ground substance ECM components","elastin","Phagocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Producing collagen', 'Fibroblasts are the principal cells that produce collagen, the major structural protein, along with other matrix components. Secreting histamine belongs to mast cells, and phagocytosis belongs to macrophages.'),
('2108eedd-ef65-5739-b117-2c89e7028341'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Fibroblasts are responsible for: Identify the correct response.', '["Secreting histamine","Producing collagen","and ground substance ECM components","elastin","Phagocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Producing collagen', 'Fibroblasts synthesize collagen and the ground substance components of the extracellular matrix. Histamine secretion is a mast cell function, and phagocytosis is performed by macrophages.'),
('14343927-3f36-535d-8ed3-faa31fa61366'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Fibroblasts are responsible for: Select the best answer.', '["Secreting histamine","Producing collagen","and ground substance ECM components","elastin","Phagocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Producing collagen', 'Fibroblasts are the main cells that produce collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins such as elastin. They do not secrete histamine, which is a function of mast cells, nor do they perform phagocytosis.'),
('39dbca52-0cad-5b4f-9b61-844f0e74a1b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Fibroblasts are responsible for: Which response is correct?', '["Secreting histamine","Producing collagen","and ground substance ECM components","elastin","Phagocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Producing collagen', 'Fibroblasts are responsible for producing collagen and other extracellular matrix components, such as elastin and ground substance. Secreting histamine and phagocytosis are functions of mast cells and macrophages, respectively.'),
('e10ff09e-2174-5795-984f-93c87cc43cac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Which option correctly describes myofibroblasts?', '["Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)","Smooth muscle cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrocytes without function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mast cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)', 'Myofibroblasts are modified fibroblasts that acquire contractile actin and myosin filaments, enabling them to contract wounds and scars. They are not smooth muscle cells, macrophages, or inactive fibrocytes.'),
('881e6d37-9f0c-557c-a0f1-b70e4c855c0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Myofibroblasts are: Which option is most accurate?', '["Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)","Smooth muscle cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrocytes without function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mast cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)', 'Myofibroblasts are fibroblasts with contractile properties derived from the incorporation of actin and myosin filaments, important for wound contraction. This distinguishes them from smooth muscle cells and immune cells.'),
('f712078c-a72a-5af7-a50e-c8620581ec28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Myofibroblasts are: Choose the most accurate statement.', '["Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)","Smooth muscle cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrocytes without function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mast cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)', 'Myofibroblasts are fibroblasts that have developed contractile properties through the presence of actin and myosin filaments, enabling them to contract scar tissue. They are distinct from smooth muscle cells and from non-contractile fibrocytes.'),
('8d7ffe81-7bcd-5524-8088-279d2a490431'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Myofibroblasts are: Identify the correct response.', '["Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)","Smooth muscle cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrocytes without function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mast cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)', 'Myofibroblasts possess contractile properties because they contain actin and myosin filaments, allowing them to contribute to wound and scar contraction. Smooth muscle cells, macrophages, and mast cells are separate cell lineages.'),
('9aac747d-d8d5-5766-9441-1fe766abaf00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Myofibroblasts are: Select the best answer.', '["Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)","Smooth muscle cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrocytes without function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mast cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)', 'Myofibroblasts are contractile fibroblasts that contain actin and myosin filaments, allowing them to pull wound edges together during healing. They are not smooth muscle cells, macrophages, or functionless fibrocytes.'),
('3eeff847-0852-569a-ad7a-5869625f272a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Myofibroblasts are: Which response is correct?', '["Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)","Smooth muscle cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrocytes without function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mast cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblasts with contractile properties (contain actin and myosin)', 'Myofibroblasts are fibroblasts with contractile properties, holding actin and myosin filaments that enable them to contract and promote wound healing. The other options describe unrelated cell types.'),
('6f866264-84a8-5f3c-bc8f-08f213a8365d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Macrophages are derived from: Which option is most accurate?', '["Neutrophils resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Platelets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)"]'::jsonb, 'Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)', 'Tissue macrophages arise from circulating blood monocytes, which emigrate into tissues and mature into resident macrophages. Neutrophils, lymphocytes, and platelets belong to other lineages and do not give rise to macrophages.'),
('571ae74d-3bee-5951-bf95-c11260e03c81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Macrophages are derived from: Choose the most accurate statement.', '["Neutrophils resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Platelets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)"]'::jsonb, 'Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)', 'Macrophages differentiate from blood monocytes that leave the circulation and enter tissues, where they mature into macrophages. Neutrophils, lymphocytes, and platelets are separate blood cell types and not their precursors.'),
('e4d74149-66bd-5407-8be7-bf92251ebaf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Macrophages are derived from: Identify the correct response.', '["Neutrophils resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Platelets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)"]'::jsonb, 'Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)', 'The precursors of tissue macrophages are circulating blood monocytes, which migrate through vessel walls into tissues and differentiate. Neutrophils, lymphocytes, and platelets are distinct cell lineages rather than sources of macrophages.'),
('d18d231e-2932-58aa-a0fd-cea61cb6022d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Macrophages are derived from: Select the best answer.', '["Neutrophils resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Platelets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)"]'::jsonb, 'Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)', 'Macrophages originate from blood monocytes, which enter tissues and mature into tissue macrophages. Neutrophils, lymphocytes, and platelets come from different hematopoietic lineages and do not develop into macrophages.'),
('0befd393-0da8-50a5-bc7d-38c3d05e797c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Macrophages are derived from: Which response is correct?', '["Neutrophils resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Platelets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)"]'::jsonb, 'Monocytes (blood-borne precursors)', 'Tissue macrophages descend from blood monocytes that migrate into tissue and mature. Fibroblasts, neutrophils, lymphocytes, and platelets are all different cell types that do not give rise to macrophages.'),
('ecced8a8-0f4b-5cfb-ad90-4a125ef65277'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'Which option correctly describes the primary function of macrophages?', '["and apoptotic cells","Phagocytosis of debris","Producing collagen","microorganisms","Secreting mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Phagocytosis of debris', 'The primary function of macrophages is phagocytosis of cellular debris, apoptotic cells, and microorganisms, along with antigen presentation. Collagen production is a fibroblast role, and mucus secretion is a function of glandular epithelium.'),
('d081b4b9-2dc2-5afc-8984-4bafa840f417'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Connective Tissue Bone Cartilage', 'The primary function of macrophages is: Which option is most accurate?', '["and apoptotic cells","Phagocytosis of debris","Producing collagen","microorganisms","Secreting mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Phagocytosis of debris', 'Macrophages primarily engulf and digest debris, apoptotic cells, and microorganisms through phagocytosis. They do not produce collagen, which is done by fibroblasts, and they do not secrete mucus.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('bedb4901-956a-5080-8411-4f997e420b4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'What is the primary characteristic of epithelial tissue?', '["Rounded cells within abundant extracellular matrix","Spindle-shaped cells with little ECM","Multinucleated cells with ECM","Closely aggregated cells with minimal extracellular matrix","Cells arranged in spirals"]'::jsonb, 'Closely aggregated cells with minimal extracellular matrix', 'Epithelial tissue consists of closely packed, polyhedral cells with very little extracellular matrix, unlike connective tissue in which cells are separated by abundant matrix. Rounded cells in matrix and spindle-shaped cells describe connective tissue elements rather than epithelia.'),
('46374074-8ee6-5128-b154-5949568922fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Epithelial tissue is derived from which primary germ layers?', '["Ectoderm","and mesoderm","Mesoderm only","endoderm","Ectoderm and endoderm only"]'::jsonb, 'Ectoderm', 'Epithelia arise from all three germ layers: ectoderm forms the epidermis, endoderm lines the gut and respiratory tract, and mesoderm gives the mesotheliomas and kidney tubules. No choice lists the complete set, so Ectoderm is the only intact, valid germ-layer source offered.'),
('23366375-b642-5eae-92f7-dd6ce2b5f8e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Simple squamous epithelium is best described as:', '["Multiple layers of flattened cells","scale-like cells","Single layer of cube-shaped cells","Single layer of flattened","Single layer of tall columnar cells"]'::jsonb, 'Single layer of flattened', 'Simple squamous epithelium is a single layer of thin, flat cells specialized for diffusion and filtration. Multiple layers, cube-shaped cells, and tall columnar cells each describe different epithelial types.'),
('b67118a6-7cfb-5503-ade7-53f98366312b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Where is simple squamous epithelium found?', '["Trachea lining resulting in decreased functional activity","Lining of blood vessels (endothelium)","Bladder lining mediated by specific receptor activation","Stomach lining without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epidermis of skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lining of blood vessels (endothelium)', 'Simple squamous epithelium lines blood and lymphatic vessels as endothelium, and body cavities as mesothelium, where its thinness allows rapid exchange. The trachea, bladder, stomach, and epidermis are lined by taller or multi-layered epithelia.'),
('e01dc66b-9c1b-5875-9773-41f475fdf06e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Simple cuboidal epithelium is found in:', '["Kidney tubules and thyroid follicles","Bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epidermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Kidney tubules and thyroid follicles', 'Simple cuboidal epithelium covers kidney tubules, thyroid follicles, and many glandular ducts, performing secretion and absorption. The bladder, blood vessels, trachea, and epidermis are lined by other epithelial types.'),
('c748204a-f726-5a25-a6a4-5a4a835313d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Simple columnar epithelium is characterized by:', '["Cube-shaped cells","elongated cells with nuclei near the base","Tall","Flat cells with central nuclei","Stratified arrangement"]'::jsonb, 'Tall', 'Simple columnar epithelium is made of tall, column-like cells that are taller than they are wide, with oval nuclei near the base. Cube-shaped cells, flat cells, and a stratified arrangement describe other epithelial types.'),
('16e93b51-7698-5569-8a1a-fd81569da643'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Where is non-ciliated simple columnar epithelium found?', '["Stomach and intestines","Ventricles of brain","Oviducts","Trachea and bronchi","Spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach and intestines', 'Non-ciliated simple columnar epithelium lines the stomach and intestines, where it absorbs nutrients and secretes mucus and enzymes. The oviducts and airways carry ciliated versions, and the brain and spinal cord are not lined by this epithelium.'),
('2b051d53-4ed8-5cda-9aa2-d0d90969cb5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Where is ciliated simple columnar epithelium found?', '["Epidermis resulting in decreased functional activity","Bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oral cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","Esophagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oviducts (fallopian tubes) and small bronchi"]'::jsonb, 'Oviducts (fallopian tubes) and small bronchi', 'Ciliated simple columnar epithelium lines the oviducts, where it propels the ovum, and the small bronchi, where cilia move mucus upward. The epidermis, bladder, oral cavity, and esophagus are lined by other epithelial types.'),
('c945a718-d275-5517-86b6-e3cf61136871'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Pseudostratified columnar epithelium appears stratified because:', '["Nuclei are at varying levels","Some cells are squamous","Nuclei are all at the same level","There are multiple layers of cells","but all cells contact the basement membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclei are at varying levels', 'Although every pseudostratified cell touches the basement membrane, the nuclei lie at different heights, creating a false impression of several layers. There is no true stacking of cells, and nuclei at one level would look simple rather than stratified.'),
('464588ac-da6c-5449-abe4-6b083597485a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium is characteristic of:', '["Vagina","Oral cavity","Urinary bladder","Esophagus","Trachea and larger airways"]'::jsonb, 'Trachea and larger airways', 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the trachea and larger airways, where coordinated cilia sweep mucus toward the pharynx. The vagina, oral cavity, bladder, and esophagus carry different epithelial linings.'),
('2ce33ad6-d03d-5036-98d5-6ab82a6860c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Stratified squamous keratinized epithelium is found in:', '["Vagina","Oral mucosa","Esophagus","Epidermis of the skin","Cornea"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermis of the skin', 'The epidermis is the prototype of stratified squamous keratinized epithelium, where surface cells fill with keratin to resist abrasion and water loss. The vagina, oral mucosa, esophagus, and cornea are non-keratinized.'),
('4a422f14-fbf5-5bd4-b6c0-cafb38918bdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium is found in:', '["Oral cavity","Trachea","esophagus","Epidermis","and vagina"]'::jsonb, 'Oral cavity', 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium lines the oral cavity, esophagus, and vagina, withstanding friction without forming a cornified layer. Among the choices, the oral cavity is the intact site; the epidermis is keratinized and the trachea is pseudostratified.'),
('16863777-eeb7-5662-8367-df5b820fbeee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Stratified cuboidal epithelium is relatively rare and found in:', '["Ducts of sweat glands and developing ovarian follicles","Stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Large arteries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epidermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ducts of sweat glands and developing ovarian follicles', 'Stratified cuboidal epithelium is uncommon and lines the ducts of sweat glands and the walls of developing ovarian follicles, providing two or more cuboidal layers. The stomach, trachea, large arteries, and epidermis have different linings.'),
('795d9e2c-0e7a-5f07-891f-b8495097518a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Transitional epithelium (urothelium) is characterized by:', '["Simple columnar cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Large superficial cells (umbrella cells) that can change shape","layers mediated by specific receptor activation","Pseudostratified arrangement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multiple layers of flattened cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large superficial cells (umbrella cells) that can change shape', 'Transitional epithelium is unique in having large dome-shaped superficial umbrella cells that flatten as the bladder distends, allowing the lining to accommodate stretch. This shape change distinguishes urothelium from ordinary stratified or simple epithelia.'),
('142e35f4-a50c-5b40-81ac-5f970a9a890e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The basement membrane consists of:', '["Reticular fibers and collagen only","Elliptical fibers","Basal lamina only","Reticular lamina only","Basal lamina and reticular lamina"]'::jsonb, 'Basal lamina and reticular lamina', 'The basement membrane comprises the basal lamina, secreted by epithelial cells, and the underlying reticular lamina, produced by connective tissue cells. Either lamina alone, or collagen fibers alone, represents only a fraction of the whole structure.'),
('8cc43943-6c10-5371-ba95-7d3526dc413f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which is a major component of the basal lamina?', '["Fibrillin","Decorin a","Type IV collagen","Elastin","Type I collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV collagen', 'Type IV collagen forms the lattice-work meshwork that is the major structural component of the basal lamina. Fibrillin, elastin, and type I collagen belong to other connective tissue structures, and decorin is a proteoglycan of the matrix.'),
('c2e493cf-b440-5774-a9f3-3df813171853'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The basal lamina functions include:', '["Regulation of cell behavior","Barrier function","All of the above","Cell attachment","Structural support and filtration"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The basal lamina anchors and supports cells, acts as a selective barrier, regulates cell behavior, and participates in filtration in the renal glomerulus. Because every listed function is genuine, All of the above is the answer.'),
('f5108ebb-a570-5415-88be-aa8cbb688fbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Laminin is a glycoprotein found in:', '["Collagen fibrils","Reticular fibers","Hyaluronic acid","Basal lamina","Elastic fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Basal lamina', 'Laminin is a large cross-shaped glycoprotein of the basal lamina that links cell surface integrins to type IV collagen and other matrix molecules. It does not reside in collagen fibrils, reticular fibers, hyaluronic acid, or elastic fibers.'),
('39e41b7f-4f63-56ba-9e15-1fc881fd9761'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which junction forms a tight seal between adjacent epithelial cells to prevent paracellular leakage?', '["Hemidesmosome","Gap junction","Desmosome (macula adherens)","Adherens junction (zonula adherens)","Tight junction (zonula occludens)"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junction (zonula occludens)', 'The tight junction (zonula occludens) seals adjacent cell membranes near the apex and blocks paracellular diffusion of molecules and ions. Desmosomes and adherens junctions provide adhesion, and gap junctions allow communication, not sealing.'),
('0e4352b3-3376-595f-9bc1-1ead7d8daffd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The transmembrane proteins that form tight junctions are:', '["Connexins","Selectins","Integrins","Claudins and occludins","Cadherins"]'::jsonb, 'Claudins and occludins', 'Claudins and occludins are the transmembrane proteins that polymerize into the sealing strands of tight junctions. Connexins build gap junctions, cadherins form adherens junctions and desmosomes, and integrins bind the extracellular matrix.'),
('03b35193-a04c-5c36-aaf7-3721ced3f8ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Tight junctions are located at the:', '["Basal part of the cell resulting in decreased functional activity","Apical surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Most apical part of the lateral cell membrane","Desmosomal plaques without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemidesmosomes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most apical part of the lateral cell membrane', 'Tight junctions form a belt around each cell at the most apical portion of the lateral cell membrane, just beneath the free surface. They do not occupy the true apical surface, the basal membrane, or desmosomal/hemidesmosomal plaques.'),
('795db56a-1c44-5c13-9ee2-be7ac5ce9939'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which junction is belt-like and surrounds the entire cell circumferentially?', '["Macula adherens (desmosome) resulting in decreased functional activity","Gap junction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Focal adhesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Hemidesmosome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Zonula occludens (tight junction) and zonula adherens"]'::jsonb, 'Zonula occludens (tight junction) and zonula adherens', 'The prefix ''zonula'' denotes a belt-like band encircling the entire cell, as seen with the zonula occludens and zonula adherens. Desmosomes, gap junctions, focal adhesions, and hemidesmosomes are localized spot contacts, not circumferential belts.'),
('95e61376-128b-5919-9a87-4e83089d4439'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The zonula adherens (adherens junction) uses which transmembrane protein?', '["Occludin","Claudin","Connexin","Cadherin (E-cadherin)","Integrin a"]'::jsonb, 'Cadherin (E-cadherin)', 'The zonula adherens uses calcium-dependent cadherins, chiefly E-cadherin, to bind adjacent cells while actin filaments insert on the cytoplasmic plaques. Occludins and claudins belong to tight junctions, connexins to gap junctions, and integrins to matrix attachments.'),
('5e312838-7cd0-5341-a487-0102206e92ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Desmosomes (macula adherens) are characterized by:', '["Gap between cells with connexons resulting in decreased functional activity","Electron-dense plaques on both cytoplasmic sides with intermediate filaments","Actin filament attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","visible plaques without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sealing of the intercellular space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Electron-dense plaques on both cytoplasmic sides with intermediate filaments', 'Desmosomes show electron-dense plaques on both cytoplasmic faces into which keratin intermediate filaments insert, providing strong rivet-like adhesion. They do not seal the space, connect through actin, or form connexon channels.'),
('1f631723-8390-51ad-9e16-bcca02746d81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The transmembrane cadherins of desmosomes are:', '["VE-cadherin","N-cadherin","P-cadherin","E-cadherin","Desmoglein and desmocollin"]'::jsonb, 'Desmoglein and desmocollin', 'Desmogleins and desmocollins are the desmosomal cadherins that span the intercellular gap and link the plaques of adjacent cells. E-, N-, P-, and VE-cadherins operate in other adhesion complexes, not the desmosome.'),
('4f7d1958-e1e9-5aa5-b0b6-e40a94b42961'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Hemidesmosomes attach epithelial cells to:', '["Apical surface","Adjacent cells","Basal surface only of columnar cells","Basal lamina (extracellular matrix)","Lateral membranes"]'::jsonb, 'Basal lamina (extracellular matrix)', 'Hemidesmosomes anchor the basal side of epithelial cells to the basal lamina, securing the epithelium to the underlying extracellular matrix. They are not found on apical, lateral, or adjacent-cell interfaces.'),
('2ba7bd66-5d58-5c69-ac3f-886d43e09eea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The intracellular attachment of hemidesmosomes is to:', '["Intermediate filaments (keratins)","Desmin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vimentin mediated by specific receptor activation","Actin filaments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Microtubules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate filaments (keratins)', 'On the cytoplasmic side, hemidesmosomes connect to intermediate filaments composed of keratins, tying the cell to the basal lamina. Actin, desmin, vimentin, and microtubules are associated with other cytoskeletal anchorages.'),
('a83186b4-c126-5830-9499-e5fb265e3968'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Gap junctions are composed of proteins called:', '["Integrins resulting in decreased functional activity","Connexins (forming connexons)","Claudins mediated by specific receptor activation","Cadherins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Occludins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connexins (forming connexons)', 'Gap junctions are built from connexins, six of which assemble into a hemicanal called a connexon; connexons from adjacent cells pair to form the communicating channel. Claudins and occludins make tight junctions, cadherins make adherens junctions, and integrins bind matrix.'),
('8c2c2011-a6a7-5ece-95f7-5be71bedd77e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Gap junctions allow passage of:', '["RNA a resulting in decreased functional activity","Whole organelles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small molecules and ions (< 1 kDa)","Large proteins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small molecules and ions (< 1 kDa)', 'Gap junction channels permit free diffusion of small molecules and ions below about 1 kDa, enabling metabolic and electrical coupling between cells. RNA, DNA, and whole organelles are far too large to pass through.'),
('1459746f-49c1-5cbe-87aa-6a7757a8cd55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which junction allows direct electrical coupling between cells?', '["Hemidesmosome","Tight junction","Adherens junction","Gap junction","Desmosome"]'::jsonb, 'Gap junction', 'Gap junctions form low-resistance ion channels that allow direct passage of ions between cells, producing electrical coupling in cardiac muscle and smooth muscle. Tight, adherens, hemidesmosomal, and desmosomal junctions do not conduct current.'),
('3aed8c0d-cc5a-59ac-b5f8-ad3cda4aec23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Microvilli are finger-like projections of the:', '["Basal cell membrane","Entire cell","Nucleus","Lateral cell membrane","Apical cell membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Apical cell membrane', 'Microvilli project from the apical (free) cell membrane, greatly increasing surface area for absorption. They are not found on the basal or lateral membranes or at the nucleus.'),
('c5c0f462-9028-50cb-80db-b38d59a99ade'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The core of microvilli contains:', '["Actin filaments (bundled)","Microtubules","Myosin filaments","Intermediate filaments","Keratin filaments"]'::jsonb, 'Actin filaments (bundled)', 'Each microvillus contains a core of bundled actin filaments anchored in the terminal web and linked laterally to the membrane. Microtubules, myosin filaments, and intermediate or keratin filaments are not part of the microvillar core.'),
('31a376e8-2615-5440-9226-bb2980693eda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Stereocilia are actually:', '["Folded cilia","Modified cilia","Long","branched microvilli (not true cilia)","True cilia with motile function"]'::jsonb, 'Long', 'Stereocilia are long, non-motile microvillus-like extensions, so the ''Long'' option captures their defining elongated microvillar nature. True cilia contain a 9+2 microtubule axoneme and are motile, whereas stereocilia are neither folded nor truly branched, nor motile.'),
('d79308fc-8230-55f5-972e-ff9d813e8498'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Where are stereocilia found?', '["Trachea resulting in decreased functional activity","Oviducts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchi mediated by specific receptor activation","Nasal cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epididymis and hair cells of inner ear"]'::jsonb, 'Epididymis and hair cells of inner ear', 'Stereocilia occur in the epididymis, where they enhance absorption, and on the hair cells of the inner ear, where they serve mechanosensation. The trachea, oviducts, bronchi, and nasal cavity carry true cilia, not stereocilia.'),
('5993ab3a-7492-5401-a865-b11cd70c6ac7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Cilia are motile structures containing:', '["Intermediate filaments","Myosin","Thick filaments","Microtubules (9+2 arrangement)","Actin filaments"]'::jsonb, 'Microtubules (9+2 arrangement)', 'Motile cilia contain a 9+2 axoneme of microtubules, with nine peripheral doublets surrounding a central pair. Actin, myosin, thick filaments, and intermediate filaments are not components of the ciliary core.'),
('6fddca23-a2f9-5365-ba20-7b32893e1c0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Primary (non-motile) cilia have a:', '["microtubules","Actin core","axoneme a","9+2 arrangement","9+0 microtubule arrangement"]'::jsonb, '9+0 microtubule arrangement', 'Primary (non-motile) cilia lack the central pair of microtubules, giving them a 9+0 arrangement; they act as sensory organelles. The 9+2 pattern characterizes motile cilia, and an actin core is found in microvilli instead.'),
('a17041e7-93d8-5ac9-bd34-fd6e946d3429'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Dynein is a motor protein that powers ciliary movement by:', '["Sliding outer microtubule doublets past each other","Rotating the central pair","Bending intermediate filaments","Pushing the cell forward","Pulling actin filaments"]'::jsonb, 'Sliding outer microtubule doublets past each other', 'Dynein arms project from each outer doublet and, using ATP, cause the doublets to slide relative to one another; this sliding is converted into bending of the cilium. It does not rotate the central pair, bend intermediate filaments, or act on actin.'),
('9a7c104b-79ea-5ae2-ba9b-d005747bd460'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Ciliary movement requires energy from:', '["ATP hydrolysis","CAMP","GTP hydrolysis","NADH","Calcium"]'::jsonb, 'ATP hydrolysis', 'Ciliary dynein is an ATPase, so ciliary beating is driven directly by ATP hydrolysis. GTP, cyclic AMP, and NADH do not power this motor, and calcium only regulates the beat rather than supplying its energy.'),
('093003fe-1819-5424-a530-c8d913cb1453'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome) is due to defects in:', '["Cadherins","Dynein arms","Connexins","Claudins a","Integrins"]'::jsonb, 'Dynein arms', 'Primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome) results from defective dynein arms in the axoneme, leaving cilia immotile and impairing mucus clearance. Cadherins, connexins, claudins, and integrins are unrelated to ciliary motility.'),
('101d82a2-608b-5f99-b854-91e8a7867739'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Kartagener syndrome classically presents with:', '["deafness","chronic sinusitis","Cleft palate","Situs inversus","and bronchiectasis"]'::jsonb, 'Situs inversus', 'Kartagener syndrome classically includes the triad of situs inversus, chronic sinusitis, and bronchiectasis; among the intact choices, situs inversus is the hallmark feature. The other items listed are fragmented components or unrelated findings.'),
('1d1d60eb-8f9e-5807-bad3-548d53f366f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The glycocalyx is:', '["The lysosomal membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","A carbohydrate-rich layer on the apical surface of cells","The cell coat on the basolateral surface mediated by specific receptor activation","The nuclear envelope without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The basal lamina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A carbohydrate-rich layer on the apical surface of cells', 'The glycocalyx is a carbohydrate-rich cell coat on the apical surface, formed by oligosaccharide chains of membrane glycoproteins and glycolipids. It is not the lysosomal membrane, nuclear envelope, basal lamina, or a basolateral coat.'),
('d7ac8164-08e7-51cb-b103-9063b1e7f0ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Terminal web is a network of:', '["Intermediate filaments at desmosomes","Actin and myosin filaments at the base of microvilli","Microtubules at the cell apex","Collagen fibers","Keratin filaments at the base"]'::jsonb, 'Actin and myosin filaments at the base of microvilli', 'The terminal web is a horizontal mesh of actin and myosin filaments just beneath the microvilli, into which the actin cores of microvilli anchor. It is not composed of intermediate filaments, keratin, microtubules, or collagen.'),
('22ca03fd-e8f0-544f-80b8-dd8c07e5def7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which type of epithelium lines the urinary bladder?', '["Transitional epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar","Stratified squamous","Simple columnar","Simple squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Transitional epithelium', 'The urinary bladder is lined by transitional epithelium (urothelium), whose superficial umbrella cells flatten as the bladder stretches. Pseudostratified, stratified squamous, and simple epithelia line other organs.'),
('7dc04b35-31aa-5d99-80d8-d9352390f122'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which epithelium lines the esophagus?', '["Transitional","Pseudostratified columnar","Stratified squamous keratinized","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous non-keratinized"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The esophagus is lined by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, which tolerates friction during swallowing without cornification. Keratinized, transitional, pseudostratified, and simple columnar linings belong to other sites.'),
('c613b383-8d77-5a80-b6b6-68ae9c4fe530'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Goblet cells are found in which type of epithelium?', '["Simple cuboidal resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple columnar and pseudostratified columnar","Stratified squamous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple squamous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar and pseudostratified columnar', 'Goblet cells are unicellular mucous glands scattered within simple columnar and pseudostratified columnar epithelia, as in the intestines and respiratory tract. Simple cuboidal, transitional, stratified, and simple squamous linings do not contain them.'),
('41825316-b1be-5f78-a954-e77ec293b005'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Goblet cells secrete:', '["Antibodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Serous fluid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enzymes mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucin (forms mucus when hydrated)","Hormones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mucin (forms mucus when hydrated)', 'Goblet cells are unicellular mucous glands that synthesize and secrete mucin, a glycoprotein that forms mucus when hydrated. They do not produce antibodies, serous fluid, digestive enzymes, or hormones.'),
('94202a7e-f77a-525d-a971-a6b38d3f6b79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The nucleus of a goblet cell is:', '["Apically located","Basally located","Central","flattened or triangular","Rounded at the apex"]'::jsonb, 'Basally located', 'Mucigen granules fill the apex of a goblet cell, pushing and compressing the nucleus toward the base. Apical, central, or apically rounded positions are not consistent with this secretory organization.'),
('69c7e37c-db1e-56be-abb3-b2c2caba6a0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which epithelium is best adapted for rapid diffusion?', '["Stratified squamous","Simple squamous","Pseudostratified","Simple columnar","Transitional"]'::jsonb, 'Simple squamous', 'A single thin layer of flat cells minimizes the diffusion distance, making simple squamous epithelium ideal for rapid gas and fluid exchange. Thick, tall, or multi-layered epithelia slow the passage of molecules.'),
('0a57c63a-7194-550e-a3b2-61b0519f4ebb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which epithelium is best adapted for absorption?', '["Pseudostratified","Simple squamous","Transitional","Stratified squamous","Simple columnar with microvilli"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar with microvilli', 'Simple columnar epithelium with microvilli provides a large apical surface area for absorption, as in the small intestine. Flat, thick, or multi-layered epithelia are not specialized for taking up nutrients.'),
('7fd2f8ba-0a02-53c3-a948-be42328f4069'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which epithelium is best adapted for protection against abrasion?', '["Stratified squamous (keratinized or non-keratinized)","Simple cuboidal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transitional mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple squamous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple columnar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous (keratinized or non-keratinized)', 'Stratified squamous epithelium, keratinized or non-keratinized, has multiple layers of cells that withstand friction and abrasion, with keratin adding extra resistance. Thin, single-layered epithelia provide little protection against wear.'),
('b5d6e42c-d01f-5ab0-9419-4a174ab03551'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which epithelium is best adapted for stretch and distension?', '["Simple squamous","Pseudostratified","Transitional epithelium","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Transitional epithelium', 'Transitional epithelium (urothelium) is uniquely able to change shape as the bladder distends: its surface cells flatten when stretched and bulge when relaxed, accommodating large volume changes without tearing. The other listed epithelia lack this reversible change in cell shape.'),
('99584f87-73e1-5b53-bf5f-a2b4dfe3e677'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The brush border (striated border) seen in light microscopy corresponds to:', '["Stereocilia","Tight junctions","Cilia","Glycocalyx","Microvilli"]'::jsonb, 'Microvilli', 'The brush or striated border is the light-microscopic appearance of tightly packed apical microvilli on absorptive cells such as enterocytes and proximal tubule cells. Stereocilia are longer non-motile processes, cilia are motile, and the glycocalyx is a surface carbohydrate coat.'),
('d3e57744-6326-5949-96d4-6579853599c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The main calcium-dependent adhesion molecules in adherens junctions are:', '["Ig superfamily","Connexins","Selectins","Integrins","Cadherins"]'::jsonb, 'Cadherins', 'Cadherins are the calcium-dependent transmembrane adhesion molecules that mediate homophilic cell-cell binding in adherens junctions and desmosomes. Connexins form gap junctions, selectins mediate leukocyte adhesion, integrins bind the extracellular matrix, and Ig superfamily members are calcium-independent.'),
('120c5392-66af-5201-8ea9-e4d155b0b851'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which adhesion molecule is involved in leukocyte homing?', '["Selectins","Cadherins","Claudins a","Integrins","Connexins"]'::jsonb, 'Selectins', 'Selectins are lectin-like adhesion molecules that mediate the initial rolling of leukocytes on activated endothelium, a key step in leukocyte homing and extravasation. Cadherins mediate cell-cell adhesion, claudins form tight junctions, integrins bind matrix, and connexins form gap junctions.'),
('4a5f544e-f537-529d-8b90-df81ef30e348'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Integrins are:', '["Gap junction proteins","Tight junction proteins","Transmembrane receptors linking ECM to cytoskeleton","Intermediate filament proteins","Cell-cell adhesion molecules only"]'::jsonb, 'Transmembrane receptors linking ECM to cytoskeleton', 'Integrins are transmembrane receptors whose extracellular domains bind extracellular matrix components and whose cytoplasmic domains link to the actin cytoskeleton, mediating cell-matrix adhesion and signaling. Connexins and claudins belong to gap and tight junctions, and intermediate filaments are not transmembrane receptors.'),
('99b41f66-92fd-5f85-af82-e314610dcfd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The most common intercellular junction in the heart (intercalated discs) includes:', '["Desmosomes only","desmosomes","and gap junctions","Tight junctions only","Fascia adherens"]'::jsonb, 'Fascia adherens', 'Fascia adherens is the belt-like adherens junction of the intercalated disc of cardiac muscle, anchoring actin filaments at the ends of cardiomyocytes. Desmosomes and gap junctions also contribute to the intercalated disc, but fascia adherens is a major defining component.'),
('dcd3ad7e-9646-5472-870f-3dabfa8c2aa7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Pemphigus vulgaris is an autoimmune disease against:', '["Desmoglein 3 (desmosomal cadherin)","Laminin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Claudin a mediated by specific receptor activation","Type IV collagen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Integrin beta 4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Desmoglein 3 (desmosomal cadherin)', 'Pemphigus vulgaris is caused by autoantibodies against desmoglein 3, a desmosomal cadherin, leading to loss of cell-cell adhesion in the epidermis with acantholysis. Laminin, claudin, type IV collagen, and integrin beta-4 are not the autoantigen targets in this disease.'),
('0a59c976-56aa-5224-a2e7-d4be0ef7ef93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Epidermolysis bullosa (some forms) is caused by mutations in:', '["Desmoglein 3 resulting in decreased functional activity","Claudin 1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occludin mediated by specific receptor activation","Keratin 5/14 or type VII collagen","Connexin 26 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Keratin 5/14 or type VII collagen', 'Mutations in keratin 5/14 cause epidermolysis bullosa simplex and type VII collagen mutations cause the dystrophic form, both impairing the dermal-epidermal junction. The other options are not implicated in this blistering disorder.'),
('4ff1ecee-c917-5fbb-8a87-5d38536185c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Enterocytes (intestinal absorptive cells) have abundant:', '["mitochondria","Cilia","and rough ER","Microvilli","Glycocalyx only"]'::jsonb, 'Microvilli', 'Enterocytes possess a brush border of densely packed apical microvilli that greatly increase the absorptive surface area. Cilia and glycocalyx are not the defining adaptation, and mitochondria, though plentiful, are intracellular organelles rather than a surface feature.'),
('801522bc-ad08-5e96-bb44-46c4f776a908'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Paneth cells are found in the:', '["Small intestinal crypts (base)","Trachea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gastric pits mediated by specific receptor activation","Colon crypts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oviducts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small intestinal crypts (base)', 'Paneth cells are located at the base of the small intestinal crypts (crypts of Lieberkuhn), where they secrete antimicrobial peptides such as lysozyme and defensins. They are not found in the trachea, gastric pits, colon crypts, or oviducts.'),
('5ee0ea27-488b-58b3-84d2-8ab9c830d6a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which epithelium has the fastest turnover rate?', '["Stratified squamous epithelium of the epidermis","Simple cuboidal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transitional mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple squamous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pseudostratified leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous epithelium of the epidermis', 'The stratified squamous epithelium of the epidermis has the fastest turnover of all epithelia; basal cells divide continuously and keratinocytes reach the surface and desquamate within roughly two to four weeks. The simple and transitional epithelia renew much more slowly.'),
('1880e763-4a0b-517d-b6ce-1cfa96fbeff8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The keratin in epithelial cells is a type of:', '["Intermediate filament","Microtubule","Microfilament","Thick filament","Thin filament"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate filament', 'Keratin is the intermediate filament of epithelial cells, forming durable cytoplasmic networks anchored to desmosomes. It is distinct from microtubules, actin microfilaments, and the thick and thin filaments of muscle.'),
('2faa7544-b1ca-5360-adab-89c0bacf7037'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Simple cuboidal epithelium of the thyroid follicles is involved in:', '["Absorption of nutrients resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretion of thyroglobulin and absorption of colloid","Filtration mediated by specific receptor activation","Protection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diffusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secretion of thyroglobulin and absorption of colloid', 'Simple cuboidal follicular cells of the thyroid secrete thyroglobulin into the colloid and later absorb it back for hormone synthesis. Absorption of nutrients, filtration, protection, and diffusion describe functions of other epithelia.'),
('d04d1015-8d84-5f1a-b925-604d84a2bc76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The mesothelium is a type of:', '["Simple cuboidal epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium lining body cavities","Stratified squamous epithelium","Pseudostratified epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Simple squamous epithelium lining body cavities', 'Mesothelium is the simple squamous epithelium that lines serous body cavities such as the pleura, pericardium, and peritoneum. It is not cuboidal, stratified, transitional, or pseudostratified.'),
('8cb71893-3e8f-56b6-8c72-f9af54a6f5f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The endothelium is a type of:', '["Stratified squamous epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudostratified epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple squamous epithelium lining blood and lymphatic vessels","Transitional epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple cuboidal epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple squamous epithelium lining blood and lymphatic vessels', 'Endothelium is the simple squamous epithelium lining the inner surface of blood and lymphatic vessels. It differs from mesothelium, which lines serous cavities, and from stratified or cuboidal epithelia.'),
('b7c4ed3d-b5f1-5afd-bea4-bc645704ecad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which junction is most responsible for the blood-brain barrier?', '["Gap junctions resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemidesmosomes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Desmosomes mediated by specific receptor activation","Tight junctions between endothelial cells","Adherens junctions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between endothelial cells', 'Tight junctions between brain capillary endothelial cells form the structural basis of the blood-brain barrier by sealing the paracellular pathway. Gap junctions, desmosomes, adherens junctions, and hemidesmosomes cannot create such an impermeable seal.'),
('923c4a82-1ab3-58bb-a2c1-76f73bdf1bf9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Podocytes are specialized epithelial cells found in:', '["Intestinal villi","Renal glomerulus","Lung alveoli","Choroid plexus","Epidermis"]'::jsonb, 'Renal glomerulus', 'Podocytes are visceral epithelial cells of the renal glomerulus whose interdigitating foot processes form the filtration slits of the glomerular filtration barrier. They are not found in intestinal villi, alveoli, choroid plexus, or epidermis.'),
('8cc529c2-80a9-5ea5-ac70-d80aefda6167'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Myoepithelial cells are found in:', '["Epidermis","Intestinal crypts","and salivary glands","Mammary","sweat"]'::jsonb, 'Mammary', 'Myoepithelial cells are contractile epithelial cells that surround the secretory acini of the mammary, sweat, and salivary glands and aid in expelling secretions. They are not present in the epidermis or intestinal crypts.'),
('446e5230-4b46-5421-ade5-49e4cd839818'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The epithelium of the cornea is:', '["Transitional","Simple squamous","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Stratified squamous keratinized","Simple columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The anterior corneal epithelium is stratified squamous non-keratinized, providing a moist protective surface that contributes to corneal transparency. Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium covers the skin, not the cornea.'),
('b4398738-eb03-5e7f-8966-efacd824074b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Junctional complex (terminal bar) consists of:', '["zonula adherens","Desmosome and hemidesmosome","and macula adherens (desmosome)","Zonula occludens","Tight and gap junctions only"]'::jsonb, 'Zonula occludens', 'The junctional complex (terminal bar) of epithelial cells consists of the zonula occludens (tight junction), zonula adherens, and macula adherens (desmosome). The zonula occludens is the most apical component of this complex.'),
('b27f2fc2-c122-50e0-b4cc-1bfcb8dc58b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The zonula occludens prevents:', '["Cell contraction","Ciliary movement","Cell adhesion","Paracellular diffusion of molecules","Nuclear transport"]'::jsonb, 'Paracellular diffusion of molecules', 'The zonula occludens (tight junction) seals the intercellular space and blocks paracellular diffusion of molecules and ions. It does not mediate contraction, ciliary movement, cell adhesion, or nuclear transport.'),
('a762384e-952f-5048-8ea2-293fd241c8aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The molecular sieve of tight junctions is determined by:', '["Connexin type","Claudin isoform composition","Cadherin type","Occludin concentration","Integrin expression a"]'::jsonb, 'Claudin isoform composition', 'Claudin isoforms are the major proteins that determine the size and charge selectivity (molecular sieve) of tight junctions. Connexins, cadherins, occludin, and integrins do not set this permeability property.'),
('558cfa41-30f9-5a07-88dc-a3381739cbef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Occludin is a:', '["Adhesion molecule of adherens junctions","Desmosomal cadherin","Gap junction protein","Transmembrane protein of tight junctions","Hemidesmosomal protein"]'::jsonb, 'Transmembrane protein of tight junctions', 'Occludin is a transmembrane protein of tight junctions that contributes to the barrier function of the paracellular seal. It is not part of adherens junctions, desmosomes, gap junctions, or hemidesmosomes.'),
('044a192d-a890-5d01-b7d8-09bff5bd1c82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Adherens junctions provide:', '["Electrical coupling resulting in decreased functional activity","Attachment to the basement membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An impermeable seal mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct cytoplasmic continuity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Strong mechanical adhesion between cells via cadherins and actin"]'::jsonb, 'Strong mechanical adhesion between cells via cadherins and actin', 'Adherens junctions provide strong mechanical adhesion between cells through calcium-dependent cadherins linked to the actin cytoskeleton. Electrical coupling belongs to gap junctions, an impermeable seal to tight junctions, and basement membrane attachment to hemidesmosomes or focal adhesions.'),
('6eea6e4e-1ed1-58ca-89fc-db9704679732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Beta-catenin also functions as a:', '["Transcriptional co-activator in the Wnt signaling pathway","Cell surface receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hormone mediated by specific receptor activation","Extracellular matrix protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Growth factor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transcriptional co-activator in the Wnt signaling pathway', 'Beta-catenin, besides anchoring in adherens junctions, acts as a transcriptional co-activator in the Wnt signaling pathway after accumulating in the nucleus. It is not a cell surface receptor, hormone, extracellular matrix protein, or growth factor.'),
('6b8db728-ad60-527d-9acb-f5338c489552'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The Wnt signaling pathway involves:', '["Connexin assembly resulting in decreased functional activity","Actin polymerization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Claudin expression a mediated by specific receptor activation","Collagen synthesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta-catenin stabilization and nuclear translocation"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-catenin stabilization and nuclear translocation', 'Wnt signaling stabilizes beta-catenin, which then translocates to the nucleus to drive target-gene transcription. Connexin assembly, actin polymerization, claudin expression, and collagen synthesis are not central to this pathway.'),
('34e72d7f-776d-5041-aa0b-e3d69c11fe91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The composition of desmosomal plaques includes:', '["Claudins and occludins","Cadherins and catenins","Connexins","Integrins","Desmoplakin and plakoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Desmoplakin and plakoglobin', 'Desmosomal plaques contain plakoglobin and desmoplakin, which anchor desmosomal cadherins to intermediate filaments. Claudins and occludins belong to tight junctions, cadherins and catenins to adherens junctions, connexins to gap junctions, and integrins to cell-matrix adhesion.'),
('500372e5-d407-59d4-bf2a-51540529681f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Gap junction plaques can contain how many connexons?', '["Only two resulting in decreased functional activity","Single accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","10-20 mediated by specific receptor activation","Hundreds to thousands","Fifty leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hundreds to thousands', 'Gap junction plaques are aggregates of hundreds to thousands of connexons (hexameric connexin channels) clustered at sites of cell-cell contact. Only one, two, or a few dozen channels would not provide the efficient electrical coupling seen at these sites.'),
('6da12d98-efc8-521a-9a28-0c54fcba197d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Gap junction channels allow the passage of:', '["Ca2+","cAMP","IP3","Only ions","and small metabolites"]'::jsonb, 'IP3', 'Gap junction channels permit diffusion of small molecules under about 1 kDa, including ions such as Ca2+, second messengers such as IP3 and cAMP, and small metabolites. IP3 is a classic second messenger that passes readily through these channels.'),
('424ce457-fa40-5a83-ac3f-2fb57e229b1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Connexin 43 is the major gap junction protein in:', '["Skin","Liver","Cardiac muscle","Neurons","Pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac muscle', 'Connexin 43 is the major gap junction protein in cardiac muscle, enabling synchronized contraction through electrical coupling at intercalated discs. Skin, liver, neurons, and pancreas predominantly express different connexin subtypes.'),
('38dd72b6-3118-5166-8421-90f8a6625e33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Connexin 26 mutations cause:', '["Non-syndromic sensorineural deafness","Cardiac arrhythmias accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Cataract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-syndromic sensorineural deafness', 'Mutations in connexin 26 (GJB2) are the most common genetic cause of non-syndromic sensorineural (hereditary) deafness. Cardiac arrhythmias, skin disease, cataract, and liver disease result from mutations in other genes.'),
('3dfe48ec-1760-5418-a917-b8d50ef2b3be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The lateral cell membrane of epithelial cells is specialized for:', '["Cell-cell adhesion and communication","Protection","Secretion of hormones","Excretion a","Diffusion of nutrients"]'::jsonb, 'Cell-cell adhesion and communication', 'The lateral cell membrane of epithelial cells is specialized for cell-cell adhesion and communication through tight junctions, adherens junctions, desmosomes, and gap junctions. Protection, secretion, excretion, and diffusion belong to other cell surfaces.'),
('a2f0a327-e519-5e16-91d5-7aecad97c6ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The basal cell membrane is specialized for:', '["Secretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Absorption of nutrients accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cell-cell adhesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Protection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Attachment to the basement membrane via hemidesmosomes and focal adhesions"]'::jsonb, 'Attachment to the basement membrane via hemidesmosomes and focal adhesions', 'The basal cell membrane attaches the cell to the basement membrane via hemidesmosomes, which link intermediate filaments, and focal adhesions, which link actin filaments. Secretion, absorption, cell-cell adhesion, and protection belong to other membrane domains.'),
('7cd63064-871e-51ea-892e-5392a6443397'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Focal adhesions are:', '["Hemidesmosomal attachments resulting in decreased functional activity","Integrin-mediated attachments linking actin filaments to ECM components","Desmosomal attachments mediated by specific receptor activation","Cadherin-mediated attachments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gap junctions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Integrin-mediated attachments linking actin filaments to ECM components', 'Focal adhesions are integrin-mediated attachment sites that link actin filaments to extracellular matrix components such as fibronectin and laminin. They differ from hemidesmosomes (intermediate filaments) and from desmosomal or cadherin junctions (cell-cell).'),
('a8cf406e-888f-5d4b-af13-13a2c44eb680'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Focal adhesions contain:', '["Integrins","Claudins and occludins","vinculin","and focal adhesion kinase (FAK)","talin"]'::jsonb, 'Integrins', 'Focal adhesions contain integrins, which bind extracellular matrix proteins and connect through cytoplasmic adaptors including talin, vinculin, and focal adhesion kinase (FAK). These complexes mediate cell-matrix adhesion and signaling.'),
('e8053399-2535-5b43-98f1-b224c5d5ac99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Epithelial cells are polarized with distinct:', '["and basal domains","Apical","Cytoplasmic domains","lateral","Nuclear domains only"]'::jsonb, 'Apical', 'Epithelial cells are polarized and possess distinct apical, lateral, and basal membrane domains, each with specialized functions and protein composition. The apical domain faces the lumen and typically carries microvilli or cilia.'),
('d16b2007-5930-5a70-8fcb-3f5d1325e3e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Epithelial to mesenchymal transition (EMT) is important in:', '["Epithelial cell division","wound healing","Embryonic development","Epithelial death","and cancer metastasis"]'::jsonb, 'Embryonic development', 'Epithelial-mesenchymal transition is a process in which epithelial cells lose polarity and cell-cell adhesion and acquire migratory mesenchymal properties; it is essential during embryonic development, for example in gastrulation and neural crest formation. It is later reactivated in wound healing and cancer metastasis.'),
('78d285be-7dc0-55bf-8058-d85dc3603774'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which transcription factor is a key driver of EMT?', '["RUNX2","PAX6","MyoD","GATA4 a","Snail (SNAI1)"]'::jsonb, 'Snail (SNAI1)', 'Snail (SNAI1) is a key transcription factor that represses E-cadherin expression and drives epithelial-mesenchymal transition. RUNX2, PAX6, MyoD, and GATA4 direct osteoblast, ocular, muscle, and cardiac or endodermal fates, not EMT.'),
('19f15893-3ccf-5a1d-9c5e-8b43424f3a18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'E-cadherin is a marker of:', '["Muscle phenotype","Epithelial phenotype","Mesenchymal phenotype","Endothelial phenotype","Neuronal phenotype"]'::jsonb, 'Epithelial phenotype', 'E-cadherin is a cell-cell adhesion molecule characteristic of the epithelial phenotype, expressed at adherens junctions. Mesenchymal cells lose E-cadherin and instead express vimentin.'),
('395e76d3-e762-5c1c-a2a9-5ed9cb017edb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Loss of E-cadherin in cancer is associated with:', '["Better prognosis","Increased invasiveness and metastasis","Improved differentiation a","Increased apoptosis","Decreased proliferation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased invasiveness and metastasis', 'Loss of E-cadherin weakens cell-cell adhesion, promotes epithelial-mesenchymal transition, and is associated with increased invasiveness and metastasis in carcinomas. It correlates with a worse, not better, prognosis.'),
('dbb45223-9c8a-5798-9de6-a8591d9aeac9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Vimentin is a marker of:', '["Epithelial cells","Mesenchymal cells","Cardiac muscle","Neurons","Smooth muscle a"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal cells', 'Vimentin is an intermediate filament protein that marks mesenchymal cells such as fibroblasts and cells that have undergone EMT. Normal epithelial cells express keratins rather than vimentin.'),
('5eba9376-3dc9-5e49-8e01-15bad98eb190'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The basal lamina can be visualized with which special stain?', '["PAS (Periodic acid-Schiff) stain","HE stain","Giemsa stain a","Ziehl-Neelsen stain","Gram stain"]'::jsonb, 'PAS (Periodic acid-Schiff) stain', 'The basal lamina is rich in glycoproteins and glycosaminoglycans, so it stains magenta with the PAS (Periodic acid-Schiff) reaction. Hematoxylin-eosin, Giemsa, Ziehl-Neelsen, and Gram stains do not specifically highlight basement membranes.'),
('7491e56a-59fb-5ccd-96a0-0ddec83cd0b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The lamina lucida (lamina rara) of the basal lamina is adjacent to the:', '["Reticular lamina resulting in decreased functional activity","Collagen fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hemidesmosomes a mediated by specific receptor activation","Connective tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plasma membrane of epithelial cells (via hemidesmosomes)"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma membrane of epithelial cells (via hemidesmosomes)', 'The lamina lucida (lamina rara) is the electron-lucent layer of the basal lamina immediately adjacent to the plasma membrane of the epithelial cell, where hemidesmosomes anchor the cell. The lamina densa and reticular lamina lie further from the cell surface.'),
('238693f9-6fe5-565b-a03a-5c640118488c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The lamina densa of the basal lamina is composed mainly of:', '["Perlecan","Fibronectin","Laminin","Type IV collagen","Collagen Ia"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV collagen', 'The lamina densa is composed mainly of type IV collagen, which forms a meshwork providing structural support to the basement membrane. Laminin predominates in the lamina lucida, and perlecan and fibronectin are other extracellular matrix components.'),
('58a2d7cf-a8dc-5669-bd75-a2467bb10c92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Anchoring fibrils (type VII collagen) attach the:', '["Epithelial cells to the basal lamina","Epithelial cells to each other","Actin to focal adhesions","Intermediate filaments to desmosomes","Basal lamina to the reticular lamina (dermis)"]'::jsonb, 'Basal lamina to the reticular lamina (dermis)', 'Type VII collagen anchoring fibrils extend from the basal lamina into the underlying reticular lamina (dermis), securing the basement membrane to the connective tissue. They do not directly attach epithelial cells to each other or to the basal lamina.'),
('07e75265-5527-58fe-835c-510ef2aac783'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The reticular lamina is produced by:', '["Lymphocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Macrophages accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibroblasts in the underlying connective tissue","Endothelial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epithelial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblasts in the underlying connective tissue', 'The reticular lamina is produced by fibroblasts of the underlying connective tissue and is composed largely of type III (reticular) collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins. The epithelial cells themselves produce the basal lamina.'),
('ec8dc100-fd4f-5ba2-917d-b1bf35860e7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Goodpasture syndrome is an autoimmune disease against:', '["Type I collagen resulting in decreased functional activity","Type III collagen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type IV collagen (alpha-3 chain) in basement membranes","Laminin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type II collagen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV collagen (alpha-3 chain) in basement membranes', 'Goodpasture syndrome results from autoantibodies against the non-collagenous domain of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, causing nephritis and pulmonary hemorrhage. Types I, II, and III collagen and laminin are not the targets.'),
('13b25888-c352-5252-b801-a18eb4858436'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Simple squamous epithelium lining the pleural cavity is called:', '["Endocardium a","Epidermis","Endothelium","Mesothelium","Neuroepithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Mesothelium', 'The simple squamous epithelium lining the pleural cavity, and other serous cavities, is called mesothelium. Endothelium lines blood vessels, and the endocardium lines the chambers of the heart.'),
('12920bd6-24c7-59b9-a2ca-39968f931e5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Simple squamous epithelium lining blood vessels is called:', '["Epidermis","Mesothelium","Myocardium a","Endocardium","Endothelium"]'::jsonb, 'Endothelium', 'The simple squamous epithelium lining blood vessels is called endothelium. Mesothelium lines serous cavities, the endocardium lines the inner surface of the heart, and the epidermis covers the skin.'),
('19f629ce-acf6-59a6-ba58-6c6396258515'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The surface of epithelial cells may have modifications like:', '["cilia","mitochondria","Nuclei","or stereocilia","Microvilli"]'::jsonb, 'Microvilli', 'Epithelial cell surfaces may show modifications such as microvilli for absorption, cilia for motile transport, or stereocilia for sensory or absorptive roles. Nuclei and mitochondria are organelles rather than surface modifications.'),
('55a5e2e2-9784-557c-967e-fe14ae6c220d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which type of epithelium has the most protective function against wear and tear?', '["Simple columnar","Transitional a","Simple squamous","Stratified squamous keratinized","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous keratinized', 'Stratified squamous keratinized epithelium forms multiple layers capped by dead, keratin-filled cells, giving the greatest resistance to friction and desiccation (e.g., skin). Simple epithelia are too thin to offer durable wear resistance, and transitional or pseudostratified types lack this surface keratin layer.'),
('481a34ae-e062-55dd-9554-b0dd347f6895'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The epithelium lining the small intestine is:', '["Simple cuboidal resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple columnar with microvilli and goblet cells","Transitional mediated by specific receptor activation","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stratified squamous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar with microvilli and goblet cells', 'The small intestinal mucosa is a simple columnar epithelium whose absorptive cells bear a brush border of microvilli, interspersed with mucus-secreting goblet cells. Cuboidal, transitional, pseudostratified, or stratified types do not provide this absorptive and mucous lining.'),
('2a327ef6-68d0-5a96-862e-5b08acff3f83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The epithelium lining the stomach is:', '["Stratified squamous","Transitional","Simple columnar (mucous secreting)","Pseudostratified","Simple squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar (mucous secreting)', 'Gastric surface and foveolar cells form a single layer of columnar cells that secrete mucus, shielding the mucosa from acid and enzymes. Stratified squamous, transitional, pseudostratified, or simple squamous linings do not provide this protective secretory surface.'),
('bdf8dbca-cc79-5269-95f8-6cb713a28e61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The epithelium lining the colon is:', '["Simple columnar with many goblet cells","Stratified squamous","Pseudostratified","Simple columnar with microvilli only","Transitional"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar with many goblet cells', 'The colonic mucosa is simple columnar and remarkably rich in goblet cells that produce mucus to lubricate feces. It is not a microvilli-only or stratified/pseudostratified design because its main role is mucus secretion rather than maximal absorption.'),
('aa94dc72-3414-55b5-b9b2-54c16e31b9d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The epithelium of the trachea is:', '["Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Pseudostratified without cilia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stratified squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple columnar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple squamous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells', 'The trachea is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells; the beating cilia sweep mucus and trapped particles toward the pharynx. Pseudostratified epithelium without cilia, stratified, simple columnar, or simple squamous types do not describe the airway lining.'),
('e74c42d3-6f2c-58bb-97c5-720bf50a4e0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The epithelium of the oral cavity (cheek) is:', '["Pseudostratified","Simple columnar","Transitional","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Stratified squamous keratinized"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The buccal mucosa is covered by stratified squamous epithelium that retains its nuclei because the moist environment prevents keratinization. Keratinized squamous epithelium lines drier sites, while simple, transitional, and pseudostratified types are inappropriate for the cheek.'),
('803e259b-5404-5bc5-957c-392b4e35d6ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The epithelium of the hard palate is:', '["Simple columnar resulting in decreased functional activity","Stratified squamous keratinized (similar to skin)","Transitional mediated by specific receptor activation","Pseudostratified without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stratified squamous non-keratinized leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous keratinized (similar to skin)', 'The hard palate bears keratinized stratified squamous epithelium because it endures the friction of chewing against food. Non-keratinized squamous epithelium lines regions subjected to less mechanical stress.'),
('9c706821-dd22-5887-9aa4-127c53f13614'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The epithelium of the vagina is:', '["Simple columnar","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Transitional","Pseudostratified","Stratified squamous keratinized"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The vaginal wall is lined by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, which thickens and desquamates under estrogen influence. Simple, transitional, pseudostratified, and keratinized squamous types do not characterize the moist vaginal lining.'),
('de17a8cf-3cd8-55a4-a4d7-505ac0cc9889'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which adhesion molecule mediates leukocyte extravasation through endothelium?', '["Occludins a","Integrins and selectins","Connexins","Cadherins","Claudins"]'::jsonb, 'Integrins and selectins', 'Selectins initiate leukocyte rolling on endothelium while integrins mediate firm adhesion and transmigration. Occludins, claudins, and connexins are junction proteins, and cadherins support cell-cell adhesion rather than the leukocyte-endothelial recruitment pathway.'),
('c6de6226-a8f9-5c61-a890-5975e74cfce1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'VE-cadherin is specific to:', '["Epithelial adherens junctions","Neural cadherin","Endothelial adherens junctions","Testicular cadherin","Muscle cadherin"]'::jsonb, 'Endothelial adherens junctions', 'VE-cadherin (vascular endothelial cadherin) is the calcium-dependent adhesion molecule of endothelial adherens junctions that stabilizes vessel walls. It is not the cadherin type found in epithelia, nerve, testis, or muscle.'),
('73fde0f0-13c9-5606-a133-5351e06ceb8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'N-cadherin is found in:', '["cardiac muscle","and fibroblasts","Epithelia","Neural tissue","Endothelium"]'::jsonb, 'Neural tissue', 'N-cadherin (neural cadherin) is the defining cadherin of nervous tissue, although it also appears in cardiac muscle. E-cadherin, not N-cadherin, characterizes epithelial cells, and endothelium uses VE-cadherin.'),
('4d02d9e1-891b-50dc-a417-fde465dcf702'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'P-cadherin is found in:', '["Cardiac muscle","Neural tissue","Liver","Placenta and epidermis","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Placenta and epidermis', 'P-cadherin (placental cadherin) is expressed in the placenta and the basal layers of the epidermis. Neural tissue and cardiac muscle are sites of N-cadherin, while the liver mainly uses L-cadherin.'),
('32be2684-e513-5601-889e-449a0e921f8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The basal surface of epithelial cells rests on which structure?', '["Lateral membrane","Apical membrane","Basement membrane","Desmosomes","Terminal web"]'::jsonb, 'Basement membrane', 'Epithelial sheets rest on a basement membrane that separates them from the underlying connective tissue and provides anchorage. Apical and lateral membranes face the lumen or adjacent cells, while desmosomes and the terminal web are junctional or cytoskeletal structures.'),
('a80607b3-c9e7-5f4c-bc88-e21f57b7fd0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'What is the predominant cell-cell adhesion molecule in desmosomes?', '["N-cadherin","E-cadherin","VE-cadherin","OCLN","Desmoglein"]'::jsonb, 'Desmoglein', 'Desmosomes are held together by the cadherin-like desmogleins and desmocollins, which link to intermediate filaments via desmoplakin. N-, E-, and VE-cadherins belong to adherens-type junctions, and OCLN denotes occludin of the tight junction.'),
('45511a75-8f5c-57f3-82ba-7ce3bb077e4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Fibronectin is a:', '["Cytoskeletal protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Proteoglycan accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elastic fiber mediated by specific receptor activation","Major ECM glycoprotein mediating cell adhesion (contains RGD sequence)","Collagen fiber leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Major ECM glycoprotein mediating cell adhesion (contains RGD sequence)', 'Fibronectin is a large extracellular glycoprotein whose RGD domain binds integrins, mediating cell-matrix adhesion. It is neither a cytoskeletal protein, a proteoglycan, an elastic fiber, nor a collagen fibril.'),
('6b1c844a-a741-5e2e-992e-335a7a9301bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Laminin is a major component of the:', '["Bone matrix a","Elastic fibers","Hyaline cartilage","Reticular fibers","Basal lamina"]'::jsonb, 'Basal lamina', 'Laminin is a large cross-shaped glycoprotein that forms the structural scaffold of the basal lamina, linking cells to type IV collagen. Bone matrix, elastic fibers, hyaline cartilage, and reticular fibers depend on other matrix proteins.'),
('509588e7-a846-54d0-a2b9-2701550339eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Entactin (nidogen) is a:', '["Proteoglycan resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastic fiber accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collagen type mediated by specific receptor activation","Integrin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glycoprotein that bridges laminin and type IV collagen in the basal lamina"]'::jsonb, 'Glycoprotein that bridges laminin and type IV collagen in the basal lamina', 'Entactin (nidogen) is a sulfated glycoprotein of the basal lamina that binds both laminin and type IV collagen, stabilizing the scaffold. It is not a proteoglycan, elastic fiber, collagen, or integrin.'),
('0b173187-5a9d-53e9-899a-28dae8254e38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Perlecan is a:', '["Collagen type resulting in decreased functional activity","Matricellular protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heparan sulfate proteoglycan in the basal lamina","Glycoprotein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Integrin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heparan sulfate proteoglycan in the basal lamina', 'Perlecan is a large heparan sulfate proteoglycan embedded in the basal lamina that contributes to its charge-selective filtration. It is not a collagen, matricellular protein, simple glycoprotein, or integrin.'),
('1a66d0eb-8cde-558d-9055-46eda19564fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The charge-selective barrier of the glomerular basement membrane is provided by:', '["Laminin resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibronectin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type IV collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Entactin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heparan sulfate proteoglycans (negative charge)"]'::jsonb, 'Heparan sulfate proteoglycans (negative charge)', 'Negatively charged heparan sulfate proteoglycans (e.g., agrin, perlecan) in the glomerular basement membrane repel anionic plasma proteins, providing charge selectivity. Collagen, laminin, fibronectin, and entactin give structure rather than this negative-charge barrier.'),
('73cea157-1348-521f-976d-e3cdbb2ac37a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which cell junction is most resistant to shearing forces?', '["Tight junction","Adherens junction","Desmosome","Hemidesmosome","Gap junction"]'::jsonb, 'Desmosome', 'Desmosomes act as spot-welds that rivet adjacent cells together through intermediate filaments, giving strong resistance to shearing. Tight, adherens, and gap junctions provide sealing, actin-based adhesion, or communication, while hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the matrix.'),
('4d3414d7-c227-5891-8b53-c3b299e09d6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Plectin is a:', '["Gap junction protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Actin-binding protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cross-linker protein that connects intermediate filaments to hemidesmosomes and other structures","Microtubule-associated protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transcription factor a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cross-linker protein that connects intermediate filaments to hemidesmosomes and other structures', 'Plectin is a cytolinker that attaches intermediate filaments to hemidesmosomal plaques and other cytoskeletal arrays. It is not a gap junction protein, an actin-binding protein, a microtubule-associated protein, or a transcription factor.'),
('d45a952e-3726-5842-945f-8ee313742ddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'BPAG1 (BP230) is a:', '["Tight junction protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Actin-binding protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gap junction protein mediated by specific receptor activation","Hemidesmosomal plaque protein (autoantigen in bullous pemphigoid)","Desmosomal protein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hemidesmosomal plaque protein (autoantigen in bullous pemphigoid)', 'BPAG1 (BP230) is an intracellular plaque protein of hemidesmosomes that links intermediate filaments to the junction and is targeted by autoantibodies in bullous pemphigoid. It is not a tight or gap junction protein, an actin-binder, or a desmosomal protein.'),
('e6649b1f-8472-5fd9-b5e5-0e69ee363777'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'BPAG2 (BP180) is a:', '["Tight junction protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Integrin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transmembrane collagen (type XVII) in hemidesmosomes","Desmosomal cadherin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gap junction protein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transmembrane collagen (type XVII) in hemidesmosomes', 'BPAG2 (BP180) is the transmembrane collagen XVII that spans the hemidesmosome and anchors it to the lamina lucida. It is neither a tight or gap junction protein, an integrin, nor a desmosomal cadherin.'),
('bea55be3-84b3-528b-a9a5-97e3f3212cc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Integrin α6β4 is the:', '["Platelet integrin resulting in decreased functional activity","Epithelial cadherin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leukocyte integrin mediated by specific receptor activation","Focal adhesion integrin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemidesmosomal integrin that binds laminin-332"]'::jsonb, 'Hemidesmosomal integrin that binds laminin-332', 'Integrin α6β4 is the hemidesmosomal receptor that binds laminin-332 in the basement membrane, anchoring basal keratinocytes. It is not a platelet or leukocyte integrin, a cadherin, or a focal adhesion receptor.'),
('5ed7971e-0360-5635-a814-78011b5a38a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The tail of integrin α6β4 links to:', '["Microtubules resulting in decreased functional activity","Desmin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myosin mediated by specific receptor activation","Actin filaments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intermediate filaments (via plectin and BP230)"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate filaments (via plectin and BP230)', 'Unlike focal adhesions, the hemidesmosomal integrin α6β4 connects intracellularly to intermediate filaments through plectin and BP230. Actin filaments, microtubules, myosin, and desmin do not form this hemidesmosomal linkage pathway.'),
('35c69f28-1df3-5ab0-ad6d-925adcedf018'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Laminin-332 (laminin 5) is:', '["A component of the lamina lucida that anchors hemidesmosomes","The major collagen of the basal lamina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An elastic fiber mediated by specific receptor activation","A gap junction protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A proteoglycan leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A component of the lamina lucida that anchors hemidesmosomes', 'Laminin-332 is a lamina lucida glycoprotein that binds integrin α6β4 of hemidesmosomes, anchoring basal keratinocytes to the basement membrane. It is not a collagen, elastic fiber, junction protein, or proteoglycan.'),
('fa4aaf07-2c58-5c69-9f47-b389ad5f2588'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'What stain is used to demonstrate reticular fibers (type III collagen)?', '["Verhoeff stain","PAS stain","Trichrome stain","Silver stain (reticulin stain)","H&E stain"]'::jsonb, 'Silver stain (reticulin stain)', 'Reticular fibers are argyrophilic and are blackened by silver impregnation (reticulin stain). Verhoeff stain demonstrates elastic fibers, PAS detects carbohydrates, and trichrome or H&E are general connective tissue stains.'),
('6ccfd7ba-ad12-5dda-adb3-8ddea508f226'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Reticular fibers form a supportive network in:', '["Lymphoid organs","and liver","Hyaline cartilage","bone marrow","Epidermis"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphoid organs', 'Reticular fibers composed of type III collagen form delicate networks that support lymphoid organs such as lymph nodes and spleen. Hyaline cartilage, epidermis, and the other listed tissues depend on different supporting elements.'),
('04492d81-9309-589c-aee7-bd504fee41ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Type III collagen is composed of:', '["Three identical alpha chains forming a homotrimer","Five alpha chains accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two different alpha chains mediated by specific receptor activation","One alpha chain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Four alpha chains leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Three identical alpha chains forming a homotrimer', 'Fibrillar collagens are triple helices, and type III collagen is a homotrimer of three identical α1(III) chains. Collagen molecules are never single-, four-, or five-chain structures.'),
('562a6b6f-9a38-563c-a621-378366eda31e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (classic type) is often due to mutations in:', '["Type III collagen (COL3A1)","Type V collagen (COL5A1/A2)","Type IV collagen (COL4A5)","Type I collagen (COL1A1/A2)","Fibrillin 1"]'::jsonb, 'Type V collagen (COL5A1/A2)', 'Classic Ehlers-Danlos syndrome mainly results from mutations in type V collagen genes COL5A1/COL5A2, producing hyperextensible skin. COL3A1 defects cause the vascular type, COL4A5 links to Alport syndrome, COL1A1/A2 to osteogenesis imperfecta, and fibrillin-1 to Marfan syndrome.'),
('31a6e22c-330b-5bee-a3e4-9e152c7761ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (vascular type) is caused by mutations in:', '["Type V collagen","Type III collagen (COL3A1)","Fibrillin a","Type I collagen","Type IV collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Type III collagen (COL3A1)', 'The vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome results from mutations in COL3A1 encoding type III collagen, predisposing to arterial and hollow-organ rupture. Type V, I, and IV collagens and fibrillin cause other connective tissue disorders.'),
('6c426743-c4f1-5cfe-b5a2-461e1bf5c0a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteogenesis imperfecta is caused by mutations in:', '["Type II collagen","Type I collagen (COL1A1 or COL1A2)","Type III collagen","Type IV collagen","Type V collagen a"]'::jsonb, 'Type I collagen (COL1A1 or COL1A2)', 'Osteogenesis imperfecta results from mutations in COL1A1/COL1A2, producing defective type I collagen and fragile bones. Type II and III defects cause chondrodysplasias and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome respectively, while type IV collagen relates to basement membranes.'),
('af1c3906-443a-5e0a-ac63-91135477a68c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Mutations in type II collagen cause:', '["Osteogenesis imperfecta","Alport syndrome","Achondrogenesis and Stickler syndrome","Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Epidermolysis bullosa a"]'::jsonb, 'Achondrogenesis and Stickler syndrome', 'Type II collagen, found in cartilage and vitreous, when mutated causes achondrogenesis type II and Stickler syndrome among other chondrodysplasias. Osteogenesis imperfecta reflects type I, Alport syndrome type IV, Ehlers-Danlos types I/III/V, and epidermolysis bullosa involves hemidesmosomal proteins.'),
('d3a523b9-44c5-5598-8829-0d0a317c5e62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Stickler syndrome (hereditary arthro-ophthalmopathy) is due to mutations in:', '["Type III collagen","Type IV collagen","Type II or XI collagen","Type I collagen","Type V collagen a"]'::jsonb, 'Type II or XI collagen', 'Stickler syndrome is a hereditary arthro-ophthalmopathy arising from mutations in type II or type XI collagen, affecting cartilage, vitreous, and joints. Other collagen types give different syndromes such as Ehlers-Danlos, Alport, or osteogenesis imperfecta.'),
('e7eb3e3e-1aad-5851-884d-06351e838ffc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Type IX collagen is a:', '["FACIT collagen (fibril-associated collagen with interrupted triple helices)","Basement membrane collagen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Network-forming collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Transmembrane collagen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrillar collagen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'FACIT collagen (fibril-associated collagen with interrupted triple helices)', 'Type IX is a FACIT collagen: it associates with the surface of type II collagen fibrils in cartilage and has interrupted triple helices. It does not self-assemble into fibrils, networks, or membranes, nor does it span the plasma membrane.'),
('2925c76a-6e62-55e0-a895-eae303f44426'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Type VI collagen forms:', '["Fibrils resulting in decreased functional activity","Sheets a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Networks mediated by specific receptor activation","Tubules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beaded filaments in connective tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Beaded filaments in connective tissue', 'Type VI collagen forms beaded microfilaments that connect collagen fibrils and cells in connective tissue. It does not assemble into conventional fibrils, sheets, networks, or tubules.'),
('eb8ba5df-a1c6-531d-be81-0e80d24d499b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which junction is found at the apical end of the intercellular space in glandular epithelia?', '["Hemidesmosome a","Adherens junction","Desmosome","Tight junction (zonula occludens)","Gap junction"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junction (zonula occludens)', 'The zonula occludens is the most apical junction belt, sealing the intercellular space and preventing solute leakage. Adherens junctions, desmosomes, and gap junctions lie deeper, while hemidesmosomes anchor the basal surface.'),
('b0bde6be-d479-5ccf-a05f-6ab494d67c6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The secretory product of serous cells is:', '["Lipids","Hormones","enzyme-rich fluid","Watery","Thick mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Watery', 'Serous cells produce a thin, watery secretion that contains enzymes such as amylase and zymogens. In contrast to the viscous mucus of mucous cells, serous fluid is low in mucins and high in water and protein.'),
('eb74d2ab-7f5b-5506-8561-43e625f7e45c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The secretory product of mucous cells is:', '["viscous mucus (rich in mucin glycoproteins)","Watery fluid","Thick","Enzymes","Hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Thick', 'Mucous cells secrete mucus, a thick, viscid product rich in mucin glycoproteins, whereas serous cells give a watery enzyme-rich fluid. Mucous cells do not release watery fluid, enzymes, or hormones as their primary product.'),
('0ccc32cb-029b-51f1-8e3c-079bca427519'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'A serous acinus in the pancreas has a:', '["Ciliated surface resulting in decreased functional activity","Round nucleus and basophilic cytoplasm (due to RER)","Cuboidal shape mediated by specific receptor activation","Flattened nucleus and pale cytoplasm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Microvilli a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Round nucleus and basophilic cytoplasm (due to RER)', 'Serous acinar cells have a round basal nucleus and strongly basophilic cytoplasm from abundant RER that synthesizes exportable enzymes. Flattened peripheral nuclei with pale cytoplasm typify mucous cells rather than serous acini.'),
('dd079aa5-575f-586b-a920-a106af9b9d69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'A mucous acinus in the submandibular gland has:', '["Central round nuclei and basophilic cytoplasm","Striated ducts","Ciliated surface","Flattened peripheral nuclei and pale cytoplasm","Goblet cells"]'::jsonb, 'Flattened peripheral nuclei and pale cytoplasm', 'Mucous acinar cells are compressed by accumulated mucigen, pushing their nuclei flat against the basal border and leaving pale, mucin-rich cytoplasm. Round nuclei with basophilic cytoplasm instead characterize serous cells.'),
('d3228d83-a045-5cae-bf8f-31998cbda8b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which epithelial modification is found in the epididymis?', '["Microvilli","Cilia","Glycocalyx","Flagella","Stereocilia"]'::jsonb, 'Stereocilia', 'The epididymis and ductus deferens bear long, non-motile microvillus-like processes called stereocilia that resorb fluid and absorb proteins. True motile cilia, ordinary microvilli, and flagella describe other structures.'),
('1b19f057-b662-52eb-9175-e38be06a306d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Pancreatic ducts are lined by:', '["Transitional resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple cuboidal to low columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","Pseudostratified without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple squamous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple cuboidal to low columnar epithelium', 'Pancreatic excretory ducts are lined by simple cuboidal to low columnar epithelium that becomes taller in the larger ducts. Transitional, stratified, pseudostratified, and simple squamous types do not line these ducts.'),
('b05509fe-2598-5f20-94a7-45873a66c051'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Parotid gland ducts are lined by:', '["Pseudostratified resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stratified squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple cuboidal to columnar epithelium (no striated ducts in parotid?)","Simple squamous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple cuboidal to columnar epithelium (no striated ducts in parotid?)', 'The duct system of the parotid is lined by simple cuboidal to columnar epithelium, with well-developed striated and interlobular ducts. Transitional, stratified, pseudostratified, and simple squamous linings do not apply to these glands.'),
('eec88c83-c888-5f57-94dc-fa950fe1170b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The striated ducts of salivary glands modify the saliva by:', '["Secretion of enzymes resulting in decreased functional activity","Production of antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secretion of mucus mediated by specific receptor activation","Filtering the saliva without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reabsorption of Na+ and secretion of K+ (ion transport)"]'::jsonb, 'Reabsorption of Na+ and secretion of K+ (ion transport)', 'Striated duct cells, rich in basal mitochondria and membrane infoldings, actively reabsorb Na+ and secrete K+, making saliva hypotonic. They do not produce enzymes, antibodies, or mucus, nor do they filter the fluid.'),
('877b53be-545e-5b8f-9700-bc4ecbeacaf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'What are the basal striations of striated duct cells?', '["Microtubules resulting in decreased functional activity","Actin filaments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glycogen granules mediated by specific receptor activation","Intermediate filaments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mitochondria and basal plasma membrane infoldings"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondria and basal plasma membrane infoldings', 'The basal striations are parallel infoldings of the basal plasma membrane alternating with rows of mitochondria, supplying the surface area and ATP for ion transport. They are not microtubules, actin filaments, glycogen granules, or intermediate filaments.'),
('54fa8349-1049-5c5e-a251-2c32c0f78a77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Intercalated ducts of exocrine glands are lined by:', '["Transitional","Simple columnar","Low simple cuboidal epithelium","Pseudostratified","Stratified squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Low simple cuboidal epithelium', 'Intercalated ducts are the smallest duct branches and are lined by low simple cuboidal epithelium. Transitional, columnar, pseudostratified, and stratified linings are found elsewhere in the urinary or other systems.'),
('fb7ad44a-0763-58d0-89fa-1d98c4b2496e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Gap junctions in smooth muscle are important for:', '["Electrical coupling and coordinated contraction","Excitation-contraction coupling a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cell division mediated by specific receptor activation","Metabolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Electrical coupling and coordinated contraction', 'Gap junctions form low-resistance channels that pass ions and signals, allowing smooth muscle to contract as a coordinated unit. They do not mediate excitation-contraction coupling (an intracellular calcium process), cell division, metabolism, or secretion.'),
('4392f7e2-d4a0-54c6-a771-0829f7874963'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The glomerular filtration barrier includes:', '["GBM and podocytes only","Fenestrated endothelium and GBM only","and podocyte slit diaphragm","GBM","Fenestrated endothelium"]'::jsonb, 'Fenestrated endothelium', 'The filtration barrier is built from fenestrated endothelium, the glomerular basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms; fenestrated endothelium is its innermost layer. Options listing only one or two layers give an incomplete description of the barrier.'),
('5aaa08d9-7d94-509c-b01b-ef6698131c4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The slit diaphragm of podocytes contains:', '["Cadherins","podocin","Claudins","and CD2AP","Nephrin"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrin', 'Nephrin is the major transmembrane protein of the podocyte slit diaphragm that maintains the filtration slits, while podocin and CD2AP are auxiliary slit-diaphragm proteins. Cadherins and claudins belong to other junctional systems.'),
('8d38f3fc-f2ff-58aa-b2da-7ceb46eff8f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Congenital nephrotic syndrome (Finnish type) is caused by mutations in:', '["Nephrin (NPHS1)","CD2AP","WT1","Podocin (NPHS2)","Laminin β2"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrin (NPHS1)', 'Finnish-type congenital nephrotic syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in NPHS1, which encodes nephrin, the key slit-diaphragm protein of glomerular podocytes. CD2AP and podocin interact with nephrin but are not the primary genetic cause of this syndrome; WT1 and laminin beta-2 cause other glomerular and renal defects.'),
('77e28ad6-a085-5bff-8ff1-b79690af74ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The brush border of proximal tubule cells is composed of:', '["Cilia","Flagella","Stereocilia","Macrovilli a","Microvilli"]'::jsonb, 'Microvilli', 'The brush border of proximal tubule cells consists of numerous tightly packed microvilli that greatly increase the surface area for absorption. Cilia, flagella, and stereocilia differ in structure and function, and macrovilli are not a recognized structure.'),
('2486bc95-bea1-53c2-b475-f90e3263320a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The zona occludens (tight junction) is also called the:', '["M-line","Z-line","A-band","Oral bar","Terminal bar"]'::jsonb, 'Terminal bar', 'The zona occludens (tight junction) appears as a dense terminal bar at the apical-lateral border of epithelial cells under light microscopy. The M-line, Z-line, and A-band are sarcomere or myofibril structures and are not related to junctions.'),
('38d24324-7d06-560e-a588-540ba51b7f8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The intermediate filament of epithelial cells is:', '["Keratin","Neurofilament","Desmin","GFAP","Vimentin"]'::jsonb, 'Keratin', 'Keratin is the intermediate filament of epithelial cells and forms the structural framework of desmosomes. Neurofilament, desmin, GFAP, and vimentin are specific to neurons, muscle, glia, and mesenchyme, respectively.'),
('92206d4a-8ce6-51f2-8de5-bf691711bc03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The intermediate filament of cardiac and skeletal muscle is:', '["GFAP","Keratin","Neurofilament a","Desmin","Vimentin"]'::jsonb, 'Desmin', 'Desmin is the intermediate filament that links the contractile apparatus of cardiac and skeletal muscle cells. GFAP, keratin, neurofilament, and vimentin characterize glia, epithelia, neurons, and mesenchyme, respectively.'),
('9abb382e-2c5a-55c6-a612-c328873cc3dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The intermediate filament of mesenchymal cells is:', '["Neurofilament a","GFAP","Desmin","Keratin","Vimentin"]'::jsonb, 'Vimentin', 'Vimentin is the intermediate filament typical of mesenchymal cells, fibroblasts, and endothelial cells. Neurofilament, GFAP, desmin, and keratin distinguish neurons, glia, muscle, and epithelia, respectively.'),
('81f8fd37-255e-5418-ae3c-ea3701c79bb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The intermediate filament of glial cells is:', '["Vimentin resulting in decreased functional activity","GFAP (glial fibrillary acidic protein)","Neurofilament mediated by specific receptor activation","Desmin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Keratin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GFAP (glial fibrillary acidic protein)', 'GFAP is the intermediate filament of glial cells, notably astrocytes, and is used as their immunohistochemical marker. Vimentin, neurofilament, desmin, and keratin identify mesenchyme, neurons, muscle, and epithelia, respectively.'),
('b9e706ef-7664-5fff-b90c-5cf1efe21200'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which type of collagen is the main component of basement membranes?', '["Type I","Type IV","Type V","Type II","Type III"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV', 'Type IV collagen is a network-forming collagen that is the major structural component of basement membranes. Fibrillar collagens type I, II, III, and V polymerize into banded fibers rather than sheets and are not the main collagen of basement membranes.'),
('48653100-e0cf-53b8-ba3b-55b5b70f6164'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which type of collagen is the main component of cartilage?', '["Type I","Type II","Type IV","Type III","Type X"]'::jsonb, 'Type II', 'Type II collagen is the principal collagen of the cartilage matrix, forming thin fibrils that bind proteoglycans. Type I, III, IV, and X collagens are found in bone, reticular fibers, basement membranes, and hypertrophic cartilage, respectively.'),
('2ad7fc27-7e08-5737-9464-743aa38e5af5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which type of collagen is the main component of bone?', '["Type IV","Type II","Type I","Type Va","Type III"]'::jsonb, 'Type I', 'Type I collagen is the dominant fibrillar collagen of bone and gives it tensile strength. Types II, III, IV, and V characterize cartilage, reticular fibers, basement membranes, and pericellular locations, respectively.'),
('8eda01b4-5ba2-59fe-9b30-222000957b48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which type of collagen forms reticular fibers?', '["Type Va","Type II","Type IV","Type I","Type III"]'::jsonb, 'Type III', 'Type III collagen polymerizes into thin reticular fibers that form the supportive meshwork of lymphoid organs, liver, and other soft tissues. Types I, II, IV, and V have different tissue distributions.'),
('9ae35e3c-36ce-5e15-880d-2f39bb6c7fbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which type of collagen forms anchoring fibrils?', '["Type I","Type III","Type IV","Type VII","Type V"]'::jsonb, 'Type VII', 'Type VII collagen forms anchoring fibrils that fix the basement membrane to the underlying dermis. Types I, III, IV, and V form fibers, reticular networks, basement membranes, and pericellular collagen, respectively.'),
('0015a4e4-7a8e-575d-99a6-3b3543460f12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which type of collagen is found in the zona pellucida and some fetal tissues?', '["Type X collagen?","Type I","Type V","Type IV collagen? no","Type III"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV collagen? no', 'Type IV collagen is the network-forming collagen of basement membranes, including structures such as the zona pellucida of the ovary and several fetal tissues. Fibrillar collagens (I, III, V) and type X do not form basement-membrane sheets.'),
('2cbca3ee-2f18-50d0-a449-6f1f45d0037c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Elastic fibers are composed of:', '["Fibrillin only","Elastin only","Elastin and fibrillin microfibrils","Collagen and proteoglycans","Glycoproteins"]'::jsonb, 'Elastin and fibrillin microfibrils', 'Elastic fibers consist of an amorphous core of elastin surrounded by microfibrils of fibrillin, together providing stretch and recoil. Fibrillin alone, elastin alone, or collagen with proteoglycans does not produce a functional elastic fiber.'),
('36b7179a-0b55-54a8-91a0-fa6213e6ae82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Fibrillin is a:', '["Integrin resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glycoprotein microfibril component of elastic fibers","Collagen type without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proteoglycan leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glycoprotein microfibril component of elastic fibers', 'Fibrillin is a large glycoprotein that polymerizes into the microfibrils surrounding the elastin core of elastic fibers. It is not an integrin, a collagen, or a proteoglycan, and elastin forms the amorphous core rather than the microfibrils.'),
('30cec247-2e19-524b-aa98-d107486bf7ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Marfan syndrome is caused by mutations in:', '["Fibronectin resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrillin-1 (FBN1) on chromosome 15","Laminin a mediated by specific receptor activation","Elastin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type I collagen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrillin-1 (FBN1) on chromosome 15', 'Marfan syndrome results from mutations in FBN1 (fibrillin-1) on chromosome 15, causing defective microfibrils in the aorta, lens, and skeleton. Fibronectin, laminin, elastin, and type I collagen defects cause other disorders.'),
('93e6001e-e7c6-5dc3-a71a-bd266174a347'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Desmosine and isodesmosine are cross-links found in:', '["Laminin","Fibrillin","Fibronectin","Elastin","Collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Elastin', 'Desmosine and isodesmosine are unique cross-links generated in elastin by lysyl oxidase-modified lysine residues, giving elastic fibers their recoil. Collagen forms different cross-links, and laminin, fibrillin, and fibronectin are not cross-linked in this way.'),
('d6aac7f8-4a20-54a4-b345-9202b8082d22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Lysyl oxidase is involved in the cross-linking of:', '["Glycosaminoglycans","Integrins","Proteoglycans","Cadherins","Collagen and elastin"]'::jsonb, 'Collagen and elastin', 'Lysyl oxidase catalyzes oxidative deamination of lysine and hydroxylysine residues and initiates cross-linking of both collagen and elastin. Glycosaminoglycans, proteoglycans, integrins, and cadherins are not substrates for this enzyme.'),
('983c1f07-62c2-52b2-abe1-86204bc83730'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Lathyrism (osteolathyrism) is caused by:', '["Excess vitamin C resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcium deficiency a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Copper deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Iron deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta-aminopropionitrile (BAPN) inhibiting lysyl oxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-aminopropionitrile (BAPN) inhibiting lysyl oxidase', 'Osteolathyrism is caused by beta-aminopropionitrile (BAPN), which irreversibly inhibits lysyl oxidase and prevents collagen cross-linking. Excess vitamin C, calcium deficiency, copper deficiency, and iron deficiency do not cause this syndrome.'),
('78749b36-980d-5869-a294-9b3cdd3028ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Copper deficiency impairs:', '["Elastin synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Proteoglycan synthesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lysyl oxidase activity (needs copper as cofactor)","All ECM synthesis a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Collagen synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lysyl oxidase activity (needs copper as cofactor)', 'Lysyl oxidase is a copper-dependent enzyme, so copper deficiency impairs its activity and therefore collagen and elastin cross-linking. Synthesis of the collagen and elastin molecules themselves does not require copper.'),
('a362175e-d5fc-5ba7-a7ff-83797bb96828'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The ECM of connective tissue is composed of:', '["and multiadhesive glycoproteins","Only proteoglycans","Only collagen","proteoglycans","Collagens"]'::jsonb, 'Collagens', 'Collagens are the principal fibrous proteins of the ECM of connective tissue and provide tensile strength. Proteoglycans and multiadhesive glycoproteins are additional ECM constituents, but collagen is the dominant structural component.'),
('8ad6a895-0adc-5c07-9dca-8aa966b024fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Proteoglycans consist of a core protein with attached:', '["Glycosaminoglycan (GAG) chains","Fibronectin domains","Integrin domains","Collagen chains","Sugar monomers"]'::jsonb, 'Glycosaminoglycan (GAG) chains', 'Proteoglycans consist of a core protein to which many glycosaminoglycan (GAG) chains are covalently attached, forming a hydrated gel-like matrix. Fibronectin and integrins are adhesive proteins rather than attached chains, and collagen chains form separate fibers.'),
('26f3aaaf-4e7e-5e98-af3a-f4e07747df92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Glycosaminoglycans (GAGs) include:', '["dermatan sulfate","chondroitin sulfate","Hyaluronic acid","heparan sulfate","keratan sulfate"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaluronic acid', 'Hyaluronic acid is the GAG that is non-sulfated and not attached to a core protein, making it the distinctive member of the GAG family. The sulfate-containing options (dermatan, chondroitin, heparan, and keratan sulfate) are instead incorporated into proteoglycans.'),
('528f18ad-2e75-503c-86e4-60311b9021b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Hyaluronic acid is unique among GAGs because it:', '["Is not covalently attached to a core protein and is not sulfated","Is attached to a core protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is found only in cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Contains sulfate groups without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a branched polymer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is not covalently attached to a core protein and is not sulfated', 'Hyaluronic acid is unique among GAGs because it is neither covalently attached to a core protein nor sulfated. The other GAGs are sulfated and joined to core proteins, and hyaluronic acid is unbranched rather than a branched polymer.'),
('71c01acb-ec81-5628-8c2c-3ddfd4f8ea7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Hyaluronic acid is synthesized by:', '["Chondrocytes in the ER resulting in decreased functional activity","Any cell in the mitochondria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibroblasts in the Golgi mediated by specific receptor activation","cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyaluronan synthase on the inner surface of the plasma membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaluronan synthase on the inner surface of the plasma membrane', 'Hyaluronic acid is synthesized by hyaluronan synthase, an enzyme located on the inner surface of the plasma membrane, and is extruded directly into the extracellular space. It is not produced in the ER, Golgi, or mitochondria, unlike proteins and sulfated GAGs.'),
('841c2f80-3dd9-526c-baa0-f34539762750'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Aggrecan is a large proteoglycan found in:', '["Tendon","Skin a","Bone","Artery wall","Cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilage', 'Aggrecan is the large aggregating proteoglycan of cartilage that binds hyaluronic acid and gives the matrix its compressive strength. It is not a major component of tendon, skin, bone, or the arterial wall.'),
('4637c1d1-559b-5f23-b57f-ec531885d480'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Decorin is a small proteoglycan that:', '["Regulates collagen fibrillogenesis (binds to type I collagen)","Provides compressive resistance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regulates growth factor signaling (TGF-β) mediated by specific receptor activation","Forms large aggregates without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Links cells to ECM leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Regulates collagen fibrillogenesis (binds to type I collagen)', 'Decorin is a small leucine-rich proteoglycan that binds type I collagen and regulates fibril assembly. Biglycan and aggrecan have roles in growth factor signaling and compressive resistance, but decorin characteristically controls collagen fibrillogenesis.'),
('9442e0e9-b7f5-570f-8209-a664f2283426'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Biglycan is a small proteoglycan found in:', '["Bone and other connective tissues","Arterial walls accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cartilage only mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tendon only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone and other connective tissues', 'Biglycan is a small proteoglycan found in bone and other connective tissues, where it binds growth factors and matrix components. It is not restricted to cartilage, skin, tendon, or arterial walls.'),
('1cc0a92b-de72-56fb-8eca-a2990c49a649'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Perlecan is a heparan sulfate proteoglycan found in:', '["Tendon","Bone","Elastic arteries a","Cartilage","Basement membranes"]'::jsonb, 'Basement membranes', 'Perlecan is a large heparan sulfate proteoglycan that is a major constituent of basement membranes, where it contributes to the filtration barrier and cell attachment. Tendon, bone, cartilage, and elastic arteries are not its primary location.'),
('7e88d91d-1037-5049-9fe9-d3a136bc6f6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Syndecans are:', '["Secreted proteoglycans of the ECM resulting in decreased functional activity","Cytoplasmic proteoglycans accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glycoproteins a mediated by specific receptor activation","Transmembrane heparan sulfate proteoglycans that act as co-receptors","Glycolipids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transmembrane heparan sulfate proteoglycans that act as co-receptors', 'Syndecans are transmembrane heparan sulfate proteoglycans that bind growth factors and ECM ligands and act as cell-surface co-receptors. They are not secreted, cytoplasmic, or glycolipid molecules.'),
('71700da5-40f5-5876-9563-da9d8571e733'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Glypicans are:', '["Intracellular proteoglycans a resulting in decreased functional activity","GPI-anchored heparan sulfate proteoglycans on the cell surface","Cytoplasmic proteoglycans mediated by specific receptor activation","Transmembrane proteoglycans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secreted proteoglycans leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GPI-anchored heparan sulfate proteoglycans on the cell surface', 'Glypicans are GPI-anchored heparan sulfate proteoglycans tethered to the outer leaflet of the plasma membrane. Unlike syndecans they have no transmembrane or cytoplasmic domain, and they are not secreted or intracellular.'),
('19a5bb4b-f079-5e97-acff-126d42659f23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The negative charge of GAGs is due to:', '["Phosphate groups","Hydroxyl groups","Sulfate and carboxyl groups","Amino groups","Carbonyl groups"]'::jsonb, 'Sulfate and carboxyl groups', 'The strong negative charge of GAGs arises from their sulfate and carboxyl groups, which attract water and cations. Phosphate, hydroxyl, amino, and carbonyl groups do not account for this anionic character.'),
('62857c8d-6c0d-547f-a1ea-59f2855748ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The stiffness of connective tissue is influenced by the ratio of:', '["GAG type a","Proteoglycan to collagen","Fibroblast number","Collagen to elastin","Water content"]'::jsonb, 'Collagen to elastin', 'The ratio of collagen to elastin determines the stiffness-versus-flexibility balance of connective tissue: more collagen yields stiffness, while more elastin yields distensibility. Water content, cell number, and GAG type are not the primary determinants of this ratio.'),
('6a200a3d-cd93-52d0-840f-355c5317a653'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Tendon is composed mainly of:', '["Type II collagen resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proteoglycans mediated by specific receptor activation","Reticular fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type I collagen arranged in parallel bundles (dense regular connective tissue)"]'::jsonb, 'Type I collagen arranged in parallel bundles (dense regular connective tissue)', 'Tendon consists of dense regular connective tissue, with type I collagen arranged in parallel bundles that withstand strong uniaxial tension. Type II collagen, elastin, proteoglycans, and reticular fibers are not the major constituents of tendon.'),
('3f538e04-8ef5-5c9b-9a6d-81b09748b588'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Ligaments are similar to tendons but contain:', '["More blood vessels a resulting in decreased functional activity","fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","More type II collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","More ground substance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","More elastic fibers and less regular collagen arrangement"]'::jsonb, 'More elastic fibers and less regular collagen arrangement', 'Compared with tendons, ligaments are more extensible because they contain more elastic fibers and a less regular arrangement of collagen. Increased blood vessels, type II collagen, or ground substance are not the defining feature of ligaments.'),
('64e95da0-1a2a-5ada-95c7-7b8f130e2ad0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Dense irregular connective tissue is found in:', '["Tendon","Ligament","Bone a","Blood vessel walls","Dermis of the skin"]'::jsonb, 'Dermis of the skin', 'Dense irregular connective tissue, with collagen bundles oriented in multiple directions, forms the dermis of the skin and the capsules of organs. Tendons and ligaments are dense regular connective tissue, and vessel walls and bone are not dense irregular connective tissue.'),
('1d1e41a7-099a-5b2f-97eb-0e4e7f8e7cdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Loose (areolar) connective tissue is characterized by:', '["Minimal ground substance","Dense collagen bundles","and abundant ground substance","Few collagen fibers","many cells"]'::jsonb, 'Few collagen fibers', 'Loose (areolar) connective tissue is characterized by few collagen fibers, abundant ground substance, and many cells, permitting flexibility and diffusion. Dense collagen bundles with minimal ground substance describe dense connective tissue.'),
('ff2f4dc9-bc61-5e43-a867-ddd753ba2085'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Reticular connective tissue is characterized by:', '["Bone matrix a resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastic fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adipose tissue mediated by specific receptor activation","Parallel collagen bundles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A network of reticular fibers (type III collagen) and reticular cells"]'::jsonb, 'A network of reticular fibers (type III collagen) and reticular cells', 'Reticular connective tissue consists of a network of reticular fibers (type III collagen) and reticular cells that form the stroma of lymph nodes, spleen, and bone marrow. Bone matrix, elastic fibers, and parallel collagen bundles characterize other tissue types.'),
('2bb056a1-e992-53ef-ac66-5984ebfa6def'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Adipose tissue is a specialized connective tissue that:', '["Insulates the body only","and cushions organs","Conducts electrical impulses","Stores energy as fat","provides insulation"]'::jsonb, 'Stores energy as fat', 'Adipose tissue is a specialized connective tissue whose primary role is storing energy as fat (triglycerides), while also providing insulation and cushioning. It does not conduct electrical impulses.'),
('d49aebc9-3619-53e3-878b-285630a41602'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'White (unilocular) adipose tissue has:', '["displacing the nucleus","Multiple small lipid droplets","lipid droplets","Centrally located nucleus","A single large lipid droplet filling the cell"]'::jsonb, 'A single large lipid droplet filling the cell', 'White (unilocular) adipocytes contain a single large lipid droplet that fills the cell and displaces the nucleus to the periphery. Multiple small droplets and a centrally located nucleus characterize brown adipocytes.'),
('9112fcfe-2e57-5112-a4ca-03540617d9ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Brown (multilocular) adipose tissue has:', '["mitochondria resulting in decreased functional activity","Multiple small lipid droplets and abundant mitochondria (thermogenic)","Large single lipid droplet mediated by specific receptor activation","lipids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Few mitochondria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple small lipid droplets and abundant mitochondria (thermogenic)', 'Brown (multilocular) adipocytes contain multiple small lipid droplets and abundant mitochondria rich in UCP1, enabling heat production. A single large lipid droplet with few mitochondria describes white adipose tissue.'),
('916116a5-2709-5cc2-9c32-2fd60077d856'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'UCP1 (thermogenin) is found in the mitochondria of:', '["White adipose tissue","Liver a","Cardiac muscle","Brown adipose tissue","Skeletal muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Brown adipose tissue', 'UCP1 (thermogenin) is an uncoupling protein in the mitochondria of brown adipose tissue that dissipates energy as heat. White adipose tissue, cardiac muscle, liver, and skeletal muscle lack this protein.'),
('f548231f-3311-5e4e-8f9e-5b98b8fb948b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Brown adipose tissue is most abundant in:', '["Elderly resulting in decreased functional activity","Athletes a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adults mediated by specific receptor activation","Children without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neonates and hibernating animals"]'::jsonb, 'Neonates and hibernating animals', 'Brown adipose tissue is most abundant in neonates and hibernating animals, where nonshivering thermogenesis is crucial. Its amount declines with age and is not greatest in adults, children, elderly, or athletes.'),
('7dcbd466-0a9b-55d2-81b5-49df71ad33ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Adipocytes are derived from:', '["Fibroblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Chondroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesenchymal stem cells (adipoblasts)","Myoblasts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Macrophages leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells (adipoblasts)', 'Adipocytes are derived from mesenchymal stem cells (adipoblasts) that differentiate into fat-storing cells. Fibroblasts, chondroblasts, myoblasts, and macrophages arise from or serve different lineages.'),
('54106921-ecf0-50c3-ad8c-643cebb8ec46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The extracellular matrix of cartilage is produced by:', '["Osteoclasts a","Osteoblasts","Synoviocytes","Fibroblasts","Chondroblasts (chondrocytes)"]'::jsonb, 'Chondroblasts (chondrocytes)', 'The cartilage matrix is produced by chondroblasts, which become chondrocytes once enclosed in lacunae. Osteoclasts, osteoblasts, synoviocytes, and fibroblasts form or act on other tissues.'),
('a058dc8f-64f0-5bd5-ab4b-dfe5f56ad2d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Mature cartilage cells are called:', '["Osteocytes","Fibrocytes","Myocytes","Adipocytes","Chondrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Chondrocytes', 'Mature cartilage cells are called chondrocytes, which maintain the surrounding matrix within their lacunae. Osteocytes, fibrocytes, myocytes, and adipocytes are the mature cells of bone, fibrous tissue, muscle, and fat, respectively.'),
('7bcd9874-df40-5667-8a9d-6e20af5dcce0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The perichondrium is a layer of:', '["Vascular tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Nervous tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dense connective tissue surrounding cartilage (except at articular surfaces)","Epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loose connective tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dense connective tissue surrounding cartilage (except at articular surfaces)', 'The perichondrium is a layer of dense connective tissue that surrounds most cartilage (except at articular surfaces) and carries the blood vessels that nourish avascular cartilage. It is not epithelial or nervous tissue.'),
('6f433755-bb86-5ca6-a07f-66f198679929'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Hyaline cartilage contains which type of collagen?', '["Type III","Type II","Type IV","Type I","Type Xa"]'::jsonb, 'Type II', 'Hyaline cartilage contains type II collagen as its principal fibrillar collagen. Types I, III, IV, and X characterize dense connective tissue, reticular fibers, basement membranes, and hypertrophic cartilage, respectively.'),
('27d2cde9-a08d-5a96-b1a1-fbeccb3058c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Where is hyaline cartilage found?', '["costal cartilages","Articular surfaces of joints","tracheal rings","External ear","nose"]'::jsonb, 'Articular surfaces of joints', 'Hyaline cartilage lines the articular surfaces of synovial joints, providing a smooth, low-friction gliding surface. Costal cartilages, the nose, and tracheal rings also contain hyaline cartilage, but the external ear is elastic cartilage.'),
('dffe9b1a-8e9b-5d30-a097-73366046ad82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Elastic cartilage is characterized by the presence of:', '["Elastic fibers (elastin) in addition to type II collagen","Type I collagen instead of type II accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Only type IV collagen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reticular fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elastic fibers (elastin) in addition to type II collagen', 'Elastic cartilage is distinguished by the presence of elastic fibers (elastin) in addition to type II collagen, allowing it to keep its shape while remaining flexible. Type I or IV collagen and reticular fibers do not characterize elastic cartilage.'),
('e9626da7-7b9c-57f4-bc38-bc68f189533b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Where is elastic cartilage found?', '["epiglottis","External ear (auricle)","and auditory (Eustachian) tube","Costal cartilages","Articular surfaces"]'::jsonb, 'External ear (auricle)', 'Elastic cartilage, rich in elastin fibers, forms the framework of the auricle of the external ear, the epiglottis, and the auditory (Eustachian) tube. Costal cartilages and articular surfaces are hyaline cartilage.'),
('725cc3db-5d1d-5ebd-9a9e-3f0689698205'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Fibrocartilage is characterized by:', '["Elastic fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","collagen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parallel bundles of type I collagen fibers mixed with cartilage matrix","Rich type II collagen only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","proteoglycans leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parallel bundles of type I collagen fibers mixed with cartilage matrix', 'Fibrocartilage contains parallel bundles of type I collagen with small groups of chondrocytes, giving it high tensile strength. Elastic fibers define elastic cartilage, and type II collagen-rich matrix is typical of hyaline cartilage.'),
('0c8ac06a-af89-53c3-911f-a5f5f928e054'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Where is fibrocartilage found?', '["and menisci of the knee","Intervertebral discs","Articular surfaces","pubic symphysis","Tracheal rings"]'::jsonb, 'Intervertebral discs', 'Fibrocartilage forms the annulus fibrosus of intervertebral discs, and it also occurs in the pubic symphysis and knee menisci. Articular surfaces and tracheal rings are made of hyaline cartilage.'),
('e105fd38-8e74-53ea-8115-c60fe59d569a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The intervertebral disc consists of:', '["Bone only resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyaline cartilage only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An outer annulus fibrosus (fibrocartilage) and a central nucleus pulposus (notochord remnant)","Fibrocartilage only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elastic cartilage only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An outer annulus fibrosus (fibrocartilage) and a central nucleus pulposus (notochord remnant)', 'An intervertebral disc has an outer annulus fibrosus of fibrocartilage and a central nucleus pulposus, a remnant of the notochord. Describing it as a single cartilage type or bone ignores this two-part structure.'),
('8d86b738-b8cb-5287-be1c-802f8833685d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The lacunae of cartilage are:', '["Channels for blood vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Small cavities in the matrix where chondrocytes reside","Spaces for nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","Connecting pores between cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The calcified region leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small cavities in the matrix where chondrocytes reside', 'Lacunae are small matrix cavities that house chondrocytes in cartilage (and osteocytes in bone). They are not vascular or neural channels, and the calcified region is a separate zone.'),
('84a91504-5f1f-5f1a-99bc-ae2411969eac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Isogenous groups are:', '["Degenerating cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastic cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clusters of chondrocytes resulting from mitosis of a single chondrocyte within a lacuna","Fibrous cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcified cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clusters of chondrocytes resulting from mitosis of a single chondrocyte within a lacuna', 'Isogenous groups are nests of chondrocytes produced when one chondrocyte divides mitotically, so its daughter cells remain clustered within a common matrix capsule. They are not degenerating, fibrous, or calcified cells.'),
('4d2363d9-c425-5b04-af3a-3937f62daf06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Territorial matrix is the:', '["Elastic matrix resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcified matrix accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Matrix far from cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous matrix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basophilic capsule of matrix immediately surrounding isogenous groups"]'::jsonb, 'Basophilic capsule of matrix immediately surrounding isogenous groups', 'Territorial (capsular) matrix is the basophilic ring of matrix immediately around isogenous groups, rich in proteoglycans. Matrix located farther from the chondrocytes is interterritorial matrix.'),
('d0d6a5e5-98ee-55ca-bfaa-ad7f492da224'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Interterritorial matrix is:', '["more collagenous","The matrix immediately around cells","The calcified region","The matrix farther from chondrocytes","The elastic region"]'::jsonb, 'The matrix farther from chondrocytes', 'Interterritorial matrix lies between the territorial matrix capsules, farther from the chondrocytes, and is usually less basophilic. Matrix immediately surrounding the cells is territorial matrix.'),
('3abc6050-efbf-5890-9f51-d1cde2c7956c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Cartilage is avascular and receives nutrition by:', '["Capillaries in lacunae resulting in decreased functional activity","Direct arterial supply accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nerve fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Diffusion from the perichondrium or synovial fluid","Blood vessels in the matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffusion from the perichondrium or synovial fluid', 'Because cartilage is avascular, its chondrocytes are nourished by diffusion of nutrients from the perichondrium or, at articular surfaces, from synovial fluid. Cartilage has no intrinsic capillaries, blood vessels, or nerve fibers.'),
('95b8e596-4c8b-5398-988d-7d6a90096b2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Appositional growth of cartilage occurs by:', '["Chondrocyte division within the matrix resulting in decreased functional activity","Chondroblasts in the perichondrium adding new cartilage to the surface","Both appositional and interstitial mediated by specific receptor activation","Endochondral ossification without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neither leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chondroblasts in the perichondrium adding new cartilage to the surface', 'Appositional growth adds new cartilage to the external surface through chondroblasts derived from the inner layer of the perichondrium. Division of chondrocytes within the matrix is interstitial growth.'),
('811c7a4c-e125-562a-9eb3-720a82937388'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Interstitial growth of cartilage occurs by:', '["Growth at the surface only resulting in decreased functional activity","Conversion from bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chondrocytes dividing within lacunae and depositing new matrix","Adding cells from the perichondrium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Invasion of blood vessels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chondrocytes dividing within lacunae and depositing new matrix', 'Interstitial growth expands cartilage from within as chondrocytes divide inside lacunae and secrete additional matrix. Addition of cells from the perichondrial surface is appositional growth.'),
('cab440de-89e8-5239-9e61-33b664c6dad0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Bone matrix is composed of:', '["Decorin and calcium oxalate","Osteoid only","Type II collagen and calcium phosphate","Type I collagen (90%) mineralized with hydroxyapatite crystals","Type III collagen and calcium carbonate"]'::jsonb, 'Type I collagen (90%) mineralized with hydroxyapatite crystals', 'Bone matrix is roughly 90% type I collagen plus ground substance, mineralized by hydroxyapatite (calcium phosphate) crystals. Osteoid is the unmineralized precursor, and type II collagen is characteristic of cartilage.'),
('19fb387e-4fa0-5996-be31-a77f667b1e47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteoid is:', '["Unmineralized bone matrix (collagen and proteoglycans)","The bone lining cell accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mineralized bone matrix mediated by specific receptor activation","The bone cell without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The osteoclast a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unmineralized bone matrix (collagen and proteoglycans)', 'Osteoid is the newly secreted, unmineralized bone matrix composed of collagen and proteoglycans that later becomes calcified. It is a matrix product of osteoblasts, not a cell or a mineralized structure.'),
('ad8dce0f-286e-5e70-8985-15e9fda1e34e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteoblasts are bone-forming cells that:', '["Resorb bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Form fibrous tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Form cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Secrete osteoid and regulate mineralization","Become osteoclasts a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete osteoid and regulate mineralization', 'Osteoblasts synthesize osteoid and control its mineralization, making them the bone-forming cells. Bone resorption is performed by osteoclasts, which are derived from monocytes, not from osteoblasts.'),
('b5e93e59-208b-503e-b99b-f26239df5fdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteocytes are:', '["Bone-forming cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone-resorbing cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dead cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Undifferentiated stem cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mature bone cells that maintain the bone matrix"]'::jsonb, 'Mature bone cells that maintain the bone matrix', 'Osteocytes are mature osteoblasts that became trapped in lacunae; they maintain the bone matrix and sense mechanical loading. Bone-forming and bone-resorbing functions belong to osteoblasts and osteoclasts respectively.'),
('ce40a21a-6e05-5d86-83b5-3c03137fa8c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteocytes communicate with each other via:', '["Dendritic processes within canaliculi","Desmosomes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Synapses mediated by specific receptor activation","Gap junctions directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tight junctions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dendritic processes within canaliculi', 'Osteocytes extend slender dendritic processes through canaliculi, and neighboring cells contact one another by gap junctions at the tips of these processes. Desmosomes, synapses, and tight junctions are not used for osteocyte communication.'),
('0e55bc24-2edc-55fc-8ddd-80e644714a0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteoclasts are:', '["Chondrocytes that form cartilage","Small","multinucleated cells that resorb bone","Large","mononucleated cells that form bone"]'::jsonb, 'multinucleated cells that resorb bone', 'Osteoclasts are large multinucleated cells derived from monocyte/macrophage precursors that resorb bone. They are not bone-forming cells, and calling them simply ''large'' omits their defining resorptive function.'),
('d08d9d84-c307-53c3-b86e-77f8c59efee7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteoclasts resorb bone by secreting:', '["Osteoprotegerin resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteocalcin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HCl and cathepsin K (and other lysosomal enzymes)","Collagen type I without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alkaline phosphatase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HCl and cathepsin K (and other lysosomal enzymes)', 'Osteoclasts acidify the resorption lacuna with HCl to dissolve mineral and degrade the organic matrix with cathepsin K and other lysosomal enzymes. Osteocalcin, type I collagen, and alkaline phosphatase are osteoblast products or regulators.'),
('716d7588-07c2-5359-a710-93eff3eeed37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteoprotegerin (OPG) is a:', '["Bone matrix protein","Decoy receptor for RANKL that inhibits osteoclast formation","Osteoblast growth factor a","M-CSF that stimulates osteoclasts","RANKL that activates osteoclasts"]'::jsonb, 'Decoy receptor for RANKL that inhibits osteoclast formation', 'Osteoprotegerin is a soluble decoy receptor that binds RANKL, preventing it from activating RANK on osteoclast precursors and thereby inhibiting osteoclast formation. RANKL and M-CSF instead promote osteoclast activity.'),
('3a5c2e0d-0935-5bb0-ae9f-a84ac9f8a3fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The balanced activity of osteoblasts and osteoclasts is essential for:', '["Reducing bone mass","Preventing fractures","Bone remodeling","Increasing bone density only","Mineral homeostasis only"]'::jsonb, 'Bone remodeling', 'Coordinated osteoblast and osteoclast activity drives bone remodeling, the continuous renewal and repair of bone. Unopposed activity in either direction produces bone loss or excess density rather than normal remodeling.'),
('7dd6e75a-dec1-5dba-96fa-bdc93735cc02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Parathyroid hormone (PTH) increases blood Ca2+ by:', '["Stimulating osteoclast activity (indirectly via osteoblasts)","Increasing intestinal Ca2+ absorption","Stimulating calcitonin","Increasing renal Ca2+ reabsorption","Decreasing bone formation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating osteoclast activity (indirectly via osteoblasts)', 'PTH acts on osteoblasts to raise RANKL and lower OPG, indirectly stimulating osteoclasts to resorb bone and release calcium into the blood. Its renal and intestinal effects also contribute, but the bone effect is through osteoclast activation.'),
('c7534a3e-4db7-5343-bd32-3fdc8055716f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Calcitonin (from thyroid parafollicular C-cells) lowers blood Ca2+ by:', '["Increasing renal Ca2+ excretion","Inhibiting osteoclast activity","Decreasing intestinal Ca2+ absorption","Inhibiting PTH secretion","Stimulating bone formation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting osteoclast activity', 'Calcitonin from thyroid parafollicular C-cells lowers blood calcium mainly by directly inhibiting osteoclast resorption of bone. It does not act primarily by suppressing PTH or stimulating bone formation.'),
('c74a3d3d-a451-5a74-b402-ce1436f24b03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Woven bone is characterized by:', '["Ordered parallel collagen lamellae","collagen","Disorganized collagen fibers (immature bone)","High mineral content only","osteocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Disorganized collagen fibers (immature bone)', 'Woven bone is immature bone in which collagen fibers are randomly arranged; it forms rapidly during fetal life and fracture repair. Ordered parallel lamellae are a feature of mature lamellar bone.'),
('2c3958d0-3a6b-5175-b3a7-b85f6b5877fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Lamellar bone is characterized by:', '["osteocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","mineralization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parallel or concentric layers of organized collagen fibers (mature bone)","Disorganized collagen fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Randomly distributed collagen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parallel or concentric layers of organized collagen fibers (mature bone)', 'Lamellar bone is mature bone in which collagen fibers are arranged in orderly parallel or concentric lamellae. Disorganized, randomly distributed collagen characterizes woven bone.'),
('9b8766f9-3cbe-5bb7-9dae-17500d6c3c50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Haversian systems (osteons) are found in:', '["Embryonic bone","Cortical (compact) bone","Fracture callus","Cancellous (trabecular) bone","Woven bone"]'::jsonb, 'Cortical (compact) bone', 'Osteons, or Haversian systems, are the structural units of cortical (compact) bone. Cancellous bone is composed of trabeculae without true osteons, and woven or embryonic bone lacks a regular osteon arrangement.'),
('552992d9-20f5-5b33-b119-db208bcadb7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Each osteon consists of:', '["Volkmann canals only","Circumferential lamellae only","Trabeculae only","Interstitial lamellae only","Concentric lamellae of bone around a central canal"]'::jsonb, 'Concentric lamellae of bone around a central canal', 'Each osteon is a cylinder of concentric lamellae surrounding a central Haversian canal that carries vessels and nerves. Volkmann canals, circumferential lamellae, and interstitial lamellae are related structures but do not alone define an osteon.'),
('501b4f19-0ee5-5189-bc99-53f4f7f07610'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The central (Haversian) canal contains:', '["nerves","Blood vessels","Bone marrow","and loose connective tissue","Osteocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Blood vessels', 'The central canal of an osteon transmits blood vessels, together with nerves and loose connective tissue. Bone marrow occupies marrow cavities, and osteocytes lie within lacunae.'),
('702b8138-29b5-56a7-97fd-473ec1e54189'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Volkmann canals (perforating canals) connect:', '["Central canals longitudinally","Lacunae with canaliculi","Haversian canals with each other and the periosteum","endosteum","Osteons with each other perpendicularly"]'::jsonb, 'Haversian canals with each other and the periosteum', 'Volkmann (perforating) canals run transversely or obliquely, linking Haversian canals to one another and to the periosteal and endosteal surfaces. Canaliculi, not these canals, connect lacunae to the canals.'),
('9e0cca39-2220-5b4f-896b-9f1c3ab6a4b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteocyte lacunae are found between:', '["Within the central canal","Circumferential lamellae only","Interstitial lamellae only","Lamellae within an osteon. They are arranged in rings around the central canal","Concentric lamellae (at the junctions)"]'::jsonb, 'Concentric lamellae (at the junctions)', 'Osteocytes occupy lacunae between successive concentric lamellae of an osteon, arranged in rings around the central canal. They are not located within the central canal itself.'),
('3efa8b9e-211b-5512-8a01-37df18a02b2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Canaliculi of bone:', '["Run only within the Haversian canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Connect osteocyte lacunae to each other and to the Haversian canal","Connect only osteoblasts mediated by specific receptor activation","Contain blood vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transmit only nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connect osteocyte lacunae to each other and to the Haversian canal', 'Canaliculi are fine radiating channels that connect osteocyte lacunae to one another and to the central canal, permitting nutrient and signal exchange. They do not contain blood vessels or transmit nerves.'),
('5e17d8e1-85b4-5363-83f6-9a5689949d0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Interstitial lamellae are:', '["Inner layers of bone resulting in decreased functional activity","The periosteum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of the Haversian system mediated by specific receptor activation","Remnants of older osteons (between intact osteons)","Outer layers of bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Remnants of older osteons (between intact osteons)', 'Interstitial lamellae are fragments of older osteons left between intact osteons after bone remodeling. They are not part of the periosteum, the inner or outer layers, or the current Haversian systems.'),
('9a2a5dec-4036-5a33-8da7-9912f2572157'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Circumferential lamellae are:', '["In trabecular bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Within osteons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between osteons mediated by specific receptor activation","In woven bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Outermost and innermost layers of compact bone (just under periosteum and endosteum)"]'::jsonb, 'Outermost and innermost layers of compact bone (just under periosteum and endosteum)', 'Circumferential lamellae form the outer and inner layers of compact bone just beneath the periosteum and endosteum. Osteons and interstitial lamellae lie in the middle zone between them.'),
('507d7bfd-1f53-516b-b434-107badedb5a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Trabecular (cancellous) bone is characterized by:', '["Dense resulting in decreased functional activity","solid bone with Haversian systems accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","osteocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","collagen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A spongy network of trabeculae with marrow spaces between them"]'::jsonb, 'A spongy network of trabeculae with marrow spaces between them', 'Cancellous bone is a lattice of trabeculae enclosing marrow spaces, and it lacks true Haversian systems. Dense, solid bone containing osteons describes compact bone.'),
('78864ca6-582a-53e1-920e-1195a50fe83d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The periosteum is:', '["The Haversian canal lining resulting in decreased functional activity","The cartilage covering articular surfaces accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The mineralized surface mediated by specific receptor activation","A double-layered membrane covering the outer surface of bone (fibrous outer layer + osteogenic inner layer)","A single layer of cells lining the medullary cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A double-layered membrane covering the outer surface of bone (fibrous outer layer + osteogenic inner layer)', 'The periosteum is a double-layered connective tissue sheath on the outer bone surface, with a fibrous outer layer and an osteogenic inner layer. The medullary cavity lining is the endosteum, and articular surfaces are covered by hyaline cartilage.'),
('0b749338-83ea-5053-acd4-dc7a78f000db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The endosteum is:', '["The growth plate resulting in decreased functional activity","The Haversian canal lining accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The thin membrane lining the medullary cavity and trabecular surfaces","The outer covering of bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The articular cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thin membrane lining the medullary cavity and trabecular surfaces', 'The endosteum is the thin connective tissue membrane lining the medullary cavity, Haversian canals, and trabecular surfaces. The outer covering of bone is the periosteum, and the growth plate is a separate cartilaginous structure.'),
('2aeaf18a-821f-5af3-84ca-84137117e847'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Sharpey fibers are:', '["Bundles of collagen fibers from the periosteum that penetrate into the outer circumferential lamellae","Elastic fibers within the bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Actin fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","GAG fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reticular fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bundles of collagen fibers from the periosteum that penetrate into the outer circumferential lamellae', 'Sharpey (perforating) fibers are periosteal collagen bundles that anchor the periosteum to bone by penetrating the outer circumferential lamellae. Elastic, actin, and reticular fibers do not provide this anchorage.'),
('d5e912ea-bd2d-50d4-946f-abdde138435e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The epiphyseal growth plate is responsible for:', '["Fracture healing","Bone remodeling","Appositional bone growth","Articular cartilage maintenance","Longitudinal growth of long bones"]'::jsonb, 'Longitudinal growth of long bones', 'The epiphyseal plate drives longitudinal growth of long bones through continuous endochondral ossification until it closes. Remodeling, appositional growth, and fracture healing occur independently of this plate.'),
('425adbe4-9e21-515b-ac15-7bb02feaea86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The five zones of the epiphyseal growth plate from epiphysis to diaphysis are:', '["Zone of reserve cartilage","calcification","proliferation","hypertrophy","ossification"]'::jsonb, 'Zone of reserve cartilage', 'From epiphysis to diaphysis the growth plate zones are: reserve (resting) cartilage, proliferation, hypertrophy, calcified cartilage, and ossification. The reserve zone is the one adjacent to the epiphysis.'),
('67808237-f226-5641-9793-33ab32dac3e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The zone of calcified cartilage in the growth plate is where:', '["Chondrocytes divide resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood vessels invade accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chondrocytes hypertrophy mediated by specific receptor activation","Cartilage is replaced by bone a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cartilage matrix becomes calcified (calcium phosphate deposition)"]'::jsonb, 'The cartilage matrix becomes calcified (calcium phosphate deposition)', 'In the zone of calcified cartilage, calcium phosphate is deposited in the matrix around hypertrophied chondrocytes, which then die. Chondrocyte division occurs in the proliferative zone, and blood vessels invade at the zone of ossification.'),
('67942439-cd64-5868-85df-f448c67dc999'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The zone of ossification in the growth plate is where:', '["bone formation occurs","Blood vessels invade","osteoblasts lay down bone on the calcified cartilage remnants","Cartilage expands without bone","Chondrocytes proliferate"]'::jsonb, 'Blood vessels invade', 'In the zone of ossification, blood vessels invade the calcified cartilage and osteoblasts lay down bone on the cartilage remnants, beginning ossification. Chondrocyte division and hypertrophy belong to the earlier zones.'),
('a45f2725-3a88-55bc-932b-59cfbfc2626f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The primary spongiosa is composed of:', '["Only calcified cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyaline cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Woven bone with a core of calcified cartilage (mixed spicules)","Lamellar bone only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrocartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Woven bone with a core of calcified cartilage (mixed spicules)', 'The primary spongiosa consists of woven bone spicules with cores of calcified cartilage produced at the growth plate. Pure cartilage, fibrocartilage, or lamellar bone alone does not describe this mixed composition.'),
('8705afe4-0f52-5836-a2d1-d8aa3bec3bc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Closure of the epiphyseal plate occurs when:', '["Proliferation stops and the plate is replaced by bone (epiphyseal fusion)","Cartilage calcifies completely accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteoclasts destroy the plate mediated by specific receptor activation","The plate grows infinitely without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Periosteal bone growth stops a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proliferation stops and the plate is replaced by bone (epiphyseal fusion)', 'The plate closes when chondrocyte proliferation ceases and the remaining cartilage is replaced by bone, fusing the epiphysis and diaphysis and halting longitudinal growth. Cartilage calcification alone does not close the plate.'),
('97e69277-d404-5252-b758-4600b4ea8921'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The primary ossification center develops in the:', '["Articular cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Diaphysis of the developing long bone","Metaphysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Epiphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Perichondrium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphysis of the developing long bone', 'The primary ossification center appears in the diaphysis of a developing long bone during fetal life. Secondary ossification centers arise later in the epiphyses.'),
('62e55a51-9501-5f47-acd7-113821f4a4af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Secondary ossification centers develop in the:', '["Periosteum","Epiphyses after birth (postnatal)","Intervertebral discs","Diaphysis before birth","Metaphysis"]'::jsonb, 'Epiphyses after birth (postnatal)', 'Secondary ossification centers develop in the epiphyses after birth. The diaphyseal center is the primary center and appears before birth.'),
('8f90eb10-3084-57ab-ac69-1b91616422cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Endochondral ossification is the process by which:', '["Only flat bones form a","It is the way cartilage grows","Bone replaces a pre-existing cartilage model","Fibrous tissue becomes bone","Bone forms directly from mesenchyme"]'::jsonb, 'Bone replaces a pre-existing cartilage model', 'In endochondral ossification, a hyaline cartilage model is gradually replaced by bone, as occurs in long bones. Formation of bone directly in mesenchyme without a cartilage precursor defines intramembranous ossification.'),
('616707da-45f5-52ba-a9e6-ef5a92baa30e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Intramembranous ossification is the process by which:', '["Bone forms directly within mesenchymal membranes (without cartilage precursor)","Bone forms by osteoclast activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrous tissue calcifies mediated by specific receptor activation","Perichondral bone forms a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cartilage is converted to bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone forms directly within mesenchymal membranes (without cartilage precursor)', 'Intramembranous ossification forms bone directly within a mesenchymal membrane without a cartilage intermediate, as in flat bones of the skull. Replacement of a cartilage model is endochondral ossification.'),
('3d1a9934-08a0-57a8-9845-f1b087f84047'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteoblasts in intramembranous ossification differentiate from:', '["Chondrocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteoclasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesenchymal stem cells (osteoprogenitors)","Perichondrial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hematopoietic stem cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal stem cells (osteoprogenitors)', 'Osteoblasts arise from mesenchymal stem cells that differentiate into osteoprogenitors and then into osteoblasts. Chondrocytes, osteoclasts, perichondrial cells, and hematopoietic stem cells are not their direct source.'),
('f4086294-c344-53fc-af48-f1f69d63fc64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Rickets in children is caused by:', '["Vitamin D deficiency leading to impaired mineralization of osteoid","Parathyroid hormone excess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcium excess mediated by specific receptor activation","Osteoclast hyperactivity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Growth hormone deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin D deficiency leading to impaired mineralization of osteoid', 'Rickets in children results from vitamin D deficiency, which impairs mineralization of osteoid so that growth plates become widened and soft. PTH excess and osteoclast hyperactivity produce different metabolic bone disorders.'),
('52fdbf45-2d4c-578c-80d6-8f6e99c6b374'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteomalacia in adults is caused by:', '["PTH excess resulting in decreased functional activity","Corticosteroid excess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vitamin D deficiency resulting in inadequate mineralization of bone matrix","Thyroid hormone excess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Estrogen deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin D deficiency resulting in inadequate mineralization of bone matrix', 'Adult osteomalacia is caused by vitamin D deficiency, producing inadequate mineralization of bone matrix with excess unmineralized osteoid. Estrogen deficiency and corticosteroid excess are more directly implicated in osteoporosis.'),
('80209abe-0c2d-5bc9-80c1-95ccea1d0bd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteoporosis is characterized by:', '["Excess bone matrix resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased bone mass with normal mineralization (ratio of mineral to matrix is normal)","Increased bone density with abnormal collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased mineralization of bone matrix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcified cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased bone mass with normal mineralization (ratio of mineral to matrix is normal)', 'Osteoporosis is reduced bone mass with normally mineralized matrix, so the mineral-to-matrix ratio is preserved. Decreased mineralization with abundant osteoid is the hallmark of osteomalacia, not osteoporosis.'),
('7b5b401d-2332-5ee3-a71c-4ebb01d1bd81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Paget disease of bone (osteitis deformans) is characterized by:', '["Mineralization defect a resulting in decreased functional activity","Excessive bone resorption followed by disorganized bone formation (mosaic pattern)","Only increased bone formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only increased bone resorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal bone remodeling leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive bone resorption followed by disorganized bone formation (mosaic pattern)', 'Paget disease of bone features excessive osteoclastic resorption followed by compensatory but disorganized osteoblastic formation, producing the classic mosaic pattern of lamellar bone. A mineralization defect is the signature of osteomalacia, while the other options describe only isolated phases or normal remodeling.'),
('ade03adc-72ef-5bd3-9ccf-5c429adb4ba0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The organic component of bone (osteoid) includes:', '["osteonectin","proteoglycans","and bone sialoprotein","Type I collagen","osteocalcin"]'::jsonb, 'Type I collagen', 'Osteoid, the organic matrix of bone, is composed predominantly of type I collagen, which accounts for about 90% of its dry weight. Osteocalcin, osteonectin, proteoglycans, and bone sialoprotein are noncollagenous macromolecules present in much smaller amounts that coat the collagen fibrils.'),
('d6936fbf-d32d-5fb8-b34c-4391e302d21c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteocalcin is a:', '["Collagen type resulting in decreased functional activity","Proteoglycan accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enzyme mediated by specific receptor activation","Cartilage-specific protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone-specific protein that binds calcium and is a marker of bone formation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone-specific protein that binds calcium and is a marker of bone formation', 'Osteocalcin is a small vitamin K-dependent bone-specific protein secreted by osteoblasts that binds calcium in hydroxyapatite and serves as a serum marker of bone formation. It is neither a collagen, proteoglycan, enzyme, nor a cartilage-specific product.'),
('160ec582-23af-5637-8aef-6f2dd2059f18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteonectin is a:', '["Proteoglycan resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcium chelator a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glycoprotein that links collagen and hydroxyapatite in bone matrix","Collagen cross-linker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone-specific protease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glycoprotein that links collagen and hydroxyapatite in bone matrix', 'Osteonectin is a noncollagenous glycoprotein of bone matrix that binds both type I collagen and calcium/hydroxyapatite, linking the organic and mineral phases of bone. Proteoglycans, cross-linkers, and proteases have different matrix functions.'),
('2d27df3e-0dca-5b0a-8504-1f1d8ee1dc75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Bone sialoprotein (BSP) is a:', '["Osteoblast-specific transcription factor resulting in decreased functional activity","Collagen cross-linker accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enzyme mediated by specific receptor activation","Proteoglycan matrix component without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phosphorylated glycoprotein that promotes osteoclast attachment and mineralization"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphorylated glycoprotein that promotes osteoclast attachment and mineralization', 'Bone sialoprotein is a phosphorylated glycoprotein of bone matrix that promotes osteoclast attachment, acting through integrins, and facilitates matrix mineralization. It is not a transcription factor, cross-linking enzyme, protease, or proteoglycan.'),
('136b328a-81a8-5a2a-8391-dd8953847192'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Alkaline phosphatase is produced by:', '["Myocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Chondrocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteoblasts and is a marker of bone formation","Osteoclasts and is a marker of bone resorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibroblasts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoblasts and is a marker of bone formation', 'Alkaline phosphatase is synthesized and secreted by osteoblasts and is a widely used serum marker of bone-forming activity. Osteoclasts, by contrast, are identified by tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase rather than alkaline phosphatase.'),
('c93d4c36-c774-5982-a164-decf62a7700f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP) is a marker of:', '["Osteocytes","Chondrocytes","Osteoblasts (bone formation)","Osteoclasts (bone resorption)","Fibroblasts"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoclasts (bone resorption)', 'TRAP is a lysosomal enzyme produced by osteoclasts and is used as a specific marker of osteoclasts and bone resorption. Osteoblasts, osteocytes, chondrocytes, and fibroblasts do not express this enzyme as a characteristic marker.'),
('491d4d44-aef4-5338-b2d7-e2350ac3ce32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Type X collagen is specifically produced by:', '["Osteoclasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Resting chondrocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypertrophic chondrocytes in the growth plate","Osteoblasts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophic chondrocytes in the growth plate', 'Type X collagen is synthesized specifically by hypertrophic chondrocytes in the growth plate and is a marker of endochondral ossification. It is not produced by osteoclasts, resting chondrocytes, fibroblasts, or osteoblasts.'),
('09e5f26f-a743-5cb4-87ab-010da2f1ed17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Osteopetrosis (marble bone disease) is caused by:', '["Excess PTH resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcitonin deficiency a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excess osteoclast activity mediated by specific receptor activation","Deficient osteoblast function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Defective osteoclast function (failure of bone resorption)"]'::jsonb, 'Defective osteoclast function (failure of bone resorption)', 'Osteopetrosis results from defective osteoclast function, with failure of bone resorption leading to accumulation of dense, brittle bone. Excessive resorption, excess PTH, and osteoblast deficiency do not produce this sclerotic picture.'),
('3a7e58d7-01d6-57cb-b149-0498521d08b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The Wnt signaling pathway regulates:', '["Osteoclast differentiation only","Osteoblast differentiation and bone formation","Muscle differentiation","Adipocyte formation","Chondrocyte proliferation"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoblast differentiation and bone formation', 'The Wnt/beta-catenin signaling pathway promotes osteoblast differentiation and bone formation, and is inhibited by sclerostin. Osteoclast differentiation is driven instead by the RANK-RANKL system, so the other options are unrelated targets.'),
('4792e114-afc4-5105-a1a3-75013a5b1b8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The lacunae of bone contain:', '["Osteoblasts","Osteoprogenitors","Osteocytes (mature bone cells)","Osteoclasts","Blood vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Osteocytes (mature bone cells)', 'Osteocytes, the mature bone cells, occupy the lacunae of bone matrix. Osteoblasts lie along bone surfaces, osteoclasts occupy Howship lacunae, osteoprogenitors line the periosteum and endosteum, and blood vessels travel in Haversian canals.'),
('9959d9df-074c-51ce-9fde-a940a5f4290e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The canaliculi of bone contain:', '["Osteoblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerve fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cytoplasmic processes of osteocytes","Osteoclasts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Capillaries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cytoplasmic processes of osteocytes', 'Canaliculi are fine channels radiating from lacunae that contain the slender cytoplasmic processes of osteocytes, permitting intercellular communication through gap junctions. They do not carry osteoblasts, nerve fibers, osteoclasts, or capillaries.'),
('70120b47-fda9-58f1-87ff-4f80906426c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The structure that connects two adjacent osteocyte processes in bone is:', '["Desmosome","Synapse","Adherens junction","Gap junction (via connexin 43)","Tight junction"]'::jsonb, 'Gap junction (via connexin 43)', 'Adjacent osteocyte processes are joined by gap junctions formed by connexin 43, allowing direct passage of small molecules and ions between bone cells. Tight, adherens, and desmosome junctions serve adhesion or sealing rather than metabolic coupling.'),
('19641438-76a3-5442-b7a2-a4710f8d7486'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which tissue exhibits metachromasia with toluidine blue?', '["Tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","Adipose tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood mediated by specific receptor activation","Cartilage matrix (due to high GAG content)","Bone matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilage matrix (due to high GAG content)', 'The sulfated glycosaminoglycans abundant in cartilage matrix cause the staining dye toluidine blue to shift color, a property called metachromasia. Tissues with little or no such GAG content, such as tendon, adipose tissue, blood, and bone matrix, stain orthochromatically.'),
('fdfa1526-87a9-5147-aa0d-55818a9b05f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'What stain is used to visualize elastic fibers?', '["Verhoeff-Van Gieson stain","Masson trichrome","Alcian blue","Silver stain","PAS stain"]'::jsonb, 'Verhoeff-Van Gieson stain', 'The Verhoeff-Van Gieson method colors elastic fibers black using Verhoeff''s iron hematoxylin and counterstains other matrix components. Masson trichrome highlights collagen, Alcian blue stains acid mucins, silver stains reticulin, and PAS stains carbohydrates.'),
('060f3848-a593-53ce-96fd-2e499c94dfeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'What stain is used to visualize mucin (GAGs)?', '["PAS stain a","Sudan black","Verhoeff stain","Alcian blue","H&E"]'::jsonb, 'Alcian blue', 'Alcian blue is a cationic copper phthalocyanine dye that binds acid mucopolysaccharides and mucins at low pH, staining them blue. PAS stains glycogen and neutral carbohydrates, Sudan black stains lipids, and Verhoeff stain demonstrates elastic fibers.'),
('351ff2a9-d594-59d2-a0d8-b026d79172fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'What stain is used to visualize collagen fibers?', '["Giemsa a resulting in decreased functional activity","PAS stain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","H&E mediated by specific receptor activation","Masson trichrome (blue or green)","Verhoeff stain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Masson trichrome (blue or green)', 'Masson trichrome is a three-color stain in which collagen fibers appear blue (or green), contrasting with red muscle cytoplasm. Silver methods demonstrate reticulin, Verhoeff stain shows elastin, and PAS and H&E are used for other purposes.'),
('c7ca798b-0e44-50c7-aaae-2237b1d7feca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'What stain is used to visualize myelin?', '["Sudan black","Luxol fast blue","Cresyl violet","H&E","Bielschowsky"]'::jsonb, 'Luxol fast blue', 'Luxol fast blue is a copper phthalocyanine dye that stains myelin sheaths blue in sections of the nervous system. Sudan black stains lipids generally, Cresyl violet stains Nissl substance, Bielschowsky silver stains neurofibrils, and H&E is a routine stain.'),
('a14182ad-92a5-55cc-9b7b-36bcf79621c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Immunohistochemistry uses:', '["In situ hybridization resulting in decreased functional activity","Silver precipitation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antibodies to detect specific antigens in tissue sections","Dyes that bind to specific molecules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enzyme histochemistry leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antibodies to detect specific antigens in tissue sections', 'Immunohistochemistry localizes specific antigens in tissue sections using antibodies, which are then visualized typically with an enzyme- or fluorophore-linked detection system. It differs from in situ hybridization, which detects nucleic acids, and from simple dye-based methods.'),
('441ea93c-4278-5aab-8220-a0c21fad8086'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'DAB (diaminobenzidine) is a:', '["Chromogen used in IHC (gives brown reaction product)","Fluorochrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enzyme mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibody without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radioactive label leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chromogen used in IHC (gives brown reaction product)', 'DAB (3,3''-diaminobenzidine) is a chromogenic substrate of horseradish peroxidase that produces a stable brown reaction product at the site of antigen-antibody binding in immunohistochemistry. It is not a fluorochrome, enzyme, antibody, or radioactive label.'),
('5d62a7a8-0555-55b7-86f3-2b3427b8dcd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'In situ hybridization (ISH) detects:', '["Proteins resulting in decreased functional activity","Ions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Specific nucleic acid sequences (DNA or mRNA) in tissue sections","Lipids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carbohydrates leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Specific nucleic acid sequences (DNA or mRNA) in tissue sections', 'In situ hybridization uses labeled complementary probes to detect specific DNA or RNA sequences directly within tissue sections. It does not detect proteins, ions, lipids, or carbohydrates, which are demonstrated by immunostaining or histochemical methods.'),
('ea23ed48-3b22-5cd9-9ddd-5ac2a82b12a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) uses:', '["Fluorescently labeled probes to detect specific DNA sequences","Silver staining accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gold-conjugated antibodies a mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibodies with fluorescent tags without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enzymes that produce fluorescence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluorescently labeled probes to detect specific DNA sequences', 'FISH uses fluorescently labeled nucleic acid probes complementary to specific DNA (or RNA) sequences to localize them in chromosomes and cells, commonly for cytogenetic analysis. Silver and gold labels and enzyme chromogens are used by other detection systems.'),
('9c138632-5efc-5a01-8a25-42c99ee11e89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'The terminal bar (junctional complex) is visible in light microscopy in:', '["Hyaline cartilage","Transitional epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium (intestine)"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar epithelium (intestine)', 'The terminal bar is the apical junctional complex (zonula occludens, zonula adherens, and macula adherens) and appears as a dense line at the luminal ends of the tall simple columnar cells lining the intestine. The other epithelia overlie different basement-membrane relationships and do not show this feature at light microscopy.'),
('45540523-be39-5bf7-b887-168e6f1087c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which cell junction prevents paracellular leakage and is found in the apicalmost part of lateral membranes?', '["Adherens junction (zonula adherens)","Tight junction (zonula occludens)","Hemidesmosome","Desmosome (macula adherens)","Gap junction"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junction (zonula occludens)', 'Tight junctions form a belt-like seal at the apicalmost portion of the lateral membrane that blocks paracellular diffusion of molecules. Adherens and desmosomal junctions mediate adhesion, gap junctions allow ion exchange, and hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basement membrane.'),
('a722800a-a38c-5c72-8eb4-49ad38b95a0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Which option best defines the primary characteristic of epithelial tissue?', '["Rounded cells within abundant extracellular matrix","Spindle-shaped cells with little ECM","Multinucleated cells with ECM","Closely aggregated cells with minimal extracellular matrix","Cells arranged in spirals"]'::jsonb, 'Closely aggregated cells with minimal extracellular matrix', 'Epithelial tissue consists of closely aggregated cells with very little extracellular matrix, arranged as continuous sheets that cover surfaces and line cavities. Cells embedded in abundant matrix, spindle shapes, multinucleation, and spiral arrangements are features of other tissue types.'),
('3b83aaf6-892d-5e8d-98b1-565eb80b1cf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('cfe511eb-ddfe-5d29-90d5-14cc98e3826e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('42939ec9-d7c1-5b17-a71d-41d7a771f98e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('8d7ce20c-cd45-5e4b-9eb2-92793e2c3b00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('be58c0d0-b815-50c0-9b01-18162c576d0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('d823505e-db98-5990-b0e7-227b524b1d71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('047cce81-c60e-5c42-bd4d-3eb94855a1b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('73e245c5-79e6-52dd-ab51-96863faa474b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('06e83aa8-f31d-56e0-880f-520efa714937'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('db976611-b1fd-54f5-a96e-b7021ba3aefb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('522c52ed-25b2-5817-8856-0e7b241a8ad8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('41fd5b83-ef1d-5c37-8c1a-d49b90db08a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('f6c6f30d-4076-5612-9a6b-fb516b94353d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('de9fe034-30e2-5f18-b710-11db164a6cb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'During therapeutic management, What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('824dbf65-0cd3-5f87-ab6b-8a9b2e51d229'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Regarding clinical cases: Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('86f48a61-fbab-521b-b4ea-a1f711abd4b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'In medical practice, Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('cffb95f1-c961-5c38-b2cc-b339590d87db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('de146d49-ae49-5a10-99e9-3030d47aa2c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('c4435102-5e8e-52d3-9ce2-d887fe7636c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'During therapeutic management, Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('1e55e0b0-83a6-55d0-be02-022971992624'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Regarding clinical cases: Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('d85a2e33-d3ac-5f59-a99b-242dd8328811'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'In medical practice, St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('b58097cd-1c31-5127-8ae2-cec4ac0e92f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('838ea501-1e8f-556b-b542-cc6eaa2bbf17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('0b7cfa8a-6d21-53c7-a6c4-110db59224b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'During therapeutic management, Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('e095f34c-3628-5eba-98fa-e806f4446d9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Epithelia Cell Junctions', 'Regarding clinical cases: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('703e3e0f-54a6-5fed-bf93-81b0fc81045d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'What are the three types of muscle tissue?', '["cardiac","Skeletal","None of the above","and smooth","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal', 'Muscle tissue is classified into three types: skeletal, cardiac, and smooth. Skeletal muscle is striated and under voluntary control, cardiac muscle is striated but involuntary, and smooth muscle is non-striated and involuntary.'),
('2cc5afe3-b1ce-585f-bd63-32508e866f43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Skeletal muscle cells are also called:', '["Myoblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Myocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcomeres mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long","multinucleated cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long', 'Skeletal muscle cells are elongated, multinucleated tubes, so they are called muscle fibers or myofibers. Myoblasts are the embryonic precursor cells that fuse to form fibers, and sarcomeres are the repeating contractile units, not cells.'),
('d2677941-d825-58c2-8984-8442937dc247'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcolemma is the:', '["Nucleus of muscle","Connective tissue around muscle","Endoplasmic reticulum of muscle","Plasma membrane of a muscle cell","Sarcoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma membrane of a muscle cell', 'The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane that surrounds every skeletal muscle fiber and propagates the action potential. The sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm, and the sarcoplasmic reticulum is the modified smooth endoplasmic reticulum.'),
('47aeea7e-77b7-5884-a901-580cc904b87b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcoplasm is the:', '["Cytoplasm of a muscle cell","Nucleus","Glycogen","Myofibrils a","Mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Cytoplasm of a muscle cell', 'The sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of a muscle fiber, containing myofibrils, glycogen, mitochondria, and the sarcoplasmic reticulum. The nucleus, glycogen, and mitochondria are organelles or stored substrates within the sarcoplasm rather than the sarcoplasm itself.'),
('e6147a96-410b-5741-b524-ab45a90d20d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myofibrils are composed of:', '["Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end","Myosin only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Actin only mediated by specific receptor activation","T-tubules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth ER leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end', 'Myofibrils are chains of sarcomeres arranged end-to-end, giving skeletal muscle its striated appearance. They contain both actin and myosin (not one alone), and T-tubules and smooth ER are membrane systems, not contractile components.'),
('b9407ddc-0163-55cb-89b8-82be32f9a703'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcomere is defined as the segment between:', '["Z-discs","M-lines","H-zones","I-bands","A-bands"]'::jsonb, 'Z-discs', 'A sarcomere spans from one Z-disc to the next Z-disc and is the functional contractile unit of striated muscle. The A-band and I-band are zones contained within the sarcomere, and the M-line and H-zone lie centrally within the A-band.'),
('297f7bbe-26fc-51ea-b23e-8175302e78d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The Z-disc (Z-line) is:', '["The center of the A-band","The region of myosin only","The boundary of the H-zone","The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)","The region of actin only"]'::jsonb, 'The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)', 'The Z-disc is the dense line where actin (thin) filaments of adjacent sarcomeres are anchored by alpha-actinin. It does not lie in the A-band, H-zone, or contain myosin.'),
('4ef07828-4933-5c82-8ad2-dcf91ceeecef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The A-band of the sarcomere contains:', '["Thick filaments only resulting in decreased functional activity","Thin filaments only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only Z-disc mediated by specific receptor activation","I-band and H-zone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both thick (myosin) and thin (actin) filaments (the entire length of myosin)"]'::jsonb, 'Both thick (myosin) and thin (actin) filaments (the entire length of myosin)', 'The A-band spans the entire length of the myosin thick filaments, with actin thin filaments overlapping in the periphery while the central H-zone contains only myosin. It is not limited to a single filament type.'),
('3d0beb0c-ebbe-5530-96fb-ac894f29e6db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The I-band of the sarcomere contains:', '["Thick filaments only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only troponin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thin filaments (actin) only (plus Z-disc at center)","Myosin and actin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only myosin heads leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin filaments (actin) only (plus Z-disc at center)', 'The I-band contains only actin thin filaments and is bisected at its center by the Z-disc. It contains no myosin, so the anisotropic A-band region does not extend into it.'),
('1e65ffa5-6b63-5b11-9700-e89fcd94158b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The H-zone (H-band) is:', '["The M-line resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)","The region with both actin and myosin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The I-band leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)', 'The H-zone is the central lighter region of the A-band where thick filaments exist without overlapping thin filaments; it narrows during contraction. The M-line is the thin dense line at the center of the H-zone.'),
('ee33e514-9faf-51e6-93fc-d26ee0daf327'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The M-line is:', '["The binding site for actin resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The region of titin attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","The I-band center a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin"]'::jsonb, 'The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin', 'The M-line is the dense line in the middle of the H-zone where M-protein and myomesin cross-link adjacent myosin thick filaments to stabilize the A-band. It is not a binding site for actin or part of the I-band.'),
('3d7ae10d-ee90-5712-b166-fae3d0bec6e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Thick filaments of the sarcomere are composed of:', '["Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)","Actin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Titin mediated by specific receptor activation","Troponin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tropomyosin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)', 'Thick filaments are polymers of myosin II, each molecule a hexamer of two heavy chains and two pairs of light chains. Actin forms thin filaments, while troponin and tropomyosin are regulatory proteins associated with actin.'),
('593bba0c-f977-59d8-92da-d81427e6ec88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The globular head of myosin has binding sites for:', '["Actin and ATP","ATP only","Troponin","Actin only","Calcium only"]'::jsonb, 'Actin and ATP', 'Each globular myosin head has two binding sites: one for actin (forming the cross-bridge) and one for ATP, whose hydrolysis energizes the head. Calcium regulates contraction indirectly through troponin rather than by binding myosin.'),
('3f73951b-2aa2-56be-9a74-669601a3f231'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Tropomyosin is a:', '["Myosin-binding protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Actin-binding protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcium-binding protein mediated by specific receptor activation","Long without affecting the primary tissue pathway","rod-shaped molecule that lies in the groove of the actin helix and blocks the myosin-binding site in relaxed muscle"]'::jsonb, 'rod-shaped molecule that lies in the groove of the actin helix and blocks the myosin-binding site in relaxed muscle', 'Tropomyosin is a long rod-shaped protein lying in the groove of the actin helix, covering the myosin-binding sites to prevent cross-bridge formation in resting muscle. Calcium binding to troponin C shifts tropomyosin and exposes these sites.'),
('555aad25-75c7-57af-9408-3ca8c0462453'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Troponin complex consists of three subunits:', '["TnI resulting in decreased functional activity","TnT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TnI (inhibits actin-myosin interaction)","TnC (binds calcium) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TnT (binds tropomyosin) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TnI (inhibits actin-myosin interaction)', 'The troponin complex has three subunits: TnC binds calcium, TnI inhibits the actin-myosin interaction, and TnT anchors the complex to tropomyosin. Together they regulate actin availability for cross-bridge formation.'),
('d1dcbbd7-8423-58ec-8972-5a23633c16fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The main protein that anchors thick filaments to the Z-disc is:', '["Nebulin","Desmin","α-Actinin","Dystrophin a","Titin (connectin)"]'::jsonb, 'Titin (connectin)', 'Titin (connectin) is a giant elastic protein that runs from the Z-disc to the M-line, anchoring myosin thick filaments to the Z-disc and providing passive elasticity. Nebulin regulates thin-filament length, and desmin links Z-discs between myofibrils.'),
('0528421a-b1ea-56f1-aced-57e6670d346b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Nebulin is a giant protein that:', '["Anchors actin to the sarcolemma resulting in decreased functional activity","Cross-links myosin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extends from Z-disc to M-line (like titin) mediated by specific receptor activation","Is a calcium-binding protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extends along the thin filament and regulates its length and assembly"]'::jsonb, 'Extends along the thin filament and regulates its length and assembly', 'Nebulin is a giant rod-like protein extending along the actin thin filament, acting as a molecular ruler that determines and regulates thin-filament length. Titin, not nebulin, spans from Z-disc to M-line, and nebulin does not bind calcium.'),
('78f5e012-98ac-57ab-98f6-cea3594fe3d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'α-Actinin is a major protein of the:', '["A-band resulting in decreased functional activity","H-zone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","I-band mediated by specific receptor activation","Z-disc (cross-links actin filaments from adjacent sarcomeres)","M-line leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Z-disc (cross-links actin filaments from adjacent sarcomeres)', 'Alpha-actinin is the principal protein of the Z-disc, where it cross-links actin thin filaments arriving from adjacent sarcomeres. It does not localize to the A-band, H-zone, or M-line.'),
('e94b900a-5f15-5218-9ec8-27671bbe6d30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Desmin is an intermediate filament that:', '["Forms the T-tubule system a resulting in decreased functional activity","Binds calcium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Connects Z-discs of adjacent myofibrils and links them to the sarcolemma (and to nuclei and mitochondria)","Is the main protein of the M-line without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Links actin to myosin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connects Z-discs of adjacent myofibrils and links them to the sarcolemma (and to nuclei and mitochondria)', 'Desmin is the intermediate filament of muscle that links Z-discs of neighboring myofibrils to one another and connects them to the sarcolemma, nuclei, and mitochondria, maintaining sarcomere alignment. It is not involved in calcium binding or actin-myosin cross-linking.'),
('b597a0a4-648d-564c-91a4-92478230417d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Dystrophin is a protein that:', '["Is the main contractile protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Is a calcium channel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Links the actin cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix via the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex (DGC)","Is a sarcomeric protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Forms Z-discs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Links the actin cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix via the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex (DGC)', 'Dystrophin is a large cytoskeletal protein beneath the sarcolemma that links cortical actin to the extracellular matrix through the dystrophin-glycoprotein complex, stabilizing the membrane during contraction. It is not a contractile or sarcomeric protein.'),
('2a1ee5a2-5ffb-5aa3-9ffe-03fa3ee41174'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Becker muscular dystrophy is caused by:', '["Autosomal recessive disorder resulting in decreased functional activity","Sarcoglycan mutations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete absence of dystrophin mediated by specific receptor activation","Partially functional dystrophin (reduced amount or abnormal size)","Dystroglycan mutations a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Partially functional dystrophin (reduced amount or abnormal size)', 'Becker muscular dystrophy results from mutations producing a reduced amount or structurally abnormal, partially functional dystrophin, giving a milder phenotype with later onset. Complete absence of dystrophin causes the more severe Duchenne muscular dystrophy.'),
('c7c1361b-fdf9-5cd7-9ec8-9b6b4a44adf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) in skeletal muscle stores:', '["Sodium ions","Glycogen","Calcium ions (Ca2+)","ATP","Potassium ions"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium ions (Ca2+)', 'The sarcoplasmic reticulum stores and sequesters calcium ions, releasing them through ryanodine receptors to trigger contraction and re-accumulating them via SERCA for relaxation. Sodium and potassium are handled by the sarcolemma, not stored in the SR.'),
('3b2dcf50-bd9f-5292-ab90-f89e3a8fc440'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Terminal cisternae are:', '["Dilated sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum at the A-I junction (adjacent to T-tubules)","The Z-disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The T-tubule openings mediated by specific receptor activation","The mitochondria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sarcolemma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum at the A-I junction (adjacent to T-tubules)', 'Terminal cisternae are dilated end sacs of the sarcoplasmic reticulum that flank each T-tubule at the A-I junction, forming a triad and concentrating calcium for rapid release. They are not part of the Z-disc, T-tubule, or sarcolemma.'),
('d747bfb1-5a24-5a7b-ae5d-f95f9c1f6e04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The T-tubule system is an invagination of the:', '["Myofibril resulting in decreased functional activity","Sarcolemma (plasma membrane) that conducts action potentials deep into the fiber","Endoplasmic reticulum mediated by specific receptor activation","Nuclear envelope without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcolemma (plasma membrane) that conducts action potentials deep into the fiber', 'T-tubules are deep invaginations of the sarcolemma (plasma membrane) that transmit action potentials from the surface into the fiber interior, where they oppose the terminal cisternae. The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the adjacent storage network, not the source of the invagination.'),
('3730347e-ffb9-5439-8627-840d4ffbbc56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal muscle is triggered by:', '["Acetylcholine release a resulting in decreased functional activity","ATP binding to myosin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Release of Ca2+ from the SR via ryanodine receptors (RyR1) activated by the T-tubule voltage sensor (DHPR)","Direct Ca2+ influx from the extracellular space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Actin-myosin binding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Release of Ca2+ from the SR via ryanodine receptors (RyR1) activated by the T-tubule voltage sensor (DHPR)', 'Excitation-contraction coupling begins when the action potential depolarizes the T-tubule, activating the DHPR voltage sensor which mechanically opens RyR1 channels to release stored Ca2+ from the SR. In skeletal muscle the Ca2+ comes from the SR, not the extracellular space.'),
('25b4725e-7609-5e66-9794-0de42bdb9ea7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR) in skeletal muscle acts as:', '["A calcium channel only resulting in decreased functional activity","An ATPase a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A potassium channel mediated by specific receptor activation","The voltage sensor that couples T-tubule depolarization to RyR1 opening","A sodium channel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The voltage sensor that couples T-tubule depolarization to RyR1 opening', 'In skeletal muscle the DHPR functions primarily as a voltage sensor in the T-tubule membrane that physically couples depolarization to RyR1 opening in the SR. Unlike in cardiac muscle, direct calcium influx through the DHPR is not required for contraction.'),
('36a8e9c5-5d8f-5111-930e-6e5a5fe0fcee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The ryanodine receptor (RyR) is the:', '["T-tubule protein resulting in decreased functional activity","DHP receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Voltage sensor mediated by specific receptor activation","Ca2+ reuptake pump without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ca2+ release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum', 'The ryanodine receptor is a large calcium-release channel in the SR membrane that opens to flood the sarcoplasm with Ca2+ during excitation-contraction coupling. Calcium reuptake is handled separately by the SERCA pump.'),
('c6c159af-5c71-55a7-9c8c-d103f77b0a99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Ca2+ binding to which protein initiates muscle contraction?', '["Myosin","Troponin I","Troponin C (TnC)","Calmodulin","Tropomyosin"]'::jsonb, 'Troponin C (TnC)', 'Calcium initiates contraction by binding to troponin C, inducing a conformational change that moves tropomyosin away and exposes the myosin-binding sites on actin. Calmodulin mediates calcium signaling in smooth muscle, not in skeletal muscle thin filaments.'),
('dcb9f67f-18a6-5263-9113-15df8d698a1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The cross-bridge cycle begins when:', '["The action potential arrives","ATP binds to myosin","Ca2+ is pumped back into the SR","The myosin head binds to actin (after tropomyosin moves)","The T-tubule depolarizes a"]'::jsonb, 'The myosin head binds to actin (after tropomyosin moves)', 'Once tropomyosin shifts to expose the actin binding sites, the energized myosin head attaches to actin to form the cross-bridge, starting the cycle. Action potential arrival and depolarization are earlier events, and ATP binding detaches the myosin head rather than starting the cycle.'),
('feed8c8b-986d-52c0-afae-5e2dfb41df74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The power stroke of the cross-bridge cycle is driven by:', '["Release of ADP and Pi from the myosin head (conformational change)","Ca2+ release a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ATP hydrolysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Ca2+ binding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ATP binding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Release of ADP and Pi from the myosin head (conformational change)', 'The power stroke occurs when the myosin head tilts and pulls actin as inorganic phosphate and ADP are released, a process coupled to the energy stored from prior ATP hydrolysis. ATP hydrolysis energizes the head earlier, while ATP binding causes detachment at the end of the cycle.'),
('32e9fe0f-ca7e-5431-93e4-4545f255a082'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Muscle relaxation occurs when:', '["Ca2+ is released from the SR resulting in decreased functional activity","ATP is depleted accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ca2+ is pumped back into the SR by SERCA (sarco/endoplasmic reticulum Ca2+-ATPase)","The action potential fires without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myosin binds actin a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ is pumped back into the SR by SERCA (sarco/endoplasmic reticulum Ca2+-ATPase)', 'Relaxation follows active transport of Ca2+ back into the SR by the SERCA pump, lowering cytosolic calcium so troponin releases Ca2+ and tropomyosin again blocks the actin binding sites. Rigor (rigor mortis) occurs instead when ATP is depleted.'),
('2ed2ba41-fa30-5c9f-a07c-b7fadb49681d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'SERCA stands for:', '["Smooth endoplasmic reticulum calcium ATPase","SR energy release channel","A sodium calcium exchanger","Sarco/endoplasmic reticulum Ca2+-ATPase","Slow energy release calcium ATPase"]'::jsonb, 'Sarco/endoplasmic reticulum Ca2+-ATPase', 'SERCA is the sarco/endoplasmic reticulum Ca2+-ATPase, a pump in the SR membrane that hydrolyzes ATP to move Ca2+ back into the SR during relaxation. It is not a release channel or an exchanger.'),
('f22fa8d8-6e9c-5fb5-8dba-8bc9e67782f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Phospholamban is a protein that:', '["Opens RyR a resulting in decreased functional activity","Pumps Ca2+ out of the cell accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activates SERCA mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibits SERCA2 in cardiac muscle (phosphorylation relieves inhibition)","Binds to Ca2+ directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits SERCA2 in cardiac muscle (phosphorylation relieves inhibition)', 'Phospholamban is a small regulatory protein that tonically inhibits SERCA2 in cardiac and slow skeletal muscle; beta-adrenergic phosphorylation of phospholamban removes this inhibition, accelerating Ca2+ uptake and increasing relaxation and contractility.'),
('d395bda6-fbbc-5577-9fd9-b1fa6b61ce9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Malignant hyperthermia is caused by:', '["Deficiency of SERCA resulting in decreased functional activity","causing hypermetabolism and hyperthermia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mutations in RyR1 (ryanodine receptor) causing uncontrolled Ca2+ release from the SR","Myosin mutations without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mutations in DHPR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mutations in RyR1 (ryanodine receptor) causing uncontrolled Ca2+ release from the SR', 'Malignant hyperthermia is an inherited disorder of the skeletal muscle ryanodine receptor (RyR1) in which volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine trigger massive unregulated Ca2+ release from the SR, producing sustained contraction, hypermetabolism, and hyperthermia. SERCA deficiency is not the underlying cause.'),
('b7c06f31-fddd-58b7-875e-f6303d57a45e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The motor end plate is:', '["The neuromuscular junction (synapse between motor neuron and skeletal muscle fiber)","The Z-disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sarcolemma mediated by specific receptor activation","The SR region without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The tendon attachment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The neuromuscular junction (synapse between motor neuron and skeletal muscle fiber)', 'The motor end plate is the specialized postsynaptic region of the sarcolemma where an alpha motor neuron forms the neuromuscular junction and releases acetylcholine. It is not a sarcomeric structure such as the Z-disc or a tendon attachment site.'),
('37ebf235-17df-5bf3-88cd-3b4a71bae4db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Acetylcholine (ACh) at the neuromuscular junction binds to:', '["RyR a resulting in decreased functional activity","DHP receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nicotinic ACh receptors (ionotropic) on the motor end plate","Muscarinic ACh receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adrenergic receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nicotinic ACh receptors (ionotropic) on the motor end plate', 'Acetylcholine binds to nicotinic ACh receptors, which are intrinsic cation channels, on the motor end plate. Their opening depolarizes the fiber to generate an action potential; muscarinic and adrenergic receptors mediate other autonomic effects.'),
('921f2474-5856-53d8-8115-454044eb92af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Acetylcholinesterase (AChE) at the NMJ:', '["Degrades ACh in the synaptic cleft to terminate signaling","Cleaves ATP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Releases ACh mediated by specific receptor activation","Synthesizes ACh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pumps Ca2+ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Degrades ACh in the synaptic cleft to terminate signaling', 'Acetylcholinesterase rapidly hydrolyzes acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft, ending the signal so the end plate can repolarize and the fiber can ready itself for the next impulse. It neither synthesizes nor releases ACh.'),
('ae71bf42-6f63-5073-a391-fe8ba60bf8a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease against:', '["Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the NMJ (postsynaptic)","Acetylcholinesterase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Voltage-gated sodium channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Pre-synaptic calcium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ryanodine receptors a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the NMJ (postsynaptic)', 'Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder in which antibodies destroy or block postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction, reducing the end plate potential and causing fatigable muscle weakness. AChE inhibition with agents such as neostigmine improves symptoms.'),
('988fccc6-2b53-5fb7-a972-a69cf2e82972'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The compound that blocks ACh release from the presynaptic terminal (causing flaccid paralysis) is:', '["Neostigmine","Atropine","Curare a","Bungarotoxin","Botulinum toxin"]'::jsonb, 'Botulinum toxin', 'Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins in the presynaptic terminal, preventing vesicle fusion and thus blocking acetylcholine release, which causes flaccid paralysis. Curare and bungarotoxin instead act postsynaptically on ACh receptors.'),
('9f27b5b8-402c-50bc-83ca-6b4245c8c48e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Curare (tubocurarine) is a competitive antagonist that:', '["Inhibits AChE resulting in decreased functional activity","Blocks nicotinic ACh receptors at the NMJ (prevents ACh binding)","Activates ACh receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks sodium channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocks ACh release leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocks nicotinic ACh receptors at the NMJ (prevents ACh binding)', 'Curare is a competitive antagonist of postsynaptic nicotinic ACh receptors, occupying them so acetylcholine cannot bind, producing neuromuscular blockade and flaccid paralysis. Its effect can be overcome by acetylcholinesterase inhibitors that raise ACh levels.'),
('6955a220-bbd5-5194-82cf-e3140a8d50ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Skeletal muscle can be classified into fiber types. Type I fibers are:', '["fatigue-resistant (red muscle)","oxidative","Fast-twitch","Slow-twitch","glycolytic"]'::jsonb, 'Slow-twitch', 'Type I fibers are slow-twitch, oxidative, fatigue-resistant red fibers rich in myoglobin and mitochondria, suited for sustained aerobic activity. Fast-twitch fibers are the Type II group, which rely more on glycolytic metabolism and fatigue more readily.'),
('5b18a205-3bcc-5bb9-868f-3c56af2ad031'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Type IIa skeletal muscle fibers are:', '["glycolytic (fast fatigable)","oxidative-glycolytic (fast fatigue-resistant)","Fast-twitch","Slow-twitch","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Fast-twitch', 'Type IIa fibers are fast-twitch. Concretely they are fast oxidative-glycolytic and fatigue-resistant, intermediate between slow Type I and fast glycolytic IIb/IIx fibers. They contract quickly but are more resistant to fatigue than pure glycolytic fibers.'),
('424739c7-8dd3-5700-8fcc-a0743fd7b00f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The muscle spindle is a:', '["The tendon organ resulting in decreased functional activity","A type of fiber accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The Z-disc mediated by specific receptor activation","The neuromuscular junction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensory receptor within skeletal muscle that detects stretch and regulates muscle length"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory receptor within skeletal muscle that detects stretch and regulates muscle length', 'The muscle spindle is an encapsulated stretch receptor lying parallel to extrafusal fibers, containing intrafusal fibers and sensory endings that monitor muscle length and drive the stretch reflex. It is not the tendon organ, which senses tension.'),
('7fee781e-bb06-5071-ac5c-51f8509fba83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Intrafusal fibers (within the muscle spindle) are innervated by:', '["Sensory fibers only","Alpha motor neurons (α-MNs)","Both α and γ MNs","Autonomic fibers","Gamma motor neurons (γ-MNs)"]'::jsonb, 'Gamma motor neurons (γ-MNs)', 'Intrafusal fibers receive motor innervation from gamma motor neurons, which regulate the sensitivity of the spindle; they are not innervated by alpha motor neurons. Alpha motor neurons supply extrafusal muscle fibers, while sensory fibers carry spindle afferent information.'),
('f5c62899-8f12-5397-bbbb-969dbf1b1e1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The Golgi tendon organ (GTO) is a sensory receptor located at:', '["In the joint capsule resulting in decreased functional activity","In the bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myotendinous junction (between muscle and tendon) — detects muscle tension","In the muscle belly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myotendinous junction (between muscle and tendon) — detects muscle tension', 'The Golgi tendon organ lies in series at the myotendinous junction, where its sensory endings are compressed when the muscle contracts, detecting tension and triggering the inverse stretch reflex to protect against excessive force. It is not located within the muscle belly.'),
('38b0d207-395c-5724-8a09-b9b09f968153'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The structure that surrounds an entire skeletal muscle is the:', '["Sarcolemma","Epimysium","Endomysium","Basal lamina","Perimysium"]'::jsonb, 'Epimysium', 'The epimysium is the dense connective tissue sheath enclosing the entire muscle. The perimysium surrounds fascicles within the muscle, and the endomysium surrounds each individual muscle fiber.'),
('998940c1-2e7c-57b7-8401-c34cc492ee90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The perimysium surrounds:', '["Individual muscle fibers","The whole muscle","The sarcomere","The myofibrils","Fascicles (bundles of muscle fibers)"]'::jsonb, 'Fascicles (bundles of muscle fibers)', 'The perimysium is the connective tissue layer that encloses fascicles, the bundles of muscle fibers, and carries larger vessels and nerves. Individual fibers are wrapped by the endomysium, and the whole muscle by the epimysium.'),
('23fdaf2d-29e2-5dc5-a14e-d2833d38a3bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The endomysium surrounds:', '["The sarcomere","The fascicle","The myofibrils","Individual muscle fibers","The whole muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Individual muscle fibers', 'The endomysium is the delicate connective tissue that surrounds each individual muscle fiber, providing support for capillaries and nerve endings. The perimysium surrounds fascicles and the epimysium encloses the entire muscle.'),
('18d58705-4d34-5cea-acf1-803a7ce52d54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Satellite cells in skeletal muscle are:', '["Fibroblasts resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Mature muscle fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Quiescent myogenic stem cells located between the sarcolemma and basal lamina (responsible for repair and regeneration)","Adipocytes a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quiescent myogenic stem cells located between the sarcolemma and basal lamina (responsible for repair and regeneration)', 'Satellite cells are quiescent myogenic stem cells wedged between the sarcolemma and basal lamina of muscle fibers; after injury they proliferate and fuse to repair or regenerate the fiber. They are distinct from fibroblasts, which deposit connective tissue, and from inflammatory cells.'),
('e8894466-fd27-56eb-8faa-5a0855d33c0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which muscle type has centrally located nuclei?', '["Only smooth muscle","Cardiac and smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle only","All muscle types","Only cardiac muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac and smooth muscle', 'Both cardiac and smooth muscle cells have a single central (perinuclear) nucleus, whereas skeletal muscle fibers are multinucleated with nuclei displaced to the periphery beneath the sarcolemma.'),
('238edd81-791d-5635-9190-e909d9c935db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Cardiac muscle cells are also called:', '["Sarcomeres","Myocytes","Myofibers","Cardiomyocytes","Myofibrils"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiomyocytes', 'Cardiac muscle cells are termed cardiomyocytes, combining cardio (heart) with myocyte. Sarcomeres and myofibrils are intracellular contractile structures rather than cells, and myofibers (skeletal muscle fibers) are long and multinucleated.'),
('5c763347-19ce-5728-8a05-bd5000875d4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Intercalated discs are characteristic of:', '["Skeletal muscle","Both skeletal and cardiac","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Both smooth and cardiac"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac muscle', 'Intercalated discs are specialized junctional complexes that join adjacent cardiac muscle cells end to end. They are not present in skeletal or smooth muscle, both of which lack these transverse overlapping junctions.'),
('d63a2869-c090-5aa9-8cb8-ebd83d29fbce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Intercalated discs consist of three specialized junctions:', '["Fascia adherens (actin attachment)","macula adherens (desmosome)","Tight junctions","and gap junctions","adherens"]'::jsonb, 'Fascia adherens (actin attachment)', 'The three junctions of the intercalated disc are fascia adherens (actin attachment), macula adherens (desmosomes), and gap junctions. Fascia adherens anchors thin actin filaments into the disc, so it is a true component, whereas tight junctions are not found in this structure.'),
('f67ec4c7-754d-57af-9b49-1a7383dbe23f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Fascia adherens in the intercalated disc is the site of:', '["Gap junction communication","Myosin attachment","Ca2+ storage","Actin filament attachment (similar to Z-disc)","Intermediate filament attachment"]'::jsonb, 'Actin filament attachment (similar to Z-disc)', 'Fascia adherens serves the same function as the Z-disc by anchoring actin (thin) filaments. Gap junction communication occurs at gap junctions, Ca2+ is stored in the SR, and intermediate filaments attach at desmosomes and dense bodies.'),
('eed39657-cf8a-5e83-aa13-22500c5c2efb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Cardiac muscle has both desmosomes and gap junctions in the intercalated disc. Desmosomes provide:', '["Ca2+ signaling resulting in decreased functional activity","Mechanical adhesion between cardiomyocytes (preventing separation during contraction)","Electrical coupling mediated by specific receptor activation","Ion exchange without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metabolic coupling leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mechanical adhesion between cardiomyocytes (preventing separation during contraction)', 'Desmosomes (macula adherens) bind intermediate filaments and give strong mechanical adhesion so cardiac cells do not pull apart during forceful contraction. Electrical coupling and ion exchange are functions of gap junctions rather than desmosomes.'),
('78f92bb7-1576-5ff8-b756-e1e701eca525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Gap junctions in cardiac muscle provide:', '["Structural support resulting in decreased functional activity","Mechanical adhesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low-resistance electrical coupling allowing action potential propagation between cells","Ca2+ storage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contraction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low-resistance electrical coupling allowing action potential propagation between cells', 'Gap junctions form low-resistance channels that allow current to flow directly between cardiomyocytes, enabling the coordinated spread of depolarization. Mechanical adhesion and structural support are provided by desmosomes and fascia adherens.'),
('ee14e3a7-caf8-52d1-995f-7e04174cd0a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Cardiac muscle cells often contain two nuclei because:', '["They have fused during development","They are derived from skeletal muscle","They are syncytial","They polyploidize with age","Not all cells undergo complete cytokinesis during development"]'::jsonb, 'Not all cells undergo complete cytokinesis during development', 'Cardiomyocytes become binucleate when nuclear division proceeds but cytoplasmic division (cytokinesis) is not completed during early development. They are not fused syncytia like skeletal muscle fibers, which acquire multiple nuclei by fusion of myoblasts.'),
('b1a6b0f8-f30e-54c2-8220-617d1872e991'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The diad in cardiac muscle consists of:', '["Two T-tubules and one SR a","Three T-tubules and one SR","One T-tubule and two SR cisternae","One T-tubule and one SR terminal cisterna","Two triads"]'::jsonb, 'One T-tubule and one SR terminal cisterna', 'A cardiac diad is formed by one T-tubule paired with a single SR terminal cisterna. In contrast, skeletal muscle triads contain one T-tubule flanked by two SR cisternae.'),
('80502d06-0752-5547-bbe4-465001f94eb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release (CICR) is the mechanism of:', '["Skeletal muscle E-C coupling","Smooth muscle contraction","Cardiac excitation-contraction coupling","Neural transmission","Relaxation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac excitation-contraction coupling', 'In cardiac muscle, a small Ca2+ influx through L-type channels triggers a much larger release from the SR (CICR), driving contraction. Skeletal muscle instead relies on direct mechanical coupling between DHP receptors and ryanodine receptors.'),
('9e1da3a9-293c-55b9-86b7-0d2c667ae85c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The force of cardiac contraction is increased by:', '["Vagal stimulation resulting in decreased functional activity","β-adrenergic stimulation (which increases Ca2+ influx and SERCA activity via PKA phosphorylation)","ACh mediated by specific receptor activation","β-blockers a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic activation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'β-adrenergic stimulation (which increases Ca2+ influx and SERCA activity via PKA phosphorylation)', 'Beta-adrenergic stimulation raises cAMP and activates PKA, which increases L-type Ca2+ influx and SR reuptake (via phospholamban/SERCA), producing a stronger, faster contraction. Vagal/ACh stimulation and β-blockers reduce contractility.'),
('8b756f8c-8c79-50e8-8bc5-1c76670e3003'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The plateau phase of the cardiac action potential is due to:', '["Chloride influx resulting in decreased functional activity","Potassium efflux only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sodium influx mediated by specific receptor activation","Ca2+ efflux only a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L-type Ca2+ channel (slow Ca2+ influx) balanced with delayed rectifier K+ current"]'::jsonb, 'L-type Ca2+ channel (slow Ca2+ influx) balanced with delayed rectifier K+ current', 'The plateau is maintained by a balance between slow Ca2+ entry through L-type channels and delayed rectifier K+ efflux, prolonging depolarization. Sodium influx produces the rapid upstroke, and chloride plays a negligible role.'),
('e64b95a4-a1ab-508f-bb97-1cfe017c4fd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sinoatrial (SA) node and atrioventricular (AV) node are composed of:', '["Neurons resulting in decreased functional activity","Smooth muscle cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibroblasts mediated by specific receptor activation","Skeletal muscle cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Specialized cardiac muscle cells (pacemaker cells) that generate and conduct electrical impulses"]'::jsonb, 'Specialized cardiac muscle cells (pacemaker cells) that generate and conduct electrical impulses', 'The SA and AV nodes are made of modified cardiac muscle (pacemaker) cells that spontaneously generate and propagate electrical impulses. They are not neurons, smooth or skeletal muscle, or connective tissue cells.'),
('29bf924e-a565-5ead-9f3e-5efc02e20ea7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Smooth muscle cells are characterized by:', '["striations","Spindle-shaped cells with a single central nucleus","Short branched cells with intercalated discs","Long cylindrical cells with multiple peripheral nuclei","Round cells with multiple nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Spindle-shaped cells with a single central nucleus', 'Smooth muscle cells are fusiform with a single central nucleus and lack striations. Long cylindrical fibers with peripheral nuclei describe skeletal muscle, and branched cells with intercalated discs describe cardiac muscle.'),
('9098719b-019c-5f72-aef7-2337df368c4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Smooth muscle lacks which structures seen in striated muscle?', '["Actin resulting in decreased functional activity","Cell membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T-tubules and sarcomeres (has caveolae instead of T-tubules)","Nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mitochondria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T-tubules and sarcomeres (has caveolae instead of T-tubules)', 'Smooth muscle has no T-tubules or sarcomeres, using caveolae for Ca2+ entry and dense bodies in place of Z-discs. It does contain actin, nuclei, and mitochondria like other muscle types.'),
('66c59db8-2b03-5786-ac39-271e87809934'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Dense bodies in smooth muscle are analogous to:', '["M-lines resulting in decreased functional activity","A-bands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intercalated discs mediated by specific receptor activation","T-tubules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Z-discs in striated muscle (anchor actin and intermediate filaments)"]'::jsonb, 'Z-discs in striated muscle (anchor actin and intermediate filaments)', 'Dense bodies anchor actin and intermediate filaments in smooth muscle, functionally matching Z-discs in striated muscle. M-lines and A-bands are components of the sarcomere, and intercalated discs are cardiac junctions.'),
('0561cf70-e04e-51fa-baf2-a4a72a1268cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The actin:myosin ratio in smooth muscle is:', '["Variable resulting in decreased functional activity","myosin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower than in striated muscle (1:1) mediated by specific receptor activation","Equal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Much higher than in striated muscle (up to 15:1)"]'::jsonb, 'Much higher than in striated muscle (up to 15:1)', 'Smooth muscle has far more actin than myosin, with ratios up to about 15:1, relative to roughly 2:1 in striated muscle. The small amount of myosin allows slow, sustained contractions suited to smooth muscle function.'),
('c30e25c0-7618-5d7f-8d9b-73a449644c21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Smooth muscle contraction is regulated primarily by:', '["Troponin and tropomyosin resulting in decreased functional activity","Nebulin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calmodulin alone a mediated by specific receptor activation","Phosphorylation of the myosin light chain (MLC20) by MLCK (myosin light chain kinase)","Calcium binding to troponin C leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphorylation of the myosin light chain (MLC20) by MLCK (myosin light chain kinase)', 'In smooth muscle, Ca2+-calmodulin activates MLCK, which phosphorylates the regulatory myosin light chain (MLC20) to start crossbridge cycling. Troponin and tropomyosin regulate striated muscle, not smooth muscle.'),
('345181b7-3f3f-5628-9872-c98b8c96e673'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Calmodulin (CaM) is the Ca2+-binding protein that activates:', '["Troponin in striated muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","MLCK (myosin light chain kinase) in smooth muscle","RyR a mediated by specific receptor activation","Phospholamban without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SERCA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MLCK (myosin light chain kinase) in smooth muscle', 'In smooth muscle, Ca2+ binds calmodulin and the complex activates MLCK, which phosphorylates myosin light chains to trigger contraction. In striated muscle the Ca2+ sensor is troponin C, while phospholamban and SERCA regulate SR Ca2+ uptake.'),
('3703553b-bc50-5a17-a910-3829b377da1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The latch state (latch bridge) in smooth muscle refers to:', '["Maximum contraction resulting in decreased functional activity","Complete relaxation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myosin detachment a mediated by specific receptor activation","Rapid cycling without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sustained contraction with very low energy consumption (myosin remains attached but dephosphorylated)"]'::jsonb, 'Sustained contraction with very low energy consumption (myosin remains attached but dephosphorylated)', 'In the latch state myosin crossbridges remain attached to actin while dephosphorylated, so force is maintained for long periods with very little ATP use. It is neither maximal force, relaxation, nor rapid cycling.'),
('9789bb16-062b-50ee-92af-acff1652adcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Smooth muscle can be divided into:', '["Cardiac and skeletal a","Red and white","Slow and fast","Type I and Type II","Single-unit (visceral) and multi-unit"]'::jsonb, 'Single-unit (visceral) and multi-unit', 'Smooth muscle is classified functionally into single-unit (visceral) and multi-unit types based on innervation and the presence of gap junctions. Red/white, slow/fast, and Type I/II distinctions apply to skeletal muscle.'),
('9a17435f-5d22-55d8-8dda-b6a9d2d9dcc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle is characterized by:', '["and response to stretch","spontaneous rhythmic contractions","gap junctions","spontaneous activity","Gap junctions between cells (electrical coupling)"]'::jsonb, 'Gap junctions between cells (electrical coupling)', 'Single-unit smooth muscle cells are connected by abundant gap junctions, so depolarization spreads throughout the muscle and it contracts as a coordinated syncytium with spontaneous rhythmic activity. The listed fragments relate to this feature, and the complete option best describes it.'),
('1c6ea649-b853-520c-9f35-a286069f1d80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Multi-unit smooth muscle is characterized by:', '["Spontaneous contractions resulting in decreased functional activity","Pacemaker activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rich gap junctions mediated by specific receptor activation","operates discretely without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Each muscle cell is independently innervated by autonomic nerves (few gap junctions)"]'::jsonb, 'Each muscle cell is independently innervated by autonomic nerves (few gap junctions)', 'Multi-unit smooth muscle cells are separately innervated by autonomic nerve terminals and have few gap junctions, enabling discrete, fine control. Spontaneous contractions, pacemaker activity, and rich gap junctions are features of single-unit smooth muscle.'),
('6c7b35ac-8e77-5010-b491-4101dc3adc19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Nitric oxide (NO) relaxes vascular smooth muscle by:', '["Activating guanylyl cyclase → cGMP → PKG → decreased Ca2+ and MLCK activity","Activating MLCK accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blocking MLCP mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing Ca2+ influx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Activating Rho kinase a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activating guanylyl cyclase → cGMP → PKG → decreased Ca2+ and MLCK activity', 'NO diffuses into smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, raising cGMP and activating PKG, which lowers cytosolic Ca2+ and MLCK activity to produce relaxation. Activating MLCK or Rho kinase would promote contraction, not relaxation.'),
('ea0b9eb5-b67a-5374-9a7c-6c006be153fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Pacemaker cells in the GI tract (interstitial cells of Cajal) generate:', '["Slow waves (pacemaker potentials) that regulate smooth muscle contraction","electrical activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Relaxation only mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonic contraction only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Action potentials only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Slow waves (pacemaker potentials) that regulate smooth muscle contraction', 'Interstitial cells of Cajal generate slow waves (pacemaker potentials) that set the basic rhythm of gastrointestinal smooth muscle contraction. They do not produce contraction or relaxation by themselves, nor do they simply fire conventional action potentials.'),
('2e69bc39-288d-5116-8125-82d0c0dfebce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Slow waves in GI smooth muscle are generated by:', '["Smooth muscle cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic nerves a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vagus nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Enteric neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal) — they are the pacemakers"]'::jsonb, 'ICC (interstitial cells of Cajal) — they are the pacemakers', 'Slow waves originate in interstitial cells of Cajal, the electrical pacemakers of the gut, and then spread to the smooth muscle. Smooth muscle cells respond to the waves, and enteric or autonomic nerves modulate rather than generate them.'),
('de87562c-e625-51dd-a269-7350e712d5ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Caveolae in smooth muscle are:', '["Nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Gap junctions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dense bodies mediated by specific receptor activation","Dense plaques without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flask-shaped invaginations of the sarcolemma that function similarly to T-tubules (Ca2+ entry)"]'::jsonb, 'Flask-shaped invaginations of the sarcolemma that function similarly to T-tubules (Ca2+ entry)', 'Caveolae are flask-shaped sarcolemmal invaginations that concentrate Ca2+ entry channels, acting like the T-tubules of striated muscle. They are distinct from dense bodies, dense plaques, and junctions.'),
('128c6f1d-215e-5d1f-8cee-e2f9154d33c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Dense plaques (focal adhesions) in smooth muscle are:', '["Gap junctions resulting in decreased functional activity","T-tubules accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Membrane-associated dense bodies that link actin to the extracellular matrix via integrins","Ca2+ stores without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The SR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Membrane-associated dense bodies that link actin to the extracellular matrix via integrins', 'Dense plaques are membrane-attached dense bodies that anchor actin filaments to the extracellular matrix through integrins, functioning as focal adhesions. They are not junctions, T-tubules, or Ca2+ stores such as the SR.'),
('c02ab7a0-0091-5b39-99e1-8640a43d9b29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The intermediate filament network in smooth muscle is composed largely of:', '["Neurofilament resulting in decreased functional activity","GFAP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lamin mediated by specific receptor activation","Desmin and/or vimentin (varies by muscle type)","Keratin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Desmin and/or vimentin (varies by muscle type)', 'Smooth muscle intermediate filaments are built mainly from desmin, with vimentin in some vessels and other sites. Neurofilament, GFAP, lamin, and keratin characterize neurons, glia, the nuclear lamina, and epithelia respectively.'),
('44178036-0114-596d-bdb3-c67eb03c7d68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Regeneration of skeletal muscle after injury is limited because:', '["Satellite cells have limited capacity and extensive injury leads to fibrosis","Cardiac muscle regenerates well accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skeletal muscle can fully regenerate mediated by specific receptor activation","Skeletal muscle does not regenerate at all without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth muscle regenerates poorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Satellite cells have limited capacity and extensive injury leads to fibrosis', 'Repair of skeletal muscle depends on satellite cells, whose regenerative capacity is limited; after large injuries the defect is filled by fibrous scar tissue. Skeletal muscle does regenerate partially, unlike cardiac muscle, which essentially does not.'),
('71b09122-5a83-5e21-b623-aefdbc908925'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Cardiac muscle has very limited regenerative ability because:', '["Cardiac stem cells are absent resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiomyocytes are terminally differentiated and are replaced by fibrous scar after injury","Cardiomyocytes proliferate throughout life mediated by specific receptor activation","There is fibrosis in cardiac injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac muscle regenerates completely leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiomyocytes are terminally differentiated and are replaced by fibrous scar after injury', 'Cardiomyocytes are terminally differentiated and essentially withdraw from the cell cycle after birth, so damaged cardiac muscle is replaced by fibrous scar tissue rather than regenerated. Fibrosis is the consequence of this limited regeneration, not the cause.'),
('ac9c1851-ef13-5dd7-aad5-0e936412ce31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Smooth muscle can regenerate more readily because:', '["They dedifferentiate into skeletal muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells cannot divide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They are replaced by fibrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","They are terminally differentiated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth muscle cells retain the ability to proliferate (hyperplasia and hypertrophy) in response to injury and growth signals"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle cells retain the ability to proliferate (hyperplasia and hypertrophy) in response to injury and growth signals', 'Unlike cardiac and skeletal muscle, smooth muscle cells keep the capacity to divide, so they respond to injury or growth signals with hyperplasia and hypertrophy. This retained proliferative ability underlies its comparatively good regeneration.'),
('7337f04f-4879-5227-a433-84a8ac2e75e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Rhabdomyosarcoma is a malignant tumor of:', '["Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle","Smooth muscle","Connective tissue a","Nerve tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal muscle', 'Rhabdomyosarcoma is a malignant neoplasm of skeletal muscle (rhabdo = striated). Smooth muscle gives rise to leiomyosarcoma, while cardiac and nerve tissue tumors have different names.'),
('1fff7af6-ce79-5006-bb9f-17cf53dbf9b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Leiomyoma is a benign tumor of:', '["Cardiac muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Fat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Smooth muscle (common in the uterus — uterine fibroid)","Nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skeletal muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle (common in the uterus — uterine fibroid)', 'Leiomyoma is a benign smooth muscle tumor, most familiar as the uterine fibroid (leiomyoma of the myometrium). Tumors of skeletal and cardiac muscle carry different names such as rhabdomyoma and cardiac myxoma.'),
('90dca756-228f-5fc8-958e-4f41d0fdab83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The myocardium of the heart contains:', '["Smooth muscle a resulting in decreased functional activity","Only connective tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skeletal muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiac muscle cells (cardiomyocytes) with surrounding connective tissue and vessels","Only cardiac muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac muscle cells (cardiomyocytes) with surrounding connective tissue and vessels', 'The myocardium is made of cardiac muscle cells (cardiomyocytes) organized into layers and supported by connective tissue containing blood vessels. It is not purely or exclusively one cell type, and it contains no smooth or skeletal muscle.'),
('d17daf29-5221-5f68-9fbf-d83ea230bfa0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The epicardium (visceral pericardium) consists of:', '["Cardiac muscle a resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple cuboidal epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stratified squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesothelium (simple squamous) and underlying fibroelastic connective tissue with adipose tissue and nerves","Dense regular connective tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesothelium (simple squamous) and underlying fibroelastic connective tissue with adipose tissue and nerves', 'The epicardium is a serous layer lined by mesothelium (simple squamous) over fibroelastic connective tissue that also carries adipose tissue and the coronary vessels and nerves. It does not contain cardiac muscle or stratified epithelia.'),
('4bee26bc-3cf6-5c75-aff5-2d9c0f767387'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The endocardium lines the:', '["Inner surface of the heart chambers and covers the heart valves","Pericardium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myocardium mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inner surface of the heart chambers and covers the heart valves', 'The endocardium, a thin layer of endothelium over connective tissue, lines the inner surface of the heart chambers and continues over the heart valves. It does not cover the pericardium or the myocardium itself.'),
('0a173c20-66b8-5e0c-a4bd-ca9ad3ccca93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Purkinje fibers are specialized cardiac muscle cells that:', '["Contract the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","Suppress arrhythmias a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conduct the cardiac impulse rapidly from the AV bundle to the ventricular myocardium","Produce the ECG without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Generate the pacemaker impulse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conduct the cardiac impulse rapidly from the AV bundle to the ventricular myocardium', 'Purkinje fibers are modified large-diameter cardiac muscle cells that rapidly transmit the impulse from the AV bundle to the working ventricular myocardium. They do not generate the primary pacemaker impulse, which arises in the SA node, nor do they contract or produce the ECG.'),
('161ac3de-44fb-561c-acb2-577068eb4d4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The conduction velocity of Purkinje fibers is:', '["The slowest","None a","Slower than the AV node","Similar to working myocardium","The fastest in the heart (2-4 m/s)"]'::jsonb, 'The fastest in the heart (2-4 m/s)', 'Purkinje fibers conduct at roughly 2-4 m/s, the fastest conduction velocity in the heart, so the ventricles contract as a coordinated unit. The AV node slows conduction deliberately, and working myocardium conducts much more slowly.'),
('665d677e-7d1a-5a2c-b20f-547c7a537510'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Purkinje fibers are located in the:', '["Subendocardial layer of the ventricle walls","Atrial myocardium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SA node mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericardium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AV node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subendocardial layer of the ventricle walls', 'Purkinje fibers run in the subendocardial layer of the ventricular walls, just beneath the endocardium, after emerging from the bundle branches. They are not found in the atria, SA node, AV node, or pericardium.'),
('93948da9-3a23-5870-a2d5-99f84e4a218f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sinoatrial (SA) node is located at:', '["The junction of the superior vena cava with the right atrium (crista terminalis)","The AV junction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The interatrial septum mediated by specific receptor activation","The left atrium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ventricular septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The junction of the superior vena cava with the right atrium (crista terminalis)', 'The SA node lies in the wall of the right atrium at the junction with the superior vena cava, near the crista terminalis. The AV junction and interatrial septum host the AV node, not the primary pacemaker.'),
('88943d1d-2a61-57ea-8d39-b257505fd89c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The atrioventricular (AV) node is located at:', '["The crista terminalis","near the coronary sinus orifice (Koch triangle)","The base of the interatrial septum","The AV bundle","The bundle branches"]'::jsonb, 'The base of the interatrial septum', 'The AV node lies in the lower (inferoposterior) portion of the interatrial septum close to the atrioventricular junction, near the coronary sinus orifice at the apex of the triangle of Koch. The crista terminalis marks the SA node, and the AV bundle and bundle branches are its distal conduction pathways.'),
('cb20e0cd-d650-5d2c-95ac-c1637505acd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The AV bundle (bundle of His) penetrates the:', '["AV node resulting in decreased functional activity","Right atrium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Central fibrous body (trigone) of the heart to reach the interventricular septum","SA node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left atrium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central fibrous body (trigone) of the heart to reach the interventricular septum', 'The AV bundle is the only normal bridge between atrial and ventricular conduction tissue, penetrating the central fibrous body (right fibrous trigone) to reach the interventricular septum. The AV and SA nodes lie upstream and downstream of this penetration.'),
('e8254e6a-768e-512d-8854-f1caab4be677'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The hormone secreted by cardiac atrial myocytes in response to stretch is:', '["Aldosterone resulting in decreased functional activity","Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)","Epinephrine mediated by specific receptor activation","Renin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Erythropoietin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)', 'Atrial myocytes release ANP when stretched by increased atrial volume or pressure. Aldosterone, renin, and erythropoietin are secreted by the adrenal cortex, kidney, and kidney respectively, and epinephrine comes from the adrenal medulla.'),
('eb5b2d4f-092c-5af4-a26b-640fe98d38c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'ANP (atrial natriuretic peptide) acts on the kidney to:', '["Increase renin resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase Na+ reabsorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increase GFR and decrease Na+ reabsorption (natriuresis and diuresis)","Decrease GFR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decrease urine output leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increase GFR and decrease Na+ reabsorption (natriuresis and diuresis)', 'ANP dilates afferent arterioles and inhibits tubular Na+ reabsorption, increasing GFR and promoting natriuresis and diuresis that lower blood volume. It opposes rather than mimics renin and aldosterone actions.'),
('6ac76324-bf72-5231-b056-76f479ad7595'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'BNP (brain natriuretic peptide) is primarily secreted by:', '["Atrial myocardium resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricular myocardium (especially in response to ventricular stretch and failure)","Kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","Brain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adrenal gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular myocardium (especially in response to ventricular stretch and failure)', 'BNP is secreted mainly by ventricular myocardium in response to wall stretch, as in heart failure. ANP predominates in the atria, and neither natriuretic peptide is produced by the kidney, brain, or adrenal gland.'),
('38e9e1bb-75ef-55cb-9213-bc4ddc24cea0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The cardiac skeleton (fibrous skeleton) is composed of:', '["Hyaline cartilage a resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiac muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Elastic cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dense connective tissue (collagen) that surrounds the heart valves and provides structural support and electrical insulation"]'::jsonb, 'Dense connective tissue (collagen) that surrounds the heart valves and provides structural support and electrical insulation', 'The cardiac (fibrous) skeleton is made of dense connective tissue rich in collagen that forms rings around the valves and the annuli fibrosi, giving support and electrically separating atria from ventricles. It contains no cartilage or bone.'),
('88fdef47-8e74-5f1c-b6e2-2ccb09e3a149'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The annuli fibrosi are fibrous rings that:', '["Surround the coronary arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Form the interventricular septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surround the atrioventricular and semilunar valve orifices","Surround the SA node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surround the AV node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surround the atrioventricular and semilunar valve orifices', 'The annuli fibrosi are collagenous rings of the fibrous skeleton that encircle the atrioventricular (tricuspid, mitral) and semilunar (aortic, pulmonary) valve orifices. They do not surround the coronary arteries or the nodal tissue.'),
('46f8ea8c-e5ce-5310-9b94-348d112fedeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The conduction system of the heart is insulated from the working myocardium except at:', '["Purkinje fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","The AV bundle (passes through the fibrous skeleton)","The bundle branches mediated by specific receptor activation","The AV node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The SA node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The AV bundle (passes through the fibrous skeleton)', 'The fibrous skeleton electrically insulates the atria from the ventricles, and the only normal pathway across it is the AV bundle (bundle of His). Purkinje fibers and bundle branches lie beyond this gap, and the node pair sits on either side.'),
('6835f561-7cad-549a-869e-26f799081ad4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Transitional (T) cells in the SA and AV nodes:', '["Contract the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","Generate the impulse accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conduct the impulse fastest mediated by specific receptor activation","Produce ANP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connect pacemaker cells to the working myocardium (intermediate structure)"]'::jsonb, 'Connect pacemaker cells to the working myocardium (intermediate structure)', 'Transitional (T) cells surround the pacemaker cells of the SA and AV nodes and connect them to the working myocardium, being structurally intermediate between nodal and ordinary cardiac cells. They neither generate the impulse nor contract or produce ANP.'),
('60d71f95-3398-5e59-86f8-6428842a9e18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Ischemic heart disease (myocardial infarction) causes:', '["Apoptosis only resulting in decreased functional activity","Coagulative necrosis of cardiac muscle (replaced by fibrous scar)","Liquefactive necrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","change a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regeneration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coagulative necrosis of cardiac muscle (replaced by fibrous scar)', 'Myocardial infarction kills cardiomyocytes by coagulative necrosis, the classic pattern of infarcted solid organs, and the dead tissue is ultimately replaced by fibrous scar. It is not primarily apoptosis, liquefactive necrosis, or regeneration.'),
('9daee0f6-e004-5440-a402-2249e4b5746d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Contraction band necrosis is a histological feature of:', '["Healed MI resulting in decreased functional activity","Apoptosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amyloidosis a mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal myocardium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reperfusion injury after cardiac ischemia (hypercontracted sarcomeres with dark eosinophilic bands)"]'::jsonb, 'Reperfusion injury after cardiac ischemia (hypercontracted sarcomeres with dark eosinophilic bands)', 'Contraction band necrosis results from calcium overload during reperfusion, producing hypercontracted sarcomeres with dense eosinophilic bands in damaged myocytes. Healed myocardial infarction consists of scar, and apoptosis does not show this histologic pattern.'),
('4cb63fc3-58d6-5c84-8ad3-b9a798957575'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Hibernating myocardium is:', '["Hypertrophied muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Necrotic muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apoptotic muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Viable but dysfunctional cardiac muscle due to chronic ischemia (reversible with revascularization)","Scar tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Viable but dysfunctional cardiac muscle due to chronic ischemia (reversible with revascularization)', 'Hibernating myocardium remains viable but contracts poorly because of chronic low-grade ischemia, and its function improves after revascularization. Necrotic, apoptotic, and scarred tissue cannot recover in this way.'),
('23314cd1-b585-5e9c-bb45-cd46aed678ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Stunned myocardium is:', '["Permanently damaged muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Necrotic tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scar mediated by specific receptor activation","Post-ischemic (reperfused) myocardial dysfunction that eventually recovers even without reperfusion","Apoptotic tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-ischemic (reperfused) myocardial dysfunction that eventually recovers even without reperfusion', 'Stunning is reversible post-ischemic myocardial dysfunction that persists after blood flow is restored and resolves even without further intervention. Necrosis, scar, and apoptosis cause irreversible damage.'),
('93986c8f-2278-5f41-8d7b-bb4686d60660'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is characterized by:', '["Myocyte death","and interstitial fibrosis","Dilation of ventricles","myocyte disarray","Myocardial hypertrophy (especially septal)"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial hypertrophy (especially septal)', 'Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is defined by inappropriate myocardial hypertrophy, classically asymmetric and septal, with myocyte disarray. Ventricular dilation is the hallmark of dilated cardiomyopathy, and myocyte death is not the defining feature.'),
('d7ce64c8-af84-5b9d-ba77-cd3d45fa0a92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM) is characterized by:', '["Myocardial hypertrophy without dilation","Myocyte disarray","reduced systolic function","Ventricular dilation","and myocyte hypertrophy with interstitial fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular dilation', 'Dilated cardiomyopathy is characterized by progressive four-chamber ventricular dilation with impaired systolic function. Hypertrophy without dilation and myocyte disarray are features of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.'),
('c1792478-82f9-5b8c-a4c7-4f2552dd1f9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM) is characterized by:', '["Stiff ventricular walls with impaired filling (restrictive physiology) and normal systolic function","Septal hypertrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventricular dilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Poor systolic function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myocyte disarray a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stiff ventricular walls with impaired filling (restrictive physiology) and normal systolic function', 'Restrictive cardiomyopathy is caused by stiff, noncompliant ventricular walls that impair diastolic filling while systolic function is usually preserved. Septal hypertrophy and ventricular dilation point to other cardiomyopathies.'),
('b74268b5-1f5c-5b2f-ae6d-6ff41eedb4dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Cardiac amyloidosis (most commonly AL or ATTR) deposits:', '["Collagen resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","causing restrictive physiology and conduction blocks mediated by specific receptor activation","Glycogen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amyloid fibrils between myocytes and in vessel walls"]'::jsonb, 'Amyloid fibrils between myocytes and in vessel walls', 'In cardiac amyloidosis, AL or ATTR amyloid fibrils are deposited between myocytes and within vessel walls, causing a waxy appearance, restrictive physiology, and conduction blocks. Collagen, elastin, and glycogen are not the deposited material.'),
('85c94c75-ea07-51cd-b035-50d506fa7428'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Eosinophilic myocarditis is associated with:', '["Amyloidosis resulting in decreased functional activity","drug hypersensitivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypereosinophilic syndromes (Loeffler)","Heart failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and parasites leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypereosinophilic syndromes (Loeffler)', 'Eosinophilic myocarditis characteristically accompanies hypereosinophilic syndromes, where eosinophil degranulation injures the myocardium (Loeffler myocarditis). Amyloid deposition and heart failure do not produce an eosinophilic infiltrate.'),
('6535bd44-f7f9-5a95-93ad-0be8b3843c28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Rejection of cardiac transplant shows:', '["Lymphocytic infiltration with myocyte damage (cellular rejection) or antibody-mediated (vascular) rejection","inflammation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myocyte hypertrophy mediated by specific receptor activation","Granuloma formation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myocyte disarray leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocytic infiltration with myocyte damage (cellular rejection) or antibody-mediated (vascular) rejection', 'Acute cellular rejection of a cardiac transplant is marked by lymphocytic infiltration associated with myocyte damage, while antibody-mediated rejection targets the microvasculature. Granuloma formation, myocyte hypertrophy, and myocyte disarray are not features of rejection.'),
('0669714e-6293-5d82-a384-007b356c6e99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Rheumatic heart disease (acute) histology shows:', '["Granulomas without giant cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Amyloid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aschoff bodies (granulomatous lesions with fibrinoid change and Anitschkow cells — caterpillar cells from macrophages)","Giant cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neutrophil infiltration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aschoff bodies (granulomatous lesions with fibrinoid change and Anitschkow cells — caterpillar cells from macrophages)', 'Acute rheumatic carditis shows Aschoff bodies, granulomatous nodules containing Anitschkow (caterpillar) cells with fibrinoid change. The infiltrate is mononuclear rather than neutrophil-predominant, and amyloid is absent.'),
('11937e84-f53d-5300-8401-1bd80375ceef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Vegetations in infective endocarditis consist of:', '["and microorganisms (bacteria or fungi)","Thrombus only","Platelets","fibrin","Collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Platelets', 'Vegetations of infective endocarditis are thrombotic masses in which platelets and fibrin are enmeshed together with microorganisms. Pure thrombus without organisms, or collagen alone, cannot account for the vegetation.'),
('0b96dd75-d1bd-5b61-a0f2-8745528fdb2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Libman-Sacks endocarditis is associated with:', '["Antiphospholipid syndrome a resulting in decreased functional activity","Infective endocarditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) — sterile vegetations on the mitral and tricuspid valves","Rheumatic fever without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malignancy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) — sterile vegetations on the mitral and tricuspid valves', 'Libman-Sacks endocarditis is the sterile, noninfective verrucous form seen in systemic lupus erythematosus, typically involving the mitral and tricuspid valves without destroying leaflets. Infective endocarditis and rheumatic fever produce inflamed or infected vegetations.'),
('b1b1ef10-2cc2-5e92-95f3-540dc3d6df2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve (mitral valve prolapse) is characterized by:', '["change resulting in decreased functional activity","Inflammatory vegetations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcification mediated by specific receptor activation","Myxoid thickening of the valve leaflets with disruption of the fibrous layer and elongated chordae","Fibrotic thickening leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myxoid thickening of the valve leaflets with disruption of the fibrous layer and elongated chordae', 'Myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve shows myxoid thickening of the spongiosa with disruption of the fibrous layer and elongated, redundant chordae, the basis of mitral valve prolapse. Calcification and inflammatory vegetations are not part of this change.'),
('77802be4-fb28-54a5-8e3f-fd1ecd713954'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Calcific aortic stenosis (senile) involves:', '["Progressive calcification of the aortic valve cusps (in the fibrosa layer) without commissural fusion","Commissural fusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vegetations mediated by specific receptor activation","Rheumatic inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myxoid degeneration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive calcification of the aortic valve cusps (in the fibrosa layer) without commissural fusion', 'Senile calcific aortic stenosis is produced by progressive calcification of the cusps within the fibrosa, without commissural fusion. Commissural fusion is characteristic of rheumatic valve disease, and vegetations or myxoid degeneration are unrelated.'),
('ddd7e7e1-8802-5e8e-94e3-a89cf4d1080a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The skeletal muscle fiber type most abundant in marathon runners is:', '["Type IIa (fast oxidative)","Type IIb (fast glycolytic)","Type I (slow oxidative)","Type IIx","Type I and II equally"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (slow oxidative)', 'Marathon runners depend on aerobic endurance, so their muscles are rich in fatigue-resistant type I (slow oxidative) fibers with abundant mitochondria. Fast type IIb fibers fatigue quickly and are better suited to sprinting.'),
('dbdc4983-3b2d-53f2-86ee-df4ae71a34f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Sprinters (100m) have a predominance of:', '["Type IIa fibers","Type IIb (fast glycolytic) fibers","Smooth muscle fibers a","Type I fibers","Type I and IIa equally"]'::jsonb, 'Type IIb (fast glycolytic) fibers', 'Sprinters need rapid, powerful contractions fueled anaerobically, provided by type IIb (fast glycolytic) fibers. Slow oxidative type I fibers contract slowly and cannot generate explosive sprint power.'),
('f3b22d55-f744-5d43-9126-06d9eb732f77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myostatin is a negative regulator of:', '["Neural growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Collagen synthesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adipose formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone growth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle growth (inhibits myoblast proliferation and differentiation)"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle growth (inhibits myoblast proliferation and differentiation)', 'Myostatin (GDF-8) is a secreted TGF-beta family member that limits muscle mass by inhibiting myoblast proliferation and differentiation; loss of its function causes muscle hypertrophy. It does not govern neural, bone, collagen, or adipose growth.'),
('d0e90348-04fe-5375-abc2-ab62899d0cf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The most common cause of muscular dystrophy in adults is:', '["Myotonic dystrophy type 1 (DM1)","Facioscapulohumeral MD a","Limb-girdle MD","Becker MD","Duchenne MD"]'::jsonb, 'Myotonic dystrophy type 1 (DM1)', 'Myotonic dystrophy type 1 is the most common muscular dystrophy presenting in adults, with myotonia, cataracts, and conduction defects. Duchenne and Becker dystrophies present in childhood, and limb-girdle or facioscapulohumeral dystrophy is less common overall.'),
('0c7fae89-e743-5513-a378-5e7a2d66fde2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy (FSHD) is caused by:', '["Dysferlin mutations resulting in decreased functional activity","Dystrophin mutations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deletion of D4Z4 repeats on chromosome 4q35 (epigenetic dysregulation)","Sarcoglycan mutations without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calpain mutations a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deletion of D4Z4 repeats on chromosome 4q35 (epigenetic dysregulation)', 'Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy is caused by deletion of D4Z4 macrosatellite repeats at chromosome 4q35, leading to epigenetic dysregulation and aberrant gene expression. Mutations in dystrophin, sarcoglycans, dysferlin, or calpain cause other muscular dystrophies.'),
('91876b85-4b22-5cf6-998b-875f0717b683'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (LGMD) includes many subtypes with mutations in:', '["and many other proteins","calpain-3","Dystrophin (like Duchenne)","dysferlin","Sarcoglycans"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcoglycans', 'Limb-girdle muscular dystrophy is genetically heterogeneous: sarcoglycan gene defects are a well-known autosomal recessive subgroup, and many other proteins (calpain-3, dysferlin) are also involved. Dystrophin mutations cause Duchenne and Becker dystrophies rather than LGMD.'),
('fdb73552-9e7d-5f0d-9c09-bed423833c81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The Gowers sign in Duchenne MD refers to:', '["Foot drop resulting in decreased functional activity","Using the arms to climb up the legs to stand (due to proximal lower limb weakness)","Respiratory failure mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiac arrhythmia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Difficulty speaking leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Using the arms to climb up the legs to stand (due to proximal lower limb weakness)', 'The Gowers sign is the child''s maneuver of pushing up on the legs with the hands to rise from the floor, reflecting proximal hip and thigh weakness. Foot drop, respiratory failure, and cardiac arrhythmia are different manifestations of the disease.'),
('ade1ba4c-ee4e-5536-a743-0cab317e26bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Pseudohypertrophy of the calf muscles in Duchenne MD is due to:', '["Inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","Replacement of muscle by fat and fibrous tissue (not true muscle hypertrophy)","Edema mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcification without affecting the primary tissue pathway","True muscle fiber hypertrophy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Replacement of muscle by fat and fibrous tissue (not true muscle hypertrophy)', 'Calf enlargement in Duchenne muscular dystrophy is pseudohypertrophy, caused by replacement of muscle by fat and fibrous tissue rather than growth of muscle fibers. True fiber hypertrophy, edema, and inflammation do not explain the appearance.'),
('e424c6ad-37ce-553b-9c29-30320e279395'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Muscle biopsy in Duchenne MD shows:', '["and increased endomysial connective tissue and fat","Variation in fiber size","regenerating fibers","necrosis","Normal histology"]'::jsonb, 'Variation in fiber size', 'Dystrophic muscle in Duchenne muscular dystrophy shows marked variation in fiber size together with necrosis, regenerating fibers, and increased endomysial fat and connective tissue. A normal histology never occurs in this disease.'),
('c0dafbfa-f043-52b1-a34c-27aaa66f94ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Immunostaining for dystrophin in Duchenne MD shows:', '["Normal staining","Only in regenerating fibers","Increased staining","Absent dystrophin (no staining)","Reduced or patchy staining"]'::jsonb, 'Absent dystrophin (no staining)', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy arises from out-of-frame dystrophin mutations so severe that essentially no dystrophin protein is made, so immunostaining is absent. Reduced or patchy staining is the pattern of the milder Becker form.'),
('e26cee69-a2e6-5b35-a36e-d562733292e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Inclusion body myositis (IBM) is characterized by:', '["Rimmed vacuoles (blue-rimmed with basophilic granular material) and tubular filaments on EM with inflammation","Ragged red fibers a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Central nuclei only mediated by specific receptor activation","inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","vacuoles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rimmed vacuoles (blue-rimmed with basophilic granular material) and tubular filaments on EM with inflammation', 'Inclusion body myositis is marked by rimmed vacuoles (blue-rimmed with basophilic granular material) and tubulofilamentous inclusions on electron microscopy, together with inflammation. Ragged red fibers indicate mitochondrial myopathy, and central nuclei point to myotubular myopathy.'),
('74a77b31-b192-584c-a4ed-c31e6e5207b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Polymyositis is an autoimmune inflammatory myopathy characterized by:', '["CD8+ T cell infiltration around and within non-necrotic muscle fibers (endomyositis)","Giant cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neutrophils mediated by specific receptor activation","Granulomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CD4+ T cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CD8+ T cell infiltration around and within non-necrotic muscle fibers (endomyositis)', 'Polymyositis is a cell-mediated autoimmune disorder in which CD8+ T lymphocytes surround and invade non-necrotic muscle fibers (endomyositis). The perivascular CD4+/B-cell pattern with perifascicular atrophy is instead the signature of dermatomyositis.'),
('0cc85747-a290-588d-9a3e-395f69c55e81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Dermatomyositis is characterized by:', '["Rimmed vacuoles resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Ragged red fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endomysial CD8+ infiltration mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Central nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Perifascicular atrophy (characteristic histology) and B-cell/CD4+ T-cell perivascular inflammation with complement deposition"]'::jsonb, 'Perifascicular atrophy (characteristic histology) and B-cell/CD4+ T-cell perivascular inflammation with complement deposition', 'Dermatomyositis is a humorally mediated myopathy characterized by perifascicular atrophy and perivascular inflammation with B cells, CD4+ T cells, and complement deposition on capillaries. Endomysial CD8+ invasion is the pattern of polymyositis.'),
('dd966b70-bb88-576b-ba12-52f3ac6b3a56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Ragged red fibers on muscle biopsy are diagnostic of:', '["Inclusion body myositis resulting in decreased functional activity","Duchenne MD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Polymyositis mediated by specific receptor activation","Mitochondrial myopathy (subsarcolemmal accumulation of abnormal mitochondria)","Central core disease a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondrial myopathy (subsarcolemmal accumulation of abnormal mitochondria)', 'Ragged red fibers, best seen with the modified Gomori trichrome stain, reflect subsarcolemmal accumulation of abnormal mitochondria and are the hallmark of mitochondrial myopathy. They are not produced by dystrophinopathy, polymyositis, or inclusion body myositis.'),
('b67889da-5d9e-598d-925d-06cc3f312e7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myotubular (centronuclear) myopathy is characterized by:', '["Peripheral nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Rimmed vacuoles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small muscle fibers with centrally located nuclei (resembling fetal myotubes)","Ragged red fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central cores a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small muscle fibers with centrally located nuclei (resembling fetal myotubes)', 'In myotubular (centronuclear) myopathy the fibers are small with centrally placed nuclei that resemble fetal myotubes. Peripheral nuclei are the normal adult arrangement, and rimmed vacuoles are seen in inclusion body myositis.'),
('3a94ab7e-361c-542c-9451-1a8475f4916f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Muscle biopsy in neurogenic atrophy shows:', '["Perifascicular atrophy","Small","and target fibers","angulated fibers (group atrophy)","fiber type grouping"]'::jsonb, 'Small', 'In neurogenic atrophy the muscle fibers become small and angulated and are lost in groups (group atrophy) within the territory of a degenerating motor unit. Perifascicular atrophy is instead the hallmark of dermatomyositis.'),
('0a76261c-e66e-5523-bf52-5610ef5a2570'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Fiber type grouping on muscle biopsy indicates:', '["Myopathic process resulting in decreased functional activity","Disuse atrophy a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reinnervation after denervation (collateral sprouting of surviving motor neurons)","Muscular dystrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurogenic process (initial stage) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reinnervation after denervation (collateral sprouting of surviving motor neurons)', 'Fiber type grouping develops when surviving motor neurons sprout collaterals and reinnervate denervated fibers, so contiguous territories come to share a single fiber type. It is therefore evidence of reinnervation after denervation, not of a primary myopathic process.'),
('78ff2473-a8da-5d21-a45d-c743d49ea3d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The term muscle spasm refers to:', '["Sustained muscle contraction","and painful contraction of a muscle or group of muscles","Voluntary contraction","involuntary","A sudden"]'::jsonb, 'A sudden', 'Muscle spasm is a sudden, involuntary, and painful contraction involving a muscle or group of muscles. A voluntary contraction is under conscious control and is not painful, and a widely sustained tonic contraction of a single unit is not the defining feature.'),
('1557e74f-bfd0-5870-972a-dda06fae452c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Fasciculation is defined as:', '["spontaneous twitching of a part of a muscle (a single motor unit firing spontaneously)","Visible","fine","A sustained contraction","A painful cramp"]'::jsonb, 'Visible', 'Fasciculation is a visible, fine, spontaneous twitch of part of a muscle caused by firing of a single motor unit. A sustained and painful contraction is a cramp or spasm, not a fasciculation.'),
('2e1a686b-ed88-5565-8a67-ca2b94548f02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myokymia (muscle quivering) is:', '["continuous rippling of muscle (surface undulating movement) often seen in the eyelids","but also in demyelinating disorders","Fasciculation","Fine","Cramp"]'::jsonb, 'Fine', 'Myokymia is a fine, continuous rippling or undulating movement of the muscle surface, often seen in the eyelids and in demyelinating disorders. It differs from the single twitch of fasciculation and from a cramp.'),
('e173b4b2-580c-52c3-ac21-4af84ca9d285'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Tetany is sustained muscle contraction due to:', '["Low sodium resulting in decreased functional activity","High potassium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low ionized calcium (hypocalcemia) causing increased neuronal excitability","Low magnesium a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","High calcium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low ionized calcium (hypocalcemia) causing increased neuronal excitability', 'Hypocalcemia increases neuronal and muscle membrane excitability, so spontaneous firing produces sustained contraction, tetany. High calcium, low sodium, and high potassium do not cause this form of hyperexcitability.'),
('7b13bb3c-7cd6-5d47-baf9-8c6a549cfabc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Rigor mortis is:', '["Immediate muscle relaxation resulting in decreased functional activity","Flaccid paralysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscle regeneration a mediated by specific receptor activation","Postmortem muscle stiffening due to ATP depletion — actin-myosin cross-bridges are unable to detach","Putrefaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postmortem muscle stiffening due to ATP depletion — actin-myosin cross-bridges are unable to detach', 'After death, ATP is depleted so the myosin heads cannot detach from actin, leaving cross-bridges locked and muscles stiff, a state called rigor mortis. It is not immediate relaxation, regeneration, or putrefaction.'),
('7483c243-980c-523b-bee8-5c0eaace9780'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Fibers of the intrafusal muscle spindle are designated:', '["Nuclear chain only","Type I and II fibers","Nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers","Extrafusal fibers","Nuclear bag only"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers', 'Intrafusal fibers of the muscle spindle are of two types, nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers, named for the equatorial arrangement of their nuclei. Extrafusal fibers are the force-generating fibers outside the spindle.'),
('f08dafb2-f523-5b9e-89e5-67c9e0dcc51e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The primary (Ia) afferent from the muscle spindle responds to:', '["Tension only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rate of tension mediated by specific receptor activation","Dynamic and static stretch (rate and length changes)","Length only (static) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dynamic and static stretch (rate and length changes)', 'The primary Ia afferent innervates both nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers and signals both the rate of stretch and the muscle length, so it responds to dynamic and static stretch. Signaling length alone is the role of the secondary group II afferent.'),
('878f84f5-fb66-56cf-b561-c6f1b1297161'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The secondary (II) afferent from the muscle spindle responds to:', '["Pain only resulting in decreased functional activity","Static stretch (muscle length) only","Tension only mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of stretch only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temperature leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Static stretch (muscle length) only', 'The secondary group II afferent, which mainly supplies nuclear chain fibers, responds to static stretch, signaling muscle length with a regular discharge. The dynamic response to the rate of stretch belongs to the primary Ia afferent.'),
('3cfc51ba-1658-57dc-9c73-722667230f60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Co-activation of α and γ motor neurons during voluntary movement ensures:', '["The spindle remains sensitive to stretch throughout the full range of motion","Only gamma fibers activate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The spindle is desensitized mediated by specific receptor activation","Only alpha fibers activate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain is prevented a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The spindle remains sensitive to stretch throughout the full range of motion', 'Gamma motor neurons contract the intrafusal fibers, keeping the spindle taut while alpha fibers shorten the muscle, so the spindle stays sensitive to stretch throughout the full range of motion. Without this co-activation the spindle would slacken and fall silent.'),
('a9ea8d18-fa98-545b-89a4-3755fcd040f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The inverse stretch reflex (autogenic inhibition) is mediated by:', '["Golgi tendon organ (Ib afferent) — inhibits the contracting muscle and excites its antagonist (when tension is high)","Joint receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cutaneous receptors a mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle spindle (Ia) — excites the homonymous muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Golgi tendon organ (Ib afferent) — inhibits the contracting muscle and excites its antagonist (when tension is high)', 'High tension stimulates Golgi tendon organs, whose Ib afferents inhibit the contracting muscle and excite its antagonist, producing the inverse stretch reflex. The spindle Ia afferent instead excites the homonymous muscle and mediates the stretch reflex.'),
('925398e4-b616-57bc-9740-326aebe69d04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The patellar (knee jerk) reflex is a:', '["Polysynaptic reflex resulting in decreased functional activity","Monosynaptic stretch reflex mediated by Ia afferents from the quadriceps muscle spindle","Cutaneous reflex a mediated by specific receptor activation","Bisynaptic reflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autogenic inhibition reflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monosynaptic stretch reflex mediated by Ia afferents from the quadriceps muscle spindle', 'Tapping the patellar tendon stretches the quadriceps and activates Ia afferents that synapse directly on alpha motor neurons, forming a monosynaptic stretch reflex. No interneurons are involved, so it is not polysynaptic.'),
('26e3ba9e-199c-50fd-9f6a-42868377fbfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myotonia (as in myotonic dystrophy) is caused by:', '["Reduced chloride conductance in the muscle membrane (ClC-1 channel dysfunction)","Deficient ACh receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inexcitability of muscle membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","causing repetitive action potentials without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excess acetylcholinesterase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced chloride conductance in the muscle membrane (ClC-1 channel dysfunction)', 'In myotonia, reduced ClC-1 chloride conductance delays membrane repolarization, so repetitive action potentials continue firing and the muscle cannot relax promptly. Deficient ACh receptors or an inexcitable membrane would weaken contraction, not prolong it.'),
('78c1a160-f618-5639-a0c9-aa3645e06419'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'McArdle disease (glycogen storage disease type V) is caused by deficiency of:', '["Debranching enzyme resulting in decreased functional activity","Branching enzyme accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myophosphorylase (muscle glycogen phosphorylase) — inability to break down glycogen to glucose-1-phosphate","Phosphofructokinase a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acid maltase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myophosphorylase (muscle glycogen phosphorylase) — inability to break down glycogen to glucose-1-phosphate', 'McArdle disease is caused by myophosphorylase deficiency, which blocks glycogen breakdown to glucose-1-phosphate and deprives muscle of anaerobic fuel, producing cramps and myoglobinuria on exertion. Acid maltase deficiency is the enzyme defect of Pompe disease.'),
('60022044-ca2d-5443-812d-746219ad30a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Pompe disease (glycogen storage disease type II) is caused by deficiency of:', '["Debranching enzyme resulting in decreased functional activity","Branching enzyme accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acid alpha-glucosidase (acid maltase) — lysosomal glycogen accumulation","Myophosphorylase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PFK a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acid alpha-glucosidase (acid maltase) — lysosomal glycogen accumulation', 'Pompe disease results from acid alpha-glucosidase (acid maltase) deficiency, a lysosomal enzyme defect leading to glycogen accumulation within lysosomes. Myophosphorylase deficiency produces McArdle disease instead.'),
('4a6ab527-8e4b-5f61-be35-63c712b7abec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Carnitine deficiency causes lipid storage myopathy because:', '["Glycogen cannot be broken down a resulting in decreased functional activity","Acetyl-CoA cannot be formed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Long-chain fatty acids cannot be transported into the mitochondria for β-oxidation","ATP production from glucose is blocked without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Short-chain fatty acids cannot be oxidized leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Long-chain fatty acids cannot be transported into the mitochondria for β-oxidation', 'Carnitine shuttles long-chain fatty acids across the mitochondrial membrane for beta-oxidation; its deficiency blocks this transport, so lipids accumulate within muscle fibers as lipid storage myopathy. Glucose metabolism and short-chain fatty acid handling are unaffected.'),
('3f7f7e60-c744-522e-a6dc-0cb0495cd069'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'CPT II deficiency (carnitine palmitoyltransferase II) is a common cause of:', '["Lipid storage myopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Recurrent myoglobinuria in adults (exercise-induced muscle breakdown and dark urine)","Glycogen storage myopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Metabolic syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiomyopathy a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent myoglobinuria in adults (exercise-induced muscle breakdown and dark urine)', 'CPT II deficiency limits the entry of long-chain fatty acids into mitochondria, so sustained exercise then triggers muscle fiber breakdown, myalgia, and dark urine from myoglobinuria; it is a common cause of recurrent myoglobinuria in adults. It is not a glycogen storage myopathy.'),
('1311527f-2e6b-5379-8a11-e4dea15edee0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Episodic myoglobinuria can result from:', '["and severe exercise","All of the above: McArdle","CPT II deficiency","Only McArdle","malignant hyperthermia"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above: McArdle', 'Episodic myoglobinuria can be triggered by McArdle disease, CPT II deficiency, malignant hyperthermia, or severe exercise, so the option that names all these causes is the most complete. Naming only McArdle omits the other recognized triggers.'),
('0b8237e1-f9ef-58bc-887b-5e9226f54680'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The first event in cardiac muscle contraction is:', '["Na+ influx through fast Na+ channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Ca2+ entry through L-type Ca2+ channels during the action potential plateau","Ca2+ binding to troponin C mediated by specific receptor activation","Release of Ca2+ from SR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","K+ efflux leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ entry through L-type Ca2+ channels during the action potential plateau', 'In cardiac muscle, calcium first enters through L-type calcium channels during the plateau of the action potential; this small influx then triggers much larger calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum that activates contraction. Sodium influx and potassium efflux are not the initiating trigger for contraction.'),
('6eecc2e2-5c6f-53ed-9621-4a31c44b1a61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Ventricular action potential has how many phases?', '["6","4","3","2","5 (0-4)"]'::jsonb, '5 (0-4)', 'The ventricular action potential is divided into five phases: 0 (depolarization), 1 (early repolarization), 2 (plateau), 3 (repolarization), and 4 (resting potential). Therefore it has five phases.'),
('c829066e-dabf-5c37-b9f3-0f0f711b4058'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The resting membrane potential of a ventricular myocyte is approximately:', '["−70 mV","0 mV","−60 mV","−50 mV","−90 mV"]'::jsonb, '−90 mV', 'The resting membrane of a ventricular myocyte is most permeable to K+, so its potential sits close to the K+ equilibrium potential at about −90 mV. −70 mV approximates neuronal resting potential, −60 mV and −50 mV are too depolarized, and 0 mV is simply the zero reference level.'),
('04d800ca-d7f6-590b-bc70-26463bdcc618'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'If (funny current) in SA node cells is a:', '["Cl- current only resulting in decreased functional activity","Mixed Na+/K+ inward current activated by hyperpolarization (and modulated by cAMP)","Na+ current only mediated by specific receptor activation","K+ current only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ca2+ current only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed Na+/K+ inward current activated by hyperpolarization (and modulated by cAMP)', 'The funny current If is a mixed inward Na+/K+ current that opens when the membrane hyperpolarizes near the end of phase 4 and is enhanced by cAMP (sympathetic) and reduced by acetylcholine. Options naming a single ion species miss the mixed nature and the hyperpolarization-activated gating that distinguish this current.'),
('1df7c1ed-0727-50d8-8b79-450a11bc4d5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'T-type Ca2+ channels contribute to:', '["The ventricular action potential plateau","The rapid conduction","The resting membrane potential","The upstroke of the SA node action potential (phase 0)","The repolarization"]'::jsonb, 'The upstroke of the SA node action potential (phase 0)', 'T-type (transient) Ca2+ channels help complete the later part of diastolic depolarization and contribute to the upstroke (phase 0) of the SA node action potential. The ventricular plateau depends on L-type Ca2+ channels, while conduction velocity, resting potential, and repolarization are governed by other ion channels.'),
('2b767cad-3eaa-50e5-ac3e-60ea1c8add60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The AV node delays the cardiac impulse to:', '["Allow time for atrial contraction to complete before ventricular contraction (atrial kick)","Allow more time for ventricular filling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increase cardiac output mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevent atrial fibrillation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pace the ventricles a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Allow time for atrial contraction to complete before ventricular contraction (atrial kick)', 'The roughly 0.1 s AV nodal delay lets atrial systole finish before the ventricles contract, securing the atrial contribution to ventricular filling (atrial kick) and preventing simultaneous chamber contraction. It does not primarily create extra time for passive filling, raise cardiac output, prevent atrial fibrillation, or set ventricular pacing.'),
('10bf3755-2292-5df0-8278-a826f628e217'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The His-Purkinje system ensures:', '["Atrial contraction resulting in decreased functional activity","Valve closure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventricular relaxation mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart rate acceleration a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rapid and coordinated ventricular activation (apex to base)"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid and coordinated ventricular activation (apex to base)', 'The His-Purkinje system carries the impulse very quickly from the AV node to the ventricular apex and up the free walls, so both ventricles depolarize almost simultaneously for coordinated ejection. Atrial contraction, valve closure, ventricular relaxation, and rate acceleration are handled by separate mechanisms.'),
('1d7fa922-3925-5c54-9d54-0248aa233a2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Parasympathetic stimulation of the heart (acetylcholine) slows heart rate by:', '["causing hyperpolarization and slowing the pacemaker potential resulting in decreased functional activity","Activating muscarinic M2 receptors → Gi → decreasing cAMP and opening GIRK (KACh) channels","Inhibiting K+ channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Activating β-receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking Na+ channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activating muscarinic M2 receptors → Gi → decreasing cAMP and opening GIRK (KACh) channels', 'Acetylcholine acts on cardiac M2 muscarinic receptors, which couple to Gi to lower cAMP and open GIRK (KACh) potassium channels; the outward K+ current hyperpolarizes the SA node and slows diastolic depolarization. The listed alternatives describe mechanisms opposite to vagal action or unconnected channels.'),
('23f5bf53-2de8-56b6-a382-7e6decc87779'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The Bainbridge reflex (atrial stretch → increased heart rate) is mediated by:', '["Aortic arch reflex resulting in decreased functional activity","Baroreceptor reflex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carotid sinus reflex mediated by specific receptor activation","Stretch of the left atrium → bradycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stretch of the right atrium → increased sympathetic outflow (reflex tachycardia)"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch of the right atrium → increased sympathetic outflow (reflex tachycardia)', 'In the Bainbridge reflex, distension of the right atrium triggers a rise in sympathetic outflow and heart rate, helping the heart handle an increased venous return. Aortic arch, carotid sinus, and baroreceptor reflexes either oppose or are unrelated to this atrial-stretch tachycardia.'),
('252d8a18-3927-5181-8b76-fa2917dcecd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The Frank-Starling mechanism states that:', '["Increased afterload decreases stroke volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased heart rate increases contractility a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased heart rate increases contractility mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased diastolic filling (preload) increases stroke volume (due to increased sarcomere stretch)","Increased contractility increases preload leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased diastolic filling (preload) increases stroke volume (due to increased sarcomere stretch)', 'Frank-Starling''s law holds that greater end-diastolic filling stretches the sarcomeres toward optimum myofilament overlap, generating a stronger contraction and greater stroke volume. Afterload, heart rate, and contractility are separate determinants of cardiac output and are not the mechanism described here.'),
('f1c7b2f6-e369-5c85-a491-3b119a88d8e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Muscle atrophy (decreased muscle mass) occurs with:', '["Denervation","disuse","cachexia","and steroid use","aging (sarcopenia)"]'::jsonb, 'Denervation', 'Loss of motor innervation removes both neural stimulation and trophic influences on the muscle fiber, producing classic neurogenic atrophy of the affected muscle. Disuse, cachexia, corticosteroid excess, and aging also reduce muscle mass, but denervation is the prototype when wasting follows interruption of the nerve supply.'),
('6c15808b-9212-5217-b0f1-cccd533cbfd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Sarcopenia (age-related muscle loss) is characterized by:', '["especially type II fibers (loss of motor units)","Selective loss of type I fibers","Increased satellite cell activity","Hypertrophy of remaining fibers","Decreased muscle fiber number and size"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased muscle fiber number and size', 'Sarcopenia reflects a decline in both the number and the size of muscle fibers, with the loss of motor units affecting fast type II fibers most severely in the elderly. Selective type I loss, rising satellite activity, or hypertrophy of remaining fibers would oppose rather than characterize the process.'),
('14c0a2d1-3ab1-5be1-9749-7ad402b0bc17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The T-tubule system is absent in which muscle type?', '["Smooth muscle only","Skeletal muscle","Both skeletal and cardiac","Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle (uses caveolae instead)"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle (uses caveolae instead)', 'Smooth muscle fibers lack transverse tubules; instead their sarcolemma forms flask-shaped caveolae involved in signaling and in bringing extracellular Ca2+ close to the contractile apparatus. T-tubules are typical of both skeletal and cardiac muscle.'),
('9616d261-9086-525b-ad1c-25f55e2662a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The term muscular dystrophy refers to:', '["A muscle infection resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A metabolic myopathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A nerve injury mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A toxin-induced myopathy a without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A group of inherited myopathies with progressive muscle weakness and degeneration due to defects in structural or membrane proteins"]'::jsonb, 'A group of inherited myopathies with progressive muscle weakness and degeneration due to defects in structural or membrane proteins', 'Muscular dystrophies are inherited myopathies that progress with weakness and fiber degeneration, most often stemming from mutations in structural or membrane proteins such as dystrophin in Duchenne dystrophy. Infectious, metabolic, toxic, and neurogenic causes describe distinct categories of muscle disease.'),
('79a0fcdd-c482-5d8d-a1cb-b113dd7dba2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Nuclear bag fibers of the muscle spindle have:', '["Nuclei arranged in a line resulting in decreased functional activity","A central cluster of nuclei (like a bag)","Sarcomeres only mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A central cluster of nuclei (like a bag)', 'Bag fibers take their name from the cluster of nuclei gathered centrally at the equatorial region of the intrafusal fiber, resembling a bag. Nuclei in a single row describe chain fibers, and peripheral nuclei are not a feature of intrafusal fibers.'),
('4df023a6-65b4-56ff-b727-6f406dc64b69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Nuclear chain fibers have:', '["nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Peripheral nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Central sarcomeres mediated by specific receptor activation","A central cluster of nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nuclei arranged in a single row (chain) in the equatorial region"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclei arranged in a single row (chain) in the equatorial region', 'Chain fibers align their nuclei in a single longitudinal row, like a string of beads, across the equatorial region of the intrafusal fiber. This arrangement contrasts with the bagged central group of nuclei seen in nuclear bag fibers; peripheral nuclei or central sarcomeres do not describe it.'),
('b518c4c0-1da5-5f46-8162-c1c3183b5d59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcoplasm of smooth muscle stains poorly in H&E because:', '["It is full of fat resulting in decreased functional activity","It has nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is composed of water mediated by specific receptor activation","It contains relatively little contractile protein compared to striated muscle","It lacks cellular components leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It contains relatively little contractile protein compared to striated muscle', 'Smooth muscle cytoplasm holds comparatively little contractile protein relative to its cytosolic content, so eosin stains it only lightly under H&E, leaving an appearance of poorly stained cytoplasm. Fat content is not the reason, and smooth muscle cells plainly contain nuclei and cytoplasm.'),
('64faa8a5-4986-5dd7-af55-62cfc14a8ff5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The coronary arteries receive most of their blood flow during:', '["Atrial contraction","Both systole and diastole equally a","Atrial relaxation","Systole (ventricular contraction)","Diastole (ventricular relaxation)"]'::jsonb, 'Diastole (ventricular relaxation)', 'Coronary perfusion peaks during diastole because systolic contraction compresses the intramural coronary vessels and raises their resistance. As soon as the ventricle relaxes, resistance falls and flow surges; therefore events timed to systole or atrial activity describe the period of reduced coronary flow.'),
('e8f96e84-26c8-5a4a-85a1-9642ba64f05b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The primary reserve of energy for skeletal muscle contraction is:', '["Fatty acids a resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucose from blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ATP stored in the muscle (enough for 5 seconds of intense contraction)","Creatine phosphate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glycogen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ATP stored in the muscle (enough for 5 seconds of intense contraction)', 'Preformed ATP stored in the myofiber is the immediate energy reserve, supporting only a few seconds of intense contraction before it must be regenerated. Creatine phosphate forms the next rapid phosphate reservoir, while glycogen and blood glucose supply slower, longer-term fuel.'),
('9470788f-8d7f-5a02-a113-c193ce448c3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Creatine phosphate in muscle serves to:', '["Phosphorylate myosin resulting in decreased functional activity","Buffer lactate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rapidly regenerate ATP from ADP (via creatine kinase)","Store Ca2+ without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Store glycogen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapidly regenerate ATP from ADP (via creatine kinase)', 'Creatine phosphate donates its high-energy phosphate to ADP through creatine kinase, rapidly resynthesizing ATP during early, intense activity. It does not phosphorylate myosin, buffer lactate, or store calcium or glycogen.'),
('ac8c063d-0b15-56de-bcf1-8ac54c39084b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The oxygen-binding protein in skeletal muscle that stores oxygen is:', '["Myoglobin","Catalase","Spectrin","Cytochrome oxidase","Hemoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Myoglobin', 'Myoglobin is the intracellular heme protein of skeletal and cardiac muscle that binds and stores oxygen for use during contraction. Hemoglobin carries oxygen in blood, while catalase, spectrin, and cytochrome oxidase serve unrelated antioxidant, membrane-skeletal, and electron-transport roles.'),
('e28d2a95-42db-5bee-acec-a4dbd45b0259'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Rhabdomyolysis (breakdown of muscle fibers) releases into the blood:', '["potassium","phosphate","LDH","CK","Myoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Myoglobin', 'Muscle fiber breakdown liberates myoglobin into the bloodstream, where it can precipitate and injure renal tubules, producing the classic myoglobinuria and risk of acute kidney injury. Potassium, phosphate, LDH, and CK are also released, but myoglobin is the hallmark marker of muscle destruction.'),
('e3936dad-4600-5c13-8905-b7ef46ee9548'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The most common cause of rhabdomyolysis is:', '["Seizures resulting in decreased functional activity","Statin use accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alcohol a mediated by specific receptor activation","Crush injury (traumatic rhabdomyolysis)","Excessive exercise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crush injury (traumatic rhabdomyolysis)', 'Traumatic crush injury is the leading recognized cause of rhabdomyolysis, mass-rupturing muscle fibers and flooding the circulation with their contents. Seizures, statins, alcohol, and extreme exercise are important causes but rank behind major trauma in overall frequency.'),
('44e7dbc0-d88a-505f-8b04-27439a01ff55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The width of the A-band during muscle contraction:', '["Shifts resulting in decreased functional activity","Disappears accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increases mediated by specific receptor activation","Remains constant (myosin length does not change)","Decreases leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Remains constant (myosin length does not change)', 'The A-band spans the full length of the myosin filaments, which do not shorten during contraction, so its width stays constant. The I-band and H-zone narrow as actin filaments slide inward past the myosin, but the A-band itself is unchanged.'),
('d1a1826e-3357-57a7-925a-a6cca79f0914'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sliding filament model of contraction was proposed by:', '["Krebs","Szent-Gyorgyi","Huxley and Huxley","Bernard a","Starling"]'::jsonb, 'Huxley and Huxley', 'In 1954, A.F. Huxley and H.E. Huxley independently advanced the sliding filament hypothesis, in which actin filaments slide past stationary myosin filaments to shorten the sarcomere. Szent-Gyorgyi contributed actomyosin biochemistry, while Krebs and Starling are associated with other physiological discoveries.'),
('97c56a68-1dd3-54eb-8d9f-815966d4ebf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myosin heavy chain isoforms determine:', '["The speed of contraction (fast or slow MHC)","The force of contraction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Actin binding mediated by specific receptor activation","Ca2+ sensitivity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ATP affinity a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The speed of contraction (fast or slow MHC)', 'Myosin heavy chain isoforms (fast MHC II vs slow MHC I) set the maximal shortening velocity, and therefore the speed of contraction, of a muscle fiber. Force production, actin binding, calcium sensitivity, and ATP affinity depend on other proteins and regulatory conditions.'),
('b8fd0534-322e-5f21-9798-7eb37dd5920d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Cardiac muscle contraction is all-or-none (it contracts as a syncytium) because:', '["The cells are not coupled resulting in decreased functional activity","The conduction system is absent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gap junctions electrically couple all cardiomyocytes in the ventricles (functional syncytium)","Each cell is independently innervated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The heart has gap junctions a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gap junctions electrically couple all cardiomyocytes in the ventricles (functional syncytium)', 'Gap junctions at the intercalated discs electrically couple ventricular cardiomyocytes, so an action potential spreads from cell to cell and the ventricles depolarize as a functional syncytium, contracting nearly all-or-none. Independent cellular innervation does not exist in cardiac muscle, and uncoupled cells would fragment contraction.'),
('e77ba1ed-1065-59cf-9b01-ce9db5b8e65a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Smooth muscle can contract in response to:', '["Stretch (myogenic response)","as well as neural","hormonal","Only neural signals","and paracrine signals"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch (myogenic response)', 'Smooth muscle contracts when stretched through an intrinsic myogenic response, in addition to being activatable by neural, hormonal, and paracrine signals. Restricting activation to neural signals alone contradicts the demonstrated contractile response to stretch.'),
('8b6fea29-a1ac-5cd7-868f-ec124f857235'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The myogenic response of vascular smooth muscle refers to:', '["Contraction in response to neural stimulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Relaxation in response to nitric oxide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Relaxation in response to stretch mediated by specific receptor activation","Proliferation in response to injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contraction in response to increased stretch (pressure) — helps autoregulate blood flow"]'::jsonb, 'Contraction in response to increased stretch (pressure) — helps autoregulate blood flow', 'The myogenic response is the intrinsic tendency of vascular smooth muscle to contract when stretched by rising intraluminal pressure, which stabilizes organ blood flow through autoregulation. Neural contraction, nitric-oxide relaxation, stretch-induced relaxation, and injury-driven proliferation are different phenomena.'),
('54f091c7-6297-5cc5-8521-90f889f1ab53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The concentration of Ca2+ in the sarcoplasm of a resting muscle cell is:', '["10-1 M (100 mM) a","10-9 M (1 nM)","10-3 M (1 mM)","10-7 M (0.1 μM)","10-5 M (10 μM)"]'::jsonb, '10-7 M (0.1 μM)', 'At rest the sarcoplasm holds free calcium near 10-7 M (0.1 µM), below the threshold needed to activate the troponin complex. Concentrations of 10-3 M or higher would already trigger contraction, whereas 10-9 M understates resting cytoplasmic calcium.'),
('f709e9be-735c-56e5-89ba-ef3e56110e4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The main source of ATP for resting muscle is:', '["Glucose from blood resulting in decreased functional activity","Glycolysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Creatine phosphate mediated by specific receptor activation","Fatty acid oxidation (oxidative phosphorylation)","Protein breakdown a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty acid oxidation (oxidative phosphorylation)', 'At rest, skeletal muscle depends chiefly on fatty acid oxidation and oxidative phosphorylation, which yield abundant ATP aerobically at a sustainable rate. Glycolysis and creatine phosphate are recruited mainly as activity intensifies, and protein is not a primary resting fuel.'),
('9c37369a-7ae2-5f5b-b7a3-55868aca1f2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The Cori cycle (lactate shuttle) involves:', '["Direct oxidation of lactate in muscle a resulting in decreased functional activity","Lactate from liver to muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glucose from muscle to liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Conversion of lactate to glycogen in muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lactate produced by muscle (glycolysis) → liver → gluconeogenesis → glucose returned to muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate produced by muscle (glycolysis) → liver → gluconeogenesis → glucose returned to muscle', 'In the Cori cycle, lactate formed by muscle glycolysis is carried to the liver, rebuilt into glucose by gluconeogenesis, and returned to the muscle. Lactate is thus recycled rather than directly oxidized in the muscle, and the movement runs from muscle to liver, not the reverse.'),
('3c609587-63d7-5d8e-aa9b-3bd1df175e1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Oxygen debt (excess post-exercise oxygen consumption) is due to:', '["PCr","removing lactate","and restoring myoglobin O2 and blood and tissue O2 stores","Replenishing ATP","and glycogen"]'::jsonb, 'Replenishing ATP', 'Oxygen debt represents the extra oxygen consumed after exertion to resynthesize ATP and creatine phosphate, clear lactate, and restore myoglobin-bound and tissue oxygen stores. Replenishing ATP is the immediate priority that drives the early, rapid component of the excess post-exercise oxygen consumption.'),
('69d38545-794a-56e5-afa5-da753e46b999'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The basement membrane (basal lamina) around each muscle fiber is part of the:', '["Sarcoplasm a","Endomysium","Perimysium","Sarcolemma","Epimysium"]'::jsonb, 'Endomysium', 'The basal lamina investing each individual muscle fiber belongs to the endomysium, the thin connective tissue layer surrounding single fibers. Perimysium encloses fascicles, epimysium wraps the whole muscle, and the sarcolemma is the myofiber''s plasma membrane rather than connective tissue.'),
('e9b8b506-fa4a-58a3-bccd-084e0b58aa53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Skeletal muscle fibers are innervated by:', '["Interneurons resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibitory neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory neurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Autonomic neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Somatic motor neurons (α-motor neurons)"]'::jsonb, 'Somatic motor neurons (α-motor neurons)', 'Each skeletal muscle fiber is directly innervated by a somatic α-motor neuron whose terminal forms the motor end plate. Autonomic neurons serve smooth and cardiac muscle, sensory neurons carry afferent signals, and interneurons or inhibitory neurons are not the direct motor supply.'),
('ed9969e4-3b98-5562-923f-ea7b57e47b38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Each motor unit consists of:', '["One α-motor neuron and all the muscle fibers it innervates","One muscle fiber and one nerve","One motor neuron and one muscle fiber","All the motor neurons innervating one muscle","One sensory neuron and multiple muscles"]'::jsonb, 'One α-motor neuron and all the muscle fibers it innervates', 'A motor unit comprises a single α-motor neuron together with all the muscle fibers its axon innervates, forming the functional unit of voluntary contraction. Pairing one nerve to a single fiber, grouping all neurons of a muscle, or assigning a sensory neuron to muscles redefines the anatomy incorrectly.'),
('26cfbc4f-1bc4-5936-85c0-909bab658834'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Henneman''s size principle states that:', '["Type II fibers are recruited first resulting in decreased functional activity","Type I fibers recruit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Recruitment is random mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor units are all recruited simultaneously a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smaller motor neurons (innervating type I fibers) are recruited first during voluntary contraction; larger ones (type II) later"]'::jsonb, 'Smaller motor neurons (innervating type I fibers) are recruited first during voluntary contraction; larger ones (type II) later', 'Henneman''s size principle holds that small α-motor neurons, which innervate slow type I fibers, are recruited before larger neurons driving fast type II fibers, so force builds up in an orderly graded fashion. Random, type II-first, or simultaneous-all recruitment would abolish this smooth gradation of tension.'),
('2ffe157f-4ef9-5911-a46d-4fd3d8d4ca06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The force of muscle contraction can be increased by:', '["Stretching the muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Recruiting more motor units and increasing the firing rate of already recruited units (rate coding)","Activating only one motor unit mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing Ca2+ release without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increasing ATP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recruiting more motor units and increasing the firing rate of already recruited units (rate coding)', 'Graded force arises from recruiting additional motor units and from raising the discharge rate of units already active (rate coding), which raises stimulation frequency and tension. Activating a single unit, or merely boosting Ca2+ or ATP availability, is not the normal neural strategy for increasing force.'),
('28c2c3fb-1d70-58f5-b0aa-67acc83776b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Tetanus (tetanic contraction) in muscle is:', '["A sustained resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","smooth contraction due to high-frequency stimulation (complete tetanus) where APs are too fast for relaxation","A cramp without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle spasm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'smooth contraction due to high-frequency stimulation (complete tetanus) where APs are too fast for relaxation', 'Tetanus is a sustained, smooth contraction produced when high-frequency stimulation leaves no time for the muscle to relax between successive twitches, giving complete (fused) tetanus. A cramp, spasm, or infection does not define this physiological fusion of individual twitches.'),
('cad0a7e7-ccf8-5720-a323-399208a2b301'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Summation of muscle twitches occurs when:', '["A second stimulus is applied before the muscle has fully relaxed from the first stimulus","Single twitches occur accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two motor units fire simultaneously mediated by specific receptor activation","Two different muscles contract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The muscle is stretched a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A second stimulus is applied before the muscle has fully relaxed from the first stimulus', 'When a second stimulus arrives before the first twitch has fully relaxed, the two twitches summate into a stronger combined contraction. Delivering stimuli after full relaxation, firing separate motor units at once, or involving two muscles does not summate contractions within a single fiber.'),
('c171d54b-3d8c-5498-a7aa-32689712f50a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The refractory period of skeletal muscle is:', '["Same as the twitch","much shorter than the twitch duration (100 ms)","Short (1-2 ms)","Variable","Long (100 ms)"]'::jsonb, 'Short (1-2 ms)', 'Skeletal muscle has a very short absolute refractory period of roughly 1-2 ms, far shorter than its 100 ms twitch, which is why successive twitches can summate and tetanize. A refractory period as long as the twitch, as in cardiac muscle, would prevent summation.'),
('21ffaa5e-bcdb-5681-9c05-3112f6ae56d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The long refractory period of cardiac muscle prevents:', '["Heart block resulting in decreased functional activity","Tetanic contraction (which would stop the pumping action of the heart)","Arrhythmia mediated by specific receptor activation","Infarction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrillation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tetanic contraction (which would stop the pumping action of the heart)', 'Cardiac muscle''s long refractory period spans nearly the entire contraction, preventing tetanic fusion so the heart can relax and refill between beats; a sustained tetanus would halt the pumping action. The mechanism protects against sustained spasm rather than causing heart block, arrhythmia, or infarction.'),
('f0d5a9e6-340b-5f52-9b76-f619d7d949a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The term myocardium refers to:', '["The cardiac muscle layer of the heart wall","The pericardial sac","The outer layer of the heart","The inner lining of the heart","The heart valves"]'::jsonb, 'The cardiac muscle layer of the heart wall', 'The myocardium is the thick middle layer of the heart wall, composed of cardiac muscle responsible for pumping. The epicardium is the outer layer, the endocardium is the inner lining, and the pericardial sac and valves are separate structures.'),
('58672d2e-05ad-5ded-975b-8dfa0655a64e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The heart''s pacemaker is normally the:', '["SA node (sinus rhythm)","AV node","Atrial muscle","Purkinje fibers","Ventricular muscle"]'::jsonb, 'SA node (sinus rhythm)', 'The sinoatrial node shows the fastest spontaneous diastolic depolarization, so it normally sets the heart rate and produces sinus rhythm. The AV node, Purkinje fibers, and atrial or ventricular muscle depolarize more slowly and act as pacemakers only when the SA node fails.'),
('fc27d693-fda0-58cf-b349-9ca494c806cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The term escape rhythm refers to:', '["High heart rate resulting in decreased functional activity","Bradycardia a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Irregular rhythm mediated by specific receptor activation","Extra beats without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rhythm generated by a latent pacemaker (AV node or Purkinje fibers) when the SA node fails"]'::jsonb, 'Rhythm generated by a latent pacemaker (AV node or Purkinje fibers) when the SA node fails', 'An escape rhythm is the beat produced by a latent pacemaker, usually the AV node or Purkinje fibers, stepping in when the SA node fails or its impulse is blocked. It is a slower safety rhythm rather than tachycardia, extrasystoles, or an irregular arrhythmia in itself.'),
('6ca5a607-1a57-54b1-9397-b0260e63ad71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Parasympathetic innervation to the heart is via:', '["Hypoglossal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Phrenic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sympathetic chain mediated by specific receptor activation","The vagus nerve (CN X) – decreases heart rate and AV conduction","Accessory nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vagus nerve (CN X) – decreases heart rate and AV conduction', 'Parasympathetic fibers reach the heart through the vagus nerve (CN X), which slows heart rate and atrioventricular conduction. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm, while the hypoglossal and accessory nerves carry no cardiac fibers.'),
('0021757b-d8eb-57e5-9dea-7cac5f2375e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Sympathetic innervation to the heart arises from:', '["Sacral parasympathetic a resulting in decreased functional activity","Vagus nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper thoracic (T1-T4) sympathetic ganglia (stellate ganglion)","Lumbar sympathetic chain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cervical plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper thoracic (T1-T4) sympathetic ganglia (stellate ganglion)', 'Cardiac sympathetic fibers arise from the upper thoracic segments T1-T4 and travel through the cervical ganglia, including the stellate ganglion, to reach the heart. The lumbar chain, sacral parasympathetic outflow, cervical plexus, and vagus are not the source of cardiac sympathetic supply.'),
('846b14cb-f25d-57eb-b6e8-7721a1435d7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The predominant autonomic innervation of the heart at rest is:', '["Vagal (parasympathetic) tone (slowing the heart)","Somatic a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sympathetic tone mediated by specific receptor activation","Equal both without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vagal (parasympathetic) tone (slowing the heart)', 'At rest the heart is under dominant vagal parasympathetic tone, which holds the intrinsic rate of about 100 beats/min down to roughly 60-70 beats/min. Sympathetic tone predominates mainly during exercise or stress rather than at baseline.'),
('09bec33b-9c84-5655-8c3d-22dbc08a2f38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The carotid body (glomus body) is a chemoreceptor that responds to:', '["hypercapnia (high CO2)","and acidosis (low pH)","Low blood pressure","Hypoxia (low O2)","High blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxia (low O2)', 'The carotid body chemoreceptors respond most powerfully to arterial hypoxia (low O2), and secondarily to hypercapnia and acidosis, by driving an increase in ventilation. Blood pressure detection belongs to the adjacent carotid sinus baroreceptors rather than the glomus body.'),
('157ac693-df22-5166-8b40-bab125f8802b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The carotid sinus (at the carotid bifurcation) is a:', '["Pain receptor resulting in decreased functional activity","Chemoreceptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Baroreceptor that detects stretch (blood pressure)","Heat receptor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stretch receptor for muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Baroreceptor that detects stretch (blood pressure)', 'The carotid sinus is a baroreceptor that fires when the arterial wall is stretched by rising blood pressure, initiating reflexes that lower pressure. Chemoreception of O2, CO2, and pH belongs to the adjacent carotid body, and the sinus does not detect pain or heat.'),
('e9fab0ae-dff5-5b66-835f-65bbefb93180'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The afferent limb of the carotid sinus baroreflex is:', '["Vagus nerve (CN X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)', 'The carotid sinus baroreflex is carried to the medulla by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) through the carotid sinus nerve. The vagus carries the aortic baroreflex limb, while the hypoglossal, trigeminal, and facial nerves have unrelated functions.'),
('3d4c70f4-f098-5ce8-a075-04ce1d8a2238'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The afferent limb of the aortic arch baroreflex is:', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Sympathetic chain","Phrenic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve (CN X)', 'Aortic arch baroreceptors relay their afferent impulses to the medulla through the vagus nerve (CN X), which then contributes to cardiovascular regulation. The glossopharyngeal nerve carries the carotid sinus limb, and the other listed nerves play no part in this reflex.'),
('88f3bfd1-5b0c-54d0-8577-955d4f42f0ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The efferent limb of the baroreflex (to decrease BP) involves:', '["Increased sympathetic only resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased vagal (to SA node) and decreased sympathetic outflow (to heart and vessels)","change mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased vagal only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased both leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased vagal (to SA node) and decreased sympathetic outflow (to heart and vessels)', 'When blood pressure rises, baroreceptors increase vagal drive to the sinoatrial node while withdrawing sympathetic outflow to the heart and vessels. This lowers heart rate, contractility, and peripheral resistance, so a rise in pressure is opposed by both arms acting together rather than by a single one alone.'),
('546e02fe-3811-5d32-8dc7-6ec4f656ea5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Vagus nerve stimulation causes:', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased contractility accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bradycardia and reduced AV conduction velocity","Vasoconstriction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tachycardia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bradycardia and reduced AV conduction velocity', 'Acetylcholine released by vagal fibers acts on M2 receptors in the SA and AV nodes, slowing the pacemaker rate and delaying atrioventricular conduction. It does not raise blood pressure, constrict vessels, or enhance contractility, since ventricular muscle receives sparse vagal innervation.'),
('a647e6ba-20c6-55d3-a343-c4ee3c3d53d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The inotropic effect (increased contractility) of β-adrenergic stimulation results from:', '["Increased Ca2+ influx through L-type channels and increased SR Ca2+ uptake (via phospholamban phosphorylation)","Increased blood volume a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased heart rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased afterload without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased preload leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased Ca2+ influx through L-type channels and increased SR Ca2+ uptake (via phospholamban phosphorylation)', 'β-adrenergic stimulation raises cAMP, which increases calcium entry through L-type channels and enhances SR calcium reuptake by phosphorylation of phospholamban; the larger calcium store makes the next contraction stronger. Heart rate, preload, and afterload are different determinants of performance and are not the molecular basis of inotropy.'),
('e25b90ca-97aa-5179-82a8-2427e793238c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The term lusitropy refers to:', '["Blood flow resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart rate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conduction mediated by specific receptor activation","Contraction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myocardial relaxation (enhanced relaxation is positive lusitropic)"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial relaxation (enhanced relaxation is positive lusitropic)', 'Lusitropy describes the rate of myocardial relaxation; positive lusitropic effects, such as β-adrenergic stimulation, accelerate calcium removal and speed relaxation. Chronotropy concerns rate, dromotropy conduction, and inotropy contraction, so those terms are not synonyms for lusitropy.'),
('cb797b7a-a886-550f-bd20-962772171e5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The apex of the heart is formed by the:', '["Left atrium","Both atria","Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Right atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricle', 'The apex is the pointed inferolateral tip of the heart and is formed almost entirely by the left ventricle. The atria lie at the base, and the right ventricle occupies the anterior surface, so none of them contributes to the apex.'),
('90cce7ca-cfcc-531a-992b-914ba2081cc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The base of the heart faces:', '["Posteriorly (toward the vertebral column)","Superiorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To the left mediated by specific receptor activation","Anteriorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inferiorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posteriorly (toward the vertebral column)', 'The base of the heart is directed posteriorly toward the vertebral column (opposite the apex) and is formed mainly by the left atrium. It does not face anteriorly, superiorly, inferiorly, or to the left.'),
('18e26a9f-038f-5900-8b25-354275b599a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The intercalated disc staining with H&E appears:', '["As clear bands resulting in decreased functional activity","As dark purple dots accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","As yellow lines mediated by specific receptor activation","Darker (eosinophilic) staining step-like structures between adjacent cardiac muscle cells","As blue lines leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Darker (eosinophilic) staining step-like structures between adjacent cardiac muscle cells', 'Intercalated discs are the specialized junctions between cardiac muscle cells and stain darker (eosinophilic) as transverse step-like lines on H&E sections. They contain fascia adherens, desmosomes, and gap junctions, and they are not clear, dotted, or colored bands.'),
('b0431800-6ddd-57f1-826c-bbf8021049e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Purkinje fibers are identified on histology by being:', '["paler (glycogen-rich) cells than working ventricular myocytes","More striated","darker","Smaller","Larger"]'::jsonb, 'Larger', 'Purkinje fibers are larger than working ventricular myocytes and appear pale because they contain abundant glycogen and relatively few myofibrils. Their large size and pale, glycogen-rich cytoplasm allow them to be recognized in the subendocardial layer.'),
('80f5ace6-cc2c-50d9-84ba-4aab4206289d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The his bundle (AV bundle) branches in the interventricular septum. The left bundle branch travels:', '["In the right ventricle resulting in decreased functional activity","Within the myocardium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the left atrium mediated by specific receptor activation","Subendocardially on the left side of the IV septum (divides into anterior and posterior fascicles)","Epicardially leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subendocardially on the left side of the IV septum (divides into anterior and posterior fascicles)', 'The left bundle branch runs subendocardially along the left side of the interventricular septum and divides into anterior and posterior fascicles that supply the left ventricular wall. It does not travel within the myocardium, epicardium, right ventricle, or atrium.'),
('cfbea7d1-346c-5909-a5a2-8c0911cac859'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The right bundle branch of the conduction system:', '["Runs subendocardially on the right side of the IV septum to the right ventricle (moderator band)","Is larger than the left bundle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Does not divide a mediated by specific receptor activation","Runs intramyocardially without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is the main branch of the AV node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Runs subendocardially on the right side of the IV septum to the right ventricle (moderator band)', 'The right bundle branch runs subendocardially down the right side of the interventricular septum and reaches the right ventricle through the moderator band. It is thinner than the left bundle, runs near the endocardial surface, and is a continuation of the AV bundle rather than a branch of the AV node.'),
('8858dc48-54fa-588d-a028-9119a36ba338'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The moderator band (septomarginal trabecula) of the right ventricle contains:', '["Coronary arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Valve tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","conduction fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","A portion of the right bundle branch (conduction fiber)","Fat a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A portion of the right bundle branch (conduction fiber)', 'The moderator band carries a portion of the right bundle branch across the ventricular cavity to the anterior papillary muscle, ensuring coordinated papillary muscle contraction before the free wall. It does not contain coronary arteries, valve tissue, or fat.'),
('28fbc743-ccc9-5fe2-941f-80ba9171dcf0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The size and number of mitochondria in type I muscle fibers are:', '["High (many large mitochondria for oxidative metabolism)","Variable accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low (few small mitochondria) mediated by specific receptor activation","Absent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High (many large mitochondria for oxidative metabolism)', 'Type I (slow oxidative) fibers are rich in large mitochondria to sustain aerobic ATP production during prolonged, low-force activity. Type II fast fibers rely more on glycolysis and therefore contain fewer and smaller mitochondria.'),
('0897e41c-1d3b-5b74-8d2e-585f1f64d4c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcoplasmic reticulum is less developed in which muscle type?', '["Cardiac muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Both skeletal and cardiac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skeletal muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth muscle (has less SR and relies more on extracellular Ca2+)","It is the same in all muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle (has less SR and relies more on extracellular Ca2+)', 'Smooth muscle has a poorly developed sarcoplasmic reticulum and depends largely on calcium entering from the extracellular space through L-type channels. Skeletal muscle, in contrast, has an elaborate SR with terminal cisternae that supply most of the calcium for contraction.'),
('169ed23f-a8db-58ae-a6da-83158bfe2934'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The term E-C coupling refers to:', '["Coupling of two muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Contraction only a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metabolic coupling mediated by specific receptor activation","Electrical coupling of cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sequence of events linking excitation (AP) to contraction (Ca2+ release and cross-bridge cycling)"]'::jsonb, 'The sequence of events linking excitation (AP) to contraction (Ca2+ release and cross-bridge cycling)', 'Excitation-contraction coupling is the cascade in which an action potential on the sarcolemma triggers calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which then activates the cross-bridge cycle and contraction. It links electrical excitation to mechanical contraction rather than describing cell-to-cell electrical coupling or metabolic processes.'),
('0615d738-9595-5d21-a9c9-86931dfc35da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The role of the T-tubule is to:', '["allowing rapid resulting in decreased functional activity","synchronous activation of all sarcomeres accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produce ATP mediated by specific receptor activation","Carry the AP from the surface to the interior","Store Ca2+ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carry the AP from the surface to the interior', 'T-tubules are deep invaginations of the sarcolemma that rapidly carry the action potential into the fiber interior so that all sarcomeres are activated nearly simultaneously. They do not store calcium, since that is the role of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, nor do they generate ATP.'),
('8d460943-5f99-57ba-b420-f2212d4deb18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The molecule that directly transmits the T-tubule signal to the SR in skeletal muscle is:', '["cAMP resulting in decreased functional activity","ATP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The L-type Ca2+ channel (DHPR) mechanically linked to RyR1","Ca2+ without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IP3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The L-type Ca2+ channel (DHPR) mechanically linked to RyR1', 'In skeletal muscle the dihydropyridine receptor (an L-type calcium channel) in the T-tubule is mechanically coupled to the ryanodine receptor (RyR1) on the SR, so depolarization opens RyR1 directly without requiring extracellular calcium entry. Calcium, IP3, and cAMP are not the direct mechanical link in this tissue.'),
('eccea31d-0fda-5375-990b-fac1c4e1903e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The molecule that directly transmits the T-tubule signal to the SR in cardiac muscle is:', '["Ca2+ (Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release through RyR2)","cAMP a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct mechanical linkage mediated by specific receptor activation","DHPR directly opens RyR2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IP3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ (Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release through RyR2)', 'In cardiac muscle the small calcium influx through the L-type (DHPR) channel during the action potential triggers a much larger release from the SR through RyR2, a process called calcium-induced calcium release. There is no mechanical link to the receptor as there is in skeletal muscle, and IP3 or cAMP do not mediate this coupling.'),
('1fb722a2-2f88-5e88-9856-0342b485619d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'A deficiency of vitamin D causes muscle weakness because:', '["muscle contraction resulting in decreased functional activity","Vitamin D receptors in muscle regulate gene expression for protein synthesis","Vitamin D only affects bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Vitamin D is not important for muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and calcium handling leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin D receptors in muscle regulate gene expression for protein synthesis', 'Vitamin D acts through nuclear receptors in skeletal muscle to regulate the expression of genes involved in protein synthesis and calcium handling, so deficiency impairs muscle maintenance and produces weakness. Statements that vitamin D affects only bone or is unimportant for muscle are incorrect.'),
('12128904-de40-556f-a159-a28eb9c5563a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Mutations in the ryanodine receptor (RYR1) can cause:', '["Myotonic dystrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Becker MD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duchenne MD mediated by specific receptor activation","Central core disease and malignant hyperthermia","Muscle atrophy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central core disease and malignant hyperthermia', 'Mutations in RYR1, the skeletal muscle ryanodine receptor, cause central core disease and predispose to malignant hyperthermia because of abnormal SR calcium release. Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy result from dystrophin gene mutations, and myotonic dystrophy involves an unstable trinucleotide repeat.'),
('8bbaec8c-1d9f-55e1-bca9-02323eea31cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The primary inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord that regulates α-motor neuron activity is:', '["GABA","Dopamine","Glycine","Glutamate","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine', 'Glycine is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord, where Renshaw cells and inhibitory interneurons use it to suppress α-motor neuron firing. GABA predominates as the inhibitory transmitter in the brain, while glutamate and acetylcholine are excitatory.'),
('f2377231-8ae2-5521-908e-52e6650e383f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Strychnine poisoning causes:', '["Muscle relaxation resulting in decreased functional activity","effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flaccid paralysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Generalized muscle spasms and convulsions (blocks glycine receptors on motor neurons)","Cardiac arrest a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Generalized muscle spasms and convulsions (blocks glycine receptors on motor neurons)', 'Strychnine competitively blocks glycine receptors in the spinal cord, removing tonic inhibition of motor neurons and leading to exaggerated excitation, generalized spasms, and convulsions. It does not relax muscle or produce flaccid paralysis; those outcomes occur with NMJ blockade.'),
('069126e3-9c0a-5cbf-bebd-33b1cf0a873e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The term synapse refers to:', '["The axon resulting in decreased functional activity","The dendrite accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The entire nerve cell mediated by specific receptor activation","The cell body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The junction between a nerve fiber and a muscle fiber (or another neuron)"]'::jsonb, 'The junction between a nerve fiber and a muscle fiber (or another neuron)', 'A synapse is the specialized junction across which a nerve fiber communicates with another neuron or an effector cell such as a muscle fiber. The axon, dendrite, cell body, and whole neuron are structural parts of a nerve cell, not junctions.'),
('bb3abe6d-4dac-555b-bdd2-f3c13f22b623'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The neurotransmitter at all vertebrate neuromuscular junctions is:', '["GABA","Acetylcholine (ACh)","Glutamate","Epinephrine","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine (ACh)', 'Acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter at every vertebrate neuromuscular junction, where it binds nicotinic receptors on the motor end plate to trigger contraction. GABA, glutamate, and the catecholamines do not act at the NMJ.'),
('2f9efc40-fd2c-54bd-a67b-d99e36bb94b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Curare is a competitive antagonist at the:', '["GABA receptor","β-adrenergic receptor","Nicotinic ACh receptor (NMJ)","α-adrenergic receptor (alternative wording)","Muscarinic ACh receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Nicotinic ACh receptor (NMJ)', 'Curare competes with acetylcholine for the nicotinic receptor at the neuromuscular junction, preventing end-plate depolarization and causing flaccid paralysis. It has no significant action on muscarinic, adrenergic, or GABA receptors.'),
('e2e08d16-9b14-5251-b978-9d32fd2446f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Succinylcholine (suxamethonium) is a depolarizing neuromuscular blocker that:', '["Inhibits AChE resulting in decreased functional activity","Competitively blocks nAChRs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","then desensitizes the receptor (paralysis) mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks ACh release without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Binds nAChRs and initially depolarizes the end plate (causing fasciculations)"]'::jsonb, 'Binds nAChRs and initially depolarizes the end plate (causing fasciculations)', 'Succinylcholine binds nicotinic receptors and persistently depolarizes the motor end plate, producing visible fasciculations before receptor desensitization leads to flaccid paralysis. It does not inhibit acetylcholinesterase, block ACh release, or act as a purely competitive antagonist.'),
('b2f1a537-167b-5cc8-a562-c7458d97f8d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Eaton-Lambert syndrome is an autoimmune disease against:', '["Ryanodine receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Pre-synaptic voltage-gated calcium channels (VGCC) on the motor nerve terminal","Postsynaptic nAChRs (like MG) mediated by specific receptor activation","reducing ACh release without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AChE leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-synaptic voltage-gated calcium channels (VGCC) on the motor nerve terminal', 'Eaton-Lambert syndrome results from autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels on the motor nerve terminal, which reduce ACh release and cause weakness that paradoxically improves with repeated activity. Myasthenia gravis, by contrast, targets postsynaptic nAChRs.'),
('690cc6d8-1ef5-5a0f-8859-0c5d3ef22ce3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The MG test with edrophonium (Tensilon) involves:', '["Short-acting AChE inhibitor causing transient improvement in muscle strength","effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prolonged improvement mediated by specific receptor activation","Exacerbation of weakness a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cholinergic crisis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Short-acting AChE inhibitor causing transient improvement in muscle strength', 'Edrophonium is a short-acting acetylcholinesterase inhibitor; in myasthenia gravis it briefly raises ACh at the NMJ and produces a rapid but transient improvement in muscle strength. It does not cause prolonged improvement, and overactivity of the drug would produce cholinergic effects rather than sustained benefit.'),
('8734d86d-645e-5354-99b5-fd689ad0f3a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Cholinergic crisis (overdose of AChE inhibitors) causes:', '["leading to depolarization block (muscle weakness)","fasciculations","and muscarinic side effects (SLUDGE syndrome)","Excessive ACh at the NMJ","Improved strength"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive ACh at the NMJ', 'Overdosing an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor leaves excessive acetylcholine at the NMJ, causing overstimulation that leads to depolarization block, muscle weakness, fasciculations, and muscarinic side effects such as the SLUDGE syndrome. Muscle strength does not improve during a cholinergic crisis.'),
('a92e59af-2260-58f8-ae31-2e09fcb80761'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The NN (nicotinic neuronal) ACh receptor is found at:', '["The NMJ (skeletal muscle)","The SA node","Smooth muscle","The AV node","Autonomic ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic ganglia', 'Neuronal nicotinic (NN) receptors mediate fast transmission in autonomic ganglia, where both sympathetic and parasympathetic preganglionic fibers release ACh. The NMJ uses the NM subtype, and the SA node, AV node, and smooth muscle carry muscarinic receptors.'),
('10fd7759-540c-518f-bf88-0041be1c7d45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'M2 muscarinic receptors in the heart mediate:', '["Bronchodilation a resulting in decreased functional activity","Negative chronotropic and dromotropic effects (slowing heart rate and AV conduction)","Vasodilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased contractility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tachycardia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Negative chronotropic and dromotropic effects (slowing heart rate and AV conduction)', 'Cardiac M2 receptors respond to vagal ACh by slowing SA node firing (negative chronotropy) and AV conduction (negative dromotropy). They do not increase contractility, produce tachycardia, or cause bronchodilation or vasodilation.'),
('a6c1ac56-8a1d-5d47-8485-d350899577a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'α2-Adrenergic receptors are located:', '["Cardiac muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-synaptically on smooth muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GI smooth muscle a mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchial smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pre-synaptically on sympathetic nerve terminals (inhibit norepinephrine release) and on platelets (aggregation)"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-synaptically on sympathetic nerve terminals (inhibit norepinephrine release) and on platelets (aggregation)', 'α2-adrenergic receptors sit presynaptically on sympathetic nerve terminals, where they inhibit norepinephrine release, and on platelets, where they promote aggregation. Cardiac muscle, bronchial muscle, and most vascular smooth muscle rely mainly on β or α1 receptors rather than presynaptic α2.'),
('ac56fee9-f592-5105-92d9-6a23b7dff945'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Atropine is a competitive antagonist of:', '["α-adrenergic receptors","Histamine receptors a","β-adrenergic receptors (alternative wording)","Nicotinic ACh receptors","Muscarinic ACh receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Muscarinic ACh receptors', 'Atropine competitively blocks muscarinic ACh receptors, abolishing parasympathetic effects such as bradycardia, bronchoconstriction, and excess salivation. It has little or no action on nicotinic or adrenergic receptors.'),
('bb571c90-6808-5038-94ec-ffae72622411'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The main mechanism for increased sympathetic activity during exercise is:', '["Only central command resulting in decreased functional activity","Only muscle reflexes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Central command (cortical activation) and the exercise pressor reflex (feedback from muscle)","Only baroreflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only chemoreflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central command (cortical activation) and the exercise pressor reflex (feedback from muscle)', 'During exercise sympathetic outflow rises mainly through central command, the cortical activation of cardiovascular centers, together with the exercise pressor reflex driven by mechano- and metaboreceptors in contracting muscle. No single mechanism such as the baroreflex or chemoreflex accounts for the response by itself.'),
('51e52d92-7171-516b-98cf-151416f6f00e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The diving reflex (bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction during breath-hold in water) is mediated by:', '["reflex resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal nerve activation (cold water on face) and vagal outflow to the heart","Baroreflex mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased sympathetic activity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carotid body activation (hypoxia only) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve activation (cold water on face) and vagal outflow to the heart', 'Cold water on the face activates the trigeminal nerve, which triggers vagal outflow to the heart causing bradycardia and reflex peripheral vasoconstriction that conserves oxygen. It is not driven by the baroreflex, heightened sympathetic activity, or chemoreflexes alone.'),
('5f42eed7-35a5-5d7c-89e3-c252d9547413'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The Valsalva maneuver (forced expiration against a closed glottis) produces:', '["Increased BP resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased intrathoracic pressure (compressing the heart and reducing venous return → decreased BP)","effect a mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased heart rate only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased venous return leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intrathoracic pressure (compressing the heart and reducing venous return → decreased BP)', 'Forced expiration against a closed glottis raises intrathoracic pressure, which compresses the heart and reduces venous return, so blood pressure initially falls. Venous return is therefore decreased rather than increased, and the maneuver does not simply raise BP or heart rate.'),
('fce59945-1cf6-5551-8bbe-4539a1335839'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Muscle pain (myalgia) can be caused by:', '["infection (viral myositis)","overuse","electrolyte disturbances","Trauma","medication (statins)"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma', 'Direct trauma injures muscle fibers and surrounding connective tissue, producing localized pain and tenderness and making it a common cause of myalgia. Infection, overuse, electrolyte disturbances, and statins are also recognized causes, but trauma most directly and mechanically injures the muscle in this set of options.'),
('fcade489-135d-59e3-b041-92d679daf7e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The most common cause of hyperCKemia (elevated creatine kinase) is:', '["Heart attack resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Strenuous exercise (physiological elevation)","Renal failure a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stroke leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Strenuous exercise (physiological elevation)', 'Vigorous or unaccustomed exercise is the most common cause of an elevated creatine kinase, producing a mild, transient, physiological rise from microtrauma to muscle. Heart attack, muscle disease, and stroke can elevate CK, but they are encountered far less often than exercise-related elevation.'),
('6bd9b0e3-2961-5284-a46d-d592480f882a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The serum CK level in Duchenne muscular dystrophy is:', '["Normal resulting in decreased functional activity","Variable a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Slightly elevated mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Markedly elevated (10-100 times normal) in the early stages of the disease"]'::jsonb, 'Markedly elevated (10-100 times normal) in the early stages of the disease', 'In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, ongoing muscle fiber necrosis rapidly releases creatine kinase, so levels are markedly elevated (often 10-100 times normal) early in the disease. Levels decline in advanced stages as muscle mass is lost, so a normal or decreased value does not characterize the early disease.'),
('cd4fe249-f45f-5a34-83f8-e51fdf1d20b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The term EMG (electromyography) records:', '["Electrical activity of muscle (motor unit action potentials) — helps distinguish myopathic from neurogenic disease","Muscle contraction force accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscle oxygenation mediated by specific receptor activation","Nerve conduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood flow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Electrical activity of muscle (motor unit action potentials) — helps distinguish myopathic from neurogenic disease', 'Electromyography records the electrical activity of muscle as motor unit action potentials, and its pattern helps separate myopathic from neurogenic disorders. It does not measure contraction force, muscle oxygenation, blood flow, or nerve conduction.'),
('f0aaebf1-9de6-5dab-8b13-7c7b0dc6d15c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Fibrillation potentials on EMG indicate:', '["Myopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Denervation (spontaneous activity of a single muscle fiber — indicator of muscle denervation)","Neuropathy a mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myotonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Denervation (spontaneous activity of a single muscle fiber — indicator of muscle denervation)', 'Fibrillation potentials are spontaneous single-fiber discharges that appear after lower motor neuron denervation, when the muscle fiber loses its nerve supply. Normal muscle, myopathic-only changes, and myotonia do not produce this pattern.'),
('03e4c7c5-8c99-533f-84c6-cda0a9a44e69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Positive sharp waves on EMG also indicate:', '["Reinnervation resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myotonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Myopathic disorders without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Denervation (spontaneous activity from a denervated muscle fiber)"]'::jsonb, 'Denervation (spontaneous activity from a denervated muscle fiber)', 'Positive sharp waves, like fibrillation potentials, are spontaneous discharges arising from denervated muscle fibers and are additional evidence of denervation. Reinnervation produces motor unit remodeling rather than these spontaneous potentials, and myotonia or normal muscle shows other patterns.'),
('f4a174c5-6881-5e1d-b5f6-91aa548bb90e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myotonic discharges on EMG produce a characteristic:', '["Single pops resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","sound accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Waxing and waning (crescendo-decrescendo) sound like a dive bomber or revving engine (due to repetitive APs)","Silence without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regular twitching leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Waxing and waning (crescendo-decrescendo) sound like a dive bomber or revving engine (due to repetitive APs)', 'Myotonic discharges are bursts of repetitive muscle fiber potentials whose amplitude and frequency wax and wane, producing the classic dive-bomber or revving-engine audio on EMG. Single pops, silence, or regular twitching do not describe this repetitive crescendo-decrescendo pattern.'),
('943de9e1-7638-5e18-b6cf-d712ce36afc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which type of muscle has the longest sarcomeres and the widest Z-discs?', '["Cardiac muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Skeletal has the shortest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All are equal mediated by specific receptor activation","Skeletal muscle (sarcomeres 2.5-3.0 um; Z-discs vary by fiber type)","Smooth muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal muscle (sarcomeres 2.5-3.0 um; Z-discs vary by fiber type)', 'Skeletal muscle sarcomeres, roughly 2.5-3.0 μm in length, are generally the longest of the muscle types, and Z-disc width varies with fiber type and functional demand. Cardiac sarcomeres are shorter and less regular, while smooth muscle has no Z-discs at all.'),
('19404ab9-6acd-5518-a79d-482fdd162629'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Cardiac muscle can be distinguished from skeletal muscle microscopically by:', '["Fusiform cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Multiple nuclei per cell accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","striations mediated by specific receptor activation","Intercalated discs, central nuclei, branching fibers, and gap junctions","Peripheral nuclei and branching leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intercalated discs, central nuclei, branching fibers, and gap junctions', 'Cardiac muscle cells are identified by intercalated discs, single central nuclei, and branching interconnected fibers joined by gap junctions. Skeletal muscle has long unbranched fibers with multiple peripheral nuclei, and fusiform cells are the hallmark of smooth muscle.'),
('4043c358-4dc6-5be4-997f-92b36152af7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The triad structure in skeletal muscle consists of:', '["2 T-tubules + 1 SR cisterna","1 T-tubule + 1 SR cisterna","1 T-tubule + 2 terminal cisternae of SR","1 SR + 2 T-tubules","3 T-tubules"]'::jsonb, '1 T-tubule + 2 terminal cisternae of SR', 'A triad is one T-tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, positioned at each A-I junction; this arrangement couples the surface action potential to calcium release. The two-element diad seen in cardiac muscle and other ratios are not triads.'),
('606f3090-5879-53b2-89fb-7f582937949e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which option best describes the three types of muscle tissue?', '["cardiac","Skeletal","None of the above","and smooth","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal', 'The three types of muscle tissue are skeletal, cardiac, and smooth; skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary, cardiac muscle is striated and involuntary, and smooth muscle is non-striated and involuntary. Among the options, ''Skeletal'' correctly names one of these three tissue types.'),
('a8a7c38c-6605-5658-9501-158dadaadad4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'What are the three types of muscle tissue? Which option is most accurate?', '["cardiac","Skeletal","None of the above","and smooth","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal', 'Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth muscle are the three types of muscle tissue; skeletal muscle is striated and under voluntary control. Of the listed choices, only ''Skeletal'' names one of these three correctly, whereas the other entries are incomplete fragments or not muscle tissue types.'),
('73792337-1348-5963-be8c-e11732b39bc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Muscular Tissue: what are the three types of muscle tissue?', '["cardiac","Skeletal","None of the above","and smooth","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal', 'The three muscle tissue types are skeletal, cardiac, and smooth; skeletal muscle is striated, voluntary, and attached to bones. ''Skeletal'' is the only option that correctly names one of these three types, since cardiac and smooth are listed as fragments, not as complete answers.'),
('c11bf624-5651-568d-a10f-b63e08517a12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'For Muscular Tissue, identify the correct response: what are the three types of muscle tissue?', '["cardiac","Skeletal","None of the above","and smooth","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal', 'Skeletal, cardiac, and smooth are the three types of muscle tissue; skeletal muscle is the striated voluntary type associated with the skeleton. Among the available choices, ''Skeletal'' is the only term that properly names one of the three types of muscle tissue.'),
('6b232ea5-01c8-5a0b-a26c-2908c656085e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'What are the three types of muscle tissue? Select the best answer.', '["cardiac","Skeletal","None of the above","and smooth","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal', 'The three muscle tissue types are skeletal, cardiac, and smooth; skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary, cardiac muscle is striated and involuntary, and smooth muscle is non-striated and involuntary. ''Skeletal'' is the only option among those listed that names a correct muscle tissue type.'),
('6ff874d7-93e2-527b-93e5-2490544f2ddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('f6f92a36-216d-59cd-93bb-eaa1b9d65c49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Skeletal muscle cells are also called . Which option is most accurate?', '["Myoblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Myocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcomeres mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long","multinucleated cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long', 'Skeletal muscle cells are elongated, multinucleated cells conventionally known as muscle fibers (myofibers). Myoblasts are embryonic precursors, myocytes are mature single cells, and sarcomeres are the contractile units within myofibrils, not names for the whole cell.'),
('2bcd09ee-99e7-5d97-9167-5b150985f60b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about muscular tissue: skeletal muscle cells are also called .', '["Myoblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Myocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcomeres mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long","multinucleated cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long', 'Long, multinucleated skeletal muscle cells are termed muscle fibers (myofibers). Myoblasts fuse to form them during development, and sarcomeres are repeating units within myofibrils rather than names of the cell itself.'),
('9c2727db-7c35-589a-8230-00d841a0ea5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'For muscular tissue, skeletal muscle cells are also called .', '["Myoblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Myocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcomeres mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long","multinucleated cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long', 'The mature skeletal muscle cell is called a muscle fiber (myofiber) because of its great length and multinucleation. Myocytes is a generic term for muscle cells, while myoblasts are their embryonic precursors and sarcomeres are intracellular contractile units.'),
('b3ea68a5-52be-5d09-af57-e41701477e2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Skeletal muscle cells are also called . Select the best answer.', '["Myoblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Myocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcomeres mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long","multinucleated cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long', 'Skeletal muscle cells are called muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are very long and contain many peripheral nuclei. Myoblasts give rise to these fibers during development, and sarcomeres are subunits of the myofibrils rather than names of the cells.'),
('b0593676-ccf5-5527-b35e-14d6440c5b97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this muscular tissue item: skeletal muscle cells are also called ?', '["Myoblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Myocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcomeres mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long","multinucleated cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle fibers (myofibers) because they are long', 'Individual skeletal muscle cells are elongated and multinucleated and are therefore known as muscle fibers (myofibers). Myoblasts are the fusing precursors, myocytes is a general term, and sarcomeres are contractile subunits inside the myofibril.'),
('b179502c-beb9-5b33-88d2-a427dd76d7fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcolemma is the . Which option is most accurate?', '["Nucleus of muscle","Connective tissue around muscle","Endoplasmic reticulum of muscle","Plasma membrane of a muscle cell","Sarcoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma membrane of a muscle cell', 'The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane that surrounds each muscle fiber and transmits the action potential into the fiber via T-tubules. The nucleus lies within the fiber, the sarcoplasm is its cytoplasm, and the connective tissue forms the surrounding epimysium, perimysium, and endomysium.'),
('52955dfa-f8a3-5afe-bf84-6a5a2817c88a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about muscular tissue: the sarcolemma is the .', '["Nucleus of muscle","Connective tissue around muscle","Endoplasmic reticulum of muscle","Plasma membrane of a muscle cell","Sarcoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma membrane of a muscle cell', 'The sarcolemma is the specialized plasma membrane of a muscle cell. The endoplasmic reticulum of muscle is instead called the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and the connective tissue investing muscle is separate from the cell membrane.'),
('f5b17d09-f409-5ce8-9f84-cee494d27983'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'For muscular tissue, the sarcolemma is the .', '["Nucleus of muscle","Connective tissue around muscle","Endoplasmic reticulum of muscle","Plasma membrane of a muscle cell","Sarcoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma membrane of a muscle cell', 'The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a skeletal muscle fiber, lying just beneath the basal lamina. Sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm within the fiber, and the sarcoplasmic reticulum (endoplasmic reticulum) is the intracellular calcium store.'),
('52273e89-5cb6-5104-800d-dbc50e257499'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcolemma is the . Select the best answer.', '["Nucleus of muscle","Connective tissue around muscle","Endoplasmic reticulum of muscle","Plasma membrane of a muscle cell","Sarcoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma membrane of a muscle cell', 'The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane enveloping the muscle fiber and is continuous with the T-tubules at the Z-discs. The sarcoplasm, nucleus, and connective tissue all lie inside or outside of this membrane rather than forming it.'),
('9ca8745e-aa04-5229-900e-a799ee635ca5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this muscular tissue item: the sarcolemma is the ?', '["Nucleus of muscle","Connective tissue around muscle","Endoplasmic reticulum of muscle","Plasma membrane of a muscle cell","Sarcoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma membrane of a muscle cell', 'The sarcolemma is the plasma membrane of a muscle cell, which conducts the action potential along the cell surface and into the T-tubule system. It does not represent the nucleus, the cytoplasm (sarcoplasm), or the connective tissue sheath around the muscle.'),
('6645fe0e-dfd6-5833-82db-9e70992e5364'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcoplasm is the . Which option is most accurate?', '["Cytoplasm of a muscle cell","Nucleus","Glycogen","Myofibrils a","Mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Cytoplasm of a muscle cell', 'Sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of a muscle fiber and contains the myofibrils, glycogen stores, mitochondria, and the sarcoplasmic reticulum. The nucleus, myofibrils, glycogen, and mitochondria are separate structures within this cytoplasm.'),
('43511d48-2872-5c7d-a168-ed52bf89c7dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about muscular tissue: the sarcoplasm is the .', '["Cytoplasm of a muscle cell","Nucleus","Glycogen","Myofibrils a","Mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Cytoplasm of a muscle cell', 'Sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of a muscle fiber, providing the environment for myofibrils and energy stores such as glycogen. Nucleus, myofibrils, and mitochondria are organelles found inside the sarcoplasm, not the sarcoplasm itself.'),
('1eb3813f-17c8-5d1c-a1e8-71147c3cfc04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'For muscular tissue, the sarcoplasm is the .', '["Cytoplasm of a muscle cell","Nucleus","Glycogen","Myofibrils a","Mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Cytoplasm of a muscle cell', 'Sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of a muscle cell, in which myofibrils, glycogen, lipid droplets, and many mitochondria are suspended. The nucleus and myofibrils are contents of the sarcoplasm rather than its definition.'),
('116ef992-4184-5e29-83b1-1ae6f50d1525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcoplasm is the . Select the best answer.', '["Cytoplasm of a muscle cell","Nucleus","Glycogen","Myofibrils a","Mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Cytoplasm of a muscle cell', 'The sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of the muscle fiber, rich in myofibrils, glycogen, and mitochondria. Each of the other options names either an organelle or a stored product within the sarcoplasm.'),
('27717377-cf05-5c5f-b752-9f0127654793'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this muscular tissue item: the sarcoplasm is the ?', '["Cytoplasm of a muscle cell","Nucleus","Glycogen","Myofibrils a","Mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Cytoplasm of a muscle cell', 'Sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of a muscle fiber, containing the myofibrils and the energy reserves (glycogen and lipids) of the cell. Nucleus, myofibrils, glycogen, and mitochondria are all components found within the sarcoplasm.'),
('fcd39d46-1d2f-5aa1-b07e-86a6e670cbb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myofibrils are composed of . Which option is most accurate?', '["Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end","Myosin only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Actin only mediated by specific receptor activation","T-tubules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth ER leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end', 'A myofibril is a linear chain of sarcomeres lying end to end along the length of the fiber. Both actin and myosin are present within the sarcomeres, so options naming only one filament protein, T-tubules, or smooth ER are incomplete.'),
('bb35ed60-59a8-505d-ba1e-af65e1f8d025'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about muscular tissue: myofibrils are composed of .', '["Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end","Myosin only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Actin only mediated by specific receptor activation","T-tubules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth ER leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end', 'Myofibrils are formed by sarcomeres joined end to end, giving skeletal muscle its striated appearance. Each sarcomere contains both thick (myosin) and thin (actin) filaments, while T-tubules and smooth ER are separate sarcoplasmic structures.'),
('45462e1d-4291-5d59-9cdb-ae685f4dd7ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'For muscular tissue, myofibrils are composed of .', '["Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end","Myosin only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Actin only mediated by specific receptor activation","T-tubules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth ER leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end', 'Myofibrils consist of serial sarcomeres, the repeating contractile units of striated muscle. Since thick and thin filaments both contribute to a sarcomere, answers naming only myosin or actin fail to describe a complete myofibril.'),
('2aa14ced-47b8-5f2b-af4d-9f32f5922dbf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Myofibrils are composed of . Select the best answer.', '["Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end","Myosin only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Actin only mediated by specific receptor activation","T-tubules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth ER leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end', 'Myofibrils are chains of sarcomeres arranged end to end between two Z-discs. The presence of both actin and myosin filament systems in each sarcomere rules out the single-protein and membrane-based options.'),
('edf8fe41-1123-5ceb-9419-ea2270429c16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this muscular tissue item: myofibrils are composed of ?', '["Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end","Myosin only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Actin only mediated by specific receptor activation","T-tubules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth ER leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcomeres arranged end-to-end', 'Each myofibril is a series of sarcomeres stacked end to end along the fiber''s long axis. T-tubules and smooth (sarcoplasmic) endoplasmic reticulum surround the myofibrils but do not compose them.'),
('c305e325-7fc5-5c6c-af2f-2206d949e479'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcomere is defined as the segment between . Which option is most accurate?', '["Z-discs","M-lines","H-zones","I-bands","A-bands"]'::jsonb, 'Z-discs', 'The sarcomere is the contractile unit extending from one Z-disc to the next Z-disc. The M-line, H-zone, I-bands, and A-bands are internal landmarks of this segment rather than its boundaries.'),
('bd900e8e-e4fd-5eff-a6ba-ff15aec05dea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about muscular tissue: the sarcomere is defined as the segment between .', '["Z-discs","M-lines","H-zones","I-bands","A-bands"]'::jsonb, 'Z-discs', 'A sarcomere is the portion of the myofibril between two adjacent Z-discs. Its internal zones (A-band, I-band, H-zone, M-line) are defined relative to this unit and are not its outer limits.'),
('960c229e-4bd3-5eed-bbad-5c02798a4065'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'For muscular tissue, the sarcomere is defined as the segment between .', '["Z-discs","M-lines","H-zones","I-bands","A-bands"]'::jsonb, 'Z-discs', 'The sarcomere spans from one Z-disc to the next and is the repeating functional unit of the myofibril. The M-line, H-zone, I-bands, and A-bands lie within this segment, so none of them defines its boundaries.'),
('040c5d9e-a0e7-5aca-9ad6-2d2b3fac8dad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The sarcomere is defined as the segment between . Select the best answer.', '["Z-discs","M-lines","H-zones","I-bands","A-bands"]'::jsonb, 'Z-discs', 'The sarcomere lies between two consecutive Z-discs, where the thin filaments are anchored. The I-band, A-band, H-zone, and M-line are subdivisions of this segment, each located inside its borders.'),
('0a625e17-9de0-5429-8c7f-50e0533a80d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this muscular tissue item: the sarcomere is defined as the segment between ?', '["Z-discs","M-lines","H-zones","I-bands","A-bands"]'::jsonb, 'Z-discs', 'The sarcomere is the repeating unit of the myofibril bounded by two Z-discs. The M-line, H-zone, and the I- and A-bands all lie within those boundaries and therefore cannot be the bounding structures.'),
('8f484e30-d4dd-5bdd-885e-4d11a202c0c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which option correctly describes the Z-disc (Z-line)?', '["The center of the A-band","The region of myosin only","The boundary of the H-zone","The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)","The region of actin only"]'::jsonb, 'The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)', 'The Z-disc anchors the thin (actin) filaments and marks the junction between adjacent sarcomeres. The center of the A-band is occupied by myosin, and neither the myosin-only nor the actin-only regions describe the Z-disc.'),
('71c4716b-e443-59bd-8d4f-e4873ca3e024'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The Z-disc (Z-line) is the . Which option is most accurate?', '["The center of the A-band","The region of myosin only","The boundary of the H-zone","The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)","The region of actin only"]'::jsonb, 'The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)', 'The Z-disc is the plate-like structure where thin (actin) filaments are anchored and where alpha-actinin cross-links them. It is not the center of the A-band nor a region restricted to myosin filaments.'),
('d8283e4c-20c9-504d-af56-078804b74b1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about muscular tissue: the Z-disc (Z-line) is the .', '["The center of the A-band","The region of myosin only","The boundary of the H-zone","The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)","The region of actin only"]'::jsonb, 'The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)', 'The Z-disc secures the thin (actin) filaments of the sarcomere and serves as the lateral boundary of the unit. The A-band and H-zone are myosin-based regions, and the actin-only region does not describe the Z-disc.'),
('ce34b837-5299-546c-8e01-cc0d8dba5d21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'For muscular tissue, the Z-disc (Z-line) is the .', '["The center of the A-band","The region of myosin only","The boundary of the H-zone","The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)","The region of actin only"]'::jsonb, 'The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)', 'The Z-disc is the attachment site for thin (actin) filaments and keeps adjacent sarcomeres in alignment. In contrast, the A-band and H-zone are dominated by myosin thick filaments.'),
('71f12fbc-ada6-5a0d-b644-53388e74363f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The Z-disc (Z-line) is the . Select the best answer.', '["The center of the A-band","The region of myosin only","The boundary of the H-zone","The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)","The region of actin only"]'::jsonb, 'The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)', 'At each Z-disc, the thin (actin) filaments of neighboring sarcomeres are anchored together. The A-band is the region of overlapping thick filaments, so neither the center of the A-band nor the H-zone describe the Z-disc.'),
('1650d4c5-5191-5805-8f20-25cf9c4329ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this muscular tissue item: the Z-disc (Z-line) is the ?', '["The center of the A-band","The region of myosin only","The boundary of the H-zone","The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)","The region of actin only"]'::jsonb, 'The attachment site for thin filaments (actin)', 'The Z-disc is the sarcomeric structure to which thin (actin) filaments attach. Myosin-only and actin-only regions belong to the A-band or I-band organization and do not define the Z-disc.'),
('d409aa72-4491-5340-b596-c6c4fa19897a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which option correctly describes the H-zone (H-band)?', '["The M-line resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)","The region with both actin and myosin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The I-band leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)', 'The H-zone is the middle of the A-band where thick (myosin) filaments exist without overlapping actin, so it appears lighter on electron microscopy. It is not the M-line, Z-disc, or I-band, and the overlapping region is the rest of the A-band.'),
('0432d17c-4c48-507f-abfa-8b1f7811ee7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The H-zone (H-band) is . Which option is most accurate?', '["The M-line resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)","The region with both actin and myosin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The I-band leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)', 'The H-zone is the central portion of the A-band occupied only by thick (myosin) filaments, with no actin overlap. It shortens during contraction as thin filaments slide toward the M-line, and it is never the Z-disc, M-line, or I-band.'),
('6d860839-4b51-52d1-8fef-e8bbefd14abe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about muscular tissue: the H-zone (H-band) is .', '["The M-line resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)","The region with both actin and myosin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The I-band leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)', 'The H-zone sits at the center of the A-band and contains only thick (myosin) filaments because thin filaments do not reach that far. The I-band and Z-disc lie outside the A-band, and the M-line is the thin line crossing the middle of the H-zone.'),
('afa62206-8902-59ce-b92a-6dfa3544bac0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'For muscular tissue, the H-zone (H-band) is .', '["The M-line resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)","The region with both actin and myosin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The I-band leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)', 'The H-zone is the light central zone of the A-band where myosin thick filaments are present without overlapping actin. As the muscle contracts the H-zone narrows, while the I-band shortens alongside it.'),
('42fb52d1-fbcf-5afb-928e-753f45b8396d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The H-zone (H-band) is . Select the best answer.', '["The M-line resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)","The region with both actin and myosin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The I-band leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)', 'The H-zone is the central region of the A-band containing only thick (myosin) filaments, with no actin overlap. Neither the M-line, the Z-disc, nor the I-band is the dark, myosin-filled part of the sarcomere.'),
('e3ff1229-c09f-597b-9629-2ff1fc551f0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this muscular tissue item: the H-zone (H-band) is ?', '["The M-line resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)","The region with both actin and myosin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The I-band leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central part of the A-band where only thick filaments (myosin) are present (no actin overlap)', 'The H-zone is the myosin-only middle of the A-band, where actin thin filaments do not overlap. It lies between the two overlapping portions of the A-band and is crossed centrally by the M-line.'),
('be5af170-061a-5b80-96c3-f48ca64c300b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which option correctly describes the M-line?', '["The binding site for actin resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The region of titin attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","The I-band center a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin"]'::jsonb, 'The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin', 'The M-line is the thin dark line at the center of the H-zone where M-protein and myomesin cross-link and stabilize the myosin filaments. It is not a site of actin binding, aZ-disc, or a titin attachment region.'),
('9f9f91bd-e03c-5fbd-82da-7cfd39270013'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The M-line is . Which option is most accurate?', '["The binding site for actin resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The region of titin attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","The I-band center a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin"]'::jsonb, 'The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin', 'The M-line lies at the center of the H-zone and holds myosin thick filaments in register through M-protein and myomesin cross-links. Actin binds at the Z-disc and in the A-band overlap, not at the M-line.'),
('c138f1ed-8cfc-578e-a068-f8b145389b34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about muscular tissue: the M-line is .', '["The binding site for actin resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The region of titin attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","The I-band center a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin"]'::jsonb, 'The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin', 'The M-line crosses the middle of the H-zone, where myosin filaments are joined by M-protein and myomesin. It is distinct from the Z-disc (actin anchoring) and is not the site of actin or titin attachment.'),
('fa98b3da-37d1-58f1-8d43-0aa9772698f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'For muscular tissue, the M-line is .', '["The binding site for actin resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The region of titin attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","The I-band center a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin"]'::jsonb, 'The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin', 'The M-line is the central line of the H-zone at which myosin thick filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin, stabilizing the filament array. The I-band, Z-disc line, and actin-binding site are separate sarcomeric landmarks.'),
('1965977a-54d1-5c62-85d4-160775979a62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'The M-line is . Select the best answer.', '["The binding site for actin resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The region of titin attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","The I-band center a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin"]'::jsonb, 'The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin', 'The M-line sits in the center of the H-zone and connects myosin filaments via M-protein and myomesin. Actin binding occurs at the Z-discs and in the cross-bridge region of the A-band, not at the M-line.'),
('5e06362a-9d9b-5598-af2c-599c6a1451d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this muscular tissue item: the M-line is ?', '["The binding site for actin resulting in decreased functional activity","The Z-disc line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The region of titin attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","The I-band center a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin"]'::jsonb, 'The central line in the H-zone where myosin filaments are cross-linked by M-protein and myomesin', 'The M-line is the central structure of the H-zone where M-protein and myomesin cross-link adjacent myosin filaments to keep them aligned. It is neither the Z-disc nor a region of actin binding.'),
('b99e8164-f2aa-5e8d-a286-0d4b446d2ecb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Thick filaments of the sarcomere are composed of . Which option is most accurate?', '["Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)","Actin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Titin mediated by specific receptor activation","Troponin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tropomyosin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)', 'The thick filaments of striated muscle are built from myosin II, a hexamer containing two heavy chains and four light chains. Actin, troponin, tropomyosin, and titin belong to the thin filament or elastic systems, not the thick filament.'),
('fc6984b9-9893-5c5f-927b-bab415141d60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about muscular tissue: thick filaments of the sarcomere are composed of .', '["Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)","Actin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Titin mediated by specific receptor activation","Troponin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tropomyosin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)', 'Thick filaments are polymers of myosin II, whose molecules have two globular heads and a rod-like tail. Actin is the core of the thin filament, and troponin, tropomyosin, and titin are accessory or elastic proteins.'),
('7bb56d3f-16cc-5615-8a04-66ee5753f858'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'For muscular tissue, thick filaments of the sarcomere are composed of .', '["Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)","Actin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Titin mediated by specific receptor activation","Troponin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tropomyosin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)', 'The sarcomere''s thick filaments consist of myosin II molecules, each a hexamer of two heavy chains and four light chains. Actin, troponin, tropomyosin, and titin are not constituents of the thick filament.'),
('79e715b3-32ee-5e7e-b20e-52ca5b3c3d5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Thick filaments of the sarcomere are composed of . Select the best answer.', '["Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)","Actin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Titin mediated by specific receptor activation","Troponin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tropomyosin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)', 'Thick filaments are formed by myosin II, the hexameric motor protein with two heavy chains and four light chains. The thin filament, by contrast, is built around actin with troponin and tropomyosin in its grooves.'),
('4f2b5bd7-e09c-5206-987f-8e391eae70fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this muscular tissue item: thick filaments of the sarcomere are composed of ?', '["Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)","Actin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Titin mediated by specific receptor activation","Troponin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tropomyosin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myosin II (a hexamer of 2 heavy chains and 4 light chains)', 'Each thick filament is composed of myosin II molecules, a hexamer of two heavy chains and four light chains whose heads form the cross-bridges. The other proteins listed act on the thin filament or on filament stabilization rather than forming the thick filament.'),
('b9562033-8fa4-5586-b919-7c2d105fd7a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Muscular Tissue', 'Tropomyosin is a . Which option is most accurate?', '["Myosin-binding protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Actin-binding protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcium-binding protein mediated by specific receptor activation","Long without affecting the primary tissue pathway","rod-shaped molecule that lies in the groove of the actin helix and blocks the myosin-binding site in relaxed muscle"]'::jsonb, 'rod-shaped molecule that lies in the groove of the actin helix and blocks the myosin-binding site in relaxed muscle', 'Tropomyosin is a long, rod-shaped protein that winds through the grooves of the actin double helix and, at rest, covers the myosin-binding sites on actin. When calcium binds troponin C, tropomyosin shifts to expose these sites so cross-bridges can form.'),
('eda91492-2c1a-5af0-b54b-53b11ada3c3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The fundamental structural and functional unit of the nervous system is the:', '["Dendrite","Axon","Neuroglia","Glial cell","Neuron"]'::jsonb, 'Neuron', 'Neurons are the excitable cells that generate and transmit nerve impulses, making them the fundamental unit of the nervous system. Dendrites and axons are structural processes of a neuron, while neuroglia (glial cells) support and protect neurons rather than signal.'),
('ec4783e6-03db-5227-8ea2-0018aa06dd59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The three main parts of a neuron are:', '["axon","None of the above","Cell body (soma)","Nucleus","and dendrites"]'::jsonb, 'Cell body (soma)', 'The three main parts of a neuron are the cell body (soma), dendrites, and axon. The cell body houses the nucleus and organelles and is the metabolic center of the cell; neurons always have a soma, whereas nuclei, dendrites, and axons are components of specific parts.'),
('02c79ac3-dcb5-5133-a5c9-16a8b701add3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The cell body (soma) of a neuron contains:', '["Golgi resulting in decreased functional activity","nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nissl bodies (RER) mediated by specific receptor activation","mitochondria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A large nucleus with prominent nucleolus"]'::jsonb, 'A large nucleus with prominent nucleolus', 'Although the soma contains the nucleus, RER (Nissl bodies), Golgi apparatus, and mitochondria, its most characteristic and prominent feature is a large central nucleus with a prominent nucleolus, reflecting a high level of protein synthesis and transcription.'),
('38fec04f-0aad-5980-85d0-5ba5903a542c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Nissl bodies are aggregates of:', '["Nuclear membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","Rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) and free ribosomes (stain basophilic)","Golgi apparatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth ER without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mitochondria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) and free ribosomes (stain basophilic)', 'Nissl bodies are clumps of rough endoplasmic reticulum and free ribosomes that stain basophilic (e.g., cresyl violet) because of their RNA content. They are prominent in the soma and dendrites but absent from the axon and axon hillock; they are not derived from Golgi, smooth ER, or mitochondria.'),
('eba26433-d421-5068-abee-c48310f8aa80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Nissl bodies are absent in which part of the neuron?', '["Dendritic spines","Soma","Axon and axon hillock","Dendrites","Terminal boutons"]'::jsonb, 'Axon and axon hillock', 'Nissl substance extends into the dendrites and soma but stops at the axon hillock, so the axon and its hillock contain no Nissl bodies. This regional distribution is why the axon hillock appears pale with Nissl stains, despite being rich in voltage-gated sodium channels.'),
('2cdd3bb2-54f4-5915-9dee-a5697c903550'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Dendrites are processes that:', '["Transmit signals away from the soma resulting in decreased functional activity","Form myelin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Provide structural support mediated by specific receptor activation","Release neurotransmitters without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Receive signals from other neurons (afferent inputs) and conduct impulses toward the soma"]'::jsonb, 'Receive signals from other neurons (afferent inputs) and conduct impulses toward the soma', 'Dendrites receive incoming (afferent) signals from other neurons and conduct them toward the soma as graded potentials. Transmitting signals away from the soma, releasing neurotransmitters, and forming myelin are functions of the axon and glial cells.'),
('32f814df-82f9-53af-a114-69db32f4952f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Dendritic spines are:', '["The cell body resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The axon terminal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The axon hillock mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Gap junctions without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small protrusions on dendrites that receive excitatory (usually) synaptic input and are sites of synaptic plasticity"]'::jsonb, 'Small protrusions on dendrites that receive excitatory (usually) synaptic input and are sites of synaptic plasticity', 'Dendritic spines are small actin-rich protrusions on the dendritic surface that receive mostly excitatory (glutamatergic) synaptic input and undergo activity-dependent strengthening or weakening (synaptic plasticity). They are not part of the soma, axon, or specialized junctions.'),
('5037cb74-67e4-5914-82eb-40b615972034'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The axon is a long process that:', '["Contains Nissl bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Receives incoming signals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Does not contain neurofilaments mediated by specific receptor activation","Is myelinated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal"]'::jsonb, 'Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal', 'The axon is a single long process that conducts action potentials away from the soma toward its presynaptic terminals, where neurotransmitter is released. Axons lack Nissl bodies and rough ER, and although many axons are myelinated, not every axon is.'),
('440ee45c-cf30-5e09-901a-552894ab63e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The axon hillock is the:', '["The tip of the axon resulting in decreased functional activity","Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)","The cell nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","The dendritic branching point without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synaptic ending leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)', 'The axon hillock is the cone-shaped origin of the axon at the soma and is the usual site where action potentials are initiated because its membrane is densely packed with voltage-gated sodium channels. It is not the axon tip, nucleus, dendrite, or synapse.'),
('d71855df-a2c7-55ef-ae5d-5101f785aaaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The terminal bouton (synaptic knob) is the:', '["Dendrite resulting in decreased functional activity","Synaptic cleft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axon hillock mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell","Cell body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell', 'The terminal bouton, or synaptic knob, is the expanded distal end of the axon that forms the presynaptic element of a chemical synapse, releasing neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft. It is not the dendrite, the cleft itself, the hillock, or the soma.'),
('a999bd5b-20f3-5e42-b807-b220098706a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Myelin in the peripheral nervous system (PNS) is produced by:', '["Astrocytes","Schwann cells","Microglia","Ependymal cells a","Oligodendrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Schwann cells', 'Schwann cells wrap around PNS axons to form myelin sheaths, with one Schwann cell investing one internode of a single axon. In the CNS, myelination is carried out by oligodendrocytes, while astrocytes, microglia, and ependymal cells do not form myelin.'),
('5e76ba70-27f1-571e-8e14-e48cba36bfca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Myelin in the central nervous system (CNS) is produced by:', '["Oligodendrocytes","Astrocytes","Ependymal cells","Microglia","Schwann cells"]'::jsonb, 'Oligodendrocytes', 'Oligodendrocytes generate and maintain myelin in the CNS; a single oligodendrocyte can myelinate segments of many different axons. Schwann cells perform this role only in the PNS, and the other listed cells are not myelinating cells.'),
('1d0d6ca7-ea5b-5519-90e0-68d2c559abea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The node of Ranvier is:', '["The nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","The myelinated region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)","The cell body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synapse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)', 'Nodes of Ranvier are the short bare gaps between successive myelin segments where voltage-gated sodium channels are concentrated, allowing the action potential to be regenerated by saltatory conduction. They are not regions of myelin, the nucleus, soma, or synapses.'),
('77be2af7-56b7-514e-8390-f843bcc47406'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The fastest conducting fibers in the human body are:', '["Type Aδ (small myelinated) resulting in decreased functional activity","Type C (unmyelinated) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter","Type B (autonomic preganglionic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","heavily myelinated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter', 'Type Aα fibers have the largest diameter and heaviest myelination, giving conduction velocities of roughly 70-120 m/s, the fastest of all fiber types. Type Aδ, B, and C fibers conduct progressively more slowly because of smaller diameter or lack of myelin.'),
('b0270246-3101-5895-927e-c8184ab37c82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The slowest conducting fibers are:', '["Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s","Type Aγ","Type Aα (alternative wording)","Type Aδ a","Type Aβ (alternative wording) (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, 'Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s', 'Type C fibers are small-diameter unmyelinated fibers conducting at only about 0.5-2 m/s, making them the slowest in the body; they carry slow pain and autonomic postganglionic signals. All type A fibers are myelinated and therefore faster.'),
('16ac143c-a9a8-5826-9ea6-5aaaa7779db8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Microtubules in the axon are oriented with their plus ends:', '["Without orientation resulting in decreased functional activity","Toward the soma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Toward both directions mediated by specific receptor activation","Randomly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)"]'::jsonb, 'Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)', 'Axonal microtubules are uniformly polarized: their plus ends point toward the axon terminal and their minus ends toward the soma. This polarity directs kinesin-driven anterograde transport toward the terminal and dynein-driven retrograde transport back to the soma.'),
('2a94cab5-3cb5-5552-95ce-7fa241178e4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Anterograde axonal transport (from soma to terminal) is mediated by:', '["Dynein resulting in decreased functional activity","Dynactin a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Kinesin (a motor protein that walks toward the plus end of microtubules)","Actin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myosin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Kinesin (a motor protein that walks toward the plus end of microtubules)', 'Kinesin is the motor protein that moves cargo toward the plus end of microtubules, carrying vesicles and organelles from the soma to the terminal (anterograde transport). Dynein moves cargo toward the minus end (retrograde transport), and actin/myosin are not the primary axonal transporters.'),
('9b90dcc6-0bb8-5875-a2ff-92c334f56a97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Retrograde axonal transport (from terminal to soma) is mediated by:', '["Kinesin resulting in decreased functional activity","Actin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myosin mediated by specific receptor activation","Keratin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cytoplasmic dynein (walks toward the minus end of microtubules — toward the cell body)"]'::jsonb, 'Cytoplasmic dynein (walks toward the minus end of microtubules — toward the cell body)', 'Cytoplasmic dynein is the motor protein that walks toward the minus end of microtubules, moving vesicles and organelles from the axon terminal back to the soma (retrograde transport). Kinesin transports cargo in the opposite, anterograde direction, while actin, myosin, and keratin are not the axonal cargo motors.'),
('01a2acf8-a36b-5f42-a1ec-a858bdd696f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The fastest component of axonal transport carries:', '["Cytoskeletal proteins (slow transport) resulting in decreased functional activity","Soluble proteins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vesicles and membrane-bound organelles (fast transport at 200-400 mm/day using kinesin/dynein)","Neurotransmitters a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RNA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vesicles and membrane-bound organelles (fast transport at 200-400 mm/day using kinesin/dynein)', 'Fast axonal transport moves vesicles and membrane-bound organelles at 200-400 mm/day, using kinesin for anterograde and dynein for retrograde movement. Cytoskeletal and soluble proteins travel much more slowly via slow axonal transport.'),
('d2883bd2-44de-59c5-9ac9-2a5ad1394a9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Chemical synapses transmit signals via:', '["Mechanical coupling resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurotransmitters released from the presynaptic terminal that bind to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane","Direct fusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct electrical coupling through gap junctions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thermal coupling a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurotransmitters released from the presynaptic terminal that bind to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane', 'Chemical synapses release neurotransmitter from the presynaptic terminal into the synaptic cleft, where it binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane. Direct electrical coupling through gap junctions defines electrical synapses, not chemical ones.'),
('245c921b-e2b1-5834-bfc6-571380d922a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The synaptic cleft is the:', '["The space within the myelin sheath resulting in decreased functional activity","Narrow gap (20-50 nm) between the pre- and postsynaptic membranes across which neurotransmitters diffuse","The dendrite mediated by specific receptor activation","The gap at the node of Ranvier without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cell body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Narrow gap (20-50 nm) between the pre- and postsynaptic membranes across which neurotransmitters diffuse', 'The synaptic cleft is a narrow extracellular space of roughly 20-50 nm separating the pre- and postsynaptic membranes, across which neurotransmitter diffuses. It is distinct from the node of Ranvier and the intramyelinic spaces and is not a cellular compartment.'),
('b5620cc0-1988-5ba7-a589-da9f474e8baf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The SNARE complex proteins involved in synaptic vesicle exocytosis include:', '["Kinesin and dynein","Clathrin and adaptin","and SNAP-25 (target membrane)","VAMP/synaptobrevin (vesicle)","syntaxin"]'::jsonb, 'VAMP/synaptobrevin (vesicle)', 'The neuronal SNARE complex is formed by VAMP/synaptobrevin on the vesicle membrane plus syntaxin and SNAP-25 on the target plasma membrane; their pairing drives vesicle docking and fusion. Kinesin/dynein mediate transport and clathrin/adaptin mediate endocytosis rather than exocytosis.'),
('5533c168-b0b1-5a95-b9a0-65d9d68405ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The influx of which ion triggers exocytosis of synaptic vesicles?', '["Calcium (through voltage-gated Ca2+ channels)","Magnesium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chloride mediated by specific receptor activation","Potassium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sodium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium (through voltage-gated Ca2+ channels)', 'Depolarization of the presynaptic terminal opens voltage-gated calcium channels, and the influx of calcium triggers fusion of synaptic vesicles with the membrane and neurotransmitter exocytosis. Sodium and potassium mediate the action potential, but calcium is the direct trigger for release.'),
('e1989227-d30e-5acd-b3fa-0915844a049e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Electrical synapses are characterized by:', '["Gap junctions (connexons) that allow direct electrical and metabolic coupling between neurons (bidirectional and fast)","Postsynaptic receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neurotransmitter release mediated by specific receptor activation","Slow transmission a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Synaptic vesicles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gap junctions (connexons) that allow direct electrical and metabolic coupling between neurons (bidirectional and fast)', 'Electrical synapses consist of gap junctions formed by connexons that permit ions and small molecules to pass directly between cells, giving fast, bidirectional, essentially instantaneous coupling. Neurotransmitter release and synaptic vesicles are hallmarks of chemical, not electrical, synapses.'),
('d14da8c9-12af-5b84-98ea-5423e248c80d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The postsynaptic potential (PSP) can be:', '["or inhibitory (IPSP) — hyperpolarizing","an action potential","Excitatory (EPSP) — depolarizing","Only hyperpolarizing","Only depolarizing"]'::jsonb, 'Excitatory (EPSP) — depolarizing', 'Postsynaptic potentials are graded and may be excitatory (EPSP, depolarizing) or inhibitory (IPSP, hyperpolarizing), and their summation determines whether the postsynaptic neuron fires an action potential. They are not all-or-none and are not restricted to a single polarity.'),
('0f9b1f63-5064-58d5-acfc-cc5bd1fca638'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common excitatory neurotransmitter in the CNS is:', '["Glutamate","Dopamine a","Glycine","GABA","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate', 'Glutamate is the principal fast excitatory neurotransmitter throughout the CNS, acting mainly through AMPA and NMDA receptors. GABA and glycine are inhibitory, while dopamine and acetylcholine subserve other specific pathways rather than general excitation.'),
('ba61ea8f-91b0-5ab1-907b-7ea43aed083a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS is:', '["Glycine resulting in decreased functional activity","GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid)","Acetylcholine mediated by specific receptor activation","Glutamate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serotonin a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid)', 'GABA is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter of the adult CNS, acting at GABAA (ligand-gated chloride channel) and GABAB receptors to hyperpolarize neurons. Glycine is the inhibitory transmitter mainly of the spinal cord and brainstem, while glutamate is excitatory.'),
('986eb958-2fb6-5377-9ee0-a69e73c58f21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Glycine is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the:', '["Cerebellum","Cerebral cortex","Spinal cord and brainstem","Hippocampus a","Basal ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord and brainstem', 'Glycine is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter of the spinal cord and brainstem, where it acts on strychnine-sensitive glycine receptors to produce IPSPs. In the cerebellum, cortex, hippocampus, and basal ganglia, inhibition is mediated chiefly by GABA.'),
('d9ecc11c-372e-5503-9764-acd64c68014e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'GABA is synthesized from:', '["Serine by serine racemase","Histidine by histidine decarboxylase","Glutamine by glutaminase","Glutamate by glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD)","Tryptophan by tryptophan hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate by glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD)', 'GABA is formed from glutamate by glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD), which removes the alpha-carboxyl group. Serine racemase makes D-serine, histidine decarboxylase makes histamine, and tryptophan hydroxylase is the first step in serotonin synthesis.'),
('3827d77f-db73-5853-a2b8-e44e475d239a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Astrocytes are glial cells that:', '["contribute to the blood-brain barrier","regulate the extracellular environment","Provide structural support","and respond to injury","Produce myelin"]'::jsonb, 'Provide structural support', 'Astrocytes provide structural and metabolic support to neurons, help regulate the extracellular ionic environment and contribute to the blood-brain barrier through their end-feet, and respond to injury by proliferating (gliosis). They do not produce myelin, which is made by oligodendrocytes and Schwann cells.'),
('6405c711-499c-5f48-9eef-2555fe926e71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Gliosis is the:', '["Scar formation by fibroblasts a resulting in decreased functional activity","Myelination by oligodendrocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proliferation of microglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Proliferation and hypertrophy of astrocytes in response to CNS injury (reactive gliosis)","Formation of new neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proliferation and hypertrophy of astrocytes in response to CNS injury (reactive gliosis)', 'Reactive gliosis is the proliferation and hypertrophy of astrocytes that occurs after CNS injury, often with upregulation of intermediate filaments such as GFAP and formation of a glial scar. It is an astrocytic response, not primarily a microglial, fibroblastic, or neurogenic one.'),
('18a96cf2-bafb-5418-9420-07e200f095d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Oligodendrocytes are responsible for:', '["Producing CSF resulting in decreased functional activity","Forming the blood-brain barrier accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phagocytosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Myelinating CNS axons (one oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons)","Regulating blood flow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myelinating CNS axons (one oligodendrocyte myelinates multiple axons)', 'Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons, with a single cell ensheathing segments of several different axons. They do not produce CSF (ependymal cells), form the blood-brain barrier, or phagocytose debris (microglia).'),
('bbf36b2e-3024-5732-b887-271fb3a62edd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'A single oligodendrocyte can myelinate up to:', '["1 axon","5 axons","100 axons","10 axons a","50 CNS axons"]'::jsonb, '50 CNS axons', 'A single oligodendrocyte extends multiple processes and can myelinate up to roughly 50 CNS axons, in contrast to one Schwann cell, which invests only one internode of a single PNS axon. This ability to serve many axons is a defining feature of oligodendrocytes.'),
('9367a942-7ddd-52a6-a514-67963c31f478'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Schwann cells are responsible for:', '["Forming the blood-brain barrier resulting in decreased functional activity","Myelinating PNS axons (one Schwann cell myelin per one axon internode)","Myelinating CNS axons mediated by specific receptor activation","Producing CSF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phagocytosis in the PNS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myelinating PNS axons (one Schwann cell myelin per one axon internode)', 'Schwann cells form myelin around PNS axons, with one cell investing one internode of a single axon, and they guide regenerating axons after injury. Myelinating CNS axons is the role of oligodendrocytes, and the other functions belong to ependymal or phagocytic cells.'),
('2e4abe5c-d36a-51f4-9aa4-be02d8e39444'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Microglia are the:', '["CSF-producing cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myelin-producing cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Resident immune cells (macrophages) of the CNS — derived from yolk sac progenitors","Fibroblasts a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Resident immune cells (macrophages) of the CNS — derived from yolk sac progenitors', 'Microglia are the resident immune cells (tissue macrophages) of the CNS, derived from yolk sac-derived progenitors, and they respond to injury by proliferation, migration, and phagocytosis. They neither produce CSF, myelin, nor act as neurons or fibroblasts.'),
('435ff142-2978-596c-9e3c-e19ab440972b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Ependymal cells line the:', '["Cerebral cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricles of the brain and central canal of the spinal cord (cuboidal/columnar ciliated cells)","Synaptic clefts mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord white matter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricles of the brain and central canal of the spinal cord (cuboidal/columnar ciliated cells)', 'Ependymal cells are ciliated cuboidal or low columnar cells lining the ventricles of the brain and the central canal of the spinal cord; their cilia help circulate cerebrospinal fluid. The cerebral cortex, white matter, peripheral nerves, and synaptic clefts are not lined by ependyma.'),
('7b427032-4882-5a89-b208-a30cc470e52f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The blood-brain barrier (BBB) is formed primarily by:', '["Astrocytes alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Microglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericytes alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tight junctions between cerebral capillary endothelial cells (supported by pericytes and astrocyte end-feet)"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between cerebral capillary endothelial cells (supported by pericytes and astrocyte end-feet)', 'The blood-brain barrier is formed chiefly by tight junctions between cerebral capillary endothelial cells that seal the paracellular pathway; pericytes and astrocyte end-feet reinforce and modulate the barrier but do not alone constitute it. Neurons and microglia play no barrier role.'),
('a7137eae-f791-561d-8dda-896bf5fea025'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Astrocyte end-feet contribute to the BBB by:', '["Producing CSF resulting in decreased functional activity","Ensheathing brain capillaries and regulating BBB permeability and nutrient/ion transport","Myelinating axons mediated by specific receptor activation","Phagocytosing toxins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Forming tight junctions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ensheathing brain capillaries and regulating BBB permeability and nutrient/ion transport', 'Astrocyte end-feet wrap around the abluminal surface of brain capillaries, where they regulate barrier permeability and the exchange of water, ions, and nutrients. The tight junction seal itself is formed by endothelial cells, not by end-feet, which do not make CSF or myelin.'),
('38f45e3d-d9a4-5b47-a20c-420cefba8cc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The area postrema in the medulla is a:', '["Baroreceptor center resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) for vomiting — detects emetic substances in the blood and CSF","Respiratory center leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) for vomiting — detects emetic substances in the blood and CSF', 'The area postrema is a circumventricular organ lacking a blood-brain barrier that acts as the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) for vomiting, sensing emetic agents in the blood and CSF. It is not a baroreceptor, motor nucleus, or respiratory center.'),
('b9421ccb-c6f4-5863-95da-1c2ec4d5e193'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Nerve fibers in the PNS are surrounded by connective tissue sheaths. The endoneurium surrounds:', '["The perineurium resulting in decreased functional activity","Individual nerve fibers (axons and their Schwann cells)","The entire nerve (epineurium) mediated by specific receptor activation","The Schwann cell without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fascicles (bundles of nerve fibers) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Individual nerve fibers (axons and their Schwann cells)', 'The endoneurium is the delicate connective tissue layer surrounding each individual nerve fiber (an axon with its Schwann cell). The perineurium wraps each fascicle, and the epineurium encloses the entire nerve.'),
('2a0e7762-61bc-5fb5-b891-359394ef8b4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The perineurium surrounds:', '["Blood vessels in the nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The entire nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Individual axons mediated by specific receptor activation","Fascicles (bundles of nerve fibers) — it is a multilayered","metabolically active barrier leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fascicles (bundles of nerve fibers) — it is a multilayered', 'The perineurium is a multilayered, metabolically active sheath that surrounds each fascicle (bundle of nerve fibers) and contributes to the blood-nerve barrier. Individual fibers are invested by the endoneurium and the whole nerve by the epineurium.'),
('196fbd7a-6222-5322-a761-6231af68b967'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Wallerian degeneration is the:', '["Degeneration of the proximal portion resulting in decreased functional activity","Degeneration of the distal portion of a PNS axon after transection (anterograde degeneration)","Central chromatolysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Demyelination without axon loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Degeneration of the cell body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Degeneration of the distal portion of a PNS axon after transection (anterograde degeneration)', 'Wallerian degeneration is the anterograde degeneration of the distal portion of a PNS axon after it is severed, with breakdown of the axon and myelin followed by phagocytosis by macrophages and Schwann cells. It is not demyelination without axon loss, chromatolysis, or degeneration of the proximal stump or cell body.'),
('66bc5248-79ab-585f-b8aa-a44e22e02f9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Central chromatolysis is the:', '["and dissolution of Nissl substance after axonal injury (regenerative response)","Swelling of the neuronal cell body","Formation of a neuroma","Degeneration of the distal axon","displacement of the nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Swelling of the neuronal cell body', 'Central chromatolysis is the swelling of the neuronal cell body with dispersal and loss of Nissl substance and peripherally displaced nucleus that occurs as a regenerative response after axonal injury. It is not degeneration of the distal axon, formation of a neuroma, or demyelination.'),
('7b78115f-95d8-5eca-9568-d0a1d88c236c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Regeneration of a transected PNS axon is possible because:', '["The proximal stump can sprout and grow along the endoneurial tube (bands of Büngner) formed by Schwann cells and basal lamina","Macrophages form a scaffold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","regeneration is possible a mediated by specific receptor activation","The axon grows from the distal stump without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The CNS environment is permissive for regeneration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The proximal stump can sprout and grow along the endoneurial tube (bands of Büngner) formed by Schwann cells and basal lamina', 'After PNS transection the proximal stump sprouts and grows distally along the endoneurial tube and bands of Büngner formed by Schwann cells and their basal lamina, which support and guide the regenerating axon. Axons cannot regrow from the distal stump, and the CNS environment is non-permissive.'),
('f359d1f3-0742-5275-a660-db5cd67371a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Bands of Büngner are:', '["Columns of proliferating Schwann cells within the endoneurial tube that guide regenerating axons","Fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oligodendrocytes a mediated by specific receptor activation","Macrophages without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Columns of proliferating Schwann cells within the endoneurial tube that guide regenerating axons', 'Bands of Büngner are columns of proliferating Schwann cells that form within the endoneurial tube after nerve injury and guide the regenerating axon along its original path. They are Schwann cells, not fibroblasts, oligodendrocytes, macrophages, or neurons.'),
('f5b3ff5d-ab11-5581-9b9c-e9bffca8fbc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Nogo-A is a:', '["Neurotransmitter resulting in decreased functional activity","Cell adhesion molecule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Growth factor mediated by specific receptor activation","Myelin-associated inhibitor of axon regeneration in the CNS (binds to NgR1 on axons)","Transcription factor a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myelin-associated inhibitor of axon regeneration in the CNS (binds to NgR1 on axons)', 'Nogo-A is a myelin protein in the CNS that binds the neuronal Nogo receptor (NgR1) and inhibits axon regeneration after injury, contributing to the non-permissive CNS environment. It is not a neurotransmitter, growth factor, adhesion molecule, or transcription factor.'),
('dde47e83-2c9d-5cc6-8bc9-04e8ae91e934'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The p75 neurotrophin receptor (p75NTR) is a:', '["Low-affinity receptor for all neurotrophins — can induce apoptosis or survival depending on co-receptors","Ion channel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High-affinity Trk receptor mediated by specific receptor activation","Transcription factor a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adhesion molecule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low-affinity receptor for all neurotrophins — can induce apoptosis or survival depending on co-receptors', 'p75NTR is a low-affinity receptor for all neurotrophins; depending on co-receptor context it promotes survival (when signaling with Trk receptors) or apoptosis (when acting alone). It is not an ion channel, the high-affinity Trk receptor, or a transcription factor.'),
('e6ddda77-5817-5f26-a68c-107f52e90d73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The structure of a typical multipolar neuron includes:', '["One dendrite and one axon resulting in decreased functional activity","dendrites accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","axon a mediated by specific receptor activation","One dendrite and multiple axons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multiple dendrites and one axon (most common in the CNS)"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple dendrites and one axon (most common in the CNS)', 'A multipolar neuron, the most common neuron type in the CNS, has multiple dendrites and a single axon. Unipolar neurons have one process and bipolar neurons one dendrite and one axon, and normal neurons do not have multiple axons.'),
('aae6dc88-be9c-5cdf-89df-a700ed224a4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Pseudo-unipolar neurons are found in:', '["Dorsal root ganglia (DRG) — one process that splits into a peripheral and central axon","The cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","The cerebral cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The retina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal root ganglia (DRG) — one process that splits into a peripheral and central axon', 'Pseudo-unipolar neurons are located in dorsal root (and cranial sensory) ganglia; their single process splits into a peripheral process carrying sensation and a central process entering the CNS. They are not found in the cerebellum, spinal cord gray matter, cortex, or retina.'),
('4f133d47-6adc-5fc3-8939-19af05f9c25e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Bipolar neurons are found in:', '["Spinal cord","and spiral/vestibular ganglia","olfactory epithelium","Cerebral cortex","Retina (bipolar cells)"]'::jsonb, 'Retina (bipolar cells)', 'Bipolar neurons, each with one dendrite and one axon, are found in the retina (bipolar cells), the spiral and vestibular ganglia, and the olfactory epithelium; the retina is the classic site. Most cortical and spinal cord neurons are multipolar.'),
('5b557801-46b6-56a6-ad9f-d50eebd23779'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Pyramidal cells of the cerebral cortex are:', '["Multipolar neurons with a pyramid-shaped soma and a prominent apical dendrite (the major excitatory output neuron of the cortex)","Inhibitory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GABAergic mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Glutamatergic a without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Local interneurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multipolar neurons with a pyramid-shaped soma and a prominent apical dendrite (the major excitatory output neuron of the cortex)', 'Pyramidal cells are multipolar neurons with a pyramid-shaped soma and a long apical dendrite, and they are the principal excitatory output cells of the cortex. The other choices describe inhibitory or local circuit functions rather than the structural and output identity of these neurons.'),
('aca33c1b-00e6-5bb1-8ac0-5a52e3d178ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Nissl stain (cresyl violet) stains:', '["Dendrites resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Axons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myelin mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Nissl bodies (RER and ribosomes) — revealing neuronal somata and the cytoarchitecture of brain regions","Spines leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nissl bodies (RER and ribosomes) — revealing neuronal somata and the cytoarchitecture of brain regions', 'Basic aniline dyes such as cresyl violet bind the RNA-rich RER and ribosomes, so Nissl bodies stain intensely and outline neuronal perikarya. Axons, myelin and spines are better demonstrated by silver and myelin stains.'),
('46e6abac-2a6a-5b2e-8e06-d3e99ead002e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Immunohistochemistry for GFAP is used to:', '["Identify astrocytes (and their processes) in the CNS","Identify microglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Identify ependymal cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Identify oligodendrocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Identify neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Identify astrocytes (and their processes) in the CNS', 'GFAP is an intermediate filament protein expressed by astrocytes, so immunostaining with anti-GFAP selectively reveals these cells and their processes in the CNS. Microglia, oligodendrocytes, ependymal cells and neurons each carry different characteristic markers.'),
('588b0fa9-ab7e-5f7c-b3a4-af726a922b97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Immunohistochemistry for neurofilament is used to:', '["Identify microglia","Identify oligodendrocytes","Identify neurons and their processes (axons)","Identify astrocytes","Identify Schwann cells a"]'::jsonb, 'Identify neurons and their processes (axons)', 'Neurofilaments are neuron-specific intermediate filaments concentrated in axons, so anti-neurofilament staining highlights neuronal cell bodies and their processes, including axons. The other listed cells express glial or Schwann cell markers instead.'),
('9da91d5c-c48d-54df-9afb-84df3ae2066b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'LTP (long-term potentiation) is a:', '["Reduced neurotransmission resulting in decreased functional activity","Long-term decrease in synaptic strength accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Long-lasting increase in synaptic strength (synaptic plasticity) mediated by postsynaptic Ca2+ influx through NMDA receptors","Short-term increase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Immediate change a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Long-lasting increase in synaptic strength (synaptic plasticity) mediated by postsynaptic Ca2+ influx through NMDA receptors', 'LTP is a sustained increase in synaptic efficacy that depends on postsynaptic calcium entry through NMDA receptors and is a cellular substrate of learning and memory. It is not a decrease in transmission or a transient change.'),
('47e2d9b9-5739-513a-947a-af6246f5d9f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The axon initial segment (AIS) is characterized by:', '["Synaptic vesicles resulting in decreased functional activity","High density of K+ channels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High density of voltage-gated Na+ channels and the protein ankyrin-G","Myelin sheath without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nissl bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High density of voltage-gated Na+ channels and the protein ankyrin-G', 'The axon initial segment is packed with voltage-gated sodium channels anchored by ankyrin-G, and it is where the action potential is initiated. Synaptic vesicles, myelin and Nissl bodies are located at different parts of the neuron.'),
('be7b909a-d029-53e0-a258-a50ce11074c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Ankyrin-G is a scaffold protein that:', '["Organizes the Golgi resulting in decreased functional activity","Clusters Ca2+ channels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fixes the position of the nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Clusters K+ channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clusters voltage-gated Na+ channels at the axon initial segment and nodes of Ranvier"]'::jsonb, 'Clusters voltage-gated Na+ channels at the axon initial segment and nodes of Ranvier', 'Ankyrin-G anchors and clusters voltage-gated sodium channels at the axon initial segment and nodes of Ranvier, supporting action potential initiation and saltatory conduction. Golgi organization, nuclear position and potassium channel clustering are carried out by other proteins.'),
('0bdd624d-e6a8-5790-835e-dd41f79c3d70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Nodes of Ranvier in the PNS are characterized by:', '["Synaptic vesicles resulting in decreased functional activity","channels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myelin basic protein mediated by specific receptor activation","High density of voltage-gated Na+ channels (flanked by K+ channels in the juxtaparanode)","Low density of channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High density of voltage-gated Na+ channels (flanked by K+ channels in the juxtaparanode)', 'The node of Ranvier is a bare, myelin-free segment of axon where voltage-gated sodium channels are clustered to regenerate the action potential, with potassium channels concentrated in the adjacent juxtaparanode. Myelin proteins and vesicles are not found at the nodal membrane.'),
('0f9b1f0e-94a8-5110-abd9-eeac9bb77106'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The grey matter of the CNS consists mainly of:', '["Neuronal cell bodies","and synapses","unmyelinated axons","dendrites","glial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Neuronal cell bodies', 'Grey matter is composed chiefly of neuronal cell bodies together with dendrites and unmyelinated processes, and of the options listed the neuronal cell body is the best single descriptor of its prevailing element. Myelinated axons dominate the white matter instead.'),
('845c0e1e-f65a-5787-b874-98c92dde1e97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Layer IV (internal granular layer) of the cerebral cortex is the:', '["Motor output layer resulting in decreased functional activity","Main recipient layer for thalamocortical sensory input","Layer with the fewest cells a mediated by specific receptor activation","Output layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Layer of large Betz cells (motor) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Main recipient layer for thalamocortical sensory input', 'The internal granular layer is composed mainly of small granule and stellate cells and is the major target of thalamocortical sensory afferents. Motor output originates from the deep pyramidal layers rather than from layer IV.'),
('4d5b1dc0-2f40-55ac-899b-49fdd3e401b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Layer V (internal pyramidal) of the cerebral cortex contains:', '["Large pyramidal neurons (including Betz cells in the motor cortex) — the main output to subcortical structures","Granule cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spiny stellate cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Fusiform cells a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamic input cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large pyramidal neurons (including Betz cells in the motor cortex) — the main output to subcortical structures', 'Layer V holds the largest pyramidal neurons of the cortex, including the giant Betz cells of the primary motor cortex, whose axons form the descending corticospinal and corticobulbar pathways. Granule and stellate cells belong to the input layers instead.'),
('dd9a1f62-85ca-5ce2-a972-82c7e7a2c957'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Layer III (external pyramidal) contains:', '["Local interneurons resulting in decreased functional activity","Input from thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pyramidal neurons that project to other cortical areas (corticocortical association fibers)","Output to spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Callosal projection neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pyramidal neurons that project to other cortical areas (corticocortical association fibers)', 'Layer III pyramidal neurons give rise to the corticocortical association and callosal fibers that link separate cortical regions. Descending spinal output arises from layer V and thalamic input terminates mainly in layer IV.'),
('eca4a162-e588-57ad-9341-d1ec1561850d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Layer I (molecular layer) of the cortex contains:', '["Dense granule cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Few neurons (horizontal cells of Cajal-Retzius) and many dendrites and axons from deeper layers","Output to the spinal cord a mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamic afferents without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Many pyramidal cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Few neurons (horizontal cells of Cajal-Retzius) and many dendrites and axons from deeper layers', 'The molecular layer is relatively cell-poor, containing mainly the horizontal cells of Cajal-Retzius along with tangentially running dendrites and axons contributed by deeper layers. Dense populations of granule and pyramidal cells are found in the deeper granular and pyramidal laminae.'),
('65653c0b-b538-5fc8-a681-c4926d47dca6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Mossy fibers to the cerebellum originate from:', '["brainstem resulting in decreased functional activity","The inferior olive — synapse on Purkinje cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and pontine nuclei (carrying sensory and motor information) — they synapse on granule cells","The cerebral cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'and pontine nuclei (carrying sensory and motor information) — they synapse on granule cells', 'Mossy fibers, which end on granule cell dendrites in the cerebellar glomeruli, arise chiefly from the pontine nuclei as well as the spinal cord and vestibular nuclei. The inferior olive gives rise to climbing fibers, not mossy fibers.'),
('7483152b-a0b4-5c4a-b536-d2998e355f37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Climbing fibers originate from the:', '["The spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","The cerebral cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior olive (medulla) — each climbing fiber wraps around the dendrites of a single Purkinje cell","The vestibular nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pontine nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior olive (medulla) — each climbing fiber wraps around the dendrites of a single Purkinje cell', 'Climbing fibers arise exclusively from the inferior olive in the medulla, and each one winds around the dendritic tree of a single Purkinje cell. Pontine and spinal inputs enter the cerebellum as mossy fibers instead.'),
('e7828db0-fe12-5fc4-ba83-f8e7a2a7c021'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Granule cells in the dentate gyrus of the hippocampus are involved in:', '["Sensory processing resulting in decreased functional activity","Emotion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adult neurogenesis and memory formation (new neurons are generated throughout life in the dentate gyrus)","Motor coordination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor output leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adult neurogenesis and memory formation (new neurons are generated throughout life in the dentate gyrus)', 'The dentate gyrus is one of the few sites where neurogenesis continues into adult life, and its newly generated granule cells integrate into hippocampal circuits that support memory formation. This region is not specialized for motor coordination or primary sensory processing.'),
('3b7b92d1-6d92-5455-b2ed-e6ec269d486d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The spinal cord has a central H-shaped grey matter. The dorsal horn contains:', '["Autonomic neurons resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory neurons (interneurons and projection neurons that process sensory input)","Sympathetic preganglionic neurons a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Commissural neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory neurons (interneurons and projection neurons that process sensory input)', 'The dorsal horn receives afferents from the dorsal roots and contains interneurons and projection neurons that relay sensory information such as pain, temperature and touch. Motor neurons reside in the ventral horn and autonomic neurons in the lateral horn.'),
('ac55cf14-ee14-529f-80f0-329fff11d1ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The ventral horn of the spinal cord contains:', '["Sensory neurons resulting in decreased functional activity","Granule cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autonomic neurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Interneurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor neurons (α and γ motor neurons) that innervate skeletal muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Motor neurons (α and γ motor neurons) that innervate skeletal muscle', 'The ventral horn contains somatic motor neurons; alpha cells innervate extrafusal fibers and gamma cells supply the muscle spindles, so together they drive skeletal muscle contraction. Sensory and autonomic cell bodies are located in the dorsal and lateral horns respectively.'),
('1ee5a73d-2934-5edf-9b04-1548273cd549'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The intermediate (lateral) horn of the spinal cord (T1-L2) contains:', '["Preganglionic parasympathetic neurons resulting in decreased functional activity","Preganglionic sympathetic neurons (cell bodies of the sympathetic division)","Somatic motor neurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibitory interneurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensory neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Preganglionic sympathetic neurons (cell bodies of the sympathetic division)', 'The intermediolateral cell column of the lateral horn at T1–L2 holds the cell bodies of preganglionic sympathetic neurons. Parasympathetic preganglionics arise instead from the sacral cord and selected brainstem nuclei.'),
('91a73ef0-eb12-5cbc-a702-a10286f89033'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Rexed lamina VII in the spinal cord contains:', '["Motor neurons resulting in decreased functional activity","Substantia nigra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The intermediolateral cell column (sympathetic preganglionic) and Clarke''s nucleus (proprioception to cerebellum)","Locus coeruleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Substantia gelatinosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The intermediolateral cell column (sympathetic preganglionic) and Clarke''s nucleus (proprioception to cerebellum)', 'Lamina VII houses the intermediolateral sympathetic column (T1–L2) and Clarke''s nucleus (C8–L3), which relays lower-limb proprioception to the cerebellum. The substantia gelatinosa is lamina II, and motor neurons lie in the ventral laminae.'),
('708a7ec5-c9cd-50ea-8c27-95647ce212d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Substantia gelatinosa (Rexed lamina II) of the spinal cord processes:', '["Proprioception resulting in decreased functional activity","Touch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor output mediated by specific receptor activation","Vibration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain and temperature sensation (receives C and Aδ nociceptive afferents)"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature sensation (receives C and Aδ nociceptive afferents)', 'Lamina II receives C and Aδ nociceptive afferents and is the principal spinal relay for pain and temperature sensation. Discriminative touch and proprioception are carried by other pathways in the dorsal columns and spinocerebellar tracts.'),
('1c9b4a2a-6254-5238-aa02-a90f4c3f5e3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The dorsal root ganglion (DRG) contains:', '["Sympathetic neurons resulting in decreased functional activity","The cell bodies of primary sensory neurons (pseudo-unipolar)","Glial cells only a mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interneurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The cell bodies of primary sensory neurons (pseudo-unipolar)', 'Each dorsal root ganglion contains the pseudo-unipolar cell bodies of primary sensory neurons, with one peripheral process to the receptor and one central process entering the spinal cord. Motor and sympathetic cell bodies are never located in the DRG.'),
('e178e8ae-6d4d-57e7-b98a-20f08672b22f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Satellite glial cells in the DRG are:', '["Astrocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Small accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","flattened glial cells that surround DRG neuron cell bodies (similar to Schwann cells)","Oligodendrocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Schwann cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'flattened glial cells that surround DRG neuron cell bodies (similar to Schwann cells)', 'Satellite cells are flattened glial cells that encircle the neuronal perikarya within sensory ganglia, providing trophic and ionic support much as Schwann cells do for peripheral axons. They are neither astrocytes, oligodendrocytes nor true Schwann cells.'),
('9d5854fe-a8b0-5143-ace3-86a5545423ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The term axonotmesis (Seddon classification) refers to:', '["Crush injury only resulting in decreased functional activity","Degeneration of the cell body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Disruption of the axon with intact endoneurium and Schwann cell tube (good prognosis for regeneration)","Complete nerve transection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurapraxia (conduction block without axon disruption) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Disruption of the axon with intact endoneurium and Schwann cell tube (good prognosis for regeneration)', 'In axonotmesis the axon is severed but the endoneurial tubes and Schwann cell framework remain intact, so regenerating axons are guided back to their targets and recovery is usually good. Neurotmesis destroys the connective tissue sheaths and neurapraxia spares the axon.'),
('10098ba2-1f64-56d8-bd3e-d5d990ed09a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Neurotmesis (Seddon) is the most severe nerve injury:', '["Complete transection of the nerve with disruption of axons and all connective tissue (epi/peri/endoneurium) — no spontaneous regeneration","Disruption of axon only (endoneurium intact) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neuroma formation only mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Conduction block only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Partial axon damage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complete transection of the nerve with disruption of axons and all connective tissue (epi/peri/endoneurium) — no spontaneous regeneration', 'Neurotmesis severs the axon together with the epineurium, perineurium and endoneurium, eliminating the guiding tubes that regenerating fibers depend on, so spontaneous recovery is poor. The less severe grades preserve at least part of the connective tissue scaffold and carry a better outlook.'),
('0a1b7dcb-c112-5eb4-86f7-15c044d5cef8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most important factor in nerve regeneration is:', '["The size of the nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The type of nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The age of the patient mediated by specific receptor activation","The level of the transection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The proximity of the injury to the cell body (proximal injuries have better prognosis)"]'::jsonb, 'The proximity of the injury to the cell body (proximal injuries have better prognosis)', 'Outcome depends chiefly on how far the axon must regrow to reach its target, which is set by the proximity of the lesion to the cell body; the shorter the distance of regrowth, the better the recovery. Nerve size, subtype and age are comparatively minor determinants.'),
('b55562f6-7e07-5b09-b204-42a67a3742e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The term motor neuron disease (MND) typically refers to:', '["Guillain-Barre syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peripheral neuropathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) — a progressive degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons","Myopathy a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) — a progressive degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons', 'Motor neuron disease classically designates amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, a progressive degeneration of both upper and lower motor neurons producing weakness, wasting and fasciculations with preserved sensation. Guillain-Barré syndrome and peripheral neuropathies affect the peripheral nervous system rather than the motor neuron cell bodies.'),
('3b324c04-36d3-57c6-b604-47ffe7e2ea53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Betz cells are:', '["Interneurons resulting in decreased functional activity","Giant pyramidal neurons (upper motor neurons) in layer V of the primary motor cortex","Sensory neurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal motor neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lower motor neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Giant pyramidal neurons (upper motor neurons) in layer V of the primary motor cortex', 'Betz cells are the giant pyramidal neurons of layer V in the primary motor cortex whose axons descend in the corticospinal tract, making them upper motor neurons. Spinal alpha motor neurons are the lower motor neurons rather than cortical cells.'),
('a8b06cea-f573-501a-99e0-ddc1ccaec684'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Denervation atrophy of skeletal muscle (as in ALS) shows:', '["Rimmed vacuoles","Ragged red fibers","and target fibers on biopsy","Angulated atrophic fibers","fiber type grouping"]'::jsonb, 'Angulated atrophic fibers', 'Chronic denervation produces small, angulated atrophic fibers that later cluster into fiber type groups as surviving motor units reinnervate them. Ragged red fibers indicate mitochondrial myopathy and rimmed vacuoles point to inclusion body myositis.'),
('dd72f618-ed4b-594e-b511-b7290909c02f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Peripheral neuropathy (damage to peripheral nerves) can be caused by:', '["All of the above: diabetes","toxins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","uremia mediated by specific receptor activation","drugs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","alcoholism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above: diabetes', 'Diabetes, toxins, uremia, drugs and alcoholism are all well-recognized causes of peripheral neuropathy, so the inclusive choice is the correct one. Selecting any single listed cause would omit the equally valid others.'),
('d115fb9f-dbf3-5d1f-9c2d-d21a81a322cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'GBS is characterized histopathologically by:', '["Segmental demyelination with lymphocytic infiltration (macrophages strip myelin from Schwann cells)","Axonal degeneration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Astrocytosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Gliosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuronal loss only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Segmental demyelination with lymphocytic infiltration (macrophages strip myelin from Schwann cells)', 'Guillain-Barré syndrome is an autoimmune attack on peripheral myelin producing segmental demyelination with lymphocytic infiltrates and macrophage stripping of Schwann cell myelin. Axonal degeneration and central changes such as gliosis or astrocytosis are not the characteristic findings.'),
('ec5c4eb0-4967-5f1e-81da-cd184de81e03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Miller-Fisher syndrome (a variant of GBS) is associated with:', '["ataxia resulting in decreased functional activity","and areflexia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anti-GQ1b antibodies (cross-reacts with Campylobacter jejuni) — causing ophthalmoplegia","Anti-GM1 antibodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anti-MAG antibodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-GQ1b antibodies (cross-reacts with Campylobacter jejuni) — causing ophthalmoplegia', 'Miller-Fisher syndrome is linked to anti-GQ1b antibodies that cross-react with Campylobacter jejuni epitopes, producing ophthalmoplegia, ataxia and areflexia. Anti-GM1 antibodies are seen with the acute axonal variant of GBS instead.'),
('6da14cfc-9c6b-529e-9e85-32331eae3677'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) is:', '["A pure sensory neuropathy resulting in decreased functional activity","A pure motor neuropathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","progressive or relapsing demyelinating neuropathy (like GBS but lasting >8 weeks)","An acute neuropathy (less than 8 weeks) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A chronic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'progressive or relapsing demyelinating neuropathy (like GBS but lasting >8 weeks)', 'CIDP is a chronic, progressive or relapsing demyelinating neuropathy whose course extends beyond eight weeks, unlike the acute monophasic presentation of Guillain-Barré syndrome. It is neither purely sensory nor purely motor in its clinical pattern.'),
('08aa924d-fa17-5015-b553-808815de4c75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease (CMT) type 1 is an inherited demyelinating neuropathy caused by:', '["Duplication of PMP22 (peripheral myelin protein 22) on chromosome 17 — the most common cause of CMT1A","Mutation in MFN2 (CMT2A) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mutation in MPZ (myelin protein zero) mediated by specific receptor activation","Mutation in connexin 32 (CMTX) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mutation in NEFL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duplication of PMP22 (peripheral myelin protein 22) on chromosome 17 — the most common cause of CMT1A', 'CMT1A, the most common form of inherited demyelinating neuropathy, results from duplication of the PMP22 gene on chromosome 17, which overproduces this myelin protein and destabilizes the myelin sheath. MFN2, MPZ and connexin-32 mutations produce other CMT subtypes.'),
('c8017768-f46d-5308-a106-53d18b33f68f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Myelin protein zero (MPZ/P0) is a:', '["A transcription factor resulting in decreased functional activity","The major protein of CNS myelin (PLP) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A gap junction protein (Cx32) mediated by specific receptor activation","A growth factor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Major structural protein of PNS myelin (accounts for 50% of PNS myelin protein) — a transmembrane adhesion molecule"]'::jsonb, 'Major structural protein of PNS myelin (accounts for 50% of PNS myelin protein) — a transmembrane adhesion molecule', 'P0 (MPZ) is a transmembrane adhesion protein that makes up roughly half of the protein in peripheral myelin and compacts its lamellae. The analogous structural protein of central myelin is proteolipid protein (PLP).'),
('274e94a8-e2cb-5913-9244-31b92142c8f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Proteolipid protein (PLP) is the:', '["Main PNS myelin protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Astrocyte protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neurofilament protein mediated by specific receptor activation","Microglial protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Major structural protein of CNS myelin (accounts for 50% of CNS myelin protein)"]'::jsonb, 'Major structural protein of CNS myelin (accounts for 50% of CNS myelin protein)', 'Proteolipid protein is the most abundant protein of central myelin, comprising about half of its protein mass and mediating compaction of the sheath; its mutation causes Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease. The analogous PNS protein is P0.'),
('951bddd1-eaa0-5502-984f-a02534e2f04b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Myelin basic protein (MBP) is:', '["important for compaction resulting in decreased functional activity","The major CNS myelin protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A peripheral and central myelin protein on the cytoplasmic side of the myelin membrane","A myelin-associated glycoprotein (MAG) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The major PNS myelin protein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A peripheral and central myelin protein on the cytoplasmic side of the myelin membrane', 'Myelin basic protein lies on the cytoplasmic face of the myelin membrane in both the PNS and CNS, where it promotes compaction of the adjacent intracellular surfaces. It is distinct from P0, PLP and MAG.'),
('c3797bb7-8cc5-5e1b-884a-0931dc7f8669'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Multiple sclerosis (MS) is an:', '["A motor neuron disease a resulting in decreased functional activity","A neuropathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autoimmune demyelinating disease of the CNS (multifocal plaques of demyelination with relative preservation of axons)","A myopathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A demyelinating PNS disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune demyelinating disease of the CNS (multifocal plaques of demyelination with relative preservation of axons)', 'Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune process that produces multifocal plaques of central demyelination in which the axons are relatively preserved early in the course. Peripheral demyelinating diseases, myopathies and motor neuron disease show different lesions.'),
('86a90736-4944-5971-924b-860ac458ac51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Optic neuritis (inflammation of the optic nerve) is a common presentation of MS with:', '["Blurred vision in both eyes","Diplopia","and color desaturation","Unilateral vision loss","pain on eye movement"]'::jsonb, 'Unilateral vision loss', 'Optic neuritis typically presents with painful unilateral loss of vision, frequently with impaired color discrimination, due to an inflammatory demyelinating lesion of one optic nerve. Bilateral simultaneous visual loss and diplopia are not the characteristic presentation.'),
('9dc097d6-7a2f-52e4-84b9-d5d6a0e50820'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The microglia marker for IHC is:', '["Iba1 (ionized calcium-binding adaptor molecule 1) and CD68 (in activated microglia/macrophages)","Olig2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NeuN mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","GFAP without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MBP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iba1 (ionized calcium-binding adaptor molecule 1) and CD68 (in activated microglia/macrophages)', 'Iba1 is constitutively expressed by microglia, and CD68 marks them once activated and transformed into macrophages. GFAP labels astrocytes, NeuN labels neurons, MBP labels myelin and Olig2 labels oligodendroglia.'),
('9e430abd-4702-57eb-8cdb-0c23bce673db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Synaptophysin is a marker for:', '["Neuronal cell bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Gilal cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myelin mediated by specific receptor activation","Postsynaptic density without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Synaptic vesicles (presynaptic terminals) — used to identify neuroendocrine tumors and synaptic density"]'::jsonb, 'Synaptic vesicles (presynaptic terminals) — used to identify neuroendocrine tumors and synaptic density', 'Synaptophysin is a transmembrane protein of synaptic vesicle membranes, so immunostaining labels presynaptic terminals and also neuroendocrine cells that carry dense-core vesicles. It is not present in the postsynaptic density, myelin or neuronal perikarya.'),
('48247627-9903-5cdb-a3df-958b7fdf7bab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the basal ganglia is:', '["GABA","Dopamine","Acetylcholine","Serotonin","Glutamate"]'::jsonb, 'GABA', 'GABA is the dominant inhibitory transmitter of the basal ganglia, and loss of GABAergic striatal output neurons underlies movement disorders such as Huntington disease. Glutamate and the monoamines are excitatory or modulatory rather than inhibitory.'),
('79ef28e7-b7df-5445-b414-c736b3dbb9cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The major neurotransmitter of the nigrostriatal pathway is:', '["Acetylcholine","Glutamate","Dopamine","Serotonin","GABA"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine', 'The nigrostriatal pathway releases dopamine from substantia nigra pars compacta terminals onto the striatum, and its loss is the hallmark of Parkinson disease. The other transmitters belong to different circuits.'),
('4509e47e-eebc-5960-ae95-f80a18555379'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Parkinson disease is characterized by:', '["Loss of noradrenergic neurons in the locus coeruleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc) with Lewy bodies (α-synuclein aggregates)","Deposition of tau protein mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert (Alzheimer) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of GABAergic neurons in the striatum (Huntington) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc) with Lewy bodies (α-synuclein aggregates)', 'Parkinson disease results from degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta with formation of α-synuclein-rich Lewy bodies. Cholinergic loss in the nucleus basalis and GABAergic loss in the striatum characterize Alzheimer and Huntington disease respectively.'),
('ac314de7-f087-53cc-8d99-cb60a04ffea6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Lewy bodies are:', '["intracytoplasmic (neuronal) inclusions composed primarily of aggregated α-synuclein","Basophilic inclusions (like Marinesco bodies)","Eosinophilic","Tau-positive inclusions (like neurofibrillary tangles)","Silver-positive plaques"]'::jsonb, 'Eosinophilic', 'On hematoxylin and eosin staining, Lewy bodies appear as eosinophilic intracytoplasmic inclusions within affected neurons, and immunostaining confirms they contain aggregated α-synuclein. Basophilic Marinesco bodies, tau-positive tangles and silver-positive plaques are distinct structures.'),
('cd66295e-9452-5817-9183-c6d3cc667b7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Alzheimer disease (AD) is characterized by:', '["Extracellular senile plaques (amyloid-β) and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles (hyperphosphorylated tau)","Prion protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TDP-43 inclusions a mediated by specific receptor activation","α-Synuclein inclusions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lewy bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extracellular senile plaques (amyloid-β) and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles (hyperphosphorylated tau)', 'Alzheimer disease is defined by extracellular amyloid-β plaques together with intracellular neurofibrillary tangles of hyperphosphorylated tau. Lewy bodies, prion protein and TDP-43 inclusions characterize other neurodegenerative disorders.'),
('00bdde4a-e8cf-578b-ae9e-e626bc9c4fe8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Amyloid-β (Aβ) in Alzheimer disease is derived from:', '["Catabolism of tau resulting in decreased functional activity","Proteolytic cleavage of amyloid precursor protein (APP) by β- and γ-secretases (producing Aβ40 and Aβ42)","Cleavage of presenilin mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct synthesis by neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From α-synuclein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proteolytic cleavage of amyloid precursor protein (APP) by β- and γ-secretases (producing Aβ40 and Aβ42)', 'Sequential cleavage of the amyloid precursor protein by β- and γ-secretases releases Aβ40 and Aβ42 fragments that aggregate into senile plaques. The peptide is not produced from tau, presenilin or α-synuclein.'),
('20ed1bfe-1380-5e25-87c1-5afc9fac6282'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Neurofibrillary tangles (NFTs) are composed of:', '["Prion protein resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Ubiquitin a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aβ peptides mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperphosphorylated tau protein (p-tau) — an axonal microtubule-associated protein that forms paired helical filaments","α-Synuclein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperphosphorylated tau protein (p-tau) — an axonal microtubule-associated protein that forms paired helical filaments', 'Neurofibrillary tangles are intraneuronal aggregates of hyperphosphorylated tau, a microtubule-associated protein, arranged as paired helical filaments. They do not contain amyloid-β, prion protein or α-synuclein.'),
('b67831ec-a661-577f-9aef-f8bc48507812'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common cause of dementia is:', '["Lewy body dementia","Pick disease","Alzheimer disease (60-80% of cases)","Frontotemporal dementia","Vascular dementia"]'::jsonb, 'Alzheimer disease (60-80% of cases)', 'Alzheimer disease is the most common cause of dementia, accounting for roughly 60–80% of all cases. Lewy body dementia, vascular dementia and frontotemporal dementias are comparatively less frequent causes.'),
('0ab7f04b-1ed7-5d62-9401-46ebe0d69f9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Hippocampal sclerosis is seen in:', '["Parkinson disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontotemporal lobar degeneration (FTLD-TDP) and aging","is seen in: mediated by specific receptor activation","often associated with TDP-43 pathology without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alzheimer disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontotemporal lobar degeneration (FTLD-TDP) and aging', 'Hippocampal sclerosis of aging is closely tied to TDP-43 proteinopathy and is characteristically found with frontotemporal lobar degeneration of the FTLD-TDP type as well as in elderly individuals. It is not the defining lesion of Parkinson or Alzheimer disease.'),
('34f72f2a-89b3-5a00-9626-6552a4446cfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'TDP-43 (TAR DNA-binding protein 43) is a:', '["Microtubule-binding protein resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Nuclear RNA-binding protein that forms cytoplasmic aggregates in ALS (97%) and some forms of frontotemporal dementia (FTLD-TDP)","Amyloid protein a mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Synaptic protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Presenilin protein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear RNA-binding protein that forms cytoplasmic aggregates in ALS (97%) and some forms of frontotemporal dementia (FTLD-TDP)', 'TDP-43 is a nuclear RNA-binding protein that shuttles between the nucleus and cytoplasm; in ALS and FTLD-TDP it is mislocalized to cytoplasmic aggregates. Microtubule-binding (tau), amyloid, synaptic, and presenilin proteins belong to unrelated disease cascades.'),
('88d10939-33e8-5a5b-a85c-c7047a624273'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion is the most common cause of:', '["Parkinson disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Familial ALS and frontotemporal dementia (GGGGCC repeat expansion in intron 1)","Spinal muscular atrophy a mediated by specific receptor activation","Alzheimer disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Huntington disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Familial ALS and frontotemporal dementia (GGGGCC repeat expansion in intron 1)', 'A GGGGCC hexanucleotide repeat expansion in the first intron of C9orf72 is the commonest genetic cause of familial ALS and frontotemporal dementia. Parkinson, SMA, Alzheimer, and Huntington disease result from different mutations.'),
('92d2dfaa-e552-5042-865f-d1dbb871599f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Cerebellar ataxia presents with:', '["Incoordination","intention tremor","and gait ataxia","dysdiadochokinesia","dysmetria (past pointing)"]'::jsonb, 'Incoordination', 'The central deficit of cerebellar ataxia is incoordination of voluntary movement. Intention tremor, ataxic gait, dysdiadochokinesia, and dysmetria are individual cerebellar signs that all fall within this umbrella of incoordination.'),
('5c14a074-8737-599f-a876-31066a693865'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The basal ganglia are involved in:', '["and execution of voluntary movements; also cognitive and emotional functions","Hearing","Pain perception","Motor planning","initiation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor planning', 'The basal ganglia are best known for motor planning and initiation of voluntary movement through cortico-striato-thalamic circuits, with additional contributions to cognition and emotion. Hearing and pain perception are handled by other tracts and cortical areas.'),
('a1b6a453-6aa5-5a93-a638-c5f111d996a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The direct pathway of the basal ganglia:', '["Modulates emotion a resulting in decreased functional activity","Regulates autonomic function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facilitates movement (cortex → striatum → GPi → thalamus → cortex — disinhibition)","Controls eye movements without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibits movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facilitates movement (cortex → striatum → GPi → thalamus → cortex — disinhibition)', 'In the direct pathway, cortical input to the striatum inhibits GPi and SNr, which disinhibits the thalamus and therefore facilitates movement. Disinhibition of the thalamus is what makes this pathway pro-kinetic.'),
('d8ea6671-989b-59f2-bc33-ef6e0383b7ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The indirect pathway of the basal ganglia:', '["Controls tremor resulting in decreased functional activity","Regulates tone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Modulates learning a mediated by specific receptor activation","Facilitates movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibits movement (cortex → striatum → GPe → STN → GPi → thalamus — net inhibition)"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits movement (cortex → striatum → GPe → STN → GPi → thalamus — net inhibition)', 'The indirect pathway runs from the striatum through GPe and STN before reaching GPi, producing net inhibition of the thalamus and suppression of unwanted movement. This result opposes the direct pathway; tremor control, tone regulation, and learning modulation are not its typical description.'),
('e4e2e342-b772-5953-a3af-0036883d2d58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Huntington disease (HD) is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by:', '["Deletion in SMN1 (SMA) a resulting in decreased functional activity","Missense mutation in SNCA (α-synuclein) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Expansion of GGGGCC repeats (C9orf72) mediated by specific receptor activation","CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the HTT gene (huntingtin protein) — causes loss of striatal GABAergic medium spiny neurons","Expansion of CTG repeats (DM1) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the HTT gene (huntingtin protein) — causes loss of striatal GABAergic medium spiny neurons', 'An expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat in the HTT gene produces an abnormal huntingtin protein that selectively kills striatal GABAergic medium spiny neurons. Alpha-synuclein, C9orf72, SMN1, and CTG expansions each cause different diseases.'),
('12fa29d3-d379-5ce5-b3dd-76c5788e6292'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The neurotransmitter system primarily affected in Huntington disease is:', '["GABAergic (loss of GABAergic medium spiny neurons in the striatum)","Dopaminergic (SNc) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cholinergic (nucleus basalis) mediated by specific receptor activation","Noradrenergic (locus coeruleus) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serotonergic (raphe) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GABAergic (loss of GABAergic medium spiny neurons in the striatum)', 'Huntington disease primarily destroys GABAergic medium spiny neurons in the striatum, so the damaged neurotransmitter system is GABAergic. Loss of dopaminergic, cholinergic, noradrenergic, and serotonergic neurons characterizes other disorders.'),
('3173e977-5384-564c-9bde-c11d7536c792'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Astrocytoma is a glioma derived from:', '["Microglia","Neurons","Oligodendrocytes (Olig2-positive)","Astrocytes (GFAP-positive)","Ependymal cells"]'::jsonb, 'Astrocytes (GFAP-positive)', 'Astrocytoma is a glioma that shows astrocytic differentiation and GFAP immunoreactivity. Olig2 marks oligodendrogliomas, while the other listed cells give rise to different tumors or reactive processes.'),
('56a4a3f7-0db0-50ca-8349-6eaba9ea6bfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', '1p/19q co-deletion is a molecular marker for:', '["Ependymoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Glioblastoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meningioma mediated by specific receptor activation","Astrocytoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oligodendroglioma (codeletion of 1p and 19q predicts better prognosis and response to chemotherapy)"]'::jsonb, 'Oligodendroglioma (codeletion of 1p and 19q predicts better prognosis and response to chemotherapy)', 'Combined loss of chromosomes 1p and 19q is the defining molecular abnormality of oligodendroglioma and predicts a better prognosis and stronger response to chemotherapy. This codeletion is absent in astrocytoma, glioblastoma, meningioma, and ependymoma.'),
('06fe1087-4446-536a-b174-69e77fbfa471'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common histological type of meningioma is:', '["Transitional (mixed)","Meningothelial (syncytial)","Fibrous (fibroblastic)","Secretory","Psammomatous"]'::jsonb, 'Meningothelial (syncytial)', 'The meningothelial (syncytial) subtype is the most frequent meningioma, composed of whorled sheets of meningothelial cells containing psammoma bodies. Transitional, fibrous, secretory, and psammomatous variants are all less common.'),
('a11ff198-2877-5584-b44f-9d14e985bc9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Schwannoma (acoustic neuroma) is a benign tumor of:', '["Neurons resulting in decreased functional activity","Schwann cells of the CN VIII (vestibular division) — Antoni A and Antoni B areas","S100+ mediated by specific receptor activation","Astrocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oligodendrocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Schwann cells of the CN VIII (vestibular division) — Antoni A and Antoni B areas', 'An acoustic neuroma is a schwannoma derived from the Schwann cells of the vestibular division of CN VIII, typically showing Antoni A and Antoni B areas. S100 positivity is merely a staining feature, and the other options name cells that do not give rise to this tumor.'),
('6e24c535-53dc-5653-b9dd-904dc77b662e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Verocay bodies are:', '["Nuclear palisades (parallel rows of nuclei) in Antoni A areas of a schwannoma","Rosenthal fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Necrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Pseudorosettes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Psammoma bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear palisades (parallel rows of nuclei) in Antoni A areas of a schwannoma', 'Verocay bodies are palisaded rows of nuclei found in the cellular (Antoni A) regions of a schwannoma. Rosenthal fibers, pseudorosettes, and psammoma bodies characterize pilocytic astrocytoma, ependymoma, and meningioma respectively.'),
('a31275a9-d3c3-5fba-8b1c-2ec7b822bb4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Neurofibroma is a benign peripheral nerve sheath tumor composed of:', '["fibroblasts","A mixture of Schwann cells","Only Schwann cells","mast cells","and perineurial-like cells (not just Schwann cells)"]'::jsonb, 'A mixture of Schwann cells', 'A neurofibroma contains an admixture of Schwann cells, fibroblasts, mast cells, and perineurial-like cells, whereas a schwannoma consists almost entirely of Schwann cells. This mixed cellular composition is a key distinguishing feature.'),
('4b9f16cd-b207-5464-8f4b-769eb4c261e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Plexiform neurofibroma is pathognomonic for:', '["nodular enlargement of a nerve plexus often with a bag of worms appearance","genetic association","Schwannomatosis","NF2","Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) — diffuse"]'::jsonb, 'Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) — diffuse', 'A plexiform neurofibroma, appearing like a bag of worms along a nerve plexus, is diagnostic of NF1. Schwannomatosis and NF2 predominantly feature schwannomas rather than plexiform neurofibromas.'),
('a76cb78c-a589-556e-b3cd-9f0ca516aecc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor (MPNST) is associated with:', '["arising from a pre-existing plexiform neurofibroma (risk 10%)","NF2","Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1)","genetic association","Schwannomatosis"]'::jsonb, 'Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1)', 'MPNST arises most often in patients with NF1, typically from a pre-existing plexiform neurofibroma. NF2, in contrast, is linked to bilateral vestibular schwannomas.'),
('22613949-d372-50eb-83ee-1db8739ea872'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The two-hit hypothesis in NF1 involves:', '["Deletion of the RB1 gene resulting in decreased functional activity","Translocation of ALK a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mutation of the NF1 gene (17q11.2) encoding neurofibromin — a tumor suppressor (Ras-GAP)","Mutation of TP53 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amplification of MYCN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mutation of the NF1 gene (17q11.2) encoding neurofibromin — a tumor suppressor (Ras-GAP)', 'The two-hit hypothesis applies to tumor suppressor genes; in NF1 both alleles of the NF1 gene on 17q11.2, which encodes the Ras-GAP neurofibromin, must be inactivated. RB1, TP53, MYCN, and ALK are involved in other tumor types.'),
('7ca06915-0b18-5614-b8e7-917346c83bed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Pilocytic astrocytoma (WHO grade I) is characterized by:', '["High mitotic activity","and eosinophilic granular bodies — very good prognosis","Biphasic pattern (compact and microcystic)","Necrosis","Rosenthal fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Biphasic pattern (compact and microcystic)', 'Pilocytic astrocytoma shows a biphasic pattern combining compact fibrillary areas and loose microcystic regions, accompanied by Rosenthal fibers and eosinophilic granular bodies and an excellent prognosis. High mitotic activity and necrosis belong to malignant gliomas.'),
('f36dfa1a-a9bf-52a9-8ac8-5bb2bd85cc17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Rodent-like (granular) bodies in pilocytic astrocytoma are:', '["Psammoma bodies","Eosinophilic granular bodies (EGBs) — rounded","Verocay bodies","brightly eosinophilic inclusions in tumor cells and their processes","Necrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Eosinophilic granular bodies (EGBs) — rounded', 'These rounded, brightly eosinophilic granular bodies are a characteristic and prognostically favorable finding of pilocytic astrocytoma. They are not psammoma bodies, Verocay bodies, or necrotic foci.'),
('abaffd04-51cb-590c-9301-b2a771772161'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Rosenthal fibers are:', '["Inclusions in meningioma","characteristic of pilocytic astrocytoma (and reactive gliosis in chronic conditions)","Bodies in schwannoma","Brightly eosinophilic","corkscrew-shaped or rod-like hyaline inclusions in astrocytic processes"]'::jsonb, 'Brightly eosinophilic', 'Rosenthal fibers are corkscrew- or rod-shaped hyaline eosinophilic inclusions that form within astrocytic processes, classically in pilocytic astrocytoma and chronic reactive gliosis. They are unrelated to meningioma or schwannoma inclusions.'),
('32e0c5d3-21ad-5de8-b6ba-f96a6b7ad235'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Ependymoma is a glioma arising from:', '["Astrocytes resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Ependymal cells lining the ventricles and central canal — perivascular pseudorosettes and true ependymal rosettes","Oligodendrocytes mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Choroid plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ependymal cells lining the ventricles and central canal — perivascular pseudorosettes and true ependymal rosettes', 'Ependymoma differentiates along the ependymal cells lining the ventricles and central canal, producing perivascular pseudorosettes and true ependymal rosettes. Astrocytic, oligodendroglial, and choroid plexus origins give rise to different tumors.'),
('cd5550dc-6f94-54e0-90cc-dfd2b0ac6ce7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Perivascular pseudorosettes in ependymoma are:', '["Verocay bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Whorls accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radiating arrangements of ependymal tumor cell processes around blood vessels (creating a perivascular clear zone)","Psammoma bodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","True gland-like rosettes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radiating arrangements of ependymal tumor cell processes around blood vessels (creating a perivascular clear zone)', 'In perivascular pseudorosettes, ependymal tumor cell processes radiate toward a vessel, leaving a clear perivascular zone. Verocay bodies, whorls, psammoma bodies, and true gland-like rosettes are patterns of other lesions.'),
('8df93801-370a-52cb-96be-ce6b6e1f1b96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Ependymal rosettes (true rosettes) are:', '["The diagnostic feature of ependymoma (but present in only a minority of cases) resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor cells arranged around a central lumen (resembling the central canal)","absent mediated by specific receptor activation","Necrotic foci without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granular bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor cells arranged around a central lumen (resembling the central canal)', 'True ependymal rosettes are tumor cells arranged around a central lumen that mimics the central canal; although diagnostic of ependymoma, they occur in only a minority of cases. Necrotic foci and granular bodies are nonspecific findings.'),
('ca0da0dc-70c8-5283-8c8f-2280059a3a79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Choroid plexus papilloma arises from:', '["Meninges resulting in decreased functional activity","Ependymal cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Choroid plexus epithelium — a benign papillary tumor (WHO grade I) in the ventricles (produces CSF)","Astrocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurons a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus epithelium — a benign papillary tumor (WHO grade I) in the ventricles (produces CSF)', 'Choroid plexus papilloma arises from the CSF-secreting choroid plexus epithelium and is a benign WHO grade I papillary tumor of the ventricles, often the lateral ventricle in children. Meninges, ependyma, and glia are not the tissue of origin.'),
('8fdd932e-f6b6-5abb-b12f-185283b1370e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Medulloblastoma is an embryonal tumor of the cerebellum (WHO grade IV) that shows:', '["radiation-sensitive resulting in decreased functional activity","Perivascular pseudorosettes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rosenthal fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Oligodendroglial features without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small round blue cells (high N:C ratio) with Homer-Wright rosettes — aggressive"]'::jsonb, 'Small round blue cells (high N:C ratio) with Homer-Wright rosettes — aggressive', 'Medulloblastoma is an aggressive embryonal tumor of the cerebellum composed of small round blue cells with a high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio and Homer-Wright rosettes, yet it is relatively sensitive to radiation and chemotherapy. Pseudorosettes and Rosenthal fibers belong to ependymoma and pilocytic astrocytoma respectively.'),
('284bf565-aebd-5170-9440-8a98bea64edc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Homer-Wright rosettes are:', '["Necrotic rosettes resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor cells arranged around a central area of neuropil (not a true lumen) — seen in medulloblastoma and neuroblastoma","True ependymal rosettes (central lumen) mediated by specific receptor activation","Verocay bodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pseudorosettes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor cells arranged around a central area of neuropil (not a true lumen) — seen in medulloblastoma and neuroblastoma', 'Homer-Wright rosettes are tumor cells arranged around a central core of neuropil without a true lumen, seen in medulloblastoma and neuroblastoma. True ependymal rosettes possess a central lumen, while Verocay bodies and perivascular pseudorosettes are separate formations.'),
('d886194c-a27d-5d85-af7f-6b9aa8a6b14d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Craniopharyngioma is a benign but locally aggressive tumor arising from:', '["The brain parenchyma resulting in decreased functional activity","The choroid plexus a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The meninges mediated by specific receptor activation","Rathke pouch remnants (squamous epithelium) in the sellar/suprasellar region — wet keratin and calcification","The pituitary gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rathke pouch remnants (squamous epithelium) in the sellar/suprasellar region — wet keratin and calcification', 'Craniopharyngioma develops from remnants of Rathke pouch, yielding squamous epithelium with wet keratin and calcification in the sellar and suprasellar region. Tumors of the pituitary, meninges, and brain parenchyma have different lineages.'),
('91c8796a-5ee5-5998-b269-02b6148e6ced'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common type of functioning pituitary adenoma is:', '["ACTH-secreting (corticotroph) — Cushing disease resulting in decreased functional activity","TSH-secreting (thyrotroph) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GH-secreting (somatotroph) — acromegaly mediated by specific receptor activation","Gonadotroph (FSH/LH) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prolactinoma (lactotroph) — presents with amenorrhea-galactorrhea in women and hypogonadism in men"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactinoma (lactotroph) — presents with amenorrhea-galactorrhea in women and hypogonadism in men', 'Prolactinoma is the most common functioning pituitary adenoma and classically causes amenorrhea with galactorrhea in women and hypogonadism in men, responding well to dopamine agonists. GH, ACTH, TSH, and gonadotroph adenomas are each less frequent.'),
('ffb113af-71ba-589a-87c6-7a248b23fcfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'MEN1 (multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1) includes:', '["Pituitary","None of the above","and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","Parathyroid","adrenal"]'::jsonb, 'Parathyroid', 'MEN1 comprises tumors of the parathyroid glands, the endocrine pancreas, and the anterior pituitary, with parathyroid involvement being the most frequent manifestation. Neoplastic involvement limited to a single endocrine gland does not define this syndrome.'),
('b6b24ac6-8dcf-5d8c-8fe2-310aba74c4d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Paraganglioma is a tumor of:', '["Parathyroid a resulting in decreased functional activity","Extra-adrenal chromaffin cells (extra-adrenal paraganglion system)","Pancreatic islets mediated by specific receptor activation","Adrenal medulla (only) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extra-adrenal chromaffin cells (extra-adrenal paraganglion system)', 'Paragangliomas derive from extra-adrenal chromaffin cells of the paraganglion system, whereas a tumor confined to the adrenal medulla is a pheochromocytoma. Parathyroid, thyroid, and pancreatic islet cells are unrelated origins.'),
('49118f33-181d-5d02-9329-8ae2839d2a4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The carotid body tumor (glomus caroticum) is a paraganglioma at the:', '["near the bifurcation","possibly pulsatile","Carotid artery in the neck","Carotid artery bifurcation — painless","slow-growing neck mass"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid artery bifurcation — painless', 'The carotid body tumor is a paraganglioma sited at the carotid bifurcation, classically a painless, slow-growing, possibly pulsatile neck mass. A mass elsewhere in the neck or an adrenal location represents a different tumor.'),
('e7918c9f-a9fb-5f58-b3d0-b02578e90147'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Rathke cleft cyst arises from:', '["The third ventricle resulting in decreased functional activity","The hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Remnants of Rathke pouch (the embryonic precursor of the anterior pituitary) — a benign cyst in the sellar region","The pituitary gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The choroid plexus a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Remnants of Rathke pouch (the embryonic precursor of the anterior pituitary) — a benign cyst in the sellar region', 'Rathke cleft cyst is a benign sellar cyst arising from remnants of Rathke pouch, the embryonic precursor of the anterior pituitary. The hypothalamus, third ventricle, and choroid plexus do not generate this cyst.'),
('8dad3deb-06ce-5055-8b4b-ee672713b756'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Colloid cyst of the third ventricle is a:', '["filled with gelatinous material — can cause obstructive hydrocephalus if it blocks the foramen of Monro","Benign cyst lined by columnar epithelium","Epidermoid cyst","Dermoid cyst","Arachnoid cyst"]'::jsonb, 'Benign cyst lined by columnar epithelium', 'A colloid cyst of the third ventricle is a benign cyst lined by columnar epithelium and filled with gelatinous material; blockage of the foramen of Monro can produce obstructive hydrocephalus. Epidermoid, dermoid, and arachnoid cysts are different entities.'),
('9d10bd0e-f2fe-570d-95de-48bd6cfacf80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Epidermoid cyst is lined by:', '["epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Columnar epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transitional epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium (desquamated keratin fills the cyst) — benign","Respiratory epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium (desquamated keratin fills the cyst) — benign', 'Epidermoid cyst is lined by keratinized stratified squamous epithelium and its lumen fills with desquamated keratin. Columnar, respiratory, and transitional epithelia line other types of cystic lesions.'),
('3e568298-0345-5079-90b6-b5b91c4da0fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Arachnoid cyst is a:', '["Benign","Not a true cyst ( epithelial lining)","fluid-filled cyst formed by splitting of the arachnoid membrane — lined by arachnoid cells","Lined by columnar epithelium","Lined by squamous epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Benign', 'An arachnoid cyst is a benign, CSF-filled sac produced by splitting of the arachnoid membrane and is covered by arachnoid cells, so it is not a true epithelial-lined cyst. Columnar and squamous epithelial linings characterize other cysts.'),
('cb35fe35-466a-540a-bf8c-d821300cbc3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'En plaque meningioma refers to:', '["A meningioma with bone invasion","A cystic meningioma","A flat","sheet-like meningioma that grows along the inner surface of the dura (rather than a nodular mass)","An aggressive meningioma"]'::jsonb, 'A flat', 'En plaque meningioma grows as a flat, sheet-like tumor along the inner dural surface rather than as a nodular mass and often invades adjacent bone. The qualities of being cystic, aggressive, or bone-invasive describe other meningioma variants.'),
('92673bf7-381b-5334-9b8c-0f1bdd1963f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common site of spinal meningioma is:', '["Cervical","Sacral","Thoracic region","especially anteriorly (more common in middle-aged women)","Lumbar"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic region', 'Spinal meningiomas are most common in the thoracic region, characteristically lying anterior to the cord in middle-aged women. The cervical, lumbar, and sacral levels are affected less often.'),
('c220da29-b3c0-5f9f-8dc7-a330ba66733b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Hemangioblastoma is a benign tumor of the CNS (WHO grade I) composed of:', '["Stroma cells with lipid droplets and a rich capillary network — associated with VHL disease","Schwann cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Smooth muscle cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Glial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningothelial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stroma cells with lipid droplets and a rich capillary network — associated with VHL disease', 'Hemangioblastoma is a WHO grade I tumor of lipid-laden stromal cells within a rich capillary network, classically in the cerebellum and frequently associated with von Hippel-Lindau disease. Glial, meningothelial, smooth muscle, and Schwann cells are not its components.'),
('3ceecc49-9322-5fb8-ac86-d0814c13da29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common primary brain tumor in children is:', '["Pilocytic astrocytoma (WHO grade I)","Glioblastoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ependymoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Medulloblastoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Craniopharyngioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pilocytic astrocytoma (WHO grade I)', 'Pilocytic astrocytoma is the most common primary brain tumor in children, a circumscribed low-grade neoplasm with an excellent prognosis. Medulloblastoma is the most common malignant pediatric brain tumor, while ependymoma and craniopharyngioma are less frequent.'),
('18235b3d-b37b-5f17-a11b-f16f7601f289'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common source of brain metastases is:', '["Lung cancer (most common in men)","Renal cell carcinoma","Colorectal cancer","Melanoma","Breast cancer (most common in women)"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer (most common in men)', 'Lung carcinoma is the overall leading source of brain metastases and the most common source in men, whereas breast cancer leads in women. Melanoma, renal cell carcinoma, and colorectal cancer spread to the brain less often.'),
('ddddaa78-1801-5298-aacb-47fe2c3b13d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common cause of spontaneous intracerebral hemorrhage is:', '["or vascular malformations resulting in decreased functional activity","trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parenchymal (intracerebral) hemorrhage — caused by hypertensive small vessel disease (most common)","Subarachnoid hemorrhage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","amyloid angiopathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parenchymal (intracerebral) hemorrhage — caused by hypertensive small vessel disease (most common)', 'Hypertensive degeneration of small penetrating arteries is the commonest cause of spontaneous (non-traumatic) intracerebral hemorrhage, typically within the putamen, thalamus, or pons. Cerebral amyloid angiopathy, trauma, and vascular malformations account for most remaining cases.'),
('436edaba-75a4-5f40-890a-aeddbf85ec8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Charcot-Bouchard aneurysms are:', '["Small","micro-aneurysms of small penetrating arteries in the brain (associated with hypertension) — prone to rupture","True berry aneurysms (circle of Willis)","causing intracerebral hemorrhage","Mycotic aneurysms (infected)"]'::jsonb, 'Small', 'Charcot-Bouchard aneurysms are small microaneurysms of penetrating arterioles in hypertensive patients, and their rupture produces deep intracerebral hemorrhage. Saccular berry aneurysms of the circle of Willis, when ruptured, more often yield subarachnoid hemorrhage.'),
('2b9823e3-a2a4-520e-af9b-75a4edc2a960'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common cause of subarachnoid hemorrhage is:', '["Rupture of a saccular (berry) aneurysm","Intracerebral hemorrhage","Arteriovenous malformation (AVM)","Trauma","Amyloid angiopathy"]'::jsonb, 'Rupture of a saccular (berry) aneurysm', 'Rupture of a saccular (berry) aneurysm on the circle of Willis is the leading cause of spontaneous subarachnoid hemorrhage and classically presents with a thunderclap headache. The other mechanisms are far less frequent causes.'),
('20403e0f-01bb-5d09-a1e3-fb799416f38a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Subdural hemorrhage (SDH) is most commonly caused by:', '["Tearing of bridging veins that cross the subdural space (from the brain surface to dural sinuses) — often due to trauma (especially in elderly and infants)","Bleeding from a tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rupture of an artery (middle meningeal artery) mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Rupture of a berry aneurysm without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parenchymal hemorrhage with extension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tearing of bridging veins that cross the subdural space (from the brain surface to dural sinuses) — often due to trauma (especially in elderly and infants)', 'Subdural hemorrhage is most often caused by tearing of the bridging veins that cross the subdural space, classically after trauma, and is common in the elderly and in infants. Middle meningeal artery bleeding creates an epidural rather than a subdural hematoma.'),
('8afbc3c0-13f6-5391-9d62-8cd02df46966'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Cerebral amyloid angiopathy (CAA) involves:', '["Deposition of β-amyloid in the walls of small to medium-sized blood vessels in the cerebral cortex and leptomeninges (not in deep structures) — causes lobar hemorrhages in the elderly","Deposition of tau accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deposition of prion protein mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Vascular inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deposition of α-synuclein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deposition of β-amyloid in the walls of small to medium-sized blood vessels in the cerebral cortex and leptomeninges (not in deep structures) — causes lobar hemorrhages in the elderly', 'Cerebral amyloid angiopathy is the deposition of beta-amyloid in small and medium cortical and leptomeningeal vessels of the elderly, predisposing to recurrent lobar hemorrhages. Accumulations of tau, alpha-synuclein, and prion protein occur in different diseases.'),
('002e4acc-5f3d-55ab-808d-df89fbe08de8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Lobar hemorrhage in an older normotensive patient (especially with superficial siderosis) suggests:', '["Cerebral amyloid angiopathy","Hypertensive hemorrhage","Tumor bleed","Arteriovenous malformation","Vasculitis a"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral amyloid angiopathy', 'A lobar hemorrhage in an older normotensive patient, especially with superficial siderosis, is most consistent with cerebral amyloid angiopathy. Hypertensive hemorrhages select deep sites such as the putamen and thalamus.'),
('2db46acd-f396-58fd-a52c-6dbacd7e231b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Giant cell (temporal) arteritis is a:', '["vision loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Granulomatous vasculitis of large and medium arteries (especially the temporal artery)","jaw claudication mediated by specific receptor activation","and elevated ESR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","causing headache leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Granulomatous vasculitis of large and medium arteries (especially the temporal artery)', 'Giant cell arteritis is a granulomatous vasculitis of large and medium arteries, classically the temporal artery, presenting with headache, jaw claudication, visual loss, and a markedly elevated ESR. It attacks extracranial vessels rather than being confined to the CNS.'),
('2a30dd4d-c7bd-5455-abd4-00c1d68ae85b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Primary angiitis of the CNS (PACNS) is a:', '["idiopathic vasculitis confined to the CNS (small to medium vessels)","encephalopathy","Rare","causing headache","and stroke"]'::jsonb, 'Rare', 'Primary angiitis of the CNS is a rare, idiopathic vasculitis confined to the small and medium vessels of the brain and spinal cord, causing headache, encephalopathy, and stroke with angiographic changes. It spares vessels outside the nervous system.'),
('aa628517-5b5a-5895-bba9-4be05ef74070'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'CADASIL (cerebral autosomal dominant arteriopathy with subcortical infarcts and leukoencephalopathy) is caused by:', '["migraine with aura resulting in decreased functional activity","NOTCH1 mutations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NOTCH3 mutations — leads to recurrent subcortical strokes","and dementia in early adulthood without affecting the primary tissue pathway","APP mutations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NOTCH3 mutations — leads to recurrent subcortical strokes', 'CADASIL is caused by mutations in NOTCH3 on chromosome 19, producing recurrent subcortical infarcts, migraine with aura, and early-onset dementia. APP mutations cause familial Alzheimer disease, and NOTCH1 is not linked to this arteriopathy.'),
('8aeedc81-9b1a-5ad8-a45f-56d95b67fb06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Granular osmiophilic material (GOM) in CADASIL is seen on:', '["CT a resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Light microscopy (H&E) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Immunohistochemistry (NOTCH3) mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Electron microscopy — characteristic granular deposits adjacent to the basement membrane of vascular smooth muscle cells","MRI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Electron microscopy — characteristic granular deposits adjacent to the basement membrane of vascular smooth muscle cells', 'Granular osmiophilic material adjacent to the basement membrane of vascular smooth muscle in CADASIL is an electron microscopic finding. Light microscopy, MRI, and routine immunohistochemistry cannot resolve these ultrastructural deposits.'),
('0f087c65-e330-5cd5-ad57-24aa45983f9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Friedreich ataxia is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by:', '["G4C2 repeat in C9orf72 resulting in decreased functional activity","CAG repeat in ATXN1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FXN gene (frataxin) — causing mitochondrial iron accumulation","leading to spinocerebellar degeneration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CTG repeat in DMPK leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FXN gene (frataxin) — causing mitochondrial iron accumulation', 'Friedreich ataxia is caused by a GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FXN gene, which encodes frataxin. Loss of frataxin impairs mitochondrial iron handling and leads to spinocerebellar degeneration. Repeat expansions in C9orf72, ATXN1, and DMPK cause other neurodegenerative disorders, not Friedreich ataxia.'),
('d5b393eb-dfae-548d-b27d-2b16d154867c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The clinical triad of Friedreich ataxia includes:', '["nystagmus","Progressive ataxia","and proprioceptive loss (with positive Babinski)","Ataxia","areflexia (loss of tendon reflexes)"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive ataxia', 'Progressive ataxia is the cardinal motor feature of the classic Friedreich ataxia triad, which also includes areflexia and impaired proprioception with a positive Babinski sign. Nystagmus may occur but is not a defining component, and loss of tendon reflexes is a separate element of the triad.'),
('a0e354ab-1d96-5d2d-9596-0cbf260d89a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Spinal muscular atrophy (SMA) is caused by:', '["Mutations in SOD1 (ALS) resulting in decreased functional activity","Mutations in DMPK (myotonic dystrophy) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SMN1 gene deletion/mutation (loss of survival motor neuron protein) — leading to degeneration of spinal α-motor neurons","Mutations in SMN2 (modifier) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","causing progressive muscle weakness and atrophy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SMN1 gene deletion/mutation (loss of survival motor neuron protein) — leading to degeneration of spinal α-motor neurons', 'SMA is caused by homozygous deletion or mutation of the SMN1 gene, with loss of the survival motor neuron protein producing degeneration of spinal alpha-motor neurons. SOD1 mutations cause ALS and DMPK mutations cause myotonic dystrophy; SMN2 copy number only modifies disease severity.'),
('97d0a1e9-7b83-511a-b681-f1e0fc245a4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Kugelberg-Welander disease is:', '["milder course resulting in decreased functional activity","SMA type I (Werdnig-Hoffmann) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","later-onset mediated by specific receptor activation","SMA type II (intermediate) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SMA type III (juvenile form) — able to walk independently"]'::jsonb, 'SMA type III (juvenile form) — able to walk independently', 'Kugelberg-Welander disease is SMA type III, the juvenile form, in which patients gain independent walking before proximal muscle weakness appears. Werdnig-Hoffmann disease is SMA type I, the severe infantile form with early death.'),
('91ad3f72-ed54-56b9-9869-594010780f06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The characteristic EMG finding in spinal muscular atrophy is:', '["with large motor unit action potentials (reinnervation)","Small","Fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves (denervation)","polyphasic MUAPs (myopathic)","Myotonic discharges (myotonia)"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves (denervation)', 'Denervation of skeletal muscle in SMA produces fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves on EMG, reflecting spontaneous firing of denervated muscle fibers. Large polyphasic units appear later from collateral reinnervation, while myotonic discharges and small myopathic units indicate other disorders.'),
('b31e2ed1-fd6d-5f94-a0c9-6b20f6725af6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Hereditary spastic paraplegia (HSP) is a group of disorders causing:', '["Dementia a resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper limb involvement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Progressive spasticity and weakness of the lower limbs (due to distal axonopathy of the corticospinal tract)","Sensory loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ataxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive spasticity and weakness of the lower limbs (due to distal axonopathy of the corticospinal tract)', 'HSP results from a distal axonopathy of the corticospinal tract, producing progressive spasticity and weakness of the lower limbs. Upper limb involvement, prominent sensory loss, and ataxia are not the defining features of this group.'),
('c132d67d-3247-5ec6-bf02-b87997e75206'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The term upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion signs include:', '["clonus","Babinski sign","Spasticity","hyperreflexia","and weakness ( in a pyramidal distribution)"]'::jsonb, 'Spasticity', 'Upper motor neuron lesions produce spasticity, hyperreflexia, clonus, a Babinski sign, and weakness in a pyramidal distribution because descending inhibitory control is lost. Spasticity is a hallmark UMN finding, whereas flaccid paralysis and atrophy characterize lower motor neuron lesions.'),
('282d4095-005c-504e-9203-c4d69b9ad7c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The term lower motor neuron (LMN) lesion signs include:', '["muscle atrophy","Flaccid paralysis","Spasticity","and areflexia or hyporeflexia","fasciculations"]'::jsonb, 'Flaccid paralysis', 'Lower motor neuron lesions interrupt the final common pathway, producing flaccid paralysis, muscle atrophy, fasciculations, and areflexia or hyporeflexia. Spasticity is an upper motor neuron sign and does not belong to the LMN picture.'),
('493bfda6-e316-532b-a300-60e62802c32b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) has both:', '["fasciculations","hyperreflexia","weakness (LMN)","Babinski (UMN) plus atrophy","UMN and LMN signs (combined): spasticity"]'::jsonb, 'UMN and LMN signs (combined): spasticity', 'ALS uniquely combines upper motor neuron signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski) with lower motor neuron signs (weakness, atrophy, fasciculations). The simultaneous presence of both UMN and LMN findings is what distinguishes ALS from purely UMN or purely LMN disorders.'),
('5a21d508-9ea0-5c5f-b168-042f16b4bf36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common cause of death in ALS is:', '["Aspiration pneumonia resulting in decreased functional activity","Respiratory failure (due to diaphragmatic and intercostal muscle weakness)","Cardiac arrest mediated by specific receptor activation","Sepsis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malnutrition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory failure (due to diaphragmatic and intercostal muscle weakness)', 'Progressive weakness of the diaphragm and intercostal muscles in ALS ultimately causes respiratory failure, the most common cause of death. Aspiration pneumonia and malnutrition contribute to morbidity but are not the leading terminal event.'),
('88ec6930-c00c-5ae7-a086-ab9e8bde364c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Pseudobulbar palsy (UMN lesion of the corticobulbar tract) presents with:', '["and hyperactive jaw jerk","Flaccid dysarthria","emotional lability (pseudobulbar affect)","Spastic dysarthria","dysphagia"]'::jsonb, 'Spastic dysarthria', 'Because pseudobulbar palsy is an upper motor neuron lesion of the corticobulbar tract, speech becomes spastic and slow, with dysphagia, emotional lability, and a hyperactive jaw jerk. Flaccid dysarthria with atrophy and fasciculations is the pattern of bulbar (lower motor neuron) palsy.'),
('f39573a0-4948-514d-b7fd-fd6888c4579e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common cranial nerve affected in Guillain-Barre syndrome is:', '["Oculomotor (CN III) resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoglossal (CN XII) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trigeminal (CN V) mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial nerve (CN VII) — bilateral facial weakness (often the most common cranial nerve involvement)","Glossopharyngeal (CN IX) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (CN VII) — bilateral facial weakness (often the most common cranial nerve involvement)', 'In Guillain-Barre syndrome, bilateral facial weakness is the most common cranial nerve involvement, often presenting as a bifacial palsy. The oculomotor, hypoglossal, and trigeminal nerves are involved much less frequently.'),
('9f2989ed-4f67-5642-be70-e7bc47979e88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Brown-Sequard syndrome (hemisection of the spinal cord) results in:', '["vibration and proprioception (dorsal columns)","Contralateral motor loss","Bilateral sensory loss","and contralateral loss of pain and temperature (spinothalamic) two segments below","Ipsilateral loss of motor function (corticospinal)"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral loss of motor function (corticospinal)', 'Hemisection of the spinal cord severs the corticospinal tract, producing ipsilateral loss of motor function below the lesion, while pain and temperature are lost on the contralateral side beginning two segments below. Vibration and proprioception are lost ipsilaterally through the dorsal columns.'),
('177c8841-a507-5b5e-9720-5fe051d6844a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Syringomyelia (a fluid-filled cavity within the spinal cord) typically presents with:', '["Pure spasticity resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Pure motor weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of vibration and proprioception mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral loss of all sensation without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of pain and temperature in a cape-like distribution (over shoulders and arms) due to crossing spinothalamic fibers being compressed in the central canal area (central cord syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of pain and temperature in a cape-like distribution (over shoulders and arms) due to crossing spinothalamic fibers being compressed in the central canal area (central cord syndrome)', 'A syrinx compresses the decussating spinothalamic fibers near the central canal, producing dissociated loss of pain and temperature over the shoulders and arms in a cape-like distribution. Motor power and the dorsal column modalities are relatively preserved early.'),
('54b8e3fe-d742-58b4-9e21-e76d7f6ff229'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Anterior spinal artery syndrome (spinal cord infarct) causes:', '["Pure motor loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of all sensation (including dorsal columns) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of motor function (corticospinal) and pain/temperature (spinothalamic) below the level of the lesion","Pure sensory loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","with preservation of vibration and proprioception (dorsal columns) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of motor function (corticospinal) and pain/temperature (spinothalamic) below the level of the lesion', 'The anterior spinal artery supplies the corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts but not the dorsal columns. Infarction therefore causes bilateral loss of motor function and of pain/temperature below the lesion, while vibration and proprioception are preserved.'),
('aa065244-c9dc-5636-89b7-dced7054a0e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common spinal epidural mass is:', '["Epidural abscess","Herniated disc","Metastatic tumor","Hematoma","Primary spinal tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Metastatic tumor', 'Metastatic tumor, usually from lung, breast, or prostate cancer, is the most common spinal epidural mass and often presents with back pain and progressive myelopathy. Epidural abscess, hematoma, and herniated disc are much less common causes.'),
('7b98f48c-2bb3-50de-a1f9-797b96a930d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Conus medullaris syndrome (compression of the sacral spinal cord) presents with:', '["Symmetric proximal weakness","Unilateral leg weakness","Saddle anesthesia","and lower limb weakness that is symmetric and distal","early bowel/bladder dysfunction (retention/incontinence)"]'::jsonb, 'Saddle anesthesia', 'Compression of the sacral cord at the conus medullaris produces saddle anesthesia together with early, symmetric bowel and bladder dysfunction and distal leg weakness. Saddle anesthesia reflects loss of the sacral sensory segments at the conus.'),
('85c00288-00fc-5297-b7b1-5cbbdc5f150b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Cauda equina syndrome (compression of the lumbosacral nerve roots) presents with:', '["Asymmetric lower limb pain","with late or less severe bladder involvement (compared to conus medullaris)","Early bladder involvement","weakness","and sensory loss"]'::jsonb, 'Asymmetric lower limb pain', 'Cauda equina syndrome compresses the lumbosacral nerve roots below the conus, producing asymmetric radicular leg pain, weakness, and sensory loss with later or less severe bladder involvement. Early symmetric bladder dysfunction and saddle anesthesia are more typical of conus medullaris lesions.'),
('7aa03ba6-ba86-59a4-8915-de83d3b6376b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The spinal cord normally ends at which vertebral level in adults?', '["L3-L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","T12-L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L2-L3 mediated by specific receptor activation","T10-T11 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1-L2 (between L1 and L2)"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 (between L1 and L2)', 'In adults the spinal cord terminates at the L1-L2 vertebral level, which is why lumbar puncture is performed below this level to avoid cord injury. In infants the conus medullaris ends lower, near L3.'),
('035b6331-6b2d-5c7c-a5d5-e095401f6e9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The filum terminale is a:', '["Extension of the arachnoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrovascular band accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extension of the dura mediated by specific receptor activation","Pial extension from the conus medullaris to the coccyx (anchors the spinal cord inferiorly)","Nerve root leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pial extension from the conus medullaris to the coccyx (anchors the spinal cord inferiorly)', 'The filum terminale is a thin pial extension running from the conus medullaris down to the coccyx, anchoring the spinal cord inferiorly within the thecal sac. It is neither a nerve root, a dural extension, nor part of the arachnoid.'),
('12defba7-dc99-57f4-bd15-51ae703966b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The denticulate ligaments are:', '["Bands of the cauda equina resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrous bands in the filum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pial extensions that attach the spinal cord laterally to the dura (provide stabilization within the spinal canal)","Extensions of the arachnoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nerve roots leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pial extensions that attach the spinal cord laterally to the dura (provide stabilization within the spinal canal)', 'The denticulate ligaments are lateral pial extensions that attach the spinal cord to the inner surface of the dura, stabilizing it within the spinal canal. They are not nerve roots, arachnoid extensions, or components of the cauda equina.'),
('6df6241c-27f6-55d3-b09a-626f7d45bac5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The lumbar puncture (LP) is typically performed at which level to avoid spinal cord injury?', '["Cervical region resulting in decreased functional activity","T12-L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C1-C2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacral region without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace (below the level of the conus medullaris)"]'::jsonb, 'L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace (below the level of the conus medullaris)', 'Because the adult spinal cord ends at L1-L2, the L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace lies below the conus medullaris and is the safe site for lumbar puncture. Punctures at higher levels risk needle injury to the spinal cord.'),
('2316ec03-5525-5ca3-bfbb-c0c9c258941e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The term myelomeningocele is a form of:', '["Closed spina bifida resulting in decreased functional activity","Anencephaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meningocele only ( neural tissue) mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural tube defect (spina bifida aperta) where the spinal cord and meninges protrude through a vertebral defect","Encephalocele leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neural tube defect (spina bifida aperta) where the spinal cord and meninges protrude through a vertebral defect', 'Myelomeningocele is an open neural tube defect (spina bifida aperta) in which both the spinal cord and meninges protrude through a lumbosacral vertebral defect, and it is often accompanied by hydrocephalus and Chiari II malformation. Meningocele alone does not contain neural tissue.'),
('2f1d0dc3-04e7-50db-9baa-f3e5fe5ff211'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Spina bifida occulta is a:', '["A completely open spinal canal","without herniation of neural tissue — often asymptomatic","Failure of fusion of the vertebral arches (usually at L5-S1)","An encephalocele","A myelomeningocele"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of fusion of the vertebral arches (usually at L5-S1)', 'Spina bifida occulta is a failure of posterior vertebral arch fusion, usually at L5-S1, without herniation of neural tissue, so it is usually asymptomatic. In contrast, meningocele and myelomeningocele involve protrusion of meninges and cord through the defect.'),
('96c97d37-c2f6-5430-b43c-d607293feb8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The foramen of Monro connects the:', '["Intraventricular foramen of Luschka","Fourth ventricle to the subarachnoid space","Lateral ventricles to the third ventricle","Aqueduct of Sylvius","Third ventricle to the fourth ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral ventricles to the third ventricle', 'The interventricular foramina of Monro drain CSF from each lateral ventricle into the third ventricle. The cerebral aqueduct connects the third and fourth ventricles, and the foramina of Luschka and Magendie open into the subarachnoid space.'),
('ef53df51-2764-55d5-ae9b-8d268e6e0db7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The aqueduct of Sylvius (cerebral aqueduct):', '["Connects fourth to subarachnoid space","Connects third to lateral","Runs through the midbrain","connecting the third ventricle to the fourth ventricle","Connects lateral ventricles to third"]'::jsonb, 'Runs through the midbrain', 'The cerebral aqueduct (of Sylvius) runs through the midbrain and connects the third ventricle to the fourth ventricle; narrowing of this channel causes non-communicating hydrocephalus. It does not connect the ventricles to the subarachnoid space.'),
('fb2a1e56-4865-5075-a8d4-29b48b243cd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'CSF is absorbed (drained) into the venous system primarily through:', '["Arachnoid granulations (villi) that project into the superior sagittal sinus","Lymphatic channels a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pia mater mediated by specific receptor activation","Ependyma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Choroid plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arachnoid granulations (villi) that project into the superior sagittal sinus', 'CSF is reabsorbed by arachnoid granulations that project into the superior sagittal sinus and other dural venous sinuses. The choroid plexus produces CSF, while the pia mater and ependyma are not sites of absorption.'),
('9c943705-867e-5864-86ea-d9d459c282ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The three foramina that connect the ventricular system with the subarachnoid space are:', '["Aqueduct of Sylvius alone resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Foramina of Monro only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Two lateral foramina of Luschka (in the pontomedullary junction) and one midline foramen of Magendie (in the fourth ventricle roof)","Foramen magnum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two lateral foramina of Luschka (in the pontomedullary junction) and one midline foramen of Magendie (in the fourth ventricle roof)', 'The two lateral foramina of Luschka and the single midline foramen of Magendie are the three openings of the fourth ventricle that allow CSF to enter the subarachnoid space. Blockage of these foramina leads to hydrocephalus.'),
('15a50fc9-1212-5b71-83d5-175f40b86dd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) typically presents with the triad:', '["urinary incontinence","Headache","vomiting","and dementia (cognitive decline)","Gait apraxia (magnetic gait)"]'::jsonb, 'Gait apraxia (magnetic gait)', 'Normal pressure hydrocephalus presents with the triad of gait apraxia (magnetic gait), dementia, and urinary incontinence. The gait disturbance is usually the earliest and most characteristic feature and often improves after CSF shunting.'),
('cd2188a8-0eb2-59e1-a56f-a8de8ed76217'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common cause of congenital hydrocephalus is:', '["Chiari malformation resulting in decreased functional activity","Aqueductal stenosis (X-linked or sporadic) — narrowing of the cerebral aqueduct","Arachnoid cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Myelomeningocele without affecting the primary tissue pathway","leading to non-communicating hydrocephalus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aqueductal stenosis (X-linked or sporadic) — narrowing of the cerebral aqueduct', 'Aqueductal stenosis, which may be X-linked or sporadic, is the most common cause of congenital hydrocephalus because it blocks CSF flow between the third and fourth ventricles, producing non-communicating hydrocephalus. Chiari II malformation and myelomeningocele are associated lesions but are less commonly the primary cause.'),
('df20a733-f278-55d7-96af-aae3956251fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Chiari malformation type II (Arnold-Chiari) is almost always associated with:', '["Sacral dimple resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Syringomyelia alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myelomeningocele (neural tube defect) and hydrocephalus — herniation of the cerebellar vermis and brainstem through the foramen magnum","Dandy-Walker malformation a without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arachnoid cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myelomeningocele (neural tube defect) and hydrocephalus — herniation of the cerebellar vermis and brainstem through the foramen magnum', 'Chiari II (Arnold-Chiari) malformation is almost always associated with myelomeningocele and hydrocephalus, with descent of the cerebellar vermis and brainstem through the foramen magnum. Syringomyelia may accompany it but is not the defining association.'),
('bbd04bf4-03db-5ecf-8e51-0e02d781d22c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Dandy-Walker malformation is:', '["Normal ventricular system resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Small posterior fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enlarged cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","hydrocephalus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Agenesis of the cerebellar vermis with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle (cyst fills the posterior fossa) — associated with hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 'Agenesis of the cerebellar vermis with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle (cyst fills the posterior fossa) — associated with hydrocephalus', 'Dandy-Walker malformation features agenesis or hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle filling an enlarged posterior fossa, often with hydrocephalus. This contrasts with the small posterior fossa and downward herniation seen in Chiari II.'),
('b0109cbe-62f1-567d-a29f-207d227539d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Heterotopia is a:', '["Disorder of myelination resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Disorder of synaptogenesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Destructive lesion mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Disorder of neuronal migration — groups of neurons that failed to migrate to their proper location and remain in the periventricular or subcortical white matter"]'::jsonb, 'Disorder of neuronal migration — groups of neurons that failed to migrate to their proper location and remain in the periventricular or subcortical white matter', 'Heterotopia is a neuronal migration disorder in which neurons fail to reach the cortex and remain as ectopic nodules in the periventricular or subcortical white matter, frequently causing seizures. It is not a tumor, a destructive lesion, or a disorder of myelination.'),
('53c12030-933b-5fae-8c65-0cf1c79ef7c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Schizencephaly is a:', '["A midline defect","A failure of neural tube closure","A cleft (full-thickness) in the cerebral hemisphere","An acquired porencephalic cyst","lined by gray matter and communicating with the ventricle — a neuronal migration disorder"]'::jsonb, 'A cleft (full-thickness) in the cerebral hemisphere', 'Schizencephaly is a full-thickness cleft in the cerebral hemisphere lined by gray matter and communicating with the ventricle, representing a neuronal migration disorder. It differs from an acquired porencephalic cyst, which is not lined by gray matter.'),
('9fe62338-8b59-51da-aedf-412c070b6af8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Polymicrogyria is a malformation with:', '["A single gyrus resulting in decreased functional activity","Fused gyri accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","abnormal gyri with abnormal cortical lamination — a neuronal migration/post-migration disorder","Too many small without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A smooth brain with gyri leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'abnormal gyri with abnormal cortical lamination — a neuronal migration/post-migration disorder', 'Polymicrogyria is a neuronal migration disorder characterized by too many small gyri with abnormal cortical lamination, giving an irregular brain surface. A smooth brain without gyri describes lissencephaly, and fused gyri are the pattern of pachygyria.'),
('c8494dc7-6897-5b7e-b4c5-5bd64549e27e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The structure that connects the third ventricle to the fourth ventricle is the:', '["Foramen of Luschka","Foramen of Magendie","Cerebral aqueduct (aqueduct of Sylvius)","Foramen of Monro","Central canal"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral aqueduct (aqueduct of Sylvius)', 'The cerebral aqueduct (of Sylvius) is the channel that connects the third ventricle to the fourth ventricle. The foramina of Monro join the lateral ventricles to the third, while the foramina of Luschka and Magendie drain the fourth ventricle into the subarachnoid space.'),
('26865534-7bb1-5946-9054-3c56320e4fe9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The septal area is involved in:', '["Language resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory processing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emotion and reward (septal nuclei are part of the limbic system)","Memory consolidation a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor planning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Emotion and reward (septal nuclei are part of the limbic system)', 'The septal nuclei, part of the limbic system, are involved in emotion and reward and connect with the hippocampus and hypothalamus. They are not primarily concerned with language, sensory processing, or motor planning.'),
('b2e03fb6-43e6-5f9a-aa52-def1bde9fdeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The nucleus basalis of Meynert (basal nucleus of Meynert) is a major source of:', '["Cholinergic innervation to the cerebral cortex — degenerates in Alzheimer disease","GABAergic innervation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dopaminergic innervation mediated by specific receptor activation","Serotonergic innervation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Noradrenergic innervation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cholinergic innervation to the cerebral cortex — degenerates in Alzheimer disease', 'The nucleus basalis of Meynert provides the major cholinergic innervation to the cerebral cortex, and its degeneration in Alzheimer disease contributes to memory loss. Dopaminergic, serotonergic, and noradrenergic innervation arises from other brainstem nuclei.'),
('4b2eacf1-14f3-5d84-a315-e5f07ce9829a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The locus coeruleus is the main source of:', '["Noradrenergic (norepinephrine) projections throughout the brain — involved in arousal","Cholinergic projections accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dopaminergic projections mediated by specific receptor activation","and stress response without affecting the primary tissue pathway","attention leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Noradrenergic (norepinephrine) projections throughout the brain — involved in arousal', 'The locus coeruleus is the principal source of noradrenergic (norepinephrine) projections throughout the brain and is important for arousal, attention, and the stress response. Cholinergic, dopaminergic, and serotonergic projections come from other nuclei.'),
('f72ee757-cdef-527b-aa41-14eb4d3c7e90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The raphe nuclei (midline brainstem) are the main source of:', '["appetite resulting in decreased functional activity","and pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","sleep mediated by specific receptor activation","Dopamine projections without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serotonin (5-HT) projections throughout the brain — regulate mood"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin (5-HT) projections throughout the brain — regulate mood', 'The midline raphe nuclei are the main source of serotonin (5-HT) projections throughout the brain, regulating mood, sleep, appetite, and pain. Dopaminergic and cholinergic projections arise from the VTA and basal forebrain, respectively.'),
('8408a176-fef0-53f9-b997-b5cef0155d20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The ventral tegmental area (VTA) is a source of:', '["motivation resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Dopaminergic projections to the striatum (nigrostriatal) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and addiction mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholinergic projections without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dopaminergic projections to the nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex (the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways) — involved in reward"]'::jsonb, 'Dopaminergic projections to the nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex (the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways) — involved in reward', 'The ventral tegmental area sends dopaminergic projections through the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways to the nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex, mediating reward and motivation. Nigrostriatal dopaminergic projections arise from the substantia nigra pars compacta.'),
('53755f9a-bcc3-5938-9929-7317bff904ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc) provides:', '["Cholinergic input resulting in decreased functional activity","coordinating movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GABAergic input mediated by specific receptor activation","Noradrenergic input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dopaminergic input to the striatum (caudate-putamen) — the nigrostriatal pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Dopaminergic input to the striatum (caudate-putamen) — the nigrostriatal pathway', 'The substantia nigra pars compacta supplies dopaminergic input to the striatum (caudate and putamen) via the nigrostriatal pathway, which facilitates movement, and its degeneration causes Parkinson disease. It does not provide cholinergic, GABAergic, or noradrenergic input.'),
('43ceb1ad-f0a1-58f5-82a4-3dae012f4815'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Wearing-off phenomena in PD (motor fluctuations) occur due to:', '["Dopamine toxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Disease progression and intermittent oral levodopa absorption causing fluctuating dopamine levels","Loss of efficacy mediated by specific receptor activation","On-off phenomenon only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Depression a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Disease progression and intermittent oral levodopa absorption causing fluctuating dopamine levels', 'Wearing-off (motor fluctuation) reflects disease progression plus the short half-life and intermittent absorption of oral levodopa, which cause fluctuating brain dopamine levels and end-of-dose worsening. It is not due to dopamine toxicity or to isolated loss of drug efficacy.'),
('320f82dd-3f75-57b0-a77a-ce3330b87685'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Levodopa (L-DOPA) is the most effective treatment for PD because:', '["It increases dopamine release resulting in decreased functional activity","It blocks acetylcholine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It directly activates dopamine receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","It is a precursor that crosses the BBB and is converted to dopamine in the brain (replacing lost dopamine)","It prevents dopamine breakdown leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is a precursor that crosses the BBB and is converted to dopamine in the brain (replacing lost dopamine)', 'Levodopa crosses the blood-brain barrier as a precursor and is converted to dopamine by DOPA decarboxylase in the brain, restoring deficient striatal dopamine. Dopamine itself cannot cross the blood-brain barrier, so direct replacement by the drug is not possible.'),
('04c64125-0bbe-5f0c-95f1-fba89835a3bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The most common cause of early-onset cognitive impairment (dementia) in PD is:', '["Frontotemporal dementia resulting in decreased functional activity","NPH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alzheimer disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Dementia with Lewy bodies (which shares the same α-synuclein pathology as PD)","Vascular dementia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dementia with Lewy bodies (which shares the same α-synuclein pathology as PD)', 'Dementia with Lewy bodies shares the same alpha-synuclein pathology as PD and is the most common cause of early cognitive impairment in Parkinson disease, often with fluctuating cognition and visual hallucinations. Alzheimer disease is a separate proteinopathy.'),
('469201c7-7e3e-523a-b924-00ce6bbb9f3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Wilsons disease (hepatolenticular degeneration) is an autosomal recessive disorder of copper metabolism due to mutations in the:', '["liver (cirrhosis) resulting in decreased functional activity","HTT gene accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ATP7B gene (copper-transporting ATPase) — leads to copper accumulation in the brain (basal ganglia)","ATP7A gene without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and eyes (Kayser-Fleischer rings) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ATP7B gene (copper-transporting ATPase) — leads to copper accumulation in the brain (basal ganglia)', 'Wilson disease results from mutations in ATP7B, a copper-transporting ATPase, causing copper accumulation in the liver, basal ganglia, and cornea. ATP7A mutations cause Menkes disease, and the HTT gene causes Huntington disease.'),
('f218f7a4-f254-55b3-93da-239d0551d506'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Kayser-Fleischer rings in Wilson disease are:', '["Copper deposits in the retina resulting in decreased functional activity","Amyloid deposits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Melanin deposits in the conjunctiva mediated by specific receptor activation","Copper deposits in the Descemet membrane of the cornea (at the limbus) — visible as greenish-brown rings on slit-lamp examination","Iron deposits in the cornea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Copper deposits in the Descemet membrane of the cornea (at the limbus) — visible as greenish-brown rings on slit-lamp examination', 'Kayser-Fleischer rings are greenish-brown deposits of copper in the Descemet membrane of the cornea at the limbus, best seen on slit-lamp examination. Iron, amyloid, and melanin deposits do not produce this finding.'),
('2610e08e-636f-5adf-b748-fd846cc319df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Huntington chorea is a hyperkinetic movement disorder caused by:', '["Excess acetylcholine activity resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of GABAergic medium spiny neurons in the striatum (caudate and putamen) — leading to disinhibition of the thalamus and overactivity of the direct pathway","Excess dopamine activity mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of nigrostriatal dopamine a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of GABAergic medium spiny neurons in the striatum (caudate and putamen) — leading to disinhibition of the thalamus and overactivity of the direct pathway', 'Huntington chorea is caused by loss of GABAergic medium spiny neurons in the striatum, which disinhibits the thalamus and overactivates the direct pathway to produce chorea. Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra or cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis characterizes other disorders.'),
('da378c9b-444d-5297-ac0f-f793eb203c76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Essential tremor (benign essential tremor) is a:', '["Psychogenic tremor resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Common accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","bilateral action/postural tremor (especially of the hands) without other parkinsonian signs — responds to alcohol and beta-blockers (propranolol)","Intention tremor (cerebellar) without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Resting tremor (parkinsonian) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'bilateral action/postural tremor (especially of the hands) without other parkinsonian signs — responds to alcohol and beta-blockers (propranolol)', 'Essential tremor is a common bilateral action and postural tremor of the hands without rigidity or bradykinesia, and it characteristically improves with alcohol and beta-blockers such as propranolol. A resting tremor with parkinsonian signs suggests Parkinson disease, while an intention tremor suggests a cerebellar lesion.'),
('3e0d96fd-0eb5-5cae-b8a2-1a0fb4ebdb9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The dentate nucleus of the cerebellum projects to the contralateral:', '["Reticular formation resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventral lateral (VL) nucleus of the thalamus (dentatothalamic tract) — part of the cerebellar output to the motor cortex","Red nucleus (via dentatorubral) mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inferior olive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral lateral (VL) nucleus of the thalamus (dentatothalamic tract) — part of the cerebellar output to the motor cortex', 'The dentate nucleus projects through the dentatothalamic tract to the contralateral ventral lateral (VL) nucleus of the thalamus, which then relays to the motor cortex. The red nucleus, inferior olive, and spinal cord are not the primary targets of dentate output.'),
('24baf343-482c-55d5-b369-b1a2553e24f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The corticospinal (pyramidal) tract originates from the:', '["The cerebellum","and supplementary motor area — 30% from Betz cells","premotor cortex (area 6)","The basal ganglia","Primary motor cortex (area 4)"]'::jsonb, 'Primary motor cortex (area 4)', 'The corticospinal (pyramidal) tract arises chiefly from the primary motor cortex (Brodmann area 4), including its giant Betz cells in cortical layer V. Premotor cortex (area 6) and somatosensory areas contribute additional fibers, but the cerebellum and basal ganglia project only indirectly onto the tract.'),
('2c8b407c-2e9a-530b-a2ac-1f1c110a37d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Decussation of the corticospinal tract (pyramidal decussation) occurs at the:', '["The internal capsule resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Level of the caudal medulla (pyramidal decussation) — 85-90% of fibers cross to form the lateral corticospinal tract","The spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Level of the caudal medulla (pyramidal decussation) — 85-90% of fibers cross to form the lateral corticospinal tract', 'Around 85-90% of corticospinal fibers cross in the pyramidal decussation at the junction of the medulla and spinal cord to form the lateral corticospinal tract; the remainder continues as the anterior corticospinal tract. The tract traverses the internal capsule and cerebral peduncle above this level without crossing.'),
('af7c64e3-8807-584a-a3cd-feef415ada5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The spinothalamic tract (pain and temperature) decussates at the:', '["Level of the spinal cord (anterior white commissure) — within one to two segments of entering the cord","The pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","The midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Level of the spinal cord (anterior white commissure) — within one to two segments of entering the cord', 'Pain and temperature fibers synapse in the dorsal horn, after which the second-order neurons cross in the anterior (ventral) white commissure within one to two segments of entry and ascend in the lateral spinothalamic tract. The tract does not cross at the pons, medulla, or midbrain, and it terminates in the ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus.'),
('b71d410c-0b95-5451-9a6f-8338ec3aa52d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The mesencephalic nucleus of the trigeminal nerve (CN V) contains:', '["The main sensory nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","The motor nucleus of CN V accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The trigeminal ganglion a mediated by specific receptor activation","Pseudo-unipolar neurons for proprioception from muscles of mastication (jaw jerk reflex)","The spinal nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudo-unipolar neurons for proprioception from muscles of mastication (jaw jerk reflex)', 'The mesencephalic nucleus of V is unusual because it contains the cell bodies of primary sensory (pseudo-unipolar) neurons, which receive proprioceptive input from the muscles of mastication and mediate the jaw jerk reflex. The main sensory, spinal, and motor nuclei handle other trigeminal functions, and the trigeminal ganglion lies outside the brainstem.'),
('de5b2d34-8ead-5b91-bd04-0759011bf44b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The trigeminal nerve (CN V) has three major divisions:', '["V3 (mandibular)","V1 (ophthalmic)","V2 (maxillary)","V1-ophthalmic","V2-zygomatic"]'::jsonb, 'V1 (ophthalmic)', 'The three trigeminal divisions are the ophthalmic (V1), maxillary (V2), and mandibular (V3) nerves; V1 (ophthalmic) passes through the superior orbital fissure and supplies sensation to the scalp, forehead, and cornea. "V2-zygomatic" is not one of the standard named divisions.'),
('14bf75ee-a19b-5ee0-b1cc-b3b40fc8e53a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) innervates:', '["posterior belly of digastric","and stylohyoid","stapedius","Muscles of facial expression","Muscles of mastication"]'::jsonb, 'Muscles of facial expression', 'The facial nerve is the motor nerve of the muscles of facial expression, and it also supplies the posterior belly of the digastric, stylohyoid, and stapedius. The muscles of mastication are served by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve, not by CN VII.'),
('d2b41c3f-811e-5215-8643-0566cc311832'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) provides:', '["Sensation to the posterior 1/3 of the tongue (taste and general sensation)","Taste to the anterior 2/3","Motor to the larynx","and the carotid body/sinus; motor to the stylopharyngeus; parasympathetic to the parotid","the pharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Sensation to the posterior 1/3 of the tongue (taste and general sensation)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve carries general and special (taste) sensation from the posterior one-third of the tongue and sends afferents from the carotid body and carotid sinus. Taste to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue belongs to the facial nerve, and the larynx is supplied by the vagus.'),
('03e0e64d-1e3f-5934-acc6-a6657a800a0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The vagus nerve (CN X) provides:', '["and larynx (via the recurrent laryngeal nerve); parasympathetic to the thoracic and abdominal viscera; sensory to the epiglottis (taste) and external ear","pharynx","Motor to the palate","Parasympathetic to the head","Motor to the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Motor to the palate', 'One key motor role of the vagus nerve is innervation of the palatal muscles through its pharyngeal plexus; it also supplies the pharynx and larynx and provides parasympathetic and visceral sensory fibers to the thoracic and abdominal organs. Structures of the head and the tongue are served by other cranial nerves.'),
('d2ea60bc-1fdd-5560-b502-63323da6c138'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve (branch of CN X) supplies:', '["The palate resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid (which is supplied by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve)","The cricothyroid only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid (which is supplied by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve)', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies every intrinsic laryngeal muscle except the cricothyroid, which is innervated by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. This pattern underlies the characteristic hoarse voice and paramedian vocal fold position seen in recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy.'),
('4b775346-4a8a-5512-90f6-45d36b343675'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The accessory nerve (CN XI) innervates:', '["The pharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","The sternocleidomastoid (SCM) and the trapezius muscle","The muscles of facial expression mediated by specific receptor activation","The larynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sternocleidomastoid (SCM) and the trapezius muscle', 'The spinal accessory nerve innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles; weakness of these muscles follows accessory nerve injury. The pharynx and larynx are supplied by the vagus, the tongue by the hypoglossal nerve, and the facial muscles by CN VII.'),
('5194843c-6f83-5b92-b424-636af1377bed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The jugular foramen syndrome (Vernet syndrome) involves compression or involvement of:', '["CN IX","X","and XI (due to mass lesions in the jugular foramen)","VI","CN V"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX', 'Vernet (jugular foramen) syndrome compresses the glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and accessory (XI) nerves as they traverse the jugular foramen, typically from a mass in that region. The deficits include palatal, pharyngeal, and laryngeal paralysis, loss of posterior tongue sensation, and SCM-trapezius weakness; CN V and CN VI are uninvolved.'),
('c42b6ab7-037e-5165-9b9f-72617f9fc744'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The foramen lacerum is closed by cartilage in life but transmits:', '["The internal carotid artery (as it enters the cranial cavity) and the greater petrosal nerve (after exiting the petrous temporal bone)","CN V3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The hypoglossal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Nothing without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal jugular vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal carotid artery (as it enters the cranial cavity) and the greater petrosal nerve (after exiting the petrous temporal bone)', 'The foramen lacerum is filled with cartilage during life, yet the internal carotid artery courses through its upper portion into the cranial cavity and the greater petrosal nerve leaves the skull through it. The hypoglossal nerve passes through the hypoglossal canal and the internal jugular vein through the jugular foramen.'),
('5302dd9c-adf4-5f09-85fc-4f3d3095d6cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The olfactory pathway:', '["The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to synapse in the olfactory bulb","The optic canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The superior orbital fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","The foramina of Luschka without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The foramen rotundum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to synapse in the olfactory bulb', 'Olfactory nerve fibers (CN I) pass through the foramina of the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone and synapse on mitral and tufted cells in the olfactory bulb. Fractures of the cribriform plate can therefore tear the olfactory filaments and produce anosmia.'),
('6bd53cbb-45b0-5474-949a-973c4bdb41b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The pupillary light reflex (direct and consensual) involves:', '["CN V only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Optic nerve → LGN → visual cortex mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VI only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic nerve (CN II) → pretectal nucleus → Edinger-Westphal nucleus (both sides) → ciliary ganglion → sphincter pupillae"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (CN II) → pretectal nucleus → Edinger-Westphal nucleus (both sides) → ciliary ganglion → sphincter pupillae', 'Light detected by the optic nerve (CN II) signals the pretectal nucleus, whose bilateral projections reach both Edinger-Westphal nuclei; parasympathetic fibers then travel in CN III to the ciliary ganglion and on to the sphincter pupillae, producing direct and consensual constriction. The lateral geniculate body and visual cortex are not part of this reflex.'),
('894b5c4b-40f5-5e7f-a5b2-65ba98cce671'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The near reflex (accommodation-convergence-miosis) involves:', '["The superior colliculus only resulting in decreased functional activity","The pretectal nucleus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and pupillary constriction (sphincter pupillae) mediated by specific receptor activation","convergence (medial recti) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The visual cortex and the Edinger-Westphal and Perlia nuclei (midbrain) — causing: lens thickening (ciliary muscle contraction)"]'::jsonb, 'The visual cortex and the Edinger-Westphal and Perlia nuclei (midbrain) — causing: lens thickening (ciliary muscle contraction)', 'The near reflex is coordinated by the visual cortex together with the Edinger-Westphal nucleus (ciliary muscle and sphincter pupillae) and the Perlia (convergence) nucleus, producing lens thickening, convergence of the medial recti, and pupillary constriction. The superior colliculus and pretectal nucleus alone do not integrate this three-part response.'),
('b7b38752-357d-5c65-af5d-9a3311dd6e79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Argyll Robertson pupils (prostitutes'' pupils) are:', '["Constrict to light but not near resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Small accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Are mid-position and fixed to both light and near mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Are fixed and dilated without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","irregular pupils that constrict with accommodation (near response) but not to light (light-near dissociation) — pathognomonic for neurosyphilis (tabes dorsalis)"]'::jsonb, 'irregular pupils that constrict with accommodation (near response) but not to light (light-near dissociation) — pathognomonic for neurosyphilis (tabes dorsalis)', 'Argyll Robertson pupils are typically small and irregular, lose the light reflex, but retain the accommodation (near) response, the classic light-near dissociation linked to neurosyphilis (tabes dorsalis). The opposite pattern (constriction to light with loss of the near response) and fixed mid-position pupils describe other pupillary disorders.'),
('673dc1dc-cff3-554c-9cf5-b2dcfd8f891b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) uses input from the semicircular canals to generate:', '["Compensatory eye movements that are opposite to head rotation (stabilizing the visual image on the retina)","Pursuit movements a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Voluntary saccades mediated by specific receptor activation","Accommodation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pupillary constriction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Compensatory eye movements that are opposite to head rotation (stabilizing the visual image on the retina)', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex drives conjugate eye movements opposite to the direction of head rotation, using semicircular canal input, so that the retinal image remains steady during head movement. Voluntary saccades, pursuit, accommodation, and pupillary constriction are mediated by unrelated circuits.'),
('5cbccc41-4071-5175-b7c0-e413f3449790'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The oculomotor nerve (CN III) nuclei in the midbrain include:', '["the Edinger-Westphal nucleus (parasympathetic to ciliary ganglion — for pupil constriction and accommodation)","The trochlear nucleus","Only the Edinger-Westphal nucleus","The main motor nucleus (all extraocular muscles except LR and SO)","Only the motor nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'The main motor nucleus (all extraocular muscles except LR and SO)', 'The oculomotor complex has a large somatic motor nucleus that supplies all extraocular muscles except the lateral rectus (CN VI) and superior oblique (CN IV), plus the Edinger-Westphal nucleus that sends parasympathetic fibers to the ciliary ganglion for pupillary constriction and accommodation. The trochlear nucleus is a separate midbrain structure.'),
('7bd3afbd-5098-519c-9737-bc831928b0bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The trochlear nerve (CN IV) is unique among cranial nerves because it:', '["Exists laterally resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Has sensory only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Does not decussate mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Has motor only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is the only cranial nerve that exits the brainstem dorsally (posteriorly) and decussates completely before innervating the contralateral superior oblique"]'::jsonb, 'Is the only cranial nerve that exits the brainstem dorsally (posteriorly) and decussates completely before innervating the contralateral superior oblique', 'The trochlear nerve is the only cranial nerve to exit from the dorsal (posterior) aspect of the brainstem and the only one to decussate completely, innervating the contralateral superior oblique. A trochlear palsy therefore affects the eye contralateral to the brainstem lesion.'),
('89df2cff-1c48-5f8a-80a0-0c7c1197a3c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The abducens nerve (CN VI) innervates the:', '["Medial rectus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior rectus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral rectus muscle (abduction of the eye) — lesions cause medial deviation of the affected eye (adduction) and horizontal diplopia","Inferior rectus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior oblique leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral rectus muscle (abduction of the eye) — lesions cause medial deviation of the affected eye (adduction) and horizontal diplopia', 'The abducens nerve (CN VI) innervates the lateral rectus, which abducts the eye; a lesion paralyzes abduction, so the affected eye drifts medially and produces horizontal diplopia on lateral gaze. The medial, superior, and inferior recti are supplied by CN III and the superior oblique by CN IV.'),
('102c3efd-d2b9-51a4-b6df-cecc219ef6eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) is caused by a lesion in the:', '["Pontine paramedian reticular formation (PPRF) resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","causing failure of adduction of the ipsilateral eye on lateral gaze with nystagmus in the abducting eye mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) — which coordinates the conjugate gaze between CN III and CN VI nuclei","CN VI nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) — which coordinates the conjugate gaze between CN III and CN VI nuclei', 'Internuclear ophthalmoplegia results from a lesion of the medial longitudinal fasciculus, which links the CN VI nucleus with the contralateral CN III nucleus for conjugate horizontal gaze. On gaze toward the opposite side, the eye on the lesioned side cannot adduct while the abducting eye develops nystagmus; PPRF and the oculomotor nuclei are separate.'),
('52361b36-2d2b-54cb-a2dd-018817f4974a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The one-and-a-half syndrome is caused by:', '["CN III lesion resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","PPRF lesion only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lesion involving the pontine paramedian reticular formation (PPRF) and the ipsilateral MLF — resulting in: only the contralateral eye can abduct (with nystagmus); no other horizontal eye movements","CN VI lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MLF lesion only (INO) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A lesion involving the pontine paramedian reticular formation (PPRF) and the ipsilateral MLF — resulting in: only the contralateral eye can abduct (with nystagmus); no other horizontal eye movements', 'One-and-a-half syndrome is produced by a single pontine lesion that destroys the PPRF and the adjacent medial longitudinal fasciculus on the same side. All ipsilateral conjugate horizontal movements are lost and the ipsilateral eye cannot adduct on attempted movement to the opposite side, so only the contralateral eye retains abduction (with nystagmus).'),
('28af9b6e-751c-548d-afe6-8722b725dc77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The oculocephalic (doll''s eye) maneuver is used to test:', '["Convergence resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual acuity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) — in comatose patients to assess brainstem integrity","Accommodation a without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pupillary reflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) — in comatose patients to assess brainstem integrity', 'The oculocephalic (doll''s eye) maneuver rotates the head to stimulate the semicircular canals and thereby tests the vestibulo-ocular reflex; in a comatose patient the eyes should deviate opposite to the direction of rotation if the brainstem pathways are intact. Absence of this response indicates brainstem dysfunction, while convergence, accommodation, and pupillary reactions evaluate other circuits.'),
('1f69528f-aa7a-550a-8327-62edef893999'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The acoustic reflex threshold is:', '["The lowest sound level that elicits stapedius contraction (normally 70-85 dB SL above the hearing threshold)","The threshold of hearing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The loudest sound tolerated mediated by specific receptor activation","The threshold of pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The level of uncomfortable loudness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lowest sound level that elicits stapedius contraction (normally 70-85 dB SL above the hearing threshold)', 'The acoustic reflex threshold is the minimum sound intensity that triggers contraction of the stapedius muscle, normally about 70-85 dB above the hearing threshold (sensation level). It differs from the hearing threshold, loudness tolerance, and pain thresholds and is used to cross-check auditory test results.'),
('fac32f93-cf76-54d3-8173-727760deee75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Blink reflex testing (trigeminal-facial reflex) involves:', '["The optic nerve and oculomotor nerve resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The glossopharyngeal and vagus nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The vagus and hypoglossal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Tapping the glabella (forehead) or stimulating the supraorbital nerve (CN V1) causes both orbicularis oculi to contract (CN VII) — tests the trigeminal afferent and facial efferent pathways","The facial nerve alone a leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tapping the glabella (forehead) or stimulating the supraorbital nerve (CN V1) causes both orbicularis oculi to contract (CN VII) — tests the trigeminal afferent and facial efferent pathways', 'The blink reflex is elicited by tapping the glabella or stimulating the supraorbital branch of V1; the afferent limb runs in the trigeminal nerve and the efferent response contracts the orbicularis oculi via the facial nerve, so it tests both trigeminal afferent and facial efferent pathways. The optic and oculomotor nerves, the glossopharyngeal/vagus pair, and the vagus/hypoglossal pair are not involved.'),
('e61dfab2-8b4f-5f16-a2fc-5a5f8adf30ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'In medical practice, Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('8a65b80f-6ca2-5ca8-a34b-948ea76f3e65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('f57816f1-39a9-5057-917f-c0b0221ecf6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('683c71d2-8903-55a2-8686-8c0d4c7618fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'During therapeutic management, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('90af96d8-4218-5229-9f2e-f4424a831c16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Regarding clinical cases: Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('00024129-0032-5b55-bcf0-87c3de4439b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'In medical practice, Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('02aa2185-f36e-5b09-a09d-229989702ba5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('86cf85da-bace-5a56-8515-e607ad79ccd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('95b35d48-4cee-5518-a3bc-b48299afc89a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'During therapeutic management, Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('b605287b-a2cd-5fa6-a288-c8f11f9e95ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Regarding clinical cases: Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('f38f8e4e-7a3d-5ff2-a87d-c697a2201438'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'In medical practice, P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('0a71fc4f-babb-594c-b3aa-3c0185da0561'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('2a013718-2913-5299-b065-9e450ff4d6f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('171aa7e2-8d9d-575e-8862-a29681058f09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'During therapeutic management, Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('135762c3-0da7-5d27-b0ff-7713202143ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Regarding clinical cases: Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('b91616f4-94e2-5b38-8c9a-fd101e67cca4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'In medical practice, Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('b19d3c3f-9db0-5f2e-b7fe-a94614ec20f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('c3a0a36b-1a86-57de-9a65-df362f2b10e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('175560fe-e7d9-546e-a78a-be4c1af4a964'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'During therapeutic management, Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('05a2c018-64dd-551d-8011-d17a16e809d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Regarding clinical cases: Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('e96951aa-fd39-572e-a4be-cdd6579d2370'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'In medical practice, Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('a382fd9d-c241-51ec-8881-e7c1c9a63627'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which option correctly describes Dendritic spines?', '["The cell body resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The axon terminal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The axon hillock mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Gap junctions without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small protrusions on dendrites that receive excitatory (usually) synaptic input and are sites of synaptic plasticity"]'::jsonb, 'Small protrusions on dendrites that receive excitatory (usually) synaptic input and are sites of synaptic plasticity', 'Dendritic spines are small membrane protrusions on dendrites that typically receive excitatory (glutamatergic) synapses and are key sites of synaptic plasticity such as long-term potentiation. The axon terminal, axon hillock, and cell body are not spine structures, and gap junctions provide electrical coupling between cells.'),
('88723341-f1e7-5402-87e5-90e9fce63179'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('dcab02f8-b64e-5a28-a7b5-fd67b2a52543'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'During therapeutic management, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('cbfa6c0c-0c0f-529e-8f3a-4a1ebf759ddd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Regarding clinical cases: EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('5edf9dfc-c6ca-5b07-89d0-337a6f570630'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Dendritic spines are. Select the best answer.', '["The cell body resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The axon terminal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The axon hillock mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Gap junctions without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small protrusions on dendrites that receive excitatory (usually) synaptic input and are sites of synaptic plasticity"]'::jsonb, 'Small protrusions on dendrites that receive excitatory (usually) synaptic input and are sites of synaptic plasticity', 'Dendritic spines are small membrane protrusions that form the postsynaptic sites for most excitatory glutamatergic synapses, and their size and number change with synaptic plasticity. The cell body, axon terminal, and axon hillock are distinct neuronal parts, and gap junctions are cell-to-cell channels, so none of them describe spines.'),
('95bcfbe3-fd57-56d1-93b3-679202688c54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this Nervous Tissue item: dendritic spines are?', '["The cell body resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The axon terminal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The axon hillock mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Gap junctions without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small protrusions on dendrites that receive excitatory (usually) synaptic input and are sites of synaptic plasticity"]'::jsonb, 'Small protrusions on dendrites that receive excitatory (usually) synaptic input and are sites of synaptic plasticity', 'Dendritic spines are tiny protrusions along the dendritic surface that host the postsynaptic apparatus of excitatory synapses and undergo structural remodeling with learning. The other options name neuronal compartments or intercellular channels rather than a dendritic specialization.'),
('4ce77555-6164-51ab-a93e-dff5174fabbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The axon is a long process that. Which option is most accurate?', '["Contains Nissl bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Receives incoming signals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Does not contain neurofilaments mediated by specific receptor activation","Is myelinated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal"]'::jsonb, 'Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal', 'The axon is specialized to propagate action potentials from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal, where neurotransmitter is released. Nissl bodies are confined to the soma and dendrites, dendrites receive incoming signals, neurofilaments are present in axons, and myelination is a sheath property rather than the axon''s defining function.'),
('8cbf75e4-eb31-5e5c-baa8-6883a145130b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Nervous Tissue: the axon is a long process that?', '["Contains Nissl bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Receives incoming signals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Does not contain neurofilaments mediated by specific receptor activation","Is myelinated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal"]'::jsonb, 'Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal', 'Axons carry impulses away from the soma to the presynaptic terminal, making them the output process of the neuron. They lack Nissl bodies, do not receive incoming signals as their primary role, contain neurofilaments, and may or may not be myelinated depending on caliber and site.'),
('f71037f1-441f-57e7-bfaa-7aa2761ce759'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'For Nervous Tissue, identify the correct response: the axon is a long process that?', '["Contains Nissl bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Receives incoming signals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Does not contain neurofilaments mediated by specific receptor activation","Is myelinated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal"]'::jsonb, 'Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal', 'The axon is the efferent process of the neuron, transmitting action potentials away from the cell body to the presynaptic terminal. It is free of Nissl substance, contains neurofilaments, and is not universally myelinated, and receiving signals is the role of dendrites.'),
('c7b1d993-9ee2-5ab2-b2bc-a36d11924748'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The axon is a long process that. Select the best answer.', '["Contains Nissl bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Receives incoming signals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Does not contain neurofilaments mediated by specific receptor activation","Is myelinated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal"]'::jsonb, 'Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal', 'An axon conducts nerve impulses away from the soma toward the presynaptic terminal, where synaptic transmission occurs. Nissl bodies are absent from axons, dendrites receive incoming signals, neurofilaments are abundant in axons, and myelination varies between fibers.'),
('577ce004-8aef-5e4f-9c41-4f1539a8bfcc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this Nervous Tissue item: the axon is a long process that?', '["Contains Nissl bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Receives incoming signals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Does not contain neurofilaments mediated by specific receptor activation","Is myelinated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal"]'::jsonb, 'Conducts action potentials away from the cell body toward the presynaptic terminal', 'The axon serves as the neuron''s conduction line, carrying action potentials away from the cell body to the presynaptic terminal for neurotransmitter release. The alternatives describe somatic, dendritic, structural, or sheath features, not the axon''s primary role.'),
('4f977ea0-e82a-58f3-86a8-2329dd99c385'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The axon hillock is the. Which option is most accurate?', '["The tip of the axon resulting in decreased functional activity","Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)","The cell nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","The dendritic branching point without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synaptic ending leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)', 'The axon hillock is the cone-shaped region where the axon emerges from the soma; because it is enriched in voltage-gated sodium channels it is the usual site of action potential initiation. The tip of the axon, nucleus, dendritic branching point, and synaptic ending are all different structures.'),
('ce4d723e-4b79-5c7a-b6c4-969f8a4cea6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Nervous Tissue: the axon hillock is the?', '["The tip of the axon resulting in decreased functional activity","Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)","The cell nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","The dendritic branching point without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synaptic ending leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)', 'The axon hillock marks the origin of the axon from the soma and, with its dense concentration of voltage-gated sodium channels, is the site where the action potential is normally triggered. The other options identify unrelated regions of the neuron.'),
('f91e12f3-9007-5c92-bad6-6da1d012ed82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'For Nervous Tissue, identify the correct response: the axon hillock is the?', '["The tip of the axon resulting in decreased functional activity","Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)","The cell nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","The dendritic branching point without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synaptic ending leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)', 'Located where the axon leaves the cell body, the axon hillock has a high density of voltage-gated sodium channels and is the site of action potential initiation. The axon tip, nucleus, dendritic branch point, and synaptic ending occupy other locations.'),
('39d372e6-057e-5d94-8d52-4000a4f5b3c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The axon hillock is the. Select the best answer.', '["The tip of the axon resulting in decreased functional activity","Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)","The cell nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","The dendritic branching point without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synaptic ending leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)', 'The axon hillock is the origin of the axon from the soma and the site where action potentials are generated because of its concentration of voltage-gated sodium channels. The tip of the axon, nucleus, dendritic branching point, and synaptic ending are separate neuronal components.'),
('7d3a90b9-fab9-5dd0-b260-6cdee7d73673'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this Nervous Tissue item: the axon hillock is the?', '["The tip of the axon resulting in decreased functional activity","Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)","The cell nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","The dendritic branching point without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synaptic ending leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Region where the axon originates from the soma (site of action potential initiation)', 'The axon hillock is the transition zone between the soma and the axon, serving as the site of action potential initiation. The tip of the axon, the nucleus, dendritic branching points, and synaptic endings are all distinct structures elsewhere in the neuron.'),
('b5820cc4-b684-5a73-baf0-c3833da179d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The terminal bouton (synaptic knob) is the. Which option is most accurate?', '["Dendrite resulting in decreased functional activity","Synaptic cleft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axon hillock mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell","Cell body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell', 'The terminal bouton is the expanded distal ending of the axon that contacts a target neuron or effector and releases neurotransmitter across the synapse. A dendrite receives signals, the synaptic cleft is the gap, the axon hillock is the axon''s origin, and the cell body is the soma.'),
('28943a5c-3c88-5f38-a837-297d405df05d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Nervous Tissue: the terminal bouton (synaptic knob) is the?', '["Dendrite resulting in decreased functional activity","Synaptic cleft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axon hillock mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell","Cell body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell', 'The terminal bouton is the swollen end of the axon where synaptic vesicles fuse to release neurotransmitter onto the target cell. It is not the dendrite, the synaptic cleft, the axon hillock, or the cell body, all of which are different parts of the synapse or neuron.'),
('9646e353-557c-57b3-8b65-6f889295ca94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'For Nervous Tissue, identify the correct response: the terminal bouton (synaptic knob) is the?', '["Dendrite resulting in decreased functional activity","Synaptic cleft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axon hillock mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell","Cell body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell', 'The terminal bouton is the presynaptic expansion at the end of the axon that establishes a synapse with the target cell and secretes neurotransmitter. The dendrite, synaptic cleft, axon hillock, and cell body each represent other components of the neuron or synapse.'),
('1ed53df2-75c4-5ee5-96d7-9e6452ee0d3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The terminal bouton (synaptic knob) is the. Select the best answer.', '["Dendrite resulting in decreased functional activity","Synaptic cleft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axon hillock mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell","Cell body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell', 'The terminal bouton is the distal end of the axon that forms the synapse with the target cell and releases neurotransmitter from its vesicles. A dendrite, synaptic cleft, axon hillock, and cell body are distinct structures with separate functions.'),
('05d2f7e0-aaa7-5c3f-b8b0-15a7eb9405b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this Nervous Tissue item: the terminal bouton (synaptic knob) is the?', '["Dendrite resulting in decreased functional activity","Synaptic cleft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axon hillock mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell","Cell body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal end of the axon that forms a synapse with the target cell', 'The terminal bouton is the terminal enlargement of the axon that makes the synaptic contact with a target cell and discharges neurotransmitter. The remaining options name the receiving process, the synaptic gap, the axon origin, and the perikaryon.'),
('9ddc5bd3-2a67-50ac-8d78-498e962852df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Myelin in the peripheral nervous system (PNS) is produced by. Which option is most accurate?', '["Astrocytes","Schwann cells","Microglia","Ependymal cells a","Oligodendrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Schwann cells', 'In the peripheral nervous system, Schwann cells wrap around axons to form the myelin sheath, with each Schwann cell myelinating a single internode. Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons, while astrocytes, microglia, and ependymal cells are glia with different roles.'),
('3dab2e7a-5d2b-5a05-b65d-ca23e511b00f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Nervous Tissue: myelin in the peripheral nervous system (PNS) is produced by?', '["Astrocytes","Schwann cells","Microglia","Ependymal cells a","Oligodendrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Schwann cells', 'Peripheral myelin is formed by Schwann cells, which spiral their plasma membrane around a single axon segment. Oligodendrocytes serve the CNS, and the other glial cells listed do not produce peripheral myelin.'),
('70f1f38a-e4f1-5d27-98c6-5e34f6321c2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'For Nervous Tissue, identify the correct response: myelin in the peripheral nervous system (PNS) is produced by?', '["Astrocytes","Schwann cells","Microglia","Ependymal cells a","Oligodendrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Schwann cells', 'Schwann cells are the myelinating cells of the PNS, enveloping peripheral axons in concentric layers of membrane. CNS myelination is the job of oligodendrocytes, and the other listed glia have supportive or immune functions rather than myelination.'),
('c06890a5-2542-5976-a4ec-5fb2d8f72590'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Myelin in the peripheral nervous system (PNS) is produced by. Select the best answer.', '["Astrocytes","Schwann cells","Microglia","Ependymal cells a","Oligodendrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Schwann cells', 'Schwann cells generate the myelin sheath of peripheral nerves by wrapping their membranes around the axon. Astrocytes, microglia, and ependymal cells are CNS glia, and oligodendrocytes produce myelin only in the CNS.'),
('a7775007-1ce0-5da5-9b46-006ae8f2d2de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this Nervous Tissue item: myelin in the peripheral nervous system (PNS) is produced by?', '["Astrocytes","Schwann cells","Microglia","Ependymal cells a","Oligodendrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Schwann cells', 'Peripheral myelin comes from Schwann cells, each of which ensheaths a single internode of a peripheral axon. Oligodendrocytes are the CNS counterparts, and the other choices are CNS glia with non-myelinating functions.'),
('b5fb1a4e-1a58-53d7-982b-5499148e7aad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Myelin in the central nervous system (CNS) is produced by. Which option is most accurate?', '["Oligodendrocytes","Astrocytes","Ependymal cells","Microglia","Schwann cells"]'::jsonb, 'Oligodendrocytes', 'In the central nervous system, oligodendrocytes form myelin by wrapping their cytoplasmic processes around multiple axons, so one oligodendrocyte can myelinate several fibers. Schwann cells myelinate the PNS, and the other glia listed have non-myelinating roles.'),
('d29d2a22-720a-54bc-954e-1ad930986d16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Nervous Tissue: myelin in the central nervous system (CNS) is produced by?', '["Oligodendrocytes","Astrocytes","Ependymal cells","Microglia","Schwann cells"]'::jsonb, 'Oligodendrocytes', 'CNS myelin is produced by oligodendrocytes, each of which extends processes to wrap multiple axons. Astrocytes support and maintain the blood-brain barrier, ependymal cells line the ventricles, microglia are immune cells, and Schwann cells act only in the PNS.'),
('be6d322f-67c6-5b52-8d77-e31a880fe37e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'For Nervous Tissue, identify the correct response: myelin in the central nervous system (CNS) is produced by?', '["Oligodendrocytes","Astrocytes","Ependymal cells","Microglia","Schwann cells"]'::jsonb, 'Oligodendrocytes', 'Oligodendrocytes are the myelinating cells of the CNS, each ensheathing segments of several axons. Schwann cells myelinate peripheral fibers, and the remaining glial types have structural, lining, or immune functions.'),
('3c5ea06a-cf20-55d0-a9b0-6438bb1f75e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Myelin in the central nervous system (CNS) is produced by. Select the best answer.', '["Oligodendrocytes","Astrocytes","Ependymal cells","Microglia","Schwann cells"]'::jsonb, 'Oligodendrocytes', 'Oligodendrocytes synthesize the myelin sheath in the central nervous system, with each cell myelinating multiple axonal internodes. Astrocytes, ependymal cells, and microglia are not myelinating cells, and Schwann cells belong to the PNS.'),
('21102dce-4b12-5080-8082-af85a5141068'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this Nervous Tissue item: myelin in the central nervous system (CNS) is produced by?', '["Oligodendrocytes","Astrocytes","Ependymal cells","Microglia","Schwann cells"]'::jsonb, 'Oligodendrocytes', 'CNS myelin is made by oligodendrocytes, whose processes wrap around many axons simultaneously. Peripheral myelin is produced by Schwann cells, while the other glia listed serve supportive, lining, or immune functions.'),
('339dc2d5-77c4-5e12-8b57-da0faac89929'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which option correctly describes The node of Ranvier?', '["The nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","The myelinated region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)","The cell body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synapse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)', 'A node of Ranvier is the bare axolemma between consecutive myelin segments, where voltage-gated sodium channels are concentrated and the action potential is regenerated during saltatory conduction. The nucleus, myelinated internode, cell body, and synapse are distinct structures.'),
('17ffd54c-cfc0-5b7c-9026-7d7e0ed06173'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The node of Ranvier is. Which option is most accurate?', '["The nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","The myelinated region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)","The cell body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synapse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)', 'The node of Ranvier is the unmyelinated gap between adjacent myelin segments, exposing the axolemma rich in voltage-gated sodium channels, which allows the action potential to be regenerated. The other choices describe unrelated neuronal or synaptic structures.'),
('e5ca8ad4-e518-53c5-8ba1-9d65843a4952'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Nervous Tissue: the node of Ranvier is?', '["The nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","The myelinated region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)","The cell body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synapse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)', 'At the node of Ranvier the axon is not covered by myelin, and the clustering of voltage-gated sodium channels there regenerates the action potential in a saltatory manner. The nucleus, myelinated regions, soma, and synapse are not nodal structures.'),
('db89a823-1fd3-5459-b3fd-8a73142b021f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'For Nervous Tissue, identify the correct response: the node of Ranvier is?', '["The nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","The myelinated region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)","The cell body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synapse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)', 'Nodes of Ranvier are periodic gaps in the myelin sheath where the axolemma is exposed; their high density of voltage-gated sodium channels supports action potential regeneration and fast saltatory conduction. The other options name unrelated parts of the neuron.'),
('d47685e5-830e-5f51-b8ff-b83eaad0a85e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The node of Ranvier is. Select the best answer.', '["The nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","The myelinated region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)","The cell body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synapse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)', 'The node of Ranvier is the myelin-free gap between successive Schwann cells or oligodendrocyte processes, where sodium channels regenerate the action potential. It is not the nucleus, the myelinated segment, the soma, or a synapse.'),
('44cff71d-d1d3-504a-80c6-8a43fa63a7e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this Nervous Tissue item: the node of Ranvier is?', '["The nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","The myelinated region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)","The cell body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The synapse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gap between adjacent myelin segments where the axon is exposed (site of action potential regeneration)', 'A node of Ranvier is the short unmyelinated interval between adjacent myelin segments, and its concentrated voltage-gated sodium channels allow action potential regeneration during saltatory conduction. The nucleus, myelinated region, cell body, and synapse are separate structures.'),
('d8d502b5-50c6-5e45-95ed-965baa3bc50a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which option correctly describes The fastest conducting fibers in the human body?', '["Type Aδ (small myelinated) resulting in decreased functional activity","Type C (unmyelinated) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter","Type B (autonomic preganglionic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","heavily myelinated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter', 'Type Aα fibers have the largest diameter and thickest myelin sheath, achieving the fastest conduction velocity of roughly 70-120 m/s; they include Ia afferents and α-motor neurons. Smaller myelinated, unmyelinated, and thinly myelinated fibers conduct more slowly, and ''heavily myelinated'' alone does not identify a specific fastest fiber class.'),
('27499981-fc17-5378-a29c-d23fc3cc370a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The fastest conducting fibers in the human body are. Which option is most accurate?', '["Type Aδ (small myelinated) resulting in decreased functional activity","Type C (unmyelinated) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter","Type B (autonomic preganglionic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","heavily myelinated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter', 'Conduction velocity rises with fiber diameter and degree of myelination, so the large-diameter, thickly myelinated Type Aα fibers conduct fastest, including Ia afferents and α-motor neurons. Type Aδ, Type B, and unmyelinated Type C fibers are progressively slower.'),
('964a2667-4487-5f4c-8b4f-6bb43d63e255'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Nervous Tissue: the fastest conducting fibers in the human body are?', '["Type Aδ (small myelinated) resulting in decreased functional activity","Type C (unmyelinated) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter","Type B (autonomic preganglionic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","heavily myelinated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter', 'The fastest fibers in the body are the large-diameter, heavily myelinated Type Aα fibers, which conduct at up to about 120 m/s. Type Aδ, Type B, and unmyelinated Type C fibers have smaller diameters or thinner myelin and therefore conduct more slowly.'),
('b7489316-a982-542d-bd30-eedeaa47677c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'For Nervous Tissue, identify the correct response: the fastest conducting fibers in the human body are?', '["Type Aδ (small myelinated) resulting in decreased functional activity","Type C (unmyelinated) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter","Type B (autonomic preganglionic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","heavily myelinated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter', 'Type Aα fibers combine the largest diameter with the thickest myelin, giving the highest conduction velocity in the body. The smaller and less heavily myelinated fiber classes conduct at progressively lower speeds.'),
('b3ff2035-bef4-5de8-b062-270e62ff91e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The fastest conducting fibers in the human body are. Select the best answer.', '["Type Aδ (small myelinated) resulting in decreased functional activity","Type C (unmyelinated) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter","Type B (autonomic preganglionic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","heavily myelinated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter', 'Conduction speed is greatest in large-diameter, thickly myelinated Type Aα fibers, such as Ia afferents and α-motor neurons. Type Aδ, Type B, and unmyelinated Type C fibers are smaller or more thinly myelinated and conduct more slowly.'),
('0e5cf607-92ee-55eb-9935-e07b46cd6671'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this Nervous Tissue item: the fastest conducting fibers in the human body are?', '["Type Aδ (small myelinated) resulting in decreased functional activity","Type C (unmyelinated) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter","Type B (autonomic preganglionic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","heavily myelinated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type Aα (afferent Ia and efferent α-motor neurons) — large diameter', 'Large-diameter Type Aα fibers with thick myelin achieve the fastest conduction velocities, serving Ia afferents and α-motor neurons. Type Aδ, Type B, and Type C fibers are smaller or less myelinated and so conduct more slowly.'),
('2a62bd25-6144-599d-9c3d-93b31d5d1af0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which option correctly describes The slowest conducting fibers?', '["Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s","Type Aγ","Type Aα (alternative wording)","Type Aδ a","Type Aβ (alternative wording) (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, 'Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s', 'Type C fibers are unmyelinated and of small diameter, so they conduct slowly at roughly 0.5-2 m/s. The various Type A fibers are myelinated and conduct much faster.'),
('2c697b95-fefe-5c11-924e-863e4fbb217d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The slowest conducting fibers are. Which option is most accurate?', '["Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s","Type Aγ","Type Aα (alternative wording)","Type Aδ a","Type Aβ (alternative wording) (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, 'Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s', 'Lacking myelin and having a small diameter, Type C fibers have the lowest conduction velocity, about 0.5-2 m/s. Myelinated Type A fibers conduct at far higher speeds.'),
('170708b9-554f-5cea-9c07-ff507b15dec5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Nervous Tissue: the slowest conducting fibers are?', '["Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s","Type Aγ","Type Aα (alternative wording)","Type Aδ a","Type Aβ (alternative wording) (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, 'Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s', 'Unmyelinated, small-diameter Type C fibers are the slowest conductors, transmitting at only about 0.5-2 m/s. All myelinated Type A fibers conduct impulses considerably faster.'),
('280c9af7-4929-574e-9fbb-7dc3e04a70b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'For Nervous Tissue, identify the correct response: the slowest conducting fibers are?', '["Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s","Type Aγ","Type Aα (alternative wording)","Type Aδ a","Type Aβ (alternative wording) (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, 'Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s', 'Type C fibers lack myelin and are the thinnest fibers, giving them the slowest conduction velocity of roughly 0.5-2 m/s. The myelinated Type A subgroups conduct much more rapidly.'),
('38123b68-e560-5e20-b847-ac49eaf05736'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'The slowest conducting fibers are. Select the best answer.', '["Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s","Type Aγ","Type Aα (alternative wording)","Type Aδ a","Type Aβ (alternative wording) (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, 'Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s', 'Because they are unmyelinated and have the smallest caliber, Type C fibers conduct most slowly, at 0.5-2 m/s. Myelinated Type A fibers conduct at substantially higher speeds.'),
('7ac44861-b7e5-5377-8699-864e229f1d8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this Nervous Tissue item: the slowest conducting fibers are?', '["Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s","Type Aγ","Type Aα (alternative wording)","Type Aδ a","Type Aβ (alternative wording) (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, 'Type C (unmyelinated) — 0.5-2 m/s', 'Type C fibers are unmyelinated and small in diameter, so their conduction velocity is the slowest, about 0.5-2 m/s. The myelinated Type A fibers conduct far more quickly.'),
('93e81797-e0ad-5629-83f3-9ba08a8f728b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Microtubules in the axon are oriented with their plus ends. Which option is most accurate?', '["Without orientation resulting in decreased functional activity","Toward the soma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Toward both directions mediated by specific receptor activation","Randomly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)"]'::jsonb, 'Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)', 'Axonal microtubules are uniformly polarized with plus ends directed toward the axon terminal and minus ends toward the soma. This arrangement lets kinesin move cargo in the anterograde direction and dynein carry it retrograde, and it rules out random or non-directional organization.'),
('d457085e-7be2-5092-b57a-0f3df4e01bd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Nervous Tissue: microtubules in the axon are oriented with their plus ends?', '["Without orientation resulting in decreased functional activity","Toward the soma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Toward both directions mediated by specific receptor activation","Randomly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)"]'::jsonb, 'Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)', 'Microtubules in the axon are all oriented with their plus ends pointing to the axon terminal and minus ends toward the soma. Kinesin then drives anterograde transport to the terminal while dynein mediates retrograde transport to the cell body, so the arrangement is uniform rather than random.'),
('e78c6dfb-0358-53bc-b823-54f6780c7b16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'For Nervous Tissue, identify the correct response: microtubules in the axon are oriented with their plus ends?', '["Without orientation resulting in decreased functional activity","Toward the soma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Toward both directions mediated by specific receptor activation","Randomly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)"]'::jsonb, 'Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)', 'Axon microtubules share a single polarity, with plus ends at the axon terminal and minus ends near the soma. This polarity is what allows kinesin to perform anterograde transport and dynein retrograde transport, so the orientation is fixed, not random or bidirectional.'),
('2b0c0d4f-3987-522d-8f28-f1a7523d2362'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Microtubules in the axon are oriented with their plus ends. Select the best answer.', '["Without orientation resulting in decreased functional activity","Toward the soma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Toward both directions mediated by specific receptor activation","Randomly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)"]'::jsonb, 'Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)', 'Axonal microtubules are uniformly polarized, with plus ends oriented toward the axon terminal and minus ends toward the soma. Kinesin motors use this polarity for anterograde transport and dynein for retrograde transport, which excludes random or disorganized arrangements.'),
('7272beac-c0fb-52b1-a5d3-3bba74205eb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'General Histology - Nervous Tissue', 'Which response correctly addresses this Nervous Tissue item: microtubules in the axon are oriented with their plus ends?', '["Without orientation resulting in decreased functional activity","Toward the soma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Toward both directions mediated by specific receptor activation","Randomly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)"]'::jsonb, 'Toward the axon terminal (anterograde transport) and minus ends toward the soma (retrograde transport)', 'Microtubules in the axon point their plus ends toward the axon terminal and their minus ends toward the soma. This uniform polarity directs kinesin-mediated anterograde transport to the terminal and dynein-mediated retrograde transport back to the cell body, ruling out random or opposite orientations.'),
('e5650daa-c359-565c-a66a-a750750a819e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the two main categories of endocrine glands?', '["Only ductless resulting in decreased functional activity","Hormonal and neural accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ducted (exocrine) and ductless (endocrine)","Primary and secondary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ducted leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ducted (exocrine) and ductless (endocrine)', 'Glands are broadly divided into exocrine glands, which deliver secretions through ducts, and endocrine glands, which are ductless and release hormones directly into the blood. Options that name only ''ductless'' or only ''ducted'' list a single category, so they do not answer the question about the two main categories.'),
('488ab88a-9fed-5739-a11a-a3a5e98a0f91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the structural organization of endocrine glands?', '["Tubules and ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","Squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ducts and acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network"]'::jsonb, 'Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network', 'Endocrine cells are typically arranged as cords or follicles intimately surrounded by fenestrated capillaries so that secreted hormones pass quickly into the bloodstream. Ducts, acini, and surface-type epithelia characterize exocrine or lining tissues rather than endocrine glands.'),
('207f9b80-8bc8-5d60-8f20-37045beeabbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the approximate size (height) of the pituitary gland?', '["2 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","5 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 1 cm (pea-sized)","3 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","0.5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 cm (pea-sized)', 'The pituitary gland measures roughly 1 cm in height and is often described as pea-sized, weighing about 0.5 g. The larger dimensions listed overestimate its size.'),
('eb465e7d-ec06-5459-8c53-4351b6436312'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the two lobes of the pituitary gland?', '["Pars distalis and pars intermedia resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)","Superior and inferior lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","Left and right lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex and medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)', 'The pituitary is divided into the adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and the neurohypophysis (posterior lobe). Pars distalis and pars intermedia are subdivisions of the anterior lobe, not the two main lobes themselves.'),
('9ed919f8-ffb0-5be8-ae1e-7ea1ac158159'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the pars distalis of the pituitary?', '["The main anterior lobe portion containing hormone-secreting cells","The pituitary stalk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posterior lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","The intermediate lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The main anterior lobe portion containing hormone-secreting cells', 'The pars distalis is the largest part of the adenohypophysis and contains the acidophils, basophils, and chromophobes that secrete the anterior pituitary hormones. The stalk, posterior lobe, intermediate lobe, and capsule are separate structures.'),
('aea8964f-823a-5722-afd7-ef07aa74b591'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the pars intermedia of the pituitary?', '["A thin zone between the anterior and posterior lobes","The infundibulum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The main anterior lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","The posterior lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The median eminence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin zone between the anterior and posterior lobes', 'The pars intermedia is a thin, often rudimentary zone lying between the pars distalis and the neurohypophysis. The infundibulum, median eminence, and the main lobes are distinct pituitary regions.'),
('4706bff0-6d1c-5b17-bdc7-f4412b57bb54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the pars tuberalis?', '["An extension of the adenohypophysis wrapping around the infundibulum","The anterior lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The capsule mediated by specific receptor activation","The intermediate lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The posterior lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An extension of the adenohypophysis wrapping around the infundibulum', 'The pars tuberalis is an upward sleeve of adenohypophyseal tissue that wraps around the infundibulum (pituitary stalk). It is neither the main anterior lobe, the capsule, nor the posterior or intermediate lobes.'),
('a27f3837-a57c-5282-bda0-87b29718aa6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the neurohypophysis?', '["Anterior pituitary resulting in decreased functional activity","Intermediate lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pars distalis mediated by specific receptor activation","The posterior pituitary lobe consisting of neural tissue and axon terminals","Pars tuberalis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior pituitary lobe consisting of neural tissue and axon terminals', 'The neurohypophysis is the posterior pituitary lobe, formed by the axon terminals of hypothalamic neurons that store and release oxytocin and ADH. The anterior lobe structures (pars distalis, pars tuberalis) and the intermediate lobe are not neural in this way.'),
('ddd65cfc-faa0-5619-b519-0362a714467f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the pituitary stalk (infundibulum)?', '["The pars intermedia resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sella turcica mediated by specific receptor activation","The posterior lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The connection between the hypothalamus and the pituitary gland"]'::jsonb, 'The connection between the hypothalamus and the pituitary gland', 'The infundibulum is the stalk that connects the hypothalamus to the pituitary gland, carrying portal vessels to the anterior lobe and neurosecretory axons to the posterior lobe. The sella turcica is the bony fossa of the sphenoid that houses the gland.'),
('92e52004-8d92-5f22-98af-64ed28643fe0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are chromophobe cells?', '["Pale-staining cells with few secretory granules; may be degranulated or reserve cells","Basophil cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acidophil cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Cells with many granules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hormone-producing cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pale-staining cells with few secretory granules; may be degranulated or reserve cells', 'Chromophobes stain poorly because they contain few secretory granules, and they likely represent degranulated or reserve cells. Acidophils and basophils, by contrast, are the granulated staining classes of anterior pituitary cells.'),
('9318ea86-f314-5722-87c3-907dcec6d1b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of somatotropes?', '["Secrete prolactin","Secrete TSH","Secrete growth hormone (GH)","Secrete FSH","Secrete ACTH"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete growth hormone (GH)', 'Somatotropes are acidophilic cells of the anterior pituitary that secrete growth hormone, which drives linear growth and metabolism. Prolactin, TSH, FSH, and ACTH are produced by other anterior pituitary cell types.'),
('49cdb19f-bb67-520a-bbf4-cd08a175094f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of lactotropes?', '["Secrete FSH","Secrete GH","Secrete ACTH","Secrete prolactin","Secrete TSH"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete prolactin', 'Lactotropes (mammotropes) are anterior pituitary cells that secrete prolactin, the hormone that promotes milk production. FSH, GH, ACTH, and TSH come from other cell types.'),
('c0ec5705-e837-5bb5-a42d-31f77818dffe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of corticotropes?', '["Secrete prolactin resulting in decreased functional activity","Secrete TSH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secrete ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone)","Secrete GH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secrete FSH/LH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone)', 'Corticotropes are basophilic anterior pituitary cells that secrete ACTH, which stimulates the adrenal cortex to release glucocorticoids. Prolactin, TSH, GH, and FSH/LH are produced by different cell types.'),
('d1242fee-eb14-5bae-a58f-629d88099594'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of thyrotropes?', '["Secrete TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone)","Secrete GH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secrete LH mediated by specific receptor activation","Secrete ACTH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secrete FSH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone)', 'Thyrotropes are anterior pituitary cells that secrete TSH, which stimulates thyroid follicular cells to produce T3 and T4. GH, LH, ACTH, and FSH are secreted by other anterior pituitary cells.'),
('8370f78d-a468-5c50-a0ae-1370ee8eab65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of gonadotropes?', '["Secrete GH","Secrete FSH and LH","Secrete prolactin","Secrete ACTH","Secrete TSH"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete FSH and LH', 'Gonadotropes secrete follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH), which control gonadal function. GH, prolactin, ACTH, and TSH are produced by other anterior pituitary cells.'),
('eaaf6543-aa1b-5f21-9c59-b24471e7fb19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system?', '["A lymphatic system resulting in decreased functional activity","An arterial system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A venous drainage mediated by specific receptor activation","A nerve tract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A network of capillaries and veins connecting the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary"]'::jsonb, 'A network of capillaries and veins connecting the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary', 'The hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system is a capillary–venous network that carries releasing and inhibiting hormones from the median eminence to the anterior pituitary. It is not a lymphatic, purely arterial, venous-drainage, or neural pathway.'),
('6f7f5bf2-864d-5337-b1e9-d97a9463c12c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the hypothalamic releasing hormones?', '["GHRH","GH","TRH","CRH","GnRH"]'::jsonb, 'GHRH', 'GHRH (growth hormone-releasing hormone) is a hypothalamic releasing hormone that stimulates growth hormone secretion from the anterior pituitary. GH itself is a pituitary hormone, so it is released by the pituitary rather than acting as a hypothalamic releasing hormone.'),
('385b368d-0e93-580e-8997-a2348cf3954c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the hypothalamic inhibiting hormones?', '["Dopamine (prolactin inhibitor) and somatostatin (GH inhibitor)","Oxytocin and ADH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FSH and LH mediated by specific receptor activation","CRH and GnRH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GHRH and TRH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine (prolactin inhibitor) and somatostatin (GH inhibitor)', 'Dopamine inhibits prolactin release and somatostatin inhibits growth hormone release, making these the two principal hypothalamic inhibitory hormones. Oxytocin, ADH, FSH, LH, and the releasing hormones CRH, GnRH, GHRH, and TRH are not inhibitory factors.'),
('56ef9e93-906c-563e-910f-ab6dcd1c4771'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are Herring bodies?', '["Capillary loops resulting in decreased functional activity","Nuclei of pituicytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Colloid-filled cysts mediated by specific receptor activation","Dilated axon terminals in the posterior pituitary containing neurosecretory granules","Secretory vesicles of anterior pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated axon terminals in the posterior pituitary containing neurosecretory granules', 'Herring bodies are swollen axon terminals within the posterior pituitary that store neurosecretory granules containing oxytocin or ADH. They are not capillaries, pituicyte nuclei, colloid cysts, or anterior pituitary vesicles.'),
('9888b537-2a40-51e6-ac3a-580e700a7fb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are pituicytes?', '["Hormone-producing cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Glial cells in the posterior pituitary that support the neurosecretory axons","Neurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibroblasts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endothelial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glial cells in the posterior pituitary that support the neurosecretory axons', 'Pituicytes are modified glial cells of the neurohypophysis that provide structural support to the neurosecretory axon terminals. They do not synthesize hormones like the neuronal or anterior pituitary endocrine cells.'),
('5dd5f025-b4a8-54cc-b8cb-b4150226de1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of oxytocin?', '["Stimulates uterine contraction during labor and milk ejection during lactation","Water reabsorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortisol secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroid stimulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Growth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates uterine contraction during labor and milk ejection during lactation', 'Oxytocin triggers uterine contraction during labor and myoepithelial-cell contraction that causes milk ejection during breastfeeding. Water reabsorption, cortisol secretion, thyroid stimulation, and growth are governed by ADH, ACTH, TSH, and GH respectively.'),
('0264265a-58d8-58c2-9990-5ba7213f51bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of ADH (vasopressin)?', '["Milk ejection resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterine contraction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increases water reabsorption in the kidney collecting ducts (concentrates urine)","Thyroid function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Growth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases water reabsorption in the kidney collecting ducts (concentrates urine)', 'ADH (vasopressin) acts on the renal collecting ducts to increase water reabsorption, thereby concentrating urine and conserving body water. Milk ejection, uterine contraction, thyroid function, and growth depend on other hormones.'),
('df9d116e-ad54-58c3-b921-1d735ff9d1ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is diabetes insipidus?', '["High blood sugar resulting in decreased functional activity","ADH deficiency (central) or resistance (nephrogenic) causing dilute polyuria and polydipsia","Autoimmune thyroiditis mediated by specific receptor activation","Excessive ADH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adrenal insufficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ADH deficiency (central) or resistance (nephrogenic) causing dilute polyuria and polydipsia', 'Diabetes insipidus is caused by deficient ADH secretion (central) or renal resistance to ADH (nephrogenic), producing large volumes of dilute urine and compensatory thirst. It is unrelated to blood sugar, the thyroid, or adrenal function.'),
('184cee5a-1a46-525b-9364-b547e3709534'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone)?', '["Diabetes mellitus","and concentrated urine","Excessive ADH causing water retention","hyponatremia","ADH deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive ADH causing water retention', 'SIADH is caused by excessive, inappropriate ADH secretion, which produces water retention, dilutional hyponatremia, and concentrated urine. ADH deficiency causes diabetes insipidus and is functionally opposite.'),
('890abb00-f00e-56e1-ac86-ea2ba4f91944'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the hypothalamic nuclei that produce ADH and oxytocin?', '["Mammillary body resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventromedial nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Supraoptic nucleus (ADH) and paraventricular nucleus (oxytocin and ADH)","Arcuate nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Preoptic nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supraoptic nucleus (ADH) and paraventricular nucleus (oxytocin and ADH)', 'The supraoptic nucleus synthesizes ADH and the paraventricular nucleus synthesizes oxytocin (plus some ADH); their axons form the hypothalamo-neurohypophyseal tract to the posterior lobe. The mammillary body, ventromedial, arcuate, and preoptic nuclei serve other functions.'),
('25018f93-c14d-5d25-8a9a-ec5cf40eded7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the axonal transport pathway from the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary?', '["Arterial supply","Hypothalamo-neurohypophyseal tract","Cerebrospinal fluid","Lymphatic drainage","Portal venous system"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamo-neurohypophyseal tract', 'The hypothalamo-neurohypophyseal tract carries the axons of supraoptic and paraventricular neurons from the hypothalamus down the infundibulum to the posterior pituitary. The portal venous system serves the anterior lobe, and arteries, CSF, and lymphatics are not axonal transport routes.'),
('12f133b5-7103-5ad8-bca4-12f5bbe64a60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the blood supply of the pituitary?', '["Circle of Willis resulting in decreased functional activity","Basilar artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior hypophyseal artery (to median eminence) and inferior hypophyseal artery (to posterior lobe)","Internal carotid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ophthalmic artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior hypophyseal artery (to median eminence) and inferior hypophyseal artery (to posterior lobe)', 'The superior hypophyseal artery supplies the median eminence, feeding the portal system to the anterior lobe, while the inferior hypophyseal artery supplies the posterior lobe. The circle of Willis, basilar, internal carotid, and ophthalmic arteries are not the primary pituitary branches.'),
('aa80fc2d-6587-5080-bc48-ccd472fb3575'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the structure of the thyroid gland?', '["Ducts and acini","lined by cuboidal follicular cells","Follicles filled with colloid","Islets of cells","Cords of cells"]'::jsonb, 'Follicles filled with colloid', 'The thyroid is composed of spherical follicles, each lined by follicular cells and filled with colloid (stored thyroglobulin). Ducts, acini, islets, and cords describe other glands rather than thyroid architecture.'),
('a8f2b018-67e7-5f2b-a956-14327b8215b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of thyroid follicular cells?', '["Store iodine resulting in decreased functional activity","Produce TSH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secrete calcitonin mediated by specific receptor activation","Synthesize and secrete T3 (triiodothyronine) and T4 (thyroxine)","Regulate calcium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesize and secrete T3 (triiodothyronine) and T4 (thyroxine)', 'Thyroid follicular cells synthesize and secrete the thyroid hormones T3 and T4, which regulate metabolism. TSH comes from the anterior pituitary, calcitonin from parafollicular cells, and calcium regulation is separate.'),
('0cfe49c5-5f95-5bcc-a74a-0e292b1420be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the colloid in thyroid follicles?', '["the precursor for thyroid hormones resulting in decreased functional activity","Mucus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A proteinaceous material containing thyroglobulin","Hormonal crystals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A proteinaceous material containing thyroglobulin', 'The colloid is a proteinaceous substance rich in thyroglobulin, the glycoprotein precursor used to build T3 and T4. Mucus, crystals, and pus are unrelated materials.'),
('99f73d10-e88d-5630-a58a-a8a231735ae0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are parafollicular C cells?', '["Cells that produce T3/T4 resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells in the adrenal medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cells in the pituitary mediated by specific receptor activation","Cells in the thyroid that secrete calcitonin to lower blood calcium","Cells in the parathyroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells in the thyroid that secrete calcitonin to lower blood calcium', 'Parafollicular C cells lie between thyroid follicles and secrete calcitonin, which lowers blood calcium by opposing osteoclast activity. T3/T4 come from follicular cells, and the C cells are not located in the adrenal, pituitary, or parathyroid.'),
('ede2ed6a-6602-551b-9432-63cd5567ac60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of calcitonin?', '["Increases intestinal calcium absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Lowers blood calcium by inhibiting osteoclast activity and increasing renal calcium excretion","Raises blood calcium mediated by specific receptor activation","Activates vitamin D without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulates bone resorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lowers blood calcium by inhibiting osteoclast activity and increasing renal calcium excretion', 'Calcitonin lowers serum calcium by inhibiting osteoclast bone resorption and increasing renal calcium excretion. The calcium-raising effects of bone resorption and vitamin D activation belong to PTH.'),
('0744ac69-7e98-528f-87c5-3b728244160d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the source of calcitonin in the thyroid?', '["Parafollicular (C) cells derived from the neural crest","Thyroglobulin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Follicular cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Parathyroid cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid epithelial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parafollicular (C) cells derived from the neural crest', 'Calcitonin is produced by parafollicular C cells, which are derived from the neural crest and are scattered between thyroid follicles. Follicular cells synthesize T3/T4 and thyroglobulin, while the parathyroid makes PTH.'),
('612aa419-765a-548d-a1f1-06b27c2b2bb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone)?', '["A thyroid hormone resulting in decreased functional activity","An adrenal hormone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A parathyroid hormone mediated by specific receptor activation","A pituitary hormone that stimulates thyroid follicular cells to produce T3 and T4","A hypothalamic hormone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A pituitary hormone that stimulates thyroid follicular cells to produce T3 and T4', 'TSH is the anterior pituitary hormone that drives thyroid follicular cells to synthesize and secrete T3 and T4. It is not produced by the thyroid, adrenal, parathyroid, or hypothalamus.'),
('bdf5dcef-1a13-58ce-a654-315480aa987d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is TRH (thyrotropin-releasing hormone)?', '["A hypothalamic hormone that stimulates TSH secretion from the anterior pituitary","A pituitary hormone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thyroid hormone mediated by specific receptor activation","An adrenal hormone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A parathyroid hormone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A hypothalamic hormone that stimulates TSH secretion from the anterior pituitary', 'TRH is a hypothalamic releasing hormone transported through the portal system to the anterior pituitary, where it stimulates thyrotropes to release TSH. It is not formed by the pituitary, thyroid, adrenal, or parathyroid.'),
('b2f2d503-0a80-5a32-9804-8c04bc74ffed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of an active thyroid follicle?', '["vacuolated colloid (resorption vacuoles)","Atrophic cells","Cuboidal to columnar follicular cells with pale","Flat follicular cells with abundant colloid","Hyperplastic cells"]'::jsonb, 'Cuboidal to columnar follicular cells with pale', 'An active thyroid follicle (under TSH stimulation) is lined by cuboidal to columnar follicular cells and shows pale, vacuolated colloid with resorption vacuoles. Flat cells with abundant dense colloid characterize the resting follicle.'),
('381e886d-b7e2-5ba8-8e63-df2eb0d3869e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of a resting thyroid follicle?', '["dense colloid","Flat to low cuboidal cells with abundant","Columnar cells","Many resorption vacuoles","Scalloped colloid"]'::jsonb, 'Flat to low cuboidal cells with abundant', 'A resting thyroid follicle is lined by flat to low cuboidal follicular cells and contains abundant dense colloid with few or no resorption vacuoles. Columnar cells, many vacuoles, and scalloped colloid indicate an active follicle.'),
('d0a4cdb8-b603-5f0a-b75a-149bbc21ca92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What type of epithelium forms the parathyroid?', '["Squamous epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Ductal structures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cords of chief cells and nests of oxyphil cells in a fibrovascular stroma","Transitional epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Follicles of thyrocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cords of chief cells and nests of oxyphil cells in a fibrovascular stroma', 'The parathyroid is organized into cords of chief cells with scattered nests of oxyphil cells supported by a fibrovascular stroma. It does not form follicles, ducts, or surface-type epithelia like the options given.'),
('eb6c01c7-5142-5b23-b298-2d27865f72d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are chief cells of the parathyroid?', '["Calcitonin-producing cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Hormone-inactive cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Large acidophilic cells mediated by specific receptor activation","PTH-responsive cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small polygonal cells that secrete parathyroid hormone (PTH)"]'::jsonb, 'Small polygonal cells that secrete parathyroid hormone (PTH)', 'Chief cells are the small polygonal cells of the parathyroid that synthesize and secrete PTH, which raises blood calcium. The large acidophilic cells are oxyphil cells, and calcitonin comes from thyroid C cells, not the parathyroid.'),
('4499f3c5-bcee-5a25-b3bd-00e63209f99c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of PTH?', '["and vitamin D activation","renal calcium reabsorption","Lowers blood calcium","Stimulates thyroid function","Raises blood calcium by stimulating bone resorption"]'::jsonb, 'Raises blood calcium by stimulating bone resorption', 'PTH raises serum calcium by stimulating osteoclast bone resorption, increasing renal calcium reabsorption, and promoting vitamin D activation. Lowering blood calcium is the opposite action, characteristic of calcitonin.'),
('e5a465d6-cf35-55d8-b871-7e354b0bc199'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are oxyphil cells of the parathyroid?', '["Cells that respond to PTH","Cells that secrete calcitonin","increasing in number with age","Cells that secrete PTH","Large acidophilic cells with abundant mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Large acidophilic cells with abundant mitochondria', 'Oxyphil cells are large cells packed with mitochondria, giving them an acidophilic granular cytoplasm; they appear in the parathyroid after puberty and increase with age. They do not secrete PTH or calcitonin.'),
('02ddafc5-6709-5cb5-b464-3628a053a878'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of oxyphil cells?', '["Inhibit PTH resulting in decreased functional activity","Secrete PTH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regulate calcium sensing mediated by specific receptor activation","Unknown; may be a variant of chief cells or a degenerative form","Secrete calcitonin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unknown; may be a variant of chief cells or a degenerative form', 'The function of oxyphil cells remains unknown; they may represent an aging or degenerative variant of chief cells. PTH is secreted by chief cells and calcitonin by thyroid C cells, not by oxyphil cells.'),
('53178f9b-c388-5758-8321-8ada31018ca1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the major regulator of PTH secretion?', '["Vitamin D resulting in decreased functional activity","TSH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcitonin mediated by specific receptor activation","Phosphate levels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serum ionized calcium (low Ca2+ stimulates PTH release)"]'::jsonb, 'Serum ionized calcium (low Ca2+ stimulates PTH release)', 'Low serum ionized calcium is the principal stimulus for PTH release, acting through the calcium-sensing receptor on chief cells, while high calcium suppresses it. Vitamin D calms PTH indirectly, and TSH and phosphate act on other pathways.'),
('fb566438-ac45-5c00-8012-8b003c7bd4f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is primary hyperparathyroidism?', '["Low PTH from gland destruction","causing hypercalcemia","from a parathyroid adenoma","Excess PTH secretion","Autoimmune parathyroid disease"]'::jsonb, 'Excess PTH secretion', 'Primary hyperparathyroidism is autonomous excess secretion of PTH, most often from a parathyroid adenoma, leading to hypercalcemia. Low PTH, gland destruction, and autoimmune disease describe different conditions.'),
('b80ca03d-2c35-559f-91e8-a93a716760f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of parathyroid adenoma?', '["Atrophic gland resulting in decreased functional activity","Well-circumscribed nodule of chief cells with a rim of normal parathyroid tissue","Carcinoma with invasion mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperplasia of all four glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oxyphil cell predominance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Well-circumscribed nodule of chief cells with a rim of normal parathyroid tissue', 'A parathyroid adenoma forms a well-circumscribed nodule of chief cells with a compressed rim of normal parathyroid tissue. Carcinoma invades adjacent tissues, and hyperplasia involves all four glands rather than a single nodule.'),
('0bf07fbf-f8be-5def-8add-3ec5d3a5002c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is parathyroid hyperplasia?', '["often in secondary hyperparathyroidism (renal failure)","Atrophy of glands","Adenoma of one gland","Enlargement of all four parathyroid glands","Parathyroid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of all four parathyroid glands', 'Parathyroid hyperplasia is enlargement of all four parathyroid glands, classically seen in secondary hyperparathyroidism from chronic renal failure. Adenoma and carcinoma involve a single gland rather than all four.'),
('7dc4a356-f295-5d3c-a036-aa7df0d02948'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the two main parts of the adrenal gland?', '["Left and right lobes","Superior and inferior poles","Pars distalis and pars intermedia","Thyroid and parathyroid","Adrenal cortex (outer) and adrenal medulla (inner)"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal cortex (outer) and adrenal medulla (inner)', 'The adrenal gland consists of an outer cortex, which secretes steroids, and an inner medulla, which secretes catecholamines. Lobes, poles, and the pituitary or thyroid-parathyroid parts do not describe adrenal anatomy.'),
('a8818329-6e72-5439-848f-6703741e2ea3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the three zones of the adrenal cortex?', '["Zona glomerulosa","zona reticularis","zona fasciculata","Cortex","medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Zona glomerulosa', 'The adrenal cortex has three zones: the outer zona glomerulosa, the middle zona fasciculata, and the inner zona reticularis. Of the options listed, both cortex and medulla are the whole gland parts rather than cortical zones.'),
('c8a35a27-219e-5701-81a3-b2a3a7362de6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the zona glomerulosa?', '["The outermost zone secreting mineralocorticoids (aldosterone)","The medulla secreting catecholamines","The inner zone secreting androgens","The capsule","The middle zone secreting cortisol"]'::jsonb, 'The outermost zone secreting mineralocorticoids (aldosterone)', 'The zona glomerulosa is the outermost adrenal cortical zone and secretes mineralocorticoids, chiefly aldosterone. The middle zone secretes cortisol, the inner zone secretes androgens, and the medulla secretes catecholamines.'),
('dc82d171-01c8-5b56-ba42-c4af2eded6d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the zona fasciculata?', '["The inner zone resulting in decreased functional activity","The middle zone secreting glucocorticoids (cortisol) under ACTH control","The medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","The capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The outer zone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The middle zone secreting glucocorticoids (cortisol) under ACTH control', 'The zona fasciculata is the middle adrenal cortical zone and secretes glucocorticoids, mainly cortisol, under the control of ACTH. The outer zone makes aldosterone and the inner zone makes androgens.'),
('ed7beaac-f800-5ce4-b976-d2911bc8ba0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of aldosterone?', '["Suppresses immunity resulting in decreased functional activity","Increases sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the kidney","Stimulates protein catabolism mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases blood sugar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","regulating blood pressure and volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion in the kidney', 'Aldosterone, a mineralocorticoid from the zona glomerulosa, acts on distal tubule cells to enhance sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion, raising blood pressure. Immunosuppression, protein catabolism, and elevated blood sugar are glucocorticoid (cortisol) actions.'),
('aa8e35e9-4711-56ea-a559-55c13211d964'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of cortisol?', '["Sex hormone production","suppresses immunity","Sodium reabsorption","and modulates metabolism in response to stress","Increases blood glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Increases blood glucose', 'Cortisol, the main glucocorticoid from the zona fasciculata, raises blood glucose through gluconeogenesis and also suppresses immunity, but among the choices the rise in blood glucose is its hallmark metabolic effect. Sodium reabsorption belongs to aldosterone, and sex hormone production belongs to adrenal androgens.'),
('fb355b19-2104-5ced-9a7d-34d590b28026'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of adrenal androgens?', '["libido","function","Regulate sodium","Contribute to puberty","and estrogen/androgen production in peripheral tissues"]'::jsonb, 'Contribute to puberty', 'Weak adrenal androgens such as DHEA and androstenedione promote pubic and axillary hair growth and contribute to the pubertal growth spurt, especially in females. Regulation of sodium is a mineralocorticoid function.'),
('c1ecbd8e-ccc9-5b19-be57-1bed0292a6ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the adrenal medulla?', '["The zona reticularis resulting in decreased functional activity","The outermost zone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The middle zone mediated by specific receptor activation","The inner part of the adrenal gland that secretes catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine)","The capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inner part of the adrenal gland that secretes catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine)', 'The adrenal medulla is the central portion of the gland containing chromaffin cells that secrete catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine) in response to sympathetic stimulation. The surrounding cortex zones and capsule are the other layers of the gland.'),
('830e6228-928a-540a-a15e-7211b263c5ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are chromaffin cells?', '["Cells of the pituitary resulting in decreased functional activity","Follicular cells of thyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chief cells of parathyroid mediated by specific receptor activation","Neuroendocrine cells in the adrenal medulla that contain catecholamine granules (stain brown with chromium salts)","Cells of the adrenal cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuroendocrine cells in the adrenal medulla that contain catecholamine granules (stain brown with chromium salts)', 'Chromaffin cells are modified sympathetic postganglionic neurons in the adrenal medulla that store catecholamines in granules, which stain brown with chromium salts. The other choices are endocrine cells belonging to different glands.'),
('6eefaedc-1db9-5b94-a45f-e0f2ef3269b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the connective tissue capsule of the adrenal?', '["The periadrenal fat resulting in decreased functional activity","The zona glomerulosa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","The zona reticularis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A dense fibrous capsule surrounding the entire gland"]'::jsonb, 'A dense fibrous capsule surrounding the entire gland', 'A dense fibrous capsule invests the entire adrenal gland and sends thin septa into the cortex. The cortical zones and the medulla all lie deep to this capsule.'),
('382cdf9e-559a-5629-b3c7-839a51c10352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the blood supply of the adrenal cortex?', '["Portal system resulting in decreased functional activity","Direct arterial supply to medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphatic drainage mediated by specific receptor activation","Capsular arteries branch into fenestrated sinusoids in the cortex","draining into medullary veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Capsular arteries branch into fenestrated sinusoids in the cortex', 'Capsular arteries enter the gland from the periphery and give rise to fenestrated sinusoidal capillaries that course through the cortex toward the medulla. This cortical sinusoidal flow delivers cortisol-rich blood to the medullary cells.'),
('248daa8c-ad84-5391-8211-2d9d32dea60c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the appearance of cells in the zona fasciculata?', '["vacuolated cytoplasm","basophilic cells","Small","Large","lipid-laden cells (spongiocytes) with foamy"]'::jsonb, 'Large', 'Zona fasciculata cells are large polyhedral cells packed with lipid, giving them a vacuolated spongiocyte appearance; they store cholesterol for glucocorticoid synthesis. They are distinctly larger than the cells of the inner zona reticularis.'),
('c6b1eb7b-812c-5196-ad9f-fbc041b02b60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the appearance of cells in the zona reticularis?', '["Columnar cells","forming a compact zone","eosinophilic cells with lipofuscin pigment (brown)","Large foamy cells","Smaller"]'::jsonb, 'Smaller', 'Zona reticularis cells are smaller than those of the zona fasciculata and contain eosinophilic cytoplasm with brown lipofuscin pigment. They form the innermost layer of the cortex adjacent to the medulla.'),
('813ce544-3c94-54f2-99ab-61c56ff1763d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the appearance of chromaffin cells on H&E?', '["Clear vacuolated cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Small round cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cuboidal cells with colloid mediated by specific receptor activation","Pink cells with lipid droplets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large polygonal cells with basophilic cytoplasm and fine brown granules"]'::jsonb, 'Large polygonal cells with basophilic cytoplasm and fine brown granules', 'On H&E, chromaffin cells appear as large polygonal cells with basophilic cytoplasm containing fine brown granules that reflect catecholamine storage. The granules acquire this brown color after fixation with chromium salts.'),
('7b7e91af-8087-51da-b114-2160b109a192'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the structure of the adrenal medulla?', '["Cords of chromaffin cells with large","Follicles of cells","Squamous nests","irregular nuclei; fenestrated sinusoids; and sympathetic ganglion cells","Ducts and acini"]'::jsonb, 'Cords of chromaffin cells with large', 'The medulla consists of cords of large chromaffin cells with irregular nuclei, separated by fenestrated sinusoids and accompanied by scattered sympathetic ganglion cells. Follicles, ducts, and acinar patterns belong to other glands.'),
('fab056c0-d122-59eb-b6d4-9f4e0a0e07d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the difference between epinephrine and norepinephrine cells?', '["Both are in the medulla only resulting in decreased functional activity","Epinephrine cells are in the cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Epinephrine cells are lighter staining with larger granules; norepinephrine cells are darker with denser granules","Norepinephrine cells are in the adrenal capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","difference leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epinephrine cells are lighter staining with larger granules; norepinephrine cells are darker with denser granules', 'Epinephrine-secreting chromaffin cells stain more lightly and contain larger, less dense granules, whereas norepinephrine cells stain darker and contain smaller, denser granules. Both cell types reside within the medulla, not in the cortex or capsule.'),
('4b391e2f-39dd-582d-8f37-c17da87a4da7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the enzyme that converts norepinephrine to epinephrine?', '["DDC (dopa decarboxylase)","PNMT (phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase)","induced by cortisol from the adrenal cortex","TH (tyrosine hydroxylase)","DBH (dopamine beta-hydroxylase)"]'::jsonb, 'PNMT (phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase)', 'PNMT methylates norepinephrine to epinephrine, and its synthesis is induced by cortisol arriving from the adrenal cortex. Tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting step to dopamine, dopa decarboxylase converts dopa to dopamine, and dopamine beta-hydroxylase converts dopamine to norepinephrine.'),
('c62a47e0-9eda-52ec-b8ca-d48c1f78bcb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the vascular pathway through the adrenal?', '["Capsular arteries → cortical sinusoids → medullary sinusoids → central vein → adrenal vein","Lymphatic system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Portal vein → hepatic vein mediated by specific receptor activation","specific pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct arterial to medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Capsular arteries → cortical sinusoids → medullary sinusoids → central vein → adrenal vein', 'Blood enters via capsular arteries, traverses cortical sinusoids into the medullary sinusoids, then drains through the central vein into the adrenal vein. This portal-like pathway exposes the medulla to high cortisol concentrations.'),
('cba6efd8-0ed2-504f-a3f3-2170255fc14e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the significance of the cortical-medullary blood flow?', '["Medulla heats the cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","significance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla filters cortical blood mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortisol from cortex reaching medulla induces PNMT for epinephrine synthesis","Cortex provides nutrients to medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortisol from cortex reaching medulla induces PNMT for epinephrine synthesis', 'The high concentration of cortisol delivered to the medulla induces PNMT, the enzyme required for epinephrine synthesis. This arrangement explains why epinephrine production depends on an intact adrenal cortex.'),
('5c7228e1-6e74-5d67-bf9a-3a3c92d2db14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is Cushing syndrome?', '["Pheochromocytoma resulting in decreased functional activity","or ectopic ACTH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excess cortisol with various causes: pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease)","Addison disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","adrenal adenoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excess cortisol with various causes: pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease)', 'Cushing syndrome is the clinical state of cortisol excess, which may arise from a pituitary corticotrope adenoma (Cushing disease), an adrenal adenoma, or ectopic ACTH secretion. Addison disease is adrenal insufficiency, and pheochromocytoma involves catecholamine excess.'),
('c056ba03-bbdd-5870-8a17-62d358612582'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency?', '["Metastatic cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemorrhage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tuberculosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Adrenalectomy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autoimmune adrenalitis (anti-21-hydroxylase antibodies)"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune adrenalitis (anti-21-hydroxylase antibodies)', 'Autoimmune adrenalitis with antibodies to 21-hydroxylase is the most common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) in developed countries. Tuberculosis, hemorrhage, and metastases are less common secondary causes.'),
('b97093d9-70ed-5b37-8771-7595cefb26e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is Conn syndrome (primary hyperaldosteronism)?', '["Excess aldosterone from adrenal adenoma (or hyperplasia) causing hypertension and hypokalemia","Excess catecholamines accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Adrenal insufficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excess cortisol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excess aldosterone from adrenal adenoma (or hyperplasia) causing hypertension and hypokalemia', 'Primary hyperaldosteronism results from an aldosterone-producing adenoma or bilateral adrenal hyperplasia, producing hypertension with hypokalemia and suppressed renin. Cortisol excess (Cushing syndrome) and catecholamine excess (pheochromocytoma) are distinct entities.'),
('50fc7981-133b-5b3a-88be-b421c9966f45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is pheochromocytoma?', '["A catecholamine-secreting tumor of the adrenal medulla causing paroxysmal hypertension","A tumor of the adrenal cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pituitary tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A parathyroid tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A thyroid tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A catecholamine-secreting tumor of the adrenal medulla causing paroxysmal hypertension', 'Pheochromocytoma is a chromaffin cell tumor of the adrenal medulla that secretes catecholamines and classically causes paroxysmal hypertension with palpitations and sweating. The remaining options describe tumors of other endocrine glands.'),
('6cea6910-3b8b-5e6d-bdcc-ab432b304f6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common benign adrenal tumor?', '["Adrenal cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Myelolipoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adrenal cortical adenoma (incidentaloma)","Pheochromocytoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adrenal cortical carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal cortical adenoma (incidentaloma)', 'Adrenal cortical adenoma is the most common benign adrenal tumor and is frequently discovered incidentally on imaging (incidentaloma). Myelolipoma and adrenal cysts are less common, and adrenal cortical carcinoma is malignant.'),
('00cd405a-8e81-58f9-9552-1432c371eac0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of an adrenal cortical adenoma?', '["invasive tumor","Well-circumscribed","necrotic","Large","yellow nodule with lipid-laden cells resembling zona fasciculata; capsule invasion"]'::jsonb, 'Well-circumscribed', 'Adrenal cortical adenomas are well-circumscribed, encapsulated yellow nodules of lipid-laden cells resembling the zona fasciculata. Capsular or vascular invasion and necrosis favor a diagnosis of carcinoma rather than adenoma.'),
('8be13d17-5959-596c-b969-3d9b5a00a3bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of pheochromocytoma?', '["Spindle cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Follicular pattern with colloid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Large polygonal cells with basophilic granular cytoplasm in nests (Zellballen pattern) with rich vascular network","Clear lipid-laden cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Necrotic invasive cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large polygonal cells with basophilic granular cytoplasm in nests (Zellballen pattern) with rich vascular network', 'Pheochromocytomas grow in nests (Zellballen) of large polygonal chromaffin cells with granular basophilic cytoplasm, surrounded by a rich vascular network. Lipid-laden clear cells suggest a cortical tumor, and a follicular pattern with colloid suggests the thyroid.'),
('cad8c049-2ed0-5b61-bc14-8394565fd96b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the islets of Langerhans?', '["Connective tissue septa resulting in decreased functional activity","Ductular structures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exocrine acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spherical clusters of endocrine cells scattered throughout the exocrine pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Spherical clusters of endocrine cells scattered throughout the exocrine pancreas', 'The islets of Langerhans are spherical collections of endocrine cells embedded within the exocrine pancreatic parenchyma, and they regulate blood glucose. Acini, ducts, septa, and vessels are the surrounding exocrine and stromal components.'),
('60758480-073c-5835-8c4d-cca0b09c3ee3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the main cell types in the islets of Langerhans?', '["Alpha (glucagon)","epsilon (ghrelin)","delta (somatostatin)","beta (insulin)","PP (pancreatic polypeptide)"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha (glucagon)', 'The islets contain several endocrine cell types: alpha cells secrete glucagon, beta cells secrete insulin, delta cells secrete somatostatin, and PP and epsilon cells secrete pancreatic polypeptide and ghrelin. Together they regulate glucose metabolism and islet function.'),
('394154b6-b00b-583a-aff5-a29fe4dd8fc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the spatial arrangement of islet cells?', '["Random distribution","alpha and delta cells at the periphery","Delta cells in the core","Beta cells in the core","Alpha cells in the core"]'::jsonb, 'Beta cells in the core', 'Beta cells occupy the central core of each islet, whereas alpha and delta cells are distributed at the periphery. This orderly arrangement supports the local paracrine regulation of insulin and glucagon secretion.'),
('aa6bc0f4-0307-513b-a5f9-de5627a3618f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of glucagon?', '["Stimulates insulin secretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Promotes fat storage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lowers blood glucose mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibits glucose production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Raises blood glucose by stimulating glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Raises blood glucose by stimulating glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis', 'Glucagon from alpha cells raises blood glucose by promoting hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, opposing the effects of insulin. Insulin lowers blood glucose and promotes storage rather than glucose production.'),
('fa76bdc5-45da-585d-9d56-ff4d8be668ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of somatostatin in the islet?', '["Stimulates glucagon resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulates insulin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Raises blood glucose mediated by specific receptor activation","Promotes growth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Paracrine inhibition of insulin and glucagon secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Paracrine inhibition of insulin and glucagon secretion', 'Somatostatin from delta cells acts in a paracrine manner to inhibit the secretion of both insulin and glucagon, serving as a local brake on islet hormone release. The other options describe the actions of different islet hormones.'),
('2b46208e-63e8-54e3-9cb4-385bc104e874'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of pancreatic polypeptide (PP)?', '["Stimulates growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Controls calcium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regulates appetite mediated by specific receptor activation","Regulates glucose without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regulates pancreatic exocrine secretion and inhibits gallbladder contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Regulates pancreatic exocrine secretion and inhibits gallbladder contraction', 'Pancreatic polypeptide, secreted by PP cells, regulates pancreatic exocrine secretion and inhibits gallbladder contraction. It does not primarily control blood glucose, calcium, appetite, or growth.'),
('7aea031a-52ce-5fdb-9b13-909ec5860ce6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of ghrelin in the islet?', '["Stimulates somatostatin resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibits appetite accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Raises insulin mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulates appetite and growth hormone release; role in islet function still being studied","Lowers glucose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates appetite and growth hormone release; role in islet function still being studied', 'Ghrelin from epsilon cells stimulates appetite and growth hormone release, and its precise role within the pancreatic islet remains under investigation. The other listed effects belong to different islet hormones.'),
('b6f9f055-86cb-511d-9f95-3366ac892d20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the blood supply of pancreatic islets?', '["blood supply resulting in decreased functional activity","Arterioles only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Rich capillary network (fenestrated) for direct hormone secretion into the bloodstream","Lymphatic drainage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rich capillary network (fenestrated) for direct hormone secretion into the bloodstream', 'Islets receive a rich network of fenestrated capillaries that allows their hormones to pass directly into the bloodstream for rapid systemic delivery. They are not supplied primarily by a portal system or by lymphatics.'),
('c33bc041-e20b-508e-ab25-f972bbfb6ff5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the innervation of pancreatic islets?', '["Only parasympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","nerve supply accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatic nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autonomic nerves (sympathetic and parasympathetic) regulating hormone secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic nerves (sympathetic and parasympathetic) regulating hormone secretion', 'The islets receive both sympathetic and parasympathetic autonomic innervation that modulates hormone secretion. Vagal parasympathetic input promotes insulin release, whereas sympathetic input favors glucagon secretion.'),
('364d4244-dcd4-5cfc-a5a0-8fd4476c4cb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is type 1 diabetes mellitus?', '["Excess glucagon resulting in decreased functional activity","Autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells causing absolute insulin deficiency","Insulin resistance mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatic cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-autoimmune beta cell loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells causing absolute insulin deficiency', 'Type 1 diabetes results from autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells, producing absolute insulin deficiency that requires exogenous insulin. Insulin resistance with relative deficiency is the mechanism of type 2 diabetes.'),
('924410d7-9b1a-5c67-b7ba-6b0819e3a200'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of the islet in type 1 diabetes?', '["then loss of beta cells and atrophic islets","Insulitis (lymphocytic infiltration) early","Hyperplastic islets","Amyloid deposition","Normal islets"]'::jsonb, 'Insulitis (lymphocytic infiltration) early', 'Early in type 1 diabetes, the islets show insulitis, an inflammatory lymphocytic infiltration, which is followed by beta-cell loss and islet atrophy. Amyloid deposition in islets is characteristic of type 2 diabetes.'),
('f5611310-f247-59d7-ba63-874ab2ea22e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is type 2 diabetes mellitus?', '["Congenital absence of islets resulting in decreased functional activity","Absolute insulin deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excess insulin production mediated by specific receptor activation","Autoimmune islet destruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progressive insulin resistance with relative (not absolute) insulin deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive insulin resistance with relative (not absolute) insulin deficiency', 'Type 2 diabetes is driven by progressive insulin resistance combined with relative, rather than absolute, insulin deficiency. Autoimmune beta-cell destruction and absolute deficiency are hallmarks of type 1 diabetes.'),
('d4aeeb0d-babb-5c4f-9a6c-babc73c6e77a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common pancreatic endocrine tumor?', '["Glucagonoma","Somatostatinoma","Gastrinoma","VIPoma","Insulinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Insulinoma', 'Insulinoma is the most common functional pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor and typically causes hypoglycemia with neuroglycopenic symptoms. Gastrinoma, glucagonoma, VIPoma, and somatostatinoma are all encountered less frequently.'),
('b981119e-8fa2-5ccb-a599-e1234a7d7bd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor (PanNET)?', '["Nested (organoid) pattern of uniform cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin","Ductal structures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acinar pattern with zymogen granules mediated by specific receptor activation","rich capillary network without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mucinous epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nested (organoid) pattern of uniform cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin', 'PanNETs exhibit a nested or organoid growth pattern of uniform cells with finely stippled salt-and-pepper neuroendocrine chromatin. Ductal and mucinous patterns suggest pancreatic adenocarcinoma, while acinar cells with zymogen granules indicate an acinar tumor.'),
('d870131d-b18b-5f40-8a75-6e20f1bafafd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common site of a gastrinoma?', '["Pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","Stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duodenum (more common than pancreas)","Liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spleen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum (more common than pancreas)', 'Although classically described in the pancreas, gastrinomas most commonly arise in the duodenum, particularly in its first and second parts, and this is especially true in multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1.'),
('f890ecf9-d62e-56d4-bc27-6b2866191011'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is VIPoma (Verner-Morrison syndrome)?', '["hypokalemia resulting in decreased functional activity","A gastrinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A VIP-secreting tumor causing watery diarrhea","An insulinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and achlorhydria (WDHA syndrome) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A VIP-secreting tumor causing watery diarrhea', 'VIPoma is a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor secreting vasoactive intestinal peptide, producing the WDHA syndrome of watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria. Gastrinomas cause peptic ulcers, and insulinomas cause hypoglycemia.'),
('8c684a3a-8ab7-5ecd-93f4-2a121a988c0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is glucagonoma syndrome?', '["and weight loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoglycemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A glucagon-secreting tumor causing hyperglycemia","necrolytic migratory erythema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peptic ulcers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A glucagon-secreting tumor causing hyperglycemia', 'Glucagonoma is a glucagon-secreting tumor that raises blood glucose and is classically associated with necrolytic migratory erythema and weight loss. Hypoglycemia would instead suggest an insulinoma.'),
('6b88a0ac-8cb3-5b16-a607-d400cbc16fcd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common pituitary tumor?', '["Craniopharyngioma","Meningioma","Metastatic tumor","Pituitary adenoma (usually benign)","Pituitary carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary adenoma (usually benign)', 'Pituitary adenoma, a usually benign epithelial neoplasm of the adenohypophysis, is the most common pituitary tumor. Craniopharyngioma and meningioma arise from different tissues and are much rarer.'),
('85a3e5b8-ff4c-5895-9680-32521f3a2dde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common functioning pituitary adenoma?', '["Somatotrope adenoma (GH-secreting)","Prolactinoma (lactotrope adenoma)","Thyrotrope adenoma (TSH-secreting)","Gonadotrope adenoma","Corticotrope adenoma (ACTH-secreting)"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactinoma (lactotrope adenoma)', 'Prolactinoma, a lactotrope adenoma, is the most common functioning pituitary adenoma and causes hyperprolactinemia with galactorrhea and hypogonadism. Somatotrope, corticotrope, thyrotrope, and gonadotrope adenomas are all less frequent.'),
('8db3e54b-51d7-5270-8b88-7bfd1212dec6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of a pituitary adenoma?', '["Monotonous proliferation of one cell type with loss of reticulin network","Mixed cell types with preserved reticulin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Necrotic tissue mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrotic tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal pituitary architecture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monotonous proliferation of one cell type with loss of reticulin network', 'Pituitary adenomas show a monotonous proliferation of a single cell type with destruction of the normal reticulin framework, whereas the normal gland retains its acinar reticulin pattern. Loss of the reticulin network helps distinguish adenoma from hyperplasia.'),
('54463176-ae73-5e93-9f25-eec7971c11f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common type of pituitary adenoma by size?', '["Microadenoma (<1 cm)","Carcinoma","Macroadenoma (>1 cm)","Mixed adenoma","Giant adenoma"]'::jsonb, 'Microadenoma (<1 cm)', 'Microadenomas, measuring less than 1 cm, are more common than macroadenomas because many are small, non-functioning, and found incidentally at imaging or autopsy. Macroadenomas grow beyond 1 cm and may cause mass effects such as bitemporal hemianopia.'),
('7f0471f4-f374-5c8b-b886-ded1ef9b8192'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is acromegaly?', '["Diabetes insipidus resulting in decreased functional activity","Sheehan syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excess GH in adults causing bone and soft tissue enlargement","Cushing disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excess GH in children causing gigantism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excess GH in adults causing bone and soft tissue enlargement', 'Acromegaly is caused by excess growth hormone in adults after epiphyseal closure, producing soft tissue and acral bone enlargement such as of the hands, feet, and jaw. Excess GH before closure causes gigantism, and diabetes insipidus and Sheehan syndrome are unrelated pituitary disorders.'),
('2110ff8d-7a2a-5fbc-a8ef-94ea270fae95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is Sheehan syndrome?', '["Empty sella syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Pituitary carcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary adenoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Postpartum pituitary infarction (apoplexy) causing panhypopituitarism","Pituitary hyperplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postpartum pituitary infarction (apoplexy) causing panhypopituitarism', 'Sheehan syndrome is ischemic infarction (apoplexy) of the pituitary after severe postpartum hemorrhage, leading to panhypopituitarism. It is distinct from empty sella syndrome and from neoplastic pituitary lesions.'),
('cf101416-3622-5a58-b97c-bda42883f1f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is empty sella syndrome?', '["Absent pituitary resulting in decreased functional activity","Pituitary cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","An enlarged sella turcica with a flattened pituitary gland","often asymptomatic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An enlarged sella turcica with a flattened pituitary gland', 'Empty sella syndrome is an enlarged sella turcica containing a flattened, compressed pituitary gland and is frequently asymptomatic or an incidental finding. The gland is thinned rather than absent, and no tumor or cyst fills the sella.'),
('a8249dbf-f909-586d-9e98-697b4adf9b34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is craniopharyngioma?', '["A pituitary adenoma","occurring in the sellar/suprasellar region","often calcified","A benign tumor derived from Rathke''s pouch","A meningioma"]'::jsonb, 'A benign tumor derived from Rathke''s pouch', 'Craniopharyngioma is a benign tumor derived from remnants of Rathke''s pouch and typically arises in the sellar or suprasellar region, often with calcification. It is distinct from pituitary adenoma, which arises from adenohypophyseal cells.'),
('b32ac91e-a3d2-500d-9475-2aea1adf6c3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of craniopharyngioma?', '["Monotonous endocrine cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Papillary architecture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nests of squamous epithelium with peripheral palisading and wet keratin (calcified debris)","Mucus-secreting cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granular cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nests of squamous epithelium with peripheral palisading and wet keratin (calcified debris)', 'The adamantinomatous type of craniopharyngioma shows nests of squamous epithelium with peripheral palisading and wet keratin, which commonly calcifies. A papillary pattern is a separate variant, and these features are not seen in pituitary adenomas.'),
('db1a21da-c221-5756-a016-91ed738d00db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is lymphocytic hypophysitis?', '["Pituitary infection","causing hypopituitarism","often in pregnancy","Autoimmune inflammation of the pituitary","Pituitary tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune inflammation of the pituitary', 'Lymphocytic hypophysitis is autoimmune inflammation of the pituitary, seen most often in pregnant or postpartum women, and can cause hypopituitarism. It is an inflammatory process rather than an infection or neoplasm.'),
('9ab569db-b00e-512d-8bcc-b9a6e89b7ef1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is Hashimoto thyroiditis?', '["Subacute thyroiditis","Autoimmune hypothyroidism with goiter","Acute thyroiditis","lymphocytic infiltration","and Hurthle cell change"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune hypothyroidism with goiter', 'Hashimoto thyroiditis is an autoimmune disorder that causes hypothyroidism, usually with a goiter, and is marked histologically by lymphocytic infiltration and Hurthle (oncocytic) cell change. Subacute and acute thyroiditis have infectious or inflammatory etiologies rather than autoimmunity.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('220c0e7b-5cb4-5b14-a53f-6b7320a5910d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of Hashimoto thyroiditis?', '["Granulomas","Hurthle cell (oncocytic) metaplasia","and fibrosis","Neutrophil infiltration","Lymphocytic infiltration with germinal centers"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocytic infiltration with germinal centers', 'Hashimoto thyroiditis shows dense lymphocytic infiltration that often forms germinal centers, together with Hurthle cell (oncocytic) metaplasia of the follicular epithelium. Granulomas and neutrophil infiltration belong to other inflammatory patterns and are not features of Hashimoto thyroiditis.'),
('6b0bf770-434d-5a8a-9fbc-3d6fcf318967'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism?', '["Toxic multinodular goiter","Thyroid cancer","Graves disease (diffuse toxic goiter)","Toxic adenoma","Subacute thyroiditis"]'::jsonb, 'Graves disease (diffuse toxic goiter)', 'Graves disease is the leading cause of hyperthyroidism, driven by TSH-receptor stimulating antibodies that cause diffuse toxic goiter. Toxic multinodular goiter and toxic adenoma are less common, while thyroid cancer and subacute thyroiditis are not typical causes of sustained hyperthyroidism.'),
('38758eb4-971f-5c70-a6c2-f83a0a47a03e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of Graves disease?', '["and scanty colloid with scalloped edges","Follicular hyperplasia with tall columnar cells","papillary infoldings","Fibrosis","Lymphocytic infiltration"]'::jsonb, 'Follicular hyperplasia with tall columnar cells', 'Graves disease produces follicular hyperplasia lined by tall columnar cells, with scanty colloid and scalloped resorption edges of the remaining colloid. Lymphocytic infiltration characterizes Hashimoto thyroiditis, and fibrosis appears in end-stage gland destruction rather than in active Graves disease.'),
('d218973d-4434-58fe-98ca-1a796b032bb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common thyroid cancer?', '["Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Hurthle cell carcinoma","Follicular thyroid carcinoma","Papillary thyroid carcinoma (80% of all thyroid cancers)","Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary thyroid carcinoma (80% of all thyroid cancers)', 'Papillary thyroid carcinoma accounts for about 80% of all thyroid cancers, making it the most common type. Follicular, medullary, Hurthle cell, and anaplastic carcinomas are all less frequent.'),
('26123312-c483-5706-a59e-4d458308c9ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of papillary thyroid carcinoma?', '["nuclear grooves resulting in decreased functional activity","Follicular architecture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Papillary architecture with fibrovascular cores","and psammoma bodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Orphan-Annie nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary architecture with fibrovascular cores', 'Papillary thyroid carcinoma is defined by papillary architecture with fibrovascular cores and also shows nuclear grooves, Orphan-Annie nuclei, and psammoma bodies. A pure follicular architecture without these nuclear features points to follicular neoplasms.'),
('2648fc5c-a68b-599c-9b32-ff2f64babf58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of follicular thyroid carcinoma?', '["Lymphoid infiltration resulting in decreased functional activity","Anaplastic cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Solid growth with amyloid mediated by specific receptor activation","Papillary features without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Follicular architecture with capsular and/or vascular invasion on histology; lacks nuclear features of papillary"]'::jsonb, 'Follicular architecture with capsular and/or vascular invasion on histology; lacks nuclear features of papillary', 'Follicular carcinoma is diagnosed by capsular and/or vascular invasion, which separates it from follicular adenoma, and it lacks the nuclear features of papillary carcinoma. Papillary features, amyloid-containing solid growth, and anaplastic cells point to other tumor types.'),
('fb33c590-df3b-5c02-8196-f55bde4d4272'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC)?', '["associated with MEN 2A and 2B resulting in decreased functional activity","A papillary variant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A follicular cell tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","AC-cell (parafollicular) tumor secreting calcitonin","An anaplastic tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'AC-cell (parafollicular) tumor secreting calcitonin', 'Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from C-cells (parafollicular cells) and secretes calcitonin, and it is associated with MEN 2A and 2B. It does not derive from follicular cells, unlike papillary, follicular, and anaplastic carcinomas.'),
('9d09a39a-5767-5bf9-8c2a-8496caae7bcd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of medullary thyroid carcinoma?', '["Oncocytic cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Follicular pattern accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Solid nests of polygonal/spindle cells with amyloid stroma (calcitonin-derived)","Clear cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Papillary architecture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Solid nests of polygonal/spindle cells with amyloid stroma (calcitonin-derived)', 'Medullary carcinoma grows as solid nests of polygonal or spindle cells separated by amyloid stroma, the amyloid representing polymerized calcitonin. Follicular and papillary architectures are hallmarks of tumors of follicular cell origin, not of this C-cell tumor.'),
('e8796490-9118-50fb-b8ee-80603f92450f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is anaplastic thyroid carcinoma?', '["Undifferentiated","and high mitotic rate","highly aggressive thyroid cancer with giant cells","Well-differentiated cancer","spindle cells"]'::jsonb, 'Undifferentiated', 'Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma is an undifferentiated, highly aggressive tumor composed of pleomorphic giant and spindle cells with a high mitotic rate. This contrasts with the well-differentiated papillary and follicular carcinomas.'),
('a3488cf6-e2be-5d98-95c5-3d4a350fc9dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common genetic alteration in papillary thyroid carcinoma?', '["RET/PTC rearrangement (fusion of RET with various partner genes)","CTNNB1 mutation","PAX8/PPARG fusion","BRAF V600E mutation","RAS mutation"]'::jsonb, 'BRAF V600E mutation', 'The BRAF V600E mutation is the most frequent genetic alteration in papillary thyroid carcinoma, occurring in roughly half of cases. RET/PTC rearrangements are seen more often in radiation-associated tumors, while PAX8/PPARG fusion and RAS mutations are linked to follicular neoplasms.'),
('3af553d8-96b0-5e3d-99d5-5ee11cb09c01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of a thyroid follicular adenoma?', '["Papillary architecture resulting in decreased functional activity","Nuclear atypia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Encapsulated nodule with uniform follicular cells; no capsular or vascular invasion","Invasive into capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Psammoma bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Encapsulated nodule with uniform follicular cells; no capsular or vascular invasion', 'Follicular adenoma is an encapsulated nodule of uniform follicular cells with no capsular or vascular invasion; the presence of invasion is what distinguishes follicular carcinoma. Papillary architecture and psammoma bodies are features of papillary carcinoma.'),
('ed592a52-7049-5f79-9de4-a72f1204b17d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the Hurthle cell (oncocytic) variant?', '["Papillary variant resulting in decreased functional activity","A malignant feature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A benign change mediated by specific receptor activation","A variant of follicular cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm (packed with mitochondria)","C-cell hyperplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A variant of follicular cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm (packed with mitochondria)', 'Hurthle cells are follicular cells distended by abundant mitochondria, giving abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm; they can be present in benign adenomas or in Hurthle cell carcinoma. They derive from follicular epithelium, not C-cells, and are not inherently benign or malignant.'),
('065accd6-c77f-5f47-8242-1c18bcbc7209'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the significance of psammoma bodies in thyroid?', '["Seen in follicular adenoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Laminated calcified spherules that are characteristic of papillary thyroid carcinoma","Seen in anaplastic carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Seen in medullary carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Seen in benign goiter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laminated calcified spherules that are characteristic of papillary thyroid carcinoma', 'Psammoma bodies are laminated calcified spherules that are highly characteristic of papillary thyroid carcinoma. They are not typically found in follicular adenoma, benign goiter, medullary carcinoma, or anaplastic carcinoma.'),
('47d363da-e938-5643-bb72-849eac5ee34b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the AJCC staging of thyroid cancer?', '["lymph node involvement","Based on age (<55 vs >55)","Based only on size","and metastases","tumor size"]'::jsonb, 'lymph node involvement', 'In the AJCC system, lymph node involvement represents the N component of thyroid cancer staging and is assessed alongside tumor size (T) and distant metastases (M). For differentiated thyroid cancer, patient age (<55 vs >55 years) also determines the final stage group, and staging is never based on size alone.'),
('f9670044-5983-5830-acdb-18f551214cb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the treatment of differentiated thyroid cancer (papillary/follicular)?', '["Observation only resulting in decreased functional activity","Surgery alone without I-131 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroidectomy followed by radioactive iodine (I-131) ablation and TSH suppression with levothyroxine","Radiation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chemotherapy only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroidectomy followed by radioactive iodine (I-131) ablation and TSH suppression with levothyroxine', 'Differentiated thyroid cancer is treated with total thyroidectomy followed by radioactive iodine (I-131) ablation of residual thyroid tissue and levothyroxine to suppress TSH, which reduces stimulation of any remaining tumor. Observation, radiation alone, or chemotherapy alone are not the standard approach.'),
('357aecd9-4a8c-53a5-a004-59f31b710e39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the role of the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS)?', '["Converts T4 to T3 resulting in decreased functional activity","Actively transports iodide into thyroid follicular cells against a gradient","Pumps sodium out mediated by specific receptor activation","Transports thyroglobulin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Synthesizes T3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Actively transports iodide into thyroid follicular cells against a gradient', 'The sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) on the basolateral membrane of follicular cells actively transports iodide into the cell against its concentration gradient, coupling this transport to the inward sodium gradient. T4-to-T3 conversion occurs peripherally in tissues, not at this transporter.'),
('54487bd9-fcfa-5086-a7c2-eef20b99e36c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the Wolff-Chaikoff effect?', '["Decreased organification of iodide at high serum iodide concentrations (protective)","Stimulation of follicular cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibition of TSH mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased T4 production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased iodide uptake leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased organification of iodide at high serum iodide concentrations (protective)', 'The Wolff-Chaikoff effect is the protective decrease in iodide organification that occurs when serum iodide is high, limiting excess thyroid hormone synthesis. Excess iodine therefore temporarily reduces rather than increases hormone production.'),
('89a63442-1e57-5f12-a769-83f179e74551'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the role of thyroid peroxidase (TPO)?', '["Binds TSH","Oxidizes iodide","and couples iodotyrosines to form T3 and T4","Transports iodide","iodinates tyrosine residues"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidizes iodide', 'Thyroid peroxidase (TPO) oxidizes iodide to a reactive form, iodinates tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin, and couples iodotyrosines to form T3 and T4. TSH binds to its own receptor, and iodide transport into the cell is performed by the sodium-iodide symporter.'),
('ec06a179-05bf-51e0-8c5c-4ad8b0bd23c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of thyroglobulin?', '["Active thyroid hormone resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulates follicular cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Binds TSH mediated by specific receptor activation","Transports iodine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acts as a scaffold for T3 and T4 synthesis and storage within the follicular colloid"]'::jsonb, 'Acts as a scaffold for T3 and T4 synthesis and storage within the follicular colloid', 'Thyroglobulin is a large glycoprotein produced by follicular cells that serves as the scaffold on which iodinated tyrosines are coupled to form T3 and T4, and it stores these hormones within the luminal colloid. It is not itself an active hormone and does not bind TSH or transport iodine.'),
('c4fa5fba-2864-5f30-bb09-5c04f9971f26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the ratio of T4 to T3 secreted by the thyroid?', '["100% T4 resulting in decreased functional activity","50% each accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","75% T3 and 25% T4 mediated by specific receptor activation","100% T3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Approximately 90% T4 and 10% T3"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 90% T4 and 10% T3', 'The thyroid gland secretes mostly thyroxine, approximately 90% T4 and 10% T3. The more potent T3 is generated largely in peripheral tissues by deiodination of T4.'),
('9d635ebc-4510-5b7b-bd65-3269c2bcf1a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most active thyroid hormone?', '["Calcitonin","T4 (thyroxine)","TSH","Reverse T3 (rT3)","T3 (triiodothyronine)"]'::jsonb, 'T3 (triiodothyronine)', 'T3 (triiodothyronine) is the most biologically active thyroid hormone, binding nuclear receptors with much higher affinity than T4. Reverse T3 is metabolically inactive, and TSH and calcitonin are not thyroid hormones.'),
('cafef32e-6e1c-5773-a0c5-f6f510174e6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of thyroid hormone receptors?', '["Cytoplasmic receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Membrane-bound receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Receptors on TSH mediated by specific receptor activation","Receptors on TRH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nuclear receptors that regulate gene transcription when bound by T3"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear receptors that regulate gene transcription when bound by T3', 'Thyroid hormone receptors are nuclear receptors that regulate gene transcription when bound by T3. Because T3 binds these receptors with far greater affinity than T4, T3 accounts for most of the physiologic effects of thyroid hormone.'),
('a8981422-6ab9-57a2-89fb-0b71a053c09b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the effects of thyroid hormone on metabolism?', '["and heat production (calorigenic effect)","Decreases metabolism","Increases basal metabolic rate","effect on metabolism","oxygen consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Increases basal metabolic rate', 'Thyroid hormone increases basal metabolic rate and oxygen consumption in most tissues, with a calorigenic effect that raises heat production. That is why hyperthyroid patients develop weight loss, heat intolerance, and tachycardia, whereas hypothyroid patients show the opposite.'),
('94570883-8b66-5c3e-a955-d743966ed79b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the role of thyroid hormone in fetal brain development?', '["Minor role resulting in decreased functional activity","and synapse formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","myelination mediated by specific receptor activation","role without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Essential for neuronal migration"]'::jsonb, 'Essential for neuronal migration', 'Thyroid hormone is essential for fetal brain development, driving neuronal migration as well as myelination and synapse formation. Deficiency during gestation causes irreversible intellectual impairment, as seen in cretinism.'),
('a22ab90f-c116-5f15-8e98-471526a27448'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is hyperparathyroidism?', '["Excess PTH leading to hypercalcemia","Parathyroid agenesis","Normal PTH function","Autoimmune parathyroiditis","Low PTH leading to hypocalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Excess PTH leading to hypercalcemia', 'Hyperparathyroidism is caused by excess parathyroid hormone, which raises serum calcium by increasing bone resorption, intestinal absorption, and renal reabsorption of calcium. Low PTH with hypocalcemia, agenesis, or normal gland function are features of hypoparathyroidism rather than hyperparathyroidism.'),
('99cde881-4209-508e-a16a-25965154f215'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the symptoms of hypercalcemia?', '["Bones (osteitis fibrosa cystica)","stones (renal calculi)","groans (abdominal pain)","and psychiatric overtones (depression)","Tetany"]'::jsonb, 'Bones (osteitis fibrosa cystica)', 'Hypercalcemia classically presents with ''stones, bones, abdominal groans, and psychic groans''; the bony manifestation is osteitis fibrosa cystica caused by increased osteoclastic resorption of bone. Tetany is a feature of hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia.'),
('21c364d9-8aea-508c-89f6-8edcfee55945'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is secondary hyperparathyroidism?', '["Low PTH from hypocalcemia resulting in decreased functional activity","PTH from carcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PTH from a parathyroid adenoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Compensatory increase in PTH from chronic hypocalcemia (most commonly renal failure)","Autoimmune hyperparathyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Compensatory increase in PTH from chronic hypocalcemia (most commonly renal failure)', 'Secondary hyperparathyroidism is a compensatory rise in PTH driven by chronic hypocalcemia, most commonly from chronic kidney disease or vitamin D deficiency. Unlike primary hyperparathyroidism, the gland itself is not the primary problem and serum calcium tends to be low or low-normal.'),
('6ed64c31-8132-5b06-b4a0-87cf5c6d630d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is tertiary hyperparathyroidism?', '["Hypoparathyroidism resulting in decreased functional activity","Autonomous PTH secretion after prolonged secondary hyperparathyroidism","Primary hyperparathyroidism mediated by specific receptor activation","often in dialysis patients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secondary hyperparathyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomous PTH secretion after prolonged secondary hyperparathyroidism', 'Tertiary hyperparathyroidism is autonomous, unregulated PTH secretion that follows prolonged secondary hyperparathyroidism, often in long-term dialysis patients whose hyperplastic glands no longer respond to feedback. It is neither hypoparathyroidism nor de novo primary disease.'),
('7866dc29-00df-5395-bf17-3255804b4222'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is hypoparathyroidism?', '["Insufficient PTH causing hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia","PTH resistance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excess PTH mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypercalcemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parathyroid adenoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Insufficient PTH causing hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia', 'Hypoparathyroidism results from insufficient PTH, producing low serum calcium and high serum phosphate because renal phosphate excretion is reduced. It most commonly follows surgical removal or damage to the parathyroid glands.'),
('1830e694-f709-58b9-ac67-a83e3a52a8ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the symptoms of hypocalcemia?', '["Tetany (carpopedal spasm)","Trousseau sign","perioral numbness","and seizures","Chvostek sign"]'::jsonb, 'Tetany (carpopedal spasm)', 'Hypocalcemia increases neuromuscular excitability, causing tetany with carpopedal spasm, perioral numbness, and seizures. Chvostek and Trousseau signs are bedside tests used to elicit latent tetany.'),
('3fa57fc6-71a2-588f-9a49-d3fc3e1f8c6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR)?', '["Binds vitamin D resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Activates PTH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transports calcium mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Detects extracellular Ca2+ and regulates PTH secretion; mutations cause familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia or neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism","Degrades PTH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detects extracellular Ca2+ and regulates PTH secretion; mutations cause familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia or neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism', 'The calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) on parathyroid chief cells detects extracellular Ca2+ and inhibits PTH secretion when calcium is high. Inactivating mutations impair this sensing and cause familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia or neonatal severe hyperparathyroidism, while activating mutations cause hypocalcemia.'),
('f028382d-f76d-5dc3-b48a-d6dda6d80603'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histological difference between parathyroid adenoma and hyperplasia?', '["Adenoma affects all glands resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperplasia is a single nodule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adenoma: single gland with rim of normal tissue; Hyperplasia: all four glands enlarged","difference without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adenoma has rim of normal tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenoma: single gland with rim of normal tissue; Hyperplasia: all four glands enlarged', 'A parathyroid adenoma is a single enlarged gland with a rim of compressed normal tissue, whereas hyperplasia enlarges all four glands. This distinction matters surgically: adenoma is excised, while hyperplasia usually requires subtotal parathyroidectomy.'),
('1ca7f4d0-a2f8-5d6b-9568-9763fd035100'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is parathyroid carcinoma?', '["Ectopic parathyroid resulting in decreased functional activity","Rare malignant tumor of parathyroid cells with invasion and metastasis","Parathyroid cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Benign adenoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Common parathyroid tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rare malignant tumor of parathyroid cells with invasion and metastasis', 'Parathyroid carcinoma is a rare malignant tumor of parathyroid cells capable of local invasion and metastasis, typically causing severe hypercalcemia. It must be distinguished from the far more common benign parathyroid adenoma.'),
('9282c5a4-1f99-5d78-ae7b-655d9018306f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of parathyroid carcinoma?', '["Encapsulated nodule with rim of normal","mitoses","Hyperplasia of all glands","capsular and vascular invasion; thick fibrous bands","Sheets of cells with fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'capsular and vascular invasion; thick fibrous bands', 'Parathyroid carcinoma shows thick fibrous bands separating trabeculae together with capsular and vascular invasion and mitoses, findings absent in adenoma. In contrast, an adenoma is encapsulated with a rim of normal gland and shows no invasion, and hyperplasia involves all glands.'),
('706f5eef-1af1-57ac-9925-fc88f29a0fde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the pineal gland?', '["A salivary gland resulting in decreased functional activity","A sweat gland accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A part of the pituitary mediated by specific receptor activation","A small endocrine gland in the epithalamus that secretes melatonin to regulate circadian rhythms","A part of the hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small endocrine gland in the epithalamus that secretes melatonin to regulate circadian rhythms', 'The pineal gland is a small endocrine organ located in the epithalamus that secretes melatonin to regulate circadian rhythms. It is neither a sweat or salivary gland, nor a part of the pituitary or hypothalamus.'),
('26e928cf-474c-5c9a-a50c-90466c9d6529'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What cells are found in the pineal gland?', '["Follicular cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Only neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only glial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Pinealocytes (chief cells) and glial cells (astrocytes)","Chromaffin cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pinealocytes (chief cells) and glial cells (astrocytes)', 'The pineal gland contains pinealocytes (chief cells), which synthesize melatonin, supported by interstitial glial cells (astrocytes). Follicular cells belong to the thyroid and chromaffin cells to the adrenal medulla.'),
('41c85cc5-b060-5254-9276-c03e7742408e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of melatonin?', '["Regulates growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Regulates thyroid function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","low during the day mediated by specific receptor activation","Regulates blood pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regulates sleep-wake cycles (circadian rhythms); secretion is high at night"]'::jsonb, 'Regulates sleep-wake cycles (circadian rhythms); secretion is high at night', 'Melatonin regulates sleep-wake cycles and circadian rhythms, with secretion rising in darkness and peaking at night. Light suppresses its release via the retinohypothalamic pathway that drives pineal activity.'),
('18a1d0a8-2fac-5d56-8e23-c36ae8e23177'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are corpora arenacea (brain sand)?', '["Glial scars resulting in decreased functional activity","Pineal tumors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Degenerating pinealocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcified concretions in the pineal gland that increase with age; visible on imaging","Pineal cysts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcified concretions in the pineal gland that increase with age; visible on imaging', 'Corpora arenacea (brain sand) are calcified concretions that accumulate within the pineal gland with increasing age and are often visible on imaging. They are a normal age-related finding, not a tumor, cyst, or glial scar.'),
('fc2b3a67-c748-5cea-9fcd-6a4f60002d72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the blood supply of the pineal gland?', '["Vertebral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior choroidal arteries (branches of the posterior cerebral artery)","Anterior cerebral artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Basilar artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Internal carotid artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior choroidal arteries (branches of the posterior cerebral artery)', 'The pineal gland is supplied by the posterior choroidal arteries, branches of the posterior cerebral artery. The close relation of the pineal to the dorsal midbrain explains why large pineal lesions can compress the superior colliculi and cause Parinaud syndrome.'),
('0d11a737-502a-5af5-8e3a-f58501e43335'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is a pineal cyst?', '["often asymptomatic cyst in the pineal gland","A benign","A degenerated pineal gland","A malignant tumor","common on MRI"]'::jsonb, 'A benign', 'A pineal cyst is a benign, fluid-filled lesion of the pineal gland that is usually asymptomatic and commonly discovered incidentally on MRI. It is not a malignant tumor and rarely produces symptoms.'),
('fc047082-c6fa-5463-abd8-5b5d84b5fc2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of the pineal gland in seasonal reproduction in animals?', '["Controls all reproduction","regulating seasonal breeding cycles","role","Direct reproduction","Melatonin secretion encodes day length"]'::jsonb, 'Melatonin secretion encodes day length', 'The pineal gland influences seasonal reproduction because melatonin secretion encodes day length: longer nights produce more melatonin, providing photoperiodic information that regulates seasonal breeding cycles in animals.'),
('fd43385f-490f-529b-9361-f24cb9e29a73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the innervation of the pineal gland?', '["Postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion","Parasympathetic fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","innervation mediated by specific receptor activation","Somatic nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensory nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion', 'The pineal gland is innervated by postganglionic sympathetic fibers arising from the superior cervical ganglion; norepinephrine released on these fibers stimulates nocturnal melatonin synthesis. Parasympathetic, somatic, or sensory innervation is not involved.'),
('975a4705-dcb2-5a6b-b9c8-88712c9aa63a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN)?', '["A pineal gland nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","The master circadian pacemaker in the hypothalamus that receives light input from the retina","A thalamic nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","A pituitary nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A hippocampal nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The master circadian pacemaker in the hypothalamus that receives light input from the retina', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus is the master circadian pacemaker; it receives light input from the retina via the retinohypothalamic tract and drives pineal melatonin secretion.'),
('0c64d395-357a-567f-9f8b-dc50756b008b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the diffuse neuroendocrine system (DNES)?', '["Scattered endocrine cells throughout the body that secrete hormones locally or into the bloodstream","The central nervous system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A single endocrine gland mediated by specific receptor activation","The peripheral nervous system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The exocrine system leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Scattered endocrine cells throughout the body that secrete hormones locally or into the bloodstream', 'The diffuse neuroendocrine system (DNES) comprises scattered endocrine cells distributed across many organs that secrete hormones locally (paracrine) or into the bloodstream. Examples include enteroendocrine cells and pulmonary neuroendocrine cells.'),
('a9b09637-c33f-5ffa-8f05-55e215026c56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are enteroendocrine cells?', '["Hormone-secreting cells scattered in the GI epithelium that regulate digestion and motility","Cells of the thyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cells of the stomach mucosa mediated by specific receptor activation","Cells of the pancreas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cells of the pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hormone-secreting cells scattered in the GI epithelium that regulate digestion and motility', 'Enteroendocrine cells are hormone-secreting cells scattered throughout the gastrointestinal epithelium that release peptides such as gastrin, secretin, CCK, and GIP to regulate digestion and motility. They are not confined to the thyroid, pituitary, pancreas, or stomach alone.'),
('f3560e11-08c2-5f12-8b56-a1dab41005ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are pulmonary neuroendocrine cells (PNECs)?', '["Immune cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Mucus-secreting cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Solitary cells or clusters (neuroepithelial bodies) in the lung that respond to hypoxia","Bronchial epithelial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveolar cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Solitary cells or clusters (neuroepithelial bodies) in the lung that respond to hypoxia', 'Pulmonary neuroendocrine cells (PNECs) are solitary cells or clusters called neuroepithelial bodies that sense airway hypoxia and release mediators such as serotonin and calcitonin gene-related peptide. They are neither mucus-secreting, immune, nor ordinary alveolar cells.'),
('84eec691-c3b5-5535-93aa-f17953808526'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of gastrin?', '["Stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate","Inhibits gastric acid","Stimulates gastric acid secretion from parietal cells","Stimulates bile release","Regulates gut motility"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates gastric acid secretion from parietal cells', 'Gastrin, released by antral G cells in response to food, stimulates gastric acid secretion from parietal cells, largely by promoting histamine release from enterochromaffin-like cells. Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and bile release are driven by secretin and CCK respectively.'),
('c387a762-e96b-5232-bfdf-d6d747efa160'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of secretin?', '["Stimulates pancreatic ductal cells to secrete bicarbonate-rich fluid","Stimulates insulin release accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulates gastric acid mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibits gastric emptying without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulates gallbladder contraction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates pancreatic ductal cells to secrete bicarbonate-rich fluid', 'Secretin, released from duodenal S cells in response to acid, stimulates pancreatic ductal cells to secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid that neutralizes gastric acid in the duodenum. It does not stimulate insulin release, gastric acid, or gallbladder contraction.'),
('b9155192-36c4-563d-ad24-0b9eadd2e449'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of cholecystokinin (CCK)?', '["Stimulates gastric acid resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibits pancreatic secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulates bicarbonate mediated by specific receptor activation","Relaxes the gallbladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion', 'Cholecystokinin (CCK), released from duodenal I cells in response to fat and protein, stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion. It does not relax the gallbladder or inhibit pancreatic secretion.'),
('f32b99cd-5eda-54da-8d04-eff4b0bf84fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP)?', '["Stimulates insulin release (incretin effect) and inhibits gastric acid secretion","Stimulates glucagon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibits insulin mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulates digestion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulates gastric acid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates insulin release (incretin effect) and inhibits gastric acid secretion', 'Gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP) is an incretin that potentiates glucose-stimulated insulin release and also inhibits gastric acid secretion. It does not stimulate glucagon, gastric acid, or inhibit insulin.'),
('833648bb-4e34-536f-b5ec-da045de2830d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)?', '["Stimulates glucagon resulting in decreased functional activity","Incretin hormone that stimulates insulin secretion","Inhibits insulin mediated by specific receptor activation","and slows gastric emptying without affecting the primary tissue pathway","inhibits glucagon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Incretin hormone that stimulates insulin secretion', 'GLP-1 is an incretin released from intestinal L cells after a meal; it potentiates glucose-dependent insulin secretion and slows gastric emptying. The distractors misstate its actions: it suppresses, not stimulates, glucagon and increases, not inhibits, insulin.'),
('a16a99c6-abfc-541a-8edf-174f1c3d73bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of motilin?', '["Constricts the pylorus resulting in decreased functional activity","Relaxes the stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulates gastrointestinal motility and the migrating motor complex during fasting","Stimulates acid secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibits motility leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates gastrointestinal motility and the migrating motor complex during fasting', 'Motilin is secreted by duodenal M cells and coordinates the migrating motor complex of the fasting state, propelling interdigestive contractions along the gut. It does not constrict the pylorus, relax the stomach, stimulate acid, or inhibit motility.'),
('7c80c836-ee88-52cf-9d5f-40927a7df169'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of ghrelin?', '["Inhibits GH resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulates appetite and growth hormone secretion (hunger hormone)","Suppresses appetite mediated by specific receptor activation","Induces satiety without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulates insulin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates appetite and growth hormone secretion (hunger hormone)', 'Ghrelin, the ''hunger hormone,'' is released by stomach endocrine cells and stimulates appetite while promoting growth hormone secretion. The other options describe effects of satiety hormones (suppression of appetite, satiety) or of insulin, not of ghrelin.'),
('62b65df7-56ce-5b42-9d2e-49ba92473e47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is neuroblastoma?', '["A malignant tumor of the sympathetic nervous system (neural crest) occurring in children","An adrenal cortical tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pheochromocytoma mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign adrenal tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A paraganglioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A malignant tumor of the sympathetic nervous system (neural crest) occurring in children', 'Neuroblastoma is a malignant embryonal tumor of the sympathetic nervous system arising from neural crest cells, chiefly in young children (adrenal medulla or sympathetic chain). The other choices describe adrenal cortical tumors, pheochromocytoma, or other paraganglia lesions.'),
('89f44352-5bb9-5950-a10b-4ba41c014811'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of neuroblastoma?', '["Large clear cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Spindle cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small blue round cells forming Homer-Wright pseudorosettes with neuropil in the center","Polygonal chromaffin cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Papillary structures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small blue round cells forming Homer-Wright pseudorosettes with neuropil in the center', 'Neuroblastoma is composed of small, round, blue cells that form Homer-Wright pseudorosettes with a central core of neuritic neuropil. This pattern distinguishes it from spindle-cell, clear-cell, chromaffin-cell, or papillary neoplasms.'),
('7afa6c7f-a007-5f4a-93fe-e9e9db96c279'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is a paraganglioma?', '["A thyroid tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","An extra-adrenal pheochromocytoma arising from the sympathetic or parasympathetic paraganglia","A pituitary tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A parathyroid tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An adrenal pheochromocytoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An extra-adrenal pheochromocytoma arising from the sympathetic or parasympathetic paraganglia', 'A paraganglioma is an extra-adrenal neuroendocrine tumor arising from the sympathetic or parasympathetic paraganglia (e.g., carotid body, jugulotympanic region). The distractors refer to the adrenal pheochromocytoma or to unrelated endocrine organs.'),
('4845c19e-a94c-5440-a716-81edef6424de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the rule of 10 for pheochromocytoma?', '["10% bilateral","10% pediatric","10% extra-adrenal","10% malignant","10% familial"]'::jsonb, '10% bilateral', 'Pheochromocytoma follows a ''rule of 10'': about 10% are bilateral, 10% extra-adrenal, 10% malignant, and 10% familial (MEN 2, VHL, NF1). ''10% bilateral'' is one of these classic figures, and ''10% pediatric'' is not part of the rule.'),
('7375c2f5-954c-52bc-aaa2-ef9968cc7c53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the familial syndromes associated with pheochromocytoma?', '["von Hippel-Lindau (VHL)","MEN 2B","SDH mutations","MEN 2A","neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1)"]'::jsonb, 'MEN 2A', 'Pheochromocytoma occurs in several familial settings, classically MEN 2A, MEN 2B, VHL, NF1, and SDH-gene mutations; ''MEN 2A'' is the archetypal association being tested here. The other options are real associations but represent the same fact rather than a different one.'),
('1c6cf330-91d7-53d7-b09d-623854728da3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the histology of a paraganglioma?', '["Glandular pattern resulting in decreased functional activity","Diffuse sheets of cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Papillary pattern mediated by specific receptor activation","rich capillary network without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Zellballen (nests of chief cells surrounded by sustentacular cells)"]'::jsonb, 'Zellballen (nests of chief cells surrounded by sustentacular cells)', 'Paragangliomas grow in the characteristic ''Zellballen'' pattern: nests of chief cells surrounded by sustentacular cells within a rich capillary network. The other patterns describe glandular, papillary, or diffuse epithelial tumors, not paraganglia architecture.'),
('60b25b0f-8f66-54d0-956b-bba3060ea615'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the 24-hour urinary metanephrines test?', '["A test for renin resulting in decreased functional activity","A test for aldosterone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A diagnostic test for pheochromocytoma measuring catecholamine metabolites in urine","A test for cortisol without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A test for PTH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A diagnostic test for pheochromocytoma measuring catecholamine metabolites in urine', 'The 24-hour urinary metanephrines test measures catecholamine metabolites (metanephrine and normetanephrine) and is the preferred diagnostic test for pheochromocytoma. The other options describe unrelated hormone assays (renin, aldosterone, cortisol, PTH).'),
('d114d6f7-173b-5221-bb55-026a387f05ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the clinical features of pheochromocytoma?', '["Episodic hypertension","headache","sweating","and anxiety (paroxysmal attacks)","palpitations"]'::jsonb, 'Episodic hypertension', 'Pheochromocytoma classically produces paroxysmal episodes of episodic hypertension together with headache, palpitations, and sweating. Episodic hypertension is the hallmark feature among the listed findings.'),
('7ff9e3d8-d89c-5baf-ba7d-e7b0206be31f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common extra-adrenal pheochromocytoma (paraganglioma)?', '["Zuckerkandl organ paraganglioma resulting in decreased functional activity","Para-aortic paraganglioma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glomus jugulare tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Intra-thyroidal paraganglioma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carotid body tumor (chemodectoma) at the carotid bifurcation"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid body tumor (chemodectoma) at the carotid bifurcation', 'Among extra-adrenal paragangliomas, the carotid body tumor (chemodectoma), located at the carotid bifurcation, is the most common; the Zuckerkandl organ, para-aortic, and glomus jugulare sites are less frequent locations.'),
('6e8d9308-5047-5373-bb3e-b9f9cffcba1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the treatment of choice for pheochromocytoma?', '["Observation resulting in decreased functional activity","Radiation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chemotherapy mediated by specific receptor activation","Surgical resection after alpha-blockade (phenoxybenzamine) to prevent intraoperative hypertensive crisis","Beta-blockers alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical resection after alpha-blockade (phenoxybenzamine) to prevent intraoperative hypertensive crisis', 'Surgical resection after alpha-blockade with phenoxybenzamine is the treatment of choice for pheochromocytoma, because it prevents intraoperative hypertensive crisis. Beta-blockers are added only after alpha-blockade to avoid unopposed alpha-mediated vasoconstriction.'),
('e11d6bb3-4f81-5f16-8ddc-9d01e832ad82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the incidences of different PanNETs?', '["then gastrinoma","Insulinoma (most common)","somatostatinoma","glucagonoma","VIPoma"]'::jsonb, 'Insulinoma (most common)', 'Among pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors, insulinoma is the most common, followed by gastrinoma; glucagonoma, VIPoma, and somatostatinoma are rarer. ''Insulinoma (most common)'' is therefore the point emphasized.'),
('7c25931a-5a1e-588e-9f72-46316f6558e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the Whipple triad?', '["Hyperglycemic symptoms","and relief with glucose administration (diagnostic for insulinoma)","low blood glucose (<50 mg/dL)","Pancreatic pain","Hypoglycemic symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglycemic symptoms', 'The Whipple triad for hypoglycemia (classically insulinoma) consists of hypoglycemic symptoms, low blood glucose (<50 mg/dL), and relief after glucose administration. ''Hypoglycemic symptoms'' is the first-listed element being tested.'),
('1f032f1d-dad6-5111-95fb-a99d21311c18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common malignancy of the pancreas?', '["Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (exocrine)","Pancreatic metastasis","Pancreatic sarcoma","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor","Pancreatic lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (exocrine)', 'Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is the most common malignancy of the pancreas and is of exocrine origin; pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors, sarcomas, and lymphomas are far less common.'),
('301ddfe8-2e94-593a-8c4d-9024500e4d38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common location of insulinoma?', '["Body of the pancreas","Head of the pancreas","Uncinate process","Equally distributed throughout the pancreas","Tail of the pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Equally distributed throughout the pancreas', 'Insulinomas are usually small and benign and are distributed roughly equally throughout the pancreatic parenchyma, in contrast to ductal adenocarcinoma, which favors the head. ''Equally distributed throughout the pancreas'' captures this.'),
('0bec81da-800a-509d-ab1e-743e6d5c5b11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the gastrinoma triangle?', '["the pancreatic head/neck","The region bounded by the cystic duct","The head of the pancreas alone","and the duodenum","The tail of the pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'The region bounded by the cystic duct', 'The gastrinoma triangle is the region bounded by the cystic duct/porta hepatis superiority, the junction of the second and third parts of the duodenum inferiorly, and the neck of the pancreas medially. It is the usual site of primary gastrinomas.'),
('7ae1d91d-a6fa-5072-8dea-cd944779582e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the MEN 1 syndrome?', '["pituitary resulting in decreased functional activity","and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (Wermer syndrome): parathyroid","Only pancreatic tumors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MEN 2A and 2B leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (Wermer syndrome): parathyroid', 'MEN 1 (Wermer syndrome) characteristically shows the ''3 P''s'': parathyroid hyperplasia, pituitary adenomas, and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors. The other options describe single components or the MEN 2 syndromes rather than the overall syndrome.'),
('6106fe58-3a00-572e-a183-56b92b0bc0a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the MEN 2A syndrome?', '["pheochromocytoma","None of the above","MTC","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","and hyperparathyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Medullary thyroid carcinoma', 'MEN 2A (Sipple syndrome) comprises medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, and parathyroid hyperplasia. MTC is the defining, and earliest appearing, component of the syndrome.'),
('693f9269-1f3f-5dda-b635-5ff6b627717b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the MEN 2B syndrome?', '["pheochromocytoma","Same as MEN 2A","and marfanoid habitus ( hyperparathyroidism)","mucosal neuromas","MTC"]'::jsonb, 'MTC', 'MEN 2B includes medullary thyroid carcinoma and pheochromocytoma together with mucosal neuromas and a marfanoid habitus, and it lacks hyperparathyroidism. MTC is the shared, defining endocrine tumor being tested.'),
('6019b171-8c54-5b1b-b6f6-e6c6738692b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) syndrome?', '["MEN 1 resulting in decreased functional activity","renal cell carcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hereditary syndrome with hemangioblastomas","and pancreatic NETs/cysts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","pheochromocytoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hereditary syndrome with hemangioblastomas', 'von Hippel-Lindau syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder featuring hemangioblastomas of the cerebellum and retina, renal cell carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, and pancreatic NETs/cysts. The hemangioblastoma component is the hallmark being tested.'),
('1304d417-1df1-5dc7-8dc5-ad5eca94b361'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the best initial test for hyperthyroidism?', '["T3 level","T4 level","Radioactive iodine uptake","Thyroid ultrasound","TSH (low in primary hyperthyroidism)"]'::jsonb, 'TSH (low in primary hyperthyroidism)', 'TSH is the best initial and most sensitive test for hyperthyroidism; in primary hyperthyroidism it is suppressed (low). T4/T3 levels and radioactive iodine uptake are confirmatory rather than first-line screening tests.'),
('7e498daf-b826-5cea-a621-d9bbd370eb16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the radioactive iodine uptake (RAIU) test?', '["Measures TSH levels resulting in decreased functional activity","Measures TPO antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measures thyroid hormone levels mediated by specific receptor activation","Measures the percentage of administered I-123 taken up by the thyroid at 4-24 hours","Measures thyroglobulin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measures the percentage of administered I-123 taken up by the thyroid at 4-24 hours', 'The RAIU test measures the percentage of administered I-123 taken up by the thyroid at 4-24 hours. High uptake (Graves disease, toxic multinodular goiter) versus low uptake (thyroiditis) separates causes; it does not measure hormone or antibody levels.'),
('b4adf5c1-d70b-5274-bdc1-17f01fee9b48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is a TIRADS classification for thyroid nodules?', '["A staging system for thyroid cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","A grading of goiter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A classification of thyroiditis mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroid Imaging Reporting and Data System (TIRADS) to stratify cancer risk of nodules","A cytology reporting system leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid Imaging Reporting and Data System (TIRADS) to stratify cancer risk of nodules', 'TIRADS is an ultrasound-based classification that scores nodule features to stratify the risk of thyroid cancer. It is neither a cancer staging system, a goiter grading, nor a cytology reporting system.'),
('acb55b4d-b566-5f2b-b190-a81a72be1e23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is a fine needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy of the thyroid?', '["A minimally invasive procedure to sample thyroid nodule cells for cytology (Bethesda System)","An imaging study accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A surgical biopsy mediated by specific receptor activation","A blood test without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A core needle biopsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A minimally invasive procedure to sample thyroid nodule cells for cytology (Bethesda System)', 'FNA is a minimally invasive procedure that samples thyroid nodule cells for cytologic examination, reported using the Bethesda System. It is not an imaging study, a blood test, a surgical biopsy, or a core needle biopsy.'),
('9aad22b5-035c-587a-87ee-1f2bff5c03e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the Bethesda System for thyroid cytology?', '["A classification for goiter","A 6-category reporting system for thyroid FNA results","A staging system","each with an associated cancer risk and management recommendation","A grading system"]'::jsonb, 'A 6-category reporting system for thyroid FNA results', 'The Bethesda System categorizes thyroid FNA results into six diagnostic categories, each carrying an associated cancer risk and corresponding management recommendation. It is a cytology reporting scheme, not a staging or grading system.'),
('4eee7c0a-e7f6-5144-8ac3-701b9787f566'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the blood test for medullary thyroid carcinoma?', '["Tg (thyroglobulin) resulting in decreased functional activity","TPO antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TSH receptor antibodies mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcitonin (elevated) and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA)","T3 and T4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcitonin (elevated) and carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA)', 'Medullary thyroid carcinoma arises from parafollicular C cells, so serum calcitonin is elevated and, along with CEA, serves as its tumor marker. Thyroglobulin, TSH-receptor antibodies, and T3/T4 reflect follicular cell function and are unhelpful here.'),
('d6fdb61c-b2e1-5469-a373-27992f4ee700'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is serum thyroglobulin used for?', '["Diagnosing hypothyroidism resulting in decreased functional activity","Diagnosing Graves disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Monitoring for recurrence after thyroidectomy for differentiated thyroid cancer","Assessing goiter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Screening for thyroid nodules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monitoring for recurrence after thyroidectomy for differentiated thyroid cancer', 'Serum thyroglobulin is measured to monitor for recurrence after thyroidectomy for differentiated (papillary or follicular) thyroid cancer. It is not used to diagnose hypothyroidism, Graves disease, goiter, or to screen nodules.'),
('40bfae59-ddd7-5514-ab62-7fdacf24fa0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal serum calcium level?', '["12-14 mg/dL resulting in decreased functional activity","14-16 mg/dL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5-8 mg/dL mediated by specific receptor activation","10-12 mg/dL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","8.5-10.5 mg/dL (2.2-2.6 mmol/L)"]'::jsonb, '8.5-10.5 mg/dL (2.2-2.6 mmol/L)', 'The normal total serum calcium is approximately 8.5-10.5 mg/dL (2.2-2.6 mmol/L). The 10-12 and 12-14 mg/dL ranges represent hypercalcemia, while 5-8 mg/dL is appreciably low.'),
('63d83d84-fa4a-5c00-a1e1-c767fdc5ce98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the corrected calcium formula?', '["Corrected Ca (mg/dL) = Measured Ca + 0.8 × (4 - albumin in g/dL)","Corrected Ca = Measured Ca + albumin","Corrected Ca = Measured Ca × albumin (alternative wording)","Corrected Ca = Measured Ca - albumin (alternative wording) (alternative wording)","Corrected Ca = Measured Ca / albumin (alternative wording) (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, 'Corrected Ca (mg/dL) = Measured Ca + 0.8 × (4 - albumin in g/dL)', 'The formula compensates for hypoalbuminemia: Corrected Ca = Measured Ca + 0.8 × (4 − albumin in g/dL), since each 1 g/dL fall in albumin lowers measured calcium by about 0.8 mg/dL. The other options incorrectly add, multiply, or divide by albumin.'),
('485a514b-f8c2-5bf2-9cc2-971952ebac72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the cytology of papillary thyroid carcinoma on FNA?', '["Papillary clusters","nuclear grooves","powdery chromatin","and psammoma bodies","pseudoinclusions"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary clusters', 'Papillary thyroid carcinoma yields papillary clusters, but the cytologic clues that confirm the diagnosis are nuclear grooves, intranuclear pseudoinclusions, powdery chromatin, and psammoma bodies. The distractor pseudoinclusions and psammoma bodies are part of that set.'),
('ce570a08-19d4-5221-a433-a97c2bd40742'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the cytology of follicular neoplasm on FNA?', '["Monotonous microfollicles with scant colloid; cannot distinguish adenoma from carcinoma without capsular invasion","Papillary clusters accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Giant cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphoid cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nuclear inclusions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monotonous microfollicles with scant colloid; cannot distinguish adenoma from carcinoma without capsular invasion', 'Follicular neoplasms show monotonous microfollicles with scant colloid; adenoma and carcinoma cannot be separated on FNA because that distinction requires finding capsular or vascular invasion on surgical histology. The other options belong to papillary, medullary, or inflammatory lesions.'),
('baf88931-3d6f-5200-8be1-703431b455ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the cytology of medullary thyroid carcinoma on FNA?', '["Plasmacytoid or spindle cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin","Papillary clusters accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","amyloid deposits mediated by specific receptor activation","Colloid-filled follicles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and neuroendocrine features leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Plasmacytoid or spindle cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin', 'Medullary thyroid carcinoma on FNA shows plasmacytoid or spindle cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin and neuroendocrine features, often with amyloid deposits. This pattern is distinct from papillary clusters or colloid-filled follicles.'),
('6c3c39c4-aae2-517e-ac3e-d974e82a2325'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the cytology of a parathyroid adenoma on FNA?', '["and PTH-positive by immunostain","Colloid-filled follicles","scattered oxyphil cells","Cellular aspirate with sheets of chief cells","Papillary architecture"]'::jsonb, 'Cellular aspirate with sheets of chief cells', 'A parathyroid adenoma yields a cellular aspirate composed of sheets of chief cells, and the diagnosis is confirmed by positive PTH immunostain. Colloid-filled follicles would suggest a thyroid lesion, and papillary architecture is not typical.'),
('76cd142d-5e49-503d-aab0-705df1251fd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the cytology of a pituitary adenoma?', '["salt-and-pepper chromatin resulting in decreased functional activity","Pleomorphic giant cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Papillary formations mediated by specific receptor activation","and variable cytoplasm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monotonous population of uniform cells with round nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Monotonous population of uniform cells with round nuclei', 'Pituitary adenomas are composed of a monotonous population of uniform cells with round nuclei and variable cytoplasm. Pleomorphic giant cells, papillary formations, and salt-and-pepper chromatin belong to other or higher-grade neoplasms.'),
('5e0c9375-2030-5781-8e31-cd4f9fc50522'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the cytology of a carcinoid tumor (low-grade NET)?', '["Pleomorphic giant cells","and positive chromogranin/synaptophysin","Necrotic debris","organoid clusters","Uniform small cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin"]'::jsonb, 'Uniform small cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin', 'Carcinoid tumors (low-grade NETs) show uniform small cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin arranged in organoid clusters, and they stain positively for chromogranin and synaptophysin. Pleomorphic giant cells and necrotic debris reflect high-grade, not low-grade, tumors.'),
('7f189838-4b54-51cc-8ed5-4cb9caa41462'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the cytology of small cell carcinoma (high-grade NEC)?', '["nuclear molding","and prominent necrosis","Plasmacytoid cells with abundant cytoplasm","fine granular chromatin","Small cells with scant cytoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Small cells with scant cytoplasm', 'Small cell carcinoma (high-grade NEC) is distinguished by small cells with scant cytoplasm, fine granular chromatin, nuclear molding, and prominent necrosis. The other options describe abundant cytoplasm or benign-appearing cells, not this high-grade pattern.'),
('fcf6cd50-26c0-5a5c-bf44-db413e95575a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the cytology of a pheochromocytoma on FNA?', '["eccentric nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Large polygonal cells with granular basophilic cytoplasm","and Zellballen pattern mediated by specific receptor activation","Clear vacuolated cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small blue cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large polygonal cells with granular basophilic cytoplasm', 'Pheochromocytoma consists of large polygonal cells with granular basophilic cytoplasm arranged in a Zellballen pattern, and the cells mark with chromogranin. Clear vacuolated cells point to an adrenal cortical lesion and small blue cells to embryonal or small-cell tumors.'),
('a461f2d8-d003-5adc-9bd9-fa537b0c5e1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the cytology of an adrenal cortical adenoma?', '["Spindle cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Clear or vacuolated cells with small uniform nuclei and low N:C ratio; lipid-rich background","Oncocytic cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Small cells with nuclear molding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granular basophilic cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clear or vacuolated cells with small uniform nuclei and low N:C ratio; lipid-rich background', 'Adrenal cortical adenoma cells are clear or vacuolated with small uniform nuclei and a low N:C ratio, set in a lipid-rich background. Granular basophilic cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin and nuclear molding point toward medullary or high-grade lesions instead.'),
('947b3b8e-bcf6-5b84-848c-1c4c7c48b407'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the immunostain profile of Adrenal cortical tumors?', '["calretinin+","synaptophysin+ (but chromogranin-)","inhibin+","Melan-A+","Chromogranin+"]'::jsonb, 'Melan-A+', 'Adrenal cortical tumors are classically positive for Melan-A, inhibin, and calretinin and are chromogranin-negative (with only weak, focal synaptophysin). Melan-A positivity with chromogranin negativity separates a cortical tumor from a medullary pheochromocytoma.'),
('4c878494-891f-5d37-97e2-ff44cafa1ff1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the embryological origin of the pituitary?', '["Endoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Rathke''s pouch (ectoderm) for the anterior lobe; neural ectoderm for the posterior lobe","Surface ectoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rathke''s pouch (ectoderm) for the anterior lobe; neural ectoderm for the posterior lobe', 'The anterior pituitary develops from Rathke''s pouch (oral ectoderm), while the posterior lobe arises from neural ectoderm outgrowth of the diencephalon. This dual ectodermal origin contrasts with the mesodermal and endodermal tissues in the other options.'),
('00ffbfb6-d3a3-50a1-a616-ee84c69492c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the embryonic origin of the thyroid?', '["Endoderm from the foregut resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Ectoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endoderm from the floor of the pharynx (foramen cecum of the tongue)"]'::jsonb, 'Endoderm from the floor of the pharynx (foramen cecum of the tongue)', 'The thyroid gland develops from endoderm at the floor of the pharynx near the foramen cecum of the tongue and descends along the thyroglossal duct. Mesodermal, ectodermal, and foregut endoderm origins are incorrect.'),
('02c11fa1-8caf-55d9-8ffe-2fd5b8307e56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is a thyroglossal duct cyst?', '["A parathyroid cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","moves with swallowing and tongue protrusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lateral neck cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","A thyroid nodule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A midline cervical cyst from a persistent thyroglossal duct"]'::jsonb, 'A midline cervical cyst from a persistent thyroglossal duct', 'A thyroglossal duct cyst is a midline cervical cyst that forms when the thyroglossal duct persists, and it characteristically moves with swallowing and tongue protrusion. It is distinct from lateral branchial cysts and parathyroid cysts.'),
('aacc8d25-2438-5539-8b3d-aa7f564a0412'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the embryological origin of the parathyroid glands?', '["Second pharyngeal pouch resulting in decreased functional activity","First pharyngeal pouch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ectoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Third and fourth pharyngeal pouches (endoderm)","Neural crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Third and fourth pharyngeal pouches (endoderm)', 'The inferior parathyroid glands develop from the third pharyngeal pouch and the superior pair from the fourth pouch, both of endodermal origin. First and second pouch derivations refer to other branchial structures, not the parathyroids.'),
('3c127bf1-15e7-5be9-83d9-e838a737c867'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the embryological origin of the adrenal cortex?', '["Ectoderm","Splanchnic mesoderm","Endoderm","Mesoderm (coelomic epithelium)","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoderm (coelomic epithelium)', 'The adrenal cortex derives from mesodermal coelomic epithelium, whereas the splanchnic mesoderm and neural crest give rise to other structures. The medullary component, by contrast, comes from neural crest cells.'),
('a682c502-5f99-5902-9c2e-dbab77538653'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the embryological origin of the adrenal medulla?', '["Splanchnic mesoderm","Neural crest cells (ectoderm)","Coelomic epithelium","Mesoderm","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest cells (ectoderm)', 'The adrenal medulla arises from neural crest cells (ectoderm) that migrate into the cortex and differentiate into chromaffin cells. It therefore contrasts with the mesodermal-derived adrenal cortex.'),
('fc941014-6fd8-5635-9d11-0b22308f0393'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the embryological origin of the thyroid C cells?', '["Neural crest (from the fourth pharyngeal pouch ultimobranchial body)","Surface ectoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroid follicular cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural tube without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endoderm of the thyroid diverticulum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest (from the fourth pharyngeal pouch ultimobranchial body)', 'Thyroid C cells derive from neural crest cells that enter the gland via the ultimobranchial body of the fourth pharyngeal pouch; they produce calcitonin. They are distinct from the endodermal follicular cells that make thyroid hormone.'),
('59c140da-7a64-5784-af69-4a65d23bcb27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of 21-hydroxylase?', '["Converts corticosterone to aldosterone resulting in decreased functional activity","Converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Converts cholesterol to pregnenolone mediated by specific receptor activation","Converts progesterone to 11-deoxycorticosterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone to 11-deoxycortisol","Converts DHEA to androstenedione leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Converts progesterone to 11-deoxycorticosterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone to 11-deoxycortisol', '21-hydroxylase (CYP21A2) converts progesterone to 11-deoxycorticosterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone to 11-deoxycortisol in the zona glomerulosa and fasciculata. Deficiency causes congenital adrenal hyperplasia with salt wasting and androgen excess.'),
('9f6ebc10-310b-52d4-b8a8-8f89a8c4b97e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of 11β-hydroxylase?', '["Converts cholesterol to pregnenolone resulting in decreased functional activity","Converts androgen to estrogen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Converts T4 to T3 mediated by specific receptor activation","Converts progesterone to 11-deoxycorticosterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol and 11-deoxycorticosterone to corticosterone"]'::jsonb, 'Converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol and 11-deoxycorticosterone to corticosterone', '11β-hydroxylase converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol and 11-deoxycorticosterone to corticosterone in the zona fasciculata. Deficiency causes virilization plus hypertension, because 11-deoxycorticosterone, an aldosterone precursor, accumulates and retains salt.'),
('e8da93fe-d3ab-5874-8ab7-c64e4a5ad5e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of 17α-hydroxylase?', '["Converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol resulting in decreased functional activity","Converts cholesterol to pregnenolone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hydroxylates pregnenolone and progesterone for cortisol and androgen synthesis; deficiency causes hypertension and sex hormone deficiency","Converts T4 to T3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aromatizes androgens to estrogens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydroxylates pregnenolone and progesterone for cortisol and androgen synthesis; deficiency causes hypertension and sex hormone deficiency', '17α-hydroxylase (CYP17) adds a hydroxyl group at C17 of the C21 steroids, producing the 17-hydroxypregnenolone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone intermediates needed for cortisol and adrenal androgen synthesis. Deficiency diverts precursors toward mineralocorticoids, raising blood pressure, while deficient cortisol and sex steroids cause the gonadal and adrenal effects. The enzyme does not affect cholesterol side-chain cleavage, 11-deoxycortisol conversion, T4-to-T3 conversion, or aromatization.'),
('ea664d16-682d-55be-9cb8-38ac72d4a7f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the function of aldosterone synthase?', '["Converts 11-deoxycortisol to cortisol resulting in decreased functional activity","Converts androstenedione to testosterone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Converts corticosterone to aldosterone in three steps (11β-hydroxylase then 18-hydroxylase then 18-oxidase)","Converts T4 to T3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Converts cholesterol to pregnenolone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Converts corticosterone to aldosterone in three steps (11β-hydroxylase then 18-hydroxylase then 18-oxidase)', 'Aldosterone synthase (CYP11B2), located in the zona glomerulosa, performs three sequential reactions converting corticosterone to aldosterone: 11β-hydroxylation, 18-hydroxylation, and 18-oxidation. It is the terminal enzyme of mineralocorticoid synthesis, so it does not act on 11-deoxycortisol, androstenedione, T4, or cholesterol.'),
('6c3f3b3c-edc3-5eba-bdce-6159a1359c2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the development of the zona reticularis?', '["The zona reticularis develops after birth and matures during adrenarche (around age 6-8)","It is present in the fetus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It develops in the first month mediated by specific receptor activation","It develops from the medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It appears only at menopause leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The zona reticularis develops after birth and matures during adrenarche (around age 6-8)', 'The zona reticularis is not a fetal structure; it appears after birth and reaches full maturity during adrenarche around age 6-8, when adrenal androgen secretion (DHEA and DHEA-S) rises. It develops from the cortical precursor cells rather than the medulla and is prominent in adulthood, not only near the first month or at menopause.'),
('bebbb6e4-4e58-585f-baf0-7e8004202a1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the significance of the X-zone in the adrenal?', '["The fetal cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","A transient zone between the fetal and adult cortex that involutes in males at puberty and later in females","The zona glomerulosa mediated by specific receptor activation","The adult reticularis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A transient zone between the fetal and adult cortex that involutes in males at puberty and later in females', 'The X-zone is a transient adrenal cortical layer lying between the fetal cortex and the definitive adult cortex. It involutes after birth, at puberty in males and later in females, so it is not the permanent glomerulosa, adult reticularis, or medulla.'),
('10ffb8e6-7e5b-53c1-804c-77276adbe3e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the role of ACTH in adrenal steroidogenesis?', '["Directly produces hormones","stimulating cholesterol transport and steroidogenesis","Binds to MC2R (melanocortin-2 receptor) on adrenal cortical cells","Regulates aldosterone only","Inhibits enzyme activity"]'::jsonb, 'Binds to MC2R (melanocortin-2 receptor) on adrenal cortical cells', 'ACTH acts by binding the melanocortin-2 receptor (MC2R) on adrenocortical cells, activating the cAMP pathway that promotes cholesterol transport into mitochondria and stimulates steroidogenic enzyme activity, chiefly in the fasciculata and reticularis zones. It is a signaling hormone, not a direct hormone producer, and it does not act selectively on aldosterone nor inhibit enzymes.'),
('346d92cf-66c0-501c-9b15-013930bbb588'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is an adrenal incidentaloma?', '["A symptomatic adrenal tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","An adrenal mass detected incidentally on imaging for other reasons","A malignant adrenal mass mediated by specific receptor activation","An adrenal metastasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A normal adrenal gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An adrenal mass detected incidentally on imaging for other reasons', 'An adrenal incidentaloma is an unsuspected adrenal mass found serendipitously on cross-sectional imaging performed for an unrelated indication. By definition the patient is asymptomatic from the mass at detection, unlike a symptomatic tumor, metastasis, or known malignant lesion.'),
('39474c20-400f-5fb8-85ff-2453d3b4e42c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the attenuation (density) of a benign adrenal adenoma on CT?', '["≤10 Hounsfield units (lipid-rich)",">10 Hounsfield units accompanied by associated clinical symptoms",">30 HU mediated by specific receptor activation",">20 HU without affecting the primary tissue pathway",">40 HU leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '≤10 Hounsfield units (lipid-rich)', 'Benign adrenal adenomas are lipid-rich and typically measure ≤10 Hounsfield units on unenhanced CT, a threshold used to distinguish them from other lesions. Masses denser than 10 HU are indeterminate or non-adenomatous, such as metastases or pheochromocytomas, and generally need further characterization.'),
('4d4bf119-509c-57ab-a77f-c8686fa17edb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the absolute percentage washout calculation for adrenal masses?', '["(Delayed - Non-contrast) / Non-contrast resulting in decreased functional activity","(Non-contrast - Delayed) / Non-contrast accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","(Enhanced - Non-contrast) / Enhanced mediated by specific receptor activation","(Enhanced - Delayed) / (Enhanced - Non-contrast) × 100%; adenomas wash out >50%","(Delayed - Enhanced) / Enhanced leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '(Enhanced - Delayed) / (Enhanced - Non-contrast) × 100%; adenomas wash out >50%', 'Absolute percentage washout is computed as (enhanced attenuation − delayed attenuation) divided by (enhanced − non-contrast attenuation), times 100%. Because adenomas clear contrast faster than malignant lesions, an absolute washout above 50% strongly supports adenoma, even for lipid-poor masses.'),
('2d6c0b27-304c-5b4d-8e11-5c3e4c6402cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the dexamethasone suppression test for?', '["Testing for adrenal insufficiency","Testing for thyroid function","Testing for autonomous cortisol production (Cushing syndrome)","Testing for pheochromocytoma","Testing for primary hyperaldosteronism"]'::jsonb, 'Testing for autonomous cortisol production (Cushing syndrome)', 'The dexamethasone suppression test probes whether the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis can be suppressed: in normal individuals exogenous dexamethasone lowers cortisol, whereas autonomous cortisol production (Cushing syndrome) resists suppression. Dexamethasone does not interfere with cortisol assays, making the cortisol result interpretable; the test does not evaluate adrenal insufficiency, thyroid, catecholamine, or aldosterone disorders.'),
('d9ba57a2-cc31-5b24-9881-7e94a5bc62d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the 1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression test?', '["Measure cortisol before and after","A 3-day test","Give dexamethasone at 11 pm","measure cortisol at 8 am; normal suppresses to <1.8 mcg/dL","Measure ACTH after dexamethasone"]'::jsonb, 'Give dexamethasone at 11 pm', 'In the 1 mg overnight test, 1 mg of dexamethasone is taken at 11 pm and serum cortisol is measured at 8 am the next morning; suppression below about 1.8 mcg/dL excludes Cushing syndrome. The 11 pm dose timing is the defining feature of this protocol, which is a single-dose test rather than the 3-day high-dose version.'),
('1de5d856-ffc3-5cb4-92f7-df59980ec34d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the corticotropin (ACTH) stimulation test for?', '["Testing for adrenal insufficiency by measuring cortisol response to synthetic ACTH (cosyntropin)","Testing for thyroid function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Testing for pheochromocytoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Testing for hypercortisolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Testing for aldosterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Testing for adrenal insufficiency by measuring cortisol response to synthetic ACTH (cosyntropin)', 'This test administers synthetic ACTH (cosyntropin) and measures the rise in serum cortisol to assess adrenocortical secretory reserve; a blunted rise indicates adrenal insufficiency. A normal increment excludes inadequate cortisol production, whereas the test is not intended for thyroid, catecholamine, cortisol-excess, or aldosterone evaluation.'),
('7fe71f4b-8e87-5841-8e4b-85d3e53170c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio (ARR)?', '["A test for Cushing resulting in decreased functional activity","A test for ADH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A test for adrenal insufficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","A screening test for primary hyperaldosteronism; elevated ratio suggests aldosterone excess","A test for pheochromocytoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A screening test for primary hyperaldosteronism; elevated ratio suggests aldosterone excess', 'The aldosterone-to-renin ratio (ARR) screens for primary aldosteronism by combining these two measurements: autonomous aldosterone excess suppresses renin, so a high ratio points toward the diagnosis. It is not used for Cushing syndrome, ADH disorders, adrenal insufficiency, or pheochromocytoma.'),
('04f10b54-27ef-5a52-a7da-84b794c226f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the saline suppression test for aldosterone?', '["A test for Cushing resulting in decreased functional activity","A test for pheochromocytoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infuse 2 L of saline over 4 hours; aldosterone >10 ng/dL indicates autonomous secretion (primary hyperaldosteronism)","A test for adrenal insufficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A test for PTH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infuse 2 L of saline over 4 hours; aldosterone >10 ng/dL indicates autonomous secretion (primary hyperaldosteronism)', 'In this confirmatory test, 2 L of saline is infused over 4 hours to expand volume and suppress renin and aldosterone; aldosterone remaining above 10 ng/dL indicates autonomous aldosterone secretion in primary hyperaldosteronism. A normal gland suppresses aldosterone under this maneuver, and the test says nothing about Cushing, pheochromocytoma, adrenal insufficiency, or PTH.'),
('6e81afe0-1aeb-5ddb-8a34-8725d0998c58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the role of MIBG scan in pheochromocytoma?', '["A CT scan resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An MRI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","I-123 or I-131 MIBG (meta-iodobenzylguanidine) is taken up by chromaffin cells for imaging and targeted therapy","An ultrasound without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A PET scan leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'I-123 or I-131 MIBG (meta-iodobenzylguanidine) is taken up by chromaffin cells for imaging and targeted therapy', 'MIBG is a guanethidine analogue taken up by chromaffin cell amine transporters, so iodine-labeled MIBG localizes pheochromocytoma and neuroblastoma on scintigraphy. I-131 MIBG can also deliver targeted radiotherapy, giving the modality value beyond anatomical forms of imaging such as CT, MRI, or ultrasound.'),
('b0315c72-be0b-5167-90e2-5ee45e85e5f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1)?', '["Pheochromocytoma only resulting in decreased functional activity","Autosomal dominant syndrome with parathyroid","pituitary mediated by specific receptor activation","and pancreatic NETs (Wermer syndrome) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MTC only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal dominant syndrome with parathyroid', 'MEN1 is an autosomal dominant familial syndrome caused by MEN1 tumor-suppressor mutations, classically affecting the parathyroid, anterior pituitary, and pancreatic neuroendocrine cells (Wermer syndrome). Pheochromocytoma and medullary thyroid carcinoma belong to MEN2 rather than MEN1.'),
('0d0b933f-5244-5684-99a2-ebed45fe8062'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common initial manifestation of MEN1?', '["Pancreatic NET resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperparathyroidism (hypercalcemia)","Pituitary tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","MTC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pheochromocytoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperparathyroidism (hypercalcemia)', 'Primary hyperparathyroidism with hypercalcemia is the earliest and most frequent manifestation of MEN1, often appearing by the third decade, and is usually the entry point to diagnosis. Pancreatic NETs and pituitary tumors can follow but are not the typical first finding, while MTC and pheochromocytoma are features of MEN2.'),
('a24ddd27-c5c2-5a0c-bc10-1e5559d9ceee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the most common pancreatic NETs in MEN1?', '["Somatostatinomas resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-functioning PanNETs and gastrinomas","Insulinomas mediated by specific receptor activation","VIPomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucagonomas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-functioning PanNETs and gastrinomas', 'In MEN1, non-functioning pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors and gastrinomas are the most common pancreatic neoplasms, with gastrinomas often causing recurrent peptic ulcer disease (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). Functional tumors such as insulinomas, VIPomas, glucagonomas, and somatostatinomas are encountered less frequently.'),
('2d1539a6-5d01-5c6e-910c-f9ad38cd0b48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 (MEN2)?', '["Autosomal dominant RET mutation with MTC","and either hyperparathyroidism (2A) or mucosal neuromas (2B)","pheochromocytoma","MEN1","VHL"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal dominant RET mutation with MTC', 'MEN2 is an autosomal dominant syndrome caused by gain-of-function mutations of the RET proto-oncogene, with medullary thyroid carcinoma (MTC) as the hallmark tumor in every affected carrier. The type 2A variant adds pheochromocytoma and hyperparathyroidism, while type 2B features mucosal neuromas and a marfanoid habitus.'),
('d73e65e2-1dd3-5c61-8d7c-396b984e9876'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the penetrance of MTC in MEN2?', '["50% resulting in decreased functional activity","10% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nearly 100% by age 40-50 in MEN2A; nearly 100% in early childhood in MEN2B","25% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Variable leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nearly 100% by age 40-50 in MEN2A; nearly 100% in early childhood in MEN2B', 'Medullary thyroid carcinoma shows near-complete penetrance in MEN2, developing in almost all carriers by age 40-50 in MEN2A and in early childhood in the more aggressive MEN2B. This near-100% penetrance justifies early prophylactic thyroidectomy in mutation carriers.'),
('d8ac8d82-9f14-541c-abe6-14681ae84a3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the relationship between RET mutation codon and phenotype?', '["All mutations cause MEN 2B","Codon 634 (MEN 2A)","Codon 918 (MEN 2B)","genotype-phenotype correlation","Codon 609/611/618/620 (familial MTC)"]'::jsonb, 'Codon 634 (MEN 2A)', 'Specific RET codon mutations correlate closely with the MEN2 phenotype: codon 634 is typical of MEN2A, codon 918 of MEN2B, and codons 609/611/618/620 with familial MTC. Identifying the exact codon therefore guides screening intensity and the timing of prophylactic thyroidectomy.'),
('709fb32d-641d-52d5-a311-e64884c39efe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the screening for MEN2?', '["Annual ultrasound resulting in decreased functional activity","Genetic testing for RET mutation in at-risk family members","Annual CT scan mediated by specific receptor activation","screening needed without affecting the primary tissue pathway","then prophylactic thyroidectomy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic testing for RET mutation in at-risk family members', 'Screening begins with genetic testing for the RET mutation in at-risk relatives, which identifies carriers before clinical disease appears. Carriers then undergo serial calcitonin testing and prophylactic thyroidectomy, whereas non-carriers need no further intervention; anatomical imaging is not the first-line screening tool.'),
('05885ab1-6bc9-59d7-a6d3-513a860e6cf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the difference between sporadic and MEN-associated PanNETs?', '["MEN-associated PanNETs are more likely to be multifocal and present at younger age","Sporadic are benign accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","difference mediated by specific receptor activation","Sporadic are more likely multifocal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MEN-associated are malignant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MEN-associated PanNETs are more likely to be multifocal and present at younger age', 'Because a germline MEN1 mutation is present in every cell, MEN-associated pancreatic NETs tend to be multifocal, develop at a younger age, and recur after resection. Sporadic PanNETs are usually solitary and appear later, rather than being uniformly benign or more multifocal.'),
('13bb195b-a48c-537d-8b1d-2aabb0dae320'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the second most common cause of death in MEN2?', '["Pancreatic cancer","Cardiovascular disease","Pancreatic NET","Pheochromocytoma (after MTC)","Hyperparathyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Pheochromocytoma (after MTC)', 'After medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma is the second leading cause of death in MEN2, because an unrecognized catecholamine surge can cause a fatal hypertensive crisis during surgery, stress, or anesthesia. Cardiovascular disease, pancreatic NETs, and hyperparathyroidism are not the competing lethal tumors in this syndrome.'),
('9eec05e9-0fde-5f8c-ae56-5ac3e25ff84f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is familial isolated hyperparathyroidism?', '["Same as MEN1 resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperparathyroidism without other MEN features; can be due to MEN1 mutation (phenotypic variability)","Same as MEN2 mediated by specific receptor activation","genetic cause without affecting the primary tissue pathway","due to CASR mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperparathyroidism without other MEN features; can be due to MEN1 mutation (phenotypic variability)', 'Familial isolated hyperparathyroidism presents with parathyroid overactivity alone, lacking the pituitary and pancreatic tumors of MEN1. It may result from a MEN1 mutation with variable expression or from other genetic causes such as a calcium-sensing receptor (CASR) mutation, so it is not identical to full MEN1 or MEN2.'),
('d6e27464-dd9a-54db-b2b9-6a23c23b2839'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is resistance to thyroid hormone (RTH)?', '["Primary hyperthyroidism resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid-binding protein abnormality accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary hypothyroidism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A rare syndrome due to mutation in the thyroid hormone receptor beta (THRB) causing elevated T4/T3 with non-suppressed TSH"]'::jsonb, 'A rare syndrome due to mutation in the thyroid hormone receptor beta (THRB) causing elevated T4/T3 with non-suppressed TSH', 'Resistance to thyroid hormone is a rare syndrome, usually from an inactivating mutation of the THRB gene, in which target tissues respond poorly to thyroid hormone; elevated free T4 and T3 coexist with an inappropriately non-suppressed (normal or high) TSH. This pattern distinguishes it from primary hyperthyroidism, where TSH is suppressed, and from a TSH-secreting adenoma, which is autonomous.'),
('0799e613-b174-569f-9bf4-07d1e1ebe6a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the difference between RTH and TSHoma?', '["difference resulting in decreased functional activity","RTH responds to T3 (reduced TSH); TSHoma does not (autonomous TSH secretion)","Both are the same mediated by specific receptor activation","TSHoma has low T4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RTH has low TSH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'RTH responds to T3 (reduced TSH); TSHoma does not (autonomous TSH secretion)', 'In resistance to thyroid hormone the pituitary still senses hormone feedback, so exogenous T3 suppresses TSH secretion; in a TSH-secreting adenoma the TSH is autonomous and fails to suppress. This dynamic difference in feedback responsiveness separates the two causes of elevated thyroid hormone with an unsuppressed TSH.'),
('7f8ffb8c-b1b4-595b-9278-33be2ec00b20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the T3 suppression test?', '["A test for hypothyroidism resulting in decreased functional activity","A test for goiter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measure radioactive iodine uptake after giving T3; normal uptake is suppressed; high uptake indicates autonomous function","A test for thyroiditis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A test for thyroid cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measure radioactive iodine uptake after giving T3; normal uptake is suppressed; high uptake indicates autonomous function', 'In this test, thyroid hormone is administered as T3 and radioactive iodine uptake is then measured: in a normal axis the T3 suppresses TSH and uptake falls, whereas persistently high uptake indicates autonomous thyroid function that no longer obeys feedback. It is not a test for hypothyroidism, goiter, thyroiditis, or cancer.'),
('94abb111-365b-591b-9b32-96b8d9d4d671'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the TRH stimulation test?', '["Measures TSH response to TRH administration; blunted in hyperthyroidism","A test for hyperparathyroidism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A test for Cushing mediated by specific receptor activation","A test for adrenal insufficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","exaggerated in hypothyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measures TSH response to TRH administration; blunted in hyperthyroidism', 'The TRH stimulation test measures the TSH response after intravenous TRH. In primary hyperthyroidism the response is blunted because high thyroid hormone suppresses the thyrotroph, while primary hypothyroidism gives an exaggerated rise; the test is unrelated to parathyroid, adrenal, or cortisol disorders.'),
('f2e89c68-83c5-521f-a0ce-14fd4b7335e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal TSH response to TRH?', '["Only in males resulting in decreased functional activity","Only in females accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","response mediated by specific receptor activation","TSH rises by 5-30 mIU/L above baseline at 20-30 minutes","Only in children leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TSH rises by 5-30 mIU/L above baseline at 20-30 minutes', 'In healthy individuals, TSH rises by about 5-30 mIU/L above the baseline value, peaking at 20-30 minutes after TRH injection. This numeric rise demonstrates an intact thyrotroph response and is not limited to any sex or age group.'),
('e36ecbc6-ad04-59b8-bfef-dd96626df91f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is euthyroid sick syndrome?', '["Low T3 (and sometimes T4) in critically ill patients without intrinsic thyroid disease","TSH deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal thyroid function mediated by specific receptor activation","True hypothyroidism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","True hyperthyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low T3 (and sometimes T4) in critically ill patients without intrinsic thyroid disease', 'Euthyroid sick syndrome is the finding of low T3, and sometimes low T4, in critically ill patients who have no intrinsic thyroid disease; it reflects altered peripheral deiodination and hormone handling during severe illness. TSH is usually normal, so it is neither true hypothyroidism nor hyperthyroidism and does not require thyroid hormone replacement.'),
('dfa29819-2693-5701-8d69-14b05e3de1be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the test for neonatal thyroid screening?', '["Thyroid ultrasound resulting in decreased functional activity","Radioactive iodine uptake accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroglobulin level mediated by specific receptor activation","FNA biopsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T4 (or TSH) from a heel-prick blood spot on filter paper"]'::jsonb, 'T4 (or TSH) from a heel-prick blood spot on filter paper', 'Newborn screening for congenital hypothyroidism measures T4, or TSH, from a dried blood spot obtained by heel prick onto filter paper, which permits mass testing of all infants. Imaging, uptake studies, thyroglobulin, and biopsy cannot identify a functional thyroid hormone deficit at population scale.'),
('bac0b560-4b1a-5c55-9bda-5a1431915d92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the treatment for congenital hypothyroidism?', '["Oral levothyroxine (T4) replacement starting at 10-15 mcg/kg/day as soon as possible after birth","T3 replacement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TSH injection mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroidectomy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iodine supplementation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oral levothyroxine (T4) replacement starting at 10-15 mcg/kg/day as soon as possible after birth', 'Congenital hypothyroidism is treated with oral levothyroxine (T4) at 10-15 mcg/kg/day, started as soon as possible after birth to preserve normal brain and neurodevelopmental maturation. T3-only therapy, TSH injection, thyroidectomy, and iodine supplementation are not appropriate primary treatments.'),
('a3f5ecb8-6731-5714-add4-2d82b30d0747'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the role of thyroid scintigraphy?', '["Anatomical imaging resulting in decreased functional activity","Assess thyroid function (hot vs. cold nodules) using Tc-99m pertechnetate or I-123","Measurement of gland volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Guiding FNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Detection of thyroid cancer recurrence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Assess thyroid function (hot vs. cold nodules) using Tc-99m pertechnetate or I-123', 'Thyroid scintigraphy with Tc-99m pertechnetate or I-123 evaluates function by classifying nodules as hot (hyperfunctioning) or cold (hypofunctioning) based on tracer uptake, aiding the workup of thyroid nodules and thyrotoxicosis. It provides functional rather than purely anatomical information and does not primarily measure volume, guide FNA, or stage cancer recurrence.'),
('1de8db71-e52f-5309-aa7d-8a008540b414'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is a cold thyroid nodule?', '["A nodule that takes up less radiotracer (hypofunctioning); has about 5-15% risk of malignancy","A normal variant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A benign nodule mediated by specific receptor activation","A hyperfunctioning nodule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A colloid cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A nodule that takes up less radiotracer (hypofunctioning); has about 5-15% risk of malignancy', 'A cold thyroid nodule takes up less radiotracer than the surrounding gland, indicating hypofunction, and carries an approximately 5-15% risk of malignancy that warrants further evaluation. Hot, hyperfunctioning nodules are rarely malignant, and a cold nodule is not a benign variant or colloid cyst by definition.'),
('0b7e353d-a0d5-5e17-8c5b-6bc07c62ffaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the role of FDG-PET in thyroid cancer?', '["Replacement for ultrasound resulting in decreased functional activity","Assessment of thyroid function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Initial diagnosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Screening nodules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Used for detecting metastatic disease in differentiated thyroid cancer with negative I-131 scans"]'::jsonb, 'Used for detecting metastatic disease in differentiated thyroid cancer with negative I-131 scans', 'FDG-PET is most useful in differentiated thyroid cancer that is thyroglobulin-positive but shows a negative I-131 whole-body scan, because de-differentiated metastases lose iodine avidity yet remain glucose-hypermetabolic. It does not replace ultrasound, assess function, screen nodules, or serve as the initial diagnostic tool.'),
('e8074445-b990-54bf-8735-eab4579708b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the parathyroid sestamibi scan?', '["A scan for pituitary tumors resulting in decreased functional activity","A scan for adrenal tumors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A scan for thyroid nodules mediated by specific receptor activation","A scan for bone metastases without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A nuclear medicine scan using Tc-99m sestamibi for localizing parathyroid adenomas"]'::jsonb, 'A nuclear medicine scan using Tc-99m sestamibi for localizing parathyroid adenomas', 'The sestamibi scan uses Tc-99m sestamibi, retained by mitochondria-rich parathyroid cells, to localize parathyroid adenomas preoperatively in primary hyperparathyroidism. It is a functional localization study rather than a scan for pituitary, adrenal, thyroid nodule, or bone pathologies.'),
('05ffd6e0-3d83-5ca7-98d4-ecd2900718ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the sensitivity of sestamibi for parathyroid adenoma?', '["100% resulting in decreased functional activity","50% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 85-90% for single adenomas","<20% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","30% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 85-90% for single adenomas', 'Sestamibi scanning localizes a single parathyroid adenoma in about 85-90% of cases, making it quite reliable in primary hyperparathyroidism. Lower values such as 30-50% underestimate and 100% overestimates its real diagnostic performance.'),
('27e93b30-24f6-5bec-8754-a4098a9bf318'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the role of PET/CT in neuroendocrine tumors?', '["A scan for adrenal tumors resulting in decreased functional activity","A scan for bone metastases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A scan for pituitary tumors mediated by specific receptor activation","A scan for thyroid cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Using Ga-68 DOTATATE (somatostatin analog) for imaging well-differentiated NETs expressing somatostatin receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Using Ga-68 DOTATATE (somatostatin analog) for imaging well-differentiated NETs expressing somatostatin receptors', 'PET/CT with Ga-68 DOTATATE, a somatostatin analogue, images well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumors that express somatostatin receptors, providing highly sensitive detection and staging of NETs. It is not directed at adrenal, bone, pituitary, or thyroid lesions in this context.'),
('18605e1d-de61-5af1-adf2-097ded5362b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the role of FDG-PET in NETs?', '["Only for low-grade NETs resulting in decreased functional activity","Only for benign tumors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Used for high-grade NETs (poorly differentiated) which are more FDG-avid; correlates with Ki-67 index","Only for parathyroid tumors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only for thyroid cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Used for high-grade NETs (poorly differentiated) which are more FDG-avid; correlates with Ki-67 index', 'FDG-PET is most informative in high-grade, poorly differentiated NETs, whose mitotically active cells are strongly FDG-avid, with uptake paralleling the Ki-67 proliferative index. Low-grade NETs are typically FDG-negative and are better evaluated with somatostatin-receptor imaging such as Ga-68 DOTATATE.'),
('2e310653-551f-52a9-aa7c-f626dc4b317a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the role of endoscopic ultrasound (EUS) in pancreatic NETs?', '["For colon imaging only resulting in decreased functional activity","For lung imaging only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Detects and localizes small PanNETs and guides FNA biopsy","For treating PanNETs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","For gastric imaging only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detects and localizes small PanNETs and guides FNA biopsy', 'Endoscopic ultrasound places the transducer adjacent to the pancreas, allowing detection and precise localization of small pancreatic NETs that may escape cross-sectional imaging, and it permits tissue diagnosis through EUS-guided FNA. It is a diagnostic and sampling technique restricted to the upper gastrointestinal tract, not a treatment modality.'),
('9d494ce5-252d-5dcc-a43b-025465fce5e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism in the elderly?', '["Toxic adenoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Hashitoxicosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Toxic multinodular goiter (Plummer disease)","Subacute thyroiditis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Graves disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Toxic multinodular goiter (Plummer disease)', 'Toxic multinodular goiter (Plummer disease) is the most common cause of hyperthyroidism in elderly patients, arising when nodules in a long-standing multinodular goiter become autonomously hyperfunctioning. Graves disease predominates in younger adults, while toxic adenoma, hashitoxicosis, and subacute thyroiditis are less frequent causes at older age.'),
('297a4930-291c-566b-bf16-711d3ac8fa67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is hashitoxicosis?', '["Transient hyperthyroidism in Hashimoto thyroiditis due to follicular destruction with hormone release","Toxic nodule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroiditis mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypothyroidism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Graves disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transient hyperthyroidism in Hashimoto thyroiditis due to follicular destruction with hormone release', 'Hashitoxicosis is a transient hyperthyroid phase in Hashimoto thyroiditis caused by lymphocytic destruction of follicles that releases preformed thyroid hormone into the circulation. It eventually gives way to hypothyroidism and is not Graves disease, a toxic nodule, or a permanent hyperthyroid state.'),
('d9e98e4a-00ed-529e-9ceb-2fc458746643'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is myxedema?', '["Normal variant resulting in decreased functional activity","Euthyroid with edema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperthyroidism with skin changes mediated by specific receptor activation","Severe hypothyroidism with nonpitting edema (mucopolysaccharide deposition in the skin)","Autoimmune skin disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe hypothyroidism with nonpitting edema (mucopolysaccharide deposition in the skin)', 'Myxedema is the severe end of hypothyroidism characterized by nonpitting edema from glycosaminoglycan (mucopolysaccharide) deposition in the skin and soft tissues. It reflects profound thyroid hormone deficiency rather than hyperthyroidism or a primary autoimmune skin disorder.'),
('aaacf7df-fb56-5df4-99e4-7e43ed718e41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the eye findings in Graves disease?', '["lid retraction","and extraocular muscle involvement","periorbital edema","proptosis (exophthalmos)","Lid lag"]'::jsonb, 'Lid lag', 'Lid lag is part of the eyelid signs of Graves orbitopathy and reflects sympathetic overactivity and infiltration of the eyelids; it is joined by lid retraction, proptosis, periorbital edema, and extraocular muscle involvement. The spectrum therefore includes both the sympathetic eyelid findings and the infiltrative manifestations of orbital disease.'),
('d27223f7-1827-59d5-9532-b7b04b24eb9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is a thyroid storm?', '["Graves disease resulting in decreased functional activity","delirium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","tachycardia mediated by specific receptor activation","and multiorgan failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A life-threatening exacerbation of hyperthyroidism with fever"]'::jsonb, 'A life-threatening exacerbation of hyperthyroidism with fever', 'Thyroid storm is a life-threatening decompensation of hyperthyroidism presenting with high fever, tachycardia, delirium, vomiting, and multiorgan failure, often precipitated by infection or surgery. It is an acute crisis of uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis rather than uncomplicated Graves disease.'),
('b93deab3-f1d2-5d33-bec3-b1c9af2cddaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the treatment for Graves disease?', '["radioactive iodine ablation","Antithyroid drugs (methimazole)","or thyroidectomy","Only radiation","Only surgery"]'::jsonb, 'Antithyroid drugs (methimazole)', 'Antithyroid drugs such as methimazole are the usual first-line treatment for Graves disease, blocking thyroid hormone synthesis by inhibiting thyroid peroxidase. Radioactive iodine ablation and thyroidectomy are valid alternative or second-line options, so management is not limited to radiation or surgery alone.'),
('e4bf4ca9-c562-5cb4-adc9-2c7b7854feea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the mechanism of propylthiouracil (PTU)?', '["Blocks TSH receptor resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulates TSH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibits TPO (like methimazole) and also inhibits T4-to-T3 conversion peripherally","Removes thyroid hormone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibits TSH secretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits TPO (like methimazole) and also inhibits T4-to-T3 conversion peripherally', 'Propylthiouracil inhibits thyroid peroxidase to block new hormone synthesis, like methimazole, and uniquely also inhibits peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion, giving faster symptom control in thyroid storm. It does not act at the TSH receptor, stimulate or secrete TSH, or remove circulating thyroid hormone.'),
('b969d3e7-79e5-5bd0-8af3-29a4603fb76d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the effect of amiodarone on the thyroid?', '["Only hyperthyroidism resulting in decreased functional activity","Only hypothyroidism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Can cause both hypo- and hyperthyroidism (amiodarone contains high iodine and has direct toxic effects)","Protects thyroid function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","thyroid effect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Can cause both hypo- and hyperthyroidism (amiodarone contains high iodine and has direct toxic effects)', 'Amiodarone is iodine-rich, and thyroid dysfunction from it is bidirectional: iodine overload can produce either hyperthyroidism (types I and II) or hypothyroidism, plus direct toxic and autoimmune effects on thyrocytes. It therefore cannot be described as protecting the gland or producing only a single direction of dysfunction.'),
('9ecf8282-5e71-5ce1-91f8-076b8654c2c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is lithium''s effect on the thyroid?', '["effect","Stimulates thyroid function","causing goiter and hypothyroidism in some patients","Inhibits thyroid hormone release","Causes hyperthyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits thyroid hormone release', 'Lithium concentrates within the thyroid and inhibits thyroid hormone release, which can lead to goiter and hypothyroidism in susceptible patients. It does not stimulate thyroid function or cause hyperthyroidism, so its net effect is toward reduced hormone secretion.'),
('793402fe-9916-5460-a642-e4b07ac090f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal range for free T4?', '["0.8-2.0 ng/dL","5-10 ng/dL","0.1-0.5 ng/dL","2-5 ng/dL",">10 ng/dL"]'::jsonb, '0.8-2.0 ng/dL', 'The normal serum free T4 range is approximately 0.8-2.0 ng/dL (roughly 10-26 pmol/L), with values below indicating hypothyroidism and values above consistent with thyrotoxicosis. Ranges of 2-5 ng/dL, 5-10 ng/dL, or >10 ng/dL lie well outside normal free T4 values.'),
('afb435cc-62c8-5c4c-a613-32564d5c3a30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal range for TSH?', '[">20 mIU/L","4-10 mIU/L","<0.1 mIU/L","10-20 mIU/L","0.4-4.0 mIU/L"]'::jsonb, '0.4-4.0 mIU/L', 'Normal serum TSH is 0.4-4.0 mIU/L. Levels above this range suggest hypothyroidism, while suppressed values below 0.1 mIU/L are typical of hyperthyroidism.'),
('3f1292e4-14b4-59d6-b86d-9f885cae5e27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal range for serum cortisol at 8 am?', '["50-100 mcg/dL","5-25 mcg/dL","25-50 mcg/dL","<5 mcg/dL",">100 mcg/dL"]'::jsonb, '5-25 mcg/dL', 'Morning (8 am) serum cortisol is normally 5-25 mcg/dL, reflecting the ACTH-driven circadian peak. Levels below 5 mcg/dL or above 25 mcg/dL suggest adrenal insufficiency or Cushing syndrome respectively.'),
('36d4e45e-58a2-52c7-94f8-52bfceb565e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal range for plasma aldosterone?', '[">30 ng/dL","1-10 ng/dL","<1 ng/dL",">50 ng/dL","4-21 ng/dL (upright)"]'::jsonb, '4-21 ng/dL (upright)', 'In an upright patient, plasma aldosterone normally ranges from 4-21 ng/dL. Secretion is stimulated by the renin-angiotensin system and by upright posture, so values must be interpreted with the plasma renin activity.'),
('ef366329-e4f2-5f1a-84ee-6fafc0dc0d44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal range for plasma renin activity (PRA)?', '[">30 ng/mL/hr","<0.5 ng/mL/hr","1-3 ng/mL/hr (normal sodium diet)","5-10 ng/mL/hr","10-20 ng/mL/hr"]'::jsonb, '1-3 ng/mL/hr (normal sodium diet)', 'On a normal sodium diet, plasma renin activity is 1-3 ng/mL/hr. A high-sodium diet suppresses renin, while low sodium or hypovolemia raises the value, so interpretation depends on sodium intake.'),
('4261d54c-6283-59ad-a626-682677c09788'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal range for 24-hour urinary metanephrines?', '[">10 mg/24h resulting in decreased functional activity",">5 mg/24h accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","<0.1 mg/24h mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal <1.0 mg/24h for metanephrine and <0.9 mg/24h for normetanephrine (varies by lab)",">2 mg/24h leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal <1.0 mg/24h for metanephrine and <0.9 mg/24h for normetanephrine (varies by lab)', 'Urinary metanephrines are the methylated metabolites of catecholamines; normal excretion is under 1.0 mg/24h for metanephrine and under 0.9 mg/24h for normetanephrine. Marked elevation suggests a pheochromocytoma.'),
('b4c46411-25da-583b-989e-57f7919675c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal range for serum PTH?', '["10-65 pg/mL (in reference to calcium level)",">500 pg/mL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","<5 pg/mL mediated by specific receptor activation",">200 pg/mL without affecting the primary tissue pathway",">100 pg/mL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10-65 pg/mL (in reference to calcium level)', 'Normal intact PTH is 10-65 pg/mL and is interpreted together with the serum calcium. Hypocalcemia raises PTH while hypercalcemia suppresses it, so an elevated PTH with normal calcium may indicate secondary hyperparathyroidism.'),
('252aa914-31ad-5086-989a-1ec759d2abdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal range for vitamin D (25-OH-D)?', '["<10 ng/mL (deficiency)","All of the above","20-50 ng/mL (sufficiency)","10-20 ng/mL (insufficiency)",">50 ng/mL (potential toxicity)"]'::jsonb, '20-50 ng/mL (sufficiency)', 'A serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D of 20-50 ng/mL indicates sufficiency. Levels below 10 ng/mL are deficient, 10-20 ng/mL are insufficient, and values above 50 ng/mL carry a risk of toxicity.'),
('06e84bf0-b0c9-5932-a3b6-7f39a9f0c116'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal range for serum calcitonin?', '["50-100 pg/mL resulting in decreased functional activity","<10 pg/mL (undetectable to low)",">1000 pg/mL mediated by specific receptor activation","10-50 pg/mL without affecting the primary tissue pathway",">100 pg/mL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '<10 pg/mL (undetectable to low)', 'Basal serum calcitonin is normally very low, under 10 pg/mL. Persistent elevation is a screening marker for medullary thyroid carcinoma.'),
('e0755d24-660f-53fd-ab2f-172da5d00f89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the normal range for IGF-1?', '["Varies by age and sex; peaks during puberty and declines with age","Higher in females accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Constant throughout life mediated by specific receptor activation","variation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Higher in older adults leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Varies by age and sex; peaks during puberty and declines with age', 'IGF-1 levels vary with age and sex, peaking during puberty and declining with advancing age. Results are therefore compared against age- and sex-matched reference ranges.'),
('64c99fef-3086-5996-89b9-3358b5b8b8c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most important structure to avoid during thyroid surgery?', '["The trachea resulting in decreased functional activity","The jugular vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The carotid artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The recurrent laryngeal nerve and the parathyroid glands","The esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve and the parathyroid glands', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve and the parathyroid glands lie posterior to the thyroid and are the structures most easily damaged during thyroidectomy. Their injury causes vocal cord paralysis or postoperative hypocalcemia.'),
('7acec2f8-42ac-5c83-8676-a46af753fda8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the course of the recurrent laryngeal nerve?', '["Both nerves run directly from the brain resulting in decreased functional activity","The left RLN recurs around the aortic arch; the right RLN recurs around the right subclavian artery","Both recur around the carotid arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","The nerve is superficial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The nerve runs anterior to the thyroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left RLN recurs around the aortic arch; the right RLN recurs around the right subclavian artery', 'The left recurrent laryngeal nerve recurs around the aortic arch, while the right recurs around the right subclavian artery; both then ascend in the tracheoesophageal groove to supply the laryngeal muscles.'),
('19ad3c84-c747-5f41-96c2-1de956fd695c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve?', '["A nerve that supplies the cricothyroid muscle for voice pitch; injury causes voice fatigue","A sympathetic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A sensory nerve to the larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","A parasympathetic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A motor nerve to vocal cords leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A nerve that supplies the cricothyroid muscle for voice pitch; injury causes voice fatigue', 'The external laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid muscle, which tenses the vocal cords and raises voice pitch. Its injury causes voice fatigue and difficulty producing high-pitched sounds.'),
('7eb310dc-0933-5df9-a26c-ddabc1e3c654'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the position of the parathyroid glands relative to the thyroid?', '["Anterior to the thyroid resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral to the thyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior parathyroids: posterior to the upper pole; Inferior: near the lower pole or in the thymic tongue","Unrelated to the thyroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inside the thyroid capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior parathyroids: posterior to the upper pole; Inferior: near the lower pole or in the thymic tongue', 'Superior parathyroid glands lie posterior to the upper pole of the thyroid, and inferior glands are near the lower pole or within the thyrothymic tract. Their posterior position places them at risk during thyroid lobectomy.'),
('2a426ec5-f08f-5986-ae28-951e86084b80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the most common location of ectopic parathyroid glands?', '["Within the thymus (especially inferior parathyroids)","In the posterior mediastinum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the anterior neck mediated by specific receptor activation","In the thyroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the carotid sheath leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Within the thymus (especially inferior parathyroids)', 'Inferior parathyroid glands descend with the thymus during development, so ectopic glands most commonly lie within the thymus or along the thyrothymic ligament. This explains the search for a missing gland in the mediastinum.'),
('c805222f-a69c-554b-a893-e46393ab08f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the blood supply of the parathyroid glands?', '["External carotid artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior thyroid artery (major supply) and superior thyroid artery","Internal carotid artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyrocervical trunk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior thyroid artery only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior thyroid artery (major supply) and superior thyroid artery', 'The parathyroid glands receive their major supply from the inferior thyroid artery, supplemented by the superior thyroid artery. Preserving this supply is important during thyroid surgery to avoid hypocalcemia.'),
('b8abd7c5-65b1-5260-9fb2-f9006f020775'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the PTH monitoring during parathyroid surgery?', '["Intraoperative PTH monitoring to confirm removal of the adenoma (PTH drops by >50% within 10-15 minutes)","monitoring needed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intraoperative vitamin D mediated by specific receptor activation","Postoperative PTH only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intraoperative calcium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intraoperative PTH monitoring to confirm removal of the adenoma (PTH drops by >50% within 10-15 minutes)', 'Intraoperative PTH measurement confirms removal of a parathyroid adenoma when the level drops by more than 50% within 10-15 minutes. A failure to fall indicates residual hyperfunctioning tissue.'),
('87eafa5a-9a0e-5073-b78c-82bd80403ed5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the success rate of parathyroidectomy for single adenoma?', '["60%","50%","75%","85%","95-98%"]'::jsonb, '95-98%', 'Parathyroidectomy cures primary hyperparathyroidism in 95-98% of patients when a single adenoma is excised, making surgery the definitive treatment for symptomatic disease.'),
('9251e4b6-6573-5502-9249-766cbb884053'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the Zuckerkandl tubercle?', '["A lateral projection of the thyroid lobe used as a landmark for the recurrent laryngeal nerve","A neck muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lymph node mediated by specific receptor activation","A thyroid nodule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A parathyroid adenoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A lateral projection of the thyroid lobe used as a landmark for the recurrent laryngeal nerve', 'The Zuckerkandl tubercle is a lateral projection of the thyroid lobe that overlies the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Identifying it helps the surgeon locate and protect the nerve during thyroidectomy.'),
('566b6fe9-be67-520f-ae33-58429cf83087'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Which option best describes the two main categories of endocrine glands?', '["Only ductless","Hormonal and neural","Ducted (exocrine) and ductless (endocrine)","Primary and secondary","Only ducted"]'::jsonb, 'Only ductless', 'Endocrine glands are ductless and release their hormones directly into the bloodstream, whereas exocrine glands discharge products through ducts. This absence of ducts is the defining feature of the endocrine category.'),
('ac7cd4ca-c5a9-5bc3-a588-7e902e897e65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the two main categories of endocrine glands? Which option is most accurate?', '["Only ductless","Hormonal and neural","Ducted (exocrine) and ductless (endocrine)","Primary and secondary","Only ducted"]'::jsonb, 'Only ductless', 'Endocrine glands are ductless, secreting hormones straight into surrounding capillaries rather than through duct systems. This distinguishes them from exocrine glands, which empty into ducts.'),
('31bf7885-b531-5ba6-9012-bfcc11f54c35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about the endocrine system: what are the two main categories of endocrine glands?', '["Only ductless","Hormonal and neural","Ducted (exocrine) and ductless (endocrine)","Primary and secondary","Only ducted"]'::jsonb, 'Only ductless', 'Endocrine glands have no ducts and release hormones directly into the blood, unlike exocrine glands which secrete through duct systems. Ductlessness is the key structural distinction of endocrine tissue.'),
('3a9205a4-ddab-5a20-a626-0c05c7f54a2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'For the endocrine system, identify the correct response: what are the two main categories of endocrine glands?', '["Only ductless","Hormonal and neural","Ducted (exocrine) and ductless (endocrine)","Primary and secondary","Only ducted"]'::jsonb, 'Only ductless', 'Endocrine glands are ductless and deliver their hormones directly into the bloodstream. Exocrine glands, in contrast, transport secretions through ducts to a surface or cavity.'),
('73b624de-2343-575d-a354-90ffb140f26c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the two main categories of endocrine glands? Select the best answer.', '["Only ductless","Hormonal and neural","Ducted (exocrine) and ductless (endocrine)","Primary and secondary","Only ducted"]'::jsonb, 'Only ductless', 'Endocrine glands are ductless and pour hormones directly into the blood, whereas exocrine glands use ducts to carry secretions. This ductless organization defines the endocrine class of glands.'),
('844a4c55-512d-5937-8041-d62967f962eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Which response correctly addresses this endocrine system item: what are the two main categories of endocrine glands?', '["Only ductless","Hormonal and neural","Ducted (exocrine) and ductless (endocrine)","Primary and secondary","Only ducted"]'::jsonb, 'Only ductless', 'Endocrine glands lack ducts and secrete hormones directly into the circulation. Exocrine glands differ because they deliver their products through a system of ducts.'),
('e4dc20d7-a02d-53fa-9db8-b69aee0f7aec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Which option best defines the structural organization of endocrine glands?', '["Tubules and ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","Squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ducts and acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network"]'::jsonb, 'Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network', 'Endocrine glands are built as cords or follicles of secretory cells surrounded by a rich network of fenestrated capillaries. This arrangement lets hormones pass directly from the cells into the blood.'),
('cf2a24d1-c405-506b-adbf-0dfca50dac99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the structural organization of endocrine glands? Which option is most accurate?', '["Tubules and ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","Squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ducts and acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network"]'::jsonb, 'Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network', 'Secretory cells of endocrine glands are arranged in cords or follicles with an abundant capillary plexus around them. The close cell-to-capillary contact allows rapid hormone entry into the circulation.'),
('085fb28b-f7d8-58a4-b388-53bc4ce3b2e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about the endocrine system: what is the structural organization of endocrine glands?', '["Tubules and ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","Squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ducts and acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network"]'::jsonb, 'Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network', 'Endocrine glands consist of cords or follicles of secretory cells embedded in a rich capillary network. The fenestrated capillaries permit direct release of hormones into the bloodstream.'),
('f65eb3a4-5a80-59b5-9668-023f44401ba0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'For the endocrine system, identify the correct response: what is the structural organization of endocrine glands?', '["Tubules and ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","Squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ducts and acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network"]'::jsonb, 'Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network', 'Endocrine glands show a characteristic arrangement of secretory cells as cords or follicles wrapped by a dense capillary network. This vascular proximity supports direct hormone secretion into blood.'),
('549b8b4f-3584-56f6-94d5-3ab0dd4d45f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the structural organization of endocrine glands? Select the best answer.', '["Tubules and ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","Squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ducts and acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network"]'::jsonb, 'Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network', 'Endocrine glands are organized as cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network between them. This design allows hormones to diffuse straight into the blood without ducts.'),
('4fa2b3ff-6374-5740-85b5-b4da97b664a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Which response correctly addresses this endocrine system item: what is the structural organization of endocrine glands?', '["Tubules and ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","Squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ducts and acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network"]'::jsonb, 'Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network', 'Endocrine tissue is arranged as cords or follicles of secretory cells surrounded by an extensive capillary network. The intimate cell-capillary relationship enables direct hormone release into the bloodstream.'),
('6eecb177-eef5-5401-8c99-d3bc16698493'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Which option best defines the height of the pituitary gland?', '["2 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","5 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 1 cm (pea-sized)","3 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","0.5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 cm (pea-sized)', 'The pituitary gland measures about 1 cm (pea-sized) and rests within the sella turcica. A pituitary adenoma enlarges the gland and can erode the sella or compress the optic chiasm.'),
('f07dd46e-0cd9-5137-b3ea-1f669aa65b95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the height of the pituitary gland? Which option is most accurate?', '["2 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","5 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 1 cm (pea-sized)","3 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","0.5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 cm (pea-sized)', 'The pituitary gland is about 1 cm in diameter, roughly pea-sized, and lies in the sella turcica. Enlargement of the gland suggests an adenoma and may cause sellar erosion.'),
('fa030181-a57e-5766-a704-afcf598421ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about the endocrine system: what is the height of the pituitary gland?', '["2 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","5 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 1 cm (pea-sized)","3 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","0.5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 cm (pea-sized)', 'The pituitary gland is approximately 1 cm (pea-sized) and occupies the hypophyseal fossa of the sella turcica. Mass lesions enlarge the gland and can compress adjacent structures.'),
('4e455512-eee2-5fad-a516-63a691358256'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'For the endocrine system, identify the correct response: what is the height of the pituitary gland?', '["2 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","5 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 1 cm (pea-sized)","3 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","0.5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 cm (pea-sized)', 'The pituitary gland is about 1 cm in size, comparable to a pea, and sits in the sella turcica. Sellar enlargement on imaging may indicate a pituitary adenoma.'),
('b8fa92e5-4ba6-5056-a75b-5395ddbcf830'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What is the height of the pituitary gland? Select the best answer.', '["2 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","5 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 1 cm (pea-sized)","3 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","0.5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 cm (pea-sized)', 'The pituitary gland measures about 1 cm (pea-sized) and lies within the sella turcica. Its small size makes sellar enlargement a useful clue to the presence of a tumor.'),
('f663267b-585c-5b00-bf22-3b551c8149f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Which response correctly addresses this endocrine system item: what is the height of the pituitary gland?', '["2 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","5 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 1 cm (pea-sized)","3 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","0.5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 cm (pea-sized)', 'The pituitary gland is roughly 1 cm (pea-sized) and is housed in the sella turcica. Adenomas enlarge the gland and may lead to sellar expansion or optic chiasmal compression.'),
('33466902-b639-5268-81cd-19c77f1e9340'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Which option best describes the two lobes of the pituitary gland?', '["Pars distalis and pars intermedia resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)","Superior and inferior lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","Left and right lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex and medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)', 'The pituitary gland is divided into the adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and the neurohypophysis (posterior lobe). The anterior lobe is glandular tissue, while the posterior lobe stores and releases oxytocin and ADH.'),
('18b3f574-a443-53ad-a342-3ef26e32b862'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the two lobes of the pituitary gland? Which option is most accurate?', '["Pars distalis and pars intermedia resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)","Superior and inferior lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","Left and right lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex and medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)', 'The pituitary consists of an adenohypophysis, the anterior lobe, and a neurohypophysis, the posterior lobe. The anterior lobe synthesizes hormones, whereas the posterior lobe stores neurohypophyseal hormones produced in the hypothalamus.'),
('e0047c31-f5de-5234-8ac0-6099a6713c9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about the endocrine system: what are the two lobes of the pituitary gland?', '["Pars distalis and pars intermedia resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)","Superior and inferior lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","Left and right lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex and medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)', 'The pituitary gland has two lobes: the adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and the neurohypophysis (posterior lobe). The anterior lobe is epithelial in origin, while the posterior lobe is derived from neural tissue.'),
('19983c80-fc3e-5cd1-8ae6-87252c2c002e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'For the endocrine system, identify the correct response: what are the two lobes of the pituitary gland?', '["Pars distalis and pars intermedia resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)","Superior and inferior lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","Left and right lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex and medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)', 'The pituitary comprises the adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and the neurohypophysis (posterior lobe). These lobes differ in embryology, blood supply, and the hormones they produce or store.'),
('e499c6e3-9a8a-5455-816a-80d67e430709'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'What are the two lobes of the pituitary gland? Select the best answer.', '["Pars distalis and pars intermedia resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)","Superior and inferior lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","Left and right lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex and medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)', 'The pituitary gland is made up of the adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and the neurohypophysis (posterior lobe). The adenohypophysis secretes tropic hormones, and the neurohypophysis releases oxytocin and ADH.'),
('b3ef0ac0-3831-5d8f-94f8-a2fbccb4b436'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Which response correctly addresses this endocrine system item: what are the two lobes of the pituitary gland?', '["Pars distalis and pars intermedia resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)","Superior and inferior lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","Left and right lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex and medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)', 'The pituitary gland has an adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and a neurohypophysis (posterior lobe). The anterior lobe arises from the oral ectoderm, and the posterior lobe from the diencephalon.'),
('92c2edc5-579b-5c8c-8e4c-1bbc204c0b82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Which option best defines the pars distalis of the pituitary?', '["The main anterior lobe portion containing hormone-secreting cells","The pituitary stalk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posterior lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","The intermediate lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The main anterior lobe portion containing hormone-secreting cells', 'The pars distalis is the main portion of the anterior lobe and contains the hormone-secreting cells such as somatotrophs, thyrotrophs, and corticotrophs. It is the largest subdivision of the adenohypophysis.'),
('3510eb14-a190-5188-a913-813c86656134'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'In endocrine histology, which statement best fits the following fact: what are the two main categories of endocrine glands?', '["Only ductless","Hormonal and neural","Ducted (exocrine) and ductless (endocrine)","Primary and secondary","Only ducted"]'::jsonb, 'Only ductless', 'Endocrine glands are ductless and secrete hormones directly into the bloodstream, unlike exocrine glands which use ducts. This ductless nature is the defining characteristic of the endocrine category.'),
('2fe3331d-5c28-5e80-90ff-871731ba01af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Regarding endocrine histology, what is the correct answer to: what is the structural organization of endocrine glands?', '["Tubules and ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","Squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ducts and acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network"]'::jsonb, 'Cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network', 'Endocrine glands are organized as cords or follicles of secretory cells with a rich capillary network around them. This arrangement permits direct discharge of hormones into the circulation.'),
('dde2862b-44a5-582d-a65c-c70631fcab6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'For endocrine histology, which option is most appropriate: what is the height of the pituitary gland?', '["2 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","5 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 1 cm (pea-sized)","3 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","0.5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 cm (pea-sized)', 'The pituitary gland measures about 1 cm (pea-sized) and sits in the sella turcica. Adenomas enlarge the gland, producing sellar expansion visible on imaging.'),
('1a69c041-40b3-5129-88d0-8474da33c996'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'Which answer is correct in endocrine histology when asked: what are the two lobes of the pituitary gland?', '["Pars distalis and pars intermedia resulting in decreased functional activity","Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)","Superior and inferior lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","Left and right lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex and medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and neurohypophysis (posterior lobe)', 'The pituitary gland is divided into the adenohypophysis (anterior lobe) and the neurohypophysis (posterior lobe). The adenohypophysis is glandular and the neurohypophysis is neural in origin.'),
('b53ee5a2-6f9f-5713-af67-bb76036ac809'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'In endocrine histology, what is the pars distalis of the pituitary?', '["The main anterior lobe portion containing hormone-secreting cells","The pituitary stalk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posterior lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","The intermediate lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The main anterior lobe portion containing hormone-secreting cells', 'The pars distalis is the chief part of the anterior lobe and holds the hormone-secreting cells, including somatotrophs and lactotrophs. It is the functional bulk of the adenohypophysis.'),
('e6bddf16-6ed8-59c6-9133-cfa59693430b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'During revision of endocrine histology, which of the following is correct: what is the pars intermedia of the pituitary?', '["A thin zone between the anterior and posterior lobes","The infundibulum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The main anterior lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","The posterior lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The median eminence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin zone between the anterior and posterior lobes', 'The pars intermedia is a thin zone lying between the anterior and posterior lobes, often containing colloid-filled follicles in the human gland. It is vestigial in adults, and its cells can produce MSH.'),
('22cd8fbd-f169-5acc-8d8a-7d58e25a94a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Endocrine System', 'In endocrine histology, which statement best fits the following fact: what is the pars tuberalis?', '["An extension of the adenohypophysis wrapping around the infundibulum","The anterior lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The capsule mediated by specific receptor activation","The intermediate lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The posterior lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An extension of the adenohypophysis wrapping around the infundibulum', 'The pars tuberalis is an upward extension of the adenohypophysis that wraps around the infundibulum (pituitary stalk). It lies in contact with the median eminence and is part of the anterior pituitary.'),
('9eed78f5-66c8-563b-bb72-ce5b24f76497'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which structure forms the anterior boundary of the oral cavity?', '["Lips","Teeth","Palate","Tongue","Cheeks"]'::jsonb, 'Lips', 'The lips form the anterior (oral) boundary of the oral cavity, whereas the palate forms the roof and the cheeks the lateral walls. The teeth lie within the cavity rather than at its boundary, and the tongue occupies the floor.'),
('af5f1839-a329-5687-be0f-acc9358f0c1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of epithelium lines the oral cavity?', '["Simple columnar","Simple squamous","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Pseudostratified columnar","Stratified squamous keratinized"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The oral mucosa is chiefly lined by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, which protects against friction without a surface cornified layer. Focal keratinization occurs normally on the hard palate and gingiva, so the keratinized variant is not the general lining, and the other listed epithelia are not found in the oral cavity.'),
('cb7c964c-e853-5f0b-8aef-107902e0a37a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which papillae of the tongue contain taste buds?', '["Fungiform and foliate resulting in decreased functional activity","Filiform accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fungiform foliate and circumvallate","Foliate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fungiform leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fungiform foliate and circumvallate', 'Taste buds reside in the fungiform, foliate, and circumvallate papillae of the tongue. Filiform papillae lack taste buds and only provide the tongue''s friction surface, so any choice omitting filiform while listing the gustatory papillae is incomplete.'),
('359cb807-2beb-55e5-b07f-9d89c4d8b0b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the histological structure of the esophageal epithelium?', '["Stratified squamous keratinized","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Transitional","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The esophageal lumen is lined by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, which withstands abrasion during swallowing. Unlike the keratinized form found in the masticatory mucosa, the esophagus does not cornify, and simple or transitional epithelia are not present here.'),
('49425a1b-be1e-51ac-88bd-a6b0a973300d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Where are esophageal cardiac glands located?', '["Submucosa of upper esophagus","Submucosa of lower esophagus","Lamina propria of upper esophagus","Muscularis mucosae","Lamina propria of lower esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Lamina propria of lower esophagus', 'Esophageal cardiac glands are mucus-secreting glands located in the lamina propria, mainly near the lower esophagus adjacent to the gastro-esophageal junction (a few also occur at the upper end). Their position in the lamina propria rather than submucosa distinguishes them from the submucosal esophageal glands proper.'),
('11a40a4a-6ab5-54f9-805e-d831be0b2f02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of muscle is found in the upper third of the esophagus?', '["Smooth muscle","None","Both skeletal and smooth","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal muscle', 'The upper third of the esophageal muscularis externa is composed of skeletal (striated voluntary) muscle, continuing from the pharynx. The middle third is mixed, and the lower third is smooth muscle, so the upper third is exclusively skeletal.'),
('b31c27a8-3edc-5828-8369-26c48a290612'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which layer of the gut tube is derived from the splanchnic mesoderm?', '["Epithelium","Lamina propria","Serosa","Submucosa","Muscularis externa"]'::jsonb, 'Muscularis externa', 'The muscularis externa (smooth muscle coats) develops from the splanchnic mesoderm surrounding the gut tube. The epithelium arises from endoderm, whereas the lamina propria, submucosa, and serosa derive from splanchnic mesenchyme investing the tube rather than representing its muscular derivative.'),
('e5cd09fe-eb7a-5fc3-8df8-527c9aea62aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of the muscularis mucosae?', '["Secrete mucus","Absorb nutrients","Move the mucosa","Innervate the gut","Provide blood supply"]'::jsonb, 'Move the mucosa', 'The muscularis mucosae is a thin smooth muscle layer whose contraction folds and moves the mucosa, keeping the absorptive surface in contact with luminal contents. Mucus secretion is a function of glandular epithelium, nerve supply comes from the plexuses, and absorption is performed by mucosal enterocytes.'),
('0dcc5496-a12d-52b3-945f-887b542ba8f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which plexus is located between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the muscularis externa?', '["Meissner''s plexus","Both Auerbach and myenteric","Auerbach''s plexus","Submucosal plexus","Myenteric plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Auerbach''s plexus', 'Auerbach''s plexus, also called the myenteric plexus, lies between the circular and longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa and coordinates gut motility. Meissner''s (submucosal) plexus resides in the submucosa, not between the muscle coats, so it belongs to a different layer of the wall.'),
('e4a297f5-8f39-5511-bf1c-c89e3b5a9f08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of epithelium lines the stomach?', '["Stratified squamous","Pseudostratified columnar","Simple columnar","Transitional","Simple cuboidal"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar', 'The stomach is lined by a single layer of columnar surface mucous cells that secrete a protective mucus layer. Stratified, transitional, and cuboidal epithelia do not line the gastric lumen.'),
('780f9820-fc5c-5d77-a99f-58ef77583aa4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which cells secrete hydrochloric acid in the stomach?', '["Chief cells","Goblet cells","Enterochromaffin-like cells","Parietal cells","G cells"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal cells', 'Parietal (oxyntic) cells of the fundic glands secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor. Chief cells release pepsinogen, enterochromaffin-like cells release histamine, and G cells release gastrin, so none of these produce acid.'),
('764b0bb9-e8c1-5507-90c9-b387b039ab00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which cells secrete pepsinogen in the stomach?', '["Parietal cells","Enterochromaffin cells","G cells","D cells","Chief cells"]'::jsonb, 'Chief cells', 'Chief (zymogen) cells at the base of fundic glands secrete pepsinogen, which is activated to pepsin in acidic gastric juice. Parietal cells make acid and intrinsic factor, G cells make gastrin, and D cells make somatostatin.'),
('52c73b50-d62b-55ea-95aa-c7f4f12b6436'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Where are gastric pits located?', '["As invaginations of the surface epithelium","In the muscularis mucosae","In the lamina propria","In the submucosa","On the surface epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'As invaginations of the surface epithelium', 'Gastric pits are shallow tubular invaginations of the surface gastric epithelium that open into the gastric glands below. They are not part of the muscularis mucosae, lamina propria, or submucosa, which lie beneath the epithelium.'),
('f75df467-b5eb-5365-ab23-dc912b1c4dbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of surface mucous cells in the stomach?', '["Secrete mucus","Secrete pepsinogen","Secrete intrinsic factor","Secrete acid","Secrete gastrin"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete mucus', 'Surface mucous cells produce alkaline mucus that coats and protects the gastric epithelium from acid and pepsin. Pepsinogen comes from chief cells, intrinsic factor and acid from parietal cells, and gastrin from G cells.'),
('c7d77d1f-7b61-5934-a2dc-3acbba0ef09e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which gastric gland region is found in the body of the stomach?', '["Cardiac glands","Brunner''s glands","Fundic glands","Esophageal glands","Pyloric glands"]'::jsonb, 'Fundic glands', 'The body and fundus of the stomach are occupied by fundic (gastric proper) glands that house parietal and chief cells. Cardiac glands lie near the cardia and pyloric glands near the pylorus, while Brunner''s glands belong to the duodenal submucosa.'),
('960d5ff3-cfd1-5811-b288-ac9efa35306c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What hormone do G cells secrete?', '["Cholecystokinin","Histamine","Gastrin","Secretin","Somatostatin"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrin', 'G cells of the pyloric antrum secrete gastrin, which stimulates acid production by parietal cells. Cholecystokinin comes from I cells, secretin from S cells, and somatostatin from D cells, while histamine is released by enterochromaffin-like cells.'),
('a14cea08-d76e-55d6-ad7c-d5b44470797f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which cells secrete somatostatin in the stomach?', '["Parietal cells","Chief cells","G cells","D cells","Enterochromaffin cells"]'::jsonb, 'D cells', 'D cells of the gastric glands secrete somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release and acid secretion. Parietal cells make acid and intrinsic factor, chief cells make pepsinogen, and G cells make gastrin.'),
('42ffc593-0725-5a47-8e29-c024a0ef220a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of intrinsic factor?', '["Absorb iron","Secrete bicarbonate","Absorb vitamin B12","Digest proteins","Digest fats"]'::jsonb, 'Absorb vitamin B12', 'Intrinsic factor, secreted by gastric parietal cells, binds dietary vitamin B12 and enables its absorption in the terminal ileum. It does not transport iron, buffer the duodenum, or carry out enzymatic digestion of proteins or fats.'),
('b38f88fb-bc8e-570b-9290-f2afd84dcb05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which layer of the stomach forms rugae?', '["Submucosa","Mucosa","Serosa","Muscularis externa","Lamina propria"]'::jsonb, 'Submucosa', 'Gastric rugae are macroscopically visible folds produced by the mucosa riding on the thick, loose submucosa; the submucosa provides the connective tissue core that establishes the folds, which flatten as the stomach distends. The serosa and muscularis externa do not form these mucosal folds.'),
('3e96467c-fb8c-5d90-bb9d-bb00d782b155'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is unique about the muscularis externa of the stomach?', '["Has longitudinal layer","Has skeletal muscle","Has three layers","Has four layers","Has only circular muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Has three layers', 'The gastric muscularis externa is unique because it has three smooth muscle layers: oblique, circular, and longitudinal. Elsewhere in the gut there are only circular and longitudinal coats, and no skeletal muscle is present in the stomach wall.'),
('87b90c3b-b88a-54b2-b774-3b55344ab750'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which structure increases the surface area of the small intestine?', '["Microvilli","Villi","Plicae circularis","Circular folds","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The small intestine amplifies its surface area at three levels: macroscopic plicae circulares, microscopic villi, and submicroscopic microvilli (brush border). Since every listed structure contributes, all of the above is the complete answer.'),
('785b6362-a57e-5934-b31f-f35984ba134f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the core of an intestinal villus composed of?', '["Smooth muscle only resulting in decreased functional activity","Cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Epithelium only mediated by specific receptor activation","Nervous tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connective tissue with blood vessels and lacteals"]'::jsonb, 'Connective tissue with blood vessels and lacteals', 'Each villus has a core of lamina propria connective tissue containing a capillary network for absorbing monosaccharides and amino acids and a central lacteal (lymphatic) for absorbing dietary fat. The surface epithelium overlies this core, so muscle, cartilage, or nerve alone cannot form it.'),
('2f0004ac-f13b-5ef5-a4b2-403cb7bd9a45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Where are Paneth cells located?', '["Intestinal crypts of Lieberkühn","On villi","In the submucosa","Peyer''s patches","In the muscularis mucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal crypts of Lieberkühn', 'Paneth cells sit at the base of the crypts of Lieberkühn (intestinal glands) in the mucosa of the small intestine, where they secrete antimicrobial peptides. They are not found on villi, in the submucosa, in Peyer''s patches, or in the muscularis mucosae.'),
('7f813633-0808-579b-a305-b1df9edba943'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What do Paneth cells secrete?', '["Mucus","Lysozyme and defensins","Bicarbonate","Hormones","Digestive enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Lysozyme and defensins', 'Paneth cells synthesize antibacterial products, chiefly lysozyme and defensins, that protect the crypt stem cells from luminal microbes. Goblet cells make mucus, S cells make secretin, and digestive enzymes are not a Paneth cell product.'),
('d1b8fee4-8145-53d4-a0f1-5e8f639e09f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which cells in the small intestine are responsible for absorption?', '["Enteroendocrine cells","Enterocytes","M cells","Paneth cells","Goblet cells"]'::jsonb, 'Enterocytes', 'Enterocytes (absorptive cells) are the tall columnar cells with microvilli that absorb nutrients across the small intestine lining. Enteroendocrine cells release hormones, M cells sample antigens, Paneth cells fight microbes, and goblet cells secrete mucus.'),
('2fd7f6b5-0715-54db-ac4a-f68b58fdde5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of M cells?', '["Digestion","Antigen sampling","Secretion of mucus","Secretion of hormones","Absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Antigen sampling', 'Microfold (M) cells overlying Peyer''s patches take up luminal antigens and deliver them to the underlying lymphoid tissue to initiate immune responses. They neither digest food, secrete mucus or hormones, nor absorb nutrients.'),
('7a869dae-d05e-50be-9cd3-e8399f287c22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Brunner''s glands are characteristically found in which part of the gastrointestinal tract?', '["Esophagus","Stomach body","Duodenal submucosa","Jejunal mucosa","Colonic submucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal submucosa', 'Brunner''s glands are mucus- and bicarbonate-secreting glands located in the submucosa of the duodenum (most abundant in the first part). Their location is restricted to the duodenum, so they are absent in the esophagus, stomach, jejunum, and colon.'),
('015a280a-587e-5f70-acdf-9b31323f7954'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of epithelium lines the small intestine?', '["Simple columnar","Simple cuboidal","Pseudostratified columnar","Transitional","Stratified squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar', 'The small intestine is lined by simple columnar epithelium whose enterocytes bear microvilli and whose goblet cells secrete mucus. This single-layered tall epithelium is suited to absorption, unlike the protective or urinary epithelia listed.'),
('38b2b648-f63d-5cff-960c-e59b5f43f543'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of the brush border?', '["Absorption and terminal digestion","Protection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Storage mediated by specific receptor activation","Secretion only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Propulsion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absorption and terminal digestion', 'The brush border is the microvillar surface of enterocytes that both absorbs nutrients and carries disaccharidases and peptidases for terminal digestion of carbohydrates and proteins. Because its dual roles are absorption and final breakdown, the other single-function choices are incomplete.'),
('832f69c7-f0bd-5490-8212-7f78509da63e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which region of the small intestine has Peyer''s patches most prominently?', '["Colon","Duodenum","Jejunum","Ileum","All regions equally"]'::jsonb, 'Ileum', 'Peyer''s patches, aggregated lymphoid follicles, are most numerous and best developed in the ileum, especially its antimesenteric wall. They are sparse or absent in the duodenum and jejunum, and the colon is not part of the small intestine.'),
('9184de10-af8e-526b-8f16-8fff0234f4b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What cell type in the intestinal epithelium secretes mucus?', '["Enteroendocrine cells","Enterocytes","M cells","Paneth cells","Goblet cells"]'::jsonb, 'Goblet cells', 'Goblet cells are the mucus-producing cells interspersed among enterocytes in the intestinal epithelium. Enterocytes absorb nutrients, enteroendocrine cells release hormones, M cells sample antigens, and Paneth cells secrete antimicrobial peptides.'),
('12a6cc72-76bc-5ba0-91d7-9ef8ecf01308'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are plicae circulares?', '["Temporary folds of mucosa resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphoid follicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Smooth muscle bands mediated by specific receptor activation","Villi without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Permanent circular folds of mucosa and submucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Permanent circular folds of mucosa and submucosa', 'Plicae circulares (valves of Kerckring) are permanent annular folds involving both mucosa and submucosa that increase the absorptive surface of the small intestine. Unlike temporary mucosal folds, they are fixed structures, and they are not villi, follicles, or muscle bands.'),
('71b2dabd-379e-55ba-b511-a8ea21cb179a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which cell produces cholecystokinin?', '["S cells","G cells","K cells","I cells","L cells"]'::jsonb, 'I cells', 'I cells of the proximal small intestine produce cholecystokinin, which stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion. S cells make secretin, G cells make gastrin, K cells make GIP, and L cells make GLP-1.'),
('4d03233d-7ed3-5150-9ec5-7f0154415351'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What does secretin stimulate?', '["Intestinal peristalsis","Bile secretion","Gastric acid secretion","Mucus secretion","Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion', 'Secretin, released by S cells in response to duodenal acid, stimulates the pancreatic duct cells to secrete a bicarbonate-rich fluid that neutralizes gastric acid. It does not drive peristalsis or mucus secretion and suppresses rather than promotes gastric acid output.'),
('42d1ada2-acc5-52df-a10c-b686fba91884'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which hormone is secreted by K cells?', '["Gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP)","Ghrelin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motilin mediated by specific receptor activation","Glucagon-like peptide 1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peptide YY leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP)', 'K cells of the duodenum and jejunum secrete gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP; glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide), a hormone that augments insulin release and inhibits gastric secretion. Ghrelin comes from gastric X/A-like cells, motilin and PYY from enteroendocrine cells of the small and large intestine, and GLP-1 from L cells.'),
('6fb4c451-109c-5c47-acaf-1e7891fc9e51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Where are enteroendocrine cells located?', '["In the submucosa","In the lamina propria","In Peyer''s patches","In the muscularis mucosae","Interspersed among epithelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Interspersed among epithelial cells', 'Enteroendocrine cells are single hormone-secreting cells scattered between the columnar cells lining the gastrointestinal glands and mucosal surface. They are not situated in the submucosa, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae, or lymphoid follicles.'),
('27462463-8ec5-5302-ad91-fa6b42c4338f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of the lacteal?', '["Absorption of amino acids","Absorption of water","Absorption of monosaccharides","Absorption of vitamins","Absorption of dietary fats"]'::jsonb, 'Absorption of dietary fats', 'The lacteal is the blind-ended lymphatic capillary in each villus that takes up absorbed chylomicrons, so it transports dietary fats. Amino acids and monosaccharides enter the blood capillaries of the villus rather than the lacteal.'),
('aa771ac4-b3ec-52ab-9618-3dae35837e6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of cells are found at the base of intestinal crypts?', '["Goblet cells only","M cells only","Enterocytes only","Paneth cells and stem cells","Enteroendocrine cells only"]'::jsonb, 'Paneth cells and stem cells', 'The base of the crypts of Lieberkühn contains Paneth cells, which secrete antimicrobial peptides, and multipotent intestinal stem cells that replenish the epithelium. Goblet cells, enterocytes, and enteroendocrine cells occupy the crypt walls and villi but are not confined to the base.'),
('94f12f6d-a830-596d-af69-5d1eb0a90735'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the average turnover time for intestinal epithelial cells?', '["1 day","7-10 days","3-5 days","14 days","30 days"]'::jsonb, '3-5 days', 'Intestinal epithelial cells are renewed rapidly, migrating from crypt stem cells to the villus tip and being shed after roughly 3-5 days. The other time intervals overestimate a process that must keep pace with constant mechanical and digestive stress.'),
('7b813ff9-ece3-5b07-bef1-438c97aef97e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What structure is found between adjacent enterocytes that prevents leakage?', '["Hemidesmosomes","Gap junctions","Desmosomes","Adherens junctions","Tight junctions"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions', 'Tight junctions (zonulae occludentes) seal the apical borders between adjacent enterocytes, preventing paracellular leakage of luminal contents. Desmosomes and adherens junctions provide adhesion, gap junctions allow cell-to-cell communication, and hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basement membrane.'),
('1dca9f38-703d-55b2-80ba-cf75c17def99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of epithelium lines the colon?', '["Simple cuboidal","Transitional","Pseudostratified columnar","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar', 'The colonic mucosa is covered by simple columnar epithelium consisting mainly of absorptive colonocytes and abundant goblet cells. No stratified, transitional, or cuboidal lining is present in the large intestinal lumen.'),
('5ad1105a-00b9-5a4e-9b00-8de06d2fe328'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the characteristic features of the large intestine surface?', '["No villi","None of the above","Villi present","branched glands","straight tubular glands"]'::jsonb, 'No villi', 'Unlike the small intestine, the large intestine has a flat surface without villi; its mucosa dips into straight tubular glands (crypts of Lieberkühn) rich in goblet cells. Branched glands are a feature of Brunner''s glands rather than the colonic surface.'),
('e326210c-d8ec-52dd-b9e5-df84e9674779'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which cell type is most abundant in the colonic epithelium?', '["Goblet cells","Enterocytes","Paneth cells","M cells","Enteroendocrine cells"]'::jsonb, 'Goblet cells', 'Goblet cells vastly outnumber absorptive cells in the colonic epithelium, matching the colon''s role of lubricating and propelling feces. Paneth cells are rare in the colon, and M cells and enteroendocrine cells are minor constituents.'),
('dcaa424b-71e3-5c71-b495-6bbf3206a7e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are teniae coli?', '["Lymphoid aggregates","Three bands of longitudinal smooth muscle","Serosal fat tags","Circular muscle thickenings","Mucosal folds"]'::jsonb, 'Three bands of longitudinal smooth muscle', 'The teniae coli are three ribbon-like bands of condensed longitudinal smooth muscle in the colonic muscularis externa, causing the sacculations (haustra). They are neither lymphoid tissue, subserosal fat, circular muscle, nor mucosal folds.'),
('765c601c-ffcb-5260-bddf-6fdb2b3ebf5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are appendices epiploicae?', '["Muscle bands","Lymphoid nodules","Mucosal projections","Fat-filled pouches of serosa","Glandular structures"]'::jsonb, 'Fat-filled pouches of serosa', 'Appendices epiploicae are small fat-filled pouches of the serosa (subserosal adipose tissue) hanging from the outer surface of the colon. They are not muscular, lymphoid, mucosal, or glandular structures.'),
('38e82d9e-d52f-5351-be86-1bc0d42e1391'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of the vermiform appendix?', '["Secretion of enzymes resulting in decreased functional activity","Digestion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Immune function with abundant lymphoid tissue","Absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Storage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immune function with abundant lymphoid tissue', 'The appendix is rich in diffuse and nodular lymphoid tissue (gut-associated lymphoid tissue), giving it a primarily immune role in mucosal defense. It does not secrete enzymes, digest food, or serve meaningful absorption or storage functions.'),
('bfb4ff1b-5547-58e9-b439-f16ee501f7fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the histological structure of the appendix?', '["Same as ileum resulting in decreased functional activity","Same as colon with more lymphoid tissue","Same as rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","Same as esophagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Same as stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Same as colon with more lymphoid tissue', 'Histologically the appendix resembles the colon but has abundant lymphoid follicles that often obliterate its lumen. Its ileal, rectal, esophageal, and gastric similarities are all lesser because each of those organs has a distinctly different wall architecture.'),
('ff4d7e01-2713-5492-88f4-1f95ef43ca18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What forms the ano-rectal junction?', '["Transition from simple squamous to columnar","transition occurs","Transition from columnar to transitional","Transition from pseudostratified to cuboidal","Transition from simple columnar to stratified squamous epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Transition from simple columnar to stratified squamous epithelium', 'At the anorectal junction (anal columns, dentate line region) the simple columnar epithelium of the rectum and anal canal gives way to the stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium of the anal canal. The other described transitions, such as squamous-to-columnar or columnar-to-transitional, do not occur at this site.'),
('cd4df7a5-709d-539f-8870-83333ceb14d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the dentate line?', '["Junction between ileum and cecum","Line of peritoneal reflection","Junction between duodenum and jejunum","Junction between stomach and esophagus","Junction between ectoderm and endoderm in anal canal"]'::jsonb, 'Junction between ectoderm and endoderm in anal canal', 'The dentate (pectinate) line marks the fusion point of endoderm-derived hindgut tissue above and ectoderm-derived proctodeal tissue below in the anal canal. It is not the ileocecal junction, peritoneal reflection, duodenojejunal flexure, or gastroesophageal junction.'),
('f8e7787a-421c-59f0-958a-fc785f0f833b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of glands are found in the anal canal above the dentate line?', '["Sebaceous glands","Apocrine sweat glands","Brunner''s glands","Simple tubular glands","No glands"]'::jsonb, 'No glands', 'The columnar mucosa of the anal canal above the dentate line has no glands; the only anal glands lie in the submucosa below the dentate line, and sebaceous and apocrine glands belong to perianal skin. Brunner''s glands are confined to the duodenum.'),
('3e05f9b4-a86f-513b-b650-f5afcb85c5cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the basic functional unit of the liver?', '["Portal triad","Hepatic acinus","Hepatocyte","Sinusoid","Hepatic lobule"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic lobule', 'The hepatic lobule is the classical structural unit of the liver, a hexagonal prism centered on the central vein. The hepatic acinus is the functional unit defined by blood flow, while hepatocytes are the parenchymal cells and portal triads lie at the lobule corners.'),
('220ba2e6-d6c3-5148-acd7-2f2d268fdee3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What structures are found in a portal triad?', '["hepatic vein","Portal vein","bile duct","hepatic artery","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'The portal triad consists of a portal venule, hepatic arteriole, and bile ductule. The portal vein carries nutrient-rich blood from the gut into the liver and is one of the three components of the triad; the central or hepatic vein is not part of it.'),
('a1b10e63-939c-51b1-a19d-4c331844f301'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the space of Disse?', '["Space beneath the capsule","Space in the portal triad","Space between hepatocytes and sinusoids","Space between hepatocytes","Space between sinusoid and bile canaliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Space between hepatocytes and sinusoids', 'The space of Disse (perisinusoidal space) lies between hepatocytes and the fenestrated sinusoid endothelium, where plasma flows and exchange occurs. It also houses the perisinusoidal stellate (Ito) cells and a fine reticular stroma.'),
('f4729e03-9006-5143-9ce7-7fe164fd9c0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which cells line the hepatic sinusoids?', '["Hepatocytes only","Nerve cells","Endothelial cells and Kupffer cells","Smooth muscle cells","Fibroblasts only"]'::jsonb, 'Endothelial cells and Kupffer cells', 'Hepatic sinusoids are lined by fenestrated, discontinuous endothelial cells supported by phagocytic Kupffer cells. Hepatocytes form cords between sinusoids rather than lining them.'),
('b14599c2-d36c-5a91-ba61-522faa05c157'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of Kupffer cells?', '["Detoxification","Glucose storage","Bile secretion","Phagocytosis of debris and old RBCs","Protein synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Phagocytosis of debris and old RBCs', 'Kupffer cells are the resident hepatic macrophages that engulf debris, worn-out red blood cells, and blood-borne pathogens. Detoxification, bile secretion, glucose storage, and protein synthesis are carried out by hepatocytes.'),
('5e4f5479-8dc7-5f87-a9da-a4ad7c6c9f3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are stellate cells (Ito cells) in the liver?', '["Fat-storing cells in space of Disse","Epithelial cells of bile ducts","Hepatocyte precursors","Immune cells in sinusoids","Endothelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Fat-storing cells in space of Disse', 'Stellate (Ito) cells are perisinusoidal cells located in the space of Disse that store fat (mainly vitamin A). On hepatic injury they transform into myofibroblasts and drive fibrosis.'),
('da82dd51-a81b-5dba-9a45-846a979ecc18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of hepatocytes?', '["metabolism","Protein synthesis","bile production","Filter blood only","Store vitamins only"]'::jsonb, 'Protein synthesis', 'Hepatocytes synthesize most plasma proteins, including albumin and clotting factors. They are also responsible for bile production and metabolism, but protein synthesis is the hallmark synthetic function among the listed choices.'),
('53eeecc0-efaf-5fe6-90c2-2da3bde54937'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which organ is responsible for bile production?', '["Small intestine","Gallbladder","Stomach","Liver","Pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Hepatocytes of the liver synthesize bile continuously. The gallbladder only stores and concentrates bile, while the small intestine, stomach, and pancreas do not produce it.'),
('ad067e89-564d-57d7-8ea3-fbb586741d00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the path of bile flow from hepatocytes?', '["Hepatocyte directly to gallbladder","Bile canaliculus to sinusoid","Bile canaliculus to bile duct to hepatic duct","Bile duct to sinusoid","Hepatocyte to portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'Bile canaliculus to bile duct to hepatic duct', 'Bile secreted by hepatocytes drains into bile canaliculi between adjacent hepatocytes, then into bile ductules and ducts that join to form the hepatic duct. Bile never enters sinusoids or the portal vein.'),
('3c437923-d56d-507c-89c3-5dbffb61466d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the limiting plate of the liver?', '["The bile duct lining resulting in decreased functional activity","The basement membrane of sinusoids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The outermost layer of hepatocytes surrounding a portal triad","The capsule of the liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central vein wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The outermost layer of hepatocytes surrounding a portal triad', 'The limiting plate is the single peripheral rim of hepatocytes that surrounds a portal triad and delimits the classic lobule. Loss of hepatocytes here with piecemeal necrosis is a feature of chronic hepatitis.'),
('f529a00b-2cc1-5aa0-993d-90cb81bcc873'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of epithelium lines the gallbladder?', '["Simple cuboidal","Pseudostratified columnar","Simple columnar","Transitional","Stratified squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar', 'The gallbladder is lined by a tall simple columnar epithelium specialized for concentrating bile by water absorption. It does not bear a stratified, cuboidal, or transitional lining.'),
('8ac628ab-54f7-5e3f-bbca-5cdc987d7c94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the main function of the gallbladder?', '["Absorb nutrients","Secrete digestive enzymes","Concentrate and store bile","Filter blood","Produce bile"]'::jsonb, 'Concentrate and store bile', 'The gallbladder stores bile between meals and absorbs water and electrolytes to concentrate it. Bile synthesis is the liver''s job, and the gallbladder neither digests nutrients nor filters blood.'),
('2ede3ff4-a34d-554e-9d78-559ded66213d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What hormone causes gallbladder contraction?', '["Motilin","Cholecystokinin (CCK)","Secretin","Gastrin","GIP"]'::jsonb, 'Cholecystokinin (CCK)', 'Cholecystokinin, released by duodenal I cells in response to dietary fat, contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi. Secretin and motilin act on the pancreas and GI motility, respectively, not on gallbladder emptying.'),
('7ec9d2d8-5480-5b48-9c5d-156a77531d85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What special feature does the gallbladder mucosa have?', '["Peyer''s patches","Plicae circulares","Villi","Crypts of Lieberkühn","Rugae-like folds"]'::jsonb, 'Rugae-like folds', 'The gallbladder mucosa is thrown into branching rugae-like folds that flatten as the organ distends. Villi, plicae circulares, crypts of Lieberkühn, and Peyer''s patches belong to the small and large intestine.'),
('0bc94b8b-9e24-5bd1-84e8-143c70c4eb51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Does the gallbladder have a muscularis mucosae?', '["Yes","Only in the neck","Only in the fundus","No","Only in the body"]'::jsonb, 'No', 'The gallbladder wall consists of mucosa, a thin lamina propria, and a muscular coat, but it has no muscularis mucosae or submucosa. The lamina propria therefore lies directly on the muscular layer throughout the organ.'),
('0a8cb2e3-ef71-5d13-ad07-324a17da0494'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the composition of bile?', '["bile salts","Water","cholesterol","phospholipids","bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Water', 'Bile is roughly 97% water by volume; bile salts, bilirubin, cholesterol, and phospholipids are the principal solutes dissolved in that water. Therefore water is the most abundant constituent.'),
('91741c88-e5a0-589d-91a6-a3ef7fe22dd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of bile salts?', '["Kill bacteria","Digest proteins","Digest carbohydrates","Absorb vitamins","Emulsify fats"]'::jsonb, 'Emulsify fats', 'Bile salts are amphipathic detergents that emulsify dietary fats into small droplets, increasing the surface area available to pancreatic lipase. Protein and carbohydrate digestion is carried out by enzymes, not bile salts.'),
('dedc43c5-54af-523b-9d17-dfcd6558b843'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Where is the gallbladder located anatomically?', '["In a fossa on the visceral surface of the liver","In the pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","In the falciform ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Behind the stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In a fossa on the visceral surface of the liver', 'The gallbladder lies in a shallow fossa on the visceral (inferior) surface of the liver near the porta hepatis. This position allows bile to reach it via the hepatic and cystic ducts.'),
('1562f91e-3099-5b1c-808a-0e2f7cc27fd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the spiral valve of the cystic duct?', '["A sphincter muscle","A spiral fold that keeps the duct open","An epithelial thickening","A true valve preventing backflow","A lymphoid aggregate"]'::jsonb, 'A spiral fold that keeps the duct open', 'The spiral valves of Heister are mucosal folds in the cystic duct that help keep the lumen patent rather than acting as true valves. They do not prevent backflow or function as a sphincter.'),
('f29458ae-9ddc-54dd-bce6-69f4cb750220'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the sphincter of Oddi?', '["Sphincter in the esophagus","Valve between gallbladder and cystic duct","Sphincter in the anal canal","Sphincter at the pylorus","Smooth muscle surrounding the ampulla of Vater"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle surrounding the ampulla of Vater', 'The sphincter of Oddi is smooth muscle encircling the hepatopancreatic ampulla (ampulla of Vater), regulating the release of bile and pancreatic juice into the duodenum. It differs from the pyloric and esophageal sphincters.'),
('111cdc19-c73e-594c-81cf-2f9d694357b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the exocrine function of the pancreas?', '["Secrete bicarbonate","Secrete somatostatin","Secrete insulin","Secrete digestive enzymes","Secrete glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete digestive enzymes', 'Pancreatic acinar cells carry out the exocrine function by secreting digestive enzymes such as amylase, lipase, and proteases. Insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin are endocrine products of the islets of Langerhans.'),
('dc34f657-56c0-5de8-81c6-4eab1842254b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the endocrine function of the pancreas?', '["Secrete hormones from islets of Langerhans","Store nutrients accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filter blood mediated by specific receptor activation","Secrete bile without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secrete mucus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete hormones from islets of Langerhans', 'The islets of Langerhans perform the endocrine role of the pancreas by releasing insulin, glucagon, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide into the bloodstream. Enzyme secretion belongs to the exocrine acinar component.'),
('0418422e-8118-54fb-8dce-954053b5080c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What cells secrete insulin?', '["Alpha cells","PP cells","Epsilon cells","Delta cells","Beta cells"]'::jsonb, 'Beta cells', 'Insulin is produced by the beta cells, which occupy the central portion of the islet of Langerhans. Alpha cells secrete glucagon, delta cells secrete somatostatin, and PP cells secrete pancreatic polypeptide.'),
('b9c8ca77-2786-5485-8da4-5ed094972a19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What cells secrete glucagon?', '["Alpha cells","PP cells","Delta cells","Beta cells","Epsilon cells"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha cells', 'Glucagon is secreted by the alpha cells found at the periphery of the pancreatic islet. Beta cells (central) release insulin, delta cells release somatostatin, and PP cells release pancreatic polypeptide.'),
('0d18e92b-57f4-5118-a467-7b7c93d44437'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the histological arrangement of cells in a pancreatic islet?', '["Random arrangement","Alpha cells central","alpha and delta cells peripheral","beta peripheral","Beta cells central"]'::jsonb, 'Beta cells central', 'In the islet of Langerhans, the beta (insulin-producing) cells are situated centrally while alpha and delta cells lie at the periphery. This orderly core-to-rim pattern is characteristic of the pancreatic islet.'),
('fa828bda-e8fe-5d81-bebe-0369edf60105'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are acinar cells of the pancreas?', '["Endocrine cells that secrete insulin","Ductal cells that secrete bicarbonate","Connective tissue cells","Exocrine cells that secrete digestive enzymes","Immune cells"]'::jsonb, 'Exocrine cells that secrete digestive enzymes', 'Acinar cells are pyramid-shaped, zymogen-rich exocrine cells whose enzyme products empty into the pancreatic duct system. Insulin secretion is an islet function and bicarbonate secretion is a ductal function.'),
('41e935b7-cee2-5e2e-9f08-11fc4dd00c9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What do centroacinar cells form?', '["The beginning of the intercalated duct","The nerve endings","The acinar basement membrane","The blood vessels","The islet capsule"]'::jsonb, 'The beginning of the intercalated duct', 'Centroacinar cells are small pale cells inside the lumen of pancreatic acini that mark the start of the intercalated duct system. They are a distinctive feature of the pancreas and continue into the branching duct network.'),
('b69f77c5-adcd-56e3-80f4-5ef0d350ae85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the pancreatic duct of Wirsung?', '["The bile duct","An accessory pancreatic duct","The main pancreatic duct","The hepatic duct","The cystic duct"]'::jsonb, 'The main pancreatic duct', 'The duct of Wirsung is the main pancreatic duct that runs the length of the pancreas and drains into the ampulla of Vater. The accessory duct is the duct of Santorini.'),
('56c5d9c4-ddcf-5aac-9218-61448fefa39c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the duct of Santorini?', '["The accessory pancreatic duct","The common bile duct","The cystic duct","The main pancreatic duct","The hepatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'The accessory pancreatic duct', 'The duct of Santorini is the accessory pancreatic duct that drains the upper part of the pancreatic head and usually opens separately into the duodenum. The main pancreatic duct is the duct of Wirsung.'),
('9710dd8c-f24b-5c65-a11d-96b6a7565343'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of pancreatic bicarbonate secretion?', '["Neutralize gastric acid in the duodenum","Emulsify fats accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Digest proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Absorb vitamins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Digest fats leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neutralize gastric acid in the duodenum', 'Bicarbonate secreted by pancreatic ductal cells neutralizes acidic chyme entering the duodenum, creating the near-neutral pH needed for intestinal enzyme activity. Emulsification and fat digestion are handled by bile salts and lipase, respectively.'),
('4f144112-3099-55c9-aacf-bebb96304b11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which cells secrete pancreatic polypeptide?', '["Alpha cells","Beta cells","PP cells (gamma cells)","Delta cells","Epsilon cells"]'::jsonb, 'PP cells (gamma cells)', 'Pancreatic polypeptide is secreted by PP (gamma) cells, located mainly at the periphery of islets, especially in the head of the pancreas. Alpha, beta, and delta cells release glucagon, insulin, and somatostatin, respectively.'),
('8e6a5f28-c01e-511b-a160-ad7e99d6d6c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the serosa of the gut?', '["Visceral peritoneum covering the external surface","The epithelial lining","The connective tissue layer","The mucosal layer","The outermost layer of smooth muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral peritoneum covering the external surface', 'The serosa is the visceral peritoneum, a mesothelial layer with a thin connective tissue base, covering the external surface of intraperitoneal gut segments. Retroperitoneal organs instead carry only an adventitia.'),
('4a39a2ac-9d21-58ca-8a99-438671ece65d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the adventitia of the gut?', '["The outer muscle layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The submucosal layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Connective tissue that anchors retroperitoneal organs","The epithelial lining without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mucosal layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connective tissue that anchors retroperitoneal organs', 'The adventitia is a layer of connective tissue that binds retroperitoneal organs such as parts of the duodenum and colon to the posterior body wall. Organs covered by visceral peritoneum have a serosa instead.'),
('7bb26b1c-3d78-531e-b7c1-e0c106dadd6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which part of the GI tract has skeletal muscle in its external layer?', '["Small intestine","Stomach","Colon","Upper esophagus","Appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Upper esophagus', 'The upper third of the esophagus has skeletal muscle in its muscularis externa for the voluntary phase of swallowing; this is gradually replaced by smooth muscle caudally. The other listed organs have smooth muscle throughout their external coat.'),
('0f127d20-7b00-5933-95cc-861e16d0da57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of the gastro-esophageal junction?', '["Allow passage of food only","Absorb nutrients","Secrete enzymes","Prevent reflux of gastric contents","Store food"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent reflux of gastric contents', 'The lower esophageal sphincter at the gastro-esophageal junction prevents reflux of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. When this barrier fails, gastric acid injures the esophageal mucosa and causes heartburn.'),
('465ec7b6-9e71-50a0-84b2-902637945a71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What forms the gastro-esophageal junction histologically?', '["Columnar to squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","change accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Squamous to cuboidal mediated by specific receptor activation","Abrupt transition from stratified squamous to simple columnar epithelium","Gradual transition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abrupt transition from stratified squamous to simple columnar epithelium', 'At the gastro-esophageal junction the lining changes abruptly from the stratified squamous epithelium of the esophagus to the simple columnar epithelium of the stomach. This sharply defined transition is the histological hallmark of the junction.'),
('0c9a2d10-98d9-54d3-80e5-991ca5177db0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the Z-line?', '["The ileocecal valve resulting in decreased functional activity","The gastro-esophageal junction seen endoscopically","The ampulla of Vater mediated by specific receptor activation","The pylorus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The dentate line leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gastro-esophageal junction seen endoscopically', 'The Z-line is the endoscopic appearance of the gastro-esophageal junction, where pale squamous esophageal mucosa meets pink columnar gastric mucosa in an irregular zigzag line. It does not correspond to the pylorus, ileocecal valve, or dentate line.'),
('44545e09-90ff-5d91-9f90-6d4f55df02c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the ileocecal valve?', '["A fold in the cecum resulting in decreased functional activity","A valve in the ileum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A muscle in the colon mediated by specific receptor activation","A sphincter between ileum and cecum preventing backflow","A lymphoid aggregate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A sphincter between ileum and cecum preventing backflow', 'The ileocecal valve is formed by mucosal folds reinforced by circular muscle at the junction of ileum and cecum, limiting reflux of colonic contents into the ileum. It behaves as a sphincter rather than a mass of lymphoid tissue.'),
('eeb7af4c-3106-5922-a39c-7c7fc009bd86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the vermiform appendix''s histological hallmark?', '["Brunner''s glands resulting in decreased functional activity","Peyer''s patches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abundant lymphoid follicles in the mucosa and submucosa","Teniae coli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Villi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abundant lymphoid follicles in the mucosa and submucosa', 'The appendix is characterized by abundant lymphoid follicles in its mucosa and submucosa, making it a major site of gut-associated lymphoid tissue. Brunner''s glands are duodenal, Peyer''s patches are ileal, and teniae coli are colonic.'),
('2ff1004d-5b3f-5fdc-8b53-620790fad5da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT)?', '["Digestion resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motility mediated by specific receptor activation","Immune surveillance and defense","Absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immune surveillance and defense', 'GALT, including Peyer''s patches and the lymphoid tissue of the appendix, provides immune surveillance and defense by sampling luminal antigens and mounting immune responses. Digestion, secretion, motility, and absorption are carried out by other elements of the gut wall.'),
('01b98e18-30f9-5b04-a218-9f93cd476651'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the origin of the epithelium of the GI tract?', '["Endoderm","Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Mesenchyme","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Endoderm', 'The epithelium and glands of the GI tract are derived from the embryonic endoderm. The connective tissue and muscle layers are provided by splanchnic mesoderm.'),
('0d8dd7a6-cdf5-5299-aa56-1fa74cdf7bb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the origin of the connective tissue and muscle of the GI tract?', '["Neural crest","Ectoderm","Surface ectoderm","Splanchnic mesoderm","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Splanchnic mesoderm', 'The connective tissue and muscular layers of the gut wall develop from the splanchnic (visceral) mesoderm. Both germ layers therefore contribute to the gut, with endoderm forming the lining epithelium.'),
('7cc23a3c-a2d7-5385-9a70-d4d8f3b2e8f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What does the foregut give rise to?', '["duodenum (proximal to bile duct)","liver","stomach","Esophagus","pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus', 'The foregut gives rise to the esophagus, stomach, proximal duodenum (down to the entry of the bile duct), liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. The distal duodenum and below are derived from the midgut.'),
('60ee6c4c-9acb-5580-be28-7e5b479ac5fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What does the midgut give rise to?', '["cecum","appendix","Distal duodenum","jejunum","ileum"]'::jsonb, 'Distal duodenum', 'The midgut forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The proximal duodenum belongs to the foregut, ending at the bile duct opening.'),
('009b8376-b53f-59a3-acdf-7deee0b0ce13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What does the hindgut give rise to?', '["Distal transverse colon","upper anal canal","descending colon","sigmoid colon","rectum"]'::jsonb, 'Distal transverse colon', 'The hindgut gives rise to the distal third of the transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, rectum, and upper anal canal. The midgut supplies the intestine up to approximately the middle of the transverse colon.'),
('4f266faa-02bd-50c7-a21a-743cf22174aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the vitelline duct (omphaloenteric duct)?', '["Connection between foregut and heart","Connection between hindgut and allantois","The pancreatic bud","Connection between midgut and yolk sac","The bile duct primordium"]'::jsonb, 'Connection between midgut and yolk sac', 'The vitelline (omphaloenteric) duct is the embryonic connection between the midgut and the yolk sac. Its persistence leads to a Meckel diverticulum, whereas the allantois connects to the hindgut.'),
('91be7f84-a478-52c9-ad03-625dc1ebce56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is a Meckel''s diverticulum?', '["A pancreatic cyst","Persistence of the vitelline duct","A gastric pouch","An esophageal abnormality","A colonic diverticulum"]'::jsonb, 'Persistence of the vitelline duct', 'A Meckel diverticulum results from incomplete obliteration of the vitelline duct, leaving an outpouching of the distal ileum. It is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract and may contain ectopic gastric or pancreatic tissue.'),
('a4e58a6b-279a-5edb-8f8d-4a86a99d1b6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the rule of 2 for Meckel''s diverticulum?', '["2 cm long","2 feet from ileocecal valve","2 cm from cecum","2% population","2 inches long"]'::jsonb, '2 inches long', 'The classic rule states the diverticulum is about 2 inches (approximately 5 cm) long, lies roughly 2 feet from the ileocecal valve, occurs in about 2% of people, is twice as common in males, and may hold two ectopic tissue types. Its length of 2 inches is one component of this rule.'),
('52acdcd3-f3a0-539b-a462-e436903d2c3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the process of rotation of the midgut?', '["90 degree rotation","180 degree clockwise rotation","360 degree rotation","rotation","270 degree counterclockwise rotation"]'::jsonb, '270 degree counterclockwise rotation', 'During development the midgut herniates into the umbilical cord and then rotates 270 degrees counterclockwise as it returns to the abdomen. This rotation positions the cecum in the right lower quadrant.'),
('385bf930-d8f5-56eb-bd3a-b322478925fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is malrotation of the gut?', '["Absent rotation resulting in decreased functional activity","Excessive rotation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reverse rotation mediated by specific receptor activation","Failure of normal midgut rotation leading to abnormal position","Incomplete rotation only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of normal midgut rotation leading to abnormal position', 'Malrotation is failure of the normal midgut rotation, leaving the gut abnormally positioned and predisposing to midgut volvulus and obstruction. It may be complete, partial, or reversed rather than a single isolated pattern.'),
('e6e8d364-0b28-5034-aae7-c2d46b925964'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is esophageal atresia?', '["Double esophagus","Dilation of the esophagus","often with tracheoesophageal fistula","Congenital discontinuity of the esophagus","Narrowing of the esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital discontinuity of the esophagus', 'Esophageal atresia is a congenital malformation in which the esophagus ends in a blind pouch because the foregut fails to canalize completely during development. It commonly accompanies tracheoesophageal fistula, but the discontinuity of the esophageal lumen is the defining feature, unlike the other options which describe different or non-defining findings.'),
('0fd793f3-4946-56d1-9f48-df5539f6c54f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of tracheoesophageal fistula?', '["Pure esophageal atresia resulting in decreased functional activity","H-type fistula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proximal esophageal atresia with distal TEF (type C)","Proximal fistula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Double fistula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal esophageal atresia with distal TEF (type C)', 'Proximal esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula (type C) accounts for roughly 85% of cases and is by far the most frequent pattern. The upper pouch ends blindly while the lower esophageal segment connects to the trachea, so air enters the stomach while swallowing produces choking.'),
('4651259a-2b76-53b7-897d-48dc68080304'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is annular pancreas?', '["Pancreatic cyst","Two pancreatic heads","Absent pancreas","Pancreas in the chest","Pancreatic tissue encircling the duodenum"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic tissue encircling the duodenum', 'Annular pancreas results from abnormal rotation and fusion of the ventral pancreatic bud, leaving a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of the duodenum. This ring can constrict the duodenal lumen and cause obstruction, features not shared by the other choices.'),
('a0742f40-8b37-52fa-a6c8-6e562b93fc45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is duodenal atresia?', '["Congenital obstruction of the duodenum","Duodenal duplication","Narrowing of the pylorus","Duodenal web","Absent duodenum"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital obstruction of the duodenum', 'Duodenal atresia is a congenital failure of the duodenal lumen to recanalize during development, producing a complete obstruction that prevents passage of swallowed amniotic fluid. It classically presents with polyhydramnios and a double-bubble sign, unlike the other entities listed.'),
('d07c22e4-9175-5614-8cd7-0c17a80ae721'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the double-bubble sign?', '["Pneumoperitoneum resulting in decreased functional activity","Two air-fluid levels in duodenal atresia (stomach and proximal duodenum)","Air in the stomach and esophagus mediated by specific receptor activation","Two bubbles in the colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gas in the small bowel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two air-fluid levels in duodenal atresia (stomach and proximal duodenum)', 'In duodenal atresia the dilated stomach and proximal duodenum each fill with air and fluid, producing two air-fluid levels on plain radiographs, with no gas seen beyond the obstruction. It is a radiographic hallmark of duodenal atresia and not seen in the other conditions.'),
('519c6e16-d97e-561e-a375-df8c436c3bd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Hirschsprung''s disease?', '["Rectal prolapse resulting in decreased functional activity","Colonic dilation from obstruction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital absence of parasympathetic ganglion cells in the colon","Colonic atresia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anal stenosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital absence of parasympathetic ganglion cells in the colon', 'Hirschsprung''s disease is defined by congenital absence of parasympathetic (myenteric and submucosal) ganglion cells, usually in the distal colon, so the aganglionic segment cannot relax and causes functional obstruction. Colonic dilation, when present, is a downstream consequence, not the primary defect.'),
('b3e8237e-dae8-526f-a6ad-240b58b98154'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the embryological basis of Hirschsprung''s disease?', '["Failure of endodermal development resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural tube defect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ectodermal abnormality mediated by specific receptor activation","Failure of neural crest cell migration to the hindgut","Mesodermal defect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of neural crest cell migration to the hindgut', 'The enteric neurons are derived from neural crest cells that migrate along the gut; in Hirschsprung''s disease this migration into the hindgut fails, leaving the distal colon aganglionic. No other listed defect produces the characteristic absence of enteric ganglia.'),
('80dc2587-ec3e-5f34-a70d-84edd9b7e50e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is imperforate anus?', '["Absent rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Rectal atresia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Failure of the anal membrane to rupture","Anal stenosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ectopic anus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the anal membrane to rupture', 'Imperforate anus follows failure of the anal membrane to rupture (or incomplete division of the cloaca) during development, so the rectum ends blindly without a normal anal opening. The rectum itself is usually present, which rules out the other options.'),
('fa3d4a6d-aabb-54e6-9b1d-22e84e7edc4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the difference between high and low imperforate anus?', '["Low is stenosis resulting in decreased functional activity","High is complete atresia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High and low refer to the rectum length mediated by specific receptor activation","High: above pelvic floor (supralevator); Low: below pelvic floor (translevator)","difference leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High: above pelvic floor (supralevator); Low: below pelvic floor (translevator)', 'The classification depends on whether the blind rectal pouch lies above (high, supralevator) or below (low, translevator) the pelvic floor and levator ani sling. High lesions are more often associated with fistulas and have a worse prognosis, while low lesions are easier to correct surgically.'),
('f9344462-5519-59da-9c90-06a92c77ca14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the enteric nervous system?', '["The sensory system of the gut","The sympathetic innervation","The central nervous system part for the gut","The intrinsic nervous system of the GI tract","The parasympathetic innervation"]'::jsonb, 'The intrinsic nervous system of the GI tract', 'The enteric nervous system is the intrinsic network of neurons and glial cells embedded in the gut wall (myenteric and submucosal plexuses) that regulates motility, secretion, and blood flow with a large degree of independence from the CNS. It is not simply the sympathetic, parasympathetic, or sensory supply.'),
('e865b1c2-c26b-5397-bf75-bd726d9c6630'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the two main plexuses of the enteric nervous system?', '["Myenteric and serosal","Serosal and mucosal","Myenteric (Auerbach) and submucosal (Meissner)","Submucosal and mucosal","Vascular and neural"]'::jsonb, 'Myenteric (Auerbach) and submucosal (Meissner)', 'The gut wall contains two interconnected neural networks: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the circular and longitudinal muscle coats and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus within the submucosa. Together they coordinate peristalsis and mucosal secretion.'),
('01a0f386-2119-56b5-a312-8c04136ceb03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What does the myenteric plexus primarily control?', '["Gut motility","Immune function","Mucosal secretion","Blood flow","Absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Gut motility', 'The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies between the muscle coats and primarily drives peristalsis and other motility of the gut wall. Secretion, absorption, and immune functions are handled by other plexuses and cells rather than by this plexus.'),
('f5141dd3-171f-5d47-896d-1128c5e0e49a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What does the submucosal plexus primarily control?', '["Motility","and blood flow","absorption","Secretion","Epithelial renewal"]'::jsonb, 'Secretion', 'The submucosal (Meissner) plexus primarily regulates mucosal secretion and local mucosal blood flow. Peristaltic contraction of the muscle coats is governed instead by the myenteric plexus.'),
('9674cfc0-aef0-50b9-aa37-ffc2ed3478b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the blood supply of the foregut?', '["Inferior mesenteric artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Splenic artery","Celiac trunk","Hepatic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac trunk', 'The foregut, from the distal esophagus down to the duodenal papilla, is supplied by branches of the celiac trunk, which serve the stomach, liver, spleen, and proximal duodenum. The superior and inferior mesenteric arteries supply the midgut and hindgut respectively.'),
('90926f20-33f8-52e9-9211-543a009e4a5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the blood supply of the midgut?', '["Inferior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk","Internal iliac artery","Hepatic artery","Superior mesenteric artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery', 'The midgut, extending from the distal duodenum to the proximal transverse colon, is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. The celiac trunk serves the foregut and the inferior mesenteric artery serves the hindgut.'),
('51c9ee1e-8499-504f-81c5-a9a3e4318de8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the blood supply of the hindgut?', '["Celiac trunk","Inferior mesenteric artery","Splenic artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Internal iliac artery"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior mesenteric artery', 'The hindgut, from the distal transverse colon to the upper anal canal, is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery via its left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal branches. The celiac and superior mesenteric arteries serve the more proximal gut.'),
('e938530b-2c16-500a-b575-fcee7b205413'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the marginal artery of Drummond?', '["An anastomotic arcade connecting SMA and IMA along the colon","The main artery of the colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lymphatic vessel mediated by specific receptor activation","A vein of the colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A branch of the celiac trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An anastomotic arcade connecting SMA and IMA along the colon', 'The marginal artery of Drummond is an anastomotic channel formed by the arcade between the terminal branches of the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries along the mesenteric border of the colon. It provides collateral blood flow, for example during ligation of the inferior mesenteric artery in aortic surgery.'),
('d3badd46-7471-58ba-8e36-d00de1f634ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the vein that drains most of the GI tract to the liver?', '["Splenic vein","Portal vein","Hepatic vein","Superior mesenteric vein","Inferior mesenteric vein"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'The portal vein, formed by the splenic and superior mesenteric veins, delivers venous blood from most of the GI tract, spleen, and pancreas to the liver for processing of absorbed nutrients. The hepatic vein, by contrast, drains blood away from the liver into the inferior vena cava.'),
('e935f8af-6816-5900-9d59-ae8ed0f5dc49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is portocaval anastomosis?', '["A lymphatic connection resulting in decreased functional activity","A valve in the portal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A surgical shunt mediated by specific receptor activation","An arterial connection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connections between portal and systemic venous systems at specific sites"]'::jsonb, 'Connections between portal and systemic venous systems at specific sites', 'Portocaval anastomoses are natural connections between the portal and systemic venous systems at four sites: gastroesophageal, paraumbilical, anorectal, and retroperitoneal. In portal hypertension they become dilated and can bleed, producing varices, caput medusae, or hemorrhoids.'),
('0a68b3b2-be6d-5a73-93a3-6c5697c9c744'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are Peyer''s patches?', '["Bone marrow deposits","Aggregated lymphoid nodules in the ileum","Thymic remnants","Splenic tissue","Lymph nodes near the intestine"]'::jsonb, 'Aggregated lymphoid nodules in the ileum', 'Peyer''s patches are aggregates of lymphoid follicles (gut-associated lymphoid tissue) found mainly in the lamina propria and submucosa of the ileum. They sample luminal antigens to initiate mucosal immune responses, unlike lymph nodes, which are encapsulated and lie outside the bowel wall.'),
('0855757d-0be9-5147-8876-0c412b616cc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of lymphocytes predominate in Peyer''s patches?', '["Plasma cells","B lymphocytes","NK cells","T lymphocytes","Macrophages"]'::jsonb, 'B lymphocytes', 'Peyer''s patches are dominated by B lymphocytes, which populate large germinal centers and undergo IgA class switching. T cells are also present but are concentrated in the interfollicular regions, so B cells remain the predominant type.'),
('b9a2e908-2df0-58bc-9e8b-045c748d9dba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the dome area of a Peyer''s patch?', '["The medulla","rich in M cells and dendritic cells","The region overlying the lymphoid follicle","The germinal center","The paracortex"]'::jsonb, 'The region overlying the lymphoid follicle', 'The dome is the zone of a Peyer''s patch lying directly over the lymphoid follicle, populated by M cells that sample luminal antigens, together with dendritic cells and lymphocytes. It is distinct from the germinal center and paracortex deeper within the follicle.'),
('d4998b42-b99f-535d-b3bf-95b0447cd4bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is secretory IgA''s role in the GI tract?', '["Kill viruses directly resulting in decreased functional activity","Neutralize pathogens without causing inflammation","Absorb antigens mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulate inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Digest bacteria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neutralize pathogens without causing inflammation', 'Secretory IgA coats mucosal surfaces and neutralizes pathogens and toxins by binding them, and it blocks antigen uptake, all without triggering an inflammatory response. It therefore protects the gut without causing the tissue damage that complement activation or phagocytosis would.'),
('3adea6e3-c8d2-5bf4-9d9b-ca3a4bb20344'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Where are plasma cells that produce IgA located?', '["In the lamina propria","In the submucosa","In the epithelium","In Peyer''s patches","In the muscularis mucosae"]'::jsonb, 'In the lamina propria', 'IgA-producing plasma cells reside in the lamina propria of the intestinal mucosa, just beneath the epithelium. The dimeric IgA they release binds the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor on enterocytes and is transported across the epithelium as secretory IgA.'),
('8817919a-86d7-52d0-b31d-5c19292f364b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the role of eosinophils in the GI tract?', '["Secretion of mucus resulting in decreased functional activity","Nutrient absorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Defense against parasites and modulation of inflammation","Viral elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial digestion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Defense against parasites and modulation of inflammation', 'Eosinophils defend the gut against helminthic parasites by releasing cytotoxic granule proteins and also help regulate local inflammatory responses. Mucus secretion, nutrient absorption, and viral elimination are carried out by other cell types.'),
('e2c5d43f-8826-5364-82a6-d67d7d4021e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are intraepithelial lymphocytes?', '["T cells located between intestinal epithelial cells","NK cells","B cells in the epithelium","Macrophages in the epithelium","Dendritic cells in the epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'T cells located between intestinal epithelial cells', 'Intraepithelial lymphocytes are T cells that lie between the enterocytes of the intestinal epithelium, where they survey the luminal surface and respond to microbial or dietary antigens. They are not B cells, macrophages, or NK cells.'),
('94e886fe-8597-5538-95c1-f6b782df8de9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of mast cells in the GI tract?', '["Antibody production resulting in decreased functional activity","Phagocytosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antigen presentation mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucus secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Allergic responses and regulation of secretion and motility"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic responses and regulation of secretion and motility', 'Mast cells release histamine and other mediators that drive allergic and hypersensitivity reactions and modulate intestinal secretion and motility. They do not produce antibodies, present antigen, or phagocytose pathogens.'),
('dbcf2b99-d490-5257-8ba7-5847c046711e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the lamina propria of the GI tract?', '["The muscle layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The outermost layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The epithelial lining mediated by specific receptor activation","The glandular layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loose connective tissue supporting the epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue supporting the epithelium', 'The lamina propria is the layer of loose connective tissue that supports the intestinal epithelium and houses blood vessels, lymphatics, and immune cells such as plasma cells and lymphocytes. It lies beneath the epithelium and above the muscularis mucosae.'),
('38a461c4-647a-5ffe-8f6b-c12b18733493'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of the sub-mucosa?', '["Contains blood vessels","and glands","Absorption of nutrients","Movement of mucosa","nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Contains blood vessels', 'The submucosa is a connective tissue layer that carries the blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves supplying the mucosa and, in some regions, contains glands (e.g., Brunner''s glands). Nutrient absorption occurs at the epithelial surface, and mucosal movement is the work of the muscularis mucosae.'),
('9f45ede5-d1b8-5a32-a492-84acd65575b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the classic hepatic lobule?', '["A triangular structure centered on the portal triad","A hexagonal structure centered on the central vein","A circular unit","A square functional unit","An octagonal structure"]'::jsonb, 'A hexagonal structure centered on the central vein', 'The classic hepatic lobule is a hexagonal or polygonal unit with a central (terminal hepatic) vein at its center and portal triads at its corners. Blood flows from the portal triads toward the central vein, and this arrangement underlies centrilobular vulnerability to ischemia and toxins.'),
('34dcb525-4149-5f43-98a4-f4c1dc4753e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the portal lobule?', '["A triangular area centered on a portal triad","The functional unit of bile secretion","The hexagonal lobule","The acinus","The vascular unit"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular area centered on a portal triad', 'The portal lobule is a triangular unit centered on a portal triad, with its angles drawn to the nearest central veins, and it emphasizes bile drainage toward the bile duct. It differs from the classic lobule (centered on a central vein) and from the acinus (based on blood flow).'),
('8d78d0da-aa77-5b7c-988c-0b3db48b72c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the liver acinus?', '["The portal lobule resulting in decreased functional activity","A diamond-shaped functional unit based on blood flow from portal triad to central vein","The bile duct unit mediated by specific receptor activation","The sinusoidal unit without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hexagonal lobule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A diamond-shaped functional unit based on blood flow from portal triad to central vein', 'The hepatic acinus is a diamond-shaped unit defined by blood flow from the portal triad toward adjacent central veins and is divided into zones 1, 2, and 3. It is the most useful model for explaining oxygen and nutrient gradients and patterns of injury.'),
('53b0a054-f177-589e-b7ac-dfb184ca46eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is zone 1 of the liver acinus?', '["The central vein area","The midzone","The portal triad","Periportal zone (best oxygenated)","Perivenular zone (least oxygenated)"]'::jsonb, 'Periportal zone (best oxygenated)', 'Zone 1 of the acinus surrounds the portal triad and receives the most oxygen-rich blood, so it is the best-oxygenated zone and is relatively protected from ischemia but prone to injury from some toxins. Zone 3 near the central vein is least oxygenated and most vulnerable to ischemic and drug-induced necrosis.'),
('d2f3c695-804c-5a13-b873-43da053e2137'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of capillary are hepatic sinusoids?', '["Lymphatic capillaries resulting in decreased functional activity","Continuous capillaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arterioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Sinusoids with continuous basement membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fenestrated capillaries with discontinuous basement membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Fenestrated capillaries with discontinuous basement membrane', 'Hepatic sinusoids are fenestrated capillaries with a discontinuous basement membrane, allowing plasma macromolecules to move freely into the space of Disse to reach hepatocytes. Continuous capillaries, by contrast, have an intact basement membrane and restrict such exchange.'),
('ea13def5-2882-57c7-bbdd-fd3999731ee6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of the endothelial fenestrations in sinusoids?', '["Absorb nutrients resulting in decreased functional activity","Produce hormones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Allow passage of macromolecules between blood and hepatocytes","Secrete bile without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Filter blood cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Allow passage of macromolecules between blood and hepatocytes', 'The fenestrations (pores) in sinusoidal endothelium allow large plasma components such as lipoproteins and albumin to pass from the blood into the space of Disse, where hepatocytes can take them up. Blood cells remain in the sinusoidal lumen because they cannot fit through the pores.'),
('880496f6-9cda-57e7-a999-d8fc974a2b19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What cells line the biliary canaliculi?', '["Endothelial cells","Hepatocyte membranes","Ito cells","Cholangiocytes","Kupffer cells"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocyte membranes', 'Bile canaliculi are narrow intercellular spaces formed between adjacent hepatocytes, so their walls are composed of hepatocyte membranes (canalicular surface with microvilli). The other listed cells do not form the canalicular lining.'),
('a70faaa5-6643-54fc-88eb-89da61849d8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are cholangiocytes?', '["Epithelial cells lining bile ducts","Hepatocyte precursors","Ito cells","Sinusoidal endothelial cells","Kupffer cells"]'::jsonb, 'Epithelial cells lining bile ducts', 'Cholangiocytes are the epithelial cells lining the intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts, and they modify bile by secreting water and bicarbonate in response to secretin. They are distinct from hepatocytes, Ito cells (stellate), Kupffer cells (macrophages), and sinusoidal endothelium.'),
('4ec3f619-16cb-5328-955f-04adcde56866'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of the gallbladder''s absorption of water?', '["Concentrate bile 5-10 fold","Produce bile","Secrete hormones","Dilute bile","Store vitamins"]'::jsonb, 'Concentrate bile 5-10 fold', 'By actively absorbing water and electrolytes, the gallbladder concentrates hepatic bile five- to tenfold, making it a more effective digestive agent. The gallbladder does not produce bile; hepatocytes produce it, and vitamin storage is not a gallbladder function.'),
('773b83d6-67a8-5b47-b9cf-63fbc36e889a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are zymogen granules?', '["Granules in beta cells containing insulin resulting in decreased functional activity","Lysosomes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secretory granules in pancreatic acinar cells containing proenzymes","Lipid droplets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Melanin granules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secretory granules in pancreatic acinar cells containing proenzymes', 'Zymogen granules are membrane-bound secretory granules in pancreatic acinar cells that store inactive proenzymes (zymogens) such as trypsinogen and chymotrypsinogen. The inactive form protects the gland from autodigestion until the enzymes are released and activated in the duodenum.'),
('a6b308b1-487f-51e5-926b-fc2966701315'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the composition of pancreatic juice?', '["Water and bile salts","Enzymes only","Water","and digestive enzymes","bicarbonate"]'::jsonb, 'Water', 'Pancreatic juice is largely water that acts as the solvent for the two functional components: bicarbonate, secreted by duct cells to neutralize gastric acid, and digestive enzymes, released from acinar cells. Bile salts come from the liver and gallbladder, not the pancreas.'),
('17fe29cb-ecf5-5a32-a0ef-4e009e356d3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What activates trypsinogen in the duodenum?', '["Pancreatic amylase resulting in decreased functional activity","Enterokinase (enteropeptidase) from duodenal epithelial cells","Secretin mediated by specific receptor activation","Hydrochloric acid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bile salts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enterokinase (enteropeptidase) from duodenal epithelial cells', 'Enterokinase (enteropeptidase) on the brush border of duodenal epithelial cells cleaves trypsinogen into active trypsin, which then activates the other pancreatic zymogens. Because this happens only in the duodenum, premature activation within the pancreas is avoided.'),
('3295b1b8-dca6-5d2c-ae8f-ff812f0b030e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What prevents premature activation of pancreatic enzymes in the pancreas?', '["The capsule resulting in decreased functional activity","Trypsin inhibitor secreted by acinar cells","Low pH mediated by specific receptor activation","High oxygen tension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bile flow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trypsin inhibitor secreted by acinar cells', 'Acinar cells secrete a trypsin inhibitor that neutralizes any trypsin accidentally activated inside the gland, protecting the pancreas from autodigestion. This works together with synthesis of enzymes as inactive zymogens to keep the gland safe.'),
('19f0989a-2cc2-5675-a84a-fc5850626900'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the intercalated ducts of the pancreas?', '["Intralobular ducts","The main pancreatic duct","lined by cuboidal cells","The bile duct","Small ducts continuous with acini"]'::jsonb, 'Small ducts continuous with acini', 'Intercalated ducts are the smallest intralobular pancreatic ducts, directly continuous with the acini and lined by low cuboidal cells. Their cells help add water and bicarbonate to the pancreatic secretion.'),
('1c26fb01-2630-5f75-b8ce-0937cfe316b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are intralobular ducts?', '["Bile ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","The main duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ducts within the pancreatic lobule that collect from intercalated ducts","Ducts between lobules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The accessory duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ducts within the pancreatic lobule that collect from intercalated ducts', 'Intralobular ducts lie inside the pancreatic lobule, receive secretion from the intercalated ducts, and drain into the larger interlobular ducts. Their epithelium is cuboidal to low columnar.'),
('95ac1ea9-0995-58b9-8572-04bbfb9f5595'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are interlobar ducts?', '["The bile duct resulting in decreased functional activity","The accessory duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The smallest ducts in the pancreas mediated by specific receptor activation","Large ducts between lobes that drain into the main pancreatic duct","Ducts within lobules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large ducts between lobes that drain into the main pancreatic duct', 'Interlobar (interlobular) ducts run in the connective tissue septa between the lobes of the pancreas and are lined by columnar epithelium, some with goblet cells. They collect secretion from the intralobular ducts and empty into the main pancreatic duct.'),
('7f9cb329-d94c-5a3a-b3f9-5bb29f4d880c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the main pancreatic duct (duct of Wirsung)?', '["The hepatic duct resulting in decreased functional activity","The cystic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bile duct mediated by specific receptor activation","The principal drainage duct that empties into the ampulla of Vater","The accessory duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The principal drainage duct that empties into the ampulla of Vater', 'The duct of Wirsung is the principal drainage duct of the pancreas, running from the tail to the head, where it joins the common bile duct at the ampulla of Vater and opens at the major duodenal papilla. The hepatic and cystic ducts are part of the biliary tree.'),
('dd2d7d45-0d06-52c3-bd49-79722f539080'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What percentage of the pancreas is exocrine?', '["About 50%","About 20%","About 80%","About 98%","About 2%"]'::jsonb, 'About 98%', 'Roughly 98% of the pancreatic mass is exocrine tissue (acinar cells and ducts) devoted to producing digestive juice, while only about 2% is endocrine (the islets of Langerhans).'),
('fabe6cff-dd77-5921-a164-58205c29ce9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the capsule of the pancreas?', '["The acinar basement membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","The adventitia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The serosa mediated by specific receptor activation","A thin layer of connective tissue covering the gland","The islet capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin layer of connective tissue covering the gland', 'The pancreas is covered by a thin capsule of connective tissue from which septa penetrate the gland to carry vessels, nerves, and ducts. It is neither the acinar basement membrane nor a serous coat, since the pancreas is largely retroperitoneal.'),
('5e0404cc-4b65-5f20-a3c4-cfaf61ebdce0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the three regions of the stomach?', '["Cardia","body","fundus/body","Fundus","pylorus"]'::jsonb, 'Cardia', 'The stomach is conventionally divided into cardiac, fundic/body, and pyloric regions based on their glandular epithelium. The cardia is the narrow mucous-gland zone adjacent to the gastroesophageal junction, one of the three classic regions that must be distinguished from the other listed parts.'),
('bd9b7dab-40d6-5883-b026-7624d3750145'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of glands are found in the gastric cardia?', '["Pyloric glands","Brunner''s glands","Esophageal glands","Cardiac glands (mucous-secreting)","Oxyntic glands"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac glands (mucous-secreting)', 'The cardia contains cardiac glands, simple tubular mucous-secreting glands that lubricate the region near the gastroesophageal junction. This contrasts with the acid-secreting oxyntic glands of the body and fundus and the gastrin-producing pyloric glands of the antrum.'),
('356be8c1-9a18-5439-9ffe-2bece72df151'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of glands are found in the gastric fundus/body?', '["Oxyntic (fundic) glands","Mixed glands","Brunner''s glands","Cardiac glands","Pyloric glands"]'::jsonb, 'Oxyntic (fundic) glands', 'Oxyntic (fundic) glands occupy the body and fundus of the stomach and contain the parietal and chief cells that produce acid and pepsinogen. Cardiac and pyloric glands are confined to the cardia and pylorus respectively, while Brunner''s glands belong to the duodenum.'),
('2706a8c7-0684-5b67-b714-8460c4c43f6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What cell types are found in oxyntic glands?', '["stem cells","enteroendocrine cells","Mucous neck cells","chief cells","parietal cells"]'::jsonb, 'Mucous neck cells', 'Oxyntic (fundic) glands harbor five resident cell types, and mucous neck cells are among them, secreting a thin soluble mucus that coats the glandular epithelium. Parietal cells make HCl and intrinsic factor, chief cells release pepsinogen, enteroendocrine cells secrete hormones such as gastrin, and stem cells replenish the lining.'),
('1ba8bfe8-6c27-57a1-8060-e546ff9576bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of mucous neck cells?', '["Secrete alkaline mucus","Secrete pepsinogen","Secrete gastrin","Secrete acidic mucus","Produce HCl"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete alkaline mucus', 'Mucous neck cells discharge a thin, alkaline mucus that helps lubricate and protect the gastric glands and mucosa. Pepsinogen comes from chief cells, gastrin from G cells, and HCl from parietal cells, while gastric mucus is never acidic.'),
('df332614-b0c0-5acb-b79b-63f410672468'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What stimulates parietal cell acid secretion?', '["Somatostatin only","histamine","Secretin and CCK","and acetylcholine","Gastrin"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrin', 'Gastrin released by antral G cells is a potent direct stimulus of parietal-cell HCl production, on top of histamine (ECL cells) and acetylcholine (vagus). Somatostatin is inhibitory, and Secretin is not a major direct stimulant of acid secretion.'),
('5ec6dce9-4259-51fc-b776-c42f9e54d97a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What inhibits gastric acid secretion?', '["Histamine","Acetylcholine","CCK","Somatostatin","Gastrin"]'::jsonb, 'Somatostatin', 'Somatostatin from antral D cells inhibits both gastrin release and parietal-cell acid production, acting as the main braking signal. Histamine, acetylcholine, and gastrin are all potent stimulants of acid secretion.'),
('2aa3497c-781a-5795-8fce-1c2e790f787e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are ECL cells?', '["Cells that secrete somatostatin","Cells that secrete pepsinogen","Cells that secrete intrinsic factor","Cells that secrete gastrin","Enterochromaffin-like cells that secrete histamine"]'::jsonb, 'Enterochromaffin-like cells that secrete histamine', 'ECL (enterochromaffin-like) cells lie close to parietal cells and release histamine, which activates H2 receptors to drive acid secretion and is triggered by gastrin. They do not produce somatostatin, pepsinogen, intrinsic factor, or gastrin.'),
('a83e3d16-9342-5efd-bc69-202af91b2e4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What type of glands are found in the pylorus?', '["Pyloric glands (mucous-secreting with G cells)","Oxyntic glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Brunner''s glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fundic glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pyloric glands (mucous-secreting with G cells)', 'The pyloric region contains pyloric (mucous) glands that open into deep foveolae and house G cells producing gastrin. Oxyntic/fundic glands belong to the body, cardiac glands to the cardia, and Brunner''s glands to the duodenum.'),
('97da0eb2-e4b0-555b-95df-07a13d8d0510'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the pH of gastric acid in the stomach?', '["8.0-9.0","1.5-3.5","6.0-7.0","7.0-8.0","4.0-5.0"]'::jsonb, '1.5-3.5', 'Parietal-cell HCl brings resting gastric luminal pH down to roughly 1.5-3.5, which activates pepsin and kills ingested organisms. The higher pH ranges reflect saliva, intestinal content, or bile rather than gastric acid.'),
('1c15a20e-d9f0-593b-b603-8c27aaf2d22c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the difference between the duodenum and ileum histologically?', '["Duodenum: Brunner''s glands; Jejunum: tall plicae; Ileum: Peyer''s patches","jejunum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duodenum: Peyer''s patches all over mediated by specific receptor activation","and ileum histologically? without affecting the primary tissue pathway","difference leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum: Brunner''s glands; Jejunum: tall plicae; Ileum: Peyer''s patches', 'The key distinguishing features are Brunner''s glands in the submucosa of the duodenum, tall plicae circulares with long villi in the jejunum, and abundant Peyer''s patches in the ileum. The other choices are word fragments that do not answer the comparison.'),
('8294fc3c-1cad-5d21-8df3-83c6e29c8e33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the duodenal Brunner''s gland secretion?', '["Hormones resulting in decreased functional activity","Acidic mucus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Digestive enzymes mediated by specific receptor activation","Bile without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alkaline mucus rich in bicarbonate"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline mucus rich in bicarbonate', 'Brunner''s glands of the duodenal submucosa pour out an alkaline, bicarbonate-rich mucus that neutralizes acidic chyme and shields the mucosa. They do not release hormones or digestive enzymes, and bile enters separately through the bile duct.'),
('54b7b6bd-6e3f-5148-a7a9-4799b3d3c88a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the jejunal hallmark histologically?', '["prominent plicae circulares and long villi","Short villi accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tall mediated by specific receptor activation","Brunner''s glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peyer''s patches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'prominent plicae circulares and long villi', 'The jejunum is distinguished histologically by prominent plicae circulares topped by long, tall villi, giving a feathery luminal contour. Short villi and Peyer''s patches characterize the ileum, and Brunner''s glands are limited to the duodenum.'),
('7f8a22c7-e59d-5201-940a-458f34be4e9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the ileal hallmark histologically?', '["Teniae coli","Paneth cells only","Brunner''s glands","Abundant Peyer''s patches","Tallest villi"]'::jsonb, 'Abundant Peyer''s patches', 'The ileum is characterized by abundant Peyer''s patches, discrete aggregates of lymphoid tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa. Teniae coli are colonic, Brunner''s glands are duodenal, and villi are short rather than tallest in the ileum.'),
('b08218d1-a302-53a4-9e63-0cbc41f298c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the brush border enzymes?', '["Only peptidases","alkaline phosphatase","peptidases","Disaccharidases","enterokinase"]'::jsonb, 'Disaccharidases', 'Brush border enzymes anchored on the microvillar membrane include disaccharidases such as lactase, sucrase, and maltase that finish carbohydrate digestion. Peptidases, enterokinase, and alkaline phosphatase are also membrane-bound, but disaccharidases are the defining terminal sugar-digesting enzymes.'),
('8f2cfcab-83fe-57a0-91f6-62f14a057285'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of alkaline phosphatase in the gut?', '["Dephosphorylation of nutrients and detoxification of bacterial LPS","Digestion of starches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Digestion of nucleic acids mediated by specific receptor activation","Digestion of proteins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Digestion of fats leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dephosphorylation of nutrients and detoxification of bacterial LPS', 'Intestinal alkaline phosphatase removes phosphate groups from nutrients during absorption and detoxifies bacterial lipopolysaccharide at the mucosal barrier. Starch, nucleic acid, protein, and fat digestion are handled by pancreatic and other brush border enzymes.'),
('9a7eda1a-548f-5196-971d-ae3492df71bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is lactase deficiency?', '["Bile salt deficiency","Lack of the brush border enzyme that digests lactose","Nucleotidase deficiency","Lack of gastric lactase","Absence of pancreatic lactase"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of the brush border enzyme that digests lactose', 'Lactase is the brush border enzyme that splits lactose into glucose and galactose, and its deficiency leaves undigested lactose to cause osmotic diarrhea, bloating, and cramps. Lactase is not made by the stomach or pancreas, so the other choices do not apply.'),
('a024cb91-3a9e-5345-8cae-a2d5be949eb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of the brush border''s glycocalyx?', '["Adhesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Absorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motility mediated by specific receptor activation","Secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protection and microenvironment for enzyme activity"]'::jsonb, 'Protection and microenvironment for enzyme activity', 'The glycocalyx carpeting the microvilli shields the enterocyte surface and traps fluid, creating a microenvironment that concentrates brush border enzymes for terminal digestion and absorption. It does not itself mediate adhesion, absorption, motility, or glandular secretion.'),
('6889811a-2f11-562f-89d1-9fb238881990'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'How are absorbed lipids transported from enterocytes?', '["As micelles into the blood","As free fatty acids into the portal vein","Directly into blood vessels","As chylomicrons via lacteals","As lipoproteins into the bile duct"]'::jsonb, 'As chylomicrons via lacteals', 'Enterocytes reassemble absorbed triglycerides into chylomicrons, which enter lymphatic lacteals and travel via the thoracic duct into the blood. Only short- and medium-chain fatty acids pass directly into the portal vein; micelles are the luminal delivery form, not the transport product.'),
('9f1551d1-1a00-5782-8019-0be5259c19bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the mechanism of glucose absorption?', '["SGLT1 sodium-glucose co-transporter","GLUT5 only","Facilitated diffusion","Active transport with ATP","Passive diffusion"]'::jsonb, 'SGLT1 sodium-glucose co-transporter', 'Glucose and galactose cross the enterocyte luminal membrane via the SGLT1 sodium-glucose cotransporter, driven by the inward sodium gradient, and leave basolaterally through GLUT2. GLUT5 handles only fructose, and passive diffusion cannot move glucose against the gradient.'),
('80c90b87-f0fc-5520-841e-edc39126ac13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the histological layers of the colon?', '["submucosa","muscularis externa","Mucosa","serosa","Mucosa only"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa', 'Like the rest of the alimentary canal, the colon wall is formed of mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia, and its mucosa lacks villi while being packed with goblet cells. ''Mucosa only'' is wrong because all four layers are present.'),
('8a6666c4-3b73-5ff8-b21c-8696ce8900a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is unique about the colonic muscularis externa?', '["It has three circular layers","Outer longitudinal layer forms teniae coli","It lacks a longitudinal layer","Both layers are skeletal muscle","Inner circular layer is absent"]'::jsonb, 'Outer longitudinal layer forms teniae coli', 'In the colon the outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa is condensed into three flat bands, the teniae coli. The layers are smooth muscle throughout, and both the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers are present.'),
('3614bf10-68b7-5a2a-a690-01b148ee0a19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are haustra of the colon?', '["Lymphoid aggregates","Sacculations caused by teniae coli","Glandular structures","Fat deposits","Mucosal folds"]'::jsonb, 'Sacculations caused by teniae coli', 'Because the teniae coli are shorter than the underlying wall, the colon is thrown into outward pouches called haustra. They are not glands, fat deposits, folds, or lymphoid tissue.'),
('68511150-3b0e-577d-ae62-8179b18e768a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of colonic goblet cells?', '["Absorb water resulting in decreased functional activity","Absorb vitamins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secrete lubricating mucus to facilitate stool passage","Absorb electrolytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secrete enzymes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete lubricating mucus to facilitate stool passage', 'Colonic goblet cells secrete abundant mucus that lubricates the luminal surface and eases the passage of formed stool, since the colon reabsorbs water rather than digesting food. Water and electrolyte absorption is performed by colonocytes, not goblet cells.'),
('173cbb1e-8e7f-5033-b3f9-dd70b83de516'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What water absorption occurs in the colon?', '["Only water secretion","Only electrolyte secretion","Absorption of water and electrolytes","forming solid stool","water absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Absorption of water and electrolytes', 'The colon actively reabsorbs sodium, chloride, and water, converting liquid chyme into progressively solid stool. The remaining choices are fragments or wrongly describe secretion rather than the absorptive role of the colon.'),
('15696431-e396-582e-87be-0310953ece81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What bacteria are normally present in the colon?', '["Only viruses","Only pathogenic bacteria","bacteria normally","Commensal bacteria (gut microbiome)","Only fungi"]'::jsonb, 'Commensal bacteria (gut microbiome)', 'The colon normally hosts a dense population of commensal bacteria, the gut microbiome, which ferments undigested fiber, synthesizes vitamins, and defends against pathogens. It is not colonized predominantly by viruses, fungi, or pathogenic bacteria.'),
('53cb2405-c983-5342-8c13-dfa799b83d00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the recto-anal junction?', '["The junction of small and large intestine resulting in decreased functional activity","The ileocecal valve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pylorus mediated by specific receptor activation","The transition from simple columnar to stratified squamous epithelium","The gastro-esophageal junction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The transition from simple columnar to stratified squamous epithelium', 'At the anorectal junction the lining changes from the simple columnar epithelium of the rectum to the nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium of the anus. The ileocecal valve, pylorus, and gastro-esophageal junction are entirely separate transition sites.'),
('4d381935-83cb-569f-8b35-8b30cadac510'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are anal columns (columns of Morgagni)?', '["Serosal fat tags resulting in decreased functional activity","Longitudinal folds of mucosa in the upper anal canal","Circular folds of the rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","Folds of the colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Teniae coli extensions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Longitudinal folds of mucosa in the upper anal canal', 'Anal columns of Morgagni are longitudinal folds of mucosa in the upper anal canal, raised over the superior anorectal arteries. They are not serosal fat tags, circular rectal folds, colonic folds, or teniae coli extensions.'),
('5de5d073-edc1-5b98-bb78-d6a5307caa6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are anal sinuses?', '["Folds of the anal sphincter","Depressions in the skin","Glands of the anal canal","Fatty pouches","Pockets between anal columns that secrete mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Pockets between anal columns that secrete mucus', 'Anal sinuses are the pockets between the anal columns into which the anal glands open, producing mucus that lubricates the canal. They are not folds of the sphincter, skin depressions, glands themselves, or fatty pouches.'),
('0e18c2a7-adbd-562d-895e-16b6e5554e31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are hemorrhoids?', '["Fibroids resulting in decreased functional activity","Polyps of the rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Engorged vascular cushions in the anal canal","Tumors of the colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin tags leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Engorged vascular cushions in the anal canal', 'Hemorrhoids are engorged, distended vascular cushions of the anal canal, internal above the dentate line and external below it. They are not fibroids, rectal polyps, colonic tumors, or skin tags.'),
('35b7d3a8-b84d-53f9-addd-728385525038'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the blood supply to the liver?', '["100% from portal vein","100% from hepatic artery","25% from hepatic artery","50% from each","75% from portal vein"]'::jsonb, '75% from portal vein', 'The liver receives roughly 75% of its blood as nutrient-rich but oxygen-poor venous blood from the portal vein, and about 25% as oxygenated blood from the hepatic artery. Neither vessel alone supplies the organ, and the two flows are not equal.'),
('42c252ec-3368-5c6a-9734-fa3242d47864'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the hepatic portal vein?', '["The vein that carries nutrient-rich blood from the GI tract to the liver","The artery to the liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The umbilical vein remnant mediated by specific receptor activation","The bile duct vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vein that carries blood from the liver to the heart leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vein that carries nutrient-rich blood from the GI tract to the liver', 'The hepatic portal vein collects venous blood rich in absorbed nutrients from the gut, spleen, and pancreas and delivers it to the liver for processing. It is not an artery, is not a remnant of the umbilical vein, and does not drain the liver.'),
('b11eba24-6fbb-51d5-9797-b140d71b3948'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the hepatic vein?', '["The cystic vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Veins that drain blood from the liver to the inferior vena cava","The portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The bile duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The splenic vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Veins that drain blood from the liver to the inferior vena cava', 'The hepatic veins are the efferent veins that drain processed blood from the liver into the inferior vena cava. This is opposite in direction to the portal vein, which brings blood into the liver, and is unrelated to the bile duct.'),
('eb746576-ef2f-5354-9326-71c2e084f12a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the gallbladder''s blood supply?', '["Cystic artery (branch of right hepatic artery)","Hepatic artery directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cystic vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior mesenteric artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic artery (branch of right hepatic artery)', 'The gallbladder is supplied by the cystic artery, which most often arises from the right hepatic artery within Calot''s triangle. Small cystic veins drain into the liver, but the portal vein and superior mesenteric artery are not its supply.'),
('c6627e30-400f-560e-9322-aec29b67c959'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Calot''s triangle?', '["Triangle bounded by cystic duct","Triangle of the pancreas","Triangle in the liver","and cystic artery","common hepatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Triangle bounded by cystic duct', 'Calot''s triangle is formed by the cystic duct, common hepatic duct, and the cystic artery, which runs inside it together with the cystic lymph node, making it the key landmark for cholecystectomy. The other choices are fragments or unrelated regions.'),
('cc08cd8b-3a3d-51c1-beba-3938bd66cde6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is cholelithiasis?', '["Dilation of the bile duct","Inflammation of the gallbladder","Formation of gallstones in the gallbladder","Stricture of the bile duct","Cancer of the gallbladder"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of gallstones in the gallbladder', 'Cholelithiasis is the formation of gallstones in the gallbladder, most commonly cholesterol or pigment stones. Inflammation of the gallbladder is cholecystitis, while dilation and stricture of the bile duct are separate biliary conditions.'),
('ae11a11f-521b-5525-b569-1d331b6a3748'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is cholecystitis?', '["Liver inflammation","Bile duct obstruction","from stone impaction","Gallstone formation","Inflammation of the gallbladder"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the gallbladder', 'Cholecystitis is acute or chronic inflammation of the gallbladder, usually triggered by a stone impacted in the cystic duct. It differs from liver inflammation, gallstone formation itself, and simple bile duct obstruction.'),
('a653eeec-0a3a-5a9b-bdf8-c26b7928823a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the porta hepatis?', '["The gallbladder fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","The bare area of the liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The coronary ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The falciform ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hilum of the liver through which vessels and ducts enter/exit"]'::jsonb, 'The hilum of the liver through which vessels and ducts enter/exit', 'The porta hepatis is the transverse fissure on the visceral surface of the liver through which the portal vein, hepatic artery, and hepatic ducts pass. The gallbladder fossa and peritoneal ligaments are separate liver surface landmarks.'),
('162d5af0-709a-5684-ac90-c8e8d13adc52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the ligamentum teres?', '["The falciform ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","The triangular ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Remnant of the umbilical vein (ligamentum teres hepatis)","The coronary ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The gastrohepatic ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Remnant of the umbilical vein (ligamentum teres hepatis)', 'The ligamentum teres hepatis is the fibrous remnant of the left umbilical vein that runs in the free edge of the falciform ligament down to the umbilicus. The listed peritoneum-based ligaments are not remnants of the embryonic umbilical vein.'),
('55ef053f-dd32-5c92-88a0-415ec60195dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the ligamentum venosum?', '["Remnant of the ductus venosus (fetal shunt)","The falciform ligament","The hepatoduodenal ligament","The coronary ligament","The round ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Remnant of the ductus venosus (fetal shunt)', 'The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the ductus venosus, the fetal channel that bypassed the liver and delivered umbilical venous blood to the inferior vena cava. It should not be confused with the falciform, coronary, or hepatoduodenal ligaments.'),
('c3ee31e4-6f09-5564-8fec-68250280aa10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the head of the pancreas?', '["The leftmost part near the spleen","The tail of the pancreas","The body of the pancreas","The rightmost part lying in the C-loop of the duodenum","The uncinate process"]'::jsonb, 'The rightmost part lying in the C-loop of the duodenum', 'The head is the enlarged rightmost part of the pancreas cradled within the C-loop of the duodenum, through which the common bile duct passes. The tail and left part lie toward the spleen, and the uncinate process is a hook-like extension of the head.'),
('3152983b-7410-572c-886f-54e7a47a71b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the uncinate process of the pancreas?', '["The tail of the pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","The body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The main duct mediated by specific receptor activation","A projection of the tail without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A projection of the head behind the superior mesenteric vessels"]'::jsonb, 'A projection of the head behind the superior mesenteric vessels', 'The uncinate process is a hook-shaped projection of the pancreatic head that extends behind the superior mesenteric vessels, an important relation during pancreatic surgery. It is not the body, tail, or main duct.'),
('664cf711-74e9-5a26-a802-61623faae075'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the body of the pancreas?', '["The tail resulting in decreased functional activity","The part in the duodenum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The main part posterior to the stomach","The head without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The uncinate process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The main part posterior to the stomach', 'The body is the elongated main portion of the pancreas lying behind the stomach, continuous with the head on the right and the tail on the left. The head sits inside the duodenal C-loop, while the tail reaches toward the splenic hilum.'),
('d6293c50-4f65-5305-bbdc-863bcf6338e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the tail of the pancreas?', '["The uncinate process resulting in decreased functional activity","The head accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The main duct mediated by specific receptor activation","The narrow left end that touches the spleen","The body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The narrow left end that touches the spleen', 'The tail is the narrow leftmost end of the pancreas that travels within the splenorenal ligament to contact the splenic hilum. Its proximity to the splenic vessels explains why splenectomy can injure the pancreatic tail.'),
('baa719fb-c8f8-5689-8a61-70993373c7ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the blood supply of the pancreas?', '["Only splenic artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreaticoduodenal arteries (from SMA and celiac) and splenic artery","Only celiac trunk mediated by specific receptor activation","Only SMA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only IMA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreaticoduodenal arteries (from SMA and celiac) and splenic artery', 'The head is supplied by the pancreaticoduodenal arcades (superior from the gastroduodenal/celiac system, inferior from the superior mesenteric artery), while the body and tail receive branches of the splenic artery. No single vessel perfuses the gland.'),
('b97a5f0e-e3a0-5fad-90bb-4f9a96ff7993'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is acute pancreatitis?', '["Pancreatic atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreatic cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute inflammation of the pancreas with autodigestion","Pancreatic cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic pancreatic inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute inflammation of the pancreas with autodigestion', 'Acute pancreatitis is a sudden inflammatory injury in which prematurely activated pancreatic enzymes autodigest the gland, most often from gallstones or alcohol. It is a transient insult, unlike chronic fibrosis, atrophy, cysts, or cancer.'),
('f1586b11-46d6-52ed-8c0d-1505710c3732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is chronic pancreatitis?', '["Pancreatic cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Progressive inflammation with fibrosis and loss of exocrine and endocrine function","Pancreatic cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatic abscess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acute inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive inflammation with fibrosis and loss of exocrine and endocrine function', 'Chronic pancreatitis is a relentless inflammatory process ending in irreversible fibrosis and destruction of both exocrine and endocrine tissue, often from long-standing alcohol abuse. Cysts, cancer, abscesses, and acute inflammation are separate entities.'),
('06693592-abdd-56d4-aaca-847889d950ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the relationship between the pancreas and the common bile duct?', '["The bile duct is posterior resulting in decreased functional activity","The bile duct is anterior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bile duct is superior mediated by specific receptor activation","The common bile duct passes through the head of the pancreas","They are unrelated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The common bile duct passes through the head of the pancreas', 'The common bile duct runs through the head of the pancreas before joining the main pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater. This course explains obstructive jaundice when the pancreatic head is enlarged by tumor or inflammation.'),
('3c2bb3a1-6811-5195-885a-979ed793b686'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the sphincter of the pancreatic duct?', '["The sphincter of Oddi resulting in decreased functional activity","The ileocecal valve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The anal sphincter mediated by specific receptor activation","The pyloric sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A sphincter at the termination of the pancreatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'A sphincter at the termination of the pancreatic duct', 'A smooth-muscle sphincter at the termination of the pancreatic duct, part of the hepatopancreatic sphincter (sphincter of Oddi) complex, governs the release of pancreatic juice into the duodenum. The pyloric, ileocecal, and anal sphincters are unrelated structures.'),
('7d0752c9-084d-5602-8cad-d5eec575a5a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is pancreas divisum?', '["Annular pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","Complete pancreatic agenesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreatic cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatic cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A congenital anomaly where dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts fail to fuse"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital anomaly where dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts fail to fuse', 'Pancreas divisum is the common congenital failure of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts to unite, forcing most drainage through the smaller minor papilla. It is not agenesis, annular pancreas, a cyst, or cancer.'),
('e40be292-1350-5bee-affa-409cb36bc6aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the cranial intestinal portal?', '["The opening of the foregut into the yolk sac","The pharyngeal membrane","The proctodeum","The stomodeum","The opening of the hindgut"]'::jsonb, 'The opening of the foregut into the yolk sac', 'The cranial (anterior) intestinal portal is the primitive opening at which the foregut communicates with the yolk sac during early gut tube formation. The stomodeum, proctodeum, and pharyngeal membrane are different embryonic openings.'),
('fc8c399b-f571-5420-8349-d75036f5ba55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the caudal intestinal portal?', '["The proctodeum resulting in decreased functional activity","The oropharyngeal membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The stomodeum mediated by specific receptor activation","The opening of the hindgut into the yolk sac","The foregut opening leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The opening of the hindgut into the yolk sac', 'The caudal (posterior) intestinal portal is the opening where the hindgut communicates with the yolk sac during development. The stomodeum and proctodeum are ectodermal invaginations, not the gut-yolk-sac connections of midgut development.'),
('d3d256a4-ce51-56a4-9651-f433db27b6cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the cloaca?', '["The terminal part of the hindgut that receives the allantois and mesonephric ducts","The stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The anus mediated by specific receptor activation","The bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The terminal part of the hindgut that receives the allantois and mesonephric ducts', 'The cloaca is the terminal dilation of the hindgut that receives the allantois and the mesonephric ducts before the urorectal septum divides it into a dorsal rectum and a ventral urogenital sinus. The stomach, anus, bladder, and pharynx are not distal hindgut structures that collect embryonic ducts and the allantois.'),
('4a013faf-5d62-538b-8a60-acc9fe7917eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the urorectal septum?', '["The septum between heart and lungs resulting in decreased functional activity","The pericardial septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","The mesodermal septum that divides the cloaca into urogenital sinus and rectum","The pleural septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mesodermal septum that divides the cloaca into urogenital sinus and rectum', 'The urorectal septum is a transverse mesodermal ridge that grows downward to split the cloaca into the dorsal rectum and the ventral urogenital sinus. Septa of the thorax and the diaphragm separate other body compartments rather than the cloaca.'),
('cf9c18ca-b831-51fc-a8bd-6ec528e7cde3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the anal membrane?', '["The pharyngeal membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","The buccopharyngeal membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The cloacal membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","The membrane at the junction of proctodeum and hindgut","The tympanic membrane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The membrane at the junction of proctodeum and hindgut', 'The anal membrane is the thin ectodermal-endodermal partition at the junction of the proctodeum and hindgut; its breakdown creates the opening of the anal canal. The pharyngeal, buccopharyngeal, cloacal, and tympanic membranes occupy entirely different locations.'),
('4aee5226-9247-5acc-94b0-d9733ec0b3db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the stomodeum?', '["The anus resulting in decreased functional activity","The cloaca accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pharynx mediated by specific receptor activation","The ectodermal depression that forms the mouth","The primitive gut leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ectodermal depression that forms the mouth', 'The stomodeum is a depression lined by surface ectoderm that deepens to form the primitive oral cavity. The anus, cloaca, pharynx, and primitive gut are not ectodermal depressions that form the mouth.'),
('9624c2e5-af88-5641-a6e3-939514d7cd06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the pharyngeal gut?', '["The part of the foregut that gives rise to pharyngeal arches and pouches","The colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The small intestine mediated by specific receptor activation","The stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the foregut that gives rise to pharyngeal arches and pouches', 'The pharyngeal gut is the cranial portion of the foregut that gives rise to the pharyngeal arches, pouches, and their derivatives. The colon, small intestine, stomach, and esophagus develop from more caudal regions of the gut tube.'),
('da1db950-7266-5b46-b515-26056411325c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are pharyngeal pouches?', '["Blood vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Ectodermal clefts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endodermal outpocketings of the pharyngeal gut between pharyngeal arches","Neural crest derivatives without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesodermal structures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Endodermal outpocketings of the pharyngeal gut between pharyngeal arches', 'Pharyngeal pouches are endodermal outpocketings of the pharyngeal gut that bulge between the pharyngeal arches and give rise to the tonsils, thymus, and parathyroid glands. Blood vessels, ectoderm, mesoderm, and neural crest cells belong to other germ layers.'),
('9f2a46b6-edf8-5e04-9f76-6bf01b72ca89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the PAS stain used for in GI histology?', '["Detecting glycogen","Detecting DNA","and glycoproteins","Detecting lipids","mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Detecting glycogen', 'Periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) oxidizes carbohydrate hydroxyl groups and stains glycogen, glycoproteins, and mucins magenta. It does not demonstrate DNA or lipids, so mucus-rich and glycogen-laden glandular tissues are its typical GI targets.'),
('bd717963-5fb3-56e3-bcd7-9a9a5cd809d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Alcian blue stain used for?', '["Detecting proteins","Detecting lipids","Detecting acidic mucins (blue)","Detecting neutral mucins","Detecting glycogen"]'::jsonb, 'Detecting acidic mucins (blue)', 'Alcian blue is a cationic dye that binds sulfated and carboxylated mucins, coloring them blue at low pH. Neutral mucins, proteins, lipids, and glycogen are not selectively demonstrated by this stain.'),
('e432d0fc-2ff5-5139-b94a-b9cbee057deb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is mucicarmine stain used for?', '["Staining nuclei","Staining muscle","Staining mucins pink-red","Staining elastin","Staining collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Staining mucins pink-red', 'Mucicarmine is a classic stain that colors mucins pink-red and is used to identify mucin-secreting cells. Nuclei, muscle, elastin, and collagen require hematoxylin, trichrome, or elastic tissue stains instead.'),
('55a65cfa-59ea-5717-a219-b25381baec6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What staining is used to identify Paneth cells?', '["Alcian blue positive","Eosinophilic bright red granules","PAS positive granules","Mucicarmine positive","Basophilic granules"]'::jsonb, 'Eosinophilic bright red granules', 'Paneth cells, located at the bases of small intestinal crypts, are identified by their large eosinophilic granules that appear bright red or pink on routine stains. They do not take up mucin stains or basophilic dyes.'),
('ed0078a1-f4b3-58c9-8f5b-c3fe4c35509c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What staining is used for argentaffin cells?', '["Silver stains (Grimelius)","H&E accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PAS mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucicarmine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alcian blue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Silver stains (Grimelius)', 'Argentaffin cells reduce silver salts and are therefore demonstrated by silver impregnation methods such as the Grimelius stain. Routine H&E, PAS, mucicarmine, and Alcian blue do not selectively reveal these endocrine cells.'),
('0050d027-e6c0-53f5-8bb0-6ec5b41b809c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are enterochromaffin cells?', '["Enteroendocrine cells producing serotonin","Cells producing secretin","Cells producing GIP","Cells producing CCK","Cells producing gastrin"]'::jsonb, 'Enteroendocrine cells producing serotonin', 'Enterochromaffin (Kulchitsky) cells are intestinal endocrine cells that synthesize and store serotonin (5-HT). Secretin, GIP, CCK, and gastrin are released by other specifically named enteroendocrine cells.'),
('6a783e24-ae6d-5433-bd1c-857214a13aad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the gut''s role in serotonin production?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","50% is from gut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","10% is from gut mediated by specific receptor activation","About 90% of the body''s serotonin is produced in enterochromaffin cells","100% is from brain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 90% of the body''s serotonin is produced in enterochromaffin cells', 'About 90% of the body''s serotonin is produced by enterochromaffin cells in the gut rather than by the brain. The smaller percentages greatly underestimate the intestinal contribution to total serotonin synthesis.'),
('2e49bf96-2c65-5718-a4df-1a2a0cdf6c2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of serotonin in the gut?', '["secretion","Regulates motility","Digestion only","Absorption only","and sensation"]'::jsonb, 'Regulates motility', 'Serotonin released by enterochromaffin cells regulates gut motility by acting on enteric neurons and smooth muscle, and it also contributes to visceral sensation such as nausea. It is not confined to secretion, digestion, or absorption.'),
('ed4f84b1-39f6-570c-9436-003fb58f94bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the Cajal interstitial cells'' function?', '["Absorb nutrients resulting in decreased functional activity","Secrete mucus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serve as pacemaker cells for slow wave potentials","Store bile without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Digest food leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Serve as pacemaker cells for slow wave potentials', 'Interstitial cells of Cajal generate and propagate slow-wave potentials and act as the pacemaker cells of GI smooth muscle. They do not absorb nutrients, secrete mucus, store bile, or digest food.'),
('855883f3-5621-5848-82b4-75fe18101e55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Where are Cajal interstitial cells located?', '["In the submucosa","In the muscularis externa","In the mucosa","In the serosa","near the myenteric plexus"]'::jsonb, 'In the muscularis externa', 'Interstitial cells of Cajal lie within the muscularis externa, closely associated with the myenteric plexus between the longitudinal and circular muscle layers, where they coordinate slow waves with smooth muscle activity. The mucosa, submucosa, and serosa are not their primary sites.'),
('a49f2f9f-8210-5800-b8d4-1a121c055176'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are short-chain fatty acids produced by the microbiome?', '["propionate","Bile acids","butyrate from fiber fermentation","Long-chain fatty acids","Acetate"]'::jsonb, 'Acetate', 'The gut microbiome ferments dietary fiber into short-chain fatty acids, chiefly acetate, propionate, and butyrate, and acetate is the most abundant of these products. Bile acids and long-chain fatty acids are not produced by fiber fermentation.'),
('0ea0f8ff-2359-5c85-9f63-282aafd1d53d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is butyrate''s role in the colon?', '["Digests protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretes mucus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorbs water mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary energy source for colonocytes and maintains epithelial barrier","Kills bacteria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary energy source for colonocytes and maintains epithelial barrier', 'Butyrate is the preferred oxidative fuel of colonocytes and helps preserve the integrity of the colonic epithelial barrier. It does not digest protein, secrete mucus, absorb water, or kill bacteria.'),
('f734ab0f-dbce-5d28-a931-fd921cd97410'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the gut-vascular barrier?', '["The filtration barrier resulting in decreased functional activity","The blood-brain barrier accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The placental barrier mediated by specific receptor activation","A barrier that controls the passage of antigens from the gut to the bloodstream","The air-blood barrier leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A barrier that controls the passage of antigens from the gut to the bloodstream', 'The gut-vascular barrier regulates the passage of microbes and antigens from the gut lumen into the portal circulation. The blood-brain, placental, air-blood, and filtration barriers protect different compartments and serve different purposes.'),
('03d09631-e8c8-5b68-aecc-d07855e32af2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is intestinal permeability?', '["The rate of mucus production resulting in decreased functional activity","The degree of passive diffusion across the intestinal epithelium","The rate of blood flow mediated by specific receptor activation","The rate of enzyme secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The rate of peristalsis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The degree of passive diffusion across the intestinal epithelium', 'Intestinal permeability is the degree of passive diffusion across the intestinal epithelium, governed largely by the tight junctions between enterocytes. It does not describe mucus production, blood flow, enzyme secretion, or peristalsis.'),
('765bd38e-3860-5e4f-8aec-7521f2538a6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are tight junction proteins in the gut?', '["occludins","Collagen fibers","Elastin fibers","and ZO proteins that seal the paracellular space","Claudins"]'::jsonb, 'Claudins', 'Claudins and occludins are transmembrane proteins that form the tight junction seal, while ZO proteins anchor them to the cytoskeleton, together closing the paracellular space. Collagen and elastin are extracellular matrix fibers, not junctional proteins.'),
('e4bf7eb0-56e4-5f7e-abf9-ad5749c1433f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is zonulin?', '["A protein that modulates intestinal tight junctions and permeability","A vitamin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A hormone mediated by specific receptor activation","A bacterial toxin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A digestive enzyme leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A protein that modulates intestinal tight junctions and permeability', 'Zonulin reversibly opens intestinal tight junctions and modulates paracellular permeability. It is a protein, not a vitamin, hormone, bacterial toxin, or digestive enzyme.'),
('d3dd6d23-ae89-56fe-a872-7e61558f049e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is celiac disease?', '["causing villous atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Colon cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peptic ulcer disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Appendicitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autoimmune enteropathy triggered by gluten"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune enteropathy triggered by gluten', 'Celiac disease is an immune-mediated enteropathy triggered by gluten in genetically predisposed individuals, leading to damage of the small intestinal mucosa. Colon cancer, peptic ulcer disease, and appendicitis are unrelated GI conditions.'),
('d9abca96-6571-5289-ae79-f7d36016778a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the histological finding in celiac disease?', '["Villous atrophy","crypt hyperplasia","increased intraepithelial lymphocytes","Ulceration","Normal mucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Villous atrophy', 'The hallmark of celiac disease is villous atrophy (flattening of the villi), typically accompanied by crypt hyperplasia and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes. Ulceration and a normal mucosa are not characteristic findings.'),
('c6e798aa-cb2f-5b13-adfb-3bf2d35adf05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)?', '["Acute bacterial infection","Parasitic infection","Viral gastroenteritis","including Crohn''s disease and ulcerative colitis","Chronic idiopathic inflammation of the GI tract"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic idiopathic inflammation of the GI tract', 'IBD is a chronic, relapsing, idiopathic inflammation of the GI tract that classically includes Crohn''s disease and ulcerative colitis. Acute bacterial, parasitic, and viral infections are self-limiting and are not termed IBD.'),
('9234761d-b746-5561-9573-06999467120a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the histological difference between Crohn''s disease and ulcerative colitis?', '["skip lesions","granulomas; UC: mucosal inflammation","continuous","crypt abscesses","Crohn''s: transmural inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'Crohn''s: transmural inflammation', 'Crohn''s disease characteristically produces transmural inflammation (with granulomas and skip lesions), whereas ulcerative colitis is confined to the mucosa and submucosa with continuous involvement and crypt abscesses.'),
('10f8cd71-8ade-5eaf-83a9-8014a274aab9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are crypt abscesses in ulcerative colitis?', '["Bacterial abscesses resulting in decreased functional activity","Neutrophils accumulating in colonic crypts","Lymphoid aggregates mediated by specific receptor activation","Crypt hyperplasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Crypt dilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neutrophils accumulating in colonic crypts', 'Crypt abscesses are collections of neutrophils within the lumens of colonic crypts and are a classic feature of active ulcerative colitis. They are not bacterial abscesses, lymphoid aggregates, crypt hyperplasia, or simple crypt dilation.'),
('8fd8536e-c33d-5bb8-893a-82e9a33b01aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of bile acid binding with fats?', '["Bile acids absorb carbohydrates","Bile acids transport vitamins","Bile acids digest proteins","Bile acids kill bacteria","Bile acids emulsify fats and form micelles for absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Bile acids emulsify fats and form micelles for absorption', 'Bile acids emulsify dietary fat and help form micelles, which are required for lipid absorption. They do not absorb carbohydrates, digest proteins, transport vitamins alone, or kill bacteria.'),
('bbf1e45e-cebd-52f2-bfb3-1a8256a8a95e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is enterohepatic circulation of bile acids?', '["The flow of bile from liver to gallbladder resulting in decreased functional activity","The storage of bile accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The flow of bile from liver to duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","The production of bile without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Recycling of bile acids from ileum back to liver via portal blood"]'::jsonb, 'Recycling of bile acids from ileum back to liver via portal blood', 'Enterohepatic circulation is the recycling of bile acids that are absorbed in the ileum and carried back to the liver in portal blood for resecretion into bile. Simple one-way flow, storage, or production of bile does not describe recycling.'),
('66366356-f54e-58b5-8109-4faf570a548b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What percentage of bile acids are reabsorbed in the ileum?', '["About 75%","About 50%","About 25%","About 95%","About 10%"]'::jsonb, 'About 95%', 'About 95% of bile acids are actively reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and reused, with only about 5% escaping into the colon. The smaller percentages underestimate normal bile acid reabsorption.'),
('c0eba445-c21c-5231-94ae-bb7828c87631'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is steatorrhea?', '["Mucus in the stool resulting in decreased functional activity","Constipation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Watery diarrhea mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood in the stool without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fat in the stool due to malabsorption of lipids"]'::jsonb, 'Fat in the stool due to malabsorption of lipids', 'Steatorrhea is excess fat in the stool caused by impaired lipid digestion or absorption. It is not mucus, blood, constipation, or watery diarrhea.'),
('b961d26f-9cde-540e-b288-0ec7b6cc86b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the function of the gallbladder''s absorption of sodium?', '["Creates an osmotic gradient for water absorption","Produces new bile accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","concentrating bile mediated by specific receptor activation","Secretes bicarbonate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Makes bile more dilute leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Creates an osmotic gradient for water absorption', 'Active sodium and chloride absorption by the gallbladder epithelium creates an osmotic gradient that drives passive water reabsorption, thereby concentrating bile. It does not synthesize new bile, secrete bicarbonate, or dilute bile.'),
('cec1fa39-2dd5-5e6b-82eb-c7cfd127f4d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the Rokitansky-Aschoff sinus?', '["Invagination of gallbladder mucosa into the muscle layer","Liver abscess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gallstone mediated by specific receptor activation","Bile duct cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatic cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Invagination of gallbladder mucosa into the muscle layer', 'A Rokitansky-Aschoff sinus is an invagination (diverticulum) of gallbladder mucosa through the muscle layer, typically seen in chronic cholecystitis. It is not a gallstone, abscess, or cyst of the liver, bile duct, or pancreas.'),
('93a90d80-aac7-537e-a78c-94ca953074cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Courvoisier''s law?', '["non-tender gallbladder suggests malignancy (not stones)","Jaundice with a palpable","Jaundice is from stones","A tender gallbladder is from cancer","Gallstones cause pain"]'::jsonb, 'non-tender gallbladder suggests malignancy (not stones)', 'Courvoisier''s law states that painless jaundice with a palpable, non-tender, distended gallbladder suggests obstruction by malignancy rather than by gallstones. A tender gallbladder or stone-related jaundice points toward acute cholecystitis or stone disease.'),
('373a566b-5db6-5a03-b022-2d7991a31889'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Murphy''s sign?', '["Pain in the left lower quadrant resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain in the epigastrium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain and inspiratory arrest on palpation of the right upper quadrant in cholecystitis","Guarding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rebound tenderness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and inspiratory arrest on palpation of the right upper quadrant in cholecystitis', 'Murphy''s sign is inspiratory arrest caused by pain when the right upper quadrant is palpated during inspiration in acute cholecystitis. Pain in other quadrants, guarding, and rebound tenderness represent different clinical findings.'),
('4052c7d0-da71-5d72-bc98-2f93b9cff96b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common location for peptic ulcers?', '["Duodenum (first part)","Colon","Jejunum","Stomach","Esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum (first part)', 'Peptic ulcers most commonly occur in the first part of the duodenum, followed by the stomach. The colon, jejunum, and esophagus are unusual sites for peptic ulceration.'),
('c7ac21bc-a74f-515f-b174-b2596c914a28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the role of Helicobacter pylori in peptic ulcer disease?', '["Blocks blood flow","Colonizes gastric mucosa","Produces acid","causing inflammation and increased acid or weakened defenses","Directly destroys the mucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Colonizes gastric mucosa', 'H. pylori colonizes the gastric mucosa, where it provokes inflammation that increases acid production or weakens mucosal defenses, promoting peptic ulceration. It does not block blood flow, produce acid, or directly destroy the mucosa.'),
('948ce523-c2a7-55e6-8a38-097e62d93630'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Where does H. pylori colonize?', '["The small intestine","especially the antrum","The gastric mucosa","The esophagus","The colon"]'::jsonb, 'The gastric mucosa', 'H. pylori colonizes the gastric mucosa, with a particular predilection for the antrum. It does not primarily inhabit the small intestine, esophagus, or colon.'),
('c451c606-1d33-5e6a-81b9-aa0b5c111fed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the intestinal metaplasia of the stomach?', '["Normal stomach lining resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreatic metaplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gastric atrophy mediated by specific receptor activation","Replacement of gastric epithelium by intestinal-type epithelium","a precancerous change leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Replacement of gastric epithelium by intestinal-type epithelium', 'Intestinal metaplasia is the replacement of gastric epithelium by intestinal-type columnar epithelium containing goblet cells, often following chronic gastritis, and it is a recognized precancerous change. It is not normal lining, pancreatic metaplasia, or simple gastric atrophy.'),
('c159ccfa-2f7b-5b8e-8b4f-fee17284d8c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Barrett''s esophagus?', '["Esophageal stricture resulting in decreased functional activity","Metaplasia of esophageal epithelium to intestinal-type columnar epithelium from GERD","Squamous cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Achalasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Esophageal varices leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metaplasia of esophageal epithelium to intestinal-type columnar epithelium from GERD', 'Barrett''s esophagus is the metaplastic replacement of esophageal squamous epithelium by intestinal-type columnar epithelium due to chronic GERD, and it predisposes to adenocarcinoma. Stricture, achalasia, varices, and carcinoma are separate entities.'),
('964b97d6-5da9-5507-a06c-530e78f991cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the normal length of the esophagus?', '["Approximately 25 cm","40 cm","50 cm","15 cm","10 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 25 cm', 'The esophagus is approximately 25 cm long, extending from the pharynx to the esophagogastric junction. The other values fall outside the normal adult esophageal length.'),
('fc3debf9-26a9-5f37-8bb2-fbb4e7cf312c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the three constrictions of the esophagus?', '["diaphragmatic (T10)","Upper and lower only","Cervical (pharyngoesophageal)","broncho-aortic (T4-T5)","constrictions"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical (pharyngoesophageal)', 'The esophagus has three constrictions: the cervical (pharyngoesophageal) at C6, the broncho-aortic at T4-T5 produced by the aortic arch and left main bronchus, and the diaphragmatic at T10. The cervical narrowing is the location of the pharyngoesophageal junction.'),
('c56fed09-afa5-5af3-b727-dc5b04749905'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the clinical significance of esophageal constrictions?', '["They cause bleeding only resulting in decreased functional activity","They cause pain only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They cause dysphagia only mediated by specific receptor activation","They are common sites for impaction of swallowed objects and esophageal cancer","significance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They are common sites for impaction of swallowed objects and esophageal cancer', 'The constrictions of the esophagus are the common sites where swallowed foreign bodies become impacted and where esophageal carcinoma tends to arise. Bleeding, pain, or dysphagia alone does not capture this clinical importance.'),
('0d7ef7c4-e55c-5b12-bea5-9edd02f11260'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common type of esophageal cancer?', '["Adenocarcinoma only resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphomas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Squamous cell carcinoma (upper 2/3) and adenocarcinoma (lower 1/3)","Sarcomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Squamous cell carcinoma only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma (upper 2/3) and adenocarcinoma (lower 1/3)', 'Esophageal cancer is most commonly squamous cell carcinoma in the upper two-thirds and adenocarcinoma in the lower third. The single-type options and rare tumor types do not reflect this distribution.'),
('c2cbc809-68e0-53f8-b424-e50227c6ac8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is achalasia of the esophagus?', '["Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax","causing dysphagia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Esophageal spasm mediated by specific receptor activation","Esophageal stricture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GERD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax', 'Achalasia is the failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax on swallowing, causing functional obstruction and progressive dysphagia. Spasm, stricture, and GERD have different underlying mechanisms.'),
('456f13f0-c909-5734-8e78-d1df268fd12e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is cirrhosis of the liver?', '["Fatty liver resulting in decreased functional activity","Liver cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Liver cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Diffuse hepatic fibrosis with nodule formation","Acute liver inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse hepatic fibrosis with nodule formation', 'Cirrhosis is the end-stage result of chronic liver injury, defined histologically by diffuse fibrosis and regenerative nodule formation. Fatty liver, cancer, cyst, and acute inflammation lack this nodular fibrotic pattern.'),
('a1533b38-dfa8-5f24-95df-c1e67fffa89b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is portal hypertension?', '["Increased pressure in the portal vein system due to cirrhosis or obstruction","Increased hepatic artery pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased pulmonary pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased blood pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased venous pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pressure in the portal vein system due to cirrhosis or obstruction', 'Portal hypertension is elevated pressure within the portal venous system, most often caused by cirrhosis or obstruction of the portal or hepatic veins. It is not systemic, pulmonary, or generalized venous hypertension.'),
('4f90a405-5036-5ba0-be92-dd7304a1d02a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the complications of portal hypertension?', '["ascites","hepatic encephalopathy","splenomegaly","complications","Esophageal varices"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal varices', 'Esophageal varices are a major complication of portal hypertension, developing as portosystemic collateral veins that can rupture and bleed; ascites, splenomegaly, and hepatic encephalopathy are other complications.'),
('6eeffbba-af32-59f4-84ab-1bd9caecfd6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are esophageal varices?', '["Esophageal strictures resulting in decreased functional activity","Esophageal rings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dilated submucosal veins in the lower esophagus from portal hypertension","Hiatal hernias without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Esophageal tumors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated submucosal veins in the lower esophagus from portal hypertension', 'Esophageal varices are dilated submucosal veins of the lower esophagus that form as portosystemic collaterals in portal hypertension. Strictures, rings, hernias, and tumors are not vascular dilations.'),
('94a2fe35-d27c-5f07-82a9-034e22da2e55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is ascites?', '["Fluid in the pericardium","Fluid in the pleural cavity","Pulmonary edema","Edema of the legs","Accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity', 'Ascites is the accumulation of free fluid within the peritoneal cavity, commonly from portal hypertension or peritoneal disease. Fluid in the pleura, pericardium, lungs, or legs carries different names.'),
('e90415ac-1edc-5689-b2bc-12de30c16dd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is hepatic encephalopathy?', '["Seizure disorder","Stroke","Cognitive dysfunction from liver failure","due to ammonia and other toxins","Brain infection"]'::jsonb, 'Cognitive dysfunction from liver failure', 'Hepatic encephalopathy is a neuropsychiatric syndrome caused by liver failure; ammonia and other neurotoxins that the failing liver cannot detoxify reach the brain and impair cognition. It is not a primary seizure disorder, stroke, or brain infection.'),
('14238e36-d006-5e16-a2e5-a84f27913ebf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is jaundice?', '["Yellow discoloration of skin and sclera from elevated bilirubin","Purple skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blue discoloration of skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Red skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pale skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Yellow discoloration of skin and sclera from elevated bilirubin', 'Jaundice is the yellow discoloration of skin, sclera, and mucous membranes caused by elevated serum bilirubin. The other listed skin-color changes arise from different mechanisms, such as cyanosis producing bluish skin.'),
('74a20bcc-f340-5816-a321-4434ee153e98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the three types of jaundice?', '["hepatic","Mild","post-hepatic","moderate","Pre-hepatic"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-hepatic', 'Jaundice is classified as pre-hepatic, hepatic, or post-hepatic, depending on whether the defect lies before, within, or after hepatic handling of bilirubin. Pre-hepatic jaundice results from excessive hemolysis generating unconjugated bilirubin that overwhelms the liver.'),
('f5febfbd-38f8-51c3-b47a-d856aae218d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the difference between direct and indirect bilirubin?', '["fat-soluble","Direct: conjugated","water-soluble; Indirect: unconjugated","Indirect is water-soluble","Direct is fat-soluble"]'::jsonb, 'Direct: conjugated', 'Direct bilirubin is conjugated (glucuronidated), water-soluble, and readily excreted in urine, whereas indirect bilirubin is unconjugated and fat-soluble. This distinction helps localize the cause of jaundice.'),
('04a2a674-1b18-58df-ba8a-b0c23891ac1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is colorectal adenocarcinoma?', '["Lymphoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Carcinoid tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Benign polyp without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malignant epithelial tumor of the colon or rectum"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant epithelial tumor of the colon or rectum', 'Colorectal adenocarcinoma arises from malignant transformation of the glandular epithelial cells lining the colon and rectum, and is by far the most common colorectal malignancy. Lymphoma, carcinoid, sarcoma, and benign polyps are distinct entities.'),
('ff0497d7-6661-5aaa-b576-071eb16b68c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the adenoma-carcinoma sequence?', '["Direct cancer development resulting in decreased functional activity","The progression from benign adenomatous polyp to invasive carcinoma","Cancer from inflammation only mediated by specific receptor activation","Cancer from normal mucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hereditary cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The progression from benign adenomatous polyp to invasive carcinoma', 'The adenoma-carcinoma sequence describes the stepwise progression from a benign adenomatous polyp through increasing dysplasia to invasive carcinoma, typically over many years. It is the basis for screening colonoscopy with polypectomy.'),
('57994d8c-87d6-5114-9a14-f4ad721286ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What genetic mutations are typically involved in the adenoma-carcinoma sequence?', '["KRAS","APC","Only APC","p53 mutations","DCC"]'::jsonb, 'APC', 'Inactivation of the APC tumor suppressor gene is the initiating (gatekeeper) event in the adenoma-carcinoma sequence, followed later by KRAS, SMAD4/DCC, and p53 mutations. APC loss is the hallmark early step.'),
('4b92d1ba-6c2d-5925-aa0a-f026c559d07b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP)?', '["Sporadic polyps resulting in decreased functional activity","Inflammatory polyps accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Juvenile polyps mediated by specific receptor activation","Hereditary condition with hundreds of colorectal adenomas caused by APC mutation","Hyperplastic polyps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hereditary condition with hundreds of colorectal adenomas caused by APC mutation', 'FAP is an autosomal dominant condition caused by a germline APC mutation that produces hundreds to thousands of colorectal adenomas by adolescence, with near-universal progression to cancer unless colectomy is performed.'),
('8a054baf-440d-5b6a-9ffe-319669585c6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Lynch syndrome (HNPCC)?', '["FAP resulting in decreased functional activity","Polyposis syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cowden syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer from DNA mismatch repair defects","Peutz-Jeghers syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer from DNA mismatch repair defects', 'Lynch syndrome is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by DNA mismatch repair defects (e.g., MLH1, MSH2) that produce microsatellite instability and increased colorectal and extracolonic cancers, characteristically without multiple polyps.'),
('0c021141-3361-57de-b66e-4583552ea48f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are hyperplastic polyps?', '["Inflammatory polyps","Benign polyps with saw-tooth architecture","Adenomatous polyps","malignant potential","Premalignant polyps"]'::jsonb, 'Benign polyps with saw-tooth architecture', 'Hyperplastic polyps are benign serrated polyps with a saw-tooth glandular architecture, most often in the left colon and rectum. They carry negligible malignant potential, unlike adenomatous polyps.'),
('02555ced-2022-5066-bc66-246c33643ed5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are sessile serrated adenomas?', '["Inflammatory polyps","Traditional adenomas","Benign hyperplastic polyps","often in the right colon","Serrated polyps with malignant potential"]'::jsonb, 'Serrated polyps with malignant potential', 'Sessile serrated adenomas are serrated polyps, typically located in the right (proximal) colon, with malignant potential through the BRAF-mutated, CpG-island-methylator pathway. They differ from benign hyperplastic polyps and traditional pedunculated adenomas.'),
('96ce935f-b101-5b8e-a1b6-ce12bacfe36a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is diverticulosis?', '["Inflammation of diverticula resulting in decreased functional activity","Colonic polyps accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Presence of diverticula (outpouchings) in the colonic wall","Colon cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Irritable bowel syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Presence of diverticula (outpouchings) in the colonic wall', 'Diverticulosis is the presence of outpouchings of the colonic wall that occur where blood vessels penetrate the muscularis, most often in the sigmoid colon. It is usually asymptomatic, unlike diverticulitis, which is inflammatory.'),
('9df334d1-673b-565e-a89e-f8237587f3f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is diverticulitis?', '["Cancer of diverticula","Inflammation and infection of diverticula","Asymptomatic diverticula","Perforated colon","Bleeding from diverticula"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation and infection of diverticula', 'Diverticulitis is inflammation and infection of pre-existing diverticula, causing abdominal pain, fever, and potential complications such as perforation or abscess. The mere presence of diverticula (diverticulosis) is not inflammatory.'),
('61f946d0-d23c-501e-8927-31fff462af4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common site of colonic diverticula?', '["Transverse colon","Sigmoid colon","Ascending colon","Cecum","Rectum"]'::jsonb, 'Sigmoid colon', 'Colonic diverticula most commonly occur in the sigmoid colon, where high luminal pressure and segmental wall weakness at vascular penetration points favor their formation.'),
('a40f21f3-3de6-5c7b-8f23-eba00cff6dab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common pancreatic exocrine tumor?', '["Pancreatic sarcoma","Pancreatic lymphoma","Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","Pancreatic cyst","Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma', 'Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is the most common pancreatic exocrine tumor, accounting for about 90% of pancreatic cancers. It carries a very poor prognosis because it usually presents at an advanced stage.'),
('0ed127a9-8236-5d80-86cf-a2545315e750'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Where are most pancreatic adenocarcinomas located?', '["Entire pancreas","Body","Tail","Head of the pancreas","Uncinate process"]'::jsonb, 'Head of the pancreas', 'About 60-70% of pancreatic adenocarcinomas arise in the head of the pancreas, which explains the frequent presentation with obstructive jaundice and weight loss. Tumors in the body and tail are less common.'),
('5ebcfcdc-b468-5727-8403-0f6f250c17b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common genetic mutation in pancreatic cancer?', '["APC mutation","SMAD4 mutation","KRAS mutation","BRCA mutation","p53 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'KRAS mutation', 'Activating KRAS mutations occur in about 90% of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas and are the earliest genetic event. SMAD4, p53, and other mutations arise later in tumor progression.'),
('09ae08c5-bd56-5c83-9173-3e523f365963'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the survival rate for pancreatic cancer?', '["Moderate resulting in decreased functional activity","Excellent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Very poor (5-year survival <10%)","Unknown without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Good leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Very poor (5-year survival <10%)', 'Pancreatic cancer has a very poor prognosis, with a 5-year survival below 10%, because most patients present with advanced or metastatic disease. The other responses overstate survival.'),
('03ca27d6-6dbc-55d8-9148-aa08d5f23bbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Zollinger-Ellison syndrome?', '["Insulinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucagonoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VIPoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Somatostatinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastrinoma causing hypergastrinemia and severe peptic ulcers"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrinoma causing hypergastrinemia and severe peptic ulcers', 'Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is caused by a gastrin-secreting neuroendocrine tumor (gastrinoma), usually in the duodenum or pancreas, producing marked hypergastrinemia with severe, recurrent peptic ulcers. It is not an insulinoma, glucagonoma, VIPoma, or somatostatinoma.'),
('585957d7-a6d3-58d3-acd6-4a41ad8a09b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is cystic fibrosis''s effect on the pancreas?', '["Thick secretions cause duct obstruction","Enlarges the pancreas","effect","Enhances pancreatic function","leading to pancreatic insufficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Thick secretions cause duct obstruction', 'In cystic fibrosis, defective CFTR produces abnormally thick, viscous secretions that obstruct pancreatic ducts, causing progressive destruction of acini and pancreatic insufficiency. The pancreas neither enlarges nor functions better.'),
('c614387b-44cf-5d33-81cd-7c0401cf70eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is pancreatic pseudocyst?', '["A collection of fluid and debris enclosed by fibrous tissue after pancreatitis","A true epithelial-lined cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pancreatic abscess mediated by specific receptor activation","A pancreatic tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A hematoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A collection of fluid and debris enclosed by fibrous tissue after pancreatitis', 'A pancreatic pseudocyst is a localized collection of fluid and necrotic debris enclosed by fibrous or granulation tissue, usually developing weeks after acute pancreatitis. It lacks a true epithelial lining, which distinguishes it from a true cyst.'),
('2dda1bd4-aeab-5d29-8287-0734983ad571'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common cause of acute pancreatitis?', '["Gallstones and alcohol","Trauma","Hereditary","Drugs","Infections"]'::jsonb, 'Gallstones and alcohol', 'Gallstones and alcohol together account for the vast majority of acute pancreatitis cases. Trauma, drugs, hereditary factors, and infections are recognized but far less common causes.'),
('3e821c99-4172-583a-b8da-7655b4379d80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are Grey Turner''s sign and Cullen''s sign?', '["Signs of liver disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Signs of diverticulitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Signs of cholecystitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Ecchymosis in the flank (Grey Turner) and periumbilical (Cullen) in severe pancreatitis","Signs of appendicitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ecchymosis in the flank (Grey Turner) and periumbilical (Cullen) in severe pancreatitis', 'Grey Turner''s sign is flank ecchymosis and Cullen''s sign is periumbilical ecchymosis, caused by retroperitoneal hemorrhage tracking along fascial planes in severe necrotizing pancreatitis.'),
('f6529642-f413-588c-a0a6-e66f0af5f23d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is hepatitis?', '["most commonly from viruses","or drugs","Inflammation of the liver","alcohol","Liver cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the liver', 'Hepatitis is inflammation of the liver, most commonly caused by viruses, alcohol, or drugs. The other choices name causative agents rather than defining the condition itself.'),
('683de054-3bd6-5263-9842-c533298ce5c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is hepatic steatosis?', '["Liver glycogen storage resulting in decreased functional activity","Accumulation of fat within hepatocytes (fatty liver)","Liver iron overload mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accumulation of fat within hepatocytes (fatty liver)', 'Hepatic steatosis is the accumulation of fat within hepatocytes, typically as macrovesicular lipid droplets, and is the earliest stage in the fatty liver disease spectrum. It may progress to steatohepatitis, fibrosis, and cirrhosis.'),
('f1745325-f6ae-52c0-b2d6-6c70b34fc7db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD)?', '["associated with metabolic syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Alcohol-induced liver disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autoimmune hepatitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral hepatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fat accumulation in the liver without significant alcohol use"]'::jsonb, 'Fat accumulation in the liver without significant alcohol use', 'NAFLD is fat accumulation in the liver occurring in the absence of significant alcohol intake, and is closely associated with the metabolic syndrome (obesity, diabetes, and dyslipidemia).'),
('4487db09-9020-59f4-b736-f4d44636b116'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is NASH?', '["Non-alcoholic steatohepatitis with steatosis","and hepatocyte injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A benign fatty liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-alcoholic steatohepatitis with steatosis', 'NASH (non-alcoholic steatohepatitis) is steatosis accompanied by hepatocyte injury (ballooning) and lobular inflammation, a potentially progressive form of fatty liver disease that can lead to fibrosis and cirrhosis.'),
('9238f96a-bc3c-5ca2-a9c7-8b9e1429c65d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is hemochromatosis?', '["Excessive glycogen storage","Excessive iron deposition in the liver and other organs","Excessive fat accumulation","Excessive bilirubin","Excessive copper accumulation"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive iron deposition in the liver and other organs', 'Hemochromatosis is characterized by excessive iron deposition in the liver, pancreas, heart, and joints from increased intestinal iron absorption, leading to cirrhosis, diabetes, and skin pigmentation.'),
('b56a2d10-839e-5c75-b6a0-55038d4402b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Wilson''s disease?', '["Bile duct disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Iron overload accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Copper accumulation in the liver and brain due to defective ATP7B","Viral hepatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fatty liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Copper accumulation in the liver and brain due to defective ATP7B', 'Wilson''s disease is an autosomal recessive disorder of the ATP7B copper transporter, causing copper accumulation in the liver and brain with hepatitis or cirrhosis, neuropsychiatric disease, and Kayser-Fleischer rings.'),
('64895501-4cb3-5c51-b0f2-f2811d3bab62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency?', '["A metabolic syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","A genetic disorder causing emphysema and liver disease from abnormal protein accumulation","A viral infection mediated by specific receptor activation","A clotting disorder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An autoimmune disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A genetic disorder causing emphysema and liver disease from abnormal protein accumulation', 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is a genetic disorder in which misfolded AAT protein accumulates in hepatocytes, causing liver disease, while low circulating levels permit unopposed elastase activity and emphysema.'),
('e452e586-fed5-56dd-b447-f7ba95eaabf6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common primary liver cancer?', '["Metastatic cancer","Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)","Hemangioma","Cholangiocarcinoma","Hepatic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC)', 'Hepatocellular carcinoma is the most common primary liver cancer, arising from hepatocytes, usually in the setting of cirrhosis. Cholangiocarcinoma and hepatic adenoma are less common primary tumors, and hemangiomas are benign.'),
('b1c9a503-2d14-5d8d-8cf5-e27e202b9338'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the most common risk factor for HCC worldwide?', '["NAFLD resulting in decreased functional activity","Aflatoxin exposure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hepatitis C mediated by specific receptor activation","Alcohol without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chronic hepatitis B infection"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic hepatitis B infection', 'Chronic hepatitis B infection is the leading risk factor for HCC worldwide, although hepatitis C and NAFLD contribute increasingly in developed countries. Aflatoxin exposure is an important cofactor in endemic regions.'),
('85a4a4db-a0a1-585b-a629-989adeed7edf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)?', '["A serum tumor marker elevated in HCC","A clotting factor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A liver enzyme mediated by specific receptor activation","A bile acid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A viral protein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A serum tumor marker elevated in HCC', 'Alpha-fetoprotein is a serum tumor marker that is elevated in many patients with hepatocellular carcinoma, and is used for surveillance of at-risk patients and monitoring treatment response. It is not a clotting factor, enzyme, bile acid, or viral protein.'),
('b93ae514-6324-58b9-afdf-23cdc30510a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the length of the small intestine?', '["About 3 meters","About 1 meter","About 12 meters","About 10 meters","About 6-7 meters"]'::jsonb, 'About 6-7 meters', 'In adults the small intestine is approximately 6-7 meters long, with the ileum accounting for most of its length. This large surface area supports digestion and absorption.'),
('452d6786-393e-59ee-81f6-5d63778d5a03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the length of the large intestine?', '["About 5 meters","About 3 meters","About 1.5 meters","About 2.5 meters","About 0.5 meters"]'::jsonb, 'About 1.5 meters', 'The large intestine is approximately 1.5 meters long, extending from the cecum to the anus and including the colon and rectum.'),
('cd0a627e-52d8-5bb3-8f37-a1525a95a446'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the diameter of the appendix?', '["Approximately 6 mm (similar to a pencil)","1 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","8 mm mediated by specific receptor activation","3 mm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 6 mm (similar to a pencil)', 'The appendix is a narrow blind-ended tube approximately 6 mm in diameter, similar to a pencil. Its narrow lumen is prone to obstruction by fecaliths, which may precipitate appendicitis.'),
('a124520c-8862-5332-88a5-51b8bdd027c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Meissner''s plexus?', '["The submucosal nerve plexus","The serosal plexus","The mucosal plexus","The myenteric plexus","The vascular plexus"]'::jsonb, 'The submucosal nerve plexus', 'Meissner''s plexus is the submucosal nerve plexus that lies within the submucosa and chiefly regulates mucosal secretion, absorption, and blood flow. It is distinct from the myenteric plexus, which sits between the muscle layers.'),
('4eced298-f4d3-564d-8a1f-1907ac5ce8ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is Auerbach''s plexus?', '["The mucosal plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","The myenteric plexus between circular and longitudinal muscle","The serosal plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","The perivascular plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The submucosal plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The myenteric plexus between circular and longitudinal muscle', 'Auerbach''s plexus is the myenteric plexus located between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the gut wall, where it primarily controls gastrointestinal motility.'),
('85ab1cf4-30f7-5dc6-ae98-c2f67ae2d97c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What are the two types of intestinal movements?', '["Tonic contraction only resulting in decreased functional activity","Relaxation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peristalsis only mediated by specific receptor activation","Segmentation (mixing) and peristalsis (propulsion)","Segmentation only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Segmentation (mixing) and peristalsis (propulsion)', 'Intestinal movement consists of segmentation contractions, which mix chyme and promote absorption, and peristalsis, which propels contents distally along the gut.'),
('f6804d63-ffd1-5af2-844b-9e8eeedc20fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the migrating motor complex (MMC)?', '["Postprandial peristalsis resulting in decreased functional activity","Cyclic pattern of smooth muscle contraction during fasting that sweeps debris","Tonic contractions mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphincteric contractions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Segmentation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic pattern of smooth muscle contraction during fasting that sweeps debris', 'The migrating motor complex is a cyclic pattern of smooth muscle contractions that occurs between meals during fasting and sweeps food remnants and debris toward the colon, clearing the small bowel.'),
('489ba9e5-98ca-513a-83b4-c7b51cd6f7e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the gastrocolic reflex?', '["The defecation reflex resulting in decreased functional activity","The swallowing reflex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gag reflex mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased colonic motility after gastric distension","The reflex to vomit leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased colonic motility after gastric distension', 'The gastrocolic reflex is the increase in colonic motility and the urge to defecate triggered by distension of the stomach, as typically experienced after a meal.'),
('e28f728e-2454-536b-bdbd-9687b3693c46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the defecation reflex?', '["Sensory signals from rectal distension trigger relaxation of the internal anal sphincter","Contraction of the colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Voluntary relaxation of the external sphincter mediated by specific receptor activation","Signal from the stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vomiting reflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory signals from rectal distension trigger relaxation of the internal anal sphincter', 'The defecation reflex begins when rectal distension sends afferent signals that relax the involuntary internal anal sphincter, allowing feces into the anal canal; voluntary control of the external sphincter then determines whether defecation proceeds.'),
('5e5cb197-e154-5648-be79-0be4582eb96d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the internal anal sphincter made of?', '["Fibrous tissue","Elastic tissue","Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle (voluntary)","Smooth muscle (involuntary)"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle (involuntary)', 'The internal anal sphincter is composed of smooth muscle, a thickened continuation of the circular muscle layer, and is involuntary. The external anal sphincter, in contrast, is skeletal muscle under voluntary control.'),
('8027c757-5465-517f-bc16-c134851ab921'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which option best defines the histological structure of the esophageal epithelium?', '["Stratified squamous keratinized","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Transitional","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The esophageal mucosa is lined by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, which resists abrasion from swallowed food. It is not columnar, transitional, or keratinized because the lumen remains moist.'),
('4247c123-4ad4-5188-bb0a-e7eed6850a10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'What is the histological structure of the esophageal epithelium. Which option is most accurate?', '["Stratified squamous keratinized","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Transitional","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The esophageal epithelium is stratified squamous and non-keratinized, providing protection against mechanical trauma from food passage. Keratinization would not be expected in this moist, continuously abraded but non-dry surface.'),
('1f38111d-cee1-5e1f-857a-2d113a57349c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Gastrointestinal Tract: what is the histological structure of the esophageal epithelium?', '["Stratified squamous keratinized","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Transitional","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The esophagus is lined by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, an adaptation to the friction of ingested food. Simple columnar, transitional, and pseudostratified columnar epithelia do not line the esophagus.'),
('b79b6f72-d8a6-5172-b268-0dc09c0294a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'In GI histology, which statement best fits the following fact: which structure forms the anterior boundary of the oral cavity?', '["Lips","Teeth","Palate","Tongue","Cheeks"]'::jsonb, 'Lips', 'The lips form the anterior boundary of the oral cavity. The palate forms the superior boundary, while the tongue and cheeks form the floor and lateral walls respectively.'),
('53682e9c-8b89-548f-a1ee-3b591bb46011'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Regarding GI histology, what is the correct answer to: what type of epithelium lines the oral cavity?', '["Simple columnar","Simple squamous","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Pseudostratified columnar","Stratified squamous keratinized"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The oral cavity is lined by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, which withstands the abrasion of chewing and food movement. Keratinized epithelium covers only specialized surfaces such as the hard palate and gingiva.'),
('1dadc7e4-b7ca-5587-85af-2e4e50d3f6fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'For GI histology, which option is most appropriate: which papillae of the tongue contain taste buds?', '["Fungiform and foliate resulting in decreased functional activity","Filiform accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fungiform foliate and circumvallate","Foliate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fungiform leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fungiform foliate and circumvallate', 'Taste buds are located on the fungiform, foliate, and circumvallate papillae of the tongue. Filiform papillae are the most numerous but contain no taste buds, serving only a mechanical role.'),
('d94edb27-9d57-5949-94c0-78063b85f508'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Gastrointestinal Tract', 'Which answer is correct in GI histology when asked: what is the histological structure of the esophageal epithelium?', '["Stratified squamous keratinized","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Transitional","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The esophageal epithelium is stratified squamous and non-keratinized, suited to endure friction from food passage. The other epithelial types are not found lining the esophageal lumen.'),
('3b3ac8e5-51d1-527f-ae69-f79dc6eee226'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the primary lymphoid organs?', '["Peyer''s patches and bone marrow","Tonsils and appendix","Bone marrow and thymus","Lymph nodes and spleen","Thymus and spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow and thymus', 'Primary lymphoid organs are the sites where lymphocytes develop and mature from precursor cells. The bone marrow matures B lymphocytes, while the thymus matures T lymphocytes. Secondary organs such as lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and Peyer''s patches instead house mature cells that encounter antigens.'),
('2e27f3a7-45eb-5346-b1c7-e841451c9603'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the secondary lymphoid organs?', '["tonsils","spleen","Lymph nodes","Peyer''s patches","appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph nodes', 'Secondary lymphoid organs are where mature lymphocytes encounter antigens and mount immune responses; they include lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, Peyer''s patches, and the appendix. Lymph nodes are the classic example, filtering lymph from peripheral tissues. They are distinct from the primary organs where lymphocytes mature.'),
('d64d60e8-04f7-50f7-a011-f33fbd4d68c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is MALT?', '["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","scattered lymphoid cells in mucosal surfaces","A blood cell","A type of lymphoma","A primary lymphoid organ"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue', 'MALT stands for mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, the organized lymphoid tissue found in the gut, respiratory tract, and other mucosal surfaces. The abbreviation itself expands directly to mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue. It defends mucosae against antigens and is not a blood cell, lymphoma, or primary lymphoid organ.'),
('c7c23f94-1c72-5746-bbdf-c971c7bea4c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of the thymus?', '["B cell maturation","T cell maturation and selection","Produce antibodies","Filter blood","Store lymphocytes"]'::jsonb, 'T cell maturation and selection', 'The thymus is the site where T lymphocytes mature and undergo positive and negative selection to become self-tolerant and self-MHC-restricted. B cells mature in the bone marrow, while antibody production, blood filtration, and lymphocyte storage are not thymic functions.'),
('756358c2-01a0-52ec-ab8a-d27542dae6d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Where is the thymus located?', '["In the pelvis","In the superior mediastinum","In the abdomen","In the neck","posterior to the sternum"]'::jsonb, 'In the superior mediastinum', 'The thymus lies in the superior mediastinum, anterior to the great vessels and posterior to the sternum, extending upward toward the neck in infancy. It is most prominent in childhood and progressively involutes after puberty. It is not located in the pelvis, abdomen, or neck proper.'),
('e3ea60e6-dbd9-5010-9aa9-580e4f7a9842'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are Hassall''s corpuscles?', '["Blood vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphoid follicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Concentric whorls of epithelial reticular cells in the thymic medulla","Germinal centers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nerve endings leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Concentric whorls of epithelial reticular cells in the thymic medulla', 'Hassall''s corpuscles are distinctive concentric whorls formed by epithelial reticular cells in the thymic medulla. They are a hallmark feature used to identify the thymus microscopically. They are not blood vessels, follicles, germinal centers, or nerve endings.'),
('49c0719c-d3e1-506b-b4de-09a20a33e3f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What cells form the thymic stroma?', '["macrophages","and dendritic cells","Only lymphocytes","Fibroblasts only","Epithelial reticular cells"]'::jsonb, 'Epithelial reticular cells', 'The thymic stroma is composed of epithelial reticular cells, which form a supporting meshwork, secrete factors needed for T cell development, and contribute to the blood-thymus barrier and Hassall''s corpuscles. Macrophages, dendritic cells, and lymphocytes are transient populations rather than structural stroma.'),
('405c6127-822f-5d88-a86a-49011f906bd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the blood-thymus barrier?', '["The perivascular space resulting in decreased functional activity","The basement membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A barrier in the cortex that prevents circulating antigens from entering the thymus","The epithelial cell layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The thymic capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A barrier in the cortex that prevents circulating antigens from entering the thymus', 'The blood-thymus barrier surrounds cortical capillaries and prevents circulating antigens from reaching developing T lymphocytes. It is formed by capillary endothelium, a basement membrane, and perivascular epithelial reticular cells. This isolation protects immature thymocytes during education.'),
('9490c87e-9fba-5de4-a924-8d4b8ec902b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is thymic involution?', '["Inflammation of the thymus resulting in decreased functional activity","Age-related shrinkage and replacement of thymic tissue with fat","Thymic hyperplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Thymic enlargement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thymic tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Age-related shrinkage and replacement of thymic tissue with fat', 'Thymic involution is the age-related shrinkage of the thymus, with progressive replacement of lymphoid tissue by adipose tissue. It begins after puberty and reduces the output of naive T cells. It is a normal physiological change rather than inflammation, hyperplasia, enlargement, or tumor.'),
('3d5349f9-c987-5642-80de-cfe286dfb483'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of lymph nodes?', '["present antigens","and activate lymphocytes","Produce lymph","Filter lymph","Filter blood"]'::jsonb, 'Filter lymph', 'Lymph nodes filter lymph as it flows through their sinuses, where macrophages and antigen-presenting cells trap pathogens and particulate matter. Trapped antigens are then presented to lymphocytes to initiate immune responses. Lymph nodes filter lymph, not blood, and they do not produce lymph.'),
('31a085d6-84e0-581d-81c2-618f9200beab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the outer layer of a lymph node?', '["The hilum resulting in decreased functional activity","The capsule (dense connective tissue)","The cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","The medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The subcapsular sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The capsule (dense connective tissue)', 'The capsule is the outermost layer of a lymph node, composed of dense connective tissue, and sends trabeculae inward to support the node. Beneath it lie the subcapsular sinus, cortex, and medulla. The hilum is the concave indentation where vessels enter, not the outer covering.'),
('1d0488fd-d4e7-5cfc-a5aa-3e43e9a6f34e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the three regions of a lymph node?', '["middle","Cortex","Outer","medulla","paracortex"]'::jsonb, 'Cortex', 'A lymph node has three main regions: the outer cortex containing B cell follicles, the deeper paracortex rich in T cells, and the inner medulla with cords and sinuses. Among the listed structures, the cortex is the outermost of these three zones. The listed options correspond to the components being tested.'),
('97acb22a-7e63-5b57-a9ec-cdfae3fbb146'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What cells are found in the lymphoid follicles?', '["B lymphocytes","T lymphocytes only","and macrophages","follicular dendritic cells","Plasma cells only"]'::jsonb, 'B lymphocytes', 'Lymphoid follicles are the B cell regions of lymph nodes and other secondary lymphoid organs, containing follicular dendritic cells as well. Naive B cells populate these follicles and form germinal centers after antigen stimulation. B lymphocytes are the characteristic and predominant population.'),
('e405fed0-a41d-584d-8ff9-bb0f4f437c0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is a germinal center?', '["The center of a lymph node resulting in decreased functional activity","The site of B cell proliferation and affinity maturation within a secondary follicle","The center of the thymus mediated by specific receptor activation","The site of plasma cell formation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The site of T cell maturation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The site of B cell proliferation and affinity maturation within a secondary follicle', 'A germinal center develops within a secondary lymphoid follicle during an active immune response, where B cells proliferate and undergo somatic hypermutation and affinity maturation. This process is aided by follicular dendritic cells and T follicular helper cells. Plasma cell differentiation occurs after germinal center exit.'),
('8e83e034-f4d3-528e-82e0-a73c52888c1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What cells are in the germinal center?', '["and tingible body macrophages","Only T cells","Centrocytes","follicular dendritic cells","centroblasts"]'::jsonb, 'Centrocytes', 'Germinal centers contain two zones of dividing B cells: centroblasts in the dark zone and centrocytes in the light zone. Centrocytes are the small B cells that undergo selection for high-affinity antigen binding. Tingible body macrophages and follicular dendritic cells are accessory cells rather than the defining population.'),
('d74ed1da-ae11-55fe-85e1-22aa559a12b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the paracortex of a lymph node?', '["The B cell zone resulting in decreased functional activity","The T cell zone between cortical follicles and medulla","The medullary cords mediated by specific receptor activation","The capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hilum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The T cell zone between cortical follicles and medulla', 'The paracortex lies between the cortical follicles and the medulla of a lymph node and is the T cell-dependent zone. It contains high endothelial venules through which naive lymphocytes enter from blood. B cells populate follicles, while the medullary cords, capsule, and hilum are separate structures.'),
('9c8982c1-98aa-57d5-90e2-60fdc139d7e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are high endothelial venules (HEVs)?', '["Lymphatic capillaries resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinusoids in the medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arteries in the lymph node mediated by specific receptor activation","Veins in the capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Specialized postcapillary venules in the paracortex where lymphocytes enter from blood"]'::jsonb, 'Specialized postcapillary venules in the paracortex where lymphocytes enter from blood', 'High endothelial venules are specialized postcapillary venules in the paracortex lined by plump cuboidal endothelium. Their adhesion molecules and chemokines allow circulating naive lymphocytes to bind and migrate into the node. They are not lymphatics, sinusoids, arteries, or capsular veins.'),
('c0f3b91c-3779-5114-a56e-86e179b671a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the medulla of a lymph node?', '["The outer region resulting in decreased functional activity","The cortical region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inner region with medullary cords (plasma cells) and medullary sinuses","The follicular region without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The paracortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inner region with medullary cords (plasma cells) and medullary sinuses', 'The medulla is the inner region of a lymph node formed by medullary cords packed with lymphocytes and plasma cells, separated by medullary sinuses. Plasma cells here secrete antibodies that drain into efferent lymph. The cortex, follicles, and paracortex lie more peripherally.'),
('5c185a61-7070-53be-aef3-588fb45cea6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the path of lymph through a lymph node?', '["Directly to the hilum resulting in decreased functional activity","Subcapsular sinus → medulla → hilum only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Afferent → medulla → cortex → efferent mediated by specific receptor activation","Afferent → subcapsular sinus → cortex → medullary sinuses → efferent at hilum","Efferent → medulla → cortex → afferent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Afferent → subcapsular sinus → cortex → medullary sinuses → efferent at hilum', 'Afferent lymphatic vessels deliver lymph to the subcapsular sinus on the convex surface of the node. The lymph then percolates through the cortex, paracortex, and medullary sinuses before leaving through efferent vessels at the hilum. This slow flow maximizes contact between antigens and immune cells.'),
('7f4e5507-5c9e-58bd-9b01-b1b1bd027b99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of the spleen?', '["Filters blood","and initiates immune responses to blood-borne antigens","stores platelets","Filters lymph","removes old RBCs"]'::jsonb, 'Filters blood', 'The spleen is the only secondary lymphoid organ that filters blood rather than lymph, removing old red blood cells and blood-borne pathogens while initiating immune responses to blood-borne antigens. Platelet storage and removal of aged RBCs are red pulp activities performed as part of blood filtration.'),
('9064f2ba-a742-5931-a235-1b76fb128037'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the spleen covered by?', '["Transitional epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Serosa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adventitia mediated by specific receptor activation","A fibroelastic capsule with smooth muscle","Mucosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibroelastic capsule with smooth muscle', 'The spleen is enclosed by a fibroelastic capsule containing scattered smooth muscle cells, which send trabeculae into the parenchyma. This capsule gives the spleen its elastic contractile behavior. Serosa, adventitia, mucosa, and transitional epithelium do not describe the splenic capsule.'),
('b48c54b2-5cc4-57ea-a687-922826e2f080'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the two main compartments of the spleen?', '["White pulp and red pulp","Sinuses and cords","Follicles and cords","Cortex and medulla","T and B zones"]'::jsonb, 'White pulp and red pulp', 'The spleen is divided into white pulp, the lymphatic tissue arranged around central arterioles, and red pulp, the blood-filtering cords and sinuses. Cortex and medulla describe lymph nodes, and T and B zones are not the overall splenic compartments. These two types of pulp define splenic structure.'),
('75a774eb-bb78-5b95-85ea-7542e3200ba9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the white pulp of the spleen?', '["The red pulp resulting in decreased functional activity","The venous sinuses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The splenic cords mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatic tissue surrounding the central arteriole (periarteriolar lymphoid sheaths)","The capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic tissue surrounding the central arteriole (periarteriolar lymphoid sheaths)', 'White pulp consists of lymphatic tissue that surrounds the central arterioles of the spleen, forming periarteriolar lymphoid sheaths and lymphoid follicles. It is where immune responses to blood-borne antigens begin. Red pulp, venous sinuses, and splenic cords are the blood-filtering compartments.'),
('3840b2e7-bfb8-5130-90fb-e338f623b586'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is a PALS (periarteriolar lymphoid sheath)?', '["A germinal center resulting in decreased functional activity","AB cell follicle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A sheath of T cells surrounding the central arteriole in splenic white pulp","A sinusoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A red pulp cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A sheath of T cells surrounding the central arteriole in splenic white pulp', 'A periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (PALS) is a cylindrical sleeve of T lymphocytes surrounding the central arteriole in splenic white pulp. B cells form follicles at the periphery of the PALS. Germinal centers, sinusoids, and red pulp cords are separate splenic components.'),
('7b01b9ce-d88f-5925-b1ec-4ad5d50e425b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the marginal zone of the spleen?', '["The capsule resulting in decreased functional activity","The red pulp accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The area between white pulp and red pulp containing specific B cells and macrophages","The germinal center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The PALS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The area between white pulp and red pulp containing specific B cells and macrophages', 'The marginal zone is the transition zone between the white pulp and red pulp of the spleen, containing distinctive marginal zone B cells and macrophages. It is the first site where blood-borne antigens are trapped. It is neither the capsule, germinal center, PALS, nor red pulp itself.'),
('f12b9a4d-aab2-5417-86a3-6d4093549469'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the red pulp of the spleen?', '["The trabeculae resulting in decreased functional activity","The germinal centers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lymphoid compartment mediated by specific receptor activation","The blood-filtering compartment containing splenic cords (of Billroth) and sinuses","The capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The blood-filtering compartment containing splenic cords (of Billroth) and sinuses', 'Red pulp is the blood-filtering compartment of the spleen, composed of splenic cords (of Billroth) and venous sinuses. Here old and abnormal red blood cells are removed and iron is recycled. White pulp handles immune functions, while trabeculae and the capsule are supporting structures.'),
('d4436b09-8f9a-5d1e-8d02-9e858a236c78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the splenic cords (of Billroth)?', '["Blood vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Reticular cell meshworks in red pulp containing RBCs","macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation","and plasma cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trabeculae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reticular cell meshworks in red pulp containing RBCs', 'Splenic cords of Billroth are meshworks of reticular cells and fibers in the red pulp that contain red blood cells, macrophages, and other blood cells. Blood percolates slowly through them, allowing macrophages to remove aged erythrocytes. They are not vessels or trabeculae.'),
('acd3a186-7796-512f-8802-2397571a1ece'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the splenic sinus?', '["Nerve fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Venous sinusoids with fenestrated endothelium lining the red pulp","Lymphatic vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Bile ducts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arteries in the spleen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Venous sinusoids with fenestrated endothelium lining the red pulp', 'Splenic sinuses are venous sinusoids lined by longitudinally arranged fenestrated endothelial cells with a discontinuous basement membrane. This structure allows blood cells to pass from the cords into the sinus lumen. They are venous channels, not arteries, lymphatics, or bile ducts.'),
('cc8371db-25e2-513d-9cbd-3c340758f370'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of splenic macrophages?', '["Remove old RBCs","Produce antibodies","and phagocytose blood-borne pathogens","recycle iron","Secrete hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Remove old RBCs', 'Splenic macrophages in the red pulp phagocytose old or damaged red blood cells and blood-borne pathogens as blood filters through the cords. They also recycle iron from degraded hemoglobin. Antibody production and hormone secretion are not their functions.'),
('17679d61-37d4-5417-82a3-5ba3e43eabdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the three types of tonsils?', '["Cervical","pharyngeal","Palatine","lingual","axillary"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine', 'The three tonsils are the palatine, pharyngeal, and lingual tonsils, which form a ring of lymphoid tissue at the entrance to the pharynx. Among the listed choices, the palatine tonsils are the paired structures of the oropharynx. Cervical and axillary are not tonsillar types.'),
('ab8ff93c-d28a-5b20-8641-ce3474e638fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the structure of the palatine tonsil?', '["Fibrous tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphoid tissue with crypts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphoid tissue with villi mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple columnar epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphoid follicles covered by stratified squamous epithelium with crypts"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphoid follicles covered by stratified squamous epithelium with crypts', 'The palatine tonsil consists of lymphoid follicles underlying stratified squamous epithelium that dips into deep crypts. The crypts greatly increase the surface area exposed to antigens. Lymphoid tissue with villi and simple columnar epithelium do not describe tonsils.'),
('b6b09021-e835-518d-a8de-a745113391e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the pharyngeal tonsil (adenoid)?', '["A lingual tonsil resulting in decreased functional activity","A lymph node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lymphoid mass in the nasopharynx covered by pseudostratified columnar epithelium","A palatine tonsil without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A salivary gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A lymphoid mass in the nasopharynx covered by pseudostratified columnar epithelium', 'The pharyngeal tonsil, or adenoid, is a lymphoid mass in the nasopharynx covered by pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory) epithelium. It can enlarge in children and obstruct the nasopharynx. Palatine and lingual tonsils, lymph nodes, and salivary glands are distinct structures.'),
('a32d59f0-8e52-5cea-8cb0-5d0ac2ce2b8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the lingual tonsil?', '["Muscles of the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste buds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aggregates of lymphoid follicles at the base of the tongue","Foliate papillae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Salivary glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aggregates of lymphoid follicles at the base of the tongue', 'The lingual tonsil is formed by aggregates of lymphoid follicles beneath the mucosa at the base of the tongue, covered by stratified squamous epithelium. It is part of the tonsillar ring guarding the pharyngeal entrance. Taste buds, papillae, and salivary glands are separate structures.'),
('b8c90742-b088-5e02-8ff0-cd1a70e5d7b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the appendix''s role in the immune system?', '["Contains abundant lymphoid tissue for immune surveillance of gut bacteria","Only absorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only digestive function mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hormone production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","immune function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contains abundant lymphoid tissue for immune surveillance of gut bacteria', 'The appendix is rich in lymphoid tissue, including follicles and Peyer''s patch-like aggregates, that monitors the commensal bacteria of the gut and maintains the intestinal microbiome. It has no significant digestive, absorptive, or hormonal function.'),
('2ee35dd1-4061-5ee3-9ffb-0b58a6250f3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of M cells in Peyer''s patches?', '["Digest food resulting in decreased functional activity","Produce hormones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secrete mucus mediated by specific receptor activation","Absorb nutrients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sample antigens from the intestinal lumen and transport them to immune cells"]'::jsonb, 'Sample antigens from the intestinal lumen and transport them to immune cells', 'M (microfold) cells are specialized epithelial cells overlying Peyer''s patches that sample antigens from the intestinal lumen. They endocytose and transport these antigens to underlying lymphocytes and antigen-presenting cells, initiating gut immune responses. They do not digest food, secrete mucus, absorb nutrients, or produce hormones.'),
('d0a03565-6f39-5674-ba5c-2923b691de97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are bronchus-associated lymphoid tissue (BALT)?', '["especially at bronchial bifurcations","Thymic tissue","Lymph nodes in the lung","Splenic tissue","Lymphoid aggregates in the respiratory tract"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphoid aggregates in the respiratory tract', 'Bronchus-associated lymphoid tissue (BALT) consists of organized aggregates of lymphoid cells in the walls of bronchi, especially at bifurcations, that respond to inhaled antigens. It is part of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue. It is not thymic tissue, splenic tissue, or the lymph nodes of the lung.'),
('c269b628-09bb-5e94-b35c-1409b621c76f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the skin-associated lymphoid tissue (SALT)?', '["and lymphocytes in the skin","Langerhans cells","dermal dendritic cells","Lymph nodes in the skin","Sweat glands"]'::jsonb, 'Langerhans cells', 'Skin-associated lymphoid tissue (SALT) includes Langerhans cells, dermal dendritic cells, and intraepidermal T lymphocytes that protect the skin. Langerhans cells are the antigen-presenting dendritic cells of the epidermis and the best-known component of SALT. Sweat glands and lymph nodes are not part of it.'),
('f36a3eea-810a-51c1-a99b-edb9d43b73b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of Langerhans cells?', '["Antigen capture in the skin and migration to lymph nodes for T cell presentation","Synthesize keratin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produce melanin mediated by specific receptor activation","Secrete sweat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contract to raise hair leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antigen capture in the skin and migration to lymph nodes for T cell presentation', 'Langerhans cells are epidermal dendritic cells that capture antigens in the skin and then migrate via lymphatics to draining lymph nodes. There they process and present antigen to T cells to initiate immunity. They do not produce keratin, melanin, sweat, or contract muscles.'),
('3c226071-6fbf-5020-bf2d-e5cc1d558dde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Where do B lymphocytes mature?', '["In the liver","In the thymus","In the bone marrow","In the spleen","In lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'In the bone marrow', 'B lymphocytes mature in the bone marrow, where they undergo immunoglobulin gene rearrangement and selection against self-antigens. From the marrow they enter the circulation to populate secondary lymphoid organs. The thymus matures T cells, not B cells.'),
('616ff26e-101f-5bc5-b49d-81bce62bb8e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Where do T lymphocytes mature?', '["In the bone marrow","In the lymph nodes","In the thymus","In the spleen","In the liver"]'::jsonb, 'In the thymus', 'T lymphocytes mature in the thymus, where they undergo positive and negative selection to acquire self-MHC restriction and central tolerance. They then emigrate to secondary lymphoid organs. B cells, in contrast, mature in the bone marrow.'),
('e73b2c54-7054-5e63-bd37-c789753b63b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is positive selection in T cell development?', '["Selection of T cells that recognize self-MHC molecules in the thymic cortex","Selection of macrophages accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elimination of self-reactive T cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Selection of NK cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Selection of B cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Selection of T cells that recognize self-MHC molecules in the thymic cortex', 'Positive selection occurs in the thymic cortex, where cortical epithelial cells present self-MHC molecules and only T cells whose receptors bind them survive. This ensures T cells recognize antigen only in the context of self-MHC. Negative selection later removes self-reactive cells.'),
('49c80485-5079-5404-8f3b-a7c1d7080a64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is negative selection in T cell development?', '["Selection of gamma-delta T cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Selection of NK cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elimination of T cells with high affinity for self-peptides in the medulla","Selection of self-MHC reactive T cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Selection of B cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elimination of T cells with high affinity for self-peptides in the medulla', 'Negative selection occurs mainly in the thymic medulla, where medullary epithelial cells and dendritic cells present self-peptides and T cells with high-affinity binding are eliminated by apoptosis. This produces central tolerance. It removes self-reactive T cells rather than selecting cells that recognize self-MHC.'),
('c09c1c3e-2790-532f-ab0b-44d8a1faf767'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are AIRE (autoimmune regulator) and its role?', '["An antibody resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A transcription factor in thymic medullary epithelial cells that expresses tissue-specific antigens for negative selection","AT cell receptor mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A cytokine without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A complement protein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A transcription factor in thymic medullary epithelial cells that expresses tissue-specific antigens for negative selection', 'AIRE (autoimmune regulator) is a transcription factor expressed by thymic medullary epithelial cells that drives expression of tissue-specific antigens. These self-antigens are presented to developing T cells for negative selection, eliminating self-reactive clones. Loss of AIRE causes autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome.'),
('1c3a2aa7-f0eb-5e4d-a069-ad46ac0c1498'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are naive lymphocytes?', '["Memory lymphocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Apoptotic lymphocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activated lymphocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Mature lymphocytes that have not yet encountered their specific antigen","Effector lymphocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mature lymphocytes that have not yet encountered their specific antigen', 'Naive lymphocytes are mature cells that have passed selection in primary lymphoid organs but have not yet met their specific antigen. They recirculate through blood and secondary lymphoid organs seeking that antigen. After activation they differentiate into effector or memory cells.'),
('4673ffc7-7b28-597b-aa62-d2411e48d26e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is lymphocyte recirculation?', '["Migration only to bone marrow resulting in decreased functional activity","One-way migration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Migration only to the thymus mediated by specific receptor activation","movement after maturation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The constant migration of naive lymphocytes from blood through lymphoid tissues and back to blood"]'::jsonb, 'The constant migration of naive lymphocytes from blood through lymphoid tissues and back to blood', 'Lymphocyte recirculation is the continuous traffic of naive lymphocytes from blood through lymph nodes and other secondary lymphoid organs via high endothelial venules, and back to blood through efferent lymphatics. This surveillance maximizes the chance of encountering their specific antigen. It is not one-way or restricted to a single organ.'),
('58e3f33c-c502-5e04-b273-282c8f03e8c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the lifespan of a naive lymphocyte?', '["Weeks to months","Hours","Years","Does not survive","Days"]'::jsonb, 'Weeks to months', 'Naive lymphocytes that never encounter their antigen survive for weeks to months before dying by apoptosis. This limited lifespan reflects their dependence on continuous survival signals. Memory lymphocytes, in contrast, live far longer.'),
('1a9234cb-4730-566c-a474-8a9dc016b78a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the lifespan of a memory lymphocyte?', '["Hours","Weeks","Days","Minutes","Years to decades"]'::jsonb, 'Years to decades', 'Memory lymphocytes survive for years to decades because they have been activated and are maintained by low-level homeostatic signals. They enable rapid, robust responses upon re-exposure to the same antigen. Their lifespan far exceeds that of short-lived effector or naive cells.'),
('068bbe3d-511c-5df7-a2d0-fbf51bcf4b18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of plasma cells?', '["Secrete cytokines resulting in decreased functional activity","Present antigens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Directly kill infected cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Phagocytose pathogens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produce and secrete large quantities of antibodies"]'::jsonb, 'Produce and secrete large quantities of antibodies', 'Plasma cells are terminally differentiated B cells specialized for producing and secreting large quantities of antibodies, with abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum for immunoglobulin synthesis. Antigen presentation, phagocytosis, direct killing, and cytokine secretion are functions of other leukocytes.'),
('857b1311-26d7-516a-9378-0704df3fcb78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the structure of the lymph node hilum?', '["The convex surface resulting in decreased functional activity","The concave region where blood vessels and efferent lymphatics exit","The medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","The capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The concave region where blood vessels and efferent lymphatics exit', 'The hilum is the concave, indented region of a lymph node where blood vessels enter and efferent lymphatic vessels leave. Afferent lymphatics enter on the convex surface. The cortex, medulla, and capsule occupy other areas of the node.'),
('e2bdd563-27d5-51a2-8fa0-cff861923096'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the subcapsular sinus of a lymph node?', '["The germinal center resulting in decreased functional activity","The cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A space beneath the capsule where afferent lymph first enters","The medullary sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The paracortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A space beneath the capsule where afferent lymph first enters', 'The subcapsular sinus is a channel just beneath the capsule of a lymph node into which afferent lymphatic vessels deliver lymph. From here lymph flows into cortical and medullary sinuses. It is the first channel of lymph through the node.'),
('b1c8e4e7-c0f1-5395-a32f-ffd18aa0f3c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the reticular fibers in lymphoid organs?', '["Type II collagen resulting in decreased functional activity","Microfibrils accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type I collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Elastic fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type III collagen fibers that form a supporting scaffold"]'::jsonb, 'Type III collagen fibers that form a supporting scaffold', 'Reticular fibers are made of Type III collagen and form a fine meshwork that supports the cellular compartments of lymph nodes, spleen, and lymphoid nodules. Type I and Type II collagens, elastic fibers, and microfibrils are not the structural framework of lymphoid organs.'),
('a4507bb3-8f3a-56d1-b951-9ec60c869aa0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are fibroblastic reticular cells (FRCs)?', '["Fibroblasts in the capsule resulting in decreased functional activity","Specialized cells that produce reticular fibers and guide lymphocyte migration in lymph nodes","Dendritic cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Endothelial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth muscle cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Specialized cells that produce reticular fibers and guide lymphocyte migration in lymph nodes', 'Fibroblastic reticular cells secrete the reticular fiber network of lymph nodes and form the stromal tracks along which lymphocytes migrate. Capsular fibroblasts, dendritic cells, endothelial cells, and smooth muscle cells do not build the nodal fiber-mesh stroma.'),
('fa08a4f4-ab1a-5002-8a02-bce8921837b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the conduit system in lymph nodes?', '["The nerve fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","The blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lymphatic sinuses mediated by specific receptor activation","The trabeculae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A network of channels within reticular fibers that rapidly transport small molecules from sinuses to HEVs"]'::jsonb, 'A network of channels within reticular fibers that rapidly transport small molecules from sinuses to HEVs', 'The conduit system is a channel network within the reticular fiber web that rapidly carries small molecules and antigens from the subcapsular sinus to the high endothelial venules. Nerves, blood vessels, trabeculae, and the lymphatic sinuses themselves are distinct nodal elements.'),
('7b1a57ad-0a3c-5f7c-ba94-b4e4711f79f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of follicular dendritic cells (FDCs)?', '["Secrete histamine resulting in decreased functional activity","Phagocytose bacteria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Present unprocessed antigens to B cells in follicles and maintain germinal centers","Present processed antigens to T cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produce antibodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Present unprocessed antigens to B cells in follicles and maintain germinal centers', 'Follicular dendritic cells hold unprocessed antigen in immune complexes on their surface and present it to B cells within follicles, helping maintain germinal centers. They do not phagocytose, process antigen for T cells, make antibodies, or secrete histamine.'),
('aab60374-a671-5a83-9137-1959fdc89992'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of interdigitating dendritic cells (IDCs)?', '["Phagocytose debris resulting in decreased functional activity","Produce antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secrete cytokines mediated by specific receptor activation","Present processed antigens to T cells in the paracortex","Present antigens to B cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Present processed antigens to T cells in the paracortex', 'Interdigitating dendritic cells are professional antigen-presenting cells of the paracortex that display processed, MHC-bound antigen to T cells. Their function is distinct from antibody production, bulk phagocytosis, and presentation to B cells.'),
('0a637c07-a862-50bc-86ab-fa5122511037'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are tingible body macrophages?', '["Macrophages in the red pulp resulting in decreased functional activity","Macrophages in germinal centers that phagocytose apoptotic B cells","Macrophages in sinuses mediated by specific receptor activation","Macrophages in the thymus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Macrophages in the bone marrow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Macrophages in germinal centers that phagocytose apoptotic B cells', 'Tingible body macrophages reside within germinal centers and ingest the debris of apoptotic B cells, accounting for their ''starry sky'' appearance. They are not the resident macrophages of the red pulp, sinuses, thymus, or bone marrow.'),
('3e5f4861-3578-598e-bca1-62aafd7d1ebc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are sinus histiocytes?', '["Plasma cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Dendritic cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages lining the lymphatic sinuses of lymph nodes","Epithelial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Macrophages lining the lymphatic sinuses of lymph nodes', 'Sinus histiocytes are the macrophages that line the lymphatic (medullary) sinuses of lymph nodes and clear particulate material from the lymph. Plasma cells, dendritic cells, epithelial cells, and lymphocytes do not line these sinuses.'),
('075745e5-a4a3-553f-b350-d93ad251bc97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the structure of a splenic trabecula?', '["Nerve bundles resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphatic vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth muscle bands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connective tissue septa extending from the capsule into the splenic parenchyma"]'::jsonb, 'Connective tissue septa extending from the capsule into the splenic parenchyma', 'Trabeculae are connective tissue septa that extend inward from the splenic capsule into the parenchyma and carry the trabecular vessels that later branch into the pulp. They are not nerves, vessels, lymphatics, or smooth muscle bands in themselves.'),
('817397ee-d476-5e28-aa8b-cffdc4cd9603'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the central arteriole of the spleen?', '["The central vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The arteriole that runs through the white pulp and is surrounded by PALS","The sinusoidal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The main splenic artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The trabecular artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The arteriole that runs through the white pulp and is surrounded by PALS', 'The central arteriole is a branch of the trabecular artery that travels through the white pulp wrapped in the periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (PALS). It is an arteriole within the pulp, not the central vein, sinusoidal artery, main splenic artery, or trabecular artery.'),
('9a1754d3-778e-5608-a7f5-f3925928d107'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the splenic circulation path?', '["Through the hilum only resulting in decreased functional activity","Splenic artery → trabecular artery → central arteriole → penicillar arterioles → sinuses or cords","Splenic vein → artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Directly to sinuses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery → trabecular artery → central arteriole → penicillar arterioles → sinuses or cords', 'Splenic blood flows from the splenic artery into trabecular arteries, then through central arterioles and penicillar arterioles before reaching the sinuses or cords. The other options give incomplete or reversed routes.'),
('2a74d2ae-0e19-5499-9535-69c9ff7dd73d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are penicillar arterioles?', '["The terminal branches of central arterioles in the red pulp","The lymphatic vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The veins in the trabeculae mediated by specific receptor activation","The arteries in the capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The nerve fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The terminal branches of central arterioles in the red pulp', 'Penicillar arterioles are the final divisions of the central arteriole located in the red pulp, where some become sheathed by macrophages. They are not part of the lymphatics, trabecular veins, capsular arteries, or nerve supply.'),
('f0cef998-8547-5741-a672-00071e947b94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are sheathed capillaries?', '["Capillaries in the capsule resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphatic capillaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Venous sinusoids mediated by specific receptor activation","Terminal arterioles surrounded by a sheath of macrophages in the red pulp","Capillaries in the white pulp leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Terminal arterioles surrounded by a sheath of macrophages in the red pulp', 'Sheathed capillaries are terminal arterioles in the red pulp encased in a sleeve of macrophages and reticular cells. They are not ordinary or lymphatic capillaries, venous sinusoids, or white pulp capillaries.'),
('49363995-58b2-561b-a2ee-7a4929b4cd0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the open circulation of the spleen?', '["Blood flows directly from arterioles to sinuses","Blood flows through continuous capillaries","Blood bypasses the spleen","then percolates into sinuses through slits","Blood flows from arterioles into splenic cords"]'::jsonb, 'Blood flows from arterioles into splenic cords', 'In the open circulation, blood empties from terminal arterioles into the splenic cords and only then percolates through slit-like gaps into the venous sinuses. A direct arteriole-to-sinusus continuous route describes the closed circulation instead.'),
('934c5ed2-4019-54f4-a109-d134b0a18d57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the closed circulation of the spleen?', '["Blood flows into the cords resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood flows through lymphatics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood bypasses the spleen mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood flows directly from arterioles to sinuses via continuous endothelium","Blood flows through the capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blood flows directly from arterioles to sinuses via continuous endothelium', 'In the closed circulation, blood passes directly from arterial capillaries into venous sinuses through a continuous endothelium without entering the cords. Flow through the cords, lymphatics, capsule, or bypassing the spleen are not this route.'),
('da66bb2b-1f9c-50c1-ad8a-229e02b3f70d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the splenic cord blood filtration?', '["Blood in the cords is filtered by macrophages that remove old RBCs","Blood is filtered in the sinuses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood is filtered in the white pulp mediated by specific receptor activation","and pathogens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","particles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blood in the cords is filtered by macrophages that remove old RBCs', 'As blood slowly percolates through the splenic cords, resident macrophages engulf old, damaged, or rigid red cells and debris. Filtration is not carried out by the white pulp or the sinus lining but by the macrophages inside the cords.'),
('8194c6c4-4908-56c2-ab60-e5f87371cbfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is extramedullary hematopoiesis?', '["Hematopoiesis in lymph nodes","often in the spleen or liver","Blood cell formation outside the bone marrow","Hematopoiesis in the thymus","Hematopoiesis in the bone marrow"]'::jsonb, 'Blood cell formation outside the bone marrow', 'Extramedullary hematopoiesis is the production of blood cells outside the marrow, occurring normally in fetal life and reappearing in the liver and spleen when the marrow fails. It is by definition not marrow, lymph node, or thymus hematopoiesis.'),
('a38f1e18-08f5-5cf1-a667-7c1a1d351ae6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the bone marrow?', '["The endosteum resulting in decreased functional activity","The soft tissue in medullary cavities of bones where hematopoiesis occurs","The periosteum mediated by specific receptor activation","The joint cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hard outer part of bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The soft tissue in medullary cavities of bones where hematopoiesis occurs', 'Bone marrow is the soft tissue occupying the medullary cavities of bones and is the primary site of hematopoiesis in the adult. The endosteum, periosteum, joint cartilage, and compact bone are separate skeletal tissues.'),
('72216797-d4bc-53aa-8c15-1ea1a1b9e48a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the two types of bone marrow?', '["Compact and spongy","Red (hematopoietic) and yellow (fatty)","Cortical and medullary","Active and inactive","Primary and secondary"]'::jsonb, 'Red (hematopoietic) and yellow (fatty)', 'The two functional types of marrow are red marrow, which is active in blood cell formation, and yellow marrow, which is fatty and largely inactive. Compact/spongy and cortical/medullary describe bone tissue, not marrow types.'),
('8f283eb6-9c02-5850-a7ab-1fb072a8fc26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Where is red marrow found in adults?', '["skull resulting in decreased functional activity","sternum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","pelvis mediated by specific receptor activation","ribs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Axial skeleton: vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'Axial skeleton: vertebrae', 'In the adult, active red marrow is retained mainly in the axial skeleton, including the vertebrae, sternum, ribs, skull, and pelvis, while the shafts of long bones become yellow marrow. The single option naming the axial skeleton as a whole covers the full distribution.'),
('2f235daa-5025-5c84-9a59-d24a75284635'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs)?', '["Stromal cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Mature blood cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Committed progenitor cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Self-renewing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","multipotent stem cells that give rise to all blood cell lineages"]'::jsonb, 'multipotent stem cells that give rise to all blood cell lineages', 'Hematopoietic stem cells are multipotent marrow cells that self-renew and give rise to every blood cell lineage. The option stating multipotency and formation of all lineages captures this identity; self-renewal alone describes only one of their two defining properties.'),
('0cccce8f-622b-50b8-9d47-92187d183bf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the bone marrow stroma?', '["adipocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","fibroblasts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The supporting microenvironment of reticular cells","and vasculature without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hematopoietic cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The supporting microenvironment of reticular cells', 'The marrow stroma is the supporting framework built from reticular cells, adipocytes, fibroblasts, and vasculature that anchors and nurtures the blood-forming population. Hematopoietic cells themselves are the parenchyma, not the stroma.'),
('27627231-d232-5a76-bd0a-cc8c91d935f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the hematopoietic niche?', '["Specific microenvironments that maintain and regulate HSCs","The blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bone cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","The periosteum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central marrow cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Specific microenvironments that maintain and regulate HSCs', 'The hematopoietic niche denotes the specialized marrow microenvironments, notably the endosteal and perivascular niches, that keep HSCs in a quiescent, self-renewing state. Vessels, cortex, periosteum, and the cavity alone do not constitute this functional niche.'),
('64ef7952-02c3-5129-bbbb-9a2c67613bb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is megakaryopoiesis?', '["Monocyte formation resulting in decreased functional activity","The formation of megakaryocytes that produce platelets","White blood cell formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Red blood cell formation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphocyte formation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The formation of megakaryocytes that produce platelets', 'Megakaryopoiesis is the lineage that produces megakaryocytes, which shed platelets by fragmentation of their cytoplasm. It does not generate monocytes, leukocytes, erythrocytes, or lymphocytes.'),
('4262c5a8-5e92-561c-b7f8-e8fbb176ce7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is erythropoiesis?', '["Platelet production","Lymphocyte production","White blood cell production","The production of red blood cells (erythrocytes)","Monocyte production"]'::jsonb, 'The production of red blood cells (erythrocytes)', 'Erythropoiesis is the formation of red blood cells (erythrocytes) from erythroid progenitors, promoted by erythropoietin in the marrow. Platelet, leukocyte, and monocyte formation are separate lineages.'),
('002f09ce-ea08-57fa-9445-5135b7f39764'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are neutrophils?', '["Platelets","Red blood cells","The least abundant white blood cells","first responders to bacterial infection","The most abundant white blood cells (50-70%)"]'::jsonb, 'The most abundant white blood cells (50-70%)', 'Neutrophils are the most abundant circulating leukocytes, comprising 50-70% of white blood cells, and act as first responders against bacterial infection. The ''least abundant'' description belongs to basophils, and platelets and red cells are not leukocytes.'),
('a120b17c-b03b-5c89-8f39-0686981aeceb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the appearance of a neutrophil?', '["Round nucleus with dark blue granules","Multilobed nucleus (3-5 lobes) with pale pink cytoplasm and fine granules","U-shaped nucleus basophilic cytoplasm","Large round nucleus with granules","Bilobed nucleus with large red granules"]'::jsonb, 'Multilobed nucleus (3-5 lobes) with pale pink cytoplasm and fine granules', 'A mature neutrophil shows a nucleus divided into 3-5 lobes, pale pink cytoplasm, and fine granules. A bilobed nucleus with large red granules marks eosinophils, coarse dark granules mark basophils, and a large round or U-shaped nucleus is seen in other cells.'),
('6a9ccd75-b5c8-53e0-99bb-fb2253200472'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are eosinophils?', '["Granulocytes that defend against parasites and modulate allergic inflammation","Antigen-presenting cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antibody-producing cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Virus-fighting cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacteria-fighting cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Granulocytes that defend against parasites and modulate allergic inflammation', 'Eosinophils are granulocytes whose granules combat helminth parasites and help regulate allergic inflammation. They do not present antigen, produce antibody, or serve as the main antibacterial or antiviral effectors.'),
('8bef4b67-0f70-5baf-859c-30247cf68dbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are basophils?', '["Granulocytes that release histamine and heparin","Bacteria-fighting cells","Antigen-presenting cells","Antibody-producing cells","involved in allergic reactions"]'::jsonb, 'Granulocytes that release histamine and heparin', 'Basophils are granulocytes that release histamine and heparin from their metachromatic granules, notably during type I hypersensitivity. The other options describe different leukocyte functions rather than the basophil''s granule content.'),
('dcc10cfc-9ae5-5087-bf21-e21524fca2d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are mast cells?', '["Circulating basophils resulting in decreased functional activity","involved in allergies and anaphylaxis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tissue-resident cells with basophil-like granules","Eosinophils without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neutrophils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue-resident cells with basophil-like granules', 'Mast cells are tissue-resident cells whose metachromatic, basophil-like granules release histamine and heparin, driving allergic and anaphylactic responses. Unlike basophils, they mature and persist in tissues; eosinophils and neutrophils are separate granulocytes.'),
('fc4f673c-1f22-5447-b19d-edd85edce7c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are monocytes?', '["Lymphocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Circulating precursor cells that migrate into tissues to become macrophages or dendritic cells","Granulocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Dendritic cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mature tissue macrophages leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Circulating precursor cells that migrate into tissues to become macrophages or dendritic cells', 'Monocytes are circulating leukocytes that leave the blood and differentiate into macrophages or dendritic cells in tissues. They are not granulocytes, lymphocytes, mature macrophages, or resident dendritic cells themselves.'),
('b8c765c6-f871-558b-9bf9-0569978d7a24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are macrophages?', '["Large phagocytic cells in tissues derived from monocytes","Circulating monocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","involved in innate immunity and tissue homeostasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neutrophils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large phagocytic cells in tissues derived from monocytes', 'Macrophages are large tissue cells derived from circulating monocytes that phagocytose pathogens and debris, participating in innate immunity and tissue homeostasis. Circulating monocytes are their precursors; lymphocytes and neutrophils are separate leukocyte classes.'),
('d92207ef-e5f9-5df4-b7cc-45b945a7effb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are alveolar macrophages?', '["Dendritic cells of the lung resulting in decreased functional activity","Type I pneumocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type II pneumocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Macrophages in the interstitium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Macrophages in the alveolar spaces that phagocytose inhaled particles and pathogens"]'::jsonb, 'Macrophages in the alveolar spaces that phagocytose inhaled particles and pathogens', 'Alveolar macrophages are the phagocytes that dwell in the alveolar spaces and ingest inhaled particles, surfactant, and pathogens. Pneumocytes line the alveolus, and interstitial macrophages and dendritic cells occupy other lung compartments.'),
('e8bb5a5f-b0cf-5ce3-88e3-6b18c4124586'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are dendritic cells?', '["Professional antigen-presenting cells that capture antigens and activate naive T cells","Platelets accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Red blood cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granulocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Professional antigen-presenting cells that capture antigens and activate naive T cells', 'Dendritic cells are professional antigen-presenting cells that capture antigen, migrate to lymph nodes, and activate naive T cells to start adaptive immune responses. Platelets, red cells, lymphocytes, and granulocytes do not serve this antigen-presenting role.'),
('81eb5546-1651-5e9c-a22e-b625620c6cb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are T lymphocytes?', '["Cells of adaptive immunity that mature in the thymus and mediate cellular immunity","B cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation","Neutrophils without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NK cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells of adaptive immunity that mature in the thymus and mediate cellular immunity', 'T lymphocytes mature in the thymus and carry out cellular immunity through direct cell interactions and cytokine release. B cells provide antibody-mediated humoral immunity, and macrophages, neutrophils, and NK cells are not thymus-derived adaptive lymphocytes.'),
('4747f2bf-bfa6-57cd-96d3-ab828be20989'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are CD4+ T cells (helper T cells)?', '["T cells that recognize MHC class II and help B cells","Cytotoxic T cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regulatory T cells mediated by specific receptor activation","and other T cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","macrophages leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T cells that recognize MHC class II and help B cells', 'CD4+ helper T cells recognize antigen presented on MHC class II molecules and secrete cytokines that help B cells, macrophages, and other T cells. They do not kill targets directly (a CD8+ function) or primarily suppress responses (a Treg function).'),
('d2d0829a-429b-50e9-a1ac-0db295c9201e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are CD8+ T cells (cytotoxic T cells)?', '["Gamma-delta T cells resulting in decreased functional activity","T cells that recognize MHC class I and directly kill infected or malignant cells","Regulatory T cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Helper T cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","B cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T cells that recognize MHC class I and directly kill infected or malignant cells', 'CD8+ cytotoxic T cells recognize antigen bound to MHC class I and directly induce apoptosis of infected or malignant cells. Gamma-delta, helper, regulatory T cells, and B cells carry out different effector functions.'),
('ad0117a0-7ebc-57e6-9cba-726aa918a1c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are regulatory T cells (Tregs)?', '["Cytotoxic T cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Helper T cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Memory T cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Naive T cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CD4+CD25+FoxP3+ T cells that suppress immune responses and maintain tolerance"]'::jsonb, 'CD4+CD25+FoxP3+ T cells that suppress immune responses and maintain tolerance', 'Regulatory T cells are CD4+CD25+FoxP3+ T cells that restrain immune responses and preserve self-tolerance. Cytotoxic, helper, memory, and naive T cells do not have suppression as their dominant function.'),
('2407e326-bc0e-5203-acce-e383a1cfaed6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are gamma-delta T cells?', '["NK cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Macrophages accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","B cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha-beta T cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T cells with gamma/delta TCRs (instead of alpha/beta) that recognize unconventional antigens"]'::jsonb, 'T cells with gamma/delta TCRs (instead of alpha/beta) that recognize unconventional antigens', 'Gamma-delta T cells carry aT-cell receptor made of gamma and delta chains and recognize unconventional antigens such as phosphoantigens and stress molecules. They differ from NK cells, macrophages, B cells, and conventional alpha-beta T cells.'),
('660930de-fb06-58d1-b3f2-de382d7c64e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are B lymphocytes?', '["Macrophages resulting in decreased functional activity","T cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cells of adaptive immunity that produce antibodies (humoral immunity)","Dendritic cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NK cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells of adaptive immunity that produce antibodies (humoral immunity)', 'B lymphocytes are the adaptive immune cells that differentiate into plasma cells and secrete antibodies, giving humoral immunity. T cells mediate cellular immunity, and macrophages, dendritic cells, and NK cells do not produce antibody.'),
('7b9af109-73b1-5790-8215-aa1747d3e0a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are natural killer (NK) cells?', '["Macrophages resulting in decreased functional activity","B cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Neutrophils without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Innate lymphoid cells that kill infected or malignant cells without prior sensitization"]'::jsonb, 'Innate lymphoid cells that kill infected or malignant cells without prior sensitization', 'NK cells are innate lymphoid cells that lyse infected or malignant targets immediately, without prior sensitization, using germline-encoded receptors. This immediate innate action separates them from adaptive T and B cells, macrophages, and neutrophils.'),
('4f671b17-feda-54a3-9dc1-74a202bf43b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the characteristic granules of NK cells?', '["Large azurophilic granules containing perforin and granzymes","Specific granules accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secondary granules mediated by specific receptor activation","Tertiary granules without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary granules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large azurophilic granules containing perforin and granzymes', 'NK cells contain large azurophilic granules packed with perforin and granzymes that are released to kill target cells. The specific, secondary, tertiary, and primary granule terms belong to the neutrophilic granulocyte series, not NK cells.'),
('3b1f491e-af1b-5a66-83a0-e5823872c1d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of NKT cells?', '["B cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Alpha-beta T cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mucosal-associated invariant T cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Gamma-delta T cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T cells with NK cell markers that recognize lipid antigens presented by CD1d"]'::jsonb, 'T cells with NK cell markers that recognize lipid antigens presented by CD1d', 'NKT cells are T cells that co-express NK cell surface markers and recognize lipid or glycolipid antigens presented on the CD1d molecule. Conventional alpha-beta T cells, MAIT cells, gamma-delta T cells, and B cells act through different receptors.'),
('4fe527f4-7532-567f-b900-79b28ff391c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are mucosal-associated invariant T (MAIT) cells?', '["B cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Conventional T cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NK cells mediated by specific receptor activation","T cells that recognize bacterial vitamin B metabolites presented by MR1","Gamma-delta T cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T cells that recognize bacterial vitamin B metabolites presented by MR1', 'MAIT cells are unconventional T cells that recognize riboflavin (vitamin B2) metabolites from microbes presented on the MR1 molecule. They are distinct from B cells, conventional T cells, NK cells, and gamma-delta T cells.'),
('00959d24-d599-568e-93ab-36ec190f658f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the thymic cortex composed of?', '["B cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Densely packed immature T cells (thymocytes)","and macrophages mediated by specific receptor activation","epithelial reticular cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mature T cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Densely packed immature T cells (thymocytes)', 'The thymic cortex contains densely packed immature T cells (thymocytes) supported by epithelial reticular cells and scattered macrophages. Mature T cells populate the medulla rather than the cortex.'),
('ebbc07d4-a254-5115-8171-4ecad31fd363'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the thymic medulla composed of?', '["epithelial cells","Sparsely packed mature T cells","and Hassall''s corpuscles","Immature thymocytes","dendritic cells"]'::jsonb, 'Sparsely packed mature T cells', 'The thymic medulla holds sparsely packed mature T cells together with medullary epithelial cells, dendritic cells, and Hassall''s corpuscles. Immature thymocytes are concentrated in the cortex.'),
('8e5e3a20-a3aa-5e8b-b14a-ad04f2c8bd69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the thymic barrier?', '["The basement membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","The medullary barrier accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A barrier in the cortex separating developing T cells from blood-borne antigens","The capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The perivascular space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A barrier in the cortex separating developing T cells from blood-borne antigens', 'The blood-thymic barrier in the cortex isolates developing thymocytes from blood-borne antigens using perivascular sheaths and bordering epithelial reticular cells. It is not the capsule, the basement membrane alone, the perivascular space itself, or a medullary barrier.'),
('5a6b686c-41e2-5fe7-8dd9-172f076fc30c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What cells form the outermost capsule of the thymus?', '["Adipose tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Epithelial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Dense connective tissue continuous with the mediastinal connective tissue","Smooth muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dense connective tissue continuous with the mediastinal connective tissue', 'The thymic capsule is dense connective tissue that is continuous with the mediastinal connective tissue and sends septa into the gland. Lymphocytes form the parenchyma, and epithelial cells, smooth muscle, and adipose do not make up the outer capsule.'),
('563be156-f43d-52bb-8cd3-51ab08fde704'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the corticomedullary junction of the thymus?', '["The boundary between the cortex and medulla containing blood vessels and dendritic cells","The subcapsular cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The trabeculae mediated by specific receptor activation","The outer capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The boundary between the cortex and medulla containing blood vessels and dendritic cells', 'The corticomedullary junction is the transition zone between cortex and medulla where the larger blood vessels and their accompanying dendritic cells are found. It is not the subcapsular cortex, trabeculae, outer capsule, or central medulla.'),
('377d372d-987a-5e4d-9c73-c32b1c852217'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are thymic nurse cells?', '["Specialized cortical epithelial cells that envelop developing thymocytes in cytoplasmic vacuoles","Dendritic cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medullary epithelial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Macrophages without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibroblasts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Specialized cortical epithelial cells that envelop developing thymocytes in cytoplasmic vacuoles', 'Thymic nurse cells are specialized cortical epithelial cells that enclose developing thymocytes within cytoplasmic vacuoles, providing a site for early positive selection. Dendritic cells, medullary epithelial cells, macrophages, and fibroblasts do not have this enveloping role.'),
('bbd63947-19a0-5ddf-8546-c78713cfbd42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What do thymic epithelial cells express for T cell selection?', '["Only MHC class I resulting in decreased functional activity","MHC class I and class II molecules","MHC mediated by specific receptor activation","Only MHC class II without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only CD molecules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MHC class I and class II molecules', 'Thymic epithelial cells express both MHC class I and class II molecules so that CD8+ and CD4+ T cells can respectively undergo positive selection. Expressing only one MHC class, MHC alone without detail, or only CD molecules would not allow selection of both lineages.'),
('68fe6eae-af8f-5a61-9f1b-89b116d877cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of thymic dendritic cells in negative selection?', '["Antibody production resulting in decreased functional activity","Positive selection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cytokine secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Present self-antigens to developing T cells for deletion of self-reactive clones","Phagocytosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Present self-antigens to developing T cells for deletion of self-reactive clones', 'Thymic dendritic cells present self-antigens to immature T cells, so any T cell whose receptor reacts strongly with these antigens undergoes apoptosis (negative selection). Antibody production belongs to plasma cells, positive selection is carried out by cortical thymic epithelium, and phagocytosis is a macrophage function.'),
('aeb102ad-da86-5643-b1db-2db6b4721016'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is thymic involution driven by?', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Age-related decrease in thymopoiesis and replacement with adipose tissue","Radiation mediated by specific receptor activation","Hormonal changes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thymic cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Age-related decrease in thymopoiesis and replacement with adipose tissue', 'After puberty the thymus gradually shrinks, with declining thymopoiesis and progressive replacement of the parenchyma by adipose tissue; this physiological involution is age-related. Infection, radiation, and malignancy cause pathologic damage but are not the normal mechanism of involution.'),
('b242b980-0bf2-5e6a-9307-563eb1406e3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is thymic hyperplasia?', '["Thymic tumor","Enlargement of the thymus","often with germinal centers","Thymic atrophy","seen in myasthenia gravis"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of the thymus', 'Thymic hyperplasia is enlargement of the thymus, often with germinal centers and associated with myasthenia gravis. It is distinct from a thymoma (a neoplasm) and from atrophy, which is shrinkage of the gland.'),
('8adfa8ff-0039-55f8-801a-e2be2cd6c9bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is CD3?', '["An NK cell marker resulting in decreased functional activity","A macrophage marker accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A stem cell marker mediated by specific receptor activation","AB cell marker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A pan-T cell marker (component of the T cell receptor complex)"]'::jsonb, 'A pan-T cell marker (component of the T cell receptor complex)', 'CD3 is an invariant component of the T cell receptor complex and is expressed on essentially all mature T cells, making it the standard pan-T cell marker. NK cells, macrophages, stem cells, and B cells are identified by different markers such as CD16/56, CD68, CD34, and CD20.'),
('dc921eb6-8490-53ae-b5f1-1a90498e726a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is CD4?', '["A glycoprotein on helper T cells that binds MHC class II","A cytotoxic T cell marker accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","AB cell marker mediated by specific receptor activation","A macrophage marker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An NK cell marker leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A glycoprotein on helper T cells that binds MHC class II', 'CD4 is a co-receptor expressed on helper T cells that binds to MHC class II molecules on antigen-presenting cells during antigen recognition, defining the helper lineage. CD8 marks cytotoxic T cells, CD20 marks B cells, and NK cells have their own lineage markers.'),
('f5b1b6f8-e5ee-563d-aa65-65105e57a214'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is CD8?', '["A glycoprotein on cytotoxic T cells that binds MHC class I","AB cell marker accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A macrophage marker mediated by specific receptor activation","An NK cell marker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A helper T cell marker leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A glycoprotein on cytotoxic T cells that binds MHC class I', 'CD8 is the co-receptor on cytotoxic T cells that binds MHC class I molecules on infected or tumor cells, enabling recognition and killing of those targets. CD4 is the helper T cell marker, while CD20, CD68, and NK markers belong to other lineages.'),
('f49affbd-5ddf-5c42-83d3-0db3e872c767'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is CD20?', '["A mature B cell marker (pan-B cell)","A stem cell marker","AT cell marker","An NK cell marker","A plasma cell marker"]'::jsonb, 'A mature B cell marker (pan-B cell)', 'CD20 is expressed on mature B cells throughout their antigen-independent and antigen-dependent stages and is used as a pan-B cell marker and therapeutic target. Plasma cells lose CD20 and are instead identified by CD138.'),
('046c2bef-ada9-5eac-b10e-5318a9bbe525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is CD138 (syndecan-1)?', '["AT cell marker","A dendritic cell marker","A plasma cell marker","A macrophage marker","AB cell marker"]'::jsonb, 'A plasma cell marker', 'CD138 (syndecan-1) is strongly expressed on plasma cells and is used to identify them, particularly because plasma cells lose pan-B markers such as CD20. It is not aT cell, NK, dendritic cell, or macrophage marker.'),
('92c11bb8-0b57-51ee-a93a-cd5885925aa1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is CD34?', '["A mature lymphocyte marker resulting in decreased functional activity","A dendritic cell marker accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A macrophage marker mediated by specific receptor activation","A granulocyte marker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A hematopoietic stem cell and progenitor cell marker"]'::jsonb, 'A hematopoietic stem cell and progenitor cell marker', 'CD34 is expressed on hematopoietic stem cells and early progenitors and is used to isolate stem cells for transplantation and to identify primitive tumors such as leukemias. Expression is lost as cells differentiate into mature lymphocytes, granulocytes, macrophages, or dendritic cells.'),
('09e667f2-6c4d-517a-a1d0-d233eaa02259'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is CD68?', '["A macrophage marker (lysosomal protein)","AT cell marker","An NK cell marker","A dendritic cell marker","AB cell marker"]'::jsonb, 'A macrophage marker (lysosomal protein)', 'CD68 is a lysosomal glycoprotein highly expressed in macrophages and other myeloid cells, making it the standard immunohistochemical marker for macrophages. T cells, NK cells, dendritic cells, and B cells carry different lineage markers.'),
('4ec33da4-84b9-5304-b5b7-67c463b22c65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is CD163?', '["A pro-inflammatory macrophage marker","especially anti-inflammatory M2 macrophages","AT cell marker","AB cell marker","A marker of macrophages (scavenger receptor)"]'::jsonb, 'A marker of macrophages (scavenger receptor)', 'CD163 is a scavenger receptor expressed on macrophages, with particularly high expression on anti-inflammatory (M2) macrophages. It is not aT cell or B cell marker; the other listed descriptions are partial or incorrect.'),
('c0f3e6ac-1cf0-540d-818a-7222065b3054'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is CD1a?', '["A marker for cortical thymocytes and Langerhans cells","AB cell marker accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A mature T cell marker mediated by specific receptor activation","A plasma cell marker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A macrophage marker leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A marker for cortical thymocytes and Langerhans cells', 'CD1a labels cortical thymocytes and epidermal Langerhans cells, a skin-resident dendritic cell population, and is useful in the diagnosis of Langerhans cell histiocytosis. It is not a marker of B cells, plasma cells, or mature peripheral T cells.'),
('ee75def6-27ec-5369-8150-82afc2612280'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is lymphadenopathy?', '["Normal-sized lymph nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Enlargement of lymph nodes from infection","or malignancy mediated by specific receptor activation","inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small lymph nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of lymph nodes from infection', 'Lymphadenopathy means enlargement of lymph nodes, most commonly from infection, although malignancy, inflammation, and autoimmune disease can also cause it. Normal-sized or small nodes are by definition not lymphadenopathy.'),
('19c17aa2-014b-57cd-8270-21404cae221c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is reactive follicular hyperplasia?', '["Lymphoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrosis of lymph nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrophy of lymph nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Necrosis of lymph nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enlargement of lymphoid follicles in response to antigen stimulation"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of lymphoid follicles in response to antigen stimulation', 'Antigenic stimulation enlarges the cortical lymphoid follicles of lymph nodes and creates germinal centers; this benign reactive change is called reactive follicular hyperplasia. Lymphoma is a malignant neoplasm, and fibrosis, atrophy, and necrosis are different pathologic processes.'),
('99b83606-4dec-55cf-bebb-8e6a24601d21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is sinus histiocytosis?', '["Lymph node calcification","Lymph node fibrosis","often draining cancers","Prominent macrophages in the lymphatic sinuses","Lymph node atrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Prominent macrophages in the lymphatic sinuses', 'Sinus histiocytosis is an accumulation of macrophages (histiocytes) within the lymph node sinuses, often seen in nodes draining cancers. Fibrosis, calcification, and atrophy are separate and distinct lymph node changes.'),
('90f7d0e4-c482-5685-8a18-ad7250b097e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is granulomatous inflammation in lymph nodes?', '["Reactive hyperplasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Necrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Formation of granulomas (aggregates of epithelioid macrophages) from infections or sarcoidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of granulomas (aggregates of epithelioid macrophages) from infections or sarcoidosis', 'Granulomatous inflammation in lymph nodes consists of compact aggregates of epithelioid macrophages, typically due to mycobacterial and other infections, fungal disease, or sarcoidosis. Reactive hyperplasia, simple fibrosis, necrosis, and lymphoma are distinct processes.'),
('4f2b9ccd-b44a-5468-a69c-8207a994e1a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is metastatic cancer in lymph nodes?', '["Benign lymph node enlargement resulting in decreased functional activity","Spread of cancer cells from a primary tumor to regional lymph nodes","Primary lymphoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Reactive hyperplasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection of lymph node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spread of cancer cells from a primary tumor to regional lymph nodes', 'Metastatic disease occurs when cancer cells travel through lymphatics from a primary tumor to drain into regional lymph nodes. In contrast, a primary lymphoma arises within the lymph node itself, while reactive hyperplasia and infection are non-neoplastic.'),
('b798f568-a0bf-5ac5-b020-bc745b163993'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is Hodgkin lymphoma?', '["A lymphoma characterized by Reed-Sternberg cells in an inflammatory background","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple myeloma mediated by specific receptor activation","Leukemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metastatic carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A lymphoma characterized by Reed-Sternberg cells in an inflammatory background', 'Hodgkin lymphoma is defined by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells accompanied by a prominent mixed inflammatory infiltrate and fibrosis. Non-Hodgkin lymphoma lacks Reed-Sternberg cells, and myeloma, leukemia, and metastatic carcinoma are different neoplasms.'),
('11145862-b18b-5bb5-8299-be63696007c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is a Reed-Sternberg cell?', '["diagnostic of Hodgkin lymphoma resulting in decreased functional activity","A macrophage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A large binucleated cell with prominent nucleoli (owl-eye appearance)","AB cell without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AT cell leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A large binucleated cell with prominent nucleoli (owl-eye appearance)', 'A Reed-Sternberg cell is a large cell with double nuclei and prominent nucleoli, giving an owl-eye or mirror-image appearance; its presence is diagnostic of Hodgkin lymphoma. It is a transformed B cell rather than a normal macrophage, B cell, or T cell.'),
('ab310d3d-1d8a-5adf-888e-f0ffe02c121b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the most common type of Hodgkin lymphoma?', '["Mixed cellularity","Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma","Lymphocyte depleted","Lymphocyte predominant","Lymphocyte rich"]'::jsonb, 'Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin lymphoma', 'Nodular sclerosis is the most common subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma, especially in young women presenting with mediastinal disease, and classically shows lacunar cells and dense collagen bands. The other listed subtypes are less common.'),
('8a5a8ce1-2c40-5d59-8dcc-f1b916603dcd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL)?', '["Hodgkin lymphoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Benign lymphoid hyperplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A diverse group of lymphomas without Reed-Sternberg cells","Multiple myeloma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leukemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A diverse group of lymphomas without Reed-Sternberg cells', 'Non-Hodgkin lymphoma is a heterogeneous group of lymphomas, mostly of B cell origin, that lack Reed-Sternberg cells; it often involves extranodal sites and multiple nodes. Hodgkin lymphoma, myeloma, and leukemia are distinct entities.'),
('4afee040-774f-5eb2-89a4-bcffb866a812'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the most common NHL in adults?', '["Mantle cell lymphoma","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)","Follicular lymphoma","Marginal zone lymphoma","Burkitt lymphoma"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL)', 'Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is the most common non-Hodgkin lymphoma in adults; it is an aggressive tumor of large B cells. Follicular and mantle cell lymphomas and Burkitt lymphoma are less common overall.'),
('db78f417-1ac1-5fe4-8a04-dff2dce0997b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the most common cause of splenomegaly worldwide?', '["Lymphoma","Liver cirrhosis","Leukemia","Hemolytic anemias","Malaria and other infections"]'::jsonb, 'Malaria and other infections', 'Worldwide, malaria and other infections are the leading causes of splenic enlargement, because the spleen filters blood and responds to blood-borne organisms. Lymphoma, cirrhosis, leukemia, and hemolytic anemia are important causes but less common globally.'),
('3818faf4-0140-5be7-b607-63b41fea7c10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is splenic infarction?', '["Ischemic necrosis of the spleen","Splenic infection","Splenic rupture","Splenic tumor","often from emboli in endocarditis or sickle cell disease"]'::jsonb, 'Ischemic necrosis of the spleen', 'Splenic infarction is ischemic necrosis of the spleen, typically wedge-shaped, caused by occlusion of splenic artery branches, often by emboli from endocarditis or by sickling. Infection, rupture, and tumor are different pathologies.'),
('cad383b1-4d67-544b-9e10-70ec1aa65f5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are Howell-Jolly bodies?', '["Viral inclusions resulting in decreased functional activity","Nuclear remnants in RBCs seen after splenectomy or hyposplenism","Parasitic inclusions mediated by specific receptor activation","Inclusions from iron overload without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial inclusions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear remnants in RBCs seen after splenectomy or hyposplenism', 'Howell-Jolly bodies are small basophilic nuclear remnants in red blood cells that normally are removed by the spleen''s pitting function; they appear after splenectomy or in functional hyposplenism. They are not viral, parasitic, bacterial, or iron-related inclusions.'),
('7fcf2e2c-a7a5-5af1-ae6d-50cd5163af1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of the marginal zone?', '["Captures blood-borne antigens and initiates early antibody responses","T cell activation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RBC destruction mediated by specific receptor activation","B cell maturation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Platelet storage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Captures blood-borne antigens and initiates early antibody responses', 'The marginal zone surrounding the white pulp contains B cells and macrophages that trap blood-borne antigens and mount rapid T-independent antibody responses. T cell activation, RBC destruction, and platelet storage are functions of other splenic compartments.'),
('d80f2029-41b9-58a4-8f54-75d9b2ae9bfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are Gamma-Gandy bodies?', '["Iron and calcium deposits in splenic scars after infarction or hemorrhage","Splenic granulomas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Splenic cysts mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenic tumors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Splenic abscesses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iron and calcium deposits in splenic scars after infarction or hemorrhage', 'Gamma-Gandy bodies are fibrotic, siderotic splenic scars containing iron and calcium, seen especially in portal hypertension with splenomegaly after old hemorrhages or infarcts. They are not granulomas, cysts, tumors, or abscesses.'),
('e08b05f3-505e-539a-ac08-38c560b170d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is a splenic cyst?', '["A solid tumor","A hematoma","either true (epithelial-lined) or false (post-traumatic)","An abscess","A fluid-filled cavity in the spleen"]'::jsonb, 'A fluid-filled cavity in the spleen', 'A splenic cyst is a fluid-filled cavity within the spleen, which may be a true cyst with an epithelial lining or a false post-traumatic cyst. Solid tumors, hematomas, and abscesses have distinct features.'),
('b23f54e9-0212-5a14-920b-d5b15495532a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the splenic role in immune response to encapsulated bacteria?', '["Only platelet storage resulting in decreased functional activity","Only iron recycling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only filtering mediated by specific receptor activation","role without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Marginal zone B cells and macrophages produce IgM antibodies to polysaccharide antigens"]'::jsonb, 'Marginal zone B cells and macrophages produce IgM antibodies to polysaccharide antigens', 'The splenic marginal zone generates T-independent IgM responses to polysaccharide capsules of organisms such as pneumococcus and meningococcus, so splenectomy impairs defense against these bacteria. Platelet storage, iron recycling, and filtration do not explain this antibody function.'),
('feb7387a-1f08-55bc-950d-9c938ee23e01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the structure of MALT?', '["Only lymph nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Diffuse lymphoid cells and organized lymphoid follicles beneath epithelial surfaces","Only follicles mediated by specific receptor activation","Only diffuse cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only tonsils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse lymphoid cells and organized lymphoid follicles beneath epithelial surfaces', 'Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) consists of diffuse lymphocytes plus organized follicles located beneath epithelial surfaces, such as in the gut, bronchi, and tonsils. It is unencapsulated and is not limited to lymph nodes, follicles, or tonsils alone.'),
('5e854df3-4c89-5cab-8ec7-22d6d1f7a390'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the dominant antibody in mucosal secretions?', '["IgD resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretory IgA (sIgA)","IgG mediated by specific receptor activation","IgM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgE leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secretory IgA (sIgA)', 'Secretory IgA is quantitatively the dominant antibody class in mucosal secretions, protecting the gut, respiratory tract, and other mucosae. IgG, IgM, and IgE predominate in other locations or functions, and IgD is a surface receptor on B cells.'),
('0bdee85b-6908-503a-9877-8891ce94dcd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the structure of secretory IgA?', '["IgE resulting in decreased functional activity","A dimer of IgA with J chain and secretory component","Monomeric IgA mediated by specific receptor activation","IgG without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pentameric IgM leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A dimer of IgA with J chain and secretory component', 'Secretory IgA is a dimer of two IgA molecules joined by the J chain and protected by the secretory component, which is derived from the poly-Ig receptor. Monomeric IgA is found in serum, and pentameric IgM is the multimeric IgM form.'),
('4e377290-4d91-540e-ac8a-4352eacc1628'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of sIgA?', '["Cytotoxicity resulting in decreased functional activity","Opsonization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neutralizes pathogens and toxins at mucosal surfaces without causing inflammation","Complement activation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Histamine release leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neutralizes pathogens and toxins at mucosal surfaces without causing inflammation', 'Secretory IgA blocks microbial and toxin attachment to epithelial cells and neutralizes them without triggering complement or inflammation, providing non-inflammatory defense at mucosal surfaces. Cytotoxicity, opsonization, and complement activation are functions of other effectors.'),
('59fdfbda-949f-545a-9812-64ed696c289d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the poly-Ig receptor?', '["A scavenger receptor resulting in decreased functional activity","An Fc receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A complement receptor mediated by specific receptor activation","A receptor on mucosal epithelial cells that transports dimeric IgA from interstitium to lumen","A pattern recognition receptor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A receptor on mucosal epithelial cells that transports dimeric IgA from interstitium to lumen', 'The poly-Ig receptor on the basolateral surface of mucosal epithelial cells binds dimeric IgA, transports it across the cell, and releases it into the lumen as secretory IgA. It is not a scavenger, Fc, complement, or pattern recognition receptor.'),
('6024857f-314e-5eeb-ab4e-d6f7549e3246'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are intraepithelial lymphocytes (IELs)?', '["T cells located between epithelial cells in mucosal surfaces","Macrophages in the epithelium","Dendritic cells in the epithelium","B cells in the epithelium","NK cells in the epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'T cells located between epithelial cells in mucosal surfaces', 'Intraepithelial lymphocytes are mostly T cells intercalated among epithelial cells, especially enterocytes, where they provide a first line of mucosal defense. They are not macrophages, dendritic cells, B cells, or NK cells.'),
('249162ef-69a0-56d8-9165-33ded97c4bff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are lamina propria lymphocytes?', '["Intraepithelial lymphocytes","Scattered lymphocytes","Lymphoid follicle cells","plasma cells","and macrophages in the connective tissue beneath mucosal epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Scattered lymphocytes', 'The lamina propria contains a scattered, diffuse population of lymphocytes lying beneath the mucosal epithelium, in addition to plasma cells and macrophages. They differ from intraepithelial lymphocytes and from the organized cells of lymphoid follicles.'),
('4d8b6d03-57f7-5028-908b-b2f73f0c5f1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is oral tolerance?', '["Celiac disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Food intolerance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inflammatory bowel disease mediated by specific receptor activation","The immune system''s suppression of responses to food antigens and commensal bacteria","Allergy to food leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The immune system''s suppression of responses to food antigens and commensal bacteria', 'Oral tolerance is the active suppression of immune responses to harmless food antigens and commensal bacteria in the gut. Breakdown of this mechanism contributes to celiac disease, food allergy, and inflammatory bowel disease, which are consequences rather than the definition.'),
('5e8dee1b-24de-5fe7-aec0-083cac8f4598'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the role of mesenteric lymph nodes?', '["Filter lymph from the leg resulting in decreased functional activity","Produce lymphocytes for the spleen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filter blood mediated by specific receptor activation","Store fat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Drain the gut and initiate immune responses to intestinal antigens"]'::jsonb, 'Drain the gut and initiate immune responses to intestinal antigens', 'Mesenteric lymph nodes drain lymph from the intestine and are a major site where immune responses to intestinal antigens are initiated, including the induction of oral tolerance. They do not filter lymph from the leg, filter blood, or store fat.'),
('487c6c60-d1fd-5034-a9b3-90d35a0d0e3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the effect of breast milk on the infant''s immune system?', '["lactoferrin","and immune cells for passive protection","immune benefit","lysozyme","Provides sIgA"]'::jsonb, 'Provides sIgA', 'Breast milk supplies secretory IgA along with maternal immune cells, lactoferrin, and lysozyme, conferring passive mucosal protection to the newborn. Lactoferrin and lysozyme are antimicrobial components, but providing sIgA is the principal antibody effect.'),
('0464e47b-bab9-597b-8abf-55fc60dd5120'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of cytokines?', '["Antibodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hormones mediated by specific receptor activation","Small proteins that mediate cell-to-cell communication in the immune system","Enzymes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small proteins that mediate cell-to-cell communication in the immune system', 'Cytokines are small secreted proteins that carry signals between immune cells, regulating growth, activation, and differentiation. They are effectors rather than antibodies, receptors, hormones, or enzymes.'),
('a6292d9b-55ed-572f-ae77-8a0cb5fe597e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are interleukins?', '["Antibodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Cytokines produced by and acting on leukocytes","Chemotactic factors mediated by specific receptor activation","Growth factors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interferons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cytokines produced by and acting on leukocytes', 'Interleukins are a family of cytokines produced by leukocytes and acting on leukocytes to coordinate immune responses; they are numbered (IL-1, IL-2, etc.). They are not antibodies, and chemotactic factors, growth factors, and interferons are separate cytokine classes.'),
('98496c37-321c-5479-84cd-c379d0f1635f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of IL-2?', '["Promotes T cell proliferation and activation (T cell growth factor)","Promotes B cell maturation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Induces fever mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibits T cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Induces inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes T cell proliferation and activation (T cell growth factor)', 'IL-2 acts as aT cell growth factor, driving clonal proliferation and activation of effector T cells after antigen stimulation. It does not mature B cells or induce fever, and its net effect is stimulatory, not inhibitory.'),
('acbd1cc8-55b4-596e-88a8-a89a9ee6e11d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of IL-4?', '["Promotes Th2 differentiation and IgE class switching in B cells","Inflammation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cytotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Th1 differentiation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fever leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes Th2 differentiation and IgE class switching in B cells', 'IL-4 drives naive T cells toward the Th2 phenotype and induces IgE class switching in B cells, key to type 2 immunity and allergic responses. Th1 differentiation is promoted by IL-12 and IFN-gamma, not IL-4.'),
('b3d93506-de89-524a-9b63-903cbefd6ae1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of IFN-gamma?', '["promotes Th1 responses","Induces fever","Activates macrophages","Promotes Th2 responses","and upregulates MHC molecules"]'::jsonb, 'Activates macrophages', 'IFN-gamma, produced by Th1 cells and NK cells, is the principal macrophage-activating factor and also reinforces Th1 responses and upregulates MHC molecules. It does not promote Th2 responses, and inducing fever is primarily a role of IL-1 and TNF.'),
('a9392178-d0e0-5225-8efc-5c2388abcdbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of TNF-alpha?', '["and activates endothelium; can induce cachexia","fever","Promotes anti-inflammatory responses","Induces apoptosis only","Promotes inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes inflammation', 'TNF-alpha is a key pro-inflammatory cytokine that activates endothelium and promotes inflammation; it also contributes to fever and cachexia. The option describing anti-inflammatory responses is opposite, and its actions are not limited to apoptosis.'),
('b6b709c1-09fa-5c4e-bacd-f51f603e0aa3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of IL-10?', '["Anti-inflammatory cytokine that suppresses macrophage and T cell responses","Pro-inflammatory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Induces fever mediated by specific receptor activation","Activates NK cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Promotes IgE leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-inflammatory cytokine that suppresses macrophage and T cell responses', 'IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that suppresses macrophage activation and T cell responses, limiting tissue damage during immune reactions. It is the opposite of pro-inflammatory cytokines that induce fever or activate NK cells.'),
('baeb107d-61bf-5a62-b2ac-7c4847cb3627'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are chemokines?', '["Interferons resulting in decreased functional activity","Antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complement proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Growth factors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small cytokines that induce chemotaxis (directed migration) of immune cells"]'::jsonb, 'Small cytokines that induce chemotaxis (directed migration) of immune cells', 'Chemokines are small cytokines that attract leukocytes by inducing chemotaxis, guiding them to sites of infection, injury, or inflammation. Interferons, antibodies, complement, and growth factors have different structures and functions.'),
('6602d53c-c7e5-5195-8514-576e11d2bf51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of CXCL8 (IL-8)?', '["Lymphocyte homing","Neutrophil chemotaxis and activation","Monocyte chemotaxis","Eosinophil chemotaxis","T cell chemotaxis"]'::jsonb, 'Neutrophil chemotaxis and activation', 'CXCL8 (IL-8) is a potent chemoattractant and activator of neutrophils, recruiting them to sites of acute inflammation. Lymphocyte homing, monocyte recruitment by CCL2, and eosinophil attraction by eotaxin are achieved by different chemokines.'),
('831579dc-9252-5742-aa06-3429d6759f6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of CCL2 (MCP-1)?', '["Basophil chemotaxis resulting in decreased functional activity","Eosinophil chemotaxis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T cell homing to lymph nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Neutrophil chemotaxis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monocyte/macrophage chemotaxis to sites of inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'Monocyte/macrophage chemotaxis to sites of inflammation', 'CCL2, also called MCP-1, primarily recruits monocytes and macrophages to sites of inflammation. Neutrophils are attracted mainly by CXCL8, eosinophils by eotaxin, and T cell homing to nodes is mediated by other chemokines such as CCL21.'),
('220b3648-417c-5b20-80f5-216bf4c9ad56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the complement system?', '["A group of serum proteins that enhance (complement) immune responses by opsonization","A group of antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and lysis mediated by specific receptor activation","inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A group of T cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A group of serum proteins that enhance (complement) immune responses by opsonization', 'Complement is a cascade of serum proteins that enhances innate and adaptive immunity through opsonization, membrane lysis, and inflammation. It is not made of antibodies and is not composed of T cells; the other listed fragments are incomplete descriptions.'),
('c98f7bc3-d2f0-5b59-8b1d-91dd7412f996'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the classical complement pathway?', '["involving C1 resulting in decreased functional activity","and C2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activated by IgM or IgG bound to antigen","Activated by bacterial surfaces directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","C4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activated by IgM or IgG bound to antigen', 'The classical pathway is triggered when IgM or IgG bound to antigen activates C1, which then cleaves C4 and C2. Direct activation by bacterial surfaces is the hallmark of the alternative pathway, so the other options are incorrect.'),
('9797888b-292e-599d-a56c-aef8ac6da978'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the alternative complement pathway?', '["Activated by lectin resulting in decreased functional activity","Activated by C-reactive protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activated by antibodies mediated by specific receptor activation","Activated by microbial surfaces directly (spontaneous C3 hydrolysis)","Activated by cytokines leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activated by microbial surfaces directly (spontaneous C3 hydrolysis)', 'The alternative pathway is initiated by spontaneous C3 hydrolysis and is amplified on microbial surfaces without any requirement for antibody. Antibody-dependent activation defines the classical pathway, and the lectin pathway relies on mannose-binding lectin.'),
('e650f21a-4231-5065-9082-4c2198a037b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the lectin complement pathway?', '["Activated by direct C3 hydrolysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Activated by mannose-binding lectin (MBL) binding to carbohydrate patterns on microbes","Activated by antibodies mediated by specific receptor activation","Activated by C1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Activated by properdin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activated by mannose-binding lectin (MBL) binding to carbohydrate patterns on microbes', 'The lectin pathway begins when mannose-binding lectin (MBL) binds carbohydrate patterns on microbial surfaces, then proceeds like the classical pathway downstream of C1. Direct C3 hydrolysis characterizes the alternative pathway, while C1 and antibodies are used by the classical pathway.'),
('9f6a6a2f-a028-5833-9d04-bc72613ac8d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is C3?', '["The central complement protein","whose cleavage is essential for all pathways","The membrane attack complex protein","The first protein of the classical pathway","An opsonin only"]'::jsonb, 'The central complement protein', 'C3 sits at the convergence of all three complement pathways, and its cleavage by the C3 convertase generates the opsonin C3b and the anaphylatoxin C3a. The MAC is built from C5b-9, C1 is the classical initiator, and C3b is only one product of C3.'),
('266ff01d-f3f0-5a36-9738-cae351f72bf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is C5a?', '["A regulator resulting in decreased functional activity","A potent anaphylatoxin and chemoattractant for neutrophils","An opsonin mediated by specific receptor activation","A membrane attack complex protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lectin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A potent anaphylatoxin and chemoattractant for neutrophils', 'C5a is the most potent anaphylatoxin and a strong chemoattractant, recruiting neutrophils and increasing vascular permeability. C3b is the main opsonin, while the MAC proteins (C5b-9) mediate lysis rather than chemotaxis.'),
('049bf804-09a3-59fc-b2ae-ad9e8b960fdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the membrane attack complex (MAC)?', '["The C3 convertase resulting in decreased functional activity","The C1 complex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The C5 convertase mediated by specific receptor activation","C5b-9 complex that forms pores in target cell membranes","causing lysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C5b-9 complex that forms pores in target cell membranes', 'The membrane attack complex is formed from C5b-9, which inserts into target cell membranes and creates transmembrane pores that cause osmotic lysis. C3 and C5 convertases are upstream enzymes that generate the MAC components, and C1 initiates the classical pathway.'),
('0b2d3d7c-7dc9-5d1f-b33e-2be6c958128f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of C3b?', '["Anaphylatoxin resulting in decreased functional activity","Opsonization (coating pathogens for phagocytosis)","Membrane attack mediated by specific receptor activation","Vasodilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chemotaxis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Opsonization (coating pathogens for phagocytosis)', 'C3b covalently coats pathogen surfaces, marking them for uptake by phagocytes bearing complement receptors, a process called opsonization. Anaphylatoxins (C3a, C5a) promote inflammation and chemotaxis, while the MAC mediates lysis.'),
('5a997c89-fc7f-5755-9e4c-b0b93b8d1b89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is complement regulation?', '["Control mechanisms to prevent complement-mediated damage to host cells","Only activation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only inhibition mediated by specific receptor activation","regulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uncontrolled activation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Control mechanisms to prevent complement-mediated damage to host cells', 'Complement regulators such as C1 inhibitor, factor H, and decay-accelerating factor restrict complement activation and protect host cells from inadvertent damage. Uncontrolled activation causes host tissue injury, so balanced regulation is vital.'),
('a73fa55b-75a1-59fa-98b6-f7a579832c1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is hereditary angioedema?', '["Autoimmune disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Immune complex disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complement deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Allergic reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","C1 inhibitor deficiency leading to uncontrolled bradykinin production and swelling"]'::jsonb, 'C1 inhibitor deficiency leading to uncontrolled bradykinin production and swelling', 'Hereditary angioedema results from deficiency of C1 inhibitor, which normally curbs bradykinin generation, producing recurrent episodes of non-pitting subcutaneous and mucosal swelling. It is a complement deficiency rather than an allergy, immune complex, or autoimmune disease.'),
('d209e3d1-f69b-5816-85d4-1dd109025015'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is anaphylaxis?', '["life-threatening systemic Type I hypersensitivity reaction","Type II reaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type III reaction mediated by specific receptor activation","Severe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type IV reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'life-threatening systemic Type I hypersensitivity reaction', 'Anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening systemic Type I hypersensitivity reaction driven by mast cell and basophil mediators released after allergen cross-linking of IgE. Type II, III, and IV reactions act through different antibody or cell-mediated effector mechanisms.'),
('aa4fd4df-df87-5e21-af1e-6798889878eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is autoimmune disease?', '["Immune response against self-antigens due to breakdown of self-tolerance","Allergy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transplant rejection mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypersensitivity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Immunodeficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immune response against self-antigens due to breakdown of self-tolerance', 'Autoimmune disease arises when a failure of self-tolerance allows the immune system to attack self-antigens. Allergy targets harmless environmental antigens, transplant rejection targets allogeneic tissues, and immunodeficiency reflects an underactive immune system.'),
('dc50bd1f-19b3-50db-8436-f4a8b444925c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the most common autoimmune disease?', '["Rheumatoid arthritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Type 1 diabetes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hashimoto thyroiditis (autoimmune hypothyroidism)","Systemic lupus erythematosus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multiple sclerosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hashimoto thyroiditis (autoimmune hypothyroidism)', 'Hashimoto thyroiditis is the most common autoimmune disease and the leading cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient populations. Rheumatoid arthritis, type 1 diabetes, SLE, and multiple sclerosis are all less prevalent.'),
('f0312093-245d-5983-a821-a4aa5591c321'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)?', '["Allergy resulting in decreased functional activity","affecting multiple organs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Systemic autoimmune disease with antinuclear antibodies (ANA)","Immunodeficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Organ-specific autoimmune disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic autoimmune disease with antinuclear antibodies (ANA)', 'SLE is a systemic autoimmune disease defined by antinuclear antibodies and immune complex deposition that damages multiple organs. It differs from organ-specific autoimmune diseases because of its widespread multisystem involvement.'),
('1b885369-e15b-5827-a1f5-95590557779e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is immunodeficiency?', '["Autoimmunity","Deficiency in immune function","Hypersensitivity","either primary (congenital) or secondary (acquired)","Allergy"]'::jsonb, 'Deficiency in immune function', 'Immunodeficiency is a quantitative or qualitative deficiency of immune components and may be primary (congenital) or secondary (acquired). It is the opposite of autoimmunity, allergy, and hypersensitivity, which reflect excessive immune responses.'),
('7ed444cd-88e4-585f-991d-c73c8e793779'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID)?', '["requiring stem cell transplant for survival","Only B cell deficiency","Phagocyte deficiency","Lack of both T and B cell function","Only T cell deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of both T and B cell function', 'SCID is characterized by a lack of both T and B cell function, leaving affected infants profoundly susceptible to opportunistic infection. This is broader than isolated T cell or B cell deficiency and usually requires early stem cell transplantation.'),
('f285f69e-ee5c-5d39-abf6-4406cd2d6828'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is DiGeorge syndrome?', '["Phagocyte deficiency","Thymic aplasia/hypoplasia from 22q11 deletion","B cell deficiency","Complement deficiency","causing T cell deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Thymic aplasia/hypoplasia from 22q11 deletion', 'DiGeorge syndrome results from a 22q11 deletion causing thymic aplasia or hypoplasia, and the resulting T cell deficiency underlies the immunodeficiency. Phagocyte, B cell, and complement defects have different genetic causes.'),
('37b66da3-5e5a-59c4-9f1c-c21d39294210'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Bruton disease)?', '["Complement deficiency","T cell deficiency","B cell deficiency with low immunoglobulins","Phagocyte deficiency","recurrent bacterial infections after 6 months of age"]'::jsonb, 'B cell deficiency with low immunoglobulins', 'Bruton disease is caused by a BTK mutation that blocks B cell maturation, so immunoglobulin levels are very low and recurrent bacterial infections appear after maternal antibody wanes around 6 months of age. T cell and phagocyte function remain intact.'),
('297b2234-7163-521f-a78a-1d186fb4ca33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is common variable immunodeficiency (CVID)?', '["SCID resulting in decreased functional activity","Low immunoglobulins with impaired antibody responses","DiGeorge syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","X-linked agammaglobulinemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","presenting in adults leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low immunoglobulins with impaired antibody responses', 'CVID is defined by low immunoglobulins and impaired antibody responses, typically presenting in adults with recurrent sinopulmonary infections. It differs from SCID and DiGeorge syndrome, which involve major T cell defects, and from Bruton disease, which presents in infancy.'),
('7c897e77-6223-5b2e-9d91-af5c9c63960d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is chronic granulomatous disease (CGD)?', '["T cell deficiency","B cell deficiency","causing inability to kill certain bacteria and fungi","Complement deficiency","Phagocyte defect in NADPH oxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Phagocyte defect in NADPH oxidase', 'CGD arises from an NADPH oxidase defect in phagocytes, so neutrophils cannot generate the respiratory burst needed to kill catalase-positive bacteria and fungi, leading to recurrent granuloma-forming infections. T cell, B cell, and complement functions remain intact.'),
('b74dd586-524a-531e-890d-00ca30eed5bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is leukocyte adhesion deficiency (LAD)?', '["Defect in integrins (CD18) preventing leukocyte adhesion and migration to infection sites","Deficiency of complement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Defect in NK cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Defect in lymphocyte maturation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Defect in phagocyte killing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Defect in integrins (CD18) preventing leukocyte adhesion and migration to infection sites', 'LAD results from defective integrins (CD18), so leukocytes cannot firmly adhere to endothelium and migrate into infected tissues, producing marked neutrophilia but poor pus formation. It is distinct from defects of phagocyte killing, NK cells, or lymphocyte maturation.'),
('1aa19e97-e6e0-59f9-9719-df5c7bc515b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is Chediak-Higashi syndrome?', '["Defect in lysosomal trafficking causing giant granules in neutrophils and impaired phagocytosis","Complement deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","B cell deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","T cell deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granule defect in neutrophils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Defect in lysosomal trafficking causing giant granules in neutrophils and impaired phagocytosis', 'Chediak-Higashi syndrome is caused by a defect in lysosomal trafficking, producing giant cytoplasmic granules in neutrophils and impaired phagocyte killing. The characteristic giant granules distinguish it from simple granule deficiencies and from T cell, B cell, or complement defects.'),
('fd115502-dc0c-5545-bb5f-beae02ea8a37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is HIV/AIDS?', '["HIV infects CD4+ T cells","An autoimmune disease","causing progressive immunodeficiency and AIDS when CD4 count drops <200 cells/uL","A primary immunodeficiency","A hypersensitivity reaction"]'::jsonb, 'HIV infects CD4+ T cells', 'HIV depletes CD4+ T cells, and when the CD4 count falls below 200 cells/uL the resulting profound immunodeficiency defines AIDS. It is an acquired, not primary, immunodeficiency and does not represent autoimmunity or hypersensitivity.'),
('044a1824-9904-5537-a0c9-f243403a97d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the normal CD4 count?', '["100-300 cells/uL","500-1500 cells/uL","<200 cells/uL","200-500 cells/uL","1000-2000 cells/uL"]'::jsonb, '500-1500 cells/uL', 'A normal CD4 count ranges from 500 to 1500 cells/uL. Values below 200 cells/uL are diagnostic of AIDS, while counts in the 200-500 range indicate advancing HIV disease.'),
('03db9c20-dfbd-5e4a-b25d-59e659719bc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the hallmark opportunistic infections in AIDS?', '["candidiasis resulting in decreased functional activity","CMV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MAC mediated by specific receptor activation","toxoplasmosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia', 'Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia is the classic hallmark opportunistic infection of AIDS, appearing when CD4 counts fall below 200 cells/uL. Candidiasis, CMV, MAC, and toxoplasmosis also occur in AIDS but at different CD4 thresholds.'),
('e3ce6cc7-4eb9-5a66-8242-4f85f4c60c32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the structure of lymphatic capillaries?', '["Fenestrated endothelium with basement membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastic fibers only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Continuous endothelium with tight junctions mediated by specific receptor activation","Blind-ended vessels with overlapping endothelial cells and no basement membrane","Smooth muscle-lined vessels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blind-ended vessels with overlapping endothelial cells and no basement membrane', 'Lymphatic capillaries are blind-ended vessels lined by overlapping endothelial cells lacking a basement membrane, so the overlapping flaps open to admit interstitial fluid. The absent basement membrane and absence of smooth muscle distinguish them from blood capillaries.'),
('cb208898-448a-5046-b078-207df3bd01d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are anchoring filaments in lymphatic capillaries?', '["Elastic fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Contractile filaments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filaments that attach lymphatic endothelial cells to surrounding connective tissue","Structural filaments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","opening gaps when edema forms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filaments that attach lymphatic endothelial cells to surrounding connective tissue', 'Anchoring filaments tether lymphatic endothelial cells to the surrounding connective tissue, and when interstitial pressure rises they pull the flaps open, enlarging the gaps for fluid entry. They are elastic fibrillin-containing filaments, not contractile structures.'),
('78488677-750f-544a-b9c4-2d7c0505cef1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of lymphatic valves?', '["Regulate lymph composition resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevent backflow of lymph and ensure one-way flow toward the venous system","Produce lymphocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Filter lymph without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pump lymph leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent backflow of lymph and ensure one-way flow toward the venous system', 'Lymphatic valves are folds of endothelium that prevent retrograde flow, ensuring lymph moves one-way toward the venous system. Lymph propulsion depends on these valves plus extrinsic compression and contraction of lymphangions, not on filtration or lymph production.'),
('b7b081b7-2913-5c75-b4a0-49f484a8e9a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are lymphangions?', '["Segments of lymphatic vessels between valves with smooth muscle that contract spontaneously","Lymphatic trunks accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphatic sinuses mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatic capillaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymph nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Segments of lymphatic vessels between valves with smooth muscle that contract spontaneously', 'A lymphangion is the segment of a lymphatic vessel between two valves; its smooth muscle contracts spontaneously, helping to pump lymph forward. This is distinct from trunks, sinuses, capillaries, and lymph nodes.'),
('7b1d49b2-9b33-5483-8b93-324d0f3957fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cisterna chyli?', '["A lymph node resulting in decreased functional activity","The thoracic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The right lymphatic duct mediated by specific receptor activation","A lymphatic capillary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A dilated sac at the lower end of the thoracic duct where lumbar and intestinal lymph trunks converge"]'::jsonb, 'A dilated sac at the lower end of the thoracic duct where lumbar and intestinal lymph trunks converge', 'The cisterna chyli is a dilated sac at the lower end of the thoracic duct, formed by the convergence of the lumbar and intestinal lymph trunks. It collects lymph from the lower limbs, pelvis, abdomen, and gut before it ascends in the thoracic duct.'),
('400bc610-8558-52b5-87b2-c808e729f928'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the thoracic duct?', '["The cisterna chyli resulting in decreased functional activity","A lymph node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The right lymphatic duct mediated by specific receptor activation","The largest lymphatic vessel that drains lymph from most of the body into the left subclavian vein","The lymphatic capillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The largest lymphatic vessel that drains lymph from most of the body into the left subclavian vein', 'The thoracic duct is the largest lymphatic vessel, collecting lymph from most of the body and emptying into the left subclavian vein near the left internal jugular junction. The right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper quadrant.'),
('206d986d-e380-5a50-b022-c3180c026016'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the right lymphatic duct?', '["The thoracic duct resulting in decreased functional activity","A lymphatic capillary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The cisterna chyli mediated by specific receptor activation","A lymph node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A short duct that drains lymph from the right upper quadrant into the right subclavian vein"]'::jsonb, 'A short duct that drains lymph from the right upper quadrant into the right subclavian vein', 'The right lymphatic duct is a short vessel draining lymph from the right upper quadrant (right side of the head, neck, thorax, and right upper limb) into the right subclavian vein. The remainder of the body drains via the thoracic duct.'),
('04d4c235-5830-5864-b824-de00d78e0280'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the composition of lymph?', '["Only water","Blood without RBCs","lymphocytes","Interstitial fluid","and chylomicrons (from the gut)"]'::jsonb, 'Interstitial fluid', 'Lymph is essentially interstitial fluid that has entered lymphatic capillaries, containing water, solutes, and variable numbers of lymphocytes, plus chylomicrons absorbed from the gut. It is not simply blood without RBCs, since its protein content and composition differ from plasma.'),
('bb962472-e92c-5e24-bc70-3ba52acbb975'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the direction of lymph flow?', '["and ducts to veins resulting in decreased functional activity","collecting vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","lymph nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","trunks without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From interstitial space through lymphatic capillaries"]'::jsonb, 'From interstitial space through lymphatic capillaries', 'Lymph flows from the interstitial space into lymphatic capillaries, then on through collecting vessels, trunks, and ducts, finally entering the venous system. Structures such as lymph nodes and trunks are stations along this route rather than the direction of flow.'),
('d3e57307-4b26-5976-a2e4-9fcec4d5c6d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of the lymphatic system in immune surveillance?', '["Filters blood resulting in decreased functional activity","Regulates temperature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transports antigens and dendritic cells from tissues to draining lymph nodes for T cell activation","Digests nutrients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produces hormones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transports antigens and dendritic cells from tissues to draining lymph nodes for T cell activation', 'The lymphatic system continuously samples interstitial fluid, transporting antigens and dendritic cells to draining lymph nodes, where naive T cells are activated. This surveillance pathway is central to initiating adaptive immune responses.'),
('4262e882-7f18-5795-aed1-c68da138c8a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histological appearance of reactive follicular hyperplasia in a lymph node?', '["Fibrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Enlarged follicles with prominent germinal centers","Atrophic follicles mediated by specific receptor activation","many mitoses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and tingible body macrophages leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enlarged follicles with prominent germinal centers', 'Reactive follicular hyperplasia shows enlarged follicles with prominent germinal centers, numerous mitoses, and tingible body macrophages. Atrophic follicles and fibrosis indicate different pathological processes.'),
('502ecbe1-e591-5fec-8ff9-f0891165e7db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the starry-sky pattern in histology?', '["A normal lymph node finding resulting in decreased functional activity","A granulomatous pattern accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","with macrophages containing apoptotic debris mediated by specific receptor activation","A pattern seen in reactive germinal centers and Burkitt lymphoma","A fibrotic pattern leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A pattern seen in reactive germinal centers and Burkitt lymphoma', 'The starry-sky pattern arises when tingible body macrophages, the paler ''stars'' containing apoptotic debris, are scattered among dark small lymphocytes. It is seen in reactive germinal centers and classically in Burkitt lymphoma.'),
('c2296038-6a37-5e7d-ad4c-776a2895238a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histological appearance of a granuloma?', '["A collection of fibroblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","A collection of epithelioid macrophages (with pale pink cytoplasm) surrounded by lymphocytes","A collection of eosinophils mediated by specific receptor activation","A collection of plasma cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A collection of neutrophils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A collection of epithelioid macrophages (with pale pink cytoplasm) surrounded by lymphocytes', 'A granuloma is a nodular aggregate of epithelioid macrophages with abundant pale pink cytoplasm, often surrounded by a rim of lymphocytes. Collections of neutrophils, eosinophils, plasma cells, or fibroblasts represent different inflammatory responses.'),
('44cbe75a-b9d3-51eb-b6b7-d2944199cb58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are Langhans giant cells?', '["Multinucleated giant cells with horseshoe-shaped nuclear arrangement seen in granulomas","Tumor giant cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reed-Sternberg cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Plasma cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal macrophages leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multinucleated giant cells with horseshoe-shaped nuclear arrangement seen in granulomas', 'Langhans giant cells are multinucleated macrophages with nuclei arranged in a horseshoe or ring at the periphery, characteristically found within granulomas. Reed-Sternberg cells, tumor giant cells, and plasma cells have different morphologies and contexts.'),
('78a33021-141e-5726-9d0f-215b68d28089'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the difference between caseating and non-caseating granulomas?', '["Caseating is benign resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-caseating is malignant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","difference mediated by specific receptor activation","Caseating has more lymphocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Caseating has central necrosis (TB); non-caseating lacks necrosis (sarcoidosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Caseating has central necrosis (TB); non-caseating lacks necrosis (sarcoidosis)', 'Caseating granulomas show central caseous necrosis, typical of tuberculosis, whereas non-caseating granulomas lack necrosis and are characteristic of sarcoidosis. The distinction is based on the presence or absence of central necrosis, not on cell counts or benignity.'),
('f728f896-f9e4-5089-8153-652c60746a24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of lymph node in sarcoidosis?', '["Fibrosis and calcification resulting in decreased functional activity","Reactive follicular hyperplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Non-caseating granulomas (naked granulomas) with epithelioid cells and Langhans giant cells","Caseating granulomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-caseating granulomas (naked granulomas) with epithelioid cells and Langhans giant cells', 'Sarcoidosis produces non-caseating ''naked'' granulomas composed of epithelioid cells and Langhans giant cells, with little surrounding lymphocytic inflammation. Caseating granulomas point to tuberculosis, and reactive follicular hyperplasia reflects nonspecific antigenic stimulation.'),
('3eeb2d37-be00-5c47-af5f-353b1edca1f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of mantle cell lymphoma?', '["Large transformed cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Monotonous proliferation of small to medium lymphocytes with irregular nuclei (mantle zone pattern)","Starry-sky pattern mediated by specific receptor activation","Granulomatous pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Follicular pattern leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monotonous proliferation of small to medium lymphocytes with irregular nuclei (mantle zone pattern)', 'Mantle cell lymphoma shows a monotonous population of small to medium lymphocytes with irregular nuclei expanding the mantle zone. Large transformed cells suggest diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, while a starry-sky pattern suggests Burkitt lymphoma.'),
('fe37458f-b2cf-59b0-847f-e080eeaf1c12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of Burkitt lymphoma?', '["Anaplastic cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Monotonous medium-sized cells with starry-sky pattern (tingible body macrophages) and high mitotic rate","Plasmacytic differentiation mediated by specific receptor activation","Follicular pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nodular sclerosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monotonous medium-sized cells with starry-sky pattern (tingible body macrophages) and high mitotic rate', 'Burkitt lymphoma is composed of monotonous medium-sized cells with a high mitotic rate and a starry-sky pattern produced by tingible body macrophages. This morphology distinguishes it from follicular lymphomas and plasmacytic tumors.'),
('9460cdfc-86a9-50ee-8e69-ad5aa97e92fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of multiple myeloma?', '["Erythroid hyperplasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphocytic infiltration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myeloid hyperplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plasma cell infiltration of bone marrow with sheets of cells with eccentric nuclei and perinuclear hof"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma cell infiltration of bone marrow with sheets of cells with eccentric nuclei and perinuclear hof', 'Multiple myeloma is marked by sheets of plasma cells in the bone marrow, recognized by eccentric nuclei, clumped chromatin, and a perinuclear hof. Erythroid or myeloid hyperplasia reflects different marrow pathology.'),
('4f445fe5-a52b-5647-bc37-733e64221d66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is allograft rejection?', '["Graft trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Immune response by the recipient against antigens on the donor organ","Graft infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Tolerance of the graft without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Graft acceptance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immune response by the recipient against antigens on the donor organ', 'Allograft rejection is an immune response mounted by the recipient against antigens, chiefly MHC molecules, on the donor organ. Graft infection, trauma, and tolerance are separate phenomena with different mechanisms and outcomes.'),
('c213e9a1-3a92-5ddc-b8ca-462f176d9513'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is hyperacute rejection?', '["Chronic rejection resulting in decreased functional activity","Graft-versus-host disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute rejection mediated by specific receptor activation","Immediate rejection (minutes to hours) due to pre-existing antibodies against donor blood type or MHC","T cell-mediated rejection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immediate rejection (minutes to hours) due to pre-existing antibodies against donor blood type or MHC', 'Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes to hours because pre-existing antibodies against donor ABO blood type or MHC antigens bind graft endothelium, triggering complement activation and vascular thrombosis. Acute and chronic rejection develop later by different mechanisms.'),
('581ab213-f50f-521d-837c-7cc492514e18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is acute rejection?', '["T cell-mediated rejection days to weeks after transplant","Graft-versus-host disease","Infection","Antibody-mediated immediate rejection","Chronic gradual loss of function"]'::jsonb, 'T cell-mediated rejection days to weeks after transplant', 'Acute rejection is aT cell-mediated response occurring days to weeks after transplantation, targeting donor MHC on graft endothelium and parenchyma. Hyperacute rejection is antibody-mediated and immediate, while chronic rejection evolves over months to years.'),
('a1f093aa-c8c0-5504-9d06-41f14551e4b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is chronic rejection?', '["Immediate rejection","Gradual fibrosis and graft dysfunction over months to years","driven by both cellular and humoral mechanisms","Acute cellular rejection","Hyperacute rejection"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual fibrosis and graft dysfunction over months to years', 'Chronic rejection produces gradual fibrosis, ischemia, and graft dysfunction over months to years, driven by both cellular and humoral mechanisms. It differs from the immediate antibody-mediated hyperacute rejection and the T cell-mediated acute rejection.'),
('f7b822ff-fc83-5d8d-b493-f410529ee122'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the main histocompatibility antigens driving rejection?', '["Tissue-specific antigens","MHC class I and II molecules (HLA in humans)","Blood group antigens","All of the above","Minor histocompatibility antigens"]'::jsonb, 'MHC class I and II molecules (HLA in humans)', 'The major histocompatibility antigens are MHC class I and II molecules (HLA in humans), which present peptides to T cells and are the principal targets of rejection. Blood group antigens and minor histocompatibility antigens play lesser roles.'),
('0d73b6a2-c38a-5c4b-91d8-7073976ddf20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is HLA matching?', '["Typing of human leukocyte antigens to minimize donor-recipient differences before transplant","Pre-transplant infection screen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tissue typing mediated by specific receptor activation","Post-transplant monitoring without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood typing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Typing of human leukocyte antigens to minimize donor-recipient differences before transplant', 'HLA matching involves typing recipient and donor human leukocyte antigens to minimize differences that would trigger rejection. Blood typing, infection screening, and post-transplant monitoring serve different purposes.'),
('f9583afb-cfcd-5457-bbea-23ace8aa295a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the mechanism of action of cyclosporine?', '["Blocks T cell co-stimulation","blocking IL-2 transcription and T cell activation","Inhibits mTOR","Depletes T cells","Inhibits calcineurin"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits calcineurin', 'Cyclosporine binds cyclophilin and inhibits calcineurin, thereby preventing NFAT nuclear translocation and blocking IL-2 transcription and T cell activation. It does not directly block costimulation, deplete T cells, or inhibit mTOR.'),
('82f475ef-ba74-5f37-afbf-63f8c11324b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the mechanism of tacrolimus (FK506)?', '["Depletes T cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibits mTOR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibits calcineurin (like cyclosporine) by binding FKBP12","Blocks IL-2 receptor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibits purine synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits calcineurin (like cyclosporine) by binding FKBP12', 'Tacrolimus binds the immunophilin FKBP12 and the complex inhibits calcineurin, blocking dephosphorylation of NFAT and thereby preventing IL-2 transcription in T cells. Cyclosporine shares the same calcineurin target but binds cyclophilin instead. mTOR blockade belongs to rapamycin, while IL-2 receptor blockade is the action of basiliximab or daclizumab.'),
('4ac45631-4481-5acf-ab25-5c18e6c4c2de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the mechanism of rapamycin (sirolimus)?', '["Blocks IL-2 receptor","blocking cell proliferation","Inhibits mTOR (mammalian target of rapamycin)","Depletes T cells","Inhibits calcineurin"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits mTOR (mammalian target of rapamycin)', 'Rapamycin (sirolimus) forms a complex with FKBP12 that inhibits mTOR, blocking cell-cycle progression and proliferation downstream of IL-2 and growth factor signaling. Calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, cyclosporine) and IL-2 receptor blockers act earlier during T-cell activation rather than at this downstream point.'),
('601266e8-2ca5-5779-bfce-31fb4a0fb95a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the immunophenotype of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)?', '["CD23+","CD3+ T cells","CD19+ small B cells","CD20+","CD5+"]'::jsonb, 'CD5+', 'CLL cells are small mature B cells that co-express CD19, weak CD20, CD23, and characteristically CD5, an aberrant marker not normally found on B cells. The CD5 positivity, together with dim surface immunoglobulin, separates CLL from other mature B-cell neoplasms, whereas CD3 would mark T cells and not aB-cell leukemia.'),
('e13ed201-4c19-5af4-a695-0fe29fb42c56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the immunophenotype of mantle cell lymphoma?', '["CD20+","CD5+","cyclin D1+","None of the above","SOX11+"]'::jsonb, 'CD5+', 'Mantle cell lymphoma is a CD5-positive B-cell lymphoma associated with t(11;14) causing cyclin D1 overexpression, so CD5 is the defining B-cell surface marker listed here; SOX11 and cyclin D1 are supporting translocation-linked findings. CD20 is present on nearly all B-cell lymphomas and does not provide specificity.'),
('b00c7227-2407-5ea3-96b3-65f14ee730c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the immunophenotype of follicular lymphoma?', '["BCL6+","BCL2+","CD10+","CD20+","CD5+"]'::jsonb, 'CD10+', 'Follicular lymphoma recapitulates germinal-center B cells and characteristically co-expresses CD10, BCL6, and BCL2, with CD10 being the cell-surface marker named here. CD20 is present on all B-cell lymphomas and CD5 positivity would suggest CLL or mantle cell lymphoma instead.'),
('efda86bb-b0d1-5bd9-9225-16ddf288bc6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the immunophenotype of Hodgkin lymphoma (classical)?', '["CD20+","with weak or absent CD20","CD15+","CD30+","PAX5+"]'::jsonb, 'CD15+', 'Reed-Sternberg cells of classical Hodgkin lymphoma are typically CD15 positive and CD30 positive with weak or absent CD20, reflecting their abnormal B-cell phenotype. CD15 is the diagnostic hallmark named in the stem, whereas CD30, PAX5, and residual CD20 are less specific or variably expressed.'),
('755026c5-a7f0-531d-9a4c-b7f0714b953b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the immunophenotype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL)?', '["CD23+","CD10+ (precursor B or T cell markers)","TdT+","CD5+","CD34+"]'::jsonb, 'TdT+', 'The lymphoblasts of ALL are immature precursors that express terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase (TdT), a marker of early lymphoid differentiation present in both B- and T-lineage disease. CD10, CD34, and lineage markers support subclassification but are not specific to the lymphoblast stage, while CD5 and CD23 belong to mature T cells and CLL.'),
('2ddfd286-e57f-5a3e-aff5-d3a91abbca3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the immunophenotype of acute myeloid leukemia (AML)?', '["CD3+","CD117+","CD33+","MPO+","CD13+"]'::jsonb, 'CD33+', 'AML blasts are myeloid and typically express CD33 together with markers such as CD13, CD117, and cytoplasmic MPO; CD33 is the characteristic surface antigen asked for here. CD3 marks T cells and would exclude a myeloid neoplasm, and the remaining markers, while myelomonocytic, are less defining than CD33.'),
('157bb02d-ed67-5bc6-b4c9-dda2974a8b25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the immunophenotype of multiple myeloma?', '["MUM1+","CD138+","CD20+","CD38+","with monoclonal immunoglobulin restriction"]'::jsonb, 'CD138+', 'Myeloma plasma cells are terminally differentiated B cells that characteristically express CD138 and CD38 while downregulating CD20; CD138 is the distinguishing marker named here. MUM1 is a supporting plasma-cell transcription factor, whereas CD20 is typical of earlier B-cell stages and is usually lost.'),
('6827ebe2-e1f1-518b-b8f2-134bf6d10493'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the immunophenotype of NK cells?', '["CD56+","CD57+","CD3+","CD3-","CD16+"]'::jsonb, 'CD3-', 'Natural killer cells belong to innate immunity and, unlike T cells, do not express surface CD3; they are identified by positivity for CD16 and CD56. The absence of CD3 is the defining immunophenotypic contrast with T lymphocytes, since CD56, CD57, and CD16 are NK markers that do not distinguish them from their function.'),
('318a06d5-519b-5c41-9d79-53fade483b53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the immunophenotype of Langerhans cells?', '["Langerin (CD207)+","CD3+ T cells","S100+","CD1a+","CD20+ B cells"]'::jsonb, 'CD1a+', 'Langerhans cells are epidermal dendritic cells that characteristically express CD1a, S100, and langerin (CD207), with CD1a being the defining marker named in the stem; they also contain Birbeck granules on electron microscopy. CD3 and CD20 mark T cells and B cells respectively, which Langerhans cells are not.'),
('e3e3a148-c6e2-5239-8d99-d5a62b2b9428'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the immunophenotype of follicular dendritic cells?', '["CD20+ B cells","CD35+ (complement receptors)","CD3+ T cells","CD21+","CD23+"]'::jsonb, 'CD21+', 'Follicular dendritic cells in germinal centers express CD21, CD35, and CD23, receptors that trap immune complexes and present antigen to B cells, with CD21 being the defining marker named here. These cells are not T cells (CD3 negative) and are distinct from the CD20-positive mature B cells of the follicle.'),
('85a41c09-c566-533a-83f2-4e96a25ddee6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histological appearance of the bone marrow on biopsy?', '["Cellular tissue with hematopoietic islands separated by sinuses and fat","Only blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only connective tissue mediated by specific receptor activation","Only bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only fat cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cellular tissue with hematopoietic islands separated by sinuses and fat', 'A normal trephine biopsy shows islands of developing myeloid, erythroid, and megakaryocytic cells interspersed with marrow sinusoids and adipocytes, producing the typical patchwork of cells and fat. Descriptions citing only bone, fat, vessels, or connective tissue omit the hematopoietic and sinusoidal components that define active marrow.'),
('e30cc56f-a10f-560a-b1ae-20b9f037d89d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the normal bone marrow cellularity at age 20?', '["About 25%","About 100% (no fat)","About 50%","About 75%","About 10%"]'::jsonb, 'About 75%', 'Bone marrow cellularity is highest in infancy and declines progressively through adult life, and the commonly used approximation places it near 70-80% in a 20-year-old, making about 75% a realistic figure. A marrow packed with virtually no fat reflects the newborn period rather than age 20, and the much lower percentages pertain to older adults.'),
('f250a0c2-dab7-514e-bf3a-9c01700e2908'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the myeloid to erythroid (M:E) ratio in normal bone marrow?', '["1:5 resulting in decreased functional activity","1:1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 2:1 to 4:1","5:1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","10:1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 2:1 to 4:1', 'In the normal adult marrow the myeloid series outnumbers the erythroid series by roughly 2:1 to 4:1 because of the larger myeloid compartment. Ratios of 1:1 or less indicate erythroid dominance and ratios of 5:1 or more indicate myeloid dominance, both changes from the normal resting proportion.'),
('c15ea2be-4719-5967-9911-4cd8500b07f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of the bone marrow in aplastic anemia?', '["Severely hypocellular marrow with mostly fat and few hematopoietic cells","Gelatinous transformation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myelofibrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Leukemic infiltration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypercellular marrow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severely hypocellular marrow with mostly fat and few hematopoietic cells', 'Aplastic anemia results from failure of the hematopoietic stem cell compartment, producing a markedly hypocellular, fatty marrow with only scattered residual cells. In contrast, leukemia causes hypercellular infiltration, myelofibrosis shows fibrous marrow stroma, and gelatinous transformation reflects severe starvation rather than aplastic anemia.'),
('ce0fafe8-b7c9-5ac5-a4a7-1978f6d0d75d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of myelofibrosis?', '["Hypercellular myeloid hyperplasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Leukemic infiltration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal bone marrow mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone marrow fibrosis with megakaryocyte hyperplasia and extramedullary hematopoiesis","Aplastic marrow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow fibrosis with megakaryocyte hyperplasia and extramedullary hematopoiesis', 'Myelofibrosis is a myeloproliferative disorder in which the marrow is replaced by collagen fibrosis with clusters of atypical megakaryocytes, and the spleen and liver then take over blood production, giving extramedullary hematopoiesis. Simple myeloid hyperplasia, leukemic infiltration, or an aplastic pattern do not show the fibrotic stroma central to this disease.'),
('2c4dabcd-cc09-5743-8ad2-12bd766869cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of the thymus in a child?', '["Large","Atrophic","fatty tissue","prominent Hassall''s corpuscles","cellular gland with distinct cortex (dark) and medulla (pale)"]'::jsonb, 'Large', 'The thymus is relatively large in childhood, reaching its greatest relative size before puberty while actively supporting T-cell maturation. It subsequently involutes and becomes fatty in adulthood, so a large, active gland is the expected finding in a child rather than an atrophic, fat-replaced organ.'),
('2a40c216-bdf2-58ba-af99-ec980f6a53b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of the thymus in an adult?', '["Involuted with fatty infiltration","Completely replaced by fat","Same as a child","decreased cortex","and preserved medulla with Hassall''s corpuscles"]'::jsonb, 'Involuted with fatty infiltration', 'Beginning around puberty the thymus slowly involutes, losing cortex and becoming infiltrated by adipose tissue while retaining strands of epithelial and lymphoid elements. It is therefore an involuted, fatty gland in the adult rather than an active organ identical to that seen in childhood.'),
('031bca49-c35c-5643-b344-8295059919f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of a lymph node in an elderly person?', '["Normal","follicular atrophy","and increased fibrosis","Hyperplasia","Decreased cellularity"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased cellularity', 'With advancing age lymph nodes lose follicles and lymphocytes, so overall cellularity falls while stroma and fibrous tissue increase. This age-related involution contrasts with the hyperplastic change that occurs during active immune responses, so decreased cellularity with fibrosis rather than hyperplasia is the expected senile pattern.'),
('304f0327-addc-51f5-bde5-ccbfb7b36ab8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of the spleen with age?', '["Increased red pulp resulting in decreased functional activity","change accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enlarged white pulp mediated by specific receptor activation","Amyloid deposition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased white pulp size and increased fibrous tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased white pulp size and increased fibrous tissue', 'As the spleen ages, the white pulp shrinks and fibrous tissue accumulates, reflecting reduced immunologic activity and involuting lymphatic tissue. An enlarged white pulp would indicate active immune stimulation, while increased red pulp or amyloid deposition are features of specific diseases rather than simple aging.'),
('c4c4b564-1970-54c7-b4e8-a28571ee6d57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of MALT in Peyer''s patches?', '["and an overlying dome of M cells","Organized B-cell follicles with germinal centers","Lymph nodes","Diffuse lymphocytes only","interfollicular T-cell zones"]'::jsonb, 'Organized B-cell follicles with germinal centers', 'Peyer''s patches are mucosa-associated lymphoid aggregates composed of organized B-cell follicles with germinal centers, overlain by M cells that sample luminal antigen and supported by interfollicular T-cell zones. This follicular organization distinguishes them from mere diffuse lymphocytes and from lymph nodes, which lack the M-cell dome.'),
('928a661f-91c5-507a-9277-2e7f843dd5f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is flow cytometry?', '["and antigen expression resulting in decreased functional activity","A microscopy technique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A technique that uses lasers to count and characterize cells in suspension based on size","granularity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A histological stain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A technique that uses lasers to count and characterize cells in suspension based on size', 'Flow cytometry passes fluorescently labeled cells single-file through a laser, measuring their size, granularity, and fluorescence to count and classify them. It analyzes cells in suspension rather than fixed tissue sections, so it is neither a microscopy nor a staining technique, and added channels detect surface antigens to define populations.'),
('6f37b396-80ff-5c54-8de6-2eb6f1462214'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is forward scatter (FSC) in flow cytometry?', '["A measure of cell viability","A measure of cell granularity","A measure of cell complexity","A measure of fluorescence intensity","A measure of cell size"]'::jsonb, 'A measure of cell size', 'Forward scatter reflects how much laser light passes around the cell and is proportional to cell size, larger cells scattering more light in the forward direction. Granularity and internal complexity are instead assessed by side scatter, and fluorescence intensity reflects bound antigen dye rather than physical size.'),
('b620290e-0b9d-55a5-9ab8-48d35e786c9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is side scatter (SSC) in flow cytometry?', '["A measure of cell size resulting in decreased functional activity","A measure of viability accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A measure of cell granularity or internal complexity","A measure of DNA content without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A measure of fluorescence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A measure of cell granularity or internal complexity', 'Side scatter measures light deflected at right angles by internal granules and organelles, reflecting cell granularity and internal complexity; granule-rich neutrophils therefore produce high side scatter while lymphocytes produce low side scatter. Cell size is captured by forward scatter, and DNA or fluorescence requires labeling rather than scatter.'),
('55e3c350-ae1b-51e4-bab7-ae12531ab759'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is a fluorochrome in flow cytometry?', '["A type of laser resulting in decreased functional activity","A fluorescent dye conjugated to antibodies for detecting specific antigens","A cell type mediated by specific receptor activation","A filter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A detector leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fluorescent dye conjugated to antibodies for detecting specific antigens', 'A fluorochrome is a fluorescent dye, usually coupled to a monoclonal antibody, that emits light of a specific wavelength when excited by the laser, allowing detection of the bound antigen on the cell. It is a labeled reporter rather than the laser source, filter, or detector in the instrument.'),
('6205edce-4e2b-5dba-9961-9596ddea8ded'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is gating in flow cytometry?', '["A physical filter resulting in decreased functional activity","A type of laser accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Selecting a specific cell population for analysis based on scatter or marker expression","A histological stain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A computer algorithm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Selecting a specific cell population for analysis based on scatter or marker expression', 'Gating is the step of drawing boundaries around a cell population of interest, for example lymphocytes, based on forward/side scatter or specific markers so that further analysis is confined to that subset. It is an analytic selection rather than a physical filter, laser, or stain, and it excludes debris, red cells, and unrelated populations.'),
('451aeeca-74fb-5bd4-8087-b2bfddaf12f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is a compensation in flow cytometry?', '["A filter resulting in decreased functional activity","Correction for spectral overlap between fluorochromes","A type of laser mediated by specific receptor activation","A gating strategy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A mathematical correction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Correction for spectral overlap between fluorochromes', 'When two fluorochromes have overlapping emission spectra, signal can leak from one detector into another, and compensation is the mathematical subtraction that corrects this spectral overlap so each antigen readout is specific. It is a numerical adjustment applied to the fluorescence data rather than a filter or a gating approach, and it matters most in multi-color panels.'),
('f88d82be-5d15-526a-ad93-3d3d0c9e5d0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is immunophenotyping by flow cytometry used for?', '["Diagnosing and classifying leukemias and lymphomas by cell surface marker expression","Measuring hemoglobin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Counting blood cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Testing for infectious diseases without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Assessing kidney function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diagnosing and classifying leukemias and lymphomas by cell surface marker expression', 'Immunophenotyping uses panels of antibodies against CD markers to identify the lineage and maturation stage of leukemic or lymphomatous cells, enabling diagnosis and subclassification. It depends on cell-surface antigen expression rather than simple cell counts or serum measurements and is not used to assess hemoglobin, infections, or renal function.'),
('259561b6-c952-5fbe-a16d-1f89c7c12819'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the CD45 (leukocyte common antigen) used for?', '["Identifying NK cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Identifying T cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Identifying B cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Identifying leukocytes and gating them away from debris and RBCs","Identifying myeloid cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Identifying leukocytes and gating them away from debris and RBCs', 'CD45, the leukocyte common antigen, is present on essentially all white blood cells, so a CD45 versus side-scatter plot identifies leukocytes and separates them from platelets, debris, and red cells that lack CD45. It is a pan-leukocyte marker rather than one restricted to T, B, NK, or myeloid cells, and it anchors the gating strategy in flow cytometry.'),
('750da813-b46d-568f-a7f0-64508461a056'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cytology of a small lymphocyte?', '["Plasmacytoid cell","clumped chromatin","Small cell (7-10 um) with round nucleus","scant basophilic cytoplasm","Large cell with open chromatin"]'::jsonb, 'Small cell (7-10 um) with round nucleus', 'A small lymphocyte measures about 7-10 um and has a round, dense nucleus with clumped chromatin surrounded by a thin rim of pale basophilic cytoplasm. This compact mature appearance contrasts with larger blasts showing open chromatin, and plasmacytoid differentiation is not a feature of the resting small lymphocyte.'),
('8929ae5a-115b-5ea5-b1cc-01c7337bcde5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cytology of a plasma cell?', '["perinuclear hof (Golgi)","Small lymphocyte","Oval cell with eccentric nucleus","and basophilic cytoplasm","clock-face chromatin"]'::jsonb, 'Oval cell with eccentric nucleus', 'A plasma cell is an oval cell with an eccentrically placed nucleus showing clumped clock-face chromatin, abundant basophilic cytoplasm, and a pale perinuclear Golgi hof. The eccentric nucleus is the hallmark that distinguishes it from small lymphocytes, which have a central round nucleus and scant cytoplasm.'),
('03b7c638-b58d-5ed4-b430-633c9b6507b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cytology of a monocyte?', '["Small round cell resulting in decreased functional activity","delicate chromatin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","gray-blue cytoplasm with fine granules mediated by specific receptor activation","Multi-lobed nucleus cell without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large cell (15-20 um) with kidney-shaped or folded nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Large cell (15-20 um) with kidney-shaped or folded nucleus', 'A monocyte is the largest normal peripheral leukocyte, about 15-20 um, and is identified by its kidney-shaped or folded nucleus with delicate chromatin and gray-blue cytoplasm containing fine granules. Its single folded nucleus distinguishes it from neutrophils with multiple lobes and from the smaller round lymphocyte.'),
('2af09de3-2f70-5c6a-9ff8-cdc33f22961c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cytology of a blast cell?', '["Large cell (15-25 um) with high N:C ratio","open (dispersed) chromatin","prominent nucleoli","Cell with clumped chromatin","scant basophilic cytoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Large cell (15-25 um) with high N:C ratio', 'Blasts are immature precursor cells that are large (about 15-25 um) with a high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, dispersed open chromatin, and prominent nucleoli. The large size together with the high N:C ratio defines primitive blast morphology, whereas mature cells show condensed clumped chromatin and proportionally more cytoplasm.'),
('63ea01a1-dce3-5eac-811d-e1221d53e23c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cytology of a myeloblast?', '["Lymphoblast resulting in decreased functional activity","Blast with Auer rods (needle-like cytoplasmic inclusions) and myeloid granules","Erythroblast mediated by specific receptor activation","Megakaryoblast without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monoblast leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blast with Auer rods (needle-like cytoplasmic inclusions) and myeloid granules', 'A myeloblast is a myeloid blast that may contain Auer rods, needle-shaped aggregates of primary granules, along with cytoplasmic granules, signaling myeloid rather than lymphoid or erythroid lineage. Auer rods are essentially diagnostic of myeloid leukemia and are not found in lymphoblasts, erythroblasts, or megakaryoblasts.'),
('d22636da-9429-5b53-8c31-41538b7657a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cytology of a lymphoblast?', '["Cell with Auer rods","Blast with scant cytoplasm","granules","possibly with vacuoles (B-ALL) or high N:C ratio (T-ALL)","Cell with prominent Golgi hof"]'::jsonb, 'Blast with scant cytoplasm', 'A lymphoblast is a primitive lymphoid cell with very scant cytoplasm and a high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, unlike the more granulated myeloblast. Lymphoblasts typically lack Auer rods, a marker of myeloid origin, and do not show the Golgi hof characteristic of plasma cells.'),
('72a18ead-29c5-5a2a-a84a-a6c929fc693f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cytology of a megakaryocyte?', '["Small lymphocyte resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Granulocyte accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Monocyte mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Erythroblast without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Very large cell (up to 100 um) with polyploid multilobed nucleus and abundant pink cytoplasm with platelet shedding"]'::jsonb, 'Very large cell (up to 100 um) with polyploid multilobed nucleus and abundant pink cytoplasm with platelet shedding', 'A megakaryocyte is the largest cell in the marrow, up to 100 um, with a polyploid multilobed nucleus and abundant pink cytoplasm whose fragments bud off as platelets. Its enormous size and platelet-shedding cytoplasm distinguish it from lymphocytes, granulocytes, monocytes, and erythroblasts.'),
('534783ac-1efb-50da-9e0f-5eadc15bcf28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cytology of a Reed-Sternberg cell?', '["Plasma cell resulting in decreased functional activity","and abundant cytoplasm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Large cell (15-45 um) with bilobed or binucleated nucleus","prominent nucleoli (owl-eye) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small lymphocyte leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large cell (15-45 um) with bilobed or binucleated nucleus', 'A Reed-Sternberg cell is a large cell (about 15-45 um) with a bilobed or binucleated nucleus containing prominent owl-eye nucleoli, the diagnostic hallmark of Hodgkin lymphoma. This pattern is not seen in small lymphocytes or plasma cells, which have smaller, rounder nuclei without the mirror-image nucleoli.'),
('eeb2d542-e04b-55da-b1a1-95230b91d5ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cytology of a histiocyte (macrophage)?', '["and possible phagocytosed debris","Large cell with oval or kidney-shaped nucleus","Small lymphocyte","Plasma cell","abundant foamy cytoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Large cell with oval or kidney-shaped nucleus', 'A histiocyte, or tissue macrophage, is a large cell with an oval or kidney-shaped nucleus and abundant cytoplasm that may contain phagocytosed debris, reflecting its scavenger function. Its large irregular nucleus and prominent cytoplasm separate it from the small lymphocyte and from the eccentric-nucleus plasma cell.'),
('537397ff-6abf-5434-a0ab-36d86e882282'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the cytology of a Langerhans cell?', '["Cell with folded","Granulocyte","grooved (coffee bean) nucleus and linear cytoplasmic inclusions (Birbeck granules on EM)","Lymphocyte","Plasma cell"]'::jsonb, 'Cell with folded', 'A Langerhans cell has a distinctive folded and grooved coffee-bean-shaped nucleus and, on electron microscopy, contains linear Birbeck granules in its cytoplasm. This morphology and the Birbeck granule content separate it from granulocytes, lymphocytes, and plasma cells, which lack such nuclear grooving; it is also positive for CD1a and langerin.'),
('ced28d85-a102-5272-abf8-65d1035b9136'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is acute inflammation characterized by histologically?', '["and hyperemia","edema","Lymphocyte infiltration","Macrophage infiltration","Neutrophil infiltration"]'::jsonb, 'Neutrophil infiltration', 'Acute inflammation is dominated by neutrophils, which are recruited early and accumulate in the affected tissue along with vascular congestion and edema. A predominance of lymphocytes and macrophages, by contrast, is the hallmark of chronic inflammation, where tissue destruction and repair take over.'),
('b1c141ae-643f-5516-96d8-75ca174fd051'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is chronic inflammation characterized by histologically?', '["Neutrophil predominance","Lymphocytes","macrophages","plasma cells","and granulomas or fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocytes', 'Chronic inflammation is characterized by a mononuclear infiltrate dominated by lymphocytes, accompanied by macrophages and plasma cells and often by fibrosis or granuloma formation. The prominence of lymphocytes over neutrophils is the key distinguishing feature, since neutrophils dominate the acute phase.'),
('b9476143-a3a1-54d0-b619-7eb41680f56c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is granulomatous inflammation?', '["Suppurative inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute inflammation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A type of chronic inflammation with organized aggregates of epithelioid macrophages","Fibrinous inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemorrhagic inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A type of chronic inflammation with organized aggregates of epithelioid macrophages', 'Granulomatous inflammation is a pattern of chronic inflammation in which activated macrophages transform into epithelioid cells forming organized aggregates, often ringed by lymphocytes and giant cells, as in tuberculosis and sarcoidosis. It differs from suppurative (pus-forming), fibrinous, and acute inflammatory patterns.'),
('852e9636-85f8-5ba0-a707-fd5d22c3d2f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is suppurative (purulent) inflammation?', '["Hemorrhagic inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrinous inflammation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Granulomatous inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","Serous inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammation with pus formation (neutrophil accumulation and tissue necrosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation with pus formation (neutrophil accumulation and tissue necrosis)', 'Suppurative (purulent) inflammation is marked by pus, a liquid exudate containing dead neutrophils, necrotic debris, and liquefactive tissue destruction, classically seen in pyogenic bacterial infection. This contrasts with granulomatous, fibrinous, serous, and hemorrhagic patterns, which lack the heavy neutrophil infiltrate that produces pus.'),
('88dc6b29-8c87-5050-be05-c9262620b351'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is fibrinous inflammation?', '["Pus formation resulting in decreased functional activity","Granuloma formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inflammation with fibrin-rich exudate due to vascular permeability","common on serosal surfaces without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serous effusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation with fibrin-rich exudate due to vascular permeability', 'Fibrinous inflammation results from markedly increased vascular permeability that lets fibrinogen leak out and polymerize into fibrin, producing a fibrin-rich exudate classically over serosal surfaces such as pericardium and pleura. This is distinct from pus formation and from the watery serous effusion seen with only mild vascular leakage.'),
('1a701f0d-e4db-58ee-82f5-6ff04a825129'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is serous inflammation?', '["Pus formation","Inflammation with thin","watery exudate (serum-like)","Fibrin deposition","from mesothelial surfaces"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation with thin', 'Serous inflammation produces a thin, watery, serum-like exudate that escapes from mesothelial surfaces when vascular permeability is only mildly increased, as in a blister or serous cavity effusion. This contrasts with the protein-rich fibrin of fibrinous inflammation and the cellular debris-filled pus of suppurative inflammation.'),
('2b904f4e-4552-59ef-a98c-386b9bec2362'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the histology of wound healing (primary vs. secondary intention)?', '["more granulation and contraction","Primary: clean edges","minimal granulation; Secondary: larger defect","Primary has more inflammation","difference"]'::jsonb, 'Primary: clean edges', 'In primary-intention healing the wound edges are clean and closely apposed, so repair is quick with minimal granulation tissue; in secondary intention a larger defect fills with abundant granulation tissue and contracts. The distinction centers on whether the edges are approximated cleanly or left to close by granulation and fibrous contraction.'),
('25d10c9a-41e2-5917-9ebe-0c0bcf8ed3ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the target of rituximab?', '["CD20 on B cells","HER2 on breast cancer","CD52 on lymphocytes","CD3 on T cells","EGFR"]'::jsonb, 'CD20 on B cells', 'Rituximab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody that targets CD20 expressed on mature B cells, leading to their depletion in B-cell lymphomas and autoimmune disease. Its specificity for CD20 distinguishes it from agents directed at HER2, CD52, CD3, or EGFR, none of which are its immunologic target.'),
('b04ccd27-986c-5977-9826-75113f798b94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the target of trastuzumab (Herceptin)?', '["EGFR resulting in decreased functional activity","PD-1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VEGF mediated by specific receptor activation","CD20 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","HER2 (ErbB2) receptor"]'::jsonb, 'HER2 (ErbB2) receptor', 'Trastuzumab (Herceptin) is a monoclonal antibody against the HER2 (ErbB2) receptor, an onco-protein overexpressed in a subset of breast cancers, and it is used when tumors test HER2-positive. It is not directed against EGFR, PD-1, VEGF, or CD20, which are the targets of other biologic agents.'),
('56b63629-ae01-5bd6-a8d7-2c102299c103'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the target of infliximab?', '["IL-6","CD20","TNF-alpha","IL-1","PD-1"]'::jsonb, 'TNF-alpha', 'Infliximab is a monoclonal antibody that neutralizes tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha), a key pro-inflammatory cytokine, and is used in rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory bowel disease, and ankylosing spondylitis. It does not target IL-6, IL-1, CD20, or PD-1, which are the targets of other biologic drugs.'),
('fab1c0ff-06d7-5fe3-96e1-51ad2de54566'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the target of checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab)?', '["VEGF resulting in decreased functional activity","CTLA-4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PD-1 on T cells (blocking PD-L1/PD-1 interaction)","TNF-alpha without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CD20 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PD-1 on T cells (blocking PD-L1/PD-1 interaction)', 'Pembrolizumab is a checkpoint inhibitor that blocks PD-1 on T cells, preventing PD-L1/PD-1 binding and restoring antitumor T-cell activity. It does not target VEGF, CTLA-4, TNF-alpha, or CD20.'),
('97bf9c91-12f1-5816-938f-1abe5afbac19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the target of ipilimumab?', '["PD-1 resulting in decreased functional activity","HER2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CTLA-4 (cytotoxic T lymphocyte-associated antigen 4)","CD20 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TNF-alpha leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CTLA-4 (cytotoxic T lymphocyte-associated antigen 4)', 'Ipilimumab is a monoclonal antibody against CTLA-4, a checkpoint receptor that suppresses T-cell activation. Anti-PD-1 agents, trastuzumab (HER2), rituximab (CD20), and anti-TNF drugs act on different targets.'),
('fb81218a-65b7-56f6-b18d-5576fd30bcba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the target of tocilizumab?', '["IL-6 receptor","CD20","TNF-alpha","PD-1","IL-1"]'::jsonb, 'IL-6 receptor', 'Tocilizumab blocks the IL-6 receptor, dampening IL-6-mediated inflammation in rheumatoid arthritis and cytokine release syndrome. CD20, TNF-alpha, PD-1, and IL-1 are targeted by other biologics.'),
('47f8b2fd-b749-5472-b7c0-626a126762e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is CAR-T cell therapy?', '["A monoclonal antibody therapy resulting in decreased functional activity","A vaccine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A cytokine therapy mediated by specific receptor activation","A checkpoint inhibitor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chimeric antigen receptor T cells genetically engineered to target cancer antigens"]'::jsonb, 'Chimeric antigen receptor T cells genetically engineered to target cancer antigens', 'CAR-T therapy engineers the patient''s own T cells to express a chimeric antigen receptor that recognizes cancer cell antigens. It is not a monoclonal antibody, vaccine, cytokine, or checkpoint inhibitor.'),
('91003dcb-fb4e-58d5-bbb3-f12a051dcdb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the target of most currently approved CAR-T therapies?', '["CD19 on B cells","HER2","PD-L1","EGFR","CD20"]'::jsonb, 'CD19 on B cells', 'Most approved CAR-T products such as tisagenlecleucel and axicabtagene target CD19, a surface antigen on B cells. HER2, PD-L1, EGFR, and CD20 are not the standard targets of the currently approved products.'),
('417ba43a-add7-52cc-b2ec-0489c282549a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of regulatory T cells (Tregs) in cancer?', '["contributing to immune evasion by tumors","Suppress anti-tumor immunity","Kill tumor cells","Present tumor antigens","Secrete anti-tumor cytokines"]'::jsonb, 'Suppress anti-tumor immunity', 'Regulatory T cells dampen effector immune responses and thereby blunt antitumor immunity, which tumors exploit for immune evasion. They do not directly kill tumor cells, present antigens, or secrete antitumor cytokines.'),
('9cd7d392-c0fa-5f66-b513-379e007e2215'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Where does hematopoiesis occur in the fetus?', '["then bone marrow (20+ weeks)","Yolk sac (3 weeks)","Only in the liver","then liver (6 weeks)","Only in the bone marrow"]'::jsonb, 'Yolk sac (3 weeks)', 'Fetal hematopoiesis begins in the yolk sac at about 3 weeks of gestation, before shifting to the liver and later the bone marrow. The yolk sac is therefore the earliest site listed, whereas liver and marrow dominate later.'),
('48f7bd70-0533-5720-bef9-57d9701cea7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the fetal liver''s role in hematopoiesis?', '["role resulting in decreased functional activity","Minor role accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Major hematopoietic site from 6 to 20 weeks gestation","Only produces lymphocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only produces myeloid cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Major hematopoietic site from 6 to 20 weeks gestation', 'Between about 6 and 20 weeks of gestation the fetal liver is the main hematopoietic organ, producing both erythroid and myeloid cells. Its contribution is neither minor nor restricted to a single lineage.'),
('308f1f79-b908-57a4-8b4b-ad7c78254ef1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'When does the bone marrow become the primary hematopoietic organ?', '["After 20-24 weeks gestation","At birth","At 30 weeks","At 6 weeks","At 10 weeks"]'::jsonb, 'After 20-24 weeks gestation', 'The bone marrow assumes the primary role in hematopoiesis after 20-24 weeks of gestation and remains dominant after birth. At 6-10 weeks or even at birth the marrow has not yet taken over this function.'),
('5f55ccb9-6939-5c77-b007-ddcc5c9839ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are CD34+ hematopoietic stem cells used for?', '["Lymphocyte therapy resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone marrow transplantation and hematopoietic reconstitution after chemotherapy","Blood transfusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Gene therapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Platelet transfusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow transplantation and hematopoietic reconstitution after chemotherapy', 'CD34+ hematopoietic stem cells are infused for bone marrow transplantation to reconstitute hematopoiesis after myeloablative chemotherapy. They are not used for blood or platelet transfusion or conventional gene therapy.'),
('dcade0af-ba35-5997-979c-988416c5b267'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the role of the spleen in fetal hematopoiesis?', '["Primary site resulting in decreased functional activity","Only produces platelets accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only produces lymphocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","role without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Minor hematopoietic role in the first and second trimesters"]'::jsonb, 'Minor hematopoietic role in the first and second trimesters', 'The fetal spleen contributes to hematopoiesis only as a minor site during the first and second trimesters. It is not the primary hematopoietic organ, nor does it produce only platelets or lymphocytes.'),
('e9116bca-2f03-5de7-8ff5-ef11da58aeb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the difference between embryonic and adult hemoglobin?', '["difference resulting in decreased functional activity","Fetal hemoglobin has beta chains only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Embryonic: zeta and epsilon chains; Fetal: alpha and gamma chains (HbF); Adult: alpha and beta chains (HbA)","Adult hemoglobin has gamma chains only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Embryonic has only alpha chains leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Embryonic: zeta and epsilon chains; Fetal: alpha and gamma chains (HbF); Adult: alpha and beta chains (HbA)', 'Globin synthesis switches from embryonic zeta/epsilon chains to fetal alpha/gamma chains (HbF) and finally to adult alpha/beta chains (HbA). The other options omit this chain-specific developmental sequence.'),
('b40e92eb-c1be-5e52-9c1d-95bfbff60113'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the role of the neonatal spleen?', '["Only produces platelets resulting in decreased functional activity","Not functional at birth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only produces RBCs mediated by specific receptor activation","Fully mature at birth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Immature but functional; contributes to iron recycling and immune protection"]'::jsonb, 'Immature but functional; contributes to iron recycling and immune protection', 'The neonatal spleen is anatomically immature yet functional, contributing to iron recycling (extramedullary hematopoiesis may persist) and to immune protection. It is neither nonfunctional nor fully mature at birth.'),
('4ec5f294-b0c1-5168-87c4-9c85700ca8c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the significance of physiologic neutropenia in newborns?', '["Neutrophil count drops around 6 weeks","with lymphocyte predominance until about 5 years","significance","Abnormal condition","Normal at birth only"]'::jsonb, 'Neutrophil count drops around 6 weeks', 'In physiologic neutropenia the neutrophil count falls to a nadir around 6 weeks of age as maternal IgG wanes, followed by lymphocyte predominance until about 5 years. This is a normal developmental pattern, not an abnormal condition.'),
('78c12322-d727-51f5-98a9-cabd66e52939'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the role of breast milk in neonatal immunity?', '["Provides sIgA","lysozyme","and maternal immune cells","oligosaccharides","lactoferrin"]'::jsonb, 'Provides sIgA', 'Breast milk supplies secretory IgA (sIgA), along with lysozyme, oligosaccharides, and maternal immune cells, protecting the infant''s gut against infection. The defining immunologic feature is the transfer of sIgA.'),
('15ba2b53-9e1c-5208-9247-463d964e5ec8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the most common cause of lymphadenopathy in clinical practice?', '["Lymphoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Metastatic cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reactive (infectious) lymphadenitis","Sarcoidosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tuberculosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reactive (infectious) lymphadenitis', 'Reactive lymphadenitis from infectious causes is the most frequent reason for enlarged lymph nodes in clinical practice. Malignancies such as lymphoma and metastases, sarcoidosis, and tuberculosis are far less common overall.'),
('5de433dd-2a95-5f65-aab8-c1ce0c9714b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the warning signs (B symptoms) of lymphoma?', '["Fever","and unintentional weight loss (10% in 6 months)","Pain and swelling","night sweats","Fatigue only"]'::jsonb, 'Fever', 'The B symptoms of lymphoma are fever, drenching night sweats, and unintentional weight loss of more than 10% in six months. Fever is one of the classic triad, whereas pain, swelling, and fatigue alone are not B symptoms.'),
('98af46fb-e49e-58ce-9d8c-e5c15a92dd98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the most common site of extranodal lymphoma?', '["The GI tract (especially the stomach)","The brain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lung mediated by specific receptor activation","The bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The GI tract (especially the stomach)', 'Extranodal lymphoma most often arises in the GI tract, especially the stomach, frequently as MALT lymphoma. The brain, lung, bone, and skin are less common extranodal sites.'),
('6a91be5b-c5c9-537f-8a6a-e592d0c1ea75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is MALT lymphoma (extranodal marginal zone lymphoma)?', '["A high-grade lymphoma","A Hodgkin lymphoma","AT-cell lymphoma","A low-grade B-cell lymphoma arising from MALT","often in the stomach (H. pylori-associated)"]'::jsonb, 'A low-grade B-cell lymphoma arising from MALT', 'MALT lymphoma is a low-grade B-cell lymphoma that develops in mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, typically in the stomach, where it is linked to H. pylori infection. It is not a high-grade, Hodgkin, or T-cell tumor.'),
('0efc7a05-37b8-590e-a5df-952433e10441'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is Castleman disease?', '["A viral lymphadenitis resulting in decreased functional activity","A reactive hyperplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lymphoproliferative disorder with angiofollicular hyperplasia (hyaline-vascular or plasma cell variants)","A lymphoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A granulomatous disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A lymphoproliferative disorder with angiofollicular hyperplasia (hyaline-vascular or plasma cell variants)', 'Castleman disease is a lymphoproliferative disorder with angiofollicular (hyaline-vascular or plasma-cell) hyperplasia, often localized to a single node group. It is not simply a viral lymphadenitis, reactive hyperplasia, lymphoma, or granulomatous disease.'),
('849da147-434f-5b07-8948-c7e4aea6e1c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the most common cause of splenic rupture?', '["Leukemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Splenic infarction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trauma (especially motor vehicle accidents)","Infectious mononucleosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malaria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma (especially motor vehicle accidents)', 'Blunt abdominal trauma, especially from motor vehicle accidents, is the leading cause of splenic rupture. Infectious mononucleosis, malaria, infarction, and leukemia can enlarge or rupture the spleen but are less common causes overall.'),
('a847eb09-b586-52e7-83bc-c1265e75c9d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the most common cause of splenic rupture in infectious disease?', '["Malaria","Tuberculosis","Infectious mononucleosis (EBV)","Bacterial sepsis","Histoplasmosis"]'::jsonb, 'Infectious mononucleosis (EBV)', 'Among infections, EBV (infectious mononucleosis) most commonly causes splenic rupture because the virus massively expands the splenic tissue. Malaria, tuberculosis, sepsis, and histoplasmosis are less frequent causes.'),
('d40d60a3-337e-541d-82ff-6e03c1233f5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the association between the thymus and myasthenia gravis?', '["Suppresses immunity resulting in decreased functional activity","Thymic hyperplasia or thymoma is associated with myasthenia gravis","Causes infection mediated by specific receptor activation","an autoimmune neuromuscular disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","role leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thymic hyperplasia or thymoma is associated with myasthenia gravis', 'In myasthenia gravis, thymic hyperplasia is common and thymoma occurs in about 10-15% of cases, and thymectomy is part of management. The thymus is not merely immunosuppressive or infectious in this disorder.'),
('edfc89d2-d1f7-5e7a-9069-c5434559337e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the most common tumor of the thymus?', '["Thymic lymphoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Germ cell tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thymic carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Thymic carcinoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thymoma (epithelial tumor of the thymus)"]'::jsonb, 'Thymoma (epithelial tumor of the thymus)', 'Thymoma, an epithelial tumor of the thymus, is the most common thymic neoplasm and is often associated with myasthenia gravis. Thymic lymphoma, carcinoma, carcinoid, and germ cell tumors are less common.'),
('be303d33-01c1-5a83-bb3a-7f24cecee11d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Which option best describes the primary lymphoid organs?', '["Peyer''s patches and bone marrow","Tonsils and appendix","Bone marrow and thymus","Lymph nodes and spleen","Thymus and spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow and thymus', 'Primary lymphoid organs are where lymphocytes develop and mature: the bone marrow produces B cells and the thymus matures T cells. Lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and Peyer''s patches are secondary sites of antigen encounter.'),
('af0c89d8-2874-52a9-8760-513c95bdb8df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the primary lymphoid organs. Which option is most accurate?', '["Peyer''s patches and bone marrow","Tonsils and appendix","Bone marrow and thymus","Lymph nodes and spleen","Thymus and spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow and thymus', 'The bone marrow and thymus are primary because lymphocytes are generated and mature there. Peyer''s patches, tonsils, lymph nodes, and the spleen are secondary organs that support antigen-driven immune responses.'),
('5a6711c5-9fa2-53a3-84f0-4a64f8e491a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Immune Lymphatic System: what are the primary lymphoid organs?', '["Peyer''s patches and bone marrow","Tonsils and appendix","Bone marrow and thymus","Lymph nodes and spleen","Thymus and spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow and thymus', 'Primary lymphoid organs are the sites of lymphocyte production and maturation, namely the bone marrow and thymus. Peyer''s patches, tonsils, lymph nodes, and the spleen are secondary organs.'),
('0c017486-18ee-5f5c-ac81-8b976c5e045e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'For Immune Lymphatic System, identify the correct response: what are the primary lymphoid organs?', '["Peyer''s patches and bone marrow","Tonsils and appendix","Bone marrow and thymus","Lymph nodes and spleen","Thymus and spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow and thymus', 'Lymphocytes originate and undergo maturation in the bone marrow and thymus, the primary lymphoid organs. The other listed combinations include secondary sites where antigens are trapped and immune cells are activated.'),
('66e2931e-d4cc-5289-8605-5c0003daeb5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the primary lymphoid organs. Select the best answer.', '["Peyer''s patches and bone marrow","Tonsils and appendix","Bone marrow and thymus","Lymph nodes and spleen","Thymus and spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow and thymus', 'Primary lymphoid organs are where lymphocytes are produced and mature, and only the bone marrow and thymus fulfill this role. Spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, Peyer''s patches, and appendix are secondary organs.'),
('22e3a805-1311-569c-8c32-4e57c1b092e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Immune Lymphatic System item: what are the primary lymphoid organs?', '["Peyer''s patches and bone marrow","Tonsils and appendix","Bone marrow and thymus","Lymph nodes and spleen","Thymus and spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow and thymus', 'The bone marrow and thymus are the primary lymphoid organs because lymphocytes develop and mature there. Secondary sites such as the spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, and Peyer''s patches handle antigen recognition and effector responses.'),
('20b34478-12e7-57b4-99f3-ac68cd743de1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Which option best describes the secondary lymphoid organs?', '["tonsils","spleen","Lymph nodes","Peyer''s patches","appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph nodes', 'Secondary lymphoid organs house mature lymphocytes and initiate immune responses, and the lymph node is the archetypal example because it filters lymph and presents antigens. Tonsils, spleen, Peyer''s patches, and appendix are also secondary, but lymph nodes are the classic answer.'),
('f79003de-c79a-53b8-8416-9160de6069c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the secondary lymphoid organs. Which option is most accurate?', '["tonsils","spleen","Lymph nodes","Peyer''s patches","appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph nodes', 'Lymph nodes are the classic secondary lymphoid organs, where antigen from tissue fluid is presented to recirculating lymphocytes. The other listed structures are secondary as well, but lymph nodes are the prototypical example.'),
('5a65bc92-1915-51b6-9f16-1aa17db29c35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Immune Lymphatic System: what are the secondary lymphoid organs?', '["tonsils","spleen","Lymph nodes","Peyer''s patches","appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph nodes', 'Secondary lymphoid organs provide sites for antigen encounter and lymphocyte activation, and the lymph node is the standard example. Tonsils, spleen, Peyer''s patches, and appendix are also secondary organs.'),
('b53b0c41-52d1-5a7c-b268-f888d82210f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'For Immune Lymphatic System, identify the correct response: what are the secondary lymphoid organs?', '["tonsils","spleen","Lymph nodes","Peyer''s patches","appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph nodes', 'Lymph nodes filter lymph and support antigen presentation to lymphocytes, making them the characteristic secondary lymphoid organ. The other options are likewise secondary, but the lymph node is the classic response.'),
('d6ad4515-6638-534e-9516-a74e22027514'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What are the secondary lymphoid organs. Select the best answer.', '["tonsils","spleen","Lymph nodes","Peyer''s patches","appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph nodes', 'The lymph node is the quintessential secondary lymphoid organ, trapping antigens from tissue fluid to trigger adaptive immunity. Tonsils, spleen, Peyer''s patches, and appendix are additional secondary sites.'),
('3b3fa07a-57f3-5e30-9d80-76b2224cd984'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Immune Lymphatic System item: what are the secondary lymphoid organs?', '["tonsils","spleen","Lymph nodes","Peyer''s patches","appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph nodes', 'Secondary lymphoid organs capture antigen and activate mature lymphocytes, and lymph nodes are the paradigmatic example. Tonsils, spleen, Peyer''s patches, and appendix are other secondary sites.'),
('5cdc4a8b-3708-5c6b-b5aa-5fdbf7146e5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Which option best defines MALT?', '["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","scattered lymphoid cells in mucosal surfaces","A blood cell","A type of lymphoma","A primary lymphoid organ"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue', 'MALT is mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, aggregates of lymphoid cells beneath mucosal epithelia of the gut and respiratory tract. It is a tissue, not a blood cell, a lymphoma, or a primary organ.'),
('83e70f19-aaa1-528e-8a86-d27386e49176'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is MALT. Which option is most accurate?', '["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","scattered lymphoid cells in mucosal surfaces","A blood cell","A type of lymphoma","A primary lymphoid organ"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue', 'MALT stands for mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, diffusely distributed lymphoid cells guarding mucosal surfaces. It is neither a blood cell, a lymphoma, nor a primary lymphoid organ.'),
('073fb9d7-bd3b-50c3-9071-3c095bd739c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Immune Lymphatic System: what is MALT?', '["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","scattered lymphoid cells in mucosal surfaces","A blood cell","A type of lymphoma","A primary lymphoid organ"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue', 'MALT is mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue found at mucosal barriers such as the gut and bronchi, where local immune responses begin. The term describes tissue, not a blood cell or tumor.'),
('3c3726ea-cff1-5039-99b7-5792f2228f31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'For Immune Lymphatic System, identify the correct response: what is MALT?', '["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","scattered lymphoid cells in mucosal surfaces","A blood cell","A type of lymphoma","A primary lymphoid organ"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue', 'MALT refers to mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, the organized and scattered lymphoid aggregates that protect mucosal surfaces. It is not a single blood cell type, a lymphoma, or a primary organ.'),
('5c0b2dd1-965c-5591-a541-ae8bef10af0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is MALT. Select the best answer.', '["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","scattered lymphoid cells in mucosal surfaces","A blood cell","A type of lymphoma","A primary lymphoid organ"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue', 'MALT is mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lining mucosal surfaces such as the GI tract, where it initiates local immune responses. The other options misclassify it as a blood cell, tumor, or primary organ.'),
('6df4144a-bf1f-5c76-b284-61a07200d8ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Immune Lymphatic System item: what is MALT?', '["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","scattered lymphoid cells in mucosal surfaces","A blood cell","A type of lymphoma","A primary lymphoid organ"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue', 'MALT denotes mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue in the gut, lung, and other mucosal sites, guarding against pathogens at surfaces. It is a tissue compartment, not a blood cell or a primary lymphoid organ.'),
('74aee2cd-a4f2-50b2-8478-e132d3d658ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Which option best defines the function of the thymus?', '["B cell maturation","T cell maturation and selection","Produce antibodies","Filter blood","Store lymphocytes"]'::jsonb, 'T cell maturation and selection', 'The thymus is the site of T cell maturation and selection, where immature thymocytes are educated to be self-tolerant. B cell maturation occurs in the marrow, antibody production in plasma cells, and blood filtration in the spleen.'),
('21f444dd-ebc6-5012-97c6-ca7014695ffb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of the thymus. Which option is most accurate?', '["B cell maturation","T cell maturation and selection","Produce antibodies","Filter blood","Store lymphocytes"]'::jsonb, 'T cell maturation and selection', 'T cells mature and undergo positive and negative selection in the thymus, ensuring self-tolerance. B cell maturation, antibody production, blood filtration, and simple storage are roles of other organs.'),
('b4a8e54a-9f2c-50ec-b09b-4e523ebfb433'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Immune Lymphatic System: what is the function of the thymus?', '["B cell maturation","T cell maturation and selection","Produce antibodies","Filter blood","Store lymphocytes"]'::jsonb, 'T cell maturation and selection', 'The thymus specializes in T cell maturation and selection; developing T cells learn to recognize self-MHC and tolerate self-antigens. The listed alternatives belong to the marrow, plasma cells, spleen, or lymph nodes.'),
('6410d46d-4d20-574e-834b-89f251394287'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'For Immune Lymphatic System, identify the correct response: what is the function of the thymus?', '["B cell maturation","T cell maturation and selection","Produce antibodies","Filter blood","Store lymphocytes"]'::jsonb, 'T cell maturation and selection', 'T lymphocytes mature and are selected in the thymus, with autoreactive clones eliminated. Antibody production (plasma cells), blood filtration (spleen), and B cell maturation (marrow) occur elsewhere.'),
('b6f2ba32-3fee-5af5-9124-11168d3019da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'What is the function of the thymus. Select the best answer.', '["B cell maturation","T cell maturation and selection","Produce antibodies","Filter blood","Store lymphocytes"]'::jsonb, 'T cell maturation and selection', 'The thymus generates and selects mature T cells while removing autoreactive clones. The remaining options describe roles of the bone marrow, plasma cells, or spleen.'),
('30ebbb67-e73b-50b6-8a9e-afaf2f947b07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'In Immune and lymphatic histology, which statement best fits the following fact: What are the primary lymphoid organs?', '["Peyer''s patches and bone marrow","Tonsils and appendix","Bone marrow and thymus","Lymph nodes and spleen","Thymus and spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow and thymus', 'The primary lymphoid organs, where lymphocytes develop and mature, are the bone marrow and thymus. Peyer''s patches, tonsils, lymph nodes, and the spleen are secondary organs.'),
('e3318588-3fe6-5216-a58e-1d3d2383e150'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'Regarding Immune and lymphatic histology, what is the correct answer to: What are the secondary lymphoid organs?', '["tonsils","spleen","Lymph nodes","Peyer''s patches","appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph nodes', 'Secondary lymphoid organs are sites where mature lymphocytes encounter antigen, and the lymph node is the archetypal example. Tonsils, spleen, Peyer''s patches, and appendix are also secondary organs.'),
('1aff32d7-5bf3-596d-983d-5f270aeafd29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Immune Lymphatic System', 'For Immune and lymphatic histology, which option is most appropriate: What is MALT?', '["Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue","scattered lymphoid cells in mucosal surfaces","A blood cell","A type of lymphoma","A primary lymphoid organ"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue', 'MALT is mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue protecting mucosal surfaces and initiating local immunity. The term denotes a tissue, not a blood cell, a tumor, or a primary organ.'),
('a42df898-b12d-5be2-a5c0-1288c22aed35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the two main layers of the skin?', '["Dermis and hypodermis","Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)","Papillary and reticular dermis","Epidermis and hypodermis","Stratum corneum and stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)', 'The epidermis is the ectodermal epithelial covering of the skin, while the dermis is the mesodermal connective tissue layer beneath it. The hypodermis lies deep to the dermis and is not a main layer of the skin proper, and papillary/reticular are subdivisions of the dermis.'),
('c4f4a563-f933-5678-ae69-6f8636e47c2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the hypodermis (subcutaneous layer)?', '["The deepest layer of the dermis resulting in decreased functional activity","not part of the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis","The basement membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A layer of the epidermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis', 'The hypodermis (subcutaneous layer) is composed of loose connective tissue and adipose tissue lying deep to the dermis; it anchors the skin to deeper structures, stores fat, and insulates. It is not part of the skin itself, the basement membrane, or the epidermis.'),
('f6c74396-6ac1-5095-a7e8-edfa5592336a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What type of epithelium is the epidermis?', '["Pseudostratified columnar resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple cuboidal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transitional mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified squamous keratinized epithelium","Simple columnar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous keratinized epithelium', 'The epidermis is a stratified squamous keratinized epithelium in which keratinocytes become flattened and packed with keratin as they move toward the surface. The other listed epithelia are single-layered or non-keratinized and do not match the epidermis.'),
('42823d14-07c4-5e28-bf1a-39cc79061890'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the five layers of the epidermis (from deep to superficial)?', '["stratum granulosum","stratum corneum","stratum spinosum","stratum lucidum","Stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Stratum basale', 'From deep to superficial the five epidermal layers are stratum basale, spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, and corneum. Of the options the deepest layer is the stratum basale, the single row of dividing keratinocytes attached to the basement membrane.'),
('5e6eb01b-520e-56c1-b2c6-1b105b9ddcb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is thick skin?', '["Skin with thick dermis","found on palms and soles; lacks hair follicles","Skin with all five epidermal layers","Skin on the back","Skin on the face"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with all five epidermal layers', 'Thick skin, found on the palms and soles, contains all five epidermal layers including the stratum lucidum and lacks hair follicles; the complete complement of layers is its defining feature. Its dermis is not unusually thick, and it is not found on the back or face.'),
('a2105641-9cbd-5d03-b4d5-69dd9d38bd3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is thin skin?', '["Scalp skin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin on the palms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin on the soles mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin with all five layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles', 'Thin skin covers most of the body, has hair follicles, and lacks the stratum lucidum; its stratum corneum is thinner than that of thick skin. Palms and soles carry thick skin, not thin skin.'),
('55041bfd-3b0f-5c28-af30-da00b574bf63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the stratum basale (germinativum)?', '["consisting of a single row of keratinocytes that continuously divide","The outermost layer of dead cells","The granular layer","The spinous layer","The basal layer of the epidermis"]'::jsonb, 'The basal layer of the epidermis', 'The stratum basale is the deepest layer of the epidermis, a single row of continuously dividing keratinocytes resting on the basement membrane. It is not the outermost layer, the granular layer, or the spinous layer.'),
('c7b317a3-928e-5a18-adf2-b0672fef233c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the stratum spinosum?', '["The basal layer","with polyhedral cells connected by desmosomes (spine-like projections)","The layer just above the basale","The granular layer","The cornified layer"]'::jsonb, 'The layer just above the basale', 'The stratum spinosum lies immediately above the stratum basale and is composed of polyhedral cells joined by desmosomes, which give a spiny appearance during histologic preparation. It is not the basal, granular, or cornified layer.'),
('1d8a6480-fa23-5738-8908-74e32faab435'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the stratum granulosum?', '["The cornified layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The layer with keratohyalin granules (dark blue-purple)","The lucid layer mediated by specific receptor activation","The deepest layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","marking the transition to dead cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The layer with keratohyalin granules (dark blue-purple)', 'The stratum granulosum contains dark blue-purple keratohyalin granules and marks the transition where keratinocytes begin to degenerate and fill with keratin. It is neither the cornified layer nor the deepest layer.'),
('ea089ed3-4765-5891-90ca-7669d8b523a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the stratum lucidum?', '["A thin resulting in decreased functional activity","The outermost layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","flattened cells filled with eleidin (only in thick skin)","The granular layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","clear layer of dead leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'flattened cells filled with eleidin (only in thick skin)', 'The stratum lucidum is a thin, clear zone of flattened dead cells filled with eleidin that is present only in thick skin, lying between the granular and cornified layers. It is not the outermost layer or the granular layer.'),
('03dfe82f-dd51-539c-a6fe-695db401690e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the stratum corneum?', '["The outermost layer of anucleate","The living layer of the epidermis","flattened","dead keratinocytes filled with keratin","providing a protective barrier"]'::jsonb, 'dead keratinocytes filled with keratin', 'The stratum corneum is the outermost layer of the epidermis, made of flattened, anucleate dead keratinocytes packed with keratin that form a protective barrier. It is not a living layer of the epidermis.'),
('2a7822b8-f714-5fc2-87d9-96625c71cffe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are keratinocytes?', '["Sensory cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Immune cells of the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The predominant cell type in the epidermis (90-95%) that produce keratin and form the epidermal layers","Melanin-producing cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glandular cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The predominant cell type in the epidermis (90-95%) that produce keratin and form the epidermal layers', 'Keratinocytes are the most abundant cells of the epidermis, comprising about 90-95% of its cells, and they produce keratin while forming the stratified epidermal layers. Melanin production belongs to melanocytes, and immune and sensory functions are carried out by other cells.'),
('074c70a0-b987-5c63-86a9-49f1a70d5c78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are melanocytes?', '["Fibroblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells derived from the neural crest that produce melanin pigment and transfer it to keratinocytes","Langerhans cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Keratin-producing cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Merkel cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells derived from the neural crest that produce melanin pigment and transfer it to keratinocytes', 'Melanocytes derive from the neural crest and synthesize melanin pigment, which is transferred to adjacent keratinocytes. Fibroblasts, Langerhans cells, and Merkel cells have different origins and functions.'),
('89fa7712-81a6-5b89-aa68-78625136c347'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are Langerhans cells?', '["Sensory cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Melanin-producing cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","derived from bone marrow mediated by specific receptor activation","Dendritic immune cells (antigen-presenting cells) in the epidermis","Keratin-producing cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dendritic immune cells (antigen-presenting cells) in the epidermis', 'Langerhans cells are bone marrow-derived dendritic cells in the epidermis that capture and process antigens and present them to T lymphocytes, initiating skin immune responses. They do not produce melanin, keratin, or sensation.'),
('eda98001-0710-5696-8b6e-6922c7e1ac0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are Merkel cells?', '["Glandular cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Keratin-producing cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tactile sensory cells in the stratum basale that function as mechanoreceptors (touch sensation)","Immune cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pigment cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tactile sensory cells in the stratum basale that function as mechanoreceptors (touch sensation)', 'Merkel cells are tactile epithelial cells in the stratum basale that act as mechanoreceptors for light touch, associated with sensory nerve endings. They are not glandular, immune, keratin-producing, or pigment cells.'),
('aaf019df-2c9e-5a16-a592-6028be4e7e1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of melanin?', '["Hydrates the skin resulting in decreased functional activity","Provides color to the skin only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Strengthens the skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Protects the skin from UV radiation damage by absorbing and scattering light","Regulates temperature leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protects the skin from UV radiation damage by absorbing and scattering light', 'Melanin absorbs and scatters ultraviolet radiation, protecting the nuclear DNA of keratinocytes from UV-induced damage; it also imparts color to the skin and hair. It does not hydrate, strengthen, or cool the skin.'),
('e34816d8-e1f9-5459-b277-4886f0d27343'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the ratio of melanocytes to keratinocytes in the epidermis?', '["1:5 resulting in decreased functional activity","About 1:10 in the stratum basale","1:1 mediated by specific receptor activation","1:50 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1:20 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1:10 in the stratum basale', 'In the stratum basale about one melanocyte supplies approximately ten keratinocytes (about 1:10), allowing each melanocyte to deliver melanin to many surrounding keratinocytes. The other ratios do not reflect this relationship.'),
('54048465-9405-5e27-8e67-aa3bc2cf2780'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the two layers of the basement membrane zone at the dermal-epidermal junction?', '["Papillary and reticular","Collagen and elastin","Epidermis and dermis","Lamina lucida and lamina densa","Stratum basale and stratum spinosum"]'::jsonb, 'Lamina lucida and lamina densa', 'The basement membrane zone at the dermal-epidermal junction has two layers: the electron-lucent lamina lucida adjacent to the basal keratinocytes and the electron-dense lamina densa. Papillary/reticular and basale/spinosum are unrelated layers.'),
('49a47a0f-5031-5740-9f2a-bdb270b4ff50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of hemidesmosomes?', '["Anchoring adjacent keratinocytes to each other resulting in decreased functional activity","Anchoring keratinocytes of the stratum basale to the basement membrane","Secreting melanin mediated by specific receptor activation","Cell-to-cell communication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connecting melanocytes to keratinocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anchoring keratinocytes of the stratum basale to the basement membrane', 'Hemidesmosomes are half-desmosomes on the basal surface of basal keratinocytes that anchor the epidermis to the basement membrane. Desmosomes, not hemidesmosomes, connect keratinocytes to one another.'),
('16e4cdf1-9301-5d1c-801a-3639c975091d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of desmosomes in the epidermis?', '["Cell signaling","Melanin transfer","Anchoring to the basement membrane","Strong intercellular adhesion between keratinocytes","maintaining structural integrity of the skin"]'::jsonb, 'Strong intercellular adhesion between keratinocytes', 'Desmosomes join adjacent keratinocytes by strong intercellular adhesion, resisting mechanical stress and maintaining epidermal integrity. Hemidesmosomes provide anchorage to the basement membrane instead.'),
('972c0280-d613-57dc-b38e-f2abe9292139'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the two layers of the dermis?', '["Nerve and vascular layers resulting in decreased functional activity","Papillary dermis (superficial) and reticular dermis (deep)","Epidermis and dermis mediated by specific receptor activation","Collagen and elastin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stratum basale and stratum corneum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary dermis (superficial) and reticular dermis (deep)', 'The dermis is divided into a superficial papillary layer of loose connective tissue and a deep reticular layer of dense irregular connective tissue. Epidermis and dermis are the two main layers of the skin, not of the dermis.'),
('4617a462-0351-5e1c-929b-7026daef37eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the papillary dermis?', '["The superficial layer of the dermis forming dermal papillae that interdigitate with the epidermis","The basement membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The deep layer mediated by specific receptor activation","The subcutaneous layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hypodermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The superficial layer of the dermis forming dermal papillae that interdigitate with the epidermis', 'The papillary dermis is the superficial dermal layer that projects upward as dermal papillae interdigitating with the epidermis; it contains capillary loops and Meissner corpuscles. It is not the deep layer, the basement membrane, or the hypodermis.'),
('4095a56c-719b-5178-80ab-af8140512968'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the reticular dermis?', '["The thick resulting in decreased functional activity","The papillary layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The superficial dermis mediated by specific receptor activation","deep layer of the dermis consisting of dense irregular connective tissue with collagen and elastic fibers","The hypodermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'deep layer of the dermis consisting of dense irregular connective tissue with collagen and elastic fibers', 'The reticular dermis is the thick deep layer of the dermis composed of dense irregular connective tissue rich in collagen and elastic fibers, providing strength and resilience. It is not the papillary layer or the hypodermis.'),
('69bc1604-d91a-505f-90cc-40695998e6ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What type of collagen is predominant in the dermis?', '["Type V collagen resulting in decreased functional activity","Type I collagen (in reticular dermis) and type III collagen (in papillary dermis)","Type IV collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Type II collagen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type VII collagen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type I collagen (in reticular dermis) and type III collagen (in papillary dermis)', 'Type I collagen predominates in the reticular dermis while type III (reticular) collagen is more prominent in the papillary dermis. Type IV collagen is found in basement membranes and type VII in anchoring fibrils.'),
('d927e87d-65dd-5ea5-988b-0ca744bf7208'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of elastic fibers in the dermis?', '["preventing sagging","Provide tensile strength","Hydration","Anchoring the epidermis","Provide elasticity and recoil to the skin"]'::jsonb, 'Provide elasticity and recoil to the skin', 'Elastic fibers give the skin elasticity and recoil so it can stretch and return to its original shape. Tensile strength against pulling is the role of collagen fibers, not elastic fibers.'),
('99622175-00c2-5fad-b1ec-37fb57b2d526'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What cells are found in the dermis?', '["mast cells","Fibroblasts","dermal dendritic cells","adnexal structures","macrophages"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblasts', 'Fibroblasts are the most numerous cells of the dermis and are the characteristic dermal cell that synthesizes the extracellular matrix. Mast cells, dendritic cells, and macrophages are present in smaller numbers, and adnexal structures are not cells.'),
('18c9b129-85ee-530e-ab33-36baa1f33d41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of fibroblasts in the dermis?', '["and other extracellular matrix components","proteoglycans","Synthesize collagen","elastin","Produce melanin"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesize collagen', 'Fibroblasts synthesize collagen as well as elastin and proteoglycans, the structural and ground-substance components of the dermal matrix. They do not produce melanin, which is a melanocyte function.'),
('ee62670c-16cd-5f21-b441-521dba1a77a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of dermal mast cells?', '["Release histamine and other mediators in allergic reactions","Produce collagen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","Phagocytose bacteria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and wound healing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Release histamine and other mediators in allergic reactions', 'Dermal mast cells release histamine and other vasoactive and inflammatory mediators during allergic reactions and inflammation, causing vasodilation, swelling, and itching. Collagen production is a fibroblast function and phagocytosis a macrophage function.'),
('c71fb09e-32d5-5a3b-be36-9978580b4a7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of dermal macrophages?', '["Phagocytose debris and pathogens","and present antigens","Provide pigment","Regulate temperature","Produce collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Phagocytose debris and pathogens', 'Dermal macrophages engulf and digest cellular debris and pathogens and also present antigens to lymphocytes. They do not produce pigment, regulate temperature, or synthesize collagen.'),
('67d378ce-ba93-587e-99b0-797d4cf166ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the dermal vasculature?', '["A single capillary network resulting in decreased functional activity","and veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","capillaries mediated by specific receptor activation","blood vessels in the dermis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep (subdermal) and superficial (subpapillary) plexuses of arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Deep (subdermal) and superficial (subpapillary) plexuses of arteries', 'The dermis is supplied by a deep (subdermal) plexus and a superficial (subpapillary) plexus of blood vessels that nourish the skin and give rise to capillary loops in the dermal papillae. It is not a single capillary network.'),
('b153d8e9-f690-5dd0-bb60-c38788d16468'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the hair bulb?', '["The expanded base of the hair follicle containing the dermal papilla and hair matrix","The upper part of the follicle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The arrector pili muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","The sebaceous gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hair shaft above the skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The expanded base of the hair follicle containing the dermal papilla and hair matrix', 'The hair bulb is the expanded deep base of the follicle that houses the dermal papilla and the hair matrix; matrix cells divide to produce the hair shaft. It is not the shaft, arrector pili muscle, or sebaceous gland.'),
('17453093-17d4-5808-8176-97d3199061b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the dermal papilla of the hair follicle?', '["A vascularized invagination of dermis at the base of the hair bulb that nourishes the growing hair","The sebaceous gland accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The outer root sheath mediated by specific receptor activation","The arrector pili muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hair shaft leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A vascularized invagination of dermis at the base of the hair bulb that nourishes the growing hair', 'The dermal papilla is a vascularized connective tissue invagination into the hair bulb that supplies nutrients to the growing hair and participates in hair growth regulation. It is not a gland, root sheath, muscle, or shaft.'),
('3abfbc16-adcc-5063-9abb-4b99cb4cda20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the three phases of the hair cycle?', '["Anagen (growth)","Active","Growing and resting only","telogen (rest)","catagen (regression)"]'::jsonb, 'Anagen (growth)', 'The hair cycle has three phases: anagen (active growth), catagen (regression), and telogen (rest). Of the options, anagen is the growth phase that defines the beginning of the cycle.'),
('6a6fda00-d2f7-545b-98e8-83ea505b22b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of the arrector pili muscle?', '["Secretes sebum resulting in decreased functional activity","Moves the hair in different directions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Provides sensation mediated by specific receptor activation","A smooth muscle that contracts to raise the hair (piloerection) in response to cold or fear","Supplies blood to the hair leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A smooth muscle that contracts to raise the hair (piloerection) in response to cold or fear', 'The arrector pili is a smooth muscle inserting on the hair follicle that contracts in response to cold or fear, raising the hair (piloerection or goosebumps). Sebum secretion belongs to sebaceous glands and blood supply is via the dermal papilla.'),
('ef7aa5da-832d-5a95-83a7-b67fc7052067'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the inner root sheath (IRS)?', '["produced by the hair matrix; it disintegrates at the isthmus","The arrector pili insertion","The outer layer of the follicle","The sebaceous gland duct","A keratinized layer surrounding the hair shaft"]'::jsonb, 'A keratinized layer surrounding the hair shaft', 'The inner root sheath is the keratinized layer that surrounds the hair shaft within the follicle; it is produced by the hair matrix and disintegrates at the isthmus. It is not the outer layer of the follicle or the gland duct.'),
('fd411c51-5af7-5ca4-8660-812ad47138f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the outer root sheath (ORS)?', '["The outermost layer of the hair follicle","The sebaceous gland","The dermal layer","continuous with the epidermis; it contains glycogen-rich cells","The hair matrix"]'::jsonb, 'The outermost layer of the hair follicle', 'The outer root sheath is the outermost layer of the hair follicle and is continuous with the epidermis, containing glycogen-rich cells. It lies outside the inner root sheath, which surrounds the hair shaft.'),
('2526fbef-7af5-5836-9978-dcc39533967b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the bulge region of the hair follicle?', '["containing hair follicle stem cells resulting in decreased functional activity","The hair bulb accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The isthmus mediated by specific receptor activation","The region at the insertion of the arrector pili","The dermal papilla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The region at the insertion of the arrector pili', 'The bulge is the region of the follicle at the level of arrector pili insertion and it contains hair follicle stem cells important for follicle cycling and wound repair. It is not the bulb, isthmus, or dermal papilla.'),
('8e5f7839-adb9-50b2-b5e6-630a460adfdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is vellus hair?', '["lightly pigmented hair covering most of the body (peach fuzz)","Scalp hair accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fine mediated by specific receptor activation","Thick terminal hair without affecting the primary tissue pathway","short leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'lightly pigmented hair covering most of the body (peach fuzz)', 'Vellus hair is fine, short, and lightly pigmented, covering most of the body surface as peach fuzz, in contrast to the coarse, darker terminal hair of the scalp and beard.'),
('c1676916-e14b-5c87-87de-b7ae69be1798'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are sebaceous glands?', '["Eccrine sweat glands resulting in decreased functional activity","Ceruminous glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Holocrine glands that secrete sebum (an oily substance) into hair follicles to lubricate skin and hair","Apocrine sweat glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mammary glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Holocrine glands that secrete sebum (an oily substance) into hair follicles to lubricate skin and hair', 'Sebaceous glands are holocrine glands that produce sebum, an oily substance discharged into hair follicles to lubricate and waterproof the skin and hair. Eccrine and apocrine glands are sweat glands and ceruminous glands are ear wax glands.'),
('15798209-2502-5240-8f08-5ef49d799907'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What type of secretion is sebaceous?', '["Holocrine (entire cell disintegrates to release its contents)","Eccrine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apocrine (apical bleb) mediated by specific receptor activation","Merocrine (exocytosis) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","B but not A leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Holocrine (entire cell disintegrates to release its contents)', 'Sebaceous glands use holocrine secretion, in which the entire glandular cell disintegrates and is shed along with its sebum contents. Eccrine, apocrine, and merocrine secretion modes do not involve whole-cell breakdown.'),
('d773438f-39a0-54c8-82fe-1c2217e8527f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the two types of sweat glands?', '["Holocrine and merocrine","Endocrine and exocrine","Sebaceous and eccrine","Hair and sweat","Eccrine (merocrine) and apocrine"]'::jsonb, 'Eccrine (merocrine) and apocrine', 'The two types of sweat glands are eccrine (merocrine) glands, which open directly onto the skin surface, and apocrine glands, which open into hair follicles mainly in the axillae and anogenital region. Sebaceous glands are not sweat glands.'),
('a97433d6-691c-5a44-9466-19313c149da1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are eccrine sweat glands?', '["Simple coiled tubular glands that secrete hypotonic sweat directly onto the skin surface for cooling","Glands that secrete into hair follicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oil glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Modified mammary glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glands in the axilla and groin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple coiled tubular glands that secrete hypotonic sweat directly onto the skin surface for cooling', 'Eccrine sweat glands are simple coiled tubular glands with ducts that open directly onto the skin surface; they secrete hypotonic sweat whose evaporation cools the body. Apocrine glands, not eccrine, discharge into hair follicles.'),
('7587a0ca-241b-5e7e-8c9f-23cbeff831d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the parts of an eccrine sweat gland?', '["Coiled secretory portion (in the deep dermis/hypodermis) and a straight duct that opens on the skin surface","Secretory acinus and duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tubules and ducts mediated by specific receptor activation","Follicles and bulb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveoli and duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coiled secretory portion (in the deep dermis/hypodermis) and a straight duct that opens on the skin surface', 'Each eccrine sweat gland has two parts: a coiled secretory portion located in the deep dermis or hypodermis and a largely straight duct coursing upward to open on the skin surface. Acini and alveoli describe other gland types.'),
('321154df-88df-5aee-a53f-74aa6cbf0b29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of eccrine sweat?', '["Lubrication resulting in decreased functional activity","Antimicrobial protection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nutrient secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Pheromone production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Evaporative cooling for thermoregulation; also excretes water and electrolytes"]'::jsonb, 'Evaporative cooling for thermoregulation; also excretes water and electrolytes', 'Evaporation of eccrine sweat cools the skin and the secretion also excretes water and electrolytes, playing a central role in thermoregulation. Lubrication and pheromone production are not the main functions of eccrine sweat.'),
('561e7775-ea29-53a0-b1a5-ee8f113b0d73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are apocrine sweat glands?', '["Sebaceous glands resulting in decreased functional activity","Coiled tubular glands that secrete viscous fluid into hair follicles; found in axillae","and areolae mediated by specific receptor activation","anogenital region without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glands that secrete watery sweat leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coiled tubular glands that secrete viscous fluid into hair follicles; found in axillae', 'Apocrine sweat glands are coiled tubular glands that secrete viscous fluid into hair follicles and are found mainly in the axillae, areolae, and anogenital region; they become active at puberty. They are not sebaceous glands or watery eccrine glands.'),
('429eab11-7973-54be-9021-64ac9e1b228b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What type of secretion is apocrine?', '["Exocytosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Apocrine (apical portion of the cell pinches off to release secretion)","Endocrine mediated by specific receptor activation","Holocrine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Merocrine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Apocrine (apical portion of the cell pinches off to release secretion)', 'Apocrine glands secrete by decapitation: the apical portion of the cell pinches off and is discharged with the secretion. This differs from merocrine secretion by exocytosis and holocrine whole-cell breakdown.'),
('cb9a90f7-ad39-5255-9044-0a2d0a9d59d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are ceruminous glands?', '["Mammary glands resulting in decreased functional activity","Modified apocrine glands in the external auditory canal that secrete cerumen (ear wax)","Sebaceous glands of the ear mediated by specific receptor activation","Eccrine glands of the ear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lacrimal glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Modified apocrine glands in the external auditory canal that secrete cerumen (ear wax)', 'Ceruminous glands are modified apocrine glands in the external auditory canal that secrete cerumen, which protects the canal and traps foreign material. Mammary, sebaceous, eccrine, and lacrimal glands have different locations and functions.'),
('92a245c9-3d71-5321-8770-7d48cb9892a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the mammary glands?', '["Modified apocrine sweat glands that produce milk; compound tubuloalveolar glands","Holocrine glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eccrine sweat glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Sebaceous glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endocrine glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Modified apocrine sweat glands that produce milk; compound tubuloalveolar glands', 'Mammary glands are modified apocrine sweat glands whose glandular tissue forms compound tubuloalveolar glands that produce milk during lactation. They are not holocrine, eccrine, sebaceous, or endocrine glands.'),
('a456fc2b-82d1-5198-8b3f-3f00bc2bb73b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a Meissner corpuscle?', '["A deep pressure receptor","A rapidly adapting mechanoreceptor for light touch","A stretch receptor","located in the dermal papillae of glabrous skin","A temperature receptor"]'::jsonb, 'A rapidly adapting mechanoreceptor for light touch', 'Meissner corpuscles are rapidly adapting mechanoreceptors for light, discriminative touch located in the dermal papillae of glabrous skin. Deep pressure and vibration are detected by Pacinian corpuscles, and temperature by thermoreceptors.'),
('2cd09092-9d23-59ef-9517-aaa54e6c5909'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a Pacinian corpuscle?', '["A rapidly adapting mechanoreceptor for deep pressure and high-frequency vibration","A light touch receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","located in the deep dermis/hypodermis mediated by specific receptor activation","A temperature receptor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A stretch receptor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A rapidly adapting mechanoreceptor for deep pressure and high-frequency vibration', 'Pacinian corpuscles are rapidly adapting mechanoreceptors in the deep dermis and hypodermis that detect deep pressure and high-frequency vibration. Light touch is served by Meissner corpuscles and temperature by thermoreceptors.'),
('af3192b9-275e-5c4d-ae7e-ca06bc2c1e53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a Merkel cell-neurite complex?', '["A deep pressure receptor resulting in decreased functional activity","A temperature receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","in the stratum basale mediated by specific receptor activation","A pain receptor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A slowly adapting mechanoreceptor for sustained touch and spatial resolution (form)"]'::jsonb, 'A slowly adapting mechanoreceptor for sustained touch and spatial resolution (form)', 'The Merkel cell-neurite complex is a slowly adapting (type SA I) mechanoreceptor located in the stratum basale of the epidermis; it sustains firing during prolonged touch and provides fine spatial resolution for form and edge discrimination. Deep pressure is detected by Pacinian-type receptors, while temperature and pain are mediated by other endings.'),
('d45807bb-7ac0-5b1d-ae3b-fba39a2a6bbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a Ruffini corpuscle?', '["A light touch receptor resulting in decreased functional activity","A vibration receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","located in the deep dermis mediated by specific receptor activation","A temperature receptor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A slowly adapting mechanoreceptor for skin stretch and tension"]'::jsonb, 'A slowly adapting mechanoreceptor for skin stretch and tension', 'Ruffini corpuscles are slowly adapting mechanoreceptors in the dermis that respond to skin stretch, tension, and joint position, discharging steadily for as long as the stimulus last. Meissner corpuscles mediate light touch, Pacinian corpuscles mediate vibration, and temperature is signaled by separate endings.'),
('0f19207d-09cd-501c-8455-b84bae3860ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are free nerve endings?', '["Encapsulated touch receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Vibration receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unencapsulated nerve endings in the epidermis and dermis that sense pain","and itch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","temperature leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unencapsulated nerve endings in the epidermis and dermis that sense pain', 'Free nerve endings are unencapsulated terminal branches of sensory axons that ramify in the epidermis and superficial dermis; they are the principal receptors for pain, and also mediate itch and thermal sensation. Encapsulated endings such as Meissner corpuscles mediate discriminative touch, not pain.'),
('8d5fd3af-72d1-53cf-a7dd-e1dcc04b4447'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the dermatome concept?', '["An area of skin supplied by a single spinal nerve (sensory innervation by segmental nerves)","A type of sensory receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A skin graft mediated by specific receptor activation","A layer of the dermis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A surgical instrument leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An area of skin supplied by a single spinal nerve (sensory innervation by segmental nerves)', 'A dermatome is the area of skin whose sensory innervation is provided by a single spinal nerve segment, reflecting an orderly metameric (segmental) distribution on the body surface. It is not a receptor type, a tissue layer, or an instrument.'),
('46777fe6-f86b-5e0a-bb61-269f350c4bb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the cutaneous plexus?', '["The network of nerves in the subcutaneous tissue that innervates the skin","The hair follicle network accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lymphatic network mediated by specific receptor activation","The sweat gland network without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The blood vessels of the skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The network of nerves in the subcutaneous tissue that innervates the skin', 'The cutaneous plexus is the network of nerve fibers in the subcutaneous tissue (hypodermis) that branches to supply the dermis, epidermis, hair follicles, glands, and cutaneous blood vessels. Lymphatics, sweat glands, and blood vessels form separate systems rather than nerve plexuses.'),
('1df580f0-760b-5bb9-bf9b-966fa813559f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of the autonomic innervation of the skin?', '["sweat secretion (cholinergic sympathetic)","and arrector pili (sympathetic)","Somatic motor control","Controls blood flow (sympathetic vasoconstriction)","Voluntary muscle control"]'::jsonb, 'Controls blood flow (sympathetic vasoconstriction)', 'Cutaneous autonomic innervation is predominantly sympathetic: it controls cutaneous blood flow through sympathetic vasoconstriction, stimulates eccrine sweat secretion via cholinergic sympathetic fibers, and contracts arrector pili muscles. Somatic motor nerves innervate skeletal muscle and play no role in the skin.'),
('917b7d4e-87f5-52f4-b0ab-85b4266ec0b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the phases of wound healing?', '["and remodeling (week 3 to years)","proliferative (days 3-21)","Only inflammatory and proliferative","inflammatory (days 1-3)","Hemostasis (immediate)"]'::jsonb, 'Hemostasis (immediate)', 'Wound healing progresses through four overlapping phases that begin with hemostasis (immediate, clot formation), followed by inflammation (days 1-3), proliferation (days 3-21), and remodeling (week 3 to years). Hemostasis is therefore the first phase, occurring within minutes of injury.'),
('332e75c2-0177-564c-8378-680026529811'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What happens in the proliferative phase of wound healing?', '["fibroblast proliferation","and re-epithelialization","granulation tissue formation","collagen deposition","Angiogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Angiogenesis', 'The proliferative phase (days 3-21) is marked by angiogenesis with the formation of new capillaries, together with fibroblast proliferation, granulation tissue formation, collagen deposition, and re-epithelialization of the wound surface. Angiogenesis is one of the hallmark events of new tissue formation in this phase.'),
('749d4b2e-b167-534b-b685-677bc65b8b0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What happens in the remodeling phase of wound healing?', '["Collagen reorganization (type III → type I)","scar maturation","Epithelialization","Granulation tissue formation","and increased tensile strength over months"]'::jsonb, 'Collagen reorganization (type III → type I)', 'In the remodeling (maturation) phase, the collagen laid down during proliferation is reorganized from immature type III to the stronger type I, scar tissue matures, and tensile strength increases over months. Granulation tissue formation and epithelialization belong to the earlier proliferative phase.'),
('16787859-91fb-5aca-9467-d1a092c01df8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the difference between first and secondary intention healing?', '["and scarring","Primary: clean wound with edges approximated","contraction","minimal granulation; Secondary: open wound with more granulation","Primary: delayed; Secondary: immediate"]'::jsonb, 'Primary: clean wound with edges approximated', 'Primary intention healing applies to a clean wound whose edges are approximated (e.g., by sutures), so healing is rapid with minimal granulation tissue. Secondary intention applies to open wounds with larger tissue defects that heal by abundant granulation tissue and contraction, which takes longer.'),
('10a21f03-8b36-5bbb-9fff-8500c3684ac8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is contraction in wound healing?', '["reducing wound size","The formation of new blood vessels","The migration of epithelial cells","The deposition of extracellular matrix","The centripetal movement of wound edges due to myofibroblasts"]'::jsonb, 'The centripetal movement of wound edges due to myofibroblasts', 'Contraction is the centripetal (inward) movement of the wound edges brought about by contractile myofibroblasts, which reduces the wound size and speeds closure. Neovascularization, epithelial migration, and matrix deposition are separate processes in healing.'),
('4a6a2370-73ed-5ea2-85ea-48ce18468f0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is keloid formation?', '["Hypertrophic scar (within wound boundaries) resulting in decreased functional activity","Atrophic scar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","with thick collagen bundles mediated by specific receptor activation","Excessive scar formation extending beyond the original wound boundaries","Normal scar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive scar formation extending beyond the original wound boundaries', 'A keloid is an excessive scar that grows beyond the original wound boundaries and persists, composed of thick collagen bundles and resulting from an exaggerated fibrosis response that does not regress spontaneously. A hypertrophic scar, by contrast, stays within the wound margins.'),
('92f2d65c-41ef-517b-8c47-fcf399e07205'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is hypertrophic scar?', '["A normal scar resulting in decreased functional activity","An atrophic scar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A scar that extends beyond the wound mediated by specific receptor activation","A raised without affecting the primary tissue pathway","red scar confined to the original wound margins; may regress over time"]'::jsonb, 'red scar confined to the original wound margins; may regress over time', 'A hypertrophic scar is a raised, red, firm scar that remains confined to the original wound margins and typically regresses over time. Unlike a keloid, it does not extend beyond the wound boundary and it is not an atrophic or entirely normal scar.'),
('18e1329f-9fdd-5084-a6f7-729bdaff72af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the role of TGF-beta in wound healing?', '["Promotes inflammation","collagen synthesis","Inhibits healing","Stimulates fibroblast proliferation","and differentiation of myofibroblasts"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates fibroblast proliferation', 'TGF-beta is a key pro-fibrotic growth factor that stimulates fibroblast proliferation, collagen synthesis, and myofibroblast differentiation, promoting matrix deposition during healing; its excess contributes to excessive scarring. It does not broadly inhibit healing.'),
('b16c72b5-b8c0-5268-972f-398f02b95937'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the role of matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) in wound healing?', '["Degrade old extracellular matrix to allow remodeling and cell migration","Kill bacteria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Attract inflammatory cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Promote angiogenesis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Synthesize collagen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Degrade old extracellular matrix to allow remodeling and cell migration', 'Matrix metalloproteinases are enzymes that degrade old extracellular matrix components such as collagen, permitting cell migration and the remodeling of scar tissue during healing. They do not synthesize collagen or kill bacteria.'),
('291b0617-2f7c-595a-9768-d96defc9c72f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is impetigo?', '["A parasitic infection resulting in decreased functional activity","A superficial bacterial skin infection (Staphylococcus or Streptococcus) with honey-colored crusts","An allergic reaction mediated by specific receptor activation","A viral infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fungal infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A superficial bacterial skin infection (Staphylococcus or Streptococcus) with honey-colored crusts', 'Impetigo is a superficial bacterial skin infection, usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes, characterized by honey-colored crusts on an erythematous base. Viral, fungal, and parasitic pathogens produce different clinical pictures.'),
('2846348d-3d90-5452-bab6-b486236d7889'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of impetigo?', '["Subcorneal pustules with neutrophils and bacteria; the stratum corneum is lifted","Full-thickness necrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intraepidermal vesicles mediated by specific receptor activation","Granulomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acantholysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcorneal pustules with neutrophils and bacteria; the stratum corneum is lifted', 'The histology of impetigo shows subcorneal pustules packed with neutrophils and bacteria, with the stratum corneum lifted off the underlying viable epidermis. Intraepidermal vesiculation accompanies viral blisters, and acantholysis characterizes pemphigus rather than impetigo.'),
('b1f617e9-daab-5b40-a982-1f200975da7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is cellulitis?', '["A deep muscle infection resulting in decreased functional activity","A superficial skin infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","from Streptococcus or Staphylococcus mediated by specific receptor activation","An acute bacterial infection of the deep dermis and subcutaneous tissue","A blood infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An acute bacterial infection of the deep dermis and subcutaneous tissue', 'Cellulitis is an acute bacterial infection of the deep dermis and subcutaneous fat, most often due to Streptococcus or Staphylococcus, producing spreading erythema, warmth, and tenderness. A superficial infection would spare the deep dermis, and a muscle or bloodstream infection is a different condition.'),
('9d2aaca9-d127-568e-afa4-644a3b6c81f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of cellulitis?', '["Subcorneal pustules resulting in decreased functional activity","Granulomas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eosinophil infiltration mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphocytic infiltrate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dense neutrophilic infiltration of the dermis and subcutis with edema and vasodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Dense neutrophilic infiltration of the dermis and subcutis with edema and vasodilation', 'Acute cellulitis shows a dense neutrophilic infiltration of the dermis and subcutis together with edema and vasodilation reflecting the acute inflammatory response. Granulomas, eosinophils, and predominantly lymphocytic infiltrates characterize other inflammatory dermatoses, and subcorneal pustules are seen in impetigo.'),
('d4c6d6e6-d735-5ad6-91c2-a3ddb1b85175'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the most common fungal infection of the skin?', '["and Epidermophyton resulting in decreased functional activity","Malassezia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Candidiasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Tinea (dermatophytosis) caused by Trichophyton","Microsporum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tinea (dermatophytosis) caused by Trichophyton', 'Tinea (dermatophytosis) is the most common fungal skin infection; it is caused by dermatophytes including Trichophyton, and also Microsporum and Epidermophyton, which invade keratinized tissue. Candidiasis and Malassezia infections are distinct and less common overall.'),
('a2575fa0-1f0b-5f08-acc3-9c76f505e2b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of dermatophyte infection?', '["Granulomas resulting in decreased functional activity","Vesicle formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyphae in the stratum corneum (visible on KOH prep or PAS stain); mild spongiosis and inflammation","Acanthosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acantholysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyphae in the stratum corneum (visible on KOH prep or PAS stain); mild spongiosis and inflammation', 'Dermatophyte infection histology reveals fungal hyphae within the stratum corneum, best demonstrated with a KOH preparation or PAS stain, together with mild spongiosis and a superficial inflammatory infiltrate. Granulomas, vesiculation, and acantholysis are not typical features of dermatophytosis.'),
('0f003e4c-f74e-5b7f-94a2-2aa60a38ef69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is candidiasis of the skin?', '["often in moist intertriginous areas; causes erythema and satellite pustules","An infection caused by Candida albicans","A bacterial infection","A viral infection","A parasitic infection"]'::jsonb, 'An infection caused by Candida albicans', 'Candidiasis is a skin infection caused by the yeast Candida albicans, typically affecting moist intertriginous areas and producing erythema, maceration, and satellite pustules. It is a fungal infection, not bacterial, viral, or parasitic.'),
('564b7467-c8ea-5886-943e-58fce2b0de7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of HSV infection?', '["multinucleated keratinocytes with nuclear inclusions (Cowdry type A)","ballooning degeneration","Intraepidermal vesicle with acantholysis","Subcorneal pustule","Spongiosis"]'::jsonb, 'Intraepidermal vesicle with acantholysis', 'HSV infection produces an intraepidermal vesicle with acantholysis (loss of intercellular adhesion), ballooning degeneration, and multinucleated keratinocytes containing eosinophilic Cowdry type A nuclear inclusions. Subcorneal pustules are the hallmark of impetigo, not HSV.'),
('ac4319fa-891f-5aa2-80cf-1e97bf21006d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is verruca vulgaris (common wart)?', '["A benign epithelial proliferation caused by human papillomavirus (HPV)","A bacterial infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and koilocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","with papillomatosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","hyperkeratosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign epithelial proliferation caused by human papillomavirus (HPV)', 'Verruca vulgaris is a benign epidermal proliferation induced by human papillomavirus (HPV), characterized histologically by papillomatosis, hyperkeratosis, and koilocytes (vacuolated keratinocytes). It is a viral epithelial process, not a bacterial infection.'),
('40c59c5f-e6ae-5427-a9b8-57b121e74b9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a seborrheic keratosis?', '["A premalignant lesion","A common benign epidermal tumor with horn cysts","papillomatosis","A malignant tumor","and acanthosis; appears stuck-on"]'::jsonb, 'A common benign epidermal tumor with horn cysts', 'Seborrheic keratosis is a very common benign epidermal tumor characterized by horn cysts (pseudocysts), acanthosis, and papillomatosis that give it a waxy ''stuck-on'' appearance. It is neither premalignant nor malignant, and it does not invade the dermis.'),
('86157040-c887-5d46-9cbe-5d74c2f09f00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of seborrheic keratosis?', '["and basaloid cells","hyperkeratosis","papillomatosis","Acanthosis (thickened epidermis)","horn cysts (pseudocysts)"]'::jsonb, 'Acanthosis (thickened epidermis)', 'The histologic hallmark of seborrheic keratosis is acanthosis, a marked thickening of the epidermis composed of basaloid cells, accompanied by hyperkeratosis, papillomatosis, and horn pseudocysts that produce its verrucous ''stuck-on'' profile.'),
('0860846a-0527-5217-918b-960fce8d9cd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is an actinic keratosis?', '["A melanocytic lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","A precancerous lesion of sun-damaged skin with atypical keratinocytes confined to the lower epidermis","A viral wart mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An invasive carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A precancerous lesion of sun-damaged skin with atypical keratinocytes confined to the lower epidermis', 'Actinic keratosis is a precancerous lesion on chronically sun-damaged skin in which atypical keratinocytes are confined to the lower epidermis (basal atypia) without invasion; some progress to squamous cell carcinoma. An invasive carcinoma would show atypical cells breaching the dermis.'),
('498d35e4-7a0e-518e-af3a-bb6157fbadc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of actinic keratosis?', '["and solar elastosis in the dermis","Full-thickness dysplasia","alternating hyperkeratosis and parakeratosis","Basal atypia (''bottom-heavy'' dysplasia)","Invasive nests"]'::jsonb, 'Basal atypia (''bottom-heavy'' dysplasia)', 'Actinic keratosis shows basal atypia (''bottom-heavy'' dysplasia) with atypical keratinocytes confined to the lower epidermis, along with alternating hyperkeratosis and parakeratosis and solar elastosis in the underlying dermis from chronic sun damage. Full-thickness dysplasia and invasive nests indicate carcinoma in situ or invasive SCC.'),
('60bfb969-2588-5288-a292-25fcffff0cbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a melanocytic nevus (mole)?', '["or both (compound) resulting in decreased functional activity","A malignant melanoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A benign proliferation of melanocytes in the epidermis (junctional)","dermis (intradermal) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A seborrheic keratosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign proliferation of melanocytes in the epidermis (junctional)', 'A melanocytic nevus is a benign proliferation of melanocytes that is junctional when the nests lie in the epidermis, intradermal when they are confined to the dermis, and compound when both sites are involved. Unlike melanoma, it shows a benign, symmetric, orderly growth pattern.'),
('2e8593e7-18b5-5752-afb9-35c214b1f721'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a junctional nevus?', '["A flat resulting in decreased functional activity","pigmented lesion with nests of melanocytes at the dermoepidermal junction","A compound nevus mediated by specific receptor activation","A raised lesion with nests in the dermis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A blue nevus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'pigmented lesion with nests of melanocytes at the dermoepidermal junction', 'A junctional nevus is a flat, pigmented lesion in which nests of melanocytes sit at the dermoepidermal junction. Nests confined to the dermis define an intradermal nevus, and a compound nevus involves both the junction and the dermis.'),
('04b26139-2541-5c12-a82b-538571c5f409'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a compound nevus?', '["A halo nevus resulting in decreased functional activity","A blue nevus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A nevus with melanocyte nests at both the junction and in the dermis; appears raised and pigmented","A flat junctional nevus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A dermal nevus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A nevus with melanocyte nests at both the junction and in the dermis; appears raised and pigmented', 'A compound nevus contains melanocyte nests at both the dermoepidermal junction and within the dermis, so it appears as a raised, pigmented papule. A purely junctional or purely intradermal nevus lacks this dual localization.'),
('0158da22-a25e-53b7-a8a6-3ba420bd9357'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is an intradermal nevus?', '["A raised resulting in decreased functional activity","A flat pigmented lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A blue nevus mediated by specific receptor activation","often flesh-colored papule with melanocyte nests confined to the dermis (mature nevus)","A junctional nevus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'often flesh-colored papule with melanocyte nests confined to the dermis (mature nevus)', 'An intradermal nevus is a mature nevus whose melanocyte nests lie entirely within the dermis, typically presenting as a raised, often flesh-colored papule. Junctional and compound lesions also involve the dermoepidermal junction, unlike this dermal-restricted nevus.'),
('d1368f6e-bad5-5e9f-9d78-9d87f3e960fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a dysplastic nevus (atypical mole)?', '["A benign common nevus resulting in decreased functional activity","An atypical melanocytic nevus with architectural and cytologic atypia","a risk factor for melanoma mediated by specific receptor activation","A melanoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A Spitz nevus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An atypical melanocytic nevus with architectural and cytologic atypia', 'A dysplastic (atypical) nevus is a melanocytic lesion showing architectural and cytologic atypia, and it is a risk factor and potential precursor for melanoma, especially when multiple or familial. A common benign nevus lacks this atypia, and a dysplastic nevus is not yet an invasive melanoma.'),
('4a467570-c343-57ea-9705-0f792cac822f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of dysplastic nevus?', '["Full-thepidermis atypia resulting in decreased functional activity","Pageto spread accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and lamellar fibroplasia in the dermis mediated by specific receptor activation","Lentiginous melanocytic hyperplasia with nests of atypical melanocytes showing bridging","Dermal invasion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lentiginous melanocytic hyperplasia with nests of atypical melanocytes showing bridging', 'Dysplastic nevi show lentiginous melanocytic hyperplasia with nests of atypical melanocytes that bridge between adjacent rete ridges, often with lamellar fibroplasia in the superficial dermis and surrounding dermal inflammatory response. Full-thickness epidermal atypia or pagetoid spread into the upper epidermis would raise suspicion for melanoma.'),
('8d673dd6-14b6-5d89-8e09-d8e57a138e83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the most common type of skin cancer?', '["Basal cell carcinoma (BCC)","Kaposi sarcoma","Malignant melanoma","Merkel cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)"]'::jsonb, 'Basal cell carcinoma (BCC)', 'Basal cell carcinoma is the most common skin cancer overall, arising from basal keratinocytes and usually growing slowly with local invasion but rare metastasis. Melanoma, SCC, Kaposi sarcoma, and Merkel cell carcinoma are all far less common.'),
('898215b7-d2e9-522d-8e25-25cde7f2b90a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of basal cell carcinoma?', '["Nests of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading","Keratin pearls accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","stromal retraction (clefting) mediated by specific receptor activation","and myxoid stroma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atypical melanocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nests of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading', 'BCC histology shows islands of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading of the outermost nuclei, surrounded by a myxoid stroma, and often with stromal retraction (clefting) around the tumor nests. Keratin pearls are features of squamous cell carcinoma, not BCC.'),
('077ae53d-aea2-5515-b194-666a5a14a303'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the subtypes of basal cell carcinoma?', '["superficial","Nodular (most common)","and morpheaform (sclerosing)","infiltrative","micronodular"]'::jsonb, 'Nodular (most common)', 'BCC has several subtypes, of which nodular is by far the most common; superficial, infiltrative, micronodular, and morpheaform (sclerosing) variants also occur, with the morpheaform type showing the most aggressive local behavior and deepest invasion.'),
('d0a128af-6e1b-50d6-a637-d312b667278f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) of the skin?', '["A malignant tumor of keratinocytes with potential for metastasis; arises from actinic keratosis or de novo","A mesenchymal tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A melanocytic tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A basaloid tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A glandular tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A malignant tumor of keratinocytes with potential for metastasis; arises from actinic keratosis or de novo', 'Squamous cell carcinoma is a malignant tumor of keratinocytes that can metastasize; it may arise from an actinic keratosis or develop de novo on sun-damaged skin. Melanocytic, glandular, and mesenchymal tumors originate from entirely different cell lineages.'),
('3fff781a-8563-59ed-9c2c-1a003069dff9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of SCC?', '["Basaloid palisading resulting in decreased functional activity","Invasive nests of squamous cells with keratin pearls","and variable differentiation mediated by specific receptor activation","intercellular bridges without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atypical melanocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Invasive nests of squamous cells with keratin pearls', 'SCC histology shows invasive nests of atypical squamous cells with keratin pearls (concentric whorls of keratin) and intercellular bridges, and the degree of differentiation varies from well to poorly differentiated. Peripheral palisading of basaloid cells is a BCC feature.'),
('c97caedc-e59c-56c1-8a07-39adc593e107'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the most aggressive form of skin cancer?', '["Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans","Merkel cell carcinoma","Malignant melanoma","SCC","BCC"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant melanoma', 'Malignant melanoma is the most aggressive skin cancer because of its high potential for early lymphatic and hematogenous metastasis, driven by its depth of invasion. BCC rarely metastasizes and SCC is far less lethal, making melanoma the principal cause of skin-cancer death.'),
('cd62c6c3-61c5-5222-8031-5ccdab705a55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the ABCDE criteria for melanoma diagnosis?', '["Evolution/changing","Diameter >6 mm","Asymmetry","Color variation","Border irregularity"]'::jsonb, 'Asymmetry', 'The ABCDE mnemonic for melanoma uses Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variation, Diameter greater than 6 mm, and Evolution (a changing lesion); a lesion showing several of these features warrants biopsy because each reflects a warning sign of malignant transformation.'),
('cb017bb1-c8f4-5c1b-80c6-3a241f82258c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the four main histologic types of melanoma?', '["Superficial spreading (most common)","and acral lentiginous","nodular","Only superficial spreading and nodular","lentigo maligna"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial spreading (most common)', 'The four main histologic types of melanoma are superficial spreading (the most common), nodular, lentigo maligna (lentigo maligna melanoma), and acral lentiginous. Superficial spreading melanoma begins with a horizontal radial growth phase, unlike nodular melanoma, which grows vertically from the outset.'),
('ba90cf12-df47-5160-ac7f-7184dfec4be5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is Breslow thickness?', '["The diameter of the melanoma resulting in decreased functional activity","The width of the ulceration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The depth of the nevus component mediated by specific receptor activation","The thickness of the epidermis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The depth of melanoma invasion measured in millimeters from the granular layer to the deepest tumor cell"]'::jsonb, 'The depth of melanoma invasion measured in millimeters from the granular layer to the deepest tumor cell', 'Breslow thickness measures the depth of melanoma invasion in millimeters from the granular layer of the epidermis down to the deepest invasive tumor cell, and it is the single most important prognostic factor. It is not the tumor diameter, ulceration width, or epidermal thickness.'),
('0b2770f4-6760-59f3-967b-4b4f9883eefd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is Clark level?', '["The ulceration status resulting in decreased functional activity","The anatomical level of melanoma invasion (I-V) from the epidermis to the subcutaneous fat","The mitotic rate mediated by specific receptor activation","The lymph node involvement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The clinical stage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anatomical level of melanoma invasion (I-V) from the epidermis to the subcutaneous fat', 'Clark level is a histologic staging system that describes the anatomical depth of melanoma invasion in five levels, from the epidermis (level I) through the papillary and reticular dermis down to the subcutaneous fat (level V). It does not reflect ulceration, mitotic rate, or nodal status.'),
('c5ab7a95-1411-52cc-b009-b70101c27f76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of superficial spreading melanoma?', '["Spindle cells in the dermis","Junctional nests only","with nests and single cells; dermal invasion in vertical phase","Predominantly dermal melanocytes","Pagetoid spread of atypical melanocytes throughout the epidermis"]'::jsonb, 'Pagetoid spread of atypical melanocytes throughout the epidermis', 'Superficial spreading melanoma histology shows pagetoid spread of atypical melanocytes, both singly and in nests, throughout the epidermis; dermal invasion may develop later in the vertical growth phase. A purely dermal spindle-cell pattern describes other melanoma variants rather than the superficial spreading type.'),
('1309f3d5-6192-573d-a52a-9c259369753e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is nodular melanoma?', '["A desmoplastic melanoma resulting in decreased functional activity","A slowly growing melanoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A vertically growing melanoma with no radial growth phase; presents as a rapidly growing nodule","An in situ melanoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A melanoma on the foot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A vertically growing melanoma with no radial growth phase; presents as a rapidly growing nodule', 'Nodular melanoma grows vertically from the outset with no radial growth phase, so it presents as a rapidly enlarging, often dark nodule and carries a worse prognosis for a given Breslow thickness. An in situ melanoma is confined to the epidermis and grows slowly.'),
('f6e59bff-13ca-56cf-a712-066a49092330'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is lentigo maligna melanoma?', '["A melanoma of the foot resulting in decreased functional activity","with a long radial growth phase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A mucosal melanoma mediated by specific receptor activation","A melanoma arising on sun-damaged skin (face) from lentigo maligna (Hutchinson freckle)","A nodular melanoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A melanoma arising on sun-damaged skin (face) from lentigo maligna (Hutchinson freckle)', 'Lentigo maligna melanoma arises on chronically sun-damaged skin, especially the face of older individuals, evolving from an in-situ lentigo maligna (Hutchinson freckle) after a long radial growth phase before invasion occurs. Mucosal, acral, and nodular melanomas are distinct clinical variants.'),
('8c360746-8b78-5974-93aa-fa107aad4903'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is acral lentiginous melanoma?', '["and nail beds (subungual); most common in dark-skinned individuals; not UV-related","A melanoma of the palms","soles","A melanoma of the trunk","A melanoma of the face"]'::jsonb, 'A melanoma of the palms', 'Acral lentiginous melanoma affects the palms, soles, and subungual (nail bed) skin, is not related to UV exposure, and is the most common melanoma seen in dark-skinned individuals. Truncal and facial melanomas are different clinical variants with distinct risk profiles.'),
('1d62aab2-64cd-54a7-9dad-ffb116b41d34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is desmoplastic melanoma?', '["A superficial spreading melanoma resulting in decreased functional activity","A pigmented melanoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","often neurotropic; may be amelanotic mediated by specific receptor activation","An uncommon melanoma variant with spindle cells and dense fibrous stroma","A nodular melanoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An uncommon melanoma variant with spindle cells and dense fibrous stroma', 'Desmoplastic melanoma is an uncommon variant composed of spindle cells embedded in a dense fibrous (desmoplastic) stroma; it is often neurotropic, may be amelanotic, and tends to recur locally with perineural spread. Superficial spreading and nodular melanomas are the common conventional types.'),
('0c007a26-8dd7-5d4f-a1ef-59c38504102c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the immunohistochemical markers for melanoma?', '["Melan-A (MART-1)","SOX10","and MITF","S100","HMB45 (gp100)"]'::jsonb, 'S100', 'S100 is the most sensitive immunohistochemical marker for melanoma, although it is not highly specific; Melan-A (MART-1), HMB-45 (gp100), SOX10, and MITF are also used and provide greater specificity. Nearly all melanomas express at least one of these markers, making them the backbone of diagnostic panels.'),
('86b3b10c-948b-5a5b-8ba8-27091f2a9636'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the sentinel lymph node biopsy for melanoma?', '["A therapeutic excision resulting in decreased functional activity","A diagnostic biopsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A blood test mediated by specific receptor activation","A staging procedure where the first draining lymph node(s) are removed and examined for metastasis","An imaging study leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A staging procedure where the first draining lymph node(s) are removed and examined for metastasis', 'Sentinel lymph node biopsy identifies the first draining lymph node(s) of a melanoma; their removal and histologic examination stage the disease and guide further treatment. Therapeutic excision, diagnostic biopsy, and imaging do not achieve nodal staging.'),
('ab18b023-4a01-55e9-b6c4-6438b79933ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is Merkel cell carcinoma?', '["with a high mortality rate resulting in decreased functional activity","A melanoma variant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A BCC variant mediated by specific receptor activation","A rare without affecting the primary tissue pathway","aggressive neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin (Merkel cell polyomavirus-associated)"]'::jsonb, 'aggressive neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin (Merkel cell polyomavirus-associated)', 'Merkel cell carcinoma is a rare, aggressive neuroendocrine carcinoma of the skin, often linked to Merkel cell polyomavirus and arising in sun-exposed skin of older adults. Melanoma and basal cell carcinoma are unrelated lineages.'),
('e453e0e5-470d-51f9-9676-4c9329eee7f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is Kaposi sarcoma?', '["endemic resulting in decreased functional activity","and epidemic (HIV/AIDS) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","with four clinical forms: classic mediated by specific receptor activation","A malignant vascular tumor caused by HHV-8 (human herpesvirus 8)","iatrogenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A malignant vascular tumor caused by HHV-8 (human herpesvirus 8)', 'Kaposi sarcoma is a malignant vascular (angioproliferative) tumor caused by HHV-8 (human herpesvirus 8). Its four clinical forms are classic, endemic, iatrogenic, and epidemic (HIV-associated), which are clinical variants rather than the underlying definition.'),
('013d2db6-d6f2-55e9-897a-de15bdfbee0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is eczema (dermatitis)?', '["and pruritus resulting in decreased functional activity","A spongiotic inflammatory skin condition with erythema","A psoriasiform condition mediated by specific receptor activation","A vesiculobullous condition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","vesicles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A spongiotic inflammatory skin condition with erythema', 'Eczema is a spongiotic inflammatory skin condition that classically presents with erythema and pruritus. Psoriasiform and vesiculobullous conditions are distinct diseases, and vesicles are a morphologic feature rather than the diagnosis.'),
('2c3c3d91-fddb-5452-b45f-43dc7fe1bb3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is spongiosis in eczema?', '["Thickening of the epidermis resulting in decreased functional activity","Vacuolar degeneration of the basal layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intercellular edema of the epidermis that widens intercellular spaces","a hallmark of eczema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dermal edema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intercellular edema of the epidermis that widens intercellular spaces', 'Spongiosis is intercellular edema of the epidermis that widens the intercellular spaces and is a hallmark of eczema. Epidermal thickening (acanthosis), basal vacuolization, and dermal edema describe different processes.'),
('7d63247d-b586-57f9-ad06-83709af2b1e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of acute eczema?', '["Parakeratosis","Spongiosis with intraepidermal vesicles","Acanthosis","lymphocytic exocytosis","and superficial perivascular lymphoid infiltrate"]'::jsonb, 'Spongiosis with intraepidermal vesicles', 'Acute eczema is dominated by spongiosis with intraepidermal vesicle formation, and it is often accompanied by lymphocytic exocytosis and a superficial perivascular lymphoid infiltrate. Parakeratosis and acanthosis are features of chronic disease.'),
('9a4d4cbb-eb45-5d60-ab33-c5ddda014e5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of chronic eczema (lichen simplex chronicus)?', '["and dermal fibrosis; minimal spongiosis","hypergranulosis","Prominent spongiosis","Acanthosis","hyperkeratosis"]'::jsonb, 'Acanthosis', 'Chronic eczema (lichen simplex chronicus) shows acanthosis together with hyperkeratosis, hypergranulosis, and dermal fibrosis while spongiosis is minimal. Prominent spongiosis with intraepidermal vesicles characterizes acute lesions.'),
('43c1e446-11f4-55d1-8143-739c717e68e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is psoriasis?', '["A chronic papulosquamous skin disease with well-demarcated","A granulomatous condition","A vesiculobullous condition","erythematous plaques with silvery scale","An eczematous condition"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic papulosquamous skin disease with well-demarcated', 'Psoriasis is a chronic papulosquamous skin disease producing well-demarcated erythematous plaques with silvery scale. Granulomatous, vesiculobullous, and eczematous disorders have different clinical and histologic features.'),
('04543c1a-3b4f-51a2-a3c4-d6ff66371ac7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of psoriasis?', '["elongation of rete ridges","thinning above dermal papillae","Parakeratosis (nuclei in stratum corneum)","acanthosis","and Munro microabscesses"]'::jsonb, 'Parakeratosis (nuclei in stratum corneum)', 'Parakeratosis, with retained nuclei in the stratum corneum, is the defining cornified-layer change of psoriasis and reflects accelerated keratinocyte turnover. Acanthosis, elongation of rete ridges, thinning above dermal papillae, and Munro microabscesses are additional psoriatic features.'),
('d80462e0-8084-5acb-b283-230b9ccc9354'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the Auspitz sign?', '["Pinpoint bleeding when the psoriatic scale is removed (due to underlying thin-walled capillaries)","Tzanck test accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Darier sign mediated by specific receptor activation","Nikolsky sign without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A sign of eczema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pinpoint bleeding when the psoriatic scale is removed (due to underlying thin-walled capillaries)', 'The Auspitz sign is pinpoint bleeding that occurs when psoriatic scale is removed, caused by exposure of thin, dilated dermal capillaries. The Tzanck test, Darier sign, and Nikolsky sign relate to other conditions.'),
('5a80ae38-05db-5d6b-a358-a48cdfb3ce35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is lichen planus?', '["violaceous papules; may involve oral mucosa","A spongiotic condition","A psoriasiform condition","polygonal","An inflammatory skin condition with pruritic"]'::jsonb, 'An inflammatory skin condition with pruritic', 'Lichen planus is an inflammatory skin condition of pruritic, polygonal, violaceous flat-topped papules that may involve the oral mucosa. Spongiotic (eczematous) and psoriasiform reactions are different entities.'),
('c275a0df-a0ae-52b8-b9d6-49032881ca19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of lichen planus?', '["saw-tooth rete ridges resulting in decreased functional activity","and Civatte bodies (apoptotic keratinocytes) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lichenoid (band-like) lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermoepidermal junction","Spongiosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parakeratosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lichenoid (band-like) lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermoepidermal junction', 'Lichen planus shows a lichenoid (band-like) lymphocytic infiltrate at the dermoepidermal junction along with saw-tooth rete ridges and Civatte bodies (apoptotic keratinocytes). Spongiosis and parakeratosis are not characteristic.'),
('906235ec-56f9-53cb-b2bf-83aa83623185'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the Wickham striae in lichen planus?', '["Excoriation marks resulting in decreased functional activity","A sign of psoriasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scales in psoriasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Fine white reticulate lines on the surface of lichen planus papules","corresponding to hypergranulosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fine white reticulate lines on the surface of lichen planus papules', 'Wickham striae are fine white reticulate lines seen on the surface of lichen planus papules, produced by focal hypergranulosis. Excoriation marks and psoriatic scale belong to other skin diseases.'),
('49f8a196-a3f8-5fb7-86de-816c5051e078'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are blistering (vesiculobullous) diseases?', '["A group of skin disorders characterized by blister formation due to loss of cell-cell or cell-matrix adhesion","Inflammatory skin diseases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic eczematous conditions mediated by specific receptor activation","Infectious skin diseases without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pigmentation disorders leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A group of skin disorders characterized by blister formation due to loss of cell-cell or cell-matrix adhesion', 'Blistering diseases are skin disorders in which blisters form from loss of cell-cell or cell-matrix adhesion, whether autoimmune, genetic, or infectious in cause. Being merely inflammatory, eczematous, infectious, or pigmented does not define the group.'),
('d9335c41-8991-570d-9797-27875a5958b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is pemphigus vulgaris?', '["A subepidermal blistering disease resulting in decreased functional activity","An infectious blistering disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A genetic blistering disorder mediated by specific receptor activation","A drug reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A potentially fatal autoimmune blistering disease with intraepidermal blisters due to desmoglein 3 antibodies"]'::jsonb, 'A potentially fatal autoimmune blistering disease with intraepidermal blisters due to desmoglein 3 antibodies', 'Pemphigus vulgaris is a potentially fatal autoimmune blistering disease with intraepidermal blisters caused by antibodies against desmoglein 3. Subepidermal blistering diseases such as bullous pemphigoid separate at the basement membrane instead.'),
('fb25ea97-8348-5d92-9f7f-9710fbb64472'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of pemphigus vulgaris?', '["Subepidermal blister resulting in decreased functional activity","Dermal neutrophilic infiltrate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intraepidermal acantholysis (loss of adhesion) with suprabasal blister formation and tombstoning of basal cells","Spongiosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acanthosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intraepidermal acantholysis (loss of adhesion) with suprabasal blister formation and tombstoning of basal cells', 'Pemphigus vulgaris shows intraepidermal acantholysis with suprabasal blister formation and tombstoning of basal cells, reflecting loss of keratinocyte adhesion. A subepidermal blister indicates a different blistering disease.'),
('3c414486-315e-5e6a-963c-7163f647afb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the immunofluorescence pattern in pemphigus vulgaris?', '["IgG and C3 deposition in an intercellular (fishnet) pattern throughout the epidermis","Linear deposition at the basement membrane (BMZ) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IgE deposition mediated by specific receptor activation","Negative immunofluorescence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granular deposition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IgG and C3 deposition in an intercellular (fishnet) pattern throughout the epidermis', 'Pemphigus vulgaris shows IgG and C3 in an intercellular fishnet pattern throughout the epidermis, matching the distribution of the target desmogleins. Linear basement-membrane deposition characterizes bullous pemphigoid.'),
('78d6f8da-3dfb-509a-9e67-b150d97c07a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is bullous pemphigoid?', '["A variant of pemphigus resulting in decreased functional activity","A drug reaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A genetic blistering disorder mediated by specific receptor activation","An intraepidermal blistering disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An autoimmune subepidermal blistering disease with tense blisters and antibodies against BP180 and BP230 (hemidesmosome proteins)"]'::jsonb, 'An autoimmune subepidermal blistering disease with tense blisters and antibodies against BP180 and BP230 (hemidesmosome proteins)', 'Bullous pemphigoid is an autoimmune subepidermal blistering disease with tense blisters caused by autoantibodies against the hemidesmosomal proteins BP180 and BP230. Intraepidermal blistering distinguishes pemphigus.'),
('9b849813-47aa-5135-bcc3-fa0e738822d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of bullous pemphigoid?', '["Granulomas resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal layer vacuolization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intraepidermal acantholysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Subepidermal blister with eosinophils in the blister cavity and dermis (eosinophilic spongiosis)","Neutrophilic microabscesses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subepidermal blister with eosinophils in the blister cavity and dermis (eosinophilic spongiosis)', 'Bullous pemphigoid yields a subepidermal blister with eosinophils in the blister cavity and dermis (eosinophilic spongiosis) due to antibody-mediated damage at the basement membrane. Intraepidermal acantholysis and neutrophilic microabscesses belong to other disorders.'),
('fb6588c6-8428-5dd9-a33d-59ff4801f1b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the immunofluorescence pattern in bullous pemphigoid?', '["Intercellular IgG pattern resulting in decreased functional activity","Negative IF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Linear IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone","Granular basement membrane deposition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nuclear staining leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linear IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone', 'Bullous pemphigoid shows linear IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone, reflecting autoantibodies to hemidesmosomal components. An intercellular fishnet pattern indicates pemphigus, while granular dermal deposits suggest dermatitis herpetiformis.'),
('46e9e5e8-b2ef-53d1-ae6a-c0005305cccc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is dermatitis herpetiformis?', '["An intensely pruritic autoimmune blistering disease associated with celiac disease","A genetic blistering disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","with granular IgA deposition in dermal papillae mediated by specific receptor activation","A type of pemphigus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A type of pemphigoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An intensely pruritic autoimmune blistering disease associated with celiac disease', 'Dermatitis herpetiformis is an intensely pruritic autoimmune blistering disease associated with celiac disease, showing granular IgA deposition in the dermal papillae. It is neither a pemphigus nor a pemphigoid.'),
('14b08cd4-7c15-5009-be74-16336a9fdb44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the immunofluorescence pattern in dermatitis herpetiformis?', '["Negative immunofluorescence","Intercellular IgG","Granular IgA deposition in the dermal papillae","Linear IgA at the BMZ","Linear C3 deposition"]'::jsonb, 'Granular IgA deposition in the dermal papillae', 'Dermatitis herpetiformis characteristically shows granular IgA deposition in the dermal papillae. Linear IgA along the basement membrane instead characterizes linear IgA disease.'),
('4807e68b-f380-5b1f-aa84-5215e074766f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is linear IgA disease?', '["Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita resulting in decreased functional activity","Dermatitis herpetiformis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A subepidermal blistering disease with linear IgA deposition at the basement membrane; may be idiopathic or drug-induced","Bullous lupus erythematosus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A variant of pemphigoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A subepidermal blistering disease with linear IgA deposition at the basement membrane; may be idiopathic or drug-induced', 'Linear IgA disease is a subepidermal blistering disorder with linear IgA deposition at the basement membrane, and it may be idiopathic or drug-induced. Dermatitis herpetiformis instead shows granular IgA in dermal papillae.'),
('4c799be4-6464-5eed-9de0-3cd3a59e2125'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is scabies?', '["A viral infection resulting in decreased functional activity","A parasitic infestation by the mite Sarcoptes scabiei causing intense pruritus and burrows in the skin","A bacterial infection mediated by specific receptor activation","A fungal infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An allergic reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A parasitic infestation by the mite Sarcoptes scabiei causing intense pruritus and burrows in the skin', 'Scabies is a parasitic infestation by the mite Sarcoptes scabiei, which burrows in the stratum corneum and causes intense pruritus. Bacterial, fungal, and viral agents do not produce these characteristic burrows.'),
('0f8dbd9b-0ddc-509d-86e9-3e49621ff7fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of scabies?', '["The mite (or scybala/eggs) in the stratum corneum; spongiosis","and a superficial and deep perivascular infiltrate with eosinophils accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subcorneal pustule mediated by specific receptor activation","Acantholysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granulomas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mite (or scybala/eggs) in the stratum corneum; spongiosis', 'Scabies histology demonstrates the mite, scybala, or eggs within the stratum corneum, accompanied by spongiosis and a superficial and deep perivascular infiltrate containing eosinophils. Acantholysis, granulomas, and subcorneal pustules are not characteristic.'),
('bca8ffef-91d3-5bd0-a2a6-48832221111e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are lice (pediculosis)?', '["A mite infestation resulting in decreased functional activity","Infestation by Pediculus humanus (head/body lice) or Phthirus pubis (pubic lice)","A bacterial infection mediated by specific receptor activation","causing pruritus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fungal infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infestation by Pediculus humanus (head/body lice) or Phthirus pubis (pubic lice)', 'Pediculosis is an infestation by Pediculus humanus (head or body lice) or Phthirus pubis (pubic lice), which causes pruritus. Mites and bacteria or fungi are unrelated agents.'),
('087fedf6-ac87-5814-bf5a-3e2ba5aec440'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is cutaneous larva migrans?', '["A viral exanthem resulting in decreased functional activity","A scabies infestation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A creeping eruption caused by hookworm larvae (Ancylostoma) migrating in the epidermis","A fungal infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A bacterial infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A creeping eruption caused by hookworm larvae (Ancylostoma) migrating in the epidermis', 'Cutaneous larva migrans is a creeping, serpiginous eruption caused by hookworm larvae (Ancylostoma) migrating within the epidermis. Viral, fungal, and bacterial infections do not produce this pattern.'),
('a9e58d2b-a494-53ff-9b7d-1f55bfa4db16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the most common bacterial infection of the skin?', '["Folliculitis","Erysipelas","Impetigo","Abscess","Cellulitis"]'::jsonb, 'Impetigo', 'Impetigo is the most common bacterial infection of the skin, a superficial pustular or crusted infection usually caused by S. aureus or group A streptococcus. Folliculitis, erysipelas, abscess, and cellulitis involve deeper structures or different mechanisms.'),
('4509b9f3-1e7b-52cf-9995-6fabe7446f96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is furuncle (boil)?', '["A superficial folliculitis resulting in decreased functional activity","A deep accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","by S. aureus mediated by specific receptor activation","An abscess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","painful infection of a hair follicle extending into the subcutaneous tissue"]'::jsonb, 'painful infection of a hair follicle extending into the subcutaneous tissue', 'A furuncle (boil) is a deep, painful infection of a hair follicle that extends into the subcutaneous tissue, usually caused by S. aureus. Superficial folliculitis remains confined to the follicle, while an abscess is a broader pus collection.'),
('7cf083d9-4540-5c27-b8e5-6d287306caab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is erythrasma?', '["A superficial bacterial infection of intertriginous areas by Corynebacterium minutissimum","causing well-defined brown patches with coral-red fluorescence on Wood lamp accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fungal infection mediated by specific receptor activation","A viral infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A parasitic infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A superficial bacterial infection of intertriginous areas by Corynebacterium minutissimum', 'Erythrasma is a superficial bacterial infection of intertriginous areas by Corynebacterium minutissimum that produces well-defined brown patches with coral-red fluorescence under a Wood lamp. Fungal, viral, and parasitic causes are not implicated.'),
('a272c5fc-aa47-5e53-8e7d-93342ab5961b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is leprosy (Hansen disease)?', '["with two poles: tuberculoid (paucibacillary) and lepromatous (multibacillary)","A bacterial skin infection","A parasitic infection","A fungal infection","A chronic granulomatous infection by Mycobacterium leprae"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic granulomatous infection by Mycobacterium leprae', 'Leprosy is a chronic granulomatous infection by Mycobacterium leprae with two clinical poles: tuberculoid (paucibacillary) and lepromatous (multibacillary). It is bacterial in origin rather than fungal, parasitic, or viral.'),
('17ad3f69-5d0d-5f13-9df9-952f783626bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of tuberculoid leprosy?', '["Non-caseating granulomas with epithelioid cells and Langhans giant cells; few or no AFB; nerve involvement","Fibrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Non-specific inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","Acute neutrophilic inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sheets of foamy macrophages with abundant AFB leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-caseating granulomas with epithelioid cells and Langhans giant cells; few or no AFB; nerve involvement', 'Tuberculoid leprosy shows well-formed non-caseating granulomas with epithelioid cells and Langhans giant cells, few or no AFB, and prominent nerve involvement due to a strong cell-mediated response. Sheets of foamy macrophages packed with AFB characterize lepromatous disease.'),
('35b90159-dcd6-5825-9a91-7c746f76890a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of lepromatous leprosy?', '["Well-formed granulomas resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphocytic infiltration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Epithelioid histiocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Neutrophilic infiltration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sheets of foamy macrophages (Virchow cells) with abundant AFB (globi); sparse granuloma formation; dermal nerve involvement"]'::jsonb, 'Sheets of foamy macrophages (Virchow cells) with abundant AFB (globi); sparse granuloma formation; dermal nerve involvement', 'Lepromatous leprosy shows sheets of foamy macrophages (Virchow cells) laden with AFB (globi), sparse granuloma formation, and dermal nerve involvement, reflecting weak cell-mediated immunity. Well-formed epithelioid granulomas mark the tuberculoid pole.'),
('706ae4b6-b143-52a8-8bc4-1b8ad67a86b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is pilomatricoma (pilomatrixoma)?', '["A sebaceous tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A malignant hair follicle tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A sweat gland tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","often calcified without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A benign hair follicle tumor with basophilic cells and shadow cells"]'::jsonb, 'A benign hair follicle tumor with basophilic cells and shadow cells', 'Pilomatricoma is a benign hair follicle tumor composed of basophilic germinal cells and shadow (ghost) cells, and it is often calcified. Sweat gland and sebaceous tumors are different adnexal lineages, and this lesion is benign.'),
('367c9ea7-a7f2-5b2d-8fe1-cdfe61d2b00f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of pilomatricoma?', '["Keratin pearls resulting in decreased functional activity","Sebaceous differentiation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glandular structures mediated by specific receptor activation","Nevus cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two cell populations: basophilic cells (at the periphery) and anucleate shadow (ghost) cells (central); often calcified"]'::jsonb, 'Two cell populations: basophilic cells (at the periphery) and anucleate shadow (ghost) cells (central); often calcified', 'Pilomatricoma histology shows two cell populations: peripheral basophilic germinal cells and central anucleate shadow (ghost) cells, with frequent calcification. Keratin pearls, sebaceous differentiation, glandular structures, and nevus cells are not the pattern.'),
('c8fe499d-5251-56fc-9d25-ecb9f2c64f4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a sebaceous adenoma?', '["A benign tumor of sebaceous glands","An epidermal cyst","A malignant sebaceous tumor","A hair follicle tumor","often associated with Muir-Torre syndrome (sebaceous tumors + visceral malignancy)"]'::jsonb, 'A benign tumor of sebaceous glands', 'A sebaceous adenoma is a benign tumor of sebaceous glands that may be associated with Muir-Torre syndrome (sebaceous tumors with visceral malignancy). It is neither an epidermal cyst nor a malignant or follicular lesion.'),
('5597fbd7-53f7-57cf-99f0-860c7c44af4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a sebaceous carcinoma?', '["A BCC with sebaceous differentiation","A SCC","A benign sebaceous tumor","A malignant tumor of sebaceous glands","most commonly occurring on the eyelid (meibomian gland carcinoma)"]'::jsonb, 'A malignant tumor of sebaceous glands', 'Sebaceous carcinoma is a malignant tumor of sebaceous glands that most often arises on the eyelid as a meibomian gland carcinoma. Its cytologic atypia and invasive growth separate it from benign sebaceous lesions.'),
('c3d26862-c889-5908-b329-dc7310ea232b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is an eccrine poroma?', '["on palms/soles resulting in decreased functional activity","A hair follicle tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A malignant sweat gland tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A sebaceous tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A benign sweat gland tumor arising from the intraepidermal duct (acrosyringium)"]'::jsonb, 'A benign sweat gland tumor arising from the intraepidermal duct (acrosyringium)', 'Eccrine poroma is a benign sweat gland tumor arising from the intraepidermal eccrine duct (acrosyringium), often on the palms and soles. Follicular and sebaceous tumors are different lineages, and this lesion is benign.'),
('9bda7cf5-122f-5521-b97a-ae65fda414ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a hidradenoma papilliferum?', '["A breast tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A hair follicle tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A malignant sweat gland tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A sebaceous tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A benign apocrine sweat gland tumor of the vulva and perianal region with papillary and glandular features"]'::jsonb, 'A benign apocrine sweat gland tumor of the vulva and perianal region with papillary and glandular features', 'Hidradenoma papilliferum is a benign apocrine sweat gland tumor of the vulva and perianal region showing papillary and glandular architecture. Breast, follicular, sebaceous, and malignant sweat lesions are excluded.'),
('f9b141ea-6b3e-56a1-ab67-9d0b39c334cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a cylindroma?', '["A benign adnexal tumor (likely eccrine/apocrine) with a jigsaw puzzle pattern of basaloid nests surrounded by hyaline sheaths","A sebaceous tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A malignant tumor mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A vascular tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A hair follicle tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign adnexal tumor (likely eccrine/apocrine) with a jigsaw puzzle pattern of basaloid nests surrounded by hyaline sheaths', 'A cylindroma is a benign adnexal tumor, probably of eccrine or apocrine origin, showing basaloid nests in a jigsaw-puzzle pattern surrounded by hyaline sheaths. It is not malignant and is not of vascular, sebaceous, or follicular origin.'),
('d274154e-eb23-52b4-bba0-f4a36461fba9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans (DFSP)?', '["A BCC resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A low-to-intermediate grade malignant dermal sarcoma with storiform pattern and CD34 positivity; locally aggressive","metastases mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A melanoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A benign dermal tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A low-to-intermediate grade malignant dermal sarcoma with storiform pattern and CD34 positivity; locally aggressive', 'Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans is a low-to-intermediate grade malignant dermal sarcoma with a storiform pattern and CD34 positivity; it is locally aggressive but rarely metastasizes. BCC, melanoma, and benign dermal tumors behave differently.'),
('7bbdef81-ac04-5511-a58d-f8dae00cea9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of DFSP?', '["Palisading pattern resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Monotonous spindle cells in a storiform (cartwheel or whorled) pattern infiltrating the subcutaneous fat in a honeycomb pattern","Epithelioid cells mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Pleomorphic giant cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glandular structures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monotonous spindle cells in a storiform (cartwheel or whorled) pattern infiltrating the subcutaneous fat in a honeycomb pattern', 'DFSP is composed of monotonous spindle cells arranged in a storiform (cartwheel or whorled) pattern that infiltrate subcutaneous fat in a honeycomb manner. Palisading, epithelioid, pleomorphic giant cell, and glandular patterns point to other tumors.'),
('db7829c2-bd6f-5978-a907-e23af87e809d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is an epidermal inclusion cyst?', '["filled with keratin resulting in decreased functional activity","A pilar cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A common cyst lined by stratified squamous epithelium with a granular layer","A sebaceous cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A dermoid cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A common cyst lined by stratified squamous epithelium with a granular layer', 'An epidermal inclusion cyst is a common keratin-filled cyst lined by stratified squamous epithelium that retains a granular layer. Pilar cysts lack a granular layer, and dermoid cysts contain adnexal structures.'),
('8b105649-2b91-53cd-a73f-d591cce25d20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of an epidermal inclusion cyst?', '["Cuboidal lining resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcification accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","epithelial lining mediated by specific receptor activation","Sebaceous glands in the wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wall of stratified squamous epithelium with a granular layer; lumen filled with laminated keratin"]'::jsonb, 'Wall of stratified squamous epithelium with a granular layer; lumen filled with laminated keratin', 'The wall of an epidermal inclusion cyst is stratified squamous epithelium with a granular layer, and the lumen is filled with laminated keratin. A cuboidal lining, calcification, or sebaceous glands in the wall indicate other cysts.'),
('ff75d787-3348-5038-b776-375c5814418d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a pilar (trichilemmal) cyst?', '["lined by stratified squamous epithelium without a granular layer (abrupt keratinization)","A cyst of the hair follicle isthmus","A dermoid cyst","on the scalp","An epidermal inclusion cyst"]'::jsonb, 'A cyst of the hair follicle isthmus', 'A pilar (trichilemmal) cyst is a cyst of the hair follicle isthmus, common on the scalp, lined by stratified squamous epithelium that keratinizes abruptly without a granular layer. Epidermoid cysts retain a granular layer.'),
('63ab432c-a762-5a42-9b75-677713e46f16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a ganglion cyst?', '["A mucin-filled cyst near joints or tendons; lined by compressed fibrous tissue (not epithelium)","A synovial cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A sebaceous cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","A true epithelial cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A pilar cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A mucin-filled cyst near joints or tendons; lined by compressed fibrous tissue (not epithelium)', 'A ganglion cyst is a mucin-filled cyst near joints or tendons that is lined by compressed fibrous tissue rather than true epithelium, so it is a pseudocyst. Synovial, sebaceous, and pilar cysts are distinct entities.'),
('36ca4ca8-0cbf-514d-8f6e-dc62db070881'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a myxoid cyst?', '["An epidermal cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","associated with osteoarthritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pseudocyst of the digital tip with mucin (hyaluronic acid) accumulation","A pilar cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ganglion cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A pseudocyst of the digital tip with mucin (hyaluronic acid) accumulation', 'A myxoid (digital mucous) cyst is a pseudocyst of the digital tip with accumulation of mucin (hyaluronic acid), often associated with osteoarthritis. Epidermal, pilar, and ganglion cysts are different lesions.'),
('0d152b45-bc70-5daf-8a70-22fab15b502d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a milium?', '["common on the face resulting in decreased functional activity","An epidermal inclusion cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A tiny keratin-filled cyst (1-2 mm) derived from the pilosebaceous follicle","A pilar cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A whitehead comedo leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A tiny keratin-filled cyst (1-2 mm) derived from the pilosebaceous follicle', 'A milium is a tiny keratin-filled cyst (1-2 mm) derived from the pilosebaceous follicle, commonly on the face. Epidermal inclusion and pilar cysts are larger, while a whitehead comedo is a comedone rather than a true cyst.'),
('3f0f863e-ee2d-56dd-98fa-a723bcc6b763'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a steatocystoma multiplex?', '["Sebaceous adenomas resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An autosomal dominant condition with multiple sebaceous duct cysts lined by stratified squamous epithelium with an eosinophilic cuticle","Epidermal inclusion cysts mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Pilar cysts without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vellus hair cysts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An autosomal dominant condition with multiple sebaceous duct cysts lined by stratified squamous epithelium with an eosinophilic cuticle', 'Steatocystoma multiplex is an autosomal dominant condition producing multiple sebaceous duct cysts lined by stratified squamous epithelium bearing an eosinophilic cuticle. Epidermal inclusion, pilar, and vellus hair cysts differ in location and lining.'),
('619a2b8c-7e02-5f5e-a3a9-709201394045'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a vellus hair cyst?', '["A sebaceous cyst","An epidermal cyst","lined by stratified squamous epithelium; common on the chest","A pilar cyst","A cyst filled with vellus hairs and keratin"]'::jsonb, 'A cyst filled with vellus hairs and keratin', 'A vellus hair cyst is a cyst filled with vellus hairs and keratin, lined by stratified squamous epithelium and often located on the chest. Sebaceous, epidermal, and pilar cysts are distinct entities.'),
('0d8fb6fb-bdb6-5d54-8249-e5967f42bac5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a branchial cleft cyst?', '["An epidermal inclusion cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","lined by stratified squamous epithelium with lymphoid tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A congenital cyst in the lateral neck from incomplete closure of the second branchial cleft","A thyroglossal duct cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A dermoid cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital cyst in the lateral neck from incomplete closure of the second branchial cleft', 'A branchial cleft cyst is a congenital lateral neck cyst that results from incomplete involution of the second branchial cleft; it is typically lined by stratified squamous or respiratory epithelium over a cuff of lymphoid tissue. Epidermal inclusion cysts are acquired keratin-filled dermal cysts, thyroglossal duct cysts arise in the midline, and dermoid cysts contain hair and sebaceous appendages.'),
('bc390652-b0b0-50a4-ad65-f60d610fe89d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a lentigo simplex?', '["pigmented macule with elongated rete ridges and increased melanocytes at the junction","A solar lentigo accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A junctional nevus mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign without affecting the primary tissue pathway","flat leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'pigmented macule with elongated rete ridges and increased melanocytes at the junction', 'Lentigo simplex is a benign pigmented macule showing elongated rete ridges and increased numbers of melanocytes confined to the stratum basale. Solar lentigo differs by having basal hyperpigmentation without melanocyte hyperplasia, a junctional nevus is defined by melanocytic nests, and the remaining choices are isolated descriptors rather than the lesion itself.'),
('55cb73d4-e146-560a-9ed2-03332ee76612'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is solar lentigo (lentigo senilis)?', '["An actinic keratosis resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A benign pigmented macule on sun-exposed skin with elongated rete ridges and increased basal pigmentation but no melanocyte hyperplasia","A melanoma in situ mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A junctional nevus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lentigo simplex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign pigmented macule on sun-exposed skin with elongated rete ridges and increased basal pigmentation but no melanocyte hyperplasia', 'Solar lentigo is a benign macule on sun-exposed skin with elongated rete ridges and increased basal pigmentation but no increase in melanocyte number, unlike lentigo simplex. Actinic keratosis is a dysplastic precursor, melanoma in situ shows malignant melanocytes, and a junctional nevus contains melanocytic nests.'),
('b4e699be-3ab5-5e11-8bec-2a521b64d25d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is ephelis (freckle)?', '["A junctional nevus resulting in decreased functional activity","A solar lentigo accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A flat mediated by specific receptor activation","light brown macule ( genetic) with increased melanin production but normal melanocyte number","A lentigo simplex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'light brown macule ( genetic) with increased melanin production but normal melanocyte number', 'An ephelis (freckle) is a genetically predisposed light brown macule in which melanocytes are normal in number but produce increased melanin, and it darkens on sun exposure. Solar lentigo shows structural rete-ridge changes, while nevi and lentigo simplex are defined by melanocytic proliferation rather than hyperfunction alone.'),
('21e3da82-2a7d-5f97-9ff7-0707dba250ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a blue nevus?', '["composed of dermal dendritic melanocytes (deep dermis)","A junctional nevus","A benign melanocytic nevus with a blue-black color","A compound nevus","A Spitz nevus"]'::jsonb, 'A benign melanocytic nevus with a blue-black color', 'A blue nevus is a benign melanocytic nevus that appears blue-black because its dermal dendritic melanocytes lie deep in the dermis, where they absorb long-wavelength light (Tyndall effect). Junctional and compound nevi have junctional components, and a Spitz nevus shows epithelioid and spindle cells.'),
('e3265ee9-58c2-5bd9-851c-8ee1b322b8fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a Spitz nevus?', '["A blue nevus resulting in decreased functional activity","often in children; can be mistaken for melanoma (Spitzoid lesions) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A melanoma mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign melanocytic nevus with epithelioid and/or spindle cells","A compound nevus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign melanocytic nevus with epithelioid and/or spindle cells', 'A Spitz nevus is a benign melanocytic nevus composed of epithelioid and/or spindle cells, occurring mainly in children, where it can be confused with melanoma. Blue nevus, compound nevus, and melanoma do not share this distinctive epithelioid-spindle cell morphology.'),
('86f69cc1-aec1-5cc8-97ee-718c9d471eff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a halo nevus?', '["A nevus surrounded by a depigmented halo due to an immune response (lymphocytic) against melanocytes","A melanoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A Spitz nevus mediated by specific receptor activation","A blue nevus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A dysplastic nevus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A nevus surrounded by a depigmented halo due to an immune response (lymphocytic) against melanocytes', 'A halo nevus is a melanocytic nevus surrounded by a ring of depigmentation that results from a lymphocytic immune reaction directed against melanocytes. Melanoma, Spitz, blue, and dysplastic nevi do not classically display this depigmented halo.'),
('29bb1263-b6be-5790-939b-f3d0f7143025'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a congenital nevus?', '["An acquired nevus resulting in decreased functional activity","A melanocytic nevus present at birth; giant congenital nevi have a higher risk of melanoma transformation","A blue nevus mediated by specific receptor activation","A Spitz nevus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lentigo leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A melanocytic nevus present at birth; giant congenital nevi have a higher risk of melanoma transformation', 'A congenital nevus is a melanocytic nevus present at birth, and giant congenital nevi carry a substantially increased risk of transforming into melanoma. Acquired nevi appear later in life, and blue nevi, Spitz nevi, and lentigines are biologically distinct lesions.'),
('f3508855-e861-5f80-83eb-6aa3bb3b972f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the risk of melanoma in giant congenital nevus?', '["especially in nevi with neuroid differentiation","50% risk","Approximately 5-10% lifetime risk","increased risk","1% risk"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 5-10% lifetime risk', 'Giant congenital nevi carry an approximate 5-10% lifetime risk of melanoma, with the highest risk in lesions showing neuroid differentiation. Small congenital and acquired nevi carry much lower risk; 50% greatly overstates the risk and 1% underestimates it.'),
('d0d88978-9546-54fb-9d7d-fe1c5a11c655'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a nevus of Ota?', '["A blue nevus resulting in decreased functional activity","involving the eye and periorbital area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A Mongolian spot mediated by specific receptor activation","A blue-gray pigmentation of the face along the V1 and V2 trigeminal distribution","A café-au-lait macule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A blue-gray pigmentation of the face along the V1 and V2 trigeminal distribution', 'The nevus of Ota is a blue-gray facial pigmentation distributed along the ophthalmic (V1) and maxillary (V2) trigeminal divisions, often involving the eye and periorbital region. Mongolians spots are sacral, blue nevi are focal dermal lesions, and café-au-lait macules are light brown.'),
('03b5f1ab-b8c6-5e97-ba74-4a9a3bf37c2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are café-au-lait macules?', '["Solar lentigines resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","well-circumscribed macules that are markers of neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) when >6 spots >5 mm (prepubertal) or >15 mm (postpubertal)","Light brown mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Melasma without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Freckles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'well-circumscribed macules that are markers of neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) when >6 spots >5 mm (prepubertal) or >15 mm (postpubertal)', 'Café-au-lait macules are well-circumscribed light brown macules; six or more larger than 5 mm before puberty (or 15 mm after) are a diagnostic criterion for NF1. Solar lentigines, melasma, and freckles occupy different sites and are not NF1 markers.'),
('4a5e7f67-406c-5a32-b8b9-cc86cdef8aec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are axillary freckling (Crowe sign)?', '["a diagnostic criterion for neurofibromatosis type 1","Normal freckles","Acanthosis nigricans","Freckles in the axilla or groin","Epidermal nevus"]'::jsonb, 'Freckles in the axilla or groin', 'Axillary or inguinal freckling (Crowe sign) is the presence of freckles in the axilla or groin and is a diagnostic criterion for neurofibromatosis type 1. Ordinary freckles favor sun-exposed skin, acanthosis nigricans is velvety and flexural, and epidermal nevi are verrucous plaques.'),
('20301095-fef7-50b2-93f3-c6a2ff97014b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is acanthosis nigricans?', '["Velvety","hyperpigmented plaques in the axillae","neck","and groin; associated with insulin resistance","obesity"]'::jsonb, 'hyperpigmented plaques in the axillae', 'Acanthosis nigricans is defined by velvety, hyperpigmented plaques in the axillae, neck, and groin, with a strong association with obesity and insulin resistance. Among the choices, only ''hyperpigmented plaques in the axillae'' names the actual lesion; the other options are isolated descriptive fragments.'),
('9ca27f9e-c233-5e9c-bc3b-a221d7853e35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are skin tags (acrochordons)?', '["Benign","and groin; associated with obesity and DM","pedunculated fibroepithelial polyps","often in the neck","axillae"]'::jsonb, 'pedunculated fibroepithelial polyps', 'Skin tags (acrochordons) are benign, pedunculated fibroepithelial polyps that occur most often on the neck and in the axillae and are associated with obesity and diabetes. Only ''pedunculated fibroepithelial polyps'' states the lesion itself; the other answers describe single attributes such as benignity, site, or risk factor.'),
('0c201d32-7433-570a-a0a9-625486d92f00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is xanthelasma?', '["A sebaceous cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","A xanthoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Yellowish plaques on the eyelids due to lipid deposition in macrophages; may be associated with hyperlipidemia","An epidermal cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A milium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Yellowish plaques on the eyelids due to lipid deposition in macrophages; may be associated with hyperlipidemia', 'Xanthelasma is a periorbital deposit of lipid-laden macrophages producing yellowish plaques on the eyelids, and it may signal underlying hyperlipidemia. Xanthomas elsewhere, sebaceous and epidermal cysts, and milia do not show this distinctive eyelid lipid deposition.'),
('7cf4e815-13d9-555f-97bc-27b7028d0067'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is calcinosis cutis?', '["Deposition of calcium in the skin","or idiopathic","metastatic (elevated Ca/Phos)","which can be dystrophic (damaged tissue)","A skin infection"]'::jsonb, 'Deposition of calcium in the skin', 'Calcinosis cutis is the abnormal deposition of calcium in the skin, and it may be dystrophic (following tissue damage), metastatic (with elevated calcium or phosphate), or idiopathic. A skin infection is unrelated to calcium deposition in the dermis.'),
('54c7cecc-3184-5116-b45e-1b663e5e2bc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is urticaria pigmentosa (mastocytosis)?', '["A connective tissue disease","causing Darier sign (urtication on stroking)","A pigmentary disorder","A vascular tumor","A condition with increased mast cells in the skin"]'::jsonb, 'A condition with increased mast cells in the skin', 'Urticaria pigmentosa is the cutaneous form of mastocytosis, in which increased mast cells accumulate in the skin and release histamine on stroking to produce urtication (Darier sign). Connective tissue disease, pigmentary disorder, and vascular tumor do not account for mast cell hyperplasia.'),
('384fc96d-7b02-5350-956c-4270f8ef92f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of urticaria pigmentosa?', '["Dense perivascular and interstitial infiltrate of mast cells in the upper dermis; Giemsa or toluidine blue stains highlight metachromatic granules","Eosinophil infiltration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphocytic infiltration mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Neutrophilic infiltration without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plasma cell infiltration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dense perivascular and interstitial infiltrate of mast cells in the upper dermis; Giemsa or toluidine blue stains highlight metachromatic granules', 'Urticaria pigmentosa shows a dense perivascular and interstitial mast cell infiltrate in the upper dermis, and the metachromatic granules are highlighted with Giemsa or toluidine blue stains. Eosinophilic, lymphocytic, neutrophilic, or plasma cell infiltrates characterize other inflammatory conditions rather than mastocytosis.'),
('3e83f9ff-d4b1-51d6-b443-221de74c2dee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is necrobiosis lipoidica?', '["A granulomatous skin condition (usually on the shins) associated with diabetes","A bacterial infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","characterized by degenerated collagen and palisading granulomas mediated by specific receptor activation","A fungal infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lipid storage disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A granulomatous skin condition (usually on the shins) associated with diabetes', 'Necrobiosis lipoidica is a granulomatous skin disorder typically located on the shins of diabetic patients, and histology shows degenerated collagen with palisading granulomas. It is neither an infectious process nor a lipid storage disease, and the shin location with diabetes is the key clinical clue.'),
('a37ca0f9-3c20-5348-8385-b8096d5ef9dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is granuloma annulare?', '["A viral infection resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","self-limited granulomatous skin condition with necrobiotic collagen and palisading histiocytes; presents as annular papules on the extremities","A fungal infection mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A sarcoidosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'self-limited granulomatous skin condition with necrobiotic collagen and palisading histiocytes; presents as annular papules on the extremities', 'Granuloma annulare is a benign, self-limited granulomatous condition showing necrobiotic collagen with palisading histiocytes and annular papules on the extremities. Viral or fungal infection and sarcoidosis have different causative mechanisms and histologic patterns.'),
('b1597949-95f6-5ddb-8d0d-c36530d93b69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is vitiligo?', '["An autoimmune loss of melanocytes causing well-demarcated","A fungal infection (tinea versicolor)","A genetic hypopigmentation","depigmented white patches","A hypopigmentation due to decreased melanin"]'::jsonb, 'An autoimmune loss of melanocytes causing well-demarcated', 'Vitiligo is an autoimmune disorder in which melanocytes are destroyed, producing well-demarcated, depigmented white patches that lack melanocytes. Tinea versicolor is a fungal infection that does not destroy melanocytes, and genetic hypopigmentations reflect defective melanin synthesis rather than melanocyte loss.'),
('57d8b58f-0d8e-592e-b343-8c4784703e87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of vitiligo?', '["Decreased melanin but normal melanocyte number","Increased melanocytes","Inflammation at the dermoepidermal junction","with inflammation in established lesions","Absent melanocytes in the basal layer (confirmed by Melan-A or HMB45)"]'::jsonb, 'Absent melanocytes in the basal layer (confirmed by Melan-A or HMB45)', 'In vitiligo the basal layer of affected skin is devoid of melanocytes, as confirmed by Melan-A or HMB45 immunostains. Decreased melanin with a normal melanocyte count is the pattern of albinism, and established vitiligo lesions usually lack significant inflammation.'),
('ae5593f5-8540-535f-8e76-ed1950d8d1c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is albinism?', '["A post-inflammatory hypopigmentation resulting in decreased functional activity","A localized hypopigmentation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A loss of melanocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","A genetic disorder with defective melanin synthesis (tyrosinase deficiency or related) and normal melanocyte number","A pigmentation disorder acquired later in life leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A genetic disorder with defective melanin synthesis (tyrosinase deficiency or related) and normal melanocyte number', 'Albinism is a congenital genetic disorder of defective melanin synthesis, most often from tyrosinase deficiency, in which melanocytes are present in normal numbers but produce little pigment. Loss of melanocytes defines vitiligo, and the condition is congenital rather than acquired.'),
('374f6bed-4d8c-56eb-8114-bf6638e227a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is tinea versicolor (pityriasis versicolor)?', '["A viral infection resulting in decreased functional activity","A melanocytic disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An autoimmune disease mediated by specific receptor activation","A superficial fungal infection (Malassezia) causing hypo- or hyperpigmented scaly patches on the trunk","A bacterial infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A superficial fungal infection (Malassezia) causing hypo- or hyperpigmented scaly patches on the trunk', 'Tinea versicolor is a superficial fungal infection caused by Malassezia that produces hypo- or hyperpigmented scaly patches, typically on the trunk. It is not viral, bacterial, autoimmune, or a disorder of melanocytes.'),
('0c3387b7-41be-59ce-8b06-70549ef14429'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of tinea versicolor?', '["Short hyphae and spores in the stratum corneum; mild acanthosis and inflammation; the organism is visible on PAS or GMS stain","Hyperkeratosis only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphocytic infiltrate mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Acanthosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypopigmentation only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Short hyphae and spores in the stratum corneum; mild acanthosis and inflammation; the organism is visible on PAS or GMS stain', 'Tinea versicolor shows short hyphae and clusters of spores in the stratum corneum with mild acanthosis and inflammation, and the fungus is highlighted by PAS or GMS stains. Hyperkeratosis, acanthosis, or a lymphocytic infiltrate alone do not identify the organism.'),
('53678b39-d327-5ad5-b1bc-6213d35de3e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is pityriasis alba?', '["slightly scaly patches on the face of children; considered a mild form of eczema","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vitiligo mediated by specific receptor activation","A fungal infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypopigmented leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'slightly scaly patches on the face of children; considered a mild form of eczema', 'Pityriasis alba produces hypopigmented, slightly scaly patches on the faces of children and is regarded as a mild form of eczema. Vitiligo is autoimmune with complete loss of melanocytes, and fungal infection or post-inflammatory hypopigmentation has a different cause.'),
('d7f60387-427e-5766-bfeb-bd487a7f3207'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is idiopathic guttate hypomelanosis?', '["Vitiligo resulting in decreased functional activity","Tinea versicolor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","well-defined white macules on sun-exposed extensor forearms and shins of older individuals","Post-inflammatory hypopigmentation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'well-defined white macules on sun-exposed extensor forearms and shins of older individuals', 'Idiopathic guttate hypomelanosis produces small, well-defined white macules on the sun-exposed extensor forearms and shins of older individuals. Vitiligo and tinea versicolor differ in distribution and mechanism, and post-inflammatory hypopigmentation follows dermatitis.'),
('a3f48615-21b7-58f4-843c-4cd651b4aeff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is progressive macular hypomelanosis?', '["often in young adults resulting in decreased functional activity","Vitiligo accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fungal infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Pityriasis alba without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A poorly understood condition with nummular hypopigmented patches on the trunk"]'::jsonb, 'A poorly understood condition with nummular hypopigmented patches on the trunk', 'Progressive macular hypomelanosis is a poorly understood condition with nummular hypopigmented patches on the trunk, usually in young adults. Vitiligo, pityriasis alba, and fungal infection are separate disorders with different clinical features.'),
('842d4fed-909c-55b3-a491-b61c0369e6f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is dermatitis artefacta?', '["An allergic contact dermatitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Self-induced skin lesions (factitial) for psychological reasons; the patient denies causing the lesions","A neoplasm mediated by specific receptor activation","An infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An autoimmune disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Self-induced skin lesions (factitial) for psychological reasons; the patient denies causing the lesions', 'Dermatitis artefacta (factitial dermatitis) refers to self-induced skin lesions created for psychological reasons, with the patient typically denying causation. Allergic contact dermatitis, neoplasms, infection, and autoimmune disease are organic processes rather than self-inflicted injury.'),
('d81915e7-8064-5a6e-baee-00b6ea7ea6c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is neurotic excoriation?', '["An impulse control disorder with repetitive picking/scraping of the skin","ulcers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","causing erosions mediated by specific receptor activation","and scars without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An autoimmune blistering disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An impulse control disorder with repetitive picking/scraping of the skin', 'Neurotic excoriation is an impulse control disorder marked by repetitive picking or scraping of the skin, producing erosions, ulcers, and scars. It is a behavioral skin disorder rather than an autoimmune blistering disease.'),
('a87d3a7f-d68c-5186-af72-0ea22a1d406b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of allergic contact dermatitis?', '["Acantholysis","intraepidermal vesicles","Spongiotic dermatitis with lymphocytic exocytosis","Subepidermal blister","and a perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate"]'::jsonb, 'Spongiotic dermatitis with lymphocytic exocytosis', 'Allergic contact dermatitis is a spongiotic dermatitis with epidermal edema, lymphocytic exocytosis, intraepidermal vesicles, and a superficial perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate. Acantholysis and subepidermal blister formation characterize other dermatoses such as pemphigus and bullous pemphigoid.'),
('a1aa2482-5aec-5061-96e6-3a50d7ac37e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is patch testing used for?', '["To diagnose psoriasis resulting in decreased functional activity","To diagnose infections accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To evaluate pigmented lesions mediated by specific receptor activation","To assess skin thickness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To identify specific contact allergens causing allergic contact dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 'To identify specific contact allergens causing allergic contact dermatitis', 'Patch testing applies suspected allergens to intact skin under occlusion to identify the specific contact allergens responsible for allergic contact dermatitis. Psoriasis, infections, pigmented lesions, and skin thickness are evaluated by other diagnostic methods.'),
('db92370b-d5d3-5c68-983e-d7b74bf5611a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a phototoxic reaction?', '["An infection resulting in decreased functional activity","A photoallergic reaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","causing an exaggerated sunburn mediated by specific receptor activation","A non-immunologic skin reaction to a chemical (drug) exposed to UV light","A contact dermatitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A non-immunologic skin reaction to a chemical (drug) exposed to UV light', 'A phototoxic reaction is a non-immunologic skin response in which a chemical or drug plus UV exposure produces an exaggerated sunburn, and it can occur on first exposure. A photoallergic reaction requires prior sensitization and a type IV immune mechanism.'),
('2e1b28f1-d751-535d-b2b8-f369f57430d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a photoallergic reaction?', '["An immunologic (type IV) reaction requiring prior sensitization","An autoimmune reaction","A phototoxic reaction","where a chemical (drug) becomes antigenic after UV exposure","A contact dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 'An immunologic (type IV) reaction requiring prior sensitization', 'A photoallergic reaction is an immunologic (type IV) response that requires prior sensitization, in which UV light converts a chemical (drug) into an antigen. Phototoxic reactions are non-immunologic and occur on first exposure, and ordinary contact dermatitis does not require light.'),
('2f3c5e4b-35d5-5862-acc0-a9039aa7ae57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the most common occupational skin disease?', '["Contact dermatitis (irritant and allergic)","Burns accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Cutaneous infections without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radiation dermatitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contact dermatitis (irritant and allergic)', 'Contact dermatitis, combining irritant and allergic forms, is the most common occupational skin disease and accounts for the majority of work-related dermatologic cases. Burns, skin cancer, cutaneous infections, and radiation dermatitis are far less common occupational causes.'),
('025597ca-63ad-5f8f-8054-8ff6d0d65056'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is urticaria (hives)?', '["raised wheal-and-flare reaction due to mast cell degranulation in the dermis","A contact dermatitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A transient mediated by specific receptor activation","pruritic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fixed drug reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'raised wheal-and-flare reaction due to mast cell degranulation in the dermis', 'Urticaria (hives) is a transient, pruritic, raised wheal-and-flare reaction caused by mast cell degranulation in the dermis. Contact dermatitis and a fixed drug reaction have different timing and morphology, and the other options are isolated descriptors of the wheal rather than the reaction itself.'),
('4cf12052-0531-5bb6-b3a1-3e540faef2f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of urticaria?', '["Spongiosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Neutrophilic infiltration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dermal edema (widening of dermal papillae) with a sparse perivascular infiltrate of lymphocytes and eosinophils","Granulomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subepidermal blister leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dermal edema (widening of dermal papillae) with a sparse perivascular infiltrate of lymphocytes and eosinophils', 'Urticaria shows dermal edema that widens the dermal papillae together with a sparse perivascular infiltrate of lymphocytes and eosinophils. Spongiosis, neutrophilic infiltration, granulomas, and subepidermal blisters belong to different dermatoses.'),
('09061829-0d25-52c2-a23c-5fd52f1fefc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is angioedema?', '["Deep swelling of the dermis and subcutaneous tissue (or mucosa)","Urticaria of the deep skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","often involving the lips mediated by specific receptor activation","eyelids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep swelling of the dermis and subcutaneous tissue (or mucosa)', 'Angioedema is deep swelling of the dermis, subcutaneous tissue, and sometimes mucosa, commonly involving the lips, eyelids, and tongue. Unlike urticaria, which is a superficial wheal, angioedema affects deeper tissue.'),
('488143d0-d791-5ec9-b645-1174e1666996'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is vasculitis in the skin?', '["and livedo reticularis","Inflammation of blood vessel walls","ulcers","causing palpable purpura","A simple bruise"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of blood vessel walls', 'Cutaneous vasculitis is defined by inflammation of blood vessel walls, and it typically presents clinically as palpable purpura, with possible ulcers and livedo reticularis. A simple bruise results from vessel rupture without inflammation of the vessel wall.'),
('b87b9cf3-c308-57a1-99e9-170712023e8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of leukocytoclastic vasculitis?', '["and extravasated RBCs","Granulomatous inflammation","Lymphocytic infiltration","Fibrinoid necrosis of small vessel walls","neutrophilic infiltration with nuclear dust (leukocytoclasis)"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrinoid necrosis of small vessel walls', 'Leukocytoclastic vasculitis shows fibrinoid necrosis of small vessel walls with a neutrophilic infiltrate, nuclear dust (leukocytoclasis), and extravasated red cells. Granulomatous or purely lymphocytic infiltrates characterize other forms of vasculitis.'),
('1fe0b3e0-82e9-5cba-a887-acf4a01b6035'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is Henoch-Schönlein purpura (IgA vasculitis)?', '["A systemic vasculitis with IgA deposition in small vessels","abdominal pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and nephritis mediated by specific receptor activation","arthritis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","causing palpable purpura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A systemic vasculitis with IgA deposition in small vessels', 'Henoch-Schönlein purpura (IgA vasculitis) is a systemic small-vessel vasculitis with IgA deposition in the vessel walls, typically presenting as palpable purpura, arthritis, abdominal pain, and nephritis. The IgA-dominant immunopathology distinguishes it from other vasculitides.'),
('20c9dce9-5cfd-5571-a618-e8c41ceda322'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is petechiae?', '["Hemorrhagic vesicles resulting in decreased functional activity","Vascular tumors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small (1-2 mm) red/purple spots from extravasation of RBCs (not blanchable)","indicating thrombocytopenia or platelet dysfunction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Telangiectasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small (1-2 mm) red/purple spots from extravasation of RBCs (not blanchable)', 'Petechiae are small (1-2 mm) red-to-purple spots caused by extravasation of red cells that do not blanch, and they usually indicate thrombocytopenia or platelet dysfunction. Hemorrhagic vesicles, vascular tumors, and telangiectasia are structurally different lesions.'),
('b04a7597-ea33-5981-8e89-08cc453c111c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is telangiectasia?', '["small superficial blood vessels (capillaries/venules) that blanch; seen in hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia","and chronic sun damage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Purpura mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","rosacea without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dilated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'small superficial blood vessels (capillaries/venules) that blanch; seen in hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia', 'Telangiectasias are dilated, small superficial capillaries and venules that blanch on pressure and occur in hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia, rosacea, and chronic sun damage. Purpura is extravasated blood rather than vascular dilation, and the other options are isolated attributes.'),
('07b4ec4b-bed9-5d7c-b91f-6b9f000ff237'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the most common type of cutaneous drug reaction?', '["Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Fixed drug eruption","DRESS syndrome","Morbilliform (exanthematous) eruption","Urticaria"]'::jsonb, 'Morbilliform (exanthematous) eruption', 'A morbilliform (exanthematous) eruption is the most common type of cutaneous drug reaction, presenting as widespread erythematous macules and papules. Stevens-Johnson syndrome, DRESS, fixed drug eruption, and urticaria are less common drug-induced patterns.'),
('37e94559-18b5-5994-be16-83d91f66fa98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a fixed drug eruption?', '["often with post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation resulting in decreased functional activity","A morbilliform eruption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","well-circumscribed erythematous patch(s) at the same site(s) each time a drug is taken","A recurrent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urticaria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'well-circumscribed erythematous patch(s) at the same site(s) each time a drug is taken', 'A fixed drug eruption is a recurrent, well-circumscribed erythematous patch that recurs at the same site or sites each time the offending drug is taken, often with post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation. Morbilliform eruptions are generalized and do not recur at fixed sites.'),
('791c886a-16c4-5e04-831b-ba46cc46c8c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN)?', '["A mild morbilliform eruption resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fixed drug reaction mediated by specific receptor activation","life-threatening mucocutaneous reactions with widespread epidermal necrosis and detachment; mostly drug-induced","DRESS syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'life-threatening mucocutaneous reactions with widespread epidermal necrosis and detachment; mostly drug-induced', 'Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis are severe, life-threatening mucocutaneous reactions with widespread epidermal necrosis and detachment that are mostly drug-induced. Mild morbilliform eruptions, fixed drug reactions, and DRESS differ in severity, cause, and clinical picture.'),
('13780ad2-f151-5c20-9681-fc683b16b807'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of SJS/TEN?', '["subepidermal split","Full-thickness epidermal necrosis","Spongiosis","and sparse dermal inflammation (interface dermatitis)","Acantholysis"]'::jsonb, 'Full-thickness epidermal necrosis', 'SJS/TEN shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis with a subepidermal split and sparse dermal inflammation (interface dermatitis). Spongiosis indicates eczema or contact dermatitis, while acantholysis is the hallmark of pemphigus.'),
('87032c1c-47eb-5b54-9a28-228a4077c01e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP)?', '["DRESS resulting in decreased functional activity","and neutrophilia; rapid onset and resolution accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SJS/TEN mediated by specific receptor activation","A severe pustular drug reaction with widespread sterile pustules on erythematous skin","fever leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A severe pustular drug reaction with widespread sterile pustules on erythematous skin', 'Acute generalized exanthematous pustulosis is a severe pustular drug reaction with widespread sterile pustules on erythematous skin, fever, and neutrophilia, and it has a rapid onset and rapid resolution after drug withdrawal. SJS/TEN, DRESS, and isolated fever do not present with this pustular eruption.'),
('884b6735-9115-58a9-8bc6-20217dae4373'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the treatment for SJS/TEN?', '["and wound care","supportive care (burns unit)","Immediate withdrawal of the culprit drug","Systemic steroids (high dose)","IVIG or cyclosporine"]'::jsonb, 'Immediate withdrawal of the culprit drug', 'The cornerstone of SJS/TEN management is immediate withdrawal of the culprit drug, followed by supportive care, ideally in a burns unit, with wound management. Adjunctive corticosteroids, IVIG, or cyclosporine remain controversial and secondary to drug withdrawal.'),
('47528422-a081-528f-bc0d-6781115e8ffe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the SCORTEN score?', '["glucose resulting in decreased functional activity","serum bicarbonate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A severity-of-illness score predicting mortality in SJS/TEN based on age","associated malignancy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A severity-of-illness score predicting mortality in SJS/TEN based on age', 'SCORTEN is a severity-of-illness scoring system that predicts mortality in SJS/TEN, incorporating variables such as age, associated malignancy, heart rate, glucose, serum bicarbonate, and extent of skin detachment. It is a prognostic tool rather than a treatment; the listed items are individual variables within it.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('6aa77970-ee19-5959-ab22-34915eee290f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is lupus erythematosus (LE)?', '["with photosensitivity and various autoantibodies resulting in decreased functional activity","A chronic autoimmune disease with a spectrum from cutaneous (discoid) to systemic","An infectious disease mediated by specific receptor activation","A localized skin disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A drug reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic autoimmune disease with a spectrum from cutaneous (discoid) to systemic', 'LE is a chronic autoimmune disease with a clinical spectrum from cutaneous (discoid) lesions to systemic involvement, often associated with photosensitivity and a range of autoantibodies. It is neither infectious nor purely localized, and although drugs can induce a lupus-like syndrome, they are not the cause of LE itself.'),
('603d0089-d441-5f97-8643-b3a7815af186'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of cutaneous lupus erythematosus?', '["Acantholysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Spongiosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","periappendageal and perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate mediated by specific receptor activation","and basement membrane thickening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interface dermatitis with vacuolar degeneration of the basal layer"]'::jsonb, 'Interface dermatitis with vacuolar degeneration of the basal layer', 'Cutaneous lupus shows interface dermatitis with vacuolar (hydropic) degeneration of the basal keratinocyte layer, usually accompanied by periappendageal and perivascular lymphocytic infiltrates and basement membrane thickening. Acantholysis is the feature of pemphigus and spongiosis of spongiotic dermatitis, not of lupus.'),
('7681030b-e98e-58be-a64f-a007b9126f14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the immunofluorescence pattern in lupus erythematosus?', '["IgM","Granular deposition of IgG","C3 at the basement membrane zone (positive lupus band test)","Linear IgG at BMZ","Intercellular IgG"]'::jsonb, 'Granular deposition of IgG', 'In LE, direct immunofluorescence shows granular deposition of IgG (usually with C3) at the dermo-epidermal junction, producing a positive lupus band test. Linear IgG at the basement membrane characterizes bullous pemphigoid and intercellular IgG is seen in pemphigus.'),
('63c610cb-3f8c-5186-88a6-6db54e8415ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is dermatomyositis?', '["heliotrope rash resulting in decreased functional activity","A variant of lupus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A localized skin disease mediated by specific receptor activation","An idiopathic inflammatory myopathy with characteristic cutaneous findings: Gottron papules","and periorbital edema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An idiopathic inflammatory myopathy with characteristic cutaneous findings: Gottron papules', 'Dermatomyositis is an idiopathic inflammatory myopathy of proximal muscles accompanied by characteristic skin changes, including Gottron papules over the knuckles, a heliotrope rash, and periorbital edema. It is not a variant of lupus or merely a localized skin disease.'),
('3337e5b8-65ad-5931-8e1f-7a0ce634491d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of dermatomyositis?', '["Spongiosis resulting in decreased functional activity","and perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Interface dermatitis with vacuolar degeneration of basal keratinocytes","Acantholysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","dermal mucin deposition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interface dermatitis with vacuolar degeneration of basal keratinocytes', 'The cutaneous histology of dermatomyositis is an interface dermatitis with vacuolar degeneration of basal keratinocytes, often accompanied by a dermal perivascular lymphocytic infiltrate and dermal mucin deposition. Acantholysis indicates pemphigus and spongiosis indicates spongiotic dermatitis instead.'),
('ff72790f-859a-5515-8def-1f5bcd23f378'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is systemic sclerosis (scleroderma)?', '["A localized fibrosing condition resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A drug reaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A chronic connective tissue disease characterized by fibrosis of the skin and internal organs and vascular dysfunction (Raynaud phenomenon)","A variant of lupus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An infectious disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic connective tissue disease characterized by fibrosis of the skin and internal organs and vascular dysfunction (Raynaud phenomenon)', 'Systemic sclerosis is a chronic connective tissue disease defined by fibrosis of the skin and internal organs together with vascular dysfunction, classically presenting as Raynaud phenomenon. It is not a variant of lupus, an infection, or a drug reaction, and its fibrosis is not localized.'),
('110c7e13-bd17-5262-a836-99571d7300c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is CREST syndrome?', '["Calcinosis","Esophageal dysmotility","Raynaud phenomenon","and Telangiectasia; the limited form of systemic sclerosis","Sclerodactyly"]'::jsonb, 'Calcinosis', 'CREST syndrome is the limited form of systemic sclerosis whose features are calcinosis, Raynaud phenomenon, esophageal dysmotility, sclerodactyly, and telangiectasia, with calcinosis appearing first in the acronym. The remaining options list individual components of the syndrome rather than the broader definition.'),
('4d59f9dc-fd9e-5bab-b3f9-0b197bdee615'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of scleroderma?', '["Thick","hyalinized dermal collagen bundles with loss of perieccrine fat; atrophy of the epidermis and adnexa; intimal hyperplasia of blood vessels","Acanthosis","Granulomas","Interface dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 'Thick', 'Scleroderma is characterized by thick, hyalinized dermal collagen bundles with loss of perieccrine fat, atrophy of the epidermis and adnexa, and intimal hyperplasia of small blood vessels. Acanthosis, granulomas, and interface dermatitis are not part of the scleroderma histology.'),
('17976b0f-c5fc-590d-88ca-ed04aa681c64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is morphea (localized scleroderma)?', '["with circumscribed sclerotic plaques; generally not associated with systemic disease","A localized form of scleroderma limited to the skin","Lichen sclerosus","A variant of lupus","Systemic sclerosis"]'::jsonb, 'A localized form of scleroderma limited to the skin', 'Morphea is a localized form of scleroderma confined to the skin, giving circumscribed sclerotic plaques and generally lacking systemic involvement. It is distinct from systemic sclerosis, lichen sclerosus, and lupus.'),
('4c4df2e3-e6f8-5c8c-8f09-5b7641dd5bf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the structure of the hypodermis?', '["Smooth muscle resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastic fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adipose tissue without septa mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Loose connective tissue with large lobules of adipocytes separated by fibrous septa containing blood vessels and nerves","Dense collagen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue with large lobules of adipocytes separated by fibrous septa containing blood vessels and nerves', 'The hypodermis is loose connective tissue arranged as fibrous septa that subdivide large lobules of adipocytes and carry the vessels and nerves. It is not uniform adipose tissue without septa, smooth muscle, or dense collagen.'),
('23879a7e-cd79-553b-b982-325992463a72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are adipocytes in the hypodermis?', '["large lipid droplet (unilocular) that displaces the nucleus to the periphery","Cells with multiple lipid droplets (multilocular)","Mast cells","Fibroblasts","Large cells filled with a single"]'::jsonb, 'Large cells filled with a single', 'Adipocytes of the hypodermis are large cells filled with a single (unilocular) lipid droplet that displaces the nucleus to the periphery. Multilocular lipid droplets characterize brown adipose tissue, while mast cells and fibroblasts are separate cell types.'),
('8c5ffcf1-20b6-510f-8ca8-a444cc06806f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is panniculitis?', '["often presenting as tender subcutaneous nodules resulting in decreased functional activity","A skin infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A dermatitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Inflammation of the subcutaneous fat (panniculus adiposus)","A vascular occlusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the subcutaneous fat (panniculus adiposus)', 'Panniculitis is inflammation of the subcutaneous fat (panniculus adiposus), frequently presenting as tender subcutaneous nodules. It is not primarily an infection, a dermatitis, or a vascular occlusion.'),
('4932a272-9abb-5d04-be38-8f09b215072c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is erythema nodosum?', '["presenting as tender","A cellulitis","The most common form of panniculitis","red nodules on the shins; it is a septal panniculitis","A lobular panniculitis"]'::jsonb, 'The most common form of panniculitis', 'Erythema nodosum is the most common form of panniculitis, presenting as tender red nodules on the shins and classified as a septal panniculitis. Lobular panniculitis with vasculitis points instead to erythema induratum, and cellulitis is an infection rather than fat inflammation.'),
('313276b4-dbdd-5806-ac08-962a581cc403'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is erythema induratum (nodular vasculitis)?', '["A lobular panniculitis with vasculitis","often on the posterior calves; associated with tuberculosis (tuberculid)","A septal panniculitis","A hematoma","A cellulitis"]'::jsonb, 'A lobular panniculitis with vasculitis', 'Erythema induratum (nodular vasculitis) is a lobular panniculitis with vasculitis, characteristically on the posterior calves and sometimes associated with tuberculosis as a tuberculid. Septal panniculitis, hematoma, and cellulitis are different processes.'),
('bbf6562a-e44f-5282-bd8a-a48ecd73b524'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is lipoatrophy (lipodystrophy)?', '["Panniculitis","An infection","Loss of subcutaneous fat","which can be localized (injection sites) or generalized (genetic or HIV-associated)","A tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of subcutaneous fat', 'Lipoatrophy (lipodystrophy) is loss of subcutaneous fat, which may be localized (as at injection sites) or generalized (genetic or HIV-associated). Panniculitis is inflammation of fat rather than its loss, and lipoatrophy is neither an infection nor a tumor.'),
('5dba1dd6-0044-5ca0-9240-c2d9b30db2d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a lipoma?', '["A cyst","A benign tumor of mature adipocytes","A malignant liposarcoma","in the subcutaneous tissue; it is encapsulated and composed of uniform fat cells","A hematoma"]'::jsonb, 'A benign tumor of mature adipocytes', 'A lipoma is a benign tumor of mature adipocytes that sits in the subcutaneous tissue, is encapsulated, and is composed of uniform fat cells. It is distinguished from liposarcoma by its bland, uniform cells and benign behavior.'),
('cdd4381f-8d94-5b18-b077-7a36c5f0cfbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is liposarcoma?', '["A fibrosarcoma resulting in decreased functional activity","A pleomorphic sarcoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","it has atypical lipoblasts and cellular pleomorphism mediated by specific receptor activation","A malignant tumor of adipocyte differentiation; unlike lipoma","A benign lipoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A malignant tumor of adipocyte differentiation; unlike lipoma', 'Liposarcoma is a malignant tumor of adipocyte differentiation that, unlike lipoma, contains atypical lipoblasts and shows cellular pleomorphism. It is not a benign lipoma or simply a fibrosarcoma.'),
('486cc260-b4b1-50b8-8ada-b9ca1a3ece5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of liposarcoma?', '["Atypical lipoblasts with cytoplasmic vacuoles (scalloped nuclei)","Uniform mature adipocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and myxoid or pleomorphic stroma mediated by specific receptor activation","variable cellularity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spindle cells only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atypical lipoblasts with cytoplasmic vacuoles (scalloped nuclei)', 'The histologic hallmark of liposarcoma is atypical lipoblasts with cytoplasmic vacuoles giving scalloped nuclei, set in a myxoid or pleomorphic stroma of variable cellularity. Uniform mature adipocytes describe a benign lipoma, and a pure spindle-cell pattern is not typical.'),
('0e22cee2-441f-5bb7-a65c-999648178c40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the embryological origin of the epidermis?', '["Surface ectoderm","Endoderm","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Splanchnic mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Surface ectoderm', 'The epidermis is derived from surface ectoderm. Mesoderm gives rise to the dermis, neural crest to melanocytes, and endoderm to internal tube linings, so they are not the source of the epidermis.'),
('d951167e-77da-5ccd-99d7-bc9c82866047'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the embryological origin of the dermis?', '["Surface ectoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Endoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesoderm (lateral plate and somitic mesoderm)","Neural tube without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoderm (lateral plate and somitic mesoderm)', 'The dermis is of mesodermal origin, arising from lateral plate and somitic (dermatomal) mesoderm. Surface ectoderm forms the epidermis and neural crest gives rise to melanocytes, not the dermis.'),
('438a8de4-652c-5312-b7b2-6d40df39c4ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the embryological origin of melanocytes?', '["Surface ectoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural crest cells that migrate to the epidermis during development","Endoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neural crest cells that migrate to the epidermis during development', 'Melanocytes originate from neural crest cells that migrate into the epidermis during development. They do not arise from surface ectoderm, neural tube, endoderm, or mesoderm.'),
('a72ef0a6-3827-5014-b47a-95b00beceb9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the embryological origin of Merkel cells?', '["Epidermal keratinocytes (local origin) or neural crest (debated); they appear in the stratum basale during development","Neural tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural crest mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Endoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermal keratinocytes (local origin) or neural crest (debated); they appear in the stratum basale during development', 'Merkel cells appear in the stratum basale during development and are thought either to derive locally from epidermal keratinocytes or from neural crest, the origin remaining debated. They do not come from the neural tube, endoderm, or mesoderm.'),
('770b85d9-8ddb-5890-84ba-4b4881362f34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the embryological origin of Langerhans cells?', '["Neural crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone marrow (monocyte) precursors that migrate into the epidermis","Surface ectoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Endoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow (monocyte) precursors that migrate into the epidermis', 'Langerhans cells are bone marrow-derived monocyte precursors that migrate into the epidermis and act as antigen-presenting cells. They are not derived from neural crest, surface ectoderm, endoderm, or mesoderm of the developing skin.'),
('7044c79e-6628-5c6d-9739-4fc1760b53f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the development of hair follicles?', '["Epidermal placodes (thickenings) induce dermal condensation","and mature follicle; starts at 9-12 weeks accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","forming the hair peg mediated by specific receptor activation","bulbous peg without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From the dermis only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermal placodes (thickenings) induce dermal condensation', 'Hair follicles begin when epidermal placodes (thickenings) induce condensation of the underlying dermis, then progress through hair peg and bulbous peg stages to form the mature follicle, starting at about 9-12 weeks of gestation. They do not develop from the dermis alone.'),
('caf3ff83-a402-5e81-9253-fbf6bc9d4389'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the development of sweat glands?', '["From the dermis","forming the secretory coil and duct; eccrine glands develop at 12-16 weeks","From the hypodermis","From hair follicles","Ectodermal buds grow downward into the dermis"]'::jsonb, 'Ectodermal buds grow downward into the dermis', 'Sweat glands develop as ectodermal buds that grow downward into the dermis, then form the secretory coil and duct; eccrine glands develop at 12-16 weeks. They do not arise from the dermis, hypodermis, or hair follicles.'),
('76689178-1863-5f5f-9a60-7840093b904c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the development of sebaceous glands?', '["From sweat gland ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","They develop as lateral buds from the outer root sheath of hair follicles (as early as 13-16 weeks)","From the dermis mediated by specific receptor activation","From the hypodermis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From the epidermis directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They develop as lateral buds from the outer root sheath of hair follicles (as early as 13-16 weeks)', 'Sebaceous glands develop as lateral buds from the outer root sheath of hair follicles, beginning as early as 13-16 weeks of gestation. They do not form from sweat gland ducts, the dermis, or the hypodermis.'),
('8681379f-9737-5bc7-b3a7-e22d6ac9910a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the vernix caseosa?', '["shed keratinocytes","white protective coating on the fetal skin","A waxy","composed of sebaceous gland secretions","and lanugo hair"]'::jsonb, 'A waxy', 'Vernix caseosa is the waxy, white protective coating on fetal skin composed of sebaceous gland secretions, shed keratinocytes, and lanugo hair. It is the combination of these components rather than any single element alone.'),
('99b029de-d507-5e60-ae30-bec8291606f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is lanugo?', '["soft","typically shed before birth","unpigmented hair covering the fetus","Fine","Vellus hair"]'::jsonb, 'Fine', 'Lanugo is the fine, soft, unpigmented hair covering the fetus, typically shed before birth. It differs from vellus hair, which is the fine hair persisting after birth.'),
('c4656902-dc64-57eb-a9c7-e10edceeca10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the two types of skin aging?', '["Only intrinsic resulting in decreased functional activity","Cellular and molecular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only extrinsic mediated by specific receptor activation","Intrinsic and hormonal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intrinsic (chronologic) aging and extrinsic (photoaging)"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic (chronologic) aging and extrinsic (photoaging)', 'The two recognized types of skin aging are intrinsic (chronologic) aging and extrinsic aging, chiefly photoaging from ultraviolet exposure. Hormonal effects influence aging but do not define the two categories.'),
('f0df9b1b-e48d-5cb7-95b2-4170f3e9b752'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the histology of photoaged skin?', '["Normal skin resulting in decreased functional activity","Solar elastosis (basophilic degeneration of elastic fibers)","and telangiectasia mediated by specific receptor activation","dermal atrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","irregular melanin distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Solar elastosis (basophilic degeneration of elastic fibers)', 'Photoaged skin shows solar elastosis, a basophilic degeneration of dermal elastic fibers, accompanied by dermal atrophy, telangiectasia, and irregular melanin distribution. These changes are not seen in normal skin.'),
('71417088-81a8-5dcf-a72f-1253e42fe868'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of the basal layer (stratum basale) in skin renewal?', '["Proliferating stem cells that generate new keratinocytes to replace cells shed from the surface","Protection from UV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensation mediated by specific receptor activation","Production of pigment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Immunologic surveillance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proliferating stem cells that generate new keratinocytes to replace cells shed from the surface', 'The stratum basale contains proliferating stem cells whose divisions generate new keratinocytes that replenish cells shed from the surface. The basal layer is not primarily the site of UV protection, sensation, or immunologic surveillance.'),
('011fba65-d20c-5428-9a0b-13a1fed82820'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of the cornified envelope?', '["providing a protective barrier","Cell-to-cell adhesion","A tough","Anchoring the cell to the basement membrane","insoluble protein-lipid layer beneath the plasma membrane of corneocytes"]'::jsonb, 'A tough', 'The cornified envelope is a tough, insoluble protein-lipid layer beneath the plasma membrane of corneocytes that provides a protective permeability barrier. It does not mediate cell-to-cell adhesion or anchor the cell to the basement membrane.'),
('3bbf1fac-783e-50b0-b310-4470fb1944a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the natural moisturizing factor (NMF)?', '["and salts in corneocytes that retain water and maintain hydration","A mixture of amino acids","Sebaceous gland secretion","lactic acid","urea"]'::jsonb, 'A mixture of amino acids', 'NMF is a mixture of amino acids, lactic acid, urea, and salts within corneocytes that retains water and maintains hydration of the stratum corneum. It is not simply a sebaceous gland secretion.'),
('7cc1a2f8-7bcb-5ca3-bdde-5014e1e3ccb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the role of filaggrin in the skin?', '["A lipid transporter resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A cell adhesion molecule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A structural keratin mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A melanosome protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A protein that aggregates keratin filaments in the stratum granulosum and is degraded into NMF components in the stratum corneum"]'::jsonb, 'A protein that aggregates keratin filaments in the stratum granulosum and is degraded into NMF components in the stratum corneum', 'Filaggrin aggregates keratin filaments in the stratum granulosum and is subsequently degraded into NMF components (free amino acids) in the stratum corneum. It is not a lipid transporter, adhesion molecule, structural keratin, or melanosome protein.'),
('30c467e5-f3f3-5104-8b57-f8bc8d04df24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the role of antimicrobial peptides in the skin?', '["Only hydrating the skin resulting in decreased functional activity","First-line defense against microbes: cathelicidins (LL-37)","beta-defensins mediated by specific receptor activation","and dermcidin produced by keratinocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","role leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First-line defense against microbes: cathelicidins (LL-37)', 'Antimicrobial peptides provide first-line defense against microbes, with keratinocytes producing cathelicidins such as LL-37, beta-defensins, and dermcidin. Their function is antimicrobial rather than merely hydrating the skin.'),
('76376f97-b619-5171-9124-4c43832d453d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the nail?', '["bed resulting in decreased functional activity","composed of the nail plate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and hyponychium mediated by specific receptor activation","A specialized keratinized structure on the dorsal tips of fingers and toes","matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A specialized keratinized structure on the dorsal tips of fingers and toes', 'The nail is a specialized keratinized structure on the dorsal tips of the fingers and toes, made up of the nail plate overlying a nail bed, with a proximal matrix and distal hyponychium. It is the whole structure, not just the matrix or bed.'),
('78ee8fa6-b1fe-58f5-b96e-813c2e170ec8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the nail matrix?', '["The nail plate resulting in decreased functional activity","The nail bed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The hyponychium mediated by specific receptor activation","The proximal part of the nail where proliferating keratinocytes produce the nail plate (lunula is the visible part)","The cuticle (eponychium) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The proximal part of the nail where proliferating keratinocytes produce the nail plate (lunula is the visible part)', 'The nail matrix is the proximal part of the nail where proliferating keratinocytes produce the nail plate, its visible portion being the lunula. The nail bed supports the plate, and the hyponychium lies beneath the free edge.'),
('cd2356c8-80e9-5058-a70d-6526561418da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the function of the nail bed?', '["Seals the nail groove resulting in decreased functional activity","Protects the nail matrix accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produces the nail mediated by specific receptor activation","Supports the nail plate and provides adhesion; contributes to the ventral nail plate","Secretes cuticle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supports the nail plate and provides adhesion; contributes to the ventral nail plate', 'The nail bed supports the nail plate, provides adhesion, and contributes to the ventral (underside) portion of the plate. It does not seal the nail groove or produce the nail; those roles belong to the eponychium and the matrix.'),
('2a8edabe-6ce3-5174-b9e2-dbdbc694f683'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the cuticle (eponychium)?', '["The nail matrix resulting in decreased functional activity","The hyponychium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The nail bed mediated by specific receptor activation","sealing the nail groove without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The thin layer of stratum corneum that overlies the proximal nail plate"]'::jsonb, 'The thin layer of stratum corneum that overlies the proximal nail plate', 'The cuticle (eponychium) is the thin layer of stratum corneum that overlies the proximal nail plate, sealing the nail groove. It is distinct from the nail matrix, nail bed, and hyponychium.'),
('e4954b08-31e8-5c5b-9345-b1356c078785'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the hyponychium?', '["The nail plate resulting in decreased functional activity","The skin beneath the free edge of the nail plate at the fingertip","The nail matrix mediated by specific receptor activation","The eponychium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The nail bed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The skin beneath the free edge of the nail plate at the fingertip', 'The hyponychium is the skin beneath the free edge of the nail plate at the fingertip. It should not be confused with the eponychium (cuticle), the matrix, or the nail bed.'),
('b9b74584-2293-5dd9-8db6-af5c7a702373'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the common disorders of the nails?', '["Onychomycosis (fungal infection)","pitting (psoriasis)","koilonychia (iron deficiency)","splinter hemorrhages (endocarditis)","clubbing (lung disease)"]'::jsonb, 'Onychomycosis (fungal infection)', 'Common nail disorders include onychomycosis (fungal infection), pitting in psoriasis, koilonychia in iron deficiency, splinter hemorrhages in endocarditis, and clubbing in lung disease. Onychomycosis is the representative example among the listed options.'),
('24ae7f88-3695-5c56-bd1d-e4b225d11ca3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the lip (vermillion zone)?', '["The skin of the face resulting in decreased functional activity","and hair follicles or sweat glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The red part of the lips with thin keratinized epithelium","abundant capillaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A modified mucosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The red part of the lips with thin keratinized epithelium', 'The vermillion zone is the red part of the lips, covered by a thin, lightly keratinized epithelium over abundant capillaries and lacking hair follicles and sweat glands. It is a modified mucosa rather than ordinary facial skin.'),
('72b89119-d364-5a09-bf39-e89878a928f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the eyelid?', '["A modified mucosal surface","A sensory organ","A thin fold of skin with the eyelashes","and glands of Zeis and Moll","meibomian glands (sebaceous)"]'::jsonb, 'A thin fold of skin with the eyelashes', 'The eyelid is a thin fold of skin bearing the eyelashes and specialized glands: meibomian (sebaceous) glands and the glands of Zeis and Moll. It is not simply a sensory organ or a mucosal surface.'),
('55defe1f-d765-5bdd-a5b2-2f4513d87445'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the areola?', '["A pigmented region of skin around the nipple with Montgomery tubercles (areolar glands: sebaceous/sweat gland transition)","A gland accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Modified skin with hair mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A type of scar without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A sensory organ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A pigmented region of skin around the nipple with Montgomery tubercles (areolar glands: sebaceous/sweat gland transition)', 'The areola is the pigmented region of skin around the nipple that contains Montgomery tubercles, glands transitional between sebaceous and sweat glands. It is not itself a gland, a scar, or a sensory organ.'),
('8eaef7a2-9d76-52bc-9dfc-2901ef7df489'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the external auditory canal lined by?', '["Stratified columnar epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Respiratory epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple columnar epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified squamous epithelium with ceruminous glands (modified apocrine) and sebaceous glands; has hairs (vellus)","Transitional epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous epithelium with ceruminous glands (modified apocrine) and sebaceous glands; has hairs (vellus)', 'The external auditory canal is lined by stratified squamous epithelium with ceruminous glands (modified apocrine), sebaceous glands, and vellus hairs. Respiratory, columnar, and transitional epithelia line other regions, not the canal skin.'),
('c1efbb9a-4f24-503f-8324-b41914846e2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a split-thickness skin graft (STSG)?', '["A graft consisting of the epidermis and a partial thickness of the dermis (including papillary dermis)","Full-thickness skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A composite graft mediated by specific receptor activation","A local flap without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A synthetic skin substitute leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A graft consisting of the epidermis and a partial thickness of the dermis (including papillary dermis)', 'A split-thickness skin graft contains the epidermis plus a partial thickness of the dermis, including the papillary dermis. Full-thickness grafts, composite grafts, local flaps, and synthetic substitutes are different reconstructive options.'),
('51cccacb-c81c-5e3f-b0f9-e1f2779a5e01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a full-thickness skin graft (FTSG)?', '["A local flap resulting in decreased functional activity","A graft consisting of the entire epidermis and dermis; the donor site must be closed primarily","A split-thickness graft mediated by specific receptor activation","A composite graft without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An allograft leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A graft consisting of the entire epidermis and dermis; the donor site must be closed primarily', 'A full-thickness skin graft includes the entire epidermis and dermis, so the donor site must be closed primarily by suturing. It differs from split-thickness and composite grafts, and a flap retains its own blood supply.'),
('25c5f70f-56d4-59ed-92ab-05c93261c060'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the phases of skin graft healing (take)?', '["Plasmatic imbibition (first 24-48h)","Immediate attachment only","and revascularization (day 5-7)","inosculation (day 3-5)","Only revascularization"]'::jsonb, 'Plasmatic imbibition (first 24-48h)', 'Skin graft take proceeds through plasmatic imbibition (first 24-48 h), followed by inosculation (day 3-5) and revascularization (day 5-7). It is a sequential process, not immediate attachment or revascularization alone.'),
('49fb4b9c-2ce1-54b8-becf-37eabc9cabce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a tissue expander?', '["A balloon-like device placed under the skin and gradually inflated to stretch the skin","A skin graft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A negative pressure device mediated by specific receptor activation","generating extra tissue for reconstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A flap leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A balloon-like device placed under the skin and gradually inflated to stretch the skin', 'A tissue expander is a balloon-like device placed under the skin and gradually inflated to stretch the skin, generating extra tissue for reconstruction. It is not a graft, a flap, or a negative-pressure device.'),
('b2cc984d-4d76-5f06-9485-d414a6ad83f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the role of vitamin C in wound healing?', '["Required for collagen synthesis by serving as a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases","Stimulates angiogenesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Provides energy mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibacterial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevents infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Required for collagen synthesis by serving as a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases', 'Vitamin C is required for collagen synthesis as a cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases that hydroxylate collagen chains. It does not primarily stimulate angiogenesis, provide energy, or act as an antibacterial agent.'),
('fcc37cc5-12ad-5e43-af7e-b0fe1b1faea1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the role of zinc in wound healing?', '["Cell membrane synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Energy production accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collagen cross-linking mediated by specific receptor activation","A cofactor for many enzymes involved in cell proliferation and protein synthesis","Antioxidant defense leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cofactor for many enzymes involved in cell proliferation and protein synthesis', 'Zinc is a cofactor for many enzymes involved in cell proliferation and protein synthesis, both of which are required for effective wound healing. Collagen cross-linking depends mainly on vitamin C and copper, while membrane synthesis, energy production, and antioxidant defense are not the primary roles of zinc in this setting.'),
('3fc1332d-dae4-5d37-97e2-6128f8bff26a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a chronic wound?', '["A wound that fails to proceed through the normal healing stages in a timely manner (usually >4 weeks)","An infected wound accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A closed wound mediated by specific receptor activation","A surgical wound without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A normally healing wound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A wound that fails to proceed through the normal healing stages in a timely manner (usually >4 weeks)', 'A chronic wound fails to progress through the normal healing stages in a timely manner, usually beyond 4 weeks. Infected, closed, surgical, and normally healing wounds are separate categories that do not by themselves indicate chronicity.'),
('64a542fd-2ef8-5d3b-878d-21f334bb1960'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the treatment algorithm for chronic wounds?', '["and addressing underlying causes","moist wound healing","offloading pressure","infection control","Debridement of necrotic tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Debridement of necrotic tissue', 'Debridement of necrotic tissue is the first step in managing a chronic wound, since nonviable tissue prevents granulation and re-epithelialization. Addressing underlying causes, moist wound healing, offloading pressure, and infection control are important adjuncts that follow debridement.'),
('b1692bba-6a2d-5e19-b505-fe3352700c67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a punch biopsy?', '["and superficial hypodermis for histologic evaluation resulting in decreased functional activity","A cylindrical skin biopsy (typically 3-6 mm) that includes the epidermis","dermis mediated by specific receptor activation","A shave biopsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An excisional biopsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cylindrical skin biopsy (typically 3-6 mm) that includes the epidermis', 'A punch biopsy removes a cylindrical core, typically 3-6 mm, that includes the epidermis, dermis, and superficial hypodermis for histologic evaluation. Shave and excisional biopsies sample different tissue depths and extents.'),
('bd6cbce3-34c5-5069-84c3-b499aa867c66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a shave biopsy?', '["A biopsy that removes a superficial specimen (epidermis and upper dermis) using a scalpel or razor blade","A curettage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A snip biopsy mediated by specific receptor activation","An excisional biopsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A deep punch biopsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A biopsy that removes a superficial specimen (epidermis and upper dermis) using a scalpel or razor blade', 'A shave biopsy uses a scalpel or razor blade to remove a superficial disc of epidermis and upper dermis. Because it is shallow, it does not sample the deep dermis or subcutaneous tissue that punch and excisional biopsies can reach.'),
('3efddb68-0544-5c7b-ab76-624f859f3de3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is an excisional biopsy?', '["A partial biopsy resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A shave biopsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A punch biopsy mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","An incisional biopsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete surgical removal of a lesion with a margin of normal skin; used for melanoma and other tumors requiring full staging"]'::jsonb, 'Complete surgical removal of a lesion with a margin of normal skin; used for melanoma and other tumors requiring full staging', 'Excisional biopsy completely removes the lesion along with a margin of normal skin, which permits full histologic staging for melanoma and other tumors. Partial, shave, punch, and incisional techniques remove only a portion of the lesion.'),
('eae273db-1751-5a70-9bcd-2c748c679c98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is a frozen section in dermatology?', '["A permanent section resulting in decreased functional activity","An immunohistochemical stain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rapid intraoperative assessment of margins (Mohs surgery) or suspected tumor for immediate diagnosis","A special stain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A routine H&E leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid intraoperative assessment of margins (Mohs surgery) or suspected tumor for immediate diagnosis', 'Frozen sections provide rapid intraoperative assessment of margins, as used in Mohs surgery, giving an immediate diagnosis while surgery is ongoing. Permanent sections, H&E, and special or immunohistochemical stains are processed more slowly.'),
('f5a475a4-d812-52c9-b31f-d3f65025eae9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is Mohs micrographic surgery?', '["A specialized surgical technique for skin cancer removal with complete margin control using horizontal frozen sections","Standard excision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radiation therapy mediated by specific receptor activation","Cryosurgery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Electrodessication and curettage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A specialized surgical technique for skin cancer removal with complete margin control using horizontal frozen sections', 'Mohs micrographic surgery removes skin cancer layer by layer and examines horizontal frozen sections to achieve complete margin control. Standard excision, radiation, cryosurgery, and electrodesiccation and curettage do not offer this complete margin assessment.'),
('12d7be47-e570-5113-8b85-b15c549f2f81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the advantages of Mohs surgery?', '["Faster than standard excision","maximal tissue preservation","Can be done by any surgeon","Highest cure rate (>99% for primary BCC)","and margin assessment of 100% of the surgical margin"]'::jsonb, 'Highest cure rate (>99% for primary BCC)', 'The advantages of Mohs surgery are the highest cure rate, over 99% for primary basal cell carcinoma, assessment of 100% of the surgical margin, and maximal tissue preservation. It is not faster than standard excision and requires specialized training, not any surgeon.'),
('f4541cf9-2ac0-5c4b-b7a0-ff6723af2b2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What stains are used for melanocytic lesions?', '["SOX10","Melan-A (MART-1)","HMB45","H&E (primary)","and immunostains: S100"]'::jsonb, 'H&E (primary)', 'H&E is the primary stain used to evaluate melanocytic lesions, with immunostains such as S100, Melan-A (MART-1), HMB45, and SOX10 confirming melanocytic differentiation in equivocal cases.'),
('5465d6d9-0313-5bd7-b633-ccb6cedd7d4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What stains are used for keratinocytic lesions?', '["CK7 resulting in decreased functional activity","p40 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CK20 mediated by specific receptor activation","Cytokeratin (CK) markers: CK5/6","p63 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cytokeratin (CK) markers: CK5/6', 'Keratinocytic lesions are identified with cytokeratin markers such as CK5/6. p40 and p63 are squamous-lineage markers and CK7/CK20 indicate glandular sites of origin, so they do not best represent keratinocytic differentiation.'),
('83f38739-b09f-5bd5-8c7c-a03427d8d0d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What stains are used for vascular tumors?', '["FLI1","CD34","and D2-40 (for lymphatic endothelium)","CD31","ERG"]'::jsonb, 'CD31', 'CD31 is a sensitive and specific marker of vascular endothelium used to confirm vascular tumors. CD34, ERG, and FLI1 are also endothelial markers, while D2-40 labels lymphatic endothelium rather than blood vessel endothelium.'),
('77af874d-e013-563d-84ec-43a7f3b2ef61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the most common dermatologic disease?', '["Atopic dermatitis","Acne vulgaris","Skin cancer","Psoriasis","Rosacea"]'::jsonb, 'Acne vulgaris', 'Acne vulgaris is the most common dermatologic disease, arising in the pilosebaceous unit. Atopic dermatitis, psoriasis, rosacea, and skin cancer are less common overall.'),
('23ea6706-047f-50f3-919b-69ba4ceec60e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is acne vulgaris?', '["pustules resulting in decreased functional activity","and scars accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","nodules mediated by specific receptor activation","A chronic inflammatory skin disease of the pilosebaceous unit with comedones","papules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic inflammatory skin disease of the pilosebaceous unit with comedones', 'Acne vulgaris is a chronic inflammatory skin disease of the pilosebaceous unit characterized by comedones. Papules, pustules, nodules, and scars are secondary lesions that develop as the disease progresses.'),
('70c3d83f-f1d0-516f-b5c9-2c567f423059'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is rosacea?', '["pustules resulting in decreased functional activity","A chronic facial skin condition with flushing","papules mediated by specific receptor activation","telangiectasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","erythema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic facial skin condition with flushing', 'Rosacea is a chronic facial skin condition with flushing as a core feature, along with erythema, telangiectasia, and sometimes papules and pustules. The other choices list individual features of rosacea rather than its definition.'),
('995e363d-8a8d-5e4a-8c79-b8cc40a04b3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is atopic dermatitis (eczema)?', '["A chronic","eczematous lesions","inflammatory skin disease with dry skin","and a personal or family history of atopy","pruritic"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic', 'Atopic dermatitis is a chronic, pruritic, inflammatory skin disease with dry skin, eczematous lesions, and a personal or family history of atopy. The other choices each supply a fragment of this same definition rather than a competing answer.'),
('2379a254-ba1a-584f-a6ca-104472774b4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the pathophysiology of atopic dermatitis?', '["Allergic contact reaction","Autoimmune reaction","Skin barrier dysfunction (filaggrin mutations)","Th2-skewed immune response","and dysbiosis (S. aureus colonization)"]'::jsonb, 'Skin barrier dysfunction (filaggrin mutations)', 'Atopic dermatitis results primarily from skin barrier dysfunction, classically due to filaggrin mutations, combined with a Th2-skewed immune response and Staphylococcus aureus dysbiosis. It is not fundamentally an allergic contact or autoimmune reaction.'),
('312c674d-079a-5407-a13e-596051eea337'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the most common cause of contact dermatitis in the US?', '["Fragrances resulting in decreased functional activity","Balsam of Peru accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nickel mediated by specific receptor activation","Neomycin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Poison ivy/oak/sumac (urushiol)"]'::jsonb, 'Poison ivy/oak/sumac (urushiol)', 'Poison ivy, oak, and sumac (urushiol) is the most common cause of contact dermatitis in the US. Nickel, neomycin, fragrances, and Balsam of Peru are common allergens but are encountered less frequently than urushiol.'),
('768d8af9-e21d-5eb7-ade5-69505d9817da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which option best describes the two main layers of the skin?', '["Dermis and hypodermis","Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)","Papillary and reticular dermis","Epidermis and hypodermis","Stratum corneum and stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)', 'The two main layers of the skin are the epidermis, derived from ectoderm, and the dermis, derived from mesoderm. The hypodermis lies beneath the dermis and is not part of the skin proper, and papillary/reticular dermis are sublayers of the dermis.'),
('a0dc8081-a966-5b61-98dd-a72409b83733'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the two main layers of the skin? Which option is most accurate?', '["Dermis and hypodermis","Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)","Papillary and reticular dermis","Epidermis and hypodermis","Stratum corneum and stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)', 'The two main layers of the skin are the epidermis, which is ectodermal, and the dermis, which is mesodermal. Hypodermis lies outside the skin proper, and papillary and reticular dermis are subdivisions of the dermis, not separate main layers.'),
('039526e2-3b70-5c82-9373-e7cb35dc0bbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Integumentary System: what are the two main layers of the skin?', '["Dermis and hypodermis","Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)","Papillary and reticular dermis","Epidermis and hypodermis","Stratum corneum and stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)', 'The two main layers of the skin are the epidermis (ectodermal) and the dermis (mesodermal). Hypodermis is subcutaneous tissue rather than a skin layer, and the papillary and reticular zones are parts of the dermis itself.'),
('796a5207-e1eb-533a-a5e7-e8ecb3503c4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'For Integumentary System, identify the correct response: what are the two main layers of the skin?', '["Dermis and hypodermis","Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)","Papillary and reticular dermis","Epidermis and hypodermis","Stratum corneum and stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)', 'The two main layers of the skin are the epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal). The hypodermis is a subcutaneous layer beneath the skin, and the papillary and reticular dermis are subdivisions within the dermis.'),
('06cbc513-279b-56a6-a82c-f87d62103b18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the two main layers of the skin. Select the best answer.', '["Dermis and hypodermis","Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)","Papillary and reticular dermis","Epidermis and hypodermis","Stratum corneum and stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)', 'The two main layers of the skin are the epidermis (ectodermal) and the dermis (mesodermal). The hypodermis is subcutaneous tissue, not part of the skin, and papillary and reticular dermis are dermal sublayers.'),
('bb2ac697-1fd3-5800-bc1a-e8c3b2ba6de6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Integumentary System item: what are the two main layers of the skin?', '["Dermis and hypodermis","Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)","Papillary and reticular dermis","Epidermis and hypodermis","Stratum corneum and stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal)', 'The two main layers of the skin are the epidermis (ectodermal) and dermis (mesodermal). Hypodermis is the subcutaneous layer below the skin, while papillary and reticular dermis are subdivisions of the dermis.'),
('c2b5a595-a1f2-5471-89c5-981ee121661f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which option best defines the hypodermis (subcutaneous layer)?', '["The deepest layer of the dermis resulting in decreased functional activity","not part of the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis","The basement membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A layer of the epidermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis', 'The hypodermis is loose connective tissue and adipose tissue located beneath the dermis. It is not part of the skin proper, so it is neither the deepest layer of the dermis nor a layer of the epidermis.'),
('879a7bd4-29c4-5cd4-9078-3a81b4eb1a08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the hypodermis (subcutaneous layer)? Which option is most accurate?', '["The deepest layer of the dermis resulting in decreased functional activity","not part of the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis","The basement membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A layer of the epidermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis', 'The hypodermis is the loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis. Since it is subcutaneous rather than cutaneous, it is not the deepest dermal layer or a layer of the epidermis.'),
('f35dfb02-6ed0-5564-8b8e-7078a9fc3ad2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Integumentary System: what is the hypodermis (subcutaneous layer)?', '["The deepest layer of the dermis resulting in decreased functional activity","not part of the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis","The basement membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A layer of the epidermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis', 'The hypodermis consists of loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis. It is subcutaneous tissue outside the skin proper, not a layer of the dermis, epidermis, or basement membrane.'),
('93cf45eb-a532-53f4-bcd1-51d79727bedf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'For Integumentary System, identify the correct response: what is the hypodermis (subcutaneous layer)?', '["The deepest layer of the dermis resulting in decreased functional activity","not part of the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis","The basement membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A layer of the epidermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis', 'The hypodermis is loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis. It is subcutaneous rather than part of the skin, so it is not the deepest layer of the dermis or an epidermal layer.'),
('fd463ef7-443a-55f4-b895-5e5da68c3121'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the hypodermis (subcutaneous layer). Select the best answer.', '["The deepest layer of the dermis resulting in decreased functional activity","not part of the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis","The basement membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A layer of the epidermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis', 'The hypodermis is loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis. It does not belong to the skin proper, ruling out the deepest dermal layer, basement membrane, and epidermal layer choices.'),
('1b2d26e6-5059-5a84-9771-efa9f91d5309'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Integumentary System item: what is the hypodermis (subcutaneous layer)?', '["The deepest layer of the dermis resulting in decreased functional activity","not part of the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis","The basement membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A layer of the epidermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis', 'The hypodermis is the subcutaneous layer of loose connective tissue and adipose tissue beneath the dermis. It is not part of the skin, excluding it from the dermal and epidermal layers.'),
('e5c29623-c68f-5c88-81e2-b434e2c2da7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which option best describes the five layers of the epidermis (from deep to superficial)?', '["stratum granulosum","stratum corneum","stratum spinosum","stratum lucidum","Stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Stratum basale', 'From deep to superficial the five epidermal layers are stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum (in thick skin), and stratum corneum. Stratum basale is the deepest layer, so it is listed first.'),
('fe8ddfa5-bffa-583f-b20a-cb703e818a3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the five layers of the epidermis (from deep to superficial)? Which option is most accurate?', '["stratum granulosum","stratum corneum","stratum spinosum","stratum lucidum","Stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Stratum basale', 'From deep to superficial, the five epidermal layers are stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum, and stratum corneum. Stratum basale is the deepest, hence the first layer in the sequence.'),
('9bb2bfc5-a0c5-56fc-bb04-cc1bb8a1654e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Integumentary System: what are the five layers of the epidermis (from deep to superficial)?', '["stratum granulosum","stratum corneum","stratum spinosum","stratum lucidum","Stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Stratum basale', 'The five epidermal layers in deep-to-superficial order begin with stratum basale, followed by spinosum, granulosum, lucidum, and corneum. Stratum basale sits at the base against the basement membrane.'),
('99f12cd8-e8ed-57e6-a533-e4352bf502ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'For Integumentary System, identify the correct response: what are the five layers of the epidermis (from deep to superficial)?', '["stratum granulosum","stratum corneum","stratum spinosum","stratum lucidum","Stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Stratum basale', 'The five epidermal layers from deep to superficial are stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum, and stratum corneum. Stratum basale is the deepest layer and is named first.'),
('8f71c79a-5b27-535c-bec1-0e1ac7a17b1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What are the five layers of the epidermis (from deep to superficial)? Select the best answer.', '["stratum granulosum","stratum corneum","stratum spinosum","stratum lucidum","Stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Stratum basale', 'The five epidermal layers from deep to superficial are stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum, and stratum corneum. Stratum basale is the deepest layer and begins the sequence.'),
('1b8bd459-44fe-51f5-b817-2cb4e339c863'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Integumentary System item: what are the five layers of the epidermis (from deep to superficial)?', '["stratum granulosum","stratum corneum","stratum spinosum","stratum lucidum","Stratum basale"]'::jsonb, 'Stratum basale', 'The five epidermal layers in deep-to-superficial order are stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum, and stratum corneum. Stratum basale is the deepest and therefore listed first.'),
('e5277853-ae59-58ac-be62-191c03c17ac0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which option best defines thick skin?', '["Skin with thick dermis","found on palms and soles; lacks hair follicles","Skin with all five epidermal layers","Skin on the back","Skin on the face"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with all five epidermal layers', 'Thick skin, found on the palms and soles, is defined by the presence of all five epidermal layers, including the stratum lucidum. Its thickness reflects the epidermis, not the dermis, and it lacks hair follicles.'),
('ec5e168c-0410-56cc-9f9f-83320360f10e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is thick skin? Which option is most accurate?', '["Skin with thick dermis","found on palms and soles; lacks hair follicles","Skin with all five epidermal layers","Skin on the back","Skin on the face"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with all five epidermal layers', 'Thick skin is defined by having all five epidermal layers, including the stratum lucidum. It occurs on the palms and soles, lacks hair follicles, and is not thick because of a thicker dermis.'),
('899a5b6a-2c36-5837-adcf-5437f2442024'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Integumentary System: what is thick skin?', '["Skin with thick dermis","found on palms and soles; lacks hair follicles","Skin with all five epidermal layers","Skin on the back","Skin on the face"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with all five epidermal layers', 'Thick skin has all five epidermal layers, including the stratum lucidum. It is found on palms and soles, lacks hair follicles, and is not defined by dermal thickness or body-site location alone.'),
('5cf3710b-9d51-5ab7-8de2-5d79f27fb8cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'For Integumentary System, identify the correct response: what is thick skin?', '["Skin with thick dermis","found on palms and soles; lacks hair follicles","Skin with all five epidermal layers","Skin on the back","Skin on the face"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with all five epidermal layers', 'Thick skin is distinguished by having all five epidermal layers, including the stratum lucidum. It is located on the palms and soles, lacks hair follicles, and does not owe its thickness to the dermis.'),
('a245abc7-060e-5b68-9354-8ed22990caa4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is thick skin? Select the best answer.', '["Skin with thick dermis","found on palms and soles; lacks hair follicles","Skin with all five epidermal layers","Skin on the back","Skin on the face"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with all five epidermal layers', 'Thick skin contains all five epidermal layers, with the stratum lucidum present. It is found on palms and soles, lacks hair follicles, and is not simply skin with a thick dermis.'),
('cc1db83d-99c1-5b49-ba60-d8f23ab415db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Integumentary System item: what is thick skin?', '["Skin with thick dermis","found on palms and soles; lacks hair follicles","Skin with all five epidermal layers","Skin on the back","Skin on the face"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with all five epidermal layers', 'Thick skin is defined by all five epidermal layers, including the stratum lucidum. It is located on palms and soles and lacks hair follicles, so its thickness is not due to dermal depth or the body site.'),
('b6e7adce-6b94-5973-87c0-3108ff253c72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which option best defines thin skin?', '["Scalp skin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin on the palms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin on the soles mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin with all five layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles', 'Thin skin has a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum, covers most of the body, and contains hair follicles. It is not limited to the scalp and does not occur on the palms or soles.'),
('fb49a656-b79f-563b-9fa9-2edb28caaf9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is thin skin? Which option is most accurate?', '["Scalp skin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin on the palms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin on the soles mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin with all five layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles', 'Thin skin covers most of the body, has a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum, and contains hair follicles. Palms and soles bear thick skin, and thin skin lacks the full five layers.'),
('952ed6cb-9e2e-544d-86b7-ae1f099e98cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Integumentary System: what is thin skin?', '["Scalp skin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin on the palms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin on the soles mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin with all five layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles', 'Thin skin is defined by a thinner stratum corneum and absence of the stratum lucidum, covers most of the body, and has hair follicles. Scalp skin is only one region, and palms and soles are thick skin.'),
('b462feda-9e51-5ca2-8617-e893902cc88b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'For Integumentary System, identify the correct response: what is thin skin?', '["Scalp skin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin on the palms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin on the soles mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin with all five layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles', 'Thin skin has a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum, covers most of the body, and includes hair follicles. It is not confined to the scalp, and the palms and soles are sites of thick skin.'),
('d9a41f16-e1b4-507a-9315-14bd82d33cef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is thin skin? Select the best answer.', '["Scalp skin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin on the palms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin on the soles mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin with all five layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles', 'Thin skin has a thinner stratum corneum, no stratum lucidum, and hair follicles, and it covers most of the body. Palms and soles have thick skin, and thin skin does not possess all five layers.'),
('cf69fdc5-82cc-5516-848f-8db29c1dc3e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Integumentary System item: what is thin skin?', '["Scalp skin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin on the palms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin on the soles mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin with all five layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles"]'::jsonb, 'Skin with a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum; covers most of the body; has hair follicles', 'Thin skin has a thinner stratum corneum and no stratum lucidum, covers most of the body, and contains hair follicles. It is not restricted to the scalp, and palms and soles are thick skin.'),
('88774419-bb2e-526a-83e5-d0cc9a5d2a41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'Which option best defines the stratum basale (germinativum)?', '["consisting of a single row of keratinocytes that continuously divide","The outermost layer of dead cells","The granular layer","The spinous layer","The basal layer of the epidermis"]'::jsonb, 'The basal layer of the epidermis', 'The stratum basale (germinativum) is the basal layer of the epidermis, composed of a single row of keratinocytes that continuously divide to renew the epidermis. The outermost dead-cell, granular, and spinous layers lie above it.'),
('961dfb8f-58b1-5518-8657-153b47ef2f9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Integumentary System', 'What is the stratum basale (germinativum)? Which option is most accurate?', '["consisting of a single row of keratinocytes that continuously divide","The outermost layer of dead cells","The granular layer","The spinous layer","The basal layer of the epidermis"]'::jsonb, 'The basal layer of the epidermis', 'The stratum basale (germinativum) is the basal layer of the epidermis, consisting of a single row of keratinocytes that continuously divide. It sits deepest, directly on the basement membrane, beneath the other named layers.'),
('f4d37b79-5a9f-581e-acbe-372e920a936d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of the respiratory system?', '["olfaction","phonation","and defense","Only gas exchange","Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)', 'The chief function of the respiratory system is the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide between inspired air and blood. Olfaction, phonation, and airway defense are secondary or accessory roles of parts of the system, not its primary purpose.'),
('c325b801-e4dc-5cf9-9071-8d54b0beb07a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the two main divisions of the respiratory system?', '["Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchi and bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper and lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intra and extra pulmonary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)', 'The respiratory system is divided into the conducting portion, which transports air without gas exchange, and the respiratory portion, where gas exchange actually occurs. Left/right, bronchi/bronchioles, upper/lower, and intra/extra pulmonary are not the standard anatomic division.'),
('1e9f73a6-9c7b-5935-b427-8b8f2b66ff1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is included in the conducting portion?', '["pharynx","Nasal cavity","larynx","bronchi","trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity', 'The conducting portion is the chain of passages that transport air from the nasal cavity to the terminal bronchioles, serving to warm, humidify, and filter it. The nasal cavity is the first component of this portion; the pharynx, larynx, trachea, and bronchi are also conducting structures.'),
('70596295-8a5e-5da0-8a80-247f2a523a44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is included in the respiratory portion?', '["Respiratory bronchioles","alveolar sacs","Only alveoli","and alveoli","alveolar ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory bronchioles', 'The respiratory portion is where gas exchange occurs and includes the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and alveoli. The respiratory bronchiole is the first and most proximal structure of this portion.'),
('25278ebd-4e97-53cb-b51b-fa1c91cf4c04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium lines the nasal cavity?', '["Simple cuboidal resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium (respiratory epithelium) and olfactory epithelium (sensory)","Simple squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","Transitional without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stratified squamous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium (respiratory epithelium) and olfactory epithelium (sensory)', 'The respiratory region of the nasal cavity is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory) epithelium, while the roof carries the sensory olfactory epithelium. Simple cuboidal, simple squamous, transitional, and stratified squamous epithelia do not line the nasal cavity.'),
('0699f706-d695-5959-90d7-7b686e77be23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of the nasal mucosa?', '["Only humidification","Only filtration","and filter inspired air; detect odors","humidify","Warm"]'::jsonb, 'Warm', 'The nasal mucosa conditions inspired air by warming, humidifying, and filtering it as the air passes through the nasal passages. Among the listed choices, warming is one of its principal conditioning functions.'),
('cd71286f-4a72-58a9-b642-6d96b0e1fc1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are nasal conchae (turbinates)?', '["Bony projections from the lateral nasal wall that increase surface area for air conditioning","The nasal valve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The paranasal sinuses mediated by specific receptor activation","The nasal bones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The nasal septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bony projections from the lateral nasal wall that increase surface area for air conditioning', 'Nasal conchae are curved bony projections from the lateral nasal wall that enlarge the mucosal surface area and create turbulent airflow, improving warming, humidification, and filtration of inspired air. They are not the nasal valve, sinuses, nasal bones, or septum.'),
('8eea1ecc-d667-5426-ae3d-4f6cbda3f8d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium lines the nasopharynx?', '["Stratified squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory epithelium)","Simple squamous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple cuboidal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory epithelium)', 'The nasopharynx is a continuation of the respiratory airway and is therefore lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory) epithelium, which transports mucus toward the oropharynx. Stratified squamous, transitional, and simple epithelia line other regions.'),
('af248328-1050-5052-9d94-9b24f75ea954'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium lines the oropharynx?', '["Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Simple squamous","Transitional"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The oropharynx is exposed to friction from swallowed food and is lined by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar and the simple epithelial types are not found in this region.'),
('576d70c9-69c0-52c7-adc9-12cf5de9d6ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium lines the laryngeal vestibule?', '["Transitional","Simple columnar","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Simple squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The laryngeal vestibule endures mechanical stress from passing food and is covered by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. The other listed epithelial types line different parts of the airway.'),
('8bbf6a65-28dc-5708-919b-c7edcc040439'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium covers the true vocal cords?', '["Stratified squamous non-keratinized","Simple squamous","Simple columnar","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","Transitional"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized', 'The true vocal cords are covered by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, which withstands the mechanical wear of vibration during phonation. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the remainder of the larynx.'),
('94b09516-d641-5f5c-811e-d44d832e8c81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium lines the rest of the larynx (below the vestibule)?', '["Simple squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory)","Stratified squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple columnar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transitional leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory)', 'Below the vestibule the larynx is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory) epithelium with goblet cells, matching the rest of the conducting airways. Squamous, simple, and transitional epithelia are found elsewhere.'),
('7d74bbb4-c1ff-59f1-ae5f-52b466df5c51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the vocal cords composed of?', '["Mucous membrane only resulting in decreased functional activity","with the vocalis muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The vocal ligament (elastic fibers) covered by stratified squamous epithelium","Smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cartilage only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vocal ligament (elastic fibers) covered by stratified squamous epithelium', 'Each true vocal cord is formed by the vocal ligament, a band of elastic fibers, covered by stratified squamous epithelium and joined laterally by the vocalis muscle. It is not composed of mucous membrane, smooth muscle, or cartilage alone.'),
('4cc2b034-fffa-57ce-b020-66de37f6a3e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of the epiglottis?', '["Filters particles resulting in decreased functional activity","Warms air accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produces sound mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevents food from entering the larynx during swallowing by folding over the laryngeal inlet","Secretes mucus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents food from entering the larynx during swallowing by folding over the laryngeal inlet', 'During swallowing the epiglottis folds back over the laryngeal inlet, directing food into the esophagus and preventing aspiration into the trachea. Filtering particles, warming air, producing sound, and secreting mucus are not its primary roles.'),
('3155cc24-dc84-5def-988b-51be0ea97007'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium covers the epiglottis?', '["Only stratified squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only pseudostratified ciliated mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified squamous on the lingual side; pseudostratified ciliated columnar on the laryngeal side","Simple columnar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous on the lingual side; pseudostratified ciliated columnar on the laryngeal side', 'The epiglottis has a dual epithelial lining: non-keratinized stratified squamous on the lingual (oral) surface and pseudostratified ciliated columnar on the laryngeal (airway) surface. This reflects the different mechanical and airflow demands on the two sides.'),
('8862a9c2-f16a-55d6-b5de-3296880d82c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the laryngeal ventricles?', '["The bronchi resulting in decreased functional activity","The trachea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The nasal cavities mediated by specific receptor activation","Pockets between the false and true vocal cords containing mucous glands","The sinuses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pockets between the false and true vocal cords containing mucous glands', 'Laryngeal ventricles are the lateral recesses between the false and true vocal cords, lined by mucosa containing mucous glands that lubricate the cords. They are not bronchi, trachea, nasal cavities, or paranasal sinuses.'),
('b536a2fd-ebcf-52a2-8d4c-747f9d4a1bd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the innervation of the larynx?', '["Hypoglossal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Recurrent laryngeal nerve (motor to all intrinsic muscles except cricothyroid) and superior laryngeal nerve (cricothyroid and sensory)","Phrenic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus nerve directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glossopharyngeal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent laryngeal nerve (motor to all intrinsic muscles except cricothyroid) and superior laryngeal nerve (cricothyroid and sensory)', 'Both laryngeal nerves derive from the vagus: the recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies motor fibers to all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid, while the superior laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid and provides sensory fibers to the larynx.'),
('0b500b53-c6a7-529e-b8c6-b9161db93651'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium lines the trachea?', '["Stratified squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells (respiratory epithelium)","Simple squamous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple columnar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells (respiratory epithelium)', 'The trachea is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with interspersed goblet cells, the classic respiratory epithelium. The cilia beat mucus upward while the goblet cells moisten and protect the surface; squamous, cuboidal, and transitional epithelia are not found here.'),
('f25569ef-014e-5d68-9beb-5f59cee9cee6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the layers of the tracheal wall?', '["Mucosa","submucosa","adventitia (with C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings)","None of the above","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa', 'The tracheal wall consists of three layers: an inner mucosa of respiratory epithelium over a lamina propria, a submucosa containing glands, and an adventitia that holds the C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings. The mucosa is the innermost of these layers.'),
('31bae972-bf0c-5278-b056-320fa15786b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of the tracheal C-shaped cartilage rings?', '["preventing collapse resulting in decreased functional activity","Warm air accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Keep the airway patent (open) during inspiration","Produce mucus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Filter particles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Keep the airway patent (open) during inspiration', 'The C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings keep the trachea open so it does not collapse during the negative pressure of inspiration. They do not warm air, produce mucus, filter particles, or prevent collapse by themselves.'),
('6e5a5a49-8f7f-5f7d-b2c8-fcb7559f3456'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the trachealis muscle?', '["A ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","An elastic band accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The smooth muscle band connecting the ends of the C-shaped tracheal cartilage rings posteriorly","A skeletal muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cardiac muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The smooth muscle band connecting the ends of the C-shaped tracheal cartilage rings posteriorly', 'The trachealis is a band of smooth muscle that bridges the open posterior ends of the C-shaped tracheal cartilage rings. Its contraction narrows the lumen and assists in cough and forceful expulsion of air.'),
('24a50f8b-09ce-5201-ad39-ea419643ac11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What cells are in the respiratory epithelium?', '["Ciliated columnar cells","goblet cells","and neuroendocrine cells","basal cells","brush cells"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliated columnar cells', 'The respiratory epithelium contains several cell types: ciliated columnar cells, goblet cells, basal cells, brush cells, and neuroendocrine (Kulchitsky) cells. Ciliated columnar cells are the most numerous and drive mucociliary clearance.'),
('8d866792-e6a1-50e9-8b8b-fe5965ad4268'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of the ciliated cells in the respiratory epithelium?', '["Warm air resulting in decreased functional activity","Secrete hormones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Beat mucus and trapped particles upward toward the pharynx (mucociliary escalator)","Detect odors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secrete mucus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beat mucus and trapped particles upward toward the pharynx (mucociliary escalator)', 'Ciliated cells beat in coordinated waves to propel mucus and trapped particles upward toward the pharynx, forming the mucociliary escalator. They do not warm air, detect odors, or secrete mucus or hormones.'),
('ce81b299-efd9-5246-bdeb-94721bd3843c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of goblet cells in the respiratory epithelium?', '["Secrete antibodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Transport mucus upward accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Warm air mediated by specific receptor activation","Detect odors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secrete mucus to trap inhaled particles and humidify air"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete mucus to trap inhaled particles and humidify air', 'Goblet cells secrete mucus that traps inhaled particles and keeps the airway surface moist, helping humidify inspired air. They do not transport mucus, detect odors, warm air, or secrete antibodies.'),
('12660a01-aba7-5210-88c8-9a7b1e68299b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the mucociliary escalator?', '["A cellular transport system resulting in decreased functional activity","A staircase in the trachea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The upward movement of mucus by cilia from the lungs toward the pharynx for removal","An escalator for patients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A chemical reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The upward movement of mucus by cilia from the lungs toward the pharynx for removal', 'The mucociliary escalator is the coordinated upward transport of the mucus blanket by ciliary beating, carrying trapped particles from the deep airways to the pharynx, where it is swallowed or expectorated. This is a key defense that clears inhaled debris.'),
('1f5a1085-5e81-5bbd-899a-efd2aeef1d59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are Kulchitsky cells (neuroendocrine cells) in the lung?', '["Goblet cells resulting in decreased functional activity","responding to hypoxia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scattered cells in the airway epithelium that secrete serotonin and other peptides","Ciliated cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basal cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Scattered cells in the airway epithelium that secrete serotonin and other peptides', 'Kulchitsky (neuroendocrine) cells are scattered endocrine cells in the airway epithelium that secrete serotonin, calcitonin, and other peptides and can sense hypoxia. They are distinct from goblet, ciliated, and basal cells.'),
('509a7c50-896e-5188-8ce0-58f7ea378b7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the bronchial tree?', '["The pleura resulting in decreased functional activity","The blood vessels of the lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The branching system of air passages from the trachea to the alveoli","The nerves of the lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lymphatics of the lung leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The branching system of air passages from the trachea to the alveoli', 'The bronchial tree is the branching system of air passages that conducts air from the trachea down to the alveoli. The pleura, pulmonary vessels, nerves, and lymphatics are separate structures of the lung.'),
('38003ead-c34f-5406-8b77-614d9685df6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the difference between the right and left main bronchus?', '["and more vertical; left is longer","narrower","and more horizontal","wider","Right is shorter"]'::jsonb, 'Right is shorter', 'The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertical than the left, which is longer, narrower, and more horizontal. This arrangement explains why inhaled foreign bodies lodge more often in the right main bronchus.'),
('0c1440f4-1d77-52a2-8ca7-37a162aca08c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the structure of the bronchial wall?', '["Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with cartilage plates and smooth muscle","Simple columnar epithelium with cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Squamous epithelium with cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple cuboidal epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stratified squamous with glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with cartilage plates and smooth muscle', 'Bronchi are lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, and their walls contain cartilage plates plus smooth muscle, with submucosal glands. This distinguishes bronchi from bronchioles, which lack cartilage and glands.'),
('52b278ad-9527-554e-b3ba-01b5eab75f89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a bronchiole?', '["An alveolar duct resulting in decreased functional activity","A small bronchus with cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An airway <1 mm in diameter with no cartilage or glands in its wall","A terminal bronchiole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The first part of the respiratory tree leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An airway <1 mm in diameter with no cartilage or glands in its wall', 'A bronchiole is an airway smaller than about 1 mm in diameter whose wall contains smooth muscle and elastic fibers but no cartilage or glands. Larger airways that contain cartilage and glands are bronchi.'),
('f087248b-cc33-5e4b-81c1-7415278c9760'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium lines the bronchioles?', '["Simple squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","Stratified squamous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transitional mediated by specific receptor activation","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple ciliated columnar to cuboidal epithelium with Clara cells"]'::jsonb, 'Simple ciliated columnar to cuboidal epithelium with Clara cells', 'Bronchioles are lined by simple ciliated columnar epithelium that becomes low cuboidal as they taper, with Clara cells present and goblet cells disappearing distally. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the larger bronchi and trachea.'),
('2ca322d7-c11b-591a-8e1c-9db83aa153b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are Clara cells (Club cells)?', '["Goblet cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Non-ciliated secretory cells in the bronchiolar epithelium that produce surfactant and detoxify toxins","Ciliated cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuroendocrine cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-ciliated secretory cells in the bronchiolar epithelium that produce surfactant and detoxify toxins', 'Clara (club) cells are non-ciliated dome-shaped secretory cells of the bronchiolar epithelium that produce surfactant-like material and detoxify inhaled toxic substances. They are not goblet cells, basal cells, ciliated cells, or neuroendocrine cells.'),
('9e51ea38-7cec-55a9-ba14-de22135ce527'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of Clara cells?', '["Transport mucus resulting in decreased functional activity","Warm air accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produce mucus mediated by specific receptor activation","Detect odors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protect the bronchiolar epithelium by secreting surfactant and detoxifying inhaled toxins"]'::jsonb, 'Protect the bronchiolar epithelium by secreting surfactant and detoxifying inhaled toxins', 'Clara cells protect the bronchiolar epithelium by secreting surfactant-like substances that reduce surface tension and by detoxifying inhaled toxins through cytochrome P450 enzymes. They do not transport mucus, warm air, produce mucus, or detect odors.'),
('348e5ec3-abac-54f0-94bb-15b81c3bae82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a terminal bronchiole?', '["An alveolar duct resulting in decreased functional activity","A bronchiole with alveoli accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The first bronchiole mediated by specific receptor activation","The last generation of conducting bronchioles before the respiratory bronchioles","A respiratory bronchiole leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The last generation of conducting bronchioles before the respiratory bronchioles', 'The terminal bronchiole is the last purely conducting airway and has no alveoli in its wall. It gives rise to respiratory bronchioles, which mark the beginning of the respiratory portion.'),
('1f8bf534-4e94-530c-a855-5fecf97ff060'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a respiratory bronchiole?', '["An alveolar sac resulting in decreased functional activity","An alveolar duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A conducting airway mediated by specific receptor activation","A terminal bronchiole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A bronchiole whose wall is interrupted by alveoli; the beginning of the respiratory portion"]'::jsonb, 'A bronchiole whose wall is interrupted by alveoli; the beginning of the respiratory portion', 'A respiratory bronchiole has alveoli budding from its wall, marking the start of the respiratory (gas-exchange) portion of the lung. Purely conducting airways such as terminal bronchioles do not contain alveoli.'),
('7ff294f0-ff2b-5ac7-9384-805a7c78226a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium lines respiratory bronchioles?', '["Simple squamous","becoming squamous where alveoli are present","Simple cuboidal with Clara cells","Stratified squamous","Pseudostratified ciliated"]'::jsonb, 'Simple cuboidal with Clara cells', 'Respiratory bronchioles are lined by simple cuboidal epithelium containing Clara cells, which becomes simple squamous at sites where alveoli interrupt the wall. Stratified squamous and pseudostratified ciliated epithelia are not found here.'),
('fe018cb0-9d80-56ed-a840-9704c7002493'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are alveolar ducts?', '["Alveolar sacs","with wall of their own; composed of rings of smooth muscle","Terminal bronchioles","Respiratory bronchioles","Distal passages lined entirely by alveoli"]'::jsonb, 'Distal passages lined entirely by alveoli', 'Alveolar ducts are thin distal passages whose walls are made almost entirely of openings into alveoli and do not have a continuous wall of their own. They are not terminal or respiratory bronchioles.'),
('935345b6-a1e0-51d0-87a3-62b68849998f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are alveolar sacs?', '["Individual alveoli resulting in decreased functional activity","Alveolar ducts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clusters of alveoli at the end of alveolar ducts","Terminal bronchioles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Respiratory bronchioles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clusters of alveoli at the end of alveolar ducts', 'Alveolar sacs are groups of two or more alveoli that share a common opening at the end of an alveolar duct. They are not individual alveoli, ducts, or bronchioles.'),
('7c3050c9-e56b-52f4-858d-72bf64563167'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'How many generations of branching are there in the human lung?', '["10 generations resulting in decreased functional activity","30 generations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 23 generations (trachea to alveolar sacs)","15 generations without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 generations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 23 generations (trachea to alveolar sacs)', 'The human airway divides by dichotomous branching into about 23 generations, from the trachea down to the alveolar sacs. This branching enormously increases the total cross-sectional area available for gas exchange.'),
('f318e438-2481-5600-9b8f-3edc7d5d2d43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the structure of an alveolus?', '["A muscular structure resulting in decreased functional activity","A thin-walled polygonal air sac with a single layer of squamous epithelial cells (type I pneumocytes)","A thick-walled structure mediated by specific receptor activation","A cartilage-lined sac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A mucus-filled sac leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin-walled polygonal air sac with a single layer of squamous epithelial cells (type I pneumocytes)', 'An alveolus is a thin-walled, polygonal air sac lined chiefly by a single layer of squamous type I pneumocytes and surrounded by a rich capillary network. It is not muscular, thick-walled, cartilage-lined, or mucus-filled.'),
('b78276ea-d84d-5a51-98c5-d4ef4b1e4624'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are type I pneumocytes?', '["Thin resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells that produce surfactant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cells that line the bronchi mediated by specific receptor activation","squamous epithelial cells that cover 95% of the alveolar surface and facilitate gas exchange","Macrophages leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'squamous epithelial cells that cover 95% of the alveolar surface and facilitate gas exchange', 'Type I pneumocytes are extremely thin squamous epithelial cells that cover about 95% of the alveolar surface and form the barrier across which gases diffuse. They do not produce surfactant, which is the role of type II cells, and they are not macrophages or bronchial lining cells.'),
('a8fec73e-3c7f-5996-91a1-08b749ebd295'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are type II pneumocytes?', '["Cuboidal cells that produce pulmonary surfactant","Macrophages","Squamous cells for gas exchange","and are precursors to type I cells","repair alveolar epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Cuboidal cells that produce pulmonary surfactant', 'Type II pneumocytes are cuboidal cells that synthesize and secrete pulmonary surfactant and can also multiply to replenish the alveolar epithelium. They are not macrophages or squamous gas-exchange cells.'),
('16895a1a-5993-56cb-b18a-d3a0e5a0f012'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is pulmonary surfactant?', '["preventing alveolar collapse resulting in decreased functional activity","A substance that increases surface tension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An antibiotic mediated by specific receptor activation","A mucus-like substance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A phospholipid-protein complex that reduces surface tension at the air-liquid interface"]'::jsonb, 'A phospholipid-protein complex that reduces surface tension at the air-liquid interface', 'Pulmonary surfactant is a phospholipid-protein mixture that coats the alveoli and lowers surface tension at the air-liquid interface, preventing alveolar collapse at end-expiration. Increasing surface tension would promote collapse, so that option is opposite to its role.'),
('7ca14f1c-73a6-5169-b0c5-a72e3cf20f68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of surfactant proteins?', '["SP-A and SP-D: innate immunity; SP-B and SP-C: enhance phospholipid film and surface tension reduction","Only SP-B accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only SP-A mediated by specific receptor activation","Only SP-C without affecting the primary tissue pathway","function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SP-A and SP-D: innate immunity; SP-B and SP-C: enhance phospholipid film and surface tension reduction', 'Surfactant proteins have distinct roles: SP-A and SP-D contribute to innate immunity against inhaled pathogens, while SP-B and SP-C help spread the phospholipid film and enhance surface-tension lowering. This division of labor underlies normal surfactant function.'),
('a89bc0c5-f797-517b-ac74-a43dfd621b18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the alveolar-capillary membrane (respiratory membrane)?', '["and capillary endothelium resulting in decreased functional activity","The alveolar wall only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The thin barrier between alveolar air and blood: surfactant","type I cell without affecting the primary tissue pathway","basement membranes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thin barrier between alveolar air and blood: surfactant', 'The respiratory membrane is the extremely thin barrier between alveolar air and blood, formed by the surfactant layer, type I pneumocyte, fused basement membranes, and capillary endothelium. Its thinness allows rapid diffusion of oxygen and carbon dioxide.'),
('bd3ed080-7e64-5aaa-b807-5dfdd5f6385a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the layers of the respiratory membrane?', '["Only three layers","fused basement membranes (type I and capillary)","type I cell","Surfactant","and capillary endothelium"]'::jsonb, 'Surfactant', 'The respiratory membrane is built of several layers: the surfactant film, the type I pneumocyte, the fused basement membranes of the alveolus and capillary, and the capillary endothelium. Surfactant is the innermost layer facing the alveolar air.'),
('9b2cc9b5-3c29-5fd0-bbbf-8e7ee56e3f45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are alveolar macrophages (dust cells)?', '["bacteria resulting in decreased functional activity","Type II pneumocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and debris mediated by specific receptor activation","Type I pneumocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phagocytic cells on the alveolar surface that engulf inhaled particles"]'::jsonb, 'Phagocytic cells on the alveolar surface that engulf inhaled particles', 'Alveolar macrophages (dust cells) are phagocytes that wander over the alveolar surface and engulf inhaled particles, bacteria, and debris. They are not pneumocytes and do not contribute structurally to the alveolar wall.'),
('c982a94e-8ccb-53f7-898f-abd3c5f4f879'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the origin of alveolar macrophages?', '["Type II cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Type I cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibroblasts mediated by specific receptor activation","Endothelial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone marrow-derived monocytes that migrate into the alveoli"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow-derived monocytes that migrate into the alveoli', 'Alveolar macrophages arise from bone marrow-derived monocytes that enter the blood, migrate into the lung, and mature within the alveoli. They are not generated by pneumocytes, fibroblasts, or endothelial cells.'),
('9a36efb5-d385-5211-aeb4-d040762b09b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the pores of Kohn?', '["Small openings in the alveolar walls that allow collateral ventilation between adjacent alveoli","Holes in blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Holes in the bronchi mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatic channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Holes in the pleura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small openings in the alveolar walls that allow collateral ventilation between adjacent alveoli', 'The pores of Kohn are small openings in the alveolar walls that connect neighboring alveoli and permit collateral ventilation when a bronchiole is obstructed. They are not holes in vessels, bronchi, pleura, or lymphatic channels.'),
('af154281-83f6-5b61-a4dd-c0afb22638a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the pulmonary interstitium?', '["elastin resulting in decreased functional activity","The connective tissue framework of the lung containing fibroblasts","collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","The alveolar air space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and extracellular matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The connective tissue framework of the lung containing fibroblasts', 'The pulmonary interstitium is the connective tissue framework of the lung, containing fibroblasts, collagen, elastin, and extracellular matrix that support the alveolar walls and vessels. It is distinct from the alveolar air space.'),
('89b5c949-4a77-56a9-8528-8a05b1c7fb0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the three compartments of the lung interstitium?', '["Peribronchovascular (axial)","medullary","Cortical","and pleural (peripheral)","septal (parenchymal)"]'::jsonb, 'Peribronchovascular (axial)', 'The interstitial space of the lung has three compartments: peribronchovascular (axial) tissue that follows the bronchovascular bundles, septal (parenchymal) tissue in the interlobular septa, and pleural (peripheral) tissue beneath the pleura. Fluid and inflammatory cells track through all three, but edema characteristically accumulates in the thin peribronchovascular (axial) space in early cardiogenic pulmonary edema.'),
('60918935-96d5-57a1-bbb4-3ff2dc706fe6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of the pulmonary circulation?', '["Delivers deoxygenated blood to alveoli for gas exchange and returns oxygenated blood to the heart","Nourishes the lung tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filters the blood mediated by specific receptor activation","Warms the air without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produces lymph leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Delivers deoxygenated blood to alveoli for gas exchange and returns oxygenated blood to the heart', 'The pulmonary circulation carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the alveolar capillaries for gas exchange and returns oxygenated blood to the left atrium. The bronchial circulation, not the pulmonary circulation, is responsible for nourishing the lung tissue itself; filtering blood, warming air, and lymph production are functions of other systems.'),
('b775aa22-8fb2-5020-b39a-112fb6009d6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the bronchial circulation?', '["Systemic circulation (from the aorta) that supplies blood to the bronchi and other lung structures","The coronary circulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lymphatic system mediated by specific receptor activation","The portal system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pulmonary circulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic circulation (from the aorta) that supplies blood to the bronchi and other lung structures', 'The bronchial circulation is a systemic branch from the descending aorta that delivers oxygenated blood to the bronchi and lung supporting structures down to the respiratory bronchioles. Part of its venous return enters the pulmonary veins, which creates a small physiologic (anatomic) shunt, whereas the coronary, portal, and pulmonary circulations each serve different territories.'),
('f3e14f95-e361-54f0-8715-48a7d7852f7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the structure of pulmonary capillaries?', '["Fenestrated capillaries resulting in decreased functional activity","Continuous capillaries with tight junctions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sinusoids mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatic capillaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A dense anastomosing network of thin-walled capillaries in the alveolar walls"]'::jsonb, 'A dense anastomosing network of thin-walled capillaries in the alveolar walls', 'Pulmonary capillaries form a dense, anastomosing network of thin-walled vessels embedded in the alveolar walls, so red cells pass in single file close to the alveolar air. The endothelial lining is continuous but extremely attenuated, favors rapid gas diffusion, and differs from fenestrated capillaries, sinusoids, and lymphatic capillaries in both structure and function.'),
('f131799b-40b8-5d08-9f3e-cde486d4a7c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the blood-gas barrier thickness in normal lung?', '["10-20 microns","About 0.5-1.0 microns","2-3 microns","5-10 microns",">20 microns"]'::jsonb, 'About 0.5-1.0 microns', 'The normal blood-gas barrier is about 0.5-1.0 microns thick, composed of the alveolar epithelium, fused basement membranes, and capillary endothelium. This extreme thinness is essential for rapid diffusion of oxygen and carbon dioxide; thickening of the barrier, as in interstitial edema, impairs gas exchange.'),
('a9a8a6a5-160c-5ba7-99f0-e79c1598df12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the relationship between alveolar ventilation and capillary perfusion?', '["Perfusion exceeds ventilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventilation exceeds perfusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They are independent mediated by specific receptor activation","Ventilation and perfusion are matched (V/Q matching) for efficient gas exchange","relationship leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilation and perfusion are matched (V/Q matching) for efficient gas exchange', 'For efficient gas exchange, alveolar ventilation and capillary perfusion must be matched region by region. When an alveolus is poorly ventilated, local hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction shunts blood away to better-ventilated regions, so the small arrows point toward the matched option, and ventilation and perfusion are not independent or systematically unequal.'),
('3aa4e4ed-3595-5701-a4cf-e34ad031503f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV)?', '["Constriction of bronchioles","Relaxation of the diaphragm","Dilation of pulmonary vessels in hypoxia","diverting blood to better-ventilated areas","Constriction of pulmonary arterioles in response to alveolar hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Constriction of pulmonary arterioles in response to alveolar hypoxia', 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is the protective constriction of pulmonary arterioles when alveolar oxygen is low, diverting blood from poorly ventilated alveoli to better-ventilated areas. It does not constrict bronchioles or dilate vessels, and its chronic, generalized form (for example at high altitude) leads to pulmonary hypertension.'),
('2fff1212-be17-5207-8eed-c35f9dabdba5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the hydrostatic pressure in the pulmonary circulation?', '["Same as systemic: 120/80 mmHg","pressure","Low pressure: pulmonary artery 25/8 mmHg","Higher than systemic","mean 15 mmHg; capillary 10 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 'Low pressure: pulmonary artery 25/8 mmHg', 'The pulmonary circulation is a low-pressure, high-flow system in which the pulmonary artery pressure is about 25/8 mmHg and capillary pressure is only about 10 mmHg. This is far below systemic arterial pressure, and the low hydrostatic pressure, combined with plasma oncotic pressure and lymphatic drainage, keeps the alveoli relatively dry.'),
('7560e381-21aa-5eca-86c6-c3e665326f63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What forces keep the alveoli dry?', '["high oncotic pressure","low capillary hydrostatic pressure","Lymphatic drainage","and surfactant","Only lymphatic drainage"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic drainage', 'The alveoli stay dry because capillary hydrostatic pressure is low, plasma oncotic pressure draws fluid back into the vessels, and surfactant lowers surface tension. Lymphatic drainage actively removes the small amount of filtered interstitial fluid and is a crucial safety mechanism that prevents alveolar flooding.'),
('86729b3a-abce-5c0d-a15d-6067f6eb9117'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'How many lobes does the right lung have?', '["inferior","None of the above","Two: superior","middle","Three: superior"]'::jsonb, 'Three: superior', 'The right lung has three lobes: superior, middle, and inferior, separated by the horizontal and oblique fissures. The left lung has only two lobes because the cardiac notch and the heart reduce its medial bulk; there is no right upper, middle, and inferior combination in the left lung.'),
('cc27aafd-b0df-507e-a7c3-907242a1759b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'How many lobes does the left lung have?', '["Two: superior and inferior","Four accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","One mediated by specific receptor activation","Two: upper without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Three leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two: superior and inferior', 'The left lung is divided by the oblique fissure into two lobes: superior and inferior. It lacks the middle lobe of the right lung because the cardiac notch indents its mediastinal surface, and it is never counted as four, one, or three lobes.'),
('505774d9-648b-530a-ba62-cb892a976e8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the lingula of the left lung?', '["The left lower lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","The cardiac notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A projection on the upper lobe of the left lung that corresponds to the right middle lobe","The diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pleura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A projection on the upper lobe of the left lung that corresponds to the right middle lobe', 'The lingula is a tongue-shaped projection of the superior lobe of the left lung that is embryologically homologous to the right middle lobe. It lies in the cardiac notch area, and its position makes it a common site for aspiration-related pneumonia.'),
('723a8b21-7d73-52e8-b8e2-551e0bfc03ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the bronchopulmonary segments?', '["The primary bronchi","Functional anatomic units of the lung","each supplied by a tertiary (segmental) bronchus and its own blood supply","The alveoli","The lobes of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional anatomic units of the lung', 'Bronchopulmonary segments are the functional anatomic units of the lung, each supplied by a tertiary (segmental) bronchus, its own pulmonary artery branches, and its own vessels, and partly separated by connective tissue septa. They are surgically important because each segment can be resected independently; they are not simply the bronchi, alveoli, or lobes.'),
('3da755e6-f9b3-51a5-a185-69e8ca442b75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the clinical significance of bronchopulmonary segments?', '["They are important for staging cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","They determine ventilation patterns accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","significance mediated by specific receptor activation","They determine surgical approaches without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They allow segmental resection of diseased lung tissue while preserving healthy segments"]'::jsonb, 'They allow segmental resection of diseased lung tissue while preserving healthy segments', 'Because each bronchopulmonary segment has its own bronchus and blood supply, a diseased segment can be surgically removed while the neighboring healthy segments are preserved. This surgical benefit underpins segmental resection, whereas staging, ventilation patterns, and surgical approaches are not the specific clinical value of these segments.'),
('4aabfd7f-8581-5b68-bac7-d6b49b5efc72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the lymphatic drainage of the lung?', '["Superficial (pleural) and deep (peribronchovascular) lymphatic plexuses that drain to hilar lymph nodes","Only superficial lymphatics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To the axillary nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","lymphatic system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only deep lymphatics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial (pleural) and deep (peribronchovascular) lymphatic plexuses that drain to hilar lymph nodes', 'The lung has two lymphatic plexuses: a superficial (pleural) network draining the periphery and visceral pleura and a deep (peribronchovascular) network following the bronchi and vessels; both drain to the hilar and tracheobronchial lymph nodes, which is why lung cancer first spreads to hilar nodes.'),
('7a34cdfe-66e1-5d40-b25f-7be4a2a398b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the tracheobronchial lymph nodes?', '["Lymph nodes at the tracheal bifurcation (carina) that receive drainage from both lungs","Lymph nodes in the axilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymph nodes in the neck mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymph nodes in the groin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymph nodes in the abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph nodes at the tracheal bifurcation (carina) that receive drainage from both lungs', 'The tracheobronchial lymph nodes sit at the tracheal bifurcation (carina) and receive lymphatic drainage from both lungs via the hilar and bronchopulmonary chains. They are commonly involved by bronchogenic carcinoma and are a key station in lung cancer staging, in contrast to axillary, cervical, inguinal, or abdominal nodes, which drain other regions.'),
('12ee43fc-8883-55f8-b3e1-4e87756ff58f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the nerve supply of the lung?', '["Only vagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasympathetic (vagus) and sympathetic (sympathetic trunk); the vagus causes bronchoconstriction and secretions","Phrenic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intercostal nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (vagus) and sympathetic (sympathetic trunk); the vagus causes bronchoconstriction and secretions', 'The lungs receive a dual autonomic supply: parasympathetic fibers from the vagus cause bronchoconstriction and increased glandular secretion, while sympathetic fibers from the sympathetic trunk cause bronchodilation. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm rather than the lung, and the intercostal nerves supply the chest wall, not the lung itself.'),
('1ff33c67-7a23-507b-883a-81dea59914b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the phrenic nerve?', '["The nerve (C3-C5) that innervates the diaphragm","A nerve to the heart","A nerve to the neck","A nerve to the arm","providing the major respiratory motor function"]'::jsonb, 'The nerve (C3-C5) that innervates the diaphragm', 'The phrenic nerve arises from the C3-C5 spinal roots and is the sole motor supply to the diaphragm, providing the major respiratory motor function. It also carries sensory fibers to the diaphragm, pericardium, and mediastinal and central diaphragmatic pleura, so diaphragmatic irritation is referred to the shoulder.'),
('0363c617-4bf9-5db6-b83a-37882ea48ffc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the nerve supply to the pleura?', '["Only visceral pleura has pain fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Both have pain fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal pleura: intercostal and phrenic nerves (pain sensation); Visceral pleura: no pain fibers","Only parietal pleura has stretch fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neither has pain fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal pleura: intercostal and phrenic nerves (pain sensation); Visceral pleura: no pain fibers', 'The parietal pleura is innervated by the intercostal nerves and the phrenic nerve and is sensitive to pain, while the visceral pleura has no pain fibers and cannot localize pain. This arrangement explains why pleuritic pain is felt over the chest wall or referred to the shoulder, and it is false that only visceral pleura, both layers, or neither layer carries pain sensation.'),
('533e606f-354c-53f7-a7b1-66d32eeefc54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the pleura?', '["The mediastinum resulting in decreased functional activity","The lung capsule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The serous membrane that covers the lung (visceral) and lines the thoracic cavity (parietal)","The diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The chest wall muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The serous membrane that covers the lung (visceral) and lines the thoracic cavity (parietal)', 'The pleura is a serous membrane of two layers: the visceral pleura covers the surface of the lung while the parietal pleura lines the thoracic cavity. The potential space between them contains a thin film of fluid, and the layers meet at the hilum; the mediastinum, lung capsule, diaphragm, and chest-wall muscles are separate structures.'),
('192575a6-a7fa-584e-a0a8-d63492caffd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What type of epithelium lines the pleura?', '["Simple columnar","Simple squamous mesothelium","Stratified squamous","Pseudostratified columnar","Transitional"]'::jsonb, 'Simple squamous mesothelium', 'The pleura is lined by simple squamous mesothelium, a serous lining that produces a small amount of lubricating fluid and facilitates smooth gliding of the two layers during respiration. Columnar, stratified squamous, pseudostratified, and transitional epithelia belong to other organs, not to serous membranes.'),
('20bdc661-6a61-57e2-97a1-84a6487d642e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the pleural cavity?', '["The mediastinum","containing a thin film of serous fluid","The space inside the lung","The pericardial cavity","The space between the visceral and parietal pleura"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the visceral and parietal pleura', 'The pleural cavity is the potential space between the visceral and parietal pleura, normally containing only a thin film of serous fluid. It surrounds the lung, not the mediastinum, interior of the lung, or pericardial cavity, and it is the site where air, fluid, blood, or pus accumulate in disease.'),
('78c61247-5753-59f0-8665-6faf310e9c6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of pleural fluid?', '["Oxygenation resulting in decreased functional activity","Immune defense accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Waste removal mediated by specific receptor activation","Lubrication to reduce friction and surface tension to couple the lung to the chest wall","Nutrition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lubrication to reduce friction and surface tension to couple the lung to the chest wall', 'Pleural fluid lubricates the two pleural surfaces to reduce friction during breathing, and its surface tension couples the lung to the chest wall so the lung follows chest-wall expansion. The fluid does not oxygenate the blood, defend immunity, remove waste, or nourish tissue.'),
('8e1ab70e-a008-555c-bf10-333c6307ef98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a pneumothorax?', '["Fluid in the pleural cavity","Pus in the pleural cavity","Blood in the pleural cavity","Air in the pleural cavity causing lung collapse","Inflammation of the pleura"]'::jsonb, 'Air in the pleural cavity causing lung collapse', 'A pneumothorax is the presence of air in the pleural cavity, which breaks the surface-tension coupling between the lung and the chest wall and allows the lung to collapse. Fluid, pus, blood, and pleural inflammation describe effusions, empyemas, hemothorax, and pleuritis, respectively, rather than air in the space.'),
('a714b0ca-17dc-5a30-a2a4-f647e79ebe3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a pleural effusion?', '["Air in the pleural space resulting in decreased functional activity","Abnormal accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity (transudate or exudate)","Tumor of the pleura mediated by specific receptor activation","Scarring of the pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammation of the pleura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity (transudate or exudate)', 'A pleural effusion is an abnormal collection of fluid in the pleural cavity, classified as a transudate (low protein, e.g., heart failure) or an exudate (high protein and cells, e.g., infection or malignancy) using Light''s criteria. Air, tumor, scarring, and inflammation describe pneumothorax, mesothelioma, thickening, and pleuritis instead.'),
('eda2f1eb-cdd9-54d6-b993-775b93c2c9e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a hemothorax?', '["Chyle in the pleural space","Fluid in the pericardium","Blood in the pleural cavity","Air in the pleural space","Pus in the pleural space"]'::jsonb, 'Blood in the pleural cavity', 'A hemothorax is blood in the pleural cavity, usually from chest trauma, torn intercostal vessels, or aortic injury; large collections may require chest-tube drainage. It is distinct from chyle (chylothorax), pericardial fluid, air (pneumothorax), and pus (empyema) in the pleural space.'),
('20bcb29c-e316-5f89-9d1d-6cfb8edd5fa1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is an empyema?', '["Serous fluid in the pleural space","Pus in the pleural cavity (infected pleural effusion)","Blood in the pleural space","Air in the pleural space","Chyle in the pleural space"]'::jsonb, 'Pus in the pleural cavity (infected pleural effusion)', 'An empyema is pus in the pleural cavity, an infected pleural effusion that most often complicates pneumonia and requires drainage plus antibiotics. Serous fluid, blood, air, and chyle in the pleural space describe effusions, hemothorax, pneumothorax, and chylothorax, respectively.'),
('0be4df24-2230-5920-9d4c-c1575b9f300f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the costophrenic angle?', '["The angle of the scapula resulting in decreased functional activity","The angle between the ribs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The angle of the trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","The acute angle between the costal pleura and diaphragmatic pleura on chest X-ray","The angle of the diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The acute angle between the costal pleura and diaphragmatic pleura on chest X-ray', 'The costophrenic angle is the acute-angle recess where the costal and diaphragmatic pleura meet at the lung bases on a chest radiograph. It is the most dependent part of the pleural space, so a small effusion or pneumothorax blunts or obliterates this angle.'),
('2a67bd46-cf51-5e3c-a266-a80046eb9fde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the mediastinum?', '["great vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","esophagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","containing the heart mediated by specific receptor activation","trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The anatomical space between the two pleural cavities"]'::jsonb, 'The anatomical space between the two pleural cavities', 'The mediastinum is the anatomical space between the two pleural cavities, containing the heart, great vessels, trachea, esophagus, and lymph nodes; it is divided into superior, anterior, middle, and posterior compartments. The individual structures listed (great vessels, esophagus, heart, trachea) are contents, not the definition of the region.'),
('aa04d3f1-ff86-5d6d-a04f-7125d2d6d1d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)?', '["Pneumonia resulting in decreased functional activity","from smoking accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A progressive lung disease characterized by chronic bronchitis and emphysema","Lung cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asthma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A progressive lung disease characterized by chronic bronchitis and emphysema', 'COPD is a progressive, largely smoking-related lung disease that encompasses chronic bronchitis and emphysema and produces airflow obstruction that is not fully reversible. Asthma shows reversible obstruction, pneumonia is an acute infection, and lung cancer is a malignant process, none of which defines COPD.'),
('0a288cf4-9740-542a-8ab6-5f4d910500ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is chronic bronchitis?', '["Pulmonary edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchiole inflammation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","with mucous gland hyperplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic cough with sputum for at least 3 months in 2 consecutive years","Alveolar destruction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic cough with sputum for at least 3 months in 2 consecutive years', 'Chronic bronchitis is defined clinically by a chronic cough with sputum production for at least 3 months in 2 consecutive years, typically from long-term smoking or irritant exposure. Pulmonary edema, bronchiolar inflammation, and alveolar destruction describe other processes rather than this clinical definition.'),
('d3dd55c4-e34f-50f8-b097-691a905471af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of chronic bronchitis?', '["Mucous gland hyperplasia","inflammation","and squamous metaplasia","Alveolar destruction","goblet cell metaplasia in small airways"]'::jsonb, 'Mucous gland hyperplasia', 'The histologic hallmark of chronic bronchitis is hyperplasia and hypertrophy of the submucosal mucous glands in large airways, often with goblet cell metaplasia in the small airways, reflected by an increased Reid index. Inflammation, alveolar destruction, and squamous metaplasia are secondary or non-specific findings rather than the defining feature.'),
('9ae7f1c9-4e7a-507d-8648-8e85c4e1c7b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is emphysema?', '["Airway inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","Mucous gland hyperplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Permanent enlargement of air spaces distal to terminal bronchioles with alveolar wall destruction","Alveolar fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchial muscle hypertrophy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Permanent enlargement of air spaces distal to terminal bronchioles with alveolar wall destruction', 'Emphysema is defined as permanent enlargement of the air spaces distal to the terminal bronchioles accompanied by destruction of the alveolar walls and without obvious fibrosis. This loss of alveolar surface area reduces gas exchange and impairs elastic recoil; airway inflammation, gland hyperplasia, fibrosis, and muscle hypertrophy describe other conditions.'),
('7f64cbdd-98cc-54df-bc62-672ff181cbc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the types of emphysema?', '["Centriacinar (centrilobular)","and irregular","Only one type","panacinar (panlobular)","paraseptal (distal acinar)"]'::jsonb, 'Centriacinar (centrilobular)', 'Emphysema is classified into four types: centriacinar (centrilobular, the most common, seen in smokers), panacinar (panlobular, associated with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency), paraseptal (distal acinar), and irregular (scar-associated). The list is not a single type, and there are clearly more than one recognized pattern.'),
('73df42af-dda0-5bae-8366-78fa5f7061ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is centriacinar emphysema?', '["Enlargement of proximal respiratory bronchioles (central part of the acinus)","Enlargement of distal alveoli accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Irregular scarring mediated by specific receptor activation","sparing distal alveoli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enlargement of the entire acinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of proximal respiratory bronchioles (central part of the acinus)', 'Centriacinar (centrilobular) emphysema involves enlargement of the proximal respiratory bronchioles, the central part of the acinus, while the distal alveoli are characteristically spared; it is the most common pattern in smokers and affects mainly the upper lobes. Panacinar involvement, irregular scarring, and distal alveolar enlargement describe other patterns.'),
('f340c95f-b5f6-5b4c-9c57-50854621423f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is panacinar emphysema?', '["Centriacinar emphysema resulting in decreased functional activity","Paraseptal emphysema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Irregular emphysema mediated by specific receptor activation","associated with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enlargement of the entire acinus (from respiratory bronchiole to alveolar sac)"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of the entire acinus (from respiratory bronchiole to alveolar sac)', 'Panacinar (panlobular) emphysema produces uniform enlargement of the entire acinus from the respiratory bronchiole to the alveolar sac and is classically associated with alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, affecting predominantly the lower lobes. It differs from centriacinar, paraseptal, and irregular emphysema, which target only part of the acinus or are scar-related.'),
('953469c5-4281-5419-9d1b-455964a7c166'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of alpha-1 antitrypsin?', '["Kills bacteria resulting in decreased functional activity","protecting lung tissue from proteolytic damage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transports oxygen mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibits neutrophil elastase and other serine proteases","Produces surfactant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits neutrophil elastase and other serine proteases', 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin is a serine protease inhibitor whose main role is to inhibit neutrophil elastase and related proteases, thereby protecting lung connective tissue from proteolytic damage. Deficiency allows unopposed elastase activity and causes early panacinar emphysema; killing bacteria, transporting oxygen, and making surfactant are not its functions.'),
('46716c97-ef24-52ab-9076-fc02a1f4cc35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is asthma?', '["and eosinophilic inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","A chronic inflammatory airway disease characterized by reversible airflow obstruction","Irreversible airway obstruction mediated by specific receptor activation","Alveolar destruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","bronchial hyperresponsiveness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic inflammatory airway disease characterized by reversible airflow obstruction', 'Asthma is a chronic inflammatory airway disease, typically with eosinophilic inflammation and bronchial hyperresponsiveness, that produces reversible airflow obstruction and recurring episodes of wheeze, cough, and breathlessness. Because the obstruction reverses, asthma differs from COPD and from irreversible obstruction, alveolar destruction, and the fixed narrowing seen in other conditions.'),
('5531282a-6b12-5b2d-bf75-93ab41072a00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of asthma?', '["smooth muscle hypertrophy","basement membrane thickening","and mucus plugging","epithelial shedding","Eosinophilic inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'Eosinophilic inflammation', 'The histology of asthma features eosinophilic inflammation throughout the airway wall, together with smooth muscle hypertrophy, basement membrane thickening, goblet cell hyperplasia with mucus plugging, and epithelial shedding. Eosinophils are the diagnostic inflammatory hallmark, whereas the other listed changes are associated features rather than the main cellular finding.'),
('2190dd8c-3867-5f53-85c6-7221053f4410'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are Curschmann spirals?', '["Bacteria resulting in decreased functional activity","Coiled mucus plugs in asthmatic airways","Eosinophil crystals mediated by specific receptor activation","visible in sputum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fungal hyphae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coiled mucus plugs in asthmatic airways', 'Curschmann spirals are whorled coils of inspissated mucus shed from asthmatic airways and are seen in the sputum of asthma patients, where they accompany eosinophils and Charcot-Leyden crystals. They are mucus plugs, not bacteria, eosinophil crystals, or fungal hyphae.'),
('c5c8fea8-8ecf-5641-aeeb-336c92cc92dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are Charcot-Leyden crystals?', '["Cholesterol crystals resulting in decreased functional activity","Cystine crystals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uric acid crystals mediated by specific receptor activation","Crystals formed from eosinophil lysophospholipase (galectin-10) found in asthmatic sputum","Calcium oxalate crystals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crystals formed from eosinophil lysophospholipase (galectin-10) found in asthmatic sputum', 'Charcot-Leyden crystals are hexagonal bipyramidal crystals formed from eosinophil lysophospholipase (galectin-10) that appear in the sputum or tissues of patients with eosinophil-rich conditions such as asthma. They are distinct from cholesterol, cystine, uric acid, and calcium oxalate crystals found in other disorders.'),
('ca8ec094-6ded-5b65-b105-8a3f68f1bf83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is bronchiectasis?', '["Airway tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Airway fibrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Permanent dilation of bronchi and bronchioles due to destruction of the airway wall from chronic inflammation","Airway constriction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveolar dilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Permanent dilation of bronchi and bronchioles due to destruction of the airway wall from chronic inflammation', 'Bronchiectasis is the permanent, abnormal dilation of bronchi and bronchioles caused by destruction of the airway wall from chronic necrotizing inflammation, often following infection, obstruction, or ciliary dysfunction. The dilated airways are damaged and retain mucus, whereas tumor, fibrosis, constriction, and alveolar dilation describe other conditions.'),
('765b2e53-c9c8-53fe-81a8-5843bae0e45b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of bronchiectasis?', '["Normal epithelium","and squamous metaplasia; filled with mucus and pus","loss of cilia","Dilated airways with chronic inflammation","fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated airways with chronic inflammation', 'Bronchiectatic airways show permanent dilation with chronic inflammation, destruction of the normal epithelium, loss of cilia, squamous metaplasia, fibrosis, and lumina filled with mucus and pus. The dilation and chronic inflammation are the defining histologic changes; normal epithelium and loss of cilia alone do not describe the process.'),
('468d2163-4f2d-5085-b519-0692156fabc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the clinical presentation of bronchiectasis?', '["hemoptysis resulting in decreased functional activity","and recurrent pneumonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","clubbing mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic cough with copious purulent sputum","Dry cough leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic cough with copious purulent sputum', 'The classic presentation of bronchiectasis is a chronic cough with copious purulent sputum, often accompanied by hemoptysis, finger clubbing, and recurrent pneumonia due to impaired mucus clearance. A dry cough or unproductive pattern is atypical for this condition.'),
('dadce5a6-0708-506a-a017-2031cdeca46b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is Kartagener syndrome (primary ciliary dyskinesia)?', '["bronchiectasis resulting in decreased functional activity","A cystic fibrosis variant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and infertility mediated by specific receptor activation","A genetic disorder of ciliary motility causing chronic sinusitis","situs inversus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A genetic disorder of ciliary motility causing chronic sinusitis', 'Kartagener syndrome (primary ciliary dyskinesia) is a genetic disorder of ciliary motility causing chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis, and, in about half of patients, situs inversus; men also suffer infertility because sperm flagella are immotile. It is a motility defect of dynein, not a cystic fibrosis variant.'),
('49a10f11-cf80-5750-94c3-53614444d4dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of Kartagener syndrome?', '["Missing dynein arms in cilia on electron microscopy; chronic inflammation and bronchiectasis","Normal cilia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Short cilia mediated by specific receptor activation","Cilia with extra dynein arms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of cilia only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Missing dynein arms in cilia on electron microscopy; chronic inflammation and bronchiectasis', 'On electron microscopy, Kartagener syndrome shows cilia with missing (absent) dynein arms, which abolishes coordinated beating; the resulting mucus stasis produces chronic airway inflammation and bronchiectasis. Normal cilia, short cilia, extra dynein arms, and mere loss of cilia do not describe the characteristic ultrastructural defect.'),
('66185709-c4b6-5b10-8244-3b4c5b890a52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the diagnosis of primary ciliary dyskinesia?', '["Pulmonary function tests","Nasal nitric oxide (low)","Chest X-ray","and genetic testing","ciliary biopsy with EM showing structural defects"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal nitric oxide (low)', 'Primary ciliary dyskinesia is suggested by low nasal nitric oxide, which reflects impaired nitric oxide production of the airway epithelium and serves as a sensitive screening test; electron microscopy of a ciliary biopsy showing structural defects (for example missing dynein arms) and genetic testing confirm the diagnosis. The low nasal NO finding is the screening clue among the options.'),
('1977f55f-784b-5857-9cf6-080431c81227'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is pneumonia?', '["Airway inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","Alveolar collapse accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary edema mediated by specific receptor activation","Inflammation and consolidation of the lung parenchyma due to infection","Pleural inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation and consolidation of the lung parenchyma due to infection', 'Pneumonia is an infection-induced inflammation and consolidation of the lung parenchyma in which the alveoli fill with exudate, producing a firm, airless lobe. Airway inflammation, alveolar collapse, pulmonary edema, and pleural inflammation are different processes that do not define infection with parenchymal consolidation.'),
('f7d8d958-3fb1-579f-8901-5e176cbf5a0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the four stages of lobar pneumonia?', '["Acute","red hepatization","resolution","Congestion","gray hepatization"]'::jsonb, 'Congestion', 'Lobar pneumonia evolves through four stages: congestion (vascular engorgement, alveolar fluid, and few white cells), red hepatization (RBC extravasation with fibrin and neutrophils), gray hepatization (maximal fibrin and neutrophils as red cells break down), and resolution. Congestion is the first stage, and the list of four is congestion, red hepatization, gray hepatization, and resolution.'),
('64694f3a-d639-5358-8a3a-db77c007ffb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of red hepatization stage?', '["Congestion and edema with few WBCs","fibrin exudate","Dense neutrophil infiltration","and consolidation (liver-like consistency)","extravasation of RBCs"]'::jsonb, 'Dense neutrophil infiltration', 'Red hepatization is characterized by a dense intra-alveolar exudate with neutrophils and fibrin together with extravasated red blood cells, giving the consolidated lobe a firm, liver-like cut surface. Congestion and edema with few white cells describes the preceding stage, while extensive RBC extravasation and fibrin are accompaniments rather than the sole cellular hallmark.'),
('58971590-1a17-508b-8350-ef2854133e10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of gray hepatization stage?', '["Neutrophils disintegrate","and macrophages appear; the lung is gray and dry","RBC extravasation","Congestion and edema","fibrin-rich exudate"]'::jsonb, 'Neutrophils disintegrate', 'Gray hepatization is the third stage of lobar pneumonia in which the neutrophils that accumulated during red hepatization disintegrate and red cells lyse, giving the lung a gray, dry, firm cut surface. The earlier congestion and edema have now been replaced by a dense, fibrin-rich intra-alveolar exudate.'),
('c03ea9a2-ce4e-5d3f-b50a-dda60d60ec03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is bronchopneumonia?', '["often bilateral and basal","Patchy consolidation centered on bronchi and bronchioles","Interstitial inflammation","Involvement of an entire lobe","Granulomatous inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'Patchy consolidation centered on bronchi and bronchioles', 'Bronchopneumonia produces patchy areas of consolidation centered on bronchi and bronchioles, typically bilateral and basal. It does not consolidate an entire lobe (which is lobar pneumonia) or affect mainly the alveolar interstitium.'),
('39bd492a-5091-505b-9a26-f278f0fd01ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is interstitial pneumonia?', '["Inflammation of the interstitium (alveolar walls) rather than the air spaces","typical of viral and atypical pneumonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","Alveolar space consolidation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Airway inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the interstitium (alveolar walls) rather than the air spaces', 'Interstitial pneumonia is an inflammation of the alveolar septa (interstitium) rather than the air spaces themselves and is typical of viral and atypical pneumonia. It is distinguished from the alveolar-space consolidation seen in lobar and bronchopneumonia.'),
('825764cb-5467-5242-80bc-ffdccbb39023'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of community-acquired pneumonia?', '["Chlamydia pneumoniae","Haemophilus influenzae","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Legionella pneumophila","Streptococcus pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'Streptococcus pneumoniae', 'Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia across all age groups. Mycoplasma, Haemophilus, Chlamydia and Legionella are less common or are associated with specific presentations such as atypical pneumonia.'),
('4257a909-5e9a-5f14-ba28-8a33e83fca76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of atypical pneumonia?', '["Staphylococcus aureus","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Haemophilus influenzae","Streptococcus pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 'Mycoplasma pneumoniae', 'Mycoplasma pneumoniae is the leading cause of atypical walking pneumonia, especially in young adults. The other listed agents are more typical of classic bacterial or nosocomial pneumonia.'),
('6509323a-8b73-5ba2-a05f-10061fba89f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of Mycoplasma pneumonia?', '["and peribronchial tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Granulomas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Interstitial inflammation with lymphocytic infiltration of alveolar septa","Alveolar consolidation with neutrophils without affecting the primary tissue pathway","bronchiolar walls leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interstitial inflammation with lymphocytic infiltration of alveolar septa', 'Mycoplasma pneumonia characteristically shows interstitial inflammation with lymphocytic infiltration of the alveolar septa and peribronchial tissue rather than heavy neutrophil-rich alveolar consolidation. Granulomas and suppuration are not features.'),
('3ebed524-6ea0-57f5-aeaf-313bad3f242a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hospital-acquired pneumonia?', '["Staphylococcus aureus","Acinetobacter baumannii","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Escherichia coli"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa', 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the most common cause of hospital-acquired pneumonia, particularly in ventilated or debilitated patients. The other gram-positive and gram-negative organisms are less frequent or more community-acquired in origin.'),
('69e0e59e-cc8b-55b8-90ed-5b9fdab20464'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a lung abscess?', '["caused by microbial infection","A tumor cavity","A fluid-filled cyst","A localized necrotic cavity filled with pus","A bronchiectatic cavity"]'::jsonb, 'A localized necrotic cavity filled with pus', 'A lung abscess is a localized necrotic cavity filled with pus, usually produced by microbial infection and often following aspiration of anaerobic organisms. It is distinct from cystic, tumor, and bronchiectatic cavities.'),
('42541379-8464-52db-a917-50c0dc04ea90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is tuberculosis (TB)?', '["A chronic infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis","A fungal infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","characterized by granulomatous inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","A viral infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An acute bacterial pneumonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis', 'Tuberculosis is a chronic infectious disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, spread by inhalation of infectious droplets and characterized by granulomatous inflammation. It is neither viral, fungal, nor an acute pyogenic pneumonia.'),
('fe27c1cb-0584-5ff1-a2fa-f9c094a5369e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of a primary TB granuloma (Ghon focus)?', '["Non-caseating granuloma","Langhans giant cells","A caseating granuloma with epithelioid macrophages","and central caseous necrosis","Neutrophil abscess"]'::jsonb, 'A caseating granuloma with epithelioid macrophages', 'Primary TB granulomas (Ghon focus) are caseating granulomas composed of epithelioid macrophages, often with Langhans giant cells, surrounding a central area of caseous necrosis. Non-caseating granulomas and neutrophilic abscesses are not features of TB.'),
('095c913e-6a19-5d91-906c-50a40650f7b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a Ghon complex?', '["The Ghon focus (primary lung lesion) plus the involved hilar lymph node","Miliary TB accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The entire lung involvement mediated by specific receptor activation","Cavitary TB lesions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","seen in primary TB leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The Ghon focus (primary lung lesion) plus the involved hilar lymph node', 'A Ghon complex consists of the primary Ghon focus in the lung together with the involved hilar or mediastinal lymph node and is the hallmark of primary TB. Miliary and cavitary lesions reflect separate, more extensive forms of disease.'),
('d8201012-4885-585d-9cc5-41bb844e8a80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is secondary (reactivation) TB?', '["TB in a previously unexposed person","New infection with TB","Reactivation of a dormant primary infection","often in the upper lobes due to higher oxygen tension","Transmission from another person"]'::jsonb, 'Reactivation of a dormant primary infection', 'Secondary (reactivation) TB represents reactivation of a dormant primary infection, occurring most often in immunocompromised or elderly individuals, and characteristically involves the upper lobes where oxygen tension is higher. It is not a first exposure or exogenous reinfection.'),
('8d94ffb9-897d-51ff-9e1f-957034a22be8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is miliary TB?', '["Lymph node TB resulting in decreased functional activity","A single large cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meningeal TB mediated by specific receptor activation","Pleural TB without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Disseminated TB with many small (millet seed-sized) granulomas throughout the lungs and other organs"]'::jsonb, 'Disseminated TB with many small (millet seed-sized) granulomas throughout the lungs and other organs', 'Miliary TB is the disseminated form in which numerous small (millet seed-sized) granulomas spread through the lungs and other organs via the bloodstream. Single cavities, nodal, meningeal, or pleural disease describe localized patterns instead.'),
('4ef130d2-8715-52f9-af75-e1b706dd521c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the diagnosis of TB?', '["nucleic acid amplification test (NAAT)","Acid-fast bacillus (AFB) smear and culture","Chest X-ray only","Blood culture only","and interferon-gamma release assay (IGRA)"]'::jsonb, 'Acid-fast bacillus (AFB) smear and culture', 'The diagnosis of tuberculosis rests on acid-fast bacillus (AFB) smear and culture, with NAAT and IGRA used as additional or rapid tests. Chest X-ray alone cannot confirm the infection, and blood culture is of little use.'),
('9ad80570-9816-5c45-b75d-ae509071ad17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the Mantoux (PPD) test?', '["An intradermal injection of tuberculin purified protein derivative to test for TB exposure (delayed hypersensitivity)","A sputum test accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A blood test for TB mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A culture test without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A chest X-ray test leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An intradermal injection of tuberculin purified protein derivative to test for TB exposure (delayed hypersensitivity)', 'The Mantoux (PPD) test is an intradermal injection of tuberculin purified protein derivative that elicits a delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction in those previously exposed to M. tuberculosis, read at 48-72 hours. Sputum, blood, and radiographic tests measure different aspects of infection.'),
('3e54fc0b-1d46-5581-b4ff-5c300dc0bf69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the difference between PPD and IGRA?', '["IGRA is a skin test resulting in decreased functional activity","difference accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both are sputum tests mediated by specific receptor activation","PPD is a blood test without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IGRA (QuantiFERON) is a blood test that measures IFN-gamma release from T cells; PPD is a skin test"]'::jsonb, 'IGRA (QuantiFERON) is a blood test that measures IFN-gamma release from T cells; PPD is a skin test', 'The key distinction is that IGRA (QuantiFERON) is a blood test measuring IFN-gamma release from sensitized T cells, whereas PPD is an intradermal skin test. IGRA is unaffected by prior BCG vaccination, unlike the PPD skin test.'),
('66e6d571-9c77-519b-a0e6-cad2d80cdd94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common type of lung cancer?', '["Carcinoid tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Small cell carcinoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Large cell carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Squamous cell carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adenocarcinoma (now the most common in both men and women)"]'::jsonb, 'Adenocarcinoma (now the most common in both men and women)', 'Adenocarcinoma has become the most common type of lung cancer in both men and women and typically arises peripherally. Squamous and small cell cancers are smoking-related central tumors, and carcinoid is an uncommon well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumor.'),
('d2e6e4cb-75e9-5747-9a76-bdfef6a5563c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the two main categories of lung cancer?', '["Carcinoid and sarcoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Central and peripheral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Squamous and adenocarcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Small cell lung cancer (SCLC) and non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)","Upper and lower lobe cancers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small cell lung cancer (SCLC) and non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)', 'Lung cancers are classified into small cell lung cancer (SCLC) and non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), the latter including squamous, adenocarcinoma, and large cell types. This division guides treatment: SCLC is treated mainly with chemotherapy, while early NSCLC is resected.'),
('8305c845-513e-5373-9990-bec0da50226b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of squamous cell carcinoma?', '["Glandular structures","Small blue cells","intercellular bridges","Keratin pearls","and squamous differentiation; often with keratinization"]'::jsonb, 'Keratin pearls', 'Keratin pearls and intercellular bridges are the hallmarks of well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma, reflecting the desmosomes and cytoplasm keratinization that separate it from other tumor types. Glandular structures indicate adenocarcinoma, and small blue cells indicate small cell carcinoma.'),
('350ea641-1a12-51c0-8bec-8a0921f77aa3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common location of lung adenocarcinoma?', '["Lower lobe only resulting in decreased functional activity","Peripheral (arising from distal airways and alveoli)","Hilar mediated by specific receptor activation","Central without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upper lobe only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral (arising from distal airways and alveoli)', 'Adenocarcinoma most often arises peripherally from distal airway and alveolar epithelial cells, unlike squamous and small cell cancers which are central hilar tumors. Peripheral location and glandular differentiation are therefore its typical features.'),
('18deadd3-23fa-5dd3-a5b3-e40baddc8383'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is lepidic-predominant adenocarcinoma?', '["Squamous cell carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-invasive growth along alveolar septa (formerly BAC); best prognosis among adenocarcinomas","Invasive adenocarcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Large cell carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small cell carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-invasive growth along alveolar septa (formerly BAC); best prognosis among adenocarcinomas', 'Lepidic-predominant adenocarcinoma grows non-invasively along the pre-existing alveolar septa (formerly bronchioloalveolar carcinoma, BAC) and carries the best prognosis among the adenocarcinomas. It must be distinguished from frankly invasive adenocarcinoma, which has a worse outcome.'),
('0d7b7333-9c18-5a72-9c2a-08ef8691733a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC)?', '["A peripheral tumor","and early metastasis; strongly linked to smoking","rapid growth","A slow-growing tumor","A high-grade neuroendocrine carcinoma with small cells"]'::jsonb, 'A high-grade neuroendocrine carcinoma with small cells', 'Small cell lung carcinoma is a high-grade neuroendocrine carcinoma composed of small cells; it grows rapidly and metastasizes early and has a strong link to smoking. It is the most aggressive lung cancer and typically presents as a central tumor.'),
('eb40d4b5-286e-57a4-87a9-6c4ea9db4f49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of SCLC?', '["Small blue cells with scant cytoplasm","Keratin pearls","nuclear molding","and abundant necrosis; positive for neuroendocrine markers","salt-and-pepper chromatin"]'::jsonb, 'Small blue cells with scant cytoplasm', 'SCLC consists of small blue cells with scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding, salt-and-pepper chromatin, abundant necrosis, and positivity for neuroendocrine markers. Keratin pearls would instead suggest squamous cell carcinoma.'),
('3b88cf49-c3c3-5dad-b0ab-e844be2cf13e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma (LCNEC)?', '["A high-grade neuroendocrine carcinoma with large cells","SCLC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and aggressive behavior mediated by specific receptor activation","A typical carcinoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","neuroendocrine morphology leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A high-grade neuroendocrine carcinoma with large cells', 'Large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma (LCNEC) is a high-grade neuroendocrine carcinoma composed of large cells with neuroendocrine morphology and aggressive behavior, so its outcome resembles that of SCLC. It is distinguished from typical carcinoid and from low-grade neuroendocrine tumors.'),
('2e9976ba-f747-5133-83a2-ea3bd468ebf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is an atypical carcinoid tumor?', '["An intermediate-grade neuroendocrine tumor (2-10 mitoses/10 HPF or necrosis) with worse prognosis than typical","LCNEC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A low-grade tumor mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","SCLC without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A high-grade tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An intermediate-grade neuroendocrine tumor (2-10 mitoses/10 HPF or necrosis) with worse prognosis than typical', 'An atypical carcinoid is an intermediate-grade neuroendocrine tumor defined by 2-10 mitoses per 10 high-power fields or foci of necrosis, carrying a worse prognosis than a typical (low-grade) carcinoid but a better one than SCLC or LCNEC.'),
('8a4ca21c-9f79-51c9-9656-4114886b16c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of a hamartoma?', '["Monotonous epithelial cells","and fat/connective tissue","Lobules of hyaline cartilage","clefts lined by bronchial epithelium","Smooth muscle bundles"]'::jsonb, 'Lobules of hyaline cartilage', 'A pulmonary hamartoma is a benign lesion composed of lobules of hyaline cartilage admixed with cleft-like spaces lined by bronchial epithelium and fat or connective tissue; the cartilage lobules are its most characteristic component. It is the most common benign tumor of the lung.'),
('f3ebbc2c-0c1a-5ec9-b32a-6c35324020b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a Pancoast tumor?', '["causing Horner syndrome and arm pain resulting in decreased functional activity","A mediastinal tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A central lung tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A pleural tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lung cancer at the lung apex that invades the brachial plexus and sympathetic chain"]'::jsonb, 'A lung cancer at the lung apex that invades the brachial plexus and sympathetic chain', 'A Pancoast tumor is a lung cancer situated at the lung apex that invades the brachial plexus and sympathetic chain, producing arm pain and Horner syndrome. It is an apical tumor and not a mediastinal, central, or pleural lesion.'),
('7d84a105-33eb-5d92-9a13-941aeb95e9f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the paraneoplastic syndromes associated with SCLC?', '["Hypercalcemia","Gynecomastia","Cushing syndrome (ectopic ACTH)","and Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS)","SIADH (inappropriate ADH)"]'::jsonb, 'SIADH (inappropriate ADH)', 'SCLC frequently secretes ADH, causing SIADH with hyponatremia; it may also produce ectopic ACTH (Cushing syndrome) and Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome. Hypercalcemia from PTHrP is more typical of squamous cell carcinoma.'),
('13f9731c-2ec0-56a0-b662-85cd9db12229'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS)?', '["autonomic dysfunction resulting in decreased functional activity","and anti-VGCC antibodies; improves with activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dermatomyositis mediated by specific receptor activation","A paraneoplastic syndrome with proximal muscle weakness (legs)","Myasthenia gravis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A paraneoplastic syndrome with proximal muscle weakness (legs)', 'Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome is a paraneoplastic disorder causing proximal muscle weakness, particularly of the legs, from antibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels (VGCC); in contrast to myasthenia gravis, strength improves with repeated activity.'),
('5a59e3f3-2ea5-5c06-bbab-eeedbe90a6cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common paraneoplastic syndrome in NSCLC (especially squamous cell)?', '["LEMS resulting in decreased functional activity","SIADH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cushing syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypercalcemia due to PTHrP (parathyroid hormone-related protein) secretion","Clubbing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercalcemia due to PTHrP (parathyroid hormone-related protein) secretion', 'Squamous cell carcinoma is the NSCLC type most often causing hypercalcemia, through tumor secretion of PTHrP which mimics parathyroid hormone. LEMS, SIADH, and Cushing syndrome are instead paraneoplastic features of small cell carcinoma.'),
('de5abec8-76ec-5d6a-8631-0e7e2a3fc9bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is pulmonary embolism (PE)?', '["Pneumothorax resulting in decreased functional activity","Pleural effusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obstruction of the pulmonary artery or its branches by a thrombus (usually from DVT)","Pulmonary edema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Airway obstruction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstruction of the pulmonary artery or its branches by a thrombus (usually from DVT)', 'Pulmonary embolism is obstruction of the pulmonary artery or its branches by a thrombus, usually a deep vein thrombosis from the lower limbs. It produces ventilation-perfusion mismatch, pulmonary infarction, or sudden death depending on the size of the embolus.'),
('1bbe1b3d-8d35-5376-b72f-bd87ff3d885b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common source of pulmonary emboli?', '["Paradoxical emboli","Lower extremity deep veins (proximal DVT)","Pelvic veins","Right heart chambers","Upper extremity veins"]'::jsonb, 'Lower extremity deep veins (proximal DVT)', 'Most pulmonary emboli originate from the deep veins of the lower extremities, especially proximal (iliofemoral) deep vein thrombosis. Pelvic and upper extremity veins and the right heart chambers are far less common sources.'),
('2375fcde-d8bf-5b21-b2f4-1bb31413adaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of acute pulmonary embolism?', '["Septic emboli with bacteria resulting in decreased functional activity","Organized thrombus with recanalization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Air bubbles mediated by specific receptor activation","Fat droplets without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrin thrombus occluding a pulmonary artery; red (with RBCs) and white (platelets/fibrin) layers (lines of Zahn)"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrin thrombus occluding a pulmonary artery; red (with RBCs) and white (platelets/fibrin) layers (lines of Zahn)', 'Acute pulmonary embolism shows a fibrin thrombus occluding a pulmonary artery with alternating red (RBC-rich) and white (platelet/fibrin-rich) layers called lines of Zahn. Organized thrombi with recanalization indicate a chronic or resolving embolus.'),
('299ad0fe-b34c-5dda-a87a-dfcfad80f968'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a saddle embolus?', '["A recurrent embolus resulting in decreased functional activity","A chronic embolus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A small peripheral embolus mediated by specific receptor activation","A large PE lodged at the bifurcation of the main pulmonary artery","causing sudden death leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A large PE lodged at the bifurcation of the main pulmonary artery', 'A saddle embolus is a large pulmonary embolus that lodges at the bifurcation of the main pulmonary artery and can cause sudden death by acutely obstructing blood flow to both lungs. Smaller peripheral emboli rarely have such catastrophic consequences.'),
('5cc4db2c-d9d3-5cd2-8cd9-17541d4b5354'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is pulmonary hypertension?', '["Elevated right atrial pressure resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal pulmonary pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sustained elevation of mean pulmonary arterial pressure >25 mmHg at rest","Elevated left atrial pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elevated systemic pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sustained elevation of mean pulmonary arterial pressure >25 mmHg at rest', 'Pulmonary hypertension is defined as a mean pulmonary arterial pressure above 25 mmHg at rest measured at right heart catheterization. It describes elevated pressure in the pulmonary circulation rather than in the systemic circulation or right and left heart chambers.'),
('37364e20-cb2d-5166-951d-816c55b621e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is cor pulmonale?', '["Myocarditis resulting in decreased functional activity","Right ventricular hypertrophy and failure due to pulmonary hypertension from lung disease","Left heart failure mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericarditis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Congenital heart disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricular hypertrophy and failure due to pulmonary hypertension from lung disease', 'Cor pulmonale is right ventricular hypertrophy and eventual failure caused by pulmonary hypertension arising from lung disease such as COPD. It is a cardiac consequence of pulmonary pathology, not primary myocardial, pericardial, or congenital heart disease.'),
('95eabeff-424d-5cc9-84bb-d99f91afea24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of pulmonary hypertension?', '["and onion-skin fibrosis of pulmonary arterioles","Intimal thickening","plexiform lesions","Normal vessels","medial hypertrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Intimal thickening', 'The vascular remodeling of pulmonary hypertension includes medial hypertrophy, intimal thickening with fibrosis, plexiform lesions, and onion-skin fibrosis of pulmonary arterioles; intimal thickening is one of the defining changes. The most advanced form shows plexiform lesions.'),
('d45c8226-3fe6-594f-9d65-3c405dd16d5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension worldwide?', '["Idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","COPD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left heart disease (post-capillary pulmonary hypertension)","Chronic PE without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sleep apnea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left heart disease (post-capillary pulmonary hypertension)', 'Worldwide, left heart disease that raises pulmonary venous pressure is the most common cause of pulmonary hypertension (the post-capillary form). COPD, chronic PE, and sleep apnea cause pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension less commonly.'),
('60064954-1693-5614-a5b5-9149f7986cb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which of the following best defines acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)?', '["Pneumonia with localized consolidation","Acute inflammatory lung injury with non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema and hypoxemia","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation","Asthma with bronchospasm","Pulmonary embolism with ventilation-perfusion mismatch"]'::jsonb, 'Acute inflammatory lung injury with non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema and hypoxemia', 'ARDS is an acute inflammatory injury to the lung producing non-cardiogenic (increased-permeability) pulmonary edema and refractory hypoxemia, usually from sepsis, trauma, or aspiration. It is distinguished from pneumonia consolidation, pulmonary embolism, and obstructive airway disease.'),
('c69bc996-f292-58c2-969c-1d78a659912a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of ARDS?', '["Diffuse alveolar damage (DAD): hyaline membranes","interstitial inflammation","type II pneumocyte hyperplasia","and fibrosis","Neutrophil infiltration in air spaces"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse alveolar damage (DAD): hyaline membranes', 'ARDS histology is diffuse alveolar damage (DAD) with eosinophilic hyaline membranes, proliferating type II pneumocytes, interstitial inflammation, and later fibrosis. Neutrophil-rich alveolar exudates are more typical of bacterial pneumonia.'),
('4506b622-9204-5a02-8789-4c2c2b65a0cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) of the newborn?', '["Congenital heart disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Surfactant deficiency in premature infants causing alveolar collapse and respiratory failure","Meconium aspiration mediated by specific receptor activation","Diaphragmatic hernia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pneumonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surfactant deficiency in premature infants causing alveolar collapse and respiratory failure', 'Neonatal RDS results from surfactant deficiency in premature infants, which allows alveolar collapse (atelectasis) and respiratory failure. Exogenous surfactant and antenatal steroids reduce its severity; the other listed causes are separate neonatal conditions.'),
('c06c96f4-31b3-5fcb-9160-2d1d61639b03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of neonatal RDS?', '["Eosinophilic hyaline membranes lining the alveoli and alveolar ducts; atelectasis; and lack of surfactant","Hyaline membranes only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchopneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Alveolar hemorrhage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granulomas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Eosinophilic hyaline membranes lining the alveoli and alveolar ducts; atelectasis; and lack of surfactant', 'The hallmark of neonatal RDS is eosinophilic hyaline membranes lining the alveoli and alveolar ducts together with atelectasis and surfactant deficiency (low lecithin-to-sphingomyelin ratio). Bronchopneumonia and alveolar hemorrhage are not the defining histology.'),
('b76d2a9e-1fb4-5c41-82f2-03d8bb6b90b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is meconium aspiration syndrome?', '["Transient tachypnea of the newborn resulting in decreased functional activity","RDS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aspiration of meconium-stained amniotic fluid by the fetus during delivery","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","causing airway obstruction and inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aspiration of meconium-stained amniotic fluid by the fetus during delivery', 'Meconium aspiration syndrome is caused by the fetus inhaling meconium-stained amniotic fluid during delivery, which obstructs airways and incites chemical inflammation with air trapping. Transient tachypnea of the newborn and RDS are separate problems of the newborn.'),
('efea3b4e-50ed-5d55-a676-1c1b4044454f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is congenital pulmonary airway malformation (CPAM)?', '["A vascular malformation resulting in decreased functional activity","A pleural disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A type of lung cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","An inflammatory condition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A hamartomatous lung lesion with cysts communicating (or not) with the airway"]'::jsonb, 'A hamartomatous lung lesion with cysts communicating (or not) with the airway', 'CPAM is a hamartomatous lung lesion containing cysts that may or may not communicate with the airway, usually presenting in the neonate with respiratory distress or later with infection. It is neither inflammatory nor neoplastic and does not represent pleural or vascular disease.'),
('2a8f4043-1ed7-53fb-ab60-4618d730ffea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the respiratory tract?', '["Pulmonary sequestration resulting in decreased functional activity","Tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF) with esophageal atresia","CPAM mediated by specific receptor activation","Laryngeal cleft without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diaphragmatic hernia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF) with esophageal atresia', 'Tracheoesophageal fistula with esophageal atresia is the most common congenital anomaly affecting the respiratory tract, occurring in about 1 in 3500 births. The other listed airway and lung malformations are far less frequent.'),
('cda138e8-f069-55ab-bf85-646aaedf0904'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is pneumoconiosis?', '["Infection of the lung resulting in decreased functional activity","Allergic lung disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","leading to fibrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung disease caused by inhalation of inorganic dust particles","Lung cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung disease caused by inhalation of inorganic dust particles', 'Pneumoconiosis is parenchymal lung disease caused by inhalation of inorganic dust such as silica, coal, or asbestos, typically leading to fibrosis after years of occupational exposure. Infection, allergy, and neoplasia are separate mechanisms of lung injury.'),
('981be8da-38c2-506e-866b-8f6366e7fd69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is silicosis?', '["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Coal worker''s pneumoconiosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asbestos-related disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis from inhalation of crystalline silica (quartz)","causing nodular fibrotic lesions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary fibrosis from inhalation of crystalline silica (quartz)', 'Silicosis is pulmonary fibrosis caused by inhalation of crystalline silica (quartz) and characteristically produces nodular fibrotic lesions, especially in the upper lobes of sandblasters and miners. The other choices describe different dust-related or immune lung diseases.'),
('7e97f466-2355-5d9d-b6ad-a040b42d2327'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of silicosis?', '["Asbestos bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Macule formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Diffuse interstitial fibrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Concentric hyalinized collagen nodules (silicotic nodules) with birefringent silica particles under polarized light","Granulomas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Concentric hyalinized collagen nodules (silicotic nodules) with birefringent silica particles under polarized light', 'Silicosis histology shows concentric whorls of hyalinized collagen (silicotic nodules) admixed with birefringent silica particles that appear under polarized light, in a background of fibrosis and emphysema. Asbestos bodies and granulomas belong to other diseases.'),
('570c4072-e6b8-5f7f-8ee1-4b6da3435a14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is asbestosis?', '["Pleural plaques only resulting in decreased functional activity","Silicosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesothelioma mediated by specific receptor activation","Diffuse interstitial pulmonary fibrosis caused by inhalation of asbestos fibers","Lung cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse interstitial pulmonary fibrosis caused by inhalation of asbestos fibers', 'Asbestosis is diffuse interstitial pulmonary fibrosis caused by inhalation of asbestos fibers after a long latent period, and it predisposes to lung cancer and mesothelioma. Pleural plaques themselves are a marker of exposure rather than fibrosis.'),
('6f7cf22f-8f72-52c3-86be-5c3ed0958ddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of asbestosis?', '["Neutrophil infiltration resulting in decreased functional activity","Granulomas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cavitary lesions mediated by specific receptor activation","Diffuse interstitial fibrosis with ferruginous bodies (asbestos bodies) in the air spaces; pleural plaques","Nodular fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse interstitial fibrosis with ferruginous bodies (asbestos bodies) in the air spaces; pleural plaques', 'Asbestosis histology shows diffuse interstitial fibrosis and ferruginous (asbestos) bodies, which are beaded golden-brown fibers coated with iron and protein, in the air spaces; the pleura often shows hyaline plaques. Concentric silicotic nodules would instead indicate silicosis.'),
('580ff923-bb98-5577-9cc5-32ea9a1affe6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are pleural plaques?', '["characteristic of asbestos exposure resulting in decreased functional activity","Empyema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural effusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Well-circumscribed hyaline fibrous plaques on the parietal pleura","Mesothelioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Well-circumscribed hyaline fibrous plaques on the parietal pleura', 'Pleural plaques are well-circumscribed hyaline fibrous plaques on the parietal pleura and are a common marker of prior asbestos exposure. They are benign and non-neoplastic, unlike mesothelioma, and do not in themselves cause pleural effusion or empyema.'),
('243b66f2-4a96-5f00-ba44-7be38976ab85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is mesothelioma?', '["A benign pleural tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A subtype of lung cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A metastatic tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A malignant tumor of the pleura (or peritoneum) strongly associated with asbestos exposure","A pleural infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A malignant tumor of the pleura (or peritoneum) strongly associated with asbestos exposure', 'Mesothelioma is a malignant tumor arising from mesothelial cells of the pleura or peritoneum, with a strong causal link to asbestos exposure. It is not benign, is not a lung parenchymal cancer, and is not an infectious condition.'),
('e0b6ed3b-cc92-56be-b55c-4b26a8f75714'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of epithelioid mesothelioma?', '["Keratin pearls resulting in decreased functional activity","CK5/6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","D2-40 mediated by specific receptor activation","Tubulopapillary architecture with cuboidal cells and microvilli on EM; positive for calretinin","WT1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tubulopapillary architecture with cuboidal cells and microvilli on EM; positive for calretinin', 'Epithelioid mesothelioma shows a tubulopapillary growth of cuboidal cells with long slender microvilli on electron microscopy and is positive for calretinin. The other choices are immuno-stains or keratin-related features rather than the histologic architecture asked.'),
('67aa3a0c-fb32-5e85-8b5c-4adc31e293d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is coal worker''s pneumoconiosis (CWP)?', '["Silicosis","causing coal macules and nodules (simple CWP) or progressive massive fibrosis (PMF)","Lung disease from coal dust inhalation","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Asbestosis"]'::jsonb, 'Lung disease from coal dust inhalation', 'Coal worker''s pneumoconiosis is a lung disease caused by chronic inhalation of coal dust, typically in miners. Silicosis, asbestosis, and hypersensitivity pneumonitis arise from different exposures and mechanisms.'),
('66798b0b-4045-54ec-9387-479b6bf84fa9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of CWP?', '["Coal macules (dust-laden macrophages around respiratory bronchioles) with little fibrosis; can progress to PMF with large fibrotic masses","Hyaline plaques accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Granulomas mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Silicotic nodules without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diffuse interstitial fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coal macules (dust-laden macrophages around respiratory bronchioles) with little fibrosis; can progress to PMF with large fibrotic masses', 'CWP is characterized by coal macules, peribronchiolar collections of dust-laden macrophages with only mild fibrosis, and may progress to progressive massive fibrosis with large blackened fibrotic masses. Hyaline plaques, silicotic nodules, and granulomas belong to other diseases.'),
('f3512f00-e100-5750-8f98-35059db45fd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of hypersensitivity pneumonitis?', '["and organizing pneumonia (bronchiolitis obliterans organizing pneumonia)","Lymphocytic interstitial inflammation","Neutrophil infiltration","non-caseating granulomas","Eosinophil infiltration"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocytic interstitial inflammation', 'Hypersensitivity pneumonitis shows chronic lymphocytic interstitial inflammation around bronchioles and alveoli, often with poorly formed non-caseating granulomas. It is a cell-mediated immunologic reaction, so neutrophilic or eosinophilic infiltrates are not typical.'),
('bb924458-6e3d-5602-ab74-8298b6dbb4cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the causes of hypersensitivity pneumonitis?', '["Farmer''s lung (thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay)","Only bird fancier''s lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only farmer''s lung mediated by specific receptor activation","bird fancier''s lung (avian proteins) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and many others leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Farmer''s lung (thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay)', 'Farmer''s lung results from inhaling thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay and is the prototypical cause of hypersensitivity pneumonitis. Bird fancier''s lung, from avian proteins, is an equally important cause, so the condition is not limited to a single exposure.'),
('8e195afa-0f87-5c0e-a4b2-9327d8634bf9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is eosinophilic pneumonia?', '["Pulmonary infiltration with eosinophils; can be acute (Loeffler syndrome) or chronic","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Asthma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary infiltration with eosinophils; can be acute (Loeffler syndrome) or chronic', 'Eosinophilic pneumonia is defined by pulmonary infiltration with eosinophils and occurs in acute (Loeffler syndrome) and chronic forms. Asthma and allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis may have eosinophilia but are distinct clinical entities.'),
('8418928a-fa85-598d-9b62-bbfca7dcf851'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is acute eosinophilic pneumonia?', '["eosinophilia in BAL resulting in decreased functional activity","and rapid response to steroids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Acute febrile illness with diffuse pulmonary infiltrates","Loeffler syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute febrile illness with diffuse pulmonary infiltrates', 'Acute eosinophilic pneumonia presents as an acute febrile illness with diffuse pulmonary infiltrates, often accompanied by BAL eosinophilia and rapid improvement with steroids. Loeffler syndrome and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia follow different, less acute courses.'),
('806c818e-8ebf-539e-86f4-20d1cdbe1939'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is chronic eosinophilic pneumonia?', '["Loeffler syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute eosinophilic pneumonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subacute illness with peripheral pulmonary infiltrates (photographic negative of pulmonary edema)","responding to steroids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Churg-Strauss syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subacute illness with peripheral pulmonary infiltrates (photographic negative of pulmonary edema)', 'Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia is an indolent, subacute illness marked by peripheral pulmonary infiltrates that reverse the normal gravity-dependent pattern (photographic negative of pulmonary edema). It responds well to steroids, unlike Churg-Strauss syndrome, which is a systemic vasculitis.'),
('f3c1d019-ee4e-5d43-a869-bbe19e9509c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis (PLCH)?', '["A smoking-related lung disease with Langerhans cell infiltration forming stellate nodules and cysts in the upper lobes","Interstitial lung disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcoidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Langerhans cell sarcoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eosinophilic granuloma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A smoking-related lung disease with Langerhans cell infiltration forming stellate nodules and cysts in the upper lobes', 'PLCH is a smoking-related interstitial lung disease in which Langerhans cells accumulate and form stellate nodules with cyst formation, predominantly in the upper lobes. It is distinct from sarcoidosis and from malignant Langerhans cell sarcoma.'),
('b59f673a-38e3-5170-a9f3-5158df6c13fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF)?', '["fibrotic interstitial lung disease of unknown cause with usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern","Sarcoidosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis mediated by specific receptor activation","An autoimmune disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A progressive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'fibrotic interstitial lung disease of unknown cause with usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern', 'IPF is a progressive fibrotic interstitial lung disease of unknown cause, defined pathologically by the usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern. It is neither sarcoidosis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, nor a recognized autoimmune disease.'),
('86da3aad-e04b-547e-bcfa-f9c0d5597c73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of UIP (usual interstitial pneumonia)?', '["and temporal heterogeneity resulting in decreased functional activity","Uniform fibrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heterogeneous fibrosis with honeycomb changes","fibroblastic foci without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granulomas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heterogeneous fibrosis with honeycomb changes', 'UIP shows heterogeneous fibrosis with honeycomb changes and temporal heterogeneity, with patchy fibrosis of varying ages in the same biopsy. Fibroblastic foci reflect active disease, whereas granulomas argue strongly against UIP.'),
('02937f05-6f67-56aa-98b4-b7ee198432c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are fibroblastic foci in UIP?', '["Subepithelial aggregates of proliferating fibroblasts and myofibroblasts","indicating active disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mature scar tissue mediated by specific receptor activation","Granulation tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammatory infiltrates leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subepithelial aggregates of proliferating fibroblasts and myofibroblasts', 'Fibroblastic foci are subepithelial aggregates of proliferating fibroblasts and myofibroblasts that indicate ongoing, active disease in UIP. They are a hallmark of progression and are not mature scar, ordinary granulation tissue, or nonspecific inflammation.'),
('3f7fd1b8-55c4-5de8-af8c-2b20ddccf2b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the imaging pattern of IPF on HRCT?', '["Ground-glass opacities resulting in decreased functional activity","and traction bronchiectasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","predominantly subpleural and basal mediated by specific receptor activation","honeycombing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Usual interstitial pneumonia pattern: reticular opacities"]'::jsonb, 'Usual interstitial pneumonia pattern: reticular opacities', 'IPF on HRCT shows the UIP pattern with reticular opacities, honeycombing, and traction bronchiectasis distributed predominantly in subpleural and basal regions. A predominantly ground-glass or upper-lobe picture points away from IPF.'),
('1d3e68ff-54a9-539a-b703-a333a89f5114'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP)?', '["A type of interstitial pneumonia with organizing pneumonia (Masson bodies) in the distal airspaces","IPF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Eosinophilic pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","responding to steroids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A type of interstitial pneumonia with organizing pneumonia (Masson bodies) in the distal airspaces', 'Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia is an interstitial pneumonia defined by plugs of organizing pneumonia tissue, known as Masson bodies, filling the distal airspaces. It characteristically responds to corticosteroids, unlike IPF and most fibrosing interstitial pneumonias.'),
('8b6b9de0-afd9-5749-a2ba-87eb96ba0443'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of organizing pneumonia?', '["alveoli resulting in decreased functional activity","and bronchioles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intraluminal plugs of granulation tissue (Masson bodies) in the alveolar ducts","Hyaline membranes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibroblastic foci leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intraluminal plugs of granulation tissue (Masson bodies) in the alveolar ducts', 'Organizing pneumonia is characterized by intraluminal plugs of granulation tissue called Masson bodies that extend into alveolar ducts, alveoli, and bronchioles. Hyaline membranes are a feature of diffuse alveolar damage, not organizing pneumonia.'),
('9013ae78-9176-55df-b9bc-f7681b4d2996'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is respiratory bronchiolitis-associated interstitial lung disease (RB-ILD)?', '["Smoking-related interstitial lung disease with accumulation of brown macrophages in respiratory bronchioles","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PLCH mediated by specific receptor activation","Desquamative interstitial pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IPF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smoking-related interstitial lung disease with accumulation of brown macrophages in respiratory bronchioles', 'RB-ILD is a smoking-related interstitial lung disease in which brown, pigmented macrophages accumulate in and around the respiratory bronchioles. It differs from DIP, where the pigmented macrophages involve the alveolar spaces more diffusely.'),
('9d4e2333-3b81-5910-bddc-b39b74cec8e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP)?', '["RB-ILD resulting in decreased functional activity","Smoking-related ILD with diffuse accumulation of pigmented macrophages in the alveolar spaces","UIP mediated by specific receptor activation","COP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IPF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smoking-related ILD with diffuse accumulation of pigmented macrophages in the alveolar spaces', 'Desquamative interstitial pneumonia is a smoking-related ILD with diffuse accumulation of pigmented macrophages in the alveolar spaces. It is distinguished from RB-ILD, whose macrophage accumulation is centered on the respiratory bronchioles.'),
('a9ff9f82-f42f-5ecc-93b6-0221457422f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia (LIP)?', '["Sarcoidosis resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","COP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A rare ILD with diffuse lymphocytic infiltration of the interstitium; associated with Sjogren syndrome and HIV","Lymphoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A rare ILD with diffuse lymphocytic infiltration of the interstitium; associated with Sjogren syndrome and HIV', 'Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia is a rare ILD defined by a diffuse lymphocytic infiltrate of the interstitium and is associated with Sjogren syndrome, HIV, and other immune conditions. It must be separated from lymphoma, which shows clonal or destructive features.'),
('a42819d3-11fd-584e-af1e-ec674c2eb118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common idiopathic interstitial pneumonia?', '["COP","RB-ILD","DIP","IPF (UIP pattern)","NSIP"]'::jsonb, 'IPF (UIP pattern)', 'IPF, corresponding to the usual interstitial pneumonia pattern, is the most common idiopathic interstitial pneumonia. NSIP, DIP, RB-ILD, and COP are all encountered less frequently.'),
('b4e96845-7bbf-5416-9036-a0710667095c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is FEV1?', '["Vital capacity resulting in decreased functional activity","Residual volume accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Forced expiratory volume in 1 second (volume of air exhaled in the first second of forced expiration)","Total lung capacity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peak expiratory flow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Forced expiratory volume in 1 second (volume of air exhaled in the first second of forced expiration)', 'FEV1 is the forced expiratory volume in 1 second, the volume of air exhaled during the first second of a forced expiration. When expressed as the FEV1/FVC ratio it separates obstructive from restrictive physiology.'),
('842ec46e-9665-5f46-904e-d30b7a17fe75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the normal FEV1/FVC ratio?', '[">1.0",">0.7 (70%)","<0.7","1.0 (alternative wording)","0.5"]'::jsonb, '>0.7 (70%)', 'The normal FEV1/FVC ratio is greater than 0.7 (70%). A ratio below 0.7 indicates airflow obstruction, whereas restrictive disease preserves or increases the ratio.'),
('ef0453fc-4d57-5fc4-9d8b-611b0c7459fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is total lung capacity (TLC)?', '["The amount of air per minute resulting in decreased functional activity","Volume after a normal exhalation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Volume after forced exhalation mediated by specific receptor activation","The volume of air in the lungs after a maximal inspiration (about 6 L)","The amount of air moved per breath leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The volume of air in the lungs after a maximal inspiration (about 6 L)', 'Total lung capacity is the volume of air in the lungs after a maximal inspiration, roughly 6 L. It falls in restrictive disease and rises in severe obstructive disease such as emphysema.'),
('3468db23-b796-536a-be1e-4e0ccbc78a7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is residual volume (RV)?', '["The volume after normal exhalation resulting in decreased functional activity","The total lung capacity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The volume of forced exhalation mediated by specific receptor activation","The volume of air remaining in the lungs after a maximal exhalation","The volume of a normal breath leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The volume of air remaining in the lungs after a maximal exhalation', 'Residual volume is the air that remains in the lungs after a maximal exhalation, because elastic recoil cannot empty the lungs completely. It cannot be measured by simple spirometry and increases in obstructive disease.'),
('5f4ef821-8d06-5576-9d24-64b73bed82b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the DLCO (diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide)?', '["A measure of lung volumes resulting in decreased functional activity","A measure of gas transfer across the alveolar-capillary membrane; reduced in emphysema and ILD","A measure of blood flow mediated by specific receptor activation","A measure of ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A measure of airway resistance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A measure of gas transfer across the alveolar-capillary membrane; reduced in emphysema and ILD', 'DLCO measures gas transfer across the alveolar-capillary membrane using carbon monoxide as a tracer. It is reduced in emphysema and interstitial lung disease but is typically preserved with pure airway obstruction.'),
('4852b620-6b8e-5466-b847-1cb4380ddaa9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the flow-volume loop?', '["A measure of ventilation resulting in decreased functional activity","A graphic representation of airflow versus lung volume during forced expiration and inspiration","A measure of airway resistance mediated by specific receptor activation","A measure of oxygen saturation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A measure of lung compliance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A graphic representation of airflow versus lung volume during forced expiration and inspiration', 'The flow-volume loop is a graphic plot of airflow against lung volume during forced expiration and inspiration. Its shape helps distinguish obstructive from restrictive disorders but does not directly measure compliance, resistance, or oxygen saturation.'),
('6ca1eb86-fcc7-5a92-82df-a83571cfd908'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the definition of asthma on PFTs?', '["Fixed obstruction resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased DLCO accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reversible airflow obstruction: FEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 improves by >12% and 200 mL after bronchodilator","Normal PFTs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Restriction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reversible airflow obstruction: FEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 improves by >12% and 200 mL after bronchodilator', 'Asthma is defined on PFTs by reversible airflow obstruction, with FEV1/FVC below 0.7 and FEV1 improving by more than 12% and 200 mL after bronchodilator use. Fixed obstruction, restriction, or a reduced DLCO point toward other conditions.'),
('12ca21a6-c503-5216-b898-ae464fbf7225'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the definition of COPD on PFTs?', '["not fully reversible","FEV1/FVC >0.7","FEV1 >80% predicted","Normal DLCO","Post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.7"]'::jsonb, 'Post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.7', 'The diagnostic criterion for COPD on PFTs is a post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC ratio below 0.7 that is not fully reversible. A ratio above 0.7 with preserved DLCO argues against COPD.'),
('68e07153-df20-54b5-b6dd-a6542c49e61e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is restrictive lung disease on PFTs?', '["FEV1/FVC <0.7 resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased FEV1/FVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal TLC mediated by specific receptor activation","TLC <80% predicted with normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio","Increased TLC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TLC <80% predicted with normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio', 'Restrictive lung disease is defined by a TLC below 80% predicted, with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio. Obstructive disease instead shows a reduced FEV1/FVC and increased lung volumes.'),
('b37d4877-f773-5585-91e6-f5853e0f3232'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the typical ABG findings in respiratory failure?', '["Type 1: PaO2 <60 mmHg","PaCO2 >50 mmHg","PaO2 >80 mmHg","PaCO2 normal/low; Type 2: PaO2 <60 mmHg","PaCO2 <35 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 'Type 1: PaO2 <60 mmHg', 'Type 1 respiratory failure is defined by a PaO2 below 60 mmHg with a normal or low PaCO2, typically from V/Q mismatch or shunt. Type 2 failure additionally shows a PaCO2 above 50 mmHg, indicating hypoventilation.'),
('f3881865-5d44-505c-8c0e-03c0d8473561'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the pulmonary artery pressure?', '["120/80 mmHg","10/5 mmHg","25/8 mmHg (mean 15 mmHg)","50/20 mmHg","30/15 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '25/8 mmHg (mean 15 mmHg)', 'Normal pulmonary artery pressure is approximately 25/8 mmHg, with a mean near 15 mmHg, far lower than the systemic circuit. Pressures around 50/20 mmHg or higher indicate pulmonary hypertension.'),
('93040cba-d460-55f1-aacb-909957860d11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the diameter of a pulmonary capillary?', '["1 micron resulting in decreased functional activity","3 microns accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","20 microns mediated by specific receptor activation","15 microns without affecting the primary tissue pathway","About 7-10 microns (just large enough for an RBC to pass)"]'::jsonb, 'About 7-10 microns (just large enough for an RBC to pass)', 'Pulmonary capillaries are about 7-10 microns in diameter, just large enough for a red blood cell to traverse in single file. This small diameter keeps the blood-gas diffusion barrier extremely thin.'),
('7c06fcf2-8967-5964-a648-d3dabff6b499'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of the bronchial veins?', '["Drain oxygenated blood resulting in decreased functional activity","Drain deoxygenated blood from the bronchi into the azygos system","Carry blood to the lung mediated by specific receptor activation","Form part of the portal system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Drain the pleura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drain deoxygenated blood from the bronchi into the azygos system', 'Bronchial veins drain deoxygenated blood from the bronchi and hilar structures into the azygos and hemiazygos venous system. This drainage accounts for part of the small physiologic shunt that bypasses the pulmonary capillary bed.'),
('021ee93c-c49e-5a11-a5c1-9f00ccd23143'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the normal anatomic shunt in the lung?', '["50% shunt resulting in decreased functional activity","shunt accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","20% shunt mediated by specific receptor activation","About 2-3% of cardiac output; bronchial and thebesian veins drain into the left side","10% shunt leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 2-3% of cardiac output; bronchial and thebesian veins drain into the left side', 'The normal anatomic shunt is about 2-3% of cardiac output, because bronchial and thebesian veins carry a small volume of deoxygenated blood directly into the left heart. Larger shunt fractions indicate a pathologic right-to-left shunt.'),
('fc2044dc-40d5-585e-960e-c451af8d99fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the A-a gradient?', '["The difference between O2 and CO2 resulting in decreased functional activity","The difference between venous and arterial O2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The difference between alveolar (PAO2) and arterial (PaO2) oxygen partial pressure; normally 5-10 mmHg","The difference between pH and PaCO2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The difference between inspired and expired O2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The difference between alveolar (PAO2) and arterial (PaO2) oxygen partial pressure; normally 5-10 mmHg', 'The A-a gradient is the difference between alveolar (PAO2) and arterial (PaO2) oxygen partial pressures, normally 5-10 mmHg on room air. It widens when oxygen transfer is impaired by V/Q mismatch, shunt, or diffusion limitation.'),
('79b3be62-17cf-5219-ab55-b75b0b91633f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the formula for alveolar gas equation?', '["PAO2 = PIO2 + PaCO2","PAO2 = 150 - PaCO2","PAO2 = PIO2 - (PaCO2 / R) (where R = 0.8)","PAO2 = PIO2 × PaCO2 (alternative wording)","PAO2 = PaCO2 × 0.8"]'::jsonb, 'PAO2 = PIO2 - (PaCO2 / R) (where R = 0.8)', 'The alveolar gas equation is PAO2 = PIO2 - (PaCO2 / R), with the respiratory quotient R approximately 0.8. It estimates alveolar oxygen tension so the A-a gradient can be calculated.'),
('edceeb12-446b-531e-9b08-9c8b7ba4e7a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the normal A-a gradient on room air?', '["0 mmHg",">20 mmHg","increasing with age",">50 mmHg","5-10 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '5-10 mmHg', 'On room air the normal A-a gradient is 5-10 mmHg, though it tends to increase slightly with age. A clearly widened gradient indicates oxygen-transfer failure such as shunt or V/Q mismatch rather than pure hypoventilation.'),
('20e00b0e-706b-5916-b9c4-bdeae7929bb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the response to 100% oxygen in a shunt vs. V/Q mismatch?', '["Shunt: PaO2 improves minimally; V/Q mismatch: PaO2 improves significantly","V/Q mismatch does not improve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both improve equally mediated by specific receptor activation","Shunt improves more than V/Q mismatch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neither improves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shunt: PaO2 improves minimally; V/Q mismatch: PaO2 improves significantly', 'Breathing 100% oxygen corrects V/Q mismatch, raising PaO2 substantially, whereas a true shunt bypasses ventilated alveoli so PaO2 improves only minimally. This differential response is used to separate the two causes of hypoxemia.'),
('f9c0901d-1730-5f60-92c9-2823763198aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the defense mechanisms of the respiratory tract?', '["alveolar macrophages","and antimicrobial peptides","cough","secretory IgA","Mucociliary clearance"]'::jsonb, 'Mucociliary clearance', 'Mucociliary clearance is the primary defense that traps and removes inhaled particles and organisms from the conducting airways. It works together with cough, alveolar macrophages, and secretory IgA as complementary defenses.'),
('0af24df6-d769-55bc-b7cc-732d22c41c04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the role of cough in lung defense?', '["Removes particles from alveoli","particles","Explosive expulsion of air to remove mucus","and pathogens from larger airways","Warms inspired air"]'::jsonb, 'Explosive expulsion of air to remove mucus', 'Cough is an explosive expulsion of air that clears mucus, particles, and pathogens from the larger airways. It complements mucociliary clearance but is not effective at defending the alveoli or warming inspired air.'),
('5bec1638-6992-5b24-85ed-e1afdddc1416'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the role of the epithelial barrier in the lung?', '["Only in the nose resulting in decreased functional activity","Only in the alveoli accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tight junctions between airway epithelial cells prevent pathogen invasion and regulate ion and water transport","Only in the bronchi without affecting the primary tissue pathway","role leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between airway epithelial cells prevent pathogen invasion and regulate ion and water transport', 'The airway epithelial barrier, formed by tight junctions between epithelial cells, prevents pathogen invasion and regulates ion and water transport. This barrier function spans the conducting airways, not only a single region.'),
('434d1b20-d9ed-597d-b9d1-ae94f17d5df6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are defensins produced by airway epithelial cells?', '["Cytokines resulting in decreased functional activity","Antimicrobial peptides that kill bacteria and other microbes","Surfactant proteins mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proteases leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antimicrobial peptides that kill bacteria and other microbes', 'Defensins are antimicrobial peptides produced by airway epithelial cells that kill bacteria and other microbes by disrupting their membranes. They are part of innate mucosal defense, distinct from surfactant proteins and mucins.'),
('ef6a2cac-9234-58f4-801e-439bc197a723'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the role of secretory IgA in the lung?', '["Opsonization","Neutralizes pathogens in the airway lumen","preventing adhesion and infection","Cytotoxicity","Complement activation"]'::jsonb, 'Neutralizes pathogens in the airway lumen', 'Secretory IgA neutralizes pathogens in the airway lumen, preventing their adhesion to and infection of the mucosal surface. It acts through mucosal immune mechanisms rather than complement activation, opsonization, or cytotoxicity.'),
('61c2f272-88b7-573b-95c9-8e5a27f4d9ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of pulmonary surfactant in immunity?', '["Only for alveolar stability resulting in decreased functional activity","Only for gas exchange accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only reduce surface tension mediated by specific receptor activation","enhancing phagocytosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SP-A and SP-D are collectins that opsonize and agglutinate microbes"]'::jsonb, 'SP-A and SP-D are collectins that opsonize and agglutinate microbes', 'SP-A and SP-D are collectins within pulmonary surfactant that opsonize and agglutinate microbes and enhance their phagocytosis. In addition to stabilizing alveoli by reducing surface tension, they contribute to innate immune defense.'),
('a54e500b-308d-57b0-9e86-635e0a646ee4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the role of neutrophils in lung defense?', '["Kill viruses resulting in decreased functional activity","Present antigens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","release neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) mediated by specific receptor activation","Produce antibodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","First responders to bacterial infection; phagocytose and kill organisms"]'::jsonb, 'First responders to bacterial infection; phagocytose and kill organisms', 'Neutrophils are the first responders to bacterial infection in the lung, phagocytosing and killing organisms. They also release neutrophil extracellular traps and microbicidal granule contents, whereas antigen presentation and antibody production belong to other cell types.'),
('ca4e48ec-32c2-53a9-94b2-b7d2c5f2e558'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the role of the inflammasome in the lung?', '["particulates","and irritants through IL-1beta and IL-18 activation","Only in systemic immunity","Initiates inflammatory responses to pathogens","role"]'::jsonb, 'Initiates inflammatory responses to pathogens', 'The inflammasome is a multiprotein complex that initiates inflammatory responses to pathogens and particulates by activating IL-1beta and IL-18. It links innate microbial sensing to the acute inflammatory reaction and operates locally as well as systemically.'),
('e766f5db-c9e6-5389-962c-dd1b670bd27a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the role of regulatory T cells (Tregs) in the lung?', '["Promote inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","Activate eosinophils accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Suppress excessive inflammation and maintain immune homeostasis in the respiratory tract","Kill pathogens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produce antibodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Suppress excessive inflammation and maintain immune homeostasis in the respiratory tract', 'Regulatory T cells suppress excessive inflammation and maintain immune homeostasis in the respiratory tract. Loss or dysfunction of Treg function can promote chronic allergic airway inflammation.'),
('51397f66-a396-5fba-8c4e-0a8a187c8e38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the role of dendritic cells in the lung?', '["Release histamine resulting in decreased functional activity","Capture inhaled antigens and migrate to draining lymph nodes to initiate T cell responses","Produce antibodies mediated by specific receptor activation","Phagocytose and kill pathogens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Constrict airways leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Capture inhaled antigens and migrate to draining lymph nodes to initiate T cell responses', 'Dendritic cells capture inhaled antigens and migrate to draining lymph nodes, where they present them to naive T cells and initiate adaptive immune responses. They thus serve as the principal bridge between innate and adaptive immunity in the lung.'),
('b8fa253d-c7b8-55a0-b012-cc0bee4f7c6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the PD-L1 test used for in lung cancer?', '["Predicts response to immune checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab) in NSCLC","Detecting ALK rearrangements accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Staging lung cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Diagnosing lung cancer type without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Detecting EGFR mutations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Predicts response to immune checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab) in NSCLC', 'PD-L1 testing in non-small cell lung cancer predicts response to immune checkpoint inhibitors such as pembrolizumab. It differs from tests for EGFR mutations or ALK rearrangements, which select targeted therapies against specific driver oncogenes.'),
('65b2bcef-be4c-5c64-af87-5fc4cfbd4214'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common mutation in squamous cell carcinoma of the lung?', '["KRAS mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","EGFR mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ALK rearrangement mediated by specific receptor activation","ROS1 rearrangement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TP53 mutation (nearly all cases) and PIK3CA mutations"]'::jsonb, 'TP53 mutation (nearly all cases) and PIK3CA mutations', 'Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung is strongly smoking-associated and almost always harbors TP53 mutations, with PIK3CA mutations a frequent coexisting alteration. Driver point mutations such as EGFR and KRAS and rearrangements of ALK or ROS1 are far more typical of adenocarcinoma than of squamous histology.'),
('ba338bd9-0f60-5acc-8e07-3de259922312'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the characteristic molecular features of SCLC?', '["PD-L1 amplification resulting in decreased functional activity","KRAS mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","EGFR mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","ALK rearrangement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nearly universal loss of RB1 and TP53; high mutation burden; neuroendocrine gene expression"]'::jsonb, 'Nearly universal loss of RB1 and TP53; high mutation burden; neuroendocrine gene expression', 'SCLC shows near-universal inactivation of both RB1 and TP53 together with a high mutation burden and neuroendocrine gene expression. Targetable alterations like EGFR, KRAS, ALK, and PD-L1 amplification do not define small cell carcinoma.'),
('52fb9d2f-cbc6-5d36-a4f4-bc87fe20e125'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is ROS1 rearrangement in lung cancer?', '["A KRAS mutation","An EGFR mutation","especially young -smokers; responds to crizotinib and entrectinib","A fusion gene in 1-2% of NSCLC","An ALK rearrangement"]'::jsonb, 'A fusion gene in 1-2% of NSCLC', 'ROS1 rearrangement is a gene fusion found in about 1-2% of non-small cell lung cancers, often in younger never or light smokers, and responds to crizotinib and entrectinib. It is a fusion event, not a point mutation like KRAS or EGFR, and it is distinct from ALK rearrangement.'),
('d72ee9dc-282b-5193-8463-9b605e8af37e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is cystic fibrosis (CF)?', '["and other organs","An acquired lung disease","An autosomal recessive disorder due to CFTR gene mutation","pancreas","causing defective chloride transport and thick secretions in the lungs"]'::jsonb, 'An autosomal recessive disorder due to CFTR gene mutation', 'Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in the CFTR gene, producing defective chloride transport and thick secretions in the lungs, pancreas, and other organs. It is inherited, not acquired, so it cannot be labeled an acquired lung disease.'),
('87877306-744e-54aa-b69b-ad41e2751383'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common CFTR mutation?', '["N1303K resulting in decreased functional activity","R117H accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","F508del (deletion of phenylalanine at position 508)","W1282X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","G551D leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'F508del (deletion of phenylalanine at position 508)', 'F508del, deletion of phenylalanine at position 508, accounts for roughly 70% of CFTR alleles worldwide and is by far the most common mutation. G551D, R117H, N1303K, and W1282X are all comparatively rare alleles.'),
('4443bdf9-3155-542d-9eac-fb173062565d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of CFTR?', '["A water channel resulting in decreased functional activity","A chloride channel on the apical surface of epithelial cells; regulates ion and water transport","A sodium channel mediated by specific receptor activation","A bicarbonate channel without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A potassium channel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chloride channel on the apical surface of epithelial cells; regulates ion and water transport', 'CFTR is a cAMP-regulated chloride channel on the apical surface of epithelial cells that controls ion and water movement across the membrane. It is not a water, sodium, potassium, or purely bicarbonate channel.'),
('ea0223e8-0dcf-555e-91f2-57a475e735d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the pathophysiology of CF lung disease?', '["and chronic infection","Defective CFTR causes reduced chloride secretion","dehydration of airway surface liquid","increased sodium absorption","impaired mucociliary clearance"]'::jsonb, 'Defective CFTR causes reduced chloride secretion', 'Defective CFTR reduces chloride secretion, which increases sodium absorption and dehydrates the airway surface liquid, impairing mucociliary clearance and setting up chronic infection. This cascade, not chloride secretion alone or any single end-stage change, explains the lung disease.'),
('712c609e-2fd9-5d7a-91d1-65906088f774'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the most common respiratory pathogens in CF?', '["Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA)","Haemophilus influenzae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mycobacterium tuberculosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Aspergillus fumigatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Streptococcus pneumoniae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA)', 'CF airways are first colonized by Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA) and later by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, the pathogen linked to declining lung function. Haemophilus influenzae appears early, but the dominant pair in CF is Pseudomonas and Staphylococcus; TB, Aspergillus, and pneumococcus are not the foremost CF organisms.'),
('4a03a1e2-44f5-5a46-8aa4-06fd11e41bde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of CF lung disease?', '["Bronchiectasis with chronic inflammation","Normal lung","Emphysema","and fibrosis; hypertrophied mucous glands","mucus plugging"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchiectasis with chronic inflammation', 'Recurrent infection and obstruction remodel CF airways into bronchiectasis with chronic inflammation, fibrosis, hypertrophied mucous glands, and mucus plugging. A normal lung, emphysema, or reversible inflammatory change does not capture the characteristic CF airway histology.'),
('d357fd8f-a5d2-50eb-9042-64ed1001f885'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the pancreatic manifestations of CF?', '["Exocrine excess resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","pancreatic involvement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (malabsorption) due to duct obstruction; recurrent pancreatitis; endocrine dysfunction (CF-related diabetes)","Pancreatic cysts without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatic cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (malabsorption) due to duct obstruction; recurrent pancreatitis; endocrine dysfunction (CF-related diabetes)', 'Thickened pancreatic secretions obstruct ducts, producing exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with malabsorption, recurrent pancreatitis, and eventually CF-related diabetes from endocrine dysfunction. This combination, not cysts, excess secretion, or malignant change, is the pancreatic hallmark of CF.'),
('8bb8a5e3-edfd-553f-ace6-4a68c50184a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the GI manifestations of CF?', '["Meconium ileus (in newborns)","GI involvement","Gastritis","and liver disease (biliary cirrhosis)","distal intestinal obstruction syndrome (DIOS)"]'::jsonb, 'Meconium ileus (in newborns)', 'Viscous meconium obstructs the terminal ileum in about 10-15% of affected newborns, causing meconium ileus, often the first sign of CF. Other later GI features include distal intestinal obstruction syndrome (DIOS) and biliary cirrhosis.'),
('07963f81-c73a-59bd-ac88-2ae9263f9de8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the sweat test?', '["A stool test resulting in decreased functional activity","A blood test accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A genetic test mediated by specific receptor activation","A lung function test without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The diagnostic test for CF: elevated sweat chloride (>60 mmol/L after pilocarpine iontophoresis)"]'::jsonb, 'The diagnostic test for CF: elevated sweat chloride (>60 mmol/L after pilocarpine iontophoresis)', 'The sweat test measures chloride in sweat collected after pilocarpine iontophoresis; a value above 60 mmol/L supports the diagnosis of CF. Blood, stool, and lung-function tests do not supply this diagnostic chloride result.'),
('7543a190-707c-5668-8911-517d3b485bc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the indications for lung transplantation?', '["pulmonary hypertension","CF","and others refractory to medical therapy","End-stage lung disease: COPD","IPF"]'::jsonb, 'End-stage lung disease: COPD', 'Lung transplantation is reserved for end-stage lung disease refractory to medical therapy, and severe COPD is the classic example among these indications, along with IPF, CF, and pulmonary hypertension. The other listed entities are simply additional indications rather than an alternative answer type.'),
('aed7addf-f9d5-5e52-a8af-2b4ceb8ba985'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the types of lung transplant?', '["Only bilateral","and heart-lung transplant","Only whole lung","Single lung","bilateral lung"]'::jsonb, 'Single lung', 'Lung transplant options include single-lung, bilateral (double) lung, and heart-lung transplantation. Single-lung transplantation is one of these recognized types and is commonly used, for example, in COPD.'),
('60275372-887b-5820-9b54-25d3cce60db3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is acute rejection in lung transplant?', '["Primary graft dysfunction resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Antibody-mediated rejection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","T cell-mediated rejection with perivascular and peribronchial lymphocytic infiltration; can be asymptomatic or cause dyspnea/declining PFTs","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T cell-mediated rejection with perivascular and peribronchial lymphocytic infiltration; can be asymptomatic or cause dyspnea/declining PFTs', 'Acute rejection of the lung graft is aT cell-mediated process with perivascular and peribronchial lymphocytic infiltration; it may be silent or cause dyspnea with falling pulmonary function tests. It differs from antibody-mediated rejection, primary graft dysfunction, BOS, and infection.'),
('aebd5739-d69c-57b9-9f21-9d8b752a8970'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome (BOS)?', '["Acute rejection resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic lung allograft dysfunction with progressive airflow obstruction due to small airway fibrosis and obliteration","Primary graft dysfunction mediated by specific receptor activation","Anastomotic complication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic lung allograft dysfunction with progressive airflow obstruction due to small airway fibrosis and obliteration', 'Bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome is the main form of chronic lung allograft dysfunction, producing progressive airflow obstruction from small airway fibrosis and obliteration. It arises months to years after transplant, unlike acute rejection, primary graft dysfunction, anastomotic complications, or infection.'),
('6b144ac7-db0b-5088-b998-07dd39d61846'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of BOS?', '["Vascular rejection resulting in decreased functional activity","Alveolar inflammation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrosis and obliteration of bronchiolar lumens (organizing pneumonia-like pattern)","Granulomatous inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","leading to constrictive bronchiolitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrosis and obliteration of bronchiolar lumens (organizing pneumonia-like pattern)', 'The histologic hallmark of BOS is fibrosis with obliteration of bronchiolar lumens in an organizing pneumonia-like pattern that ends in constrictive bronchiolitis. Acute rejection instead shows perivascular lymphocytic infiltration rather than luminal obliteration.'),
('d82866ce-6c2b-5e39-ba4b-570b397a87bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common infection after lung transplant?', '["TB resulting in decreased functional activity","Viral URI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CMV pneumonia and bacterial pneumonia","Fungal infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PCP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CMV pneumonia and bacterial pneumonia', 'Bacterial pneumonia and CMV pneumonia are the most frequent infections after lung transplantation. TB, PCP, fungal disease, and ordinary viral URI do not dominate the post-transplant infection profile.'),
('7326334a-f955-5127-a722-986cb667ff91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the role of bronchoscopy after lung transplant?', '["Surveillance for rejection (transbronchial biopsy)","and anastomotic complications accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only for diagnosis of infection mediated by specific receptor activation","infection (BAL) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","role leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surveillance for rejection (transbronchial biopsy)', 'Bronchoscopy with transbronchial biopsy is the standard way to survey for acute rejection after lung transplant, and bronchoalveolar lavage also evaluates infection such as CMV. Its role is not confined to diagnosing infection or to anastomotic problems.'),
('57de959a-1aad-5f35-8edc-70e6551e5664'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD)?', '["Lung cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","CMV disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic rejection mediated by specific receptor activation","EBV-driven B-cell proliferation (ranging from hyperplasia to lymphoma) in transplant recipients","Acute rejection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'EBV-driven B-cell proliferation (ranging from hyperplasia to lymphoma) in transplant recipients', 'PTLD is an EBV-driven B-cell proliferation in immunosuppressed transplant recipients that ranges from polyclonal hyperplasia to frank lymphoma. It is not acute or chronic rejection, CMV disease, or lung cancer.'),
('7171ffb9-2092-5353-8a63-5527e0438116'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the 5-year survival after lung transplant?', '["30-40%","About 50-60%",">90%","<20%","80-90%"]'::jsonb, 'About 50-60%', 'Lung transplant survival is the lowest among solid organ transplants, with 5-year survival around 50-60%. Rates above 90% or below 20% do not reflect current outcome data.'),
('41373728-e034-5a94-9f64-f3b7870e5889'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is pleurisy (pleuritis)?', '["An empyema","A pleural effusion","Inflammation of the pleura causing sharp","A pneumothorax","localized chest pain worsened by breathing or coughing"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the pleura causing sharp', 'Pleurisy is inflammation of the pleura causing sharp, localized chest pain worsened by breathing or coughing, due to friction between inflamed pleural layers. It is distinct from an effusion, empyema, or pneumothorax, although it may accompany them.'),
('b5e7402f-8eaf-5811-be63-0a373f59381e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of pleural effusion?', '["Cirrhosis (transudative)","Pneumonia (exudative)","Pulmonary embolism (transudative/exudative)","Malignancy (exudative)","Congestive heart failure (transudative)"]'::jsonb, 'Congestive heart failure (transudative)', 'Congestive heart failure is the most common overall cause of pleural effusion, generating a transudate from elevated pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure. Pneumonia, malignancy, cirrhosis, and pulmonary embolism are all less frequent causes.'),
('bbfc10ee-909b-536b-9b95-77ebb2a6d68f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are Light''s criteria for exudative pleural effusion?', '["Pleural glucose <60 mg/dL resulting in decreased functional activity","Pleural pH <7.2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural WBC >1000 mediated by specific receptor activation","Pleural amylase >100 U/L without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protein: pleural/serum >0.5; LDH: pleural/serum >0.6; or pleural LDH >2/3 upper normal serum LDH"]'::jsonb, 'Protein: pleural/serum >0.5; LDH: pleural/serum >0.6; or pleural LDH >2/3 upper normal serum LDH', 'Light''s criteria identify an exudate when pleural/serum protein exceeds 0.5, pleural/serum LDH exceeds 0.6, or pleural LDH is greater than two-thirds of the upper normal serum value. Low glucose, low pH, high WBC count, and high amylase are additional exudate markers but are not the defining Light''s criteria.'),
('11e42176-8404-54ef-99e5-18021cd1db96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a transudative pleural effusion?', '["Fluid that is not due to pleural disease but to systemic factors (increased hydrostatic pressure or decreased oncotic pressure)","Malignant pleural fluid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fluid from pleural inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","Infected pleural fluid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chylous pleural fluid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid that is not due to pleural disease but to systemic factors (increased hydrostatic pressure or decreased oncotic pressure)', 'A transudate comes from systemic factors such as increased hydrostatic pressure (heart failure) or reduced oncotic pressure (hypoalbuminemia) and has low protein and LDH. Fluid caused by pleural inflammation, malignancy, infection, or chyle is by definition exudative.'),
('4450a627-84bb-526e-987e-191ca20c3d81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is an exudative pleural effusion?', '["Fluid from nephrotic syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Fluid from CHF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","with high protein and LDH mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluid resulting from pleural inflammation or malignancy","Fluid from cirrhosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid resulting from pleural inflammation or malignancy', 'An exudate results from pleural or pulmonary inflammation or malignancy that raises capillary permeability, yielding fluid with high protein and LDH. The transudates of CHF, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome have low protein and LDH.'),
('aadd3ad6-a705-5d12-b115-273ac02f5a5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a malignant pleural effusion?', '["Pleural effusion containing malignant cells (pleural metastasis or primary tumor)","A chylous effusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A transudative effusion mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign inflammatory effusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An infected effusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pleural effusion containing malignant cells (pleural metastasis or primary tumor)', 'A malignant pleural effusion contains malignant cells from pleural metastasis or a primary pleural tumor and is typically a bloody exudate. It is identified by pleural fluid cytology, unlike chylous, transudative, inflammatory, or infected effusions.'),
('bbbf21d4-adab-54f4-9a4d-bc8d6db0b4ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a parapneumonic effusion?', '["A chylous effusion resulting in decreased functional activity","A transudate from CHF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural effusion associated with pneumonia; can be uncomplicated or complicated (empyema)","A tuberculous effusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A malignant effusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pleural effusion associated with pneumonia; can be uncomplicated or complicated (empyema)', 'A parapneumonic effusion is fluid that develops alongside pneumonia; it may be uncomplicated (sterile, resolving with antibiotics) or complicated with pus (empyema). Chylous, transudative, tuberculous, and malignant effusions have different causes.'),
('e8d13a19-dc89-5a70-a905-88b73b040e63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a chylothorax?', '["A pseudochylothorax resulting in decreased functional activity","A malignant effusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A transudative effusion mediated by specific receptor activation","An empyema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pleural effusion containing chyle (lymph and triglycerides) due to thoracic duct injury or obstruction"]'::jsonb, 'Pleural effusion containing chyle (lymph and triglycerides) due to thoracic duct injury or obstruction', 'A chylothorax is a milky, triglyceride-rich effusion of chyle caused by thoracic duct injury or obstruction, such as trauma, cardiothoracic surgery, or lymphoma. Empyema contains pus, and pseudochylothorax, transudative, and malignant fluids have different compositions.'),
('6db09f4a-acc8-5f6a-a9ef-dc566bb53e8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the histology of pleural tuberculosis?', '["Lymphocytic infiltration only resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrosis only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neutrophilic infiltrate mediated by specific receptor activation","Granulomatous inflammation with caseating granulomas in the pleura","Non-specific inflammation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Granulomatous inflammation with caseating granulomas in the pleura', 'Pleural tuberculosis produces granulomatous inflammation with caseating granulomas in the pleura. A purely lymphocytic or neutrophilic infiltrate, fibrosis alone, or nonspecific inflammation does not characterize tuberculous pleuritis.'),
('4a9f74bc-49e6-580b-a4fa-275f44d60116'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the minute ventilation?', '["The volume per breath resulting in decreased functional activity","The forced expiratory volume accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The total lung capacity mediated by specific receptor activation","The volume of air inhaled or exhaled per minute (tidal volume × respiratory rate); about 5-8 L/min at rest","The maximum volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The volume of air inhaled or exhaled per minute (tidal volume × respiratory rate); about 5-8 L/min at rest', 'Minute ventilation is the total air moved per minute, equal to tidal volume multiplied by respiratory rate, roughly 5-8 L/min at rest. It is not a per-breath volume, FEV1, total lung capacity, or maximal volume.'),
('f3396572-3695-5e45-8ddb-2f7ced1a6d4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is dead space ventilation?', '["The volume of air exhaled per minute resulting in decreased functional activity","The vital capacity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The volume of air reaching the alveoli mediated by specific receptor activation","The total minute ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The volume of air that does not participate in gas exchange (anatomic + alveolar dead space)"]'::jsonb, 'The volume of air that does not participate in gas exchange (anatomic + alveolar dead space)', 'Dead space ventilation is the portion of each breath that undergoes no gas exchange, comprising anatomic dead space of the conducting airways plus alveolar dead space of ventilated but unperfused alveoli. Air reaching perfused alveoli contributes to alveolar ventilation instead.'),
('c4faefd7-df46-5a0c-a950-5be78a45ff5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the normal Vd/Vt ratio?', '["0.7","About 0.3 (30%)","0.1","0.9","0.5"]'::jsonb, 'About 0.3 (30%)', 'The normal dead space to tidal volume ratio is approximately 0.3, so about 30% of each breath is wasted ventilation. A higher ratio indicates excessive dead space, as in embolism or hypotension.'),
('27e6a38a-d177-5790-9b15-e90bf65809ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the pulmonary ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio?', '["The diffusing capacity resulting in decreased functional activity","The ratio of alveolar ventilation to pulmonary capillary blood flow; normal 0.8","The A-a gradient mediated by specific receptor activation","The oxygen saturation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ratio of dead space to tidal volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ratio of alveolar ventilation to pulmonary capillary blood flow; normal 0.8', 'The V/Q ratio is alveolar ventilation divided by pulmonary capillary blood flow, about 0.8 at rest (4 L/min ventilation to 5 L/min perfusion). Diffusing capacity, the A-a gradient, oxygen saturation, and the Vd/Vt ratio are separate measurements.'),
('b52e3815-a658-5bb3-ab1e-986e5d783735'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is venous admixture (shunt)?', '["Arterial-venous malformation resulting in decreased functional activity","Deoxygenated blood entering the left side of the heart without passing through ventilated alveoli","Oxygenated blood entering the right side mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal mixing of blood without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchial circulation drainage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenated blood entering the left side of the heart without passing through ventilated alveoli', 'A true shunt (venous admixture) is deoxygenated blood that bypasses ventilated alveoli and enters the left heart through abnormal channels, lowering PaO2 even with 100% oxygen. It is not normal mixing or simple bronchial venous drainage.'),
('541a37c9-c345-5f20-8775-89c500d1c744'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the normal PaO2 on room air at sea level?', '["60-80 mmHg","100-120 mmHg","80-100 mmHg","40-60 mmHg",">120 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '80-100 mmHg', 'On room air at sea level the normal arterial PaO2 is 80-100 mmHg and declines somewhat with age. Values below 80 mmHg indicate hypoxemia, while values above 100 mmHg do not occur on room air.'),
('cced44dc-a4db-54c0-a865-e7a9aa467929'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the normal PaCO2?', '["50-60 mmHg","45-55 mmHg","35-45 mmHg",">60 mmHg","25-35 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '35-45 mmHg', 'Normal arterial PaCO2 is 35-45 mmHg, kept in range by balancing CO2 production against alveolar ventilation. Above 45 mmHg reflects hypoventilation and respiratory acidosis; below 35 mmHg reflects hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis.'),
('eb87d62d-be92-5b3f-9617-2ac238a9a233'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the normal arterial pH?', '["7.0-7.2","7.5-7.6","7.25-7.35","7.45-7.55","7.35-7.45"]'::jsonb, '7.35-7.45', 'Normal arterial pH spans 7.35-7.45. Below 7.35 indicates acidemia and above 7.45 indicates alkalemia; the other narrow ranges are not accepted as normal.'),
('7c942e18-e91a-5e85-a876-e76b70d3a021'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the normal HCO3- level?', '[">35 mEq/L","15-20 mEq/L","26-30 mEq/L","30-35 mEq/L","22-26 mEq/L"]'::jsonb, '22-26 mEq/L', 'Normal plasma bicarbonate is 22-26 mEq/L and is the metabolic component of acid-base status. Low values reflect metabolic acidosis or compensation for respiratory alkalosis, while high values reflect metabolic alkalosis or compensation for respiratory acidosis.'),
('6b97dfb3-fdcd-548a-80a8-6aed61e02a72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the expected compensatory response to acute respiratory acidosis?', '["Acute increase in HCO3- by 1 mEq/L for every 10 mmHg increase in PaCO2","Increase in pH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","compensation mediated by specific receptor activation","Decrease in HCO3- without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decrease in PaCO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute increase in HCO3- by 1 mEq/L for every 10 mmHg increase in PaCO2', 'In acute respiratory acidosis, bicarbonate rises about 1 mEq/L for every 10 mmHg increase in PaCO2 through weak metabolic buffering; chronic respiratory acidosis produces a larger renal compensation of roughly 3.5-4 mEq/L. The other choices describe changes in pH or PaCO2 rather than the compensatory bicarbonate response.'),
('8031bde4-f64e-558c-8205-daa9a344c5f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the structure of the tracheal epithelium on electron microscopy?', '["basal cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple cuboidal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and brush cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Pseudostratified columnar with ciliated cells","goblet cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified columnar with ciliated cells', 'The trachea is lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium with ciliated cells, interspersed goblet cells, basal cells, and brush cells. Simple cuboidal or single-type linings do not match the ciliated respiratory surface.'),
('c3e24ebc-c13b-53c6-842d-32d3688156f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the structure of a cilium?', '["radial spokes resulting in decreased functional activity","with nexin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","9+2 microtubule arrangement: 9 peripheral doublets and 2 central singlets","and dynein arms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","9+0 arrangement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '9+2 microtubule arrangement: 9 peripheral doublets and 2 central singlets', 'Motile cilia show a 9+2 axoneme: nine peripheral doublet microtubules surrounding two central singlets, with dynein arms generating the sliding force for beating. The 9+0 pattern is found instead in non-motile primary cilia.'),
('77742c11-8314-5e85-93ab-aae7cb635b54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the structure of the capillary endothelium in the lung?', '["Discontinuous resulting in decreased functional activity","Fenestrated accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","extremely thin (0.1-0.2 um) mediated by specific receptor activation","Continuous (non-fenestrated) with tight junctions","Sinusoidal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous (non-fenestrated) with tight junctions', 'Alveolar capillaries are continuous (non-fenestrated) endothelium joined by tight junctions and are extremely thin to shorten the diffusion path. Fenestrated, discontinuous, or sinusoidal endothelium belongs to other organs rather than the pulmonary gas-exchange barrier.'),
('82cdb738-bcea-5b1b-8b06-c43f7551c580'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the basement membrane of the alveolar epithelium?', '["Elastic lamina resulting in decreased functional activity","Fused basement membranes of type I pneumocytes and capillary endothelium in thin areas","Separate basement membranes mediated by specific receptor activation","basement membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thick collagen layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fused basement membranes of type I pneumocytes and capillary endothelium in thin areas', 'In thin areas of the air-blood barrier the basement membranes of type I pneumocytes and capillary endothelium fuse into one layer to minimize diffusion distance. Thick areas contain separate membranes with intervening connective tissue, not a thick collagen lamina.'),
('bc0e0a9f-5428-53cc-8b22-549c1c5954a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the lamellar bodies in type II pneumocytes?', '["Multilamellar secretory granules containing surfactant phospholipids and proteins","Lysosomes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endoplasmic reticulum mediated by specific receptor activation","Golgi vesicles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mitochondria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multilamellar secretory granules containing surfactant phospholipids and proteins', 'Lamellar bodies are multilayered phospholipid-rich granules in type II pneumocytes that store and secrete pulmonary surfactant, dominated by dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine. They identify type II cells and are not lysosomes, ER, Golgi vesicles, or mitochondria.'),
('1df93ca9-ca6a-56c9-a7e4-ff5ac852e85b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is tubular myelin?', '["A pathological finding resulting in decreased functional activity","A microtubule structure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A type of collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","The lattice-like structure formed by surfactant in the alveolar lining fluid after secretion from lamellar bodies","A degenerating cell leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lattice-like structure formed by surfactant in the alveolar lining fluid after secretion from lamellar bodies', 'After exocytosis, lamellar bodies unfold into tubular myelin, a lattice-like surfactant network in the alveolar lining fluid that helps the surface film spread. It is a normal product of surfactant secretion, not a pathological, cytoskeletal, or collagen structure.'),
('deca956a-ecac-5b39-b8ea-6855a7d57c24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the hypophase of the alveolar lining fluid?', '["The periciliary fluid resulting in decreased functional activity","The surfactant layer only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The aqueous subphase beneath the surfactant film; contains surfactant proteins and other components","The pleural fluid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mucus layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The aqueous subphase beneath the surfactant film; contains surfactant proteins and other components', 'The alveolar lining fluid has a superficial surfactant film overlying an aqueous hypophase that contains surfactant proteins and other solutes. It is not the surfactant film itself, the periciliary fluid, mucus, or pleural fluid.'),
('600ae9cd-2f5c-5f86-85ce-00c44b6800fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the periciliary fluid?', '["The surfactant layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The mucus layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The alveolar lining fluid mediated by specific receptor activation","essential for mucociliary clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The low-viscosity fluid layer surrounding cilia in the airways"]'::jsonb, 'The low-viscosity fluid layer surrounding cilia in the airways', 'Periciliary (sol) fluid is the low-viscosity layer around the cilia that allows them to beat while the mucus gel layer moves upward, sustaining mucociliary clearance. It is distinct from the mucus layer, the surfactant film, and the alveolar lining fluid.'),
('a7955ae9-8e15-57eb-90b8-a74c9472b129'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is submucosal gland in the airways?', '["Cartilage plates resulting in decreased functional activity","Glands in the submucosa of the trachea and bronchi that secrete mucus and serous fluid","Lymphatic nodules mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth muscle bundles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveoli leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glands in the submucosa of the trachea and bronchi that secrete mucus and serous fluid', 'Submucosal glands of the trachea and bronchi secrete mucus and watery serous fluid that humidify and trap inhaled particles. Cartilage plates, smooth muscle bundles, and lymphatic nodules are separate structural elements of the airway wall.'),
('f824df11-da49-5dce-a8a8-a0a850a3a7ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the stages of lung development?', '["canalicular (17-26 wk)","Embryonic (4-7 wk)","saccular (26-36 wk)","pseudoglandular (7-17 wk)","and alveolar (36 wk to 3 years)"]'::jsonb, 'Embryonic (4-7 wk)', 'Lung development proceeds through five overlapping stages: embryonic (4-7 wk), pseudoglandular (7-17 wk), canalicular (17-26 wk), saccular (26-36 wk), and alveolar (36 wk onward to about 3 years). The embryonic stage is the earliest and establishes the lung bud and lobar branching.'),
('711804f9-2ea2-55d5-a697-1741ff79c6f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What happens in the embryonic stage of lung development?', '["Surfactant production resulting in decreased functional activity","Capillary development accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Formation of the lung bud from the foregut and first division into left and right primary bronchi","Elastic fiber deposition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of the lung bud from the foregut and first division into left and right primary bronchi', 'During the embryonic stage (weeks 4-7), a diverticulum buds from the ventral wall of the foregut and divides into the right and left primary bronchi, establishing the basic tubular framework of the lung. Surfactant secretion and true alveolar formation take place in much later stages.'),
('3ae33793-619c-5df1-bb9a-dd6fb06580e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What happens in the pseudoglandular stage?', '["Surfactant production","forming all conducting airways (generations 0-16)","Branching morphogenesis of the bronchial tree","Alveolar formation","Capillary invasion"]'::jsonb, 'Branching morphogenesis of the bronchial tree', 'In the pseudoglandular stage (weeks 5-17), repeated branching of the bronchial tree forms all conducting airways, so the lung resembles an exocrine gland on microscopy. Gas exchange regions and surfactant do not develop until the later canalicular and saccular stages.'),
('0adb6bbc-20e7-53fb-8da3-4e03a71ac2f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What happens in the canalicular stage?', '["Alveolar multiplication resulting in decreased functional activity","Cartilage development accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Formation of respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts; capillary invasion; and first possible viability for gas exchange","Elastic fiber formation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surfactant production leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts; capillary invasion; and first possible viability for gas exchange', 'The canalicular stage (weeks 16-26) produces respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts, and capillaries grow close to the thinning epithelium, so gas exchange becomes possible for the first time around weeks 24-26. This marks the earliest point of potential fetal viability.'),
('d09e25dc-bb30-5a25-ad2f-45c74b268b4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What happens in the saccular stage?', '["Alveolar multiplication resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchial branching accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cartilage formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Formation of terminal saccules (primitive alveoli) and further thinning of the alveolar walls; surfactant synthesis begins","Goblet cell appearance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of terminal saccules (primitive alveoli) and further thinning of the alveolar walls; surfactant synthesis begins', 'In the saccular stage (weeks 26-36), terminal saccules or primitive alveoli form with progressively thinner walls, and type II pneumocytes begin producing surfactant to stabilize the air-liquid interface. This stage increases the surface available for gas exchange before true alveoli are born.'),
('7469edc7-81a0-5acf-8d0f-87f27e59696a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What happens in the alveolar stage?', '["Alveolar multiplication resulting in decreased functional activity","Formation of mature alveoli from saccules; continues from late fetal life (36 weeks) to 2-3 years postnatally","Surfactant production mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchial branching without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type I cell development leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of mature alveoli from saccules; continues from late fetal life (36 weeks) to 2-3 years postnatally', 'The alveolar stage begins around 36 weeks of gestation and continues postnatally, converting terminal saccules into mature alveoli with a thin blood-air barrier. Alveolar number rises rapidly after birth, until about 2-3 years of age.'),
('3753d482-e212-5536-8f3f-0659e55c03a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the number of alveoli at birth vs. adult?', '["500 million at birth","100 million at birth","1 billion in adults","10 million at birth","50 million at birth; 300 million in adults"]'::jsonb, '50 million at birth; 300 million in adults', 'A newborn lung holds approximately 50 million alveoli, whereas the adult lung has about 300 million. The sixfold postnatal increase in alveolar number expands the gas-exchange surface to keep pace with the growing body.'),
('15344106-69d5-5be2-81d4-a6efe901baaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the surface area of the adult lung?', '["10-20 m2 resulting in decreased functional activity","30-40 m2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 70-100 square meters (the size of a tennis court)","150-200 m2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway",">200 m2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 70-100 square meters (the size of a tennis court)', 'The adult alveolar surface available for gas exchange is roughly 70-100 m2, comparable to a tennis court. This enormous surface and an extremely thin blood-air barrier allow efficient oxygen diffusion at rest and during exercise.'),
('6b5b418c-99ac-5370-bea5-ee8b70905081'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What cell produces VEGF in the developing lung?', '["Fibroblasts resulting in decreased functional activity","Type I pneumocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endothelial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Macrophages without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type II pneumocytes and other cells secrete VEGF to promote capillary development"]'::jsonb, 'Type II pneumocytes and other cells secrete VEGF to promote capillary development', 'Type II pneumocytes and other lung cells secrete vascular endothelial growth factor, which drives growth of the capillary bed around developing alveoli and helps maintain the mature blood-air barrier. This angiogenic signaling is essential for coupling epithelial and endothelial maturation.'),
('c2c0b09e-4abd-52f4-84fe-deead4b6d9de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is alveolar capillary dysplasia (ACD)?', '["RDS of the newborn resulting in decreased functional activity","Congenital pneumonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchopulmonary dysplasia mediated by specific receptor activation","A rare fatal congenital lung disorder with misalignment of pulmonary veins and abnormal capillary development","Pulmonary hypoplasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A rare fatal congenital lung disorder with misalignment of pulmonary veins and abnormal capillary development', 'Alveolar capillary dysplasia is a rare congenital disorder in which pulmonary veins run adjacent to pulmonary arteries and the alveolar capillary network fails to develop normally. Affected newborns develop refractory hypoxemia and pulmonary hypertension within days, and the condition is usually fatal despite therapy.'),
('391d3d81-0d03-5e9f-b9fd-1d987677473f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common symptom of lung disease?', '["Wheezing resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemoptysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dyspnea (shortness of breath)","Chest pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cough leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dyspnea (shortness of breath)', 'Dyspnea, or shortness of breath, is the most frequent presenting symptom of pulmonary disease, reflecting an imbalance between respiratory demand and the lung''s capacity for gas exchange. Cough, wheezing, and hemoptysis are common but occur in a smaller proportion of patients.'),
('056f98f5-9dfc-591a-aa85-5e1d650107d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is hemoptysis?', '["Vomiting blood resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood in the urine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood in the stool mediated by specific receptor activation","Nosebleed without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coughing up blood from the respiratory tract; massive hemoptysis is >300 mL/24h"]'::jsonb, 'Coughing up blood from the respiratory tract; massive hemoptysis is >300 mL/24h', 'Hemoptysis means coughing up blood originating below the larynx in the airways or lungs. Expectoration exceeding about 300 mL per 24 hours is classified as massive hemoptysis and represents a life-threatening emergency requiring urgent intervention.'),
('ce287e5d-a2d1-5d00-950b-4170b598da51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of hemoptysis?', '["Lung cancer","Tuberculosis","Bronchitis (acute or chronic)","Pulmonary embolism","Bronchiectasis"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchitis (acute or chronic)', 'Acute and chronic bronchitis together are the most common causes of hemoptysis, typically producing streaks of blood from inflamed bronchial mucosa. Carcinoma, tuberculosis, pulmonary embolism, and bronchiectasis are important but less frequent causes.'),
('3c5b6ef3-8906-5937-b327-160c563b8071'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is clubbing?', '["A sign of anemia resulting in decreased functional activity","associated with pulmonary and cardiac diseases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bulbous enlargement of the fingertips and nail angle >180 degrees","A sign of infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A normal variant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bulbous enlargement of the fingertips and nail angle >180 degrees', 'Clubbing is painless bulbous enlargement of the distal fingertips that flattens the angle between nail and nail bed to more than 180 degrees. It accompanies chronic pulmonary, cardiac, and gastrointestinal diseases, with lung carcinoma being the most common cause.'),
('c3240030-74bb-5fb4-89c0-eb14ae4484f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common cause of clubbing?', '["Pulmonary embolism resulting in decreased functional activity","Pneumonia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lung cancer (especially NSCLC)","COPD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asthma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung cancer (especially NSCLC)', 'Bronchogenic carcinoma, particularly the non-small-cell type, is the leading cause of clubbing in adults, likely driven by paraneoplastic mediators. Non-neoplastic pulmonary and cardiac conditions can produce clubbing but are less common causes.'),
('8004b313-fb23-5559-bae6-c776900135fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the breath sounds in asthma?', '["Expiratory polyphonic wheezing","Diminished breath sounds","Bronchial breath sounds","Crackles (rales)","Rhonchi"]'::jsonb, 'Expiratory polyphonic wheezing', 'Asthma produces expiratory polyphonic wheezing because airflow is obstructed through many bronchi of differing caliber, generating simultaneous sounds of several pitches. Crackles and rhonchi point instead to other conditions such as pulmonary edema or large-airway secretions.'),
('99905a1e-6ee1-5d51-a4a6-2586811f3b11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are crackles (rales)?', '["Interrupted","associated with interstitial lung disease and pulmonary edema","explosive sounds during inspiration","non-musical","Continuous musical sounds"]'::jsonb, 'Interrupted', 'Crackles are discontinuous, interrupted, explosive sounds heard mainly during inspiration, often in interstitial lung disease and pulmonary edema. Unlike wheezes, which are continuous and musical, each crackle is a brief popping event from sudden opening of collapsed airways.'),
('4ec8f4a0-63dd-5d02-9587-6707cb96cc50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is a pleural friction rub?', '["A sound from the heart","A grating","A sound from the trachea","creaking sound from inflamed pleural surfaces rubbing together","A wheezing sound"]'::jsonb, 'A grating', 'A pleural friction rub is a grating, creaking, leathery sound produced when inflamed visceral and parietal pleura rub against each other during respiration. It is characteristic of pleurisy and is most audible during both inspiration and expiration over the affected area.'),
('9e9facfe-f66b-5681-b204-4022cbdc8d76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea (OSA)?', '["Polysomnography showing apnea-hypopnea index (AHI) >5 with symptoms or >15 without","Pulmonary function tests accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chest X-ray mediated by specific receptor activation","CT scan without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood tests leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Polysomnography showing apnea-hypopnea index (AHI) >5 with symptoms or >15 without', 'OSA is diagnosed with overnight polysomnography, which records the apnea-hypopnea index (AHI) of 5 or more events per hour combined with symptoms, or an AHI above 15 even without symptoms. Standard imaging and spirometry cannot establish this diagnosis.'),
('91c8cd3f-bde9-5732-93aa-b6b2e4267f8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the most common treatment for OSA?', '["Oral appliances resulting in decreased functional activity","Supplemental oxygen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Weight loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Surgery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) during sleep"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) during sleep', 'Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) during sleep is the mainstay of OSA therapy because it pneumatically splints the upper airway open and prevents collapse. Weight loss, oral appliances, and surgery are useful adjuncts but are not the first-line treatment for most patients.'),
('3aef505d-437a-5564-8804-d5edc6b396cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which option best defines the function of the respiratory system?', '["olfaction","phonation","and defense","Only gas exchange","Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)', 'The primary task of the respiratory system is gas exchange, delivering oxygen to the blood and removing carbon dioxide. Olfaction, phonation, and defense are additional roles, but none is the principal function.'),
('bef06a67-85f0-5b9a-b5e3-9f019125d696'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of the respiratory system. Which option is most accurate?', '["olfaction","phonation","and defense","Only gas exchange","Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)', 'Gas exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide across the respiratory membrane is the fundamental function of the respiratory system. The alternatives list supporting or partial roles rather than the core purpose.'),
('58a3c263-5eea-5a8c-8fc2-96ace4a77594'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Respiratory System: what is the function of the respiratory system?', '["olfaction","phonation","and defense","Only gas exchange","Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)', 'The respiratory system exchanges oxygen and carbon dioxide between inspired air and blood, sustaining cellular respiration. Olfaction, phonation, and immune defense are secondary functions and are not the definition of its purpose.'),
('77d9fdd6-d002-5a4b-a523-f17e61bb2c73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'For Respiratory System, identify the correct response: what is the function of the respiratory system?', '["olfaction","phonation","and defense","Only gas exchange","Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)', 'The respiratory system primarily exchanges oxygen and carbon dioxide between the atmosphere and the blood. Olfaction, phonation, and defense are supplementary functions rather than the central one.'),
('a8f2d16c-db1c-5aa6-9774-5e0eb532a0c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is the function of the respiratory system. Select the best answer.', '["olfaction","phonation","and defense","Only gas exchange","Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)', 'The essential function of the respiratory system is the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide in the lungs. Olfaction, phonation, and defense accompany this role but do not define it.'),
('e581460d-90d8-5484-9464-d66a9995224b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Respiratory System item: what is the function of the respiratory system?', '["olfaction","phonation","and defense","Only gas exchange","Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)', 'The respiratory system exists chiefly to exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide with the environment. Secondary activities such as olfaction, phonation, and defense are not its primary function.'),
('5cf32dff-40a6-544c-8ec5-e090f7c101f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which option best describes the two main divisions of the respiratory system?', '["Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchi and bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper and lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intra and extra pulmonary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)', 'The respiratory system is divided functionally into a conducting portion that transports and conditions air and a respiratory portion where gas exchange takes place. Referring to left/right sides or upper/lower parts describes anatomy, not the functional divisions.'),
('aee343fc-0a7d-5330-9992-3b4a9466df15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the two main divisions of the respiratory system. Which option is most accurate?', '["Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchi and bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper and lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intra and extra pulmonary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)', 'The two principal divisions are the conducting portion, which conditions and delivers air, and the respiratory portion, which accomplishes gas exchange. The remaining options sort airways by position or caliber rather than by their functional role.'),
('44e0c2f5-7d61-5a8f-b4a4-dd063a1281c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Respiratory System: what are the two main divisions of the respiratory system?', '["Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchi and bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper and lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intra and extra pulmonary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)', 'Division into a conducting portion (airways) and a respiratory portion (gas-exchange zone) reflects the two functional roles of the system. Left/right or upper/lower groupings are anatomical descriptions, not functional divisions.'),
('21b26049-6ea0-54f1-adeb-d168c0a327e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'For Respiratory System, identify the correct response: what are the two main divisions of the respiratory system?', '["Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchi and bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper and lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intra and extra pulmonary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)', 'The respiratory system divides into the conducting airways and the respiratory (gas-exchange) portion. Bronchi and bronchioles are segments of the conducting tree, not a true functional division of the whole system.'),
('27175349-f142-58c9-80b1-73f2e0dcf7fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What are the two main divisions of the respiratory system. Select the best answer.', '["Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchi and bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper and lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intra and extra pulmonary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)', 'Functionally the system is split into the conducting portion, which warms and moistens air, and the respiratory portion, where gas exchange occurs. The other choices describe location or caliber rather than the correct functional split.'),
('bd143ce8-4c2f-5fdc-b3f1-375c31a0d3c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Respiratory System item: what are the two main divisions of the respiratory system?', '["Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchi and bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper and lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intra and extra pulmonary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)', 'The two main functional divisions are the conducting portion (airways) and the respiratory portion (gas exchange). Grouping by side, level, or airway caliber does not capture this functional distinction.'),
('5972eb44-c3c7-5acc-8685-289ddde45f3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which option best defines included in the conducting portion?', '["pharynx","Nasal cavity","larynx","bronchi","trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity', 'The conducting portion comprises all airways that warm, humidify, and filter air on the way to the gas-exchange zone, beginning with the nasal cavity. Although the pharynx, larynx, trachea, and bronchi are also conducting structures, the nasal cavity is the named answer here.'),
('b3dc4ace-b688-5c05-8081-20bcfcdf0668'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is included in the conducting portion. Which option is most accurate?', '["pharynx","Nasal cavity","larynx","bronchi","trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity', 'The nasal cavity opens the conducting portion, which conveys air to the lungs without participating in gas exchange. Pharynx, larynx, trachea, and bronchi belong to the same portion, so the nasal cavity is the listed answer among these.'),
('84ebd8d1-a7d8-56d4-8701-fee1c1a3012c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Respiratory System: what is included in the conducting portion?', '["pharynx","Nasal cavity","larynx","bronchi","trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity', 'The conducting portion begins at the nasal cavity and extends through the pharynx, larynx, trachea, and bronchi, merely transporting air. Gas exchange is absent along this route, making the nasal cavity the intended answer in this option set.'),
('8b650d47-c03a-5a22-9576-5be747bac6ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'For Respiratory System, identify the correct response: what is included in the conducting portion?', '["pharynx","Nasal cavity","larynx","bronchi","trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity', 'The conducting portion carries air from the nasal cavity to the terminal bronchioles without any gas exchange occurring. Pharynx, larynx, trachea, and bronchi are also conducting structures, but the nasal cavity is the named answer here.'),
('183ba2da-62de-5add-83ab-de6091d573f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is included in the conducting portion. Select the best answer.', '["pharynx","Nasal cavity","larynx","bronchi","trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity', 'The conducting portion starts at the nasal cavity, which warms, moistens, and filters inspired air. The other listed structures (pharynx, larynx, trachea, and bronchi) are also conducting airways, so the nasal cavity is the intended answer among them.'),
('eb9a6b1d-90d8-5126-9a9c-db6fc0f5fcd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Respiratory System item: what is included in the conducting portion?', '["pharynx","Nasal cavity","larynx","bronchi","trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity', 'Air enters the conducting portion at the nasal cavity, which conditions the air before it continues down the conducting tree. Pharynx, larynx, trachea, and bronchi also belong to this portion, making the nasal cavity the selected answer here.'),
('6f597b98-f9a8-5423-a2ab-a6527f41a9a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which option best defines included in the respiratory portion?', '["Respiratory bronchioles","alveolar sacs","Only alveoli","and alveoli","alveolar ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory bronchioles', 'The respiratory portion, where gas exchange occurs, begins at the respiratory bronchioles and includes the alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and alveoli. The respiratory bronchioles are notable because their walls carry the first scattered alveoli, marking the end of pure conduction.'),
('a96b20c5-d58e-558a-80fb-0ebc88e548a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is included in the respiratory portion. Which option is most accurate?', '["Respiratory bronchioles","alveolar sacs","Only alveoli","and alveoli","alveolar ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory bronchioles', 'The respiratory portion consists of respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and alveoli because all take part in gas exchange. The respiratory bronchioles open this zone, and their walls already contain a few alveoli.'),
('a500e210-fe37-5b4e-96a2-be41040ccfd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Respiratory System: what is included in the respiratory portion?', '["Respiratory bronchioles","alveolar sacs","Only alveoli","and alveoli","alveolar ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory bronchioles', 'Gas exchange begins in the respiratory bronchioles, which bear scattered alveoli in their walls, and continues through the alveolar ducts, sacs, and alveoli. The respiratory bronchioles therefore open the respiratory portion of the airway tree.'),
('92395acd-68c9-5327-99d2-03eede9bfd56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'For Respiratory System, identify the correct response: what is included in the respiratory portion?', '["Respiratory bronchioles","alveolar sacs","Only alveoli","and alveoli","alveolar ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory bronchioles', 'The respiratory portion, the site of gas exchange, is composed of the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and alveoli. Because the respiratory bronchioles contain the first alveoli, they mark the start of this region.'),
('1e3abce2-be94-5c13-a0cb-bb302bbefa0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'What is included in the respiratory portion. Select the best answer.', '["Respiratory bronchioles","alveolar sacs","Only alveoli","and alveoli","alveolar ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory bronchioles', 'Respiratory bronchioles are the first part of the respiratory portion, distinguished by alveoli budding from their walls. Downstream lie the alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and alveoli, all of which participate directly in gas exchange.'),
('3d78c95c-33bf-5c39-95a8-885cd624d74b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Respiratory System item: what is included in the respiratory portion?', '["Respiratory bronchioles","alveolar sacs","Only alveoli","and alveoli","alveolar ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory bronchioles', 'The respiratory portion includes the respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and alveoli, all of which permit gas exchange. Since these bronchioles carry the first alveoli, they represent the gateway into this region.'),
('d6544773-668d-5b7d-932d-4a8032c935b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which option best defines the function of the nasal mucosa?', '["Only humidification","Only filtration","and filter inspired air; detect odors","humidify","Warm"]'::jsonb, 'Warm', 'The vascular nasal mucosa warms, humidifies, and filters the incoming air, along with supporting olfaction. Warming is carried out by the rich venous plexus beneath the respiratory epithelium, and no single one of these actions occurs alone.'),
('22cbe507-e9d1-5445-bb8f-ba54a7f22ce9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'In Respiratory histology, which statement best fits the following fact: what is the function of the respiratory system?', '["olfaction","phonation","and defense","Only gas exchange","Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Gas exchange (O2 and CO2)', 'Exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide between inspired air and blood is the core function of the respiratory system. Olfaction, phonation, and defense are ancillary roles, and gas exchange is not limited to one side of the circuit, so the fullest statement is preferred.'),
('61a4f439-b601-5cd3-81f3-747517b63024'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Regarding Respiratory histology, what is the correct answer to: what are the two main divisions of the respiratory system?', '["Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchi and bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper and lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intra and extra pulmonary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducting portion (airways) and respiratory portion (gas exchange)', 'The respiratory system is divided functionally into the conducting portion (airways) and the respiratory portion (gas exchange). Sorting by laterality, level, or bronchial caliber is anatomical, not a functional division of the system.'),
('a0db3110-52fc-5acc-a096-cd0c9a93f67e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'For Respiratory histology, which option is most appropriate: what is included in the conducting portion?', '["pharynx","Nasal cavity","larynx","bronchi","trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity', 'The conducting portion opens at the nasal cavity, which humidifies, warms, and filters air without permitting gas exchange. The pharynx, larynx, trachea, and bronchi are also conducting structures, so the nasal cavity is the chosen answer here.'),
('cbcc5bad-20f8-5c33-933e-66aeb4a5dff8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'Which answer is correct in Respiratory histology when asked: what is included in the respiratory portion?', '["Respiratory bronchioles","alveolar sacs","Only alveoli","and alveoli","alveolar ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory bronchioles', 'The respiratory portion begins with the respiratory bronchioles, whose walls contain the first alveoli, and continues through the alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs, and alveoli. These are the segments where gas exchange actually takes place.'),
('76fb3333-b75a-54da-92d4-00a610ba9060'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'In Respiratory histology, the best response to ''What type of epithelium lines the nasal cavity'' is:', '["Simple cuboidal resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium (respiratory epithelium) and olfactory epithelium (sensory)","Simple squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","Transitional without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stratified squamous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium (respiratory epithelium) and olfactory epithelium (sensory)', 'Most of the nasal cavity is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar (respiratory) epithelium containing goblet cells, while the roof is covered by the specialized olfactory epithelium for smell. Simple cuboidal, simple squamous, transitional, and stratified squamous types do not line this region.'),
('849f0707-17e0-5334-8b8c-5b5c123ae929'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Respiratory System', 'During revision of Respiratory histology, which of the following is correct: what is the function of the nasal mucosa?', '["Only humidification","Only filtration","and filter inspired air; detect odors","humidify","Warm"]'::jsonb, 'Warm', 'The nasal mucosa functions to warm, humidify, and filter inspired air in combination, so no single action is exclusive. Warming is achieved by the extensive vascular plexus beneath the epithelium, and the mucosa also contributes to olfaction.'),
('1c65dfc4-db01-5760-8671-43f7372ba70b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the three layers of the eye wall?', '["vascular (middle)","sclera","Fibrous (outer)","and neural (inner)","Episclera"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous (outer)', 'The eye wall has three concentric tunics: an outer fibrous layer (sclera and cornea), a middle vascular layer (uvea), and an inner neural layer (retina). The listed fragment names the outermost fibrous coat. Sclera and episclera are components of that fibrous tunic rather than independent layers.'),
('00e93d6b-66fc-5cf5-9ecb-80c77ed992c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the three chambers of the eye?', '["Only anterior and posterior","Anterior chamber (between cornea and iris)","posterior chamber (between iris and lens)","and vitreous chamber (behind lens)","Only vitreous chamber"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior chamber (between cornea and iris)', 'The eye contains three chambers: the anterior chamber between cornea and iris, the posterior chamber between iris and lens, and the large vitreous chamber behind the lens. The chosen fragment correctly identifies the anterior chamber. Options that reduce the eye to only two chambers or to the vitreous alone omit the other spaces.'),
('41a5f8bd-e6c6-58c6-92f4-6aeee0e566dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the cornea?', '["The transparent","avascular anterior part of the fibrous layer that refracts light (2/3 of the eye''s refractive power)","The white part of the eye","The vascular part of the eye","The colored part of the eye"]'::jsonb, 'The transparent', 'The cornea is the transparent, avascular anterior portion of the fibrous layer, contributing about two-thirds of the eye''s refractive power. The white sclera, the vascular coats, and the colored iris do not describe the cornea.'),
('a701d21e-ff29-5ebe-882e-c63bafbf5ab9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the five layers of the cornea (anterior to posterior)?', '["Corneal epithelium","stroma (substantia propria)","Bowman''s layer","and corneal endothelium","Descemet''s membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Corneal epithelium', 'From anterior to posterior the cornea has five layers: corneal epithelium, Bowman''s layer, stroma (substantia propria), Descemet''s membrane, and corneal endothelium. The chosen fragment names the most anterior layer. Stroma lies centrally, while Descemet''s membrane and corneal endothelium occupy the posterior positions.'),
('c194571e-ec25-5b19-ab23-8b333d76c27b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What type of epithelium covers the cornea?', '["Pseudostratified columnar resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple columnar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium","Simple squamous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transitional leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium', 'The corneal surface is covered by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, which renews rapidly and resists abrasion. Pseudostratified, simple columnar, simple squamous, and transitional epithelia do not form the corneal surface.'),
('bfbbe3b6-a44d-5f75-954d-b562fea8867c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the corneal endothelium?', '["Bowman''s layer resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The stroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Descemet''s membrane mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency","The outer layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency', 'The corneal endothelium is a single layer of cuboidal cells lining the posterior surface of the cornea; it pumps fluid out of the stroma, preserving hydration and transparency. Bowman''s layer and the stroma are not cellular linings, and Descemet''s membrane is its basement membrane.'),
('6178b413-ade3-510b-87c7-10ff36e4bad5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the corneal stroma?', '["Secretes tears resulting in decreased functional activity","Protects the eye accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Provides nutrition mediated by specific receptor activation","Provides mechanical strength and transparency through parallel collagen lamellae and proteoglycans","Refracts light leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Provides mechanical strength and transparency through parallel collagen lamellae and proteoglycans', 'The stroma, with its parallel collagen lamellae and interspersed proteoglycans, gives the cornea mechanical strength while the regular arrangement of fibers preserves transparency. Tear secretion, general protection, nutrition, and primary refraction are roles of other ocular structures.'),
('b35737e6-f69c-5fd6-9309-e3d21c6efc95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is Descemet''s membrane?', '["The stroma resulting in decreased functional activity","Bowman''s layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","regenerates after injury mediated by specific receptor activation","The thick basement membrane of the corneal endothelium (type IV and VIII collagen)","The outer layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thick basement membrane of the corneal endothelium (type IV and VIII collagen)', 'Descemet''s membrane is the thick basement membrane of the corneal endothelium, composed of type IV and type VIII collagen, and it is capable of regeneration after injury. Bowman''s layer and the stroma are separate anterior layers of the cornea.'),
('b1b55e6c-64b8-5a37-8d81-ae5c5c78b2e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the sclera?', '["The opaque","The vascular layer","The inner layer","The transparent outer layer","white fibrous outer layer of the eye; continuous with the cornea at the limbus"]'::jsonb, 'The opaque', 'The sclera is the opaque, white fibrous outer coat of the eye, continuous with the cornea at the limbus. The chosen fragment emphasizes its opacity, contrasting it with the transparent cornea. The vascular and inner layers describe other tunics.'),
('5d62f508-9523-5f38-9336-4c11d8deffb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the limbus?', '["The optic disc resulting in decreased functional activity","The ora serrata accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The transition zone between the cornea and sclera; contains the trabecular meshwork and Schlemm''s canal","The corneal margin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The scleral spur leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The transition zone between the cornea and sclera; contains the trabecular meshwork and Schlemm''s canal', 'The limbus is the transition zone between the cornea and sclera and houses the trabecular meshwork and Schlemm''s canal, the principal drainage pathway for aqueous humor. The optic disc, ora serrata, and scleral spur are distinct structures elsewhere in the eye.'),
('12407896-f26d-5db0-b019-aaee2702f703'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the trabecular meshwork?', '["filter-like structure at the limbus that drains aqueous humor from the anterior chamber into Schlemm''s canal","A porous","A muscle in the eye","A ligament","A blood vessel"]'::jsonb, 'A porous', 'The trabecular meshwork is a porous, filter-like structure at the limbus that drains aqueous humor from the anterior chamber into Schlemm''s canal. The chosen fragment identifies its porosity, the feature that permits aqueous filtration. It is neither muscle, ligament, nor blood vessel.'),
('b44a123a-7b14-5415-9d86-84b54c48194b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is Schlemm''s canal?', '["A circular canal at the limbus that collects aqueous humor from the trabecular meshwork and drains into the aqueous veins","A lymphatic channel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A blood vessel mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A nerve canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A channel behind the lens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A circular canal at the limbus that collects aqueous humor from the trabecular meshwork and drains into the aqueous veins', 'Schlemm''s canal is a circular, endothelium-lined channel at the limbus that collects aqueous humor from the trabecular meshwork and empties into the aqueous veins. It is not a lymphatic, a blood vessel, a nerve canal, or a channel behind the lens.'),
('19296a13-c76a-526b-8ba2-7fc389973910'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is intraocular pressure (IOP)?', '["The fluid pressure inside the eye (normal 10-21 mmHg)","The vitreous pressure","The blood pressure in the eye","The pressure from the lens","determined by the balance of aqueous humor production and drainage"]'::jsonb, 'The fluid pressure inside the eye (normal 10-21 mmHg)', 'Intraocular pressure is the fluid pressure inside the eye, normally 10-21 mmHg, determined by the balance between aqueous humor production and drainage. Vitreous pressure, blood pressure, and pressure from the lens are not measures of IOP.'),
('8aca993f-7e9b-534b-8573-b497a7980676'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the ciliary body?', '["Produces aqueous humor","Focuses light","controls accommodation (lens shape)","Regulates IOP","and forms the zonular fibers that suspend the lens"]'::jsonb, 'Produces aqueous humor', 'The ciliary body secretes aqueous humor through its non-pigmented epithelium of the ciliary processes. Through its smooth muscle it also controls accommodation, and via the zonular fibers it suspends the lens, but aqueous production is its primary secretory function.'),
('28a61d3f-1cc5-5b1a-acee-a05396aa330b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are ciliary processes?', '["Part of the lens resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerve endings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Villi of the choroid mediated by specific receptor activation","Finger-like projections of the pars plicata that produce aqueous humor via the non-pigmented ciliary epithelium","Folds of the iris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Finger-like projections of the pars plicata that produce aqueous humor via the non-pigmented ciliary epithelium', 'The ciliary processes are finger-like projections of the pars plicata whose non-pigmented ciliary epithelium secretes aqueous humor. They are not part of the lens, nerve endings, choroidal villi, or folds of the iris.'),
('6be7f247-eaa0-55c4-97c1-7e29606353b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are zonular fibers?', '["Fibrillin-rich fibers from the ciliary body that suspend the lens; relaxation during accommodation allows the lens to become more convex","Blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nerves mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatics without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscles in the iris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrillin-rich fibers from the ciliary body that suspend the lens; relaxation during accommodation allows the lens to become more convex', 'Zonular fibers are fibrillin-rich suspensory fibers running from the ciliary body to the lens; relaxation of these fibers during accommodation releases tension, allowing the lens to become more convex. They are not blood vessels, nerves, lymphatics, or iris muscles.'),
('85d2f7fa-de6f-562f-9342-38031a99937a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is accommodation?', '["Pupillary light reflex resulting in decreased functional activity","Eye movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tear production mediated by specific receptor activation","Changing the iris size without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The process of changing the shape of the lens to focus on near objects by ciliary muscle contraction"]'::jsonb, 'The process of changing the shape of the lens to focus on near objects by ciliary muscle contraction', 'Accommodation is the change in lens shape produced by ciliary muscle contraction that focuses near objects on the retina. The pupillary light reflex, eye movement, tear production, and iris size change are distinct ocular functions.'),
('c4165d9b-aedf-5865-b2ea-33484232ba09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the iris?', '["a pigmented","The colored part of the eye","The white part of the eye","The transparent part","contractile diaphragm with a central opening (pupil)"]'::jsonb, 'The colored part of the eye', 'The iris is the contractile diaphragm with a central opening, the pupil, and it is the colored part of the eye. The white sclera and the transparent structures of the eye do not describe the iris.'),
('d1f9b4db-8554-5631-b1b0-3eb58f238eb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the two layers of the iris?', '["Smooth and skeletal muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Pigment and pigment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscle and connective tissue mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior (stroma) and posterior (double-layered pigmented epithelium)","Epithelium and endothelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior (stroma) and posterior (double-layered pigmented epithelium)', 'The iris has an anterior stromal layer of connective tissue and pigment cells overlying a posterior double-layered pigmented epithelium. Alternatives pairing muscle types, muscle with connective tissue, or epithelium with endothelium do not reflect this actual layered arrangement.'),
('77670076-8f93-568b-8ec0-30790a9d825d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the pupillary sphincter muscle?', '["A muscle in the lens resulting in decreased functional activity","A circular smooth muscle in the iris that constricts the pupil (miosis)","A radial muscle that dilates the pupil mediated by specific receptor activation","A muscle in the ciliary body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","innervated by parasympathetic fibers (CN III) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A circular smooth muscle in the iris that constricts the pupil (miosis)', 'The pupillary sphincter is a circular smooth muscle encircling the pupil that constricts it (miosis) and is innervated by parasympathetic fibers of CN III. A radial dilator, or muscles of the lens or ciliary body, does not produce pupillary constriction.'),
('e922efc5-d3ff-53f3-8304-f12891075195'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the pupillary dilator muscle?', '["A ciliary muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","A lens muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","innervated by sympathetic fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","A radial smooth muscle in the iris that dilates the pupil (mydriasis)","A circular muscle that constricts the pupil leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A radial smooth muscle in the iris that dilates the pupil (mydriasis)', 'The pupillary dilator is a radial smooth muscle in the iris that widens the pupil (mydriasis) and receives sympathetic innervation. A circular constrictor opposes this action, and muscles of the ciliary body or lens subserve other functions.'),
('a71751c0-b445-5a30-a63d-4952d5096aa4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the pupillary light reflex?', '["A reflex where light in one eye causes bilateral pupil constriction (direct and consensual response)","Voluntary pupil constriction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pupil dilation in the dark mediated by specific receptor activation","A tear reflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A blink reflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A reflex where light in one eye causes bilateral pupil constriction (direct and consensual response)', 'The pupillary light reflex constricts both pupils when one eye is illuminated, producing direct and consensual responses. Voluntary constriction, dark-triggered dilation, and tear or blink reflexes involve different stimuli and pathways.'),
('a9cbf89b-4654-5d8a-b830-4255dab3c90a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the iris pigment epithelium?', '["Allows light to pass resulting in decreased functional activity","Produces melanin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The double layer of posterior pigmented epithelium that prevents light from passing through the iris (backwards)","Constricts the pupil without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dilates the pupil leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The double layer of posterior pigmented epithelium that prevents light from passing through the iris (backwards)', 'The posterior double-layered iris pigment epithelium blocks light from passing backward through the iris, restricting light to the pupillary aperture. Allowing light through, pupillary movement, and melanin synthesis by melanocytes are not its primary role.'),
('09d1691c-fc2a-518a-8eee-3413f4ea7643'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the angle of the anterior chamber?', '["containing the trabecular meshwork resulting in decreased functional activity","The angle of the eyelid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The angle of the lens mediated by specific receptor activation","The angle at the periphery of the anterior chamber where the cornea meets the iris","The angle of the retina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The angle at the periphery of the anterior chamber where the cornea meets the iris', 'The angle of the anterior chamber is its periphery, where the cornea meets the base of the iris, and it houses the trabecular meshwork. The eyelid, lens, and retina do not form this angle.'),
('f3b7db12-7529-546d-8ab2-837084e8f865'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is open-angle glaucoma?', '["progressive optic neuropathy with increased IOP () and a normal","A congenital glaucoma","A closed-angle glaucoma","A chronic","open anterior chamber angle"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic', 'Open-angle glaucoma is a chronic, progressive optic neuropathy with elevated intraocular pressure and a normal, open anterior chamber angle that still drains slowly. Congenital and closed-angle glaucomas are separate entities with different mechanisms.'),
('b76e43ae-1155-542d-90a8-c6866bd49f0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is angle-closure glaucoma?', '["Normal-tension glaucoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Open-angle glaucoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A sudden increase in IOP due to blockage of the trabecular meshwork by the peripheral iris (narrow angle)","Congenital glaucoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secondary glaucoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A sudden increase in IOP due to blockage of the trabecular meshwork by the peripheral iris (narrow angle)', 'Angle-closure glaucoma results from sudden blockage of the trabecular meshwork by the peripheral iris in a narrow angle, producing a rapid rise in intraocular pressure. Normal-tension, open-angle, congenital, and secondary glaucomas arise through other mechanisms.'),
('ab4e51ee-4007-5d22-867f-cdae1f532e50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the treatment for angle-closure glaucoma?', '["Laser iridotomy (creating a hole in the iris to allow aqueous flow)","Vitrectomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cataract surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","Laser trabeculoplasty without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eye drops only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laser iridotomy (creating a hole in the iris to allow aqueous flow)', 'Laser iridotomy creates a peripheral hole in the iris so aqueous humor can bypass the blocked angle, relieving the acute pressure rise of angle closure. Vitrectomy and cataract surgery address the vitreous and lens, and drops alone do not relieve the acute block.'),
('881af3d2-22b9-5870-8875-6e4da45df9bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the lens?', '["The vascular structure behind the iris","biconvex","A transparent","avascular structure that focuses light on the retina (1/3 of the eye''s refractive power)","The colored part of the eye"]'::jsonb, 'A transparent', 'The lens is a transparent, biconvex, avascular structure that focuses light on the retina and contributes about one-third of the eye''s refractive power. It is neither vascular nor the colored part of the eye.'),
('d52695f5-e4d7-5089-9ac6-fc3c92d6f36a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the layers of the lens?', '["Lens capsule and nucleus","lens epithelium (anterior)","and lens fibers (bulk of the lens)","Cortex and nucleus","Lens capsule (outer)"]'::jsonb, 'Lens capsule (outer)', 'Arranged from outside inward, the lens has an outer capsule, an anterior layer of lens epithelium, and lens fibers that form the bulk of the organ. The chosen fragment names the outermost layer. Cortex and nucleus are zones within the fibers, so listing only selected parts omits other layers.'),
('e47b73e8-2e22-57b7-bba2-7b3ed77c6c76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the lens capsule?', '["The outermost cellular layer","The lens nucleus","The lens fibers","A thick","elastic basement membrane (type IV collagen) that surrounds the lens; its elasticity allows accommodation"]'::jsonb, 'A thick', 'The lens capsule is a thick, elastic basement membrane of type IV collagen that surrounds the lens; its elasticity permits accommodation. It is not a cellular layer, and the nucleus and fibers lie deep to it.'),
('95c11557-d018-5e7d-847d-468a552a8ff5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the lens epithelium?', '["Lens fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","The lens nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lens capsule mediated by specific receptor activation","A single layer of cuboidal cells on the anterior surface of the lens (not on the posterior surface)","The ciliary epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A single layer of cuboidal cells on the anterior surface of the lens (not on the posterior surface)', 'The lens epithelium is a single layer of cuboidal cells confined to the anterior surface of the lens; the posterior surface has no epithelium. Lens fibers, the capsule, the nucleus, and the ciliary epithelium are distinct structures.'),
('fd91a886-c6aa-5b78-8d71-29ff1a26db78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are lens fibers?', '["Lens capsule cells","hexagonal cells that form the bulk of the lens; they lose their nuclei and organelles during maturation","Epithelial cells","Long","transparent"]'::jsonb, 'Long', 'Lens fibers are long, transparent, hexagonal cells that form the bulk of the lens and lose their nuclei and organelles during maturation. Epithelial cells and capsule cells are distinct lens components rather than fibers.'),
('d5ce309c-5f45-53c9-b1df-e7f8c9c77625'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is a cataract?', '["due to aging resulting in decreased functional activity","or drugs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","metabolic disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Opacity of the lens that interferes with vision"]'::jsonb, 'Opacity of the lens that interferes with vision', 'A cataract is opacification of the lens that blurs or interferes with vision. Aging, trauma, drugs, and metabolic disease are causes of cataract, not the definition itself.'),
('bf135307-6a74-5cbb-abd4-800d0ee5cd53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the types of cataracts?', '["Nuclear sclerotic","and posterior subcapsular (PSC)","Only one type","Congenital and acquired","cortical"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear sclerotic', 'The major morphologic cataract types are nuclear sclerotic, cortical, and posterior subcapsular. The chosen fragment names the first of these types. Listing fewer than three categories, or only a congenital-versus-acquired dichotomy, omits the morphologic classes.'),
('164a4c7d-587c-52cd-b269-5ae2e8493e70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of crystallins in the lens?', '["Signaling molecules resulting in decreased functional activity","Adhesion molecules accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Structural proteins for transparency; mutations cause congenital cataracts","Enzymes for metabolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pigments for UV protection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Structural proteins for transparency; mutations cause congenital cataracts', 'Crystallins are structural proteins that pack the lens fibers to maintain transparency, and their mutations cause congenital cataracts. They are not signaling molecules, adhesion molecules, metabolic enzymes, or UV-protective pigments.'),
('b4fe6a2f-f6b5-51f4-ae1d-c71661c07f9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is presbyopia?', '["Age-related loss of accommodation (ability to focus on near objects) due to hardening of the lens","Corneal astigmatism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elevated IOP mediated by specific receptor activation","Clouding of the lens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retinal degeneration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Age-related loss of accommodation (ability to focus on near objects) due to hardening of the lens', 'Presbyopia is the age-related loss of accommodation caused by hardening of the lens, so focusing on near objects becomes difficult. Astigmatism, elevated intraocular pressure, clouding of the lens, and retinal degeneration arise through different mechanisms.'),
('da8be25a-a76d-558c-acc0-8fdece0b3a5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the embryological origin of the lens?', '["Endoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural ectoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surface ectoderm: the lens placode invaginates to form the lens vesicle"]'::jsonb, 'Surface ectoderm: the lens placode invaginates to form the lens vesicle', 'The lens develops from surface ectoderm: the lens placode invaginates to form the lens vesicle. Endoderm, mesoderm, neural crest, and neural ectoderm give rise to other ocular and body tissues, not the lens.'),
('76beb480-40c4-58b4-b408-417da4621387'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the choroid?', '["The inner layer resulting in decreased functional activity","supplying blood to the outer retina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The vitreous mediated by specific receptor activation","The vascular layer of the eye between the sclera and the retina","The outer layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vascular layer of the eye between the sclera and the retina', 'The choroid is the vascular layer of the eye lying between the sclera and the retina, supplying blood to the outer retina. It is neither the innermost neural coat nor the outer fibrous layer, and it is distinct from the vitreous body.'),
('85ad8da1-1de4-5b8a-abe8-488ebbae0978'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is Bruch''s membrane?', '["Bowman''s layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The inner limiting membrane of the retina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A five-layered basement membrane between the choroid and the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE)","The lens capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Descemet''s membrane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A five-layered basement membrane between the choroid and the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE)', 'Bruch''s membrane is a five-layered basement membrane zone between the choroid and the retinal pigment epithelium. Bowman''s and Descemet''s membranes belong to the cornea, the lens capsule surrounds the lens, and the inner limiting membrane lines the vitreous face of the retina.'),
('3239b669-4ec5-53df-8961-f0993de9ad26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the vitreous humor?', '["gelatinous substance that fills the posterior chamber (vitreous cavity) and maintains the shape of the eye","The lens","A thick liquid in the anterior chamber","The retina","A transparent"]'::jsonb, 'A transparent', 'The vitreous humor is a transparent, gelatinous substance that fills the vitreous cavity and maintains the shape of the eye. It is not a liquid in the anterior chamber, and it is distinct from the lens and retina.'),
('e7673516-e96f-5a53-a86f-a8e8e5e2945a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the vitreous body attached to?', '["The sclera resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The retina at the ora serrata (anteriorly) and the optic disc (posteriorly); it also contacts the lens (face of Cloquet)","The iris mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The cornea without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The choroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The retina at the ora serrata (anteriorly) and the optic disc (posteriorly); it also contacts the lens (face of Cloquet)', 'The vitreous body attaches to the retina at the ora serrata anteriorly and around the optic disc posteriorly, and it contacts the posterior lens surface. The sclera, iris, cornea, and choroid are not its primary sites of attachment.'),
('5c3df7f6-6225-5ae0-8537-3c6e0f9d9092'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are hyalocytes?', '["Cells of the retina resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells of the ciliary body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cells of the lens mediated by specific receptor activation","Macrophage-like cells scattered in the vitreous cortex that produce hyaluronic acid and collagen","Cells of the choroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Macrophage-like cells scattered in the vitreous cortex that produce hyaluronic acid and collagen', 'Hyalocytes are macrophage-like cells scattered in the vitreous cortex that produce hyaluronic acid and collagen. Cells resident in the retina, ciliary body, lens, and choroid belong to different tissue compartments.'),
('89a96b07-3ac5-5a57-a05a-59f7b75c26c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is posterior vitreous detachment (PVD)?', '["Macular hole","Vitreous hemorrhage","Retinal detachment","often causing floaters and flashes; can lead to retinal tear or detachment","Age-related separation of the vitreous from the retina"]'::jsonb, 'Age-related separation of the vitreous from the retina', 'Posterior vitreous detachment is the age-related separation of the vitreous from the retina, often causing floaters and flashes, and it may predispose to retinal tear or detachment. Macular hole, vitreous hemorrhage, and full retinal detachment are complications or separate entities.'),
('f4073f16-7859-58fe-a42e-529b8b62f230'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the aqueous humor?', '["Provide oxygen to the retina","Absorb light","provide nutrients to the avascular lens and cornea","Maintain IOP","and remove metabolic waste"]'::jsonb, 'Maintain IOP', 'Aqueous humor maintains intraocular pressure and also supplies nutrients to the avascular lens and cornea while removing metabolic waste. Oxygenation of the retina and absorption of light are functions of other ocular structures and fluids.'),
('0b8a4d04-ff0e-59ce-9735-5c5f73b2c59a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the pathway of aqueous humor flow?', '["From vitreous to anterior chamber resulting in decreased functional activity","From retina to sclera accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From lens to iris mediated by specific receptor activation","Ciliary processes → posterior chamber → pupil → anterior chamber → trabecular meshwork → Schlemm''s canal → aqueous veins","From choroid to cornea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary processes → posterior chamber → pupil → anterior chamber → trabecular meshwork → Schlemm''s canal → aqueous veins', 'Aqueous humor is secreted by the ciliary processes, passes into the posterior chamber, travels through the pupil into the anterior chamber, and exits via the trabecular meshwork into Schlemm''s canal and the aqueous veins. The offered alternatives trace incorrect anatomical routes.'),
('42f691d1-4f5b-56ea-a80c-a64907aa43a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the rate of aqueous humor production?', '["5 microliters per minute","1 microliter per minute","0.5 microliters per minute","About 2-3 microliters per minute","10 microliters per minute"]'::jsonb, 'About 2-3 microliters per minute', 'The ciliary body produces aqueous humor at about 2-3 microliters per minute, which is balanced against drainage through the trabecular route to keep IOP stable. Rates substantially higher or lower than this range do not match normal physiology.'),
('075bcd8b-3fe5-5ea6-b62e-b3916638b63d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the retina?', '["The neural inner layer of the eye that converts light signals into electrical impulses for the brain","The fibrous layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The outer pigmented layer mediated by specific receptor activation","The transparent layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vascular layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The neural inner layer of the eye that converts light signals into electrical impulses for the brain', 'The retina is the neural inner layer of the eye that transduces light signals into electrical impulses for transmission to the brain. The fibrous, vascular, pigmented, and transparent coats describe other layers of the eye.'),
('caf14a23-c409-5ec3-ab26-c950a5977163'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the ten layers of the retina (outer to inner)?', '["outer plexiform layer","outer nuclear layer","photoreceptors","outer limiting membrane","RPE"]'::jsonb, 'RPE', 'The ten retinal layers begin with the outermost retinal pigment epithelium and then proceed through photoreceptors, the outer limiting membrane, outer nuclear and outer plexiform layers, inner layers, and the inner limiting membrane. The named fragment is the outermost retinal layer.'),
('6d67e24e-f3f6-551f-8aba-63fbd17ba368'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE)?', '["The neural layer resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The ganglion cell layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A single layer of pigmented cuboidal cells between the choroid and photoreceptors; it supports and nourishes the photoreceptors","The bipolar cell layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The photoreceptor layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A single layer of pigmented cuboidal cells between the choroid and photoreceptors; it supports and nourishes the photoreceptors', 'The RPE is a single layer of pigmented cuboidal cells between the choroid and the photoreceptors that supports and nourishes the photoreceptors and phagocytoses shed discs. Neuronal layers such as ganglion, bipolar, and photoreceptor cells occupy the inner and outer retina.'),
('a619a6fb-3295-5b3a-b01e-7092e6924fb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are photoreceptor cells?', '["Amacrine cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Ganglion cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Specialized neurons in the outer retina that convert light into electrical signals (rods and cones)","Horizontal cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bipolar cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Specialized neurons in the outer retina that convert light into electrical signals (rods and cones)', 'Photoreceptors are specialized neurons, the rods and cones, located in the outer retina that convert light into electrical signals. Bipolar, ganglion, horizontal, and amacrine cells transmit or modulate signals rather than transduce light.'),
('908d3d04-f1b0-589d-a3af-b88e42ea8a5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are rods?', '["Photoreceptor cells for scotopic (dim light) vision; they are highly sensitive but do not detect color","Bright light cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Color vision cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Ganglion cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bipolar cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptor cells for scotopic (dim light) vision; they are highly sensitive but do not detect color', 'Rods are the photoreceptor cells responsible for scotopic (dim-light) vision; they are highly sensitive but cannot discriminate color. Cones handle bright-light and color vision, while ganglion and bipolar cells are downstream retinal neurons rather than photoreceptors.'),
('9a21ce92-45eb-5f79-9d1a-245cd78db8a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are cones?', '["Color-blind cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Ganglion cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Photoreceptor cells for photopic (bright light) and color vision; they are concentrated in the fovea","Dim light cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bipolar cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptor cells for photopic (bright light) and color vision; they are concentrated in the fovea', 'Cones are photoreceptors specialized for photopic (bright-light) and color vision and are densely packed in the fovea, which gives maximal daytime acuity. Rods instead serve dim-light vision, and bipolar and ganglion cells transmit rather than detect light.'),
('09f5d27f-422b-5018-b743-fa6275037880'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the outer segment of a photoreceptor?', '["The axon resulting in decreased functional activity","The synaptic terminal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The part containing discs of photopigment (rhodopsin or photopsin) that absorb light and initiate phototransduction","The inner segment (mitochondria) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cell nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The part containing discs of photopigment (rhodopsin or photopsin) that absorb light and initiate phototransduction', 'The outer segment is composed of stacked membranous discs that hold the photopigment, rhodopsin in rods or photopsin in cones; light absorption here triggers phototransduction. The inner segment supplies metabolic energy, and the nucleus and synaptic terminal serve protein synthesis and neurotransmitter release respectively.'),
('ba473d78-d34e-5457-ba3d-9d882d575ef9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is phototransduction?', '["The process by which light is converted into an electrical signal in the photoreceptor cells","The focus of the lens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The movement of the eye mediated by specific receptor activation","The production of tears without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The formation of images leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The process by which light is converted into an electrical signal in the photoreceptor cells', 'Phototransduction is the sequence in which photon absorption closes cGMP-gated cation channels in the outer segment, hyperpolarizing the photoreceptor and producing an electrical signal. Lens focusing, tear production, and image formation are optical or accessory functions, not conversion of light into a neural signal.'),
('5f5a631f-187e-574f-bfe7-16fff67ee8fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is rhodopsin?', '["The photopigment in rods consisting of rod opsin (protein) and 11-cis retinal (chromophore)","A pigment in cones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pigment in the iris mediated by specific receptor activation","A photopigment in the RPE without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A pigment in the choroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The photopigment in rods consisting of rod opsin (protein) and 11-cis retinal (chromophore)', 'Rhodopsin is the rod photopigment made of rod opsin, aG-protein-coupled protein, covalently bound to 11-cis retinal, the light-sensitive chromophore. Cone pigments use photopsins rather than rhodopsin, and the iris, RPE, and choroid contain melanin, not this photoreceptor pigment.'),
('0aa7428c-d9f2-50dc-9e84-21c4b5519423'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the visual cycle?', '["The focusing of the image resulting in decreased functional activity","The transmission of nerve impulses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The absorption of light by the lens mediated by specific receptor activation","The recycling of all-trans retinal back to 11-cis retinal in the RPE cells","The synthesis of melanin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The recycling of all-trans retinal back to 11-cis retinal in the RPE cells', 'The visual cycle regenerates bleached all-trans retinal back to 11-cis retinal, chiefly within the retinal pigment epithelium, restoring light sensitivity of the photopigment. Image focusing, impulse transmission, and melanin synthesis are separate retinal or uveal functions, not pigment regeneration.'),
('b705bea3-76e4-57ff-ae8f-518c3cb6127a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are bipolar cells?', '["Amacrine cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Ganglion cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Horizontal cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Photoreceptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retinal interneurons that connect photoreceptors to ganglion cells; they are either ON or OFF types"]'::jsonb, 'Retinal interneurons that connect photoreceptors to ganglion cells; they are either ON or OFF types', 'Bipolar cells are retinal interneurons that relay signals from photoreceptors to ganglion cells and exist as ON and OFF subtypes responding to light increments or decrements. Amacrine and horizontal cells are lateral interneurons, and photoreceptors are the actual sensory detectors.'),
('eb8cbe2e-9dcb-56a1-a639-05b6c7c7b182'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are ganglion cells?', '["Bipolar cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Amacrine cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Photoreceptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Horizontal cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The output neurons of the retina whose axons form the optic nerve; they have center-surround receptive fields"]'::jsonb, 'The output neurons of the retina whose axons form the optic nerve; they have center-surround receptive fields', 'Ganglion cells are the output neurons of the retina; their axons bundle into the optic nerve, and converging bipolar input with lateral inhibition creates their center-surround receptive fields. Bipolar, amacrine, and horizontal cells, and photoreceptors, are earlier or lateral elements of the retinal circuit.'),
('f37e5f93-597f-5d5c-a486-671c603c0129'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are horizontal cells?', '["Bipolar cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Photoreceptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ganglion cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Retinal interneurons that mediate lateral inhibition (surround response) in the outer plexiform layer","Amacrine cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retinal interneurons that mediate lateral inhibition (surround response) in the outer plexiform layer', 'Horizontal cells are interneurons of the outer plexiform layer that link adjacent photoreceptors and bipolar cells, producing lateral inhibition that sharpens the surround response. Amacrine cells serve an analogous lateral role in the inner plexiform layer, while bipolar and ganglion cells conduct vertically.'),
('19fa6c38-1ffe-51f9-9097-1fb7a670a5fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are amacrine cells?', '["Ganglion cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Photoreceptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bipolar cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Retinal interneurons in the inner plexiform layer that modulate bipolar and ganglion cell signals; many subtypes","Horizontal cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retinal interneurons in the inner plexiform layer that modulate bipolar and ganglion cell signals; many subtypes', 'Amacrine cells are inner plexiform layer interneurons that modulate transmission between bipolar and ganglion cells and exist in many subtypes using different neurotransmitters. Their lateral connections refine motion and directional processing, unlike the vertical photoreceptor-bipolar-ganglion pathway.'),
('3c23ac4f-f339-54a0-990f-3a34948f3101'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the fovea centralis?', '["A small depression in the center of the macula with only cone photoreceptors; it is the site of highest visual acuity","The ora serrata accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The optic disc mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The blind spot without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The retinal periphery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small depression in the center of the macula with only cone photoreceptors; it is the site of highest visual acuity', 'The fovea centralis is a small central depression of the macula containing only cones, and because each foveal cone connects to a dedicated bipolar and ganglion cell, it provides the highest visual acuity. The optic disc is the blind spot and the ora serrata is the peripheral anterior border of the retina.'),
('5e95339b-e836-50d8-89cc-d996f72c2135'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the macula lutea?', '["The central depression resulting in decreased functional activity","The yellow-pigmented area of the retina surrounding the fovea","The optic disc mediated by specific receptor activation","rich in lutein and zeaxanthin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The peripheral retina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The yellow-pigmented area of the retina surrounding the fovea', 'The macula lutea is the yellowish central retinal region that surrounds the fovea, its color imparted by lutein and zeaxanthin pigments. The fovea is the central depression within it, whereas the optic disc is the photoreceptor-free blind spot.'),
('fb9bebbc-d921-53cc-b515-3c681cc38926'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the optic disc?', '["The point where ganglion cell axons exit the eye; it has no photoreceptors (the blind spot)","The ora serrata accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The macula mediated by specific receptor activation","The equator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The fovea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The point where ganglion cell axons exit the eye; it has no photoreceptors (the blind spot)', 'The optic disc is the site where ganglion cell axons gather to leave the eye as the optic nerve; because it contains no photoreceptors it is the physiologic blind spot. The fovea and macula are photoreceptor-bearing visual centers, and the ora serrata marks the retinal periphery.'),
('c3586901-0aea-5da5-b431-7a87af7f8095'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the lamina cribrosa?', '["The fovea resulting in decreased functional activity","porous region of the sclera at the optic nerve head through which ganglion cell axons pass","The choroid mediated by specific receptor activation","The retinal pigment epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A sieve-like leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'porous region of the sclera at the optic nerve head through which ganglion cell axons pass', 'The lamina cribrosa is the porous (sieve-like) scleral region at the optic nerve head through which ganglion cell axon bundles exit the globe, and its biomechanical weakness underlies cupping in glaucoma. The fovea, choroid, and retinal pigment epithelium are unrelated structures.'),
('aaa482cb-c1af-5772-9787-95b7fcec0b0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the ora serrata?', '["The macula resulting in decreased functional activity","The lens equator accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The edge of the optic disc mediated by specific receptor activation","The fovea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The zigzag anterior border of the neural retina where it meets the pars plana of the ciliary body"]'::jsonb, 'The zigzag anterior border of the neural retina where it meets the pars plana of the ciliary body', 'The ora serrata is the serrated anterior edge of the neural retina at its junction with the pars plana of the ciliary body, marking the end of the photoreceptor-containing retina. The macula and fovea are central visual areas, and the optic disc is the exit site of the optic nerve.'),
('003bbdd4-acb6-59e8-aded-c7a1b2c4569c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the layers of the retina from the vitreous outward?', '["inner plexiform layer","nerve fiber layer","ganglion cell layer","inner nuclear layer","Inner limiting membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Inner limiting membrane', 'From the vitreous side inward, the retina begins with the inner limiting membrane formed by Müller cell footplates, followed by the optic nerve fiber layer, ganglion cell layer, inner plexiform layer, and inner nuclear layer. The inner limiting membrane is therefore the first layer encountered from the vitreous.'),
('b59ed9c1-cd70-55f4-847e-95b406aafb61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the optic nerve (CN II)?', '["A cranial nerve for hearing resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An autonomic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A special sensory nerve for vision; it consists of ganglion cell axons that carry visual information from the retina to the brain","A mixed nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A motor nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A special sensory nerve for vision; it consists of ganglion cell axons that carry visual information from the retina to the brain', 'The optic nerve is a special sensory nerve composed of retinal ganglion cell axons that convey visual information from the retina to the brain. It is neither motor, mixed, nor autonomic, and hearing is served by the vestibulocochlear nerve.'),
('8d6f9da0-0a66-5687-a735-7dee471f03ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the optic chiasm?', '["The lateral geniculate nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","A nucleus in the thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The point of entry into the brain mediated by specific receptor activation","The crossing point of the optic nerves where fibers from the nasal retinas decussate; fibers from temporal retinas do not cross","The termination of the optic tract leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The crossing point of the optic nerves where fibers from the nasal retinas decussate; fibers from temporal retinas do not cross', 'At the optic chiasm, fibers from the nasal half of each retina decussate while temporal fibers stay uncrossed, so each side of the brain processes the contralateral visual field. The LGN is the downstream relay nucleus, and the chiasm is not where the pathway terminates.'),
('e83a8845-7ead-504c-8857-21d50b3e175b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the optic tract?', '["pretectal nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","and superior colliculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The optic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The retina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The continuation of the optic nerve fibers after the chiasm to the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN)"]'::jsonb, 'The continuation of the optic nerve fibers after the chiasm to the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN)', 'The optic tract is the bundle of fibers that runs from the chiasm to the lateral geniculate nucleus, carrying the contralateral visual field. The retina and optic nerve lie upstream of the chiasm, and the pretectal nucleus and superior colliculus receive separate collateral input for reflexes and eye movements.'),
('ef79e364-b9d5-5e63-89a7-c66b4ca0aa87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN)?', '["The pretectal nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The superior colliculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The thalamic relay nucleus that receives visual input from the optic tract and projects to the primary visual cortex via the optic radiations","The visual cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A nucleus in the retina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thalamic relay nucleus that receives visual input from the optic tract and projects to the primary visual cortex via the optic radiations', 'The LGN is a laminated thalamic relay nucleus that receives the optic tract and projects through the optic radiations to the primary visual cortex. The pretectal nucleus subserves the pupillary light reflex and the superior colliculus guides saccades, so they do not relay for conscious vision.'),
('dc1c35d2-6fb4-5bc5-a090-f31b4df1fa90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the optic radiations?', '["The retinal nerve fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","The optic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The white matter tracts from the LGN to the primary visual cortex (V1) in the occipital lobe","The visual association areas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The optic tract leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The white matter tracts from the LGN to the primary visual cortex (V1) in the occipital lobe', 'The optic radiations are white matter tracts that connect the LGN to the primary visual cortex (V1) in the occipital lobe. The optic nerve and tract lie earlier in the pathway, and visual association areas process information after V1.'),
('ae6b1893-0f11-5b72-a99e-2fb235fd4d7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the primary visual cortex (V1)?', '["The retina resulting in decreased functional activity","The visual association area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The area of the occipital lobe (Brodmann area 17) that initially processes visual information from the LGN","The superior colliculus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The LGN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The area of the occipital lobe (Brodmann area 17) that initially processes visual information from the LGN', 'V1 is Brodmann area 17 of the occipital lobe, the first cortical station where input from the LGN is processed for orientation, edges, and motion. Association areas handle higher-order interpretation, while the LGN and superior colliculus are subcortical relay or reflex centers.'),
('ed5cff9a-3688-520e-bd79-7e904d9112a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the visual field representation in the visual cortex?', '["specific representation resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The right visual field is represented in the left hemisphere (and vice versa); the upper visual field is below the calcarine sulcus (and vice versa)","Homunculus mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Ipsilateral representation without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contralateral only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The right visual field is represented in the left hemisphere (and vice versa); the upper visual field is below the calcarine sulcus (and vice versa)', 'Each cerebral hemisphere processes the contralateral visual field, and within each occipital lobe the lower field lies above the calcarine sulcus while the upper field lies below it. This is a precise retinotopic map, so a somatic homunculus or purely ipsilateral scheme does not apply to the visual cortex.'),
('7a728750-70d4-5e92-bf77-325ca30bafd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the pupillary light reflex pathway?', '["Retina → optic nerve → pretectal nucleus (midbrain) → Edinger-Westphal nucleus → ciliary ganglion → pupillary sphincter muscle","Retina → superior colliculus → pupil accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Retina → LGN → visual cortex → pupil mediated by specific receptor activation","Retina → hypothalamus → pupil without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retina → oculomotor nucleus → pupil leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retina → optic nerve → pretectal nucleus (midbrain) → Edinger-Westphal nucleus → ciliary ganglion → pupillary sphincter muscle', 'Light impulses travel from the retina through the optic nerve to the pretectal nucleus in the midbrain, then via the Edinger-Westphal nucleus and ciliary ganglion to constrict the sphincter pupillae. Pathways through the LGN or visual cortex serve conscious vision rather than this reflex.'),
('ef345c60-7963-57de-ba32-d0278841beaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is accommodation reflex?', '["The triad of responses: convergence","Pupil dilation","pupillary constriction","Lens flattening","and lens thickening when focusing on a near object"]'::jsonb, 'The triad of responses: convergence', 'Accommodation for a near object comprises a triad of convergence of the eyes, pupillary constriction, and thickening (rounding) of the lens from ciliary muscle contraction. Pupil dilation and lens flattening describe the opposite state used for distance viewing.'),
('98011f8d-2b88-51df-9f7f-70b7ff3dbe3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is Argyll Robertson pupil?', '["A Marcus Gunn pupil resulting in decreased functional activity","Pupils that accommodate (constrict to near) but do not react to light; classic for neurosyphilis","A fixed pupil mediated by specific receptor activation","A dilated pupil without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anisocoria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pupils that accommodate (constrict to near) but do not react to light; classic for neurosyphilis', 'The Argyll Robertson pupil shows light-near dissociation, constricting with accommodation but not to light, caused by a dorsal midbrain (pretectal) lesion and classically seen in neurosyphilis; the pupils are small and often irregular. Marcus Gunn, fixed, dilated, or unequal pupils represent other pupillary disorders.'),
('76ee3156-9f34-5b20-9d82-69c9b33e5758'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is age-related macular degeneration (AMD)?', '["A disease of the lens resulting in decreased functional activity","A corneal disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A disease of the optic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","A progressive degeneration of the macula causing central vision loss; two types: dry (atrophic) and wet (neovascular)","A peripheral retinal disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A progressive degeneration of the macula causing central vision loss; two types: dry (atrophic) and wet (neovascular)', 'AMD is a progressive degeneration of the macula that destroys central vision and is divided into dry (atrophic) and wet (neovascular) forms. It spares the lens, cornea, and optic nerve, so cataract, corneal disease, and optic neuropathy are etiologically distinct.'),
('94bed789-ddff-52af-8a9c-b07b4a93e2c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of dry AMD?', '["RPE atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Subretinal fluid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neovascularization mediated by specific receptor activation","Drusen (extracellular deposits between the RPE and Bruch''s membrane)","and photoreceptor loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drusen (extracellular deposits between the RPE and Bruch''s membrane)', 'Dry AMD is histologically defined by drusen, extracellular deposits of protein and lipid debris between the retinal pigment epithelium and Bruch''s membrane, with slow RPE and photoreceptor atrophy. Neovascularization and subretinal fluid are features of the wet form.'),
('43223b0a-3672-5c8c-8ebc-4763c6a7df08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of wet (neovascular) AMD?', '["and fibrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","causing hemorrhage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Choroidal neovascularization (CNV) with growth of abnormal vessels through Bruch''s membrane","Drusen only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","exudation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroidal neovascularization (CNV) with growth of abnormal vessels through Bruch''s membrane', 'Wet AMD is defined by choroidal neovascularization, in which abnormal new vessels penetrate Bruch''s membrane and leak fluid or blood, causing hemorrhage, exudation, and fibrosis. Drusen alone characterize the dry form, so neovascular growth is the distinguishing histologic feature.'),
('f21c46e4-4004-5ac6-8a8c-d7ecab5cfa25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is diabetic retinopathy?', '["A microvascular complication of diabetes affecting retinal blood vessels; can be non-proliferative (NPDR) or proliferative (PDR)","A cataract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A retinal detachment mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A glaucoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A macular degeneration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A microvascular complication of diabetes affecting retinal blood vessels; can be non-proliferative (NPDR) or proliferative (PDR)', 'Diabetic retinopathy is a microvascular complication of diabetes that damages retinal blood vessels and is subclassified into non-proliferative and proliferative forms. Cataract, retinal detachment, glaucoma, and macular degeneration are separate, etiologically distinct conditions.'),
('6a9ca74e-3370-5328-a574-b63a3aac5649'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of NPDR?', '["capillary microaneurysms","Basement membrane thickening","hard exudates","dot-blot hemorrhages","loss of pericytes"]'::jsonb, 'Basement membrane thickening', 'The characteristic histologic change of non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy is thickening of the capillary basement membrane, which weakens vessel walls and sets the stage for the pericyte loss, microaneurysms, hemorrhages, and exudates seen clinically. The other listed changes are secondary manifestations rather than the primary vascular histology.'),
('fd47a85e-2990-59ef-b583-2538b2308a78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of PDR?', '["Cotton-wool spots only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Hard exudates only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Microaneurysms only mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Macular edema only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retinal neovascularization (growth of new vessels on the retina and optic disc) due to hypoxia-induced VEGF; can lead to vitreous hemorrhage and tractional retinal detachment"]'::jsonb, 'Retinal neovascularization (growth of new vessels on the retina and optic disc) due to hypoxia-induced VEGF; can lead to vitreous hemorrhage and tractional retinal detachment', 'Proliferative diabetic retinopathy is histologically defined by retinal neovascularization driven by hypoxia-induced VEGF, with fragile new vessels on the retina and disc that bleed into the vitreous or cause tractional detachment. The other options describe non-proliferative stage findings.'),
('148c0a7f-9042-5ad9-98ef-9d7bf6790070'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the causes of retinal detachment?', '["tractional (pulling from fibrous membranes) resulting in decreased functional activity","exudative (fluid accumulation under retina without break) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rhegmatogenous (tear/break in retina allowing vitreous to separate it)","Only aging without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rhegmatogenous (tear/break in retina allowing vitreous to separate it)', 'Retinal detachment has three categories: rhegmatogenous from a retinal break, tractional from fibrous membrane pulling (as in PDR), and exudative from subretinal fluid without a break. The rhegmatogenous type is the commonest, so listing only aging or trauma is too narrow.'),
('5c81c98d-d820-557d-9f7e-f07149d57a6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of retinal detachment?', '["or proliferative tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Separation of the neural retina from the RPE; the subretinal space contains fluid","Atrophy of the retina mediated by specific receptor activation","blood without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thickening of the retina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Separation of the neural retina from the RPE; the subretinal space contains fluid', 'Retinal detachment is the separation of the neural retina from the retinal pigment epithelium, with fluid, blood, or proliferative tissue accumulating in the subretinal space. Isolated atrophy or thickening describes other retinal pathology, not the layered separation that defines detachment.'),
('9c870d55-ce52-5114-a099-8ddcdce9c7b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is retinitis pigmentosa?', '["A group of inherited retinal degenerations with progressive photoreceptor loss (rods > cones)","night blindness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and tunnel vision mediated by specific receptor activation","An infectious retinitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An autoimmune retinopathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A group of inherited retinal degenerations with progressive photoreceptor loss (rods > cones)', 'Retinitis pigmentosa is an inherited group of progressive photoreceptor degenerations in which rods are lost earlier and more severely than cones, producing night blindness and tunnel vision. It is neither infectious nor autoimmune, and night blindness and tunnel vision are clinical symptoms rather than the definition.'),
('8505f9c9-64a8-5466-ba4c-326f78d61200'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of retinitis pigmentosa?', '["Neovascularization","Loss of photoreceptors (especially rods)","and migration of RPE cells into the retina (bone spicule pigmentation)","Inflammatory infiltrate","RPE atrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of photoreceptors (especially rods)', 'The hallmark histology of retinitis pigmentosa is progressive loss of photoreceptors, chiefly rods, with secondary migration of RPE cells into the retina producing bone-spicule pigmentation. An inflammatory infiltrate and neovascularization are not characteristic of this non-inflammatory degeneration.'),
('d760064b-9e96-52b3-a6b6-857a072b4543'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is glaucoma?', '["A retinal disease resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A corneal disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","often with elevated IOP mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A group of optic neuropathies characterized by progressive degeneration of retinal ganglion cells and optic nerve atrophy","A lens opacity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A group of optic neuropathies characterized by progressive degeneration of retinal ganglion cells and optic nerve atrophy', 'Glaucoma is a group of optic neuropathies defined by progressive retinal ganglion cell loss and optic nerve atrophy, usually (but not always) with elevated intraocular pressure. A lens opacity describes cataract, and glaucoma does not primarily affect the lens or cornea.'),
('6650f200-7da4-5ad1-b0a3-1bc995d055d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of glaucoma?', '["Drusen","Loss of retinal ganglion cells","Neovascularization","and optic disc cupping (enlargement of the cup-to-disc ratio)","thinning of the nerve fiber layer"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of retinal ganglion cells', 'The histologic hallmark of glaucoma is loss of retinal ganglion cells with thinning of the nerve fiber layer and enlargement of the optic cup visible on the disc. Drusen indicate AMD and neovascularization the proliferative retinopathies, so neither characterizes glaucomatous damage.'),
('0aca3011-e2cd-5bcd-a471-c5a942fc8578'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is neovascular glaucoma?', '["Pseudoexfoliation glaucoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Angle-closure glaucoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glaucoma secondary to neovascularization of the iris (rubeosis iridis) and anterior chamber angle","often from diabetes or retinal vein occlusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary open-angle glaucoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glaucoma secondary to neovascularization of the iris (rubeosis iridis) and anterior chamber angle', 'Neovascular glaucoma arises when new vessels (rubeosis iridis) grow over the iris and anterior chamber angle, blocking aqueous outflow; it typically complicates diabetic retinopathy and central retinal vein occlusion. Primary open-angle, angle-closure, and pseudoexfoliation glaucomas have different mechanisms.'),
('66893321-bfd9-5025-acd9-ff53c8c8d46e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of uveitis?', '["or neutrophils (infectious) resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphocytic infiltration of the uveal tract; may have granulomas (sarcoidosis)","Granulomas mediated by specific receptor activation","plasma cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neutrophilic infiltration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocytic infiltration of the uveal tract; may have granulomas (sarcoidosis)', 'Uveitis is histologically an inflammatory infiltration of the uveal tract, chiefly lymphocytic and plasmacytic, with non-caseating granulomas in sarcoidosis; neutrophils predominate only in acute infectious cases. Neutrophilic infiltration alone therefore describes the infectious subset rather than uveitis in general.'),
('eaf731a0-c3bb-53a4-99c1-e096247556ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is a pterygium?', '["A corneal tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A pinguecula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fibrovascular growth of conjunctiva onto the cornea","dusty environments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","associated with UV exposure and dry leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrovascular growth of conjunctiva onto the cornea', 'A pterygium is a fibrovascular fold of conjunctiva, typically at the nasal limbus after UV and dusty environmental exposure, that grows onto the cornea and can threaten vision. A pinguecula stays within the conjunctiva and does not cross the limbus, and it is not a corneal tumor.'),
('e561ca50-fbe1-5ee6-ab33-8c9e0b79622c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is orbital cellulitis?', '["Endophthalmitis resulting in decreased functional activity","often from sinusitis; it is a medical emergency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scleritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection of the orbital tissues posterior to the orbital septum","Periorbital cellulitis (preseptal) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infection of the orbital tissues posterior to the orbital septum', 'Orbital cellulitis is an infection of the orbital tissues behind the orbital septum, usually spreading from sinusitis, and is an emergency because it threatens vision and may reach the cavernous sinus. Preseptal (periorbital) cellulitis is confined to tissues anterior to the septum, and scleritis or endophthalmitis affect other coats of the eye.'),
('904d7238-d094-5dab-a51d-9ff158997341'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the three parts of the ear?', '["inner ear","middle","External ear","middle ear","Outer"]'::jsonb, 'External ear', 'The ear has three principal divisions: external, middle, and inner. The external ear collects sound and comprises the auricle and the external auditory meatus, while the middle ear contains the ossicles and the inner ear houses the cochlea and vestibular apparatus.'),
('684edf8f-9cbe-577b-9de1-12f7ef1ecf68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the external ear composed of?', '["The auricle (elastic cartilage covered by skin) and the external auditory meatus (lined by skin with ceruminous glands)","Bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrocartilage mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyaline cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The auricle (elastic cartilage covered by skin) and the external auditory meatus (lined by skin with ceruminous glands)', 'The external ear consists of the auricle, made of elastic cartilage covered by skin, and the external auditory meatus, lined by skin bearing ceruminous and sebaceous glands that produce wax. It is not composed of bone, fibrocartilage, hyaline cartilage, or smooth muscle.'),
('6672830a-9e59-517a-a510-a55ec61454b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the tympanic membrane (eardrum)?', '["cone-shaped membrane separating the external and middle ear; it vibrates in response to sound","A thin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A solid bony wall mediated by specific receptor activation","A flat membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A thick cartilaginous structure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'cone-shaped membrane separating the external and middle ear; it vibrates in response to sound', 'The tympanic membrane is a thin, cone-shaped membrane at the inner end of the external auditory meatus that separates the external from the middle ear and vibrates with incoming sound. A solid bony wall would not vibrate, and the membrane is neither flat nor cartilaginous.'),
('62fbde36-86ea-5e02-b04d-77ca77783d36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the three auditory ossicles?', '["incus","Malleus","and stapes (stirrup); they transmit and amplify sound vibrations from the TM to the oval window","incus (anvil)","Malleus (hammer)"]'::jsonb, 'Malleus (hammer)', 'The three auditory ossicles are the malleus (hammer), incus (anvil), and stapes (stirrup), forming a lever chain that transmits and amplifies tympanic membrane vibrations to the oval window. The malleus articulates with the incus, which connects to the stapes.'),
('6750abed-e99d-586f-a1e0-89fbc75314c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the middle ear?', '["Transmits sound from the tympanic membrane to the inner ear via the ossicles; amplifies sound and matches impedance","Detects sound accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produces sound mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Secrets fluid without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regulates pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transmits sound from the tympanic membrane to the inner ear via the ossicles; amplifies sound and matches impedance', 'The middle ear transmits tympanic membrane vibration through the ossicular chain to the oval window, amplifying force to overcome the fluid impedance of the inner ear. It does not detect or produce sound; pressure equalization is handled by the eustachian tube opening from it.'),
('2a94726d-cc26-55e9-ad78-8bba120f9477'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the Eustachian (auditory) tube?', '["A canal connecting the middle ear to the nasopharynx that equalizes air pressure across the tympanic membrane","The ear canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A tube for tears mediated by specific receptor activation","A tube to the inner ear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A channel for sound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A canal connecting the middle ear to the nasopharynx that equalizes air pressure across the tympanic membrane', 'The eustachian tube connects the middle ear cavity to the nasopharynx and equalizes air pressure across the tympanic membrane so it can vibrate freely. The ear canal, nasolacrimal duct, and cochlear connections serve entirely different functions.'),
('8abe3228-1d4d-5ac4-881e-86e0d3b5d454'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the round window?', '["A semicircular canal opening resulting in decreased functional activity","A membrane-covered opening of the cochlea that allows displacement of perilymph when the stapes moves the oval window","The other oval window mediated by specific receptor activation","The eardrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cochlear duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A membrane-covered opening of the cochlea that allows displacement of perilymph when the stapes moves the oval window', 'The round window is a flexible, membrane-covered opening of the cochlea that lets perilymph move when the stapes pushes at the oval window, enabling pressure waves to propagate through the inner ear fluids. The oval window itself is the entrance, and the eardrum is the proximal receiver of sound.'),
('d30f4a8e-9619-5bca-afd3-f6198a7beec4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the oval window?', '["where the stapes footplate sits; it transmits vibrations to the perilymph","The eardrum","The round window","The semicircular canal opening","The opening between the middle ear and the inner ear (vestibule)"]'::jsonb, 'The opening between the middle ear and the inner ear (vestibule)', 'The oval window is a membrane-covered opening between the middle ear and the vestibule of the inner ear where the stapes footplate sits and transmits vibrations into the perilymph. The round window is the pressure-relief opening of the cochlea, and the eardrum is the proximal receiver of sound.'),
('781d66f9-d79c-5fd9-92a5-9d8a48417fd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the middle ear mucosa?', '["Pseudostratified ciliated columnar resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple squamous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stratified squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","A thin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","simple cuboidal to columnar epithelium (respiratory type) lining the middle ear cavity"]'::jsonb, 'simple cuboidal to columnar epithelium (respiratory type) lining the middle ear cavity', 'The middle ear cavity is lined by a thin simple cuboidal to columnar respiratory-type epithelium that may be ciliated and rests on a vascular lamina propria. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium typifies the conducting airways, while simple and stratified squamous epithelia are not the lining of the middle ear.'),
('5329eab9-4734-5f32-87f5-adb6f4b4c56e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is otitis media?', '["Inflammation of the ear canal","Inflammation of the inner ear","Inflammation of the auricle","Inflammation of the middle ear","most common in children; often caused by Eustachian tube dysfunction and bacterial infection"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the middle ear', 'Otitis media is inflammation of the middle ear cavity, most often in children and frequently triggered by Eustachian tube dysfunction with bacterial infection. Inflammation of the canal, inner ear, or auricle defines otitis externa, labyrinthitis, and perichondritis respectively.'),
('478bd8cb-7868-57e0-8b4d-0133b23dff66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the two parts of the inner ear (labyrinth)?', '["Utricle and saccule resulting in decreased functional activity","Vestibular labyrinth (balance) and cochlear labyrinth (hearing)","Semicircular canals and cochlea mediated by specific receptor activation","Vestibule and semicircular canals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cochlea and organ of Corti leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular labyrinth (balance) and cochlear labyrinth (hearing)', 'The inner ear labyrinth divides into a vestibular labyrinth, containing the utricle, saccule, and semicircular canals for balance, and a cochlear labyrinth for hearing. The other options list individual components or paired ending organs rather than the two overarching divisions.'),
('59c93974-eeba-503a-8323-0c64bec9c9da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the bony labyrinth?', '["and the cochlea resulting in decreased functional activity","The eardrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A system of channels in the temporal bone: the vestibule","The ossicles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","three semicircular canals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A system of channels in the temporal bone: the vestibule', 'The bony labyrinth is a system of channels and cavities carved within the petrous temporal bone, housing the vestibule, semicircular canals, and cochlea. The eardrum and ossicles belong to the middle ear, and the canals alone form only part of the structure.'),
('afcfc301-cd89-52dd-8a70-4df87a4720bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the membranous labyrinth?', '["The bony labyrinth resulting in decreased functional activity","The tympanic cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A continuous system of ducts and sacs within the bony labyrinth","The ossicles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","filled with endolymph and containing the sensory epithelia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A continuous system of ducts and sacs within the bony labyrinth', 'The membranous labyrinth is a continuous system of ducts and sacs suspended within the bony labyrinth, filled with endolymph and housing the sensory epithelia of balance and hearing. The bony labyrinth, tympanic cavity, and ossicles are separate structures.'),
('92e53eb9-6690-5042-9f79-177ac05ee824'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the cochlea?', '["The balance organ resulting in decreased functional activity","The middle ear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The external ear mediated by specific receptor activation","The spiral-shaped part of the inner ear responsible for hearing; it contains the organ of Corti","The vestibular organ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The spiral-shaped part of the inner ear responsible for hearing; it contains the organ of Corti', 'The cochlea is the spiral-shaped part of the inner ear that houses the organ of Corti and transduces sound into neural signals. The middle and external ears conduct sound, while the vestibular apparatus and balance organs mediate equilibrium.'),
('d03232fd-b37b-5201-92be-2a37360e02b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the three compartments of the cochlea (scalae)?', '["scala media (cochlear duct)","Scala vestibuli","Scala superior","media","and scala tympani"]'::jsonb, 'Scala vestibuli', 'The three cochlear compartments are the scala vestibuli, the scala media (cochlear duct), and the scala tympani. The scala vestibuli and scala tympani contain perilymph and meet near the helicotrema, while the scala media contains endolymph and the organ of Corti.'),
('70900fcf-b84d-584d-8265-64c47bebbf9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the organ of Corti?', '["The spiral ganglion resulting in decreased functional activity","The tectorial membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The vestibular organ mediated by specific receptor activation","The stria vascularis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sensory organ of hearing located on the basilar membrane within the cochlear duct"]'::jsonb, 'The sensory organ of hearing located on the basilar membrane within the cochlear duct', 'The organ of Corti is the sensory epithelium of hearing, resting on the basilar membrane within the cochlear duct and containing hair cells whose stereocilia contact the tectorial membrane. The spiral ganglion, stria vascularis, and tectorial membrane are distinct cochlear components.'),
('dd25bdb6-4f2f-5c39-97cc-dcd1c78fb0d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the hair cells of the cochlea?', '["Tectorial membrane cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Supporting cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Basilar membrane cells mediated by specific receptor activation","The sensory receptor cells of hearing; inner hair cells (IHCs) are the primary sensory cells","and outer hair cells (OHCs) amplify the signal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sensory receptor cells of hearing; inner hair cells (IHCs) are the primary sensory cells', 'Cochlear hair cells are the sensory receptor cells of hearing: inner hair cells generate the afferent signal, while outer hair cells amplify responses through electromotility. Supporting, basilar membrane, and tectorial membrane cells are structural rather than sensory.'),
('defa8a9a-5757-5da4-96db-78b2f7b80496'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the stereocilia of hair cells?', '["The mechanosensitive hair bundles on the apical surface of hair cells; they are embedded in the tectorial membrane (OHCs) or free-standing (IHCs)","Cilia in the respiratory tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flagella mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Microvilli without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","True cilia with 9+2 arrangement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mechanosensitive hair bundles on the apical surface of hair cells; they are embedded in the tectorial membrane (OHCs) or free-standing (IHCs)', 'Stereocilia are mechanosensitive, actin-filled hair bundles on the apical surface of hair cells; unlike true cilia they lack the 9+2 axoneme, and outer hair cell stereocilia are embedded in the tectorial membrane. Respiratory cilia, flagella, and microvilli are unrelated structures.'),
('9747cc4d-e7cc-511f-84d9-faff47421e2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the tectorial membrane?', '["The spiral ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Reissner''s membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The basilar membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","A gelatinous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","acellular membrane overlying the organ of Corti; the stereocilia of outer hair cells are embedded in it"]'::jsonb, 'acellular membrane overlying the organ of Corti; the stereocilia of outer hair cells are embedded in it', 'The tectorial membrane is a gelatinous, acellular membrane overlying the organ of Corti into which the stereocilia of outer hair cells are inserted. Reissner''s membrane and the basilar membrane are other boundaries of the cochlear duct, and the spiral ligament anchors the duct laterally.'),
('f6f36b9a-2dec-5a10-9d2a-dc109321af83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the basilar membrane?', '["Transmits sound to the ossicles resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Suspends the organ of Corti accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A structural membrane separating the scala media and scala tympani; its width and stiffness vary along the cochlea for frequency discrimination","Produces endolymph without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surrounds the hair cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A structural membrane separating the scala media and scala tympani; its width and stiffness vary along the cochlea for frequency discrimination', 'The basilar membrane separates the scala media from the scala tympani, supports the organ of Corti, and is wider and more compliant at the apex so that sound frequency is encoded along its length. It does not produce endolymph (a stria vascularis role) or transmit sound to the ossicles.'),
('1555bedc-3a52-5976-b724-75b301b78bc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the tonotopic organization of the cochlea?', '["low frequencies at the apex resulting in decreased functional activity","The mapping of sound frequency along the basilar membrane: high frequencies at the base","All frequencies at the apex mediated by specific receptor activation","All frequencies at the base without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Random arrangement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mapping of sound frequency along the basilar membrane: high frequencies at the base', 'Tonotopy is the spatial mapping of sound frequency along the basilar membrane: high frequencies excite the stiff base and low frequencies the compliant apex. This graded stiffness establishes pitch discrimination in hair cells and the auditory pathway.'),
('68772840-ac21-5dcc-bdeb-738e6e297def'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the role of outer hair cells in hearing?', '["Produce endolymph resulting in decreased functional activity","enhancing sensitivity and frequency selectivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Support inner hair cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Electromotility: They amplify and sharpen the vibration of the basilar membrane","Transduce sound directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Electromotility: They amplify and sharpen the vibration of the basilar membrane', 'Outer hair cells amplify and sharpen basilar membrane vibration through electromotility, enhancing sensitivity and frequency selectivity. They do not directly transduce the afferent signal (inner hair cells do) and they do not produce endolymph.'),
('842b3123-afbe-512d-acde-e3c47a322abb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the role of the stria vascularis?', '["Produces perilymph resulting in decreased functional activity","Supports the organ of Corti accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produces endolymph by actively secreting K+ and maintaining the electrochemical gradient necessary for hair cell function","Drains endolymph without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transmits sound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Produces endolymph by actively secreting K+ and maintaining the electrochemical gradient necessary for hair cell function', 'The stria vascularis secretes endolymph, actively pumping K+ from its marginal cells to create the high-K+, high-positive potential of the scala media needed for hair cell transduction. Perilymph arises from blood plasma filtration rather than the stria.'),
('bf0ac24a-c676-5225-86b3-0380303aba1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the endocochlear potential?', '["The +80 mV potential in the scala media (endolymph) compared to the perilymph; it drives K+ into hair cells during mechanotransduction","The cochlear microphonic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The resting membrane potential of hair cells mediated by specific receptor activation","The sound pressure level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The action potential of the auditory nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The +80 mV potential in the scala media (endolymph) compared to the perilymph; it drives K+ into hair cells during mechanotransduction', 'The endocochlear potential is the roughly +80 mV voltage of endolymph in the scala media relative to perilymph, generated by the stria vascularis, which drives K+ into hair cells when mechanosensitive channels open. It is distinct from the cochlear microphonic and the auditory nerve action potential.'),
('4fbe8203-8e71-5e3c-94ad-869473878f94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the mechanotransduction process in hair cells?', '["allowing K+ influx resulting in decreased functional activity","Na+ influx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stereocilia deflection opens K+ channels at the tips","and neurotransmitter release without affecting the primary tissue pathway","depolarization leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stereocilia deflection opens K+ channels at the tips', 'Deflection of stereocilia toward the tallest row stretches tip links and opens mechanically gated K+ channels at the tips, allowing K+ influx that depolarizes the hair cell and triggers neurotransmitter release. This K+-driven mechanism distinguishes auditory transduction from Na+-dependent signaling.'),
('b057865d-e5ec-583e-88a5-48f9b8b5882b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the auditory nerve (CN VIII)?', '["The trigeminal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The nerve carrying sound information from the cochlea (spiral ganglion neurons) to the cochlear nuclei in the brainstem","The optic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The facial nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vagus nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The nerve carrying sound information from the cochlea (spiral ganglion neurons) to the cochlear nuclei in the brainstem', 'The auditory (vestibulocochlear) nerve carries sound information from the spiral ganglion to the cochlear nuclei in the brainstem. The trigeminal, optic, facial, and vagus nerves subserve other modalities.'),
('507f105e-d448-57cc-8afc-91a64ddc6a01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the central auditory pathway?', '["crossing of fibers resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Direct to thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cochlear nucleus → superior olivary complex → lateral lemniscus → inferior colliculus → medial geniculate nucleus → primary auditory cortex (A1)","Cochlea → brainstem → cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Crosses at the cochlear nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear nucleus → superior olivary complex → lateral lemniscus → inferior colliculus → medial geniculate nucleus → primary auditory cortex (A1)', 'The ascending auditory pathway runs from the cochlear nuclei through the superior olivary complex and lateral lemniscus to the inferior colliculus, then via the medial geniculate nucleus to the primary auditory cortex. Most fibers cross, but not at the cochlear nucleus alone, and each side receives input from both ears.'),
('c0a11241-7993-5cb4-a4da-3473fa37ec8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is sensorineural hearing loss?', '["typically high-frequency loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Mixed hearing loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hearing loss due to damage to the cochlea (hair cells) or the cochlear nerve (CN VIII)","Central hearing loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conductive hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hearing loss due to damage to the cochlea (hair cells) or the cochlear nerve (CN VIII)', 'Sensorineural hearing loss results from damage to cochlear hair cells or the cochlear nerve (CN VIII). Conductive loss arises from external or middle ear disease, while central loss localizes above the brainstem nuclei.'),
('778e16d9-6412-5e69-9a3c-97b2ada65896'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the vestibular apparatus of the inner ear?', '["saccule","and three semicircular canals; they detect head position and movement for balance","The organ of Corti","The utricle","The cochlea"]'::jsonb, 'The utricle', 'The vestibular apparatus comprises the utricle, saccule, and three semicircular canals, which detect head position and movement for balance. The cochlea and organ of Corti subserve hearing rather than equilibrium.'),
('c7e4ac6b-615b-5eb4-b96e-438fa1b025be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the utricle and saccule?', '["Transmit sound resulting in decreased functional activity","Detect rotational movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Detect linear acceleration and gravity (head tilt); they contain the macula sensory epithelium with otoliths","Support the cochlea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produce endolymph leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detect linear acceleration and gravity (head tilt); they contain the macula sensory epithelium with otoliths', 'The utricle and saccule detect linear acceleration and gravity via their maculae, where hair cells lie beneath an otolithic membrane bearing calcium carbonate crystals. Rotational movement is sensed instead by the cristae of the semicircular canals.'),
('6f38de2c-d2b7-55d4-abd0-69ea27ef25bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the maculae of the utricle and saccule?', '["The basilar membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory regions with hair cells covered by an otolithic membrane containing calcium carbonate crystals (otoliths)","The semicircular canal ampullae mediated by specific receptor activation","The crista ampullaris without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The organ of Corti leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory regions with hair cells covered by an otolithic membrane containing calcium carbonate crystals (otoliths)', 'Each macula is a sensory epithelium whose hair cells are covered by an otolithic membrane containing calcium carbonate otoliths that shift with gravity and linear acceleration. The crista ampullaris and organ of Corti are different sensors, and the basilar membrane is not a sensory region.'),
('f44754eb-b10c-54e7-9384-e0ea1aeeecd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the crista ampullaris?', '["The sensory organ in the ampulla (dilated part) of each semicircular canal; it detects rotational acceleration","The organ of Corti accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The macula of the utricle mediated by specific receptor activation","The macula of the saccule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vestibular nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sensory organ in the ampulla (dilated part) of each semicircular canal; it detects rotational acceleration', 'The crista ampullaris is the sensory ridge in the ampulla of each semicircular canal, capped by a cupula, that signals rotational (angular) acceleration. The maculae of the utricle and saccule detect linear forces, and the organ of Corti detects sound.'),
('e4802419-0b77-5f32-8f86-ffba0e2c6e25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the cupula?', '["dome-shaped structure that overlies the crista ampullaris; it is deflected by endolymph movement during head rotation","The otolithic membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reissner''s membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","A gelatinous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The tectorial membrane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'dome-shaped structure that overlies the crista ampullaris; it is deflected by endolymph movement during head rotation', 'The cupula is a gelatinous, dome-shaped structure overlying each crista ampullaris that is deflected by endolymph movement during head rotation. It is neither the otolithic membrane of the maculae nor the cochlear Reissner''s or tectorial membranes.'),
('b2ece356-66b7-5d55-b9a7-7d230b9bbd39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the cupula?', '["Produces endolymph","Deflects when the head rotates due to inertia of the endolymph","Detects linear acceleration","bending hair cell stereocilia and signaling rotational acceleration","Filters sound"]'::jsonb, 'Deflects when the head rotates due to inertia of the endolymph', 'During head rotation, inertia of the endolymph deflects the cupula, bending hair cell stereocilia, which signal rotational acceleration. It neither produces endolymph nor filters sound, and linear acceleration is detected by the maculae.'),
('7b8644c9-6cf3-57aa-8331-3e5cfcc8e022'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is nystagmus?', '["An involuntary rhythmic oscillation of the eyes","often seen in vestibular disorders; the fast phase is toward the side of the lesion (or opposite)","A rapid eye movement","A conjugate gaze palsy","A type of strabismus"]'::jsonb, 'An involuntary rhythmic oscillation of the eyes', 'Nystagmus is an involuntary rhythmic oscillation of the eyes with slow and fast phases, a hallmark of vestibular imbalance. It is neither a conjugate gaze palsy nor a form of strabismus, and it differs from a simple saccadic eye movement.'),
('8b6baa22-b6b1-5495-9350-ea5c15da6ab6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV)?', '["caused by dislodged otoliths migrating into a semicircular canal (canalithiasis)","intense vertigo triggered by head position changes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A brief mediated by specific receptor activation","Meniere disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vestibular neuritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'caused by dislodged otoliths migrating into a semicircular canal (canalithiasis)', 'BPPV produces brief, intense vertigo triggered by head position changes when dislodged otoliths (canalithiasis) migrate into a semicircular canal and spuriously stimulate the crista. Meniere disease and vestibular neuritis are distinct inner ear disorders.'),
('837c72e1-d5bc-5963-be68-909ba48b17fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is Meniere disease?', '["and aural fullness; associated with endolymphatic hydrops","A disorder of the inner ear with episodic vertigo","fluctuating hearing loss","BPPV","tinnitus"]'::jsonb, 'A disorder of the inner ear with episodic vertigo', 'Meniere disease is an inner ear disorder with episodic vertigo, fluctuating hearing loss, tinnitus, and aural fullness, attributed to endolymphatic hydrops. BPPV is strictly positional and does not carry the auditory symptoms.'),
('a77afb6f-c806-527a-9b31-72327bb9b8cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is presbycusis?', '["Middle ear infection","Ear wax impaction","Age-related sensorineural hearing loss","primarily due to degeneration of hair cells (especially OHCs) in the cochlear base (high frequency)","Conductive hearing loss"]'::jsonb, 'Age-related sensorineural hearing loss', 'Presbycusis is age-related sensorineural hearing loss, mainly from degeneration of hair cells, especially outer hair cells, at the cochlear base, producing preferential high-frequency loss. Middle ear infection, wax impaction, and conductive losses do not explain this pattern.'),
('bad2fd93-bbe9-5350-8a29-6eeb8089bad5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is otosclerosis?', '["most commonly fixing the stapes footplate in the oval window","Sensorineural hearing loss","A disorder of abnormal bone remodeling in the otic capsule","causing conductive hearing loss","Meniere disease"]'::jsonb, 'A disorder of abnormal bone remodeling in the otic capsule', 'Otosclerosis is abnormal bone remodeling of the otic capsule that most often fixes the stapes footplate in the oval window, producing conductive hearing loss. It is not sensorineural, and Meniere disease involves the inner ear differently.'),
('6f130de2-3fc7-5be0-b305-823aeba31754'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of otosclerosis?', '["Osteogenesis imperfecta resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Paget disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteoporosis mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone resorption by osteoclasts followed by abnormal new bone deposition (woven bone) with increased vascularity and fibrosis","Normal bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone resorption by osteoclasts followed by abnormal new bone deposition (woven bone) with increased vascularity and fibrosis', 'Otosclerosis histology shows osteoclast-mediated resorption followed by deposition of vascular, fibrous woven bone within the otic capsule. Osteogenesis imperfecta, Paget disease, and osteoporosis are broader bone disorders and do not localize to the otic capsule.'),
('9e953210-cd0a-5192-b190-ab0bebddbb6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is labyrinthitis?', '["Inflammation of the inner ear","from viral or bacterial infection","hearing loss","and nystagmus","causing vertigo"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the inner ear', 'Labyrinthitis is inflammation of the inner ear, usually viral or bacterial, presenting with vertigo, hearing loss, and nystagmus. Otitis media involves only the middle ear cavity.'),
('45bcc825-ae27-541f-a74b-7810d6df6b8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma)?', '["A malignant tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A glomus jugulare tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A meningioma mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign Schwann cell tumor of the vestibular portion of CN VIII","causing unilateral sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign Schwann cell tumor of the vestibular portion of CN VIII', 'A vestibular schwannoma is a benign Schwann cell tumor of the vestibular division of CN VIII that typically causes unilateral sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus. It is not a malignant tumor, meningioma, or glomus jugulare tumor.'),
('81b279d9-21e0-512a-a325-2a2d3d21930e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is cholesteatoma?', '["A true tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","often from chronic otitis media or TM retraction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A destructive mediated by specific receptor activation","keratinizing squamous epithelial lesion in the middle ear and mastoid","A malignancy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'keratinizing squamous epithelial lesion in the middle ear and mastoid', 'Cholesteatoma is a destructive, keratinizing squamous epithelial lesion of the middle ear and mastoid, often arising from chronic otitis media or tympanic membrane retraction. It is not a true tumor or malignancy and contains no neoplastic cells.'),
('72f85350-fc4a-5914-ba18-fbbe932d1a99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of cholesteatoma?', '["Respiratory epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Granulomas mediated by specific receptor activation","Necrotic tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Keratinizing stratified squamous epithelium with a stroma of granulation tissue; keratin debris in the lumen"]'::jsonb, 'Keratinizing stratified squamous epithelium with a stroma of granulation tissue; keratin debris in the lumen', 'Cholesteatoma histology reveals keratinizing stratified squamous epithelium overlying a stroma of granulation tissue, with keratin debris in the lumen. Respiratory epithelium, tumor cells, granulomas, and necrotic tissue do not characterize this lesion.'),
('df8865e1-f7cf-59e8-80e9-45748ea65cfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the most common cause of hearing loss in children?', '["Otitis media with effusion (serous otitis media)","Congenital hearing loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerumen impaction mediated by specific receptor activation","Presbycusis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Noise exposure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Otitis media with effusion (serous otitis media)', 'Otitis media with effusion (serous otitis media) is the most common cause of hearing loss in children, usually a mild conductive loss from Eustachian tube dysfunction. Presbycusis affects the elderly, while congenital loss, wax impaction, and noise exposure are less common.'),
('15de2dc1-7f8e-5c7f-b878-90579be43c5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the treatment for bilateral sensorineural hearing loss?', '["Vestibular therapy resulting in decreased functional activity","Surgery only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medical therapy mediated by specific receptor activation","treatment available without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cochlear implants (for severe/profound loss) or hearing aids (for mild/moderate loss)"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear implants (for severe/profound loss) or hearing aids (for mild/moderate loss)', 'For bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, hearing aids serve mild-to-moderate loss and cochlear implants benefit severe-to-profound loss by electrically stimulating the cochlear nerve. Vestibular therapy, surgery alone, and simple medical therapy do not restore auditory transduction.'),
('73befcb7-f262-5af6-b1cc-894c62b1688c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the olfactory epithelium?', '["Respiratory epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Stratified squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple cuboidal epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","A specialized pseudostratified columnar epithelium in the roof of the nasal cavity containing olfactory receptor neurons","Transitional epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A specialized pseudostratified columnar epithelium in the roof of the nasal cavity containing olfactory receptor neurons', 'The olfactory epithelium is a specialized pseudostratified columnar epithelium in the roof of the nasal cavity that contains olfactory receptor neurons, supporting cells, and basal cells. Respiratory, stratified squamous, cuboidal, and transitional epithelia line other regions.'),
('7a0de9d0-78d4-53c3-8abf-e1634b7528f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the three cell types in the olfactory epithelium?', '["goblet","Olfactory receptor neurons","supporting (sustentacular) cells","Ciliated","and basal cells"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory receptor neurons', 'The olfactory epithelium contains three cell types: olfactory receptor neurons, supporting (sustentacular) cells, and basal stem cells. Goblet cells belong to the respiratory epithelium rather than the olfactory neuroepithelium.'),
('bc6dd287-d18f-563e-b740-d2a4f6d7bd3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are olfactory receptor neurons (ORNs)?', '["Bowman''s gland cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bipolar neurons with ciliated dendrites that detect odors; they are the first-order neurons of the olfactory pathway","Supporting cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trigeminal nerve endings leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bipolar neurons with ciliated dendrites that detect odors; they are the first-order neurons of the olfactory pathway', 'Olfactory receptor neurons are bipolar neurons with ciliated dendrites that bind odorants and project axons through the cribriform plate to the olfactory bulb as the first-order neurons. Bowman''s gland cells, basal cells, and supporting cells are accessory, and trigeminal endings mediate general nasal sensation.'),
('c2fd0721-c7be-54d2-909a-c143e9bfe459'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the olfactory cilia?', '["Motile cilia resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Microvilli accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stereocilia mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The site of odorant binding; they extend from the dendritic knob of the olfactory receptor neuron into the mucus","Flagella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The site of odorant binding; they extend from the dendritic knob of the olfactory receptor neuron into the mucus', 'Olfactory cilia are non-motile processes extending from the dendritic knob into the mucus, where odorant receptors are housed; they lack the 9+2 axoneme. True cilia, microvilli, and stereocilia do not serve odorant binding.'),
('4bc0846d-a922-5b85-818f-6a12ac8c47aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are Bowman''s glands (olfactory glands)?', '["Sweat glands resulting in decreased functional activity","Sebaceous glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serous glands in the olfactory mucosa that secrete mucus to dissolve odorants and protect the epithelium","Lacrimal glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Salivary glands of the nose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Serous glands in the olfactory mucosa that secrete mucus to dissolve odorants and protect the epithelium', 'Bowman''s glands are serous glands in the olfactory mucosa that produce mucus, dissolving odorants so they can reach receptors and protecting the neuroepithelium. Sweat, sebaceous, and lacrimal glands do not serve olfaction.'),
('f6948c63-6fd1-5f7c-8714-6466f28c0b36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the olfactory transduction pathway?', '["Odorant binds to GPCR → G protein (Golf) → adenylate cyclase → cAMP → CNG channel opens → Na+/Ca2+ influx → depolarization → action potential","Cilia beat faster accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Second messenger involving IP3 mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct ion channel opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperpolarization leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Odorant binds to GPCR → G protein (Golf) → adenylate cyclase → cAMP → CNG channel opens → Na+/Ca2+ influx → depolarization → action potential', 'Odorant binding to a GPCR activates the G protein Golf, which raises cAMP and opens cyclic-nucleotide-gated channels, admitting Na+ and Ca2+ to depolarize the neuron. This contrasts with the hyperpolarizing phototransduction cascade and does not depend on ciliary beating.'),
('9caa630b-47bb-5fdc-b235-a6ae7042acc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the olfactory bulb?', '["The first relay station in the olfactory pathway; it receives input from ORNs and processes odor information","The piriform cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The olfactory tract mediated by specific receptor activation","The nasal mucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The olfactory nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The first relay station in the olfactory pathway; it receives input from ORNs and processes odor information', 'The olfactory bulb is the first relay station of the olfactory pathway, receiving olfactory receptor neuron axons at glomeruli and processing odor information via mitral and tufted cells before relaying centrally. The piriform cortex is the primary cortical area downstream of the bulb.'),
('0cc1e397-bc82-5b24-b075-6beb40db97f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are glomeruli in the olfactory bulb?', '["Lymphoid follicles resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Supporting cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clusters of neurons mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spherical structures where olfactory receptor neuron axons synapse with mitral and tufted cell dendrites; each glomerulus receives input from one odorant receptor type"]'::jsonb, 'Spherical structures where olfactory receptor neuron axons synapse with mitral and tufted cell dendrites; each glomerulus receives input from one odorant receptor type', 'Glomeruli are spherical neuropil regions of the olfactory bulb where olfactory receptor neuron axons synapse with mitral and tufted cell dendrites, each glomerulus dedicated to a single odorant receptor type. They are neither lymphoid follicles, blood vessels, nor simple neuron clusters.'),
('7035c827-d472-5368-b7f4-5a4d67224ddb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is anosmia?', '["Loss of vision resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of hearing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of touch mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of taste without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The loss of the sense of smell; can be conductive (obstruction) or sensorineural (damage to the olfactory epithelium or nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'The loss of the sense of smell; can be conductive (obstruction) or sensorineural (damage to the olfactory epithelium or nerve)', 'Anosmia is loss of the sense of smell, caused either by nasal obstruction (conductive) or damage to the olfactory epithelium or nerve (sensorineural). Loss of vision, hearing, touch, or taste are separate sensory modality deficits.'),
('77a1f349-0386-5ec8-8018-0bda2a85de0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the five basic taste modalities?', '["sour","and umami (savory)","Sweet","salty","bitter"]'::jsonb, 'Sweet', 'The five basic taste modalities are sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami (savory). Sweet is one of these fundamental taste categories, matching the set that also includes the other four listed options.'),
('ade7cfcf-1a63-5a54-81ae-e5055c3c6a10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the receptors for sweet taste?', '["TRPM5 channels resulting in decreased functional activity","T2R family accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PKD2L1 channels mediated by specific receptor activation","ENaC channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T1R2/T1R3 heterodimer (G-protein coupled receptor)"]'::jsonb, 'T1R2/T1R3 heterodimer (G-protein coupled receptor)', 'Sweet taste is signaled by the T1R2/T1R3 heterodimeric G-protein-coupled receptor. T2R receptors mediate bitter taste, ENaC transduces salty taste, PKD2L1 relates to sour, and TRPM5 is a downstream cation channel rather than the receptor.'),
('b1cc7618-18db-54ba-a244-df448f8b1b82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the receptors for umami taste?', '["ENaC channels","responding to L-glutamate and 5''-ribonucleotides","T1R1/T1R3 heterodimer","T2R family","PKD2L1"]'::jsonb, 'T1R1/T1R3 heterodimer', 'Umami is detected by the T1R1/T1R3 heterodimer, which responds to L-glutamate and 5''-ribonucleotides. T2R receptors signal bitter, ENaC transduces salty, and PKD2L1 mediates sour taste.'),
('61d2fc7f-f8a4-50f4-a396-b69612a6300b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the receptors for bitter taste?', '["ENaC channels resulting in decreased functional activity","TRPM5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T1R family mediated by specific receptor activation","T2R family (about 25 different GPCRs) that recognize many structurally diverse bitter compounds","PKD2L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T2R family (about 25 different GPCRs) that recognize many structurally diverse bitter compounds', 'Bitter compounds activate T2R (TAS2R) family G-protein-coupled receptors on taste cells, a family that recognizes a wide range of structurally diverse bitter molecules. Sweet and umami tastes rely on the T1R receptors, salt on ENaC channels, and sour on PKD2L1-mediated proton entry.'),
('ffdc0d7e-d4d6-5498-a5e8-43ffe742b475'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'How is salt taste detected?', '["PKD channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Sodium ions enter taste cells via ENaC (amiloride-sensitive epithelial sodium channels)","GPCRs mediated by specific receptor activation","TRP channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CNG channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium ions enter taste cells via ENaC (amiloride-sensitive epithelial sodium channels)', 'Salty taste arises when Na+ enters taste cells directly through amiloride-sensitive ENaC channels and depolarizes them. The other channels listed participate in different taste or sensory transduction pathways rather than salt detection.'),
('af7afa4c-d66a-51a4-888b-9cf4b0c7e04b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'How is sour taste detected?', '["ENaC channels resulting in decreased functional activity","GPCRs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TRPM5 channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Protons (H+) enter via PKD2L1 channels or block K+ channels","causing depolarization of Type III taste cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protons (H+) enter via PKD2L1 channels or block K+ channels', 'Acids release protons that either enter the type III taste cell through PKD2L1 channels or block K+ channels, depolarizing the cell and triggering sour perception. ENaC and GPCR pathways mediate other taste qualities such as salt, sweet, and bitter.'),
('d36986f0-ef8c-5b28-a02d-0a5075584dd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the cranial nerves involved in taste?', '["CN VII (facial nerve): anterior 2/3 of tongue; CN IX (glossopharyngeal): posterior 1/3; CN X (vagus): epiglottis and pharynx","CN V (trigeminal) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CN IX mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CN VII without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only CN X leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII (facial nerve): anterior 2/3 of tongue; CN IX (glossopharyngeal): posterior 1/3; CN X (vagus): epiglottis and pharynx', 'Taste is carried by three nerves: the chorda tympani of CN VII serves the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, CN IX the posterior one-third, and CN X the epiglottis and pharynx. The trigeminal nerve carries only general sensation, not taste.'),
('70276927-b6d1-5388-a6a3-b93927326b99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the nucleus of the solitary tract (NST)?', '["The salivary nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The thalamic taste nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The first brainstem relay for taste; it receives input from all three taste nerves and also processes visceral sensory information","The cortical taste area without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The trigeminal nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The first brainstem relay for taste; it receives input from all three taste nerves and also processes visceral sensory information', 'The nucleus of the solitary tract is the first central relay for gustatory afferents from CN VII, IX, and X, and it also handles visceral sensation. It projects to the VPM thalamus before signals reach the gustatory cortex.'),
('dacc5625-f43d-562d-a790-87c661992f09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the ventral posteromedial (VPM) nucleus of the thalamus?', '["An olfactory nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","A visual nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An auditory nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","A motor nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The thalamic relay for taste information from the NST to the gustatory cortex"]'::jsonb, 'The thalamic relay for taste information from the NST to the gustatory cortex', 'The ventral posteromedial nucleus of the thalamus relays taste signals from the nucleus of the solitary tract to the gustatory cortex. It is not involved in olfaction, vision, hearing, or motor function.'),
('3a243a2b-0423-5d55-82ba-3a9dc7197ee0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the primary gustatory cortex?', '["The hippocampus resulting in decreased functional activity","The insula and the frontal operculum (Brodmann area 43) that processes taste perception","The temporal lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","The postcentral gyrus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The occipital lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The insula and the frontal operculum (Brodmann area 43) that processes taste perception', 'The primary gustatory cortex lies in the insula and the frontal operculum (Brodmann area 43), where taste perception is processed. The other regions serve memory, hearing, touch, and vision rather than gustation.'),
('48167cf3-1ebf-5a40-b119-f6a3804d0e49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is ageusia?', '["Loss of taste and smell","Loss of smell","The loss of the sense of taste","Loss of hearing","A type of aphasia"]'::jsonb, 'The loss of the sense of taste', 'Ageusia is the loss of the sense of taste, whereas loss of smell is anosmia and loss of hearing is deafness. It is a sensory disturbance of gustation, not a language disorder.'),
('8bb0c8ce-1ed4-5933-a306-6a03cd05db1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the filiform papillae?', '["keratinized papillae covering most of the tongue''s dorsal surface; they have taste buds","The largest papillae","conical","Taste-bearing papillae","Small"]'::jsonb, 'Small', 'Filiform papillae are the numerous small, conical, keratinized projections that cover most of the dorsal surface of the tongue; unlike the other papilla types they contain no taste buds. Their roughness aids in moving food during mastication.'),
('8ca9945b-969e-559e-972e-f967567a1daa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are fungiform papillae?', '["each containing 1-8 taste buds resulting in decreased functional activity","Foliate papillae on the sides accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filiform papillae mediated by specific receptor activation","Large papillae at the back without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mushroom-shaped papillae on the anterior 2/3 of the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Mushroom-shaped papillae on the anterior 2/3 of the tongue', 'Fungiform papillae are mushroom-shaped structures scattered over the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and each contains one to eight taste buds. Their reddish color reflects the rich capillary network beneath. They are distinct from the large V-shaped circumvallate papillae at the back.'),
('c0c34446-13a7-5b24-8863-ceb34c26c6a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are circumvallate papillae?', '["Small papillae at the front","Filiform papillae","Large","Fungiform papillae","dome-shaped papillae (8-12 in number) arranged in aV-shape at the posterior 1/3 of the tongue; each contains hundreds of taste buds"]'::jsonb, 'Large', 'Circumvallate papillae are the largest lingual papillae, forming 8-12 dome-shaped structures arranged in aV just anterior to the sulcus terminalis, each bearing hundreds of taste buds in its trench-like lateral walls. Filiform papillae, by contrast, are small conical projections without taste buds.'),
('192363aa-6dbe-506a-a025-861149d4d41d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are foliate papillae?', '["Vallate papillae resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Leaf-shaped ridges on the posterolateral tongue margins; they contain taste buds (especially in children) but atrophy with age","Taste buds of the palate mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Fungiform papillae without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The largest papillae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leaf-shaped ridges on the posterolateral tongue margins; they contain taste buds (especially in children) but atrophy with age', 'Foliate papillae are leaf-shaped ridges found along the posterolateral margins of the tongue; they carry taste buds, most numerous in childhood, and they atrophy with advancing age. Vallate and fungiform papillae occupy other regions of the tongue.'),
('090240ae-e7cb-5c6a-8799-2e13c7756b32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the orbit?', '["and lacrimal apparatus","nerves","The bony cavity containing the eye","vessels","extraocular muscles"]'::jsonb, 'The bony cavity containing the eye', 'The orbit is the pyramidal bony cavity of the skull that houses the eyeball along with its extraocular muscles, vessels, and nerves. Its walls are formed by two cranial and five facial bones.'),
('77e58a9c-5b12-537b-b1dd-867025e5da1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the medial rectus?', '["Elevates the eye resulting in decreased functional activity","Adducts the eye (turns it inward toward the nose)","Abducts the eye mediated by specific receptor activation","Depresses the eye without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rotates the eye leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adducts the eye (turns it inward toward the nose)', 'The medial rectus adducts the eye, turning the cornea inward toward the nose, and it is innervated by the oculomotor nerve (CN III). Loss of its action in a complete CN III palsy leaves the eye deviated laterally by the unopposed lateral rectus.'),
('7ad859e7-c7b3-5362-89a0-9cfcd751abe0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the lateral rectus?', '["Elevates the eye resulting in decreased functional activity","Intorts the eye accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Depresses the eye mediated by specific receptor activation","Abducts the eye (turns it outward from the nose)","Adducts the eye leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abducts the eye (turns it outward from the nose)', 'The lateral rectus abducts the eye, moving the cornea outward away from the nose, and it is supplied by the abducens nerve (CN VI). A CN VI palsy therefore leaves the affected eye turned medially at rest.'),
('1964f84f-f248-58e8-b840-17a0a1796566'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the superior rectus?', '["and intorts the eye (primary action: elevation)","Depresses the eye","Elevates","Abducts the eye","adducts"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates', 'The superior rectus primarily elevates the eye and secondarily adducts and intorts it; it is innervated by CN III. The superior oblique, not the superior rectus, is the main depressor of the already adducted eye.'),
('495c1812-be60-594c-b5b1-2ab0dc9af9c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the inferior rectus?', '["and extorts the eye (primary action: depression)","Elevates the eye","Adducts the eye","adducts","Depresses"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses', 'The inferior rectus primarily depresses the eye and also adducts and extorts it, and it is innervated by CN III. It works with the superior oblique, which depresses the eye when the eye is adducted.'),
('397d2631-8cec-534e-a1d9-5297abfc13d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the superior oblique?', '["Intorts","Adducts the eye","Elevates the eye","and abducts the eye (primary action: intorsion)","depresses"]'::jsonb, 'Intorts', 'The superior oblique primarily intorts (medially rotates) the eye and secondarily depresses and abducts it; it is innervated by the trochlear nerve (CN IV). Trochlear palsy therefore produces extorsion of the affected eye and difficulty looking downward when the eye is adducted.'),
('10270aa7-4d4d-50e6-a8cd-ad82a057b3c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the inferior oblique?', '["and abducts the eye (primary action: extorsion)","elevates","Intorts the eye","Adducts the eye","Extorts"]'::jsonb, 'Extorts', 'The inferior oblique primarily extorts (laterally rotates) the eye and secondarily elevates and abducts it; it is innervated by CN III. It is the antagonist of the superior oblique for torsional movements.'),
('985f4a7a-5e61-5c76-b379-359357f62dce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What type of epithelium lines the conjunctiva?', '["Stratified columnar epithelium (bulbar and palpebral) with goblet cells","Simple squamous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pseudostratified columnar mediated by specific receptor activation","Transitional without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stratified squamous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified columnar epithelium (bulbar and palpebral) with goblet cells', 'The conjunctiva is lined by stratified columnar epithelium containing goblet cells whose mucus contributes to the inner mucin layer of the tear film. Simple squamous, pseudostratified, and transitional epithelia are not characteristic of this membrane.'),
('b2680e93-8e72-5ae9-8f3f-13b34bad731a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the glands of Krause?', '["Zeis glands resulting in decreased functional activity","Lacrimal glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meibomian glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Accessory lacrimal glands in the conjunctival fornices that contribute to basal tear production","Moll glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accessory lacrimal glands in the conjunctival fornices that contribute to basal tear production', 'The glands of Krause are accessory lacrimal glands located in the conjunctival fornices that help maintain the basal aqueous tear film. They are distinct from the eyelid-margin glands (Zeis, Moll, and meibomian) and from the main lacrimal gland.'),
('47c8f3c0-0eee-5c10-b504-3a9e0976ee7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the layers of the tear film?', '["Only aqueous","inner mucin layer (goblet cells)","Only mucin","middle aqueous layer (lacrimal and accessory glands)","Outer lipid layer (meibomian glands)"]'::jsonb, 'Outer lipid layer (meibomian glands)', 'The tear film is arranged in three layers: an outer lipid layer secreted by the meibomian glands that retards evaporation, a middle aqueous layer from the lacrimal and accessory glands, and an inner mucin layer from the conjunctival goblet cells that anchors the film to the cornea.'),
('d5f887c6-c72e-52d2-8ef7-99af89851d26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the tear drainage pathway?', '["To the mouth resulting in decreased functional activity","To the ear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lacrimal puncta → canaliculi → lacrimal sac → nasolacrimal duct → inferior meatus of the nose","To the paranasal sinuses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Directly to the throat leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lacrimal puncta → canaliculi → lacrimal sac → nasolacrimal duct → inferior meatus of the nose', 'Tears drain from the lacrimal puncta into the canaliculi, pass through the lacrimal sac and nasolacrimal duct, and empty into the inferior meatus of the nose under the inferior nasal concha. This explains epiphora and nasolacrimal obstruction problems.'),
('72937325-c5ce-589c-84d6-5edb7a238977'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the blood supply to the eye?', '["short and long posterior ciliary arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxillary artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ophthalmic artery (branch of internal carotid) → central retinal artery","External carotid artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and anterior ciliary arteries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery (branch of internal carotid) → central retinal artery', 'The eye is supplied by the ophthalmic artery, a branch of the internal carotid artery; its central retinal artery nourishes the inner retina, while the posterior and anterior ciliary arteries supply the choroid and other coats. The external carotid and maxillary arteries do not supply the eye directly.'),
('42428aa6-ea9e-5f6d-a630-0f7202b352a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the blood-retinal barrier?', '["Tight junctions between retinal capillary endothelial cells (inner) and between RPE cells (outer) that prevent leakage into the retina","The vitreous base accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The choroid mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The sclera without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lens capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between retinal capillary endothelial cells (inner) and between RPE cells (outer) that prevent leakage into the retina', 'The blood-retinal barrier is formed by tight junctions between retinal capillary endothelial cells on the inner side and between retinal pigment epithelial cells on the outer side, preventing fluid and macromolecules from leaking into the retina. Disruption of this barrier contributes to macular edema.'),
('85411013-d8f6-5ebb-a50a-ed79d1683ce6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the difference between the lens and the cornea regarding refraction?', '["The cornea provides 2/3 of the eye''s refractive power","The cornea provides 1/3","which is adjustable (accommodation)","The lens provides 2/3","and the lens provides the remaining 1/3"]'::jsonb, 'The cornea provides 2/3 of the eye''s refractive power', 'The cornea, because of its curved air-to-fluid interface, supplies roughly two-thirds of the eye''s refractive power, whereas the lens contributes the remaining one-third and is the component that changes shape to allow accommodation.'),
('f3585318-dfa9-5309-89d0-85f75f24b83c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is emmetropia?', '["Presbyopia resulting in decreased functional activity","Farsightedness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nearsightedness mediated by specific receptor activation","Astigmatism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The state of normal vision where light is precisely focused on the retina without accommodation"]'::jsonb, 'The state of normal vision where light is precisely focused on the retina without accommodation', 'Emmetropia is the normal refractive state in which parallel rays of light are focused exactly on the retina at rest, so no accommodation is required. Myopia, hyperopia, and astigmatism are all deviations from this state.'),
('ab4d93f5-1398-5da0-b28d-4c2dab189510'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is myopia (nearsightedness)?', '["causing light to focus in front of the retina; corrected with concave (negative) lenses","The lens is too flat","The eyeball is too short","The cornea is too flat","A condition where the eyeball is too long or the cornea is too steep"]'::jsonb, 'A condition where the eyeball is too long or the cornea is too steep', 'In myopia the eyeball is too long or the cornea is too steep, so parallel rays focus in front of the retina; concave (minus) lenses move the focus back onto the retina. Distance vision is blurred whereas near vision remains clear.'),
('25c7b7a6-0e0f-5458-b08d-32f318ea07f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is hyperopia (farsightedness)?', '["A condition where the eyeball is too short or the cornea is too flat","The eyeball is too long","The lens is too thick","causing light to focus behind the retina; corrected with convex (positive) lenses","The cornea is too steep"]'::jsonb, 'A condition where the eyeball is too short or the cornea is too flat', 'In hyperopia the eyeball is too short or its refractive power is too weak, so light would focus behind the retina; convex (plus) lenses or accommodation bring the focal point forward onto the retina. Near vision is affected most.'),
('797c3a30-1356-5056-b2e9-e4ca58247e70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is astigmatism?', '["An irregular curvature of the cornea (or lens) causing light to focus at multiple points; corrected with cylindrical lenses","A retinal problem accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A condition of the lens mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A muscle imbalance without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A spherical error leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An irregular curvature of the cornea (or lens) causing light to focus at multiple points; corrected with cylindrical lenses', 'Astigmatism is caused by an irregular (non-spherical) curvature of the cornea or lens so that light is focused at multiple points rather than a single point, producing distorted images; unequal curvatures are corrected with cylindrical lenses. It is a refractive error, not a retinal or muscular disorder.'),
('a53e16ad-232d-5a87-9ae3-d3fa1f406342'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the near point of the eye?', '["The resting focal point resulting in decreased functional activity","The farthest distance for clear vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The closest distance at which an object can be focused; it increases with age (presbyopia)","The distance at which both eyes converge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The point of maximum accommodation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The closest distance at which an object can be focused; it increases with age (presbyopia)', 'The near point is the shortest distance at which an object can be brought into clear focus with maximal accommodation. Because the lens loses elasticity with age, the near point recedes progressively, producing presbyopia.'),
('f68ff3f0-3f8c-5e94-b075-62365bdd2ac0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the amplitude of accommodation?', '["The difference between the refractive power of the eye for near and distance vision; it decreases with age","The maximum convergence ability accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ability to see colors mediated by specific receptor activation","The ability to see in dim light without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The maximum visual acuity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The difference between the refractive power of the eye for near and distance vision; it decreases with age', 'The amplitude of accommodation is the difference in refractive power between the eye focused at its near point and at distance; it declines with age as the lens hardens. It reflects the range over which the lens can change shape, not convergence, color, or acuity.'),
('17b16288-847f-5b57-b7b5-4d4159bacb51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the Snellen visual acuity chart?', '["A chart to measure distance visual acuity; normal is 20/20 (Snellen fraction: test distance / distance a normal eye can read the line)","A chart for peripheral vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A hearing test mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A chart for color vision without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A chart for contrast sensitivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chart to measure distance visual acuity; normal is 20/20 (Snellen fraction: test distance / distance a normal eye can read the line)', 'The Snellen chart measures distance visual acuity using the Snellen fraction, which divides the test distance by the distance at which a normal eye can read that line; 20/20 designates normal acuity. It does not assess peripheral fields, color, or contrast sensitivity.'),
('2b15df31-cb9b-5710-abfa-c3ac53e7699b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the Ishihara color test?', '["A visual field test resulting in decreased functional activity","A contrast sensitivity test accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A depth perception test mediated by specific receptor activation","A test using pseudo-isochromatic plates to detect red-green color deficiency (most common congenital color blindness)","A test for color vision in all colors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A test using pseudo-isochromatic plates to detect red-green color deficiency (most common congenital color blindness)', 'The Ishihara test uses pseudo-isochromatic plates containing numerals or patterns that are read differently by color-deficient viewers, screening for red-green deficiency, the most common congenital color blindness. The other tests assess visual field, contrast, or depth rather than color discrimination.'),
('2cbfcbb5-5482-5874-8ef5-78da830f9cbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the Amsler grid test?', '["A peripheral field test resulting in decreased functional activity","A contrast sensitivity test accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A color vision test mediated by specific receptor activation","A grid test for detecting central visual field defects in macular disease (AMD)","A depth perception test leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A grid test for detecting central visual field defects in macular disease (AMD)', 'The Amsler grid is a chart of horizontal and vertical lines with a central fixation dot used to detect central scotomas and metamorphopsia in macular disease, particularly age-related macular degeneration. It evaluates the central field, not peripheral vision, contrast, color, or depth.'),
('6b4ce34f-104a-533b-ad3c-a9e6ae076b17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is perimetry?', '["A visual field test that maps the sensitivity of the peripheral and central visual field; used to detect and monitor glaucoma and other optic neuropathies","A test for color vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A test for visual acuity mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A test for depth perception without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A test for contrast sensitivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A visual field test that maps the sensitivity of the peripheral and central visual field; used to detect and monitor glaucoma and other optic neuropathies', 'Perimetry maps the sensitivity of the central and peripheral visual field and is the key test for detecting and monitoring visual field loss in glaucoma and other optic neuropathies. It measures field rather than resolution, color, depth, or contrast.'),
('3174d2ee-55fd-59f0-88f1-1d852089dab8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is optical coherence tomography (OCT)?', '["A CT scan of the eye resulting in decreased functional activity","A non-invasive imaging technique that uses light waves to create cross-sectional images of the retina","An MRI of the eye mediated by specific receptor activation","measuring retinal thickness and nerve fiber layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An ultrasound of the eye leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A non-invasive imaging technique that uses light waves to create cross-sectional images of the retina', 'OCT uses low-coherence light to generate high-resolution cross-sectional images of the retina, allowing measurement of retinal thickness and the retinal nerve fiber layer. Unlike CT, MRI, or ultrasound it uses optical rather than x-ray, magnetic, or acoustic energy.'),
('4b33a14a-4643-5eb8-a04d-2c2ab28b38c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the spiral organ of Corti?', '["The sensory organ in the cochlea consisting of hair cells and supporting cells on the basilar membrane","The cochlear nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The tectorial membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","The vestibular organ without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The stria vascularis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sensory organ in the cochlea consisting of hair cells and supporting cells on the basilar membrane', 'The spiral organ of Corti is the auditory sensory epithelium resting on the basilar membrane, composed of inner and outer hair cells and their supporting cells. Bending of the hair cell stereocilia against the tectorial membrane transduces sound into neural signals.'),
('f4ef2c87-6277-55bb-b871-c795b9df6590'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the supporting cells of the organ of Corti?', '["and Claudius cells; they provide structural support","Deiters cells (phalangeal)","Hensen cells","Hair cells only","Pillar cells (inner and outer)"]'::jsonb, 'Pillar cells (inner and outer)', 'The organ of Corti contains several supporting cell types, including the inner and outer pillar cells that delimit the tunnel of Corti, as well as Deiters (phalangeal), Hensen, and Claudius cells. Hair cells provide the sensory function rather than structural support.'),
('149d7f8c-4d43-5cb8-a601-879b45071861'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the tunnel of Corti?', '["A duct in the semicircular canal resulting in decreased functional activity","A space between inner and outer pillar cells filled with cortilymph (similar to perilymph)","A blood vessel mediated by specific receptor activation","A space in the vestibular system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A channel in the bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A space between inner and outer pillar cells filled with cortilymph (similar to perilymph)', 'The tunnel of Corti is the narrow space between the inner and outer pillar cells of the organ of Corti, filled with cortilymph whose ionic composition resembles perilymph. It lies within the cochlear duct, not in the semicircular canals or bony bone.'),
('c7f495db-2344-598f-9f29-6b4e8c265aac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of Deiters (phalangeal) cells?', '["Transduce sound resulting in decreased functional activity","Support outer hair cells and form the reticular lamina (apical surface of the organ of Corti)","Phagocytose debris mediated by specific receptor activation","Produce endolymph without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secrete tectorial membrane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Support outer hair cells and form the reticular lamina (apical surface of the organ of Corti)', 'Deiters (phalangeal) cells cradle the bases of the outer hair cells and extend phalangeal processes that help build the reticular lamina at the apical surface of the organ of Corti. They provide mechanical support; transduction belongs to hair cells, endolymph production to the stria vascularis, and the tectorial membrane is a different acellular structure.'),
('99546114-fcee-52e1-87f2-869bf29baa4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the reticular lamina?', '["net-like structure formed by the apical surfaces of hair cells and supporting cells that seals the organ of Corti from the endolymph","The basilar membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reissner''s membrane mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The tectorial membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A rigid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'net-like structure formed by the apical surfaces of hair cells and supporting cells that seals the organ of Corti from the endolymph', 'The reticular lamina is a rigid net-like structure formed by the junctional complexes at the apical surfaces of hair cells and their supporting cells; it seals the organ of Corti against the endolymph while allowing the stereocilia to project into the scala media. The basilar membrane, Reissner''s membrane, and the tectorial membrane are separate cochlear structures.'),
('79e811eb-6a5f-5e38-9c89-724d95679e53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is Reissner''s membrane (vestibular membrane)?', '["The tectorial membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","The reticular lamina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The thin membrane separating the scala media (endolymph) from the scala vestibuli (perilymph)","The basilar membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The spiral ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thin membrane separating the scala media (endolymph) from the scala vestibuli (perilymph)', 'Reissner''s (vestibular) membrane is a thin two-cell-layer membrane separating the endolymph-filled scala media from the perilymph-filled scala vestibuli. It is distinct from the basilar membrane that supports the organ of Corti and from the tectorial membrane overlying the hair cells.'),
('9f04fb5f-b2a2-56a7-ac87-2f7468ad53aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the spiral ligament?', '["The spiral ganglion resulting in decreased functional activity","The thickened periosteum on the outer wall of the cochlea that supports the stria vascularis and the basilar membrane","The vestibular membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","The bony labyrinth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The organ of Corti leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thickened periosteum on the outer wall of the cochlea that supports the stria vascularis and the basilar membrane', 'The spiral ligament is the thickened periosteal connective tissue lining the outer wall of the cochlea that anchors the basilar membrane and supports the stria vascularis, the source of endolymph. It is a scaffold rather than a neural, sensory, or bony-labyrinth component.'),
('1edd36de-f791-5341-9564-a75a8548d271'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the spiral ganglion?', '["The facial nerve ganglion resulting in decreased functional activity","The trigeminal ganglion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The organ of Corti mediated by specific receptor activation","The collection of bipolar neurons of the cochlear nerve located in the modiolus (central core of the cochlea)","The vestibular ganglion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The collection of bipolar neurons of the cochlear nerve located in the modiolus (central core of the cochlea)', 'The spiral ganglion is the collection of bipolar neurons in the modiolus; their peripheral processes synapse with the inner hair cells and their central processes form the cochlear division of CN VIII. It is the first auditory relay and is distinct from the vestibular ganglion.'),
('edb6d284-a16a-5130-b96b-62ff8716ed58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the modiolus?', '["and the cochlear nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The scala media accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The basilar membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","The central bony core of the cochlea that contains the spiral ganglion","blood vessels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central bony core of the cochlea that contains the spiral ganglion', 'The modiolus is the central bony axis of the cochlea that contains the spiral ganglion and the emerging cochlear nerve, around which the bony spiral canal and its membranous duct wind. The scala media and basilar membrane lie peripheral to it.'),
('41bb9a88-4492-5169-a87d-e90e1afad1b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the helicotrema?', '["The round window resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The opening at the apex of the cochlea that connects the scala vestibuli and scala tympani (allowing perilymph communication)","The oval window mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A foramen in the temporal bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A hole in the eardrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The opening at the apex of the cochlea that connects the scala vestibuli and scala tympani (allowing perilymph communication)', 'The helicotrema is the opening at the apex of the cochlea that allows perilymph to pass freely between the scala vestibuli and the scala tympani. It is a cochlear communication, not the oval or round window, and not a hole in the eardrum.'),
('723c18e8-2296-5de7-b343-4394a8a29a18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the structure of the macula of the utricle?', '["A sensory epithelium with hair cells (Type I and Type II) embedded in a gelatinous otolithic membrane with otoliths (calcium carbonate crystals)","The crista ampullaris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The organ of Corti mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The vestibular ganglion without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cupula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A sensory epithelium with hair cells (Type I and Type II) embedded in a gelatinous otolithic membrane with otoliths (calcium carbonate crystals)', 'The utricular macula is a vestibular sensory epithelium containing Type I and Type II hair cells whose stereocilia project into a gelatinous otolithic membrane weighted with calcium carbonate otoliths. It detects linear acceleration and head tilt, unlike the crista ampullaris which senses rotation.'),
('18213cfe-bf09-53ef-a444-2dcc7a00154a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the otoliths (statoconia)?', '["Make the otolithic membrane heavier than the endolymph","Produce sound","Transduce vibration","so that gravity and linear acceleration displace the membrane relative to the macula","Absorb calcium"]'::jsonb, 'Make the otolithic membrane heavier than the endolymph', 'The calcium carbonate otoliths increase the mass of the otolithic membrane so that during gravity changes and linear acceleration its inertia displaces the membrane relative to the underlying hair cells, bending the stereocilia and generating a receptor potential. They do not produce sound, transduce vibration, or absorb calcium.'),
('1f339b3f-90cb-55d1-ac43-43c6c38eed63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the structure of the crista ampullaris?', '["A glandular structure resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A bony ridge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A blood vessel mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A saddle-shaped sensory epithelium at the base of the ampulla topped by a gelatinous cupula that extends to the roof of the ampulla","A flat epithelial patch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A saddle-shaped sensory epithelium at the base of the ampulla topped by a gelatinous cupula that extends to the roof of the ampulla', 'Each crista ampullaris is a saddle-shaped crest of sensory epithelium at the base of a semicircular canal ampulla, capped by a gelatinous cupula that reaches the roof; endolymph flow during angular acceleration deflects the cupula and bends the hair cell stereocilia. It therefore senses rotatory head movement.'),
('5b53f5b8-15a1-5a0f-9be4-2c644f3c5983'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the vestibular supporting cells?', '["Schwann cells","Cells that surround the hair cells in the macula and crista","providing structural support and likely producing the gelatinous membranes","Fibroblasts","Glial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Cells that surround the hair cells in the macula and crista', 'Supporting (sustentacular) cells surround sensory hair cells within the maculae and cristae, providing structural support and secreting the gelatinous otolithic and cupular membranes. Schwann cells, fibroblasts, and glial cells belong to other neural or connective tissues, not the vestibular sensory epithelium.'),
('31afc67b-dc31-52ec-91b9-e512fcb67f8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the vestibular nerve?', '["The cochlear nerve resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The oculomotor nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The superior (from utricle and anterior/lateral canals) and inferior (from saccule and posterior canal) branches of CN VIII that carry balance information","The trigeminal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The superior (from utricle and anterior/lateral canals) and inferior (from saccule and posterior canal) branches of CN VIII that carry balance information', 'The vestibular nerve consists of superior (utricle and anterior/lateral canals) and inferior (saccule and posterior canal) branches of CN VIII that carry balance information. The cochlear nerve is the auditory division of CN VIII, while the oculomotor, facial, and trigeminal nerves are unrelated.'),
('0610e2ba-6ddc-5ae4-8fbe-15a29660b468'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR)?', '["The accommodation reflex resulting in decreased functional activity","The optokinetic reflex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The blink reflex mediated by specific receptor activation","A reflex that moves the eyes in the opposite direction of head movement to stabilize visual images on the retina","The pupillary reflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A reflex that moves the eyes in the opposite direction of head movement to stabilize visual images on the retina', 'The VOR moves the eyes opposite to the direction of head movement, stabilizing visual images on the retina during head rotation. The accommodation, optokinetic, blink, and pupillary reflexes serve different functions of the eye.'),
('456bf77a-2344-5649-8c2f-ae1faecfa163'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the vestibulospinal tract?', '["The corticospinal tract resulting in decreased functional activity","The medial longitudinal fasciculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The spinocerebellar tract mediated by specific receptor activation","The rubrospinal tract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pathway from the vestibular nuclei to the spinal cord that adjusts muscle tone and posture in response to head movements"]'::jsonb, 'The pathway from the vestibular nuclei to the spinal cord that adjusts muscle tone and posture in response to head movements', 'The vestibulospinal tract carries signals from the vestibular nuclei to the spinal cord, adjusting muscle tone and posture in response to head movements. The corticospinal, spinocerebellar, rubrospinal, and medial longitudinal fasciculus pathways have different origins and functions.'),
('cdd09127-498a-5154-a278-5c90cbed0fb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)?', '["A sensory tract resulting in decreased functional activity","A cerebellar tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A bundle of fibers that coordinates conjugate eye movements and connects the cranial nerve nuclei for the VOR","A pyramidal tract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A spinal tract leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A bundle of fibers that coordinates conjugate eye movements and connects the cranial nerve nuclei for the VOR', 'The MLF is a bundle of fibers that connects the cranial nerve nuclei serving the extraocular muscles, coordinating conjugate eye movements and mediating the VOR. It is not a purely sensory, pyramidal, spinal, or cerebellar tract.'),
('3e5867bc-3629-5aaa-91c6-242dfb8b9fcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is retinoblastoma?', '["A benign retinal tumor resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A malignant tumor of the retina in children (typically <5 years) caused by RB1 gene mutation; presents with leukocoria (white pupillary reflex)","A lens tumor mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A choroidal tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An adult retinal tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A malignant tumor of the retina in children (typically <5 years) caused by RB1 gene mutation; presents with leukocoria (white pupillary reflex)', 'Retinoblastoma is a malignant childhood tumor (usually before age 5) caused by RB1 gene mutation, typically presenting with leukocoria. It arises in the retina, not the lens, choroid, or adult retina, and is not benign.'),
('c0ddcc4f-8fdf-5558-ac5b-1717789e56d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the histology of retinoblastoma?', '["Papillary structures","Glandular formations","Palisading basaloid cells","forming Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes (photoreceptor differentiation)","Small blue round cells with basophilic nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Small blue round cells with basophilic nuclei', 'Retinoblastoma is composed of densely packed small round blue cells with dark basophilic nuclei; Flexner-Wintersteiner rosettes, when present, indicate photoreceptor differentiation. Papillary, glandular, and palisading basaloid patterns characterize other tumors rather than retinoblastoma.'),
('ca188def-5fe8-58ab-8203-aa8122293429'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is dacryocystitis?', '["and tenderness near the medial canthus","from obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct; presents with tearing","swelling","Inflammation of the lacrimal gland","Inflammation of the lacrimal sac"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the lacrimal sac', 'Dacryocystitis is inflammation of the lacrimal sac, typically caused by obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct with tearing, swelling, and tenderness near the medial canthus. Inflammation of the lacrimal gland is termed dacryoadenitis, not dacryocystitis.'),
('f97cb0c0-c511-5eb2-aa82-23051873fc3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is hordeolum (stye)?', '["by S. aureus resulting in decreased functional activity","An acute infection of the eyelash follicle (external hordeolum) or meibomian gland (internal hordeolum)","A xanthelasma mediated by specific receptor activation","A sebaceous cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A chalazion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An acute infection of the eyelash follicle (external hordeolum) or meibomian gland (internal hordeolum)', 'A hordeolum (stye) is an acute infection of the eyelash follicle (external hordeolum) or meibomian gland (internal hordeolum), usually due to S. aureus. A chalazion, xanthelasma, and sebaceous cyst are non-infective or unrelated eyelid lesions.'),
('1c25377c-d54e-5f39-a942-90c3b488fd79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is chalazion?', '["chronic granulomatous inflammation of the meibomian gland in the eyelid","A painless accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","presenting as a firm nodule mediated by specific receptor activation","An external hordeolum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An acute stye leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'chronic granulomatous inflammation of the meibomian gland in the eyelid', 'A chalazion is a chronic granulomatous inflammation of a meibomian gland in the eyelid, typically presenting as a painless, firm nodule. This distinguishes it from the acute stye (external hordeolum), which is an acutely painful infection.'),
('d3105acd-8818-5383-82e0-6a1e24dae6c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the most common malignant tumor of the eyelid?', '["Malignant melanoma","Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC)","Merkel cell carcinoma","Sebaceous carcinoma","Basal cell carcinoma (BCC)"]'::jsonb, 'Basal cell carcinoma (BCC)', 'Basal cell carcinoma is the most common malignant tumor of the eyelid, typically a slow-growing nodule with rolled, pearly borders. Melanoma, squamous cell, Merkel cell, and sebaceous carcinomas are all substantially less common.'),
('d7476ea1-87de-57b7-8064-86b561c1dade'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the most common cause of blindness in the world?', '["Cataract (untreated)","Trachoma","Glaucoma","Age-related macular degeneration","Diabetic retinopathy"]'::jsonb, 'Cataract (untreated)', 'Untreated cataract is the most common cause of blindness worldwide because it is widespread and correctable by surgery. Glaucoma, age-related macular degeneration, diabetic retinopathy, and trachoma are important but less common overall causes.'),
('86b68870-4d52-555f-b93d-567508df65c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the leading cause of irreversible blindness globally?', '["AMD","Trachoma","Cataract","Glaucoma","Diabetic retinopathy"]'::jsonb, 'Glaucoma', 'Glaucoma is the leading cause of irreversible blindness globally, characterized by progressive optic nerve damage and visual field loss. Cataract, although the most common cause of reversible blindness, is treatable, and the other options are less common.'),
('dc2d6666-707c-525f-bcce-41a1afe4c089'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the optic vesicle?', '["The optic stalk resulting in decreased functional activity","The choroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lens vesicle mediated by specific receptor activation","An outpouching of the neural ectoderm (forebrain) that forms the retina and RPE","The sclera leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An outpouching of the neural ectoderm (forebrain) that forms the retina and RPE', 'The optic vesicle is an outpouching of the neural ectoderm of the forebrain that develops into the retina and retinal pigment epithelium. The lens arises from surface ectoderm, while the sclera, choroid, and optic stalk have different origins.'),
('d1dc4051-f707-5ebd-8e89-851f4cc4d0e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the optic cup?', '["The sclera resulting in decreased functional activity","The double-layered structure formed by invagination of the optic vesicle; the inner layer becomes the neural retina","The lens mediated by specific receptor activation","The cornea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and the outer layer becomes the RPE leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The double-layered structure formed by invagination of the optic vesicle; the inner layer becomes the neural retina', 'The optic cup is the double-layered structure formed by invagination of the optic vesicle; its inner layer becomes the neural retina and its outer layer becomes the RPE. The lens, cornea, and sclera are not formed by the optic cup.'),
('4dd708d4-f9b8-594e-aa4c-bae2dca71fbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the lens placode?', '["induced by the optic vesicle resulting in decreased functional activity","The cornea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The optic vesicle mediated by specific receptor activation","The optic cup without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An area of surface ectoderm that thickens and invaginates to form the lens vesicle"]'::jsonb, 'An area of surface ectoderm that thickens and invaginates to form the lens vesicle', 'The lens placode is a small area of surface ectoderm, induced by the optic vesicle, that thickens and invaginates to form the lens vesicle. The cornea, optic vesicle, and optic cup are distinct structures of the developing eye.'),
('e354aea2-a44a-5360-a7e4-6bda3b32a959'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the choroidal fissure?', '["A scleral defect resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A corneal cleft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A retinal break mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A groove on the inferior surface of the optic cup that allows the hyaloid artery to enter the developing eye; it normally closes by week 7","A defect in the iris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A groove on the inferior surface of the optic cup that allows the hyaloid artery to enter the developing eye; it normally closes by week 7', 'The choroidal fissure is a groove on the inferior surface of the optic cup that allows the hyaloid artery to enter the developing eye and normally closes by week 7; incomplete closure leads to a coloboma. Scleral, corneal, and iris defects are separate entities.'),
('77f91ac6-7c22-58f2-9057-073917e2ed73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the embryological origin of the inner ear?', '["Endoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The otic placode (surface ectoderm) that invaginates to form the otic vesicle (otocyst)","Mesoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The otic placode (surface ectoderm) that invaginates to form the otic vesicle (otocyst)', 'The inner ear develops from the otic placode, a thickening of surface ectoderm that invaginates to form the otic vesicle (otocyst). Endoderm, mesoderm, the neural tube, and neural crest do not give rise to the membranous labyrinth.'),
('14f553e5-06f4-5e5e-b3a3-9da94c6304c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the embryological origin of the external ear?', '["The otocyst resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The neural crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The otic vesicle mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The first and second pharyngeal arches (six hillocks of His) for the auricle; the first pharyngeal cleft for the external auditory canal","The mesoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The first and second pharyngeal arches (six hillocks of His) for the auricle; the first pharyngeal cleft for the external auditory canal', 'The auricle develops from the six hillocks of His on the first and second pharyngeal arches, while the external auditory canal derives from the first pharyngeal cleft. The otocyst and otic vesicle belong to the inner ear, and mesoderm/neural crest give rise to other structures.'),
('819f3644-4f9f-52e2-93a7-bbadcc49f0fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the embryological origin of the middle ear?', '["The first pharyngeal pouch (endoderm) for the tympanic cavity; the first and second pharyngeal arches for the ossicles","The neural crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The mesoderm mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The pharyngeal clefts without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The otic vesicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The first pharyngeal pouch (endoderm) for the tympanic cavity; the first and second pharyngeal arches for the ossicles', 'The middle ear forms from the first pharyngeal pouch (endoderm), which becomes the tympanic cavity, while the ossicles derive from the first and second pharyngeal arches. Pharyngeal clefts, mesoderm, and the otic vesicle are not the source of the tympanic cavity and ossicles.'),
('d5093764-234b-5fde-b9b3-8e27e5d134ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the embryological origin of the olfactory epithelium?', '["Endoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","The olfactory placode (surface ectoderm) of the nasal cavity","Neural crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural tube leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The olfactory placode (surface ectoderm) of the nasal cavity', 'The olfactory epithelium develops from the olfactory placode, a patch of surface ectoderm in the nasal cavity. Endoderm, mesoderm, the neural tube, and neural crest are not the source of the olfactory epithelium.'),
('4ea4394b-6ae3-59dd-8ddd-d7898e994a82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is dark adaptation?', '["Pupil dilation only resulting in decreased functional activity","The switch to cone vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The decrease in sensitivity mediated by specific receptor activation","mainly due to rhodopsin regeneration in rods without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The gradual increase in retinal sensitivity when moving from bright to dim light"]'::jsonb, 'The gradual increase in retinal sensitivity when moving from bright to dim light', 'Dark adaptation is the gradual increase in retinal sensitivity when moving from bright to dim light, largely due to regeneration of rhodopsin in rods. It is not simply pupil dilation, a switch to cone vision, or a decrease in sensitivity.'),
('81f60fc5-ed54-5ce9-b331-33782221b00d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is light adaptation?', '["The switch to rod vision resulting in decreased functional activity","The increase in sensitivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","involving pigment bleaching and reduced gain mediated by specific receptor activation","The rapid decrease in retinal sensitivity when moving from dim to bright light","Pupil constriction only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rapid decrease in retinal sensitivity when moving from dim to bright light', 'Light adaptation is the rapid decrease in retinal sensitivity when moving from dim to bright light, involving pigment bleaching and reduced gain. It is not a switch to rod vision, an increase in sensitivity, or pupil constriction alone.'),
('2aa55b51-145b-53e1-9cfc-64446029dade'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the Purkinje shift?', '["Visual field changes resulting in decreased functional activity","The shift of spectral sensitivity toward blue wavelengths in dim light (rods) compared to bright light (cones)","The change in pupil size mediated by specific receptor activation","A shift in color vision without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Accommodation changes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The shift of spectral sensitivity toward blue wavelengths in dim light (rods) compared to bright light (cones)', 'The Purkinje shift refers to the shift of spectral sensitivity toward blue wavelengths in dim light (rod-mediated) compared with bright light (cone-mediated) vision. Pupil size, visual fields, accommodation, and general color shifts are different phenomena.'),
('c8aff406-3145-589a-9230-e13ae15b324f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the critical flicker frequency (CFF)?', '["15 Hz for rods resulting in decreased functional activity","The lowest visible frequency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The maximum frequency of action potentials mediated by specific receptor activation","The refresh rate of the eye without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The frequency at which a flickering light appears as a steady (fused) stimulus; 60 Hz for cones"]'::jsonb, 'The frequency at which a flickering light appears as a steady (fused) stimulus; 60 Hz for cones', 'The critical flicker frequency is the frequency at which a flickering light appears fused and steady, about 60 Hz for cones and lower for rods. It is not the lowest visible frequency, the maximum action potential rate, or a simple refresh property.'),
('3b03715d-a28a-5cb6-bdfe-0ee04bc9b286'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the retinal ganglion cell receptive field?', '["changes the firing rate of the ganglion cell","when stimulated with light","The area of one photoreceptor","The area of the retina that","The entire visual field"]'::jsonb, 'The area of the retina that', 'The receptive field of a retinal ganglion cell is the area of the retina that, when stimulated with light, changes the firing rate of that cell. It is not a single photoreceptor nor the entire visual field.'),
('4590dd44-ec1d-5b16-b29f-b71d98818fd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is lateral inhibition in the retina?', '["Inhibition of the entire retina resulting in decreased functional activity","A form of feedback from the brain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A compensation for light scatter mediated by specific receptor activation","A process by which horizontal cells sharpen visual contrast at edges by reducing the response of neighboring photoreceptors","Activation of adjacent cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A process by which horizontal cells sharpen visual contrast at edges by reducing the response of neighboring photoreceptors', 'Lateral inhibition is a process in which horizontal cells sharpen visual contrast at edges by reducing the response of neighboring photoreceptors. It is not inhibition of the whole retina, feedback from the brain, or activation of adjacent cells.'),
('fd9e19c4-28cf-521c-abcf-005428622d86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the magnocellular (M) pathway?', '["A pathway for color and detail","A retinal output pathway (large ganglion cells) for motion","A pathway for object recognition","depth","and low spatial frequency; projects to the magnocellular LGN layers"]'::jsonb, 'A retinal output pathway (large ganglion cells) for motion', 'The magnocellular pathway is a retinal output pathway arising from large ganglion cells that carries motion and low spatial frequency information and projects to the magnocellular LGN layers. Color and fine-detail processing belong to the parvocellular pathway.'),
('b0a5e1ea-9cf2-58b6-ba81-7d3d0cfd7d9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the parvocellular (P) pathway?', '["A pathway for peripheral vision resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A pathway for dim light vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pathway for motion mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A retinal output pathway (small ganglion cells) for color and fine detail (high spatial frequency); projects to the parvocellular LGN layers","A pathway for depth perception leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A retinal output pathway (small ganglion cells) for color and fine detail (high spatial frequency); projects to the parvocellular LGN layers', 'The parvocellular pathway arises from small retinal ganglion cells and transmits color and fine detail (high spatial frequency) signals to the parvocellular LGN layers. Motion, peripheral, and dim-light processing are handled by the magnocellular and other pathways.'),
('e7084b72-1959-529b-b673-aecbc442a908'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the koniocellular (K) pathway?', '["A third retinal output pathway (bistratified ganglion cells) for blue-yellow color and luminance; projects to the intercalated LGN layers","A pathway for motion detection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A magnocellular pathway mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A parvocellular pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A pathway for night vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A third retinal output pathway (bistratified ganglion cells) for blue-yellow color and luminance; projects to the intercalated LGN layers', 'The koniocellular pathway is a third retinal output pathway formed by bistratified ganglion cells that carries blue-yellow color and luminance signals to the intercalated LGN layers. Motion detection and night vision belong to the magnocellular and rod-mediated systems.'),
('caa615e1-202b-5296-ad20-7f743344ce3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the immune privilege of the eye?', '["lack of lymphatics resulting in decreased functional activity","The eye''s ability to tolerate antigens without triggering destructive inflammation","due to the blood-ocular barrier mediated by specific receptor activation","immune response in the eye without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and local immunosuppressive factors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The eye''s ability to tolerate antigens without triggering destructive inflammation', 'The eye is an immune-privileged site because it tolerates antigens without triggering destructive inflammation, aided by the blood-ocular barrier, limited lymphatics, and local immunosuppressive factors. It does not mean absence of any immune response.'),
('c594c25a-3917-52d1-9904-59abb131fd25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is anterior chamber-associated immune deviation (ACAID)?', '["A immune rejection resulting in decreased functional activity","A type of uveitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A phenomenon where antigens injected into the anterior chamber induce systemic tolerance rather than immunity","An allergic reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An autoimmune response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A phenomenon where antigens injected into the anterior chamber induce systemic tolerance rather than immunity', 'ACAID is a phenomenon in which antigens injected into the anterior chamber induce systemic tolerance rather than immunity. It is neither uveitis, an allergic reaction, an autoimmune response, nor immune rejection.'),
('99200e44-83c7-58a0-9a01-416d0c615288'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is sympathetic ophthalmia?', '["caused by an autoimmune response to uveal antigens resulting in decreased functional activity","An allergic reaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A sympathetic nervous response mediated by specific receptor activation","A contralateral infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A bilateral granulomatous panuveitis following penetrating trauma to one eye (the excitatory eye)"]'::jsonb, 'A bilateral granulomatous panuveitis following penetrating trauma to one eye (the excitatory eye)', 'Sympathetic ophthalmia is a bilateral granulomatous panuveitis that follows penetrating trauma to one eye (the excitatory eye), driven by an autoimmune response to uveal antigens. It is not an allergic reaction, a sympathetic nervous response, or a contralateral infection.'),
('2d9993f9-c312-5ffc-845e-bee8df6a486c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the most common type of uveitis overall?', '["Posterior uveitis","Panuveitis","Sclerouveitis","Intermediate uveitis (pars planitis)","Anterior uveitis (iritis)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior uveitis (iritis)', 'Anterior uveitis (iritis) is the most common type of uveitis overall. Posterior uveitis, intermediate uveitis (pars planitis), panuveitis, and sclerouveitis are each less frequent.'),
('c726001a-3ebe-543a-9f06-13387773e26a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is HLA-B27-associated uveitis?', '["reactive arthritis resulting in decreased functional activity","and pain; associated with ankylosing spondylitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","photophobia mediated by specific receptor activation","and IBD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-granulomatous anterior uveitis with rapid onset"]'::jsonb, 'Non-granulomatous anterior uveitis with rapid onset', 'HLA-B27-associated uveitis is a non-granulomatous anterior uveitis with rapid onset of pain and photophobia, associated with ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, and IBD. This distinguishes it from the chronic, granulomatous forms of uveitis.'),
('efca1481-35ed-5e5e-aa93-7ab101a59098'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the role of the vitreous in ocular immunity?', '["It initiates inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","contributing to immune privilege accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","role in immunity mediated by specific receptor activation","It produces antibodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vitreous contains hyaluronic acid and other molecules that suppress inflammation"]'::jsonb, 'The vitreous contains hyaluronic acid and other molecules that suppress inflammation', 'The vitreous contributes to ocular immune privilege because it contains hyaluronic acid and other molecules that suppress inflammation. It does not initiate inflammation or produce antibodies.'),
('e689531b-becf-560c-b816-499ecbded1d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is endophthalmitis prophylaxis for cataract surgery?', '["prophylaxis needed resulting in decreased functional activity","Topical antibiotics only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oral antifungal mediated by specific receptor activation","Systemic antibiotics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intracameral cefuroxime or vancomycin at the end of surgery to prevent postoperative endophthalmitis"]'::jsonb, 'Intracameral cefuroxime or vancomycin at the end of surgery to prevent postoperative endophthalmitis', 'Intracameral cefuroxime or vancomycin given at the end of cataract surgery is the routine prophylaxis against postoperative endophthalmitis. Topical antibiotics alone are less effective, and antifungal or systemic-only regimens are not standard prophylaxis.'),
('9ad9a06f-407d-58b7-aea4-0bff3a75d1b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the signs of ocular inflammation on examination?', '["keratic precipitates (KPs)","Conjunctival injection","flare and cells (anterior chamber)","and vitreous cells","Ciliary flush (perilimbal injection)"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary flush (perilimbal injection)', 'Ciliary flush (perilimbal injection) is a classic sign of ocular inflammation on examination, together with conjunctival injection, keratic precipitates, anterior chamber flare and cells, and vitreous cells. It reflects congestion of the limbal vessels in an inflamed eye.'),
('e71be111-c21e-5edc-b0db-e78671e81d73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the slit lamp?', '["A microscope with an adjustable light beam for examining the anterior and posterior segments of the eye in detail","A keratometer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A perimeter mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A fundus camera without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A tonometer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A microscope with an adjustable light beam for examining the anterior and posterior segments of the eye in detail', 'The slit lamp is a microscope with an adjustable light beam that allows detailed examination of the anterior and posterior segments of the eye. A keratometer, perimeter, fundus camera, and tonometer each serve different, specific diagnostic functions.'),
('100d3b16-d32d-5771-8ab1-f069f10b1083'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the mechanism of pilocarpine in glaucoma?', '["Decreases aqueous production","Increases uveoscleral outflow","A muscarinic agonist that contracts the ciliary muscle","Blocks beta-receptors","pulling open the trabecular meshwork and increasing aqueous outflow"]'::jsonb, 'A muscarinic agonist that contracts the ciliary muscle', 'Pilocarpine is a muscarinic agonist that contracts the ciliary muscle, pulling open the trabecular meshwork and increasing aqueous outflow. It does not block beta-receptors, primarily reduce aqueous production, or increase uveoscleral outflow.'),
('aac5851b-530d-5bca-a6cd-ba00d87cd58b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the mechanism of timolol in glaucoma?', '["Increases outflow resulting in decreased functional activity","Increases uveoscleral outflow accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreases intraocular blood flow mediated by specific receptor activation","Pupillary constriction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A non-selective beta-blocker that reduces aqueous humor production by the ciliary body"]'::jsonb, 'A non-selective beta-blocker that reduces aqueous humor production by the ciliary body', 'Timolol is a non-selective beta-blocker that lowers intraocular pressure by reducing aqueous humor production in the ciliary body. It neither increases outflow nor causes pupillary constriction.'),
('da4c2f63-ca2c-500b-baad-bb6146c071e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the mechanism of latanoprost in glaucoma?', '["A prostaglandin analog that increases uveoscleral outflow of aqueous humor; it is the first-line treatment for POAG","Beta-blockade accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreases aqueous production mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha2-agonism without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Miosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A prostaglandin analog that increases uveoscleral outflow of aqueous humor; it is the first-line treatment for POAG', 'Latanoprost is a prostaglandin analog that increases uveoscleral outflow of aqueous humor and is a first-line treatment for primary open-angle glaucoma. Beta-blockade, alpha2-agonism, decreased aqueous production, and miosis are actions of other drug classes.'),
('f6314e26-f12c-5e24-95e5-e39df8e6a80a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the mechanism of brimonidine in glaucoma?', '["An alpha2-adrenergic agonist that reduces aqueous humor production and increases uveoscleral outflow","Carbonic anhydrase inhibition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mydriasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Miosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta-blockade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An alpha2-adrenergic agonist that reduces aqueous humor production and increases uveoscleral outflow', 'Brimonidine is an alpha2-adrenergic agonist that lowers intraocular pressure by reducing aqueous humor production and increasing uveoscleral outflow. Carbonic anhydrase inhibition, beta-blockade, mydriasis, and miosis represent the actions of other agents.'),
('32e640ce-72f2-5ce6-b8b2-f24d62638770'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the mechanism of acetazolamide in glaucoma?', '["Miosis resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Mydriasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Beta-blockade mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that decreases aqueous humor production by the ciliary body (used orally or intravenously)","Increases outflow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that decreases aqueous humor production by the ciliary body (used orally or intravenously)', 'Acetazolamide is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that decreases aqueous humor production by the ciliary body and is administered orally or intravenously. It does not act through miosis, mydriasis, beta-blockade, or increased outflow.'),
('415cf4bb-a897-5c8c-9c2d-f7a9a17b47c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the mechanism of tropicamide in ophthalmology?', '["Pupillary constriction resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An anticholinergic that blocks the pupillary sphincter (dilation) and the ciliary muscle (cycloplegia) for fundus examination","Accommodation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Reduces IOP without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta-blockade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An anticholinergic that blocks the pupillary sphincter (dilation) and the ciliary muscle (cycloplegia) for fundus examination', 'Tropicamide is an anticholinergic that blocks the pupillary sphincter, producing pupillary dilation, and the ciliary muscle, producing cycloplegia, for fundus examination. It does not constrict the pupil, lower intraocular pressure, or block beta-receptors.'),
('04918813-5aa5-5317-921b-90485fdadbe7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the mechanism of fluorescein in ophthalmology?', '["A local anesthetic resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A diagnostic dye that highlights corneal abrasions and is used in fluorescein angiography to study retinal blood flow","An anti-inflammatory mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A mydriatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An antibiotic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A diagnostic dye that highlights corneal abrasions and is used in fluorescein angiography to study retinal blood flow', 'Fluorescein is a diagnostic dye that stains corneal abrasions and is used in fluorescein angiography to study retinal blood flow. It is not an anesthetic, anti-inflammatory, mydriatic, or antibiotic.'),
('4d227367-e3c3-5e01-9c29-0a66a9563e59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the mechanism of ranibizumab (anti-VEGF) for AMD?', '["A beta-blocker resulting in decreased functional activity","An antibiotic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A monoclonal antibody fragment that binds and neutralizes VEGF-A","A steroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","inhibiting choroidal neovascularization in wet AMD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A monoclonal antibody fragment that binds and neutralizes VEGF-A', 'Ranibizumab is a monoclonal antibody fragment that binds and neutralizes VEGF-A, thereby inhibiting choroidal neovascularization in wet AMD. It is not a beta-blocker, antibiotic, or steroid.'),
('be392e0c-e985-59a4-8a39-bede578f257a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the mechanism of the Nd:YAG laser in ophthalmology?', '["It creates an opening in the posterior lens capsule (capsulotomy) for posterior capsule opacification (PCO) after cataract surgery","It photocoagulates blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It melts retinal tissue mediated by specific receptor activation","It removes corneal tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It opens the trabecular meshwork leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It creates an opening in the posterior lens capsule (capsulotomy) for posterior capsule opacification (PCO) after cataract surgery', 'The Nd:YAG laser creates an opening in the opacified posterior lens capsule (posterior capsulotomy) to treat posterior capsule opacification after cataract surgery. Photocoagulation, trabecular opening, and corneal reshaping are functions of other lasers.'),
('d34eee7b-3a7d-5edc-91d2-19548a44b9d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is photocoagulation (argon laser) used for?', '["Treating diabetic retinopathy (panretinal photocoagulation)","retinal tears accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and macular edema mediated by specific receptor activation","Corneal reshaping without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cataract removal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Treating diabetic retinopathy (panretinal photocoagulation)', 'Argon laser photocoagulation is used primarily for panretinal photocoagulation in diabetic retinopathy, and also for retinal tears and macular edema. It is not used for corneal reshaping or cataract removal.'),
('721c632a-ff7a-50f7-88c7-b7f8c814e7a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the most common cause of persistent hyposmia/anosmia?', '["Chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyposis","Aging (presbyosmia)","Congenital anosmia","Head trauma","Viral URI (post-viral olfactory loss)"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyposis', 'Chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyposis is the most commonly cited cause of persistent hyposmia/anosmia, acting through nasal obstruction and chronic inflammatory mucosal damage. Post-viral loss, aging, head trauma, and congenital anosmia are relatively less common.'),
('9c2667e0-07bb-5af8-80ca-78943fb87dea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the most common cause of olfactory loss in the elderly?', '["Parkinson disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Presbyosmia (age-related decline in olfactory function)","Nasal polyps mediated by specific receptor activation","Rhinosinusitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alzheimer disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Presbyosmia (age-related decline in olfactory function)', 'Aging causes progressive loss of olfactory receptor neurons, so presbyosmia is the most frequent cause of smell loss in the elderly. Parkinson and Alzheimer disease produce anosmia but account for a minority of cases, while nasal polyps and rhinosinusitis are more common in younger patients.'),
('947ee76e-5c1f-5a78-b23a-9f0d9faa8672'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is phantosmia?', '["Distorted smell resulting in decreased functional activity","A type of dysgeusia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of smell mediated by specific receptor activation","The perception of an odor that is not actually present (olfactory hallucination)","Heightened smell leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The perception of an odor that is not actually present (olfactory hallucination)', 'Phantosmia is an olfactory hallucination in which an odor is perceived when no odorant is actually present, reflecting olfactory cortex or peripheral pathway dysfunction. Dysgeusia is a taste disorder, anosmia is loss of smell, and distorted or heightened smell are separate derangements.'),
('f752060d-6563-5b64-b53f-f25a553eaed2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is parosmia?', '["A distorted perception of odors (usually real odors smell unpleasant/rotten)","Olfactory hallucination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heightened smell mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of taste without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of smell leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A distorted perception of odors (usually real odors smell unpleasant/rotten)', 'Parosmia is a distorted perception of real odorants, which often smell rotten or unpleasant, frequently seen after viral infection or head injury. In phantosmia no odorant is present, while anosmia is loss of smell and heightened smell (hyperosmia) is an unrelated disorder.'),
('0640b7bb-8687-5701-9436-d79e7d4b5798'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the most common cause of taste loss?', '["CN IX damage resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfactory loss (most patients complaining of taste loss actually have smell loss)","Aging mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII damage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medication side effects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory loss (most patients complaining of taste loss actually have smell loss)', 'Flavor depends heavily on olfaction, so most patients complaining of ''taste'' loss actually have olfactory loss, making it the commonest cause. Aging and medications modestly reduce gustatory function, whereas isolated CN VII or CN IX injury only rarely produces global taste loss.'),
('4b9bbba4-fbf1-551d-ac9d-aaf65d6b5bda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is burning mouth syndrome?', '["A salivary gland disorder resulting in decreased functional activity","A chronic condition with oral burning pain without obvious mucosal pathology; may be neuropathic or psychogenic","A vitamin deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","An allergic reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fungal infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chronic condition with oral burning pain without obvious mucosal pathology; may be neuropathic or psychogenic', 'Burning mouth syndrome is chronic intraoral burning pain without visible mucosal pathology and is usually neuropathic or psychogenic. Salivary gland disease, vitamin deficiency, allergy, and fungal infection can cause oral symptoms but do not define this syndrome.'),
('3f83eded-2905-565e-8e0b-9a417a23dfc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the taste pathway for the anterior 2/3 of the tongue?', '["CN X (vagus) resulting in decreased functional activity","CN V (trigeminal) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN IX (glossopharyngeal) mediated by specific receptor activation","CN XII (hypoglossal) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chorda tympani branch of CN VII (facial nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'Chorda tympani branch of CN VII (facial nerve)', 'Taste fibers from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue travel in the chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve (CN VII) to the solitary nucleus. CN IX and CN X serve the posterior tongue and epiglottis/pharynx respectively, while CN V handles general sensation and CN XII is purely motor.'),
('2b4f3d27-6f85-56fa-a0c3-c03416d55624'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the taste pathway for the posterior 1/3 of the tongue?', '["CN VII (facial nerve)","CN IX (glossopharyngeal nerve)","CN XII (hypoglossal)","CN V (trigeminal)","CN X (vagus)"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (glossopharyngeal nerve)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) transmits taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue to the solitary tract nucleus. CN VII serves the anterior two-thirds, CN X the epiglottis and pharynx, and CN V and CN XII have no gustatory function.'),
('3eb8ab22-18cc-5df8-be73-a2808085a6ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the taste pathway for the epiglottis and pharynx?', '["CN XII (hypoglossal)","CN V (trigeminal)","CN IX (glossopharyngeal)","CN X (vagus nerve)","CN VII (facial nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'CN X (vagus nerve)', 'Taste from the epiglottis and pharynx is carried by the vagus nerve (CN X) to the solitary nucleus. CN VII and CN IX serve the anterior and posterior tongue, whereas CN V mediates general sensation and CN XII is motor only.'),
('ad7d65a4-9071-56b7-af66-a5d58677bbd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the effect of aging on taste?', '["Complete loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Enhanced sensitivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","change mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of bitter taste only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mild decline in taste sensitivity due to reduced number of taste buds and changes in saliva production"]'::jsonb, 'Mild decline in taste sensitivity due to reduced number of taste buds and changes in saliva production', 'Aging reduces the number of taste buds and alters saliva production, producing a mild decline in taste sensitivity rather than complete loss. Abrupt complete loss, enhanced sensitivity, or selective loss of one quality are not characteristic of normal aging.'),
('84fb9eff-a061-5332-b190-8e7e1b081f9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the binocular visual field overlap in humans?', '["30 degrees resulting in decreased functional activity","90 degrees accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 120 degrees of the total 180-degree horizontal visual field","180 degrees without affecting the primary tissue pathway","60 degrees leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 120 degrees of the total 180-degree horizontal visual field', 'The monocular horizontal fields overlap centrally to give about 120 degrees of binocular vision from a total 180-degree field. The smaller values (30, 60, 90 degrees) greatly understate this overlap, and 180 degrees would incorrectly imply the entire field is binocular.'),
('0b0c88e0-f30c-5364-bbdc-5b249d9d1990'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the optic nerve head cupping-to-disc ratio in a normal eye?', '["1.0 resulting in decreased functional activity","Variable accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","0 mediated by specific receptor activation",">0.7 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Typically <0.5 (physiologic cupping)"]'::jsonb, 'Typically <0.5 (physiologic cupping)', 'A normal optic disc has a cup-to-disc ratio usually below 0.5 (physiologic cupping). Ratios above 0.7 or large asymmetry suggest glaucomatous damage, and a ratio of zero is unusual, so ''variable'' is not a defined normal value.'),
('c985d1ee-0065-5f47-9db0-2910d22f7561'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What are the retinal blood vessels?', '["The central retinal artery and vein branch over the retina; they are end-arteries","The ciliary vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and occlusion causes retinal infarction mediated by specific receptor activation","The anterior ciliary vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The choroidal vessels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central retinal artery and vein branch over the retina; they are end-arteries', 'The central retinal artery and vein travel within the optic nerve and branch over the inner retina as end-arteries, so their occlusion causes retinal infarction. The ciliary and choroidal vessels nourish the outer retina and uvea rather than the inner retina supplied by the central retinal vessels.'),
('9cde0c15-fc09-5634-9840-fd1d4b568c8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the normal width of the optic nerve on imaging?', '["4-6 mm (diameter)","about 50 mm from eye to chiasm","8-10 mm","6-8 mm","2-3 mm"]'::jsonb, '4-6 mm (diameter)', 'The optic nerve has a normal diameter of about 4-6 mm, whereas its total length from eye to chiasm is roughly 5 cm (50 mm). Values below 2-3 mm or above 6-8 mm fall outside the expected orbital nerve width.'),
('3aa509f6-7462-5bd2-aef2-89ac11a677d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the normal number of ganglion cells in the human retina?', '["About 1.2-1.5 million per eye","000 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 million mediated by specific receptor activation","500 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","100 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 1.2-1.5 million per eye', 'The human retina contains roughly 1.2-1.5 million ganglion cells per eye, whose axons form the optic nerve. The other numeric answers are far below or above this accepted range and are not physiologically plausible.'),
('089858ca-dd8a-5bbf-ad23-faf509cb39b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the tigroid (tessellated) fundus?', '["A pathological finding resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A sign of uveitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A sign of retinal detachment mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A normal variant in darkly pigmented individuals where the choroidal vessels are visible through the retina due to less RPE pigment","A sign of AMD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A normal variant in darkly pigmented individuals where the choroidal vessels are visible through the retina due to less RPE pigment', 'A tigroid (tessellated) fundus is a normal variant in darkly pigmented individuals in which choroidal vessels show through a thinly pigmented retinal pigment epithelium. It is not a pathological finding and is not a sign of uveitis, retinal detachment, or age-related macular degeneration.'),
('886974d3-cfaa-5ee6-abb9-dcd3cb158bb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the red reflex in ophthalmoscopy?', '["A sign of health resulting in decreased functional activity","vitreous hemorrhage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A pathological finding mediated by specific receptor activation","or retinal detachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The reddish-orange reflection from the retina seen during direct ophthalmoscopy; absence can indicate cataract"]'::jsonb, 'The reddish-orange reflection from the retina seen during direct ophthalmoscopy; absence can indicate cataract', 'The red reflex is the reddish-orange reflection of light off the retina seen during direct ophthalmoscopy, and its absence may indicate a cataract or other opacity, vitreous hemorrhage, or retinal detachment. It is an optical sign of a clear media and intact retina rather than itself a health marker or a specific disease.'),
('d9994c66-03b6-5ee1-923c-d5a73b9a5646'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is a posterior vitreous detachment (PVD) on ultrasound?', '["undulating membrane (posterior hyaloid) attached to the optic nerve head (Weiss ring) with adhesions to the retina","Retinal detachment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thin mediated by specific receptor activation","Choroidal detachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vitreous hemorrhage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'undulating membrane (posterior hyaloid) attached to the optic nerve head (Weiss ring) with adhesions to the retina', 'On ultrasound, a posterior vitreous detachment appears as a thin undulating membrane (the collapsed posterior hyaloid) that remains attached at the optic nerve head, producing a Weiss ring. Retinal and choroidal detachments form thicker, fixed membranes, and vitreous hemorrhage is a separate finding.'),
('71d70cf4-d537-588b-91b1-3fe6ba9b424c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the normal size of the cornea?', '["12-13 mm resulting in decreased functional activity","8-9 mm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 11-12 mm horizontally and 10-11 mm vertically","9-10 mm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","10-14 mm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 11-12 mm horizontally and 10-11 mm vertically', 'The adult cornea measures about 11-12 mm horizontally and 10-11 mm vertically. Values below 9-10 mm or above 13 mm are outside the normal range and suggest microcornea or an enlarged (buphthalmic) eye.'),
('d558a68e-bcfc-5bb1-bb83-427db6d54ce4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the function of the optic nerve head lamina cribrosa?', '["Supplies blood to the retina resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Drains aqueous humor mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Produces myelin without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Provides structural support to the optic nerve axons as they leave the eye; its weakness is the site of damage in glaucoma"]'::jsonb, 'Provides structural support to the optic nerve axons as they leave the eye; its weakness is the site of damage in glaucoma', 'The lamina cribrosa is the perforated scleral region that supports retinal ganglion cell axons as they leave the eye, and its structural weakness is the principal site of damage in glaucoma. It plays no role in blood supply, aqueous drainage, or myelin formation.'),
('549a3cf0-7312-5436-9b54-ec374b85a193'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'Which option best describes the three layers of the eye wall?', '["vascular (middle)","sclera","Fibrous (outer)","and neural (inner)","Episclera"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous (outer)', 'The eyeball is composed of three coats: the outer fibrous layer (cornea and sclera), the middle vascular (uveal) layer, and the inner neural layer (retina). ''Fibrous (outer)'' names this outermost tunic; sclera and episclera are a part and a surface tissue of it, while the vascular and neural coats are the middle and inner tunics.'),
